<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>nominative masculine n-stem ending</li><li>used to form the nominative singular n-stem (weak) adjective and subsequent nominalised form</li><li>used to form masculine agents, usually from verbs</li><li>Ending forming adverbs</li><li>Forms the nominative, accusative, and genitive plural of o-stem feminine nouns and u-stem masculine nouns</li><li>Forms the genitive plural of a-stem nouns</li><li>Forms the genitive and dative singular of u-stem nouns</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ô</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>-o</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ô</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ǫ̂</i>, from the Proto-Indo-European genitive plural ending <i>&#42;-oHom</i> as attached to thematic (<i>eh₂</i>-stem and <i>o</i>-stem) nouns.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-oc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-od</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-odon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ode</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>verbal suffix</i>) used to form the infinitive of most strong, class 1 weak, and preterite-present verbs (exceptions are verbs like <i>flēon</i> (“to flee”))</li><li>Forms adverbs with ablative direction.</li><li>Forms the accusative, genitive, and dative singular and nominative and accusative plural of <i>n</i>-stem nouns</li><li>alternative form of <i>-on</i> (strong plural past indicative suffix)</li><li>Forms the following inflections of weak adjectives&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Masculine and feminine accusative, genitive, dative, and instrumental singular</li><li>Neuter genitive, dative, and instrumental singular</li><li>Masculine, feminine, and neuter nominative and accusative plural</li></ol><li>(<i>Late Old English</i>) alternative form of <i>-um</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-aną</i> and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-janą</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-anā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-anē</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-an</i>, from a conflation of several Proto-Germanic <i>n-</i>stem suffixes: <i>&#42;-anų</i> (accusative singular), <i>&#42;-aniz</i> (genitive singular, nominative/vocative plural) and <i>&#42;-anunz</i> (accusative plural).</p><p>Supplanted Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ōn</i> during the prehistory of Old English except for in relic forms such as <i>ēastron</i> and <i>foldu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Early Old English</i>) alternative form of <i>-ena</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ende</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Old English</i>) alternative form of <i>-enne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-enne</i></li></ol><p>From <i>-enne</i>, with the first /e/ shifting due to influence from <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Plural ending of nominative and accusative cases, originally of a-stem masculine nouns, later extended to other nouns.</li><li>alternative form of <i>-ast</i>; marks the second person singular present indicative of class II weak verbs</li></ol><p>Perhaps from the Proto-Germanic accusative plural ending *<i>-anz</i>, with regularly lost <i>-n-</i> before a fricative, or perhaps from the nominative plural *<i>-ōs</i>, a voiceless variety of the regular ending *<i>-ōz</i>, or a merger of both. Akin to Saterland Frisian <i>-s</i>, West Frisian <i>-s</i>, Old Saxon <i>-os</i> (Low German <i>-s</i>), Dutch <i>-s</i>, Swedish <i>-ar</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form the second person singular present indicative of class II weak verbs</li><li>alternative form of <i>-ost</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ōs</i> with paragogic <i>-t</i> added from when <i>þū</i> follows (compare Anglian Old English <i>-as</i>), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ōsi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-eh₂yési</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-aþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Third person singular present indicative ending of class 2 weak verbs.</li><li>First, second and third person plural present indicative ending of strong verbs, class 1 weak verbs, and class 3 weak verbs.</li><li>(<i>used without postpositioned "ġē"</i>) Second person plural imperative ending of strong verbs, class 1 weak verbs, and class 3 weak verbs.</li><li>alternative form of <i>-oþ</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ōþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ōþi</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-anþ</i> (“third person plural indicative ending of weak verbs”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-anþi</i> (“third person plural indicative ending of weak verbs”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>-ad</i>, Swabian <i>-ad</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-bǣre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-blooded</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-blōdī</i>. Equivalent to <i>blōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>-bluoti</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>bearer</li></ol><p>From the verb <i>beran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>bearing, having</li><li>having the qualities of, characterized by</li><li>denoting likeness or similarity to</li><li>producing</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bēriz</i> (“bearing, carrying”). Akin to Old Frisian <i>-ber</i>, Old Saxon <i>-bāri</i>, and Old High German <i>-bāri</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Creates masculine concrete nouns (including proper nouns) from adjectives, verbs, or other nouns, usually typifying a condition or state, or characterising a diminutive aspect.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ukō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ukô</i>, equivalent to <i>-oc</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix forming diminutives from nouns, often displaying i-mutation</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ikīn</i>, <i>&#42;-ukīn</i>, equivalent to <i>-oc</i> + <i>-en</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>-ki</i>. More at -kin.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix applied to verbs and deverbal nouns to form other verbs with an intensive or frequentative force</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ukōn</i>, <i>&#42;-ikōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ukōną</i>, <i>&#42;-ikōną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>-kia</i>, Old Saxon <i>-akōn</i>, Old High German <i>-ichōn</i>, Old Norse <i>-ka</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>denoting an art or science: -ology, -ics, -craft</li><li>denoting strength or skill</li></ol><p>Identical to the noun <i>cræft</i> (“art, skill, strength”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix denoting origin, derivation, or likeness</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kundaz</i> (“born, natured”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵn̥h₁tós</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>-kundr</i>, Gothic <i>-𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (-kunds).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-cund</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>used to form the first and third person singular preterite indicative of some class I and class III weak verbs</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li><i>used to derive abstract nouns</i>: -dom</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-dōm</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-dōmaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forming adverbs from adjectives; -ly</li><li>forms adjectives from nouns, often with the meaning "having" <i>(typically causes i-umlaut)</i></li><li>Used to form the first person singular present indicative of strong verbs and class I weak verbs</li><li>Marks the dative singular of <i>a</i>-stem and <i>ō</i>-stem nouns</li><li>Marks the accusative and genitive and the nominative and accusative plural of ō-stem feminine nouns</li><li>Marks the nominative singular of feminine n-stem nouns</li><li>Forms feminine agent nouns, often as a counterpart to masculine agent nouns in -a</li><li>Forms nouns referring to female people from nouns referring to male people</li><li>(<i>used with postpositioned "ġē"</i>) Second person plural imperative ending of strong verbs, class 1 weak verbs, and class 3 weak verbs.</li></ol><p>From West Germanic *<i>-ā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ê</i>, which survives otherwise only in Gothic (and possibly Old Norse).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ī</i>. Beyond that, generally from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ijaz</i>, but also partly merged with <i>&#42;-jaz</i> and athematic <i>&#42;-iz</i> (compare Proto-Norse <i>-ᛁᛉ</i> (-iʀ)). Note though that <i>-wintre</i> is from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-wintruz</i>, since Proto-Germanic <i>u</i>-stem adjectives became <i>ja</i>-stems in West Germanic. Other suffixes derived from u-stem nouns, such as <i>-flēre</i>, might also have been zero derivations in Proto-Germanic.</p><p>Likely borrowed from the subjunctive singular Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ē</i> after high vowel apocope resulted in the loss of inherited Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-u</i> in heavy stem verbs, and then analogically extended to light stem verbs.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ē</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ai</i> (<i>a</i>-stem), <i>&#42;-ōi</i> (<i>ō</i>-stem), from the Proto-Indo-European dative singular ending <i>&#42;-ey</i> or locative singular ending <i>&#42;-i</i> as attached to <i>eh₂</i>-stem and <i>o</i>-stem nouns.</p><p>A combination of multiple sources:</p><p>This suffix was extended to the nominative plural (replacing <i>-a</i> &lt; Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ō</i>) in Old English itself.</p><p>Likely derived from the weakening of the plural subjunctive ending <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-īeġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-iċġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form the past participle of class I weak verbs</li><li>alternative form of <i>-od</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>adjectival suffix</i>) suffix applied to nouns meaning "having", "possessing", "provided with" or "equipped with"</li><li>Used to form the first and third person singular preterite indicative of most class I weak verbs</li></ol><p>Akin to the past participle ending <i>-ed</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form the second person singular preterite indicative of class I weak verbs</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form the plural preterite indicative of class I weak verbs</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-iġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-īeġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-gian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-iht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ol</i></li><li>(<i>causes i-mutation</i>) agent and instrumental suffix creating nouns from verbs</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-a-l-</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ilaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix forming nouns denoting process or action from verbs</li></ol><p>Possibly a metathetic variant of earlier Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-þlą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-else</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>masculine suffix for inanimate objects</i>) suffix creating nouns from verbs</li></ol><p>A metathetic form of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-islī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>feminine suffix for inanimate objects</i>) suffix creating nouns from verbs</li></ol><p>A metathetic form of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-islī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-eld</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-uma</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>causes i-mutation</i>) used to create diminutive neuter nouns</li><li>(<i>often causes i-mutation</i>) used to create feminine nouns from other nouns</li><li>(<i>causes i-mutation</i>) adjectival suffix meaning "material made of, consisting of”, also sometimes “characteristic of”.</li><li>(<i>verbal suffix</i>) past participle ending of strong verbs</li><li>(<i>adjective suffix</i>) meaning belonging to or characterised by</li><li>(<i>causes i-mutation</i>) forms nouns from class 1 weak verbs</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-īn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-īną</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ini</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-inī</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-īn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-īnaz</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-an</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-anaz</i>.</p><p>Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-an</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-an-</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-īni</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-īniz</i>. Sometimes with geminate -nn-, probably due to confusion with the feminine suffix from <i>&#42;-inī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forms the genitive plural of n-stem masculine, n-stem feminine, and some ō-stem feminine nouns</li><li>forms the weak genitive plural of adjectives</li></ol><p>(This etymology is missing or incomplete. Please add to it, or discuss it at the Etymology scriptorium. )</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix denoting the agent of an action; -er</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-andi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-andz</i>, taken from the present participle form of verbs. See <i>-ende</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>equivalent of English <i>-ing</i>&#59; suffix for present participles</li><li>used to form neuter nouns</li><li>alternative form of <i>-anne</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-andī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-andz</i>. Akin to Latin <i>-āns</i>, <i>-ēns</i>; Ancient Greek <i>-ων</i> (-ōn), Persian ـنده (-ande).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-andiją</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>-indi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forming adjectives from verbs with the sense of "able to, capable of"</li></ol><p>Combined form, created from <i>-ende</i> (present participle ending) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i> (“-ly, like”) and from <i>-end</i> (agent suffix, ultimately from the present participle) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i> (“-ly, like”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Old English</i>) alternative form of <i>-enne</i></li></ol><p>See <i>-enne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-unga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms the inflected infinitive of verbs.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-annjē</i>, dative inflection of the infinitive <i>-an</i>; equivalent to <i>-an</i> + <i>-e</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-or</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>masculine agent suffix, originally applied only to nouns</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ārī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ārijaz</i>.</p><p>Uncertain ultimate origin, usually held to be from Latin <i>-ārius</i>. Gąsiorowski instead suggests that <i>*-ārijaz</i> is a native formation; he derives it from earlier <i>*-azrijaz</i>, which he etymologises as a zero-grade form of <i>&#42;-sōr</i> suffixed with <i>&#42;-ih₂</i>, creating a suffix <i>*-sr-ih₂</i> for forming feminine agent nouns, which were then masculinised by attaching <i>&#42;-ós</i>. He also suggests a relation to Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-astrijā</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>-ere</i>, Old Saxon <i>-āri</i>, Old High German <i>-āri</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>A frequentative suffix used to form verbs</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-urōn</i> (frequentative suffix), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-urōną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>-arōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix meaning "a place".</li><li>Suffix meaning "toward a place"&#59; alternative form of <i>-erne</i></li></ol><p>From the adjectival use of <i>-ærn</i> (“noun suffix denoting location”), from <i>ærn</i> (“place”).</p><p>Variant of <i>-erne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Adjective suffix occurring in names for directions</li></ol><p>Akin to Old High German <i>-rōni</i>; see <i>-ern</i> for more.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>genitive case&#47;possessive marker for a-stem nouns, indicating that an object belongs to the noun</li><li>forms the strong masculine&#47;neuter genitive singular of adjectives</li><li>used in formation of adverbs, originally from the genitive of masculine and neuter nouns, but later added also to feminine nouns by analogy</li><li>Alternative form of <i>-est</i>&#59; marks the second person singular of class I weak verbs</li></ol><p>The a-stem genitive singular ending is derived from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-as</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-as</i>. Based on the voiceless *s, Ringe 2006 argues that this ending was analogically taken from the genitive singular determiner <i>&#42;þas</i>, from <i>&#42;tósyo</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-sn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix forming feminine nouns, originally from verbs</li><li>alternative form of <i>-ost</i> (“used to form superlative adjectives and adverbs”)</li><li>Forms the second-person singular of class I weak verbs</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ustaz</i>, <i>&#42;-istaz</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ēs</i> with paragogic <i>-t</i> added from when <i>þū</i> follows (compare Anglian Old English <i>-es</i>), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ēz</i> (with <i>-s</i> by analogy with present forms), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-eh₁si</i> (in <i>&#42;dʰédʰeh₁si</i>, form of <i>&#42;dʰédʰeh₁ti</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>female agent suffix, especially denoting one who is skilled in a particular occupation or craft</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-astrijā</i>, feminine agent suffix equivalent to <i>&#42;-ārī</i> (> <i>-ere</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix forming nouns from verbs, adjectives, and other nouns (<i>sometimes causing i-mutation</i>), originally as the action of verbs ending in <i>-ettan</i> (“-ise”) (compare English <i>-ism</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ati</i>, <i>&#42;-iti</i>, <i>&#42;-uti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-atją</i>, <i>&#42;-itją</i>, <i>&#42;-utją</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ettan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>used to form verbs from nouns or adjectives (compare English <i>-ate</i>, <i>-ize</i>), frequently having a causative force.</li><li>verbal suffix denoting frequency or intensification</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-attjan</i>, <i>&#42;-ittjan</i>, <i>&#42;-uttjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-atjaną</i>, <i>&#42;-itjaną</i>, <i>&#42;-utjaną</i> (verbal suffix). Related to English <i>-ise</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Third person singular present indicative ending of class 1 weak verbs</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-feald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix denoting multiplication, or consisting of so many parts; -fold</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-falþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-falþaz</i> (“-fold”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;poltos</i> (“-fold”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>-fald</i>, Old Saxon <i>-fald</i>, German <i>-falt</i>, Old Norse <i>-faldr</i>, Gothic <i>-𐍆𐌰𐌻𐌸𐍃</i> (-falþs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-feald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-footed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-fōtī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-fōtijaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>-fuozi</i>. Equivalent to <i>fōt</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>having a certain number of floors: -story, -stories</li></ol><p>Possibly goes back to a Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flōrijaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>flōr</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-fēte</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fōt</i> (“foot”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i> (adjectival suffix), but without i-mutation.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>full of; -ful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-fullaz</i> (“-ful”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fullaz</i> (“full”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ful</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>firm, secure</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>fæst</i> (“secure, firm”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ig</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Diminutive suffix, used especially in familiar names for pets.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-gō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-gjô</i>, <i>&#42;-gô</i> (suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>adjectival suffix</i>) suffix meaning perpetual or continual</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix used to denote a region or territory</li><li>suffix used in placenames meaning region of, or district of</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gawi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gawją</i>, <i>&#42;gawjō</i>, <i>&#42;gawô</i> (“country, region, environment”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form verbs from nouns and adjectives, often with an intensive effect</li></ol><p>From the fusion of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-igōn</i>, <i>&#42;-īgōn</i>, <i>&#42;-agōn</i>, <i>&#42;-ugōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-gōną</i>, equivalent to <i>-iġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>-igōn</i>, <i>-agōn</i>, Old Norse <i>-ga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forming nouns of condition or quality, from nouns or adjectives</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haidu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haiduz</i> (“state, condition”) (originally a noun, represented by Old English <i>hād</i>). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>-hēd</i>, Dutch <i>-heid</i>, Old High German <i>-heit</i> (German <i>-heit</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>bent; inclined toward, tending toward; sloping toward</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;halþaz</i> (“inclined”). Akin to Old Frisian <i>-hald</i>, Old High German <i>-hald</i>, Old Norse <i>-hallr</i>, Old English <i>heald</i> (“bent, inclined toward”), Old English <i>hielde</i> (“slope”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-handed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-handī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-handijaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>hand</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-hearted</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-hert(ī)</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-hertaz</i>, derived from <i>&#42;hertô</i> (“heart”) (Old English <i>heorte</i>). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>-hert</i>, Old High German <i>-herz</i>, Gothic <i>-𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍄𐍃</i> (-hairts).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>-hīwede</i> (“-oid”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>formed</li></ol><p>From <i>hīewian</i> (“to form, shape”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-hlīepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Forms adjectives from certain nouns. Often drops the initial h-, taking the form -līepe.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-hlaupijaz</i> (“running”), used to form adjectives, derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlaupaną</i> from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;klewb-</i> (“to spring, stumble”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>-lōpi</i> and Old Norse <i>-hleypr</i>. More at hlēapan.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-hwugu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-hwugu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-hwugu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>some- <i>(forms indefinite pronouns and adverbs from interrogatives)</i></li></ol><p>Unknown. There are no known cognates.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-hwugu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms adjectives from numerals denoting the amount (in hundreds of shillings) of wergeld a person is worth.</li></ol><p>From <i>hund</i> (“hundred”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i> (adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms demonyms from toponyms ending in -<i>hām</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-iġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms verbs from nouns and adjectives.</li></ol><p>By syncope from earlier <i>&#42;-ōjan</i>, a northern Ingvaeonic innovated form of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-oc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>feminine</i>) noun suffix denoting a female agent</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ggjā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-gjǭ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-ikyeh₂</i>. Cognate with Middle Low German <i>-igge</i>, <i>-ige</i>, <i>-ege</i>, Middle Dutch <i>-igge</i>, <i>-egge</i> (modern Dutch <i>-egge</i>), Ancient Greek <i>-ισσα</i> (-issa).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-iġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-eyed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic compounds ending in <i>&#42;-augī</i> (i.e. <i>&#42;augā</i> (“eye”) with the adjective-forming suffix <i>&#42;-ī</i>). By surface analysis, <i>ēage</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>. Cognate with Proto-Norse <i>ᚨᚢᚷᛁᛉ</i> (augiʀ), Old Norse <i>eygr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form the present participle of class II weak verbs</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form the inflected infinitive of class II weak verbs</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-y; forms adjectives from nouns and verbs</li><li>Suffix creating diminutive nouns from other nouns.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ag, -ig</i>. Still distinct in the earliest texts.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-i</i>, <i>&#42;-ī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-j-</i>, <i>&#42;-ij-</i> (diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form the first person singular present indicative of class II weak verbs</li><li>Used to form the singular present tense subjunctive of class II weak verbs</li><li>(<i>with postpositioned "ġē"</i>) Used to form the second person plural imperative of class II weak verbs</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-iende</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-gian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ig</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>having, bearing, possessing; occasionally causes i-mutation of preceding vowels</li><li>characterized by; full of, covered with; equivalent to -ous</li><li>inclined to</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-aht</i>. Akin to Old Saxon <i>-aht</i>, Old High German <i>-aht</i> (German <i>-icht</i>), Icelandic <i>-óttr</i>, Old English <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>diminutive noun suffix</li></ol><p>A metathetic alteration of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ikīna</i> (diminutive suffix) + <i>&#42;-il-</i> (diminutive suffix).</p><p>Alternately, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ingilą</i>, <i>&#42;-ingilaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-inċel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forming nouns from verbs, indicating action, process or material.</li><li>Forming derivatives of nouns with sense of ‘belonging to, son of’.</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>-ung</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ing</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ingaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-unga</i> (adverbial suffix)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>of or relating to: -ish</li><li>suffix forming nouns with a collective or group force</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-isk</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-iską</i>, <i>&#42;-iskiją</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-isċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms nouns referring to female people. Attested only in the noun <i>abbadisse</i>.</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>-issa</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>-ῐσσᾰ</i> (-ĭssă)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ost</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-estre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>noun suffix denoting activity (compare Modern English <i>-ation</i>)</li><li>practice, action or process of</li><li>act of</li><li>gift</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-laik</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-laiką</i> (“jump, spring, sport”), from <i>&#42;laiką</i> (“play, sport, fight, activity”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leyg-</i> (“to bounce, shake, tremble”). Related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laikaz</i> (“game, dance, hymn, sport”).</p><p>Akin to Old English <i>lāc</i> (“sport, play”). More at English <i>lake</i> (Etymology 2).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>only in given names</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>legacy, heir</li><li>legate, commander</li></ol></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laibō</i>. More at Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Anulaibaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>without, free from, devoid of</li><li>bereft of</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-laus</i>. Synchronically, from <i>lēas</i> (“false, devoid of, free from, without”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-līest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-līċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-linga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-hlīepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-līest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>frequentative verbal suffix&#59; indicating repetition</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-lōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-lōną</i>, perhaps related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ulaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li><i>adjective-forming suffix meaning 'like' or 'relating to</i>'</li><li><i>denoting multiplicity</i>: -fold</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-līk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-līkaz</i> (adjectival suffix, originally meaning “having form of”), derived from <i>&#42;līką</i> (“body,” earlier “form”), whence Old English <i>līċ</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>-lik</i>, Old Saxon <i>-līk</i>, Old High German <i>-līh</i>, Old Norse <i>-ligr</i>, and Gothic <i>-𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (-leiks).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ly (adverbial suffix)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-līkê</i>, equivalent to <i>-līċ</i> (adjectival suffix) + <i>-e</i> (adverbial suffix). Compare Old Norse <i>-liga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>-līċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>-līċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>West Saxon</i>) suffix denoting lack, want or absence of: -lessness</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-lausiþu</i>, equivalent to <i>-lēas</i> (“-less”) +&lrm; <i>-þ</i> (“-th, -ness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>adverbial suffix denoting direction, state or position</li><li>suffix used in forming personal nouns</li><li>suffix forming diminutives</li><li>dynasty, lineage</li></ol><p>From alteration of suffixal use of Old English <i>lang</i> (“long”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ling</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ilingaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>adverbial suffix</li></ol><p><i>-ling</i> (adverbial suffix) +&lrm; <i>-a</i> (adverbial suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-hlīepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-līest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-linga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-līest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>verbal suffix (combining form) denoting action, process or making (compare English <i>-ify</i>), from <i>lǣcan</i>, a derivative of <i>lāc</i> noun suffix denoting activity or process</li><li>to become, turn, grow</li></ol><p>Etymology treeOld English <i>lǣċan</i>Old English <i>-læcan</i></p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laikijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laikijaną</i>; related to Old English <i>lāc</i> (“sport, play, action”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-uma</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li><i>denotes having a certain number of people</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps dates back to a Proto-Germanic <i>*-mannijaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>mann</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-most; used to form the superlative of certain words, such as cardinal directions</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-umistaz</i>, from the comparative suffix <i>&#42;-umô</i> + the regular superlative suffix <i>&#42;-istaz</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-minded; also -spirited, -hearted</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-mōdaz</i>, derived from <i>&#42;mōdaz</i> (“mind”) (Old English <i>mōd</i>). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>-mōd</i>, Old High German <i>-muot</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>used to denote a fixed amount of something at a time</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-mālum</i>. Identical to the dative/instrumental plural of <i>mǣl</i> (“time, occasion”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms the strong masculine accusative singular of adjectives</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form abstract nouns, usually from adjectives but also from verbs and other nouns, denoting quality or state&#59; -ness</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-nassī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-nes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix applied to nouns and adjectives to form verbs</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-inōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-inōną</i>. Compare Old Frisian <i>-nia</i>, Old Saxon <i>-nōn</i>, Old High German <i>-inōn</i>, Old Norse <i>-na</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>day(s) old</li><li>days lasting; -day</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-nahtijaz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>-nættr</i>. Equivalent to <i>niht</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-unga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-nes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-nes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form gerunds, a type of verbal nouns, from verbs.</li></ol><p>Unknown. Possibly related to nōþ.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-unga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-nes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-nes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(Anglian,&#32;Kentish) alternative form of <i>-e</i>, as used to form the first person singular present indicative of strong verbs and class I weak verbs</li><li>alternative form of <i>-u</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>noun suffix used to form descriptive names from adjectives</li><li>diminutive suffix</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-uk</i>, <i>&#42;-ik</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ukaz</i>, <i>&#42;-ikaz</i>. Akin to Old Saxon <i>-ik-</i>, Middle Low German <i>-ken</i>, Middle Dutch <i>-kin</i>. More at English <i>-kin</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form the past participle of class II weak verbs</li><li>Forms adjectives from nouns and class 2 weak past participles, indicating the possession of a thing or a quality.</li></ol><p>From a merger of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ōdaz</i> (adjective suffix) (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-eh₂tos</i>) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ōdaz</i> (class 2 weak past participle).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms the first and third person singular preterite indicative and singular subjunctive indicative of class II weak verbs</li></ol><p>From the merger of several Proto-Germanic inflections.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form the second person singular preterite indicative of class II weak verbs</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms the plural preterite indicative of class II weak verbs</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form adjectives (usually from verbs) denoting inclination or tendency toward (compare English <i>-ative</i>)</li><li>apt to, able to</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ul</i>, <i>&#42;-il</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ulaz</i>, <i>&#42;-ilaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-eld</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Marks the plural preterite indicative of strong verbs.</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) alternative form of <i>-en</i> (suffix forming past participles)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-unga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-enne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix forming adjectives from verbs denoting tendency or causation</li><li>Suffix variant found on masculine a-stem nouns</li><li>Forms the comparative degree of adverbs</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ar-</i>. Akin to Old High German <i>-ar</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-raz</i>. Akin to Old Saxon <i>-or</i>, Old High German <i>-ur</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-erian</i>: a frequentative suffix</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form superlative adjectives and adverbs</li><li>alternative form of <i>-est</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-et</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-utją</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>used to form concrete nouns from verbs</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ōþu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ōþuz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms ordinal numbers from <i>twēntigoþa</i> (“20th”) to <i>hundendleftigoþa</i> (“110th”) (and possibly unattested <i>&#42;hundtwelftigoþa</i> (“120th”))</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tegunþ</i> (“decad”, akin to <i>&#42;tehun</i> "ten") in phrases such as Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þriddjō tegunþ</i> (“the third decad”) which came to be used as ordinals, leading the initial ordinal + <i>*-teg-</i> to be reanalysed as a cardinal number suffixed with <i>&#42;tigiwi</i> (> Old English <i>-tiġ</i>) and the remaining <i>-anþ</i>/<i>-unþ</i> as an ordinal suffix. This was then supplied with <i>*-ō</i> (> Old English <i>-a</i>) like the other ordinals, resulting in a <i>-anþō</i> or <i>-unþō</i> that regularly develops to <i>-oþa</i>.</p><p>Alternaitvely, a later analogical formation from <i>seofoþa</i> (“seventh”), <i>eahtoþa</i> (“eighth”), <i>nigoþa</i> (“ninth”), and <i>teogoþa</i>, alternative form of <i>tēoþa</i> (“tenth”), with the <i>-o-</i> of the root reanalysed as belonging to the suffix (etymologically <i>-da</i>, <i>-ta</i>, <i>-þa</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form the comparative degree of adjectives</li><li>Used to form the genitive plural of adjectives</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-iʀō</i>, <i>&#42;-ōʀō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-izô</i> or Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ōzô</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>count of</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-raþ</i> (“tally, tale, reckoning”). Akin to Gothic <i>𐍂𐌰𐌸𐌾𐍉</i> (raþjō, “number, reckoning”), Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (garaþjan, “to count”), Old English <i>rǣden</i> (“accounting, reckoning; condition”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>masculine agent suffix, originally applied only to nouns</li><li>Marks the strong feminine genitive, dative, and instrumental singular of adjectives.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ārijaz</i>, possibly from Latin <i>-ārius</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-raþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-erian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>combining form</i>) suffix appended to nouns to indicate office, ownership, guardianship, governance or dominion, equivalent to English <i>-dom</i></li></ol><p>From <i>rīċe</i> (“dominion, realm”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix meaning full of or like (compare <i>-ful</i>, <i>-ous</i>)</li><li>brave, noble, or renowned</li></ol><p>From <i>rōf</i> (“number of soldiers, array; fierce”). Akin to Old Saxon <i>rōf</i> (“numerous, vigorous, strong”), Old High German <i>ruob</i> (“numerous”), <i>ruoba</i> (“a number, multitude”), Old English <i>ġerēfa</i> (“reeve, official”). More at English <i>sheriff</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form the nominative&#47;accusative plural of z-stem nouns</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-rīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>noun suffix denoting condition or state</li><li>suffix indicating activity</li><li>area of, jurisdiction of</li></ol><p>From <i>rǣden</i> (“condition, state”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raidīniz</i> (“arrangement, ordinance, authority”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(often&#32;affects the value or quality of preceding consonants,&#32;may or may not cause i-mutation) Feminine noun suffix forming nouns from adjectives and verbs</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-issju</i>, <i>&#42;-ussju</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-isjō</i>, <i>&#42;-usjō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>saying, speaking</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>noun-forming suffix: <i>-ship</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-skapi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-skapiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-sċiepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-sċiepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-sċiepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>verbal suffix added to adjectives, nouns, and (rarely) verb roots, generally causing i-mutation in the stem</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-isōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-isōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>relating to sight: -seen, -visible, -sighted</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-siunī</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>-siuni</i>. Equivalent to <i>sīen</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>, literally “with a certain sight.”</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms feminine ō-stem nouns denoting a tool or instrument.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-snō</i> (<i>&#42;-asnō</i>, <i>&#42;-isnō</i>, <i>&#42;-usnō</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-sneh₂</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form the second person singular present indicative of strong verbs and certain Class I weak verbs</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix found in many placenames denoting "place" or "place of"</li><li>place, area; provenance of, office of, jurisdiction of</li></ol><p>From <i>stōw</i> (“place”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stōō</i> (“place”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steh₂w-</i> (“to set, place”), from <i>&#42;steh₂-</i> (“to stand”). Akin to Old Norse <i>-stó</i> (“place of”), Old Frisian <i>stō</i> (“place”), Gothic <i>𐍃𐍄𐍉𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (stōjan, “to judge, place”). Compare the similar usage in forming toponyms in the cognate Indo-Iranian suffix <i>-stan</i>. See also <i>stow</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-stringed</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>strenġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>characterized by some specific condition or quality; same as</li><li>having or exemplifying</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-sam</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-samaz</i> (“same as”). Akin to Old Frisian <i>-sum</i>, Old High German <i>-sam</i>, Old Norse <i>-samr</i>, Gothic <i>-𐍃𐌰𐌼𐍃</i> (-sams), <i>-𐍃𐌰𐌼𐌰</i> (-sama, “same as”), Old English <i>sam</i> (“whether, or”), Old English <i>same</i> (“same”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>a resident or inhabitant of (<i>usually used with placenames</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;sǣta</i> (“inhabitant, dweller”). Akin to Old Saxon <i>-sētio</i>, Old High German <i>-sāzo</i> (German <i>-saß</i>), Old English <i>sittan</i> (“to sit”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>residents, dwellers: <i>forms names of peoples and places</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-sātī</i>, originally meaning "residents" or "dwellers." Cognate with Old High German <i>-sāti</i>. Ultimately related to <i>sittan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-þ</i> and <i>-þu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Alternative form of <i>-de</i> used to form the first and third person singular preterite indicative of certain class I weak verbs</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-tīene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>West Saxon</i>) -teen; used to form numbers from 13-19</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tehun</i> (“ten”); doublet of <i>tīen</i> (“ten”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ty (used to form numerals denoting multiples of ten)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tigiwiz</i>, plural of <i>&#42;teguz</i> (“group of ten”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>homestead, village, town</li></ol><p>From <i>tūn</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tūn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tūną</i>, from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;dūnom</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewh₂-</i> (“to finish, come full circle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>ending used to form abstract nouns from adjectives (compare Modern English <i>-ness</i>), normally causing i-mutation, and remaining even when preceded by a long syllable</li><li>Used to form the first person singular present indicative of strong verbs and class I weak verbs</li><li>Used to form the nominative singular of short ō-stem nouns</li><li>Used to form the nominative and accusative singular of short u-stem nouns</li><li>Used to form the nominative and accusative plural of short a-stem and all ija-stem neuter nouns</li><li>Forms the strong nominative feminine singular and strong nominative&#47;accusative neuter plural of adjectives</li></ol><p>From suppletive fusion of ō-stem feminine singular nominative ending <i>-u</i> and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-į̄</i> (“feminine abstract ending”). Akin to Gothic feminine abstracts in <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei) (compare <i>𐌼𐌹𐌺𐌹𐌻𐌴𐌹</i> (mikilei, “greatness”); <i>𐌳𐌹𐌿𐍀𐌴𐌹</i> (diupei, “depth”)).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-u</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ō</i>. Apocope should have caused the loss of this ending after heavy stems, but the Anglian dialects generally retained it by analogy with short stems, or for its morphological significance. In West Saxon, this apocope led to replacement with the subjunctive singular <i>-e</i>, which was then extended to short stem verbs as well.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-u</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ō</i></p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-u</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-uz</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-oc</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-uk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ukaz</i> (diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-od</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ode</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-el</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>-ol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form the dative plural of essentially all nouns</li><li>Used to form the strong and weak dative and instrumental plural of adjectives</li><li>Used to form the strong dative singular of masculine and neuter adjectives</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-um</i>, possibly from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-amaz</i> with reduction and analogical zero-grade, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-mos</i>, the dative-ablative plural ending. Cognate to modern German <i>-en</i> and Polish <i>-om</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>used to form the superlative of Old English adjectives</li><li>used in combination with other superlative endings</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-umô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-on</i> (strong plural past indicative suffix)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forming nouns from verbs, denoting verbal action or an instance of it; or denoting a substance involved in the verbal action</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-ungu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ungō</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>-unga</i> (Low German <i>-inge</i>, <i>-ing</i>), Old Dutch <i>-inga</i>, <i>-unga</i> (Dutch <i>-ing</i>), Old High German <i>-unga</i> (German <i>-ung</i>), Old Norse <i>-ung</i>, <i>-ing</i> (Swedish <i>-ing</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>used to form adverbs from nouns, originally from verbal nouns</li><li>used to form adverbs from adjectives</li><li>used to create adverbs from pronouns</li><li>used to create adverbs from prepositions</li></ol><p>From <i>-ung</i>, noun suffix + <i>-a</i>, adverbial suffix.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ost</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-et</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-oþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used in the singular</i>) suffix denoting inhabitants or residents of a place</li><li>(<i>used in the plural</i>) suffix denoting inhabitants or residents of a place</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-wari</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;warjaz</i> (“dwellers of”). Cognate with Old High German <i>-āri</i> (“inhabitants of”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forming adjectives and nouns from adjectives or prepositions, with the sense of "in the direction of, toward".</li><li>Forming adverbs from adjectives or prepositions, with the sense of "in the direction of, toward".</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wardaz</i> (“turned toward, in the direction of, facing”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wert-</i> (“to turn, wind”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wer-</i> (“to turn, bend”).</p><p>Akin to Old Frisian <i>-ward</i>, Old Saxon <i>-ward</i>, Old Norse <i>verðr</i>, German <i>-wärts</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>weorþan</i> (“to turn (into), become”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-weard</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-wardas</i>, equivalent to <i>-weard</i> +&lrm; <i>-es</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix meaning tending toward, promoting, or exemplified by; -ing, -able</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wandī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wandijaz</i> (“turning, directed”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>worthy of</li><li>entitled to</li></ol><p>From <i>weorþ</i> or Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-werþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-weard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>West Saxon</i>) worthy of</li><li>(<i>West Saxon</i>) able to, capable of</li><li>(<i>West Saxon</i>) fit for, tending to</li></ol><p>Derived from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wirþī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wirþijaz</i> (“worthy, worth”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-willed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-wiljaz</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>-𐍅𐌹𐌻𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (-wiljis). Equivalent to <i>willa</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>years old</li><li>years lasting</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-wintrī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-wintruz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>-vetr</i> and Gothic <i>-𐍅𐌹𐌽𐍄𐍂𐌿𐍃</i> (-wintrus). Equivalent to <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>. Compare the German suffix <i>-jährig</i>, of similar form and meaning.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-wierþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>adjectival suffix</i>) denoting a specified way, manner, condition, habit or direction</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-wīs</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-wīsaz</i>, related to Old English <i>wīse</i> (“wise, manner, direction”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>noun suffix</i>) state of, manner of, condition; direction</li><li>(<i>noun suffix</i>) the custom or fashion of</li><li>(<i>adverbial suffix</i>) in the manner or fashion of; in the direction of</li></ol><p>From <i>wīse</i> (“manner, way, condition, direction”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>being, state, condition; action (compare English <i>-ance</i>, <i>-ence</i>)</li></ol><p>From combining form of Old English <i>wist</i> (“being”). Akin to Old English <i>wesan</i> (“to be”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forming adjectives and nouns from adjectives, with the sense of "a kind of speech".</li><li>Forming nouns from nouns or adjectives, with the sense of "a kind of speech".</li></ol><p>Compound words from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurdiją</i>. More at word.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-wierþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-isċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-estre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Early Old English</i>) alternative form of <i>-e</i> (<i>a</i>-stem&#47;<i>ō</i>-stem dative singular suffix)</li><li>(<i>Early Old English</i>) alternative form of <i>-e</i> (<i>ō</i>-stem accusative&#47;genitive singular suffix)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ern</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>early</i>) alternative form of <i>-es</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forming abstract nouns from verbs, adjectives or other nouns; generally causing mutation of root or preceding vowels</li><li>forming abstract nouns from verbs, adjectives or other nouns&#59; generally causing mutation of root or preceding vowels</li><li>(<i>no longer productive</i>) forming abstract nouns from verbs, adjectives or other nouns&#59; generally causing mutation of root or preceding vowels</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-iþu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-iþō</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-þu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-þuz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-tus</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-þi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-þiz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-tis</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-þu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>instrumental suffix creating neuter nouns from verbs</li><li>suffix used to denote the agent or means by which an action is performed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-þr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-þrą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-trom</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forming abstract nouns from verbs, adjectives or other nouns; generally causing mutation of root or preceding vowels</li></ol><p>Partly from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-iþu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-iþō</i>; and partly from <i>-þ</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>, where the <i>-u</i> denotes the īn-stem of nouns, and is thus indeclinable.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Third person singular present indicative ending of some weak verbs.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>scribal abbreviation of <i>-um</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>-aþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for evermore</li></ol><p>From <i>ā</i> (“ever”) +&lrm; <i>tō</i> (“to”) +&lrm; <i>fēore</i> (“life”), dative of <i>feorh</i> (“life, existence”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>from, away, off, out</li></ol><p>From an earlier form <i>ar-</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀ-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uz-</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>ar-</i>, <i>ir-</i> (German <i>er-</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣ</i>: law</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>on</i>: to, in</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ever, always</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aiw</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aiwaz</i> (“eternity, age”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Aaron</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Aarōn</i>, from Biblical Hebrew <i>אַהֲרֹן</i> (Ahărōn).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>afol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abare, make bare, strip</li><li>to expose, discover</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā</i> +&lrm; <i>barian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>abbātem</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbotess, abbess</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>abbātissa</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abingdon (a town in England; modern Abingdon-on-Thames)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>abbātem</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>abbod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>abbadisse</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a abbacy; an abbot's rule</li></ol><p>From <i>abbod</i> +&lrm; <i>-rīċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>abbadisse</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>abbodrīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>abbod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>abbadisse</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>abbadisse</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ABC; alphabet</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>abbadisse</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Abel, the brother of Cain and the first murder victim</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Abel</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Ἅβελ</i> (Hábel), from Hebrew <i>הֶ֫בֶל</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make angry, tick off, enrage, irritate, annoy, vex, grill, incense, cause anyone to swell with anger, offend</li><li>to hurt, distress</li><li>to be angry with</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>belgan</i> (“to be or become angry; offend, provoke”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to proclaim, announce, state, bid</li><li>to offer, provide</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>bēodan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bear, carry, tolerate, endure, suffer; thole</li><li>to bear off or away, bring forth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzberaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>beran</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>irberan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌿𐍃𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (usbairan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bearable; tolerable</li></ol><p>From <i>āberan</i> +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>between</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>betwēonum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to uncover, discover, detect</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wait <i>(+genitive for something)</i></li><li>to remain</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀbīdan</i> (“to await, expect, tolerate”), equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>bīdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make busy, employ</li><li>to worry, embarrass, harass</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā</i> +&lrm; <i>bisgian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bite, eat, consume, devour</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>bītan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ābetwēonum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blinded</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āblerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āblerienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;of hair) to make bare or bald</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;blerian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āblerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āblerienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cease, desist</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>blinnan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blush (with shame)</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;blysian</i>, from the same ultimate source as Old English <i>blysċan</i> (“to redden”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ābarian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(biblical,&#32;Abrahamism) Abraham</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Ābrahām</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to resound; crash</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>brastlian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to break, break to pieces, break down, conquer, vanquish, take by storm, capture, violate, destroy, assault</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to break out, break away, break forth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀbrekan</i> (“to break apart, smash, shatter”), equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>brecan</i> (“to break”). Cognate with Old High German <i>irbrehhan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay low; kill</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>bredwian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ābrēoþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physical&#32;or&#32;moral</i>) to degenerate, deteriorate, fall away</li><li>(weak conjugation,&#32;ergative) to destroy, ruin</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>brēoþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ābroþennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dulness, cowardice, a defect, backsliding</li><li>degeneracy, ignobleness, fallenness</li></ol><p>From <i>ābroþen</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ābroþennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>above</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>bufan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bow, incline, bend, submit, do reverence</li><li>to swerve, turn (to, from, or away), deviate</li><li>to withdraw, retire; be bent or turned, turn oneself</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>būgan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onbūtan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buy, pay for</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>byċġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oak (wood or tree)</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) an oaken ship</li><li>the runic character ᚪ (/ɑ(ː)/)</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>but</li><li>but instead: in this sense <i>ac</i> should sometimes be translated as "but," but most often it is best left untranslated</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aik</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ak</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become cold</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>calan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ache</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;akan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;akaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æx</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oak tree</li></ol><p>From <i>āc</i> (“oak”) +&lrm; <i>bēam</i> (“tree”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accent mark, diacritic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>accentus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣċen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beget, bring forth, produce</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>cennan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>native</li></ol><p>From the past participle of <i>ācennan</i>+&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birth, nativity</li></ol><p>From <i>acenned</i> (“born”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>acennednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to choke</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċēoce</i> (“cheek”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āċierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċīeġan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn away; to avert</li><li>to change; to convert; to turn</li><li>to return</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċierran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āċīeġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oakley, a placename in England</li></ol><p>From <i>āc</i> (“oak”) +&lrm; <i>lēah</i> (“meadow, clearing”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oakleaf</li></ol><p>From <i>āc</i> (“oak”) +&lrm; <i>lēaf</i> (“leaf”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frighten, to excite to fear</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to know</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>cnāwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expel, beat off (with adverb <i>ūt</i>)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>cnyssan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frightened, terrified, scared</li><li>excited, trembling</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aikulaz</i> (“fearful”), derived from <i>&#42;aikaną</i> which probably meant ("to rage, be violent").</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ācōlod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become cool</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>cōlian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bark of the oak tree</li></ol><p>From <i>āc</i> (“oak”) +&lrm; <i>rind</i> (“bark”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Axminster (a town in Devon, England)</li></ol><p>From <i>Asca</i> (“the river Axe”) +&lrm; <i>mynster</i> (“monastery”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æsċe</i> (“ash”)</li></ol><p>Alteration by metathesis of <i>asċe</i>, itself a variant with extended a-restoration of <i>æsċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āscian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ascian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āscung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āctrēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oak tree</li></ol><p>From <i>āc</i> (“oak”) +&lrm; <i>trēow</i> (“tree”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ācwician</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ācol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oakum</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tolerable</li><li>possible</li></ol><p>From <i>ācuman</i> +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p><p>For the meaning development compare with Latin <i>conveniēns</i> (“convening, meeting, accosting, fitting, suitable, meet”), Russian <i>подходя́щий</i> (podxodjáščij, “suitable, fitting, fit, appropriate”) related with <i>подходи́ть</i> (podxodítʹ, “to fit (of an object, to match another object), to come up (to), to approach, to go up (to)”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ācweorna</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to shake, brandish</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>cweċċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to die, perish</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to kill</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>cwelan</i>.</p><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>cwellan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill</li><li>to destroy</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>cwellan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to extinguish, quench</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>cwencan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squirrel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aikwernō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aikwernô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to glut, guzzle, gorge; to eat or drink immoderately</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;cweorran</i>, the latter from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwerraną</i> (“to gobble, gulp”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ācweþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say</li><li>to utter, declare</li><li>to reply, answer</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>cweþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quicken</li><li>to requicken, to come back to life</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>cwician</i>. See <i>cwic</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dwindle, waste away</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;cwinan</i>, the latter from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwīnaną</i> (“to expire, wither”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become extinguished, quenched; to vanish</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>cwincan</i>, the latter from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwinkaną</i> (“to expire, go out”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ācwiþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āċierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aversion, turning from</li><li>apostasy, revolt</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>āċyrred</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show, announce, confirm, unfold</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>cȳþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>funeral pyre</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aidaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adam (Biblical character)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>dēadian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deafen, become deaf</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>dēaf</i> (“deaf”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ādl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>filth</li><li>a filthy place; a sewer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;adal</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;adalaz</i>, <i>&#42;adalô</i> (“cattle urine, liquid manure”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig, delve, dig up, dig out</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>delfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>filthy, dirty</li></ol><p>From <i>adela</i> (“filth”) +&lrm; <i>-iht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to judge, adjudge, deem, doom</li><li>to abjudicate, deprive; bereave through judicial action</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>dēman</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>ādōmian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obscure, dim, darken, hide</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>deorcian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adze</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;adisô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to edit, compose or write</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>dihtan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy (utterly); annihilate, wipe out</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>dilegian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ādilegian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ādilegian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disease</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aidlō</i>, <i>&#42;aidlaz</i> (“burning, fever, disease”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eydʰ-</i> (“to burn, shine”). Cognate with Middle Low German <i>ādel</i> (“ulcer, wound, sore”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ādlīeġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ail, be sick, languish</li><li>to make ill, cause disease</li></ol><p>From <i>ādl</i> (“disease, sickness”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flame of the funeral pile</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ād</i> +&lrm; <i>līeġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sick, ill, diseased</li></ol><p>From <i>ādl</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>illness, ailment, disease</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ādlian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>adesa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to drown someone</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uzdrankijan</i>. Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>drenċan</i>. Causative of <i>ādrincan</i>. Cognate with German <i>ertränken</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to act, perform, practise</li><li>to bear, suffer, endure</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>drēogan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive away, repel, pursue</li><li>to expel</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>drīfan</i> (“to drive”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ādrȳġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to drown</li><li>to sink (<i>of ships</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀdrinkan</i>. Cognate with German <i>ertrinken</i>. Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>drincan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dry, rub dry, make dry, wipe</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>drȳġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ondrǣdan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive away, expel</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>dræfan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>adesa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep quiet, hold one's peace</li><li>to become dumb</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>dumb</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>down, downward</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>dūn</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>adūn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>andūstrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to staunch; quench; put out; appease</li><li>to extinguish</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> (“from, away”) +&lrm; <i>dwǣsċan</i> (“to extinguish, put out”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ādilegian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to part, divide, separate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>dǣlan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>away, off, from, away from</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aba-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to test</li><li>to experience</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>fandian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>test, experiment</li><li>(countable,&#32;uncountable) experience</li></ol><p><i>āfandian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to depart, march, go out of or from a place; fare from</li><li>to lead out, remove</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀfaran</i>. Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>faran</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>irfaran</i> (whence German <i>erfahren</i> and Dutch <i>ervaren</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rear, bring up</li><li>to feed, nourish</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>fēdan</i> (“to feed”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take away, remove</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>ferian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat or stomp with the feet</li><li>to applaud; to praise</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;fētian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āfētian</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;fētiġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Africa (the continent south of Europe and between the Atlantic and Indian Oceans)# (<i>historical</i>) Africa (a former province of the Roman Empire, containing what is now Tunisia and portions of coastal Algeria and Libya)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Africa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>An idol, image</li></ol><p>From <i>æf-</i> +&lrm; <i>god</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remove</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>fierran</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fried</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;fiġen</i>, the latter from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;figanaz</i> (“fried”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find</li><li>to find out, discover</li><li>to feel, experience</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀfinþan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>irfindan</i>. Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>findan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āflīeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put to flight, drive away, banish, scatter, disperse</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>flīeman</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āflīeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow from</li><li>to flow over</li><li>to flow away</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>flōwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āflīeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>power; power of body, might, strength</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;afl</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aflą</i> (“power”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to receive, take up</li><li>to take hold of, seize</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>fōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bitter, acidic, sour</li><li>sharp, severe, impetuous, harsh</li><li>hateful</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aibr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aibraz</i> (“sharp, bitter, vehement, dire”). Akin to Old High German <i>eipar</i>, <i>eivar</i> (“sour", "acidic”), and possibly to Old Norse <i>æfr</i> (“vehement, angry, chafing”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to alienate, estrange</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>fremde</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alienation</li></ol><p>From <i>āfremdan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>; compare German <i>Entfremdung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Africa</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Africa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an African</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>African</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>āfricānus</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>. Compare <i>Ispānisċ</i> (“Spanish”), <i>Rōmānisċ</i> (“Roman”), <i>Samaritānisċ</i> (“Samaritan”), and <i>Troiānisċ</i> (“Trojan”), which are formed similarly. Corresponds to Old Saxon <i>Āfricānisk</i>, from the same source.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Africa</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>African</li></ol><p>From <i>Āfrica</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>, or Latin <i>āfricus</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perverse</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>invention</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>fūnden</i> +&lrm; <i>-nys</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fill (with)</li><li>to fell</li><li>to strike, beat down</li><li>to overturn</li><li>to subvert, suppress</li><li>to abolish, slay</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>fyllan</i> (“to fill”)</p><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>fiellan</i> (“to fell”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to affright, terrify</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>fyrhtan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to terrify, make greatly afraid</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>fǣran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>oh certainly!, oh assuredly</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>owner, possessor</li></ol><p>See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</p><p>Ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aigô</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to own, possess</li><li>to cause to own: to give, to deliver</li><li>to go out, <i>(in the past participle)</i> gone</li><li>to pass (<i>of time</i>)</li><li>to happen</li><li>to become known: get out, come out</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aigan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aiganą</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀgān</i>. Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>gān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come to pass</li><li>to happen to, befall</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>gangan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onġēan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āgǣlwan</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;gēlwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āgælwed</i></li></ol><p>From <i>āgelwan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ed</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>against, contra</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>again, anew</li></ol><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>again, eft, back</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>own</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>property, house</li></ol><p>Variant of Old English <i>ongēan</i> (“again, eft, back”).</p><p>Literally ‘owned’: originally the past participle of <i>āgan</i>. Corresponding to Old Dutch <i>eigan</i> (Dutch <i>eigen</i>), Old High German <i>eigan</i> (German <i>eigen</i>), Old Norse <i>eiginn</i> (Swedish <i>egen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) a proper noun</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>owner</li></ol><p>From <i>āgan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>owner, lord</li></ol><p>From <i>āgend</i> (“owner”) +&lrm; <i>frēa</i> (“lord”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn again, recur</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>aġēn</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeċyrran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn back, return</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>aġēn</i> +&lrm; <i>ġehweorfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>someone who kills themselves</li></ol><p><i>āgen</i> (“own”) +&lrm; <i>slaga</i> (“slayer”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) idiom</li></ol><p>From <i>āgen</i> (“peculiar”) +&lrm; <i>sprǣċ</i> (“language”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand against</li><li>to urge, insist on</li></ol><p>From <i>āgen</i> +&lrm; <i>standan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an owning, possession, property</li><li>ownership</li></ol><p>From <i>āgan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āġiefan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pour out, shed, strew, spill</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to pour forth</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġēotan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give up, quit</li><li>to give up, abandon</li><li>to give back</li><li>to restore</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzgebaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġiefan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>āgevan</i> and Old High German <i>ergeban</i> (German <i>ergeben</i>), Gothic <i>𐌿𐍃𐌲𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (usgiban).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay</li><li>to repay</li><li>to reward</li><li>to permit, allow</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġieldan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to neglect</li><li>to despise</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġīemelēasian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āġiefan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āġieldan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin, undertake</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;ginnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misery, grief, trouble, vexation, sorrow, torment</li><li>conflict, distress</li></ol><p>Uncertain. The second element is either <i>lāc</i> (“action, sport, game”), its suffixed form <i>-lāc</i> (“-ation”), or an alteration of another element in assimilation to the aforementioned. The first element is unknown. It may possibly be from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aikaną</i> (“to be furious, rage, be violent, terrorise”), related to Old English <i>ācol</i> (“frightened, terrified”). Alternatively, it may be a back-formation from <i>āglǣċa</i>, possibly a derivative of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;agalaitijaz</i> (“licentious, renegade, rogue”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) an awesome or formidable opponent, ferocious fighter</li><li>a miserable being, wretch, monster</li></ol><p>From <i>āglāc</i> (“distress, torment, misery”). A difficult and much-debated word. Possibly in origin a compound from the root of <i>ece</i> (“ache, pain”) +&lrm; <i>lāc</i> (“ritual, performance, demonstration”) (Bosworth 1882), but this is disputed.</p><p>Found mostly in poetry, and there mostly of monsters and devils, but once of Beowulf and once of Sigemund.</p><p>The sole attestation in prose is also a unique relational adjectival use, in Byrhtferth's <i>Enchiridion</i>, where it refers to the scholar Bede as <i>se æglæca lareow</i>, making clear that the word is not inherently pejorative but carries a meaning of "awe-inspiring, formidable".</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appropriate, usurp</li></ol><p>From <i>āgnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to claim possession</li><li>to take possession, to appropriate</li><li>to possess</li></ol><p>From <i>āgen</i> (“own”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āgenung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to handle; to grasp</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>grāpian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shudder, to feel horror, to dread, to quake</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>grīsan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grīsaną</i>, related to causative verb Old English <i>grēosan</i> and Old English <i>gryre</i>. Cognate with German <i>grausen</i>. More at grisly and gruesome.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>terrible, horrible</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>āgrīsan</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magpie</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Augustus</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āġiefan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āġieldan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āġīemelēasian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stupefy, astonish</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;gǣlwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>astonished, stupefied</li></ol><p>From <i>āgælwan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ed</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Anglian</i>) alternative form of <i>ac</i> (“but”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;reflexive) to restrain one's self, abstain from (+ fram)</li><li>to support</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>habban</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call, name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀhaitan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzhaitaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>hātan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal</i>) to harden</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to become unyielding, inflexible, hardhearted, or inured</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>heardian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hardening</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>āheardian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chop, cut (up, down)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>hēawan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heave or lift up, raise from low to high, elevate, exalt, ferment</li><li>to build, erect</li><li>to raise, as to make louder</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to bear, support, uphold, give higher worth to, puff up</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to remove</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to give rise to, raise a laugh,</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀhabbjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzhabjaną</i>. Equivalent to Compound of <i>ā</i> +&lrm; <i>hebban</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>āhebbian</i>, Old High German <i>irheven</i>, <i>irheffen</i> (whence German <i>erheben</i>), Gothic <i>𐌿𐍃𐌷𐌰𐍆𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (ushafjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;poetic) To cripple; to prevent from moving.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀhaltijan</i>; by surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>healt</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>. Compare <i>āhealtian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to harden</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>hierdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hardening</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>āhierdan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āhliehhan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sound, resound</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>hlēoþrian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laugh at, to deride</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to rejoice, exult</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>hliehhan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dig out, excavate</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;hlocian</i>, <i>&#42;holcian</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;holhukōn</i>, <i>&#42;hulhikōn</i> (“to hollow out”), related to Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hulhikjan</i> (“to create a concavity, cause to bend, curve”). Cognate with Middle Low German <i>holken</i>, <i>hȫlken</i> (“to hollow out, gouge”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āgenung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hollow out</li><li>to dig</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>holian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang, suspend, hang on the gallows or cross, crucify</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>hōn</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐌿𐍃𐌷𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (ushahan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rescue, liberate, free</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>hreddan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush, fall, fall down</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>hrēosan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move</li><li>to shake</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>hrēran</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āscian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āwiht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Number</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eahta</i> (“eight”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from anywhere</li><li>in any direction</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>hwanon</i> (“whence”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive</i>) to turn or move away (physically or spiritually)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>hweorfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>somewhere</li><li>everywhere</li><li>anywhere</li><li>in any way</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>hwǣr</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>anyone, anything</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>hwæþer</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to devise, invent</li><li>to search</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>hyċġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to incline</li><li>to recline, rest</li><li>to decline, turn away, avert from</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>hyldan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āhieltan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āhierdan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āhierding</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aylsham (a market town and civil parish with a town council in Broadland district, Norfolk, England)</li></ol><p>From the genitive form of a personal name <i>&#42;Æġel</i> + <i>hām</i> (“homestead”). Attested in the 1086 Domesday Book.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fire</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ail</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ailą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éydʰ-lom</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂eydʰ-</i> (“to burn, kindle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æl-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nourish</li><li>to grow, produce</li><li>to appear</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;alaną</i> (“to nourish, grow”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂el-</i> (“to grow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an alb (type of priest vestment)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>alba</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Alhred</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eald</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ald</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aldaz</i> (“grown-up”), originally a participle form of <i>&#42;alaną</i> (“to grow up”), corresponding to Latin <i>altus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>ald</i> (“wise, old”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, variant of Ealdbeorht</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aldaberht</i>, from <i>&#42;ald</i> (“old, wise”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”). Equivalent to <i>eald</i> +&lrm; <i>beorht</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Aldabert</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ealdordōm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ealdormann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ealdormann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Aldfriþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Aldfrith of Northumbria</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aldafriþu</i>, from <i>&#42;ald</i> (“old, wise”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;friþu</i> (“peace”). Equivalent to <i>eald</i> +&lrm; <i>friþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Altfrid</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aldagīsl</i>, from <i>&#42;ald</i> (“old, wise”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;gīsl</i> (“hostage”). Equivalent to <i>eald</i> +&lrm; <i>ġīsl</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Altgīs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ealdhelm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>ald</i> (“wise, old”) + <i>hyse</i> (“warrior”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aldamann</i>, from <i>&#42;ald</i> (“old, wise”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;mann</i> (“man”). Equivalent to <i>eald</i> +&lrm; <i>mann</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Altman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ealdor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ealdorlēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ealdormann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ealdormann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ealdormann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ald</i> (“old, wise”) and <i>rēd</i> (“counsel, advice”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ealdwulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aldawini</i>, from <i>&#42;ald</i> (“old, wise”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wini</i> (“friend”). Equivalent to <i>eald</i> +&lrm; <i>wine</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Aldawin</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lay down, put down</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lay aside, give up</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzlagjaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>leċġan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>irleccan</i> (German <i>erlegen</i>), Gothic <i>𐌿𐍃𐌻𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (uslagjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>alegd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become weak, feeble, languid, disabled</li><li>alternative form of <i>ālīefan</i> (“to allow, to permit; to believe”)</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>lēfan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disability, infirmity</li></ol><p>From <i>ālēfed</i> (“disabled”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie, tell lies, deceive</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>lēogan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>alor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ālīesan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aloe</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>aloē</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἀλόη</i> (alóē), from Hebrew <i>אָהָל</i> (ʾāhāl), ultimately from Tamil <i>அகில்</i> (akil)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alexander the Great</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Alexander</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀλέξανδρος</i> (Aléxandros)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alexandria, Egypt</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Alexandrīa</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cooking vessel, cooking pot; cauldron</li></ol><p>From <i>āl</i> (“fire”) +&lrm; <i>fæt</i> (“vessel, vat”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ealh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ealhhere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Alhred of Northumbria</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to survive</li><li>to live, to live after</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>libban</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A survivor, one who lives after</li></ol><p>From <i>alibban</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to allow</li><li>to surrender, yield up</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀlaubijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzlaubijaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>līefan</i> (“to grant”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to detach, remove</li><li>to redeem a fault</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to release, rescue, free</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀlausijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzlausijaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>līesan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>urlēsa</i>, Old Saxon <i>ālōsian</i>, Old Dutch <i>irlōsen</i>, Old High German <i>irlōsen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salvation, redemption</li></ol><p>From <i>ālīesan</i> (“to rescue, release, free”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ālīesednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lather</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>līeþran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enlighten</li><li>to alight</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>līhtan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to happen, occur, take place</li></ol><p>From <i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>limpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ālīesan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eall</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Anglian form of <i>eall</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ealles</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eallīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ælmihtiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ealneġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ealning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alder</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aluʀu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aluz, &#42;alusō</i>, variant of <i>&#42;alizō</i>, <i>&#42;alisō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂élis-</i> (compare Hittite (alanza(n)), Latin <i>alnus</i>, Latvian <i>al̃ksnis</i>, Polish <i>olcha</i>, Albanian <i>halë</i> (“black pine”), Ancient Macedonian (Hesychius) <i>ἄλιζα</i> (áliza, “white poplar”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Alps (a mountain range in Western Europe)</li></ol><p>Latin <i>Alpis</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aller (a village and civil parish in Somerset, previously in South Somerset district)</li></ol><p>From <i>alor</i> (“alder”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A male given name</li><li>Alaric, king of the Visigoths</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Alarīks</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ealu</i> (“ale”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, incline</li><li>to bend down, bow down</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>lūtan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Æþelwacer</i> (<i>Domesday Book</i> spelling)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>alewe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ālīefan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ālīesan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead out, withdraw</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be produced, grow, bring forth</li><li>to lead away, carry off, abear</li><li>to rescue, save, rid</li><li>to guide, educate, teach, draw out information</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>among</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>onġemang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to demand, exact</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā</i> +&lrm; <i>manian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āmǣnsumian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āmǣnsumung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to amaze</li><li>to confuse</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;marian</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>merja</i> (“to crush, bruise”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to amaze</li><li>to confuse, confound, perplex</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;masian</i> (“to confound”), the latter from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;masōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;masōną</i> (“to confound, be weary, dream”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>servant (West Saxon)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ambahtaz</i>, whence also Old High German <i>ambaht</i>, Old Norse <i>ambátt</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌱𐌰𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (andbahts). Compare Latin <i>ambactus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>office, ministry, service</li><li>servant, attendant</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ambahtī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ambahtiją</i> (derived from the root of <i>ambiht</i> (Etymology 1)), whence also Old Saxon <i>ambaht</i>, Old High German <i>ambahti</i>, <i>ambaht</i> (German <i>Amt</i>), Old Norse <i>embætti</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌱𐌰𐌷𐍄𐌹</i> (andbahti).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to serve, minister</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ambahtijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ambahtijaną</i> (“to serve, minister”), equivalent to <i>ambeht</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>official smith or carpenter</li></ol><p>From <i>ambeht</i> +&lrm; <i>smiþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bucket</li><li>A measure</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ambrī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>servant</li><li>alternative form of <i>ambeht</i> (“service”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ambaht</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ambahtaz</i>, whence also Old High German <i>ambaht</i>, Old Norse <i>ambátt</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌱𐌰𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (andbahts). Compare Latin <i>ambactus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ambehtsmiþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>favorable</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to describe</li><li>to mark out</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>mearcian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>amen, so be it</li></ol><p>From Ecclesiastical Latin <i>āmēn</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἀμήν</i> (amḗn), from Biblical Hebrew <i>אָמֵן</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āmerienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to examine</li><li>to purify (melted metal)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>merian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āmerienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āmierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to measure</li><li>to mete out</li><li>to measure out, plan</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>metan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>irmezzan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌿𐍃𐌼𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (usmitan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>measurable, limited, brief</li></ol><p>From <i>āmetan</i> +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead astray, misguide</li><li>to defile, mar, corrupt, spoil, destroy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀmarʀijan</i>; by surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>mierran</i>. Compare Middle High German <i>ermerren</i> and Old Saxon <i>amerrian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>almond</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>amygdalum</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἀμυγδάλη</i> (amugdálē).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, Hamilcar Barca, father of Hannibal</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Hamilcar</i>, from Punic <i>𐤇𐤌𐤋𐤒𐤓𐤕</i> (ḥmlqrt, literally “brother of Melqart”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āmierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of bird (e.g. thrush or bunting)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;amaʀ</i>, <i>&#42;amaʀō</i> (“blackbird, bunting”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;amazaz</i>, <i>&#42;amazô</i> (“blackbird”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of bird, probably the ammer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;amaʀā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a flask, bottle, flagon, vial</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ampullā</i>, borrowed from Latin <i>ampulla</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) sorrel</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āmierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw out of a community, excommunicate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uzmainasumōn</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>irmeinsamōn</i>. Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġemǣnsumian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>excommunication</li></ol><p><i>āmǣnsumian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fatten up</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>mæstan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>one</li></ol><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>a certain</li><li>(<i>uncommon</i>) a, an (indefinite article)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>only</li><li>alone</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>only</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one (digit or figure)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>on</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ain</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ainaz</i>.</p><p>Germanic cognates include Old Frisian <i>ān</i>, Old Saxon <i>ēn</i>, Old High German <i>ein</i>, Old Norse <i>einn</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (ains). The Indo-European root is also the source of Latin <i>ūnus</i>, Ancient Greek <i>οἶος</i> (oîos), Old Irish <i>oen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-one</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-first</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-one</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-first</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-one</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-first</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-one</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-first</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>one hundred and one</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-one</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-first</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-one</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ain andi twaintig</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-first</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-one</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-first</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>one after another</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>one; mono-, uni-</li><li>lone, alone</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aina-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aina-</i> (“one, uni-”), equivalent to Old English <i>ān</i> (“one”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sole</li><li>alone</li><li>by itself</li><li>only</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) waste, desert, solitude</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ainōdī</i>. Cognate to Old Saxon <i>ēnōdi</i> and German <i>Einöde</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intestinal worm</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead or bring in</li></ol><p>From <i>an-</i> +&lrm; <i>belǣdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onbūgan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resistance</li></ol><p>From <i>an(d)-</i> +&lrm; <i>byrdnes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>anbyrdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>only-begotten</li></ol><p>From <i>ān</i> +&lrm; <i>cenned</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ancor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>anclēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ankle</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;ancol</i> (from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ankulaz</i>) suffixed with an unknown element.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>oncnāwan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anchor</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ankurō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anchor</li><li>ballast</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>ancora</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>angsum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come, arrive; oncome</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anakwemaną</i> (“to come to, come at, arrive”), equivalent to <i>an-</i> +&lrm; <i>cuman</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>onkuma</i>, Old Saxon <i>ankuman</i>, Old High German <i>anaqueman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>and</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>even; also</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anda, &#42;andi</i>, probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂énti</i> (“facing opposite, near, in front of, before”). Compare Old Frisian <i>and</i>, Old Saxon <i>endi</i>, Old High German <i>unti</i>, Old Norse <i>enn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>against, opposing</li><li>in return, back</li><li>facing, toward</li><li>completely, fully</li><li>without</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;anda-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anda-</i>, representative of a combining form of <i>and</i> (“and”).</p><p>Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>ont-</i> (Dutch <i>ont-</i>), Old High German <i>ant-</i> (German <i>ant-</i>, <i>ent-</i>), Old Norse <i>and-</i> (Icelandic <i>and-</i> (“against, anti-, opposed to, in the face of”), Swedish <i>an-</i>), Gothic <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>envy, jealousy; a grudge, enmity</li><li>hatred, anger; injury, wrong-doing, mischief</li><li>zeal</li><li>vexation, annoyance</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;anadō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>oncweþan</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>cweþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a reply, answer</li></ol><p><i>and-</i> (“back”) +&lrm; <i>cwiss</i> (a derivative of <i>cweþan</i> (“to say”)). More at <i>cweþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pretentious, boastful, arrogant</li></ol><p>From <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;ēaw</i>, from <i>ēawan</i> (“to show, display”). Compare Gothic <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌿𐌲𐌾𐍉</i> (andaugjō, “openly, in plain sight”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Andover (a town in Hampshire, England)</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>&#42;Ande</i> +&lrm; <i>ēa</i> +&lrm; <i>fara</i>, literally “farer over the river Ande”, or from <i>&#42;Ande</i> +&lrm; <i>ōfer</i>, literally “[town situated on the] bank of the river Ande”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>measure</li><li>quality; capacity; nature</li><li>quantity; amount; extent</li></ol><p>From <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;efne</i>, the latter from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;abnī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;abniją</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reversed, inverted</li></ol><p>From <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>hēla</i> (“heel”) +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>denoting reversal or something done in return</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;anþar</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anþeraz</i> (“other, back, in return”). Compare Old Norse <i>endr-</i> (“re-”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>andettan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(countable,&#32;uncountable) confession, admitting</li></ol><p>Composed of either <i>andettan</i> or its past participle <i>andett</i> + <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>someone who admits or confesses: confessor</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>andettan</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to admit, confess</li><li>to acknowledge</li><li>to give thanks for, praise, give recognition to</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;andhaitan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andahaitaną</i>, equivalent to <i>and-</i> (“against, back”) +&lrm; <i>hātan</i> (“to command, promise”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>andhētan</i> (“to teach, vow, promise”), Old High German <i>antheizan</i> (“to declare, pledge, promise”), Gothic <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (andhaitan, “to acknowledge, confess”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acceptance</li></ol><p>From <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>fang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>acceptable</li><li>that can receive</li><li>fit</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;andafangī</i>, equivalent to <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;fenġe</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>antfengi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>understanding, intellect, cognizance</li><li>knowledge, cognizance, perception</li><li>sense, meaning</li><li>one of the five senses</li><li>plan, purpose</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>onġietan</i> (“to understand or perceive”), earlier <i>*andġetan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>obvious, plain</li><li>manifest, clear</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intellect, understanding</li><li>perception, cognizance; knowledge</li><li>sense, meaning</li><li>the senses, one of the five senses (<i>ġesihþ</i>, <i>hlyst</i>, <i>swæċċ</i>, <i>stenċ</i>, <i>hrepung</i>)</li><li>plan, purpose</li></ol><p>From the noun <i>andġiet</i> (“intelligence, understanding”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intelligent, sensible, reasonable, understanding</li></ol><p>From <i>andġiet</i> +&lrm; <i>-ol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perceptible sign</li></ol><p>From <i>andġiet</i> +&lrm; <i>tācen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>andġiet</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>clearly, intelligently, sensibly, plainly</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intelligible</li></ol><p>From <i>andgit</i> +&lrm; <i>-līc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>clearly</li></ol><p>From <i>andgit</i> +&lrm; <i>-līce</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>andġit</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the intellect, understanding, knowledge</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>gyte</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onhweorfan</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>hweorfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to envy</li><li>to be jealous</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;anadōn</i> (“to be zealous; to be envious”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>envious</li></ol><p>From <i>anda</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>andian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>along [<i>with</i> genitive]</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>livelong; throughout, all around</li></ol><p>From <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>lang</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>endlangr</i> (“livelong”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>andlifen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>anlīcnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>andlifen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>living, food, sustenance, nourishment, pottage</li><li>that by which food is procured, money, wages, alms</li></ol><p>From <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>lifen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>utensil, vessel</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lōmō</i>, <i>&#42;lamō</i> (“tool, utensil”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>andlifen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Andreas (poem); an Old English Poem.</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Andreas</i>, displaced by the Old French ancestor of Andrew after the Norman Conquest.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anderitum, a Roman fort in southern Britain.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adversary, opponent, enemy</li><li>denier, renouncer, apostate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;andasakō</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>andsako</i>. Equivalent to <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>saca</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strive against, deny, refuse</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>sacian</i> or <i>andsaca</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ansund</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an answer</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to answer <i>(+dative who is answered) (+accusative what is said)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andaswarōną</i> (“to answer”). Akin to Old Norse <i>andsvar</i> (“answer”), a related noun cognate with Old English <i>andswaru</i>. Compare <i>andswerian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>answer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;andaswaru</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andaswarō</i>. Equivalent to <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>swaru</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>andswerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>andswerienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to answer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andaswarjaną</i> (“to answer”). Equivalent to <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>swerian</i>; compare <i>andswarian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>andswerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>andswerienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>matter, material, substance</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) reason, theme</li></ol><p><i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>timber</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deny (by oaths), swear with oaths</li><li>to hate, detest</li></ol><p>From <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;ūstrian</i>, the latter from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ūstrōną</i> (“to strive”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>andūstrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>present, current</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;andaward</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andawardaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>-weard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) present tense</li></ol><p><i>andweard</i> (“present, current”) + <i>tīd</i> (“time”). Compare Latin <i>praesens tempus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to present, to bring before</li></ol><p>From <i>andweard</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>presentially, in the presence of</li></ol><p>From <i>andweard</i> (“present”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the present</li><li>presence</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>andweard</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>substance, matter, material</li><li>metal</li><li>cause, sake</li></ol><p>From <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>weorc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>andweard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resistance</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andawīgą</i>, equivalent to <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>wīg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>andwyrdan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>expert, skillful</li></ol><p>From <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>wīs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>support, assistance</li></ol><p><i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>wist</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>face</li><li>form; appearance</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;andawliti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andawlitiz</i>, equivalent to <i>and-</i> (“facing”) +&lrm; <i>wlita</i> (“countenance”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>andlit</i> (Swedish <i>anlete</i>, Norwegian Nynorsk <i>andlet</i>) and Old High German <i>antlizzi</i> (German <i>Antlitz</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look upon; to face</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>wlītan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>face, countenance</li><li>form, appearance</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;andawliti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andawlitiz</i>, equivalent to <i>and-</i> (“facing”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wlite</i> (“countenance”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>andlit</i> (Swedish <i>anlete</i>, Norwegian Nynorsk <i>andlet</i>) and Old High German <i>antlizzi</i> (German <i>Antlitz</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to answer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;andawurdijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>answer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;andawurdī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andawurdiją</i>, equivalent to <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>-wyrde</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>andwurdi</i> (“answer”), Dutch <i>antwoord</i> (“answer”) German <i>Antwort</i> (“answer”), and Gothic <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳𐌹</i> (andawaurdi, “answer”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alone, only</li><li>once</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ān</i> (“one”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>awhile, for a while</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ānīeġed</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to declare</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>nemnan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) once</li></ol><p>A modification of <i>ǣnes</i> (“once”) after <i>ān</i> (“one”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ānfeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>simple</li><li>(<i>numeral</i>) single, sole</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) singular</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ainfald</i>, equivalent to <i>ān</i> +&lrm; <i>-feald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>simply</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) in the singular</li></ol><p>From <i>ānfeald</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>simplicity</li><li>oneness, unity</li></ol><p>From <i>ānfeald</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ānfealdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one-footed</li></ol><p><i>ān-</i> +&lrm; <i>-fēte</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anvil</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;anafalt</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>anfilt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) Solitary flyer, lone fugitive, attacking flyer, approaching flyer (<i>exact meaning debated</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ān</i> (“lone”) +&lrm; <i>floga</i> (“flyer”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave alone</li><li>to lose</li><li>to relinquish</li><li>to let go</li><li>to lose from what one owns</li><li>to leave unnoticed</li><li>to neglect</li><li>to omit</li><li>to give up what one has claim to</li><li>to lose as a result of fault</li><li>to abandon what should be kept</li><li>to forsake what should be kept</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>narrow, tight, constrained</li><li>vexed, troubled, anxious</li><li>oppressive, severe, painful, cruel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;angu-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anguz</i>, related to Old English <i>ange</i>, <i>enge</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a spike, sting, pointed tip</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one and no more, only, sole, single, singular</li><li>any, every one, all</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;angō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;angô</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ainagō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ainahô</i> (“only, sole”), equivalent to <i>ān</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i> or <i>ǣniġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asthma, shortness of breath</li></ol><p><i>ange</i> (“tight”) +&lrm; <i>brēost</i> (“chest”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>narrow, confined, tight</li><li>vexed, troubled, anxious, sorrowful</li><li>painful, grievous, cruel</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sadly, anxiously</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;angī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anguz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>angol</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>enġel</i> (“angel”)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Angel)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anglia (peninsula in Jutland, in Schleswig in northermost Germany, where the Angles are supposed to have originated)</li></ol><p>From or related to <i>Engle</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Anglo-; of England or the English</li></ol><p>Ultimately related to the plural noun <i>Engle</i> (“the English,” originally “the Angles”), possibly from an unattested singular form <i>*Angul</i> or <i>*Angel</i>, related to <i>Angel</i> (“Anglia”), the Angles' original homeland in continental Europe, or else from a root common to all these terms. Similar prefixes exist for other peoples, in words like <i>Wendelsǣ</i> (“the Mediterranean,” literally “the Vandal-sea”) and <i>Swēoland</i> (“Sweden,” literally “Swede-land”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Church of England</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>Angel-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċiriċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Angelcynn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>king of England</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>Angel-</i> +&lrm; <i>cyning</i>. Compare <i>Frīscyning</i> (“king of Frisia”), which is formed similarly.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Anglo-Saxon people; the English race</li><li>England (a medieval kingdom in Northern Europe)</li></ol><p><i>Angel-</i> +&lrm; <i>cynn</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>England</li></ol><p>Literally “land of the English.” Compare <i>Engla land</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Angelċiriċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Angles, English, people of England</li></ol><p><i>Angel-</i> +&lrm; <i>folc</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>enġellīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) the Anglo-Saxons</li></ol><p>Originally a calque of Latin <i>Anglī-Saxōnēs</i>. Equivalent to <i>Angel-</i> +&lrm; <i>Seaxan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an English wise man</li></ol><p><i>Angel-</i> +&lrm; <i>wita</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Angles, English, people of England</li></ol><p><i>Angel-</i> +&lrm; <i>þēod</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Eomer of Mercia, a semi-legendary king of the Angles</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Angliþeu</i>, from <i>&#42;Angli</i> (“an Angle”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;þeu</i> (“servant”). Equivalent to <i>Engle</i> +&lrm; <i>þēow</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Engildio</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Angelþēow</li></ol><p>From <i>Angelþēow</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lone traveler, solitary walker; loner</li><li>a rogue or solitary animal</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ān-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;genġa</i>, literally “someone who walks alone.” Compare German <i>Einzelgänger</i> (“loner”) and Icelandic <i>einfari</i> (“loner”), which are formed similarly.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>anġinn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beginning</li><li>attempt</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin, undertake</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Engle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hangnail</li></ol><p>From <i>ange</i> (“vexed, troubled, painful”) +&lrm; <i>næġl</i> (“nail”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fishhook</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;angul</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;angulaz</i> (“fishhook”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂enkulós</i> (“bent, crooked”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>&#42;ongel</i>, Old Saxon <i>angul</i>, Old High German <i>angul</i>, Old Norse <i>ǫngull</i>, and outside of Germanic, with Ancient Greek <i>ἀγκύλος</i> (ankúlos, “bent, crooked”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Angel-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Angelseaxan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ongrislīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carbuncle</li><li>pimple</li><li>pustule</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anxious</li><li>narrow</li></ol><p>From <i>ange</i> +&lrm; <i>-sum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anxiety</li></ol><p>From <i>angsum</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>angsumnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>angsumnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>angol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Angel-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a recluse, a loner, a lone warrior not tied to a lord (meaning "one-spear" alone without battle companions)</li></ol><p>From <i>ān-</i> + <i>haga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onhagian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold, keep</li></ol><p>From <i>an-</i> +&lrm; <i>healdan</i>. See <i>on-</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>onhalda</i> (“to keep, retain”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one-handed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ainahandijaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>ān-</i> +&lrm; <i>-hende</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ānhaga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unicorn</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ainahorn</i>, a calque of Latin <i>ūnicornis</i>. Equivalent to <i>ān</i> +&lrm; <i>horn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one hundred years old</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ān</i> (“one”) +&lrm; <i>hund</i> (“one hundred”) +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ānīedan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to force</li><li>to restrain, constrain</li><li>to repel, beat back</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>nīedan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one-eyed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ainaugī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ainaugijaz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>&#42;ēnōgi</i>, Old High German <i>einougi</i>, Old Norse <i>eineygr</i>. Equivalent to <i>ān-</i> +&lrm; <i>-īeġe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one-eyed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ainaugid</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ainaugidaz</i>, equivalent to <i>ān</i> +&lrm; <i>ēaġe</i> +&lrm; <i>-ed</i>. Cognate with Middle Low German <i>ēnö̂get</i>, <i>einö̂get</i> (“one-eyed”), Old Norse <i>eineygðr</i> (“one-eyed”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ānīeġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take away, to remove</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>niman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>entirely, altogether</li><li>by all means</li><li>necessarily</li><li>one by one</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ān</i> (“one”) +&lrm; <i>-unga</i> (“adverbial suffix”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Anulaib</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Anulaibaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>ana</i> (“grandfather, ancestor”) +&lrm; <i>lāf</i> (“legacy”). Cognate to Old Norse <i>Óláfr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>respect, onlook, regard</li><li>consideration</li></ol><p>From <i>an-</i> +&lrm; <i>lēċ</i> (“a look”). Akin to Old English <i>lōcian</i> (“to look”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ānlīepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ānlīepiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>only</li><li>singular, incomparable, excellent</li></ol><p>From <i>ān</i> (“one”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>likeness, resemblance</li><li>image or statue</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>anlīcnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>single; sole; only</li><li>individual</li><li>alone; solitary</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ainahlaupijaz</i> (“single, alone; unmarried”), apparently with the literal meaning “running alone.” Cognate with Old Saxon <i>ēnlōpi</i> (“single, alone”) and Old Norse <i>einhleypr</i> (“single, unmarried”). Equivalent to <i>ān</i> (“alone”) + <i>-hlīepe</i> (<i>hlēapan</i> (“to jump, to run”) + <i>-e</i> (adjective-forming suffix)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>single; sole; only</li><li>individual</li><li>alone; solitary</li></ol><p>Apparently an alteration of <i>ānlīepe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ānlīepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ānlīepiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unite</li><li>to collect, gather together</li></ol><p>From <i>ān</i> +&lrm; <i>-lǣċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onlǣdan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave alone; let alone; forbear; give up; relinquish</li></ol><p>From <i>ān-</i> (“one; alone”) +&lrm; <i>lǣtan</i> (“to let; leave”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pride, pomp, arrogance, presumption</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unanimous</li><li>steadfast, eager</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ainamōd</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>einmuot</i>. Equivalent to <i>ān-</i> +&lrm; <i>-mōd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unanimously</li></ol><p><i>ānmōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unanimity</li></ol><p><i>ānmōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unity</li><li>union</li><li>covenant, agreement</li><li>loneliness, solitude</li></ol><p><i>ān</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ānnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one-minded, unanimous, agreed</li><li>persevering, resolute</li><li>prompt, vehement</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ainarēdaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>ān</i> (“one”) +&lrm; <i>rǣd</i> (“counsel”). Cognate with Old High German <i>einrāti</i> and Old Norse <i>einráðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ānrǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hermitage</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ān-</i> +&lrm; <i>setl</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hermit, recluse</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ān-</i> +&lrm; <i>setla</i>, literally "lone settler" or "someone who settles alone". Compare German <i>Einsiedler</i> and Icelandic <i>einsetumaður</i>, which are formed similarly.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>face</li><li>look, appearance</li><li>aspect, figure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anasiuniz</i>, equivalent to <i>an-</i> +&lrm; <i>sīen</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>ansiuni</i> (“countenance, face”), Old High German <i>anasiuni</i> (“face”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a narrow path</li><li>a steep path</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from <i>ān</i> (“one”) +&lrm; <i>stīg</i> (“way”), or from <i>an-</i> (“on-”) +&lrm; <i>stīg</i> (“way”). <br>Alternatively, perhaps an alteration of <i>&#42;angstīg</i>, from <i>ang-</i> (“narrow”) +&lrm; <i>stīg</i> (“way”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sound, healthy, uninjured</li><li>whole, entire; perfect; solid</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ansīen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antichrist</li></ol><p>From Medieval Latin <i>Antichristus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀντίχριστος</i> (Antíkhristos).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>antefn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anthem</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ἀντίφωνα</i> (antíphōna), from <i>ἀντί</i> (antí, “opposite”) + <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ, “voice, sound”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) prime, the first hour or tide (3-hour period) after dawn</li></ol><p><i>ān</i> (“one”) +&lrm; <i>tīd</i> (“time; hour, tide”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>andtimber</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antioch (a number of cities in Asia, named for a Macedonian general, <i>Ἀντίοχος</i> (Antíokhos))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Antiocha</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āninga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onwadan</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>an-</i> +&lrm; <i>wadan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>anweald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>power</li><li>empire</li></ol><p>Perhaps originally a calque of Late Latin <i>monarcha</i> (“monarchy, sole power”). In any case equivalent to <i>an-</i> (“one”) +&lrm; <i>weald</i> (“power”). Compare Swedish <i>envälde</i>, Danish <i>enevælde</i>, Icelandic <i>einveldi</i> and German <i>Anwalt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duel, single combat</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ainawīg</i>, variant of <i>&#42;ainawīgī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ainawīgiją</i>. Equivalent to <i>ān-</i> (“solo”) +&lrm; <i>wīġ</i> (“battle”), so called because it was between single individuals, rather than groups or armies.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stubborn</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ainawilljī</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>einwilli</i>. Equivalent to <i>ān-</i> +&lrm; <i>-wille</i>, literally "one-willed."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>stubbornly</li></ol><p><i>ānwille</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stubbornness</li></ol><p><i>ānwille</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one year old / year-old</li><li>one-year, yearlong</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ainawintruz</i>. Equivalent to <i>ān-</i> +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ānīedan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onǣlan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>simian (monkey, ape)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;apō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;apô</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>&#42;apa</i>, Old Saxon <i>apo</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;apo</i>, Old High German <i>affo</i>, Old Norse <i>api</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æppel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apostate</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>apostata</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἀποστάτης</i> (apostátēs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apostle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>apostolus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἀπόστολος</i> (apóstolos, “messenger, one sent forth”), from <i>ἀποστέλλω</i> (apostéllō, “I send off”), from <i>ἀπό</i> (apó, “from”) + <i>στέλλω</i> (stéllō, “I set”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æppel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>April</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Aprīlis</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wormwood</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>apuldor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apple tree</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;apuldraz</i>, <i>&#42;apuldrō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apple tree</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>apuldor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pervert, turn from</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;pǣran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scribal abbreviation of <i>apostol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ācweorna</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ācweorna</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honour, glory, grace</li><li>ore, brass, copper</li><li>oar</li><li>messenger, herald</li><li>angel</li><li>missionary</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aiʀu</i> (“respect, honour”). Cognate with German <i>Ehre</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aiʀ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aiz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>eir</i> (“brass, copper”), German <i>ehern</i> (“of metal, of iron”), Gothic <i>𐌰𐌹𐌶</i> (aiz, “ore”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;áyos, h₂éyos</i>. Compare Dutch <i>oer</i> (“iron-holding earth”). Compare Latin <i>aes</i> (“bronze, copper”), Avestan <i>𐬀𐬌𐬌𐬀𐬵</i> (aiiah), Sanskrit <i>अयस्</i> (áyas, “copper, iron”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;airu</i> (“oar”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;airō</i> (“oar”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>ár</i>, Danish <i>åre</i>, Swedish <i>åra</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;airu</i> (“messenger”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;airuz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>ēru</i>, Old Norse <i>árr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌿𐍃</i> (airus).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arabia (a peninsula and region of West Asia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Arabia</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Arabic, Arabian</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) an Arab</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Arabic language</li></ol><p>Formed in Old English as <i>Arabia</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i> or from Latin <i>arabicus</i> (<i>-icus</i> was usually changed to <i>-isċ</i> in loanwords from Latin).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unwind, unravel</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crossbow, arblast</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old French <i>arbaleste</i> (modern French <i>arbalète</i>), from Late Latin <i>arcuballista</i>, from Latin <i>arcus</i> (“bow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>arch-</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>archi-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>arċebisċop</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>arċebisċoprīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archbishop</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>archiepiscopus</i>, equivalent to <i>arċe-</i> +&lrm; <i>bisċop</i>. Compare Old Frisian <i>arcebiskop</i>, Old High German <i>erzibiscof</i>, Old Norse <i>erkibiskup</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archbishopdom, archbishopric</li></ol><p>From <i>ærċebisċop</i> +&lrm; <i>-dōm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an archbishopric</li></ol><p>From <i>arċebisċop</i> +&lrm; <i>-rīċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quickly</li><li>immediately</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eardwine</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>merciful</li></ol><p>From <i>ār</i> +&lrm; <i>dǣd</i>.</p></html>:/ˈɑːre/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ār</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ārǣdan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>honorable, pious, righteous</li><li>gracious, kind, merciful</li></ol><p>From <i>ār</i> +&lrm; <i>-fæst</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honorableness, honesty, goodness</li><li>piety</li><li>clemency, mercifulness</li></ol><p>From <i>ārfæst</i> (“honorable”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>earg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parsley piert (herb)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show mercy to, spare</li><li>to pardon</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aiʀēn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aizāną</i> (“to spare; protect; honour”); equivalent to <i>ār</i> (“honour”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Arian, related to Arianism</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Arianus</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>. Compare German <i>arianisch</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rise, arise, get up</li><li>to arise, originate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀrīsan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzrīsaną</i> (“to rise up, arise”), corresponding to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>rīsan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Arīus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Ἄρειος</i> (Áreios), from <i>Ἄρης</i> (Árēs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>honorless, disgraceful, wicked, impious</li></ol><p>From <i>ār</i> (“honor, glory, grace”) +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ārlēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>honest, honorable</li><li>noble, becoming, proper</li><li>(<i>of food</i>) delicious</li></ol><p>From <i>ār</i> (“honor”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>honorably, honestly, properly</li></ol><p>From <i>ār</i> (“honor”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>earm</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;arm</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;armaz</i> (“arm”), whence also Old High German <i>arm</i>, Old Norse <i>armr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Armenia</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>contraction</i>) Northumbrian form of <i>earon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quickly, immediately</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Northumbrian form of <i>earon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) the Danes</li></ol><p><i>ār</i> (“honor”) +&lrm; <i>Sċyldingas</i> (“Danes”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coppersmith</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ār</i> +&lrm; <i>smiþ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) help, kindness</li></ol><p>From <i>ār</i> +&lrm; <i>stæf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eart</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>contraction</i>) Northumbrian form of <i>earon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Northumbrian form of <i>earon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Arwald; last King of the Isle of Wight.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aiʀuwald</i>, from <i>&#42;aiʀu</i> (“honor, reverence”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wald</i> (“power, authority”). Equivalent to <i>ār</i> +&lrm; <i>weald</i>. Possibly cognate with Old High German <i>Eroald</i>, <i>Erolt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>earh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honor, reverence</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ār</i> (“honor”) +&lrm; <i>weorþung</i> (“reverence”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ārȳþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wave from an oar</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ār</i> +&lrm; <i>ȳþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reach, get at</li><li>to hold out, reach out</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>determined, resolute, stalwart</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣde</i>. Compare oftræde, twirǣde, wiþerrǣde, unfæstrǣd.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take counsel, care for, appoint, determine, fix</li><li>to conjecture, guess, prophesy, utter, interpret; read</li><li>to read a riddle, read what is written</li><li>to prepare; ready</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzrēdaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set free, unwrap</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise, lift up, elevate</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise, lift up</li><li>to establish</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣran</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eart</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scribal abbreviation of <i>arċebisċop</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sow</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sāwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>æsca</i></li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Asca)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Axe (a river in Dorset, Somerset, and east Devon, England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake off, remove</li><li>to be removed, forsake, flee</li><li>to shake, brandish, or be shaken</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċacan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āsċeādan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shave off</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċafan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be ashamed, make ashamed</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċamian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Acsan mynster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æsċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) to separate, dissociate, or segregate</li><li>to exclude</li><li>to distinguish</li><li>to make distinct or clear</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċeādan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āsċūfan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āsċuniendlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āsċunung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shoot, move rapidly (+ preposition <i>of</i>, from)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to shoot (a missile or its target)</li><li>to shoot forth or out (+ preposition <i>of</i>, from)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċēotan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask <i>(+ genitive a question)</i> <i>(+ accusative a person)</i> <i>(+ genitive about something)</i></li><li>to demand</li><li>to learn about by asking</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aiskōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) interrogative</li></ol><p>From <i>āscian</i> (“to ask”) +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to create, originate</li><li>to appoint, give</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċieppan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine forth</li><li>to be clear or evident</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċīnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āsċirian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āsċirienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, remove</li><li>to set apart</li><li>to cut off from association</li><li>to exclude, expel</li><li>to rob</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzskarjaną</i>; by surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċirian</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐌿𐍃𐍃𐌺𐌰𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (usskarjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āsċirian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disjunctive</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>āsċiriende</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>, else <i>āsċirian</i> +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>disjunctively, severally</li></ol><p>From <i>āsċiriġendlīċ</i> (“disjunctive”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āsċirienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trip up</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċrenċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push or shove</li><li>to drive away, repel</li><li>to expel</li><li>to drive forward, impel, overthrow</li><li>to give up</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċūfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>question</li><li>inquiry</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aiskungu</i>, equivalent to <i>āscian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>āskinge</i>, Old High German <i>eiscunga</i>, Middle Low German <i>êschinge</i>, Middle Dutch <i>eischinge</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to avoid, shun, fly from</li><li>to hate, detest, abhor</li><li>to accuse, reprove, convict</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċunian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>detestable, abominable</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>āsċuniende</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>, else <i>āsċunian</i> +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>execration, abomination</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>āsċunian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, remove, take away</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċyndan</i> (“to hasten”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to search or seek out, seek for, require, demand</li><li>to seek, go to, explore; seek through, search through</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzsōkijaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tell, utter a word, speak out, assert, declare</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>seċġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expel, banish, deliver</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sellan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send, send out, send forth</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>sendan</i> (“to send”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to become sluggish, weak, or slothful; to languish</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;seolcan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look at</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzsehwaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēon</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>āsiā</i>, Old High German <i>irsehan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌿𐍃𐍃𐌰𐌹𐍈𐌰𐌽</i> (ussaiƕan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>set, fixed</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set up, set out, place, lay down</li><li>appoint</li><li>propose, suggest</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>settan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to affirm</li><li>to confirm</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asia (the largest continent, located between Europe and the Pacific Ocean)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Asia (an ancient province of the Roman Empire in western Anatolia, existing between 133 BC (during the Roman Republic) and the 7th century AD)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Asia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Ᾰ̓σῐ́ᾱ</i> (Ăsĭ́ā), from Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀯𐀹𐀊</i> (a-si-wi-ja /⁠aswijaː⁠/), in turn probably from Hittite <i>𒀸𒋗𒉿</i> (aš-šu-wa /⁠Aššuwa⁠/, “northwest Anatolia”) of uncertain origin.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decline, go down, fall down</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sīgan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(with</i> <i>upp</i><i>)</i> to sit up</li><li>to be aground (<i>of a ship</i>)</li><li>to be afraid <i>(+ genitive of something)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sittan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name &lsqb;1019&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From Eastern Old Norse <i>Áslakr</i> from <i>áss</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>leikr</i> (“play, practise”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look into closely, investigate</li><li>to consider, ponder</li><li>to find out by investigating, devise</li><li>to deem, judge</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>smēaġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āsmēaġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āsmēaġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āsmēaġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dissolute, greedy, tedious</li><li>lukewarm, remiss, inert, lazy</li><li>cowardly</li></ol><p>From <i>āseolcan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idleness, sloth, slothfulness, sluggishness, laziness</li></ol><p>From <i>āsolcen</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āsolcennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to allure from, entice, persuade, urge</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>spanan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prosper</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>spēdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend entirely, consume, squander</li><li>to bestow, distribute</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>spendan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spring up, arise</li><li>to cease, lack</li></ol><p>From <i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>springan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>defunct, deficient</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eclipse</li><li>failing, defect</li></ol><p>From <i>āsprungen</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āsprungennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cleanse, wash; purify</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;spȳlian</i>, of uncertain origin and relation. Presumably from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spūlijōn</i> or possibly <i>&#42;spaulijōn</i>, though no other cognates support either of these forms. Compare Old High German <i>spuolen</i> (“to wash”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spōlijan</i> (“to wash”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ass, donkey</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>assen</i>, from Celtic (compare Old Irish <i>asan</i>, Old Cornish <i>asen</i>), from Latin <i>asinus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>she-ass</li><li>alternative form of <i>assa</i></li></ol><p>From earlier <i>assen</i>, from Latin <i>asina</i>. By surface analysis, <i>assa</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i> (feminine)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>she-ass</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Assyrian</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand up, rise up</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>standan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set forth</li><li>to set, place</li><li>to establish, appoint</li><li>to undertake, undergo, begin</li><li>alternative form of <i>āstiellan</i></li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>stellan</i> (“to place, set”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>steorfan</i> (“to die”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leap off, rush</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>stiellan</i> (“leap”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>starry</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;stierred</i>, implying a lost verb <i>&#42;stierran</i> meaning “to adorn with stars,” derived from the noun <i>steorra</i>; cf. the modern verb “to star.” Compare <i>āġimmed</i> (“set with precious stones”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go, proceed</li><li>to come</li><li>to climb, rise</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>stīgan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gouge out</li><li>to stab</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzstinganą</i>, equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>stingan</i>, literally “to stab out.” Cognate with Gothic <i>𐌿𐍃𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (usstaggan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blunt, stint, make dull</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;stintan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āstirian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āstirienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āstyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āstirian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āstirienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch out, extend</li><li>to prostrate, bow down</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>streċċan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āstiellan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āstyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āstyrienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stir up, disturb</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>styrian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āstyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āstyrienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āstierred</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>asundrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate</li><li>to disjoin</li><li>to sunder, put asunder</li><li>to sever</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sundrian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>separable</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>āsundrod</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to languish, fail, cease</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>swāmian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confound, dismay</li><li>to abash</li><li>to fear, stand in awe of, revere</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;swārcian</i> (“to weigh down, become heavy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be grieved; be confounded</li></ol><p>From <i>āswārcian</i> +&lrm; <i>-nian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blacken, darken, obscure</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sweartian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lull, pacify, calm</li><li>to annul, destroy</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>swebban</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āswebban</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eradicate</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;swefecian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sweltan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to afflict, trouble, bother</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>swenċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to languish, fail</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sweorcan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to invade</li><li>to overrun</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>swōgan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idleness, slothfulness</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be confounded, afflicted, troubled, or grieved</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to wander away</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;swǣman</i> (“to trouble, afflict, grieve”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swaimijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swaimijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>sveima</i> (“to wander or roam about”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āsyndrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āsundrian</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>syndrian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a separation, division, divorce</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>āsyndrian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oat</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aitā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ætgār</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>horrible, horrid</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set on fire, kindle, inflame</li><li>to excite</li><li>to light up</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>tendan</i> (“to kindle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;literal&#32;or&#32;figurative) to draw, pull, or lead (somebody or something) out or away (from, out of, off, to something) (+ fram, ūt of, of, tō)</li><li>to draw a weapon</li><li>to deal with</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to treat a person</li><li>to employ property or talents</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to draw to somewhere; to go or come; to make a journey</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀteuhan</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>tēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fail</li><li>to become tired or weary</li><li>to cease, leave off</li><li>to lack, be wanting</li><li><i>(by extension, of grammar)</i> to be defective</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>teorian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ætīewan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ātorlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Athenians</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Athenian</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land used to grow oats</li></ol><p>From <i>āte</i> (“oats”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>terrible, dire, hideous, loathsome, evil</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;atalaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poison, venom</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aitr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poisonous</li></ol><p>From <i>ātor</i> +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spider</li></ol><p>From <i>ātor</i> (“poison”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;coppe</i> (“something round”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kopp</i>. Identical in formation to Old Danish <i>ederkoppæ</i> (whence Danish <i>edderkop</i>) and Old Swedish <i>eterkoppe</i>. For more, see <i>cop</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the art of poisoning</li><li>sorcery</li></ol><p>From <i>ātor</i> +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poisonlike, bitter</li></ol><p>From <i>ātor</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a spider or spiderweb</li></ol><p><i>ātor</i> (“poison”) +&lrm; <i>loppe</i> (“spider”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a "venemous destroyer," used to describe the dragon in <i>Beowulf</i>.</li></ol><p><i>ātor</i> (“venom”) +&lrm; <i>sċeaþa</i> (“destroyer, ravager”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ator</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to treat, discuss</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>trahtnian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ātor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Roman emperor Augustus</li><li>the month of August</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Augustus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āwiht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, from Greek epic poetry, Odysseus</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Ulixēs</i> (“Ulysses/Odysseus”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>always, ever, forever</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aiw</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for all time, for evermore, forever</li></ol><p>From <i>āwa</i> (“ever”) +&lrm; <i>tō</i> (“to”) +&lrm; <i>ealdor</i> (“life, age”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to wake up</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>wacan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow weak</li><li>to languish, decline, fail</li><li>to relax, be indolent</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow, to wax, arise, to come forth</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>weaxan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weft</li></ol><p>Prefix is either <i>ā-&#47;an-&#47;on-</i> (“on/upon/to, in/into”) or <i>ā-&#47;ar-</i> (“away”). See also <i>āwefan</i>, <i>wefan</i>.</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wake (somebody) up</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>weċċan</i>. Cognate with German <i>erwecken</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move</li><li>to remove</li><li>to shake</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>weċġan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onweġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onweġ-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>awl</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ahwal</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ahwalaz</i> (“fork, hook”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disfigure</li><li>to defile</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>wemman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;intransitive) to turn</li><li>to return</li><li>(transitive,&#32;intransitive) to change</li><li>to translate</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go or depart</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>wendan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw out or away</li><li>to reject</li><li>to throw off, free oneself from</li><li>to expel</li><li>to abort (a fetus)</li><li>to shed (skin)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzwerpaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>weorpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cease to be</li><li>to become insipid or unappetizing</li><li>to become worthless</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀwerþan</i>, equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>weorþan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>āwerthan</i>, Old High German <i>irwerdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āwerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āwerienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ward off</li><li>to defend, protect</li><li>to cover</li><li>to wear, wear out</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>werian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āwerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āwerienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay waste, ravage, destroy</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>wēstan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a destroyer, devastator, ravager</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>āwēstan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>, more at <i>wēstan</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Awestend)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the angel Abaddon or Apollyon</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>āwēstan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>, more at <i>wēstan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destruction, devastation</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>awēstan</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>anything, something</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at all</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aiwwiht</i>. Equivalent to <i>ā</i> +&lrm; <i>wiht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be proud; to grow exultant</li><li>to grow haughty, insolent, or arrogant</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>wlancian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make splendid, proud, or rich</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>wlenċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>always, ever</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a degenerate or worthless person</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thrown out, rejected</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rejection</li><li>throwing out or away</li></ol><p><i>āworpen</i> + <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive away, expel</li><li>to hit, strike</li><li>to relate, recite, or sing</li><li>to avenge, revenge</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrecan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to awaken</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzwrakjaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>wreċċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write, write out, write down, compose</li><li>to record</li><li>to transcribe</li><li>to inscribe, cover in writing, draw, engrave</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrītan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrītaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>awel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glorify</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>wuldrian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abide</li><li>to remain, continue</li><li>to insist</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>wunian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do, effect</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzwurkijaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strangle, suffocate</li><li>to corrupt, injure, curse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀwurgijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurgijaną</i> (“to strangle”). Cognate with German <i>erwürgen</i>. Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To root out; crush</li></ol><p>Surface Analysis ā- ("away from, out;" in this case strengthening the secondary meaning of the following) + wyrtwalian, converted from the noun wyrtwala, "root, stock;" possibly from wyrt "plant or herb" (Ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurt-</i>; see wort) + &#42;wala "root" (Compare vǫlr "round stick").</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to awaken</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>wæcnan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to awaken</li><li>to arise</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>either</li><li>each</li><li>one or the other</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>āhwæþer</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æsċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āscian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āsciendlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āsciendlīċ</i></li></ol><p>From <i>āxian</i> (“to ask”) +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æsċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āscung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>āþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āþweran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an oath</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aiþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aiþaz</i> (“oath”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āþbryċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the breach of an oath</li><li>perjury</li></ol><p>From <i>āþ</i> +&lrm; <i>bryċe</i>. Compare Dutch <i>eedbreuk</i>, German <i>Eidbruch</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to take on, receive, consume, destroy <i>(precise meaning unclear)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>þeċġan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þagjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Æþelweald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Æþelwulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think of something (meaning "conceive of" or "invent," not "think about")</li><li>to make up something</li><li>to intend</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uzþankijan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>irdenken</i> (German <i>erdenken</i>). Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>þenċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>stretch out</li><li>to extend</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> (“away; a-”) +&lrm; <i>þennan</i> (“extend”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āþīedan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āþīestrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wind, sound or blow a musical instrument</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>þēotan</i> (“to howl”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣġhwæþer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lizard</li><li>newt</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;agiþahsijā</i> (“lizard, newt”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disjoin, detach, separate</li></ol><p>From <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>þīedan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be obscured, eclipsed</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>þīestrian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>either</li><li>one of two</li><li>both</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>āhwæþer</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be annoying, to be loathsome</li><li>to weary</li><li>to bore</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> (intensive prefix) + <i>þrēotan</i> (“weary, annoy”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>thriotan</i>, <i>athriotan</i>, Dutch <i>verdrieten</i>, German <i>verdrießen</i> and Old Norse <i>þrjóta</i> (whence also Icelandic <i>þrjóta</i> and Swedish <i>tryta</i>). Related to o-grade iterative verb Old English <i>þrēatian</i> (“to threaten, to push”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oath</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aiþastabaz</i>, equivalent to <i>āþ</i> (“oath”) +&lrm; <i>stæf</i> (“staff, letter”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>son-in-law</li><li>brother-in-law</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aiþum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wash out</li><li>to cleanse</li><li>to baptize</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>þwēan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to churn</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to shake or stir together (e.g. with a <i>þwirel</i>)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>þweran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āþīestrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scribal abbreviation of <i>abbadisse</i></li><li>scribal abbreviation of <i>abbad</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scribal abbreviation of <i>abbadisse</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Babylon (an ancient city, the ancient capital of Babylonia in modern Iraq, built on the banks of the Euphrates)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Babylon (the ancient capital of the kingdom of Babylonia, in modern Iraq)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Babylon (an ancient city, the ancient capital of Babylonia in modern Iraq, built on the banks of the Euphrates)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Babylōnia</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Babylonian</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) the Bablyonians</li></ol><p>From <i>Babilōn</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>Babilōn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Babilōn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bake</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bakan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bakaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₃g-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html>:/bɑːd/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waiting; expectation</li><li>something distrained; pledge, stake</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;baidu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;baidō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Badby (a village and civil parish in West Northamptonshire, Northamptonshire, England, previously in Daventry district)</li></ol><p>From <i>Baddan</i> (a name) +&lrm; <i>burg</i> (“fortress”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take a pledge or fine</li></ol><p>From <i>bād</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Badminton (a village and civil parish in South Gloucestershire district, Gloucestershire, England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beadu</i></li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Badu)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>badu</i> (“battle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name; variant form Beadufriþ</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>badu</i> (“battle, war”) + <i>mann</i> (“man”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Badwōmundu</i>, from <i>&#42;badu</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;mundu</i> (“protection, protector”). Equivalent to <i>beadu</i> +&lrm; <i>mund</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Baudemund</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>badu</i> (“battle”) + <i>swīþ</i> (“strong, mighty”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Beaduwine</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Badwōwulf</i>, from <i>&#42;badu</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wulf</i> (“wolf”). Equivalent to <i>beadu</i> +&lrm; <i>wulf</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Baudulf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>balk, beam; bank, ridge</li><li>a covering</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;balkō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>bald</i> (“brave”) + <i>helm</i> (“defence, protection”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, variant of Bealdhere</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bealdlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>baldlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bealdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Bealdrǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Balþawini</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Balþawiniz</i>. Displaced by the Norman ancestor of modern English <i>Baldwin</i> in the late 11th century.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Balþawulf</i>, from <i>&#42;balþ</i> (“bold, brave”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wulf</i> (“wolf”). Equivalent to <i>beald</i> +&lrm; <i>wulf</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Baltolf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bone</li><li>ivory</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bain</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bainą</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bēn</i> (West Frisian <i>bien</i>), Old Saxon <i>bēn</i> (Low German <i>been</i>, <i>bein</i>), Dutch <i>been</i> (“bone, leg”), Old High German <i>bein</i> (German <i>Bein</i> (“leg”)), Old Norse <i>bein</i> (Icelandic <i>bein</i> (“bone”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>murderer</li><li>Something which causes death.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;banō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;banô</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bona</i>, Old Saxon <i>bano</i>, Old High German <i>bano</i>, Old Norse <i>bani</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bench</li><li>hillock; barrow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;banku</i>, a variant of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;banki</i> and <i>&#42;bankō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bankiz</i>, <i>&#42;bankô</i>, respectively.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) the body</li></ol><p>From <i>bān</i> (“bone”) +&lrm; <i>cofa</i> (“chamber”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bānwyrt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) the body</li></ol><p>From <i>bān</i> (“bone”) +&lrm; <i>fæt</i> (“vessel”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neck bone</li><li>vertebra</li></ol><p>From <i>bān</i> (“bone”) +&lrm; <i>hring</i> (“ring”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) body, torso</li></ol><p>Kenning of <i>bān</i> (“bone”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”). Compare <i>sāwelhūs</i> (“body”, literally “soul house”) and <i>feorhhūs</i> (“body”, literally “spirit house”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>boneless</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bainalausaz</i>, equivalent to <i>bān</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to summon, especially by proclamation</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bannan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bannaną</i> (“curse, forbid”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂-</i> (“say”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Banbury (a town in Oxfordshire, England)</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;Banna</i> (“name of a 6th-century Saxon chieftain said to have built a stockade in that place”) or <i>bana</i>, <i>bona</i> (“a byname meaning 'felon, murderer'”) + burg</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scone</li></ol><p>From Old Irish <i>bannach</i>, based on Latin <i>pānicum</i> (“millet”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bānwyrt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bānwyrt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bānwyrt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A plant used to mend bone fractures (particularly pansy, daisy or comfrey).</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bainawurti</i>; equivalent to <i>bān</i> (“bone”) +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i> (“plant”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boar</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bair</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Bar)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>bār</i> (“boar”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bark</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>bǫrkr</i> (“tree bark”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;barkuz</i>. Possibly displaced unattested, inherited <i>&#42;bearc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay bare</li><li>to remove a covering</li><li>to strip, despoil</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>bær</i> (“bare”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bartholomew, a disciple of Jesus</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Bartholomaeus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Βαρθολομαῖος</i> (Bartholomaîos), from Aramaic <i>בר תלמי</i> (bar tolmai, “son of Talmai; son of Ptolemy”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a light vessel used for sailing or rowing</li></ol><p>From or cognate with Old Norse <i>barði</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Bartholomew</li><li>Bartholomew the Apostle, one of Jesus' disciples</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Bartholomaeus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Βαρθολομαῖος</i> (Bartholomaîos), from Aramaic <i>בר תלמי</i> (bar tolmai, “son of Talmai; son of Ptolemy”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to await, expect</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bai(d)snōn</i>, probably derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;baidō</i> (“expectation”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bīdaną</i> (“to wait”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expectation</li></ol><p>From <i>bāsnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>basu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>purple; scarlet; crimson</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colour</i>) purple</li><li>a scarlet robe</li><li>berry</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;basu</i> (“crimson, purple”).</p><p>From the adjective above.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;basu</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;basi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;basją</i> (“berry”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boat</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bait</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fatten, to get fatter</li><li>to improve, to get better, to heal</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;batēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a boatman</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bat</li><li>cudgel</li><li>club</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ship's watchman; keeper or commander of a ship</li></ol><p>From <i>bāt</i> (“boat, ship, vessel”) +&lrm; <i>weard</i> (“ward, guardian, keeper”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Baþanċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bathe</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;baþōną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>badōn</i>, Old Norse <i>baða</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bath (a city in Somerset, England)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;baþą</i> (“bath”), named for its Roman baths</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bath (a city in Somerset, England)</li></ol><p>From <i>baþum</i> +&lrm; <i>ċeaster</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bathe</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;baþōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>about (concerning)</li><li>by, in various senses:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>near or next to</li><li>not later than</li><li>based on, according to</li></ol><li>for, in the account of</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>a productive prefix usually used to form verbs and adjectives, especially&#58;</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bi-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bi-</i>, equivalent to an unstressed form of <i>bī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēacn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>signal fire, lighthouse</li></ol><p>From <i>bēacen</i> +&lrm; <i>fȳr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a stone on which a beacon burns</li><li>lighthouse</li></ol><p>From <i>bēacen</i> +&lrm; <i>stān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sign, signal</li><li>beacon</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;baukn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;baukną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bāken</i>, Old Saxon <i>bōkan</i>, Old High German <i>bouhhan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēacnung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>bīecnan</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bauknōną</i>. Equivalent to <i>bēacn</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>allegorical</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) indicative</li></ol><p>From <i>bēacnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a beckoning, a nodding</li><li>speaking by tropes or figures</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>bēacnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Bedanfordsċīr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Bedanfordsċīr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beadu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beadurinc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beaduweorc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Badwōwulf</i>, from <i>&#42;badu</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wulf</i> (“wolf”). Equivalent to <i>beadu</i> +&lrm; <i>wulf</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Baudulf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>battle, war</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;badu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;badwō</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>bǫð</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warcraft, strength in war</li></ol><p>From <i>beadu</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i> (“craft”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Badwōfriþu</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Badwōfriþuz</i>, from <i>&#42;badwō</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;friþuz</i> (“peace”). Equivalent to <i>beadu</i> +&lrm; <i>friþ</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>Bárðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>Ffrom Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Badwōhildiz</i>. Equivalent to <i>beadu</i> +&lrm; <i>hild</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>Bǫðvildr</i> and Icelandic <i>Böðvildur</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>beadu</i> +&lrm; <i>rīc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warrior</li></ol><p>From <i>beadu</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>rinc</i> (“man”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong, renowned in battle</li></ol><p><i>beadu</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>rōf</i> (“strong; renowned”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a sword or blade</i>) battle-sharp, keen in fight</li></ol><p>From <i>beadu</i> +&lrm; <i>sċearp</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>battle-work, warlike operation</li></ol><p>From <i>beadu</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>weorc</i> (“work”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Badwōwini</i>, from <i>&#42;badu</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wini</i> (“friend”). Equivalent to <i>beadu</i> +&lrm; <i>wine</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Badwin</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circular object worn on the body, especially one made of gold or silver: ring, armlet, crown, collar, torque</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;baug</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;baugaz</i>, derived from <i>&#42;beuganą</i> (“to bend”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bāg</i>, Old Saxon <i>bōg</i>, Old High German <i>boug</i>, and Old Norse <i>baugr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) ring-giver</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;baugagebō</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>bōggeƀo</i> and Old High German <i>bouggebo</i>. Equivalent to <i>bēag</i> +&lrm; <i>ġiefa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēagġiefa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēagġiefa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crown</li></ol><p><i>bēag</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>bēag</i> (“crown, ring”) + <i>lāc</i> (“play, battle, offering”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>bēag</i> (“ring (circular object worn on the body)”) and <i>mund</i> (“protector”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>bēag</i> (“crown, ring”) +&lrm; <i>nōþ</i> (“daring, courage”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>bēag</i> (“crown, ring”) + <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the form or fashion of a circle</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>bēag</i> +&lrm; <i>wīse</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēag</i></li></ol><p>Inflected forms.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēagġiefa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēagġiefa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come forth</li><li>to utter</li><li>to belch</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;balkattjan</i>, <i>&#42;balkittjan</i>, equivalent to <i>bealcian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to emit, utter</li><li>to cry out</li><li>to boast</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;balkōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;belkaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brave, bold</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;balþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;balþaz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>bald</i>, Old High German <i>bald</i>, Old Norse <i>ballr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Balþahari</i>, from <i>&#42;balþ</i> (“bold”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hari</i> (“army”). Equivalent to <i>beald</i> +&lrm; <i>here</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Baldheri</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be brave, act bravely</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>beald</i> (“bold”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>boldly, confidently, earnestly, instantly, impudently, saucily</li></ol><p>From <i>beald</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>bealdlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boldness</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>beald</i> (“bold”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prince</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Balþarād</i>, from <i>&#42;balþ</i> (“bold”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;rād</i> (“advice, counsel”). Equivalent to <i>beald</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣd</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Baldrāt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>saucy, bold in speech</li></ol><p><i>beald</i> (“bold”) +&lrm; <i>-wyrde</i> (“worded”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>testicle</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;beall</i> +&lrm; <i>-uc</i> (diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bealu</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bealu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bealucwealm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bealuhyġdiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bealusīþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) harm, evil</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) baleful, deadly, dangerous, wicked, evil</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;balu</i> (“evil”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>balu</i>, Old Saxon <i>balu</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;balu</i> (“evil”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) violent or pernicious death</li></ol><p><i>bealu</i> +&lrm; <i>cwealm</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>baleful</li></ol><p><i>bealu</i> (“harm, evil”) +&lrm; <i>-ful</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) evil-minded, hostile</li></ol><p><i>bealu</i> +&lrm; <i>hyġdiġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a destructive or deadly path</li><li>death</li><li>evil fortune, misfortune, calamity</li></ol><p>From <i>bealu</i> +&lrm; <i>sīþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tree</li><li>beam of wood</li><li>gallows</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) the Cross</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;baum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tree ink; oak products, especially oak-gall, which were used in the creation of ink.</li></ol><p>From <i>bēam</i> +&lrm; <i>telg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corduroy road</li></ol><p>From <i>bēam</i> +&lrm; <i>weġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bean (especially the broad bean)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;baunu</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bāne</i>, <i>bām</i> (West Frisian <i>bean</i>), Old Saxon <i>bōna</i> (Low German <i>Bohn</i>), Dutch <i>boon</i>, Old High German <i>bōna</i> (German <i>Bohne</i>), Old Norse <i>baun</i> (Danish <i>bønne</i>, Swedish <i>böna</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land used for growing beans</li></ol><p>From <i>bēan</i> (“bean”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beard</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bard</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bardaz</i> (compare West Frisian <i>burd</i>, Dutch <i>baard</i>, German <i>Bart</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰardʰeh₂</i> (compare Latin <i>barba</i>, Lithuanian <i>barzda</i>, Russian <i>борода́</i> (borodá)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beardless</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bardalaus</i>, equivalent to <i>beard</i> (“beard”) +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>castrated boar, barrow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;barug</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;barugaz</i>: cognate with Old Frisian <i>barch</i> (West Frisian <i>baarch</i>), Old Saxon <i>barug</i> (Dutch <i>barg</i>), Old High German <i>barug</i> (dialectal German <i>Barch</i>), Old Norse <i>bǫrgr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bearg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a moment</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bosom, lap</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) possession</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>bearm</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;barmaz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>baðmr</i> (Danish <i>barm</i>, Swedish <i>barm</i>), Gothic <i>𐌱𐌰𐍂𐌼𐍃</i> (barms), Old High German <i>barm</i>, Old Saxon <i>barm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barmcloth, apron</li></ol><p>From <i>bearm</i> +&lrm; <i>clāþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an apron</li></ol><p>From <i>bearm</i> +&lrm; <i>hræġl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>child</li><li>baby</li><li>a place to store barley</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;barn</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bern</i>, Old Saxon <i>barn</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;barn</i>, Old High German <i>barn</i>, Old Norse <i>barn</i>, Gothic <i>𐌱𐌰𐍂𐌽</i> (barn). Related to <i>beran</i>.</p><p>Inflections.</p><p>Contraction of <i>bereern</i>, <i>bereærn</i> (literally “barley-place”), equivalent to <i>bere</i> +&lrm; <i>ærn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pregnant</li></ol><p><i>bearn</i> (“child”) +&lrm; <i>ēacen</i> (“enlarged, pregnant”). Compare Old Norse <i>barni aukinn</i> (“pregnant with child”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>generation, conception, pregnancy</li></ol><p>From <i>bearnēacen</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>childless</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;barnalausaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bernlās</i> and Old Norse <i>barnlauss</i>. Equivalent to <i>bearn</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bearnlīest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bearnlīest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>childlessness</li></ol><p><i>bearnlēas</i> +&lrm; <i>-þ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parental love: love for one's child</li></ol><p><i>bearn</i> +&lrm; <i>lufu</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a succession of children; issue, posterity</li><li>progeny, offspring</li><li>childbearing, procreation</li></ol><p>From <i>bearn</i> (“child”) +&lrm; <i>tēam</i> (“family, offspring”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bearu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Berkshire (a county of England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Bearrocsċīr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bærs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a grove or wood</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;baru</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;barwaz</i> (“tree, pine forest, grove”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bearg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>basu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat</li><li>to hit, strike</li><li>to box</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bautan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bautaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beater, fighter, champion</li><li>boxer</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;bēat</i> (“a beating, blow”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gadfly</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bauw</i> (“hornet; horsefly”), cognate with West Frisian <i>bau</i> (“hornet”), dialectal Dutch <i>bauw</i> (“hornet, horsefly”), German Low German <i>Bau</i> (“horsefly, gadfly”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Beaw)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Bēaw</li></ol><p>From <i>Bēaw</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bathe, wash</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>baðian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bathe, wash</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>baþian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bamburgh (a village in Northumbria, England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Bebbanburg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive of the female given name Elisabeth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to command</li><li>to give up, offer, commend, commit</li><li>to announce</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>bēodan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bibiāda</i>, Old High German <i>bibiotan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) imperative mood</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind in or about</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bibindaną</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>bindan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bibinda</i>, Gothic <i>𐌱𐌹𐌱𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (bibindan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>befer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow upon</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>blāwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>order, mandate, command</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>bebēodan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>command, authority</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>bebod</i> +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>befer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break off; deprive, destroy, or spoil by breaking off; break to pieces; consume</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bibrekan</i> (“to break off”). Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>brecan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surround, encompass</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>būgan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sell, set or put to sale</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>byċġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bury</li><li>to save</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>byrġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burying, burial</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>bebyrġan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bebyrġing</i></li></ol><p>Alteration of earlier <i>bebyrġing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be anxious over, worry about</li><li>to care for, take charge of, look after</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;carcian</i> (“to be sorrowful, be anxious, worry”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mattock, pickaxe</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bekkō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeg-</i> (“hook; to bend, curve”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Becca)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beech</li><li>alternative form of <i>bæċ</i> (“stream; brook”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bōkijā</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;baki</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sell</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bikaupōn</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċēapian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beechen; made of beech</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēacn</i></li></ol><p><i>bōc</i> (“beech”) +&lrm; <i>-en</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to becarve, cut off, cut or pare away; flay</li><li>to behead</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċeorfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be heedful, pay attention</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be observant of, attend to [<i>with</i> genitive]</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>cēpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bōcere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beċierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sell</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċīepan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn around or about</li><li>to go around, bypass, pass by, avoid</li><li>to turn, pervert</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to betray or deceive</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċierran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fetter, bind, tie</li><li>to inclose, confine</li><li>to glue together; plaster over</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;biklammjan</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>clemman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beclipian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to challenge</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>clipian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beclȳsan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clasp, embrace, hold, clip</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>clyppan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bikleppa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an embrace</li></ol><p>From <i>beclyppan</i> (“to clasp, embrace”) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to close, shut</li><li>to enclose, shut in</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>clȳsan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beclemman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēacn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bīecnan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to know</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>cnāwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēacnian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knot</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bind or tie; tie up; attach</li><li>to tie around; inclose; surround</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>cnyttan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crave</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>crafian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to creep, creep into, crawl, bring secretly, be hidden</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>crēopan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come near, by, around, to, or into; to reach</li><li>to come to pass; to come about; to happen, occur</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bikwemaną</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>cuman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to die</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>cwelan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>becweþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say or assert</li><li>to reproach</li><li>to bequeath</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>cweþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beċierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bedd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, notably held by the Venerable Bede.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Bedanford</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bedford (a town in England)</li></ol><p>From <i>Bedan</i> (“Bede's”) +&lrm; <i>ford</i> (“ford”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bedfordshire (a historical county of England)</li></ol><p>From <i>Bedanford</i> (“Bedford”) +&lrm; <i>sċīr</i> (“shire”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bedchamber</li></ol><p>From <i>bed</i> +&lrm; <i>būr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bedroom</li></ol><p><i>bedd</i> +&lrm; <i>cleofa</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bedcleofa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;badi</i> (oblique form <i>*baddj-</i>), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;badją</i> (“resting-place”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bed</i>, Old Saxon <i>beddi</i>, Dutch <i>beddi</i>, Old High German <i>betti</i>, Old Norse <i>beðr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌱𐌰𐌳𐌹</i> (badi).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>someone bedridden, paralytic</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bedridden</li></ol><p>From <i>bedd</i> (“bed”) +&lrm; <i>rida</i> (“rider”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beg (for alms)</li></ol><p>Origin obscure. Perhaps from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bedukōn</i> or <i>&#42;badikōn</i> (“to petition repeatedly, beg”), equivalent to <i>bedu</i> +&lrm; <i>-cian</i>. Compare Old High German <i>betalōn</i> (“to beg”).</p><p>Alternatively, perhaps derived from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bedagō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bedagô</i> (“beggar”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig in and around; to bury, inter</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>delfan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>bidelvan</i>, Old Frisian <i>bidelva</i>, Old High German <i>bitelban</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chapel, oratory, place for praying</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deceive</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>dyderian</i> (“to cheat, trick”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hide <i>(+ dative from someone)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bidarnijan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>bidernian</i>. Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>diernan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide, conceal, cover, keep secret</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>dīglian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>drowned</li></ol><p>Past participle of <i>bedūfan</i> (“to put under”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shut</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>dōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw aside, seduce</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>dragan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beddrida</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beddrida</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overcome</li><li>to deceive</li><li>to deprive of, bereave, despoil</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>drēosan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beddrida</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive or thrust on or upon, enforce one to do something</li><li>to surround; beset</li><li>to pursue, follow</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>drīfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drink in, drink up, absorb</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bidrinkan</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>drincan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the cutting or reaping of grain on request</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beddrida</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>straw used for or as a bed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;baddjastrau</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>beddistrō</i>, Old High German <i>bettistrō</i>. Equivalent to <i>bedd</i> +&lrm; <i>strēaw</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bedtime</li></ol><p>From <i>bedd</i> (“bed”) +&lrm; <i>tīd</i> (“time”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>request</li><li>prayer; supplication</li><li>religious worship</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bedu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bedō</i> (“request; petition”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put under, submerge, immerse, drown</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>dūfan</i> (“to dive, sink”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prone to, or tending to ask; suppliant</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bedul</i>. Equivalent to <i>biddan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deceive</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>dyderian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dip, immerse; dunk</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>dyppan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deprive (+ acc <i>someone</i>) (+ dat <i>of something</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>dǣlan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>bidēlian</i>, Old High German <i>biteilen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deprived</li><li>devoid</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fold, infold, fold up, entwine, overwhelm, involve, enwrap; wrap up, bewrap</li><li>to attach, clasp</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bifalþan</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>fealdan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>bifaltan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to roll up</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;fealdian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall, fall to or upon; befall</li><li>to fall off; be bereft of</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bifallan</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>feallan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take or bereave by fighting</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>feohtan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bifiuchta</i>. Compare Old High German <i>bifehtan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to commit, commend, deliver, grant</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bury, bear, be pleased with</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to give oneself earnestly to something, press for, continue</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bifelhan</i>. Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>fēolan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beaver</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>*bebr</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bebru</i>. The alternative form <i>beofor</i> must have the slightly different pre-form <i>*bebur</i>, since it shows back umlaut. Related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bebruz</i> and Latin <i>fiber</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deceive</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>fician</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to notice, find out</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>findan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flay, take off the skin or bark</li><li>to strip off skin or bark</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>flēan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly about or around</li><li>to come by flying, fly onto</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>flēogan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flee, flee from or away, escape</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>flēon</i>. Compare Old High German <i>bifliohan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overflow</li><li>to come by flowing, flow to</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>flōwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comprehend, grasp; begrip, take hold of, catch</li><li>to surround, encompass; beset, encircle, enwrap, clothe, receive, conceive, inhold</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>fōn</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>bifāhan</i>, Old High German <i>bifāhan</i>, Old Frisian <i>bifā</i>. See <i>fang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>befer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>before, in front of</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in front</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>foran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to foretell</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>beforan</i> +&lrm; <i>cweþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to free, make free; liberate</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>frēoġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask, ask about, ask someone, ask for something, inquire, learn</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>friġnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>befriġnan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defile, befoul</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bifūlijan</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>fȳlan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fasten; fix; ground; establish; make safe; put in safe keeping</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to apply; utilize; commend; entrust to</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to commit</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bifastijan</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> (“around”) +&lrm; <i>fæstan</i> (“to secure, fasten”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fix</li><li>to pledge, betroth; plight</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bifastinōn</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>fæstnian</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bifestnia</i>, Middle Low German <i>bevestenen</i>, Old High German <i>bifestinōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bego, go over, traverse; get to, come by, fall into, go through, traverse</li><li>to go to, visit, care for, cultivate, affect</li><li>to occupy, inhabit, dwell, surround, besiege, overrun</li><li>to practise, do, engage in, perform, commit, exercise, attend to, be diligent about, honor, serve, worship, profess; pledge, devote, train oneself</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bigān</i>. Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>gān</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>bigān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bīgang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go by, go about or around; bego, surround</li><li>to go to, attend, commit, exercise, perform, carry out, observe, practice, worship</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;biganganą</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>gangan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>bigangan</i>, Old High German <i>bigangan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mulberry bush</li><li>a bramble</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;beġ</i> (“berry”) +&lrm; <i>bēam</i> (“tree”). Compare <i>beġer</i>, <i>beriġe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>beġeall</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>both</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bōjain</i>, a compound formed from <i>&#42;bō</i> (from the neuter of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bai</i>) + <i>&#42;jain</i>; equivalent to <i>bū</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>beyond, on the other side of</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>beyond, on the other side</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeond</i>, <i>ġeondan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beġeondan</i></li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beġeondan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour over, soak</li><li>to anoint, besprinkle, drench, cover with, bestrew with</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġēotan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>bigiozan</i> (German <i>begießen</i>). Compare Old Saxon <i>bigiotan</i> (Dutch <i>begieten</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>berry</li></ol><p>Probably from a metathesis of <i>berġe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to yell, scream, screech</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġiellan</i> (“yell”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to boast about</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġielpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to care for; to take care of</li><li>to regard, govern, keep</li><li>to serve; to attend</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġīeman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get</li><li>to obtain</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bigetaną</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġietan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;biginnan</i>, derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ginnaną</i>; equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;ginnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beġeondan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beġer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begnaw, gnaw</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>gnagan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deplore</li><li>to lament</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>gnornian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beġeondan</i></li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beġeondan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bīgang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bury</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bigrabaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>grafan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overwhelm with fear</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>grēosan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>begrǣtan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chide, chasten</li><li>to lay hold of; grasp, reprehend</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bigrīpan</i>. Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>grīpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lament, bewail</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>grǣtan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beġielpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beġīeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begird; gird about or around, clothe, surround; beleaguer</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>gyrdan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>bigurten</i> (“to begird”), Gothic <i>𐌱𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (bigairdan, “to begird”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beġietan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to surround or embrace something</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hold or contain somebody or something</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to understand something (+ dative or + on)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to withhold or hold something back</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to restrain or detain somebody</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bihabbjan</i>. Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>habban</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>promise</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>hāt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to promise</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>hātan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>promised land / Promised Land</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>behāt</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to behead</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>hēafdian</i> or <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>hēafod</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>behēafdung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beheading, decapitation</li></ol><p><i>behēafdian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold, occupy, retain</li><li>to do, perform</li><li>to look upon, behold</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bihaldan</i> (“to hold with, keep”). Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>healdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat, bruise</li><li>to hew or cut off, separate from; sunder from, deprive or bereave of</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>hēawan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>bihauwan</i>, Old High German <i>bihauwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>behēfe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>necessary</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>behēfe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hedge around, behedge</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>hegian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover over</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>helmian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Bohemians</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>on this side of <i>(+ dative)</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on this side</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>heonan</i>, literally "by from here."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beheonan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>behind</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>behind</li></ol><p><i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>hindan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beheonan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beheonan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leap upon or in, fix</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>hlēapan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>behliehhan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to close, to cover or cover over</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>hlīdan</i>. The latter is related to <i>hlid</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laugh at; laugh over, deride, exult</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>hliehhan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to load (a ship)</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;hlæstan</i>. The latter is only attested with the <i>ofer-</i> and <i>ġe-</i> prefix.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>advantage, behoof, profit; need</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bihōf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have need of, need</li><li>to be needful to, necessary</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;hōfian</i>; compare <i>behōf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>behoveful, needful, proper</li></ol><p>From <i>behōf</i> (“advantage”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a custom, practice</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>behyċġan</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be anxious, careful</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>hogian</i> (to think about)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to behang, hang around</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>hōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>behangen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to deprive of a horse</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>horsian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>hrēosan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lamenting</li><li>repentance, penitence</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>hrēowsung</i> or <i>behrēowsian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scoff at, rail, trouble, plague; bedevil</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>hrōpan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bihrōpa</i>, Old High German <i>biruofōn</i>, Middle High German <i>berüefen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn; to change; to spread about</li><li>to attend to something or somebody; to field dress; to treat something; to minister to somebody</li><li>to put in order, to arrange, to direct</li><li>to exercise, to practice</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>hweorfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think something over, bear something in mind, consider, trust</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>hyċġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide, conceal, cover; bethatch</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>hȳdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>carefully</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-command, vow, promise</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bihaisi</i>; equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>hǣs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bīehþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run into/by</li><li>to happen or occur</li><li>to come into one's mind</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>irnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow around, enclose</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>lācan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to excuse, clear of guilt; exonerate</li><li>to apologize <i>(generally reflexive)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>lādian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>excuse</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>lādung</i> or <i>belādian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deprive someone of land</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bealcettan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bieldu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bieldu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to censure, reprove; charge with</li><li>to dissuade (+ dative <i>someone</i>) from; forbid; prevent</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>lēan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay upon, cover; overspread; overlay; bethatch</li><li>to afflict, load, charge; belast; beload</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>leċġan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bilega</i>, Old High German <i>bileggen</i> (German <i>belegen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>belīefan</i> (“to believe”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deprive of land, disinherit</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>lendan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deceive by lies</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bileugan</i> (“to belie”). Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>lēogan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let go, lose</li><li>to deprive of, be deprived of</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bileusaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;lēosan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bilewit</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bielġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become angry, to swell with rage</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;belgan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie by or about, beleaguer, encompass, beset</li><li>to extend by or out</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;biliggjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;biligjaną</i> (“to lie around”), equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>liċġan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>biliggen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to believe</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bilaubijan</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>līefan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stay or remain</li><li>to continue, remain over, be left</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bilīban</i>. Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; a Proto-Germanic verb meaning "to be left," which is the root of Old English <i>lāf</i> and <i>lǣfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bielġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>event, occurrence</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to happen</li><li>to belong</li><li>to have to do with, pertain</li><li>to be applicable</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>limpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to castrate</li><li>to emasculate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>lystan</i> (“to lust”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a eunuch</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>belisnian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>belle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roar, bellow, bark</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bellan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bellaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bell</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bellā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bellǭ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>signal by bell</li></ol><p>From <i>belle</i> +&lrm; <i>tācen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A belt.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;baltī̆</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;baltijaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>balz</i>, Old Norse <i>belti</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shut in; enclose, confine</li><li>to close</li><li>to lock up</li><li>to conclude (come or bring to an end)</li><li>to include</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bilūkan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bilūka</i>, Old Saxon <i>bilūkan</i>, and Old High German <i>bilūhhan</i>. Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>lūcan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>belīefan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deceive</li></ol><p><i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;lyrtan</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lurtijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Beelzebub</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Beelzebūb</i>, the Vulgate's form of Ancient Greek <i>Βεελζεβούλ</i> (Beelzeboúl), from Hebrew <i>בעל זבוב</i> (ba‘al-z'vúv, “fly-lord”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead into, bring forth, set in motion, cause</li><li>to impose, intrude</li><li>to mislead, seduce</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bilaidijan</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣdan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>bileiten</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be left; remain</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bilaibijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bilaibijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mutilate, maim</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bīeme</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to denounce someone, disclose, reveal a secret</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bimelþōn</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>meldian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bīemere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to measure by, estimate, account, consider, compare; liken</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>metan</i>. Compare Old High German <i>bimezzan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide, conceal, lie hid</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>mīþan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>bimīthan</i>, Old High German <i>bimīdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bemoan, mourn over, bewail, care for; becare</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>murnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exchange for</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;mutian</i>, from Latin <i>mūtō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bemoan, bewail, lament, mourn</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>mǣnan</i>. See <i>mean</i> and <i>moan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prayer, praying</li><li>request, entreaty</li><li>boon</li><li>alternative form of <i>benn</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bōni</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>bón</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;banjō</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>ben</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>petitioner, requester</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>benōme</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>benōmen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>benōmon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bench</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;banki</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bankiz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>banc</i> (Dutch <i>bank</i>), Old High German <i>banch</i> (German <i>Bank</i>), Old Norse <i>&#42;benkr</i> (Swedish <i>bänk</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bond, coil</li><li>ribbon</li><li>crown, ornament</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bandiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind</li><li>to bend a bow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bandijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bandijaną</i> (“to bind”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to affirm</li><li>to declare, stipulate</li><li>to appoint, settle; to name to a position</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>nemnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deprive of (<i>+ dative</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bineutaną</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>nēotan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bineoþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Benson (a village and civil parish in South Oxfordshire district, Oxfordshire, England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Beniamin</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deprive</li><li>to take, seize</li><li>to catch, apprehend</li><li>to obtain</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;binemaną</i> (“to take away”). Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>niman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wound; mortal injury</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;banjō</i> (“wound”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>beni</i> (“wound”), Old Norse <i>ben</i> (“wound”), Gothic <i>𐌱𐌰𐌽𐌾𐌰</i> (banja, “wound”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wound</li></ol><p>From <i>benn</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>benam</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to use, consume</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>notian</i>. More at <i>note</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>benotienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pray</li></ol><p>From <i>bēn</i> (“prayer”) +&lrm; <i>-sian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Benesingtūn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bee</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;biumi</i> (“am”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰew-</i> (“to become, be”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bijā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bijō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰey-</i> (“bee”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bē</i> (West Frisian <i>bij</i>), Old Saxon <i>bīa</i> (Middle Low German <i>bīe</i>), Dutch <i>bij</i>, Old High German <i>bīa</i>, <i>bini</i> (German <i>Biene</i>, dialectal <i>Beie</i>), Old Norse <i>bý</i> (Danish and Swedish <i>bi</i>). The Indo-European root is also the source of Lithuanian <i>bi̇̀tė</i>, Latvian <i>bite</i>, Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bьčela</i> (Old Church Slavonic <i>бьчела</i> (bĭčela), Russian <i>пчела́</i> (pčelá)), Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;bekos</i> (Old Irish <i>bech</i>, Irish <i>beach</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the pollen of flowers collected by bees and mixed with honey for the food of the larvae; beebread</li><li>honeycomb</li></ol><p>From <i>bēo</i> +&lrm; <i>brēad</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beekeeper</li></ol><p>Likely an alteration of earlier <i>bēocere</i> assimilated as though from <i>bēo</i> +&lrm; <i>ċeorl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beekeeper</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bijakārī</i> (“beekeeper”, literally “beehiver”), derived from <i>&#42;bijakaʀ</i> (“beehive”), equivalent to <i>bēo</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;cere</i> (“vessel-maker”). Cognate with dialectal Dutch <i>bijker</i> (“beekeeper”), Dutch Low Saxon <i>bijker</i> (“beekeeper”), French <i>bigre</i> ("woodsman"; via Old French <i>bigre</i> and Medieval Latin <i>bigrius</i>, <i>bigarus</i> (“forester, beekeeper”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>table</li><li>dish</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;beud</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to offer, give</li><li>to proclaim, announce</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;beudan</i>.</p><p>Germanic cognates include Old Frisian <i>biāda</i>, Old Saxon <i>biodan</i>, Middle Dutch <i>bieden</i> (Dutch <i>bieden</i>), Old High German <i>biotan</i> (German <i>bieten</i>), Old Norse <i>bjóða</i> (Danish <i>byde</i>, Swedish <i>bjuda</i> (“command, show”)), Gothic <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌱𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (anabiudan). The Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek <i>πυνθάνομαι</i> (punthánomai, “learn”), Sanskrit <i>बुध्यते</i> (búdhyate, “wake”), Old Church Slavonic <i>бъдети</i> (bŭdeti) (Russian <i>буди́ть</i> (budítʹ, “wake”)), Lithuanian <i>budėti</i> (“awake”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tablecloth</li></ol><p>From <i>bēod</i> (“table”) +&lrm; <i>clāþ</i> (“cloth”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dining room</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>bēod</i> +&lrm; <i>-ern</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>bēodeþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grace (prayer before a meal)</li></ol><p>From <i>bēod</i> (“table”) +&lrm; <i>fers</i> (“verse”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a table companion</li></ol><p>From <i>bēod</i> (“table”) +&lrm; <i>ġenēat</i> (“companion”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feast</li></ol><p>From <i>bēod</i> (“table, dish”) +&lrm; <i>ġereordu</i> (“meals, food”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tablecloth</li></ol><p>From <i>bēod</i> (“table”) +&lrm; <i>hræġl</i> (“garment”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tablecloth</li></ol><p>From <i>bēod</i> (“table”) +&lrm; <i>rēaf</i> (“vestment, garment”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>befer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bifian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>befer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shaking, trembling</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bibungu</i>. Equivalent to <i>bifian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>. The -eo- is from back umlaut, which did not affect <i>bifian</i> because its second syllable lacked a back vowel. The alternative form <i>bifung</i> has its -i- leveled in from <i>bifian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swarm of bees</li></ol><p>From <i>bēo</i> +&lrm; <i>gang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>agreeing, consenting</li><li>obedient, submissive</li></ol><p>From <i>bugan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Beoley (a village and civil parish in Bromsgrove district, Worcestershire, England)</li></ol><p>From <i>bēo</i> (“bee”) +&lrm; <i>lēah</i> (“clearing”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>henbane (plant)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>queen bee</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bijamōder</i>, equivalent to <i>bēo</i> +&lrm; <i>mōdor</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>bīmuoter</i> (“queen-bee”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be; exist</li><li>to become</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;beun</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;beuną</i> (“to be”), related to <i>būan</i> (“to dwell”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰew-</i>, <i>&#42;bʰuH-</i>. The past tense forms are from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wesaną</i> (from which also <i>wesan</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wes-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bentley, the name of several locations in England</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>&#42;beonot</i> +&lrm; <i>lēah</i> (“field”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;poetic</i>) beer, ale</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;beuʀ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;beuzą</i> (“beer”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰews-</i> (“dross, sediment, brewer's yeast”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grove</li></ol><p>Probably a metathetic alteration of <i>bearo</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a birch tree.</li><li>a poplar.</li><li>the runic character ᛒ (/b/)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;berku</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bark</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;berkan</i> (“to bark”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>birċen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mountain, hill</li><li>mound, heap of stones, barrow</li><li>high beach, elevated shore</li><li>shelter, protection, refuge</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;berg</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bergaz</i> (“mountain”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>berch</i>, Old Saxon <i>berg</i>, Old High German <i>berg</i> (German <i>Berg</i>), Old Norse <i>bjarg</i>, Gothic <i>𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌲-</i> (bairg-); and with Old Irish <i>brí</i> (“mountain”), Old Church Slavonic <i>брѣгъ</i> (brěgŭ) (Russian <i>бе́рег</i> (béreg)), Sanskrit <i>बृहत्</i> (bṛhát, “high”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bergu</i> from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bergō</i>. Equivalent to <i>&#42;bergan</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-u</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to save, deliver</li><li>to preserve, guard, fortify, defend, shelter</li><li>to spare</li><li>(<i>with reflexive</i>) to beware of, avoid, guard against</li><li>alternative form of <i>bierġan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bergan</i> (“to protect, preserve”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Beorgford</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Burford (a town and civil parish of West Oxfordshire district, Oxfordshire, England)</li></ol><p><i>beorg</i> (“mountain, hill”) +&lrm; <i>ford</i> (“ford”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beorg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Beorgford</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>burhlēod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bright, clear</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;berht</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;berhtaz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>berht</i>, Old High German <i>beraht</i>, Old Norse <i>bjartr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (bairhts).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>beorht</i> (“brightest”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) ‘bright-Danes’, the Danes</li></ol><p><i>beorht</i> (“bright”) +&lrm; <i>Dene</i> (“Danes”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>brightly</li></ol><p><i>beorht</i> (“bright”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i> (adverbial suffix)</p><p>See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Berhtafriþu</i>, from <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;friþu</i> (“peace”). Equivalent to <i>beorht</i> +&lrm; <i>friþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Bertfrid</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>beorht</i> (“bright”) +&lrm; <i>ġifu</i> (“gift”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Berhtagīsl</i>, from <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;gīsl</i> (“hostage”). Equivalent to <i>beorht</i> +&lrm; <i>ġīsl</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Berhtgis</i>, Latin <i>Bertegesillus</i> (from Frankish).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Berhtagunþi</i>, from <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;gunþi</i> (“battle”). Equivalent to <i>beorht</i> +&lrm; <i>gūþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Bertgund</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p><i>beorht</i> (“bright”) +&lrm; <i>hēah</i> (“high, tall”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Berhtahelm</i>, from <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;helm</i> (“helmet”). Equivalent to <i>beorht</i> +&lrm; <i>helm</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Berthelm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brighton (a coastal city in East Sussex, England)</li></ol><p><i>Beorhthelm</i> (a given name) +&lrm; <i>-es</i> (genitive) +&lrm; <i>tūn</i> (“enclosure, town”). Attested as <i>Bristelmestune</i> in the 1086 Domesday Book.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>beorht</i> +&lrm; <i>here</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glance</li><li>moment</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>beorht</i> +&lrm; <i>hwīl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine, brighten</li><li>to sound clearly or loudly</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>beorht</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beorhtian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>splendidly</li><li>(<i>of sight</i>) clearly</li></ol><p>From <i>beorht</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>breahtm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Berhtamundu</i>, from <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;mundu</i> (“protector”). Equivalent to <i>beorht</i> +&lrm; <i>mund</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Berhtmunt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Berhtamār</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Berhtamēraz</i>. Equivalent to <i>beorht</i> (“bright”) +&lrm; <i>mǣre</i> (“famous”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brightness</li></ol><p>From <i>beorht</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beorhtnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Berhtananþ</i>, from <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;nanþ</i> (“bold, daring”). Equivalent to <i>beorht</i> +&lrm; <i>nōþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Berhtnand</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beorhtnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>beorhte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>beorht</i> (“brightest”)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>beorhte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Berhtarīk</i>, from <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;rīk</i> (“king, ruler”). Equivalent to <i>beorht</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;rīċ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Berhtrīh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>beorht</i> (“bright, clear”) and <i>siġe</i> (“victory”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Berhtaswinþ</i>, from <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;swinþ</i> (“strong”). Equivalent to <i>beorht</i> +&lrm; <i>swiþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Bertswind</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Berhtawald</i>, from <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wald</i> (“might”). Equivalent to <i>beorht</i> +&lrm; <i>weald</i>. Cognate with German <i>Berthold</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Berhtaward</i>, from <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;ward</i> (“guard, guardian”). Equivalent to <i>beorht</i> +&lrm; <i>weard</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Bertwart</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Berhtawini</i>, from <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wini</i> (“friend”). Equivalent to <i>beorht</i> +&lrm; <i>wine</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Bertwin</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Berhtawulf</i>, from <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wulf</i> (“wolf”). Equivalent to <i>beorht</i> +&lrm; <i>wulf</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Berhtolf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>beorht</i> (“bright”) +&lrm; <i>wynn</i> (“joy”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Berhtaþrūþi</i>, from <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;þrūþi</i> (“strength”). Equivalent to <i>beorht</i> +&lrm; <i>þrȳþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Berhtrūt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>burhlēod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yeast, leaven</li><li>the foam or head of a beer, beerhead</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bermō</i> (“yeast, leaven”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bher-</i> (“to boil up”), from <i>&#42;bʰrewe-</i> (“to boil, bubble, burn”). Akin to West Frisian <i>berme</i> (“barm”), Middle Dutch <i>berm, barm</i> (“barm”); Low German <i>Bärm</i>, <i>Bärme</i>; Swedish <i>bärme</i>; Old English <i>brēowan</i> (“to brew”), <i>bēor</i> (“beer”). Cognate to Albanian <i>burmë</i> (“fully ripe”). More at brew, beer.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) man, warrior</li></ol><p>The origin is disputed. A phonetically exact correspondence exists in Old Norse <i>bjǫrn</i> (“bear”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bernuz</i> (more at <i>&#42;berô</i>), generally accepted as cognate with the form in proper names, but the English word is never used for "bear", and the Old Norse word is never used for "warrior". While it is not implausible that the term for a wild animal could become a poetic term for a warrior, Germanic scholars have been reluctant to accept any relationship between the two. Some consider it to be a variant of Old English <i>bearn</i> (“child, offspring, son", hence "boy, servant”), while others derive it from a Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;beron-</i> (“carrier”) (hence "servant; man, warrior", more at <i>baron</i>). Celtic origin has also been considered; thus, Rhys took this to be a Germanic reflex of the Celtic title <i>Brennus</i>, and Bradley connected British <i>Bernicia</i> (Welsh <i>brenhin</i>, <i>brenin</i>).</p><p>The word has the form <i>biorn</i> in early Old English attestations; it survives into early Middle English as <i>beorn</i> and takes the variant spellings <i>bern, berne, burn, burne, bearn, bieren, beern, beerne</i> in later Middle English. Middle English usage often interchanges it with baron. As <i>berne</i>, it survives into the 16th century in Scottish dialect, but becomes indistinguishable from bairn.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Beorn)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>beorn</i> ("warrior"). Cognate with Old Norse <i>Bjǫrn</i>, Danish <i>Bjørn</i> and Icelandic <i>Björn</i> which all mean "bear".</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Beornhere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>birnan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a "king of men"</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>beorn</i> (“man”) +&lrm; <i>cyning</i> (“king”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Bernuwulf</i>, from <i>&#42;bernu</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;wulf</i> (“wolf”). Equivalent to <i>beorn</i> +&lrm; <i>wulf</i>. Cognate with German <i>Bernfrid</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Bernuhardu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Bernuharduz</i>. Equivalent to <i>beorn</i> (“man, warrior”) +&lrm; <i>heard</i> (“harsh, hardy, brave”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name &lsqb;9th–10th C. <small>CE</small>&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Bernuheri</i>, from <i>&#42;bernu</i> (“man, warrior”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hari</i> (“army”). Equivalent to <i>beorn</i> +&lrm; <i>here</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Bernheri</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Bernicians (residents of the kingdom of Bernicia)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>beorn</i> (“man, warrior”) and <i>mōd</i> (“mind, heart, spirit”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>beorn</i> (“man, warrior”) and <i>mund</i> (“protector”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>beorn</i> (“warrior”) +&lrm; <i>rīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Beornred of Mercia</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>beorn</i> (“warrior”) +&lrm; <i>rǣd</i> (“council, advise”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>beorn</i> (“warrior”) +&lrm; <i>stān</i> (“stone”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Beornweald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>beorn</i> (“warrior”) +&lrm; <i>weald</i> (“power”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Bernuwīg</i>, equivalent to Old English <i>beorn</i> (“man, warrior”) and <i>wīġ</i> (“war, battle”), Cognate with Old High German <i>Bernwig</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>beorn</i> (“man, warrior”) and <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>beorn</i> (“man, warrior”) + <i>þrȳþ</i> (“strength, power”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who serves beer</li></ol><p>From <i>bēor</i> (“beer”) +&lrm; <i>sċealc</i> (“servant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a feast</li></ol><p>From <i>bēor</i> (“beer”) +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beerhall</li></ol><p>From <i>bēor</i> (“beer”) +&lrm; <i>sele</i> (“hall”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>bjórsalr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a beer-bench</li></ol><p>From <i>bēor</i> (“beer”) +&lrm; <i>setl</i> (“seat”); compare <i>medusetl</i> (“mead-bench”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) beer-drinking</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>midwife</li></ol><p>From <i>beorþor</i> (“childbirth”) +&lrm; <i>þīnen</i> (“female assistant, handmaid”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beestings (first milk given by a cow after giving birth)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;beust</i> (“beestings”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>basu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>promise, vow, boast</li><li>threat, danger</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>bihāt</i>, second element cognate with Old Norse <i>heit</i> with very similar semantics.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>bēoton</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to threaten</li><li>to promise, vow, boast</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barley</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;beuw</i> (“crops; barley”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Beow)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Beow, an Anglo-Saxon god associated with barley and agriculture, equivalent to Old Norse Byggvir</li></ol><p>Derived from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;beuw</i> (“crops; barley”), whence the word for barley, but likely transferred to the masculine.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) Beowulf</li></ol><p>Uncertain; traditionally derived from <i>bēo</i> (“bee”) +&lrm; <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”), used as a kenning for “bear” or “woodpecker”. Most recently analysed theophorically from <i>Bēow</i> (“Beow”) +&lrm; <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”). Probably cognate with Old Norse <i>Bjólfr</i> (from earlier <i>&#42;Bíolfr</i>, <i>&#42;Béolfr</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweet flag, <i>Acorus calamus</i></li></ol><p>From <i>bēo</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i>. Calque of Latin <i>apiastrum</i>, <i>apiāgō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>bēoþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wink</li></ol><p><i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;prīwan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deceive</li><li>to entice, seduce</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>pǣċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deceiver</li><li>seducer</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>pǣċan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a woman who deceives, entices, or flatters; seductress</li><li>harlot</li></ol><p>From <i>bepǣċan</i> +&lrm; <i>-estre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deceit</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>pǣċan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bier; a litter to transport dead people</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bāru</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bērō</i>, whence also Old High German <i>bāra</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bear</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;berō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;berô</i> (“bear”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>&#42;bera</i>, Old Saxon <i>bero</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;bero</i>, Old High German <i>bero</i>. The oblique stem <i>&#42;bern-</i> gave Old Norse <i>bjǫrn</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Bera)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry, bear, hold</li><li>to wear</li><li>to sustain, support</li><li>to bring forth, produce, give birth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;beran</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;beraną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰéreti</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bera</i>, Old Saxon <i>beran</i>, Old Dutch <i>beran</i>, Old High German <i>beran</i>, Old Norse <i>bera</i>, Gothic <i>𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (bairan). The Indo-European root was also the source of Old Irish <i>biru</i>, Ancient Greek <i>φέρω</i> (phérō), Latin <i>ferre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beorc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beorcan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Beorhtġīsl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barley</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;barī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;baraz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>barr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deprive (+ accusative of person deprived) (+ genitive of thing deprived of)</li></ol><p>Derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;biraubōną</i>. Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>rēafian</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>birōvōn</i>, Old High German <i>biroubon</i>, Gothic <i>𐌱𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌿𐌱𐍉𐌽</i> (biraubōn).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>berēafienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) barley</li><li>alternative form of <i>biren</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;barīnaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>bere</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;birīnaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrier, bearer (often used in compounds)</li></ol><p>From <i>beran</i> (“to bear”) +&lrm; <i>-end</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>berindan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bereave, deprive</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>rēofan</i>, compare <i>berēafian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deplore, bewail</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>rēotan</i> (“bewail”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bereærn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a village associated with or supplying barley or grain</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>bere</i> (“barley”) +&lrm; <i>wīc</i> (“village, abode”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a storage place for barley or corn (grains); barn, granary</li></ol><p>From <i>bere</i> (“barley”) +&lrm; <i>ærn</i> (“place”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bearg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>berry</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;baʀi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beorht</i></li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Berht)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>berht</i> (“bright, shiny”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Beorhtfriþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, variant of Beorhtġīsl</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Beorhtġīsl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Beorhthelm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>berht</i> (“bright”) +&lrm; <i>hūn</i> (“bear cub”). Compare <i>Hunberht</i> which has the same elements but reversed.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Beorhtmund</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Beorhtnōþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Beorhtrīc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Berhtarād</i>, from <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;rād</i> (“advice, counsel”). Equivalent to <i>beorht</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣd</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Berhtrāt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, alternative form of <i>Beorhtweald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Beorhtwulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bierġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ride around, surround, beset, besiege, invest</li><li>to ride after, pursue, arrest</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>rīdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>berġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>berġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to peel or strip the bark from</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;birindijan</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> (“off, away”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;rindan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>birinten</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land used for growing barley</li></ol><p>From <i>bere</i> (“barley”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bearn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, alternative form of <i>Beornhere</i> &lsqb;10th C. <small>CE</small>&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Beornhere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break, shatter, burst</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brestan</i> (with e-r metathesis in West Germanic), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brestaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰres-</i> (“to burst, break, crack, split, separate”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bersta</i> (West Frisian <i>boarste</i>), Old Saxon <i>brestan</i> (Low German <i>basten</i>), Dutch <i>bersten</i>, <i>barsten</i>, Old High German <i>brestan</i> German <i>bersten</i>), Old Norse <i>bresta</i> (Danish <i>briste</i>, Norwegian Bokmål <i>briste</i>, Swedish <i>brista</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>bersteþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bearbine</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deprive of power, betray, get the better of</li><li>to consult about; give rede to</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣdan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>birēda</i>, Middle Low German <i>birāden</i>, Old High German <i>birātan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sow, sow about</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>sāwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>genitive</i>) to separate or part from</li><li>to sprinkle</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċeādan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overshadow</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;sċeaduwian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look on, consider, regard, watch</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċēawian</i>. Compare Dutch <i>beschouwen</i> (“to contemplate, consider, regard”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>besċēawienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>considerate, thoughtful, circumspect</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>besċieran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shear, cut, or shave off, cut or hair</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;biskeran</i>; equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċieran</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>biskeran</i>, Old Frisian <i>biskera</i>, Old High German <i>biskeran</i> (whence German <i>bescheren</i> (“to cut, trim”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine upon, illuminate; alight</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;biskīnaną</i> (“to shine upon”). Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċīnan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>biskīna</i>, Old High German <i>biskīnan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌱𐌹𐍃𐌺𐌴𐌹𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (biskeinan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>besċieran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smear or shit all over, befoul, befilth</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċītan</i>. Compare with Old High German <i>biskīzan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shove, throw, project, thrust</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċūfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shield, defend oneself</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċyldian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lay fault or blame upon (someone); accuse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;biskuldigōn</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;sċyldigian</i> (“to blame”), related to Old English <i>sċyldigian</i> (“to sin, do wrong”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>biskeldigia</i> (“to blame”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>besċieran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to announce</li><li>to defend; shield</li><li>to deny a charge; say as not true; gainsay; forsay</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>seċġan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>biseggian</i>, Old Frisian <i>bisega</i>, Old High German <i>bisagēn</i> (German <i>besagen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surround; to bring on</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>sellan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>besma</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>sendan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to singe, scorch, burn</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see or look about or around</li><li>to see, behold</li><li>to go to see, visit</li><li>to see to, care for, attend to, provide for</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bisehwaną</i> (“to look, besee”), equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēon</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>bisehan</i>, Old High German <i>&#42;bisehan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌱𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌹𐍈𐌰𐌽</i> (bisaiƕan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set near, surround, beset; set about</li><li>to put one thing in another</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bisattjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bisatjaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>settan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entrap, deceive, ensnare</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>sierwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sink, submerge</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>sincan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>bisinkan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing about, charm, enchant, bewail</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bisingwan</i>. Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>singan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit near; sit about, surround, beset</li><li>to besiege</li><li>to sit as the master of, hold, possess</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bisitjaną</i> (“to sit near, sit among or around”), equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>sittan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat, strike or cut off, take away, bereave</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>literal</i>) to strike, smite.</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to strike with disease, place with violence, dash, inflict.</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to strike, give a blow, force a passage</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>slēan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bislā</i>, Old High German <i>bislahan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sleep, sleep through or over</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>slǣpan</i>. Compare Old Frisian <i>bislēpa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>broom, besom</li><li>rod</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;besmō</i> (“branch, rod, broom”); the forms <i>bisme</i>, <i>bysm</i> may come from a feminine byform <i>&#42;bismijā</i>, <i>&#42;bismiju</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consider about</li><li>to examine into</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>smēaġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to besmear, to rub with ointment or salve</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>smierwan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>bismirwen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>besmierwan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to besmut, defile, pollute, dirty</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>smītan</i> (“smear, pollute”). Compare German <i>beschmutzen</i>, <i>beschmeißen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pollute, defile</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>smittian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>covered with snow, snowy</li></ol><p>The past participle of <i>&#42;besnīwian</i> (“to snow on, besnow, cover with snow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deprive</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;snyþian</i>. Related to Old Norse <i>sneyða</i> (“to deprive”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be sorry for, care for; worry over, be anxious for, fear</li><li>to be troubled over</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bisorgōn</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>sorgian</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bisorgia</i>, Old Saxon <i>bisorgōn</i>, Old High German <i>bisorgēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entice, allure</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>spanan</i> (“to entice”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak about; bespeak, mention</li><li>to speak to, tell, plead, speak against</li><li>to complain, charge, accuse; beguilt, speak ill of</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bisprekaną</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>sprecan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>bisprekan</i>, Old High German <i>bisprehhan</i>, Old Frisian <i>bispreka</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand by or near, stand on or upon, stand about or around</li><li>to surround; beset, occupy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bistandaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>standan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to proceed stealthily; stalk</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;stealcian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal away or upon</li><li>to go stealthily, secretly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bistelaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>stelan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bestīeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tread or step upon, go, enter</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>steppan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make wet, soak, bedew</li></ol><p><i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>stīeman</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thrust in, push</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>stingan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bestirian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bestirienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bestyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bestirian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bestirienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bestrew; strew all over, besprinkle</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>strēawian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bestrēawian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a stroke about or around</li><li>to besmear</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>strīcan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bestride</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>strīdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strip</li><li>to rob</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bestīeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bestyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bestyrienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heap or pile up</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>styrian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bestyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bestyrienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sup up, swallow</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>sūpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to foul, make dirty</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind up, swathe</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to deceive, trick, beguile, mislead</li><li>to cheat</li><li>to ensnare</li><li>to betray</li><li>to circumvent</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;biswīkwan</i>. Equivalent to <i>bi-</i> +&lrm; <i>swican</i>. Cognate with Old Dutch <i>biswīcan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a trap</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>beswican</i> (“to deceive, trick”) +&lrm; <i>fealle</i> (“trap”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to labour for or at, swink, toil, make with toil</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>swincan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>besierwan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>better</li><li>rather</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;batiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to improve</li><li>to fix, repair</li><li>to atone or compensate for</li><li>to make or attend to a fire or a lamp</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bōtijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bōtijaną</i>, derived from <i>&#42;bōtō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>betest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover</li><li>to surround</li><li>to overwhelm</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>teld</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak or tell about</li><li>to answer, justify, clear, unguilt, show one's self as innocent</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>tellan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw over; pull over, cover; bethatch, inclose, protect, surround</li><li>to bestow, assign</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>tēon</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>biziohan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌱𐌹𐍄𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (bitiuhan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>gōd</i>: better</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;batiʀō</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>betera</i> (West Frisian <i>better</i>), Old Saxon <i>betiro</i> (Low German <i>beter</i>), Low German <i>beter</i>, Old High German <i>bezziro</i> (German <i>besser</i>), Old Norse <i>betri</i> (Danish <i>bedre</i>, Swedish <i>bättre</i>), Gothic <i>𐌱𐌰𐍄𐌹𐌶𐌰</i> (batiza).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to improve, to make better</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;batizōną</i>, akin to Old Frisian <i>beteria</i>, Old Saxon <i>betirōn</i>, <i>betorōn</i>, Old High German <i>bezzirōn</i>, Old Norse <i>betra</i>, German <i>bessern</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>gōd</i>: the best</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;batistaz</i>, superlative of <i>&#42;bataz</i>. Compare Old Frisian <i>betsta</i>, Old Saxon <i>betst</i>, <i>best</i>, Old High German <i>bezzisto</i>, Old Norse <i>beztr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌱𐌰𐍄𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (batists).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bethlehem (a city in Palestine)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Bēthleëm</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Βηθλεέμ</i> (Bēthleém), from Hebrew <i>בֵּית לֶחֶם</i> (bet léchem)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build, construct (with wood)</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>timbran</i> (“to build”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bethlehem (a historical city in Palestine)</li></ol><p>Latin <i>Bethlehemum</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Βηθλεέμ</i> (Bēthleém), from Hebrew <i>בֵּית לֶחֶם</i> (bēṯ léḥem)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>betest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tread upon or over, cover</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bitredan</i>. Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>tredan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>betræppan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beterian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to betrap, entrap, snare</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bitrappjan</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>træppan</i> (“to trap”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>betest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>between, among</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>between, among, inter-</li></ol><p>From <i>betwēonum</i> (“between, near to both”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>betwēonum</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>betwēonum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>between</li></ol><p>Originally a phrase made up of <i>be</i> (“by”) + <i>twēonum</i> (“the two”, dative plural noun; see <i>twegen</i>). Very rarely, these elements were still used as separate words: <i>be sǣm twēonum</i> ("between two seas," <i>Beowulf</i> 858).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>between</li></ol><p><i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>tweox</i> (“twofold”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twiskaz</i> (“twofold, double”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>inter-; between, among</li></ol><p>From <i>betweox</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interjection</li></ol><p>Calque of Latin <i>interiectiō</i>. Equivalent to <i>betweox-</i> (“between”) +&lrm; <i>āworpen</i> (“thrown out”) + <i>-nes</i> (“-ness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>betweoh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>betweoh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>betweox</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>betweox-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>between</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>betweox</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lure, draw away</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shut, shut up, close, enclose, hedge in</li><li>to end, finish, conclude</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bitūnijan</i>; cognate with Old High German <i>bizūnen</i>. By surface analysis, <i>be</i> +&lrm; <i>tȳnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn around</li><li>to prostrate oneself</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>tyrnan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show, point out, assign, destine</li><li>to send, follow, pursue, hunt</li><li>to entrust, betake, commit, impart, deliver, hand over, yield to, give, betroth</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>tǣċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>befer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep watch over</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>wacian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bewealwian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep, guard, preserve, protect</li><li>to guard one's self against</li><li>to ward off from</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;biwarōn</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>warian</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>biwaria</i>, Old High German <i>biwarōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow against</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>wāwan</i> (“blow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll around</li><li>to wallow around</li></ol><p><i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>wealwian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ward, keep, protect; shelter</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>weardian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wed, betroth, pledge, plight, give security for</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>weddian</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>biweddia</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>betrothal, wedding</li></ol><p>From By surface analysis, <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>weddung</i> or <i>beweddian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn around <i>(usually with an accusative reflexive pronoun)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;biwandijaną</i> (“to turn around”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>biwendian</i>, Old High German <i>biwenten</i>, Gothic <i>𐌱𐌹𐍅𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (biwandjan). Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>wendan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to throw away, cast down (+ <i>on</i>, into)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to throw about or over; to cover or surround (+ <i>mid</i>, with)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>weorpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weep, weep over or about, bewail, beweep</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;biwōpijan</i>, analyzable as <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>wēpan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>biwōpian</i>, Old Frisian <i>biwēpa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bewerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bewerienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defend</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;biwarjan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>biwerien</i>. Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>werian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bewerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bewerienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defense</li></ol><p><i>bewerian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bewering</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to encamp</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>wīcian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>niece</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>wīfmann</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wind around or about, enwrap, wrap, entwine, encircle, surround</li><li>to wrap an object in or with something</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;biwindaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>windan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hedge, border</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;windla</i>, from <i>windan</i> (“to wind”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overlook; oversee, watch over; bewatch, superintend, preside, govern</li><li>(<i>of people, institutions, affairs, etc.</i>) to take charge of, watch over</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>witan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bewitan</i>; to observe, watch over, take care of</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see, look, behold</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>wlātian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look, behold, spectate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>wlītan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exile, send away</li><li>to strike or beat around; to afflict</li><li>to drive or bring to</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrecan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover, protect</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;biwrīhan</i>. By surface analysis, <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrēon</i> (“to cover”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write down, inscribe; inwrite</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;biwrītan</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrītan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>indeclinable</i>) wont, accustomed</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beweorpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work, inset, insert, build round, surround, cover over; bethatch, adorn; bedeck</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beweorpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to befold, wrap around</li><li>to clothe, cover</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to offer</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disarm</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> (“off, away”) +&lrm; <i>wǣpnian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass by</li><li>to avoid</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>wærlan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bewēpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>betest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>behind</li><li>after</li><li>hereafter</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to join in marriage, to marry</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>ǣwnian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;biþakkjan</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>þeċċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think about, consider; bethink, bear in mind</li><li>to trust, entrust, confide</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;biþankijaną</i>, equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>þenċan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>bithenkian</i>, Old Frisian <i>bithanka</i>, Old High German <i>bidenken</i> (German <i>bedenken</i>), Gothic <i>𐌱𐌹𐌸𐌰𐌲𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (biþagkjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beþīedan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to heat, warm, foment</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wash, bathe</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cherish</li></ol><p>From the fusion of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;baþþjan</i> and its variant <i>&#42;baþigōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;baþjaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₁-</i> (“to warm”). More at bath.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to join, attach</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>þīedan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beþian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twist</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>þrāwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throng or press around, surround, encompass; beset, burden, oppress</li></ol><p>From <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>þringan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to need, have need of [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>to want, desire [<i>with</i> genitive]</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>þurfan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beþian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>about (concerning)</li><li>by, in various senses:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>near or next to</li><li>not later than</li><li>based on, according to</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bī</i> (“near, by, around, about”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>how</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>near, around (compare Latin <i>para-</i>)</li><li>alternative form of <i>be-</i></li></ol><p>From <i>bī</i> (“by, near, around”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bebaþian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>library</li><li>a collection of books in one volume; the Bible</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>βῐβλῐοθήκη</i> (bĭblĭothḗkē).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēobrēad</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bitch, a female dog</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bikkjā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bikjǭ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>byċġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bīecnan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēacnian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indicating, indicative</li></ol><p>From either <i>bīċnan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ol</i> or <i>bīċnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēacnung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>becweþan</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>bi-</i> +&lrm; <i>cweþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proverb</li></ol><p>From <i>bī-</i> +&lrm; <i>cwide</i> (“saying”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wait [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘for’</small>]</li><li>to stay, to remain</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bīdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask <i>(+ accusative someone, + genitive for something)</i></li><li>to beg <i>(+ accusative someone, + genitive for something)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;biddjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bidjaną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bidda</i>, Old Saxon <i>biddian</i>, Old Dutch <i>bidden</i>, Old High German <i>bitten</i>, Old Norse <i>biðja</i>, Gothic <i>𐌱𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (bidjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bedrēosan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>bedǣled</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>bedǣled</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beckon, nod</li><li>to show, indicate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bauknijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Beda</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend or curve</li><li>to turn (toward or away from something)</li><li>to humiliate, subdue</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) to inflect</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;baugijan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>beia</i>, Old Saxon <i>bōgian</i>, Old Dutch <i>bōgen</i>, Old High German <i>bougen</i>, Old Norse <i>beygja</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arch</li></ol><p><i>bīeġan</i> +&lrm; <i>-els</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>token, sign, proof</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;baųkiþu</i>, a metathesis of <i>&#42;baukniþu</i>; equivalent to Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;baukn</i> (“beacon”) +&lrm; <i>-þ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burp, throw up</li><li>to utter, emit</li><li>to scream, cry out</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;balkijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;belkaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make bold, encourage, cheer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;balþijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;balþijaną</i> (“to make bold”), from <i>&#42;balþaz</i> (“bold”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bieldu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boldness, confidence</li><li>impudence, presumption</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;balþį̄</i> (“boldness”), equivalent to <i>beald</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bag</li><li>bellows</li><li>belly</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;balgi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) a wooden instrument, especially a horn or trumpet</li><li>tablet, billet</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;baumijā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trumpeter</li></ol><p><i>bīeme</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play a trumpet</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>bīeme</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birch</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;birkijā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to taste</li><li>to partake of</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bargijan</i> (“to try, taste”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>bergja</i> (“to taste”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bierhtu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brightness, splendor</li></ol><p>From <i>beorht</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to burn</li></ol><p>Metathesis of earlier &#42;brinnan, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brinnan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brinnaną</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>brinnan</i>, Old Dutch <i>brinnan</i>, Old High German <i>brinnan</i>, Old Norse <i>brinna</i>, Gothic <i>𐌱𐍂𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (brinnan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hammer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bautil</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tremble, shake</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bibēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bifian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beforan</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beforan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beofung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bīeġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circular movement or path: circuit, orbit</li><li>path or passage of any kind: course, way</li><li>worship</li><li>undertaking; practice, exercise</li><li>business</li><li>service</li><li>cultivation, tillage</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bigangaz</i> (“circuit, course”). Equivalent to <i>bī-</i> +&lrm; <i>gang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>byġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>biġeall</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beġēat</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>beġeat</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bīeġels</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bīġenġa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>practice, exercise, doing</li><li>cultivation</li><li>practice of a religion, religious observance</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inhabitant</li><li>cultivator</li><li>one who takes care of others; benefactor</li><li>observer; worshipper</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bīgangijō</i>, equivalent to <i>bī-</i> +&lrm; <i>genġa</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>bigengo</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>practice</li><li>worship</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bīġenġa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beġeondan</i></li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beġeondan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bīġenġa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beġiellan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bīleofa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bīspell</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>behealdan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beheonan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>behongen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a kind of sword or other bladed weapon, apparently having two edges</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;billi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bilją</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>bil</i>, Old High German <i>bill</i> (German <i>Beil</i> (“pickaxe”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bill of a bird</li><li>trunk of an elephant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bili-</i>, related to <i>&#42;bilją</i> (“axe, blade, cutting weapon”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>kind, appropriate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bili-</i> (prefix), <i>&#42;biliz</i> (“gentle, kind, fitting”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰil-</i> (“decent, good, harmonious, friendly”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>bili-</i> (“uniform”), Old High German <i>bili-</i> (“suitable, proper, fit”), Ancient Greek <i>φίλο-</i> (phílo-, “friendly, kind, love of”). More at philosophy.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pure, sincere, honest</li></ol><p>From <i>bile-</i> (“gentle”) +&lrm; <i>hwīt</i> (“white”). Compare Middle High German <i>bilwīz</i> (“goblin”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food, nourishment, sustenance</li><li>subsistence, maintenance, provision</li><li>something used to acquire food; money, wages</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>innocent, simple</li><li>calm, gentle</li><li>merciful, gracious</li><li>plausible</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;biliwit-</i> (“gentle-minded”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;biliz</i> (“merciful, kind”) (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰil-</i> (“decent, good”)) + Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;witją</i> (“mind, wit”) (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyd-</i> (“to know”)), equivalent to Old English <i>bile-</i> + <i>witt</i>. Related to Middle High German <i>bilwiz</i> (“genius, mischievous person, goblin, witch”), <i>unbil</i> (“unbecoming, improper”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>bilewit</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>bil</i> (“sword”) + <i>friþ</i> (“peace”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an image, likeness</li></ol><p>This is a ghost word resulting from a misinterpretation of the spelling <i>biliðe</i> in the Old English Daniel poem as it appears in the Junius Manuscript, folio 187; the ultimate origin of which appears to be Edward Lye's <i>Dictionarium Saxonico et Gothico-Latinum</i>. However, the spelling is most likely actually a corruption of blīþe (“happy”), as part of the phrase "Blīþe wǣron eorlas Ebrēa" ("Happy were the Hebrew men"). The extra vowel may have been from association with bilewit, although there are no other such examples for this root. Despite its prevalence in the continental West Germanic languages, it would appear that neither Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;biliþī</i> nor any of its derivatives survived into Old English.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bil</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>bil</i> (“sword”) +&lrm; <i>stān</i> (“stone”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>bil</i> (“sword”) +&lrm; <i>þeġn</i> (“warrior, servant”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bemǣnan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pronoun</li></ol><p>From <i>bi-</i> +&lrm; <i>nama</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind, constrain</li><li>to fasten or join</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bindan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bindaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰendʰ-</i> (“to bind”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>bindaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>band, wreath, headband, fillet</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bindā</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;bindu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bindō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>bindeþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>beneath</li></ol><p><i>bi-</i> +&lrm; <i>neoþan</i>; compare <i>niþer</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stall</li><li>manger, crib, basket</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>benna</i>, its Celtic source, or a cognate in another Celtic language, possibly Proto-Brythonic <i>&#42;benn</i> (“cart, carriage”) (whence Middle Welsh <i>benn</i>, modern Welsh <i>ben</i>), ultimately from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;bend(n)ā</i> (whence Gaulish <i>benna</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>within</li><li>in, into</li></ol><p><i>bi-</i> +&lrm; <i>innan</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>binnen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>binnan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēo</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēo</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēobrēad</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēom</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beorg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beorn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēorsele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Bēowulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>biþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bierċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>birchen; made of birch</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;birkīnaz</i> (“made of birch”), equivalent to <i>bierċe</i> (“birch”) +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female bear</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bear; of or relating to bears</li><li>made of a bear's fur or pelt: bearskin</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;birini</i>. Equivalent to <i>bera</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>birin</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;birīnaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>birīn</i>. Equivalent to <i>bera</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>byrġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>byrġels</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bierhtu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bierhtu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, alternative form of <i>Beorhtweald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bierġan</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>byrġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Berhte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>biernan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bisċop</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bisċopdōm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bisċoprīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bisċopsetl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bishop's see or diocese</li><li>a bishop's palace</li><li>episcopal authority</li></ol><p>From <i>bisċeop</i> (“bishop”) +&lrm; <i>stōl</i> (“seat”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bisċopwyrt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bisċop</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bisċopdōm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bisċopsetl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bishop</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>episcopus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἐπίσκοπος</i> (epískopos, “overseer”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bishop's province, a bishopric</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;biskopadōm</i>, equivalent to <i>bisċop</i> +&lrm; <i>-dōm</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>biscoftuom</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bishophood, a bishop's office or state, a bishopric, episcopate</li></ol><p>From <i>bisċop</i> +&lrm; <i>-hād</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bishopric; a bishop's province, diocese</li></ol><p>From <i>bisċop</i> +&lrm; <i>-rīċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bishopric</li><li>bishop's residence or seat</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>bisċop</i> (“bishop”) +&lrm; <i>setl</i> (“seat”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bishop's-wort</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>bisċop</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scribal abbreviation of <i>bisċop</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blind</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bȳsnian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to occupy, busy (keep oneself occupied)</li><li>to harass, bother</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bisīgōn</i>, equivalent to <i>bisiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>bisgu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bisiġu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>business, occupation</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>bisgian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>busy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bisīg</i> (“diligent, zealous, busy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solicitude; care, anxiety, occupation</li></ol><p>From <i>bisiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>business, occupation, activity</li><li>care, trouble, affliction, toil</li></ol><p>From <i>bisiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mockery</li><li>blasphemy</li><li>reproach</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smarōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bismerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bismerienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to revile, blaspheme, insult</li><li>to mock, deride, reproach</li><li>to blaspheme, defame, revile</li><li>to irritate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bismarjan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>bismerien</i> (“to mock”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bismerōn</i>, variant of <i>&#42;bismerēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bismerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bismerienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>irreverently</li><li>blasphemously</li></ol><p>From <i>bismer</i> (“blasphemy”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bismer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bisenian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>bīspell</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>book of parables</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Proverbs</li></ol><p><i>bīspell</i> +&lrm; <i>bōc</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>example, pattern; proverb</li><li>parable, story, fable, allegory</li></ol><p>From <i>bī-</i> (“by”) +&lrm; <i>spell</i> (“story, news”). More at <i>bī-</i>, <i>spell</i>. Compare Old Armenian <i>առասպել</i> (aṙaspel).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bīspelbōc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>visit, going to see</li><li>alternative form of <i>bīsæcc</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bīsæċċ</i></li></ol><p>Variant of <i>bīsēċ</i>, <i>bīsœ̄ċ</i>; cognate with German <i>Besuch</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wallet</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>contested, disputed</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>bisaccium</i>.</p><p><i>bī-</i> +&lrm; <i>sæċċ</i> (“dispute”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bite (of food), mouthful</li><li>biter, one who bites</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bitô</i>. See the verb <i>&#42;bītaną</i> (“to bite”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bita</i>, Old High German <i>bizzo</i> (German <i>Bisse</i>), Old Norse <i>biti</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bite</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bītan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bītaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>bitaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bite</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;biti</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bitter (taste etc.)</li><li>bitter, severe, painful, biting</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bitr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>biter</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>bitterly</li></ol><p>From <i>biter</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the quality or state of being bitter; bitterness</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) grief</li></ol><p>From <i>biter</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>biter</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>biter</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>bitterly, cruelly</li></ol><p><i>biter</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>biter</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>biterlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>biter</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bewāwan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an adverb</li><li>a proverb</li><li>a familiar or household term</li></ol><p>From <i>bī-</i> +&lrm; <i>word</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>bewrāh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bewrecan</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>bi-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrecan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proverb</li></ol><p>From <i>bī-</i> +&lrm; <i>-wyrde</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bewærlan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>biþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beþeċċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beþenċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pale</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blaik</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blaikaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>blācaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place for light</li><li>lamp</li><li>candlestick, candle</li><li>light</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>blāc</i> +&lrm; <i>-ern</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become pale</li></ol><p>From <i>blāc</i> (“pale, shining, white”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>, alternatively spelt blǣċ, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blaikaz</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>bleikr</i> (“pale”), Faroese <i>bleikur</i> (“pale”), Icelandic <i>bleikur</i> (“pale”), Danish <i>bleg</i> (“pale”), Norwegian Bokmål <i>blek</i> (“pale”) (also <i>bleik</i>), Norwegian Nynorsk <i>bleik</i> (“pale”), Swedish <i>blek</i> (“pale”), Dutch <i>bleek</i> (“pale”), German <i>bleich</i> (“pale”), German Low German <i>bleek</i> (“pale”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blācienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>white</li><li>greyish-white, pale, pallid</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blankaz</i> (“bright", "shining", "blinding", "white”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bhleg-</i> (“to shine”). Akin to Old High German <i>blanch</i>, <i>planch</i> (“bright", "white”), hence German <i>blank</i> (“blank", "white”), Old Norse <i>blankr</i> (“white”), hence Danish <i>blank</i> (“shiny”), Swedish <i>blank</i> (“shiny”), Dutch <i>blank</i> (“white", "shining”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>white (or grey) horse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blankō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blankaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰleyǵ-</i> (“to shine, white”). Compare Old High German <i>blancros</i> (“white horse”), Old French <i>blanke</i> (“sheep”), Old Norse <i>blakkr</i> (“horse, steed”), Old Norse <i>Blanka</i> (“the mythical horse of Thideric (Dietrich) of Bern”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blend</li><li>to mix, mingle</li><li>to corrupt, make impure</li><li>to harass, disturb, trouble</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blandan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blandaną</i> (“to mix, confound, blind”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰlendʰ-</i> (“to become turbid, see badly, be blind”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>blenda</i> (“to mix”), Old Saxon <i>blandan</i> (“to mix, cloud”), Old High German <i>blantan</i> (“to mix, becloud”), Old Norse <i>blanda</i> (“to mix”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blondenfeax</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torch</li><li>blaze</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blasā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pale, livid, ghastly</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blait</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blaitaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>bleizza</i> (“stain, blue, livor”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be pale or livid</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blaitōn</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;blaitijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>blāt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blue</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blāu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blēwaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow; breathe</li><li>to be blown</li><li>to sound</li><li>to inflate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blāan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blēaną</i> (“to blow”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰleh₁-</i> (“to swell, blow up”). Akin to Old High German <i>blāen</i>, Latin <i>flō</i> (“blow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blower</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>blāwan</i> (“to blow”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a blowing</li><li>inflation</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>blāwan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wretched, miserable</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blaut</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blautaz</i>, whence also Old High German <i>blōz</i> (“naked”), Old Norse <i>blautr</i>. More at <i>blouse</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gentle; shy; cowardly; timid</li><li>slothful; inactive</li><li>effeminate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blauþaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a shoot, branch</li><li>foliage, leaves; a leaf</li><li>a flower, blossom; a bloom</li><li>fruit; a fruit</li><li>a harvest, crop; yield, produce</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blēduz</i>, <i>&#42;blōdiz</i> (“blossom, sprout”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰleh₃-</i>. Related to <i>blōwan</i> (“to bloom, blossom”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to bleed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blōdijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blōþijaną</i> (“to bleed”). Compare Old Frisian <i>blēda</i>, Old High German <i>bluoten</i>, Old Norse <i>blœða</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shoot growing quickly</li></ol><p><i>blēd</i> (“a shoot”) +&lrm; <i>hwæt</i> (“quick”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blǣdre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blētsian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blētsung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deceive</li><li>to cheat</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blankijaną</i> (“to deceive”), whence also Old Norse <i>blekkja</i> (“to deceive; to impose upon”) (Icelandic <i>blekkja</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make blind <i>(+ accusative of that which is blinded)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blandijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colour</li><li>complexion</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blue</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blīu</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blāu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blēwaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blēo</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>purple; blue-red</li></ol><p>From <i>blēo</i> (“blue”) +&lrm; <i>rēad</i> (“red”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blēorēad</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bald</li><li>alternative form of <i>bleriġ</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blaʀī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bleriġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bald</li></ol><p>From <i>blere</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bless, to consecrate</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>blēdsian</i>, <i>blœ̄dsian</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blōdisōn</i> (“to sprinkle, mark or hallow with blood”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blōþą</i> (“blood”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰleh₃-</i> (“to thrive, flourish, bloom”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>bletza</i> (“to bless”), whence Icelandic <i>blessa</i>. Equivalent to <i>blōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-sian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blētsian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blessing</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blōdisungu</i>, equivalent to <i>blētsian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine, glitter, dazzle, sparkle, twinkle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blīkan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blīkaną</i> (“to gleam, shine”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blīss</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blind</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a blind person</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blind</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>blindly</li></ol><p>From <i>blind</i> (“blind”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of persons or sight</i>) blindness, inability to see because of darkness</li><li>(of persons or a condition,&#32;figuratively) blindness</li></ol><p>From <i>blind</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blindnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>blind</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>blind</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cease, leave off, rest from</li><li>to lose, forfeit</li><li>(<i>of a bishopric</i>) to be vacant</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bilinnan</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ley-</i>, <i>&#42;leya-</i> (“to deflect, turn away, vanish, slip”), equivalent to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>linnan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>bilinnan</i> (“to yield, stop, forlet, give away”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blysa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joy, bliss</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>blīds</i>, <i>blīþs</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blīþisi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rejoice; be glad</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to gladden; make happy; cause (someone) to rejoice; delight</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blīþisōn</i> (“to be good, be kind, be joyful”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blissian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blissian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blīþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>happy</li><li>gentle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blīþī</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>blīthe</i>, <i>blīde</i>, Old Saxon <i>blīdi</i>, Old Dutch <i>blīthi</i> (<i>in placenames</i>), Old High German <i>blīdi</i>, Old Norse <i>blíðr</i> (Danish <i>blid</i>, Faroese <i>blíður</i>, Icelandic <i>blíður</i>), Gothic <i>𐌱𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (bleiþs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>happily</li></ol><p>From <i>blīþe</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>blīþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blood</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blōd</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blōþą</i>, of unknown origin.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian and Old Saxon <i>blōd</i>, Old Dutch <i>bluot</i>, Old High German <i>bluot</i>, Old Norse <i>blóð</i>, and Gothic <i>𐌱𐌻𐍉𐌸</i> (blōþ).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blōdiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blōdġian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bloodstained</li></ol><p><i>blōd</i> (“blood”) +&lrm; <i>fāh</i> (“colored, decorated”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make or become bloody</li><li>to be bloodthirsty</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blōdagōn</i>, equivalent to <i>blōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-gian</i> or possibly <i>blōdiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>bluotagōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bloodshed</li></ol><p><i>blōd</i> (“blood”) +&lrm; <i>gyte</i> (“shedding”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>blōdiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bloody</li><li>bloodlike</li><li>(<i>of colors</i>) red as blood</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) bloodthirsty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blōþagaz</i>, equivalent to <i>blōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bloodless</li></ol><p>From <i>blōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blōtmōnaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blood-red</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blōþaraudaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bloodthirsty</li></ol><p>From <i>blōd</i> (“blood”) +&lrm; <i>rēow</i> (“cruel, fierce”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lancet</li></ol><p><i>blōd</i> +&lrm; <i>seax</i>, literally “blood-knife”</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>penalty for bloodshed, bloodwite</li></ol><p><i>blōd</i> +&lrm; <i>wīte</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>metallurgy</i>) The (mass of) metal extracted from the ore; lump, mass</li></ol><p>Unknown, but probably a secondary sense to unrecorded <i>&#42;blōma</i> (“flower”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blōmō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blōmô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blanc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blanca</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blandan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blanden</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grey-haired, grizzly-haired</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) old, advanced in eld</li></ol><p>From <i>blonden</i> (“mixed”) +&lrm; <i>feax</i> (“hair”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blossom, bloom; blooming, flowering</li><li>flower, blossom</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blōstm</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blōsmaz</i>, <i>&#42;blōstmō</i> (“flower”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰleh₃-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flower, blossom</li><li>blossom, bloom; blooming, flowering</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blōstmō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blōstmô</i> (“flower”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰleh₃-</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>blómstr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blooming, blossoming</li></ol><p><i>blōstma</i> +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blossom, to bloom</li></ol><p><i>blōstma</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flowery, floral</li></ol><p><i>blōstm</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sacrifice, especially a blood sacrifice by heathens</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blōtą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sacrifice (+ dative = <i>to</i> a god)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blōtaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the eleventh month of the year according to the Anglo-Saxon calendar</li><li>November</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blōtamānōþ</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>blōtmānuđ</i>, Old High German <i>bluoʒmānōd</i>. Equivalent to <i>blōt</i> +&lrm; <i>mōnaþ</i>, so called because in Anglo-Saxon times sacrifices were made before the winter set in.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flower, blossom, bloom</li><li>to flourish, prosper</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blōaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firebrand, torch</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blusi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blusją</i> (“torch”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>blys</i> (“light, flame”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to redden</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blaskijaną</i>, from <i>&#42;blasǭ</i> (“burning candle; torch”) or alternatively from <i>&#42;bluskijaną</i>, from <i>&#42;blusjǭ</i> (“torch”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰel-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn, blaze</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torch</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;blysian</i> (“to burn, blaze”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an incendiary</li></ol><p>From <i>blysiġe</i> (“torch, flame”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blissian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>black</li><li>alternative form of <i>blac</i> (“pale”)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black</li><li>ink</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blak</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blakaz</i> (“burnt, black”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰleg-</i> (“to shine, burn, scorch”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>blac</i> (“ink”), Old High German <i>blah-</i>; related to Old Norse <i>blakkr</i> (“dusky, black”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blak</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blaką</i> (noun), from <i>&#42;blakaz</i> (“black”, adjective).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blaik</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bleach</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blaikijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blaikijaną</i>, a factitive verb from <i>&#42;blaikaz</i> (“pale”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blackness</li><li>that which is black</li></ol><p>Related to <i>blæc</i> (“black”), presumably from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blakkjā</i>, from <i>&#42;blak</i> (“black”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-jā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blācern</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inkwell, inkhorn</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blakahorn</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>blakhorn</i>, Old High German <i>blakhorn</i>. Equivalent to <i>blæc</i> +&lrm; <i>horn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paleness, pallor</li><li>leprosy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blaik</i> (“white, pale”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leaf</li><li>the broad, flat blade of a utensil (e.g., an oar or spade)</li><li>blast, blowing</li><li>inspiration; breath, life, spirit; glory, splendor</li><li>prosperity, wealth, riches</li><li>success</li><li>dignity</li><li>alternative form of <i>blēd</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blad</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bladą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰl̥h₃-tó-m</i>, from <i>&#42;bʰleh₃-</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bled</i> (West Frisian <i>bled</i>), Old Saxon <i>blad</i>, Dutch <i>blad</i>, Old High German <i>blat</i> (German <i>Blatt</i>), Old Norse <i>blað</i> (Danish <i>blad</i>, Icelandic <i>blað</i>).</p><p>From a conflation of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blēdaz</i>, <i>&#42;blēdō</i> (“flower, leaf”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blēdaz</i> (“blowing, blast”). Cognate with Old High German <i>blāt</i> (“flower, blossom, prosperity; blowing, breeze, windgust”). Related to Old English <i>blǣdre</i> (“bubble, blister, pimple”), <i>blǣst</i> (“windgust”). More at bladder, blast, blow.</p><p>See <i>blēd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blǣdre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flower, blossom</li><li>fruit</li></ol><p>From <i>blǣd</i>, <i>blēd</i> + <i>-nes</i>. Akin to Old English <i>blēd</i> (“fruit, flower”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) bladder</li><li>pimple, pustule, blister</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blādrā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blēdrǭ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gudgeon; bleak (fish)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blaigijǭ</i> (“blay, bleak, gudgeon”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰleyk-</i> (“to shine”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>light blue</li><li>dark blue</li><li>violet or purple</li></ol><p>From <i>blāw</i> (“blue”) +&lrm; <i>hǣwen</i> (“blue, purple, green, azure”) (compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hēwijaz</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blowing</li><li>blast</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blās</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blēsaną</i> (“to blow”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰel-</i> (“to blow up, swell”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blase</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blowing, blast, burst of wind, breeze</li><li>flame</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blāstu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blēstuz</i> (“blowing, burst of wind”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blēsaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰel-</i> (“to swell, blow up”).</p><p>Cognate with Old High German <i>blāst</i>, Old Norse <i>blástr</i>, Old High German <i>blāsan</i> (“to blow”) (German <i>blasen</i> (“to blow”)), Old English <i>blāwan</i> (“to blow”). More at blow.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow</li><li>to rush, "move impetuously"</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blēstijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blēstijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to baa, bleat</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blātijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blǣhǣwen</i></li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>blǣhǣwen</i> (“blue”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blēdan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;blōdijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blōþijaną</i> (“to bleed”). Compare Old Frisian <i>blēda</i>, Old High German <i>bluoten</i>, Old Norse <i>blœða</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blētsian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blētsung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blētsian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>blētsung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>book</li><li>beech</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bōk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bōks</i>, whence also Old Frisian <i>bōk</i> (West Frisian <i>boek</i>), Old Saxon <i>bōk</i> (Low German <i>Book</i>), Dutch <i>boek</i>, Old High German <i>buoh</i> (German <i>Buch</i>), Old Norse <i>bók</i> (Danish <i>bog</i>, Norwegian <i>bok</i>), Swedish <i>bok</i>), Gothic <i>𐌱𐍉𐌺𐌰</i> (bōka). The Germanic root is often taken to be related to the word for <i>beech</i>, the wood of rune-tablets.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bōkō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wealth of books, a library</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bocum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>literature</li><li>knowledge from books</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bōkakraftu</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>bōkkraft</i> and Old High German <i>buohkraft</i>. Equivalent to <i>bōc</i> (“book”) +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i> (“craft”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a writer, scribe</li><li>an author</li><li>a learned man</li><li>an instructor</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bōkārī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bōkārijaz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>bōkeri</i>, Old High German <i>buohhāri</i>, Gothic <i>𐌱𐍉𐌺𐌰𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (bōkareis).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>library</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>bōc</i> +&lrm; <i>ġestrēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>library</li></ol><p>From <i>bōc</i> (“book”) +&lrm; <i>hord</i> (“hoard”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>library</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>bōc</i> +&lrm; <i>hūs</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give by charter, to charter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bōkōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freehold; land held by a charter or writing that was not bound to a fief</li></ol><p>From <i>bōc</i> (“book”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>book-learning; booklore</li></ol><p>From <i>bōc</i> +&lrm; <i>lār</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) books; booklike, bookish, biblical, scriptural</li></ol><p>From <i>bōc</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Latin language</li></ol><p>From <i>bōc</i> (“book”) +&lrm; <i>Lǣden</i> (“Latin”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reading, book reading</li></ol><p>From <i>bōc</i> (“book”) +&lrm; <i>rǣding</i> (“reading”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) written character, letter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bōkastabaz</i>, corresponding to <i>bōc</i> (“book”) +&lrm; <i>stæf</i> (“staff”). Compare German <i>Buchstabe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beech</li></ol><p>From <i>bōc</i> (“beech”) +&lrm; <i>trēow</i> (“tree”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teachings in a book</li></ol><p>From <i>bōc</i> (“book”) +&lrm; <i>tǣċing</i> (“teaching”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a beechwood</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>bōc</i> (“book”) +&lrm; <i>wudu</i> (“wood”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a command, mandate, precept, order; bidding</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bod</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>boð</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>messenger</li><li>prophet</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bodō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;budô</i>. Related to Old English <i>bēodan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ground, foundation</li><li>bottom</li><li>abyss</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;botm</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;butmaz</i> (“ground”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰudʰmḗn</i> (“earth, bottom”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>boden</i> (“ground”), Old High German <i>bodam</i> (“ground, floor”), Old Norse <i>botn</i> (“floor, ground”). Doublet of <i>botm</i> (“bottom”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bodiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tell, proclaim, announce</li><li>to boast</li><li>to preach</li><li>to foretell, prophesy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bodōn</i> (“to proclaim, teach, instruct”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>bodiġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stature</li><li>bodily presence</li><li>body, trunk, torso, chest</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bodag</i> (“body, trunk”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preaching</li><li>announcement</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>branch or bough (of a tree)</li><li>tendril or sprig (of a plant)</li><li>arm or shoulder</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bōguz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>bōg</i>, Old High German <i>buog</i>, Old Norse <i>bógr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bow (weapon)</li><li>arch</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bogō</i>.</p><p>Related to Old Frisian <i>boga</i>, Old Saxon <i>bogo</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;bogo</i>, Old High German <i>bogo</i>, and Old Norse <i>bogi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crooked, full of bends</li></ol><p>From <i>boga</i> +&lrm; <i>-iht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bōg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Perhaps connected to <i>&#42;bōia</i> (“younger brother”), unattested as a common noun.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Boylestone (a village in Derbyshire, England).</li></ol><p>First attested in the 1086 Domesday Book.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gangway (of ship)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bolkō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bulkô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰelǵ-</i> (“beam, balk”). Cognate with Icelandic <i>búlki</i>. More at bulk.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>botl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) enraged</li></ol><p>From <i>bolgen</i> +&lrm; <i>mōd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bowl</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bullô</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>bolle</i> (Dutch <i>bol</i>), Old Norse <i>bolli</i>, Old High German <i>bolla</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Bolla)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bowl, cup, pot, beaker, measure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bullǭ</i> (“ball, round object, shell”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰln-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰel-</i> (“to blow, inflate, swell, bubble”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bolla</i> (“goblet”), Old Saxon <i>bollo</i> (“goblet”), Old High German <i>bolla</i> (“round vessel, bowl”), Old Norse <i>bolli</i> (“goblet, cup”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pillow, cushion</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bulstraz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bolt</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bolt</i>.</p><p>Compare Lithuanian <i>beldu</i> (“I knock”), <i>baldas</i> (“pole for striking”). Akin to Dutch <i>bout</i>, German <i>Bolz</i> or <i>Bolzen</i>, Danish <i>bolt</i>, Icelandic <i>bolti</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ornament</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bōgan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bōnō</i> or <i>&#42;bōnaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂-</i> (“to shine”).</p><p>Shortening of <i>bōgan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bana</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>band</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>husband</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>bóndi</i> (“farmer; husband”, literally “dweller”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bōg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bark</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;borkōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;burkōną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰrg-</i>, <i>&#42;bʰerg-</i> (“to make a noise, growl, bark”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>board, plank</li><li>table</li><li>the side of a ship; <i>(by extension)</i> the ship itself</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) shield</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bord</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;burdą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an ornamental border; fringe; embroidery</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bordō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tablecloth</li></ol><p>From <i>bord</i> +&lrm; <i>clāþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>board</li><li>table</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bordā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>side of a ship</li><li>shield wall</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>bord</i> (“shield, side of a ship”) +&lrm; <i>weall</i> (“wall”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pledge</li></ol><p>Related to the verb <i>borgian</i> (“to borrow”), which see.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to borrow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;borgōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;burgōną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>borgēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>borg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bore, pierce</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;borōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;burōną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>borōn</i> (German <i>bohren</i>), Old Norse <i>bora</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Likely from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bō-</i>, making it related to Old English <i>&#42;bōia</i> (“younger brother, boy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bosham, a village in West Sussex, England</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bōsiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an ox or cowstall</li><li>a cowshed</li><li>a manger</li></ol><p>Derivative of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bans</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bansaz</i>, perhaps equivalent to <i>&#42;bōs</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>. Compare Low German <i>Bansige</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bōsig</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breast, bosom, chest</li><li>(area contained by an) embrace</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bōsmaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bōsm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>help, assistance, rescue, remedy, cure, deliverance from evil</li><li>mending, repair, improvement</li><li>compensation for an injury or wrong; (peace) offering, recompense, amends, atonement, reformation, penance, repentance</li><li>improvement in (moral) condition, amendment</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bōtu</i> (“recompense”).</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bōte</i>, Old Saxon <i>bōta</i>, Dutch <i>boete</i>, Old High German <i>buoza</i> (German <i>Buße</i>), Old Norse <i>bōt</i> (Swedish <i>bot</i>), Gothic <i>𐌱𐍉𐍄𐌰</i> (bōta).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bōtian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to improve</li><li>to cure, remedy</li><li>to repair</li><li>to build; fence</li></ol><p>From <i>bōt</i> +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Bōtuhildi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Bōtōhildiz</i>, from <i>&#42;bōtō</i> (“betterment, recompense”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hildiz</i> (“battle”). Equivalent to <i>bōt</i> +&lrm; <i>hild</i>. Cognate with Faroese <i>Bóthild</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get better</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>bōt</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>building</li><li>house, dwelling</li></ol><p>Probably representing an earlier <i>*boþl</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bōþl</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bōþlą</i>, from an instrumental form of <i>&#42;būaną</i> (“to dwell”). Compare Old Norse <i>ból</i>. More at suffix <i>-eld</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bootless, unpardonable, what cannot be redeemed, recompensed or expiated by the payment of boot</li></ol><p>From <i>bōt</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bottom; foundation</li><li>ground</li><li>abyss</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;botm</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;butmaz</i> (“ground”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰudʰmḗn</i> (“earth, bottom”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>bodom</i> (“bottom”), Old High German <i>bodam</i> (“floor, bottom”), Old Norse <i>botn</i> (“floor, ground”), Old English <i>bytme</i> (“keel of a ship”), Sanskrit <i>बुध्न</i> (budhná, “bottom, ground”), Avestan <i>𐬠𐬏𐬥𐬋</i> (būnō, “bottom, ground”), Latin <i>fundus</i> (“bottom”). Doublet of <i>bodan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bōtwierþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pardonable, expiable, able to get atoned for</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>bōt</i> +&lrm; <i>-wierþe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Bōtuwini</i>, from <i>&#42;bōtu</i> (“aid, assistance”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wini</i> (“friend”). Equivalent to <i>bōt</i> +&lrm; <i>wine</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Buozwini</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Bōtuwulf</i>, from <i>&#42;bōtu</i> (“aid, assistance”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wulf</i> (“wolf”). Equivalent to <i>bōt</i> +&lrm; <i>wulf</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Buozolf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bōtwierþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>box tree</li><li>box</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>box tree</li></ol><p>From <i>box</i> (“box tree”) +&lrm; <i>trēow</i> (“tree”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>boþen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rosemary</li><li>darnel</li><li>thyme</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wide, broad</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brǣd</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;braid</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surface</li><li>breadth</li><li>extent (space or area to which something extends)</li></ol><p>From <i>brād</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brādnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brādnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brādnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>brād</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>brād</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firebrand; torch</li><li>blight (disease of plants)</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a sword</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brand</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brandaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brandīsen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>branding iron</li><li>tripod</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>brand</i> (“brand”); <i>īsen, īsern</i> (“iron”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trivet</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brandaraidō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tall, high, steep</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brant</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brantaz</i> (“high, steep, towering”), of uncertain origin, but possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰren-</i> (“to project”), related to Old Norse <i>brant</i> (“precipice”), Old Norse <i>bretta</i> (“to lift up, raise”), Latvian <i>bruôds</i> (“roof ridge”).</p><p>Cognate also with Old Norse <i>brattr</i> (“steep, towering, harsh, difficult”), Old Swedish <i>branter</i>, Faroese and Icelandic <i>brattur</i>, Danish <i>brat</i>, Norwegian <i>bratt</i>, Swedish <i>brant</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bræsian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to brustle; rustle; crackle; make a noise; murmur</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brastulōn</i>, ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brastōną</i> (“to crack, break, snap”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloak</li></ol><p>From Old Irish <i>bratt</i>, from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;brattos</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bit, piece, morsel, crumb</li><li>bread (foodstuff)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;braud</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;braudą</i>, whence also Old Frisian <i>brād</i> (West Frisian <i>brea</i>), Old Saxon <i>brōd</i> (German Low German <i>Broot</i>, <i>Brot</i>), Dutch <i>brood</i>, Old High German <i>brōt</i> (German <i>Brot</i>), Old Norse <i>brauð</i> and Icelandic <i>brauð</i> (Swedish <i>bröd</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brǣw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a noise; tumult; clamour; revelry, music; sound; cry</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brahtm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break,</li><li>to demolish, destroy</li><li>to kill</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brautan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brautaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰrewd-</i>. Related to Old Norse <i>breyta</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brautijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brewery, brewhouse</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>brēaw</i> +&lrm; <i>-ern</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>underpants</li></ol><p>Plural of <i>brōc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Breca the Bronding</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break</li><li>to break into a place</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brekan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brekaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰreg-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) heartbreak, grief</li></ol><p><i>brecan</i> +&lrm; <i>-þ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surface</li><li>plank, board</li><li>table, tablet</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bred</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bredą</i>, derived from the e-grade <i>&#42;bʰredʰóm</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰer-dʰ-</i>, extended form of <i>&#42;bʰer-</i> (“to carve, cut, split, rub”). Cognates include German <i>Brett</i>, Yiddish <i>ברעט</i> (bret).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>breġdan</i></li><li>to bear offspring</li></ol><p>See main entry.</p><p>Cognate to Old High German <i>bruoten</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brǣw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brego</i> (“prince, ruler”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move back and forth, vibrate</li><li>to draw a weapon</li><li>to drag something</li><li>to bend</li><li>to weave, braid</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bregdan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bregdaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brǣw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) prince, lord, ruler</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;breguz</i>, u-stem variant of <i>&#42;bragz</i> (“the first, premier, chief”). The expected outcome for <i>*bragz</i> would be non-existent <i>*bræg</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>bragr</i> (“the best, foremost, primary; chief, leader, prince”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the throne, stool, or chair of a prince</li><li>a prince's dominion or kingdom</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>brego</i> (“prince”) +&lrm; <i>stōl</i> (“seat”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a royal guard</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>brego</i> (“prince”) +&lrm; <i>weard</i> (“guard”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>brego</i> (“prince”) +&lrm; <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>brego</i> (“prince, lord”) +&lrm; <i>swiþ</i> (“strong, mighty”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brǣw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>breahtm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bramble</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) famous, renowned, glorious</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brōmi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brōmiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brǣmel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brǣmelberġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>brēme</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>brēmelum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roar, rage</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bramjaną</i>, <i>&#42;bremaną</i> (“to roar”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰrem-</i> (“to make noise”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring, lead, conduct</li><li>to carry, bear, transport</li><li>to bring forth, produce, bear, yield (as in crops or offspring)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brangijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brangijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) a ship</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brantīng</i>, perhaps derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brantijaną</i> (“to raise up, erect, build”), from <i>&#42;brantaz</i> (“high, steep”).</p><p>Alternatively from <i>brant</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brego</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brerd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breese (horsefly); gadfly</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brimsi</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;brimusi</i> ~ <i>&#42;brimussju</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brimusī</i>, related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bremô</i> (“gadfly”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chest</li><li>breast</li><li>heart (seat of emotions located in the chest area)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;breustą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰrews-</i> (“to swell”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>briāst</i>, Old Saxon <i>briost</i>, Old Norse <i>brjóst</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breastbone</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>brēost</i> (“breast”) +&lrm; <i>bān</i> (“bone”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) anxiety, sorrow</li></ol><p>From <i>brēost</i> +&lrm; <i>ċearu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) chest (Literally, “breastchamber”)</li></ol><p>From <i>brēost</i> (“breast”) +&lrm; <i>cofa</i> (“chamber”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) breast's hoard, thought, mind, heart</li></ol><p>From <i>brēost</i> (“breast”) +&lrm; <i>hord</i> (“hoard”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a stomacher, waistcoat (glosses Latin <i>thorax</i>)</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>brēost</i> (“breast”) +&lrm; <i>rocc</i> (“upper garment”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break, bruise, destroy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;breutan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;breutaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Breten</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Breten</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to brew</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;breuwan</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Dutch <i>brouwen</i>), Old High German <i>briuwan</i> (German <i>brauen</i>), Old Norse <i>brugga</i> (Swedish <i>brygga</i>, Danish <i>brygge</i>). Perhaps related to Latin <i>dēfrutum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decay, waste away</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;breuþaną</i> (“to fall into ruin, decay”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brǣr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brim, margin, rim</li><li>top of a pot or vessel</li><li>highest point of anything</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;breʀd</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brezdaz</i> (“edge, shore”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) brass</li><li>strong or bold</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>bræs</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Brett</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Britain (a large island of Northern Europe)</li></ol><p>From a British form of Latin <i>Britannia</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Brytenland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Britain (a large island of Northern Europe)</li></ol><p><i>Bret</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brȳtmælum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Breten</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Briton (native or inhabitant of Britain)</li><li>Breton (native or inhabitant of Brittany)</li></ol><p>From a British form of Latin <i>Brittō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Britons (indigenous people of Britain)</li><li>Bretons (inhabitants of Brittany)</li></ol><p>From a British form of Latin <i>Brittōnēs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>British</li><li>Breton</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a Briton or Breton</li></ol><p><i>Brett</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>title of certain Anglo-Saxon kings</li></ol><p>First attested in the 9th century, but applied to kings who ruled from the 5th century onward.</p><p>From an adjective <i>brytten</i> (“wide”) and <i>walda</i> (“ruler, wielder”).</p><p>The original meaning of the title is thus "wide-ruler", but it was reinterpreted, likely at an early period predating its 9th-century attestation, as containing the name Britain and understood in the sense of "ruler of Britain". The title <i>rex Britanniae</i> "king of Britain" is used by Æthelbald of Mercia in 736, who at the same time is also recorded as a <i>bretwalda</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Brittons of Wales</li></ol><p>From <i>Brett</i> +&lrm; <i>Wēalas</i> (“Wales”). Compare Old Dutch <i>bretwalas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bryċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chick (a young bird or fledgling, as opposed to an altricial chick or hatchling)</li><li>chicken</li></ol><p>Unknown; the word appears to be exclusive to Old English and lacks cognates in any other Germanic languages. Liberman notes that dozens of origins have been both suggested and rejected, leaving its original meaning and etymology a mystery. It might possibly be from a non-Indo-European substrate, with a likely onomatopoeic origin.</p><p>Formally, however, the word may trace back to a Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bridi ~ &#42;briddj-</i>, possibly to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bridjaz</i>, a derivative of <i>&#42;bredą</i> (“board, plank, shelf", possibly also "perch, roost”), and may have therefore been used of young birds or fowl that were fledged and able to perch but not yet able to fly (i.e. "perch-ling", or "roost-ling"; compare modern English <i>rooster</i>, <i>bougher</i>, <i>brancher</i>, etc.), as opposed to <i>fugol ~ fuglas</i> the term for birds that were able to fly. If so, then possibly related to Old English <i>bred</i> (“board, plank, tablet”). Otherwise, the derivation remains unclear.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bridle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brigdilaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of boards; wooden</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bridīn</i> (“made of boards”), equivalent to <i>bred</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brīw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Beorhtmær</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Beorhtrīc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Beorhtweald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Beorhtweald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>brīġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>brīw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) sea, ocean, water</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) surf; the surface of the sea</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) the edge of the sea or a body of water</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brimą</i> (“turbulence, surge; surf, sea”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bremaną</i> (“to roar”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰrem-</i> (“to hum, make a noise”).</p><p>cognates</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) cold as the sea</li></ol><p>From <i>brim</i> +&lrm; <i>ċeald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) sea-bird, seagull</li></ol><p>From <i>brim</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>fugol</i> (“bird”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) ship</li></ol><p>Kenning of <i>brim</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>henġest</i> (“steed”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) fish</li></ol><p>From <i>brim</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>hlæst</i> (“cargo”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) path of the sea, sea-way</li></ol><p>From <i>brim</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>lād</i> (“way”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) seafarer, sea-traveller</li></ol><p>From <i>brim</i> + present participle of <i>līþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailor</li></ol><p>From <i>brim</i> +&lrm; <i>mann</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) ship</li></ol><p>Kenning of <i>brim</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>wudu</i> (“wood”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brine</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brīnā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brīnǭ</i> (“brine”), of unknown ultimate origin, possibly Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mr-īnó-</i>, from <i>&#42;móri</i> (“sea, standing water”). Compare Middle Dutch <i>brine</i> ("brine, salt liquor"&#59; whence Dutch <i>brijn</i>), West Frisian <i>brein</i> (“brine”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bringan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bringaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰrenk-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>bringeþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brerd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brēosa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bristle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;burstilu</i>, diminutive of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bursti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;burstiz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰr̥stís</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bryttian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Brettisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>brīw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oatmeal, porridge</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brīw</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) underpants; see <i>brēċ</i></li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) butt</li><li>brook</li><li>alternative form of <i>brocc</i></li><li>labour, toil</li><li>affliction</li><li>disease</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brōk</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>badger</li></ol><p>Probably from a Celtic source akin to Irish <i>broc</i>, Welsh <i>broch</i>, Cornish <i>brogh</i> and thus ultimately from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;brokkos</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>broken</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brukanaz</i>, past participle of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brekaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brood</li><li>hatching</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brōduz</i> (“brood”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>terror, dread, horror</li><li>danger</li></ol><p>Uncertain, perhaps borrowed from Celtic. Compare Old High German <i>bruogo</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>broom (the plant)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brām</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>broomy; covered with broom plants</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brand</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brant</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>point</li><li>a spire of grass or corn</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;broʀd</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bruzdaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to corrupt, decay, to perish, to rot</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brusnōn</i>, related to <i>&#42;brausijaną</i> (“to crush, break”). Related to <i>brysan</i> (“to crush, bruise”) (modern <i>bruise</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corruption</li><li>decay</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>brosnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brōþor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>brōþor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>brōþorrǣden</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brōþor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>broth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;broþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brōþorsċiepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brother</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brōþer</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a brother's murderer, fratricide</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brōþurbanô</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>bróðurbani</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brother murder, fratricide</li></ol><p>From <i>brōþor</i> (“brother”) +&lrm; <i>cwealm</i> (“slaughter”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fraternal niece</li></ol><p>From <i>brōþor</i> (“brother”) +&lrm; <i>dohtor</i> (“daughter”). Compare Icelandic <i>bróðurdóttir</i> and Swedish <i>brorsdotter</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brotherless</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brōþerlaus</i>, equivalent to <i>brōþor</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brotherly</li><li>fraternal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brōþurlīkaz</i>, equivalent to <i>brōþor</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brotherly love</li></ol><p><i>brōþor</i> +&lrm; <i>lufu</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brotherhood, fraternity</li></ol><p>From <i>brōþor</i> (“brother”) +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i> (“-hood”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brotherliness, (brotherly) kindness or love</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brōþerskapi</i>, equivalent to <i>brōþor</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċiepe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fratricide</li></ol><p>From <i>brōþor</i> (“brother”) +&lrm; <i>sleġe</i> (“slaying, murder”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fraternal nephew</li></ol><p>From <i>brōþor</i> (“brother”) +&lrm; <i>sunu</i> (“son”). Compare Icelandic <i>bróðursonur</i> and Swedish <i>brorson</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sister-in-law</li></ol><p>From <i>brōþor</i> (“brother”) +&lrm; <i>wīf</i> (“wife”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) brotherwort</li></ol><p>From <i>broþor</i> (“brother”) +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i> (“plant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brōþor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an eyelash</li><li>an eyebrow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brūwō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃bʰrúHs</i> (“brow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to use</li><li>to enjoy</li><li>to harness, take advantage of, employ, exploit</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brūkan</i> (“to enjoy, use”).</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>brūka</i>, Old Saxon <i>brūkan</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;brūkan</i>, Old High German <i>brūhhan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌱𐍂𐌿𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (brukjan). The Indo-European root was also the source of Latin <i>fruor</i> (“enjoy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>brūcaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>function, occupation</li><li>enjoyment</li></ol><p>From <i>brūcan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brown (<i>color/colour</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brūn</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Brun)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark purple</li></ol><p>From <i>brūn</i> (“brown”) +&lrm; <i>basu</i> (“purple”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a patronymic name</li></ol><p><i>brūn</i> (“brown”) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breaking</li><li>breach, violation</li><li>fragment</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>breakable, frail, fleeting</li><li>useful</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bruki</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brukiz</i> (“fissure”).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brukiz</i> (“breakable”).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brūkiz</i> (“useful”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bridge</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bruggju</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repair or restoration of a bridge</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>bryċġ</i> +&lrm; <i>bōt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bridge</li></ol><p><i>bryċġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bristol (a city in England)</li></ol><p>From <i>bryċġ</i> (“bridge”) +&lrm; <i>stōw</i> (“place”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bride</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brūdi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brūdiz</i> (“bride, daughter-in-law”).</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>brēd</i> (West Frisian <i>breid</i>), Old Saxon <i>brūd</i> (Low German <i>Bruut</i>), Dutch <i>bruid</i>, Old High German <i>brūt</i> (German <i>Braut</i>), Old Norse <i>brúðr</i> (Swedish <i>brud</i>), Gothic <i>𐌱𐍂𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (brūþs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brȳdealu</i></li></ol><p>From <i>brȳd</i> +&lrm; <i>ealo</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wedding-feast, bridal</li></ol><p>From <i>brȳd</i> +&lrm; <i>ealu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bridegroom</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>brȳd</i> +&lrm; <i>guma</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brūdigumō</i>. Cognates include Old Frisian <i>brēdgoma</i>, Old Saxon <i>brūdigomo</i>, Old High German <i>brūtigomo</i>, and Old Norse <i>brúðgumi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wedding, bridelock</li><li>ceremony in which the bride was conducted to her new home</li></ol><p>Of Proto-Germanic origin. Cognates with German <i>Brautlauf</i>, Old High German <i>brūtlouft</i>, Old Saxon <i>brūdloht</i>, Middle Dutch <i>bruloft</i>, Dutch <i>bruiloft</i>, Old Norse <i>brúðhlaup</i>, Swedish <i>bröllop</i>, Danish and Norwegian <i>bryllup</i>. By surface analysis, <i>bryd</i> +&lrm; <i>hleapan</i>. The origin of the term may relate to the theme of weddings as simulated abduction rituals in other Indo-European cultures.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bridal</li></ol><p>From <i>brȳd</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beorhtnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Beorhtweald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burning, fire; flame, heat</li><li>inflammation, burn</li><li>torch</li><li>fervor, passion</li><li>alternative form of <i>brīne</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bruniz</i> (“fire, burning”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Bryne)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>bryne</i> (“fire, ferver, passion”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prick; goad; incite; urge; constrain</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bruʀdijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bruzdijaną</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bruzdaz</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰers-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bruise; crush</li></ol><p>From the merger of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brausijaną</i> (“to break apart”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brūsijaną</i> (“to break; crack; crackle”). Cognate with Middle High German <i>brūsen</i> (“to roar; crash”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Briton, British person (<i>especially someone Celtic</i>)</li><li>Breton (<i>especially someone Celtic</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Breten</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Britain (a large island of Northern Europe)</li></ol><p><i>Bryten</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Brytenland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Bretland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>piecemeal</li></ol><p>From <i>bryt</i> +&lrm; <i>-mǣlum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fragment</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giver, dispenser, distributor</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bruttjō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Brettas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dispense</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Brettisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Bryttwealas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Britons, especially pre-Anglo-Saxon Britons</li></ol><p>From <i>Bryttas</i> (“British”) +&lrm; <i>Wealas</i> (“Welsh”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Bryttwēalas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>British</li><li>Welsh</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brewing, drink, beverage</li></ol><p>Possibly West Saxon form of Anglian <i>&#42;breoðen</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;broþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bruþą</i> (“broth”) + <i>&#42;-īną</i> (diminutive suffix, literally “small brewing”), or from <i>&#42;bruþijaną</i> (“to make a broth”) + <i>&#42;-īniz</i> (noun-forming suffix). In either case, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰrewh₁-</i> (“to boil, brew”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flesh</li><li>roasted meat</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;brǣda</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brādō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brēdô</i> (“meat, roast”). Influenced and seen as a deverbal of <i>brǣdan</i> (“to roast”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make broad, broaden</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spread, extend</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be extended or stretched (over) (+ ġeond)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to grow or rise up</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to roast, broil, warm</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;braidijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;braidijaną</i>; equivalent to <i>brād</i> (“broad”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brādan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brēdaną</i> (class-7 strong verb).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything spread out, such as carpet or clothing</li></ol><p>Cognate with Norwegian Bokmål <i>åbredsel</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frying pan</li></ol><p>From <i>brǣdan</i> (“to fry”) +&lrm; <i>panne</i> (“pan”). Cognate with German <i>Bratpfanne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brādnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brǣdu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frying pan</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>brǣdan</i> (“to roast”) +&lrm; <i>panne</i> (“pan”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>width, breadth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;braidį̄</i>. Equivalent to <i>brād</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bræġn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brain</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bragn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bragną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skull</li></ol><p>From <i>bræġn</i> (“brain”) +&lrm; <i>panne</i> (“pan”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>later Old English</i>) alternative form of <i>brǣmel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bramble</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brāmil</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blackberry, brambleberry</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brāmilabaʀi</i>, equivalent to <i>brǣmel</i> +&lrm; <i>berġe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brǣmelberġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>briar</li></ol><p>Of unknown origin, perhaps related to Old French <i>bruiere</i> (“heather”) (French <i>bruyère</i>); compare also Old Norse <i>brǫrr</i> (“briar”), whence Icelandic <i>brörr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ore</li><li>brass or bronze</li></ol><p>Origin uncertain. Perhaps representing a backformation from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brasnaz</i> (“brazen”), from or related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brasō</i> (“fire, pyre”). Compare Old Frisian <i>bress</i> (“copper”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bresen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover in brass</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>bræs</i> (“brass”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eyelid</li></ol><p>From the merger of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brāwī</i> and <i>&#42;brāwu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brēwō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>bræþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>odour, smell, scent</li><li>steam, exhalation</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brāþi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>brēme</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live or dwell</li><li>to inhabit, to occupy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;būan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;būaną</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>būwa</i>, <i>bōwa</i> (West Frisian <i>bouwe</i>), Old Saxon <i>būan</i> (Low German <i>bugen</i>), Old Dutch <i>būwan</i> (Dutch <i>bouwen</i>), Old High German <i>būan</i> (German <i>bauen</i>), Old Norse <i>búa</i> (Danish <i>bo</i>, Swedish <i>bo</i>, Norwegian Nynorsk <i>bu</i>, Faroese <i>búgva</i>), Gothic <i>𐌱𐌰𐌿𐌰𐌽</i> (bauan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>belly, stomach</li><li>a vessel that bulges out, such as a bottle, jug, pitcher, etc.</li><li>alternative form of <i>bucc</i> (“buck”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;būk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;būkaz</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bukk</i> (“male deer”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buck (male deer)</li><li>alternative form of <i>būc</i> (“belly”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bukk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bukkaz</i> (“male deer”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰuǵ-</i> (“ram”). Akin to Old High German <i>boc</i>, Old Norse <i>bukkr</i>, Old English <i>bucca</i> (“buck, male goat”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;būk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;būkaz</i> (“belly, stomach”); perhaps representing a corruption of a diminutive (+&lrm; <i>-uc</i>) from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;būkuk</i> (“miniature vessel”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>he-goat</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bukkō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bukkô</i> (“male goat”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰugo-</i> (“buck”). Akin to Old High German <i>boc</i>, Old Norse <i>bukkr</i>, Middle Dutch <i>boc</i>, Avestan <i>𐬠𐬏𐬰𐬀</i> (būza, “buck, goat”), Old Armenian <i>բուծ</i> (buc, “lamb”), Old English <i>bucc</i> (“male deer”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Bucca)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Buckingham (a town in Buckinghamshire, England)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Bucca</i> (a male given name) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> +&lrm; <i>hām</i> (“homestead”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Buckinghamshire (a historical county of England)</li></ol><p><i>Buccingahām</i> +&lrm; <i>sċīr</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>a pitcherful</li></ol><p><i>būc</i> +&lrm; <i>-ful</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often found in combination</i>) beetle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;buʀdō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;buzdô</i> (“beetle, grub, swelling”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰūs-</i> (“to move quickly”). Cognate with Middle Low German <i>buddech</i> (“thick, swollen”), Low German <i>budde</i> (“louse, grub”), Old Norse <i>budda</i> (“purse”). See bug.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Budda)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant</li></ol><p>From <i>būan</i> (“to live or dwell, to inhabit, to occupy”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>bóndi</i>, <i>búandi</i> (“farmer, husband”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>above (Object takes dative)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;biobanā</i>; equivalent to <i>be</i> +&lrm; <i>ufan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aforementioned</li></ol><p>From <i>bufan</i> (“above”) +&lrm; <i>sprecen</i> (“spoken”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend</li><li>to bow</li><li>to turn (toward or away from something)</li><li>to yield, give way</li><li>to submit</li><li>to withdraw, retire</li><li>to incline</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;beugan</i>, with the diphthong analogically replaced by ū in the present stem, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;beuganą</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>būgan</i>, Old High German <i>biogan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌱𐌹𐌿𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (biugan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bull</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bulô</i> (“bull”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰl̥no-</i>, from <i>&#42;bʰel-</i> (“to blow, swell up”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bulgarian</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a young bull, bullock, bull-calf</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bulluk</i>, equivalent to <i>bula</i> +&lrm; <i>-oc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) cup</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>private chamber, room</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;būr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;būrą</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>būr</i>, Old High German <i>būr</i> (German <i>Bauer</i> (“birdcage”)), Old Norse <i>búr</i> (Swedish <i>bur</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of peasant birth</li></ol><p>From <i>būr</i> +&lrm; <i>byrde</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>city or town</li><li>fortified place: fortress, castle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;burgz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>burhbōt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a ruler of a city, mayor</li><li>an inhabitant of a city, citizen</li></ol><p>From <i>burg</i> (“city”) +&lrm; <i>ealdor</i> (“chief, lord, leader”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>byrġen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the land of the Burgundians; Bornholm</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Burgundians</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Burgundijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Burgundijô</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>borgundar</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Burgundians</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Burgahardu</i>, from <i>&#42;burg</i> (“fortification”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hardu</i> (“hard”). Equivalent to <i>burg</i> +&lrm; <i>heard</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Burghart</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>burg</i> (“city, fortress”) +&lrm; <i>helm</i> (“defence, protection”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>burhlēod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>burhlēod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>burg</i> (“city, fortress”) +&lrm; <i>nōþ</i> (“daring”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Burgrǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a city-dwelling (i.e. a house surrounded by a wall or rampart of earth)</li></ol><p>From <i>burg</i> +&lrm; <i>ræċed</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Burgarād</i>, from <i>&#42;burg</i> (“fortification”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;rād</i> (“advice, counsel”). Equivalent to <i>burg</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣd</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Burgrāt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizenship</li></ol><p>From <i>burg</i> (“city”) +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Burhsiġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>burg</i> (“settlement”) +&lrm; <i>stān</i> (“stone”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Burgaswinþ</i>, from <i>&#42;burg</i> (“fortification”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;swinþ</i> (“strong”). Equivalent to <i>burg</i> +&lrm; <i>swīþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Burgswind</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a city dwelling</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>burg</i> (“city”) +&lrm; <i>sæl</i> (“hall”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>burgwaras</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizens, people who live in a city</li></ol><p>From <i>burg</i> (“-city”) +&lrm; <i>-ware</i> (“residents”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>burgwaran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizenry, inhabitants of a city</li></ol><p><i>burg</i> (“city”) +&lrm; <i>-waru</i> (“residents”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>burg</i> (“city, fortress”) +&lrm; <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Burgawulf</i>, from <i>&#42;burg</i> (“fortification”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wulf</i> (“wolf”). Equivalent to <i>burg</i> +&lrm; <i>wulf</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Burgulf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>burg</i> (“city”) +&lrm; <i>þrȳþ</i> (“might”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>burg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the repairing of fortresses, which was among the burdens on all landed property</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>burh</i> +&lrm; <i>bōt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>burgealdor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Burghelm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>burg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the governor and chief magistrate of a city or town; a boroughreeve</li></ol><p>From <i>burh</i> +&lrm; <i>ġerēfa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burgess, citizen</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) townspeople, the citizenry</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>burh</i> +&lrm; <i>lēod</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burgess, citizen</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>burh</i> +&lrm; <i>lēoda</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>burg</i> (“city, fortress”) +&lrm; <i>siġe</i> (“victory”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>burgwaru</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>burhlēod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stream</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brunnō</i> (“stream, brook”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a stream, brook, river, or other running water</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brunnō</i> (“stream, brook”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bag, pouch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>bursa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>burg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>burhlēod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>without, except for, besides (+ dative)</li><li>outside of (+ dative)</li><li>for all, despite, in spite of, notwithstanding (+ dative)</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with the subjunctive</i>) unless</li><li>except for, but</li><li>except</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li><i>(usually with a negative)</i> merely, only, just</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;biūtan</i>, from <i>&#42;bī</i> (“by”) + <i>&#42;ūtan</i>, dative of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ūtō</i> (“exterior, outside”) (from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ūt</i> (“out”)), corresponding to <i>be-</i> +&lrm; <i>ūtan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butter</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;buterā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butterfly</li></ol><p>From <i>butere</i> (“butter”) +&lrm; <i>flēoge</i> (“fly”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>būtan</i></li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>būtan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Boddington (a village in Gloucestershire, England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>buterflēoge</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>buterflēoge</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwelling, habituation</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>býr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;buggjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bugjaną</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>buggian</i>, Gothic <i>𐌱𐌿𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (bugjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a buying, purchase</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>byċġan</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bīecnan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēacniendlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēacnian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēacnung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preacher</li><li>beadle, apparitor</li><li>warrant officer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;budil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;budilaz</i> (“messenger, herald”), from <i>&#42;beudaną</i> (“to stretch out, extend, offer”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰewdʰ-</i> (“to make aware”), equivalent to <i>bod</i> +&lrm; <i>-el</i>. Akin to Old High German <i>butil</i> (“beadle, court officer”), Old English <i>bēodan</i> (“to announce”). More at bid.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tub, vat</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) tub-shaped valley</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;budinu</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>budina</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>πυτίνη</i> (putínē).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>befer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bīeġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curve, bend</li><li>strait</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bugī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bugiz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>&#42;bugi</i> (attested in the compound <i>umbibugi</i>) and Old Norse <i>bugr</i> → Icelandic <i>bugur</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curve, bend; corner</li><li>bay, gulf</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;buhti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;buhtiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boil</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;būlō</i> (“boil”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bealcettan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bytla</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bytlan</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>bieldan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bieldu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bieldu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bilewit</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bielġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>belgan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bīeme</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bīemere</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>bȳme</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bīemian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inhabited, occupied</li></ol><p>From <i>būan</i> (“to dwell”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beorc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birth</li><li>bearing</li><li>burden</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;burþiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to embroider</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;burdijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;burdijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>byrða</i> (“to embroider, weave”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>born, well-born, noble, rich</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;burdijaz</i>, derived from <i>&#42;burdiz</i> (“birth, act of bearing”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>byrþen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an embroideress</li></ol><p>From <i>byrdan</i> +&lrm; <i>-estre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a weaver's tool</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turtle, tortoise</li></ol><p>Apparently from a Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;burdilingaz</i> (equivalent to <i>bord</i> +&lrm; <i>-ling</i>), though there are no cognates. Perhaps the literal meaning was originally "little shield," though in the surviving texts <i>bord</i> only means "shield" in poetry. Every modern Germanic language except English has the word "shield" in the word for "turtle": West Frisian <i>skyldpod</i>, Dutch <i>schildpad</i>, German <i>Schildkröte</i>, and Swedish <i>sköldpadda</i> all mean “shield toad,” Icelandic <i>skjaldbaka</i> and Faroese <i>skjaldbøka</i> both mean “shield back.” Also a few non-Germanic languages, cf. Estonian <i>kilpkonn</i> (literally “shield frog”) and Finnish <i>kilpikonna</i> (literally “shield toad”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>byrdling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>child, son, descendant; young man, youth</li><li>mound</li><li>strong wind, storm</li><li>time, opportunity; occurrence</li><li>stall, shed, hut</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;burī</i> (early <i>&#42;burijaz</i>), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;buriz</i> (“son”).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;buriz</i> (“hill, elevation”).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;buriz</i> (“favourable wind”).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;burjaz</i> (“opportunity”), related to Old English <i>byrian</i> (“to come up, occur”).</p><p>Probably related to Old English <i>būr</i>. Perhaps identical to the word for a farm or dwelling in German <i>-büren</i>, Dutch <i>-buren</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cupbearer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;burilijaz</i> (“carrier, manservant”), from <i>&#42;burjô</i> (“descendant, son”), from <i>&#42;beraną</i> (“to bear, carry”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour out</li><li>to serve, give to drink</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>byrele</i> (“cupbearer”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide</li><li>to bury</li><li>alternative form of <i>bierġan</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>byrian</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;burgijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;burgijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place of burial, tomb</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>byrġan</i> (“to bury”) +&lrm; <i>-els</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burial place: grave, tomb</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;burgini</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;burginī</i>, equivalent to <i>byrġan</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>byrġen</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;burginjō</i>, equivalent to <i>byrġan</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>byrienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a burier, corpse-bearer</li></ol><p>From <i>byrġan</i> (“to bury”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, variant of Beorhthēah</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>byrht</i> (“bright”) + <i>hēah</i> (“high, tall”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>breahtm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, alternative form of <i>Beorhtmund</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Berhtnōþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bierhtu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Beorhtrīc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Beorhtsiġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Beorhtwulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come up, occur</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;burjan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beorc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bierġan</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>byrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>byrienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) trunk, body</li><li>(<i>of a horse</i>) barrel</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;byril</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;biril</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>biril</i> (“a large basket, pot”). More at <i>barrel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>byrelian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>biernan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corselet, coat of mail</li></ol><p>Earlier <i>&#42;brynne</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brunjǭ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>brunja</i> (German <i>Brünne</i>), Old Norse <i>brynja</i> (Swedish <i>brynja</i>), Gothic <i>𐌱𐍂𐌿𐌽𐌾𐍉</i> (brunjō). Compare Middle English <i>brinie</i>, Scots <i>byrnie</i>, from the Old Norse.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) mailed warrior</li></ol><p>From <i>byrne</i> (“coat of mail”) +&lrm; <i>wiga</i> (“warrior”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bristle</li><li>loss, defect, failure</li><li>injury</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bursti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;burstiz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰr̥stís</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brestuz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰres‑</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>byrþen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burden</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;burþini</i>, equivalent to <i>byrd</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>byrþen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>millstone</li></ol><p>From <i>byrþen</i> +&lrm; <i>stān</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bȳsn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bisenian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bigian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bisgung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bisiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bisiġu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bismer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bismer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>example</li><li>parable</li><li>command</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;būsniz</i> (“command, precept”), from <i>&#42;beudaną</i> (“to ask, beg”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>ambusan</i> (“command, precept”), Old Norse <i>býsn</i> (“wonder, premonition”), Gothic <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌱𐌿𐍃𐌽𐍃</i> (anabūsns, “command”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exemplify; give or set an example</li></ol><p><i>bȳsn</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bisenian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>example</li></ol><p><i>bȳsnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beestings (first milk given by a cow after giving birth)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;beust</i> (“beestings”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bottle, flagon</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;buttjā</i>, from Late Latin <i>buttia</i>, likely borrowed from Byzantine Greek <i>βούττια</i> (boúttia), plural of <i>βούττιον</i> (boúttion), diminutive of <i>βοῦττις</i> (boûttis, “vessel”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bīetel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>builder</li></ol><p>From <i>bytlan</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;buþlijan</i>, from <i>&#42;buþl</i> (“house, dwelling”); equivalent to <i>botl</i> (“building, house”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>belda</i> (“to endow, equip”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>building</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>bytlan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>keel of a ship</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;botm</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>byt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>box tree</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) box tree</li></ol><p><i>box</i> (“box tree”) +&lrm; <i>-en</i>, with i-umlaut</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>back</li><li>back or rear end (of something)</li><li>stream; brook</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bak</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;baką</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;baki</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bakiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) the larboard, port, or left-hand side of a ship</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bakabord</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bakaburdaz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>&#42;bakbord</i> and Old Norse <i>bakborð</i>. Equivalent to <i>bæc</i> +&lrm; <i>bord</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baker (person who bakes and sells bread, etc)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bakārī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bakārijaz</i>, corresponding to <i>&#42;bæc</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>bakkāri</i>, <i>bekkeri</i>, Old High German <i>bekkāri</i>, <i>beckāri</i> (>German <i>Bäcker</i>), Old Norse <i>bakari</i> (Swedish <i>bagare</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bakery, bakehouse</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>&#42;bæc</i> +&lrm; <i>ærn</i> or <i>&#42;bæc</i> +&lrm; <i>-ern</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who bakes; (female) baker</li></ol><p>From <i>bæc-</i> (“bake”) +&lrm; <i>-estre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bakery</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bakahūs</i>, equivalent to <i>&#42;bæc</i> (“something baked”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”). Compare Old English <i>ġebæc</i> (“something baked”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>backbiting, slanderous</li></ol><p>From <i>bæc</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;slitol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to constrain, incite, compel, urge; to demand</li><li>to stain, defile, besmirch</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;baidijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;baidijaną</i> (“to constrain, cause to stay”), causative of <i>&#42;bīdaną</i> (“to wait, guard”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeydʰ-</i> (“persuade, agree”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>bēdian</i> (“to demand”), Old High German <i>beiten</i> (“to urge, pressure, demand”), Old Norse <i>beiða</i> (“demand, long for”), Gothic <i>𐌱𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (baidjan, “to constrain”). More at bide.</p><p>Indo-European cognates include Ancient Greek <i>πείθω</i> (peíthō), Latin <i>fidere</i> (whence English <i>confide</i>), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;běda</i> (“adversity, misery”), Albanian <i>bint</i>.</p><p>Of unknown origin; possibly a ghost word.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a person with ambiguous sexual organs or characteristics</li><li>an effeminate man</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Seemingly cognate with Old High German <i>pad</i> (“hermaphrodite”). Usually taken to be related to (an unattested Old English root of) Middle English <i>badde</i> (“wicked, wretched, bad”). Possibly related to Old English <i>bædan</i> (“to defile”) (although Fulk questions this, and the very existence of the verb has been called into question) and/or to Old English <i>bædling</i> (although the precise meaning of this word is uncertain and it has sometimes been suggested to derive from <i>bedd</i> instead).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beddrida</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a word of uncertain precise meaning, variously interpreted as delicate man, effeminate man, or hermaphrodite</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. Perhaps related to <i>bedd</i> (“bed”), suggesting "a delicate fellow, tenderling, one who lies much in bed", or perhaps to <i>bæddel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>behind</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>behind</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>bēag</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Bavarians</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Bægere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>funeral pyre, bonfire</li><li>fire, flame, blaze</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bāl</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bēlą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰel-</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>bál</i> (whence Icelandic <i>bál</i> (“a fire; a conflagration”), Danish <i>bål</i> (“fire, bonfire, pyre”), Norwegian <i>bål</i> and Swedish <i>bål</i> (“pyre, bonfire”)). Further Indo-European cognates include Sanskrit <i>भाल</i> (bhāla, “splendour”), Ancient Greek <i>φαλός</i> (phalós, “white”) and Old Armenian <i>բալ</i> (bal, “fog”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the blaze of a fire</li></ol><p>From <i>bǣl</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;blys</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>balca</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Bældæġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Bældæġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bieldu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bieldu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>a son of Woden, perhaps to be identified with the god Balder</li></ol><p>From <i>bæl</i> +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i>. Possibly related to <i>bealdor</i> (“prince”), Old Norse <i>Baldr</i> (“Balder”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;baldraz</i>, <i>&#42;Baldraz</i>, derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;balþaz</i> (“bold, brave”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Bældæġ</li></ol><p>From <i>Bældæġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>balefire, funeral fire, sacrificial fire</li></ol><p><i>bǣl</i> +&lrm; <i>fȳr</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bielġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bielġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bone</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>bān</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bare, naked, unconcealed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;baʀ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bazaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰosós</i>.</p><p><i>Germanic cognates:</i> Old Frisian <i>bar</i>, Old Saxon <i>bar</i>, Middle Dutch <i>bar</i>, <i>baer</i> (Dutch <i>bar</i>, <i>baar</i>), Old High German <i>bar</i> (German <i>bar</i>), Old Norse <i>berr</i> (Swedish <i>bar</i>, Norwegian Nynorsk <i>berr</i>).</p><p><i>Indo-European cognates:</i> Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;basas</i> (Lithuanian <i>bãsas</i>, Latvian <i>bass</i>, Russian <i>босо́й</i> (bosój), Polish <i>bosy</i>), Albanian <i>mbath</i> (“I wear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>barefoot</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bazafōts</i>. Equivalent to <i>bær</i> (“bare”) +&lrm; <i>fōt</i> (“foot”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>barley-like</li></ol><p>From <i>bere</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>publicly, openly</li><li>plainly, manifestly</li></ol><p>From <i>bær</i> (“bare, unconcealed”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to burn</li><li>to cauterize</li></ol><p>Metathesis of earlier <i>&#42;brænnan</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;brannijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brannijaną</i>, causative of <i>&#42;brinnaną</i> (Old English <i>birnan</i>).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>berna</i>, Old Saxon <i>brennian</i>, Old Dutch <i>brennen</i>, Old High German <i>brennen</i>, Old Norse <i>brenna</i>, Gothic <i>&#42;𐌱𐍂𐌰𐌽𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;brannjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an instance of combustion or burning</li><li>arson</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perch (fish)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bars</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;barsaz</i> (“perch”, literally “prickly”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bast; inner bark of a tree from which rope is made</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;bast</i> (“bast, rope”). Compare Middle Irish <i>basc</i> (“necklace”), Latin <i>fascis</i> (“bundle”), Albanian <i>bashkë</i> (“tied, linked”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bridle</li><li>to rein in, restrain, curb</li><li>to bait, hunt (with dogs); to hound, worry</li><li>to beat, make way against a current or wind</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;baitijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;baitijaną</i> (“to cause to bite, bridle”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeyd-</i> (“to cleave, split, separate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>bæþhūs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bath</li><li>baptismal font</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;baþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;baþą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bathhouse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;baþahūs</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;baþahūsą</i>. Equivalent to <i>bæþ</i> (“bath”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>beþian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Anglian</i>) alternative form of <i>bēċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bōcere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bēn</i></li></ol></html>:/k/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>the numeral 100 (<i>hundred</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>jackdaw</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung, excrement</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. Perhaps derived from <i>&#42;cacian</i> (“to defecate”), from Latin <i>cacō</i> (“to shit”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outhouse, shithouse</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>cac</i> +&lrm; <i>hūs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, variant of Ceadda</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ċeadda</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cædwalla</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cædwalla</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quick, sharp, prompt, nimble, swift</li><li>bold, brave</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kaibaz</i> (“strong, lively, brave”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alacrity, energy, promptness, boldness</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>cāf</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċiepe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cāfsċiepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cāfsċiepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċeahhettan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Cain (the brother of Abel and the first murderer)</li></ol><p>Derived from Hebrew <i>קַיִן</i> (Káyin, “craftsman”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cabbage, kale, colewort</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>cawel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be or get cold <i>(impersonal, with dative or accusative of person)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kalan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kalaną</i> (“to be cold”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>kala</i> (“to be cold”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caltrop (<i>Centaurea calcitrapa</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċeald</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ċeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>cald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>cald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Calends (“the first day of a month, particularly the first day of a month of the Roman calendar”)</li><li>a month</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>kalendās</i>, accusative plural of <i>kalendae</i> (“first day of a Roman month”), an archaic variant of <i>calandae</i>, from <i>calandus</i> (“which is to be called or announced solemnly”), the future passive participle of <i>calō</i> (“to call, announce solemnly”) (referring to the Roman practice of proclaiming the first days of the lunar month upon seeing the first signs of a new crescent moon), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kelh₁-</i> (“to call, cry, summon”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċealf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be or become cold</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kalōn</i>, variant of <i>&#42;kalēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) bald</li><li>(<i>of plants</i>) bare</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kalu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kalwaz</i> (“bald”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;golHwos</i> (“naked, bald”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>&#42;kalu</i>, Old Saxon <i>kalu</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;kalo</i>, Old High German <i>kalo</i>.</p><p>Latin <i>calvus</i> (“bald”) and Sanskrit <i>कुल्व</i> (kulva, “bald”) are false cognates and derive from a different PIE root.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>calu</i> (“bald”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baldness</li><li>hair loss; alopecia or mange</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kalwā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kalwǭ</i> (“baldness”). Equivalent to <i>calu</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jelly</li><li>pressed curds</li></ol><p>Unknown; possibly from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kalwaz</i> (see <i>sigor</i>, <i>salor</i> or <i>telgor</i> for similar examples), or Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kalwaraz</i>, possibly from a metathetic form of <i>&#42;glew-</i> (“to ball up; clump; stick”). Compare also Swedish <i>kalvdans</i> and Norwegian Nynorsk <i>kalvedans</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) alternative spelling of <i>cwōm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comb</li><li>crest of a helmet, bird, etc</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kamb</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kambaz</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>camb</i>, Dutch <i>kam</i>, Old High German <i>chamb</i> (German <i>Kamm</i>), Old Norse <i>kambr</i> (Swedish <i>kam</i>); and with Ancient Greek <i>γόμφος</i> (gómphos, “pin”), Old Armenian <i>ծամեմ</i> (camem, “to chew”), Russian <i>зуб</i> (zub).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hog fennel</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>combat, battle, warfare</li><li>an enclosed piece of land</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kamp</i>, from Latin <i>campus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warfare</li></ol><p>From <i>camp</i> +&lrm; <i>-dōm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>captain</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>camp</i> (“combat”) +&lrm; <i>ealdor</i> (“leader”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellow soldier, brother-in-arms</li></ol><p>From <i>camp</i> (“combat”) +&lrm; <i>ġefera</i> (“companion”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warfare</li></ol><p>From <i>camp</i> (“combat”) +&lrm; <i>-hād</i> (“-hood”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight, contend against</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kampōn</i>, from <i>&#42;kamp</i> (“battlefield”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>military</li></ol><p>From <i>camp</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state or condition of contest</li><li>contest, war</li></ol><p>From <i>camp</i> +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>battlefield</li></ol><p>From <i>camp</i> +&lrm; <i>stede</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fighting, contest</li></ol><p>From <i>camp</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>army, group of soldiers</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>camp</i> (“combat”) +&lrm; <i>werod</i> (“army”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an overseer of public games; agonothete</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>camp</i> +&lrm; <i>wīsa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) shield</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>camp</i> (“combat”) +&lrm; <i>wudu</i> (“wood”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jeering; scorn; derision</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kank</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kanką</i> (“laughter”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;geng-</i>, <i>&#42;genǵ-</i> (“to mock, insult”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cancer</li><li>crab</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>cancer</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cry out; chatter; mock; deride</li><li>to laugh aloud or in a cackling manner</li></ol><p>From <i>canc</i> +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cackling laughter</li></ol><p>From <i>cancettan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cancettung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>candle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>candēla</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>candel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Candlemas (Christian holiday celebrated on February 2)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>candel</i> (“candle”) +&lrm; <i>mæsse</i> (“mass”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>candlestick</li></ol><p>From <i>candel</i> (“candle”) +&lrm; <i>sticca</i> (“stick”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>candlestick</li></ol><p>From <i>candel</i> (“candle”) +&lrm; <i>stæf</i> (“staff, stick”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>candelabrum</li></ol><p><i>candel</i> (“candle”) +&lrm; <i>trēow</i> (“tree”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a piece of knowledge</li><li>cognizance</li><li>confirmation, averment, a positive assertion</li><li>clearance</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kannō</i> (“knowledge”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kunnaną</i> (“to know how”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵneh₃-</i> (“to know”). Akin to Old Frisian <i>kanna</i>, <i>kena</i> (“recognition, investigation”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kann</i> (“I, he, she can”), first and third person singular present tense of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kunnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a can; cup; krater</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kannǭ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>canon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>canonicus</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cantware</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Canterbury (a city in Kent, England)</li></ol><p>From the genitive of <i>Cantware</i> (“Kent or the people of Kent”) + <i>burg</i> (“city”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cantwara burg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the people of Kent</li><li>Kent</li></ol><p>From <i>Cent</i> +&lrm; <i>-ware</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look; look up, lie on one's back; turn, incline (oneself)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kapēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chapter-house</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chapter (of a book)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>capitulum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a long tunic with a hood; huke</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kappā</i>, from Late Latin <i>cappa</i>. Doublet of <i>cæppe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Cappadocian</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capon (a gelded cockerel)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old French <i>capon</i>, from Latin <i>capo</i> (Late Latin <i>&#42;cappo</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>care, worry</li></ol><p>Origin uncertain. Possibly from an earlier <i>&#42;caruc</i>, equivalent to <i>caru</i> +&lrm; <i>-oc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prison, jail</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>carcer</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anxious</li><li>careful</li><li>mindful</li></ol><p>From <i>caru</i> +&lrm; <i>-ful</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>carful</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>carefully, diligently</li></ol><p>From <i>carful</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to care about</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to care, to feel concern, affection or interest</li><li>to have anxiety</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;karōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;karōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>careworn; strained; anxious; worried; sorrowful; grieving</li><li>dire</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;karag</i>. Equivalent to <i>caru</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a freeman, a man of middle rank or social class (<i>in Norse and Anglo-Saxon society</i>)</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) a man</li><li>(by extension,&#32;in compounds) a male</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>karl</i> (Danish <i>karl</i> (“man”), Swedish <i>karl</i> (“man”)), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;karilaz</i> (“man, male”). Cognate with Old High German <i>karl</i>, <i>karal</i>. Doublet of <i>ċeorl</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Carl)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Charles.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>careless, reckless, without care, free</li></ol><p>From <i>caru</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freedom from care, security, carelessness</li></ol><p>From <i>carlēas</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Big Dipper; the Great Bear, Ursa Major</li></ol><p>From <i>carles</i> (“peasant's”) + <i>wǣn</i> (“wagon”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male bird; cock</li></ol><p>From <i>carl</i> +&lrm; <i>fugol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male; a man</li></ol><p>From <i>carl</i> (“freeman, man, male”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stone, rock</li></ol><p>From Celtic, perhaps from Old Welsh <i>carrecc</i>, from Proto-Brythonic <i>&#42;karreg</i>, from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;karrikā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kh₂er-</i> (“hard”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Carr)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Charmouth (a village in Dorset, England)</li><li>Carhampton (a village in Somerset, England)</li></ol><p>From <i>carr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>of Carthage (an ancient historical city in Tunisia)</li></ol><p>From the oblique cases of Latin <i>Carthāgō</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paper, piece of paper</li><li>document, deed</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>charta</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>χᾰ́ρτης</i> (khắrtēs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worry, anxiety, care</li><li>sorrow, grief</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;karu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;karō</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>kara</i>, Old High German <i>kara</i>, Old Norse <i>kǫr</i> (“sickbed”), Gothic <i>𐌺𐌰𐍂𐌰</i> (kara).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) flood of anxiety or sorrow</li></ol><p><i>caru</i> +&lrm; <i>wielm</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cāsere</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kaisar</i>, from Latin <i>Caesar</i>. The original, older spelling of <i>cāsere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cāsere</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kaisar</i>, from Latin <i>Caesar</i>. The original, older spelling of <i>cāsere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emperor</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kaisar</i>, from Latin <i>Caesar</i>. The original form must have been <i>cāser</i> (attested in the East Anglian royal genealogy and the <i>Rituale Ecclesiae Dunelmensis</i>, and, as <i>cāsaer</i>, in the <i>Liber Vitae Dunelmensis</i>), which is why "empress" is <i>cāseren</i> and not <i>*cāsestre</i>. The final <i>-e</i> was added later by analogy with the suffix <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>empress</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kaisarini</i>. Cognate with German <i>Kaiserin</i>. Equivalent to <i>cāsere</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>imperial</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kaisaralīk</i>. Equivalent to <i>cāsere</i> (“emperor”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cāseren</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Caspian Sea</li><li>Caspia, the region around the Caspian Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>castle</li><li>town or village</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kastell</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cabinet; chest</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kastanārī</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kastō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kasą</i>, <i>&#42;kazą</i> (“vessel, container”). Second element possibly Old English <i>-ere</i>, in same sense as <i>container</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old High German <i>chastanāri</i> (“encloser”), Old High German <i>kasto</i> (“box, case”) (Modern German <i>Kasten</i>), Dutch <i>kast</i> (“cupboard, case”), Gothic <i>𐌺𐌰𐍃</i> (kas, “vessel, container”), Old Norse <i>ker</i> (“vessel, container”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cat</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kattu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kattuz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>katto</i>, Old Norse <i>kǫttr</i>, Old High German <i>kazzo</i>. A related word also existed in the Germanic languages with the feminine gender, represented in Old English by <i>catte</i>. The word appears to be related to Late Latin <i>cattus</i> as well as to similar words in the Slavic and Celtic languages, but the ultimate source is uncertain. See <i>cat</i> for more.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female cat</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kattǭ</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>katte</i>, Old Saxon <i>katta</i>, Middle Dutch <i>katte</i> (Dutch <i>kat</i>), Old High German <i>kazza</i> (German <i>Katze</i>), Old Norse <i>ketta</i> (Swedish <i>katta</i>). The word existed in the Germanic languages in a masculine gender also, represented in Old English by <i>catt</i>. The word appears to be related to Late Latin <i>cattus</i> as well as to similar words in the Slavic and Celtic languages, but the ultimate source is uncertain. See <i>cat</i> for more.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cawel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caxton (a village and civil parish in South Cambridgeshire district, Cambridgeshire, England)</li></ol><p>Recorded in the 1086 Domesday Book. Equivalent to a given name, possibly Old Norse *Kakkr, with genitive <i>-s</i> + <i>tun</i> (“town”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colewort</li><li>cabbage</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kauli</i>, from Latin <i>caulis</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cawelwyrm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caterpillar</li></ol><p>From <i>cawel</i> (“cabbage”) +&lrm; <i>wyrm</i> (“worm”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basket</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>&#42;cavallum</i>, from <i>cavea</i> (“hollow, cavity; cage, enclosure”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caen (a city in Calvados department, Normandy, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old French <i>&#42;Cadom</i> (later <i>Cahom</i>, <i>Caem</i>), likely from Latin <i>&#42;Catumagus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jug; pitcher</li><li>basin; laver</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>caucus</i> (“drinking vessel”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) cheekbone</li><li>jaw, jawbone</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kākabain</i> (“cheekbone, jawbone”), equivalent to <i>ċēac</i> +&lrm; <i>bān</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>cakebeen</i> (“jawbone”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jaw; jawbone</li><li>cheek</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kākā</i> (“jaw, cheek”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) broom (plant) or gorse</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;ċeaga</i> +&lrm; <i>-ga</i> (diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, possibly a hypocorism of a name such as <i>Cædmon</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Brythonic <i>&#42;kad</i> (“battle”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Ċeadda</li></ol><p>From <i>Ċeadda</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cædwalla</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cædwalla</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cædwalla</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chaff</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kaf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) jaw; cheek</li><li>a bill; beak; snout</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kafl</i> (“jaw, cheek”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beetle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kafr</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;kefrō</i> (“beetle”). Cognate with Old High German <i>kefur</i>, <i>kefar</i> (“beetle”) (whence modern German <i>Käfer</i> (“beetle”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċeacga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jackdaw, chough</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kahu</i> (“jackdaw, crow”), ultimately imitative.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laugh loudly; laugh loud or in a cackling manner</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kahhattjan</i> (“to laugh loudly, cackle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plaster, mortar</li><li>chalk</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kalk</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a settlement in the hundred of Ossulstone, Middlesex, England; modern Chelsea &lsqb;785&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>ċealc</i> (“chalk”) +&lrm; <i>hȳþ</i> (“harbor”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chalk, chalkstone</li></ol><p>From <i>ċealc</i> (“chalk”) +&lrm; <i>stān</i> (“stone”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cold</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cold, coldness</li><li>that which is cold</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kald</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>kald</i>, Old Saxon <i>kald</i>, Old Dutch <i>kalt</i>, Old High German <i>kalt</i>, Old Norse <i>kaldr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌺𐌰𐌻𐌳𐍃</i> (kalds).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kald</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kaldą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>coldhearted</li></ol><p>From <i>ċeald</i> (“cold”) +&lrm; <i>-heort</i> (“hearted”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become cold, to chill</li></ol><p>From <i>ċeald</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coldness, cold</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kaldanassī</i>, equivalent to <i>ċeald</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċealdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ċeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ċeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calf (young cow)</li><li>veal</li><li>fawn</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kalbaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>&#42;kalf</i>, Old Saxon <i>kalf</i>, Old Dutch <i>kalf</i>, Old High German <i>kalb</i>. Also Old Norse <i>kalfr</i>, which was a masculine a-stem, and Gothic <i>𐌺𐌰𐌻𐌱𐍉</i> (kalbō), which was a feminine ōn-stem.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to calve, <i>(of a cow or deer)</i> to give birth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kalbōn</i>. Cognate with Old Dutch <i>&#42;kalvon</i> and Old High German <i>&#42;kalbōn</i>. Equivalent to <i>ċealf</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cow barn for calves</li></ol><p>Literally "house of calves," more idiomatically "calf house." Compare <i>hunda hūs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call</li><li>to cry out, shout</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kalzōną</i> (“to call, shout”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gels-</i> (“voice, cry”), perhaps via Old Norse <i>kalla</i> (“to call”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>kella</i> (“to call, name”), Old High German <i>kallōn</i> (“to speak loudly”), Old Norse <i>kalla</i> (“to call, name, shout”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>calwer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cattle, any commodity</li><li>sale</li><li>trade</li><li>bargain</li><li>price</li><li>property</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kaup</i> (“trade, purchase”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kaupōn</i> (“to buy”). Cognate with Old High German <i>koufōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċēapmann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>businessman, merchant</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kaupamann</i>; equivalent to <i>ċēap</i> +&lrm; <i>mann</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>merchant ship, trading ship</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kaupaskip</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>koufskif</i>. Equivalent to <i>ċēap</i> +&lrm; <i>sċip</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marketplace</li></ol><p>From <i>ċēap</i> +&lrm; <i>stōw</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>business, commerce, trade</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ċēapian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>carcern</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chatter, creak, crash</li><li>to gnash</li></ol><p>From a metathetic alteration of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;krakōn</i>. Doublet of <i>cracian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċearcian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ċerdic</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cariġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>carful</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>carfullīċe</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ċearful</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a dire or horrible enchantment</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ċearu</i> (“worry, anxiety”) +&lrm; <i>ġealdor</i> (“spell”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fearful ghost or spirit</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ċearu</i> (“worry, anxiety”) +&lrm; <i>gæst</i> (“spirit”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take care</li><li>to heed</li><li>to be anxious, sorry</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ċearu</i> (“care, concern”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive marker)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cariġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċyrm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>caru</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) sad home, dwelling of sorrow</li></ol><p>From <i>ċearu</i> +&lrm; <i>seld</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>caru</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>carwielm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dispute; quarrel</li><li>contention; strife</li><li>chiding; rebuke; reproof</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kausō</i> (“dispute, litigation”), from Latin <i>causa</i> (“reason, cause, case, dispute, reproach”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>kāse</i> (“lawsuit, case”), Old High German <i>kōsa</i> (“lawsuit, case”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>election; choice</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kaus-</i> (from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;keusaną</i> (“to choose”)) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strife, contention</li><li>sedition</li><li>murmuring</li><li>scandal</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Likely from <i>ċēas</i> (“strife”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fortress, fort</li><li>fortified settlement, town, city</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>castra</i> (“camp”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Ceaster)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chester (a city in Cheshire, England)</li></ol><p>From the common noun <i>ċeaster</i>, or directly from Latin <i>castrum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a citizen, townsperson</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the people of a city, the citizenry</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċeaster</i> +&lrm; <i>lēod</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cheshire (a historical county of England)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ċeaster</i> (“Chester”) +&lrm; <i>sċir</i> (“shire”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Primitive Irish <i>ᚉᚐᚈᚈᚒ</i> (cattu, “battle”), cognate with <i>Ceadda</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cawel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ċeawlin</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cawel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Ceawlin of Wessex</li></ol><p>From a Primitive Irish name, an earlier form of Old Irish <i>cuilén</i> (“pup, cub”) or <i>cuilenn</i> (“holly”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Ċeawlin</li></ol><p>From <i>Ċeawlin</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċēace</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>&#42;cēċel</i></li></ol><p>Diminutive of an unattested &#42;coc-. Possibly cognate with Dutch <i>koek</i> and German <i>Kuchen</i>, the latter's oldest form having a long root vowel like cecil and &#42;coc-. If so, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kōkô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) mercury (plant) (<i>Mercurialis perennis</i>)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) charlock (<i>Rhamphospermum arvense</i>, syns. <i>Sinapis arvensis</i>, etc.)</li></ol><p>Unknown, but apparently related to Low German <i>Ködik</i>, <i>Köddik</i> (“charlock”). Compare also Middle Low German <i>hēderik</i>, <i>hederik</i>, <i>hedderik</i> (“charlock”), Old High German <i>hederīh</i> (“hedge mustard, charlock”), possibly from Latin <i>hederāceus</i> (“made of ivy, ivy-like”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the cedar tree</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;cedru</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cedar</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;cedrubaum</i>. Equivalent to <i>ċeder</i> +&lrm; <i>bēam</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cedar tree</li></ol><p>From <i>ċeder</i> +&lrm; <i>trēow</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċedertrēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cedar</li></ol><p>From <i>ċeder</i> +&lrm; <i>-isc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċīeġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċēġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ċeġhām</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cheam (a suburban village in the borough of Sutton, Greater London).</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cheam (a suburban village in the borough of Sutton, Greater London).</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cool, make cool</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be cool, become cool</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kōlijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiele</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kaliz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċealf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hired man, servant, hireling</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;celmert</i> +&lrm; <i>mann</i>, from Latin <i>collibertus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son or descendant of Celm</li></ol><p>From <i>Celm</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>cōlnes</i></li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;cēl</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comb</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kambijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kambijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shirt</li><li>linen nightgown, chemise</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kamisi</i>, from Latin <i>camīsia</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hamiþi</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>kemis</i> (“tunic, skirt, dress”). Doublet of <i>hemeþe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soldier, warrior</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kampijō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soldier (female)</li></ol><p>Either from <i>camp</i> +&lrm; <i>-estre</i> or <i>cempa</i> +&lrm; <i>-estre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) torch</li><li>the runic character ᚳ (/k/ or /t͡ʃ/)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kēn</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Cen)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bold</li><li>valiant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kōniz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>kuoni</i>, Old Norse <i>kænn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mustache</li><li>the bit of a bridle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kanip</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kanipaz</i>. Akin to Old Frisian <i>kenep</i>, Old Norse <i>kampr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>King Cenfus of Wessex</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cyning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>keenly, boldly, notably, courageously; doughtily, stoutheartedly</li></ol><p>From <i>cēne</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make known, declare</li><li>to produce, bring forth, declare</li><li>to give birth to (a child), to deliver, to bear</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kannijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kannijaną</i>, causative form of <i>&#42;kunnaną</i> (“to know”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>kennian</i> (Dutch <i>kennen</i>), Old Frisian <i>kenna</i>, Old High German <i>kennen</i> (German <i>kennen</i>), Old Norse <i>kenna</i> (Danish <i>kende</i>, spelling before the writing reform of 1948: <i>kjende</i>), Gothic <i>𐌺𐌰𐌽𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (kannjan, “to declare”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kannjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kanjaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵonh₁éyeti</i>, the causative formation of the root <i>&#42;ǵenh₁-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parent</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>cennan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birth</li></ol><p>From <i>cennan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birthplace</li></ol><p>From <i>cenning</i> +&lrm; <i>stow</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birthtime</li></ol><p>From <i>cenning</i> +&lrm; <i>tīd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kōnirād</i>. Equivalent to <i>cēne</i> (“bold”) +&lrm; <i>rǣd</i> (“advice, counsel”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Cenrēd</li></ol><p>From <i>Cenrēd</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kōnirīk</i>, from <i>&#42;kōnī</i> (“bold”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;rīk</i> (“king, ruler”). Equivalent to <i>cēne</i> +&lrm; <i>rīce</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Chuonerīh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cenrēd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kent (a historic region of Britain)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Cantia</i>, from a Proto-Celtic source probably meaning 'border' or 'coastal region'.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the herb centaury</li></ol><p>From Medieval Latin <i>centaurēa</i>, <i>centauria</i>, from Latin <i>centaurēum</i>, <i>centaurion</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>κενταύρειον</i> (kentaúreion, “several plants related to <i>Centaurea</i>”). Compare Dutch <i>centaurie</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the people of Kent; the Kentish</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Cent</i> (“Kent”) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“belonging to, of”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Kentish</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) someone from Kent</li></ol><p><i>Cent</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kentish land, Kent</li></ol><p><i>Cent</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Centland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the kingdom of Kent</li></ol><p>From <i>Cent</i> (“Kent”) +&lrm; <i>rīċe</i> (“kingdom”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>Cent</i> (“Kent”) + <i>wald</i> (“power, authority”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Centwine of Wessex; Anglo-Saxon King of Wessex in the 7th Century.</li></ol><p>From <i>Cent</i> (“Kent”) + <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cēnwealh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kōniwald</i>, from <i>&#42;kōnī</i> (“bold”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wald</i> (“might, power”). Equivalent to <i>cēne</i> +&lrm; <i>weald</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Conald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Cenwalh of Wessex; an Anglo-Saxon King of Wessex.</li></ol><p>From <i>cēne</i> (“bold, valiant”) and <i>wealh</i> (“Celt, Welsh person”).</p><p>From <i>cyne-</i> (“royal”) and <i>wealh</i> (“Celt, Welsh person”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boldness; bravery; keenness</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kōniþō</i>, equivalent to <i>cēne</i> +&lrm; <i>-þu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a chough, a bird of the genus <i>Corvus</i>; a jay; crow; jackdaw</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kahwu</i>, probably ultimately imitative.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheek or cheekbone</li><li>jaw or jawbone</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;keukā</i> (“jaw, cheek”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pouch, bag</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;keud</i>, <i>&#42;keudu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;keudō</i> (“bag, pouch”). Cognate with Old High German <i>chiot</i>, <i>kiot</i> (“pouch”), Old English <i>codd</i> (“cod, husk, bag, scrotum”). More at cod.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċederbēam</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cheddar (a large village and civil parish of Somerset, England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ship (specifically a small flat-bottomed boat)</li><li>keel of a ship</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;keulaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gawl-</i> (“ball, swelling”). Displaced by Middle English <i>kele</i>, possibly from or related to Middle Dutch <i>kiel</i> (“keel”) cognate with Old Norse <i>kjǫlr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;keluz</i>, a related root. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>kiol</i> (“boat”), Old High German <i>kiol</i> (“boat”), Old Norse <i>kjóll</i> (“ship”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Ceol)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ċēol</i> (“ship”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ċēol</i> (“ship”) + <i>beald</i> (“brave, bold”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ċēol</i> (“ship”) and <i>berht</i> (“bright, clear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ċēol</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>burg</i> (“fortification”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ċēolburg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throat</li><li>jowl</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kelā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>St. Ceolfrith</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ċeol</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>friþ</i> (“peace”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ċēol</i> (“ship”) + <i>here</i> (“army”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ċēol</i> (“ship”) and <i>mund</i> (“protector”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ċēol</i> (“ship”) and <i>nōþ</i> (“courage”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throat, collar</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ċeole</i> (“throat”) +&lrm; <i>-or</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ċēolrǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ċēol</i> (“ship, keel”) and <i>rīċ</i> (“king, ruler”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ċēol</i> (“ship”) and <i>rǣd</i> (“counsel, advice”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ċēol</i> (“ship, keel”) and <i>siġe</i> (“victory”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ċēolwulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Ċēolwulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Ceolwald of Mercia</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ċeol</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>weald</i> (“power”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Ċeolwald</li></ol><p>From <i>Ċeolwald</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ċēol</i> (“ship”) and <i>weard</i> (“guard”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>ċēol</i> (“ship, keel”) + <i>wīġ</i> (“war, battle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ċēol</i> (“ship”) and <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ċēol</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>wynn</i> (“joy”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ċēol</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>þrȳþ</i> (“might”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kerban</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kerbaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to murmur; complain (with or without just cause)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;karēn</i> (“to complain, grieve, mourn, care”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵeh₂r-</i> (“voice, exclamation”). Cognate with Middle High German <i>karn</i> (“to mourn, groan, complain”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) a churl, a freeman ranked below a thane but above a thrall</li><li>man</li><li>peasant</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;karil</i>, <i>&#42;karul</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;karilaz</i>, <i>&#42;karulaz</i> (“man”), akin to Old Frisian <i>tzerl</i>, <i>tzirl</i> (West Frisian <i>tsjirl</i>), Old Norse <i>karl</i> (Danish/Norwegian/Swedish/Danish <i>karl</i>), Old High German <i>karal</i>, Middle Low German <i>kerl</i> (German <i>Kerl</i>). Doublet of <i>carl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>low-born, not noble; common, country or free-born</li></ol><p>From <i>ċeorl</i> (“peasant”) +&lrm; <i>boren</i> (“born”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the common people</li><li>the public</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċeorl</i> +&lrm; <i>folc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to marry (a man)</li></ol><p>From <i>ċeorl</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>churlish, rustic, common</li></ol><p>From <i>ċeorl</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>churlishness, clownishness, rudeness, vulgarity</li></ol><p>From <i>ċeorlisċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without a husband</li></ol><p>Equivalent of <i>ċeorl</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċyrm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to creak</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kerʀan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kerzaną</i> (“to creak”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chertsey, a village in Surrey, England</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ċeortes īeġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>complaint, murmuring</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ċeorian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choose, select</li><li>to elect</li><li>to accept</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;keusan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;keusaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵews-</i> (“taste, choose”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>kiāsa</i>, Old Saxon <i>kiosan</i>, Old High German <i>kiosan</i>, Old Norse <i>kjósa</i>, Gothic <i>𐌺𐌹𐌿𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (kiusan). The Indo-European root was also the source of Latin <i>gustus</i> (“taste”), Albanian <i>desha</i> (“chosen”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċeosol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċeosolstān</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gravel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kesul</i> (“small stone, pebble”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gravel</li><li>sandstone</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kesulastain</i>, equivalent to <i>ċeosol</i> +&lrm; <i>stān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>choosing, choice</li></ol><p>From <i>ċēosan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chew</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;keuwan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kewwaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵyewh₁-</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>kiuwan</i> (German <i>kauen</i>), and also Old Norse <i>tyggva</i>, <i>tyggja</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a chewing</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ċēowan</i> (“to chew”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep (an eye on); observe; regard</li><li>to keep (stock of)</li><li>to keep (to follow a rule); betake oneself to; meditate</li><li>to await; bear</li><li>to desire; take</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kōpijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kōpijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cephas, a name for the apostle Peter</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Cēphās</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Κηφᾶς</i> (Kēphâs), from Aramaic <i>כֵּיפָא</i> (kēp̄ā, “stone, rock”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-Brythonic <i>&#42;Karadọg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Ċerdic</li></ol><p>From <i>Ċerdic</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiern</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chervil (<i>Anthriscus cerefolium</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċyrlic</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċierr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ċeortes īeġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cherub, cherubim</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>cherūbīn</i>, <i>cherūbīm</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>χερουβίν</i> (kheroubín), <i>χερουβείν</i> (kheroubeín), <i>χερουβίμ</i> (kheroubím), from Hebrew <i>כְּרוּבִים</i> (k'ruvím);.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cerubim</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cerubim</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċīese</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċist</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Anglian</i>) alternative form of <i>ċȳte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċietel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cheddington (a village and civil parish in Buckinghamshire, England).</li></ol><p>Attested in the 1086 Domesday Book. Equivalent to an unattested name *Cetta + <i>tūn</i> (“town”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the village of Catterick</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Chaldea (an ancient geographic region of West Asia, in the southern portion of Babylonia in modern eastern Iraq and southwestern Iran, lying chiefly on the right bank of the Euphrates, but commonly used to refer to the whole of the Mesopotamian plain)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cerubim</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċicen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċicen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cyċel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chicken</li><li>alternative form of <i>cyċene</i></li></ol><p>Unclear. Possibly from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kiukīn</i>, with gradual shortening of the first syllable vowel; or alternatively from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kukkīną</i>, equivalent to <i>cocc</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chide, rebuke</li><li>to disapprove, criticize</li><li>to complain, grumble, blame</li><li>to dispute, argue, debate, quarrel</li></ol><p>Origin unclear, no real cognates found in other Germanic languages. May be related to Middle High German <i>erkīden</i> (“to sprout”), German <i>kiden</i> (“to sound”); Middle High German <i>kīdel</i> (“wedge”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gill</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kijō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concubine</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kabisi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kabisjō</i> (“concubine”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concubine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kabisjǭ</i> (“concubine”). Of uncertain origin. Cognate with Old High German <i>kebisa</i>, <i>chebis</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concubinage</li></ol><p>From <i>ċiefes</i> +&lrm; <i>-hād</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call; name</li><li>to call upon, invoke, summon, call together</li><li>to cry, cry out; call out</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kauwjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kawjaną</i> (“to call, name”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gewH-</i> (“to call, cry out”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a naming; name</li></ol><p>From <i>ċīeġan</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spring</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kaldijā</i>. Found in place names such as <i>Bapchild</i> (Baccancelde). Dialectical Northern English <i>keld</i> may be from Old Norse, whence <i>Halikeld</i>, <i>Salkeld</i>, <i>Kildwick</i> and <i>Threlkeld</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cold, coldness</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kaldī</i>, equivalent to <i>ċeald</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cold; coldness</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kali</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kaliz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>icicle</li></ol><p>From <i>ċiele</i> +&lrm; <i>ġiċel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a chapman, merchant, trader</li></ol><p>From <i>ċīepan</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>. Compare Old High German <i>koufo</i> (“merchant, trader”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sell</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) to buy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kaupijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>for sale</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>onion (<i>Allium cepa</i>)</li><li>shallot (<i>Allium cepa </i>var.<i> aggregatum</i>)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċēap</i> (“cost”) + <i>-e</i> (“having”). Corresponds to a Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kaupijaz</i>, though there are no cognates.</p><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>cēpa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bargaining, setting a price, marketing, commerce</li><li>marketplace</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ċīepan</i> (“to sell”) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċeorlisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) churn</li><li>carenum (boiled wine)</li></ol><p><i>The <b>spelling of this entry has been normalized</b>&nbsp;according to the principles established by Wiktionary's editor community or recent spelling standards of the language.</i></p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kirnijō</i>.</p><p>From Latin <i>carēnum</i>, <i>caroenum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a turn; an interval of time</li><li>an occasion</li><li>business; an affair</li></ol><p>From <i>cierran</i> (“to turn”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to turn; to make move</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to turn</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kaʀʀjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kazjaną</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;kaizijaną</i>, <i>&#42;kaizwijaną</i> (“to turn”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;goyswéye-</i> (“to bend, turn”). Akin to Old Saxon <i>kērian</i>, Old High German <i>chēran</i> (“to turn”) (German <i>kehren</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>choosy; dainty or fussy (in eating)</li></ol><p>Apparently from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kiusi</i> (compare <i>&#42;keusan</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheese</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kāsī</i>, borrowed from Latin <i>cāseus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rennet</li></ol><p>From <i>ċīese</i> (“cheese”) +&lrm; <i>lybb</i> (“drug”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cheese Week; the last week of eating cheese before Lent</li></ol><p><i>ċīese</i> (“cheese”) +&lrm; <i>wice</i> (“week”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheesemaker</li></ol><p><i>ċīese</i> (“cheese”) +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“maker”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kettle, cauldron</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;katil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;katilaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiefes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiefes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċīeġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċīġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chilmark (a village and civil parish in south-west Wiltshire, England)</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;ċiġel</i> (“pole, post”) +&lrm; <i>mearc</i> (“mark, boundary”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>child</li><li>baby</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kilþ</i>, <i>&#42;kelþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kelþaz</i> (“womb; fetus”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Childermas</li></ol><p><i>ċilda</i> (“children's”) +&lrm; <i>mæsse</i> (“-mas”) +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i> (“day”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swaddling cloth; <i>(in the plural)</i>, swaddling clothes</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>ċild</i> (“baby”) +&lrm; <i>clāþ</i> (“cloth”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a child's cradle</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ċild</i> (“child”) +&lrm; <i>cradol</i> (“cradle”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nursing</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>ċild</i> (“baby”) +&lrm; <i>fēdende</i> (“feeding”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>childhood</li></ol><p>From <i>ċild</i> +&lrm; <i>-hād</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>placenta</li><li>womb</li></ol><p>From <i>ċild</i> +&lrm; <i>hama</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>childish</li></ol><p>From <i>ċild</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>childish, childlike</li></ol><p>From <i>ċild</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be cold</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kilēn</i>, equivalent to <i>ċiele</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cymbal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>cymbalum</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cimbal</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to join</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kimbaną</i> (“to press together”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gem-</i> (“to grab; hold; press; pack; stuff; cram”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċinn</i>: chin</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burst; split into pieces; break into chinks</li><li>to gape; yawn</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kīnan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kīnaną</i> (“to split; crack; germinate; sprout”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jawbone, chinbone</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċinn</i> (“chin”) +&lrm; <i>bān</i> (“bone”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċincung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a loud or cackling laughter</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;ċincian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cyning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cynges tūn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kingsbury (a suburb in the borough of Brent, Greater London, England)</li></ol><p><i>cyninges</i> (“king's”) +&lrm; <i>burg</i> (“fort, city”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cyning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chin</li><li>alternative form of <i>cynn</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kinnu</i> (“chin”).</p><p>Compare Old Frisian <i>zin</i>, Old Saxon, Old Dutch, and Old High German <i>kinni</i>, Old Norse <i>kinn</i>, Gothic <i>𐌺𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌿𐍃</i> (kinnus) and Latin <i>gena</i>, Ancient Greek <i>γένυς</i> (génus), Welsh <i>gen</i>, Tocharian A <i>śanweṃ</i>, Old Armenian <i>ծնաւտ</i> (cnawt), Lithuanian <i>žandas</i>, Persian چانه (čâne), Sanskrit <i>हनु</i> (hánu).</p><p>See <i>cynn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cynrīc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chink, gap, fissure</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kinu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kinō</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċēo</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċeorl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċipp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċīepan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leek</li></ol><p><i>ċīepe</i> (“onion”) +&lrm; <i>lēac</i> (“leek”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small piece of wood; a shaving; chip</li><li>log, beam; trunk</li><li>plough-share, colter</li><li>weaver’s beam</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>&#42;ċippian</i> ("to chip&#59; chop off in pieces"&#59; compare <i>forċippian</i>), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kippōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kipp-</i> (“to chop; split”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵey-</i> (“to split; divide; germinate; sprout”). Cognate with Middle Low German <i>kēp</i> (“notch, nick, cut”), Dutch <i>keep</i> (“notch, nick, score”), Middle Dutch <i>kippen</i> (“to chip, hatch”), Middle Low German <i>kippen</i> (“to chip, hatch”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kipp</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kippaz</i>, <i>&#42;kipaz</i> (“log, beam, fetter, foot-block”), of uncertain origin. Often aligned with Latin <i>cippus</i>, <i>cīpus</i> (“post, stake, pointed column made of wood or stone”), though borrowing from the Latin is inconclusive. The Germanic and Latin terms may have been independent borrowings from a third unknown source language.</p><p>Akin to Old Saxon <i>kipp</i>, <i>kip</i> (“stick, post”), Old Dutch <i>kip</i> (“fetter”), Old High German <i>kipf</i> (“axle, stave”), Old Norse <i>keppr</i> (“cudgel, club”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chippenham, a village in Wiltshire, England</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a roaring sound, as of waves crashing</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from an earlier <i>&#42;cerċan</i>, a metathetic variant of <i>&#42;creċċan</i>, <i>&#42;cræċċan</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;krakkjan</i>. If so, then cognate with Old High German <i>krehhen</i> (“to shake, shatter, break”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċġeorn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċhālgung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċnytt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċsċeatt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċsteall</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circle</li><li>zodiac</li><li>cycle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>circulus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circle-disease; shingles</li></ol><p>From <i>circul</i> +&lrm; <i>ādl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the zodiac</li></ol><p>From <i>circul</i> (“circle”) +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċþing</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cirencester, a town in England</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church bell</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>belle</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church-book</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>bōc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church-repair</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>bōt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church-breach, a breaking into a church</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>bryċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breaking into a church</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>brǣċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chirbury, a village in Shropshire</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> (“church”) +&lrm; <i>burh</i> (“fort, town”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ċiriċburg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church door or gate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>dōr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kirikā</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>κυριακόν (δῶμα)</i> (kuriakón (dôma), “the Lord's (house)”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an ecclesiastic, a cleric</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church-peace, right of sanctuary</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>friþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church-help, ecclesiastical support</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>fultum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church-going</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>gang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church-communion, membership of a church</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>ġemāna</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diligent in attending church</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeorn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċġeorn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church-due</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeriht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church-peace</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>griþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holy order, order of the church, church-degree</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>hād</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dedication of a church</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>hālgung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an enemy of the church, persecutor</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>hata</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hill on or by which a church stands</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>hyll</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church land, land belonging to a church</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) church; ecclesiastical, ecclesiastic</li></ol><p>From <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>simony</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>mangung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church-measure</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>mitta</i> (“measure, bushel”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dedication of a church</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>mǣrsung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church duty or service</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>nytt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church path</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>pæþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church lichen or moss</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>ragu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sacrilege</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>rān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church-song</li><li>church-singing</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>sang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church singer</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>sangere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church-scot, that is a tax in the form of agricultural commodities collected by the church</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>sċeatt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>work connected with grain contributed through a tax to the church</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċsċeatt</i> (“church-scot”) +&lrm; <i>weorc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>going to church, church attendance</li><li>seeking of a church for protection or as sanctuary</li><li>territory of a church; parish</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>sōcn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the site of a church</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>steall</i>. Compare to Kirkstall.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the site of a church</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>stede</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church-path</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>stīġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church-time, time for service in a church</li></ol><p><i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>tīd</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>churchyard, cemetery</li></ol><p>From <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>tūn</i>. Piecewise doublet of <i>Kyrchetūn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church-wall</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>wāg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church-congregation</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>waru</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>churchwarden</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>weard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>road to a church</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>weġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church-dwelling, sacristy</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>wīċa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church-watch</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>wæċċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church-garment, vestment</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>wǣd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church-marriage</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>ǣwe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minister of a church</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>þeġn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church service</li></ol><p><i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>þeġnung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċþeġn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċþeġnung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a thing belonging to the church</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>þing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a priest</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ċiriċe</i> +&lrm; <i>þingere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċirse</i> (appears in compounds)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċyrlic</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċeorlisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċyrm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to make a noise, chirm, cry out, shout</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;karmijan</i>; equivalent to <i>ċyrm</i> (“noise”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċierr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) cherry</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kirsijā</i>, a borrowing from Vulgar Latin <i>ceresia</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċīes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċīese</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċeosol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċeosol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the city of Chichester</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>box, chest</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kistu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chestnut</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kastannjā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċietel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tickle</li></ol><p>Late Old English, probably borrowed from Old Norse <i>kitla</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kitilōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tickling</li></ol><p>From <i>citelian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċēowung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a young shoot of an herb or tree; a chit; sprout; germ; sprig; mote</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kīþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kīþą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clæclēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the din of battle; battle</li><li>hurt; harm; injury</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klaku</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klakō</i> (“din, noise”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;glag-</i> (“to make a sound”). Cognate with Middle High German <i>klac</i> (“a bang, crack, split”), Old Norse <i>klaka</i> (“chirping, cackling”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clǣfre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mud</li><li>bond, fetter, grip, grasp</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klaim</i> (“mortar, clay”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clǣne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) clotbur, burdock</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) goosegrass</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) clivers</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klaitā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cloister</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>claustrum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to claw or scratch</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klawjaną</i> (“to claw, scratch”). Cognate with Old Dutch <i>&#42;clōien</i>, &#42;clouen; Middle Dutch <i>clôyen</i>, clooien, clouwen; Dutch <i>klauwen</i> (<i>remodelled after the noun</i>) klooien; Old Saxon <i>&#42;clawian</i>; Middle Low German <i>klâwen</i>; Old High German <i>klawēn</i>, chlawēn, klawjan; Middle High German <i>kleuen</i>, klæwen; German <i>klauen</i> (<i>remodelled after the noun (compare dialectal or obsolete kläuen</i>)); Old Norse <i>klá</i>, kleyja; Faroese <i>kláa</i>; Icelandic <i>klá</i>; Norwegian Nynorsk <i>klå</i>; Old Swedish <i>klā</i>; Swedish <i>klå</i>.</p><p>From the same source as clawan.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clawan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>claw</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klawu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klawō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloth</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) clothes</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klaiþą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>claw, nail</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klau</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;klawu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stepping-stone</li></ol><p>Unknown. Possibly related to Old English <i>clēacian</i> (“to go nimbly, hurry”), or to Old English <i>&#42;clacian</i> (“to clack”), referring to the sound made by stones wobbling. More at <i>clēacian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go nimbly, hurry, scramble</li></ol><p>Unknown. No cognates in other languages. Perhaps from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klaukōn</i>, a derivative of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klauwjan</i> (“to claw, scratch”) + <i>&#42;-ukōn</i> (frequentative suffix). If so, equivalent to <i>clawan</i> +&lrm; <i>-cian</i>. Alternatively, perhaps a corruption of <i>tealtian</i> (“to shake, be unsteady”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is cloven or separated</li><li>a cleft, chasm</li><li>den; chamber, cubicle, cell; compartment</li><li>a room</li><li>a cellar</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klebô</i> (“chamber, cell”), related to the Old English verb <i>clēofan</i> (“to separate, split, divide”). More at cleave.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press; to squeeze or squash</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to surround, to encircle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klammjan</i> (“to squeeze”). Cognate with Old High German <i>klemmen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clinch, hold fast</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exhilarate</li><li>to adhere, remain</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klangijaną</i>, causative of <i>&#42;klinganą</i> (“to stick; adhere”) (which became Old English <i>clingan</i>). Compare <i>beclenċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clif</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clēafa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cleave, split</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kleuban</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kleubaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;glewbʰ-</i> (“cut, carve”).</p><p>Germanic cognates include Old Saxon <i>kliovan</i>, Middle Dutch <i>clieven</i> (Dutch <i>klieven</i>), Old High German <i>klioban</i>, Old Norse <i>kljúfa</i>. The Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek <i>γλύφω</i> (glúphō, “carve”), Latin <i>glūbō</i> (“strip the bark off a tree”), Russian <i>глубо́кий</i> (glubókij, “deep”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Clofeshōh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clifian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clipian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small piece of cloth or leather; a patch on a garment</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kleut</i>, <i>&#42;klūt</i>, apparently from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kleutaz</i>, <i>&#42;klūtaz</i>; related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klautaz</i>. Doublet of <i>clūt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clīewen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clīþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clīþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clipian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clergyman, clerk</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>clēricus</i> (“clergyman, priest”), from Ancient Greek <i>κληρικός</i> (klērikós).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sticky, adhesive</li><li>clinging</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klibr</i>, presumably from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klibraz</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gleybʰ-</i> (“to stick, adhere”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clifiht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sphere, ball; skein</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kliuwīn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kliuwīną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cliff; a group of rocks or crag</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klib</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klibą</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>klif</i>, Middle Dutch <i>clif</i>, <i>clef</i> (Dutch <i>klif</i>), Old High German <i>kleb</i>, Old Norse <i>klif</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cleave, stick to</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klīban</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klībaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) burdock</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klībā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>claw, talon</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick (adhere)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klibēn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klibāną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cliffy, steep</li></ol><p>From <i>clif</i> +&lrm; <i>-iht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rock</li></ol><p><i>clif</i> +&lrm; <i>stān</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to climb</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klimban</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>klimban</i>, Old High German <i>klimban</i> (German <i>klimmen</i>), Dutch <i>klimmen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wither, pine, shrink up, shrink together from heat or cold</li><li>to cling</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klinganą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clēafa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clipian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clīewen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a vocal sound; to call, call out, cry; to address, invoke, appeal</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klipēn</i>, <i>&#42;klapēn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klipjaną</i>, <i>&#42;klapjaną</i> (“to be noisy, chatter”), possibly related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klapōną</i>, <i>&#42;klappōną</i> (“to make a sound”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>kleppa</i>, <i>klippa</i> (“to sound”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vocalic</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) vocative</li></ol><p>From the present participle of <i>clipian</i> (“to speak, summon, invoke”) + <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clipiendlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sounding</li><li>vocal, vocalic</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) vowel</li></ol><p>From <i>clipian</i> (“to speak, cry out, call, summon, invoke”) +&lrm; <i>-ol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) vowel; vowel sound</li></ol><p>From <i>clipol</i> (“vocalic”) +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) diphthong; combination of vowels or vowel sounds</li></ol><p>From <i>clipola</i> (“vowel”) +&lrm; <i>ġefēġ</i> (“joining, combination”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clif</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clīewen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clīþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plaster, salve, poultice</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick, cleave, adhere</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klīþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cluck</li><li>to sigh</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klukkwōn</i> (“to cluck”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>definition uncertain</i>) probably a type of thrush, more specifically, the fieldfare (<i>Turdus pilaris</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;clod</i> +&lrm; <i>hamer</i> (“bunting”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a location in England, mentioned in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle as the site of a synod in 822</li></ol><p>The second element is from <i>hōh</i> (“promontory”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Clofeshōh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>clam</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mass, lump</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klott</i> (“lump”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bell</li></ol><p>From Medieval Latin <i>clocca</i>, from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;klokkos</i> (“bell”) (compare Welsh <i>cloch</i>, Old Irish <i>cloc</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stone; rock; boulder</li><li>hill</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klūt</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klūtaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rocky, stony</li><li>hilly</li></ol><p>From <i>clūd</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clove (<i>of garlic</i>)</li><li>a bulb or tuber (<i>of a plant</i>)</li><li>an ear (<i>of corn</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klubu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klubō</i>, from the root of <i>&#42;kleubaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buttercup</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clucge</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clūse</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bar, bolt</li><li>enclosure</li><li>a narrow pass or passageway</li><li>cell; prison</li><li>dam</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klūsā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clyster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lock, bar, barrier, cell</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>claustrum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small piece of cloth or leather, rag, patch</li><li>a piece of metal, plate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klūt</i>, <i>&#42;kleut</i>, apparently from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klūtaz</i>, <i>&#42;kleutaz</i>, related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klautaz</i>. Doublet of <i>clēot</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, incurve</li><li>to clutch, clench</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klukjaną</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klu-</i> (“to ball up”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;glew-</i> (“to ball up; lump, mass”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clēafa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>claw-footed, taloned</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>clifer</i> (“claw”) +&lrm; <i>-fēte</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clympre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clump or lump of metal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klumpô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;glembʰ-</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gel-</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clynnan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mass, lump, or ball (of metal)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kliuwīnī</i>, diminutive of <i>&#42;kliuwīn</i> (“ball”), equivalent to <i>clīewen</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll up, enfold</li><li>alternative form of <i>clynnan</i> (“to resound”)</li></ol><p>From <i>clyne</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to sound, resound</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cause to sound</li><li>(transitive,&#32;at a door) to knock</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klunnjan</i>, derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klinnaną</i> (“to sound”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gelH-</i> (“to call, sound”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clipian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clipian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clipol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clipola</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hug</li><li>to cherish</li><li>to clasp</li></ol><p>From West Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klupjaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gelb(ʰ)-</i>, <i>&#42;gleb(ʰ)-</i> (“to squeeze while holding”), from <i>&#42;gel-</i> (“to roll up together, into a ball; round object”) with an extra <i>b(ʰ)</i>. Related to Lithuanian <i>glóbti</i> (“to hug, to embrace; to take, to grab for oneself; to cover, to wrap; to save, to help”), Old Prussian <i>galbimai</i> (“help”) (dative), <i>poglabū</i> (“he cuddled, hugged”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>embrace</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>clyppan</i> (“to hug”) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calling</li></ol><p>From clypian</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to close, shut</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klūsijan</i> (“to enclose; to close up”). Equivalent to <i>clūse</i> (“bar, bolt; enclosure”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i>. More at close.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cluster</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clīewen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clīewen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clīþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clīþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free; exempt; having immunity</li></ol><p>From <i>clacu</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>. Compare Old Frisian <i>klaklās</i>, Middle Dutch <i>clackeloos</i> (Modern Dutch <i>klakkeloos</i>), Old Norse <i>klakklaust</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) clover (<i>Trifolium minus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>clæfre</i> (“clover”) +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i> (“plant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clover</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klaibrā</i> (“clover”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clay</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klaij</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klajjaz</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kli-</i> (“to stick, cleave”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gley-</i> (“to stick”).</p><p>Cognate with Middle Low German <i>klei</i> (Dutch <i>klei</i>, German <i>Klei</i>). Compare also Ancient Greek <i>γλία</i> (glía), Latin <i>glūs</i> (“glue”), Old Church Slavonic <i>глина</i> (glina, “clay”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smear</li><li>to anoint</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klaimijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klaimijaną</i> (“to smear”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gley-</i> (“to smear, glue; stick”). Cognate with Old High German <i>bikleimen</i> (“to stain”), Old Norse <i>kleima</i> (“to smear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clemman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clean</li><li>pure</li><li>clear</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klainī</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>klēni</i>, Old High German <i>kleini</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pure-hearted</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>clǣne</i> +&lrm; <i>-heort</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cleanly, pure, excellent, splendid</li></ol><p>From <i>clǣne</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>purely, cleanly</li></ol><p>From <i>clǣne</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cleanness</li><li>chastity, purity</li><li>modesty</li></ol><p>From <i>clǣne</i> (“clean”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clǣnnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clǣnnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clǣnnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clean, cleanse</li><li>to purify</li><li>to absolve, clear someone of wrongdoing</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klainisōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klainisōną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;g(e)lēy-</i> (“gleaming”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gel-</i> (“to gleam”), equivalent to <i>clǣne</i> +&lrm; <i>-sian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purification</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>clǣnsian</i> (“to purify”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throb</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klappijan</i>, related to <i>&#42;klappōn</i> (“to clap”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>kleppa</i> (“to hit, sound”), Old High German <i>biklepfen</i> (“to oppress, defeat”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>clāþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to clothe</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;klaiþijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klaiþijaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gley-</i> (“to stick to, adhere”). Equivalent to <i>clǣþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boy; lad; young man</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;knabō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boy; lad; young man</li><li>servant</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;knappō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to know</li></ol><p>Etymology treeProto-West Germanic <i>&#42;knāan</i>Old English <i>cnawan</i></p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;knāan</i>. There are Germanic cognates in Old High German <i>-cnāhen</i>, Old Norse <i>kná</i>. From the same Proto-Indo-European source are <i>cennan</i>, <i>cunnan</i>. With gecnāwan, the two words together mean “know”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cnǣwe</i> (“knowing, aware, conscious”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acknowledgment, acknowledging</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;cnāwelǣċan</i> (“to acknowledge”), equivalent to <i>cnāwe</i> (“knowing, conscious, aware”) +&lrm; <i>-lǣċan</i> (suffix denoting process or activity). Akin to Old English <i>cnāwan</i> (“to know, recognise”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cnearr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used in reference to Viking vessels</i>) a small ship, galley</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>knǫrr</i> (“ship, galley”). Probably related to Old Norse <i>kæna</i> (“little boat”). See also knorr.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contend, argue</li><li>to discuss</li><li>to investigate, search</li><li>to explain</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;knautōn</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gnew-t-n-</i> (“to press”), from <i>&#42;gen-</i> (“to press together, pinch”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a debate</li><li>an inquiry, a search, an investigation</li></ol><p>From <i>cnēatian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Cnebba of Mercia</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Cnebba</li></ol><p>From <i>Cnebba</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knead</li><li>to ferment</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;knedan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;knudaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gnet-</i> (“to press together”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cniht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cnēow</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kneu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;knewą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cniht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be diligent</li><li>to study</li></ol><p>From <i>cneord</i> (“diligent”) +&lrm; <i>-lǣċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cnēoris</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the number of (one's) kin; family; progeny</li></ol><p>From <i>cnēo</i> +&lrm; <i>rīm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a generation</li><li>posterity</li><li>race; tribe; family</li></ol><p>Likely from <i>cnēo</i> (“generation”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;risn</i>, a derivative related to Old English <i>rīsan</i> (“to rise”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cnēoris</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) knee</li><li>a generation; relationship</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>cnēo</i>, with the <i>w</i> of the oblique forms and the long diphthong of the endingless forms generalized throughout the paradigm. Ultimately from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kneu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;knewą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵónu</i>.</p><p><i>Germanic cognates:</i> Old Frisian <i>knē</i>, Old Saxon <i>knio</i>, Old High German <i>knio</i>, Old Norse <i>kné</i>, Gothic <i>𐌺𐌽𐌹𐌿</i> (kniu).</p><p><i>Indo-European cognates:</i> Sanskrit <i>जानु</i> (jānu), Avestan <i>𐬲𐬥𐬏𐬨</i> (žnūm), Old Armenian <i>ծունր</i> (cunr), Albanian <i>gju</i>, Ancient Greek <i>γόνυ</i> (gónu), Latin <i>genu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kneel</li></ol><p>From <i>cnēow</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kneel</li></ol><p>From <i>cnēow</i> +&lrm; <i>-lian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cnēorīm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dispute</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;knautijan</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gnew-t-n-</i> (“to press”), from <i>&#42;gen-</i> (“to press together, pinch”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knife</li></ol><p>This word is the expected reflex of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;knībaz</i> (“knife”), but it is not attested until the 11th century and earlier Old English instead used <i>seax</i> and <i>meteseax</i> (cognate with German <i>Messer</i>), so some suspect that <i>cnīf</i> was borrowed from or reinforced by Old Norse <i>knífr</i>. Cognate with Middle Low German <i>knīf</i>, Middle Dutch <i>cnijf</i> (Dutch <i>knijf</i>), German <i>Kneif</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boy</li><li>(male) servant, attendant</li><li>male of high military rank, ranking below a baron, usually previously having worked as a page or squire</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kneht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a boy, male child</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>cniht</i> (“boy”) +&lrm; <i>ċild</i> (“child”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boyhood</li><li>youth</li><li>the state of being unmarried; celibacy</li><li>knighthood</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;knehtahaidu</i>, equivalent to <i>cniht</i> +&lrm; <i>-hād</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>knehtheit</i>, Middle Low German <i>knechthêd</i>, <i>knechtheid</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>boyish</li></ol><p><i>cniht</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) young, boyish</li></ol><p>From <i>cniht</i> +&lrm; <i>wesende</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boyishness, the manner of a boy</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>cniht</i> +&lrm; <i>wīse</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to knock (rap one's knuckles against something)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to knock on something</li><li>to hit, strike</li><li>to pound</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;knokōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;knukōną</i>. Cognate with Middle High German <i>knochen</i> (“to nudge; prod; slap”), Old Norse <i>knoka</i> (“to knock; thump”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knoll, summit</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;knudan-</i>, <i>&#42;knudla-</i>, <i>&#42;knulla-</i> (“lump”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knob, button</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>race</li><li>progeny; offspring</li><li>family; kin</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;knōsl</i> (“gender; race; lineage”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>knōsal</i>, Old High German <i>knuosal</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>cnossaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to beat, strike (against something)</li></ol><p>Cognate with Old Norse <i>knosa</i> (“to beat, bruise”), which Norwegian <i>knuse</i>, Danish <i>knuse</i>, Scots <i>knuse</i>, Icelandic <i>knúsa</i>, and Faroese <i>knúsa</i> derive from.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knot</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;knottō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;knuttô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) the juncture of two bones; knuckle; joint</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;knukil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;knukilaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cnocian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>King Cnut of England</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Norse <i>Knútr</i>. Cognate with Danish <i>Knud</i> and English <i>Canute</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind together, connect</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cniht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a knell, ring of a bell</li><li>a stroke, clap, knock (as on a bell or door)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike, knock, clap (such as a door or bell)</li><li>to knell, ring a bell</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;knuzlijaną</i> (“to beat; push; mash”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beating, pulsation</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press, trouble</li><li>(<i>literal&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) to toss</li><li>(<i>literal&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) to strike, dash, beat</li><li>to overcome</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>cnyssaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>striking, stroke</li></ol><p>From <i>cnyssan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie; bind</li><li>to knot</li><li>to knit</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;knuttijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;knuttijaną</i> (“to knot; make knots; knit”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>top</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;knappô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>youth, stripling; boy, lad</li></ol><p>From <i>cnapa</i> +&lrm; <i>-ling</i> (diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>top, cop, knop</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>knowing, cognizant</li><li>conscious, aware</li></ol><p>Etymology treeProto-Germanic <i>&#42;knēiz</i>Old English <i>cnæwe</i></p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;knēiz</i> (“knowing, able”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵneh₃-</i> (“to know”). Akin to Old English <i>cnāwan</i> (“to know”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cocc</i></li><li>a cook</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kokk</i> (“cock, rooster”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kukkaz</i>, probably of imitative origin. Cognate with Old Norse <i>kokkr</i> (“cock”).</p><p>From Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;cocus</i>, from Latin <i>coquus</i> (“cook”), from <i>coquō</i> (“to cook”).</p><p>Akin to Old Norse <i>kokkr</i> (“cook”), German <i>Koch</i>, Dutch <i>kok</i> (“cook”), and possibly also Old English <i>āfiġen</i> (“fried”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cock, a male bird</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kokk</i>, probably ultimately of imitative origin. Cognate with Old Norse <i>kokkr</i>, Late Latin <i>coccus</i> (whence Old French <i>coc</i>, Modern French <i>coq</i>) from the same Germanic source.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darnel, cockle</li></ol><p>Unknown. Perhaps from Late Latin <i>&#42;cocculus</i>, from Latin <i>coccum</i>, <i>coccus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a quiver for arrows</li><li>a case, container</li><li>a sheath</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kokar</i>, whence also Old Frisian <i>koker</i>, Old Saxon <i>kokar</i> (Dutch <i>koker</i>), Old High German <i>kohhār</i> (German <i>Köcher</i>). The origin of the West Germanic word is unknown, but note that a similar word can be found in Turkic and Mongolic languages: see Proto-Mongolic <i>&#42;kökexür</i> for more.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to season food</li></ol><p>From <i>cōc</i> +&lrm; <i>-nian</i>. Compare <i>ġecōcsian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cocer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bag; sack</li><li>husk</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kodd</i>, <i>&#42;koddō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kuddaz</i>, <i>&#42;kuddô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gewt-</i> (“sack; pouch”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quince (<i>Cydonia oblonga</i>)</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>codd</i> (“bag”) +&lrm; <i>æppel</i> (“fruit”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiern</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chamber</li><li>ark</li><li>cave</li><li>den</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kobō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kubô</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Cofa)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Coventry (a city in England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cofantrēo</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>household god, penates</li></ol><p>From <i>cōfa</i> (“chamber, inner room”) +&lrm; <i>god</i> (“god”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Covington (a village and civil parish in Huntingdonshire district, Cambridgeshire, England)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Cofa</i> (a male given name) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> +&lrm; <i>tūn</i>. First attested in the Domesday Book as <i>Covintūne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cool (not hot or warm)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coal</li><li>charcoal</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kōl(ī)</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>kuoli</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kol</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>kole</i>, Old High German <i>kolo</i>, Old Norse <i>kol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cool</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kōlēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collect (prayer before the epistle)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>collecta</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) fierce-minded, bold of spirit, bold</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;collen</i> (“swollen”) +&lrm; <i>ferhþ</i> (“spirit”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>collenferhþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coal tit (<i>Periparus ater</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>col</i> (“coal”) +&lrm; <i>māse</i> (“titmouse”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Colchester (a town in Essex, England)</li></ol><p>From the Colne, a river in Essex, + <i>ċeaster</i> (“fortified town, city”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coolness</li><li>cool air, breeze</li></ol><p>From <i>cōl</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cōlnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>cōl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coalpit: a pit where coal is extracted or charcoal is made</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>col</i> (“coal”) +&lrm; <i>pytt</i> (“pit”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>cōl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>coal black: as black as coal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kulaswartaz</i>. Cognate with West Frisian <i>koalswart</i>, Dutch <i>koolzwart</i>, German <i>kohlschwarz</i>, Swedish <i>kolsvart</i>, Icelandic <i>kolsvartur</i>. Equivalent to <i>col</i> (“coal”) +&lrm; <i>sweart</i> (“black”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colt (a juvenile horse)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kultaz</i> (“plump; stump; thick shape, bulb”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gelt-</i> (“something round, pregnant belly, child in the womb”), from <i>&#42;gel-</i> (“to ball up, amass”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buckthorn</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Coldingham (a coastal village and community council area in Berwickshire, Scottish Borders council area, Scotland)</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;Coludes</i>, genitive of an unattested name <i>&#42;Coludes</i>, + <i>burg</i> (“fortified place”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Coludesburg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>cwōm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>camb</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>cwōme</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comet</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>cwōmon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>campian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>candel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>candel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comfrey</li></ol><p>From Old French <i>consolde</i>, <i>consoude</i>, inherited from Latin <i>cōnsolida</i> (“common comfrey”), so called because of its supposed healing power.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Constantinople/Istanbul</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Cōnstantīnopolis</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Roman consul</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>cōnsul</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cantwara burg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>copp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>copor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to plunder; pillage; steal</li></ol><p>From or related to Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kaupōn</i> and cognate with Old English <i>ceapian</i> (“to buy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>copper</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kopr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cup; vessel</li><li>top; summit</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly a borrowing from Late Latin <i>cuppa</i> (see Old English <i>cuppe</i>), or alternatively from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kopp</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kuppaz</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kuppaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>kopf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cosp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Corbridge (a village and civil parish in southern Northumberland, England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crown</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>coren</i> "chosen" and <i>beag</i> "circular piece of jewelry".</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>election</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>coren</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corn, a grain or seed</li><li>a cornlike pimple, a corn on the foot</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;korn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kurną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵr̥h₂nóm</i> (“grain”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>korn</i>, Old Saxon <i>korn</i> (Low German <i>Koorn</i>), Dutch <i>koren</i>, Old High German <i>korn</i>, Old Norse <i>korn</i>, Gothic <i>𐌺𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌽</i> (kaurn).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>corn-bearing</li></ol><p>From <i>corn</i> +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>granary</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kornahūs</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kurnahūsą</i>. Equivalent to <i>corn</i> +&lrm; <i>hūs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cranoc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cranoc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A placename.</li></ol><p>From <i>cranoc</i> (“crane”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Quarndon (a village in Amber Valley borough, Derbyshire, England)</li></ol><p>Spelling attested in the 1086 Domesday Book, from Old English <i>&#42;Cweorndūn</i>, a compound of <i>cweorn</i> (“quern, millstone”) +&lrm; <i>dūn</i> (“hill”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cornwall (a peninsula in Britain)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Cornish, the people of Cornwall</li></ol><p>From the first syllable of <i>&#42;Körnɨw</i> + <i>Wealas</i> (“Welshmen”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crown</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>corōna</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>κορώνη</i> (korṓnē, “garland, wreath”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curse</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>band, company, troop</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kurþrą</i> (“crowd, flock, herd”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂gr̥trom</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ger-</i> (“to gather”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>coss</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shackle, fetter</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kosp</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kiss</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kussaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kissing</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;cossettan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cossettung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>option, choice; possibility</li><li>condition, manner, way</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chosen, choice</li><li>tried, proven; excellent</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kust-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵews-</i> (“to choose”).</p><p>Akin to Old Saxon <i>kostōn</i> (“to try, tempt”), Old High German <i>kostōn</i> (“to taste, test, try by tasting”) (German <i>kosten</i>), Icelandic <i>kosta</i> (“to try, tempt”), Gothic <i>𐌺𐌿𐍃𐍄𐌿𐍃</i> (kustus, “test”), Old English <i>cystan</i> (“to spend, get the value of, procure”), Old English <i>cyst</i> (“proof, test, trial; choice”), <i>ċēosan</i> (“to choose”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tempter</li></ol><p>From <i>costian</i> (“to tempt”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>try, tempt</li><li>prove</li><li>examine</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kostōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kustōną</i>. Akin to Old Saxon <i>kostōn</i> (“to try, tempt”), Old High German <i>kostōn</i> (“to taste, test, try by tasting”) (German <i>kosten</i>), Icelandic <i>kosta</i> (“to try, tempt”), Gothic <i>𐌺𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (kausjan, “to taste”), Old English <i>cost</i> (“option, possibility; manner, condition”), Old English <i>cystan</i> (“to spend, get the value of, procure”), Old English <i>cyst</i> (“proof, test, trial; choice”), <i>ċēosan</i> (“to choose”). More at choose.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>costian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>costian</i></li></ol><p>see <i>costian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temptation</li></ol><p>From <i>costian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cottage</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kutą</i>, <i>&#42;kutan</i> (“shed”), probably of non-Indo-European origin, but possibly borrowed from Uralic; compare Finnish <i>kota</i> (“hut, house”) and Hungarian <i>ház</i> (“house”), both from Proto-Finno-Ugric/Proto-Uralic <i>&#42;kota</i>.</p><p>However, compare Dutch and English <i>hut</i>, as well as Old Norse <i>kot</i>, Middle High German <i>kūz</i> (“execution pit”)), Scytho-Sarmatian <i>&#42;kuta</i>, Avestan <i>𐬐𐬀𐬙𐬀</i> (kata, “chamber”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cottager</li></ol><p>From <i>cot</i> (“cottage”) +&lrm; <i>-sǣta</i> (“sitter”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mallow</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disease, sickness</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kuþō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crab</li><li>crayfish</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;krabbō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;krabbô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to croak</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;krēk-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;greh₂-g-</i>, from <i>&#42;greh₂-k-</i>, of onomatopoeic origin.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to resound</li><li>to crack (make an explosive noise)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;krakōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;krakōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cradol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cradle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kradulaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crave</li><li>to ask, implore</li><li>to demand</li><li>(<i>law</i>) to summon</li></ol><p>Probably Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;krafōn</i>, modification of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;krafjaną</i>.</p><p>Derivation from Old Norse <i>krǫf</i> (&lt; <i>&#42;krafō</i>) or <i>krafa</i> (both "demand"), is also possible, but usually now rejected.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cram, stuff</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;krammōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;krammōną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gromH-néh₂-ti</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crane (bird)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kranō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chronicle</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>chronica</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>χρονικός</i> (khronikós, “of or concerning time”), from <i>χρόνος</i> (khrónos, “time”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chronographer, chronicler</li></ol><p>From <i>cranic</i> +&lrm; <i>wrītere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crane (<i>bird</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kranuk</i>, equivalent to <i>cran</i> +&lrm; <i>-oc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cræt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To crow (make the noise of a rooster)</li><li>To make other noises typical of birds; to produce birdsong.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;krāan</i> (“to crow, shout”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;krāā</i>. Cognate with West Frisian <i>krie</i>, Dutch <i>kraai</i>, and German <i>Krähe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the land of the Greeks; Byzantine Empire (an ancient Greek empire in Southeastern Europe and Western Asia, with its capital at Constantinople)</li></ol><p>From <i>Crēca</i> (“of the Greeks”) +&lrm; <i>land</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Greeks</li><li>Greece</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Krēkō</i> (“Greeks”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Creċġan ford</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Crēcas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a town in Kent, England; modern Crayford</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Creċġan ford</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Greek language</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Greek</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a Greek</li></ol><p>From <i>Crēcas</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creed (statement of belief)</li><li>A prayer composed of a creed.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>crēdō</i> (“I believe”) in the Nicene Creed or Apostle's Creed.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>crēda</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Crema (town in Lombardy)</li></ol><p>Compare Lombardic <i>Krem</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Creoda of Mercia</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Creoda</li></ol><p>From <i>Creoda</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crawl</li><li>to creep</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kreupaną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>kriāpa</i>, Old Saxon <i>kriopan</i>, Old High German <i>kriofan</i>, Old Norse <i>krjúpa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Crete (an island of Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Cretans</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manger; stall</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kribjǭ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cryċċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Crediton (a town in Mid Devon district, Devon, England)</li></ol><p>From a Celtic river name + <i>tūn</i> (“town”). Literally, “town on the river Creedy”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cram, stuff, insert</li><li>to crumb, crumble</li><li>to mingle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;krimmaną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>krimman</i>, &#42;chrimman.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to curl</li><li>to crinkle</li></ol><p>Alternative form of the verb <i>cringan</i>. From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;krinkaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cringan</i></li></ol><p>Origin obscure. Likely a merger of Old English <i>cringan</i> and <i>&#42;crenġan</i>, <i>&#42;crenċġan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kringaną</i> and <i>&#42;krangijaną</i> respectively. Compare Old Frisian <i>krenza</i>, <i>krinza</i> (“to harm”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall, yield</li><li>to fall or die in battle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kringaną</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>krenza</i>, Old Saxon <i>krengon</i> (“heel over”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chrism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Medieval Latin <i>crisma</i>, from Ecclesiastical Latin <i>chrisma</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>χρῖσμα</i> (khrîsma).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of hair</i>) curly</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>crispus</i> (“curly”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Christ</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Christus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Χριστός</i> (Khristós, “Christ, the Messiah”, literally “the anointed one”), from <i>χρῑ́ω</i> (khrī́ō, “to anoint; to rub, smear”) (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰrey-</i> (“to smear”)) + <i>-τός</i> (-tós, suffix forming adjectives).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crystal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>crystallum</i>, <i>cristallum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Christian</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) Christian</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>chrīstiānus</i>, <i>Chrīstiānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Christianity</li><li>a time or place where Christianity prevails</li></ol><p>From <i>cristen</i> +&lrm; <i>-dōm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>cristen</i>; most Christian</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Christian</li></ol><p>From <i>crīsten</i> (“Christian”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Christmas</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Christmas Day</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the night before Christmas</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the last part of the night before Christmas</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cristes mæsse</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to Christianize</li><li>to christen</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>crīsten</i> (“christian”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>christening</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>crīstnian</i> (“to christen”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>crocc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crock, pitcher, waterpot, flagon, a little jug or lentil-shaped vessel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;krog</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a crock (earthenware jar)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kroggō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>crocwyrhta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kettle</li></ol><p>From <i>croc</i> +&lrm; <i>hwer</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>crochwer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>potter</li></ol><p>From <i>crocca</i> (“crock”) +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“wright”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an enclosed field; a croft</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a container or vessel (e.g. pot, jug, etc.)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;krōgu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;krōguz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Croyden</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>croh</i> (“saffron”) +&lrm; <i>dene</i> (“valley”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>crocc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saffron</li><li>alternative form of <i>crōg</i></li></ol><p>From Latin <i>crocus</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>croc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>crōg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garlic or possibly some other <i>Allium</i> e.g. onion or leek</li></ol><p>Maybe from <i>cropp</i> (“the head of something”) +&lrm; <i>lēac</i> (“leek”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the crop of a bird</li><li>the head of a flower; an ear of corn</li><li>a sprig of berries, a bunch of flowers</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kruppaz</i>; cognate with Old High German <i>kropf</i> (German <i>Kropf</i>), Old Norse <i>kroppr</i>, Dutch <i>krop</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crop</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cross</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) cross</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>crux</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>krūzi</i> (German <i>Kreuz</i>), Old Saxon <i>krūci</i> (Dutch <i>kruis</i>, Low German <i>Krüüz</i>), Old Irish <i>cros</i> (Welsh <i>croes</i>, Irish <i>crois</i>, Old Norse <i>kross</i> English <i>cross</i>), Old French <i>croiz</i> (French <i>croix</i>), Spanish <i>cruz</i> and many others.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a waterpot, pitcher, or cruse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;krūkā</i> (“pot, pitcher”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive</li><li>to press (forward)</li><li>to crowd</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;krūdan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;krūdaną</i>, <i>&#42;kreudaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vault</li><li>crypt</li><li>hollow place underground</li></ol><p>Cognate with Middle Low German <i>kruft</i>, German <i>Gruft</i>, Dutch <i>krocht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cruft</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fragment</li><li>crumb</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;krumô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>crump</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bent, stooped, crooked</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;krumpaz</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earthen pot, jug, pitcher</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;krūsā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cribb</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crutch</li><li>curved staff, crook</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;krukkju</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;krukjō</i> (“crutch, staff”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>krukka</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;krukka</i>, Old High German <i>krucka</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cripple, disabled person</li><li>burrow, den, tunnel, narrow passage, drain</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crippled, disabled</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;krupil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;krupilaz</i>.</p><p>Possibly from the same source as etymology 1.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>crisma</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>crīsten</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cracettan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creaking</li></ol><p><i>crācettan</i> (“to croak”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strength, power</li><li>power, ability</li><li>skill</li><li>art</li><li>profession</li><li>virtue</li><li>device, especially magical</li><li>deceit, fraud</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kraftu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kraftuz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to craft, build</li></ol><p>From <i>cræft</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>cræftiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strengthen, make powerful</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kraftugōn</i>, <i>&#42;kraftīgōn</i>, equivalent to <i>cræftiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>&#42;kreftigōn</i> (found in <i>gikreftigōn</i> (“to strengthen”), <i>unkreftigōn</i> (“to weaken”)) and Old High German <i>kreftīgen</i> (“to make whole, heal”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sage-minded, science-learned</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>cræft</i> (“skill”) +&lrm; <i>glēaw</i> (“wise, learned”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong; powerful</li><li>skillful; cunning; ingenious</li><li>learned; instructive</li><li>knowing a craft; scientific</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kraftigaz</i>, <i>&#42;kraftagaz</i>, <i>&#42;kraftugaz</i> (“powerful, mighty”), equivalent to <i>cræft</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>. Akin to Old Saxon <i>kraftag</i>, Old High German <i>chreftig</i>, <i>kreftig</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artist</li><li>workman</li><li>professional; skilled worker (e.g. craftsman, artificer, artisan, etc.)</li><li>architect</li></ol><p>From <i>cræftgian</i>, equivalent to <i>cræftiġ</i> (“skilled”) +&lrm; <i>-a</i> (agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>workmanlike, craftily, skillfully, cunningly, ingeniously</li></ol><p>From <i>cræftiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>cræftiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>artless, unskillful, skillless, innocent, simple, inexpert</li></ol><p>From <i>cræft</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>artificial, synthetic</li><li>skillful, skilled</li></ol><p>From <i>cræft</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Perhaps a calque of Latin <i>artificiālis</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>cunningly, craftily</li></ol><p>From <i>cræftlīċ</i> (“crafty”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i> or <i>cræft</i> (“skill, deceit”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crest, tuft, plume</li></ol><p>From Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;cresta</i>, from Latin <i>crista</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cart, waggon</li><li>chariot</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;krat</i> (“basket, crate”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kradô</i> (“(wicker) basket”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a carthorse</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>cræt</i> (“cart”) +&lrm; <i>hors</i> (“hors”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chariot</li><li>wain</li></ol><p>From <i>cræt</i> +&lrm; <i>wæġn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>crætwæġn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kō</i> (“cow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cwacian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cuculer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cwician</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spoon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>cochlear</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cwic</i></li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Cucu)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>unit of measure</i>) a spoon, half a drachm</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>cochlear</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cuttlefish</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kudulā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kudilǭ</i>, a diminutive from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kuddô</i> + <i>-ilǭ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gewt-</i> (“pouch, sack”), from <i>&#42;gew-</i> (“to bend, bow, arch, vault, curve”). Equivalent to <i>codd</i> +&lrm; <i>-el</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cwidu</i> (“cud”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cwēn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Cwēnburg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cuġele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cowl, hood (of a monk)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ecclesiastical Latin <i>cuculla</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cowherd</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>cū</i> +&lrm; <i>hierde</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the horn of a cow</li></ol><p>From <i>cū</i> (“cow”) +&lrm; <i>horn</i> (“horn”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cūhierde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cwichelm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cugele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>culfre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>culfre</i> (“dove, pigeon”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dove; pigeon</li></ol><p>Unknown. Speculated to be from Latin <i>columbula</i>, from Latin <i>columba</i>, though there are multiple phonetic and adoptive difficulties.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A coulter (of a plow)</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) A dagger.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>culter</i>, of unclear origin.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>culter</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>culfre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guest</li><li>stranger</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kumô</i>, equivalent to <i>cuman</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kweman</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwemaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷémt</i> (“to step”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷem-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hollow, narrow valley, combe</li><li>a measure of volume (uncertain for liquid but dry is said to be 4 bushels)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kumbaz</i>; liken Dutch <i>kom</i> (“bowl, basin”), Welsh <i>cwm</i> (“a hollow valley”), Sanskrit <i>कुम्भ</i> (kumbha, “a pot, jug”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cumbria (a Brittonic kingdom in northern England and southern Scotland)</li></ol><p>Originally <i>Cumbra land</i>, from <i>Cumbra</i>, genitive form of <i>&#42;Cumbre</i> (“Cumbrian Britons”) + <i>land</i>. <i>&#42;Cumbre</i> derives from Cumbric <i>&#42;Cumbri</i>, cognate of Welsh <i>Cymry</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cumbol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sign, image</li><li>military standard</li><li>banner</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kumbl</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kumblą</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>kumbl</i> (“sign”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cumberland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Compton (already a name of various places in Anglo-Saxon England)</li></ol><p><i>cumb</i> (“valley”) +&lrm; <i>tūn</i> (“town”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cumberland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cow urine, gomutra</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>cū</i> (“cow”) +&lrm; <i>miċġe</i> (“urine”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cow urine</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>cū</i> (“cow”) +&lrm; <i>migoþa</i> (“urine”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cumbol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cumlīþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>cumlīþian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cumlīþnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hospitable</li></ol><p>From <i>cuma</i> (“guest”) +&lrm; <i>līþe</i> (“gentle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lodge, receive as a guest</li><li>to be a guest</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>cumlīþe</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hospitality</li></ol><p>From <i>cumlīþe</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cumlīþnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>godfather</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>compater</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Congresbury (a village and civil parish in North Somerset district, Somerset, England)</li></ol><p>From a Celtic given name + <i>burg</i> (“fortress”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cyning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to know, to be familiar with</li><li>(<i>auxiliary</i>) can, to know how</li><li>to be able</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kunnaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵneh₃-</i> (“to know”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>cunnaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cynehēah</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to experience, learn to know</li><li>to explore, find out, investigate</li><li>to try, test</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kunnēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knowledge</li><li>trial, probation, experience</li><li>contact, carnal knowledge</li></ol><p>From <i>cunnan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>cwōm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>cwōme</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>cwōmen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>cwōmon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cup</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Generally regarded as a borrowing from Late Latin <i>cuppa</i> (therefore a doublet of <i>cȳf</i> and <i>cȳpe</i>). Alternatively, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kuppaz</i> (compare Old English <i>copp</i> (“vessel; cup”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knapweed (<i>Centaurea</i> spp.)</li><li>yellowwort (<i>Blackstonia perfoliata</i>)</li><li>(<i>possibly</i>) common centaury (<i>Centaurium erythraea</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown; the second element may represent <i>&#42;mealle</i> (“cudgel, pestle”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mallǭ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;melh₂-</i>; compare Latin <i>malleus</i>, and for the semantics, modern English <i>clubweed</i>, <i>drumsticks</i> (“knapweed”). Forms in <i>-elle</i>, <i>-ille</i> are likely by analogy with <i>cunille</i>, <i>gagille</i>, <i>wurmille</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>curmealle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>curmealle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cors</i> (“curse”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to curse</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>curs</i> (“curse”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) a woodpigeon or ringdove</li></ol><p>Uncertain; the traditional derivation from <i>cū</i> (“cow”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;sċote, &#42;sċeote</i> (“shoot of a plant?" or "shooter?, darter?”, an otherwise unknown derivative of <i>sċēotan</i> (“to shoot”)) is somewhat semantically dubious; however, compare Old High German <i>&#42;skozza</i> in <i>erdskozza</i> (“vine sapling, shoot”, literally “ground-shoot”). Perhaps the reference to <i>cow</i> is due to the <i>cowish</i> appearance, nature, and disposition of wood pidgeons and ring doves; or, because wood pigeons can often be seen feeding in fields where cows graze. However, there are no known reported myths or old wives' tales explaining a connection between wood pidgeons and cows (e.g. that wood pidgeons spring up out of cow excrement, etc.).</p><p>Alternatively, the first element may instead be <i>cūsċ</i> (“chaste, modest, pure, clean”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cūsċeote</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cūsċeote</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cūslyppe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cowslip (<i>Primula veris</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>cū</i> (“cow”) +&lrm; <i>slyppe</i> (“paste, sticky substance”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cowtail</li></ol><p>From <i>cū</i> (“cow”) +&lrm; <i>tæġl</i> (“tail”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fresh from the cow (<i>of milk</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kūwarm</i>; compare Dutch <i>koewarm</i>, German <i>kuhwarm</i>. By surface analysis, <i>cū</i> (“cow”) +&lrm; <i>wearm</i> (“warm”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cūþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>known</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) an acquaintance</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kunþaz</i>. Related to <i>cunnan</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>kuth</i> and <i>kunnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acquaintance, relative</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kunþō</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>kundo</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Cuþa)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From the common noun <i>cūþa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kunþabalþ</i>, from <i>&#42;kunþ</i> (“will, desire”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;balþ</i> (“bold, brave”). Equivalent to <i>cūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>beald</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Gundebald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Cuthbert; Bishop of Lindisfarne and English Saint.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kunþaberht</i>, from <i>&#42;kunþ</i> (“will, desire”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”). Equivalent to <i>cūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>beorht</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Gundberht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cūþbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cūþbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kunþaburg</i>, from <i>&#42;kunþ</i> (“will, desire”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;burg</i> (“fortification”). Equivalent to <i>cūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>burg</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Cundpurc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cūþburg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cūþbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>clearly</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kunþaflādi</i>, from <i>&#42;kunþ</i> (“known”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;flādi</i> (“cleanliness, beauty”). Equivalent to <i>cūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;flǣd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kunþagaiʀ</i>, from <i>&#42;kunþ</i> (“will, desire”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;gaiʀ</i> (“spear”). Equivalent to <i>cūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>gār</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Cundgēr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kunþahari</i>, from <i>&#42;kunþ</i> (“will, desire”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hari</i> (“army”). Equivalent to <i>cūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>here</i>. Possibly cognate with Old High German <i>Kundheri</i> (if not from <i>&#42;Gunþihari</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Cūþa</li></ol><p>From <i>Cūþa</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kunþalaik</i>, from <i>&#42;kunþ</i> (“will, desire”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;laik</i> (“play, music”). Equivalent to <i>cūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>lāc</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Cundleich</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>clearly, certainly</li><li>openly, brazenly</li><li>publicly</li></ol><p>From <i>cūþlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>cūþlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make known; publish, proclaim</li><li>to become familiar with, familiarize, befriend</li></ol><p>From <i>cūþ</i> (“known”) +&lrm; <i>-lǣċan</i>, suffix denoting process or activity.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kunþamundu</i>, from <i>&#42;kunþ</i> (“will, desire”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;mundu</i> (“protector”). Equivalent to <i>cūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>mund</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Cundamund</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>cūþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>cūþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>cūþ</i> (“known”) + <i>rēd</i> (“counsel, advice”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>cūþ</i> (“known”) + <i>rīċ</i> (“king, ruler”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Cuthred of Wessex; 8th century king of Wessex.</li></ol><p>From <i>cūþ</i> (“known”) + <i>rǣd</i> (“counsel, advice”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>cūþ</i> (“known”) +&lrm; <i>swiþ</i> (“strong, powerful”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Cuthwine of Wessex</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>cūþ</i> (“known”) +&lrm; <i>wine</i> (“friend”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Cūþwine</li></ol><p>From <i>Cūþwine</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>cūþ</i> (“known”) and <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Cūþwulf</li></ol><p>From <i>Cūþwulf</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Northumbrian</i>) alternative form of <i>cwæþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quake</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwakōną</i>, probably onomatopoeic in origin. Cognate with Dutch <i>kwak</i> (“pile of sperm”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quaking, shaking</li></ol><p>From <i>cwacian</i> (“to quake”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>killing, violent death, slaughter</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kwalu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwalō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bewail, lament, moan</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kwainōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwainōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saint-Josse-sur-Mer, France</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung, excrement</li><li>filth</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;cwǣd</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kwād</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>death</li><li>slaughter, plague, pestilence</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kwalm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deadly, death-bearing</li></ol><p>From <i>cwealm</i> +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jail, prison</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;cweart</i> (“lamentation, groaning”) +&lrm; <i>ern</i> (“place”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to shake, swing, move, vibrate; shake off, give up</li><li>(<i>of a weapon</i>) to brandish</li></ol><p>Related to <i>cwacian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quicksand</li></ol><p>From <i>cwic</i> (“agile, living”) +&lrm; <i>sand</i> (“sand”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cwedol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eloquent</li><li>talkative</li></ol><p>From a derivative of <i>cweþan</i> (“to say”) +&lrm; <i>-ol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kwelan</i> (“to suffer”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill, slay</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kwalljan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwaljaną</i> (“to torment”). Cognates include Old Saxon <i>quellian</i>, Middle Dutch <i>quellen</i> (Dutch <i>kwellen</i>), Old High German <i>quellen</i> (German <i>quälen</i>), Old Norse <i>kvelja</i> (Danish <i>kvæle</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>killer</li><li>executioner</li><li>tormentor</li></ol><p>From <i>cwellan</i> (“to kill”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i> (agent suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cwealm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to please, satisfy, gratify</li><li>to comply with, serve</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwēmijaną</i> (“to please, comfort, come”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷem-</i> (“to go, come”). Akin to Old High German <i>biquāmi</i> (Modern German <i>bequem</i> (“comfortable, convenient”)), Old English <i>cuman</i> (“to come”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pleasant, agreeable, acceptable</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kwāmī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwēmijaz</i> (“convenient, appropriate, pleasing”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷem-</i> (“to come, go”). Akin to Old High German <i>biquāmi</i> (“pleasing”), Old High German <i>kweman</i> (“to come”) (German <i>kommen</i> (“to come”)), Old English <i>cuman</i> (“to come”). Related to come, comely.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>queen (a ruling female monarch or a king's wife)</li><li>princess (a king's daughter)</li><li>woman</li><li>wife</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>cwœ̄n</i> ← <i>*kwœ̄ni</i> ← <i>*kwōni</i> ← <i>*kwą̄ni</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kwāni</i> (“woman, wife”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwēniz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷḗn</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>quān</i>, Old Norse <i>kvæn</i>, Gothic <i>𐌵𐌴𐌽𐍃</i> (qēns). The Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek <i>γυνή</i> (gunḗ), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žena</i>, and Old Irish <i>ben</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the residents of Cwenland (Lapland)</li></ol><p>Compare Old Norse <i>kvænir</i>&nbsp;pl, possibly from Sámi <i>kuöinu</i>, after Finnish <i>kainulaiset</i>. See also Icelandic <i>kvænir</i>, <i>kvenir</i>&nbsp;pl, and Kven <i>kvääni</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>cwēn</i> (“queen”) +&lrm; <i>burg</i> (“city”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cwēnburg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to extinguish, quench</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwankijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woman</li><li>lady, noblewoman; queen</li><li>common woman, harlot</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kwenā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwenǭ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷḗn</i>.</p><p>From <i>cwēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female bird; hen</li></ol><p>From <i>cwēn</i> +&lrm; <i>fugol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>cwēn</i> (“queen”) +&lrm; <i>hild</i> (“grace”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>historical Lapland, or roughly Finland</li></ol><p><i>Cwēnas</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>queenly</li><li>feminine</li></ol><p>From <i>cwēn</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the White Sea</li></ol><p><i>Cwēnas</i> +&lrm; <i>sǣ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quern</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kwernu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwernō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quernstone, millstone</li></ol><p>From cweorn + stān, from cweornstān, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwernōstainaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>quirnstein</i> and Old Norse <i>kvernsteinn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cwið</i>&nbsp;/ <i>cwiðð</i>&nbsp;/ <i>cwiðeð</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>cwæð</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>cweþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say</li><li>to speak</li><li>to mention</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kweþan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kweþaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to declare war</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alive; live, living</li><li>mentally agile; intelligent, keen</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kwiku</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwikwaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷih₃wós</i> (“alive”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quickbeam</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>cwic</i> +&lrm; <i>bēam</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>couch grass</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kwikwā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwikwaz</i> (“alive; living; quick”), related to Old High German <i>quecca</i>, Middle Low German <i>kweke</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>cwic</i> (“active, quick”) +&lrm; <i>helm</i> (“helmet”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Cwichelm</li></ol><p>From <i>Cwichelm</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quicken</li><li>to come to life, become living</li><li>to bring to life, to vivify</li></ol><p>From <i>cwic</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mercury</li></ol><p>From <i>cwic</i> +&lrm; <i>seolfor</i>. Compare Latin <i>argentum vīvum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a rowan, aspen, or poplar; quickbeam</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>cwic</i> +&lrm; <i>trēow</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cwic</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>vigorously, in a living manner</li></ol><p><i>cwicu</i> (“alive”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>cwic</i> (“live, keen, (mentally) quick”) + <i>wald</i> (“powerful, mighty”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>book of maxims</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Proverbs</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>cwide</i> +&lrm; <i>bōc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>cwide</i> (“saying”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saying</li><li>sentence</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwidiz</i>, a derivative of <i>cweþan</i> (“to say”). Cognate with Old High German <i>kwiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>song, ballad</li><li>word, speech</li><li>story, tale</li></ol><p>From <i>cwide</i> (“saying”) +&lrm; <i>giedd</i> (“story, song”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>speechless</li></ol><p><i>cwide</i> (“saying”) +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cwedol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agreement</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>cwide</i> +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cwudu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) bat (flying mammal)</li></ol><p>From <i>cwield</i> (“evening”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hræþe</i> (“swiftness; swiftie”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spring (of water)</li><li>well</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kwalljā</i> (“spring, well”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush</li></ol><p>Unknown. Possibly onomatopoeic. Perhaps instead related to German <i>quetschen</i> (“to squeeze”), itself derived from Old French <i>quasser</i> (“to break”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plague, pestilence</li><li>destruction</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwildiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evening</li></ol><p>From <i>cwild</i> (“death”) +&lrm; <i>tīd</i> (“time”), literally “dead time”</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to vanish, to disappear</li></ol><p>According to Vladimir Orel, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwinkaną</i> (reconstructed by him as <i>*kwenkaną</i>), whence also Old Frisian <i>quinka</i> (“to be extinguished”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old English transliteration of the Latin name <i>Quintus</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>aren't you?, isn't it?</li><li>right?, eh?, correct?</li><li>oh, sayest thou?</li></ol><p>Formed from <i>cweþan</i> (“to speak, to say”) +&lrm; <i>lā</i> (“lo”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>cwiþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>womb, uterus</li><li>belly, stomach</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kweþuz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak or moan in grief, mourn, lament</li></ol><p>Eventually from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷey-</i> (“to lament; complain”). Compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwainōną</i>, whence Old English cwānian.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cud</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kweduz</i> (“resin”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cwician</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cwide</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cwild</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cwiella</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cwīesan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cwēn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cwēn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small cake</li><li>a bite; morsel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kakil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kakilaz</i>, diminutive of <i>&#42;kakǭ</i> (“cake”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cyċene</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>ċicen</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kukinō</i> (“kitchen”).</p><p>Precise development uncertain, but possibly from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kiukīn</i>. Alternatively, equivalent to <i>cocc</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i> (see ċicen. Lack of palatalization of the second <i>c</i> is unexpected.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kitchen</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kukinā</i>, borrowed from Late Latin <i>cocīna</i>, from earlier <i>coquīna</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>service in a kitchen</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>cyċene</i> (“kitchen”) +&lrm; <i>þeġnung</i> (“service”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cyċenþeġnung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cudgel, a strong stick</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kuggil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kuggilaz</i> (“knobbed instrument”), derivative of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kuggǭ</i> (“cog, swelling, block of wood”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gewgʰ-</i> (“swelling, bow”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gew-</i> (“to bow, bend, arch, curve”). Cognate with German <i>Kogge</i> (“cog”, a type of ship).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vessel, vat</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kūbiju</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kūbaz</i>, perhaps from Latin <i>cūpa</i>. Possibly a doublet of <i>cuppe</i> and <i>cȳpe</i>. Alternatively, perhaps a clipping of Old English <i>cȳfel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiefes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiefes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċīeġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċīġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kiln, oven</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>culīna</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cylen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cylen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a leathern bottle, flagon, vessel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kullijaz</i>, from Late Latin <i>culleus</i>, variant of Latin <i>cōleus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>κολεός</i> (koleós, “sheath, scabbard”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>kyllir</i> (“sack, scrotum”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cyll</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cylen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spotted; speckled</li></ol><p>Unknown. Perhaps related to <i>col</i> (“coal”). Alternatively, perhaps derived from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kalwī</i> (“baldness, unfledgedness, callowness”), due to the fact that the young of many animals are often spotted or speckled.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cumaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coming, arrival; advent, approach</li><li>an event</li><li>an outcome, result</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comely, lovely, splendid, beautiful</li><li>exquisite</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kumi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kumiz</i> (“arrival”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷem-</i> (“to go, come”). Akin to Old Frisian <i>keme</i>, Old Saxon <i>kumi</i>, Old High German <i>cumi</i> (“arrival”), Gothic <i>𐌵𐌿𐌼𐍃</i> (qums), Old English <i>cuman</i> (“to come”). More at come.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kūmiz</i> (“delicate, feeble”). Akin to Old High German <i>kūmo</i> (“tender, dainty, weak”) (German <i>kaum</i> (“hardly”)), (Dutch <i>kuim</i> (“weak; hardly”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cumin</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>cuminum</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>κύμῑνον</i> (kúmīnon).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>cymeþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comely, beautiful, lovely, splendid, convenient</li></ol><p>From <i>cȳme</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>beautifully, splendidly, conveniently</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>cȳmlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cynn</i> (“type, kind, species, kin”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cyne-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cyning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is acquired or inherent from birth, nature, natural disposition</li><li>sort, kind, type; gender (male or female, etc.)</li><li>generation, race</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kundi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kinþiz</i>. Compare the related <i>&#42;kunją</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>innate</li></ol><p>From the noun <i>cynd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>natural</li></ol><p>From <i>cynde</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>cyne-</i> (“royal”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>royal, of a king</li><li>alternative form of <i>cynn</i>- (<i>in given names</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kuni</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kunją</i> (“kin, family, clan”) or perhaps in some cases from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kuniz</i>, both from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵenh₁-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cynebeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kunibalþ</i>, from <i>&#42;kuni</i> (“lineage, clan”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;balþ</i> (“bold, braver”). Equivalent to <i>cynn</i> +&lrm; <i>beald</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Kunibald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Cynebeald</li></ol><p>From <i>Cynebeald</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>child or offspring of a king: a prince or princess</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>cyne-</i> +&lrm; <i>bearn</i>, literally “royal child.”</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kuniberht</i>, from <i>&#42;kuni</i> (“lineage, clan”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”). Equivalent to <i>cynn</i> +&lrm; <i>berht</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Cunibert</i>, Early Medieval Latin <i>Cunnibertus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>born into royalty</li></ol><p><i>cyne-</i> (“royal”) +&lrm; <i>boren</i> (“born”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a king's compensation or recompense</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>cyne-</i> +&lrm; <i>bōt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palace</li></ol><p><i>cyne-</i> (“royal”) +&lrm; <i>botl</i> (“building, dwelling”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name &lsqb;c.730 CE&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kuniburg</i>, from <i>&#42;kuni</i> (“family, kin”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;burg</i> (“fortification”). Equivalent to <i>cynn</i> +&lrm; <i>burg</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Kuniburg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cyneberht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cynecynn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>royal family or lineage</li></ol><p><i>cyne-</i> +&lrm; <i>cynn</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The authority of a king; dominion.</li><li>The realm of a king; a kingdom.</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) Divine authority or power.</li></ol><p>From <i>cyne-</i> (“royal”) +&lrm; <i>-dōm</i> (abstract nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kunifriþ</i>, from <i>&#42;kuni</i> (“lineage, clan”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;friþ</i> (“peace”). Equivalent to <i>cynn</i> +&lrm; <i>friþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Cunefrid</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cyning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kunigār</i>, from <i>&#42;kuni</i> (“lineage, clan”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;gār</i> (“spear”). Equivalent to <i>cynn</i> +&lrm; <i>gār</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Chunigēr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>cyneġierd</i></li></ol><p>From <i>cyne-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeard</i> (“rod, wand, staff”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cyneġīls</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cyneġierd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A royal wand or sceptre.</li></ol><p><i>cyne-</i> (“royal”) +&lrm; <i>ġierd</i> (“staff”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kunigīsl</i>, from <i>&#42;kuni</i> (“lineage, clan”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;gīsl</i> (“hostage”). Equivalent to <i>cynn</i> +&lrm; <i>ġīsl</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Chunigīs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Cynegils</li></ol><p>From <i>Cynegils</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crown</li></ol><p><i>cyne-</i> (“royal”) +&lrm; <i>gold</i> (“gold”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cynegils</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cyneġierd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>cyne-</i> (“royal”) +&lrm; <i>hēah</i> (“high”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Cyneheard the Ætheling</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kunihard</i>, from <i>&#42;kuni</i> (“lineage, clan”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hard</i> (“spear”). Equivalent to <i>cynn</i> +&lrm; <i>heard</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Chunihard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crown</li></ol><p><i>cyne-</i> (“royal”) +&lrm; <i>helm</i> (“helmet”)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Cynehelm)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kunihelm</i>, from <i>&#42;kuni</i> (“lineage, clan”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;helm</i> (“helemt”). Equivalent to <i>cynn</i> +&lrm; <i>helm</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Chunelm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crown</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>cynehelm</i> (“crown”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kunihildi</i>, from <i>&#42;kuni</i> (“lineage, clan”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hildi</i> (“battle”). Equivalent to <i>cynn</i> +&lrm; <i>hild</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Cunihild</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>cynn</i> (“lineage, clan”) +&lrm; <i>lāf</i> (“legacy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>royal, kingly</li></ol><p>From <i>cyne-</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>cyne-</i> (“royal”) + <i>lufu</i> (“love”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kunimundu</i>, from <i>&#42;kuni</i> (“lineage, clan”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;mundu</i> (“protection, protector”). Equivalent to <i>cynn</i> +&lrm; <i>mund</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Kunimund</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kunimār</i>, from <i>&#42;kuni</i> (“lineage, clan”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;mār</i> (“famous”). Equivalent to <i>cynn</i> +&lrm; <i>mǣre</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Chunemār</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kempsford (a village and civil parish of Gloucestershire, England, United Kingdom)</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;Cynemǣre</i> (an unattested personal name) +&lrm; <i>-es</i> (genitive marker) +&lrm; <i>ford</i> (“ford”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kunirīk</i>, from <i>&#42;kuni</i> (“lineage, clan”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;rīk</i> (“king, ruler”). Equivalent to <i>cynn</i> +&lrm; <i>rīċ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Chunirīh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The authority of a king; dominion.</li><li>The realm of a king; a kingdom.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kunirīkī</i>; equivalent to <i>cyne-</i> (“king”) +&lrm; <i>-rīċe</i> (“realm”). Cognate with Old High German <i>chuneriche</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cynerīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>cyne-</i> (“royal”) and <i>rǣd</i> (“counsel, advice”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throne</li></ol><p>From <i>cyne-</i> (“royal”) +&lrm; <i>setl</i> (“seat”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>cyne-</i> (“royal”) and <i>siġe</i> (“victory”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>cyne-</i> (“royal”) and <i>stān</i> (“stone”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throne</li><li>capital city</li></ol><p>From <i>cyne-</i> (“royal”) +&lrm; <i>stōl</i> (“chair, seat”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>cyne-</i> (“royal”) + <i>swīþ</i> (“strong, mighty”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cyneweald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Cynewald of Mercia</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kuniwald</i>, from <i>&#42;kuni</i> (“lineage, clan”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wald</i> (“might, authority”). Equivalent to <i>cynn</i> +&lrm; <i>weald</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Kuniald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Cyneweald</li></ol><p>From <i>Cyneweald</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>cyne-</i> (“royal”) + <i>weard</i> (“guard”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kuniwīg</i>, from <i>&#42;kuni</i> (“lineage, clan”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wīg</i> (“battle”). Equivalent to <i>cynn</i> +&lrm; <i>wīġ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Chuniwīh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>cyne-</i> (“royal”) and <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state, nation, republic</li></ol><p><i>cyne-</i> (“royal”) +&lrm; <i>-wīse</i> (“state of, manner of”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crown, diadem</li></ol><p>From <i>cyne-</i> (“royal”) +&lrm; <i>wiþþe</i> (“band”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kuniwulf</i>, from <i>&#42;kuni</i> (“lineage, clan”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wulf</i> (“wolf”). Equivalent to <i>cynn</i> +&lrm; <i>wulf</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Kunulf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Kuniþegn</i>, from <i>&#42;kuni</i> (“lineage, clan”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;þegn</i> (“retainer, servant”). Equivalent to <i>cynn</i> +&lrm; <i>þeġn</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Cunidegan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kingly host</li><li>royal power</li><li>kingly glory or majesty</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>cyne-</i> +&lrm; <i>þrym</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>cyne-</i> (“royal”) +&lrm; <i>þrȳþ</i> (“power”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, variant of Cynferþ</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cyning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cyning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kingston (several settlements in England)</li></ol><p>From <i>cyninges</i> (“of a/the king”) +&lrm; <i>tūn</i> (“town”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, alternative form of <i>Cyneburg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cyning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>king</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kuning</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kuningaz</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵenh₁-</i> (“to procreate”). Equivalent to <i>cynn</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>cyning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>royal family or lineage</li></ol><p>Probably originally an alteration of <i>cynecynn</i>, which had the same usage and meaning. Equivalent to <i>cyning</i> (“king”) +&lrm; <i>cynn</i> (“family, lineage”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) Royal power or authority; sovereignty or rule.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kuningadōm</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kuningadōmaz</i>, equivalent to <i>cyning</i> +&lrm; <i>-dōm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female king; queen</li></ol><p>Possibly dissimilated from earlier <i>&#42;cyningen</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kuningini</i>; otherwise, equivalent to <i>cyning</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) marjoram (<i>Origanum majorana</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>cyninges</i> (“king's”) +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i> (“plant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Conisbrough (a town in South Yorkshire, England)</li></ol><p><i>cyninges</i> (“king's”) +&lrm; <i>burg</i> (“fortress”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a royal feast</li></ol><p><i>cyning</i> (“king”) +&lrm; <i>ġereord</i> (“feast”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kingdom</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kuningarīkī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kuningarīkiją</i>, equivalent to <i>cyning</i> +&lrm; <i>-rīċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind</li><li>race, species</li><li>people, tribe, nation</li><li>family (especially regarding a lineage)</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) gender</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) natural gender</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kuni</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kunją</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>kin</i>, Old Saxon <i>kunni</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;kunni</i>, Old High German <i>kunni</i>, Old Norse <i>kyn</i>, Gothic <i>𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌹</i> (kuni). The Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek <i>γένος</i> (génos), Latin <i>genus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, alternative form of <i>Cyneburg</i> &lsqb;c.670 <small>CE</small>&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Cynric of Wessex</li></ol><p>Adapted from original Primitive Irish <i>Cunorix</i>, from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;Kunorīxs</i> (“hound+king”); by folk-etymology as if <i>cyne-</i> (“royal”) + <i>rīċ</i> (“king”) ultimately from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;rīxs</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Cynrīc</li></ol><p>From <i>Cynrīc</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>generation</li><li>family</li><li>kind</li></ol><p>From <i>cynn</i> (“kind”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cynewulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċipp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cȳpe</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>ċīepa</i></li></ol><p>See <i>cȳpe</i>.</p><p>See <i>ċīepa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċīepan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a basket or hamper</li></ol><p>Origin Uncertain.</p><p>Perhaps from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kūpijā</i> (“bucket”), diminutive of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kūpu</i> (“tub, vat”). <br>Alternatively, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kīpā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kīpǭ</i>, <i>&#42;kippǭ</i> (“basket”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gey-</i> (“to bend, twist”); related to Middle Dutch <i>kiepe</i> (“basket”), Middle Low German <i>kîpe</i> (“basket”), Old Norse <i>kippa</i> (“basket”). Compare also Norwegian <i>kaup</i> (“crate, wooden box”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cypren</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċēapmann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċipp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of copper; coppery</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) copper</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kupprīn</i> (“made of copper”). Equivalent to <i>copor</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċbrǣċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċhālgung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċhata</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċweard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċþeġnung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċþingere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>choice; free will; election</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kuʀi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiern</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an instrument used for cutting</li><li>a cutting (off)</li><li>what is cut off</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kurbi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kurbiz</i>, from <i>&#42;kerbaną</i> (“to carve”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gerbʰ-</i> (“to scratch”) (compare Old Prussian <i>gīrbin</i> (“number”), Old Church Slavonic <i>жрѣбии</i> (žrěbii, “lot, tallymark”), Ancient Greek <i>γράφειν</i> (gráphein, “to scratch, etch”)). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>kerf</i> (“incision, cutting, notch”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċbōc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċbōt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċdōr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċfriþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċgang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċġemāna</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċġeriht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċgriþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċhād</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċhālgung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċhata</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċmǣrsung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċpæþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċsang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċsangere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċsċeatt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċsōcn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċsang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċtīd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ċiriċtūn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċwaru</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċweard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċweard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċwǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiriċþeġnung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charlock</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċeorlisċnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noise, shout, clamor, uproar</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;karmiz</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kernel</li><li>a hard concretion in the flesh, an indurated gland or strumous swelling</li><li>a tonsil</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kurnil</i>. Equivalent to <i>corn</i> +&lrm; <i>-el</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċierr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċirse</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kirtle, coat, gown</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>curtus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to beautify, make beautiful</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make elegant</li></ol><p>From <i>cyrten</i> (“fair, comely”) +&lrm; <i>-lǣċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċīese</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċīese</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċeosol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċīeslybb</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kiss</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kussijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kussijaną</i> (“to kiss”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>kessa</i>, Old Saxon <i>kussian</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;kussen</i>, Old High German <i>kussen</i>, Old Norse <i>kyssa</i>. Gothic <i>𐌺𐌿𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (kukjan) ← Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kukjaną</i> may be somehow related, though it differs from the expected cognate <i>*kussjan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>cyssaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>choice</li><li>choicest, best</li><li>excellence, good quality</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kustiz</i>, <i>&#42;kustuz</i> (“choice, trial”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵéwstus</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>cust</i> (Dutch <i>kust</i>), Old High German <i>chust</i>, Old Norse <i>kostr</i> (“choose, chance, opportunity, value”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get, procure</li><li>to obtain the value of</li><li>to spend</li><li>to lay out</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>cyst</i> (“choice”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>choice, preferred, select</li><li>charitable, generous, liberal</li><li>good, virtuous</li></ol><p>From <i>cyst</i> (choice) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kite</li><li>bittern</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kūtijō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kūts</i> (“bird of prey”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) hut, cabin</li></ol><p>For earlier <i>&#42;ċīete</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kautijā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kautijǭ</i> (“hut, cottage”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gewd-</i> (“to stretch, curve, vault”).</p><p>Related to <i>cote</i>, though the exact details are unclear.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċietel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make known: tell, inform, announce, reveal, let know</li><li>to declare</li><li>to state</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kunþijan</i> (“to make known”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>known</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kunþīg</i>, equivalent to <i>cȳþ</i> (“information, knowledge, science”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become known</li><li>to make known, publish</li><li>reveal; expose, bewray</li></ol><p>From <i>cȳþ</i> (“knowledge”) +&lrm; <i>-lǣċan</i> (“process or activity”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cȳþþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knowledge</li><li>country which is known to oneself; native land, home</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kunþiþō</i>, from <i>&#42;kunþ-</i> (“known”). Cognate with Old High German <i>chundida</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Proto-Brythonic</li><li>a famous Northumbrian poet, the author of Cædmon's Hymn</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;Katumandus</i> (“battle+pony”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-Brythonic <i>&#42;Kaduwallọn</i>, cognate with Middle Welsh <i>Cadwallon</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) halter</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>capistrum</i>, from <i>capi(ō)</i> (“seize”) +&lrm; <i>-trum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bit, muzzle, gag</li></ol><p>Cognate with the second element of Swedish <i>munkavel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>key</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kaiju</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>key bearer</li></ol><p><i>cǣġ</i> (“key”) +&lrm; <i>-bora</i> (“bearer”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċealf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cempa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>scribal abbreviation of <i>Cantwara burg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hat</li><li>hood</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kappijā</i>, diminutive of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kappā</i> (“covering, hood, cloak”), from Late Latin <i>cappa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chirp, chirk</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċiern</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cress, watercress</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;krassjō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;krasjô</i> (“cress”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cressy</li></ol><p>From <i>cærse</i> +&lrm; <i>-iht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċīese</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>&#42;cēċel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cōlnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cwēn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cenrēd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cēnweald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doe, female fallow deer</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;daihā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₁(y)-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dāg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>becoming, fit, suitable, fitting, proper</li><li>convenient</li></ol><p>Originally from the past participle of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dabaną</i> (“to pass, happen, occur, fit”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰh₂ebʰ-</i> (“appropriate, good, fitting”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be fitting, becoming or seemly, suit</li></ol><p>From <i>dafen</i> (“fit, suitable”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>, originally the past participle of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dabaną</i> (“to happen, be fitting”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>becoming, fit, suitable</li></ol><p><i>dafen</i> (“suitable”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dafenian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dough</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;daigaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeyǵʰ-</i> (“to knead, form, mold”). Compare Old High German <i>teig</i> (German <i>Teig</i>), Old Norse <i>deig</i> (Danish <i>dej</i>, Swedish <i>deg</i>), Gothic <i>𐌳𐌰𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (daigs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dagena</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dawn</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dagēn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dagāną</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>dagen</i> (Dutch <i>dagen</i>), Old High German <i>tagēn</i> (German <i>tagen</i>), Old Norse <i>daga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dawn, daybreak</li></ol><p>From <i>dagian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dāg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>division</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dailą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clasp, buckle, brooch, bracelet</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dalk</i>, <i>&#42;dolk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dalkaz</i>, <i>&#42;dulkaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰalg-</i>, <i>&#42;dʰalk-</i> (“pricking, stabbing, or cutting tool; needle, pin; knife”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰelg-</i>, <i>&#42;dʰelk-</i> (“to stick, prick, stab”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Daniel</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Dāniēl</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Δᾱνῑήλ</i> (Dānīḗl), from the Biblical Hebrew <i>דָּנִיֵּאל</i> (Daniyél, “Daniel”, literally “God is [my] judge”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Daniel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dopfugol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>daroþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>daroþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lurk</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>daru</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dart</li><li>spear; javelin</li><li>weapon</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;darōþu</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>darraðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>injury; hurt, harm, damage</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;darō</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>tara</i> and Dutch <i>deer</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English David.</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>Dāvīd</i>, from Koine Greek <i>Δαυῑ̈́δ</i> (Dauī̈́d), from Biblical Hebrew <i>דָּוִד</i> (dāwiḏ).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dead</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;daud</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;daudaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>dād</i>, Old Saxon <i>dōd</i>, Old High German <i>tōt</i>, Old Norse <i>dauðr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌳𐌰𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (dauþs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēaþberende</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stillborn</li></ol><p>Could date back to Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;daudaburan</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>doodgeboren</i> and German <i>totgeboren</i>, though because they have <i>-ge-</i> the correspondence isn't exact. Equivalent to <i>dēad</i> (“dead”) +&lrm; <i>boren</i> (“born”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;daudēn</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>tōdēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mortal</li></ol><p>From <i>dēad</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mortality</li></ol><p>From <i>dēadlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēadlīċnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>dēadlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deaf</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;daub</i>.</p><p>Germanic cognates include Old Frisian <i>dāf</i>, Old Saxon <i>dōf</i> (Low German <i>dow</i>), Old High German <i>toub</i> (German <i>taub</i>), Old Norse <i>daufr</i> (Swedish <i>döv</i>). The Indo-European root is also the source of Greek <i>τυφλός</i> (tyflós, “blind”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deafness</li></ol><p>From <i>dēaf</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>. Compare Gothic <i>𐌳𐌰𐌿𐌱𐌴𐌹</i> (daubei, “callousness, hardness (of heart)”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colour; hue, tinge</li><li>dye</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;daugu</i>, from <i>&#42;daugan</i> (“to hide”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēagian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dye</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>dēag</i> (“dye”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>secret</li><li>hidden, obscure</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) dark</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;daugul</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>secretly</li></ol><p>From <i>dēagol</i> (“secret”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a secret</li><li>a secret place</li><li>a recess</li><li>secrecy</li></ol><p>From <i>dēagol</i> (“secret”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i> (“-ness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēag</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēag</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>proud, eminent</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>deorf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>secretly, privately, clandestinely</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;darnungō</i>, equivalent to <i>&#42;dearnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-unga</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>darnungo</i> (“secretly, maliciously”), Old High German <i>tarningon</i> (“secretly, hiddenly, in secret”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dew</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dawwaz</i>, <i>&#42;dawwą</i> (“dew”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewh₂-</i> (“smoke, haze”). Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>dau</i>, <i>dou</i> (“dew”), Old Frisian <i>dāw</i> (“dew”), Old Saxon <i>dau</i> (“dew”), Old High German <i>tou</i> (“dew”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dewy</li></ol><p>From <i>dēaw</i> (“dew”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ringworm</li></ol><p>From <i>dēaw</i> (“dew”) +&lrm; <i>wyrm</i> (“worm”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>dēaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēaþsleġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>death</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dauþu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dauþuz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰautus</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>dāth</i>, Old Saxon <i>dōth</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;dōth</i>, Old High German <i>tōd</i>, Old Norse <i>dauðr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌳𐌰𐌿𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (dauþus).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēaþbedd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deathbed</li><li>burial plot; grave</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dauþabadi</i>, equivalent to <i>dēaþ</i> +&lrm; <i>bedd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deadly, death-bearing</li></ol><p>From <i>dēaþ</i> (“death”) +&lrm; <i>berende</i> (“bearing”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deadly, death-bearing</li></ol><p>From <i>dēaþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>day of one's death</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dauþudagaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>dēaþ</i> (“death”) +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i> (“day”). Compare German <i>Todestag</i> and Swedish <i>dödsdag</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fear of death</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēaþlīeġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deadly, mortal, subject to death</li><li>grievous</li><li>dead</li></ol><p>From <i>dēaþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>tōdlīh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mortality, liable to death</li><li>the mortal life</li></ol><p>From <i>dēaþlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>death flame</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>dēaþ</i> +&lrm; <i>līeġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a death-chamber; sepulchre, tomb</li></ol><p>From <i>dēaþ</i> +&lrm; <i>ræċed</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capital crime</li></ol><p>From <i>dēaþ</i> +&lrm; <i>sċyld</i> (“guilt”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>condemned to death, guilty of a capital crime</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>dēaþ</i> (“death”) +&lrm; <i>sċyldiġ</i> (“guilty”) or <i>dēaþsċyld</i> (“capital crime”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i> (“-y”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a "death-hall;" Hell</li></ol><p>From <i>dēaþ</i> (“death”) +&lrm; <i>sele</i> (“hall”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deathblow</li></ol><p>From <i>dēaþ</i> (“death”) +&lrm; <i>sleġe</i> (“blow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>death-weary</li></ol><p><i>dēaþ</i> +&lrm; <i>wēriġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;poetic) "abode of death"</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>dēaþ</i> (“death”) +&lrm; <i>wīc</i> (“village, abode”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fate, the events of one's death</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>dēaþ</i> (“death”) +&lrm; <i>wyrd</i> (“fate, destiny”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smear, daub</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one in charge of ten monks</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dōkijan</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dekan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>December</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) to inflect or decline</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>dēclīnāre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) declinable</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>declīniende</i> (“declining, present participle of <i>declīnian</i>”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) declension</li></ol><p><i>declīnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>suitable, befitting, proper, meek, gentle, kind</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>kindly</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dōbī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dōbiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Devon, Devonshire (a historical county of England)</li></ol><p>Literally "County of the Devonians" or "County of Devon," more idiomatically "Devon County."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Devonians</li><li>Devon</li></ol><p>Derived from an early medieval Celtic name for the <i>Dumnonii</i>, a British tribe that once lived in the area.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Defena sċīr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Devonian, (<i>relational</i>) Devon</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a Devonian</li></ol><p><i>Defenas</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dæġ</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>dēag</i></li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēag</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>dēog</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēg</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>dēag</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēag</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>digging, excavation</li><li>that which is dug: trench, quarry, canal</li></ol><p>From the verb <i>delfan</i> (“to delve, dig, dig out, burrow, bury”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;delban</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;delbaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰelbʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig</li><li>to mine</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;delban</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;delbaną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>delva</i>, Old Saxon <i>&#42;delƀan</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;delvan</i>, and Old High German <i>&#42;telban</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>digger</li><li>miner</li></ol><p><i>delfan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>digging</li><li>mining</li></ol><p><i>delfan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dell, vale</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dalljā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;daljō</i> (“a hollow”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nipple</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;delu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;delō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₁(y)-</i> (“suckle”). Cognate with Old High German <i>tila</i>, Old Norse <i>dilkr</i> (dialectal Swedish <i>däl</i>), Ancient Greek <i>θηλή</i> (thēlḗ, “nipple”), Latin <i>fēmina</i>, Russian <i>дои́ть</i> (doítʹ), Latvian <i>det</i>, Scottish Gaelic <i>deoghail</i> (“suck”) and Breton <i>denaff</i> (“suck”) and Breton <i>denañ</i> (“suck”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>judge</li></ol><p><i>dēman</i> (“to judge”) +&lrm; <i>-a</i> (agent-noun suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to judge</li><li>to sentence [with <i>tō</i> + dative = "to something"]</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dōmijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dōmijaną</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>dēma</i>, Old Saxon <i>dōmian</i>, Dutch <i>doemen</i>, Old High German <i>tuomen</i>, Old Norse <i>dǿma</i> (Danish <i>dømme</i>, Icelandic <i>dæma</i>), Gothic <i>𐌳𐍉𐌼𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (dōmjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) judge</li></ol><p>From <i>dēman</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a judge</li></ol><p>From <i>dēman</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Danelaw</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Denemearc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dengan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>valley</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;danją</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰen-</i> (“low ground”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Dene)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Danes</li><li>Denmark</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Danī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;daniz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>danir</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p><i>Dene</i> (“the Danes”) +&lrm; <i>beald</i> (“bold”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Danigīsl</i>, from <i>&#42;Dani</i> (“Dane”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;gīsl</i> (“hostage”). Equivalent to <i>Dene</i> +&lrm; <i>gīsl</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Thangelt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Denmark (a country in Northern Europe)</li></ol><p>From <i>Dene</i> +&lrm; <i>mearc</i>, a calque of Old Norse <i>Danmǫrk</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>Dene</i> (“Dane”) +&lrm; <i>mōd</i> (“mind”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Daniwald</i>, from <i>&#42;Dani</i> (“Dane”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wald</i> (“might, authority”). Equivalent to <i>Dene</i> +&lrm; <i>weald</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Teneolt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Denewulf; an Anglo-Saxon Bishop of Winchester.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Daniwulf</i>, from <i>&#42;Dani</i> (“Dane”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wulf</i> (“wolf”). Equivalent to <i>Dene</i> +&lrm; <i>wulf</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Thanolf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) To beat or strike.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dangijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dangijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Danish, Norse</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a Dane or Norseman</li></ol><p><i>Dene</i> (“the Danes”) +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i> (“-ish”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>den or lair</li><li>a woodland pasture for swine</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dani</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become slippery</li></ol><p>Unknown. Perhaps from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dannjōn</i>, from <i>&#42;dani</i> (“plain, pasture, mire, wallowing hole, smooth or slick surface”), whence also Old English <i>denn</i> (“den, lair”). If so, then equivalent to <i>denn</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Devil, Satan</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a demon, devil</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;diubul</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diabolical; fiendish</li></ol><p>From <i>dēofol</i> +&lrm; <i>-cund</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idolatry or devil worship</li><li>idol</li></ol><p><i>dēofol</i> +&lrm; <i>ġield</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idolater</li><li>devil worshiper</li></ol><p>From <i>dēofol</i> +&lrm; <i>ġielda</i>, modeled after <i>dēofolġield</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēofolġield</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēofolġielda</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēofolġield</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēofolġielda</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>demonic</li><li>Satanic</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;diubulalīk</i>. Equivalent to <i>dēofol</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>devilishly, furiously, cruelly</li></ol><p>From <i>deofollīċ</i> (“devilish”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i> or <i>deofol</i> (“devil, demon”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>possessed by a demon</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;diubulaseuk</i>. Equivalent to <i>dēofol</i> (“demon”) +&lrm; <i>sēoc</i> (“sick”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demonic possession</li></ol><p>From <i>dēofol</i> +&lrm; <i>sēocnes</i> or <i>dēofolsēoc</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>, literally "devil sickness."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēofolsēocnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēofol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēagol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēog</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suck</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dajjaną</i> (“to suckle”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₁(y)-</i> (“to suck milk; to suckle”). Akin to Old High German <i>tāen</i> (“to suck”) (Middle High German <i>tīen</i>, <i>dīen</i> (“to suck, suckle”), Swedish <i>dägga</i>, <i>dia</i> (“to suckle”), Danish <i>dægge</i>, <i>die</i> (“to suckle”), Gothic <i>𐌳𐌰𐌳𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (daddjan, “suckle”), Old English <i>delu</i> (“nipple”), Old English <i>dā</i> (“doe”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deep</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;deup</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>diāp</i>, Old Saxon <i>diop</i>, Old High German <i>tiof</i>, Old Norse <i>djúpr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌳𐌹𐌿𐍀𐍃</i> (diups).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>deeply</li></ol><p><i>dēop</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deeply</li></ol><p>From <i>dēop</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>deeply, thoroughly, profoundly</li></ol><p>From <i>dēop</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deepness, depth</li><li>a deep place, somewhere deep, an abyss</li></ol><p>From <i>dēop</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēopnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>dēop</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>dēop</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild animal, beast</li><li>deer</li><li>reindeer</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brave, bold as a wild beast</li><li>heavy, severe, dire, vehement</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;deuʀ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;deuzą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewsóm</i>.</p><p>Germanic cognates include Old Frisian <i>diār</i>, Old Saxon <i>dior</i>, Old Dutch <i>dior</i>, <i>dier</i>, Old High German <i>tior</i>, Old Norse <i>dýr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌳𐌹𐌿𐍃</i> (dius). Further cognates include Lithuanian <i>dvė̃sti</i> and Russian <i>душа́</i> (dušá).</p><p>From Etymology 1.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Deor)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Dēorbȳ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Dēorbȳ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Derby</li></ol><p>From <i>dēor, dēora</i> (“animal, animals'”) +&lrm; <i>bȳ</i> (“dwelling, habitation”), literally “habitation of the animals”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Derbyshire</li></ol><p>From <i>Dēorbȳ</i> +&lrm; <i>sċīr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;derk</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark gray</li></ol><p>From <i>deorc</i> (“dark”) +&lrm; <i>grǣġ</i> (“gray”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>deorcegrǣġ</i></li></ol><p>From <i>deorc</i> +&lrm; <i>grǣw</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to darken, grow dark</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;derkōn</i>. Equivalent to <i>deorc</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>darkly</li><li>horridly</li></ol><p>From <i>deorc</i> (“dark”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darkness, obscurity</li></ol><p>From <i>deorc</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>deorcnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>animalkind, beastkind, animals, kind of animal</li></ol><p>From <i>dēor</i> +&lrm; <i>cynn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dīere</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dīere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) of or belonging to a wild beast, bestial, animal</li></ol><p>From <i>dēor</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>labor, toil</li><li>trouble, tribulation</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>working; striving; endeavouring</li><li>bold</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;derbaz</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;derbaną</i> (“to work, perish”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰerHbʰ-</i> (“to work, perish, die”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>park or enclosure for deer</li></ol><p>From <i>dēor</i> (“wild animal, deer”) +&lrm; <i>falod</i> (“enclosure for sheep”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to labor</li><li>to be in peril, perish</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;derban</i> (“to work, perish”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of beast or animal skins</li></ol><p>From <i>dēor</i> +&lrm; <i>fellen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>game protection</li></ol><p>From <i>dēor</i> (“animal”) +&lrm; <i>friþ</i> (“peace, sanctuary”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gate for animals</li></ol><p>From <i>dēor</i> (“animal”) +&lrm; <i>ġeat</i> (“gate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dereham (town in Norfolk)</li></ol><p>From <i>dēor</i> (“wild animal”) +&lrm; <i>hām</i> (“home”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>dēor</i> (“animal”) +&lrm; <i>lāf</i> (“legacy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>preciously, dearly, worthily, richly, sincerely, acceptably</li></ol><p>From <i>dēore</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>diurlīko</i>, Old High German <i>tiurlīhho</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>dēorlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dīerling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>dēor</i> (“beast”) and <i>mōd</i> (“mind, heart, spirit”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunting net</li></ol><p>From <i>dēor</i> (“animal”) +&lrm; <i>net</i> (“net”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēornet</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>dēor</i> (“beast”) and <i>nōþ</i> (“daring”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Darlington (a large market town in County Durham, England).</li></ol><p>Attested as <i>Dearthingtunc</i>, equivalent to <i>Dēornōþ</i> (a male given name) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> +&lrm; <i>tūn</i> (“town”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>deornunga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name.</li></ol><p><i>dēore</i> (“dear, precious”) +&lrm; <i>siġe</i> (“victory”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Deuʀaswinþ</i>, from <i>&#42;deuʀ</i> (“animal, beast”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;swinþ</i> (“strong”). Equivalent to <i>dēor</i> +&lrm; <i>swiþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Teorswint</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deer park: an enclosed area containing wildlife, set up for nobles to hunt in</li></ol><p>From <i>dēor</i> (“wild animal”) +&lrm; <i>tūn</i> (“enclosure”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Deuʀawald</i>, from <i>&#42;deuʀ</i> (“animal, beast”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wald</i> (“might, authority”). Equivalent to <i>dēor</i> +&lrm; <i>weald</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Derold</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Derwent (a county, a Yorkshire in Derbyshire, Cumbria and County Durham)</li></ol><p>Of Brythonic origin, possibly from Proto-Brythonic <i>&#42;Deruentiū</i> (found in Latin as <i>Deruentiō</i>), meaning "forest of oak trees," from <i>&#42;dar</i> (“oak”). Alternative Brittonic origin suggests a compound corresponding to modern Welsh <i>dŵr</i> (“water”) + <i>gent</i> (“clear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>dēore</i> (“dear”) and <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Deuʀawulf</i>, from <i>&#42;deuʀ</i> (“animal, beast”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wulf</i> (“wolf”). Equivalent to <i>dēor</i> +&lrm; <i>wulf</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Tierolf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>precious, valuable</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>dēorwurþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>smæl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>dēor</i> (“animal”) +&lrm; <i>wynn</i> (“joy”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēorwurþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Sri Lanka</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ταπροβάνη</i> (Taprobánē)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Deira, a medieval English kingdom</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Deirans (people of Deira)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>derian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>derienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hurt, injure ( <i>+ dative</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;darjan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>noxious, nocent, harmful</li></ol><p>From <i>derian</i> +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>derian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>derienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dīerling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>deornunga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deacon</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>diāconus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>διᾱ́κονος</i> (diā́konos, “servant”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ditch; trench; dike</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dīk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dīkaz</i> (compare Old Norse <i>díki</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeygʷ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bank or mound earth; build a dike</li><li>to dig a ditch</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dīkōn</i>, from <i>&#42;dīk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dīkaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;daudijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;daudijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>dēad</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐌳𐌰𐌿𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (dauþjan) and Old Norse <i>deyða</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēagol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dip</li><li>to baptize</li><li>to deepen or increase</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;diupijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the deep</li><li>depth</li><li>the sea</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>costly, expensive</li><li>dear, precious</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>expensively, for a lot of money</li><li>dearly</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;diurī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;diurijaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>diūre</i>, Old Saxon <i>diuri</i>, Old Dutch <i>diuri</i>, Old High German <i>tiuri</i>, Old Norse <i>dýrr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a favourite; darling</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;diuriling</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;diurijalingaz</i>, equivalent to <i>dīere</i> +&lrm; <i>-ling</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>dýrlingr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to keep secret</li><li>to conceal, hide</li><li>to restrain</li><li>to repress</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>secret, hidden</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a secret</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;darnī</i> (“hidden”).</p><p>Substantified from the above.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dublin (city in Ireland)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēagolnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide (something)</li></ol><p>From the same root as <i>dēagol</i>, <i>dīegol</i> (“hidden”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēagol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēagollīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēagolnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a setting in order; disposing; contriving; disposition; conduct; consultation; deliberation; purpose; order; arrangement</li><li>a saying; dictum; oracle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;diht</i>.</p><p>Probably from Latin <i>dicta</i>, plural of <i>dictum</i>. Compare Old High German <i>dihta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dictate, direct</li><li>to compose (a piece of writing)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dihtijan</i> (“to order, instruct”). Equivalent to <i>diht</i> (“order, instruction”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set in order; arrange; appoint; compose</li><li>to order; regulate; direct</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dihtōn</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>dihtōn</i>, <i>tihtōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to order; dispose</li></ol><p>From <i>diht</i> +&lrm; <i>-nian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dill (<i>Anethum graveolens</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;deliz</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blot out, wipe off, erase</li><li>to destroy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;diligōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dilegian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dimm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dungeon</li></ol><p>From <i>dimm</i> (“dim”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dim</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dimm</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dimmaz</i>. Compare Icelandic <i>dimmur</i> (“dark”), <i>dimma</i> (“darkness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be or become dim</li><li>to darken, obscure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dimmōną</i>, equivalent to <i>dimm</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēofol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēofol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēofol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēagolnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēop</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dīerling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dīerling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plate, dish</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;disk</i>, from Latin <i>discus</i>, originally from Ancient Greek <i>δίσκος</i> (dískos, “disk, quoit, platter”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disciple</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>discipulus</i> (“pupil, student”). More at <i>disciple</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dysiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dysiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distaff</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēofol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dopfugol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bastard</li><li>mongrel, hybrid</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dōk</i> (“cloth, rag”), with a change in meaning from "cloth, rag" to "something worthless". Compare similar semantic development in the verb <i>dēċan</i> (“to daub", also "to smear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dock (genus <i>Rumex</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dokkā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dukkǭ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰew-</i> (“dark”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>late&#32;hapax legomenon</i>) a powerful kind of dog, a hound</li></ol><p>Unknown origin. There are several theories, none with much evidence. Possibly a diminutive of <i>dox</i> (“dark, dusky”) +&lrm; <i>-ga</i> (diminutive suffix) (similar to <i>frox</i> → <i>frocga</i>); or a clipping of a construction such as <i>&#42;hundocga</i>, formed from <i>hund</i> in analogy with terms such as <i>frocga</i> or <i>&#42;picga</i>; or a diminutive of <i>&#42;docce</i> (“stump, dock, doll”). See <i>dog</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bastard child</li></ol><p>From <i>dōc</i> +&lrm; <i>-incel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēmere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dover (A town, cpar and major port in Kent, England)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Dubris</i>, from Brythonic <i>Dubras</i> (“the waters”), from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;dubros</i> (“water”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰubrós</i> (“dark”), from <i>&#42;dʰewb-</i> (“deep”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>docga</i></li></ol><p>See docga.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;poetic</i>) day</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dōg-</i> (“day”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>dōgora</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dohtor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dyhtiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daughter</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dohter</i>. Cognate with German <i>Tochter</i> and Dutch <i>dochter</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grandson (lit., "daughter's son", specifically the son of one's daughter)</li></ol><p>From <i>dohtor</i> (“daughter”) +&lrm; <i>sunu</i> (“son”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dohtor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foolish</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dol</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dulaz</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old High German <i>tol</i> (German <i>toll</i>), Old Saxon <i>dol</i> (Low Low German <i>doll</i>), Dutch <i>dol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dalc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wound, gash</li><li>scar</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dolg</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dulgą</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>dolch</i>, Old High German <i>tolg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) compensation or a fine paid for wounding another</li></ol><p>From <i>dolg</i> (“wound”) +&lrm; <i>bōt</i> (“compensation”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) foolish pride, vainglory</li></ol><p>From <i>dol</i> (“foolish”) +&lrm; <i>ġielp</i> (“boast”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dolġielp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a blow that wounds (as opposed to a <i>dēaþsleġe</i> (“a killing blow”))</li></ol><p>From <i>dolg</i> (“would”) +&lrm; <i>sleġe</i> (“hit, stike, blow”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scar</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>dolg</i> (“wound”) +&lrm; <i>swæþ</i> (“trace”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dolg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dolgswæþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>judgment</li><li>sentence</li><li>law, statute</li><li>fame, repute</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dōm</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>dōm</i>, Old Saxon <i>dōm</i>, Old High German <i>tuom</i>, Old Norse <i>dómr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌳𐍉𐌼𐍃</i> (dōms). The Germanic source was from a stem verb originally meaning ‘to place, to set’ (a sense-development also found in Latin <i>statutum</i>, Ancient Greek <i>θέμις</i> (thémis)).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dōmi</i>, first-person singular of <i>&#42;dōną</i> (“to do”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>book of laws, lawbook</li></ol><p>From <i>dom</i> +&lrm; <i>boc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blessed with power; mighty, noble, potent</li></ol><p>From <i>dōm</i> +&lrm; <i>ēadiġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>judge</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>dōm</i> (“judgement, sentence”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>dómari</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a courthouse or another place of judgment</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>dōm</i> +&lrm; <i>-ern</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dōmesdæġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>judgement day</li></ol><p>From <i>dōmes</i> (“of judgement”) +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i> (“day”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>dōm</i> (“judgement, law, fame”) + <i>friþ</i> (“peace”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eager for fame, for justice</li><li>ambitious, virtuous</li></ol><p>From <i>dōm</i> (“fame, justice”) +&lrm; <i>ġeorn</i> (“eager”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Dōmahari</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Dōmaharjaz</i>, from <i>&#42;dōmaz</i> (“judgement”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;harjaz</i> (“army”). Equivalent to <i>dōm</i> +&lrm; <i>here</i>. Cognate with Early Medieval Latin <i>Dōmarius</i> (via Old High German), Old Norse <i>Dómarr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a house where a court or assembly is held</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dōmahūsą</i>, equivalent to <i>dōm</i> +&lrm; <i>hūs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to praise, glorify</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dōmōn</i> (“to dispense justice”), equivalent to <i>dōm</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>dōmia</i> (“to grant”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inglorious, powerless</li></ol><p>From <i>dōm</i> (“might, power, glory”) +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seat or platform where a judge sits: seat of judgment, tribunal</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dōmaseþl</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>dōmseđal</i>, Old High German <i>tuomsedal</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>dōm</i> (“judgement, law, fame”) + <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do</li><li>to make, cause</li><li>to put</li><li>to add</li><li>to take off, remove</li><li>to treat someone <i>(+ dative)</i> a certain way</li><li>to give (+dative)</li><li>(<i>pro-verb</i>) Used to refer back to an earlier verb to avoid repetition.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dōn</i> (“to do”). The exact development of past tense forms <i>dyde</i>, <i>dydest</i>, and <i>dydon</i> is unexplained, for such forms have <i>-y-</i> instead of expected <i>*-e-</i> (<i>*dede</i>, <i>*dedest</i>, <i>*dedon</i>) from Proto-Germanic past stem <i>*ded-</i>/<i>*dēd-</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>dwā</i>, Old Saxon <i>dōn</i>, Old Dutch <i>duon</i>, Old High German <i>tuon</i>. Non-Germanic cognates include Ancient Greek <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi), Latin <i>faciō</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>дѣти</i> (děti).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take off (remove)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put on</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>indeclinable</i>) Danube</li></ol><p>From Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;Dānowyos</i> (compare Welsh river name <i>Donwy</i>), an extended form of the river-name <i>&#42;Dānu</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;déh₂nu</i> (“river goddess”), akin to <i>&#42;dʰenh₂-</i> (“to set in motion; to flow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diving duck, moorhen, coot</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moorhen</li><li>fen duck</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;doppa</i> (“dipping”) +&lrm; <i>enid</i> (“duck”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dopfugol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A dipping-fowl or other such type of waterfowl; moorhen; gull.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dopfugl</i> (“dipping bird”), related to <i>&#42;daupijan</i>, <i>&#42;fugl</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>dopfugul</i> (“loon”). Compare also Old High German <i>tūhfogal</i> (“diving bird, dabchick”), Old English <i>fugoldoppe</i> (“dipping bird”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To dip, dip in, immerse</li></ol><p>Related to <i>diepan</i> (“to dip”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a large door, a gate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dor</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>dor</i>, Old High German <i>tor</i> (German <i>Tor</i> (“gate”)), Gothic <i>𐌳𐌰𐌿𐍂</i> (daur). The Germanic word also existed with the stem <i>&#42;durz</i> (see Old English <i>duru</i>, German <i>Tür</i>). Indo-European cognates include Greek <i>θυρα</i> (thyra), Latin <i>foris</i>, Lithuanian <i>dùrys</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>двьрь</i> (dvĭrĭ) (Russian <i>дверь</i> (dverʹ)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bumblebee</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;durô</i> (“bumblebee, humming insect”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰer-</i>, <i>&#42;dʰreh₁n-</i> (“bee, hornet, drone”). Related to Middle Low German <i>dorne</i> (“bumblebee”), Middle Dutch <i>dorne</i> (“bumblebee”), Dutch <i>dar</i> (“drone”), Old English <i>drān</i> (“drone”). More at drone, dor.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the village of Dorchester in Oxfordshire</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Dorceċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the people of Dorset</li><li>Dorset, Dorsetshire</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the town of Dorchester, Dorset</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Dornsǣte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Canterbury</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dot, speck, spot</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dott</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;duttaz</i> (“wisp”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark, swarthy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;duskaz</i> (“dark, smoky”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰuh₂s-</i> (compare Old Irish <i>donn</i> (“dark”), Latin <i>fuscus</i> (“dark, dusky”), Sanskrit <i>धूसर</i> (dhūsara, “dust-colored”)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewh₂-</i> (“smoke, mist, haze”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become dark in color</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>dox</i> (“dark”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dragon</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;drakō</i>. Alternative form <i>dræce</i>. Compare Old West Norse <i>dreki</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trouble; affliction</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drakō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw, drag</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dragan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male bee, drone</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;drānu</i> (“drone”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>drān</i> (Low German <i>drōne</i>), dialectal German <i>Drohne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drain</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joy, pleasure, gladness, rejoicing</li><li>that which causes merriment: musical instrument, music, melody, song, harmony</li><li>frenzy, ecstasy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;draum</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;draumaz</i>, whence also Old Frisian <i>drām</i>, Old Saxon <i>drōm</i> (“joy, music, dream”), Old High German <i>troum</i>, Old Norse <i>draumr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the art of music</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>drēam</i> +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>musician</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>drēam</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>joyless, sad</li></ol><p>From <i>drēam</i> (joy, pleasure) +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>drēam</i> +&lrm; <i>wulf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to annoy, trouble; torment</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to torture</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drakjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stir up, agitate, move</li><li>to trouble, disturb, afflict</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;drōbijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drōbijaną</i> (“to disturb”); equivalent to <i>drōf</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>drōƀjan</i>, Old Dutch <i>druoven</i> (Dutch <i>droeven</i>), Old High German <i>truoben</i> (German <i>trüben</i>), Gothic <i>𐌳𐍂𐍉𐌱𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (drōbjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vexation, affliction, tribulation</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>drēfed</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disturber, troubler</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>drēfan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disturbance</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>drēfan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>drēam</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>drīeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, diminutive of <i>Dreamwulf</i> &lsqb;9th C. <small>CE</small>&rsqb;</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>drēam</i> (“joy, melody”) +&lrm; <i>-ca</i> (<i>suffix of concrete nouns</i>)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drink</li><li>potion, draft</li><li>dose (of medicine, poison, etc.)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dranki</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drankiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give someone a drink</li><li>to drench</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drankijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drinking vessel; drinking-cup</li></ol><p>From <i>drenċ</i> +&lrm; <i>cuppe</i> and/or <i>drenċe</i> +&lrm; <i>cuppe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dreng</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) warrior; soldier</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;drangī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drangijaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>drȳcræft</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do, take, perform (a service, duty etc.)</li><li>to bear, suffer, undergo, endure</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dreugan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dreuganą</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐌳𐍂𐌹𐌿𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (driugan, “do military service”), Old Saxon <i>driogan</i> and Old Norse <i>drýgja</i> (“perform, practise”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>drēogaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drop, drip</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dreupan</i> (“to hang down, drop”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gore, blood</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dreuzaz</i>, <i>&#42;drauziz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰrews-</i> (“to break, break off, crumble”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>drōr</i>, Old High German <i>trōr</i>, and Old Norse and Icelandic <i>dreyri</i>. The historical sense is of something which ‘falls’: the Germanic base is also the source of Old English <i>drēosan</i> (“fall”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>drēoriġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sad</li><li>bloody, gory</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dreuzagaz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>drōrag</i>, Old High German <i>trūrag</i>, Old Norse <i>dreyrugr</i>. Equivalent to <i>drēor</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) sad-faced</li></ol><p>From <i>drēoriġ</i> (“sad”) +&lrm; <i>hlēor</i> (“face”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sadly</li></ol><p>From <i>drēoriġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sadness</li></ol><p>From <i>drēoriġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>drēoriġnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>drēoriġnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to fall</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to perish</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dreusan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dreusaną</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>driosan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌳𐍂𐌹𐌿𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (driusan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>drēoseþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike, beat, hit</li><li>to kill, slay</li><li>to overcome</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;drepan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drepaną</i> (“to beat, hit, strike, kill”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a stroke, deathblow</li><li>a slaying</li><li>a violent death</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dripi</i> (“strike, blow, deathblow”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drepaną</i> (“to strike, slay, kill”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰrebʰ-</i> (“to crush, grind, kill”).</p><p>Akin to Old Norse <i>drep</i> (“blow, deathblow”), Old Norse <i>dráp</i> (“deathblow”), Middle High German <i>tref</i> (“a strike, hit”), Old English <i>drepan</i> (“to kill”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>drȳcræftiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>drīġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rejoice, be merry</li><li>to play an instrument joyously</li><li>to sing a song joyfully</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;draumijaną</i>, equivalent to <i>drēam</i> (“joy”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to drive; force, pursue; drive (back); push</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to drive; rush with violence</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;drīban</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drībaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰreybʰ-</i> (“to drive, push”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>drīva</i> (West Frisian <i>driuwe</i>), Old Saxon <i>drīvan</i>, <i>drīƀan</i> (Low German <i>drieven</i>), Dutch <i>drijven</i>, Old High German <i>trīban</i> (German <i>treiben</i>), Old Norse <i>drífa</i> (Danish <i>drive</i>, Swedish <i>driva</i>), Gothic <i>𐌳𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (dreiban).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>drȳġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>drȳġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dryhten</i></li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Drihten)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Dryhten</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging to the lord, lordly</li></ol><p>From <i>drihten</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>according to the lord, by the lord</li></ol><p>From <i>drihten</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>drihtenlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dryhtguma</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lady, a woman of noble rank</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>drēam</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>drynċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a drink</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;drinkā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drinkǭ</i> (“a drink, drinking”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>drekka</i> (“a drink, drinking session”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drink</li><li>to inhale smoke</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;drinkan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drinkaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drinker</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drinkārijaz</i>, equivalent to <i>drincan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>drinker</i> and German <i>Trinker</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a drinking vessel, cup</li></ol><p>From <i>drinc</i> (“drink”) +&lrm; <i>fæt</i> (“vessel”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>drēoriġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>drihten</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>draffy, dreggy, dirty, troubled</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;drōbī</i> (“disturbed, cloudy, troubled”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>agitated, disturbed, irksome, sad</li></ol><p>From <i>drōf</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>truoblīh</i> (“turbid”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>drohtnian</i> (“to live (one's life)”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live</li><li>to live one's life, conduct oneself</li><li>to dwell</li><li>to inhabit</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>way of life</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>drohtnian</i> (“to live, conduct oneself”) +&lrm; <i>-noþ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>condition, life</li><li>conduct, actions</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>drohtian</i> (“to live”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drop</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dropō</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>&#42;dropa</i>, Old Saxon <i>dropo</i>, Old Dutch <i>dropo</i>, Old High German <i>tropfo</i>, Old Norse <i>dropi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drop</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dropōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drupōną</i>, equivalent to <i>dropa</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Cognate with Old Dutch <i>dropen</i>, Old High German <i>tropfōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>drop by drop</li></ol><p>From <i>dropa</i> +&lrm; <i>-mǣlum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dirt</li><li>dregs, grounds, sediment, lees</li><li>earwax</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰrā́ks</i> (“sediment, yeast”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dirt</li><li>dregs</li><li>earwax</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰrā́ks</i> (“sediment, yeast”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dryness</li><li>drought</li><li>a dry place; desert</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drūgōþuz</i>, equivalent to <i>drȳġan</i> +&lrm; <i>-aþ</i>. Cognate with Middle Low German <i>drȫgede</i>, <i>drügede</i>, <i>drȫchte</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become dry</li><li>to wither</li></ol><p>Related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drūgiz</i> (“dry”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dust</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>drunk</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a drunk</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be drunk</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to get or become drunk</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drunkanōną</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drunkanaz</i>, equivalent to <i>druncen</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>trunkanōn</i>, <i>trunkanēn</i> (“to be drunk; get drunk”), Old Norse <i>drukna</i> (“to be drowned”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>drūsian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to immerse; sink</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to become low; drop</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to become slow or inactive</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be languid</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to drowse</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drūsijaną</i>, <i>&#42;drūzijaną</i>. Akin to Old High German <i>trūrēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wizard, sorcerer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Brythonic language, from Proto-Brythonic <i>&#42;drüw</i>, from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;druwits</i> (“druid”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sorcery</li></ol><p>From <i>drȳ</i> (“wizard”) +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>skilled in sorcery or magic</li></ol><p>From <i>drȳcræft</i> (“sorcery”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>drȳcræftiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dry; make dry</li><li>to rub dry; wipe</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;draugijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dry</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drūgiz</i>, <i>&#42;draugiz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰerǵʰ-</i> (“to strengthen; become hard or solid”), from <i>&#42;dʰer-</i> (“to hold, hold fast, support”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) troop, army</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;poetic) men, mankind</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;druhtiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a noble child, a child of the people</li></ol><p>From <i>dryht</i> (“army, mankind”) +&lrm; <i>bearn</i> (“child”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a noble queen</li></ol><p>From <i>dryht</i> (“army, mankind”) +&lrm; <i>cwēn</i> (“queen”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a lord</li><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Dryhten</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;druhtinaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>drochten</i>, Old Saxon <i>drohtin</i>, Old High German <i>truhtin</i>, Old Norse <i>dróttinn</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Dryhten)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Lord, God.</li></ol><p>From the noun <i>dryhten</i>. Compare the Icelandic cognate <i>Drottinn</i>, which has the same usage and meaning.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;law) payment made as compensation for killing a freeman</li></ol><p><i>dryhten</i> (“lord”) +&lrm; <i>bēag</i> (“ring”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>drihtenlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>drihtenlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) warrior, retainer</li></ol><p><i>dryht</i> +&lrm; <i>guma</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lordly, noble, distinguished</li></ol><p>From <i>dryht</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i> (“-ly”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dryhtlīċost</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>dryhtlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dryhtnēo</i></li></ol><p>From <i>dryht</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;nē</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the body or corpse of a host (i.e. a multitude or army)</li></ol><p>From <i>dryht</i> +&lrm; <i>nēo</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>drihtnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Passover</li></ol><p><i>Dryhten</i> (“the Lord”) +&lrm; <i>færeld</i> (“journey”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>dryht</i> (“army, troop”) and <i>nōþ</i> (“daring”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Druhtiwald</i>, from <i>&#42;druhti</i> (“war band, troop”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wald</i> (“might, authority”). Equivalent to <i>dryht</i> +&lrm; <i>weald</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Dructold</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>dryhten</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) magical</li></ol><p>From <i>drȳ</i> (“sorcerer”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>drīeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sorcerer, magician</li></ol><p>From <i>drȳ</i> (“sorcerer”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drink; draught</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;drunki</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drunkiz</i>, whence also Old High German <i>trunch</i>, Old Norse <i>drykkr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>drincfæt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drip, fall in drops</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drupjaną</i> (“to fall in drops, drip”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drupô</i> (“drop”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>draca</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dread, fear</li></ol><p>Reduction of <i>ondrǣdan</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;andarādan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive (forward)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dragnet</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dragnet</li></ol><p>From <i>dræġe</i> +&lrm; <i>net</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dræġnet</i></li></ol><p>From <i>dræġe</i> +&lrm; <i>nett</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Drayton (a placename in England)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>dragan</i> (“to drag”) +&lrm; <i>tūn</i> (“town, enclosure”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>drān</i>&nbsp;/ <i>dran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>drenc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duck (bird)</li></ol><p>From the original meaning of "diver," from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dūkan</i> (“to duck, dive”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name (nickname)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Probably means <i>round</i>, <i>fat</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dive, sink</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dūban</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dūbaną</i> (“to dive, sink”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name.</li></ol><p>From the common noun <i>&#42;dūfe</i> (“dove”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A pelican</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;dūfe</i> (“diver”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;doppa</i> (“dipper”) (compare <i>diepan</i> (“to dip”)). Compare <i>dopfugol</i>, <i>dopened</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Duffield (a village and civil parish in Amber Valley borough, Derbyshire, England)</li></ol><p>Attested in the 1086 Domesday Book as Duvelle. Equivalent to <i>&#42;dūfe</i> (“dove”) +&lrm; <i>feld</i> (“field”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Irish</li></ol><p>Name of Scottish kings and an early saint, from Old Irish <i>Domnall</i> or Proto-Brythonic <i>&#42;Duβnowal</i>, from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;Dubnowalos</i> (“prince/chief of the world”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to avail, to be of use, to serve</li><li>to be fit or strong</li><li>to be good, for a purpose</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dugan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;duganą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>duguþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>duguþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>duguþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>band of warriors, host, army</li><li>prosperity, benefit</li><li>nobility</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dugunþu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dugunþō</i>; cognate with Old Frisian <i>duged</i> (“power”), Old High German <i>tugad</i>, <i>tugund</i> (“virtue”) (German <i>Tugend</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mute, dumb (unable to speak)</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a mute</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dumb</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>muteness, dumbness (inability to speak)</li></ol><p>From <i>dumb</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hill, mountain</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dunn</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dūnu</i>, <i>&#42;dūnā</i> (“sand dune”), possibly from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dūnaz</i> (“heap, pile”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewh₂-</i> (“to smoke, fume, raise dust”); or alternatively a late borrowing from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;dūnom</i> from the same Proto-Indo-European source.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>down</li></ol><p>Probably an aphetic form of <i>adūn</i>, <i>adūne</i> (“adown, down, downward”). Compare also <i>ofdūne</i> (“down”, adverb).</p><p>See <i>dūn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>down, downward</li></ol><p>From <i>dūne</i>, ablative dative form of <i>dūn</i> (“hill, down”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>descending</li></ol><p><i>dūne-</i> (“downward”) +&lrm; <i>stīgende</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark gray</li></ol><p>From <i>dun</i> (“dark”) +&lrm; <i>fealu</i> (“yellow, dusky, gray”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dungeon, prison</li><li>dung, manure</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dung</i> (“cellar”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dungu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dungō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰengʰ-</i> (“to cover”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark gray</li></ol><p>From <i>dun</i> (“dark”) +&lrm; <i>grǣġ</i> (“gray”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>County Durham (a county of England)</li><li>Durham (the county town of County Durham)</li></ol><p><i>dūn</i> (“hill, mountain”) +&lrm; <i>holm</i> (“islet”, from Old Norse <i>holmr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hulmaz</i>)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nymph associated with the Castalian Spring at the foot of Mount Helicon, mountain elf</li></ol><p>From <i>dūn</i> (“mountain”) +&lrm; <i>ielf</i> (“elf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) oread</li></ol><p>From <i>dūn</i> +&lrm; <i>ielfen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>downland, hilly or mountainous land</li></ol><p>From <i>dūn</i> (“mountain, hill”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark, dun, brown, or black in color (glosses Latin <i>fuscus</i> and <i>aquilus</i>)</li><li>bleak, dark, dim</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;duʀn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;duznaz</i>, <i>&#42;dusnaz</i> (“brown; yellow”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>dunn</i>, <i>dun</i> (“brown, dark”), Old Norse <i>dunna</i> (“female mallard”), Old Saxon <i>dosan</i> (“brown”), Old High German <i>tusin</i> (“ash-grey, dull brown”). Compare also Old Irish <i>donn</i> (“brown”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Dunn)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>dunn</i> (“dark, dim”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>dunn</i> (“dark, dim”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mountain cave</li></ol><p>From <i>dūn</i> (“mountain”) +&lrm; <i>sċræf</i> (“cave”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>dunn</i> (“dark”) +&lrm; <i>stān</i> (“stone”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>dūn</i> (“hill, mountain”) and <i>weald</i> (“power, authority”).</p><p>From <i>dunn</i> (“dark”) and <i>weald</i> (“power, authority”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dūnielf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dūnielf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dūnielfen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dare</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;durʀan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;durzaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰedʰórse</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>dyrre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>door</li><li>opening</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dur</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;durz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>doorless</li></ol><p>From <i>dūru</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doorpost, doorjamb</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;durastudō</i>. Equivalent to <i>dūru</i> (“door”) +&lrm; <i>studu</i> (“post”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>durastoð</i> (“doorpost”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doorkeeper, porter, door-guard</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;durawardaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>duru</i> (“door”) +&lrm; <i>weard</i> (“guardian, watchman”). Cognate with Gothic <i>𐌳𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰𐍅𐌰𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (daurawards, “porter, gatekeeper”), Icelandic <i>dyravörður</i> (“porter”) and German <i>Torwart</i> (“goalkeeper”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dust; powder; mill dust</li></ol><p>From the fusion of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dust</i> (“dust”) and <i>&#42;dunst</i> (“dust, mist, vapour”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dustą</i> (“dust”) and <i>&#42;dunstą</i> (“dust, mist, vapour”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰew-</i> (“vapour, smoke”). Akin to Hindi <i>धुआं</i> (dhuā̃, “smoke”), Middle Dutch <i>dost</i>, <i>donst</i>, <i>duust</i> (Dutch <i>dons</i>, <i>duist</i>), Old High German <i>tunst</i>, <i>dunst</i> (German <i>Dunst</i>), Low German <i>dust</i>, Icelandic <i>dust</i>, Norwegian <i>dust</i>, Danish <i>dyst</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dusty</li></ol><p><i>dūst</i> (“dust”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>viewing or contemplation of dust</li></ol><p>From <i>dūst</i> (“dust”) +&lrm; <i>sċēawung</i> (“inspection, contemplation”) (see <i>scavage</i> for more).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Duffield (a village and civil parish in Amber Valley borough, Derbyshire, England)</li></ol><p>Spelling attested in the 1086 Domesday Book, apparently from <i>&#42;dūfe</i> (“dove”) +&lrm; <i>feld</i> (“field”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dwola</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dwelian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to err, to go astray</li><li>to deceive, to lead astray</li></ol><p>Possibly from earlier strong <i>&#42;dwelan</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dwelan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dwelaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mislead, deceive</li><li>to hinder</li><li>to err, go astray</li><li>to dwell, remain</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dwalljan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dwaljaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwarf</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dwergaz</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>dwerch</i>, Old Saxon <i>dwerg</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;dwerg</i>, Old High German <i>twërc</i>, Old Norse <i>dvergr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dweorg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>error</li><li>heresy</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confuse, to confound or perplex</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dwilmijan</i>, related to Old English <i>dwolma</i> from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dwalmaz</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰwel-</i>, <i>&#42;dʰewel-</i> (“to dim, dull, cloud, make obscure, swirl, whirl”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phantom, ghost, apparition</li><li>illusion, delusion; error</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dwemrą</i> (“vapour, smoke, apparition”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dwemaną</i> (“to smoke”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewh₂-</i> (“to smoke, raise dust”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to waste away, languish, decline, wither</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dwīnaną</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>dvína</i> (Swedish <i>dvina</i>), Middle Low German <i>dwīnen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>error, fault, heresy</li></ol><p>From or akin to the verb <i>dwelian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foolish craft; magic</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>dwola</i> +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dwolma</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wander, go astray</li><li>to make a mistake or be wrong; to err</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chaos</li><li>chasm</li></ol><p>From or related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dwalaz</i> (“confused, stunned”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>dwalm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heretic</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>dwola</i> +&lrm; <i>mann</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stupid, foolish; dull</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a clumsy imposter; a fool</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dwās</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dwēsaz</i>.</p><p>Akin to Old Frisian <i>dwēs</i>, Middle Low German <i>dwās</i> (“stupid”), Middle Dutch <i>dwaes</i> (Dutch <i>dwaas</i>), Middle High German <i>twās</i>, <i>dwās</i>, Old English <i>dysiġ</i> (“foolish, stupid, dizzy”), Old Norse <i>&#42;dasa</i> (“to daze”), Old Norse <i>dasask</i> (“to become weary”). More at dizzy, daze.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to extinguish; quench; put out</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dwaiskijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dwaiskijaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰwey-</i> (“to slip away; dwindle; die”), from <i>&#42;dʰew-</i> (“to die; pass away”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dīedan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to deceive, delude</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dudiʀōn</i> or <i>&#42;dudirōn</i>, frequentative form derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dud-</i> (“to move, be moved”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰew-</i> (“to move, advance, move away, pass away”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deception, illusion</li></ol><p>From <i>dyderian</i> (“to deceive”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dyderian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dyderung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dive</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dūbijaną</i>; compare Icelandic <i>dýfa</i>. Compare also <i>dyppan</i> and <i>dēop</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Difelin</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>doughty, strong, valiant</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;duhtīg</i>. More at dūgan.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dyhtiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung, manure, litter</li><li>manured land, fallow land</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dungijā</i> (“dunghill, hill”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>din</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;duni</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dung</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dungijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dynnan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dunnjan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to make a noise; to resound</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dunnjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dunjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blow, strike</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dunti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;duntiz</i>; akin to Old Norse <i>dyntr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dīepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dip</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;duppjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dupjaną</i>; compare <i>&#42;daupijaną</i>, whence <i>dīepan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dīere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dīerling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>diernan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dierne</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dierne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tribulation</li></ol><p>Related to <i>durran</i> (“to dare”). Cognate with Old High German <i>gaturst</i> (“audacity”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>boldly</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bold, daring</li><li>audacious, presumptuous</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dare, presume</li></ol><p>From <i>dyrst</i> +&lrm; <i>-lǣċan</i>. The first element (also seen in <i>dyrstiġ</i>) is attested with the meaning of "tribulation", but probably also carried the meaning of "audacity" (compare Old High German <i>gaturst</i>, which glosses Latin <i>audācia</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dysiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stupid or foolish</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) an idiot or a fool</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an error, folly</li><li>ignorance</li><li>foolishness</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dusīg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>folly</li><li>blasphemy</li></ol><p>From <i>dysiġ</i> (“folly”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dusty</li></ol><p>From <i>dūst</i> (“dust”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to close up, shut</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;duttijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;duttijaną</i>, from <i>&#42;duttaz</i> (“wisp”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dagenham (a town in the borough of Barking and Dagenham, in eastern Greater London, England, United Kingdom)</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;Dæccan</i> (“Dæcca's”) +&lrm; <i>hām</i> (“home”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>action, deed</li><li>event, exploit</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dādi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dēdiz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰéh₁tis</i>, from the root <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an evil-doer, a perpetrator of murder</li></ol><p><i>dǣd</i> +&lrm; <i>bana</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an amender of one's ill-doing; a penitent</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>dǣd</i> +&lrm; <i>bēta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make amends, be penitent, repent</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>dǣd</i> +&lrm; <i>bētan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compensation for ill-doing; repentance, penitence, penance</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>dǣd</i> +&lrm; <i>bōt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mitigation of penance</li></ol><p>From <i>dǣdbōt</i> (“penance”) +&lrm; <i>līhting</i> (“lightening”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>penitence</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>dǣdbōt</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dǣdbōtnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perpetrator, doer</li><li>laborer</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>dǣd</i> (“deed”) +&lrm; <i>fruma</i> (“originator”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) persecutor, perpetrator</li></ol><p><i>dǣd</i> +&lrm; <i>hata</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>active</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) in the active voice</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>dǣd</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>accommodating; meek; mild; gentle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;daftī</i> (“fitting, suitable”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>day, (<i>usually</i>) as a period from sunrise to sunset and (<i>scientifically</i>) as a 24-hour period from sunrise to sunrise</li><li>the runic character ᛞ (/d/)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dagaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>dei</i>, Old Saxon <i>dag</i>, Old Dutch <i>dag</i>, Old High German <i>tag</i>, Old Norse <i>dagr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌳𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (dags).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Dagaberht</i>, from <i>&#42;dag</i> (“day”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”). Equivalent to <i>dæġ</i> +&lrm; <i>berht</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Tagebert</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female servant</li><li>woman in charge of a dairy; dey, dairymaid</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;daigijǭ</i> (“kneader”): cognate with Old Norse <i>deigja</i> (“female servant”).</p><p>Inflected forms.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by day; during the day</li><li>daily</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dagas</i>, <i>&#42;dagis</i>, genitive singular of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dagaz</i> (“day”), equivalent to <i>dæġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-es</i> (masculine a-stem genitive singular ending).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daisy</li></ol><p>Literally "day's eye".</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a portion of food for a day</li></ol><p>From <i>dæġ</i> +&lrm; <i>feorm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Dagahelm</i>, from <i>&#42;dag</i> (“day”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;helm</i> (“helmet”). Equivalent to <i>dæġ</i> +&lrm; <i>helm</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Dachelm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>daily</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>daily</li><li>every day</li></ol><p>From <i>dæġ</i> +&lrm; <i>hwām</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lasting a day, daylong</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dagalangaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>dæġ</i> (“day”) +&lrm; <i>lang</i> (“long”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>daglangr</i> and German <i>tagelang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>during one day, for a day</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>daily</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dagalīkaz</i> (“daily”), equivalent to <i>dæġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daily food</li></ol><p><i>dæġ</i> (“day”) +&lrm; <i>mete</i> (“food”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Dagamundu</i>, from <i>&#42;dag</i> (“day”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;mundu</i> (“protection, protector”). Equivalent to <i>dæġ</i> +&lrm; <i>mund</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Dagamunt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an instrument for telling the hour of the day; a timekeeper (e.g. clock, sundial, waterclock, etc.)</li></ol><p>From <i>dæġ</i> (“day”) +&lrm; <i>mǣl</i> (“mark”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dawn, the period of the early morning before sunrise when the sun's light begins to brighten the sky</li></ol><p>Usually explained as <i>dæġ</i> (“day”) +&lrm; <i>rēad</i> (“red”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Daltson or Dawson, England, said to be the site of the Battle of Degsastan</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a dayshield; screen</li></ol><p>From <i>dæġ</i> +&lrm; <i>sċield</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daystar</li></ol><p>From <i>dæġ</i> +&lrm; <i>steorra</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daytime</li></ol><p>From <i>dæġ</i> (“day”) +&lrm; <i>tīd</i> (“time”). Compare German <i>Tageszeit</i> (“time of day, daytime”), Danish <i>dagtid</i> (“daytime”) and Swedish <i>dagtid</i> (“daytime”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daytime</li></ol><p>From <i>dæġ</i> (“day”) +&lrm; <i>tīma</i> (“time”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a day's work</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dagawerką</i>. Equivalent to <i>dæġ</i> (“day”) +&lrm; <i>weorc</i> (“work”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Dagawini</i>, from <i>&#42;dag</i> (“day”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wini</i> (“friend”). Equivalent to <i>dæġ</i> +&lrm; <i>wine</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Daguin</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the space of a day</li></ol><p>From <i>dæġ</i> (“day”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;þern</i> (“space, extent”), the latter element of unknown origin.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dohtor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>part</li><li>a share</li><li>degree</li><li>valley, dale</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;daili</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dal</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to share</li><li>to give charitably</li><li>to spend</li><li>to divide or separate something</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dailijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>dēla</i>, Old Saxon <i>dēlian</i>, Old High German <i>teilen</i>, Old Norse <i>deila</i>, Gothic <i>𐌳𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (dailjan). The Indo-European root is also the source of Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;daili-</i> (Lithuanian <i>dailiti</i> (“divide”)), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;děliti</i> (Old Church Slavonic <i>дѣлити</i> (děliti, “divide”), Russian <i>дели́ть</i> (delítʹ)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dealer, divider, distributor, agent</li></ol><p>From <i>dǣlan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>delf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to participate</li></ol><p>Perhaps originally a calque of Latin <i>participō</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>deelnemen</i> and German <i>teilnehmen</i>. Equivalent to <i>dǣl</i> (“part”) +&lrm; <i>niman</i> (“to take”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>participant</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) participle</li></ol><p>From <i>dǣlniman</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>, literally "part-taker."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Dene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>day, as in a 24-hour period of the calendar</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dōgi</i>, from z-stem Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dōgaz</i>, whence also Old English <i>dōgor</i>. Related to Old Norse <i>dǿgr</i>. See also <i>siġe</i>, <i>sigor</i>; <i>hǣl</i>, <i>hālor</i>; <i>sele</i>, <i>salor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dēman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>river</li><li>running water, stream</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>oh, alas</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ahu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ahwō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ekʷeh₂</i> (“water”).</p><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from earlier <i>&#42;eah, &#42;æh</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;a</i>, <i>&#42;ah</i> (“ah”), related to Old High German <i>a</i>, <i>ah</i> (“ah”). Alternatively from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;au</i>, cognate with Old High German <i>au</i>, <i>ō</i> (“oh, ah”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>also, too</li><li>(in negative phrases) either</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>along with, together with</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;auk</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>āk</i>, Old Saxon <i>ok</i>, Old Dutch <i>ōk</i>, Old High German <i>ouh</i>, Old Norse <i>auk</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐌿𐌺</i> (auk).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>addition, increase, supplement</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aukô</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>auki</i>, and related to Old English <i>ēacan</i> and <i>īeċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to increase or enlarge</li><li>to conceive, become pregnant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aukaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ewg-</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>āka</i>, Old Saxon <i>ōkian</i>, Old Norse <i>auka</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐌿𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (aukan), Latin <i>augeō</i>, and Ancient Greek <i>αὐξάνω</i> (auxánō).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>increased, enlarged</li><li>endowed (with some desirable trait)</li><li>pregnant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aukanaz</i>, past participle of <i>&#42;aukaną</i> (“to add, increase”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>āken</i>, Old Saxon <i>ōkan</i> (“pregnant”), Old Norse <i>aukinn</i> (“increased, pregnant”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēacan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to increase, be augmented</li><li>to become pregnant</li><li>to bring forth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pregnant</li></ol><p>Cognate to ēacan.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) happiness, prosperity</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aud</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;audaz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>ōd</i>, Old High German <i>ōt</i>, Old Norse <i>auðr</i> (Icelandic <i>auður</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>ēad</i> (“happiness, prosperity”), cognate with German <i>Otto</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give, be prone to</li><li>to grant</li><li>to concede</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;audaną</i> (“to give, grant,”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ēad</i> +&lrm; <i>bald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Eadbald of Kent; an Anglo-Saxon King of Kent.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Audabalþ</i>, from <i>&#42;aud</i> (“wealth”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;balþ</i> (“brave, bold”). Equivalent to <i>ēad</i> +&lrm; <i>beald</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ōtbald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Eadbert of Kent; an Anglo-Saxon King of Kent.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Audaberht</i>, from <i>&#42;aud</i> (“wealth”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”). Equivalent to <i>ēad</i> +&lrm; <i>beorht</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Aotperht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ēadbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ēadbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ēadbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Audaburg</i>. Equivalent to <i>ēad</i> +&lrm; <i>burg</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ōtburg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ēadburg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>granted, given</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;audanaz</i> (“given, granted”), past participle to an otherwise unattested strong verb <i>&#42;audaną</i> (“to give, grant, loan”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>adesa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Eadfrith of Lindisfarne; Bishop of Lindisfarne and English Saint.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Audafriþu</i>, from <i>&#42;aud</i> (“wealth”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;friþu</i> (“peace”). Equivalent to <i>ēad</i> +&lrm; <i>friþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ōtfrid</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Audagaiʀ</i>, from <i>&#42;aud</i> (“wealth, riches”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;gaiʀ</i> (“spear”). Equivalent to <i>ēad</i> +&lrm; <i>gār</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ōtgēr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ēadiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ēad</i> (“happiness, prosperity”) and <i>ġifu</i> (“present, gift”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Eadgils; a semi-legendary Swedish king living around the early sixth century CE.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Audagīsl</i>, from <i>&#42;aud</i> (“wealth”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;gīsl</i> (“hostage”). Equivalent to <i>ēad</i> +&lrm; <i>ġīsl</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ōtgīs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Audagunþi</i>, from <i>&#42;aud</i> (“wealth”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;gunþi</i> (“battle”). Equivalent to <i>ēad</i> +&lrm; <i>gūþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ōtgund</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Audahelm</i>, from <i>&#42;aud</i> (“wealth”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;helm</i> (“helmet”). Equivalent to <i>ēad</i> +&lrm; <i>helm</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ōthelm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Audahari</i>, from <i>&#42;aud</i> (“wealth”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hari</i> (“army”). Equivalent to <i>ēad</i> +&lrm; <i>here</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ōtheri</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Audahildi</i>, from <i>&#42;aud</i> (“wealth”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hildi</i> (“battle”). Equivalent to <i>ēad</i> +&lrm; <i>hild</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ōthilt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ēad</i> (“happiness, prosperity”) and <i>hūn</i> (“bear cub”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>happy, prosperous, blessed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;audagaz</i>; equivalent to <i>ēad</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>happiness</li></ol><p>From <i>ēadiġ</i> (“happy”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i> (“-ness”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēaþmōd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēaþmōdlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēaþmōdlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēaþmōdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēaþmōdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēaþmōdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Audamundu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Audamunduz</i>. Equivalent to <i>ēad</i> (“happiness, prosperity”) +&lrm; <i>mund</i> (“protection, protector”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Eadmer; an English historian born shortly before the Norman Conquest.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Audamār</i>, from <i>&#42;aud</i> (“wealth”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;mār</i> (“famous”). Equivalent to <i>ēad</i> +&lrm; <i>mǣre</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ōtmār</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Audananþ</i>, from <i>&#42;aud</i> (“wealth, riches”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;nanþ</i> (“bold, daring”). Equivalent to <i>ēad</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;nōþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ōtnant</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) happiness</li></ol><p>From <i>ēad</i> +&lrm; <i>-nys</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) waterdock (<i>Rumex aquaticus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ēa</i> (“river”) +&lrm; <i>docce</i> (“dock”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ēadrǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Eadric the Wild; an 11th Century Anglo-Saxon Thegn.</li><li>Eadric of Kent; an Anglo-Saxon King of Kent.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Audarīk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Audarīks</i>. Equivalent to <i>ēad</i> +&lrm; <i>rīċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Audarād</i>, from <i>&#42;aud</i> (“wealth”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;rād</i> (“advice, counsel”). Equivalent to <i>ēad</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣd</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ōtrāt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ēad</i> (“happiness, prosperity”) and <i>siġe</i> (“victory”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ēad</i> (“happiness, prosperity”) and <i>stān</i> (“stone”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ēad</i> (“happiness, prosperity”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ēadwulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ēadweard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Eadwacer; character in the Old English poem Wulf and Eadwacer.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Audawakr</i>. Equivalent to <i>ēad</i> (“happiness, prosperity”) +&lrm; <i>wacor</i> (“watchful, vigilant”). Cognate with Latin <i>Odoacer</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ēadweard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ēad</i> (“happiness, prosperity”) and <i>weald</i> (“power, authority”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Edward</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Audawardu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Audawarduz</i>. Equivalent to <i>ēad</i> (“happiness, prosperity”) +&lrm; <i>weard</i> (“guard”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ēadwīġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Eadwig; King of Anglo-Saxon England.</li><li>Eadwig Basan; an eleventh-century English Monk.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Audawīg</i>, from <i>&#42;aud</i> (“wealth”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wīg</i> (“battle”). Equivalent to <i>ēad</i> +&lrm; <i>wīġ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ōtwīh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Audawini</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Audawiniz</i>, equivalent to <i>ēad</i> (“happiness, prosperity”) +&lrm; <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Audawulfaz</i>, equivalent to <i>ēad</i> (“happiness, prosperity”) and <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Audaþrūþi</i>, from <i>&#42;aud</i> (“wealth”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;þrūþi</i> (“strength”). Equivalent to <i>ēad</i> +&lrm; <i>þrȳþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ōtdrūd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>eafora</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>son of Eafa</li></ol><p>From <i>Eafa</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a river-dwelling fish</li></ol><p>From <i>ēa</i> (“river”) +&lrm; <i>fisċ</i> (“rush”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēafisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eofor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) son, scion, descendant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aferô</i> (“offspring; descendant”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;abar</i>, <i>&#42;abur</i> (“after; behind”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strength</li></ol><p>Unknown. Compare Old English <i>afol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a moment, the blink of an eye</li></ol><p>From <i>ēagan</i> (“eye's”) + <i>beorht</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eyelid</li></ol><p>From <i>ēage</i> (“eye”) +&lrm; <i>brǣw</i> (“eyelid”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>window</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;augōdurz</i>, equivalent to <i>ēage</i> (“eye”) +&lrm; <i>duru</i> (“door”). Cognate with Old High German <i>ougatora</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐌿𐌲𐌰𐌳𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍉</i> (augadaurō).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eye</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;augā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;augô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eyesight</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) the socket of the eye</li><li>eyebrow</li><li>eyelid</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;augahring</i> (“eyesocket”), equivalent to <i>ēage</i> +&lrm; <i>hring</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>ougring</i> (“eye-ring”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēagþȳrel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) ocean</li></ol><p>From an otherwise unattested element <i>&#42;ēagor</i> +&lrm; <i>strēam</i> (“stream”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>visible</li></ol><p><i>ēage</i> (“eye”) +&lrm; <i>-sīene</i> (“related to sight”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eyebright</li></ol><p>From <i>eage</i> (“eye”) +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i> (“plant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eyeball</li><li>pupil</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;augapl</i> (“eyeball, pupil”), equivalent to <i>ēage</i> +&lrm; <i>æppel</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>āgappel</i> (“eyeball”), Old High German <i>ougapful</i> (“eyeball”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>window</li></ol><p>From <i>ēage</i> (“eye”) +&lrm; <i>þȳrel</i> (“hole”).</p><p>For semantic parallels, compare Old Norse <i>vindauga</i> (“window”, literally “windeye" or "windhole”), from <i>auga</i> (“eye”); Gothic <i>𐌰𐌿𐌲𐌰𐌳𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍉</i> (augadaurō, “window”, literally “eyedoor”); and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;okъnò</i> (“window”), from <i>&#42;ȍko</i> (“eye”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēagþȳrel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēastrēam</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eight</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eight</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ahtō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ahtōu</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;oḱtṓw</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old High German <i>ahto</i>, Old Saxon <i>ahto</i>, Old Norse <i>átta</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐌷𐍄𐌰𐌿</i> (ahtau); and, outside the Germanic languages, with Sanskrit <i>अष्ट</i> (aṣṭa), Ancient Greek <i>ὀκτώ</i> (oktṓ), Latin <i>octo</i>, Albanian <i>tetë</i>, Old Irish <i>ocht</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>осмь</i> (osmĭ).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-eight</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-eighth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-eight</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-eighth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-eight</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-eighth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-eight</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-eighth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventy-eight</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventy-eighth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-eight</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-eighth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-eight</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-eighth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eight hundred</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eightfold</li></ol><p>From <i>eahta</i> +&lrm; <i>-feald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eight-cornered, octagonal</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eahtatīene</i> (“eighteen”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighteenth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eighteenth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ahtudô tehundô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>(<i>West Saxon</i>) eighteen</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ahtōutehun</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>ahtozehan</i>, Old Norse <i>átján</i>, Gothic <i>&#42;𐌰𐌷𐍄𐌰𐌿𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿𐌽</i> (&#42;ahtautaihun).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eighteen years old</li></ol><p><i>eahtatīene</i> +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hundeahtatiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eahtatīene</i> (“eighteen”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eight year(s) old</li><li>lasting eight years</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ahtōuwintruz</i>. Equivalent to <i>eahta</i> +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eahtoþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eahtoþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deliberate, hold council, consult</li><li>to discuss, criticize, praise, evaluate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ahtōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eighth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ahtudô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>estimation, valuation, price</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ahtungu</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>ahtinga</i>, Old Dutch <i>ahtinga</i>, Old High German <i>ahtunga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēagþȳrel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēagþȳrel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eall</i></li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eall</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eall</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æl-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>oh; hey; good heavens; frequently glosses Latin <i>O</i> and <i>eheu</i>.</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>if only</li></ol><p>Perhaps from a compound whose elements answer to <i>ēa</i> (“oh!, ah!”) +&lrm; <i>lā</i> (“lo”). Compare Old Frisian <i>ēala</i> (“hail!, hello!”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ealuhūs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>island</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;awjōlandą</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>alond</i>, <i>āland</i>, <i>eiland</i>, Old Norse <i>eyland</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣlċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>old</li><li>ancient</li><li>(<i>in compounds</i>) original</li><li>(<i>familiar</i>) grand-</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ald</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aldaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eltós</i>, a suffixed form of <i>&#42;h₂el-</i> (“grow, nourish”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grandfather</li><li>ancestor</li></ol><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>aldafeder</i> and Old Saxon <i>aldfader</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Old Testament of the Bible</li></ol><p>From <i>eald</i> (“old”) +&lrm; <i>ġecȳþnes</i> (“testament”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grandmother</li><li>female ancestor</li></ol><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>aldemōder</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>eald</i> (“wise, old”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ealdaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stale</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aldabakan</i>. Equivalent to <i>eald</i> (“old”) +&lrm; <i>bacen</i> (“baked”), from <i>bacan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aldaberht</i>, from <i>&#42;ald</i> (“old”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”). Equivalent to <i>eald</i> +&lrm; <i>beorht</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Altberht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>old woman, crone</li></ol><p>From <i>eald</i> (“old”) +&lrm; <i>cwēn</i> (“woman”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eald fæder</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ealdormann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eald fæder</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aldgunþi</i>, from <i>&#42;ald</i> (“old”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;gunþi</i> (“battle”). Equivalent to <i>eald</i> +&lrm; <i>gūþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Altigund</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Aldhelm, bishop of Sherborne</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aldahelm</i>, from <i>&#42;ald</i> (“old, wise”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;helm</i> (“helmet”). Equivalent to <i>eald</i> +&lrm; <i>helm</i>. Cognate with <i>Althelm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Ealdhelm</li></ol><p>From <i>Ealdhelm</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aldahari</i>, from <i>&#42;ald</i> (“old”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hari</i> (“army”). Equivalent to <i>eald</i> +&lrm; <i>here</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Altheri</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow or be old, become old</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aldēn</i>. Equivalent to <i>eald</i> (“old”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eald mōdor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eald mōdor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oldness, age</li><li>old age</li></ol><p>From <i>eald</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ealdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ealdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>life, the vital parts of the body</li><li>age, old age; eternity</li><li>elder, parent, head of a family; senior man</li><li>chief, lord, leader, master</li><li>prince, king</li><li>author; source, origin, authority, derivation</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) ancestors</li><li>(<i>religious</i>) elder</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aldrą</i> (“life, age”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂el-</i> (“to grow”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>aldar</i>, Old High German <i>altar</i>, Old Norse <i>aldr</i>.</p><p>According to Bammesberger, who rejects a derivation from <i>&#42;h₂el-</i> (“to grow”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-tōr</i> (familial agentive suffix), a secondary masculine noun derived from Etymology 1.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chief apostle (St. Peter)</li></ol><p><i>ealdor</i> (“age”) +&lrm; <i>apostol</i> (“apostle”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archbishop</li></ol><p>From <i>ealdor</i> (“lord, leader”) +&lrm; <i>bisċop</i> (“bishop”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metropolis</li><li>a royal city</li></ol><p>From <i>ealdor</i> (“elder, king, origin, ancestor”) +&lrm; <i>burg</i> (“city, fortress”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the days of one's life; lifetime</li></ol><p>From <i>ealdor</i> (“life, lifetime”) +&lrm; <i>dagas</i> (“days”). Directly equivalent to Old Norse <i>aldrdagar</i> (“days of one's life; lifetime”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>authority</li><li>magistracy</li></ol><p>From <i>ealdor</i> +&lrm; <i>-dōm</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>life-long</li><li>eternal</li></ol><p><i>ealdor</i> (“age”) +&lrm; <i>lang</i> (“long”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>leaderless; lacking a lord or chief</li></ol><p>From <i>ealdor</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ealdormann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A leader, ruler, or prince:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>An ealdorman (Anglo-Saxon high nobleman and governmental official).</li><li>A Roman governor.</li></ol><li>A master or overseer.</li><li>A judge.</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ealdor</i> (“elder, leader”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“man”). Cognate with Swedish <i>ålderman</i> (“(archaic) elder; spokesman, representative (of a tribe or family)”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ealdormann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ealdormann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>high priest</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ealdor</i> (“elder”) +&lrm; <i>sacerd</i> (“priest”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>old wives' tale</li></ol><p>Literally, “old women's story”. Here, <i>cwēn</i> (whence <i>queen</i>) retains its most ancient (viz. original) meaning of “woman”, much similarly to how <i>old wives' tale</i> preserves <i>wife</i>'s original meaning (which is coincidentally the same). As such, this expression might have originated in times immemorial, considering that <i>&#42;wīb</i> (whence <i>wīf</i>) had already become the normal word for “woman” by Proto-West Germanic times.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ealdrǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Ealdred (archbishop of York); last Anglo-Saxon Archbishop of York.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aldarād</i>, from <i>&#42;ald</i> (“old, wise”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;rād</i> (“advice, counsel”). Equivalent to <i>ēad</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣd</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Aldrāt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the continental Saxons</li><li>Saxony</li></ol><p><i>eald</i> (“old”) +&lrm; <i>Seaxan</i> (“Saxons”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ealdseaxan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ealdwulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an old woman</li></ol><p>From <i>eald</i> (“old”) +&lrm; <i>wīf</i> (“woman”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aldawini</i>, from <i>&#42;ald</i> (“old”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wini</i> (“friend”). Equivalent to <i>eald</i> +&lrm; <i>wine</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Aldawin</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an antiquarian, one who writes about ancient matters</li></ol><p>From <i>eald</i> +&lrm; <i>wrītere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aldawulf</i>, from <i>&#42;ald</i> (“old, wise”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wulf</i> (“wolf”). Equivalent to <i>eald</i> +&lrm; <i>wulf</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Aldolf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aldaþrūþi</i>, from <i>&#42;ald</i> (“old”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;þrūþi</i> (“strength”). Equivalent to <i>eald</i> +&lrm; <i>þrȳþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Altdrūt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>altogether</li></ol><p><i>eall</i> (“all”) +&lrm; <i>ġeador</i> (“together”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to protect; defend</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;algōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temple</li><li>residence</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;alh</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;alhs</i> (“protection, building, house, temple, settlement”), cognate to Gothic <i>𐌰𐌻𐌷𐍃</i> (alhs) and Proto-Norse <i>ᚨᛚᚺ</i> (alh).</p><p>According to Pokorny (1959:32) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂lek-</i> (“to close, ward off, protect”), i.e. the root of Ancient Greek <i>ἀλέξω</i> (aléxō) and Sanskrit <i>रक्षति</i> (rakṣati, “guard, protect”), though Kroonen (2013:22 s.v. <i>alh</i>-) dismisses this as “arbitrary[.]”</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Alhaburg</i>, from <i>&#42;alh</i> (“sanctuary”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;burg</i> (“fortification”). Equivalent to <i>ealh</i> +&lrm; <i>burg</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Alchburg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p><i>ealh</i> (“temple”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;flǣd</i> (“beauty”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Alhafriþu</i>, from <i>&#42;alh</i> (“sanctuary”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;friþu</i> (“peace”). Equivalent to <i>ealh</i> +&lrm; <i>friþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Alhafrid</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Alhahardu</i>, from <i>&#42;alh</i> (“sanctuary”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hardu</i> (“hard”). Equivalent to <i>ealh</i> +&lrm; <i>heard</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Electard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Alhahelm</i>, from <i>&#42;alh</i> (“sanctuary”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;helm</i> (“helmet”). Equivalent to <i>ealh</i> +&lrm; <i>helm</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Electelm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Alhahari</i>, from <i>&#42;alh</i> (“sanctuary”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hari</i> (“army”). Equivalent to <i>ealh</i> +&lrm; <i>here</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Alcher</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Alhamundu</i>, from <i>&#42;alh</i> (“sanctuary”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;mundu</i> (“protector”). Equivalent to <i>ealh</i> +&lrm; <i>mund</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Alcmunt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>son of Ealhmund</li></ol><p>From <i>Ealhmund</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ealh</i> (“temple”) and <i>stān</i> (“stone”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a temple, sanctuary</li><li>a place of shelter, refuge, or protection</li><li>city</li></ol><p>From <i>ealh</i> +&lrm; <i>stede</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Alhaswinþ</i>, from <i>&#42;alh</i> (“sanctuary”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;swinþ</i> (“strong”). Equivalent to <i>ealh</i> +&lrm; <i>swīþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Alahswind</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>all</li><li>whole, entire</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>everything</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) everyone</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>completely</li><li>altogether, in all, all</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;all</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;allaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>al</i>, Old Saxon <i>all</i>, Old Dutch <i>al</i>, Old High German <i>al</i>, Old Norse <i>allr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æl-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Alrīċa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>the whole time</li></ol><p>Dates back at least to Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;allā hwīlā</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>alla wīla</i>. One of many fossilized phrases that became idiomatic when the language developed a definite article. Compare <i>ealne dæġ</i>, <i>ealle niht</i>, and <i>ealne weġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>all night</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;allǭ</i> <i>&#42;nahtų</i>. Cognates include Old Norse <i>alla nátt</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐌻𐌻𐌰 𐌽𐌰𐌷𐍄</i> (alla naht).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>completely, wholly, entirely</li><li>totally, altogether, in all</li><li>fully</li></ol><p>The genitive of <i>eall</i>, equivalent to <i>eall</i> +&lrm; <i>-es</i>. Functionally and semantically cognate to Old Frisian <i>alles</i>;</p><p>Saterland Frisian <i>alles</i>, ales;</p><p>West Frisian <i>alles</i>;</p><p>Old Saxon <i>alles</i>;</p><p>Middle Low German <i>alles</i>; alles;</p><p>German Low German <i>alles</i>, alles—(<i>Münsterländisch Sauerländisch</i>), olles, (<i>Paderbornisch</i>);</p><p>Old Dutch <i>alles</i>;</p><p>Middle Dutch <i>alles</i>;</p><p>Dutch <i>alles</i>; Afrikaans <i>alles</i>;</p><p>Middle High German <i>alles</i>;</p><p>German <i>alles</i>;</p><p>Alemannic German <i>alls</i>;</p><p>Old Norse <i>alls</i>;</p><p>Icelandic <i>alls</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ælhwīt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>universal, general, catholic</li></ol><p>From <i>eall</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ealneġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Alrīċa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>just as, just so</li><li>(<i>with verb</i>) likewise, in exactly the same manner</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>as, just as, even as, as if</li><li>so, even so, likewise, also</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;allswā</i>, equivalent to <i>eall</i> +&lrm; <i>swā</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>alsa</i> (“all so”), Old Saxon <i>also</i> (“all so”), Old High German <i>alsō</i> (“so, all so”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>entirely, altogether, completely</li></ol><p>From <i>eall</i> (“all”) +&lrm; <i>-unga</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ælmihtiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>all day</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;allanǭ dagą</i>. The expected cognate phrase is attested in most old Germanic languages: Old Dutch <i>allan dag</i>, Old High German <i>allan tag</i>, Old Norse <i>allan dag</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐌻𐌻𐌰𐌽𐌰 𐌳𐌰𐌲</i> (allana dag).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>always</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;allanǭ wegą</i> (literally “all the way”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>allan veg</i>. One of many fossilized phrases that became idiomatic when the language developed a definite article.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>always, perpetually</li><li>quite</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>ealneweġ</i>, <i>eallneweġ</i>, from <i>ealne, eallne</i> (masculine accusative of <i>eall</i>, “all”) + <i>weġ</i> (“way”). More at all, way.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ealneġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>always</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ealu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēaland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ealuwǣġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>All Saints' Day</li></ol><p><i>ealra</i> (genitive plural of <i>eall</i> (“all”)) + <i>hālgena</i> (genitive plural of <i>hāliġ</i> (“holy”)) + <i>mæssedæġ</i> (“festival day”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Halloween; the day or evening before All Saints' Day</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>most of all</li></ol><p>Dates back at least to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;allaizô maist</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>allaro mēst</i>, Old High German <i>allero meist</i>, Old Norse <i>allra mest</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>most of all</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eallswā</i></li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eallswā</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beer, ale</li><li>(<i>in compounds</i>) feast</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;alu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;alu</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂elut-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ale-bench</li></ol><p>From <i>ealu</i> +&lrm; <i>benċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a vat for storing ale</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;alufat</i> (“container or vessel for ale”), equivalent to <i>ealu</i> +&lrm; <i>fæt</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>alofat</i>, <i>alufat</i> (“beer jug”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place where beer is sold and consumed; pub, tavern, inn</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ealu</i> +&lrm; <i>hūs</i>. Diachronically from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aluþhūsą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an "ale-poet;" one who performs poetry where there is drinking</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ealu</i> (“ale”) +&lrm; <i>sċop</i> (“poet”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ale-cup</li></ol><p>From <i>ealu</i> (“ale”) +&lrm; <i>wǣġe</i> (“cup”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncle (<i>especially maternal</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Mercian and Early West Saxon form of <i>eom</i></li></ol><p>Contracted from earlier <i>&#42;ēahām</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;auhaim</i> (“maternal uncle”).</p><p>See also Gothic <i>𐌰𐍅𐍉</i> (awō, “grandmother”); Latin <i>avus</i> (“grandfather”), <i>avunculus</i> (“uncle”), dialectal Russian <i>уй</i> (uj, “maternal uncle”), Ukrainian <i>вуй</i> (vuj, “uncle”), all from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;awos</i>, <i>&#42;h₂éwh₂os</i> (“maternal uncle, maternal grandfather”). The word is cognate with Old Frisian <i>ēm</i>, Middle Dutch <i>oom</i> (Dutch <i>oom</i>), Old High German <i>oheim</i> (German <i>Oheim</i>, <i>Ohm</i>).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;immi</i> (“I am”), a form of <i>&#42;wesaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ésmi</i> (“am”). More at am.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>The first compound is unknown, possibly related to ēanian ("to yean"). The second is Old English <i>bald</i> (“brave”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aunaberht</i>, from <i>&#42;aun</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”). Equivalent to <i>&#42;ēan</i> +&lrm; <i>beorht</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ōnberht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ēanbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ēanbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aunafriþu</i>, from <i>&#42;aun</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;friþu</i> (“peace”). Equivalent to <i>&#42;ēan</i> +&lrm; <i>friþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ōnfrid</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give birth (to lambs)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aunōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aunōną</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>onen</i> (“to yean”) and Swedish <i>öna</i> (“to give birth to, yean”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aunamundu</i>, from <i>&#42;aun</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;mundu</i> (“protector, protection”). Equivalent to <i>&#42;ēan</i> +&lrm; <i>mund</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ōnmund</i>, Latin <i>Aunemundus</i> (via Frankish).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aunarād</i>, from <i>&#42;aun</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;rād</i> (“advice, counsel”). Equivalent to <i>&#42;ēan</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣd</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ōnrāt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aunawulf</i>, from <i>&#42;aun</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;wulf</i> (“wolf”). Equivalent to <i>&#42;ēan</i> +&lrm; <i>wulf</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Aonolf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>billow, wave</li><li>sea, ocean</li><li>earth</li><li>the runic character ᛠ (/æ͜ɑ(ː)/)</li><li>ear (of grain)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aur</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;auraz</i>. Akin to Old Norse <i>aurr</i> (“mud”).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ahaʀ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ahaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱ-</i> (“pointed”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a chest (for storage)</li><li>the ark of Noah</li><li>the Ark of the Covenant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>arca</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>earc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little finger</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēare</i> +&lrm; <i>clǣnsend</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homeland, native soil; one's home, a dwelling</li><li>earth, land</li><li>state; station; condition; fate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ard</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ardiz</i>, <i>&#42;arduz</i>, <i>&#42;arþuz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂erh₃-</i> (“to plough”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>ard</i>, Old High German <i>art</i> (German <i>Art</i>). The Indo-European root is also the source of Latin <i>arō</i>, Ancient Greek <i>ἀρόω</i> (aróō), Old East Slavic <i>орати</i> (orati), Russian <i>ора́ть</i> (orátʹ).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) dwelling-place, the Earth</li></ol><p>From <i>eard</i> (“homeland”) +&lrm; <i>ġeard</i> (“dwelling”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>eard</i> (“homeland”) + <i>gūþ</i> (“battle, combat”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to occupy, settle; dwell</li></ol><p>From <i>eard</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant, dweller</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>eardian</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inhabitable</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>eardiende</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>, else <i>eardian</i> +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eardiendlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eardung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fatherland, (one's) country</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>eard</i> (“homeland”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) land-stepper, wanderer</li></ol><p>From <i>eard</i> (“earth”) +&lrm; <i>stapa</i> (“stepper”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eardwulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the act of living or dwelling</li><li>place of living or dwelling; abode, lair, tabernacle</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>eardian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwelling-city</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>eardung</i> (“dwelling”) +&lrm; <i>burg</i> (“settlement”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>habitation</li></ol><p>From <i>eardung</i> (“dwelling”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwelling place</li><li>tent</li><li>tabernacle</li></ol><p>From <i>eardung</i> (“living”) +&lrm; <i>stōw</i> (“place”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Ardawini</i>, from <i>&#42;ard</i> (“homeland”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wini</i> (“friend”). Equivalent to <i>eard</i> +&lrm; <i>wine</i>. Possibly cognate with Latin <i>Arduinus</i> via Lombardic or Frankish. May also be an <i>h</i>-pheresis form of <i>Heardwine</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Eardwulf of Northumbria; an Anglo-Saxon King of Northumbria.</li></ol><p>From <i>eard</i> (“homeland, earth, land”) and <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ear (organ of hearing)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;auʀā</i>, from the voiced Verner alternant of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ausô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ows-</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>āre</i>, Old Saxon <i>ōra</i>, Old Dutch <i>ōra</i>, Old High German <i>ōra</i>, Old Norse <i>eyra</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐌿𐍃𐍉</i> (ausō).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) a flap of the ear</li></ol><p>From <i>ēare</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;lippric</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the morning star</li><li>the dawn</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Auziwandilaz</i>, from <i>&#42;auzi</i> (“dawn”) + <i>&#42;wandilaz</i> (“fluctuating, variable, wandering”), perhaps via the intermediate forms *ēarwendel or *ēarwandel.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Earendel)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>a personification of the morning star</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>heathendom</i>) Ēarendel</li><li>(<i>Christendom</i>) Jesus</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Auʀiwandil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Auziwandilaz</i>, from <i>&#42;auzi</i> (“dawn”) + <i>&#42;wandilaz</i> (“fluctuating, variable, wandering”), perhaps via the intermediate forms *Ēarwendel or *Ēarwandel.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) common vetch (<i>Vicia sativa</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>ervum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>earfoþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little finger</li></ol><p>From <i>ēare</i> (“ear”) +&lrm; <i>finger</i> (“finger”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>earfoþhwīl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>earfoþlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hardship, trouble</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hard to do, difficult</li></ol><p>From <i>earfoþe</i> +&lrm; <i>dǣd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>work; labour</li><li>suffering; hardship</li><li>labour; pains; trouble; childbirth</li><li>disease</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hard, difficult</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;arbaiþi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arbaidiz</i> (“hardship”), from a verb <i>&#42;arbijaną</i>, <i>&#42;arbāną</i> (“be orphaned”).</p><p>Compare Old English <i>ierfa</i>. See also Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃órbʰos</i>, whence Ancient Greek <i>ὀρφανός</i> (orphanós), Latin <i>orbus</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>рабъ</i> (rabŭ, “slave”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>arbēd</i>, Old Saxon <i>arƀed</i>, <i>arvēd</i>, Old Dutch <i>arbeit</i> (Dutch <i>arbeid</i>), Old High German <i>arbeit</i> (German <i>Arbeit</i> (“work, labour”)), Old Norse <i>erfiði</i> (Swedish <i>arvode</i> (“salary”)), Gothic <i>𐌰𐍂𐌱𐌰𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (arbaiþs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>earfoþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) Hard time, period of hardship</li></ol><p><i>earfoþe</i> +&lrm; <i>hwīl</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>irksome</li><li>difficult</li></ol><p><i>earfoþe</i> (“hard, difficult”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with difficulty, hardly</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>earfoþlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>difficulty</li><li>hardship, tribulation, misfortune</li><li>distress</li></ol><p>From <i>earfoþe</i> (adjective) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>earfoþnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>earfoþnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>earfoþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>earfoþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) time of trouble, evil period</li></ol><p><i>earfoþe</i> +&lrm; <i>þrāg</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cowardly</li><li><i>(substantive)</i> a coward</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;arg</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;argaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>erch</i>, Old High German <i>arg</i>, Old Norse <i>argr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>normalized spelling of <i>earhlīċe</i> (“fearfully”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>earg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>earg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) arrow</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>earg</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;arhu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arhwō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂érkʷo-</i> (“bow, arrow”). Near cognates include Gothic <i>𐌰𐍂𐍈𐌰𐌶𐌽𐌰</i> (arƕazna) and Old Norse <i>ǫr</i>; further cognates include Latin <i>arcus</i> (“bow”).</p><p>See <i>earg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fearfully, timidly</li><li>disgracefully</li></ol><p><i>earg</i> (“cowardly”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earring</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;auʀahring</i>, equivalent to <i>ēare</i> (“ear”) +&lrm; <i>hring</i> (“ring”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) a bulrush</li></ol><p>From <i>ēa</i> (“river”) +&lrm; <i>risċ</i> (“rush”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ear lobe</li></ol><p>From <i>ēare</i> (“ear”) +&lrm; <i>læppa</i> (“lobe”). Compare German <i>Ohrläppchen</i> (“earlobe”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arm</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poor, miserable</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;arm</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;armaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂er-mos</i> (“joint”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>arm</i>, Old Saxon <i>arm</i>, Dutch <i>arm</i>, Old High German <i>arm</i> (German <i>Arm</i>), Old Norse <i>armr</i> (Swedish <i>arm</i>), Gothic <i>𐌰𐍂𐌼𐍃</i> (arms). The Proto-Indo-European root is also the source of Old Church Slavonic <i>рамо</i> (ramo) (Bulgarian <i>ра́мо</i> (rámo, “shoulder”)), Latin <i>armus</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;armaz</i>, further etymology uncertain.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>erm</i>, Old Saxon <i>arm</i>, Dutch <i>arm</i>, Old High German <i>arm</i> (German <i>arm</i>), Old Norse <i>armr</i> (Danish and Swedish <i>arm</i>), Gothic <i>𐌰𐍂𐌼𐍃</i> (arms).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armlet or bracelet, often one made of gold or silver</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;armabaugaz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>armbōg</i>, Old High German <i>armboug</i>, Old Norse <i>armbaugr</i>. Equivalent to <i>earm</i> +&lrm; <i>bēag</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) wretched, careworn, sad, miserable</li></ol><p>From <i>earm</i> +&lrm; <i>ċeariġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleeve</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>faint-hearted</li><li>merciful</li></ol><p>From <i>earm</i> +&lrm; <i>-heort</i>. A calque of Latin <i>misericors</i>, according to some scholars possibly under the influence of Gothic <i>𐌰𐍂𐌼𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍄𐍃</i> (armahairts).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arm ornament</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel pity; commiserate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;armōn</i>, equivalent to <i>earm</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wretched or miserable being or person</li></ol><p>From <i>earm</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>arming</i>, Old Norse <i>armingi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>miserable, wretched</li></ol><p><i>earm</i> (“miserable”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>earmlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) armbone</li></ol><p>From <i>earm</i> +&lrm; <i>sċanca</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleeve</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Great Yarmouth (a coastal town and borough of Norfolk, England).</li></ol><p><i>ēar</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>mūþa</i> (“mouth”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>iermþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eagle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;arnu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deserve</li><li>to earn</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aʀanōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>earn</i> (“eagle”) and <i>siġe</i> (“victory”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Earnwulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Arnuwald</i>, from <i>&#42;arnu</i> (“eagle”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wald</i> (“might, authority”). Equivalent to <i>earn</i> +&lrm; <i>weald</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Arnolt</i>, English <i>Arnold</i> (ultimately from Frankish).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Arnuwīg</i>, from <i>&#42;arnu</i> (“eagle”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wīg</i> (“battle”). Equivalent to <i>earn</i> +&lrm; <i>wīġ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Arnwīg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Arnuwini</i>, from <i>&#42;arnu</i> (“eagle”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wini</i> (“friend”). Equivalent to <i>earn</i> +&lrm; <i>wine</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Arnoin</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Arôwulfaz</i>, equivalent to <i>earn</i> (“eagle”) +&lrm; <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eorp</i> (“dark, dusky, swarthy”)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Earp)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ure (a river in North Yorkshire, England)</li></ol><p>Probably corrupted from <i>&#42;Ear</i> + ƿ (abbreviation for <i>ƿæter</i> (“water”)). First attested c. 1025.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butt, arse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ars</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arsaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁órsos</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>ers</i>, Old Saxon <i>ars</i>, Old High German <i>ars</i>, Old Norse <i>ars</i> ~ <i>rass</i>, and more distantly with Middle Armenian <i>ոռ</i> (oṙ, “ass”) and Modern Greek <i>ουρά</i> (ourá, “tail”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little finger</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buttocks, breech</li></ol><p>From <i>ears</i> (“buttocks, ass”) +&lrm; <i>ende</i> (“end”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toilet, privy</li><li>excrement</li></ol><p>From <i>ears</i> (“buttocks, ass”) +&lrm; <i>gang</i> (“going, path, toilet”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(used exclusively in the phrase <i>on earsling</i>) backwards, on or onto the back</li></ol><p>From <i>ears</i> +&lrm; <i>-ling</i> (“adverbial suffix”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the breech muscle; the gluteal muscles</li></ol><p>From <i>ears</i> (“buttocks, ass”) +&lrm; <i>lȳre</i>, the latter element presumably being a variant of <i>līra</i> ("brawn, muscle").</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having an arse, buttocks, breech</li></ol><p>From <i>ears</i> +&lrm; <i>-ode</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>earsþyrel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) asshole, anus</li></ol><p>From <i>ears</i> (“arse”) +&lrm; <i>þȳrel</i> (“hole”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Mercian form of <i>earon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an earwig or worm</li></ol><p><i>ēare</i> +&lrm; <i>wicga</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eart</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eorþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the east</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eastern, easterly</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from the east</li><li>towards the east</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;austr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the east</li></ol><p>From <i>east</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from the east</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;austanā</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>ostana</i> (Dutch <i>oost</i>), Old High German <i>ostana</i> (German <i>osten</i>), Old Norse <i>austan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) 'East-Danes', the Danes</li></ol><p><i>ēast</i> (“east”) +&lrm; <i>Dene</i> (“Danes”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the eastern part of a country or place</li><li>the East</li></ol><p>From <i>ēast</i> (“east”) +&lrm; <i>dǣl</i> (“part, portion”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ēast</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the East Angles</li><li>East Anglia</li></ol><p><i>ēast</i> +&lrm; <i>Engle</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ēast</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the fourth month of the year according to the Anglo-Saxon calendar</li><li>April</li></ol><p>From <i>ēastre</i> +&lrm; <i>mōnaþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eastern</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;austrōnī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eastertide</li></ol><p><i>ēastre</i> (“Easter”) +&lrm; <i>tīd</i> (“season”)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Eastertid)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>ēastertīd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēastweard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eastmost</li></ol><p>From <i>ēast</i> (“east”) +&lrm; <i>-mest</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>ēast</i> (“east”) and <i>mund</i> (“protector”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ēast</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Easter</li></ol><p>Apparently named from the dawn goddess <i>Ēastre</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;austrā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Austrǭ</i>, from a suffixed form of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ews-</i> (“dawn”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>āsteron</i>, Old High German <i>ōstarūn</i> (German <i>Ostern</i>).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Eastre)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>ēastre</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>Ēostre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water stream; river</li><li>the sea or the ocean</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ahwustraum</i>, equivalent to <i>ēa</i> +&lrm; <i>strēam</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>ahastrōm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a river-flood</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēastrēam</i> +&lrm; <i>ȳþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the eastern part of a kingdom or country</li><li>an empire located in the east; the East as a whole</li><li>a kingdom east of another</li></ol><p>From <i>ēast</i> +&lrm; <i>rīċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>due east</li></ol><p><i>ēast</i> (“east”) +&lrm; <i>rihte</i> (“straight”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēastre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēastre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the East Saxons</li><li>Essex</li></ol><p><i>ēast</i> (“east”) +&lrm; <i>Seaxan</i> (“Saxons”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ēastseaxan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>eastward</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eastward, in the east</li></ol><p><i>ēast</i> +&lrm; <i>-weard</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>etan</i></li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Eata)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Eata</li></ol><p>From <i>Eata</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>etan</i></li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Eatta)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eotol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣ</i> (found in compounds)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īewan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēalā</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>openly, publicly</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ēa</i> (“river”) +&lrm; <i>wynn</i> (“joy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) burdock (<i>Arctium lappa</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ēa</i> (“river”) +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i> (“plant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æx</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>Exanċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Exanċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Exanċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>Exanċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>Exanċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Exanċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eaxl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoulder</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ahslu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ahslō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crosspiece, crossbar</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>Exanċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēaþbēde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ēaþmōd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ēaþmōdheort</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēaþmōdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēaþe</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēaþe</i></li><li>comparative degree of <i>ēaþe</i>: more easily</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exorable</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēaþe</i> +&lrm; <i>bēde</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exorable</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēaþe</i> +&lrm; <i>bēne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>easy to do</li></ol><p>From <i>ēaþe</i> +&lrm; <i>dǣd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>easily</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īeþe</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;auþê</i> ~ <i>&#42;auþô</i>. Equivalent to <i>īeþe</i> (“easy”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i> (“-ly”).</p><p>See <i>īeþe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īeþelīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>easy to be found, easily found, easy to find</li></ol><p>From <i>ēaþe</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;fynde</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) humility</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēaþ</i> +&lrm; <i>mēd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to humble</li><li>to bow down in adoration</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>humble</li><li>submissive, obedient</li><li>gracious, gentle</li></ol><p><i>ēaþe</i> +&lrm; <i>-mōd</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>humble-minded, humble at heart</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēaþmōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-heort</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ġeēaþmōdian</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēaþmōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>humble</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēaþmōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>humble</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēaþmōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>humbly</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēaþmōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humility</li><li>gentleness, graciousness, kindness</li></ol><p><i>ēaþmōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēaþmōdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēaþmōdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ēa</i> (“river”) +&lrm; <i>þrȳþ</i> (“might”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēaþe</i> (“easily”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an ebb, receding of water</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;abjǭ</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Ebba)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ebb, recede</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;abbjōn</i> (“to ebb”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eofolsian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eofolsung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eofolsung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eoforþrote</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hebrew</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a Hebrew</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Hebrew language</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>hebraicus</i>, assimilated to the suffix <i>-isċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēac</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īeċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ache</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eternal</li><li>durable</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>eternally, ever, evermore, perpetually</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aki</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;akiz</i> (“ache, pain”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eg-</i> (“fault, guilt, sin”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ajukī</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>ewich</i>, Old Saxon <i>ēwig</i>, Old High German <i>ēwig</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐌾𐌿𐌺𐌳𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (ajukdūþs, “eternity”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æċed</i> (“vinegar”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eternal, perpetual</li></ol><p><i>ēċe</i> (“eternal”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>eternally</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eċġfriþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>edge (of a cutting tool or weapon)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aggju</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sharpen; to give an edge</li><li>to harrow</li></ol><p>From <i>eċġ</i> (“edge”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aggjubalþ</i>, from <i>&#42;aggju</i> (“edge”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;balþ</i> (“bold, brave”). Equivalent to <i>eċġ</i> +&lrm; <i>beald</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Eggibald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aggjuberht</i>, from <i>&#42;aggju</i> (“edge”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”). Equivalent to <i>eċġ</i> +&lrm; <i>beorht</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Egberht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>son of Eċġbeorht</li></ol><p>From <i>Eċġbeorht</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eċġbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eċġbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eċġbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aggjuburg</i>, from <i>&#42;aggju</i> (“edge”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;burg</i> (“fortification”). Equivalent to <i>eċġ</i> +&lrm; <i>burg</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Eggeburg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eċġbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eċġfriþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name &lsqb;16th April 808&rsqb;</li><li>alternative form of <i>Eċġfriþ</i> &lsqb;c.890&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>eċġ</i> (“edge, blade”) +&lrm; <i>ferhþ</i> (“life, soul”). First attested in 808, substituted for <i>Eċġfriþ</i> in historical records from late 9th C.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eċġfriþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Eċġfriþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aggjufriþu</i>, from <i>&#42;aggju</i> (“edge”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;friþu</i> (“peace”). Equivalent to <i>eċġ</i> +&lrm; <i>friþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Egfrid</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aggjuhardu</i>, from <i>&#42;aggju</i> (“edge”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hardu</i> (“hard”). Equivalent to <i>eċġ</i> +&lrm; <i>heard</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Eghard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aggjuhelm</i>, from <i>&#42;aggju</i> (“edge”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;helm</i> (“helmet”). Equivalent to <i>eċġ</i> +&lrm; <i>helm</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Eggihelm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aggjuhari</i>, from <i>&#42;aggju</i> (“edge”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hari</i> (“army”). Equivalent to <i>eċġ</i> +&lrm; <i>here</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Egiheri</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) edge-hatred, war</li></ol><p>From <i>eċġ</i> (“edge”) +&lrm; <i>hete</i> (“hatred”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to edge, provide with an edge</li></ol><p>From <i>eċġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>eggja</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Eċġlāf, bishop of Dunwich</li><li>the father of Unferþ in <i>Beowulf</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aggjulaibu</i>, from <i>&#42;aggju</i> (“edge”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;laibu</i> (“legacy”). Equivalent to <i>eċġ</i> +&lrm; <i>lāf</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Eggileib</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aggjumundu</i>, from <i>&#42;aggju</i> (“edge”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;mundu</i> (“protection, protector”). Equivalent to <i>eċġ</i> +&lrm; <i>mund</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Eckimunt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aggjurād</i>, from <i>&#42;aggju</i> (“edge”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;rād</i> (“advice, counsel”). Equivalent to <i>eċġ</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣd</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Egerēd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>eċġ</i> +&lrm; <i>rīc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>eċġ</i> (“edge”) +&lrm; <i>stān</i> (“stone”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eċġweald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eċġwealding</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>eċġ</i> (“edge (of a weapon)”) + <i>waru</i> (“care, guard, portection”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aggjuwald</i>, from <i>&#42;aggju</i> (“edge”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wald</i> (“might, authority”). Equivalent to <i>eċġ</i> +&lrm; <i>weald</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Egolt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Eċġweald</li></ol><p>From <i>Eċġweald</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>eċġ</i> (“edge”) +&lrm; <i>wela</i> (“wealth”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aggjuwini</i>, from <i>&#42;aggju</i> (“edge”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wini</i> (“friend”). Equivalent to <i>eċġ</i> +&lrm; <i>wine</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ecquin</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aggjuwulf</i>, from <i>&#42;aggju</i> (“edge”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wulf</i> (“wolf”). Equivalent to <i>eċġ</i> +&lrm; <i>wulf</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Egulf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eċġþeow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Ecgþeow, the father of Beowulf</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aggjuþeu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Agiþewaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>eċġ</i> (“edge”) +&lrm; <i>þēow</i> (“servant”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>Eggþér</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eċġþeow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eternity</li></ol><p><i>ēċe</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēcnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēcnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>again</li><li>against, backwards</li><li>turning</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;id-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;idi-</i> (“back, backwards, again”). Akin to Old Saxon <i>idug-</i>, Old High German <i>id-</i>, <i>ida-</i>, dialectal German <i>it-</i>, Old Norse <i>ið-</i>, Gothic <i>𐌹𐌳-</i> (id-).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>regeneration</li></ol><p>From <i>ed-</i> +&lrm; <i>cenning</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a return</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ed-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċierr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>edċierr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>edcwician</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>restored to life, regenerate</li></ol><p>From <i>ed-</i> +&lrm; <i>cwic</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to requicken, revive, regenerate, restore to life</li></ol><p>From <i>ed-</i> +&lrm; <i>cwician</i>. See <i>cwic</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>edċierr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>edċierr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>edor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>edisċhenn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>young again, rejuvenated</li></ol><p><i>ed-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeong</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repayment</li></ol><p>From <i>ed-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġild</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aftergrowth</li><li>pasture</li><li>a park</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>edisċhenn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quail</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;edisċ</i> (“park”) +&lrm; <i>henn</i> (“hen”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reward</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;idilaun</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>idlōn</i>. Equivalent to <i>ed-</i> +&lrm; <i>lēan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reward, remunerate</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>edlēan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> or <i>ed-</i> +&lrm; <i>lēanian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reciprocal</li><li>relative</li><li>related</li></ol><p>From <i>edlesende</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to repeat, renew</li></ol><p>From <i>ed-</i> +&lrm; <i>læcan</i> from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laikijaną</i>, causative of <i>&#42;laikaną</i>. Related to Old English <i>lācan</i> and <i>lāc</i> (“play, sport”), which see.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a repetition</li></ol><p>From <i>edlǣċan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>anew</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ed-</i> +&lrm; <i>nīewan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>new again, renewed</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>anew</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ed-</i> +&lrm; <i>nīewe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to renew</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ednīewe</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> or <i>ed-</i> +&lrm; <i>nīewian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>restorer, renewer</li></ol><p><i>ednīewian</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>anew</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ednīewe</i> +&lrm; <i>-inga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>renewal, renovation</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ednīewian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ednīewan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ednīewe</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ednīewe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ednīewian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ednīewiend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ednīewinga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ednīewung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enclosure, hedge, fence</li><li>shelter, dwelling, house</li><li>protector, prince</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;edaraz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>etar</i>, Old Norse <i>jaðarr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to establish again; to restore</li></ol><p>From <i>ed-</i> +&lrm; <i>staþelian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a restorer</li></ol><p>From <i>edstaþelian</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong, firm</li></ol><p><i>ed-</i> +&lrm; <i>staþol</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a reestablishment</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>edstaþelian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to return</li><li>to desist from, cease</li></ol><p>From <i>ed-</i> +&lrm; <i>wendan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) change, reversal of fortunes; turnabout</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) destruction, end, apocalypse</li></ol><p><i>ed-</i> +&lrm; <i>wenden</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whirlpool</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>anything, something</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vortex, whirlpool</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;idwindā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;idiwindǭ</i>, or derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;idiwindaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>ed-</i> +&lrm; <i>winde</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ēadwine</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>being, substance; sustenance, food</li></ol><p>From <i>ed-</i> +&lrm; <i>wist</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reproach, blame</li><li>contumely, scorn</li></ol><p>From <i>ed-</i> +&lrm; <i>wīte</i> (“punishment, penalty, torment”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reproach, scandal, disgrace, dishonor</li></ol><p>From <i>edwīt</i> (“reproach, scorn”) +&lrm; <i>stæf</i> (“letter”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eofolsung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eve (the first woman in the Bible)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Eva</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Εὔα</i> (Eúa), from Biblical Hebrew <i>חַוָּה</i> (ḥawwā).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>yfel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efn</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efn</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣfen</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ebnaz</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ēbandaz</i> (“evening”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efn-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnbyrde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efncempa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efneardian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnetan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnhlēoþor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnhlēoþrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnhlēoþrung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnhlytta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnhlytta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnlīcnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consort, equal</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>efen</i> +&lrm; <i>-ling</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnmetan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnmǣre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evenness</li></ol><p>From <i>efen</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnniht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnsċyldiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnseċġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnwerod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnwiht</i></li></ol><p><i>efen-</i> (“equal”) +&lrm; <i>wiht</i> (“being”)</p><p><i>efen-</i> (“equal”) +&lrm; <i>wiht</i> (“weight”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnwyrċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnwyrċing</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnwyrhta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnþrōwung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣfen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>edge, border, brim, brink, verge</li><li>(<i>of a forest</i>) border, edge; side</li><li>(<i>of a house</i>) eaves; the edge of a roof</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ubisu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ubiswō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut (someone's hair), give a haircut</li></ol><p>From the noun <i>efes</i> (“eaves”) or its ancestor.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hurry, rush</li></ol><p>From North Sea Germanic <i>*obusti</i>; beyond that, the word's origin is mysterious. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>oƀastlīko</i> (“quickly”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hurry, rush</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>*obustijan</i>, ultimately derived from <i>efest</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a hurry, in a rush</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>efest</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cutting in the form of an eaves, a rounding, polling, compassing</li><li>a shearing, haircut</li><li>hair clippings</li></ol><p><i>efesian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a newt or lizard</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flat</li><li>level, even</li><li>equal</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>together</li><li>equally, evenly</li><li>just</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ebn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>denoting equality: equal, equally</li><li>denoting togetherness: co-, fellow-</li></ol><p>From the adjective <i>efn</i>, descended from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ebnaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry out; do; perform; execute; labour; achieve; show; fulfill</li><li>to cause; endure; suffer</li><li>to throw down; prostrate; level; lay low; bring to a level with</li><li>to make even; level</li><li>to liken; compare</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;abnijan</i> (“to make, perform, execute”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>efna</i> (“to perform, fulfill, keep one's word”).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ebnaijaną</i> (“to make level, make even”), from <i>&#42;ebnaz</i> (“even”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of equal birth</li></ol><p>From <i>efn-</i> +&lrm; <i>byrde</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellow soldier</li></ol><p><i>efn-</i> +&lrm; <i>cempa</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>even, evenly</li><li>exactly, precisely</li><li>(<i>by extension, with numbers</i>) times one, multiplied by one</li><li>just, only, merely</li><li>alike, likewise</li><li>just now, recently</li><li>(<i>as an intensifier</i>) ever</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>lo!, behold</li><li>truly, indeed</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efn-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the same age</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a peer</li></ol><p><i>efn-</i> +&lrm; <i>eald</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dwell together</li></ol><p><i>efn-</i> +&lrm; <i>eardian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat with</li><li>to eat as much as; match or equal in eating</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ebanetan</i>, equivalent to <i>efn-</i> +&lrm; <i>etan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>ebanezzan</i> (“to eat together”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a balance</li></ol><p>From <i>efnettan</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make even; to square, adjust</li><li>to be equal; to imitate</li></ol><p>From <i>efen</i> +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>equally good</li></ol><p><i>efn-</i> +&lrm; <i>gōd</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>efnhlēoþrung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>singing together; concordance of sounds or voices</li></ol><p>From <i>efn-</i> +&lrm; <i>hlēoþor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing together</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>efn-</i> +&lrm; <i>hlēoþrian</i>, else <i>efnhlēoþor</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>singing together</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>efnhlēoþrian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>, else <i>efn-</i> +&lrm; <i>hlēoþrung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consort, companion</li></ol><p><i>efn-</i> +&lrm; <i>hlytta</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>equal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ebnalīkaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>efn</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>evenly</li><li>equally</li></ol><p>From <i>efnlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>equality</li></ol><p><i>efnlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compare</li></ol><p><i>efn-</i> +&lrm; <i>metan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>equally great or famous</li></ol><p><i>efn-</i> +&lrm; <i>mǣre</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>equinox</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ebnanaht</i> (literally “equal night”), a calque of Latin <i>aequinoctium</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>evennacht</i> and Old Norse <i>jafnnætti</i>. Equivalent to <i>efn-</i> +&lrm; <i>niht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>equally-guilty</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ebnaskuldīg</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>ebansculdig</i>. Equivalent to <i>efn-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċyldiġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to agree</li></ol><p><i>efn-</i> +&lrm; <i>seċġan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soldier of the same company; fellow-soldier, comrade</li></ol><p><i>efn-</i> +&lrm; <i>werod</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an equal being</li><li>equal weight, equipoise</li></ol><p><i>efn-</i> (“equal”) +&lrm; <i>wiht</i> (“being”)</p><p><i>efn-</i> (“equal”) +&lrm; <i>wiht</i> (“weight”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work together: cooperate, collaborate</li></ol><p><i>efn-</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrċan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coworker</li></ol><p><i>efn-</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrċend</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>working together: cooperation, collaboration</li></ol><p>From <i>efnwyrċan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> or <i>efn-</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrċing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coworker</li><li>accomplice</li></ol><p><i>efn-</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sympathize <i>(+ dative = with someone)</i></li></ol><p><i>efn-</i> +&lrm; <i>þrōwian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sympathetic (+ dative = <i>to</i> someone)</li></ol><p><i>efnþrōwian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ende</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sympathy</li></ol><p><i>efnþrōwian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i> or <i>efn-</i> +&lrm; <i>þrōwung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eofor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efesian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efesian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efestan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>again</li><li>back (<i>of return or reversal</i>)</li><li>afterwards</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;afti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;afti</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>eft</i>, Old Saxon <i>eft</i>, Old Norse <i>ept</i>, <i>aptr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>again; afterwards; back</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;afti-</i> (“after, again”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;afti</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reborn, born-again</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>eft-</i> +&lrm; <i>ācenned</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rebirth, regeneration</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>eftācenned</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eftācennednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give back</li></ol><p>From <i>eft-</i> +&lrm; <i>āġiefan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reassign, redeliver, give back</li></ol><p>From <i>eft-</i> + <i>betǣċan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>restoration to health</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>eft-</i> +&lrm; <i>bōt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eftċierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn back, return</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>eft-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċierran</i>. Calque of Latin <i>revertor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to come back, come again, return</li></ol><p>From <i>eft-</i> +&lrm; <i>cuman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a return</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>eft-</i> +&lrm; <i>cyme</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eftċierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æfter</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remember</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>eft-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġemyndiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>remembering</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>eft-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġemyndiġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to repeat, reiterate</li></ol><p>From the adverb <i>eft</i>, or from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aftōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn back, return</li></ol><p>From <i>eft-</i> +&lrm; <i>hweorfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meaning unclear&#59; (glosses Latin <i>respectus</i> (“respect, looking back”))</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>eft-</i> +&lrm; <i>lōcung</i> or <i>&#42;eftlōcian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>renewal, restoration</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>eft-</i> +&lrm; <i>nīewung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eftnīewung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a return, a journey back</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>eft-</i> +&lrm; <i>siþ</i> (“journey”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æfter</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īeġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fear</li><li>awe</li><li>alternative form of <i>ēage</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;agi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;agaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂égʰos</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>agi</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐌲𐌹𐍃</i> (agis, “fear”), Ancient Greek <i>ἄχος</i> (ákhos, “pain”).</p><p>Anglian variant of <i>ēage</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fearful</li><li>terrifying, fear-inspiring</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>eġe</i> (“fear”) +&lrm; <i>-ful</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eġeful</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fear or terror</li><li>the source of fear or terror</li><li>owner</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;agisō</i>, extension from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;agaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to terrify</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;agisōn</i>. Equivalent to <i>eġe</i> +&lrm; <i>-sian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fearful, dreadful, horrible, terrifying</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>eġesa</i> (“fear, terror”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Compare Gothic <i>𐌰𐌲𐌹𐍃𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (agisleiks).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>eġeslīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>eġeslīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rake, harrow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;agiþō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂oḱ-et-eh₂</i> (“harrow”), from <i>&#42;h₂eḱ-</i> (“sharp”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eċġfriþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to excite, egg</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Norse <i>eggja</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēagþȳrel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Egypta land</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Egyptisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trouble, pain, vex, plague, ail, molest, afflict</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aglijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aglijaną</i> (“to trouble, vex”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;agluz</i> (“troublesome, painful, loathsome”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂egʰ-lo-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂egʰ-</i> (“to be afraid, be depressed”). Akin to Gothic <i>𐌰𐌲𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (agljan), Middle Low German <i>egelen</i> (“to annoy”), Old English <i>eġle</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐌲𐌻𐍃</i> (agls, “shameful”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īeġland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hideous; loathsome; hateful; horrid; troublesome; grievous; painful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;agluz</i> (“cumbersome, tedious, tiresome”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂egʰlo-</i>, <i>&#42;h₂egʰ-</i> (“repulsive, offensive, hateful”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eġlan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īeġland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eġesa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eġesian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īeġstrēam</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ægypta land</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ægypte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Egyptian</li><li><i>(substantive)</i> an Egyptian</li></ol><p>From <i>Egypte</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēagþȳrel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eoh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late West Saxon</i>) eight</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eahtafeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chase <i>(+ genitive or accusative)</i></li><li>to persecute <i>(+ genitive or accusative)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ą̄htijan</i> (“to pursue, persecute”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pursuer, persecutor</li></ol><p><i>ēhtan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eahtoþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chaser, pursuer</li><li>persecutor</li></ol><p>From <i>ēhtan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>persecution</li><li>chase, pursuit</li></ol><p>From <i>ēhtan</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēhtnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēhtnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēhtnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eahtoþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēagþȳrel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Latin script letter <i>L</i>/<i>l</i>.</li><li>alternative form of <i>ǣl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>foreign, strange</li><li>different, other</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ali-</i>, <i>&#42;allja-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ali-</i> ~ <i>&#42;alja-</i>, combining form of <i>&#42;aljaz</i> (“other”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂élyos</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>eli-</i>, Old High German <i>eli-</i>, and Old Norse <i>alja-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>elnboga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣlċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eolh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>elsewhere; besides, except, otherwise</li></ol><p>Proto-Germanic. Akin to Old Frisian <i>ekker</i>, <i>elker</i>, <i>elkes</i>, Old Saxon <i>elkor</i>, Old High German <i>elichōr</i>, <i>ellichōr</i>, <i>elihor</i>, Icelandic <i>ellegar</i>, <i>elligar</i>, <i>ellar</i>, <i>ella</i>. Compare Old English <i>elles</i> (“other, else”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ieldu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ielde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ieldu</i></li></ol></html>:/ˈeldrɑ/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ieldra</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ieldum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oil</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;oli</i>, from Latin <i>oleum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>baked in oil</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ele</i> (“oil”) +&lrm; <i>bacen</i> (“baked”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>olive tree</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;olibaum</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>olibaum</i> (German <i>Ölbaum</i>). Compare also Gothic <i>𐌰𐌻𐌴𐍅𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌲𐌼𐍃</i> (alēwabagms), which is almost the same but has a slightly different word for "oil." Equivalent to <i>ele</i> (“oil”) +&lrm; <i>bēam</i> (“tree”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>olive</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;olibaʀi</i>, equivalent to <i>ele</i> (“oil”) +&lrm; <i>berġe</i> (“berry”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>oliberi</i>, Old High German <i>oliberi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eleberġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>container for holding oil</li></ol><p>From <i>ele</i> (“oil”) +&lrm; <i>fæt</i> (“container”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a foreign land</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elecampane</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;elunā</i> (“elecampane”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Elesa</li></ol><p>From <i>Elesa</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>olive tree</li></ol><p>From <i>ele</i> (“oil”) +&lrm; <i>trēow</i> (“tree”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ielf</i> (“elf”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ielfen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ælfflǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foreign, strange</li></ol><p>From <i>el-</i> +&lrm; <i>fremed</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eolh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ely, a cathedral city in Cambridgeshire</li></ol><p>Unknown. Often derived in folk etymologies from <i>ǣl</i> (“eel”). Dowdy prefers a derivation from <i>Elysium</i>, while Miller prefers a Celtic/Brythonic origin such as <i>helyg</i> (“willows”) or <i>heli</i> (“saltwater”). More at Ely.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Elizabeþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Elizabeþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Elizabeth</li><li>Elizabeth, mother of John the Baptist</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Elisabeth</i>, from the Ancient Greek <i>Ἐλισάβετ</i> (Elisábet), a transliteration of the Old Testament Biblical Hebrew <i>אלישבע</i> (Elisheva, “my God is an oath”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strength, courage, bravery</li><li>elder tree</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) elder tree, elder wood</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;alljan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>ellan</i>, Old High German <i>ellan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (aljan), Old Norse <i>eljan</i> (Icelandic <i>elja</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contend vigorously</li></ol><p>From <i>ellen</i> +&lrm; <i>campian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strength</li></ol><p><i>ellen</i> (“strength”) +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i> (“art, skill”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Allington (a place in Wiltshire, England), site of the Battle of Ellendun</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a powerful spirit (used in <i>Beowulf</i> to speak of Grendel)</li></ol><p>From <i>ellen</i> (“powerful”) +&lrm; <i>gāst</i> (“ghost”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ellengāst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>remarkably strong, powerful</li></ol><p><i>ellen</i> (“strength”) +&lrm; <i>rōf</i> (“strong, brave”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ellen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>else, otherwise, in another manner</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;alljas</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>elnung</i></li></ol></html>:/ˈel.lor/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>elsewhere</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aljar</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a spirit living or going elsewhere, a departing spirit</li></ol><p>From <i>ellor</i> (“elsewhere”) +&lrm; <i>gāst</i> (“ghost”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>elreord</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ellen</i> (“elder tree”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>elþēodiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elm tree</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;elm</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;elmaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁élem</i> (“mountain elm”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ell, cubit</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;alinu</i>, whence also Old High German <i>elina</i>, Old Norse <i>öln</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>ulna</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elbow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;alinabugô</i>, corresponding to <i>eln</i> +&lrm; <i>boga</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>elleboghe</i> (Dutch <i>elleboog</i>), Old High German <i>elinbogo</i> (German <i>Ellenbogen</i>), Old Norse <i>ǫllnbogi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strengthen</li><li>to emulate</li><li>to strive to be equal to</li></ol><p>From <i>ellen</i> (“strength”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zeal</li><li>jealousy</li></ol><p>From <i>ellen</i> (“zeal, strength, power, strife”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>elene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elephant</li></ol><p>Probably a clipping of <i>elpend</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>elpendbān</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>elpendbǣnen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elephant</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;elpand</i> (“elephant; ivory”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>elpendbān</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ivory</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;elpandabain</i>, equivalent to <i>elpend</i> (“elephant”) +&lrm; <i>bān</i> (“bone”). Cognate with Old Dutch <i>elpondbēn</i>, Old High German <i>elfantbein</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ivory, made of ivory</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;elpandabainīn</i>, equivalent to <i>elpend</i> +&lrm; <i>bǣnen</i> or <i>elpendbān</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>another</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ellen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foreign-speaking</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aliraʀd</i>, equivalent to <i>el-</i> +&lrm; <i>reord</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>elirart</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foreign-speaking</li></ol><p>From <i>elreord</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>elreordiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Æþelwacer</i> (<i>Domesday Book</i> spelling)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foreign country or nation; foreigners collectively</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aliþeudu</i>, equivalent to <i>el-</i> (“foreign”) +&lrm; <i>þēod</i> (“nation”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>elithioda</i>, Old High German <i>elidiota</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foreign, strange</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) foreigner, stranger</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aliþeudig</i>, <i>&#42;aliþiudīg</i>, equivalent to <i>elþēod</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>elþēodiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>elþēodiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efn-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ymb</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ymbfær</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ymbgang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ambehtan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amiens (a city in France).</li><li>the residents of Amiens</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ymbsnīþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ymbwlātian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efn</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efn-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnettan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnniht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efnettan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣmetta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æmtiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>engel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>and</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>end</li><li>extremity, (outer) limit, border</li><li>(<i>of a building</i>) section, part; corner</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;andī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andijaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂entíos</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>ende</i>, Old Saxon <i>endi</i>, Old Dutch <i>ende</i>, Old High German <i>enti</i>, Old Norse <i>endir</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (andeis).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an arrangement, order</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;andībruʀdī</i> (“order, row”, literally “end-point”). Cognate with Old High German <i>entibrurtī</i> (“order, line, rank”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set in order</li><li>to adjust</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>endebyrd</i> (“arrangement”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>orderly, belonging to order</li><li><i>(of numbers)</i> ordinal</li></ol><p>From <i>endebyrd</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>orderly</li><li>ordinal</li></ol><p>From either <i>endebyrd</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i> or <i>endebyrdlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a row, series, rank, grade, or degree of things, based on shared traits, level, or status</li><li>an order or class (of objects, beings, people, etc.)</li><li>a rank or order within a department</li><li>a succession in place or time</li><li>the order or condition in which a thing functions properly</li><li>the order or method by which a goal is reached</li><li>a mode, procedure, rite, or practice</li><li>the order of nature</li><li>the action of putting something in, or maintaining, order</li><li>an injunction or ordinance</li></ol><p>Equivalent to either <i>endebyrd</i> (“an arrangement, order”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i> (adjective suffix), or to <i>endebyrdan</i> (“to set in order, adjust”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>final death</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ende</i> +&lrm; <i>dēaþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) final day, day of death</li></ol><p>From <i>ende</i> +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>endless</li><li>infinite</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andijalausaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>ende</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>everlastingly, for eternity</li><li>without end</li></ol><p>From <i>endelēas</i> (“endless”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endlessness</li></ol><p>From <i>endelēas</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>endelēasnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a person living at the last age of the world</li></ol><p><i>ende</i> +&lrm; <i>mann</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>endenīehst</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ende</i> +&lrm; <i>nēhst</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>endenīehst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>number or order</i>) last, endmost</li><li>(<i>time</i>) last, final; latest, most recent</li><li>(<i>rank, position, or degree</i>) coming in last, last place, lowest</li><li>(<i>place or location</i>) furthest away, most remote</li></ol><p>From <i>ende</i> +&lrm; <i>nīehst</i>, literally “nearest to the end”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>endenīehst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) coastguard, border watchman</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;transitive) to end</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to finish, complete</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to abolish, destroy</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to come to an end, cease; to die</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andijōną</i> (“to end”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>endia</i> (“to finish”), Old Saxon <i>endiōn</i> (“to come to a stop”), Old High German <i>entōn</i> (“to end”), Old Norse <i>enda</i> (“to end”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eleventh</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eleventh</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ainaliftô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eleven</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ainalifun</i>, alteration of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ainalif</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ainalif</i>, a compound of <i>&#42;ainaz</i> and <i>&#42;-lif</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>andlova</i>, Old Saxon <i>ellevan</i>, Old High German <i>einlif</i>, Old Norse <i>ellifu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>endlyfta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>endleofan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eleventh</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eleventh</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ainaliftô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ending</li></ol><p>From <i>endian</i> (“to end”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duck (aquatic bird of the family Anatidae)</li><li>coot</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;anad</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anadz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ange</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;angil</i>, borrowed from Latin <i>angelus</i>, itself borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἄγγελος</i> (ángelos, “messenger”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Anglibalþ</i>, from <i>&#42;Angli</i> (“an Angle”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;balþ</i> (“bold, brave”). Equivalent to <i>Engle</i> +&lrm; <i>beald</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Engilbert</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Angelic, coming from an angel.</li></ol><p>From <i>enġel</i> (“angel”) +&lrm; <i>-cund</i> (“coming from”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>angelic, of angels</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;angilalīk</i>, equivalent to <i>enġel</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>enġel</i> (“angel”) +&lrm; <i>mǣre</i> (“famous”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>England (a medieval kingdom in Northern Europe)</li></ol><p>From the genitive of <i>Engle</i> (“Angles”) + <i>land</i> (“land”). Literally "land of the Angles."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Engla land</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the English</li><li>England</li><li>the Angles</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Angli</i>. The name is possibly derived from <i>angol</i> (“fishhook”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;angul</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;angulaz</i>, referring to either the Angles' habitat on the Jutland coast, shaped like a fishhook, or the Angles' probable occupation as fishermen.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>enġellīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the English language; Old English</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>English</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) an Englishman or an Angle</li></ol><p>From <i>Engle</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Englishman</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Englisċman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narrowness</li><li>confinement</li><li>a narrow place</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;angī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;angį̄</i>, equivalent to <i>ange</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>engī</i>, <i>engīn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ened</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣniġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>inlīhtan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ynnelēac</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ynnelēac</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giant (mythical creature)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;anti</i>, from unknown origin.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>giant; of a giant; belonging to or made by a giant</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;antisk</i> (“giant, gigantic”), equivalent to <i>ent</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>entisk</i> (“giant, gigantic”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ānwintre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eofot</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>edor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>edor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eafa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eofor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eoforwīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eoforwīcċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eoforwīċsċīr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Evesham (a town and civil parish with a town council in Wychavon district, Worcestershire, England)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ēof</i> (a male given name) +&lrm; <i>hām</i> (“home(stead)”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eofot</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Northumbrian</i>) to blaspheme; to reproach, insult</li></ol><p>From either <i>æf-</i> +&lrm; <i>hālsian</i> (“to implore/beseech”), or rare ef-, likely of similar origin; compare eofot (“sin/crime”). Forms of yfelsian are a result of folk-etymological reanalysis.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blasphemy</li></ol><p>From <i>eofolsian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boar; wild boar</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ebur</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>ebur</i>, Dutch <i>ever</i>, Old High German <i>ebur</i> (German <i>Eber</i>), Old Norse <i>jǫfurr</i>, also Latin <i>aper</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, alternative spelling of <i>Eoforhwæt</i> &lsqb;9th C. <small>CE</small>&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Eburahwat</i>, from <i>&#42;ebur</i> (“boar”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hwat</i> (“quick, sharp”). Equivalent to <i>eofor</i> +&lrm; <i>hwæt</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Eparwas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a form or figure of a boar, attested referring to the boar crest on a helmet</li></ol><p>From <i>eofor</i> +&lrm; <i>līċ</i>, the latter with the very archaic meaning “form.”</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boar-spear</li></ol><p>From <i>eofor</i> (“boar”) +&lrm; <i>sprēot</i> (“pole”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>York</li></ol><p>A folk-etymological alteration, based on <i>eofor</i> (“boar”) and the common placename suffix <i>wīc</i> (“village”), of the earlier Latin <i>Eborācum</i>, from pre-Brythonic <i>&#42;Eborākom</i>, from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;eburos</i> (“yew-tree”) (compare Welsh <i>efwr</i>, Breton <i>evor</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>York</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Eoforwīc</i> (“York”) +&lrm; <i>ċeaster</i> (“town, city”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yorkshire</li></ol><p><i>Eoforwīċ</i> (“York”) +&lrm; <i>sċīr</i> (“county”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>eofor</i> (“boar”) and <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>eofor</i> (“boar”) + <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>eoforþrote</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the constellation of Orion</li></ol><p>From <i>eofor</i> (“boar”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;þring</i> (“crowd”) (compare <i>ġeþring</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) carline thistle</li></ol><p>From <i>eofor</i> (“boar”) +&lrm; <i>þrote</i> (“throat”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sin, crime</li><li>debt</li><li>glossing Latin <i>epiphonema</i>; perhaps meaning a declaration of blame, or a condemnation</li><li>as part of the phrase flītere in <i>eofotum</i>, glossing Latin rabulus; perhaps a criminal case or suit</li></ol><p>From either <i>æf-</i> +&lrm; <i>hāt</i> (“promise, command”), or rare ef-, likely of similar origin; compare eofolsian (“to blaspheme”). The spelling <i>ebhat-</i> found in the Épinal and Erfurt glossaries (c. 700) serves as significant evidence for this etymology.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eofolsung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eofot</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) horse, steed</li><li>the runic character ᛖ (/e(ː)/)</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) yew tree</li><li>the runic character ᛇ (/eo/, /eoː/, or /ç/ depending on its use as a vowel or a consonant)</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;h₁éḱwos</td></tr></table></p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ehwaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁éḱwos</i>.</p><p>A variant of <i>īw</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;īh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eorīc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eolh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eolh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>elene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea voyage, journey</li></ol><p>Either from an unattested <i>&#42;eol</i> + <i>-et</i> (as in <i>þiccet</i> (“thicket”)), or most likely from a variant of <i>&#42;ēalēt</i> from <i>ēa</i> (“water”) + <i>ġelēt</i>, <i>ġelǣt</i> (“connector, conduit”) from <i>lǣtan</i> (“to let, leave, allow, permit”), compare also <i>wæterġelǣt</i> (“water-course, aqueduct”), <i>ġeoclet</i> (“land measure, small estate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elk (<i>UK</i>), moose (<i>US</i>) (the largest member of the deer family&#59; <i>Alces alces</i>)</li><li>the runic character ᛉ (/ks/)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;elh</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;elhaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amber</li></ol><p>From <i>eolh</i> (“elk”) +&lrm; <i>sand</i> (“sand”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Eolla; 8th century Bishop of Selsey.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>elene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>him</i>: (to) him/it/them</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;im</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;immi</i> (“I am”), via the variant <i>*imō</i> by analogy with regular first-person singular ending <i>*-ō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ésmi</i> (“I am, I exist”), a form of the verb <i>&#42;wesaną</i>. The variant <i>eam</i> is apparently after the vocalism of <i>eart</i>.</p><p>Akin to Old Norse <i>em</i> (“I am”), Gothic <i>𐌹𐌼</i> (im, “I am”), Old High German <i>bim</i> (“I am”), Ancient Greek <i>εἰμί</i> (eimí), Sanskrit <i>अस्मि</i> (ásmi).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Eomer of Mercia, a semi-legendary king of the Angles</li></ol><p>From <i>eoh</i> (“Horse”) +&lrm; <i>mǣre</i> (“famous”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Eomær</li></ol><p>From <i>Eomær</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeond-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Eoppa</li></ol><p>From <i>Eoppa</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eorcenberht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Eorcenberht of Kent, an Anglo-Saxon King of Kent.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Erknaberht</i>, from <i>&#42;erkn</i> (“pure, precious”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”). Equivalent to <i>eorcnan</i> +&lrm; <i>beorht</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Erchanberht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Erknawald</i>, from <i>&#42;erkn</i> (“pure, precious”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wald</i> (“might, authority”). Equivalent to <i>eorcnan</i> +&lrm; <i>weald</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Erchanolt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eorcnanstān</i></li></ol><p>Dissimilated from <i>eorcnanstān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>precious, noble, true(?)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;erknaz</i>, cognate with Old High German <i>erchan</i>. Attested only in composition.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>precious stone, pearl</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>eorcnan</i> (“special, noble”) +&lrm; <i>stān</i> (“stone”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>jarknasteinn</i>, which is generally regarded as a loan from Old English.</p><p>Translates <i>margarita</i> in 9th century biblical glosses but is used generically as "precious stone, gem" in <i>Beowulf</i> (line 1208) and <i>The Ruin</i> (v. 36).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eorcenberht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eorþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cavalry; band; troop; legion</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ehwaraidu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ehwaraidō</i> (“horse-riding”), equivalent to <i>eoh</i> +&lrm; <i>rād</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Eored)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēarendel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>Eiríkr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A nobleman; a male belonging to the nobility.</li><li>A warrior or hero.</li><li>In senses referring to specific noble ranks or governmental positions&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>A Norse under-king; a jarl.</li><li>An ealdorman (Anglo-Saxon high nobleman and governmental official).</li><li>A count of continental Europe.</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;erlaz</i>, further origin unknown; cognate with Old Saxon <i>erl</i>, Old High German <i>erl</i>, Old Norse <i>jarl</i>. The use of this term in reference to specific positions and ranks is likely a semantic loan from Old Norse <i>jarl</i> and Old French <i>conte</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>noble, earl-like</li></ol><p><i>eorl</i> (“noble”) +&lrm; <i>-cund</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earldom</li></ol><p>From <i>eorl</i> +&lrm; <i>-dōm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noble birth, nobility</li></ol><p>From <i>eorl</i> +&lrm; <i>ġebyrd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bravery, manliness, nobility</li></ol><p>From <i>eorl</i> (“warrior, brave man”) +&lrm; <i>-sċiepe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eorlsċiepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eorlsċipe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeormanlēaf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Ermunaburg</i>, from <i>&#42;Ermun</i> (“deity Irmin”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;burg</i> (“fortification”). Equivalent to <i>&#42;Eormen</i> +&lrm; <i>burg</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Irminburg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p><i>&#42;Eormen</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Ermunahildi</i>, from <i>&#42;Ermun</i> (“deity Irmin”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hildi</i> (“battle”). Equivalent to <i>&#42;Eormen</i> +&lrm; <i>hild</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Erminhilt</i>, Early Medieval Latin <i>Ermenildis</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eormenrǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Eormenred of Kent; probably an Anglo-Saxon King of Kent, brother of Eorcenberht of Kent.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Ermunarād</i>, from <i>&#42;Ermun</i> (“deity Irmin”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;rād</i> (“advice, counsel”). Equivalent to <i>&#42;Eormen</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣd</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Irminrāt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Ermunaþrūþi</i>, from <i>&#42;Ermun</i> (“deity Irmin”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;þrūþi</i> (“strength”). Equivalent to <i>&#42;Eormen</i> +&lrm; <i>þrȳþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Irmindrud</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>iermþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eornost</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eornostlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earnestness</li><li>vigour</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ernustuz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>ernust</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>earnestly</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>earnestly, strictly, truly</li></ol><p>From <i>eornost</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>ernustlīhho</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eornostlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēored</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark, dusky</li><li>brown, swarthy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;erpaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁erbʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Erpawini</i>, from <i>&#42;erp</i> (“brown, swarthy”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wini</i> (“friend”). Equivalent to <i>eorp</i> +&lrm; <i>wine</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Erfoin</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ierre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>eorþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eorþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>eorþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eorþhūs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>eorþsċræf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>eorþwela</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>eorþern</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eorþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earthquake</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;erþubibungu</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>erthbivinge</i>, Old Saxon <i>erđbiƀunga</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;erthbivunga</i>, Old High German <i>erdbibunga</i>. Equivalent to <i>eorþe</i> (“earth”) +&lrm; <i>beofung</i> (“shaking”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strawberry</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;erþōbazją</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>erthberi</i>, Old High German <i>erdberi</i>. Composed of <i>eorþ</i> (“ground”) +&lrm; <i>berġe</i> (“berry”), so called because its fruit lies upon the ground.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>eorþberġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eorþberġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eorþbeofung</i></li></ol><p>From <i>eorþe</i> +&lrm; <i>bifung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>geometry</li></ol><p>From <i>eorþe</i> (“earth”) +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i>. Partially a calque of Ancient Greek <i>γεωμετρία</i> (geōmetría).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>earthly, terrestrial.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;erþōkundaz</i>, equivalent to <i>eorþ</i> (“earth”) +&lrm; <i>-cund</i> (“born”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an earthly king, the king of a land</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>eorþe</i> +&lrm; <i>cyning</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) earth dragon</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>eorþ</i> (“earth”) +&lrm; <i>draca</i> (“dragon”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earthquake</li></ol><p>From <i>eorþe</i> (“earth”) +&lrm; <i>dyne</i> (“din”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ground</li><li>dirt</li><li>the planet Earth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;erþu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;erþō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ierþen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earthen place</li><li>burial chamber</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>eorþe</i> +&lrm; <i>-ern</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an earthen vessel, the body</li></ol><p>From <i>eorþe</i> +&lrm; <i>fæt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earthnut</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;erþōhnuts</i>. Equivalent to <i>eorþ</i> (“earth”) +&lrm; <i>hnutu</i> (“nut”). Cognate with Old High German <i>erdnuz</i> and Old Norse <i>jarðhnot</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>den, cave</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;erþahūs</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;erþahūsą</i>. Equivalent to <i>eorþe</i> (“earth”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>jarðhús</i> (“underground room or passage”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ground ivy</li></ol><p><i>eorþe</i> +&lrm; <i>īfiġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>earthly, terrestrial</li></ol><p>From <i>eorþe</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ierþling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eorþnafola</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asparagus</li></ol><p>From <i>eorþe</i> (“earth”) +&lrm; <i>nafola</i> (“navel”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eorþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eorþræċed</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a ground-chamber; cave</li></ol><p>From <i>eorþ</i> +&lrm; <i>ræċed</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earthen cave</li></ol><p>From <i>eorþe</i> (“earth”) +&lrm; <i>sċræf</i> (“cave”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a cave</li></ol><p>From <i>eorþe</i> (“earth, ground”) +&lrm; <i>sele</i> (“hall”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eorþstyrung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earthquake</li></ol><p>From <i>eorþe</i> (“earth”) +&lrm; <i>styrung</i> (“stirring”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asphalt</li></ol><p>From <i>eorþe</i> (“earth”) +&lrm; <i>tirwa</i> (“tar, resin”), related to <i>teoru</i> (“tar”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eorþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the inhabitants of Earth</li></ol><p>From <i>eorþe</i> (“Earth”) +&lrm; <i>-ware</i> (“residents”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a rampart, wall, or mound made of earth</li></ol><p>From <i>eorþ</i> (“earth”) +&lrm; <i>weall</i> (“wall”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) Earth's wealth</li></ol><p>From <i>eorþe</i> (“earth”) +&lrm; <i>wela</i> (“wealth”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eorþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cucumber</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;erþapplu</i>. Equivalent to <i>eorþe</i> (“dirt, ground”) +&lrm; <i>æppel</i> (“apple”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eorþern</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>esol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Eosterwine; 7th century Anglo-Saxon Abbot of Wearmouth (Sunderland)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Austrāwini</i>, from <i>&#42;Austrā</i> (“deity Eostre”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wini</i> (“friend”). Equivalent to <i>Ēastre</i> +&lrm; <i>wine</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ōstruin</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The godess Easter</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;austrā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Austrǭ</i>, from a suffixed form of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ews-</i> (“dawn”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>āsteron</i>, Old High German <i>ōstarūn</i> (German <i>Ostern</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jutland</li></ol><p>Literally "land of the Jutes."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jutland</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Eutalandą</i>, whence also Old Norse <i>Jótland</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>etan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Jutes</li><li>Jutland</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;eutōz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>jótar</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giant, monster</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;etun</i>, cognate with Old Norse <i>jǫtunn</i> (Swedish <i>jätte</i>, Danish <i>jætte</i>). Related to <i>eat</i>, see Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;etunaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jutes</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or made by a giant</li></ol><p>From <i>eoten</i> (“giant”) +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Italy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic and ultimately from Latin <i>Italia</i> (which itself was later borrowed into Old English as <i>Italia</i>, a doublet of this term). It appears not to be an Anglo-Saxon-era borrowing, as it shows the marks of a lengthy chain of vowel changes that can only be explained by a native evolution from a much earlier borrowing predating the Middle Ages. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Italians, the people of Italy</li></ol><p>From <i>Eotol</i> (“Italy”) +&lrm; <i>-ware</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>Expression of grief, sorrow, or surprise Wo!; Alas!</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īw</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;iwwiz</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;izwiz</i> (dative/accusative case of <i>&#42;jūz</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wes</i> (compare also <i>&#42;yúHs</i>, whence <i>&#42;jūz</i>).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>ju</i> (West Frisian <i>jo</i>), Old Saxon <i>iu</i>, <i>ju</i> (Low German <i>jo</i>, <i>ju</i>), Dutch <i>u</i>, Old High German <i>iu</i> (German <i>euch</i>), Old Norse <i>yðr</i> (Icelandic <i>yður</i>, Swedish <i>er</i>), Gothic <i>𐌹𐌶𐍅𐌹𐍃</i> (izwis).</p><p>Natural exclamation</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īewan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēowde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flock, herd</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;awidiją</i> (“flock”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>penis (perhaps specifically an erect one)</li></ol><p>Uncertain, but perhaps from <i>ēowend</i> (“one who shows”, literally “show-er”), from <i>ēowan</i> (“to show”), equivalent to <i>ēowan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i> (agent noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) your (<i>plural</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;iuwar</i>, whence also Old Saxon <i>iuwar</i> and Old High German <i>iuwar</i>, Old Norse <i>yðvarr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌹𐌶𐍅𐌰𐍂</i> (izwar).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheepfold</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;awistr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;awistrą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Öland</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ewe</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;awi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;awiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ephesus (An ancient Greek city in Anatolia, near Selçuk in modern İzmir Province, Turkey)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Ephesus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Ἔφεσος</i> (Éphesos), ultimately from Luwian</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a letter, epistle</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>epistola</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>epistula</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐌹𐍀𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌿𐌻𐌴</i> (aipistaulē).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>epistol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>magic</i>) holy</li></ol><p>Attested in the 11th-century <i>Æcerbot</i> ("field-remedy") charm.</p><p>The triple invocation <i>erce, erce, erce</i> is compared to the Latin <i>sanctus, sanctus, sanctus</i>, and interpreted as derived from a vocative form of eorcnan "true, genuine; holy", or a proper name <i>Erce</i>, from an earlier <i>*Eorce</i> for a fertility goddess addressed as "mother of earth".</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>arċe-</i> (“arch-”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>arċebisċop</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>arċebisċop</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eorcenberht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hercules</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Herculēs</i>, from Etruscan <i>𐌇𐌄𐌓𐌂𐌋𐌄</i> (hercle), from Ancient Greek <i>Ἡρακλῆς</i> (Hēraklês),</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Pillars of Hercules</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eorcenberht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eorcenberht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ierþling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ierfe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>erian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>erienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plough</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arjaną</i>, whence also Old High German <i>erran</i>, Old Norse <i>erja</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂erh₃-</i> (“to plough”). Non-Germanic cognates include Latin <i>arō</i>, Ancient Greek <i>ἀρόω</i> (aróō), Old Irish <i>airid</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>орати</i> (orati), Albanian <i>arë</i> (“arable land, soil”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>erian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>erienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eormenrǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>earming</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>iermþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ærn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>park, preserve</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ersċhenn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quail</li></ol><p>From <i>ersċ</i> (“park”) +&lrm; <i>henn</i> (“hen”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ersċhenn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eorþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ierþling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Exanċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scunthorpe (a town in North Lincolnshire district, Lincolnshire, England, United Kingdom)</li></ol><p>From Old Norse, equivalent to <i>Skuna</i> (a Norse given name) + <i>þorp</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eaxl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>esl</i> (“shoulder”) (in the genitive). Otherwise, possibly a nickname for Elesa.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a man of the servile class; a servant</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;asnī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;asunz</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Esne)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>donkey</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;asil</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female donkey, she-ass</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>esol</i> (“donkey”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to waste, consume</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;auþisōną</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;auþijaz</i> (“void, barren, waste”), equivalent to <i>īeþe</i> +&lrm; <i>-sian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consent, grace, favor; kindness</li><li>pleasure</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ansti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anstiz</i> (“grace, thanks”), derivative of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unnaną</i> (“to grant, thank”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ān-</i> (“to notice; face, mouth”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>anst</i> (“grace, favour”), Old High German <i>anst</i> (“goodwill, benevolence, thanks, grace”), Gothic <i>𐌰𐌽𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (ansts, “joy, grace, thankfulness”). Related to Old English <i>unnan</i> (“to grant, allow”). More at own.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Aestii, or the land owned by the Aestii</li></ol><p>From the name of the <i>Aestii</i> tribe (see Wikipedia).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gracious</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Land of the Aestii</li></ol><p>Derived from the plural noun <i>Ēste</i> (“the Aestii”), from the name of the <i>Aestii</i> tribe (see Wikipedia). The first element could also be an unattested singular form <i>&#42;Ēst</i>, from the same source. In any case, the second element is <i>land</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;etan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;etaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ed-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eoten</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ethiopia</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ethiopian</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) an Ethiopian</li></ol><p><i>Ethiopia</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eating</li></ol><p><i>etan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Etna (volcano in Sicily, Italy)</li></ol><p>Latin <i>Aetna</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Etna</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consume the produce of the land, pasture, graze</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;attjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;atjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eve (the first woman in the Bible)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Eva</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Εὔα</i> (Eúa), from Biblical Hebrew <i>חַוָּה</i> (ḥawwā).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eoforwīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Euphrates (a river in the Middle East)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Εὐφράτης</i> (Euphrátēs), from Old Persian <i>𐎢𐎳𐎼𐎠𐎬𐎢</i> (<sup>h</sup>Ufrātuš), from Akkadian <i>𒀀𒇉𒌓𒄒𒉣</i> (purattu), itself from Sumerian <i>𒀀𒇉𒌓𒄒𒉣</i> (<sup>ÍD</sup>Buranun).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēowestre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Exe (a river in Somerset and Devon, England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ex</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Exanċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Exeter (a cathedral city in modern Devon, England)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ex</i> (“River Exe”) +&lrm; <i>ċeaster</i> (“fortress, fortified town”), in some forms influenced by Latin <i>Isca</i>, both from Proto-Brythonic <i>&#42;Uɨsk</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Exanċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the mouth of the river Exe</li><li>Exmouth (a town in Devon, England)</li></ol><p>From <i>Exa</i> (variant of <i>Ex</i>) +&lrm; <i>mūþa</i> (“mouth”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Exanċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>Exanċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Exanċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>oxan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eaxl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) eclipse</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ἔκλειψις</i> (ékleipsis, “eclipse”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eardung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ēþel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēþelboda</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ēþeldrēam</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēþeleard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēþelfæsten</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ēþelland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ēþellēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēþelland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēþelmearc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ēþelrīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ēþelriht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ēþelstaþol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēþelstōl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēþelstōw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ēþelturf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēþelwynn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ēþelþrym</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Æþelrīc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣþm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣþmian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēaþmōdlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēaþmōdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Edington, a village in Wiltshire, England</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īeþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one's native country, homeland</li><li>the runic character ᛟ (/ø(ː)/)</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>œ̄þel</i> (persisting longer in the north) from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ōþil</i>, from a variant of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ōþalą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Ōþilaberht</i>, from <i>&#42;ōþil</i> (“homeland, patrimony”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”). Equivalent to <i>ēþel</i> +&lrm; <i>beorht</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Uodalberht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a native preacher, apostle of a country</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēþel</i> +&lrm; <i>boda</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joy from one's homeland</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēþel</i> +&lrm; <i>drēam</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æþele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>native dwelling</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēþel</i> +&lrm; <i>eard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a country's fastness or fortress</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēþel</i> +&lrm; <i>fæsten</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Æþelgar</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æþeling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>native land</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēþel</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>countryless, homeless</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēþel</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the boundary of a country</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēþel</i> +&lrm; <i>mearc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>native realm</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēþel</i> +&lrm; <i>rīċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the right of a country</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēþel</i> +&lrm; <i>riht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>native settlement</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēþel</i> +&lrm; <i>staþol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>native seat or dwelling</li><li>a country's chief or mother city</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēþel</i> +&lrm; <i>stōl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwelling-place</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēþel</i> +&lrm; <i>stōw</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>home turf, native country</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēþel</i> +&lrm; <i>turf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guardian of the homeland</li></ol><p>From <i>ēþel</i> +&lrm; <i>weard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Ōþilawini</i>, from <i>&#42;ōþil</i> (“homeland, patrimony”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wini</i> (“friend”). Equivalent to <i>ēþel</i> +&lrm; <i>wine</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ōdalwin</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joy of country</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēþel</i> +&lrm; <i>wynn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the native country's dignity</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēþel</i> +&lrm; <i>þrym</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to breathe</li><li>to smell</li><li>to pant</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;anþijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anþijaną</i> (“to breathe”), from <i>&#42;anþą</i> (“breath”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂enh₁-</i> (“to breathe”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēþian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēþel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣþm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breath</li><li>a breathing</li><li>an inspiration</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ēþian</i> (“to breathe”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>oþþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fāh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deceit, crime</li></ol><p>Cognate with Proto-Norse <i>ᚠᚨᛁᚲᛁᚾᚨᛉ</i> (faikinaʀ), whence Old Norse <i>feikinn</i>, <i>feikn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deceitful, crafty</li></ol><p><i>fācen</i> (“deceit”) +&lrm; <i>-ful</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>false sign</li><li>sign of crimes</li></ol><p>From <i>fācen</i> +&lrm; <i>tācen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A flatfish (plaice or flounder)</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obtain, acquire</li><li>to try to obtain; get; get to, reach</li></ol><p>Unknown, yet apparently from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;faik-</i>, whence also Old English <i>fǣċcan</i> (“to wish for, strive for”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deceit, fraud, guile</li><li>treachery</li><li>malice, wickedness, evil</li><li>crime</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;faikn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faikną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fæder</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fader</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fadēr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ph₂tḗr</i>.</p><p>Old Germanic cognates: Old Frisian <i>feder</i>, Old Saxon <i>fadar</i>, Old Dutch <i>fader</i>, Old High German <i>fater</i>, Old Norse <i>faðir</i>, Gothic <i>𐍆𐌰𐌳𐌰𐍂</i> (fadar).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to direct, guide</li><li>to arrange, set in order, dispose</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fadōn</i>, perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂-</i> (“to guard, keep watch, shepherd”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fæder</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fader</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fadēr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ph₂tḗr</i>.</p><p>Old Germanic cognates: Old Frisian <i>feder</i>, Old Saxon <i>fadar</i>, Old Dutch <i>fader</i>, Old High German <i>fater</i>, Old Norse <i>faðir</i>, Gothic <i>𐍆𐌰𐌳𐌰𐍂</i> (fadar).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a setting in order, disposing, dispensation</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fadungu</i>, equivalent to <i>fadian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>fatunga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fāh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fæġnian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change color</li></ol><p>From <i>fāh</i> +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>variety of color</li><li>scab</li><li>ulcer</li><li>eruption</li></ol><p><i>fāh</i> (“colored, decorated”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fæġnian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fāgnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) basilisk</li></ol><p>From <i>fāg</i> (“multicolored, pied”) +&lrm; <i>wyrm</i> (“worm”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>guilty; criminal</li><li>hostile</li><li>outlawed</li><li>decorated, coloured, shining, adorned</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;faih</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faihaz</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;faih</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faihaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;póyḱos</i>; cognate with Old High German <i>fēh</i>, Gothic <i>𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌿𐍆𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍃</i> (filufaihs). The Proto-Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek <i>ποικίλος</i> (poikílos, “multicoloured”).</p><p>The inflected stem <i>fāg-</i> may be because this word ultimately reflects a Proto-Germanic variant <i>&#42;faigaz</i>; alternatively, it may be due to analogy with other adjectives with an alternation between [x] and [ɣ], such as <i>smēag</i>, <i>smēah</i> (“creeping, subtle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enemy</li></ol><p>From <i>fāh</i> (“hostile”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fela</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>falod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>feallan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheepfold</li><li>ox stall</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;falud</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>falsehood, fraud, counterfeit</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of weight or coinage</i>) false</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>falsus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>falod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>falod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foam</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faimaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foaming</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fām</i> +&lrm; <i>blāwende</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to foam, froth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;faimagōn</i>, equivalent to <i>fām</i> +&lrm; <i>-gian</i> and <i>fāmiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foamy, frothy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;faimag</i>, <i>&#42;faimīg</i>, equivalent to <i>fām</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;poetic) "foamy-necked," said of a ship</li></ol><p><i>fāmiġ</i> (“foamy”) +&lrm; <i>heals</i> (“neck, prow”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bit of cloth, patch</li><li>a banner or standard; a flag</li><li>a vane</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fanō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fanô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to try, tempt, prove, test</li><li>to examine, explore; seek, research</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fandōną</i> (“to find out”), related to <i>&#42;finþaną</i> (“to come upon, find”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pent-</i> (“to go, pass, bridge”). Akin to Old Frisian <i>fandia</i>, <i>fandlia</i> (West Frisian <i>fanljen</i>), Old Saxon <i>fandōn</i>, Old High German <i>fantōn</i> (German <i>fahnden</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temptation, trial</li><li>proof</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>fandian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plunder, booty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fangą</i>, <i>&#42;fangiz</i>, <i>&#42;fanhiz</i> (“catch, catching, seizure”), from <i>&#42;fanhaną</i> (“to catch, capture”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fan (implement for winnowing grain)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;βannu</i>, <i>&#42;wannu</i> (“winnowing fan”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to winnow grain</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>font, fountain, spring</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>fons</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>fana</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fanu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fanō</i>, a byform of the more general Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fanô</i> (“cloth; flag”). More at vane.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>for-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>traveller, farer</li></ol><p>See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;farō</i> (“traveller”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go (used of long distances), to travel</li><li>to fare (to exist in any state)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;faran</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faraną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>fara</i>, Old Saxon <i>faran</i>, Old Dutch <i>faran</i>, Old High German <i>faran</i>, Old Norse <i>fara</i>, Gothic <i>𐍆𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (faran).</p><p>See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>færeld</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fara</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>fareþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pharisaic; relating to the Pharisees</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>feorm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>faroþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) tide, current</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) shore</li></ol><p>From <i>faran</i> +&lrm; <i>-oþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a journey or going</li><li>something transportable, especially one's family</li><li>a march or expedition</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;faru</i>, from the same base as <i>faran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fæs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a large whale</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ἀσπιδοχελώνη</i> (aspidokhelṓnē, “aspidochelone”, a mythical sea creature)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fetian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>father's sister, paternal aunt</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faþǭ</i>, <i>&#42;faþō</i> (“father's sister”), from a derivative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ph₂tḗr</i> (“father”). Akin to Old Frisian <i>fethe</i> (“father's sister, aunt”), Middle Low German <i>vade</i> (“father's sister”), Old English <i>fæder</i> (“father”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>father's sister, paternal aunt</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faþō</i>, <i>&#42;faþǭ</i> (“father's sister”), from a derivative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ph₂tḗr</i> (“father”). Akin to Old Frisian <i>fethe</i> (“father's sister, aunt”), Middle Low German <i>vade</i> (“father's sister”), Old English <i>fæder</i> (“father”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fēaw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>little, small; poor, lacking</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fawaz</i> (“small, few”), from <i>&#42;pau-</i> (“smallness”). Akin to Old English <i>fēawa</i> (“few”), <i>fēawe</i> (“small, little”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fæderlēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>falcon (<i>bird</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;falkan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;falkanaz</i>, related to <i>&#42;falkô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>occasion</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;falþō</i>, related to <i>&#42;fallaną</i> (“to fall”), whence also Old High German <i>falt</i>, Old Norse <i>fald</i> (“case”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fold</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;falþan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;falþaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fealwian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fallow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;falgu</i> (“fallow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fallow, break up land</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>fealg</i> (“fallow”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pipe</li><li>alternative form of <i>fealg</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;falhuz</i> (“pipe”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fiell</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fallan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>falla</i>, Old Saxon <i>fallan</i>, Old Dutch <i>fallan</i>, Old High German <i>fallan</i>, Old Norse <i>falla</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trap</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fallǭ</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>val</i>, German <i>Falle</i>, Swedish <i>fälla</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>yellow, dusky, grey</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;falwaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pel-</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>falu</i>, Middle Dutch <i>valu</i>, <i>vale</i> (Dutch <i>vaal</i>), Old High German <i>falo</i> (German <i>fahl</i>), Old Norse <i>fǫlr</i>. The common Germanic is cognate with Latin <i>pallidus</i>, Ancient Greek <i>πελιτνός</i> (pelitnós) and Old Armenian <i>ալիք</i> (alikʻ, “wave, gray hair”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fealwian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow yellow</li><li>to ripen</li><li>to wither</li></ol><p>From <i>fealu</i> (“yellow, sallow”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>faran</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;faran</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faraną</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>fara</i>, Old Saxon <i>faran</i>, Old Dutch <i>faran</i>, Old High German <i>faran</i>, Old Norse <i>fara</i>, Gothic <i>𐍆𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (faran).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piglet</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;farh</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;farhaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pórḱos</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>farah</i>,</p><p>Latin <i>porcus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freight, cargo, load</li><li>alternative form of <i>feorm</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;farm</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;farmaz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>farm</i>, Old High German <i>farm</i>, Old Norse <i>farmr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fern</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;farn</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;p(t)erH-</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>farn</i> (German <i>Farn</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ferny</li></ol><p><i>fearn</i> (“fern”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bull</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;farʀ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;farzaz</i>, a byform of <i>&#42;farzô</i> (“bull, steer”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Fearr)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astrology</i>) Taurus (Zodiac sign)</li></ol><p>From <i>fearr</i> (“bull”), a semantic loan from Latin <i>Taurus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) Having few things; poor, naked, destitute, miserable</li></ol><p>From <i>fēa-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċeaft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poor, wretched</li></ol><p>From <i>fēa</i> (“few”) +&lrm; <i>sċeaft</i> (“creature”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fæstlīċe</i> (“firmly, quickly”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>few</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fau</i>, in turn from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fawaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂w-</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Norse <i>fār</i> (Danish <i>få</i>, Swedish <i>få</i>), Old High German <i>fō</i>, <i>fōh</i>; Further cognates via the Proto-Indo-European root include Latin <i>paucus</i>, <i>pauper</i>, <i>puer</i> (“boy”); Ancient Greek <i>παῖς</i> (paîs, “child”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>fēaw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hair (on the head)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fahs</i> (“hair, mane”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>fax</i>, Old Saxon <i>fahs</i>, Old High German <i>fahs</i>, Old Norse <i>fax</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long-haired</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>feax</i> (“hair”) +&lrm; <i>-ede</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the crime of seizing someone by the hair</li></ol><p>From <i>feax</i> (“hair”) +&lrm; <i>fōn</i> (“to seize”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hairnet</li></ol><p>From <i>feax</i> (“hair”) +&lrm; <i>nett</i> (“net”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>February</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Februārius</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fæc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fetch, bring; to draw</li><li>to seek; to gain, take</li></ol><p>Apparently an alteration of <i>fetian</i>, <i>fatian</i> (“to fetch, induce, marry”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fatōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fatōną</i>, <i>&#42;fatjaną</i> (“to fetch”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ped-</i> (“foot”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>fatia</i> (“to fetch”), Old High German <i>fazzōn</i> (“to touch, grasp”) (German <i>fassen</i>), Old Norse <i>fata</i>, <i>feta</i> (“to go, step”) (Icelandic <i>feta</i>). More at foot.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fōdijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fōdijaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂-</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>fōdian</i>, Dutch <i>voeden</i>, Old High German <i>fuoten</i>, Old Norse <i>fǿða</i> (Danish <i>føde</i>, Swedish <i>föda</i>, Icelandic <i>fæða</i>), Gothic <i>𐍆𐍉𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (fōdjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fæder</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fefor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) feverfew</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>febrifugia</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fever</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>febris</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Faversham (a market town and civil parish with a town council in Swale borough, Kent, England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to attach, join</li><li>to add</li><li>to fix, adapt</li><li>to compose; to confine</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fōgijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fōgijaną</i> (“to join”), from <i>&#42;peh₂ḱ-</i> (“to secure, fasten, put down”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>fōgia</i>, Old Saxon <i>fōgian</i> (“to add”), Dutch <i>voegen</i>, Old High German <i>fuogen</i> (“to add”) (German <i>fügen</i>). Related to fang, fair.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>feoh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woolen fleece</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fahtiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>feohtan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fell</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>many</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) much</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>many</li><li>a lot, much</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) much, very</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;felu</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>felo</i>, Old Saxon <i>filu</i>, Old High German <i>filu</i>, Old Norse <i>fjǫl-</i> in compounds, Gothic <i>𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌿</i> (filu). The Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek <i>πολύς</i> (polús), Latin <i>plus</i>, Old Irish <i>il</i> (Welsh <i>lliaws</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>many, multi-</li><li>much, very</li></ol><p>See <i>fela</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>filo-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>manifold</li></ol><p>From <i>fela</i> +&lrm; <i>-feald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) very active, having done much, full of exploits</li></ol><p>From <i>fela-</i> +&lrm; <i>hrōr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel</li><li>to perceive, sense</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fōlijan</i> (“to feel”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>talkative</li></ol><p><i>fela</i> (“many”) +&lrm; <i>-wyrde</i> (“worded”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>field</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;felþu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;felþuz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>feld</i>, Old Saxon <i>feld</i>, Old High German <i>feld</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a field-bee</li><li>locust</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>feld</i> +&lrm; <i>bēo</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The fieldfare (<i>Turdus pilaris</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tent</li></ol><p>From <i>feld</i> (“field”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodnymph</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>feld</i> +&lrm; <i>ielfen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a flatland; a plain</li></ol><p>From <i>feld</i> (“field”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fieldlike</li><li>rural</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) field</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>feld</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild mint</li></ol><p>From <i>feld</i> (“field”) +&lrm; <i>minte</i> (“mint”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parsnip</li><li>carrot</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>feld</i> +&lrm; <i>more</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>feldmore</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an ox kept in a pasture</li></ol><p>From <i>feld</i> (“field”) +&lrm; <i>oxa</i> (“ox”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a field mushroom; toadstool; fungus</li></ol><p>From <i>feld</i> +&lrm; <i>swamm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fieldwort, gentian</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>feld</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>feldielfen</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>feld</i> +&lrm; <i>ælfen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fealg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fylġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fell, skin</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fell</i>, whence also Old High German <i>vel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fiellan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of skin</li></ol><p>From <i>fell</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>purple</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>felt</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;felt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild marjoram</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>felt</i> +&lrm; <i>wurma</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mullein, feltwort</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>felt</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fǣmne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fenn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>window</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fenestr</i>, from Latin <i>fenestra</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) lord, prince, king</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>fōn</i> (“to seize”) +&lrm; <i>þengel</i> (“prince”), as if from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fangil</i>. Compare <i>strenġel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vange (a suburb in Basildon borough, Essex, England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>molar</li></ol><p>From <i>feng</i> (“grasp”) +&lrm; <i>tōþ</i> (“tooth”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phoenicia</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Phoenīcia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Φοινίκη</i> (Phoiníkē), from <i>Φοῖνιξ</i> (Phoînix, “Phoenician”), from Egyptian <i>fnḫw</i> (“Phoenicians”), <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./bc349d67.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="f" alt="glyph" height="30"/><br /><img src="./6c9cd1c6.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="n" alt="glyph" height="12"/><br /><img src="./4a1fe83d.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="x" alt="glyph" height="45"/></td>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./ba1f03ff.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="w" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./7d98c359.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="V12" alt="glyph" height="45"/><br /><img src="./5d9d30a7.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Z2" alt="glyph" height="27"/></td>
</tr></table>
</td></tr></table></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythical bird</i>) phoenix</li><li>date tree or palm</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>phoenīx</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>φοῖνιξ</i> (phoînix), from Egyptian <i>bnw</i> (boinu, “grey heron”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fenland, marshy land</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;faniland</i>, <i>&#42;fannjaland</i>, equivalent to <i>fen</i> (“marsh”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>fenland</i>, <i>fenlond</i>, <i>feneland</i>, <i>fenelond</i>, <i>fenneland</i>, <i>fennelond</i> (“pastureland”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fenlike, fenny</li><li>marshy, miry, muddy, dirty</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fanilīk</i> (“swampy, boggy”), equivalent to <i>fen</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>fenilīk</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fen, marsh</li><li>mud, filth, dirt</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fani</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fanją</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fenny</li><li>marshy, muddy, dirty</li></ol><p>From <i>fenn</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water mint</li></ol><p>From <i>fenn</i> (“fen”) +&lrm; <i>minte</i> (“mint”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>finule</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marsh frog</li><li>swamp turtle</li></ol><p>From <i>fen</i> +&lrm; <i>ȳċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hate; persecute</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fijēn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fijāną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fēoġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>money</li><li>livestock, cattle</li><li>property</li><li>the runic character ᚠ (/f/)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fehu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fehu</i>. Note that <i>feoh</i> must have transferred into the a-stems (and thus lost its <i>-u</i>) before loss of medial *h, or else it would be <i>*fēo</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Germanic cognates include Old Frisian <i>fia</i> (West Frisian <i>fee</i>), Old Saxon <i>fehu</i> (Low German <i>Veeh</i>), Old Dutch <i>fē</i> (Dutch <i>vee</i>), Old High German <i>fihu</i> (German <i>Vieh</i>), Old Norse <i>fé</i> (Swedish <i>fä</i>), Gothic <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿</i> (faihu). The Indo-European root is also the source of Sanskrit <i>पशु</i> (páśu, “cattle”), Latin <i>pecus</i>, Old Armenian <i>ասր</i> (asr, “fleece”) and Lithuanian <i>pēkus</i> (“cattle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pecuniary recompense</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>feoh</i> +&lrm; <i>bōt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasury, place of money</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>feoh</i> +&lrm; <i>-ern</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>greedy of money, avaricious, covetous</li></ol><p>From <i>feoh</i> (“money”) +&lrm; <i>ġīfre</i> (“eager”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) treasure-giving, dispensing of gifts</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) costly gift</li></ol><p><i>feoh</i> (“livestock, wealth, property”) +&lrm; <i>ġift</i> (“gift, giving”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasure house</li></ol><p>From <i>feoh</i> (“money”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land used for pasture</li></ol><p>From <i>feoh</i> (“livestock”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love of money</li></ol><p><i>feoh</i> +&lrm; <i>lufu</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fight, battle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fehtą</i>, from the verb <i>&#42;fehtaną</i> (“to struggle with”). Cognate with Old Dutch <i>fehta</i>, Old High German <i>gifeht</i>, Middle High German <i>gevechte</i>, Dutch <i>gevecht</i>, German <i>Gefecht</i>, Old English <i>gefeoht</i> and West Frisian <i>gefjocht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fight</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fight to obtain something, to obtain something by fighting</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fehtan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>fiuhta</i>, Old Saxon <i>fehtan</i>, Old Dutch <i>fehtan</i>, Old High German <i>fehtan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) fighter, warrior</li></ol><p>From <i>feohtan</i> (“to fight”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fighting</li></ol><p>From <i>feohtan</i> (“to fight”) +&lrm; <i>-lāc</i> (suffix denoting activity).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fighter</li></ol><p>From <i>feohtan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ling</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fēoll</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fela</i></li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fela</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fela</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Old English</i>) companion, partner, colleague</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Norse <i>félagi</i>, from <i>fé</i> (“property”) +&lrm; <i>lag</i> (“companionship”) +&lrm; <i>-i</i>, with remodelling of the first element after native <i>fēoh</i> (“property”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Feologild</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enter</li><li>to undergo</li><li>to reach; penetrate; come; pass</li><li>to cling; cleave; stick; adhere</li><li>to persist</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;felhaną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>fela</i> (West Frisian <i>befeljen</i>), Old Saxon <i>felhan</i> and <i>bifelhan</i> (Low German <i>befehlen</i>), Dutch <i>bevelen</i>, Old High German <i>felahan</i>, Old High German <i>felhan</i> (German <i>befehlen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fela</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fela</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fela</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;felu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;felu</i>. Originally the neuter singular form of older <i>&#42;feluz</i> (“many”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₁-</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Gothic <i>𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌿</i> (filu, “much, very”), Old Irish <i>oll</i> (“great, vast”), Latin <i>plus</i>, Ancient Greek <i>πολύς</i> (polús), German <i>viel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fēoġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enemy, foe</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fijand</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fijandz</i>, originally a present participle of <i>&#42;fijāną</i> (“to hate”). Corresponds to <i>fēoġan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idol</li><li>idolatry</li></ol><p><i>fēond</i> (“enemy”) +&lrm; <i>ġield</i> (“idol”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hostile</li><li>Satanic</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fijandlīk</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>fīantlīh</i>. Equivalent to <i>fēond</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enemy</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fēond</i> (“enemy”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enmity</li></ol><p>From <i>fēond</i> +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hostility, enmity</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fijandskapi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fijandskapiz</i>. Equivalent to <i>fēond</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fēondsċiepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>feorr</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>feorr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) foreigner, alien</li></ol><p>From <i>feor</i> (“far”) +&lrm; <i>būend</i> (“dweller”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fierd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>feorh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>life (one whose safety is questioned)</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) soul</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) living being, person</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ferh</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ferhwą</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>ferch</i>, Old Saxon <i>ferah</i>, Old High German <i>fërah</i>, Middle High German <i>vërch</i>, Old Norse <i>fjǫr</i>, Gothic <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍈𐌿𐍃</i> (fairƕus).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>terminal illness, fatal disease</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>feorh</i> (“life”) +&lrm; <i>ādl</i> (“disease”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>feorhbealu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mortal evil, deadly curse</li></ol><p><i>feorh</i> +&lrm; <i>bealu</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) body</li></ol><p>Kenning of <i>feorh</i> (“life, soul”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”). Compare <i>sāwelhūs</i> (“body”, literally “soul house”) and <i>bānhūs</i> (“body”, literally “bone house”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a flight (escape), something done to preserve one's life</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>feorh</i> (“life”) +&lrm; <i>lāst</i> (“footstep”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>condemned to death, guilty of a capital crime</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>feorh</i> +&lrm; <i>sċyldiġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) mortally wounded</li></ol><p><i>feorh</i> +&lrm; <i>sēoc</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) mortal protection, guard over life</li></ol><p><i>feorh</i> +&lrm; <i>weard</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food, sustenance, provision; meal, feast, banquet</li><li>entertainment</li><li>possessions, goods, supplies; stores</li><li>profit, benefit</li><li>rent, especially rent or tax paid in food or other provisions</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fermu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fermō</i> (“means of living, subsistence”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ferhwō</i> (“life force, body, being”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;perkʷ-</i> (“life, force, strength, tree”). See English <i>farm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farm</li></ol><p>From <i>feorme</i> (combining form of <i>feorm</i>, “subsistence”) +&lrm; <i>hām</i> (“home, village”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who supplies with food, purveyor, grocer, farmer</li></ol><p>From <i>feorm</i> (“provisions, supplies”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purveyance, assistance in acquiring food</li></ol><p>From <i>feorm</i> (“food”) +&lrm; <i>fultum</i> (“support, assistance”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cleanse, scour, furbish</li><li>to entertain, receive (as a guest); to feast</li><li>to harbour, support, sustain, foster, feed</li><li>to cherish, benefit, profit</li><li>to feed on, consume, devour</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;feorbian</i>, <i>&#42;furbian</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;furbēn</i> (“to polish, clean”). Not related to <i>Etymology 2</i>, though likely influenced by it. See also Old High German <i>furbjan</i>, Middle High German <i>vürben</i>, Old French <i>forbir</i>.</p><p>Alternatively, related to Old High German <i>āfermī</i> (“uncleanness, filth”).</p><p>Equivalent to <i>feorm</i> (“entertainment, feast, food”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an instance of harboring or entertaining (a guest)</li><li>a cleansing, polishing</li></ol><p>From <i>feormian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fiermþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farness, distance</li></ol><p>From <i>feor</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>feornes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>far; distant</li><li>far back (<i>of time</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>far; at a distance</li><li>perverse; depraved</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ferr</i> (adjective), derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ferrai</i> (adverb), an old comparative form.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>feorran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remove, withdraw</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from far away</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;firrijaną</i>. Compare Gothic <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (fairjan) (see there for further cognates). By surface analysis, <i>feorr</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i> (“verbal suffix”).</p><p>From <i>feorr</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i> (“adverbial suffix”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li><i>(of language)</i> in a roundabout or circumlocutory manner</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Having a distant origin, coming from afar.</li></ol><p>From <i>feorran</i> (“from far away”) +&lrm; <i>-cund</i> (“originating”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to depart</li><li>to go far away</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ferrēn</i>. Equivalent to <i>feorr</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exceed, go beyond</li><li>to remove, expel</li><li>to depart, separate</li></ol><p>From <i>feorr</i> +&lrm; <i>-sian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farting</li></ol><p><i>&#42;feortan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a way or path leading to a distant destination; a long way or journey</li></ol><p>From <i>feorr</i> (“far”) +&lrm; <i>weġ</i> (“way”). Compare German <i>Fernweg</i> (“a long distance”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>firwitġeornnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>fēorþa</i>: fourth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fourth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fourth</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>fēowerþa</i>, from the cardinal numeral <i>fēower</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;feurþō</i>. Compare Old Frisian <i>fiārda</i>, Old Saxon <i>fiortho</i>, Old High German <i>fiordo</i>, Old Norse <i>fjórði</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fourth, a quarter</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a quarter of something</li><li>farthing</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fedurþungaz</i> (“a quarter”), equivalent to <i>fēorþa</i> (“fourth”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>four</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;feuwar</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fedwōr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷetwóres</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-four</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-fourth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-four</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-fourth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-four</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-fourth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-four</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-fourth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventy-four</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-four</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-fourth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-four</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-fourth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-four</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-fourth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>four hundred</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fourfold</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>fiþerfeald</i>, with the original prefix replaced with <i>fēower</i> by analogy. Equivalent to <i>fēower</i> +&lrm; <i>-feald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quadrupedal, four-footed</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fēower</i> +&lrm; <i>-fēte</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>four-sided, rectangular, quadrangular, square</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fēowertīene</i> (“fourteen”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fourteenth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fourteenth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;feuwartehundō</i>, descended from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fedurþô tehundô</i> (literally “fourth tenth”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fourteen</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fedurtehun</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>fiūwertīne</i>, Old Saxon <i>fiuwartein</i>, Old High German <i>fiorzehan</i>, Old Norse <i>fjórtán</i>, Gothic <i>𐍆𐌹𐌳𐍅𐍉𐍂𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿𐌽</i> (fidwōrtaihun).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fourteen year(s) old</li></ol><p><i>fēowertīene</i> +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fedwōr tigiwiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fortyfold</li></ol><p>From <i>fēowertiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-feald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fortieth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fortieth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fēowertīene</i> (“fourteen”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>four year(s) old</li><li>lasting four years</li></ol><p><i>fēower</i> +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fēorþa</i>: fourth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fēower</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fiþer-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fær</i> (“way”)</li><li>alternative form of <i>fǣr</i> (“danger”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>unstressed form of Old English prefix <i>for-</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fer-</i>, unstressed form of <i>&#42;firi-</i> (“around, across”), <i>&#42;fra-</i> (“away from, off of, fully”), <i>&#42;furi-</i> (“before, forth, forward”). Akin to Old High German <i>ver-</i>, Gothic <i>faír-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>companion, associate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fōrijō</i>, from <i>&#42;fōrijan</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go, to travel or journey</li><li>to set out, to leave or depart</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fōrijaną</i> (whence German <i>führen</i>), originally the causative of <i>faran</i> (“to travel, go”), from <i>fōr</i> (“going, course, journey”) (compare <i>fōr</i>, 1st & 3rd singular preterite of <i>faran</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go, make one's way, proceed</li><li>to bring, carry, conduct</li><li>to help, assist, support</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;farikōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;farikōną</i>, <i>&#42;farukōną</i>, equivalent to <i>faru</i> +&lrm; <i>-cian</i> or <i>fær</i> +&lrm; <i>-cian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fierd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fierdwīte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>passable, able to go</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) able, fit for action or travel</li><li>(<i>of ships</i>) seaworthy</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fēriz</i> (“passable”)</p><p>See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailor</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ferian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ferienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>feorh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ferhþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>life</li><li>heart, spirit, mind</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ferhþuz</i>, equivalent to <i>feorh</i> +&lrm; <i>-þ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) soul-enclosure, bosom, body</li></ol><p>From <i>ferhþ</i> (“spirit”) +&lrm; <i>loca</i> (“locked enclosure”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take, move, carry</li><li>to direct the course of; conduct, lead</li><li>to go, leave</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;farjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;farjaną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>ferien</i>, Old Norse <i>ferja</i>, Gothic <i>𐍆𐌰𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (farjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ferian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ferienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>society, fellowship</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fēr</i> +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>verse</li><li>sentence, title</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>versus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fresh</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frisk</i>, whence also Old Frisian <i>fersk</i>, Old High German <i>frisc</i>, and outside of West Germanic, Old Norse <i>ferskr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make verse</li></ol><p>From <i>fers</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>firwitġeornnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>firwitġeornnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>firwitġeornnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>færweġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>firwitt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ferhþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ferhþloca</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ferhþ</i></li><li>misconstruction of <i>friþ</i> (<i>in historic given names; from the late 9th C.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ferhþloca</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ferhþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fȳsan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fȳsan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fōstorfæder</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fæstlīċe</i> (“firmly, quickly”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>girdle, belt</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fatil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fatilaz</i>, whence also Old High German <i>fezzil</i>, Old Norse <i>fetill</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fetter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;feturaz</i> (“fetter”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ped-</i> (“foot, step”). Cognate with Dutch <i>veter</i> (“lace”), Old High German <i>fezzera</i>, obsolete German <i>Fesser</i>, and Old Norse <i>fjǫturr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fetch, bring near</li><li>to obtain, acquire; to induce, bring on</li><li>to marry, fetch (a wife)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fatōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fatōną</i>, <i>&#42;fatjaną</i> (“to fetch”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ped-</i> (“foot”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>fatia</i> (“to fetch”), Old High German <i>fazzōn</i> (“to touch, grasp”) (German <i>fassen</i>), Old Norse <i>fata</i>, <i>feta</i> (“to go, step”) (Icelandic <i>feta</i>). More at foot.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>feter</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fēower</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fēower</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>feax</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>feþer</i> (“feather”).</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an infantry</li><li>troop, company, host of soldiers</li><li>battle</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the ability to walk or go on foot</li><li>motion, movement, walking</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an infantry</li></ol><p>From <i>fēþa</i> (“footsoldier”) +&lrm; <i>here</i> (“army”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unable to walk</li></ol><p><i>fēþe</i> (“ability to walk”) +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foot soldier</li><li>pedestrian</li></ol><p>From <i>fēþe</i> (“walking, ambulatory”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feather</li><li>pen (writing instrument)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;feþru</i>. See there for more.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>feathered, winged</li></ol><p>From <i>feþer</i> +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>feathered</li></ol><p><i>feþer</i> (“feather”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i> (adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fiþerfēte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fiþerfēte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fiþerrīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fig or fig tree</li><li>piles, hemorrhoids</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fīk</i>, <i>&#42;fīgā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) piles, hemorrhoids</li></ol><p>From <i>fīc</i> (“fig”) +&lrm; <i>ādl</i> (“disease”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) fig tree</li></ol><p>From <i>fīc</i> (“fig”) +&lrm; <i>bēam</i> (“tree”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deceive; bewile, mislead, con</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fikōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fikōną</i> (“to deceive”), possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peyǵ-</i> (“ill-meaning, evil-minded, treacherous, hostile, bad”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) fig leaf</li></ol><p>From <i>fīc</i> (“fig”) +&lrm; <i>lēaf</i> (“leaf”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deceitful</li><li>crafty</li><li>false</li></ol><p>From <i>fician</i> (“to deceive, trick”) +&lrm; <i>-ol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fig tree</li></ol><p>From <i>fīc</i> (“fig”) +&lrm; <i>trēow</i> (“tree”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intestinal worm</li></ol><p>From <i>fīc</i> (“hemorrhoids”) +&lrm; <i>wyrm</i> (“worm”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) figwort</li></ol><p>From <i>fīc</i> (“fig”) +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i> (“plant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>fruit</i>) fig</li></ol><p>From <i>fīc</i> (“fig”) +&lrm; <i>æppel</i> (“fruit, apple”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fæder</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fold, crease (of clothing), plait, wrinkle</li><li>a revolution, turn, roll</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) turn, course (of time)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;faldi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faldiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fieldstōl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>folding chair</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;faldistōl</i>. Equivalent to <i>field</i> (“fold”) +&lrm; <i>stōl</i> (“chair”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fall</li><li>ruin, destruction; death, slaughter</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) case</li></ol><p>From a merger of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;falli</i> and <i>&#42;fall</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;falliz</i> and <i>&#42;fallaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fell</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fallijan</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>vellen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>epileptic</li></ol><p><i>fiell</i> (“fall”) +&lrm; <i>sēoc</i> (“sick”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epilepsy</li></ol><p>From <i>fiell</i> +&lrm; <i>sēocnes</i> or <i>fiellesēoc</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>, literally “falling sickness”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fēond</i></li></ol><p>Inflected form.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fer-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>army, military</li><li>military service</li><li>militia</li><li>military expedition, campaign</li><li>camp</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fardi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>military expedition or service</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fierd</i> +&lrm; <i>færeld</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soldier</li></ol><p>From <i>fierd</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fine for evading military service</li></ol><p><i>fierd</i> +&lrm; <i>wīte</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mountain</li><li>mountain forest</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fergunī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fergunją</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>fjǫrgyn</i> (“earth, land”), Gothic <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌲𐌿𐌽𐌹</i> (fairguni, “mountain”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chamois (a goat antelope (<i>Rupicapra rupicapra</i>))</li></ol><p><i>fiergen</i> (“mountain”) +&lrm; <i>gāt</i> (“goat”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) mountain steam</li></ol><p>From <i>fiergen</i> (“mountain”) +&lrm; <i>strēam</i> (“stream”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a reception to food or feast</li><li>(<i>of a guest</i>) harbouring, entertainment</li><li>a washing, cleansing</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;firmiþu</i>, equivalent to <i>feorm</i> +&lrm; <i>-þ</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;firmiþu</i>, equivalent to <i>feormian</i> (“to cleanse”) +&lrm; <i>-þ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>further</li></ol><p>Comparative of <i>feorr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>feorr</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>feorr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heel, calx</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fersnō</i> (“heel”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time (especially time granted for doing something)</li><li>threshold, ceiling, inner roof (declined as fem.)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fristaz</i>, <i>&#42;fristą</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>verst</i>, Old High German <i>frist</i> (German <i>Frist</i>), Old Norse <i>frest</i> (Swedish <i>frist</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>five</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fimf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-five</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-fifth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-five</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-fifth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-five</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-fifth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-five</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-fifth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventy-five</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-five</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-fifth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-five</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-fifth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-five</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-fifth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>five hundred</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butterfly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fifaldǭ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fīfalde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) monster, giant</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) sea-monster</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fimbil</i>, <i>&#42;fimbul</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fimbulaz</i> and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fimfilaz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>fimbul-</i> (“mighty, great”) and Old Norse <i>fífl</i> (“fool, idiot, buffoon”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) monsterkind; the race of giants or monsters</li></ol><p>From <i>fīfel</i> +&lrm; <i>cynn</i> (“kin”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet of the river Eider, the boundary between Holstein and Schleswig</li></ol><p>From <i>fīfel</i> +&lrm; <i>dōr</i>, literally “monster or terror-door”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fivefold</li></ol><p>From <i>fīf</i> +&lrm; <i>-feald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fīfalde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) cinquefoil</li></ol><p>Calque of Latin <i>quīnquefolium</i>, equivalent to <i>fīf</i> (“five”) +&lrm; <i>lēaf</i> (“leaf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fifth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fimftô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fīftīene</i> (“fifteen”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifteenth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fifteenth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fimftô tehundô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifteen</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fimftehun</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>fīftīne</i>, Old Saxon <i>fīftein</i>, Old High German <i>fimfzehan</i>, Old Norse <i>fimmtán</i>, Gothic <i>𐍆𐌹𐌼𐍆𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿𐌽</i> (fimftaihun).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fifteen year(s) old</li></ol><p><i>fīftīene</i> +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty</li></ol><p>From <i>fīf</i> +&lrm; <i>-tiġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fiftyfold</li></ol><p>From <i>fīftiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-feald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fiftieth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fiftieth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fīftīene</i> (“fifteen”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>five year(s) old</li><li>lasting five years</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fimfwintruz</i>. Equivalent to <i>fīf</i> +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fēoġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Philistines (genitive)</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>Philistinus</i>, from Koine Greek <i>Φυλιστῖνοι</i> (Phulistînoi), a variant of <i>Φυλιστιίμ</i> (Phulistiím), <i>Φυλιστιείμ</i> (Phulistieím) (compare Koine Greek <i>Παλαιστῖνοι</i> (Palaistînoi)), from Hebrew <i>פְּלִשְׁתִּים</i> (p'lishtím, plural noun), from <i>פְּלִשְׁתִּי</i> (p'lishtí, “Philistine”, adjective), from <i>פְּלֶשֶׁת</i> (p'léshet, “Philistia”). Further etymology is uncertain.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>membrane</li><li>caul</li><li>foreskin</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;filmīn-</i> (“skin, membrane”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>filmene</i> (“skin”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>finn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodpecker</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a finch</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;finkiz</i>, <i>&#42;finkô</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>vinke</i> (whence Dutch <i>vink</i>), Old High German <i>finko</i>, <i>finco</i> (whence German <i>Fink</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fȳnd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find</li><li>to meet</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;finþan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;finþaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pent-</i> (“to go, pass; path, bridge”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>capable</li><li>considerable, good, heavy</li></ol><p>From <i>findan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ig</i>; the former element is a verb from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;finþaną</i> (compare Dutch <i>vinden</i>, German <i>finden</i>, Swedish <i>finna</i>), a secondary verb from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pontHo-</i> (compare Old Irish <i>étain</i> (“I find”), <i>áitt</i> (“place”), Latin <i>pōns</i> (“bridge”), Ancient Greek <i>πόντος</i> (póntos, “sea”), Old Armenian <i>հուն</i> (hun, “ford”), Avestan <i>𐬞𐬀𐬚𐬀</i> (paθa) (gen. <i>paþō</i>), Sanskrit <i>पथ</i> (patha, “path”)). Compare to Swedish <i>fyndig</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>finger</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fingr</i>. Compare Old Frisian <i>finger</i>, Old Saxon <i>fingar</i>, Old High German <i>fingar</i>, Old Norse <i>fingr</i>, Gothic <i>𐍆𐌹𐌲𐌲𐍂𐍃</i> (figgrs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>finger muscles</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) foxglove (<i>Digitalis purpurea</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>finger</i> + <i>&#42;docce</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;dokkā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dukkǭ</i> (“strength, power”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>date (fruit)</li></ol><p>From <i>finger</i> +&lrm; <i>æppel</i>, a calque of Latin <i>dactylus</i> or calque of Ancient Greek <i>δάκτυλος</i> (dáktulos).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fin</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;finnō</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Finn)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sami</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) Lapland</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;finnaz</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>Finnr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lapland</li></ol><p>Literally "Land of the Sami."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Sami</li><li>Lapland</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;finnōz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>Finnar</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>finule</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tail</li><li>sequel</li><li>consequence; result</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fintô</i> (“tail, train”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)pend-</i> (“to pull, stretch”). Cognate with Latin <i>pendeō</i> (“to hang”). Related to spin.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>finule</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>finule</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fennel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fenukl</i>, from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;fēnuclum</i>, from Latin <i>fēnuculum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) fennel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fenukl</i>, from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;fēnuclum</i>, from Latin <i>fēnuculum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fēoġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>feoh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>feohbōt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fēond</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) sons of men, mankind</li></ol><p>From genitive plural of <i>fíras</i> + plural of <i>bearn</i>. Has a parallel in Old Saxon <i>firihō barn</i>, possibly stemming from a Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;firhwijō barnu</i>, with a possible analog in Old Norse <i>fíra synir</i>.</p></html>:/ˈfiːrɑs/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) men, humans</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;firhwijaz</i> (“living being, person”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ferhuz</i> (“life, body”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>fīrihos</i>, Old Norse <i>fírar</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fierd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crime</li><li>sin</li><li>torment, suffering</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;firinu</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>firne</i>, Old Saxon <i>firina</i>, Old High German <i>firina</i>, Gothic <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌹𐌽𐌰</i> (fairina).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>firendǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wicked deed, a crime</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;firinōdādi</i>. Equivalent to <i>firen</i> +&lrm; <i>dǣd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sinful</li></ol><p>From <i>firen</i> +&lrm; <i>-ful</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sinful</li></ol><p>From <i>firen</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeorn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a man with sinful thoughts, an adulterer</li></ol><p>From <i>firen</i> +&lrm; <i>hyċġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adulteress, harlot</li></ol><p>From <i>firenhyċġa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sin</li><li>to revile</li><li>to commit adultery</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;firinōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;firinōną</i>. Equivalent to <i>firen</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great sin</li></ol><p>From <i>firen</i> +&lrm; <i>leahtor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sinful, wicked</li></ol><p>From <i>firen</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>vehemently, rashy</li></ol><p>From <i>firenlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fornicate</li></ol><p>From <i>firen</i> +&lrm; <i>liġerian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sinful lust, luxury, extravagance</li></ol><p>From <i>firen</i> +&lrm; <i>lust</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gross sin</li></ol><p>From <i>firen</i> +&lrm; <i>synn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sinful</li></ol><p>From <i>firensynn</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mark of sin</li></ol><p>From <i>firen</i> +&lrm; <i>tācen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pollute with sin</li></ol><p>From <i>firen</i> +&lrm; <i>tācnian</i>, else <i>firentācen</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>excessively</li><li>malignantly</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>firen</i> (“crime”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) dire hardship</li></ol><p><i>firen</i> (“crime, sin, torment”) +&lrm; <i>þearf</i> (“need, hardship”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fiergen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fiergengāt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ferhþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fiermþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>firenian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fierst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>firwitġeornnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>curious</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;firiwittjagern</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>firiwizgern</i>. Equivalent to <i>firwitt</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeorn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curiosity</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>firwitġeorn</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curiosity</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>curious</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;firiwitją</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>firiwit</i>, Old High German <i>firiwizzi</i> (modern German <i>Vorwitz</i>), compare also Old Norse <i>forvitni</i> (“curiosity”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fish</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind or species of fish</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fiskakunją</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>fisckunni</i>, Old Norse <i>fiskakyn</i>. Equivalent to <i>fisċ</i> +&lrm; <i>cynn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fisherman</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fiskārijaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>fisker</i>, Old Saxon <i>fiskeri</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;fiskar</i>, Old High German <i>fiskāri</i>, Old Norse <i>fiskari</i>. Equivalent to <i>fisċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fishhouse</li></ol><p>From <i>fisċ</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fish</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fiskōną</i>. Equivalent to <i>fisċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fishpond</li></ol><p>From <i>fisċ</i> +&lrm; <i>lacu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fisċnett</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fishnet, fishing net</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fiskanatją</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>fisknet</i>, Old High German <i>fisknezzi</i>, Old Norse <i>fiskinet</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the act of fishing</li><li>fishing ground</li><li>catch of fish</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fiscian</i> (“to fish”) +&lrm; <i>-noþ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fishing</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fiskōþu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fiskōþuz</i>. Equivalent to <i>fiscian</i> +&lrm; <i>-oþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A pool or pond containing fish; a fishpond</li></ol><p>From <i>fisċ</i> (“fish”) +&lrm; <i>pōl</i> (“pool”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fishweir</li><li>fishing ground</li></ol><p>From <i>fisċ</i> (“fish”) +&lrm; <i>wēr</i> (“weir”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abounding in fish</li></ol><p>From <i>fisċ</i> (“fisch”) +&lrm; <i>weallan</i> (“to swarm”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poem, song</li><li>conflict</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fittju</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fitjō</i> (“foot, seam”) and <i>&#42;fitī</i> (“section, segment, chapter”), both from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pedio-</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>&#42;fittea</i>, attested in the borrowed Latin <i>vittea</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fyxen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fiscian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fiscnoþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fiddler, especially a male one</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;fiþele</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female fiddler</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;fiþele</i> +&lrm; <i>-estre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>four, quadri-, tetra-</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;feþur-</i> (“four”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷetwóres</i> (“four”). Akin to Old English <i>fēower</i> (“four”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bearing feathers; feathered</li><li>having wings; winged</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fiþrijabēriz</i>. Equivalent to <i>fiþre</i> +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fiþre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fourfold</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;feþurfalþaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>fiþer-</i> +&lrm; <i>-feald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>four-footed, quadrupedal</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;feþurfōtī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;feþurfōtijaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>fiþer-</i> +&lrm; <i>-fēte</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fiþerfēte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fiþrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wingless</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fiþrijalausaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>fiþre</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tetrarchy</li></ol><p>From <i>fiþer-</i> +&lrm; <i>rīċe</i> (“authority, dominion”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>four-sided, rectangular, quadrangular, square</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wing</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fiþrī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fiþriją</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give wings to</li><li>to flutter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fiþrijōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>flān</i></li></ol><p>Alteration of <i>flān</i>, reinterpreted as an n-stem.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a plaster, poultice, cataplasm</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flaguk</i> (“flat piece”), diminutive of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flagą</i> (“something flat, flat or level surface”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleḱ-</i> (“fold, pleat”) or Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₂-</i> (“flat, broad; plain”). Compare Old Norse <i>flag</i> (“flat piece of land, plain, area, ground”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of arrows</i>) flying</li><li>flickering, fluttering</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flakur</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flakuraz</i> (“shaking, wavering”), related to <i>&#42;flakurōną</i> (“to roam, wander”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;plag-</i> (“to roam, wander”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pelə-</i> (“to wander”).</p><p>Akin to Old High German <i>flagarōn</i> (“to flicker, flutter”), Old Norse <i>flakka</i> (“to wander”), Old Norse <i>flǫkra</i> (“to wander”), Old English <i>flicorian</i> (“to flutter”), Latin <i>palari</i> (“to wander”), Ancient Greek <i>πλανᾱ́σθαι</i> (planā́sthai, “to wander”), Old Norse <i>flana</i> (“to wander”). More at flaunt.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>flaċġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wily, insidious, artful, deceitful, fraudulent</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flaih</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flaihaz</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;þlaihaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flainaz</i> (“hook, spear with a tip”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleyn-</i> (“metal arrow, hook, spear-head”). Akin to Old Norse <i>fleinn</i> (“hook, barbed weapon, javelin, arrow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>flānhræd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arrow-fast: as fast as an arrow</li></ol><p><i>flān</i> +&lrm; <i>hræd</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dart, arrow</li></ol><p>From <i>flān</i> +&lrm; <i>-iht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thrust of an arrow</li></ol><p>From <i>flān</i> +&lrm; <i>þracu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flask, bottle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flaskā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flaskǭ</i> (“flask, bottle”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleḱ-</i> (“to weave”).</p><p>Akin to Old High German <i>flaska, flasca</i> (“flask, bottle”) (German <i>Flasche</i> (“bottle”)), Old Norse <i>flaska</i> (“flask, bottle”) (Icelandic <i>flaska</i>), Old English <i>fleohtan</i> (“to weave, plait”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>flasċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>web-footed</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Likely from <i>&#42;flax</i> (“a weave, web”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flahts</i>, derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flehtaną</i> (“to weave, plait”) +&lrm; <i>-fōte</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>flēah</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flea</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flauhaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;plówkos</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;plúsis</i> (“flea”). Cognate with Old High German <i>flōh</i>, Old Norse <i>fló</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>escape, flight</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flaumaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flay, pull the skin off, skin</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flahan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fraud, deceit, trickery, deception</li><li>folly, nonsense</li><li>vanity</li><li>superstition</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps tied to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flaihaz</i> (“sly, underhanded, sneaky”). Compare Old Norse <i>flærð</i> ("fraud, deceit"&#59; whence Icelandic <i>flærð</i> (“deceit”), Swedish <i>flärd</i> (“vanity, frivolity, flamboyance”)), Old English <i>flāh</i> (“insidious, artful, deceitful, fraudulent”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who acts wantonly or with (wicked) folly</li></ol><p>From <i>fleard</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trifle</li><li>to err</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>fleard</i> (“fraud, folly”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>linen, flax</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flahs</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flahsą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flaxen; of flax</li></ol><p>From <i>fleax</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>flet</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flooded</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>flōd</i> (“flood”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a flooding, overflow, inundation</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;flēdan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>flēogan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>flēoge</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>flīema</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>flīeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a white spot in the eye</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fleg-</i>, related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flekka-</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>flī</i> (“white spot”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly</li><li>(figurative,&#32;by extension) to move quickly</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fleugan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fleuganą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;plewk-</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>fliāga</i> (West Frisian <i>fleane</i>), Old Saxon <i>fliogan</i> (Low German <i>flegen</i>), Old Dutch <i>fliogan</i> (Dutch <i>vliegen</i>), Old High German <i>fliogan</i> (German <i>fliegen</i>), Old Norse <i>fljúga</i> (Swedish <i>flyga</i>). The Proto-Indo-European root is also the source of Lithuanian <i>plaũkti</i> (“swim”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fly</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fleugā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fleugǭ</i>, whence also Old High German <i>flioga</i>. Akin to Old Norse <i>fluga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bird, (<i>lit.</i>) a flyer</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>flēogan</i> (“to fly”), equivalent to <i>flēogan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ende</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weave; plait</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flehtan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flehtaną</i>, from Pre-Germanic <i>&#42;pleḱt-</i>, an extension of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleḱ-</i> (“to twine”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wicker grate</li><li>a hurdle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flehtrō</i>, <i>&#42;flehtrā</i> (“woof, woven work or structure”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flehtaną</i> (“to weave, plait”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flee</li><li>to run away, run from, escape</li><li>to avoid</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fleuhan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fleuhaną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>fliā</i>, Old Saxon <i>fliohan</i>, Old Dutch <i>flian</i>, Old High German <i>fliohan</i>, Old Norse <i>flýja</i>, Gothic <i>𐌸𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (þliuhan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fleece</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fleus</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fleusaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place where ships float&#58; a bay, arm of the sea, estuary, or river's mouth&#59; a river or stream</li><li>that which floats, the float</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>watercraft: a raft, ship, punt or vessel</li><li>cream (the unrefined milk fat skimmed from or floating on the surface of milk)</li></ol></ol><p>See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fleut</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fleutą</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>flet</i>&nbsp;n (“river”), Dutch <i>vliet</i>&nbsp;m (“rivulet, brook”), German <i>Fließ</i>&nbsp;m or n (“stream”), Middle High German <i>vliez</i>&nbsp;m or n (“rivulet”), Old High German <i>flioz</i>&nbsp;m (“river”), and Icelandic <i>fljót</i>&nbsp;n (“river”).</p><p>Nominalization of <i>flēotan</i> (“to float”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to float</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fleutaną</i>, akin to German <i>fließen</i>, Old Saxon <i>fliotan</i>, Dutch <i>vlieten</i> and Old Norse <i>fljóta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flooring</li><li>storey of a building</li><li>a stratum, layer</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;flēran</i> (“to floor”) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mutter, whisper</li></ol><p>Unknown. Perhaps a metathetic alteration of <i>&#42;flēwsian</i> (“to flow, flux, fluctuate”), suggested by Old English <i>flēwsa</i> (“flow, flux”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the floor, ground</li><li>a dwelling, habitation, house, cottage, hall</li><li>alternative form of <i>flīete</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flatją</i> (“floor”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flataz</i> (“flat”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;plat-</i> (“flat”). Akin to Old Frisian <i>flet, flette</i> (“dwelling, house”).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flautiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fighting within a house</li></ol><p>From <i>flet</i> +&lrm; <i>ġefeoht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the floor, ground</li><li>a dwelling, habitation, house, cottage, hall</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flati</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flatją</i> (“floor”), from <i>&#42;flataz</i> (“flat”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleth₂-</i> (“flat”). Akin to Old Frisian <i>flet, flette</i> (“dwelling, house”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flow, flux, issue</li><li>bodily issue, oozing; menstrual flow</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>&#42;flēwsian</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flōisōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flōaną</i> (“to flow”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;plew-</i> (“to pour, wash”). Compare also Old English <i>fleswian</i> (“to whisper, mutter”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fleax</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>side of bacon; flitch</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flikkī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flikkiją</i> (“side of bacon”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fliċċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flutter</li><li>to hover</li></ol><p>Related to <i>flicorian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fliċċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move ones wings, flap, flutter</li><li>to flicker</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Probably related to Old English <i>flēogan</i> (“to fly”) or perhaps to Old English <i>flacor</i> (“flying, flickering, fluttering”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>flēoge</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>someone who flees: fugitive, refugee, runaway</li></ol><p>From <i>flīeman</i> (“to flee, escape”) +&lrm; <i>-a</i>; compare <i>flēam</i> (“flight, flow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put to flight, banish</li></ol><p>From <i>flēam</i> (“flight”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>flēos</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cream, skimming, curds</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flautijǭ</i>. Cognate with Danish <i>fløde</i> (“cream”), Icelandic <i>fleytið</i> (“skimming”), Norwegian <i>fløte</i> (“cream”). Ultimately cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flutōną</i> (“to float”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;plewd-</i>, <i>&#42;plew-</i> (“to float, swim, fly”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flail, whip</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;fleġel</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flagil</i>, of disputed origin. Probably from Latin <i>flagellum</i>, or alternatively possibly inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flagilaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>flīeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>stone</i>) flint</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flint</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flintaz</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flint gray, the color of flint</li></ol><p>From <i>flint</i> (“flint”) +&lrm; <i>grǣġ</i> (“gray”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>argument, fight</li><li>arguing, fighting</li><li>contest, competition</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flit</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flītaz</i>, for which compare <i>&#42;flītan</i> (“to strive”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to argue, fight (in the sense "quarrel")</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to strive or contend (against something)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flītan</i> (“to strive, contend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>logic</li></ol><p>Since <i>-cræft</i> seems to be appended to nouns in all other cases, probably from <i>flit</i> +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i>. However, derivation from the verb <i>flītan</i> is possible.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>logical</li></ol><p><i>flitcræft</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flatfish, flounder</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flōk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flōką</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₂-</i> (“flat, broad”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat or strike together; to clap</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flōkan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flōkaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flock, herd</li><li>group, division, troop</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flokk</i> (“crowd, troop, flock”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flukkaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>flōcan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>flocmǣlum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in flocks, (<i>figuratively</i>) in droves</li></ol><p>From <i>flocc</i> +&lrm; <i>-mǣlum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flowing of the tide</li><li>river, stream; water as opposed to land</li><li>flood, deluge</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flōdu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flōduz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₃(w)-</i> (“flow”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>flōd</i>, Old Dutch <i>fluot</i> (Dutch <i>vloed</i>), Old High German <i>fluot</i> (German <i>Flut</i>), Old Norse <i>flóð</i> (Icelandic <i>flóð</i>), Gothic <i>𐍆𐌻𐍉𐌳𐌿𐍃</i> (flōdus).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) sea path</li></ol><p>From <i>flōd</i> (“water”) +&lrm; <i>weġ</i> (“way”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a flood-wave, tidal wave</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>flōd</i> +&lrm; <i>ȳþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flyer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flugô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is flown off; flake</li><li>fragment, piece</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flōhi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flōhiz</i> (“layer, film, coating”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₂-</i> (“flat, broad”). Cognate with Old High German <i>fluoh</i>, Old Norse <i>fló</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>web-footed</li></ol><p>From <i>flohten</i> (past participle of <i>fleohtan</i> (“to weave, web”)) +&lrm; <i>fōte</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a building</i>) pavement, floor</li><li>(<i>of a ship</i>) floor, deck; gangplank</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flōr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flōruz</i>, u-stem variant of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flōraz</i> (“ground, floor”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>floor stone, pavement</li></ol><p>From <i>flōr</i> +&lrm; <i>stān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the sea, water deep enough for a ship to sail upon it</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailor, shipman</li><li>pirate</li><li>boat, ship</li><li>fleet (of vessels)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flotō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flutô</i> (“a float; raft; boat; ship”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;plewd-</i>, <i>&#42;plew-</i> (“to fly, flow, run”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>floti</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to float</li><li>to fly, flutter</li></ol><p>From <i>flōtian</i> (“to float”) +&lrm; <i>-erian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to float</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flutōną</i> (“to float”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>flota</i> (“to float”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shipman, sailor</li><li>pirate</li></ol><p>From <i>flota</i> +&lrm; <i>mann</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>floterian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a floating ship</li><li>a light bark</li></ol><p><i>flot</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>sċip</i> (“ship”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flōan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flōaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;plew-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>flaxfōte</i></li></ol><p>Uncertain. Likely from <i>&#42;flox</i> (“a weave, web”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fluhts</i>, derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flehtaną</i> (“to weave, plait”) +&lrm; <i>-fōte</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tending to fly or flee</li><li>swift, fleeting</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flugul</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flugulaz</i>; equivalent to <i>flēogan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ol</i> or <i>flēon</i> +&lrm; <i>-ol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weave, plait, braid</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fluhts</i>, related to Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fluhtī</i> (“a woven fabric, weave”) +&lrm; <i>-rian</i>. Compare Old English <i>&#42;flox</i> in <i>floxfōte</i> (“web-footed”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>flicorian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(a) flight, flying</li></ol><p>From <i>flēogan</i> (“to fly”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flight (the act of flying)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fluhti</i>, akin to Old Saxon <i>fluht</i> (Middle Low German <i>vlucht</i>), Dutch <i>vluchten</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a woven textile; rag</li><li>patch</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fluhtī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fluhtiją</i> (“woven cloth, weave”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flehtaną</i> (“to weave, plait”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>flīema</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>flīeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>flēos</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>flīete</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fleam, lancet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Vulgar Latin <i>fletoma</i>, <i>&#42;fletomus</i>, from Late Latin <i>flebotomus</i>, <i>phlebotomus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>φλεβοτόμον</i> (phlebotómon)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flesh</li><li>meat</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flaiski</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incarnation</li></ol><p>From <i>flǣsċ</i> (“flesh”) +&lrm; <i>ġebyrd</i> (“birth”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>material body (in contrast with soul)</li><li>corporeal state of man</li></ol><p>From <i>flǣsċ</i> (“flesh”) +&lrm; <i>hama</i> (“skin”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>flǣsċhama</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fleshhouse (glosses Latin <i>carnālis</i> (“carnāle”)); meat shop</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flaiskihūs</i> (“slaughterhouse, shambles”), equivalent to <i>flǣsċ</i> (“flesh”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fleshly, carnal</li></ol><p>From <i>flǣsċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>physically, carnally (as opposed to spiritually)</li></ol><p><i>flǣsċ</i> (“flesh”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butcher</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>flǣsċ</i> +&lrm; <i>mangere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>countable</i>) a type or piece of meat</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) meat</li></ol><p>From <i>flǣsċ</i> +&lrm; <i>mete</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fleshworm or maggot</li></ol><p>From <i>flǣsċ</i> (“flesh”) +&lrm; <i>wyrm</i> (“worm”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Northumbrian,&#32;hapax legomenon) A small boat.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Norse <i>fley</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flawją</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;plew-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sneeze</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fneusan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fneusaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gasp; pant</li><li>to sneeze</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fnesan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fnesaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sneeze</li><li>sneezing</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fnoʀō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fnuzô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pnew-</i> (“to breathe; snort; sneeze”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fringe, hem or border of a garment</li></ol><p>Compare <i>fnæs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snort</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fnāʀijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fnesaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to neigh</li><li>to snort</li></ol><p>From <i>fnǣran</i> +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>. Compare Old High German <i>fneskezzen</i> (“to snort”) and <i>fnāstōn</i> (“to gasp, snort”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fringe</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fnas</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fnasą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breath, blowing</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) sneeze</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fnāst</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fnēstuz</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fnesaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to breathe heavy; pant</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fnāstōn</i>, equivalent to <i>fnǣst</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>fnāstōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fōdô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂-</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>fœða</i> (Danish <i>føde</i>, Swedish <i>föda</i>, Icelandic <i>fæða</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fodder</li><li>sheath</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fōdrą</i>, whence also Old Frisian <i>fōder</i>, Old High German <i>fuotar</i>, Old Norse <i>fóðr</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂-</i> (“guard, graze, feed”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fōdr</i>. Compare Old High German <i>fuotar</i>, Old Norse <i>fóðr</i>, Gothic <i>𐍆𐍉𐌳𐍂</i> (fōdr).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grazing</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a joining, joint</li></ol><p>Compare <i>fēġan</i> (“to join”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foal, colt</li><li>elephant calf</li><li>camel calf</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;folō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the people, especially the common people</li><li>a people, nation, or tribe</li><li>crowd</li><li>the public</li><li>(<i>in the singular or plural</i>) people (multiple individuals)</li><li>military, army; troop</li><li>(<i>in compounds</i>) popular</li><li>(<i>in compounds</i>) public, common</li><li>(<i>in compounds</i>) country, rural</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fulką</i> (“people”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Folkaberht</i>, from <i>&#42;folk</i> (“army; people”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”). Equivalent to <i>folc</i> +&lrm; <i>berht</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Fulcbert</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a battle of peoples</li><li>a great battle; pitched battle</li></ol><p>From <i>folc</i> +&lrm; <i>ġefeoht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a popular assembly; a folkmoot</li></ol><p>From <i>folc</i> (“people”) +&lrm; <i>ġemōt</i> (“meeting”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>popular (relating to the common people), mainstream</li><li>secular (<i>of clergy</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common speech: colloquial or vernacular language</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>folc</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>law of the people; public law; folk law</li></ol><p>From <i>folc</i> (“people”) +&lrm; <i>lagu</i> (“law”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land of the folk or people; land of the community</li></ol><p>From <i>folc</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>popular instruction</li><li>a sermon or homily</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>folc</i> +&lrm; <i>lār</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>popular (relating to the common people)</li><li>populous</li></ol><p>From <i>folc</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>folklīh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>folcġemōt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Folkmārī</i>. Equivalent to <i>folc</i> (“folk, people”) +&lrm; <i>mǣre</i> (“famous, great”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>law of a nation or of a people</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>folc</i> +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) public place, meeting-place</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) battle-place, battle-field</li></ol><p>From <i>folc</i> +&lrm; <i>stede</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A place in the country, a rural place.</li></ol><p><i>folc</i> (“country, rural”) +&lrm; <i>-stōw</i> (“place”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a "folk-hall;" a public building</li></ol><p>From <i>folc</i> (“people”) +&lrm; <i>sæl</i> (“hall”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Folkawald</i>, from <i>&#42;folk</i> (“army; people”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wald</i> (“might, authority”). Equivalent to <i>folc</i> +&lrm; <i>weald</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Folcold</i>, Latin <i>Folcoaldus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Folkawini</i>. Equivalent to <i>folc</i> (“folk, people”) +&lrm; <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house, castle</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who lives on the land; an earth-dweller, a man</li></ol><p><i>folde</i> (“earth, land”) +&lrm; <i>būend</i> (“inhabitant, dweller”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) land, earth, ground</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fuldǭ</i>, <i>&#42;fuldō</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>folda</i>, Old Norse <i>fold</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>earth-colored, earth-gray</li></ol><p>From <i>folde</i> (“earth, ground”) +&lrm; <i>grǣġ</i> (“gray”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>folgoþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>folgoþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>follower, attendant, disciple</li></ol><p>From <i>folgian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to follow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;folgēn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fulgāną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>retinue, train</li><li>service</li></ol><p>From <i>folgian</i> (“to follow”) +&lrm; <i>-oþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) (<i>poetic</i>) hand, palm</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fulmō</i>. Doublet of <i>palm(a)</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch, capture; seize</li><li><i>(with tō)</i> to take what is given, receive or accept what is offered</li><li><i>(with tō)</i> to conquer, take over</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>*fōhan</i> [ˈfoː.xɑn], from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fą̄han</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>fā</i>, Old Saxon <i>fahan</i>, Old Dutch <i>fān</i>, Old High German <i>fāhan</i>, Old Norse <i>fá</i>, Gothic <i>𐍆𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (fahan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fana</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fant</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>because of; out of (due to a given cause or motivation) <i>(+ dative)</i></li><li>for (duration of time)</li><li>for (for the sake of) <i>(+ dative)</i></li><li>for (on behalf of, instead of) <i>(+ accusative)</i></li><li>ago <i>(+ dative)</i></li><li>(Usually in the phrase <i>for eall</i>) for all, despite, in spite of <i>(+ dative)</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>journey, going, course, expedition, approach; passage, lifestyle, way of life</li><li>hog, pig</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;furi</i>.</p><p>see <i>faran</i></p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fōrō</i> (“trip; wagon”).</p><p>Variant of <i>fearh</i>. From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;farh</i> (“pig”). Cognate with Middle Low German <i>vōr</i> (“lean young pig”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>only</li><li>specially</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>for hwon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>why</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forþon</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>for þon þe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>because</li><li>for, on the account of</li><li>since</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>wrongly, away from, astray, abstention, prohibition, perversion, destruction (<i>verbal prefix</i>)</li><li>used to create intensified adjectives and verbs from other adjectives and verbs, with the sense of completely or fully&#59; compare Modern English use of <i>up</i></li><li>very</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fra-</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>for-</i>, Old Saxon <i>far-</i>, <i>for-</i>, Dutch <i>ver-</i>, Old High German <i>fir-</i>, <i>far-</i> (German <i>ver-</i>), and, outside Germanic, with Ancient Greek <i>περί</i> (perí), Latin <i>per-</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>пре-</i> (pre-) (Russian <i>пе́ре-</i> (pére-)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>before</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furanē</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>front, forward part</li><li>early</li></ol><p>From <i>foran</i> (“before, opposite”), from oblique case of <i>fora, fore</i> (“forepart, front”). Akin to Old English <i>fora, fore</i> (“before”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>fōrehēafod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the early part of the night</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>night</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>foresċēawung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forbid, prohibit, ban <i>(+accusative a thing)</i> <i>(+ dative to someone)</i></li><li>to restrain or stop, prevent the action of</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furibeudaną</i>, equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>bēodan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>firbiotan</i> (German <i>verbieten</i>), Gothic <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌱𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (faurbiudan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abstain, refrain, restrain, forbear; bear away from; keep away from</li><li>to endure, suffer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fraberaną</i> (“to hold back, endure”), equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>beran</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>forbera</i> (“to forfeit”), Middle High German <i>verbërn</i> (“to have not; abstain”), Gothic <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (frabairan, “to endure”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burst asunder, break, fail</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>berstan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forbirnan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind or tie up</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frabindaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>bindan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>firbintan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to burn (completely): burn down, burn up</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frabrinnan</i>. Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>birnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bite up, bite into pieces, destroy by biting</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>bītan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow away, blow up, inflate</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>blāwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guidebook, itinerary</li><li>map, mappa mundi or itinerarium</li></ol><p>From <i>fōr</i> +&lrm; <i>bōc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a forbidding, forbode, prohibition</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furibudą</i>, equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>bod</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>firbot</i> (>German <i>Verbot</i>), Dutch <i>verbod</i>; Swedish <i>förbud</i>, Danish <i>forbud</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forbidden</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break up, break in two, crush, destroy, violate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frabrekaną</i> (“to break up”), equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>brecan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>firbrehhan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover</li><li>to snatch away</li><li>to corrupt</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>breġdan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to avoid, abstain</li><li>to get or stay out of the way of something</li><li>to abstain</li><li>to miss a meeting or an appointment</li><li>to shun, eschew</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>būgan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>avoidance</li><li>evasion</li><li>declining</li></ol><p>From <i>forbūgen</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forbūgennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forbirnan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to burn (completely): burn down, burn up</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to burn (injure with heat)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frabrannijan</i>. Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>bærnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fork</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;furkō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forca</i> (found in compounds)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pitchfork</li><li>(<i>only in the plural</i>) bars, bolts, locks</li></ol><p>Probably a conflation of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furklaz</i> (“small fork”), from Latin <i>furcula</i> (“small fork”), and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ferkalaz</i>, <i>&#42;furkaz</i> (“stake, stick, pole”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;perg-</i> (“board, beam, trunk”), related to Old Saxon <i>fercal</i>, <i>ferkal</i> (“bolt, bar, lock”), Old Norse <i>forkr</i> (“iron-clad pole used for propelling a boat”), Lithuanian <i>pérgas</i> (“dugout”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut or carve out or away, cut through, cut asunder, divide</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċeorfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forċierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forċyrring</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to turn</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to turn aside</li><li>to turn in the wrong direction, subvert</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċierran</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perversity</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>forċiered</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;ċippian</i> (“to cut, chop”). Akin to Middle Dutch <i>kippen</i> (“to cut, strike, knock”), Middle Low German <i>kippen</i> (“to hatch out”), Old Swedish <i>kippa</i> (“to chop”), Old English <i>ċipp</i> (“chip”). More at chip, chop.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forċyrring</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shrink up</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>clingan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to close, shut up</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>clȳsan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overcome, surpass; overtake, destroy, consume, wear out</li><li>to reject</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frakwemaną</i>, equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>cuman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perverse</li><li>bad, wicked</li><li>infamous</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frakunþaz</i>, past participle of <i>&#42;frakunnaną</i> (“to despise”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>forcūþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>forcūþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rebuke, reprove, upbraid; berate; chide; scold (someone)</li><li>to refuse, decline to do; forbear</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frakweþaną</i>, equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>cweþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn against</li><li>to subvert</li><li>to avoid</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċyrran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forċierrednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turning aside</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>forċyrran</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surpass in knowledge</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>cȳþan</i> (“to declare, state, make known”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ford</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;furdu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furduz</i> (“ford”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>ford</i>, Old Saxon <i>ford</i>, and Old Dutch <i>ford</i>, Old High German <i>furt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to condemn, sentence; doom</li><li>to decide</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fradōmijaną</i> (“to condemn, damn”), equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>dēman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to shut or dam up</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fradammijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fradammijaną</i> (“to dam up”), equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;demman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to darken, become obscure</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill, destroy, exterminate</li><li>to seduce, corrupt, defile</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fradōną</i> (“to do away with”), equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>dōn</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>fardōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to endure, bear, suffer</li><li>to tolerate</li><li>to divert</li><li>to delay</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fradraganą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get someone drunk</li></ol><p><i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>drenċan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive away, drive off (something from rest), banish, eject, expel</li><li>to force, compel</li><li>to overtask, load with hardship</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fradrīban</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fradrībaną</i>; equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>drīfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make drunk, inebriate</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>drincan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dry up, wither, dry away, parch</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>drūgian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make drunk, bedrunken</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fordrūgian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disappear</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>dwīnan</i>. Compare Dutch <i>verdwijnen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to close or stop up entirely</li></ol><p><i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>dyttan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deal out, expend</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fradailijaną</i>, equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>dǣlan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>farteilen</i> (German <i>verteilen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>for</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>before, in front of, pro-</li><li>first, prime, occupying a prominent position</li><li>very, supremely, foremost</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;forē-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furai-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow or wax old, become old</li><li>(<i>of living things</i>) to grow weak with age</li><li>(<i>of dead matter</i>) to get worn out with long use, decay through being kept to long</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) to run out, expire</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fraaldōn</i>, variant of <i>&#42;fraaldēn</i>, equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>ealdian</i>. Compare Middle Low German <i>voralden</i>, <i>vörōlden</i>, Old High German <i>firaltēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bear before, prefer</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>beran</i>. Calque of Latin <i>praeferō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forebȳsn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to preoccupy; forebusy; busy beforehand</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>bisgian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to announce, declare, forebode</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furaibudōną</i> (“to proclaim, lere, instruct”), equivalent to <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>bodian</i>. Related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;budą</i> (“message, command, precept”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰewdʰ-</i> (“to be awake, perceive fully”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forebȳsn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>example</li><li>exemplar, archetype, model</li></ol><p><i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>bȳsn</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off the front of</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċeorfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come forth, come or go before, prevent</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>cuman</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰𐌵𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (fauraqiman).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to foretell, predict</li><li>to preach</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furaikweþaną</i>; equivalent to <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>cweþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prediction, prophecy</li><li>heading of a chapter</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>cwide</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prejudge, assume</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>dēman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to anticipate, take before; foretake</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>fōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forefather</li></ol><p>Possibly also merged with Old Norse <i>forfaðir</i>. Equivalent to <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>fæder</i>. Compare Dutch <i>voorvader</i>, German <i>Vorvater</i>, <i>Vorfahr</i>, Danish <i>forfader</i>, Swedish <i>förfader</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to precede, stand out, go before; forego</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>gān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forego, go before, precede, rank higher than</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furaiganganą</i> (“to forego, go before”). Equivalent to <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>gangan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>predecessor</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;genġa</i>, literally "someone who walks before/in front of". Compare West Frisian <i>foargonger</i>, Dutch <i>voorganger</i>, German <i>Vorgänger</i>, and Swedish <i>föregångare</i>, which are formed similarly.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a forerunner or predecessor</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very prudent</li></ol><p><i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>glēaw</i> (“wise”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forehead</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;forēhaubid</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>forahoubit</i>. Equivalent to <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>hēafod</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porch</li></ol><p>From <i>fore</i> +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preaching</li><li>public proclamation or publication</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fōre-</i> +&lrm; <i>lār</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a forelock; lock of hair that covers the forehead</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>locc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead forth</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fore-noted</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>mearcod</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a chapter heading; a title</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>mearcung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) pronoun</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> (“pro-”) + <i>nama</i> (“noun, name”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to foresee, observe beforehand, foreknow</li><li>to provide for, take steps to secure</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċēawian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a foreshowing, foreseeing, foresight, foreknowledge, forethought, providence</li><li>consideration, contemplation</li></ol><p>From <i>foresċēawian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say beforehand, foretell, predict, prophesy</li><li>to proclaim, announce, pronounce, declare</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furaisagjaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>seċġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send before, send beforehand</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>sendan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see beforehand; foresee, provide</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proposition</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) preposition</li></ol><p>From <i>foresett</i>, past participle of <i>foresettan</i> (“to place before, propose”), + the suffix <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>foresetnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set or put before, propose; put in the front or fore of</li><li>to put at the head of, prefer</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>settan</i>. Likely a calque of Latin <i>praepōnō</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>forasezzen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to preside</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>sittan</i>. Perhaps originally a calque of Latin <i>praesideō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to premeditate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>smēaġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>advocate (person who speaks on behalf of another)</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>spreca</i> (“speaker”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>already stated, aforementioned</li><li>forespoken</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>sprecen</i> (“spoken”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>advocacy</li><li>an excuse</li><li>speech by the representative of another; that which is said by a sponsor</li><li>that which has been already said; the contents of a document</li><li>a forespeech, preface, foreword, introductory, a speaking before, a fore-promise</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;forēsprāku</i>, equivalent to <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>sprǣċ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>forasprāhha</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>foresprǣċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand before, preside, lead, excel</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>standan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intervention</li><li>(<i>judicial</i>) a hindrance, obstacle</li><li>ambush, assault; an offense of waylaying on the highway or the penalty or fine for such an offense</li><li>opposition, resistance</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> (“ahead of”) +&lrm; <i>steall</i> (“position, location”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to step or come before, prevent</li><li>to go before; forego, precede</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>steppan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>excel</li><li>to go before</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>stīgan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>foresteppan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preface, introduction (to a text)</li></ol><p>From <i>foresæġd</i> (“said before”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i> (“-ness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>foresæġdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a presage; sign or token foreshadowing something</li><li>a wonder</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>tācen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>foretācen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to predetermine; predestine, foreordain</li></ol><p><i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>teohhian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>predestination</li></ol><p><i>foreteohhian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>front tooth</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>tōþ</i> (“tooth”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>foreweard</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>foreweard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forewarn; warn beforehand</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furawarnōną</i>, equivalent to <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>warnian</i>. Cognate with Middle Low German <i>vorwarnen</i>, <i>vörwarnen</i>, Middle High German <i>vorewarnen</i>, <i>forwarnen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a rampart or bulwark</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>weall</i> (“wall”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forward, fore, former, early, prior</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an agreement, compact, treaty</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>-weard</i>.</p><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>weard</i> (“ward, keeping”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to foreknow</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>witan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forewittiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foreknowing, sagacious, presaging, prophetic; foretelling</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>wittiġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to proscribe, banish</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrītan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think beforehand; forethink</li></ol><p>From <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>þenċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defend, plead for</li></ol><p><i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>þingian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go or pass away; fare away</li><li>to destroy, perish</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frafaraną</i>, equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>faran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to flee from, escape</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to flee away</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>flēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take as forfeit</li><li>to take away unexpectedly or violently</li><li>to arrest</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>fōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abstain from, pass over, neglect, forgo; go away from</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fragān</i>, equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>gān</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>firgān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forgo; go away from, forbear, abstain from</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>gangan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>fargangan</i>, Old High German <i>firgangan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forġiefnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forġiefnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forġieldan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>foregenġel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forġiefan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give <i>(+dative a person) (+accusative for something)</i></li><li>to forgive <i>(+dative a person) (+accusative for something)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fragebaną</i>, corresponding to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġiefan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>fargevan</i>, Middle Dutch <i>vergeven</i> (Dutch <i>vergeven</i>), Old High German <i>fargeban</i> (German <i>vergeben</i>), Old Norse <i>fyrgefa</i> (Icelandic <i>fyrirgefa</i>), Gothic <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (fragiban).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>given</li><li>forgiven</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fragebanaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forgivable</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) dative</li></ol><p>From <i>forġiefan</i> (“to give, bestow”) +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forgiveness</li><li>remission (of a fine, tax, etc.)</li><li>leniency, indulgence</li></ol><p>From <i>forġiefan</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay for</li><li>to repay</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frageldaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġieldan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to neglect [<i>with</i> accusative]</li><li>to pass by [<i>with</i> accusative]</li><li>to transgress [<i>with</i> accusative]</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġīeman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neglect</li></ol><p>From <i>forġīemed</i> (“neglected”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to neglect entirely</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġīemelēasian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carelessness</li><li>neglect</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>forġīemeleasian</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with genitive or accusative</i>) to forget</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fragetan</i>, equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġietan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forġiefan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forġiefendlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forġiefnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forġiefnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forġieldan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forġietan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gnaw or eat up, gnaw away</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>gnagan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grasp or seize</li><li>to seize and carry off</li><li>to assail, overwhelm</li><li>to destroy</li><li>to take before</li><li>to carry off too soon</li><li>to preoccupy</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>grīpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow up, grow into</li><li>to overgrow; grow wrongly; grow too much or in excess</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>grōwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forġiefan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forġiefnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forġieldan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to make guilty</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to become guilty; commit a sin, offence, or crime</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>gyltan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forġīeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forġīemelēasian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forġīemelēasnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to girdle, enclose; begird</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>gyrdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forġietan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divert from, prevaricate</li><li>to transgress</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;gǣġan</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaigijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transgression</li></ol><p>From <i>forgǣġan</i> (“to transgress”) +&lrm; <i>-ed</i> (past participle suffix) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hold in, restrain, retain</li><li>(transitive,&#32;reflexive) to restrain one's self from (+ fram)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to abstain, refrain (+ fram)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>habban</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to promise or vow not to do</li><li>to renounce, forswear</li><li>to declare criminal, outlaw, forbid</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>hātan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ill-named or reprobate person</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>forhāten</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep or hold back (something), withhold, disregard, neglect; hold away</li><li>to hold wrongly, not to keep in good condition</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>healdan</i>. Cognate with German <i>verhalten</i>. Compare the formation of Latin <i>prōhibeō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to harden</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>heardian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slaughter, cut down</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>hēawan</i> (“to strike, cut”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide away, conceal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frahelaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>helan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>farhelan</i>, Old High German <i>firhelan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay waste, destroy, ravage, devastate, plunder</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>hergian</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>farheriōn</i>, German <i>verheeren</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) to harden against</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>hierdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to neglect</li><li>to despise</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>hogian</i> (to think about)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fearful, afraid; timid</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;furht</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furhtaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frightful, afraid</li></ol><p>From <i>forht</i> (“afraid”) +&lrm; <i>-ful</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be afraid, to tremble</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fear, to dread</li></ol><p>From <i>forht</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Akin to Old Saxon <i>forhtian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>timid, trembling</li><li>terrible, fearful</li></ol><p><i>forht</i> (“timid”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fear</li></ol><p><i>forht</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass away or be destroyed</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>hweorfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to despise, scorn (somebody or something)</li><li>to disdain, scorn (to do something)</li><li>to reject (somebody or something) scornfully</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>hyċġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide, hide away or fully</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>hȳdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forhierdan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>restraint</li><li>abstinence</li></ol><p><i>forhæfed</i> (“abstinent”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forhæfednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forhæfednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forliġer</i> (“adulterer”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lose</li><li>to destroy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fraleusaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;lēosan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forlǣtan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie wrongly with, lie in a forbidden manner, fornicate</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>liċġan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>firliggen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shipwrecked</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shipwreck</li></ol><p>From <i>forliden</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forlidennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adultery, fornication</li><li>adulterer</li><li>adulteress</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>adulterous, immoral</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a brothel</li></ol><p>From <i>forliġer</i> +&lrm; <i>hūs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forlidennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shipwreck</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>līþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lost</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forlornness</li><li>destruction</li></ol><p>From <i>forloren</i> (“forlorn”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seduce, mislead; lead away, lead off</li><li>to lead to harm, bring to wound or destroy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fralaidijan</i>, equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣdan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>farlēdian</i>, German <i>verleiten</i>, Dutch <i>verleiden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to misteach, teach amiss, teach wrongly, deceive, seduce; mislead</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to leave [<i>with</i> accusative]</li><li>to abandon, desert, forsake</li><li>to quit</li><li>to allow</li><li>to stop an action</li><li>to lose something</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fralētaną</i>, equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣtan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>firlāzzan</i>, Gothic <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌻𐌴𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (fralētan). Compare Icelandic <i>forláta</i> (“to forsake”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>feorm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>first</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>first</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frumô</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>formo</i> and Gothic <i>𐍆𐍂𐌿𐌼𐌰</i> (fruma).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) the first person</li></ol><p>Calque of Latin <i>prīma persōna</i>, itself a calque of Ancient Greek <i>πρῶτον πρόσωπον</i> (prôton prósōpon). Equivalent to <i>forma</i> (“first”) + <i>hād</i> (“grammatical person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to melt (completely): melt away, liquify</li><li>to digest (completely)</li><li>to be consumed (destroyed completely)</li></ol><p><i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>meltan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>first</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>first</li></ol><p>Superlative of <i>forma</i>, which is itself an old simple superlative of <i>fore</i>: Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fura</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-umô</i>; compare Latin <i>optimus</i>, <i>summus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to melt</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>mieltan</i> (“to melt”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>old, longstanding, time-honoured</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>before, in front of, opposite, across from</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trout</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;forn</i>, <i>&#42;furn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furnaz</i>, variant of <i>&#42;fernaz</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;forhnu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furhnō</i> (“trout”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fornōme</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fornōmen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fornōmon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>almost</li></ol><p><i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>nēah</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fornēah</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a nephew's daughter</li></ol><p>From <i>forn</i> +&lrm; <i>nefe</i>, perhaps modelled after Latin <i>proneptis</i> (“great-granddaughter”). Alternatively from <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>nefe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take away, plunder, waste, ransack</li><li>to take hold of, seize, do away with; fordo</li><li>to consume; fret, devour</li><li>to destroy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;franemaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>niman</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>farniman</i>, Old High German <i>firneman</i>. More at <i>nim</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fornam</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>fornōmon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>broken, fractured</li><li>violated</li><li>worn out, decayed, weakened</li><li>void</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fārōd</i>, past participle of <i>&#42;fārōn</i> (“to injure, scathe”), variant of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fārijan</i>. Related to Old Norse <i>færa</i> (“to injure, scathe”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>very often</li></ol><p><i>for</i> +&lrm; <i>oft</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to block, shut up, stop up, prevent the action of; to turn away</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;pyndan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn away, pervert, ruin, destroy</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;pǣran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become fully rotten, putrefy</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>rotian</i> (“to rot”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deprive of by treachery, give counsel against, plot against</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frarādan</i>. Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣdan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>firrādan</i>, Old High German <i>firrātan</i> (German <i>verraten</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oppose, give up (something), renounce</li><li>to refuse what is given, decline; withsay</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frasakan</i>. Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>sacan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frosc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be ashamed</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċamian</i> (“to be ashamed”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scatter, disperse; scatter away</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċeādan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>firskeidan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forsċieppan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transform; shape up</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċieppan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prow</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċip</i> (“ship”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to proscribe, condemn</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċrīfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to resign, give up, abdicate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċrīhan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shrink up, dwindle away, wither</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċrincan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very guilty, wicked, flagitious</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċyldiġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make guilty; beguilt, criminate, condemn</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċyldigian</i> (“to sin”). Related to <i>sċyldiġ</i> (whence Middle English <i>shildy</i>). Compare also German <i>verschulden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>guilty, wicked, infamous</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shut off or out</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċyttan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to punish, afflict, seek to harm, attack; onfall</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say wrongly, say ill</li><li>to say against, accuse, deny; say away</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>seċġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give up, sacrifice, lose</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>sellan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send away, banish, cast or throw away</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>sendan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>firsenten</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to despise</li><li>to reject</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēon</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>firsehan</i>. See <i>for-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to boil away</li><li>to be consumed by affliction</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fraseuþan</i>, equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēoþan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>firsiodan</i> (German <i>versieden</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stop up, block, obstruct</li><li>to oppress, press down</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>settan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contempt</li></ol><p><i>forsewen</i> + <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to delay, defer, obstruct, besiege</li><li>to mis-sit, be absent from, neglect</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lose by sitting, fail to go and do, stop up</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to remain unmoved</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>sittan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exit, departure</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) death</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>sīþ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be slow or unwilling, lose by sloth, neglect through laziness</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>slāwian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break, destroy, kill, smite, slay, strike with violence</li><li>to wound by striking or with a blow, cut through, break with a stroke</li><li>to make slaughter of, defeat with slaughter, beat an enemy</li><li>to condemn</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to overthrow, rout</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>slēan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>firslahan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hinder, impede, make slow</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choke, suffocate</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>smorian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entice</li><li>to allure</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>spanan</i> (“to entice, allure”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend utterly; spend away, consume, exhaust with spending</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>spendan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to allure, entice</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pimp</li><li>solicitor</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>forspennan</i> (“to solicit”) +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dry out, empty</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>spiercan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forspillan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) [<i>with</i> accusative] to destroy</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) [<i>with</i> accusative] to waste</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;furispilþijan</i>. Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>spillan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak against, speak ill of, denounce</li><li>to speak amiss or wrongly</li><li>to deny; gainsay; forsay</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frasprekan</i>. Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>sprecan</i>. Compare Old High German <i>firsprehhan</i> (whence German <i>versprechen</i>), and Old Saxon <i>farsprekan</i> (whence Dutch <i>verspreken</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frost</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frost</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frustą</i>, <i>&#42;frustaz</i>, akin to Old High German <i>frost</i>, Old Norse <i>frost</i>. The surviving attestations show metathesis of <i>r</i>, but the descendants derive from a form without it.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand for, defend; shield, help, benefit</li><li>to be good for, avail, profit</li><li>to be as good as, stand as the same or equivalent of</li><li>to stand against; gainstand, withstand, hinder, stop (someone)</li><li>to understand</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frastandan</i>. Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>standan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(intensive)</i> to steal</li><li>to kidnap</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frastelaną</i> (“to steal, steal away”), equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>stelan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>farstelan</i>, Old High German <i>firstelan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frosty</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frostag</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frustagaz</i>, equivalent to <i>frost</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stop up</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>stoppian</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>firstopfōn</i> (German <i>verstopfen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn up, kindle</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>swelan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swallow up, devour</li><li>to absorb</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fraswelganą</i>, equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>swelgan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>farswelgan</i>, Old High German <i>firswelgan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>sweltan</i> (“to die”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be very dark</li><li>to darken, obscure</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>sweorcan</i> (“to darken”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to commit perjury, swear a false oath</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fraswarjan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>farswerian</i>, Old High German <i>farsweren</i>. Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>swerian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overcome</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>swīþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn, scorch, burn up</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>swǣlan</i> (“to burn”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw away, seduce; forlead, mislead</li><li>to pull and hurt, gripe</li><li>to pull in the way of, cover up, obscure</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frateuhan</i>, equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>tēon</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>farziohan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tread upon, tread utterly down on, tread under foot</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fratredan</i>. Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>tredan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>firtretan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obstruct by rolling; roll in front of</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>trendan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Canary Islands</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Fortūnātae Īnsulae</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw away, seduce</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overgrow, swell, grow too much; wax wrongly; grow away</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>weaxan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defile</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>wemman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overween, think too highly of</li><li>to think ill of, suspect; underlook; mistrust</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>wēnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worn-out, decayed, decrepit</li></ol><p>The past participle of the unattested verb <i>&#42;forweosan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cast away, throw away, reject</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>weorpan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>farwerpan</i>, Gothic <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (frawairpan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forweorþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to perish</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frawerþaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>weorþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forwerod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forweoren</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worn out, very old</li></ol><p>From <i>werian</i> (“to wear”), with the prefix <i>for-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forwost</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prohibit, deny, refuse</li><li>to restrain, hinder</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>wiernan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exalt, fill with pride, make very proud</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>wlenċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forweoren</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>high-ranking person, chief, prince</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furwōstaz</i>, superlative of <i>&#42;furwaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pr̥h₂wós</i> (“first”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive out, banish, expel</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrecan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut asunder</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrītan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wound badly</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frawundōn</i>. Equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>wundian</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>farwundon</i>, Middle Dutch <i>verwonden</i>, Middle High German <i>verwunden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do wrong, sin</li><li>to destroy</li><li>to forfeit, lose by evildoing</li><li>to be guilty</li><li>to convict</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frawurkijaną</i>, equivalent to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrċan</i>. Cognate with German <i>verwirken</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destruction, devastation, perdition, ruin</li></ol><p>From By surface analysis, <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an agent, one who does something for another</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“worker, maker”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forwiernan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forþ</i></li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forþon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forþon</i></li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forþon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>forþweġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>forþȳ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forþon</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>for þon þe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li><i>expresses the continuation of an action</i></li><li>out, forth <i>(so as to be seen or known)</i></li><li>forwards</li><li>forward</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>forward to, up to</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furþą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pr̥to-</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>forth</i>, Old Saxon <i>forth</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>straight ahead, straight forward</li><li>forthrightly, plainly</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>forth, forward; away; still, continuously</li><li>onto, towards</li></ol><p>From <i>forþ</i> (“forth, forward”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forþon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>because</li><li>for that, for that reason</li></ol><p>From <i>forþam</i> (“because (a variant of <i>forþon</i>)”) +&lrm; <i>þe</i> (“that, which”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forþon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry or bear forth, bring forth or forward, produce</li></ol><p>From <i>forþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>beran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring forth, produce, fulfill, accomplish</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;forþbringan</i>, equivalent to <i>forþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>bringan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call forth</li><li>to provoke</li></ol><p>From <i>forþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>clipian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forþclipian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come forth or forward, proceed, succeed, arrive; oncome</li></ol><p>From <i>forþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>cuman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to despise, disdain, distrust</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>þenċan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to darken, be dark</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>þēostrian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go or fare forth, depart, die</li></ol><p>From <i>forþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>faran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die</li></ol><p><i>forþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>fēran</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow forth</li></ol><p>From <i>forþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>flōwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forefather, ancestor</li></ol><p>From <i>forþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>fæder</i> (“father”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go forth, proceed, go or pass by</li></ol><p>From <i>forþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>gān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>going forward: advancement or an advance</li><li>progress</li><li>going out</li><li>drain</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furþagangaz</i> (“a going forth, advancement”), equivalent to <i>forþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>gang</i>. Compare German <i>Fortschritt</i> (“progress”), which is formed similarly.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go forth, proceed</li><li>to go before, precede</li></ol><p>From <i>forþ</i> +&lrm; <i>gangan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moving forward: advancing, progressing</li><li>progressive</li><li>increasing</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furþagangijaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>forþ</i> +&lrm; <i>genġe</i>, literally "going forward." Corresponds to the noun <i>forþgang</i> (“progress”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to count up, count out</li></ol><p>From <i>forþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġerīman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the world; creation</li><li>the future state of the world, one's own future</li></ol><p>From <i>forþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġesċeaft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go forward; go forth</li><li>to advance</li><li>to ascend</li></ol><p>From <i>forþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġestīgan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go forth, pass, proceed, go by</li><li>to depart, die</li></ol><p>From <i>forþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġewītan</i> (“to see, pass over, go, depart”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>past</li><li>departed</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) past tense</li></ol><p><i>forþġewiten</i> (“past, departed, gone by”) + <i>tīd</i> (“time”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the past</li><li>departure</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>forþġewiten</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>forþ</i> +&lrm; <i>here</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forþȳ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to further</li><li>to aid, assist</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furþōną</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furþą</i> (“forth, forward”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be dark, grow dark, darken</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make dark, darken</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;þīestrian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to intercede</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>þingian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead or bring forth, produce</li></ol><p>From <i>forþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣdan</i>. Calque of Latin <i>prōdūcere</i> (“to lead forth”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be deprived of, go without, lack</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>þolian</i> (“suffer”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for that reason, consequently, therefore</li><li>at first</li><li>indeed</li><li>also</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>for that, for, because</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>for</i> +&lrm; <i>þon</i></p><p>Equivalent to <i>forþ</i> +&lrm; <i>on</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oppress, suppress</li><li>to overwhelm</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>þryċċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to choke, to suffocate</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entirely bruise, break</li><li>to crush</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>þrǣstan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>progress</li></ol><p>From <i>forþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċiepe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send forth</li></ol><p>From <i>forþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>sendan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak forth, speak out</li></ol><p>From <i>forþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>sprecan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead forth</li><li>make known, discover</li><li>betray, render up</li></ol><p>From <i>forþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>tēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forwarding, furtherance, promotion</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>forþian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>path of departure, onward course, journey</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;forþweg</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>forđweg</i>. Equivalent to <i>forþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>weġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guide forth, direct</li></ol><p>From <i>forþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>wīsian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>For that; because; therefore.</li></ol><p>From <i>for</i> (“for”) + <i>þȳ</i> (“by that”), instrumental case of <i>þæt</i> (“that”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>for því</i> (“for this cause”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dry up</li></ol><p>From <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;þyrrian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forþon</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>for þon þe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a thane or noble of high rank</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>forþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>þeġn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fōstor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fostering, nourishing, rearing, feeding</li><li>food, nourishment, provisions</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fōstr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fōstrą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂-</i> (“to protect”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foster child</li></ol><p>From <i>fōstor</i> (“food”) +&lrm; <i>bearn</i> (“child”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foster brother</li></ol><p>From <i>fōstor</i> (“food”) +&lrm; <i>brōþor</i> (“brother”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foster child</li></ol><p>From <i>fōstor</i> (“food”) +&lrm; <i>ċild</i> (“child”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foster father</li></ol><p>From <i>fōstor</i> (“food”) +&lrm; <i>fæder</i> (“father”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land assigned for the procuring of provisions</li></ol><p>From <i>fōstor</i> (“food, sustenance”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foster child</li></ol><p>From <i>fōstor</i> (“food”) +&lrm; <i>-ling</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foster mother</li></ol><p>From <i>fōstor</i> (“food”) +&lrm; <i>modor</i> (“mother”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pasture</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>fōstor</i> (“sustenance”) +&lrm; <i>-noþ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foster sister</li></ol><p>From <i>fōstor</i> (“food”) +&lrm; <i>sweostor</i> (“sister”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a foot, in the following senses:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) an organ in humans and animals used for locomotion</li><li>a unit of length, especially a third of a yard</li><li>the base or bottom of something</li><li>(<i>prosody</i>) a metrical foot</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fōt</i>.</p><p>The nominative and accusative plural <i>fēt</i> is the result of an umlaut: the first vowel in Proto-West Germanic plural <i>*fōti</i> shifted into a close-mid front rounded vowel in order to harmonize it with the last vowel. After speakers dropped the last vowel, the front rounded vowel became unrounded.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Germanic cognates include Old Frisian <i>fōt</i>, Old Saxon <i>fōt</i> (Dutch <i>voet</i>), Old High German <i>fuoȥ</i> (German <i>Fuß</i>), Old Norse <i>fótr</i> (Danish <i>fod</i>, Swedish <i>fot</i>), Gothic <i>𐍆𐍉𐍄𐌿𐍃</i> (fōtus). The Proto-Indo-European root was also the source of Sanskrit <i>पद्</i> (pád), Ancient Greek <i>πούς</i> (poús) (genitive <i>ποδός</i> (podós)); Doric <i>πῶς</i> (pôs), Latin <i>pēs</i> (genitive <i>pedis</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gout</li></ol><p>From <i>fōt</i> (“foot”) +&lrm; <i>ādl</i> (“disease”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>suffering from gout; gouty, gout-ridden</li></ol><p>From <i>fōtādl</i> (“gout”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>on foot, that which is done by foot</li><li>pedestrian, low in style</li></ol><p>From <i>fōt</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>footprint</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the mark left by a foot, footprint</li><li>the space taken up by a foot</li><li>a foot (unit of length)</li></ol><p>From <i>fōt</i> +&lrm; <i>mǣl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by footsteps, step by step, by degrees</li></ol><p>From <i>fōt</i> +&lrm; <i>-mǣlum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>footstool</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fōtskamil</i>. Equivalent to <i>fōt</i> (“foot”) +&lrm; <i>sċamol</i> (“stool”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fōtsċamol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a trail of footsteps, footprints</li><li>footprint</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fōtspor</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fōtspurą</i>, equivalent to <i>fōt</i> +&lrm; <i>spor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>footprint</li></ol><p>From <i>fōt</i> (“foot”) +&lrm; <i>swæþ</i> (“footprint, track”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sole of the foot</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fox</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fuhsaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>&#42;foks</i>, Old Saxon <i>fohs</i>, Old Dutch <i>fus</i>, Old High German <i>fuhs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fox's foot (<i>Sparganium simplex</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>foxes</i> (“fox's”) +&lrm; <i>fōt</i> (“foot”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foxglove</li></ol><p>From <i>fox</i> +&lrm; <i>glōf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foxhole</li></ol><p>From <i>fox</i> (“fox”) +&lrm; <i>hol</i> (“hole”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fōþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food, fodder</li><li>covering</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>fro, from</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fracoþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an insult</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vile, filthy, unseemly, abominable</li></ol><p>From <i>forcūþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>fracoþ</i>; vilest</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wicked, criminal</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fræġning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>from [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>since [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>by [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>forward</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forward, firm, bold, stout, resolute</li><li>vigorous, strong</li><li>valiant, excellent</li><li>effective</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fram</i> (“forward”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;prom-</i>, <i>&#42;pr-</i> (“forward, through”). Akin to Old High German <i>fram</i> (“forth, forward”), Old Norse <i>fram</i> (“forward, onward”, adverb), Old Norse <i>frá</i> (preposition), Gothic <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌼</i> (fram).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fram</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;framaz</i> (“forward, protruding”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;prom-</i>, <i>&#42;pr-</i> (“forward, through”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>from, forth, away, out, out of (compare Latin <i>ex-</i>, <i>dē</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>fram</i> (“forward, from, out of”). More at <i>from</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn away or from</li><li>to take from</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fram-</i> +&lrm; <i>āċyrran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw away from</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fram-</i> +&lrm; <i>ātēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn from; to turn away; to leave</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fram-</i> +&lrm; <i>būgan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn from, avert</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fram-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċierran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) offspring; progeny; posterity</li></ol><p>From <i>fram-</i> (“from, future”) +&lrm; <i>cynn</i> (“kin”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>departure</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fram-</i> +&lrm; <i>færeld</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn from</li><li>to go or depart from</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fram-</i> +&lrm; <i>hweorfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to avail, profit</li><li>to be helpful, be of service, make progress</li><li>to further</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;framōną</i>, <i>&#42;framjaną</i> (“to further”). More at from.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fromward</li><li>turned away from, having one's face or back turned away, departing, about to depart</li></ol><p><i>frām</i> (“from”) +&lrm; <i>-weard</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lance, javelin, spear</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frankō</i> (“javelin”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>frakka</i> (“javelin”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Franca)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Frank, Frenchman</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the Franks</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Frankō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frankô</i>. Related to Frankish <i>&#42;Franko</i>, Old High German <i>Franko</i> (German <i>Franke</i>), Old Norse <i>Frakki</i> (Icelandic <i>Frakki</i>), Latin <i>francus</i> (Old French <i>franc</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Franks, the French</li><li>France or Francia</li><li><i>inflection of</i> <b>Franca</b></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Frankaniz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Francrīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the land of the Franks; France, Frankland (a medieval kingdom in Western Europe)</li></ol><p>From <i>Franca</i> (“the Franks'”) +&lrm; <i>land</i>. Compare Icelandic <i>Frakkland</i> (“France”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the kingdom of the Franks; France</li></ol><p>From <i>Franca</i> (“the Franks'”) +&lrm; <i>rīċe</i> (“kingdom, realm”). Compare Dutch <i>Frankrijk</i> (“France”), German <i>Frankreich</i> (“France”), Norwegian <i>Frankrike</i> (“France”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask, inquire, interrogate</li><li>to try, tempt</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fraisōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fraisōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asking, question, interrogation, inquiry</li><li>temptation</li></ol><p>From <i>frāsian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) lord, king</li><li>God, Christ</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) husband</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>frawō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frawjô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;proHwo-</i>, a derivation from <i>&#42;per-</i> (“to go forward”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>frāho</i>, Old High German <i>frao</i>, <i>frō</i>, Old Norse <i>Freyr</i>, Gothic <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌿𐌾𐌰</i> (frauja), and via Proto-Indo-European with Latin <i>prōvincia</i> (English <i>province</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>an intensifying prefix meaning "very", "extremely", "exceedingly"</li></ol><p>Cognate with Latin <i>prae-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lord</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Frawōwini</i>, from <i>&#42;frawō</i> (“lord”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wini</i> (“friend”). Equivalent to <i>frēa</i> +&lrm; <i>wine</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Frōwin</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Frēawine</li></ol><p>From <i>Frēawine</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>greedy, bold</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frek</i>, akin to Old Norse <i>frekr</i> (Danish <i>fræk</i>), Old High German <i>freh</i> (German <i>frech</i>), Gothic <i>&#42;𐍆𐍂𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (&#42;friks), (attested in 𐍆𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿𐍆𐍂𐌹𐌺𐍃 (faihufriks)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warrior</li><li>hero, brave man</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frekô</i> (“an active or eager man, warrior, wolf”), from <i>&#42;frekaz</i> (“active, bold, desirous, greedy”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frēcennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frēcn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>danger or a danger</li></ol><p><i>frēcne</i> (“dangerous”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i> (“-ness”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fraggjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fragjaną</i> (“to question, inquire, examine”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;preḱ-</i> (“to woo, ask”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>danger; peril</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frōkni</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frōkniz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dangerous</li><li>grievous, dire, horrible</li><li>severe, fierce, savage</li><li>daring, bold, brave, audacious</li><li>wicked</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frēcn</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frōkni</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frōkniz</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frōkni</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frōkni</i> (“danger, peril”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frēcennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to endanger</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>frēcne</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frēcennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>frēcne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel</li><li>to perceive, sense</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frōdijaną</i> (“to make wise”), derived from <i>&#42;frōdaz</i> (“wise through experience”) (Old English <i>frōd</i>). Cognate with Dutch <i>vroeden</i> and Icelandic <i>fræða</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fræfellīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fræfellīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comfort, console, soothe [<i>with</i> accusative]</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frōbrijan</i> (“to console, comfort”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frōfrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fræġn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fræġn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Northumbrian</i>) alternative form of <i>friġnan</i> (“to ask”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>friġnan</i> (“to ask”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inquiry, especially one pertaining to future events</li><li>divining; divination</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frihtiz</i>, equivalent to <i>freċġan</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>, a variant of <i>-þ</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>frétt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strange</li><li>foreign</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a stranger</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;framiþī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;framaþijaz</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fram-</i> (related to <i>from</i>). Cognates include Old Saxon <i>fremithi</i>, Dutch <i>vreemd</i>, Old High German <i>fremidi</i> (whence German <i>fremd</i>), and Gothic <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌼𐌰𐌸𐍃</i> (framaþs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to alienate, estrange, make indifferent to</li><li>to deprive of</li><li>to make an alien of a person, excommunicate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fremde</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Compare Gothic <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌼𐌰𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (framaþjan, “to alienate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vigorous, flourishing</li><li>good, strenuous, bold</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fremu, advantage, gain, benefit, profit, good</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frami-</i> (“going forth”). Akin to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;framaz</i> (“forward”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fremde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to alienate, estrange</li></ol><p>From <i>fremede</i> +&lrm; <i>-lǣċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accomplishment, fulfilment</li></ol><p>From <i>fremed</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fremde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>profitable, beneficial</li></ol><p><i>freme</i> (“benefit, profit”) +&lrm; <i>-ful</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to profit, do good, be good or expedient, avail</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;framijōn</i>, equivalent to <i>fremu</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do, accomplish, carry out, perform</li><li>to further, to advance, to promote</li><li>to urge (e.g. to an action)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;framjaną</i> (“to perform, promote”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pro-mo-</i> (“forward, front”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>frema</i>, Old Saxon <i>fremmian</i>, Old High German <i>fremman</i>, Old Norse <i>fremja</i> (Danish <i>fremme</i>). More at from.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>effect</li><li>doing</li><li>operation (of something)</li></ol><p>From <i>fremman</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>kind, benign, courteous</li></ol><p>From <i>freme</i> +&lrm; <i>-sum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>fremsum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>benign</li></ol><p><i>fremsum</i> (“kind, benign”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>advantage, benefit</li><li>profit, gain</li><li>kindness, kind deed, good</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;framī</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>fremo</i>, <i>freme</i> (“use”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>advantage, profit, a good</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fremian</i> (“to profit, do good”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Frankish language</li><li>French language</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Frankish</li><li>French</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Frankisk</i>, equivalent to <i>Franca</i> (“Frank, Frenchman”) +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i> (“-ish”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free, at liberty; exempt</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) noble, glad</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a free man, man</li><li>a woman</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;friu</i>, a byform of <i>&#42;frī</i>. The transmission of this word was complex. The Proto-Germanic form must have been *<i>frijwaz</i>. After loss of final *-az and syllabification of *-w, the resulting West-Germanic form was *<i>friju</i>, which developed further to *<i>frīju</i> after gemination and vocalization of *j between *i and another *j. The sequence *iju, *īju then contracts to *iu, yielding a final form *<i>friu</i> (cf. Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;friund</i>, from <i>&#42;frijōndz</i>), which develops normally to Old English <i>frīo, frēo</i>. The oblique forms should however have retained *-j- in all instances in which it was not followed by *u, yielding an alternation between <i>frīo</i> and <i>frīġ-</i> throughout the paradigm. This alternation, though sparsely attested in the Old English corpus, was too irregular to have been maintained for long and was eventually levelled away in all dialects in favor either of the diphthongal nominative or the oblique forms in -ġ-. Note also that *w was deleted between *īj and any front vowel, thus explaining its absence in certain oblique forms such as <i>frīġes</i>, from earlier /ˈfriːj.wæs/. See Hogg 1992 sections 3.17, 3.19(3), 4.7, and 4.9(1) for details.</p><p>Uncertain; possible etymologies include:</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peace, frith</li><li>friendship, affection, goodwill</li></ol><p>Akin to <i>friþ</i> (“peace, friendship”), <i>frēogan</i> (“to love”), <i>frēo</i> (“free, joyful”). Analogous to Gothic <i>𐍆𐍂𐌹𐌾𐌰𐌸𐍅𐌰</i> (frijaþwa).</p><p>See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freedom, state of free will</li><li>charter</li><li>emancipation, deliverance</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frijadōm</i> (“freedom”). Equivalent to <i>frēo</i> +&lrm; <i>-dōm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to free, to liberate</li><li>to like, to love, to honour</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frijōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frijōną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;preyH-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frēo</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oblation, free-will offering</li></ol><p>From <i>frēo</i> (“free”) +&lrm; <i>-lāc</i> (“gift”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free, freeborn, noble, ingenuous, comely, goodly</li></ol><p>From <i>frēo</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>frīlīk</i>, Old High German <i>frīlīh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>frēolīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>freely, without hindrance, with impunity, with freedom from control or rule</li><li>willingly, without constraint, restraint, or reluctance</li><li>nobly, splendidly</li></ol><p>From <i>frēo</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freedom</li><li>privilege, immunity</li><li>festival, day of feasting</li><li>a charter of freedom</li><li>peace</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>frīheals</i> from <i>frī</i> (“free”) + <i>heals</i> (“neck”). Akin to <i>frēo</i> (“free, joyful”). More at <i>frēo</i>, <i>heals</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to observe or keep a holy day; celebrate</li></ol><p>From <i>frēols</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a deliverer, redeemer, liberator</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>frēolsian</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>festive time, time of feasting</li><li>church festival</li><li>anniversary</li></ol><p>From <i>frēols</i> (“festival, feast”) +&lrm; <i>tīd</i> (“time, tide”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fram</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>frēo</i> (“free”) + <i>mann</i> (“man”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freeman (someone not enslaved)</li></ol><p>From <i>frēo</i> (“free”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person, man”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fremian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) free or noble kinsman</li></ol><p>From <i>frēo</i> (“noble”) +&lrm; <i>mǣġ</i> (“relative”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>frēomāgum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frēoġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friend</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) lover</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;friund</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frijōndz</i>, originally a present participle of the weak verb <i>&#42;frijōną</i> (“to love, to free”) (Old English <i>frēoġan</i>), from Indo-European <i>*prāy-, *prēy-</i> ‘like, love’. Corresponding to <i>frēoġan</i> +&lrm; <i>-nd</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>friūnd</i>, Old Saxon <i>friund</i>, Old High German <i>friunt</i>, Old Norse <i>frændi</i>, Gothic <i>𐍆𐍂𐌹𐌾𐍉𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (frijōnds).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friendly instruction</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>frēond</i> +&lrm; <i>lār</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friendly invitation</li></ol><p><i>frēond</i> (“friend”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;laþu</i> (“invitation”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>friendless</li><li>without relatives, orphan</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic; compare Old High German <i>friuntlōs</i>, Middle Dutch <i>vriendeloos</i>. By surface analysis, <i>frēond</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>friendly</li><li>kind</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frijōndlīk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frijōndlīkaz</i>, equivalent to <i>frēond</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>friōndlik</i>, Old Saxon <i>friundlīk</i>, Old High German <i>friuntlīh</i>, Old Norse <i>frýnligr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>kindly</li><li>in the manner of a friend</li></ol><p><i>frēond</i> (“friend”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>frēondlīċe</i>; friendlier, more kindly</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love between or for friends: platonic love</li></ol><p><i>frēond</i> +&lrm; <i>lufu</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friendship</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>frēond</i> +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friendship</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;friundskapi</i>. Equivalent to <i>frēond</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frēondsċiepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frēondsċiepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>freezing, cold, chilly</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;freuʀag</i>, <i>&#42;friuʀīg</i>. Compare German <i>frierig</i> (“freezing, cold”), Old Norse <i>frør</i>, <i>frer</i> (“frost”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to freeze</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;freusan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;freusaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;prews-</i> (“to freeze, frost”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>&#42;friāsa</i>, Old Saxon <i>friosan</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;friesan</i>, Old High German <i>friosan</i>, Old Norse <i>frjósa</i>, and with Latin <i>pruīna</i> (“hoar-frost”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freewoman; a woman not born into slavery</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>frēo</i> (“free”) +&lrm; <i>wīf</i> (“woman”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Friuwini</i>, from <i>&#42;friu</i> (“free”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wini</i> (“friend”). Equivalent to <i>frēo</i> +&lrm; <i>wine</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Friowin</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>friþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Freoþomund</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Freoþorǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>friþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Friþubeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Friþugār</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>friþian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Friþubeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>friþlēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Friþumund</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, variant of Friþurīc</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Friþurǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>friþowebba</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Frysa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Frīscyning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Frīsena</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Friesland</li></ol><p>From <i>Frysa</i> (“Frisian person”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to devour</li><li>to break, burst</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fraetan</i>, corresponding to <i>for-</i> +&lrm; <i>etan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>fretan</i> (Middle Low German <i>vreten</i>), Old High German <i>frezzan</i> (German <i>fressen</i>), Gothic <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (fraitan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fræfellīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>friþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frēo</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crier, herald</li></ol><p>Possibly cognate with the male given name Old High German <i>Fricco</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Frīġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inquire, ask, question, find out, seek after, learn, get information from</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;friggjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frigjaną</i> (“to ask”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;preḱ-</i> (“to woo, ask”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to desire, seek, yearn for</li><li>to dance</li><li>to leap</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frekōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frekōną</i> (“to be greedy”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frekaz</i> (“greedy, courageous, capable, active, bold”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;preg-</i> (“to yearn, covet”). Related to Old English <i>frec</i> (“bold, greedy”).</p><p>Unknown. Perhaps related to the above.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appetite</li></ol><p>Derivative of <i>frician</i> (“to desire”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>friclu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>use, utilization</li><li>enjoyment</li><li>(<i>mercantile language</i>) use of money, especially money lent; interest, usury</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frikīnō</i> (“avarice, greed, insolence, boldness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make friends</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Frysa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frēo</i></li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Frig)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Frigg, the Germanic goddess of love, wife of Odin (Old English <i>Wōden</i>).</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Frīju</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Frijjō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frēoġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frīġedæġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Friday</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Frījā dag</i>, a calque of Latin <i>diēs Veneris</i> (“Friday”, literally “day of Venus”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>question</li><li>inquiry, interrogation</li></ol><p>From <i>friġen</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thursday night</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thursday evening</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frēomann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask, pray</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fregnan</i>, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frehnaną</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>fregnan</i>, Old Norse <i>fregna</i>, Gothic <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (fraihnan), and related to Old High German <i>frāga</i> (German <i>Frage</i>). The Indo-European root also gave Old Persian <i>𐎠𐎱𐎼𐎿𐎶</i> (a-p-r-s-m /⁠aparsam⁠/, “I asked”), Old Irish <i>arcaid</i>, Middle Welsh <i>archaf</i>, Old Armenian <i>հարց</i> (harcʻ), Tocharian A <i>prak-</i>, Tocharian B <i>prek-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frigenes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fræġning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frigenes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frīju</i>, <i>&#42;frijōn</i> (“to love”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>freht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A soothsayer, diviner.</li></ol><p>From <i>friht</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frēolīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frēomann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frēomann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>friġnan</i> (“to ask”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frēodōm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frēo</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frēond</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>friþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Friþurīc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Frisian</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Frīsō</i>. See <i>Frīsan</i> for more.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Frisians</li><li>Frisia</li><li><i>inflection of</i> <b>Frīsa</b></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Frīsan</i>, whence also Old Frisian <i>Frīsa</i> and Old High German <i>Frīson</i>. The Proto-Germanic form is uncertain, since Old Norse <i>frísir</i> is an i-stem; thus the West Germanic form points to <i>*frīsaniz</i>, while Old Norse points to <i>*frīsīz</i>. Note that variants with <i>ē</i> exist, but the oldest sources attest that these are secondary developments; see especially Latin <i>Frīsiī</i>, attested since the first century.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>king of Frisia</li></ol><p>From <i>Frīsa</i> +&lrm; <i>cyning</i>. Compare <i>Angelcyning</i>, which is formed similarly.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Frisia</li></ol><p>Literally, “land of the Frisians”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Frisian</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a Frisian</li></ol><p><i>Frīsa</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Frisia</li></ol><p><i>Frīsa</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frēowīf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Friþelāf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Friþuhild</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>friþian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>friþiġend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>friþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>friþotācen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>friþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peace</li><li>refuge, sanctuary</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>splendid, beautiful</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;friþu</i>&nbsp;m, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;friþuz</i>&nbsp;m, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;preyH-</i> (“beloved, happy”).</p><p>Earlier *frīd, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frīdaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peace oath</li></ol><p>From <i>friþ</i> +&lrm; <i>āþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Friþulaib</i>, from <i>&#42;friþu</i> (“peace”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;laib</i> (“remainder”). Equivalent to <i>friþu</i> +&lrm; <i>lāf</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Friduleib</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>friþlēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Friþustain</i>, from <i>&#42;friþu</i> (“peace”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;stain</i> (“stone”). Equivalent to <i>friþ</i> +&lrm; <i>stān</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Fridostēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Friþuweald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Friþuhild</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>safehouse; asylum; sanctuary</li></ol><p>From <i>friþ</i> +&lrm; <i>hūs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to protect, defend</li><li>to keep the peace for somebody or an institution</li><li>to keep the sanctity of a holy day</li><li>to grant immunity to a criminal</li><li>to give asylum to somebody</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>friþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>friþiġend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protector</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>friþian</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>peaceless, outlawed, not included in a peace treaty</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;friþulaus</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;friþulausaz</i>, equivalent to <i>friþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>peaceable, gentle, mild, lenient</li></ol><p>From <i>friþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>peaceably, quietly</li></ol><p>From <i>friþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Friþugār</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Friþogȳþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Friþugunþi</i>, from <i>&#42;friþu</i> (“peace”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;gunþi</i> (“battle”). Equivalent to <i>friþ</i> +&lrm; <i>gūþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Fritgund</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>symbol for peace</li></ol><p>From <i>friþ</i> +&lrm; <i>tācen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peace-weaver: peacemaker</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) angel</li></ol><p>From <i>friþ</i> +&lrm; <i>webba</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Friþuwulf</i>, from <i>&#42;friþu</i> (“peace”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wulf</i> (“wolf”). Equivalent to <i>friþ</i> +&lrm; <i>wulf</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Fridolf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>refuge; sanctuary, asylum</li></ol><p><i>friþ</i> (“peace”) +&lrm; <i>-stōw</i> (“place”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>peaceful</li></ol><p>From <i>friþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-sum</i>. Compare Dutch <i>vreedzaam</i> (“peaceful”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make peaceable, reconcile</li></ol><p><i>friþsum</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frith (peace, security, protection, a refuge)</li></ol><p>Ultimately Proto-Germanic from <i>&#42;frijaz</i> (“free, loved”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-iþō</i>&nbsp;f (abstract noun suffix). Compare Gothic <i>𐍆𐍂𐌹𐌾𐌰𐌸𐍅𐌰</i> (frijaþwa) from <i>&#42;frijaz</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-þwō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Friþuberht</i>, from <i>&#42;friþu</i> (“peace”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”). Equivalent to <i>friþ</i> +&lrm; <i>berht</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Fridberht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Friþugaiʀ</i>. Equivalent to <i>friþ</i> (“peace”) +&lrm; <i>gār</i> (“spear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Friþugār</li></ol><p>From <i>Friþugār</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Friþugern</i>, from <i>&#42;friþu</i> (“peace”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;gern</i> (“eager, willing”). Equivalent to <i>friþ</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeorn</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Fridigern</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Friþuhildi</i>, from <i>&#42;friþu</i> (“peace”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hildi</i> (“battle”). Equivalent to <i>friþu</i> +&lrm; <i>hild</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Fridohilt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Friþumundu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Friþumunduz</i>. Equivalent to <i>friþ</i> (“peace”) +&lrm; <i>mund</i> (“protector”). Cognate with Old High German <i>Fridimunt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Friþurīk</i>. Equivalent to <i>friþ</i> (“peace”) +&lrm; <i>rīc</i> (“king, ruler”). Cognate with English <i>Frederick</i>, German <i>Friedrich</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Friþurād</i>. Equivalent to <i>friþu</i> (“peace”) +&lrm; <i>rǣd</i> (“counsel, advice”). Cognate with Old High German <i>Fridurāt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Friþustain</i>, from <i>&#42;friþu</i> (“peace”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;stain</i> (“stone”). Equivalent to <i>friþ</i> +&lrm; <i>stān</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Fridostēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Friþuswinþ</i>, from <i>&#42;friþu</i> (“peace”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;swinþ</i> (“strong”). Equivalent to <i>friþ</i> +&lrm; <i>swiþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Fridesuind</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Friþuweald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Friþuwald</i>. Equivalent to <i>friþ</i> (“peace”) +&lrm; <i>weald</i> (“powerful, mighty”). Cognate with Old High German <i>Fridwald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Friþuwini</i>, from <i>&#42;friþu</i> (“peace”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wini</i> (“friend”). Equivalent to <i>friþu</i> +&lrm; <i>wine</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Friduin</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frog</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;froggō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) wise, experienced</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) old, mature</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frōdaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>frōd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comfort, consolation</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frōbru</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>theology</i>) the Holy Ghost</li></ol><p>From <i>frōfor</i> +&lrm; <i>gāst</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to console</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frōbrijan</i> (“to console, comfort”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frōfor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frocga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bold, firm, resolute</li><li>abundant, excellent</li></ol><p>Of Germanic origin, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;framaz</i> (“forward, prominent”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;promo-</i> (“front, forth”).</p><p>Cognate with Old High German <i>fruma</i> (German <i>fromm</i>, Yiddish <i>פֿרום</i> (frum)), Middle Dutch <i>vrōme</i> (Dutch <i>vroom</i>), Old Norse <i>framr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fram-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>framcynn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>framċierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>framhweorfan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frāmweard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Francland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Francland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frog</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frosk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fruskaz</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>&#42;frosk</i>, Old Saxon <i>&#42;frosk</i>, Old Dutch <i>forsk</i>, Old High German <i>frosc</i>, Old Norse <i>froskr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forst</i>, produced by metathesis</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>forstiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woman, lady</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frōwā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frōwǭ, frauwǭ, &#42;frawwǭ</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>frowe</i>, <i>frouwe</i>, Old Saxon <i>frūa</i>, Old High German <i>frouwa</i> (“lady”), Old Norse <i>freyja</i> (“lady”), German <i>Frau</i> (“lady”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frosc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>from</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fruma</i> (used in compounds)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beginning, origin</li><li>prince, king, chief, ruler; often used in compounds</li><li>originator, inventor, creator</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frumô</i>, from the stem <i>&#42;fur-</i>, <i>&#42;fr-</i> (Old English <i>for</i>, <i>for-</i>) + the rarer comparative suffix <i>&#42;-umô</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐍆𐍂𐌿𐌼𐌰</i> (fruma), Latin <i>prīmus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firstborn child</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>frum</i> (“original, first”) +&lrm; <i>bearn</i> (“child”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>firstborn</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) primitive, not derived or declined</li></ol><p><i>fruma</i> +&lrm; <i>cenned</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frumċyrr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frumċyrr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frumcynn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) original kind; lineage; descent; origin; genealogy</li></ol><p>From <i>fruma</i> (“beginning”) +&lrm; <i>cynn</i> (“tribe, kin”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a first turn or time</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fruma</i> +&lrm; <i>ċyrr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a first payment, especially the first payment of a wergild</li></ol><p>From <i>frum</i> (“first, original”) +&lrm; <i>ġield</i> (“payment”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>first creation, the first created human</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>first garment (referring to the first clothes made by God after the fall of Adam and Eve)</li></ol><p>From <i>fruma</i> +&lrm; <i>hræġl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the captain of a ship, boat, or other vessel</li></ol><p>From <i>frum</i> (“first, leading”) +&lrm; <i>lida</i> (“sailor, traveller”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creation (abstract concept or created thing)</li><li>origin, original place or state</li></ol><p>From <i>fruma</i> +&lrm; <i>sċeaft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) original sin</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>authority</li></ol><p>From <i>frum-</i> +&lrm; <i>setnes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prow (of a ship)</li></ol><p>From <i>frum</i> (“first, original”) +&lrm; <i>stemn</i> (“stem”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>author, creator</li></ol><p>From <i>fruma</i> (“first, original”) +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“wright”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frēodōm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fyrhtu</i>: fright</li></ol><p>A metathetical form of <i>fyrhtu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beginning, origin</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frumiþō</i>. Equivalent to <i>fruma</i> +&lrm; <i>-þ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frymþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Frīsa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of waterbird (e.g. a bittern or kite)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Perhaps from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fruskijō</i> (“frog-catcher, frog-eater”, literally “frogger”), related to <i>frosċ</i> (“frog”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Frēslond</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>friþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frec</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>saucy, cunning, crafty, wanton</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cunning, guile, craftiness</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frafal(i)</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frafalu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fræfel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be cunning or deceptive</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frafalōn</i>, variant of <i>&#42;frafalijan</i>, <i>&#42;frafalēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>cunningly, craftily</li><li>saucily, wantonly</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;frafalilīkō</i>, equivalent to <i>fræfel</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fræfellīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>question</li><li>inquiry, interrogation</li></ol><p>Related to the verb <i>frignan</i> (“to ask”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fregnan</i>. Compare Old High German <i>frāgan</i> and <i>frāga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>friġnan</i> (“to ask”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asking, enquiry, questioning, interrogation</li></ol><p>From <i>fræġn</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fremsum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frætwian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ornament, jewelry</li><li>treasure</li><li>arms; armour</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fratwu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fratwō</i> (“ornament”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>frataha</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>frætwian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasures, ornaments</li></ol><p>Possibly contracted from <i>for-</i> + <i>geatwe</i>, a metathetical variant of <i>getawa</i>, with a sense of treasure being made and prepared. From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fratawō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dewh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decorate, adorn, embellish</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fratwōn</i>, equivalent to <i>frætu</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adornment, ornament</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>frætwian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fugol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fugolcynn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fugolcynn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fuglere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fowler, bird catcher</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fuglārī</i>, equivalent to <i>fugol</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>. Cognate with Middle Low German <i>vōgelære</i>, Old High German <i>fogalāri</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fulstow (a village and civil parish in East Lindsey district, Lincolnshire, England)</li></ol><p>Recorded in the Domesday Book as Fugelestou. From <i>fugol</i> (“bird”) +&lrm; <i>stōw</i> (“place”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch birds</li></ol><p>From <i>fugol</i> (“bird”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fuglere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bird</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fugl</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fuglaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>fugel</i>, Old Saxon <i>fugal</i>, Old Dutch <i>fogal</i>, Old High German <i>fogal</i>, Old Norse <i>fugl</i>, Gothic <i>𐍆𐌿𐌲𐌻𐍃</i> (fugls).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fugolcynn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fugolcynn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birdkind</li></ol><p>From <i>fugol</i> (“bird”) +&lrm; <i>cynn</i> (“kind, kin, race”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birdlime</li></ol><p>From <i>fugol</i> (“bird”) +&lrm; <i>līm</i> (“lime”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birdcatching, fowling</li></ol><p>From <i>fuglian</i> +&lrm; <i>-oþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fugol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moist; damp</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fų̄ht</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;funhtijaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>full</i></li><li>foul (dirty, stinking, vile, corrupt)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>very, well</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;full</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fūl</i>.</p><p>Possibly from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;full</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>full-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>born on time</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>full-</i> + <i>(ġe)boren</i>, literally “full born.”</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do fully, complete, perform, arrange</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fulladōn</i>, equivalent to <i>ful-</i> +&lrm; <i>dōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black alder</li></ol><p>From <i>fūl</i> (“foul”) +&lrm; <i>trēow</i> (“tree”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfect</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) perfect tense</li><li>fully trained</li><li>complete</li></ol><p>Past participle of <i>fulfremman</i> (“to perfect”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perfection</li></ol><p>From <i>fulfremed</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fulfremednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to perfect</li><li>to fulfill</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ful-</i> +&lrm; <i>fremman</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very abundant</li><li>quite enough</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ful-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġenihtsum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fulwiht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to foul</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fūlēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full</li><li>filled</li><li>complete</li><li>entire</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a beaker</li><li>a cup, especially one with liquor in it</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;full</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fullaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pl̥h₁nós</i> (“full”), from <i>&#42;pleh₁-</i> (“to fill”).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fullą</i> (“vessel”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pēl(w)-</i> (“a kind of vessel”). Akin to Old Saxon <i>full</i> (“beaker”), Old Norse <i>full</i> (“beaker, toast”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>full moon</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fullaz mēnô</i>. Cognate with West Frisian <i>folmoanne</i>, Low German <i>Vullmaand</i>, Dutch <i>volle maan</i>, German <i>Vollmond</i>, and Swedish <i>fullmåne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>full, fully, completely, entirely</li><li>very</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;full-</i>, <i>&#42;fulla-</i> (“fully, completely”), related to Old English <i>full</i> (“full, complete”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fulham (location in England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Fullanhām</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fuller (person who fulls cloth)</li><li>bleacher</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>fullō</i> (“fuller”) + <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fulfremed</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fulfill, accomplish</li></ol><p>From <i>full-</i> +&lrm; <i>fyllan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fulfill, perfect</li><li>alternative form of <i>fulwian</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fullōn</i>, equivalent to <i>full</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p><p>From <i>full-</i> + <i>&#42;wīhan</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wīhijan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fully, perfectly, completely, abundantly</li><li>in a full manner or measure, thoroughly, without reservation or qualification</li></ol><p>From <i>full</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fulwian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fulwiht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fulwiht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fulwiht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to help, aid, support</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ful-</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣstan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>fullēstian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>almost</li></ol><p><i>full</i> +&lrm; <i>nēah</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foulness; impurity; stench</li></ol><p>From from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fūlnassī</i>, equivalent to <i>fūl</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>fūlnussī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fully quickly, immediately</li></ol><p><i>ful-</i> +&lrm; <i>hræþe</i> (“quickly”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fultumian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fultum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>help, support; protection, reinforcements</li><li>assistance</li><li>army, forces</li></ol><p>From <i>ful-</i> +&lrm; <i>tēam</i> (“team”). Compare Old High German <i>follaziohan</i> (“to help, be at hand, support”), Middle Low German <i>vultên</i> (“to carry out, perform”), Dutch <i>voltooien</i> (“to complete, finish”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fultumian</i></li></ol><p><i>fultum</i> (“aid, assistance”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>helper, assistant</li><li>co-operator</li></ol><p><i>fultum</i> (“help, assistance”) +&lrm; <i>-end</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to prop up, support; to help, assist</li></ol><p><i>fultum</i> (“aid, assistance”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assistant</li><li>helper, co-operator</li></ol><p>From fultumian and fultuman</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without help; helpless</li></ol><p>From <i>fultum</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fuller, waulker</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Either derived from <i>fulwian</i>, a variant of <i>fullian</i> (“to full (cloth)”), equivalent to <i>fulwian</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>. Alternatively, perhaps a direct borrowing of Latin <i>fullō</i> (“fuller”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to baptize</li></ol><p>From <i>full-</i> + <i>&#42;wīhan</i> (later <i>&#42;wēon</i>), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wīhijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīhijaną</i>. Compare <i>fulwiht</i> (“baptism”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baptism</li></ol><p>An old compound from <i>fulwian</i>, <i>fulliġan</i> (“to baptize”) +&lrm; <i>-þ</i>, as if Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fullawīhiþu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fulwihtian</i></li></ol><p><i>fulwiht</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) baptizer (one who baptizes&#59; used to refer to John the Baptist)</li></ol><p>From <i>fulwiht</i> (“baptism”) +&lrm; <i>fæder</i> (“father”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to baptize</li></ol><p><i>fulwiht</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a full fine</li></ol><p>From <i>ful-</i> (“full”) +&lrm; <i>wīte</i> (“fine, punishment”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fulwiht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>fundaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set out, depart, direct one's course to</li><li>to hurry towards, be eager for</li><li>to endeavour to, strive to attain, attempt, try</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fundōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>furrow</li><li>fir, pine</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;furh</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furhs</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;perḱ-</i> (“to dig”). Cognate with Old High German <i>furh</i> and Old Norse <i>for</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;furh</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furhs</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pérk-u-s ~ &#42;pr̥kʷ-éw-s</i> (“oak”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>fura</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firwood</li><li>a firtree</li></ol><p>From <i>furh</i> +&lrm; <i>wudu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>furlong</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>furh</i> +&lrm; <i>lang</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>furþum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>further, more, forwards</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>forþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-or</i> (comparative suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>even (marking an extreme example)</li><li>just, recently</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>furþor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hastening, progress</li><li>departure (especially from the world, i.e. in death)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ready, eager, striving forward, inclined to, willing, prompt</li><li>expectant, brave, noble: ready to depart, die; dying</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;funs</i> (“ready, willing”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>fūs</i>, Old High German <i>funs</i>, Old Norse <i>fúss</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dirge, song of death</li></ol><p>From <i>fūs</i> (“progress, departure, death”) +&lrm; <i>lēoþ</i> (“song”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>readily, promptly, gladly</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>fūs</i> (“ready, easer”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fīftīene</i> (“fifteen”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>feoht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to moisten</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;funhtijaną</i>, from <i>&#42;funhtijaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fiell</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defile, pollute, befoul</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fūlijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to collect as an army</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fulkijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fulkijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;Late Old English) to arrange troops</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>(ġe)fylċe</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>; either a new formation from these bases, a loan from Old Norse <i>fylkja</i>, or a modification of earlier <i>fylċan</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fulkijan</i> (compare Old High German <i>fulken</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>field</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fieldstōl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fieldstōl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>folgian</i> (“to follow”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fulgijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fulgijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fylġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fylġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fylġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fylġean</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fylġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fill</li><li>alternative form of <i>fiellan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fullijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fyllu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one's fill, fulness of food or drink</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fullī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fullį̄</i>, from <i>&#42;fullaz</i> (“full”). Cognate with Old High German <i>fulli</i> (German <i>Fülle</i>), Old Norse <i>fyllr</i> (Swedish <i>fylle</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fūlnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to help, give aid, protect</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fȳlþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>filth</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) moral impurity, foul practice</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fūliþu</i>. Equivalent to <i>fūl</i> +&lrm; <i>-þu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>invention, device</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mold</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become moldy, musty</li></ol><p>From <i>fyniġ</i> (“moldy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moldy, damp, musty</li></ol><p><i>fyne</i> (“mold”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fire</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fierr</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fuir</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fōr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;péh₂wr̥</i>.</p><p>Comparative of <i>feor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>for-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to castrate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;p(i)eh₂w</i> (“to beat, hit”), and cognate with Lithuanian <i>pjáuti</i> (“to cut, reap”), Latin <i>paviō</i> (“to beat, strike”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flash, flicker</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly a metathetic variant of <i>flicerian</i> (“to flicker”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fierd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fierdfæreld</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) war-gear</li></ol><p>From <i>fyrd</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeatewa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an army, host</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>fierd</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythical creature</i>) firedragon, firedrake</li></ol><p>From <i>fȳr</i> (“fire”) +&lrm; <i>draca</i> (“dragon”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) warrior, soldier</li></ol><p>From <i>fyrd</i> +&lrm; <i>rinc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) armour, war equipment</li></ol><p>From <i>fyrd</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>searu</i> (“gear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fierdwīte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fiery</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>firen</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fuirīn</i>. Equivalent to <i>fȳr</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;firinō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>firenþearfe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>firenþearf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>firenþearfe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>first</li><li>front</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;furist</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furistaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fiergen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fiergengāt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fire-spark</li></ol><p>From <i>fȳr</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;gnāst</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>temperature</i>) hot as fire</li></ol><p>From <i>fȳr</i> (“fire”) +&lrm; <i>hāt</i> (“hot”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frighten, terrify</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;furhtijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furhtijaną</i>, equivalent to <i>forht</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fright</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;furhtī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furhtį̄</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a firehouse; furnace</li></ol><p>From <i>fȳr</i> +&lrm; <i>hūs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forest, wooded country</li><li>game preserve, hunting ground</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;furhiþ</i>&nbsp;n, <i>&#42;furhiþi</i>&nbsp;f (“forest, wooded country”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furhō</i>, <i>&#42;furahō</i> (“fir, pine”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;perkos</i> (“oak”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forest, wooded country</li><li>game preserve, hunting ground</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;furhiþi</i> (“forest, woodland”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furhiþją</i> (“forest, wooded country”), <i>&#42;furhiþǭ</i>, from <i>&#42;furhu</i> (“fir; pine”), from <i>&#42;furahō</i>, <i>&#42;furhō</i> (“fir; pine; (fir or pine) forest”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pérkus</i> (“oak”), from <i>&#42;perkʷ-</i> (“oak”). Both Proto-Germanic etyma are preserved in Old English.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a fire, give warmth, to cherish</li></ol><p>From <i>fȳr</i> (“fire”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>fioria</i> (“to light a fire”), Old High German <i>fiuren</i> (“to ignite, set on fire”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>far away, distant</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>feorm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>formest</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>formest</i></li></ol><p><i>i</i>-umlauted form of <i>formest</i>, likely from an intermediate form <i>frumist</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fiermþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>former</li><li>ancient</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;firnijaz</i>, <i>&#42;fernaz</i> (“foregoing, previous”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;per-</i> (“forth, forward, across, through”). Cognate with Old High German <i>firni</i> (“old”), Old Norse <i>forn</i> (“old”), Gothic <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌽𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (fairneis, “old”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an ancient conflict</li></ol><p>From <i>fyrn</i> (“ancient”) +&lrm; <i>ġeflit</i> (“fight, argument, competition”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) the ocean</li></ol><p>From <i>fyrn</i> (“ancient”) +&lrm; <i>strēamas</i> (“streams”), literally “ancient streams”</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an ancient creation</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>fyrn</i> (“ancient”) +&lrm; <i>weorc</i> (“work, creation”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>furze, gorse</li><li>buckthorn</li></ol><p>Uncertain; thought to be related to Ancient Greek <i>πῡρός</i> (pūrós, “wheat”) via Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;puHrós</i> or similar, and/or to <i>fȳr</i> (“fire”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smarting like fire; feeling or producing a burning pain</li></ol><p>Either the present participle of an otherwise unattested verb <i>fȳrsmeortan</i> ("to smart like fire; to feel a burning pain"), from <i>fȳr</i> (“fire”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;smeortan</i> (“to smart; hurt”), or formed directly from the present participle of <i>&#42;smeortan</i>, i.e. from <i>fȳr</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;smeortende</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fierst</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fyrest</i></li></ol><p>See <i>fierst</i>.</p><p>See <i>fyrest</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firestone, flint, pyrite</li></ol><p>From <i>fȳr</i> (“fire”) +&lrm; <i>stān</i> (“stone”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frosty</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fryst</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fȳrtorr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lighthouse</li></ol><p>From <i>fȳr</i> (“fire”) +&lrm; <i>torr</i> (“tower”). Compare West Frisian <i>fjoertoer</i>, Dutch <i>vuurtoren</i>, Swedish <i>fyrtorn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>firwitt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>firwitġeorn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>firwitġeornnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>firwitt</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>firwitt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oven, frying pan</li></ol><p>From <i>fȳr</i> +&lrm; <i>þolle</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hasten, make haste, prepare oneself</li><li>to send forth, drive away, impel, put to flight, banish</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;funsijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;funsijaną</i> (“to make favourable, make ready”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pent-</i> (“to send forth, drive away, banish, rush, strive”), related to Old English <i>fūs</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>fūsian</i> (“to incline, strive”), Old Norse <i>fýsa</i> (“to urge, compel, incite, provoke, exhort”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fȳsan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the fist</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fūsti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;funstiz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pnsti-</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;pnksti-</i>, <i>&#42;pn̥kʷsti</i> (“fist”), a derivative of <i>&#42;pénkʷe</i> (“five”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>fest</i> (West Frisian <i>fest</i>), Old Saxon <i>fūst</i>, Dutch <i>vuist</i>, Old High German <i>fūst</i> (German <i>Faust</i>), and with Russian <i>пясть</i> (pjastʹ, “palm of the hand”), Latin <i>pugnus</i> (“fist”), Polish <i>pięść</i> (“fist”), Serbo-Croatian <i>pȇst</i> (“fist”) and <i>pȑst</i> (“finger”), Lithuanian <i>kumštis</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vixen (female fox)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fuhsijā</i>, perhaps dissimilated from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fuhsini</i>, ultimately a female form derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fuhsaz</i> (“fox”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fox: of or relating to foxes</li><li>made of a fox's fur or pelt</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fuhsīn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fuhsīnaz</i>, equivalent to <i>fyxe</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>fuhsīn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fiþer-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fiþerfēte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a division, space</li><li>a portion, period, or space of time; while; an interval</li><li>period of five years, lustrum</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fak</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faką</i> (“division, piece, part”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂ǵ-</i> (“to nail, fasten”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>fek</i>, Old Saxon <i>fak</i> (Dutch <i>vak</i>), Old High German <i>fah</i> (German <i>Fach</i>), Latin <i>pangō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>feċċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torch</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fakilā</i>, <i>&#42;fakulā</i>, <i>&#42;fakkulā</i>, from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;faccula</i> and/or Latin <i>facula</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deceitful; fraudulent; guileful; wicked</li><li>crafty</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>maliciously</li><li>disgracefully</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faiknijaz</i> (“wicked; deceitful”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>father</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fader</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fadēr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ph₂tḗr</i>.</p><p>Old Germanic cognates: Old Frisian <i>feder</i>, Old Saxon <i>fadar</i>, Old Dutch <i>fader</i>, Old High German <i>fater</i>, Old Norse <i>faðir</i>, Gothic <i>𐍆𐌰𐌳𐌰𐍂</i> (fadar).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>father's brother, paternal uncle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fadurjō</i> (“paternal uncle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>paternal; (<i>relational</i>) father</li><li>having the same father</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fadrīnaz</i>, equivalent to <i>fæder</i> (“father”) +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Patrimony.</li><li>Heirloom.</li></ol><p>From <i>fæder</i> (“father”) +&lrm; <i>ġestrēon</i> (“treasure, wealth”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one's father's land or estate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;faderland</i>, equivalent to <i>fæder</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i>. Cognate with Middle Low German <i>vāderlant</i>, Middle High German <i>vaterlant</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fatherless</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;faderlaus</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fadurlausaz</i>, equivalent to <i>fæder</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) father; fatherly, paternal, ancestral</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fadurlīkaz</i> (“fatherly, fatherlike”), equivalent to <i>fæder</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>a doubtful word of vague meaning; see below</i></li></ol><p><i>fæder</i> +&lrm; <i>rīċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patricide (killer of one's father)</li></ol><p>From <i>fæder</i> (“father”) +&lrm; <i>slaga</i> (“slayer, killer”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who betrays their father</li></ol><p>From <i>fæder</i> (“father”) +&lrm; <i>swica</i> (“deceiver, betrayer”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fæder</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to paint</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faihijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fey, doomed to die</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faigijaz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>feigr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>joyful, rejoicing, fain</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fagan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faganaz</i> (“glad”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peḱ-</i> (“to make glad, make attractive”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>feginn</i> (“glad”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fæġnian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beautiful</li><li>pleasant</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beauty</li><li>something beautiful</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fagr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fagraz</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>fagar</i>, Old High German <i>fagar</i>, Old Norse <i>fagr</i>, Gothic <i>𐍆𐌰𐌲𐍂𐍃</i> (fagrs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become fair or beautiful</li></ol><p>Related to fæġer.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>fæġeriaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beauty</li></ol><p>From <i>fæġer</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>fagarnessi</i> (“beauty”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fæġernes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>fæġer</i>: most beautiful</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>fæġer</i></li></ol><p><i>fæġer</i> +&lrm; <i>-ra</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be glad; celebrate; rejoice</li><li>to be delighted with; applaud</li><li>to wish for</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;faginōn</i> (“to be glad, rejoice; to make glad, gladden”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faganōną</i> (“to be glad, rejoice”), from <i>&#42;faganaz</i> (“glad”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peḱ-</i> (“joyful; pretty”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fæġerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>fæġriaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fæġerost</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>fæġere</i>: most fairly, beautifully</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feyness</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;faigiþu</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enmity, hostility, feud</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;faihiþu</i>. Equivalent to <i>fāh</i> +&lrm; <i>-þ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feud-amends; compensation for engagement in a feud or quarrel</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>fǣhþ</i> +&lrm; <i>bōt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fǣhþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enmity, hostility</li><li>feud</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;faihiþu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>faithful, trusty, good</li><li>dear, beloved</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>truly, well, pleasantly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;failijaz</i> (“true, friendly, familiar, good”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cleanse</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;failisōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fām</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to foam or froth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;faimijan</i>, derived from <i>&#42;faim</i> (whence Old English <i>fām</i>). Cognate with Old High German <i>feimen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foaming</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>virgin</li><li>young woman</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;faimnijā</i> (“girl”), related to (but not directly cognate with) Old Saxon <i>fēmea</i>, <i>fēhmia</i>, West Frisian <i>faam</i>, Icelandic <i>feima</i> (“maiden”). Unrelated to Latin <i>fēmina</i> (“female, woman”), and probably also to Latin <i>famula</i> (“female servant”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>virginity</li></ol><p>From <i>fǣmne</i> +&lrm; <i>-hād</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>virgin, virginal</li></ol><p>From <i>fǣmne</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fenȳċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sudden danger, calamity</li><li>sudden attack; ambush; a blitz</li><li>way, journey, passage, expedition; highway</li><li>proceedings, way of life</li><li>movable possessions; means of subsistence</li><li>ark, ship</li><li>aspect</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fērō</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>vār</i> (“ambush”) (Dutch <i>gevaar</i> (“danger”)), Old High German <i>fāra</i> (“ambush, danger, deceit”) (German <i>Gefahr</i> (“danger”)).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;far</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;farą</i>, from the same source as Old English <i>faran</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>far</i> (“harbour, carting station”), Old Norse <i>far</i> (“pathway, vehicle, ship”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to terrify, to frighten</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fārijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>fær bū</i></li><li>(<i>erroneous</i>) hue, color</li></ol><p>This is a ghost word resulting from a misinterpretation of two words, fær (“movement, demeanour”) and bū (“both”), as one; the origin of which appears to be Samuel Fox's <i>King Alfred's Anglo-Saxon version of Boethius</i>, which Bosworth & Toller's <i>An Anglo-Saxon Dictionary</i> then cited. The sequence appears in the poetic verse "... habbað blīoh and fær bū unġelīċe" ("... (they) have appearance and demeanor, both unlike"), which would be metrically deficient, per the rules of Old English Meter, if <i>bū</i> were not part of the off verse and stressed such that it can alliterate with <i>blīoh</i>. In any case, <i>færbu</i> could not be the reflex, by regular sound change, of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;farwō</i>, whence German <i>Farbe</i>, the latter of which seems to be the basis of this misinterpretation.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sudden death</li></ol><p><i>fǣr</i> (“sudden”) +&lrm; <i>dēaþ</i> (“death”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>going, such as walking, or the ability thereof; motion</li><li>course, journey, expedition</li><li>way, road, passage</li><li>track of an animal</li><li>means of transportation</li><li>the people on a journey or a road, company; a partner in life, a relation</li><li>way of life, conduct</li><li>progress</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Passover</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>fær</i> +&lrm; <i>-eld</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>itinerary</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>færeld</i> +&lrm; <i>bōc</i>, literally “travel book.”</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Passover feast</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>færeld</i> +&lrm; <i>frēols</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>færeld</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sudden attack, seizure</li></ol><p>From <i>fǣr</i> (“sudden attack”) +&lrm; <i>gripe</i> (“grip, seizure”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) sudden horror, danger, terror</li></ol><p><i>fǣr</i> +&lrm; <i>gryre</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>suddenly, by chance</li></ol><p><i>fǣr</i> +&lrm; <i>-inga</i> (adverbial suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sudden</li></ol><p><i>fǣr</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>suddenly</li></ol><p><i>fǣrlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>feorm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fērrǣden</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cartroad</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;faraweg</i>, equivalent to <i>fær</i> +&lrm; <i>weġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fringe</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fas</i>, <i>&#42;fasō</i>. Akin to Old High German <i>faso</i>, Middle Dutch <i>vase</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offspring, progeny</li></ol><p>Cognate with Old Norse <i>fǫsull</i>, Old High German <i>fasal</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fas(u)laz</i>, probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pes-</i> (“penis”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fæs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>firm, stable; constant</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fastu</i>, <i>&#42;fast</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fastuz</i>; see it for cognates and further etymology.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fasten, make fast or firm, entrust, commit, commend</li><li>to fast (abstain from food)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fastijaną</i> (“to fasten”), <i>&#42;fastāną</i> (“to fast”), derived from <i>&#42;fastuz</i> (“fast, fixed, firm”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>fæst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>firmly, fastly, tightly</li></ol><p>From <i>fæst</i> (“firm”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i> (“-ly”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fortified place: stronghold, fortress, castle; a place of strength; wall, bulwark</li><li>an inclosed place, cloister</li><li>fasting, a fast</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fastuni</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fastunją</i>. Equivalent to <i>fæst</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a breach of a fast, fast-breaking, breakfast</li></ol><p>From <i>fæsten</i> +&lrm; <i>bryċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>fæst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>holding tightly</li><li>tight-fisted, niggardly, miserly</li></ol><p>From <i>fæst</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;hafol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>steadfast in mind</li></ol><p>From <i>fæst</i> +&lrm; <i>hyġdiġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fortified land, land that is difficult to attack</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>fæst</i> (“strong, firm”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fæstlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>firmly, constantly, fast, fast in hold; fastly</li><li>fast, quickly, speedily, at once</li></ol><p>From <i>fæst</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>fastlīhho</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>fæstlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>fæstlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fastness, firmness, firmament, fortification, stability</li><li>tenacity, vigour, resolution</li></ol><p>From <i>fæst</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fasten or fix; to establish</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fastinōn</i>. Equivalent to <i>fæst</i> +&lrm; <i>-nian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fæstnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fastening</li><li>ratification of something, especially a marital engagement</li></ol><p>From <i>fæstnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fæstnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>fæst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>container, vessel; especially a box, jar or cup</li><li>a thin plate</li><li>golden ornament</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fat</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fatą</i>.</p><p><i>Bosworth-Toller's Anglo-Saxon Dictionary</i> has this entry but does not provide an etymology. This word is also found in compounds such as <i>fætgold</i> (in Bosworth-Toller) and <i>fætedsinc</i> (in Bosworth-Toller).</p><p><i>Altenglisches Etymologisches Wörterbuch</i> has <i>geschlagenes Metall, Goldschmuck</i> ("beaten metal, golden ornament") as the definition for the word <i>fǣt</i> with a long æ instead. It etymologically associates this word with Old English <i>fǣtan</i> ("to forge, beat or work metal").</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bag</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fatness</li></ol><p><i>fǣtt</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fǣtnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fǣtnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fat</li><li>fattened</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;faitid</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>fǣtt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>fǣtt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>feax</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>fæþmian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the outstretched arms; embrace, bosom</li><li>envelopment, grasp, control, power</li><li>fathom (measurement); cubit</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;faþm</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>faþmos</i>; Dutch <i>vadem</i>, <i>vaam</i>; Old High German <i>fadum</i> (German <i>Faden</i>), Old Norse <i>faðmr</i> (Icelandic <i>faðmur</i>, Danish <i>favn</i>, Swedish <i>famn</i>). The Proto-Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek <i>πετάννυμι</i> (petánnumi) and Latin <i>pateō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>fæþm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fathom, to measure with outstretched arms</li><li>to embrace, to hug, to envelope, to devour</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faþmōną</i>, equivalent to <i>fæþm</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>fēdan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fōdijaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂-</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>fōdian</i>, Dutch <i>voeden</i>, Old High German <i>fuoten</i>, Old Norse <i>fǿða</i> (Danish <i>føde</i>, Swedish <i>föda</i>, Icelandic <i>fæða</i>), Gothic <i>𐍆𐍉𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (fōdjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Anglian</i>) alternative form of <i>fēra</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>gād</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Northumbrian</i>) alternative form of <i>gāst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scoff, mock, jest</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gabbōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gabbōną</i> (“to mock, jest”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰeh₁bʰ-</i> (“to be split, be forked, gape”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Hebrew</li><li>The angel Gabriel</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Gabrihel</i>, <i>Gabriel</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Γαβρῑήλ</i> (Gabrīḗl), from Hebrew <i>גַּבְרִיאֵל</i> (gaḇrīʾḗl, “God is my strong man”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack, want</li><li>desire, need</li><li>point, tip</li><li>spearhead or arrowhead</li><li>spike, goad</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaidu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaidwą</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaidu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaidō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring together, gather</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gadurōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a gathering, assembly, joining, council, congregation, crowd</li><li>a gathering of herbs</li><li>a gathering of words, a text</li></ol><p>From <i>gaderian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>geador</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gaderian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gaderian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gaderian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>base, vile, lewd</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gafol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gafolian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mockery, scoffing</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;gafettan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gafettung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gafettung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fork</li><li>spar</li><li>lever, pole used for leverage</li><li>gallows (in plural)</li><li>tax</li><li>rent</li><li>tribute</li><li>interest</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gabulu</i> (“fork”), from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;gablā</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gabulą</i> (“tribute”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰebʰ-</i> (“to give”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>gǫfga</i> (“to worship, offer”), Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌱𐌴𐌹</i> (gabei, “riches”), Latin <i>habeō</i> (“to own, possess, have”). More at give.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Gafulford</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taxpayer</li><li>renter, tenant</li><li>tributary</li><li>debtor</li></ol><p><i>gafol</i> +&lrm; <i>ġielda</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rent</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>gafol</i> (“rent”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a line; rod</li><li>a pair of compasses; a staff compass</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from <i>gafol</i> +&lrm; <i>rand</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tax, tribute</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>gafol</i> +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buffoonery</li></ol><p>From <i>gaf</i> (“lewd, vile, base”) +&lrm; <i>sprǣċ</i> (“speech”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gafol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A location in England, site of a battle in 823 C.E. It has been identified with Camelford, though this is disputed.</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>gaful</i> (“tax, rent”) +&lrm; <i>ford</i> (“ford”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gafolrǣden</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gale, sweet gale (<i>Myrica gale</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gagulaz</i> (“gale; sweet-willow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gagel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gagel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lascivious, wanton</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gagel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wanton, lustful; wicked</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gail</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gēl</i>, Dutch <i>geil</i> (“salacious, lustful”), Old High German <i>geil</i> (German <i>geil</i> (“lustful”)), Old Norse <i>geiligr</i> (“beautiful”). The Indo-European root may also be the source of Lithuanian <i>gailùs</i> (“sharp, biting”), Russian <i>зело́</i> (zeló, “very”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing; enchant</li><li>to cry, call, scream</li><li>to cast spells, practice sorcery, sing charms</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;galan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;galaną</i> (“to sing”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġealdor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wizard</li></ol><p>From <i>galdor</i> (“enchantment”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i> (“agent suffix”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lustful, lascivious</li></ol><p>From <i>gāl</i> +&lrm; <i>-full</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġealga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġealgtrēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Galilean</li></ol><p>Latin <i>Galilēa</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Gauls</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gaulish</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comfrey (genus <i>Symphytum</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lust, lustfulness, wantonness</li></ol><p>From <i>gāl</i> (“lustful”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġealpettan</i></li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;galpian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Gauls (as a people)</li><li>Gaul</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gamol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>game, sport, or pastime</li><li>joy, mirth, or pleasure</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaman</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gamaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entertain or enjoy oneself; to display happiness or joy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gamanōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gamanōną</i> (“to delight, enjoy, have fun”), equivalent to <i>gamen</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ridiculous, humorous</li><li>of or related to a game</li></ol><p>From <i>gamen</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sportingly</li><li>deceitfully</li></ol><p>From <i>gamenlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i> or <i>gamen</i> (“devil, demon”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gaming, jesting, playing</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>gamenian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) harp, musical instrument</li></ol><p>From <i>gamen</i> (“pleasure”) +&lrm; <i>wudu</i> (“wood”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gamenian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) old, ancient</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gamalaz</i> (“old, weak”). Cognate with</p><p>Danish <i>gammel</i> (“old, ancient”),</p><p>Dutch <i>gammel</i> (“shaky, worn, old”),</p><p>Swedish <i>gammal</i> (“old, ancient”),</p><p>Icelandic <i>gamall</i> (“old, ancient”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) gray-haired, hoary-haired</li></ol><p>From <i>gamol</i> (“ancient, old”) +&lrm; <i>feax</i> (“hair”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go</li><li>to walk</li><li>to enter</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gān</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gāną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰeh₁-</i> (“to leave”). The verb was defective in Germanic and may have existed only in the present tense.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Ganges (a river in India and Bangladesh)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Γάγγης</i> (Gángēs), from Sanskrit <i>गङ्गा</i> (gáṅgā, literally “swift-goer”), from the verbal root <i>गम्</i> (gam, “to go”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷem-</i> (“to come”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ganra</i> (“gander”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ganot</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>going, walking</li><li>path</li><li>walk, gait</li><li>toilet</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gang</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gangaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰengʰ-</i> (“to step; stride”). Related to Old English <i>gangan</i> (“to go, walk”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go, walk</li><li>to turn out</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gangan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>ganga</i>, Old Saxon <i>gangan</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;gangan</i>, Old High German <i>gangan</i>, Old Norse <i>ganga</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (gaggan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a ganger (goer, walker); footman</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gangārī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gangārijaz</i> (compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gangilaz</i> (“goer, walker”)), equivalent to <i>gang</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>gunger</i>, Middle Low German <i>genger</i>, Old High German <i>gangāri</i>. Compare also Old Norse <i>gangari</i>, <i>gangvari</i> (“horse”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>latrine</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>gang</i> +&lrm; <i>-ern</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a latrine</li></ol><p>From <i>gang</i> +&lrm; <i>pyt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A privy; toilet.</li></ol><p>From <i>gang</i> +&lrm; <i>setl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A stool or seat of a privy; a toilet seat</li></ol><p>From <i>gang</i> +&lrm; <i>stōl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a passageway; gangway</li><li>a thoroughfare; road</li></ol><p><i>gang</i> +&lrm; <i>weġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open, open up, open wide</li><li>to gape</li><li>to yawn</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gainōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gainōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gannet (sea-bird)</li><li>waterfowl, especially a fen-duck or swan</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ganut</i>, related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ganzô</i> (“gander”), see also Dutch <i>gent</i> (“gander”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;kenning) the sea</li></ol><p><i>ganotes</i> (“gannet's”) + <i>bæþ</i> (“bath”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gander (male goose)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ganʀō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ganzô</i> (“gander”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a yawning</li></ol><p>From <i>gānian</i> (“to yawn”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) spear, arrow, dart</li><li>the runic character ᚸ (/ɡ/)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaiʀ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaizaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰoysós</i> (“pointed stick, spear”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>gēr</i>, Old Saxon <i>gēr</i>, Old High German <i>gēr</i>, Old Norse <i>geirr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Gaiʀaberht</i>, from <i>&#42;gaiʀ</i> (“spear”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”). Equivalent to <i>gār</i> +&lrm; <i>berht</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Gairbert</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agrimony (genus <i>Agrimonia</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>gār</i> (“spear”) +&lrm; <i>clife</i> (“burdock”), literally “spear-burdock”</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) ‘Spear-Dane’, Dane</li></ol><p><i>gār</i> (“spear”) +&lrm; <i>Dene</i> (“Danes”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, alternative form of <i>Gārheard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Gaiʀafriþu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Gaizafriþuz</i>. Equivalent to <i>gār</i> (“spear”) +&lrm; <i>friþ</i> (“peace”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Gaiʀahard</i>. Equivalent to <i>gār</i> (“spear”) +&lrm; <i>heard</i> (“brave, bold”). Cognate with English <i>Gerard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garlic</li></ol><p>From <i>gār</i> (“spear”) +&lrm; <i>lēac</i> (“leek”). Compare Old Norse <i>geirlaukr</i> (“garlic”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Gaiʀamundu</i>, from <i>&#42;gaiʀ</i> (“spear”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;mundu</i> (“protection, protector”). Equivalent to <i>gār</i> +&lrm; <i>mund</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Gērmunt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ocean, sea</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Almost always analyzed as a compound of <i>gār</i> +&lrm; <i>seċġ</i>, but there is significant disagreement about how to interpret these components. <i>gār</i> is usually glossed as "spear," but the sense "tempest" has been suggested, and Holthausen dubiously connected it to Old English <i>gānian</i> (“to gape”), Sanskrit <i>विहायस्</i> (vihāyas, “atmosphere”). <i>seċġ</i> may be glossed several ways:</p><p>William Redbond suggested a possible loan from a Brythonic term equivalent to modern Welsh <i>môr</i> (“sea”) + <i>caseg</i> (“mare; billow, breaker”). Dahlman suggested analysis as <i>gārs</i> (“world's”) +&lrm; <i>eċġ</i> (“edge”), but this cannot be correct as <i>ecg</i> is a feminine jō-stem.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>gārseċġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Gārwulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Gaiʀawald</i>. Equivalent to <i>gār</i> (“spear”) +&lrm; <i>wald</i> (“mighty, powerful”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Gaiʀawulf</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Gaizawulfaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>gār</i> (“spear”) +&lrm; <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”). Cognate with Old High German <i>Gērolf</i>. Compare <i>Wulfgar</i> which has the same elements reversed.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spirit</li><li>ghost</li><li>breath</li><li>demon</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaist</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaistaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) destroyer of souls; devil, demon</li></ol><p><i>gāst</i> +&lrm; <i>bana</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a soul-bearer; living person</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>gāst</i> +&lrm; <i>berend</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) the breast</li></ol><p>From <i>gāst</i> (“spirit”) +&lrm; <i>cofa</i> (“chamber”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spiritual</li></ol><p>Equivalent of <i>gāst</i> +&lrm; <i>-cund</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) spirit-king; God</li></ol><p>From <i>gāst</i> (“spirit”) +&lrm; <i>cyning</i> (“king”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the parting between body and soul; death</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>gāst</i> +&lrm; <i>ġedāl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) thought of the spirit</li></ol><p>From <i>gāst</i> (“spirit”) +&lrm; <i>ġemynd</i> (“mind”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a spiritual mystery</li></ol><p>From <i>gāst</i> (“spirit”) +&lrm; <i>ġerȳne</i> (“mystery”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torment of the soul</li></ol><p>From <i>gāst</i> (“spirit”) +&lrm; <i>ġewinn</i> (“war, battle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a spiritual gift</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) divine grace</li></ol><p>From <i>gāst</i> +&lrm; <i>ġifu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiesthof</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gæsthūs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lifeless, dead</li></ol><p>From <i>gāst</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spiritual</li><li>holy, sacred</li></ol><p>From <i>gāst</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>spiritually</li></ol><p>From <i>gāstlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a spiritual son</li><li>(<i>theology</i>) Jesus Christ</li></ol><p>From <i>gāst</i> +&lrm; <i>sunu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goat</li><li>alternative form of <i>ġeat</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaits</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>&#42;gāt</i>, Old Saxon <i>gēt</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;geit</i>, Old High German <i>geiȥ</i>, Old Norse <i>geit</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌹𐍄𐍃</i> (gaits); and with Latin <i>haedus</i> (“kid”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a goat-path (path used by goats)</li></ol><p>From <i>gāt</i> (“goat”) +&lrm; <i>ānstīg</i> (“narrow path”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goatherd</li></ol><p><i>gāt</i> +&lrm; <i>hierde</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gāthierde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gāthierde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cádiz (a city, the provincial capital of Cádiz, Andalusia, Spain)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Gādēs</i>, from Phoenician <i>𐤂𐤃𐤓</i> (gdr /⁠gādēr, gādīr⁠/, “a walled enclosure”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cadiz</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Gades</i>, from Phoenician <i>𐤂𐤃𐤓</i> (gdr /⁠gādēr, gādīr⁠/, “a walled enclosure”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>and; often doubled as <i>ġe... ġe...</i> ("both... and...")</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jiʀ</i>, from *<i>jīz</i>, an early variation of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jūz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yúHs</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>jī</i>, Old Saxon <i>gi</i>, Dutch <i>gij</i>, Old High German <i>ir</i> (German <i>ihr</i>), Old Norse <i>ér</i> (Swedish <i>I</i>, later <i>ni</i>), Gothic <i>𐌾𐌿𐍃</i> (jus). The Indo-European root is also the source of Albanian <i>ju</i>, Proto-Baltic <i>&#42;ju-</i> (Lithuanian <i>jūs</i>), Tocharian A <i>yas</i>, Tocharian B <i>yes</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jahw</i>, from <i>&#42;ja</i> + <i>&#42;-hw</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>used as an intensifier for verbs, indicating completeness or perfection</li><li>forms nouns or adjectives of association or similarity; co-</li><li>forms nouns and verbs with the sense of "result" or "process"</li><li>forms past participles or participle adjectives from verbs</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ga-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ga-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱóm</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>cum</i> (“with”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>yes, yeah, yea</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ja</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gafol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cuckoo</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gauk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaukaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wood sorrel</li></ol><p>From <i>ġēaces</i> (“cuckoo's”) +&lrm; <i>sūre</i> (“sorrel”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>together, in a united manner</li><li>altogether</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gadur</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰedʰ-</i>. Cognate with Middle High German <i>gater</i> (“together”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gaderung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gædeling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gafol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) jaws</li></ol><p>Uncertain. The form suggests derivation from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gabl</i> (“jaw”), yet no known cognates in other Germanic languages exist outside of West Frisian <i>gaffel</i> (“gape, jaw, bevel”). Alternatively, perhaps a dissimilation or blend of Old English <i>ġeagl</i> (“jaw, jowl”) and <i>ċeafl</i> (“jaw”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gafol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) jaw, jowl</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gagol</i> (“light, frolicsome”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gagul</i> (“jaw, gape, yaw”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to own, possess</li><li>to secure ownership of, claim as one's own</li><li>to adopt (a child)</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>āgnian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>owning, possessive</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) possessive, genitive</li></ol><p>From <i>ġeāgnian</i> (“to own, possess”) +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeāgniendlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jowl, jaw</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spell, charm; incantation</li><li>magic</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;galdr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the art of magic, incantation, enchanting</li></ol><p>From <i>ġealdor</i> +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a magic saying</li></ol><p>From <i>ġealdor</i> (“spell, magic, incantation”) +&lrm; <i>cwide</i> (“saying”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a magician, enchanter</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġealdor</i> (“spell, magic, incantation”) +&lrm; <i>galan</i> (“to sing, enchant”) +&lrm; <i>-end</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magician, soothsayer</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġealdor</i> (“spell, magic, incantation”) +&lrm; <i>galan</i> (“to enchant, sing”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a magic song, enchantment, charm, spell</li></ol><p>From <i>ġealdor</i> (“spell, magic, incantation”) +&lrm; <i>lēoþ</i> (“poem, song”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a magic incantation</li></ol><p>From <i>ġealdor</i> (“spell, magic, incantation”) +&lrm; <i>sang</i> (“song”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a magic word</li></ol><p>From <i>ġealdor</i> (“spell, magic, incantation”) +&lrm; <i>word</i> (“word”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gealh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gallows; a gibbet or cross</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;galgō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;galgô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰalgʰ-</i>, <i>&#42;ǵʰalg-</i> (“long switch, rod, shaft, pole, perch”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>galga</i> (West Frisian <i>galge</i>), Old Saxon <i>galgo</i>, Dutch <i>galg</i>, Old High German <i>galgo</i> (German <i>Galgen</i>), Old Norse <i>galgi</i>, <i>gálgi</i> (Swedish and Danish <i>galge</i>), Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌲𐌰</i> (galga).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gloomy, sad, furious</li></ol><p>From <i>ġealh</i> (“sullen, sad”) +&lrm; <i>-mōd</i> (“minded”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a gallows tree, cross</li></ol><p>From <i>ġealga</i> +&lrm; <i>trēow</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sullen, morose, ill-tempered, sad</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gallag</i>, <i>&#42;gallīg</i> (“gally, bilious, bitter”). Doublet of <i>ġealliġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gall bladder</li><li>gall; bile</li><li>a skin sore</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gallā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gallǭ</i> (“bile; gall”). Cognate with Old High German <i>galla</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gallō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gallô</i> (“infirmity, weakness”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>galli</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full of gall; bitter, bilious</li><li>bitter; sad</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gallag</i>, <i>&#42;gallīg</i>, equivalent to <i>ġealla</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to talk or speak noisily; chatter; boast</li><li>to devour; eat greedily</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;ġealpian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>. The attested form <i>galpettað</i> shows the non-West Saxon feature of backing rather than breaking of original *æ before l + consonant.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noisy, boastful talking</li></ol><p>From <i>ġealpettan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>. The suffix <i>-ettan</i> sporadically caused i-mutation: the attested form <i>gælpettunga</i> is assumed to be a Mercian dialect form with i-mutation, which would correspond to West Saxon <i>&#42;ġielpettunga</i>. The normalized form <i>&#42;ġealpettung</i> represents a West Saxon form without i-mutation, which would correspond to Mercian <i>&#42;galpettung</i> (compare <i>galpettan</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>again</li></ol><p>A variant of <i>ġeġn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeġn-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meeting, coming against</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġeān</i> +&lrm; <i>ċierr</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a return</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġēan-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċyrr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeānod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to vex, to afflict</li><li>to make anxious or uneasy</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;angsumian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn again, turn back, return</li></ol><p>From <i>ġēan-</i> +&lrm; <i>hweorfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unite</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaainōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaainōną</i> (“to unite, make as one”), equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>ān</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>giēnōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make one, join, unite</li></ol><p>From <i>ge-</i> +&lrm; <i>ān</i> +&lrm; <i>-lǣċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made one, united</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in the way; so as to meet</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;ġēanian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>directly</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġēan</i> (“again”) +&lrm; <i>-unga</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crooked, bent, curved</li><li>symbolically crooked; devious, cunning</li><li>broad, open, spacious</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an expanse, room, or space</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaup</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaupaz</i>, related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaupnō-</i> (“hollow (of the hand)”), probably related to <i>&#42;geupaną</i> (“to be hollow”), from Pre-Germanic <i>&#42;geuppan-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰewb(ʰ)-</i> (“to bend, stoop, move”) (Lithuanian <i>gaubti</i> (“to vault, cover”), Albanian <i>gaboj</i>).</p><p>Probably related to <i>sense 1</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>gaupn</i> (“cupped hands”), <i>geypna</i> (“to encompass”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġēap</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crafty, cunning, deceitful</li></ol><p><i>ġēap</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>deceitfully</li><li>boldly</li></ol><p>From <i>ġēaplīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i> or <i>ġēap</i> (“devious, cunning”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ġeap</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>year</li><li>age, years old (+plural genitive)</li><li>(good) harvest</li><li>the runic character ᛄ (/j/)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jār</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jērą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yeh₁r-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>long ago</li></ol><p>Unknown: perhaps ultimately the same as a genitive plural of <i>ġēar</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ready, active, quick</li></ol><p>Compare <i>ġearcian</i> (“to make ready”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare, supply, make ready</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;garwakōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garwakōną</i> (“to prepare, supply”), equivalent to <i>ġearu</i> +&lrm; <i>-cian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a preparation, preparing</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġearcian</i> (“to make ready”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consul</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ġēar</i> (“year”) +&lrm; <i>cyning</i> (“king”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enclosure (an enclosed place)</li><li>yard, garden</li><li>dwelling, court</li><li>region, land</li><li>rod, staff</li><li>stake</li><li>bundle of sticks, faggot</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gard</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gardaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰórdʰos</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaʀd</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gazdaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ancient times, days of yore</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jārō dag</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jērǫ̂ dagaz</i>, equivalent to <i>ġeāra</i> (“yore”) +&lrm; <i>dagas</i> (“days”). Cognate with Old High German <i>jārtaga</i>, Old Norse <i>árdagr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġearwe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an annual day; anniversary</li><li>New Year's Day</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jāras dag</i>, equivalent to <i>ġēares</i> +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a commemoration on the anniversary of one's death</li></ol><p>From <i>ġear</i> (“year”) +&lrm; <i>ġemynd</i> (“memory”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a number, count of years</li><li>numbering by years</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġear</i> +&lrm; <i>ġerīm</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>annual, yearly</li><li>yearlong</li><li>relating to a year</li></ol><p>From <i>ġēar</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>yearly</li></ol><p><i>ġēar</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yarn</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;garn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰórnom</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰerH-</i> (“tharm, gut, intestine”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>garn</i>, Old High German <i>garn</i>, Old Norse <i>garn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeornful</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ready</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;garu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garwaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ready, prepared (<i>+ dative for something</i>)</li><li>complete, finished</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġearwe</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;garu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garwaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yarrow</li><li>gear, clothing, attire, arms, armour</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>entirely, well, enough</li><li>(<i>with verbs of knowing</i>) certainly, clearly</li><li>ready</li><li>already</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;garwā</i>, perhaps derived from <i>&#42;garu</i> (“prepared, ready (of food”)), as the plant was used medicinally for digestion.</p><p>Related to <i>gearu</i> (“ready, able”); both from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garwaz</i> (“prepared”). See also Old Norse <i>gǫrr</i> (“accomplished, skilled”).</p><p>Of North Germanic origin, probably Old Norse <i>gervi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare <i>(+dative for something)</i></li><li>to make ready</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;garwōn</i>, a byform of <i>&#42;garwijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garwijaną</i> (“to prepare”), from noun <i>&#42;garwaz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>garwian</i>, <i>gerwian</i>, Old High German <i>garawen</i> (“to prepare”) (German <i>gerben</i>), Old Norse <i>gera</i> (“to do, to make”). English <i>gar</i> (“to make, to cause to happen”) stems from the Old Norse verb instead of Old English. Related to <i>ġearu</i> (“ready, prepared”), also possibly related to noun <i>ġearwe</i> (“gear, clothing, attire, arms, armour”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ġearwe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġearu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preparation</li></ol><p>From <i>ġearwian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to discover, to find out, to learn by asking</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>āscian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gat</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gatą</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>jet</i>, Old Saxon <i>gat</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;gat</i>, Old Norse <i>gat</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Geat)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Geat</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Gaut</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gautaz</i> (“Geat”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grant; affirm; assent to</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jaattjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jahatjaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġēa</i> +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Geats</li><li>Jutes</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gautaz</i>, cognate with Old Norse <i>gautar</i>. Further etymology is unknown.</p><p>Compare Latin Jutae,</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeatwa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Geatish; of or relating to the Geats</li></ol><p>From <i>Ġēatas</i> (“Geats”) +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;poetic) ‘Geat-men’, the Geats</li></ol><p><i>Ġēat</i> (“Geat”) +&lrm; <i>meċġas</i> (“men”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) splendid, adorned</li></ol><p>From <i>ġeatwan</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. See <i>ġeatwa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>equipment</li><li>trappings</li></ol><p>Possibly a metathetical form of <i>ġetawa</i> (“instrument”) from <i>ġe-</i> + <i>tawian</i>, with a sense of equipment being made and prepared. Identical in formation to <i>frætwe</i> (“treasure, ornaments”). More at taw and tool.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) alternative form of <i>ġeatwa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gaderung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ridicule, mockery; foolishness, lightmindedness</li><li>luxury, extravagance</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gauþu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gauþō</i> (“mockery”, literally “barkings”), derived from the plural of <i>&#42;gauþą</i> (“barking”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>gauð</i> (“barking, scolding”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġebann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>edict, decree, proclamation, summons</li></ol><p>See <i>bannan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to order, command</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>bannan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expect</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>bāsnian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to color crimson or purple</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>basu</i> (“crimson, purple”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bathe, wash</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>baþian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>bīecnan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crown</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>bēagian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bearded</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prayer, religious observance</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gabed</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġebed</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>someone who lies in bed with someone else: bedfellow</li><li>husband or wife</li><li>consort</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gabaddjō</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bedda</i> and Old Saxon <i>gibeddio</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>bedd</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female who lies in bed with someone else: bedfellow</li><li>wife</li><li>female consort</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gabaddjā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gabadjǭ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gibetta</i> and Old Norse <i>beðja</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>bedd</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i> or <i>ġebedda</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anger, offense</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġebelg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive&#32;and&#32;intransitive</i>) to wound</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>bennian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to command, order, summon</li><li>to proclaim, announce</li><li>to propose, grant, give, offer</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>bēodan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gibiodan</i>, Old High German <i>gibiotan</i> (German <i>gebieten</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a drinking companion, pot-companion, guest</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>bēor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barking</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>beorcan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mountain, hill, barrow</li><li>defense, protection, shelter, refuge</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>beorg</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gibirgi</i>, whence Middle High German <i>gebirge</i> and later German <i>Gebirge</i>.</p><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>beorg</i>, from the verb <i>beorgan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to promise, vow</li><li>to boast</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>bēotian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bear, give birth, produce, bring forth, carry out</li><li>to support, sustain, endure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaberaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>beran</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>giberan</i>, Old High German <i>giberan</i> (German <i>gebären</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vexed, oppressed, crushed</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fix, repair</li><li>to cure or treat</li><li>to atone or compensate for</li><li>to make or attend to a fire or a lamp</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>bētan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to better, make better</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>beterian</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gibezzirōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bettering, an amendment</li><li>a restoration, a repairing, a renewing</li></ol><p>From <i>ġebētan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġebēacnian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) in the indicative mood</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>gebīcniende</i> (“beckoning”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wait, bide</li><li>to pause</li><li>to experience or endure</li><li>to reach, attain</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>bīdan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gibīdan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌱𐌴𐌹𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (gabeidan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pray <i>(often with a reflexive dative or accusative pronoun)</i></li><li>to pray to, worship <i>(often with a reflexive dative or accusative pronoun)</i></li><li>to ask for something (+ accusative of person asked) (+ genitive of thing requested)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gabiddjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gabidjaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>biddan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend or curve</li><li>to turn (to or away from something)</li><li>to humiliate, subdue</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) to inflect</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>bīeġan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bent, curved</li><li>turned (to or away from something)</li><li>humiliated, subdued</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) inflected</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curvature, bending</li><li>humiliation, submission</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) inflection</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) case</li></ol><p><i>ġebīeġed</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġebyrde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to taste, sample; savour, relish</li><li>to feast</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>bierġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġebīeġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġebīeġednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġebīeġednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġebīeġednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>billed; having a bill</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>bile</i> (“bill, beak”) +&lrm; <i>-od</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>binding, fastening, bond</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ġebindan</i> (“to bind”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gabindaną</i>. Closely related to Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌱𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰</i> (gabinda).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind, tie up, fasten, fetter, restrain</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gabindaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>bindan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gibindan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌱𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (gabindan). Compare Old High German <i>gibintan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġebindaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġebȳsnung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mixture, mingling, commotion</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>bland</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blend, mix, mingle</li><li>to disturb, trouble, corrupt</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>blandan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blind</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>blendan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>multicolored, variegated</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bless, consecrate</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>blētsian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make happy, blissen, gladden, fill with bliss; applaud</li><li>to be glad, rejoice, exult</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>blissian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make bloody, cover with blood</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>blōdgian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flower, blossom, bloom</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gablōan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gibluoen</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>blōwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A blowing out in the skin, blister</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>blǣd</i> (“blast, blowing”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give by charter</li><li>to furnish with books</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>bōcian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>command, order, mandate</li></ol><p>From <i>ge-</i> +&lrm; <i>bod</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gabudą</i>. Cognate with German <i>Gebot</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to announce, proclaim, make known, tell</li><li>to announce (fact or truth)</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>bodian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bent, curved</li><li>bowed</li><li>turned (to or away from something)</li><li>submitted</li><li>retired, withdrawn</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġebann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>born</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy, break, crush, shatter, bruise</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gabrekaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>brecan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>craft, cunning</li><li>agitation, tossing, moving to and fro</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>breġdan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring</li><li>to produce, adduce, bring forth, bear, lead</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>bringan</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>gibrengian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breaking</li><li>fragment</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decay, become corrupt, become ruinous</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>brosnian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a corruption, decaying (physical or moral)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġebrosnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i> or <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>brosnung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fragment</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;brot</i> (“something broken off”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enjoy, eat</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>brūcan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, bow (down)</li><li>to turn (toward or away from something)</li><li>to yield, give way</li><li>to submit</li><li>to retire, withdraw</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>būgan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inhabitant; farmer, husbandman</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gabūr</i>, from <i>&#42;ga-</i> + Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;būraz</i>.</p><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>būr</i> (“a farmer, bower”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gibūr</i> (Dutch <i>boer</i>), Old High German <i>gibūr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land occupied by farmers or bowers</li></ol><p>From <i>ġebūr</i> (“farmer, bower”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buy, purchase, procure</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>byċġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġebīeġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġebīeġednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>subject, obedient</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bend</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>byġu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corner</li><li>angle</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>byht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwelling, habitation, abode</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;byhþ</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>bygð</i> (“settlement, habitation, abode”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birth</li><li>parentage, lineage</li><li>nature, condition</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaburþiz</i>. Cognate with German <i>Geburt</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>byrd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to border, fringe</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>byrdan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birthday</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaburdidag</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>giburtitag</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġebyrd</i> (“birth”) +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i> (“day”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inborn, innate, natural</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ġebyrd</i> (“birth, origin”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time of birth</li><li>birthday</li></ol><p>From <i>ġebyrd</i> (“birth”) +&lrm; <i>tīd</i> (“time”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġebierġan</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>ġebyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġebyrienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to happen</li><li>(<i>of a person</i>) have to do with, be involved in</li><li>to belong to</li><li>to be becoming or fit, to behove (chiefly impersonal, with dative subject)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaburjan</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>byrian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġebierġan</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>ġebyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġebyrienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an example</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>bȳsnung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything baked, baking, baked good</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gabak</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;bæc</i>. Compare Dutch <i>gebak</i>, German Low German <i>Gebäck</i>, German <i>Gebäck</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to behave, conduct oneself</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>behavior</li><li>conduct, manners</li><li>disposition, demeanor</li><li>gesture, mannerism</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gabārī</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gibāri</i>, Old High German <i>gibāri</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġēac</i> (“cuckoo”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warfare</li><li>battle</li><li>combat</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>camp</i> (“combat”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mock; gibe</li><li>jeering; scorn; derision</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gakanką</i>, <i>&#42;kanką</i> (“laughter”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gang-</i>, <i>&#42;ganǵ-</i> (“to mock, insult”), equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>canc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pregnant (with a calf or fawn)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gakalbaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> (“with”) +&lrm; <i>ċealf</i> (“calf”). Compare <i>ġeēan</i>, <i>ġefearh</i>, and <i>ġefol</i>, which are formed similarly.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeċīeġan</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċēġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeċealf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coolness</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>cēlnes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beget, bear children, bring forth, produce</li><li>to clear, declare, make known, give forth a statement, prove</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>cennan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gikennan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choose, select; wale, elect, decide, approve, accept</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gakeusan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gakeusaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċēosan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gikiosan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌺𐌹𐌿𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (gakiusan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be on the lookout for (someone) [+ genitive]</li><li>to buy</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>cēpan</i> (“to keep, observe”).</p><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċēpan</i> (“to buy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeċierrednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeċyrring</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strife; contention; quarrel</li><li>chiding; reproof</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċīd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chide, reprove; berate; scold, strive, quarrel</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċīdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call upon</li><li>to call, to name</li><li>to name</li><li>to invoke</li><li>to summon</li><li>to convene</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gakauwjan</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċīeġan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gikewen</i> (“to call, name”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>calling</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) vocative</li><li>addressing</li></ol><p>From the present participle of <i>ġeċīeġan</i> (“to call, name, call upon, invoke, summon, convene”) + <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to turn back</li><li>to go to, visit</li><li>(<i>kind&#32;or&#32;submissive</i>) to turn or convert somebody to (+ tō) something or somebody</li><li>(<i>literal&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) to turn, come, or go from (+ of, fram)...to, around, upon (+ tō, ymb, on))</li><li>to turn to or from</li><li>to turn to (as for help); to assent to; to be favorable towards</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċierran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conversion</li><li>a going to (+ tō) somewhere</li><li>a turning</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeċierred</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>converting, changing</li></ol><p>From <i>ġeċierran</i> (“to turn”) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeċīeġan</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċīeġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeċīeġendlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeċierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeċierrednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick to</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>clifian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cry out</li><li>to declare</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>clipian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counterpart of a document, especially one where the original has been destroyed, backup copy</li></ol><p>Derivative of <i>clēofan</i> (“to cleave, split, separate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>split, cleft</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeclipod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cleanse</li><li>to purify</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>clǣnsian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to know, perceive, understand, acknowledge, recognize; beknow</li><li>to be familiar or acquainted with</li><li>to make known, declare</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>cnāwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make clear, explain</li><li>to investigate</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>cnēatian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intent, diligent</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to study</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġecneord</i> (“diligent”) +&lrm; <i>-lǣċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>desire, eagerness</li><li>diligence</li><li>study</li></ol><p>From <i>ġecneord</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knocking together, collision</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bond</li></ol><p>Deverbal of <i>ġecnyċċan</i> (“to bind”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind together, connect</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>cnyċċan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press, trouble</li><li>(<i>literal&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) to strike, dash, beat</li><li>to overcome</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>cnyssan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie or fasten to, annex</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>cnyttan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aware <i>(+genitive of something)</i></li><li>acknowledging or confessing (something bad about oneself) <i>(+genitive)</i></li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ġecnāwan</i> (“to recognize”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to season food</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>cōcnian</i>. Compare <i>ġecōcsian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cook</li><li>to fry</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>cōc</i> +&lrm; <i>-sian</i>. Compare <i>ġecōcnian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>choice</li><li>decision</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gakuzą</i>, <i>&#42;kuzą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġecorennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>election, choice</li><li>choiceness, goodness</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġecoren</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġecorennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>election</li><li>excellence</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġecoren</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crown, coronate</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; Latin <i>corōna</i> (“crown”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is chewed</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) food</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gakow</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;cow</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kowan</i> (“chewed”), past participle of <i>&#42;keuwan</i> (“to chew”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sink, perish , fall, die</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>cringan</i> (“to die in battle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġecrang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to crumble</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crimp, curl.</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;krimpaną</i>, with the prefix <i>ġe-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contrive, build</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>cræftan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strengthen, make powerful</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gakraftugōn</i>, <i>&#42;gakraftīgōn</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>cræftgian</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gikreftigōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to move toward something; reach (something, a location, etc.) by way of movement; arrive at</li><li>to come together, arrive, assemble.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gakwemaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>cuman</i>. Compare Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌵𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (gaqiman).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġecunnod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġecwēme</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>declaration</li></ol><p>A derivative of <i>cweþan</i> (“to say”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġecwidrǣden</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to please, satisfy, gratify, propitiate <i>(+ dative someone)</i></li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>cwēman</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwēmijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġecwēme</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pleasant, agreeable, acceptable</li><li>suitable, appropriate</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>cwēme</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwēmijaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>agreeable, well-pleased</li></ol><p><i>ġecwēman</i> (“to please”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġecweþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak</li><li>(<i>transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive&#32;or&#32;with clause</i>) to say</li><li>to tell</li><li>to declare, announce</li><li>to settle</li><li>to offer, propose</li><li>to order; to give orders for</li><li>to call, name</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>cweþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quicken</li><li>to create</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>cwician</i>. See <i>cwic</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a speech, word; saying, term, expression</li><li>phrase, sentence; clause</li><li>proverb</li><li>proposal; argument; opinion</li><li>agreement, decision; decree</li></ol><p>From a derivative of <i>cweþan</i> (“to say”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agreement, contract</li><li>statute</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġecwide</i> +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġecwician</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġecwician</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġecwidrǣden</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeċīeġan</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċȳġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nature as in the natural world</li><li>nature meaning a disposition</li><li>kind, class</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gakundi</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gikunt</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>cynd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) the book of generation, that is Genesis</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġecynd</i> +&lrm; <i>bōc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>natural</li><li>innate, inborn</li></ol><p><i>ge-</i> +&lrm; <i>cynde</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>natural</li></ol><p>From <i>ġecynd</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) idiom</li></ol><p>From <i>ġecynde</i> (“natural”) +&lrm; <i>sprǣċ</i> (“language”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>genitalia</li><li>the male genitals</li><li>vulva</li><li>the womb, uterus</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġecynd</i> +&lrm; <i>lim</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offspring, progeny, yield:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>generation (genealogical degree)</li><li>people, nation</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ġecynd</i> (“nature”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>; Middle English <i>kyndenesse</i> (“kindness, courteousness, kinship”) is likely a parallel formation.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġecyndnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeċierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeċierrednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conversion, changing, turning</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeċyrran</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to report, to make known</li><li>to announce, declare</li><li>to reveal, manifest, show</li><li>to testify</li><li>to prove</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>cȳþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>testimony, testament, manifestation</li></ol><p>From <i>ġecȳþan</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiedd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>becoming, fit, suitable</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>dafen</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be becoming or fit, to behove (chiefly impersonal, with dative subject)</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>dafenian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a division, separation</li><li>a dissolution, destruction</li><li>a parting or separating of two or more objects or people</li><li>a dividing or sharing of property</li><li>a distribution, dispensation, spending, or giving</li><li>a portion or share</li><li>a difference</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>dāl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġieddung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>dēað</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>appropriate, fitting, seemly, becoming</li><li>gentle, kind</li><li>quiet, meek</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġedēfe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġedēfe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something that is dug; a trench</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>delf</i> (“digging”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to survive, endure</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>dēah</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide, conceal</li></ol><p>Related to <i>dēagol</i>, <i>dīegol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġedīeġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a composition</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>diht</i>. Compare Dutch <i>gedicht</i>, German <i>Gedicht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put in order, arrange, conspire</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>dihtan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do, make, reach a place, effect, cause; beget</li><li>to bring or beget a condition, state</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>dōn</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gidōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trouble; disturbance</li><li>confusion; tribulation</li><li>offense; scandal</li></ol><p>From <i>ġedrēfed</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>troubler</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġedrēfan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disturbance, tempest</li><li>disturbance of mind, perturbation, confusion</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġedrēfnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do, accomplish</li><li>to suffer, live through</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>drēogan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>discreetly, modestly, cautiously, patienter, modeste, prudenter</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to fall</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to perish, disappear</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>drēosan</i> (“to fall, perish”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a driving; movement</li><li>that which is driven</li><li>stubble</li><li>fever</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gadrib</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;drif</i>.</p><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>drif</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gedryht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who drinks with another</li><li>one who sits at table with another, a guest, companion</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> (“co-”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;drinca</i> (“drinker”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defecate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drītaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;drītan</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>drijten</i>, <i>dreet</i> and <i>drits</i>, Old Norse <i>dritt</i> (whence English <i>dirt</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to sink; drown</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to get drunk</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>druncnian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) troop, body of men</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>dryht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dive, plunge</li><li>to sink, be drowned</li><li>to duck</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>dūfan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġedwola</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wandering</li><li>error, mistakenness</li><li>deception, heresy</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>dwild</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a heretic</li></ol><p>From <i>ġedwild</i> (“heresy”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġedwimor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>necromancy, sorcery</li><li>a sorcerer or magician; juggler; rascal, scoundrel</li></ol><p>From <i>ġedwimer</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>illusion</li><li>spectre, phantom</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>dwimor</i> (“magic, illusion”) (unrecorded except in compounds); see dwimor.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heretical</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>countable</i>) mistake, error</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) error</li><li>(countable,&#32;uncountable) heresy</li><li>heretic</li></ol><p>From or related to the verb <i>dwolian</i> (“to make a mistake, be wrong”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deception, deceptive art</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġedwola</i> +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>iniquitous, evil, wrong</li><li>thoughtless, unwise</li></ol><p>A fossilised past participle of the lost strong class 4 verb <i>&#42;dwelan</i> (compare <i>dwelian</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>false god, idol</li></ol><p><i>ġedwola</i> +&lrm; <i>god</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who is mistaken; a deluded doer</li><li>an opponent to Christianity; a heretic</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġedwola</i> +&lrm; <i>mann</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heretical speech</li><li>heresy</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġedwola</i> +&lrm; <i>sprǣċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>erroneous</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġedwol</i> +&lrm; <i>-sum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>necromancy</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;dwomer</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dwemaną</i> (“to smoke; fume”), possibly via a hypothetical intermediate form: Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dwumrą</i> or <i>&#42;dwamrą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġedwild</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġedwimor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dare, presume</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>dyrstlǣċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mild; gentle; meek</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;daftī</i> (“fitting, suitable”), equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>dæfte</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deal out, distribute, share</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>dǣlan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gidelian</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌳𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gadailjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġēa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow old, become old</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>ealdian</i> (“to grow old”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pregnant (with lambs)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaaunaz</i>, literally “with a lamb”. There are no exact cognates, but the archaic sense of <i>ġe-</i> and the use of <i>&#42;aunaz</i> (the original Proto-Germanic word for “lamb,” inherited from Proto-Indo-European and later displaced by <i>&#42;lambaz</i>) show that this word was quite ancient.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dwell (+ preposition <i>in</i> or <i>on</i>, in)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>eardian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deserve</li><li>to earn</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>earnian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earning, merit, deservingness</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġeearnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to humiliate</li><li>to make submissive; to subject or subdue</li><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to make oneself low, to humble oneself</li><li>to worship or adore</li><li>to condescend or deign</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>ēaþmēdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to humiliate</li><li>to condescend, deign</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>ēaþmōdian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come back to life</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>edcwician</i> (“to revive”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enfranchise again, to restore to freedom</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>edfrēolsian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to restore, renew, renovate</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>ednīewian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to imitate, copy</li><li>to match</li><li>to compare</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>efenlǣċan</i>, from <i>efen</i> +&lrm; <i>-lǣċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to egg on, urge, excite</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>eggian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frighten</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>egsian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set in order, arrange, dispose</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>endebyrdan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive&#32;and&#32;intransitive</i>) to end, finish</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>endian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ended, finished; over</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>finite</li></ol><p><i>ġeendod</i> (“ended”) + <i>-līċ</i> (“-ly”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ending, end</li><li>finish</li><li>death</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) a grammatical ending</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>endung</i> or <i>ġeendian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blaspheme; to reproach, insult</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>eofolsian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeīewan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeerienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plough</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>erian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeerienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat together; eat with</li><li>to consume, fret</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>etan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>giezzan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set in order</li><li>to arrange</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fadian</i> (“to arrange”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiefan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fasten together, join together</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;fangian</i>, from <i>fang</i> (originally meaning "something caught").</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>companion, fellow traveler</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fara</i> (“traveler”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reach by going, get to, fare to, arrive, proceed, march</li><li>to experience, carry out, accomplish (by faring), succeed</li><li>to save by going</li><li>to die, depart</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>faran</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gifaran</i>, Old High German <i>gifaran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gafehō</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gifeho</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fēa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fold up, wrap up, roll about</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fealdan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gifaltan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fall; stumble; fall from a higher to a lower position; drop; cascade; plummet</li><li>to fall dead; perish; be ruined</li><li>to decay, decline, fail</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cause (something) by falling, fall to, reach by falling</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gafallaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>feallan</i>. Cognate with Old Dutch <i>gifallan</i>, Old High German <i>gifallan</i>.</p></html>:/jeˈfeːɑ̯n/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġefēon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pregnant (with piglets)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gafarhaz</i>, literally “with a piglet.” Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fearh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Having hair; haired</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>feax</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Having hair; haired</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>feax</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joining, juncture</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to join, attach</li><li>to constrain, confine</li><li>to square, adapt</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fēġan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gifuogen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit, adapted</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gafōg(ī)</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fōg</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an association; joining, joint, conjunction</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) a conjunction</li><li>companionship</li></ol><p><i>ġefēġan</i> (“to join, fay”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel</li><li>to perceive, sense</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gafōlijan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gifōlian</i>, Old High German <i>gifuolen</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fēlan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġefiellan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(countable,&#32;uncountable) feeling</li><li>(countable,&#32;uncountable) perception, sense</li></ol><p><i>ġefēlan</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fight, fighting</li><li>battle</li><li>war</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gafehtą</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>&#42;gifeht</i>, Old High German <i>gifeht</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>feoht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to struggle, strive for supremacy, fight in a quarrel or brawl</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to gain or obtain by fighting, win</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>feohtan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gifehtan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>day of battle</li></ol><p>From <i>ġefeoht</i> +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick to, persist</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fēolan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rejoice, exult</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gafehan</i> (“to rejoice”); cognate with Old High German <i>gefehan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entertain</li><li>to harbor</li><li>to receive as a guest</li><li>to feed, support</li><li>to cherish</li><li>to feed on</li><li>to cleanse</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>feormian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>society, company</li><li>alternative form of <i>ġefǣr</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>ġefær</i></li></ol><p>Nominalization of <i>ġefēran</i> (as if "association, going together") from <i>ġe-</i> (“jointly, together”) +&lrm; <i>fēran</i> (“to go”)</p><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fer</i> (“way”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>companion, comrade</li><li>partner</li><li>member</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fēra</i>, literally “fellow traveler," related to the verb <i>faran</i> (“to go, travel”). Compare German <i>Gefährte</i>, of similar form and meaning.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Of persons:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to go, to travel or journey</li><li>to set out, to leave or depart (this life)</li></ol><li>of events:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to (come to) pass, to occur, happen</li><li>to meet with, experience</li><li>to bring about</li></ol><li>to gain, attain</li><li>to cause to move, to carry, to bring</li></ol><p>See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</p><p>From <i>ge-</i> +&lrm; <i>fēran</i> (“travel, depart”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>accessible, passable</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a companion, associate</li><li>alternative form of <i>ġefēr</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fēre</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ga-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;fōrijaną</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-az</i> (“fellow traveller”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>social</li></ol><p><i>ġefēra</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sociably</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġefēr</i> (“society”) +&lrm; <i>līþelīċe</i> (“gently, graciously”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to keep somebody's or something's company or fellowship; to associate together</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to associate one thing or person to another thing; to classify things together</li></ol><p><i>ġefēra</i> (“companion”) +&lrm; <i>-lǣcan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġefērrǣden</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>companionship</li><li>community</li><li>congregation, church</li><li>familiarity, friendship</li></ol><p><i>ġefēra</i> +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>society</li></ol><p>From <i>ġefērrǣden</i> (“community”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellowship, brotherhood</li></ol><p>From <i>ġefēra</i> (“companion”) +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i> (“-ship”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fetch, bring</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fetian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fraud, deceit; swindle, trick, wile</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; a base represented by <i>fician</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fold up</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;fieldan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill, fell, cut down, destroy, deprive of</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fiellan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enemies</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fīend</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġefylċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find, to come upon, to meet with</li><li>to find by seeking for, to recover something lost</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>findan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeflīeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive away, put to flight, banish</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>flīeman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>argument, fight</li><li>arguing, fighting</li><li>contest, competition</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>flit</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>contentious</li></ol><p>From <i>ġeflit</i> (“argument, fighting”) +&lrm; <i>-ful</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i> or <i>ġeflitful</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overflow</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>flōwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeflīeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incarnation</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>flǣsċ</i> (“flesh”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit; suitable</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a joining together</li><li>that which joins together; joint</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gafōg(ī)</i> (“fitting, suitable”). Compare Old High German <i>gifuogi</i> (“fitting, suitable”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gafōg</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gafōgą</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fōg</i>. Compare Old High German <i>gifuogi</i> (“joint, connection”), Middle Low German <i>gevôg</i>, <i>gevôch</i> (“availability, need, necessity”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pregnant (with a foal, or with an elephant calf or camel calf)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gafulaz</i>, literally “with a foal.” Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fola</i> but an adjective.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to follow, reach by following, attain</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>folgian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch, seize, capture, take</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fōn</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gifāhan</i>, Old High German <i>gifāhan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍆𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (gafahan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry out, perform, accomplish</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>forþian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to learn by asking; inquire, find out</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;frāgian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask, inquire</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>frāsian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel</li><li>to perceive, sense</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>frēdan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(countable,&#32;uncountable) feeling</li><li>(countable,&#32;uncountable) perception, sense</li></ol><p><i>ġefrēdan</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comfort, console</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>frēfran</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġefrǣġe</i> (“a knowing, knowledge, hearsay”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accomplishment, fulfillment, effect</li></ol><p><i>ġefremed</i> (past participle of ġefremman) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to promote, perfect, commit, perform</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fremman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>furtherance, effect</li></ol><p><i>ġefremman</i> (“to perform, perfect”) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>effect</li></ol><p>Perhaps a syncope of <i>ġefremednes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to free, make free</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>frēoġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġefrēoġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friends</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>frīend</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find out, hear about, discover, learn something through asking</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>friġnan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gifregnan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (gafraihnan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) inquiry, questioning</li><li>(<i>countable</i>) a question</li></ol><p>From <i>ġefriġen</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġefriġnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġefrēoġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frozen, in a frozen state</li></ol></html>:/jeˈfruːnon/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġefruġnon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) known, celebrated, remarkable, famous</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) inquiry, a knowledge, information, hearsay</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gafrāgī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gafrēgiz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gifrāgi</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;frǣġe</i>. The noun is probably derived from the adjective.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġefrǣġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adorn, deck with treasures</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>frætwe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become full</li><li>to perfect</li><li>to baptize</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fullian</i>.</p><p>alternative form of <i>ġefulwian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a helper</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a helper</li></ol><p>From <i>ġefultumian</i> (“to help”) +&lrm; <i>-end</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assist, help</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġefultuma</i> (“helper”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) comrade, ally</li></ol><p>From <i>ġefylċe</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A collection of people, army, troop, division</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> + <i>&#42;fylċe</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;fulkī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fulkiją</i> (“collection of people; band; company; troop"”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġefylċa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġefieldan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to complete, finish, accomplish, fulfill, fill, make a total, satisfy</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fyllan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gifullen</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍆𐌿𐌻𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gafulljan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>long ago, in days of yore, of old</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fyrn</i> (“ancient”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make ready</li><li>to cause to hasten, rush</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fȳsan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>orderly; tidy; well-regulated</li><li>well-behaved; calm; composed</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decorum; discretion</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> + Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fadaz</i> (“orderly; tidy”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂-</i> (“protect, guard, graze, feed”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>godfather</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gafadurjō</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fædera</i>. Akin to Low Low German <i>Gevadder</i>, <i>Gevader</i>, Old High German <i>gifatero</i> (German <i>Gevatter</i>), Middle Dutch <i>gevadere</i> (Dutch <i>gevader</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sudden danger, calamity; ambush</li><li>way, journey, course, expedition; a (collective) going</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fǣr</i>. Cognate to Old Saxon <i>gifār</i>, Dutch <i>gevaar</i>, German <i>Gefahr</i>.</p><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fær</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;farą</i>, from the same source as Old English <i>faran</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with certainty</li><li>firmly</li><li>with constancy</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fæstlīċe</i> (“firmly”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fix, confirm, make fast</li><li>to imprison, fetter</li><li>to establish (relations), settle, determine</li><li>to make steady, constant</li><li>to secure, make safe</li><li>to entrust, commit</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fæstnian</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gifastinōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pack, load</li><li>to impose</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>fǣtan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġefēlan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gafōlijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to gather, set, or bring together, unite into a whole</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to compose, join something which is broken</li><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to collect or gather into a council, assembly, meeting</li><li>(figurative,&#32;intransitive) to come together in agreement, unite, agree</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>gaderian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a joining together; union; a joint bond; that which results from joining; a joining together to form a whole</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>gaderung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġegaderian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buffoonery</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>gaf</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sigh, groan</li><li>to lament</li><li>to go, pass over</li><li>to happen, come to pass</li><li>to conquer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gauwjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gawjaną</i> (“to bark, bay, scream”).</p><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>gān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chance</li><li>event</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>gang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go, happen, befall, come in</li><li>to walk, go to a place or position</li><li>to exercise, effect, accomplish; fulfill</li><li>to go against, overcome, subdue, conquer, obtain</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>gangan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gigangan</i>, Old High German <i>gigangan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (gagaggan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare; to make ready</li><li>(<i>of ships</i>) to equip</li><li>to furnish, provide, supply</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gagarwakōn</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġearcian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare</li><li>to make ready</li><li>to endure</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>gearwian</i> (“to prepare, make read”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to meet</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;ġeġnian</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaganōn</i>, <i>&#42;geginōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaganōną</i>, <i>&#42;gaginōną</i> (“to approach, meet”). Cognate with Old High German <i>gaganōn</i>, <i>geginōn</i> (“to agree upon”), Old Norse <i>gegna</i> (“to fit, be suitable”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeġierwan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġiefan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to equip, make ready</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġierwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeġiefan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adorn, embellish</li><li>to set in order, compose</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>glenġan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(of a hand) gloved, (of a person) having gloves on</li></ol><p>The past participle of a lost verb <i>&#42;glōfian</i> (“to put on gloves”), which later shows up in Middle English as <i>glōven</i>. Derived from the noun <i>glōf</i> (“glove”). Compare <i>sċōġan</i> and <i>ġesċōd</i>, which are derived in the same way from <i>sċōh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>again</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gagin</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gagin</i>, whence also Old Saxon <i>gigin</i>, Old High German <i>gagan</i> (> German <i>gegen</i>), Old Norse <i>gegn</i> (> Swedish <i>igen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>opposition, reversal, against; counter-</li><li>again; back, in return</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gagin</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gagin</i> (“back, in return”). Compare Old Norse <i>gegn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>answer, reply</li></ol><p><i>ġeġn-</i> +&lrm; <i>cwide</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) straight, directly</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grope, touch</li><li>to reach</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>grāpian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gripe, grab, grasp, seize, snatch from, lay hold of, take with the hands, take with violence, apprehend</li><li>to get by grabbing, obtain, acquire</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>grīpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a taking or seizing</li><li>a snare</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġegripen</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>, or else to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>gripennes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġegripennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay hold of, obtain, acquire</li><li>to seize</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>grippan</i> (“to grip”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to found, build on a firm foundation</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to found</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;grundweallian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seize</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grabbōną</i>, <i>&#42;grēpōną</i> (“to seize”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰreb(ʰ)-</i>, <i>&#42;gʰrab(ʰ)-</i> (“to take, seize, rake”). More at grapple.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeġiefan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gird</li><li>to invest with attributes</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>gyrdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeġierwan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>together</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>gædere</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold onto, keep from</li><li>to maintain, retain</li><li>to detain, restrain</li><li>to preserve</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gahabjaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>habban</i>. Akin to Old Saxon <i>gihebbian</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (gahaban), Old High German <i>gihabēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ordain, consecrate</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hādian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to please</li><li>to be convenient or suitable for a person to have to do something [+accusative]</li><li>to be within the means or power of a person [+accusative]</li><li>to be within the means or power of a person [+dative]</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gahagōn</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;hagian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehealdan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehālgiend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehālgod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consecrate, dedicate, make holy, hallow</li><li>to ordain</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gahailagōn</i>, By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hālgian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who hallows; a consecrator</li></ol><p>From <i>ġehālgian</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consecration, sanctification</li><li>sanctuary</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġehālgian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to provide a home (to), domicile, lodge</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hāmettan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>promise</li><li>stipulation</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gahait</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gahaitą</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>giheiz</i>, Old Norse <i>heit</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄</i> (gahait).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to promise, vow</li><li>to command, order</li><li>to call, name</li><li>to summon</li><li>to threaten</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gahaitaną</i> (“to promise”), equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hātan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gihêtan</i>, Old High German <i>giheizan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (gahaitan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to become angry</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hāthiertan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>promised land / Promised Land</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ġehāt</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehātland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a holding, keeping guard, observing</li><li>a guardian, keeper</li><li>protection</li><li>shelter</li><li>observation, marking, noticing</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>heald</i></p><p>See <i>ġehealdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep, retain, observe, reserve, preserve, save, defend, shield</li><li>to possess, occupy, hold</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>healdan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gihaldan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sparing, frugal</li><li>taking care of, protective</li><li>exercising restraint; moderate, virtuous, sober</li><li>safe, inviolate</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>heald</i> +&lrm; <i>-sum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to restrain, control, compress</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>heaþorian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heave or raise up, ferment</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gahabjaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hebban</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gihebbian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burden, to load, to weigh down (literally or figuratively)</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hefiġian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehīeġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover with a helmet</li><li>to crown</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>helmian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>close, near; nearby</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>close, near, at hand</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>close, near</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gahandī</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gihenti</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>-hende</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nearness, proximity</li></ol><p>From <i>ġehende</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehendnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>vigorously</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>heorte</i> (“heart”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehīeran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehīerendlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeherian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeheriendlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeherienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ravage, plunder, harrow</li><li>to take captive</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hergian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to praise, honor, glorify</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>herian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>praiseworthy</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeherian</i> +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeherian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeheriendlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeherienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hearsay, tidings</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġehīeran</i> (“to hear”) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehīernes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehīersum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehīersumian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehīersumnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġēol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to perform</li><li>to execute</li><li>to hold a meeting</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;hīeġan</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hauwjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hawjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hear</li><li>to listen</li><li>to hear judicially, to try</li><li>(<i>with dative</i>) to obey</li><li>to belong</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gahauʀijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gahauzijaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hīeran</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gihōrian</i>, Old High German <i>gihōren</i> (modern German <i>gehören</i> (“to belong”)), Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gahausjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) to harden, strengthen, temper</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hierdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>audible</li></ol><p>From <i>ġehīeran</i> +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hearing</li><li>that which is heard</li></ol><p><i>ġehīeran</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>obedient</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gahauʀisam</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obey [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gahauʀisamōn</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġehīersum</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obedience</li></ol><p>From <i>ġehīersum</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hearten, encourage, animate</li><li>to revive, refresh, cherish, shelter</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hiertan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to form, shape</li><li>to color, to paint</li><li>to pretend</li><li>to imagine</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hīewian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehīeran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehīernes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehīersum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehīersumian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehīersumnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehiertan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to familiarize</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hīwcūþlīċ</i> + <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehīewian</i></li><li>to marry</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hīwian</i>.</p><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hīwian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mooing</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hlōwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cry, clamor, noise</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hlȳd</i> (“sound”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a conflict, fight</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;hnād</i>, possibly related to Old English <i>hnītan</i> (“to strike, bump, knock”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehnǣst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>drawn; pinched; wrinkled</li></ol><p>From an unrecorded verb <i>&#42;hnycnan</i>, <i>&#42;hnycnian</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hnukēn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnukāną</i> (“to bend; crook; twist; wrinkle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend down, humble, cast down, subdue</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hnǣġan</i> (“humble”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conflict, clash</li><li>slaughter, battle</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;hnǣst</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hoofed</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hōf</i> (“hoof”) +&lrm; <i>-od</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to resolve, determine</li><li>to conceive</li><li>to look for, hope for</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hogian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) friend, confidant, protector</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obtain</li><li>to hollow out</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaholōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hulōną</i> (“to call, fetch, summon”), from a conflation of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kel-</i> (“to drive”) and Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kelh₁-</i> (“to shout, call”), equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;holian</i> (“to get”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>halia</i> (“to get, drive home, take”), Middle Dutch and Dutch <i>halen</i>, Old Saxon <i>halōn</i> (“to get”), Old High German <i>halōn</i>, <i>holōn</i> (“to get, fetch”) (German <i>holen</i>), Latin <i>celer</i> (“swift”), Latin <i>calo</i> (“to call, summon”). Related to Old English <i>healdan</i> (“to grasp, hold, retain”). More at accelerate, claim, hold.</p><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>holian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang, hang with, decorate; behang</li><li>to fasten something, fasten to a cross, crucify</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to provide (someone) with a horse</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>horsian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move</li><li>to stir together, mix up</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hrēran</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to plunder</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrespan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrespaną</i> (“to tear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch, affect</li><li>to take hold of, seize</li><li>to touch (with), have intercourse</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hrīnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fall; ruin; death</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;hror</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noise, disturbance</li></ol><p>Presumably of imitative origin, compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rūskōną</i> (“to whiz, whirr”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>how else</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a snail (with a shell, as a opposed to a shellless slug)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>each, every, everyone</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hwā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from all sides</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hwanon</i> (“whence”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehwǣdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>each, every</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>each (one)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gahwalīk</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hwelċ</i>, literally “every which.”</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>converted, active</li><li>versed, practised</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gahwerbaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn, go to, direct</li><li>to exchange, be transferred, be averted, return</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hweorfan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>giwerban</i>, Old Saxon <i>gihwervan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehwierfan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change, turn, convert</li><li>to convert</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hwierfan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehwelċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehwierfan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehwelċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehwierfan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>little, moderate, scanty, slender</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>moderately, scantily</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hwǣde</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sparingness</li><li>smallness, slenderness, fewness</li><li>poorness, meanness, mediocrity</li></ol><p>From <i>ġehwǣde</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehwǣdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehwǣdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>everywhere</li><li>elsewhere</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hwǣr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>both</li><li>each</li><li>either</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gahwaþera</i> (“each”). Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hwæþer</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think of, conceive (something)</li><li>to reflect on, consider, think about (something)</li><li>to be mindful of, care about (something)</li><li>to intend, resolve (to do something)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hyċġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide, conceal</li><li>to watch, guard, heed</li><li>to bring into safety, make firm, fasten</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hȳdan</i> (“hide”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thought, contemplation, intention</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gahugdiz</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌷𐌿𐌲𐌳𐍃</i> (gahugds). Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hyġd</i>, from <i>hyċġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bent, bow-legged</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hylċ</i> (“bend, turn”) +&lrm; <i>-ed</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehīeran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehierdan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehīerendlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>horned</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehīernes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehīersum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehīersumian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehīersumnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġehiertan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deride, mock</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hysċan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deride, mock, scoff at</li><li>to reproach</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hyspan</i> (“to mock”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sausage meat</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;ġehæcc</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>. Alternatively derived from <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;hæċċan</i> (“to hack”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take captive</li><li>to bind</li><li>to detain</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hæftan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an enclosed piece of land, a hay</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heal</li><li>to cure</li><li>to save</li><li>to allay or remove anxiety</li><li>to hail</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gahailijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gahailijaną</i>; equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hǣlan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gihēlian</i>, Old High German <i>giheilen</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gahailjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to marry</li><li>to lie with, fornicate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hǣman</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>suitable; fitting; proper</li><li>convenient</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;hæp</i>, the second element from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hap(p)</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;happaz</i>, <i>&#42;hampaz</i> (“fitting; convenient”), from the noun <i>&#42;happą</i>, <i>&#42;hampą</i> (“convenience”), cognate with Old Danish <i>happ</i> (“fortunate”). More at hap.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) care, anxiety, grief</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeīeċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeīeċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to increase</li><li>to add</li><li>to enlarge</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>īeċan</i> (“to eke, increase”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) adjective</li></ol><p>From <i>ġeīeċan</i> (“to increase”) +&lrm; <i>-endlīc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show</li><li>to present / bestow</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>yoked together</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yugtós</i>, from <i>&#42;yewg-</i> (“to yoke”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>युक्त</i> (yukta).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to graft</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to introduce into, mingle in</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>impian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inlaw, to restore to the protection of the law</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>inlagian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to restore</li><li>to replace, fill in for, supplement</li><li>to make up for, repair</li><li>to bring in, put up, lodge</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>innian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run about</li><li>to run to, run and reach</li><li>to coagulate, run together</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>irnan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motion, commotion, tumultuous assembly, play</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>lāc</i> (“play”). Akin to <i>lācan</i> (“to wave, sway”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To play a trick on, delude</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>lācan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heal, cure</li></ol><p><i>ge-</i> +&lrm; <i>lacnian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to refresh</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;galabōn</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>lafian</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gilabōn</i> (“to comfort, support, nurture, refresh”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to establish by law</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>laġian</i> (“to make law”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collection, stretch, extent, expanse of water</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>lagu</i> (“sea, water”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compatriot</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġelendan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>along of</li><li>relating to, belonging to</li><li>dependent upon</li><li>resulting from, due to</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;galang</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>lang</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gilang</i>, Old High German <i>gilang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call for, send for</li><li>to deliver, liberate</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>langian</i> (“to call”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to invite, bid, call, summon, assemble</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>laþian</i> (“to invite, call”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>congregation, assembly, church</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>laþung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġield</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġieldan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;geldan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>belief</li><li>faith</li><li>religion</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;galaubō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;galaubô</i>. More at <i>ġelīefan</i> (“to believe”). Cognate with German <i>Glaube</i> (“belief”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġelēaffull</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>faithful</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unbelieving</li></ol><p><i>ġelēafa</i> (“faith, belief”) +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>faithful</li><li>credulous</li><li>credible</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġelēaffull</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accuse</li><li>to rebuke, reproach</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>leahtrian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>false</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>laþian</i></li></ol><p><i>ge-</i> +&lrm; <i>leaðian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay, set, place</li><li>to settle by law the character of</li><li>to lay a command, task (upon someone)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;galagjaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>leċġan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gileggen</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (galagjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġelīefan</i> (“to believe; to believe in”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġelynd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>landowner</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;galandijō</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;lenda</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to approach, come, arrive</li><li>to go, proceed</li><li>to endow with land</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>lendan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging, related</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>lenġe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of a nation; fellow-countryman; compatriot</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>lēod</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go, depart, emigrate, die</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>leoran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exile</li><li>removing</li><li>transmigration</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeleored</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeleorednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġelǣte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live, be alive</li><li>to flee spiritual death</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to live to do, get by living</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to experience; live through, have as a part of one's life</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>libban</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gilebēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>like, similar; the same (meaning 'alike', not 'identical'), equal</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an equal</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;galīkō</i>, from <i>&#42;galīk</i> (“alike, similar”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ō</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġelīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġelīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the same way, alike, similarly (+ dative: to)</li><li>to the same degree, alike, equally</li><li>likewise, also, too</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>in the same way as, just as</li><li>(<i>with a hypothetical clause</i>) just as if</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;galīkē</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;galīkê</i>, from <i>&#42;galīkaz</i> + <i>&#42;-ê</i>. Compare also the formations at Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;galīkō</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ġelīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġelīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie</li><li>to lie near or with</li><li>to lie down</li><li>to fail, cease, delay</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>liċġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to delight, please; gladden; blissen</li><li>to seem good</li><li>to make alike, be like, imitate; ape</li><li>to seem likely</li><li>to liken, compare</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;galīkāną</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>līcian</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;galīkōną</i>; equivalent to <i>ġelīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>appropriate, fit</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>līclīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>similarity, likeness</li><li>image</li><li>parable</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;galīkanassī</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġelīc</i> (“like, similar”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i> (“-ness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġelīcnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġelīcnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġelīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ġelīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġelīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to believe</li><li>to believe in</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;galaubijaną</i> (“to believe, to hold valuable or pleasing”), from <i>&#42;ga-laub-</i> (“dear, pleasing”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewbʰ-</i> (“like, love”). Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>līefan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gilōvian</i> (Low German <i>glöven</i>), Dutch <i>geloven</i>, Old High German <i>gilouben</i> (German <i>glauben</i>), Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌰𐌿𐌱𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (galaubjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to redeem, release, save, spare</li><li>to loosen, relax</li><li>to dissolve, break</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>līesan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lather, make frothy</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>līeþran</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make alive, quicken</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>līf</i> +&lrm; <i>fæstan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeliġre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeliġre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the act of lying with someone; (<i>by extension</i>) fornication; adultery</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;galigriją</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;liġre</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌹𐌲𐍂𐌹</i> (galigri).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to approach, come, alight</li><li>to lighten, mitigate, assuage</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>līhtan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glue, join together</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>līman</i>. Ultimately derived from <i>līm</i> (“glue”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>event</li><li>accident, chance</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to happen, come about</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>limpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of a happening</li><li>fit, suitable, meet</li><li>adapted to, fitted for</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;galimpalīk</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gilimphlīh</i> (German <i>glimpflich</i>), Middle Low German <i>gelimplīk</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġelimp</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fitly, suitably</li><li>rightly, properly</li><li>in a becoming manner</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġelimplīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>, else <i>ġelimp</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go, move</li><li>to sail</li><li>to advance, proceed</li><li>to come to land, reach port, arrive</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>līþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiellan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Anglian</i>) alternative form of <i>ġilm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silverweed</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġelod</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to place in order; arrange</li><li>to regulate</li><li>to bring together; join; unite</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>lōgian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>often, frequent</li></ol><p>See <i>ġelōme</i> for more information.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>frequently, often</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ga-</i> + Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lōma</i> (adverb), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lōmiz</i>, <i>&#42;lōmijaz</i> (“lame, halt”, adjective), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lem-</i> (“to break, soften”). Compare Old High German <i>giluomo</i>, <i>kilōmo</i> (“often, frequently”), Old English <i>lama</i> (“lame”). See lame. Compare loom. Composed as if from <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>lōme</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frequent</li><li>numerous, many</li><li>frequented or attended by many</li></ol><p><i>ġelōm</i> (“frequently”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>frequently, often</li></ol><p><i>ġelōmlīċ</i> (“frequent”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ġelōmlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġelōmlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gelanda</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġelang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lose, perish</li><li>to be lost</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>losian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>afternoon</li><li>early evening</li></ol><p>From past-participle of <i>lūtan</i> (“to lout, to bow, to stoop, to bend”) + <i>dæg</i> (“day”), literally meaning the bowed or declined day.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġielp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġielpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gylt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to love</li><li>to esteem</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>lufian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take pleasure (in)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;galustōn</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;lustian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġelīefan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġelȳfeþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grease, fat</li><li>fatness</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>lynd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grease</li><li>fat, especially suet or lard</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) joints of the spine, vertebrae</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;lynde</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġelīesan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to please</li><li>to cause a desire for</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;galustijan</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>lystan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grab (sometimes violently: snatch, catch, apprehend)</li><li>to pick up food</li><li>to catch a disease</li><li>to comprehend</li></ol><p>From <i>ge-</i> +&lrm; <i>læċċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead</li><li>to conduct, lead, derive, produce, bring out</li><li>to bring to a particular condition</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣdan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gileiten</i> (German <i>geleiten</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġelēafa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave</li><li>to be left, remain; belive</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unoccupied; empty</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> + Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lēziz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to teach, guide, advise</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) instructed, skilled, knowledgeable, wise</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) learned, erudite</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) displaying skill, wisdom, or knowledge</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) connected with or resulting from learning; learned</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣran</i> +&lrm; <i>-ed</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġelǣred</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>learning, knowledge, erudition</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġelǣred</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġelǣrednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to perform, fulfil</li><li>to stand by (someone), follow, support, attend to (someone)</li><li>to accompany (someone)</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣstan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intersection, crossroads</li></ol><p>Perhaps derived from the verb <i>lǣtan</i> (“to let go, leave”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>equal, match</li><li>spouse, consort</li><li>mate (an animal's breeding partner)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gamakō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gamakô</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>maca</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gimako</i>, Old High German <i>gimahho</i>, and Old Norse <i>maki</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make, beget, cause</li><li>to make peace</li><li>to use</li><li>to fashion, construct an object</li><li>to cause that, bring about that</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>macian</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gimakōn</i>, Old High German <i>gimahhōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>referring to health</i>) to recuperate, recover</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>magian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intercourse, whether social or sexual</li><li>companionship, community, society</li></ol><p>A substantive variant of the Germanic root of <i>ġemǣne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mixture</li><li>crowd</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>among</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>mang</i> (Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mang-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;menk-</i> (“knead”)). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gimang</i>. The Indo-European root is also the source of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mǭkà</i> (“flour”) (Old Church Slavonic <i>мѫка</i> (mǫka), Russian <i>мука́</i> (muká)), Proto-Baltic <i>&#42;minki-</i> (Lithuanian <i>minkiti</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trade, traffic, do as a monger</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>mangian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to admonish; exhort; urge; prompt</li><li>to bring to mind; remind</li><li>to remember</li><li>to demand (of someone); make a demand for a debt</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gamanōną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>manian</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gimanōn</i>, Old High German <i>gemanōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to multiply</li></ol><p>From <i>ge-</i> +&lrm; <i>maniġfeald</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gimanagfaldon</i> and Old High German <i>gimanagfalton</i>. Akin to <i>gemaniġfieldan</i>. See manifold.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to multiply, enlarge</li></ol><p>From <i>ge-</i> +&lrm; <i>manigfeald</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>. Akin to <i>ġemaniġfealdian</i>, from the same Germanic root as Old Saxon <i>gimanagfaldian</i>, Dutch <i>vermenigvuldigen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġemaniġfieldan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to man, garrison</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>mannian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġemartyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġemartyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to martyr</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>martyrian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mark; to point out</li><li>to mark out a site; to fix the boundaries of</li><li>to make a mark on</li><li>to form by marks; to describe a figure; to design</li><li>to seal</li><li>to make a sign; to make the sign of the cross</li><li>to assign; to appoint</li><li>to note or record in writing</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>mearcian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>marked; noted</li></ol><p>From the past participle of ġemearcian.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġemæċċa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something agreeable or satisfactory</li><li>permission, agreement</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġemenġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mingle, confuse, commingle, unite, combine, mix, blend</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>menġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeōmrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġemyrran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġemǣrsian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>measure</li><li>measurement</li><li>capacity, ability</li><li>rule, law</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) grammatical mood</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit, proper, meet</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gamet</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>met</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to meet, encounter, come upon</li><li>to find, discover</li><li>to mete, measure, determine the size or quantity</li><li>to measure back or again</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gamōtijaną</i> (compare Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌼𐍉𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gamōtjan)), equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>mētan</i>.</p><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>metan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gimezzan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌼𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (gamitan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to moderate, temper</li><li>to regulate, govern</li><li>to consider</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>metegian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moderate, temperate</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moderation, temperance</li><li>regulation, a regulation</li><li>governance</li></ol><p><i>ġemetegian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i> or <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>metegung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a measuring vessel</li><li>a measure</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġemet</i> +&lrm; <i>fæt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġemetegian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġemetegung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meeting, assembly</li><li>association, society</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>mēting</i> (“meeting”) or <i>ġemētan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moderate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gametalīk</i>, equivalent to <i>ġemet</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gimezlīh</i> (“measured, moderate, appropriate, modest”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to moderate</li></ol><p>From <i>ġemet</i> +&lrm; <i>-lǣċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>wearied, fatigued</li></ol><p>A past participle of either <i>mēþgian</i> or <i>ġemēþgian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mēþgian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enlarge, magnify</li><li>to extol, glorify</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>miċelian</i> (“to make great”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to urinate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>mīgan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show mercy, pardon</li><li>to pity, have compassion for</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>miltsian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>existing in the mind or memory, known</li><li>recalling</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;mimor</i> (“mindful”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mimr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mimraz</i> (“mindful”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)mer-</i> (“to be mindful, remember, think”). More at <i>&#42;mimor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lessen</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to impair the credit of</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>minsian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find, to meet</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decay, wither</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>molsnian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġemanian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġemoniaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meeting (encounter, assembly, or confluence)</li><li>court or judicial assembly</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;mōt</i>, the latter from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mōtą</i> (“meeting”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meeting place</li><li>senate house</li><li>hall</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġemōt</i> +&lrm; <i>-ern</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remember [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> genitive]</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gamunan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>memory</li><li><i>(poetic or in certain set phrases)</i> mind</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gamundi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gamundiz</i>, ultimately from a prefixed form of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;men-</i> (“to think, remember”), equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;mynd</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old High German <i>gimunt</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌼𐌿𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (gamunds); and with Sanskrit <i>मन</i> (mana), Ancient Greek <i>μέμονα</i> (mémona), Latin <i>mēns</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>мьнѣти</i> (mĭněti) (Russian <i>мнить</i> (mnitʹ)), Lithuanian <i>miñti</i>, Old Irish <i>menmae</i>, Tocharian A <i>mnu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ġemynd</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ġemynd</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>memorable</li><li>relating to memory</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġemynd</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from memory</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġemyndlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i> or <i>ġemynd</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġemyndfull</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of good memory, mindful</li></ol><p>From <i>ġemynd</i> +&lrm; <i>-full</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remind <i>(+ accusative = someone)</i> <i>(+ genitive = of something)</i></li><li>to remember <i>(+ genitive)</i></li><li>to commemorate <i>(+ accusative)</i></li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>myndgian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mindful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gamundīgaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>senseless, witless</li></ol><p>From <i>ġemynd</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call to mind, remember, mention</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>mynegian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to determine, resolve</li><li>to mean, purpose, design</li><li>to destine or condition a lot to</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>myntan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hinder, obstruct, force, trouble</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>myrran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġemȳþe</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gamunþiją</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>mūþ</i>. More at <i>ġemȳþe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouth (<i>of a river</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gamunþī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gamunþiją</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>mūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>equal, like</li><li>well-matched, suited; fitting</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gamak</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>mæc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ġemaca</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gamakkjō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gamakjô</i>, variant/diminutive of <i>&#42;gamakô</i>. More at <i>ġemaca</i>.</p><p>Alternatively from <i>ġemæc</i> (“equal”) +&lrm; <i>-ca</i> (diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġemæċċa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġemæċċa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to madden, make foolish</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;mǣdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>common, mutual</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellowship</li><li>intercourse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gamainī</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>mǣne</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>mēne</i>, Old Saxon <i>gimēni</i>, Old Dutch <i>gemeini</i>, Old High German <i>gimeini</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌼𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (gamains), also with Latin <i>commūnis</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>common, general</li></ol><p>From <i>ġemǣne</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) common noun</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>community</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gamainiskapi</i>, equivalent to <i>ġemǣne</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>gemeenschap</i> and German <i>Gemeinschaft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to communicate, share</li><li>to marry</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>border, boundary</li><li>limit</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gamairiją</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>mǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġemǣre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make known or famous</li><li>to celebrate, glorify, extol</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>mǣrsian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magnificence</li><li>celebration</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġemǣrsian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fatten</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>mæstan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fattened</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;impersonal) to dream; to occur (to one) in a dream ( <i>+ dative</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>mǣtan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>meet; of suitable dimensions; made to fit</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gamātī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gamētijaz</i>, <i>&#42;gamētiz</i>, <i>&#42;mētiz</i> (“reasonable; moderate; appreciable”), equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>mǣte</i> (related to <i>metan</i>). Cognate with Old High German <i>gimāzi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġemētan</i> (“to meet”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>still, yet</li><li>again, further</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>short, direct, near (in distance)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ju</i>.</p><p>At least partly, a variant of <i>ġēan</i></p><p>Ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gagniz</i> (“near, close”), see <i>&#42;gagin</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeġn-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>yet</li><li>still</li><li>further</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make bare or naked; to strip</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ganakwadōn</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;nacodian</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>ginackutōn</i>, <i>ginackotōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġenōmen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to name</li><li>to nominate, appoint</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>namian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeond-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>enough, sufficiently, abundantly, often</li></ol><p>Related to ġenog.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġenēalǣċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to approach, draw near</li><li>to adhere</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>nēalǣċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġener</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a companion; associate; vassal</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ganaut</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ganautaz</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>nēat</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġenīedan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nephew</li><li>a son of a cousin</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>nefa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give somebody or something a name; to name</li><li>to use a name of somebody or something; to name</li><li>to call upon the name of somebody; to invoke</li><li>to address somebody by name</li><li>to mention by name; to mention or relate</li><li>to nominate; to name to a position</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>nemnan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>ginemnen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nameable, knowable</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġenemnend</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. See <i>ġenemnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to visit</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>nēosian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a refuge; protection; asylum; sanctuary</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>ner</i>. Cognate with Middle Low German <i>genēr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġenerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġenerienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to save, deliver, take away, set free, preserve, defend</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ganazjaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>nerian</i>. Akin to Old Saxon <i>ginerian</i>, Old High German <i>ginerien</i>, <i>ginerren</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġenerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġenerienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place of refuge; asylum</li></ol><p>From <i>ġener</i> +&lrm; <i>stede</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to preserve, save, escape from; flee</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ganesaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>nesan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>ginesan</i>, Old High German <i>ginesan</i> (German <i>genesen</i>), Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌽𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (ganisan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to venture, attempt, strive</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>nēþan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeniþerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go, pass</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gangijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gangijaną</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gaggjan, “to travel, journey”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A host or band (of armed men)</li><li>A passage; drain; toilet.</li><li>An outhouse; a building used as a lavatory.</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>current, going, valid</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from Old Norse <i>gengi</i> (“help”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gangiją</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>genġe</i> (“gang, flock, company”).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gangijā</i>, from <i>&#42;gangan</i> (“to go; to defecate”).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gangī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gangiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġenīedan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to force</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>nīedan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to renew, make new, change</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>nīewian</i> (“renew”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a daughter of a cousin</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>nift</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sufficiency</li><li>abundance, plenty, fullness</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>plentiful, profuse</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeniht</i> +&lrm; <i>-ful</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become night, grow dark</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>nihtian</i> (“to become night”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>abundantly</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeniht</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abundant, plentiful, fruitful</li><li>satisfied</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeniht</i> +&lrm; <i>-sum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġenihtsum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overflow in or with, to have abundance</li><li>to be abundant</li><li>to be enough, to suffice</li><li>to have enough, to be satisfied with</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġenihtsum</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>abundantly, plentifully</li><li>sufficiently</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġenihtsum</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ġenihtsumlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abundance as a condition or an amount</li><li>sufficiency</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġenihtsum</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġenihtsumnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġenihtsumnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abundance</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġenihtsumian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeniht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grasp, comprehend</li><li>to take as a wife or queen</li><li>to receive, accept, retain, obtain what is handed over or paid</li><li>to lay hold of, steal</li><li>to take, seize, capture by violence</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ganemaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>niman</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>giniman</i>, Middle Dutch <i>genēmen</i>, Old High German <i>gineman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeniman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mist, vapours, darkness, obscurity</li><li>a cloud (in the sky), a storm-cloud</li></ol><p><i>ge-</i> +&lrm; <i>nip</i>, cognate with Old High German <i>nebul</i>, Old Norse <i>nifl</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nébʰos</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to darken, become dark</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>nīpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġenīwod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġenīewian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring down, bring low; to humble, humiliate</li><li>to subdue</li><li>to condemn</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ganiþerōn</i>, variant of <i>&#42;ganiþrijan</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>niþerian</i>. Cognate with Old Dutch <i>ginitheren</i>, <i>genitheron</i>, Old High German <i>ginidaren</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeniþerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>enough, sufficient</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>enough, sufficiently</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ganōg</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ganōgaz</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>enōch</i>, Old Saxon <i>ginōg</i>, Old Dutch <i>ginuog</i>, Old High German <i>ginuog</i>, Old Norse <i>gnógr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġenōg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġenam</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be sufficient, be enough, suffice</li><li>to not lack</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ganuganą</i> (“to be permitted, be enough”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂neḱ-</i> (“to reach, attain”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>nōgia</i> (“to be enough”), Old Saxon <i>ginuht</i> from the unrecorded verb <i>&#42;ginugan</i>, Old High German <i>ginuogen</i> (“to suffice”), Old Norse <i>nógja</i> (“to suffice”), Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌽𐍉𐌷𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (ganōhjan, “to be content”), Old English <i>ġenēah</i> (“sufficiency, abundance”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġenīedan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeniht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġenihtful</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġenihtsum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġenihtsumian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġenihtsumlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġenihtsumnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeniht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to make use of, enjoy</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>nyttian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeniþerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at some former time: once, before</li><li>already</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ju</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yoke</li><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;poetic</i>) help, rescue</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;juk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;juką</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yugóm</i>.</p><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>ēoc</i> (“help, safety”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to preserve, save</li><li>to yoke</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sobbing</li><li>hiccup</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ancient times, days of yore</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiefan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeofon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to offer, sacrifice</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>offrian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) ocean, sea</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) flood, deluge</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;geban</i> (“sea, ocean”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>geban</i>, <i>gevan</i> (“sea”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Geofon)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>ġeofon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a vessel</li></ol><p>From <i>ġeofon</i> (“sea, ocean”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”), literally “sea-house”</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sea-wave, billow</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeofon</i> +&lrm; <i>ȳþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiefu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġeoguþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>juggler</li><li>magician; sorcerer; wizard</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gug-</i> (“to laugh”). Compare Old High German <i>gougalāri</i>, <i>goukalāri</i> (“magician; juggler; sorcerer”) (Modern German <i>Gaukler</i>), Old Norse <i>kuklari</i> (“juggler; wizard”), Medieval Latin <i>caucleārius</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeoguþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeoguþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeoguþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġeoguþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>youth (quality or state of being young)</li><li>youth (young person or people)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jugunþi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jugunþiz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>jogethe</i>, Old Saxon <i>juguth</i>, Old Dutch <i>iuginde</i>, and Old High German <i>jugund</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>youth, adolescence</li></ol><p>From <i>ġeoguþ</i> (“youth”) +&lrm; <i>-hād</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġēoldæġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġēol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġēoldæġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġēoldæġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġēol</i></li></ol><p>Apparently from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jehhwl</i>, alteration of <i>&#42;jehwl</i>, with irregular doubling of a velar before /w/. This /w/ then became syllabic /u/ between consonants. Neither sound change applied consistently, but both are known to have occurred in a few words. The exact same development happened to <i>hweohhol</i>, variant of <i>hwēol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġēol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Christmas, Yule</li></ol><p>Either inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jehwl</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jehwlą</i> or its plural <i>&#42;jeulō</i>, or else borrowed from the Norse cognate <i>jól</i>, from the Proto-Germanic plural.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>two-month period covering December and January</li></ol><p>Ultimately derived from <i>ġēol</i>. See there for more.</p><p>Bede stated that the name <i>gēola</i> derived from a term for the winter solstice.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeoloca</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a day of Christmas</li><li>Christmas Day</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jehwladagaz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>jóladagr</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġēol</i> +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>yellow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gelwaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yolk</li></ol><p>From <i>ġeolu</i> (“yellow”) +&lrm; <i>-ca</i> (diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>yellow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gelu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gelwaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰelh₃-</i> (“green, yellow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jaundice</li></ol><p>From <i>ġeolu</i> (“yellow”) +&lrm; <i>ādl</i> (“disease”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>yellow-white, yellow with a hint of white (glosses Latin <i>gilvus</i> (“pale yellow, dun”))</li></ol><p>From <i>ġeolu</i> (“yellow”) +&lrm; <i>hwīt</i> (“white”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>the color orange (literally "yellow-red")</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gelwaraud</i>, equivalent to <i>ġeolu</i> +&lrm; <i>rēad</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become yellow</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Christmas Eve</li><li>the evening before Christmas</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jehwlaēbanþs</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>jólaaptann</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeōmrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeōmrung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sad, sorrowful, mournful</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jāmar</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jēmaraz</i> (“miserable, sorrowful”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yem-</i> (“to hold, match, defeat”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>jāmar</i>, Old High German <i>jāmar</i> (German substantive <i>Jammer</i> (“misery”)). More at <i>yammer</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to complain</li><li>to be sad, lament</li><li>to bewail, moan</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jāmarōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jēmarōną</i> (“to show misery or sadness”), equivalent to <i>ġeōmor</i> (“sad”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>jāmarōn</i> (“to complain, sigh”), Old Norse <i>amra</i> (“to howl, yammer”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>groaning, moaning, lamentation</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġeōmrian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that, yon</li></ol><p>From an unstressed form of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jain</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jainaz</i>, whence also Old High German <i>jenēr</i>, Old Norse <i>hinn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>through, throughout (+accusative)</li><li>over</li><li>up to, as far as</li><li>during</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>yonder</li><li>thither</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jend-</i>, <i>&#42;jand-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;i-</i> (“a pronomial stem”). Akin to Dutch <i>ginds</i>, Gothic <i>𐌾𐌰𐌹𐌽𐌳</i> (jaind, “thither”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>beyond, exceeding; entirely; excessively</li><li>expressing duration or continuation; throughout, through</li><li>over, up to</li></ol><p>From <i>ġeond</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>beyond [<i>with</i> accusative]</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to breathe upon</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>blāwan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to overspread, extend over</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>brǣdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to saturate (with liquor) (+ of)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>drenċan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to go through, pervade</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to penetrate, permeate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>faran</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fill throughout, permeate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>fēolan</i> (“to reach, come, pass”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to travel through, traverse</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>fēran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow through</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>flōwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go through or about, perambulate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>gangan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cover by pouring</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spread or diffuse over a surface</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spread through, pour into, infuse</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġēotan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to harden or temper thoroughly</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>hierdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to turn or pass through, traverse</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>hweorfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġeondhweorfeþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeondhierdan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeondyrnan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move through or over</li><li>to flow over</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>lācan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;accusative) to water, irrigate</li><li>(transitive,&#32;accusative) to wet or moisten thoroughly</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>leċċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light giver</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) the Messiah; Jesus Christ</li></ol><p>From <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>lēohtend</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to enlighten, illuminate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>līhtan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mingle throughout, confuse</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>menġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;accusative) to smoke thoroughly, fill with smoke</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>rēċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;accusative) to sow, scatter, or spread around; to disseminate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>sāwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġeondsċēawaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to look through, at, or about; to survey</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>scēawian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;accusative) to shine upon, illuminate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>scīnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to go through or about; to traverse</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċrīþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to search thoroughly, investigate</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pervade</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to overspread</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>sendan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to see beyond or through; to have wide-ranging perspective on</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to search through, examine</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to discuss</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>smēaġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to sprinkle over</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>sprenġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become diffuse, spread about</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>springan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;accusative) to spit, squirt, or syringe through</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>spǣtan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeondstirian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeondstyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeondstirian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scatter about, sprinkle</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>strēdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeondstrēdan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeondstyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeondstyrienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move or stir violently</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>styrian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeondstyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeondstyrienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush through or over (a surface)</li><li>to invade</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>swōgan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to wade thoroughly or persistently through (+ þurh) something</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;idiomatic) to study (+ þurh) something</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>wadan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to look or see through; to look over</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to look around or about; to take in a broad perspective (+ ġeond)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>wlītan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;accusative,&#32;of motion) to run over or through something</li><li>(transitive,&#32;accusative,&#32;figurative) to run over information</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>yrnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think over, consider, contemplate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeond-</i> +&lrm; <i>þenċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġeondþenċeþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>young</li><li>new, recent</li><li><i>(in the superlative)</i> latest, last</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gang</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jung</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jungaz</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>jung</i>, Old Saxon <i>jung</i> (Low German <i>jung</i>), Dutch <i>jong</i>, Old High German <i>junc</i> (German <i>jung</i>), Old Norse <i>ungr</i> (Swedish <i>ung</i>), Gothic <i>𐌾𐌿𐌲𐌲𐍃</i> (juggs); and with Latin <i>juvenis</i> (“young”), Old Irish <i>oac</i> (“young”), Albanian <i>vogël</i>, <i>vogl</i> (“small, young”), Russian <i>ю́ный</i> (júnyj, “youthful”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;geang</i>, first and third person singular indicative preterite of <i>&#42;gangan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boy</li></ol><p>From <i>ġeong</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>; compare German <i>Junge</i> (“boy”) and German Low German <i>Junge</i> (“boy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>youthful</li></ol><p>From <i>ġeong</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a youth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jungilingaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġeong</i> +&lrm; <i>-ling</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġinian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to that place (over there); thither; yonder</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jainar</i>, equivalent to <i>ġeon</i> +&lrm; <i>-re</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take up, take to oneself, receive</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;geupan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;geupaną</i> (“to be bent, arch, be hollow or concave, be deep”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰewb(ʰ)-</i> (“to bend, stoop, move”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open</li><li>to manifest, reveal</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>openian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The common mallow (<i>Malva sylvestris</i>)</li></ol><p>From an unknown first element, perhaps <i>&#42;eormen</i> (“world, earth”) or a derivative of an old <i>z</i>-stem <i>&#42;germ</i> (“manure”), and <i>lēaf</i> (“leaf”); compare Old High German <i>germarrun</i>, Alemannic German <i>Germere</i>, and German <i>Germer</i> (“white hellebore”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeormanlēaf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeormanlēaf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeormanlēaf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eager</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gern</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gernaz</i> (“willing, eager”), whence also Old Saxon <i>gern</i>, Old High German <i>gern</i>, Old Norse <i>gjarn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to desire, beg, yearn</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;girnijan</i> (“to be eager for, desire”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;girnijaną</i> (“to desire, want”), from <i>&#42;gernaz</i> (“eager, willing”) (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰer-</i> (“to yearn for”)) + <i>&#42;-janą</i> (suffix forming factitive verbs from adjectives).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġeorn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>eagerly, keenly, willingly</li><li>well</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gernō</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġeorn</i> (“eager”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i> (“-ly”, adverbial suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eager</li><li>full of desire</li><li>strenuous</li><li>anxious</li></ol><p><i>ġeorn</i> (“eager”) +&lrm; <i>-ful</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeornfulnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eagerness</li><li>earnestness</li><li>diligence</li></ol><p>From <i>ġeornful</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeornfulnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeornfulnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ġeornful</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>eagerly, readily, earnestly, willingly</li></ol><p>From <i>ġeornlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ġeorn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chatter</li><li>to creak</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gerran</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gerraną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiestran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiestran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiestrandæġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour</li><li>to shed (blood, sweat, tears)</li><li>to cast, found (metal)</li><li>alternative form of <i>ġietan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;geutan</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;getaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰed-</i> (“take, seize, grasp”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>jeta</i>, Old High German <i>gezan</i>, <i>gezzan</i>, Old Norse <i>geta</i> (whence English <i>get</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) friend of old or former times</li></ol><p>From <i>ġeō</i> (“before”) +&lrm; <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeocsa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a head cold; catarrh</li></ol><p>As a feminine noun, taken from <i>ġeposu</i>&nbsp;pl (literally “(the) snorts”), plural of <i>ġepos</i>&nbsp;n (“a sneeze, snort”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pusą</i> (“heavy breathing, sneeze, snort”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pusōną</i>, <i>&#42;pusjaną</i> (“to blow, snort”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bew-</i> (“to blow, swell”). Related to Middle Low German <i>pûsten</i> (“to snort”), Middle High German <i>phūsen</i> (“to sneeze, snort”), Old Swedish <i>pýsa</i> (“to snort”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lock up, impound</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of water) to stem the flow of</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;pyndan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġēar</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>council, judgement, wisdom, reason</li><li>condition</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>skilful, wise, clever, straight</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roar, howl</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;rār</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rair</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rairą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;rey-</i> (“to scream; roar; howl; bark”). More at Old English <i>rārian</i> (“to roar”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġierd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rob, steal</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>rēafian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rule, government</li><li>management, direction</li><li>order</li><li>a condition of order, a time of quiet</li><li>explanation, account</li><li>a tumult</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;garek</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>reċċan</i>. Compare Old High German <i>ungireh</i> (“confusion, tumult”).</p><p>The sense meaning "tumult" is likely an ellipsis or shortening of <i>unġerec</i> or <i>inġerec</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to explain, relate, put forth</li><li>to interpret</li><li>to narrate, tell</li><li>to stretch out, extend</li><li>to reach out to someone</li><li>to control, rule</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>reċċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġereċednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narrative, history</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġereċednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġereċednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to explain</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>recenian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>explanatory</li></ol><p><i>ġereċċan</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i> or <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;reclīċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a reeve or official with local jurisdiction under the king; the chief magistrate of a district</li></ol><p>Allegedly from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;garāfijō</i>, but this is unlikely on phonological grounds, since the West Saxon form has /eː/ and the Northumbrian and Mercian forms have /øː/. The Oxford English Dictionary suggests a connection to the second elements of <i>seċġrōf</i> and <i>stæfrōf</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>rēfa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an ornament</li><li>an instrument</li><li>a building</li></ol><p>From <i>ġereġnian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put, dispose, adorn</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;garaginōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garaginōną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>reġnian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġierela</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>grin</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to peel, strip off</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġereġne</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>ġerȳne</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ġerēnian</i>, a variant of <i>ġereġnian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġereġnian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to coagulate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;garannijan</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;rennan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conspire, devise</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>rēonian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a conspiracy, plot</li></ol><p>From <i>ġerēonian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speech, language</li><li>voice</li><li>a meal, refection, food</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a language</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>reord</i>.</p><p>Parallel to Etymology 1. Compare Old Norse <i>greddir</i> (“nourisher”, poetic).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġereordian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give food to, to feed</li><li>to feast, dine, to take food <i>(often with an accusative reflexive pronoun)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ġereord</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>refection, meal, refreshment</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailor, rower</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>rēþra</i> (“rower”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>right; just</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is right, just, or fair; justice; equity</li><li>a right</li><li>rule; regulation; law</li><li>duty; office</li><li>truth</li><li>direction; a straight line</li><li>account; reckoning</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garehtaz</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>riht</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (garaihts), German, Luxembourgish and Dutch <i>gerecht</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garihtiją</i>, <i>&#42;garehtuz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>girihti</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to correct, reform, amend, set right or straight, direct</li><li>to right, set in order, make clear</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;garihtijan</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>rihtan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>girihtian</i>, Old Dutch <i>girihten</i>, Old High German <i>girihten</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (garaihtjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>right; due; justice</li><li>(<i>religious</i>) Rite; office</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garihtiją</i> (“right, justice”). Cognate with Old High German <i>girihti</i>. More at right.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>number</li><li>reckoning</li><li>class</li><li>calendar, diary</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>rīm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to number, reckon</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>rīman</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>girīman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>book of numerals</li><li>calendar</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġerīm</i> +&lrm; <i>bōc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arithmetic</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>rīmcræft</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blend, run together, coagulate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;garinnan</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>rinnan</i>. Literally, "to flow or run together." Cognate with Old High German <i>girinnan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (garinnan). Compare German <i>gerinnen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġereord</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>what is fitting; the proper or appropriate thing</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>river</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;garīþī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garīþiją</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>rīþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>land encompassing all of the Germanic speaking world</li><li>alternative form of <i>Ġermanie</i>: Germania (a medieval region of Central Europe, occupied by those speaking Germanic languages, or a portion of it)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Germānia</i>, q.v.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a geographic region of Europe, comprising the lands occupied by those who speak Germanic languages, or the part of this portion in Continental Europe</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Germānia</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeornan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>græs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>græshoppa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gærsum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gærsum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>room, space</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>rūm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prepared, ready</li><li>usual</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġerihtan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mystery</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garūniją</i>, derived from <i>&#42;rūnō</i> (“whisper, secret, rune”) (→ Old English <i>rūn</i>). Cognate with Old High German <i>&#42;girūni</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌿𐌽𐌹</i> (garūni).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>secret counsel</li><li>mystery</li></ol><p>Perhaps derived from <i>ġerȳne</i>; or from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;garūnī</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>rūn</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gerisena</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġerād</i> (“counsel, judgement, wisdom, reason”).</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to advise, to give counsel</li><li>to arrange, direct, set in order, ordain</li><li>to consult for</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣdan</i> (“to advise”)</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garaidijaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣdan</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (garaidjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ready, prompt, prepared; ready to ride, mounted (on a horse)</li><li>skilled</li><li>simple, plain</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garaidaz</i> (“arranged, ready”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġerēfa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strive against, oppose; withstand, resist</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gasakan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gasakaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sacan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gisachan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐌰𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (gasakan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to satisfy, fill</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sadian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesealdnis</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġesǣliġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġesǣliġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to collect, bring together, gather</li><li>to congregate, come together, assemble</li><li>to join, draw together, unite</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>samnian</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gisamanōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a meeting, congregation, or assembly</li></ol><p>From <i>ġesamnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sow</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sāwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>provided with soul</li><li>endowed with life</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeocsa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>separation</li><li>distinction, difference</li><li>reasoning, meaning</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reason (a reason or the faculty of reason)</li><li>distinction</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reasonable</li><li>discreet</li><li>calculated, fixed</li></ol><p><i>ge-</i> +&lrm; <i>sceadan</i> (“to distinguish, separate, decide”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to distinguish, discern, decide, separate</li><li>to shed, scatter</li><li>to deprive of</li><li>to expound, write out a narrative</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċeādan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shadowy, shady, obscure</li><li>reasonable, rational</li></ol><p>From <i>sċeadu</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p><p>From <i>ġesċēad</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>reasonably, rationally</li></ol><p>From <i>gescead</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>logical, rational, reasonable</li></ol><p>From <i>ġesċeād</i> +&lrm; <i>-wīs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wisdom</li></ol><p><i>ġesċēadwīs</i> (“wise”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creation</li><li>creature</li><li>institution, destiny</li><li>element (the four classical elements)</li><li>decree, destiny, fate, doom, condition</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaskaftiz</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċeaft</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>giscaft</i> (German <i>Geschäft</i>), Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐌺𐌰𐍆𐍄𐍃</i> (gaskafts).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shape (external form)</li><li>creation</li><li>creature</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) fate, destiny</li><li>genitals</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaskapą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kep-</i> (“to split, cut”). Compare Old Saxon <i>giskap</i> (“creation, destiny”), Old Norse <i>skap</i> (“creation, fate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesċeapennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creation</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesċeapennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesċeapennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) the fated moment, the destined time (of death)</li></ol><p><i>ġesċeap</i> +&lrm; <i>hwīl</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>aptly, properly, well-fitting</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċeap</i> (“shape”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exhibit, manifest, spew</li><li>to behold, see, perceive, look round upon, view</li><li>to consider, look at with care</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċēawian</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>giscouwōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesċeaft</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to occur, happen, befall; to fall to</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaskehan</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scrape little by little</li><li>to carve or tear in pieces</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċeorpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesċot</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to create</li><li>to shape; form, mold</li><li>to destine a lot to</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaskapjaną</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>giskeppian</i>, Old High German <i>giscaffan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐌺𐌰𐍀𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gaskapjan). Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċieppan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sharpen</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċierpan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clothes, clothing</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċeorp</i> +&lrm; <i>-el</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in reference to an accent</i>) acute</li></ol><p>Akin to Old English <i>sċearp</i> (“sharp”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to protect</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċildan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protector</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċildend</i> or <i>ġesċildan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protection</li></ol><p><i>ġesċilded</i> (“protected”) + <i>-nes</i> (“-ness”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine upon, illuminate</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċīnan</i> (“to shine”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>giskīnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to provide with ships</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesċieppan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesċierpla</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(of a foot) having a shoe on, (of a person) wearing shoes</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaskōhōd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a projectile weapon</li><li>a shot, shooting</li><li>chancel</li><li>a scot (local tax), contribution</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċot</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cause to shrink</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;sċrenċan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skrankijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to contract, shrink, dry up</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċrincan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clothe</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċrȳdan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expel, eject (with violence)</li><li>to impel, drive, beget action</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċūfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesċildan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesċieppan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesċierpla</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesċeaft</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġīese</i></li></ol></html>:/jeˈseːɑ̯/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Northumbrian</i>) alternative form of <i>ġesēon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a giving</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeseald</i> +&lrm; <i>-nis</i>.</p></html>:/jeˈseːɑ̯n/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesēon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>juicy, succulent</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēaw</i> (“juice”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to seek (after)</li><li>to visit, go to, come to, approach</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gasōkijaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēċan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gisōkian</i>, Old High German <i>gisuohhen</i>, <i>gisuochen</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐍉𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gasōkjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say, tell, relate, declare, prove</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>seċġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conversation</li><li>speech</li><li>statement, report, story, legend</li></ol><p>Akin to <i>seċġan</i> (“to say”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reconcile</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sehtlian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) companion, comrade</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give</li><li>to grant or bestow (as by God)</li><li>to deliver, hand to</li><li>to hand over</li><li>to give in exchange</li><li>to give what may be demanded; to pay tribute, tax, fine or compensation</li><li>to give up, surrender, lose</li><li>to give forth or put forth words or statements; to make sound</li><li>to offer, present, or show for consideration</li><li>to assign, appoint</li><li>to make have</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sellan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle</li><li>to make peace with</li><li>to reconcile</li><li>to satisfy</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēman</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send, dispatch for an errand, send off</li><li>to send into prison or exile</li><li>to cast or throw a thing</li><li>to send forth sound; to utter a word</li><li>to send to rest, put, lay</li><li>to send a messenger or a message</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sendan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gisenten</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gasandjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesīene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see</li><li>to appear, look</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gasehwaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sitting, lying in wait, ambush</li><li>seat, habitation, house</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>set</i>.</p><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>set</i> (“seat”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesetnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesetnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesetnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesetnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>constitution; law</li><li>decree</li><li>ceremony; religion</li><li>position</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>ġesetednes</i>, equivalent to <i>ġeseted</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>. Compare Old High German <i>gisezzida</i> (“establishment, regulation, order, decree”), Old High German <i>gisezzidī</i> (“establishment, regulation, composition, structure”), German <i>Gesetz</i> (“law”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesetnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confirm, restore, appoint, decree, settle</li><li>to set, put, fix, determine the position in a series of objects</li><li>to possess, occupy</li><li>to compose, set together, constitute</li><li>to situate, found (a building or town)</li><li>to post, station</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gasatjaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>settan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gisettian</i>, Old High German <i>gisezzen</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gasatjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>visible</li></ol><p>From <i>ġesewen</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesēþnys</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesēþung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to declare true; to state as fact; to assert, affirm</li><li>to prove; to show the truth of something by evidence</li><li>to bear witness; to attest</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>somebody who affirms or asserts</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġesēþan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>, else <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēþend</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an attestation</li><li>an affirmation</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġesēþan</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an assertion</li><li>an affirmation</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġesēþan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html>:/jeˈseːɑ̯n/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesēan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesibb</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>related</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a relative</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gasibi</i>, oblique form <i>*gasibbj-</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gisibbi</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sibb</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġesibb</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pacify, appease, gladden</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sibbian</i> (“to reconcile”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġesibb</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arrange</li><li>to determine</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;ġesidu</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to salt</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>visible</li><li>evident, manifest, apparent</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>-sīene</i>. Second element from earlier <i>*-sēanī</i> or <i>*-seahnī</i>. Further etymology uncertain. Some seemingly derived forms may instead reflect Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-siunī</i> or Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;-sānī</i>; cf. Old High German <i>ougsiuni</i> (“visible to the eye”), but also Old High German <i>seltsāni</i> (“rarely seen, strange”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>victory</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;sig</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gesihþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġesiehþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sight; vision</li><li>sight of a person or object</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sihþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sink</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sincan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gisinkan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐌹𐌲𐌲𐌵𐌰𐌽</i> (gasiggqan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesīene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(countable,&#32;uncountable) marriage</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sinsċipe</i>, from <i>sin-</i> + <i>-sċipe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesēon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit</li><li>to take a seat, come to rest, sit down, recline</li><li>to settle, dwell, reside, stay, live in</li><li>to occupy, inhabit</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sittan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gisittian</i>, Old High German <i>gisizzen</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (gasitan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>companion, comrade</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gasinþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gasinþô</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sīþ</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gisīth</i>, Old High German <i>gisindo</i> (German <i>Gesinde</i>), Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐌹𐌽𐌸𐌰</i> (gasinþa), <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐌹𐌽𐌸𐌾𐌰</i> (gasinþja).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>genteel, well-born</li></ol><p>From <i>ġesīþ</i> (“companion”) +&lrm; <i>-cund</i> (“born”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>society, fellowship</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gasinþaskapi</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġesīþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hit; strike, punch</li><li>to kill</li><li>to slaughter (an animal)</li><li>(<i>of a snake, insect, or arachnid</i>) to bite or sting</li><li>to pitch (a tent)</li><li>to mint (coins, money)</li><li>to move quickly and suddenly</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>slēan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gislahan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sleeved</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>slīef</i> (“sleeve”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pat; caress, soothe</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>smācian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intrigue</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>smēag</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to search</li><li>to examine</li><li>to consider</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>smēaġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesmēag</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gǣsne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literal,&#32;intransitive) to shrivel or shrivel up</li><li>(figurative,&#32;intransitive) to faint, fall out, collapse</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;sneorcan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nasal mucus; snot</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snuttuz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>snuz</i>, <i>snuzza</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cooking</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesamnian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prove the truth of; to bear witness</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sōþian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a joining, clasp, fastening together</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entice, allure, draw</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>spanan</i> (“to entice”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to span, clasp, fasten, join; attach; link</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>spannan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gispannan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shut</li></ol><p><i>ge-</i> +&lrm; <i>sparrian</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sparrijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sparrijaną</i> (“to block”). Related to <i>&#42;sparrô</i> (“beam, log”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have success; to succeed</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>spēdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to succeed, prosper</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe</i> +&lrm; <i>spēdsum</i> +&lrm; <i>ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vicar</li><li>substitute; representative</li></ol><p>Derivative of <i>spelian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy, dissipate</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>spillan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġespyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġespirienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġespyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġespyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġespirienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to succeed <i>(+ genitive at something)</i> <i>(impersonal, with dative of person)</i></li><li>to prosper, thrive</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaspōan</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>spōwan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gispuon</i>, <i>gispuoen</i> (“to succeed”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counsellor</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>spreca</i> (“speaker”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak (with); to agree</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> + <i>sprecan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gasprakją</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>sprekan</i>, Old Dutch <i>sprekan</i>, Old High German <i>sprehhan</i>. See also Dutch <i>gesprek</i> and German <i>Gespräch</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a conversation, discourse, speech, advice</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sprǣċ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gisprāchi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speech, discourse, conversation</li><li>advice</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eloquent</li><li>affable</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gasprākī</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gisprāchi</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gasprākī</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gisprāhhi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġespyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġespyrienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to track, search, seek</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>spyrian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġespyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġespyrienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiest</i> (“guest”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remain, last, exist, stand still</li><li>to hold oneself upright</li><li>to be steady, remain undisturbed</li><li>to oppose, stand against, oppress</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gastandaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>standan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gistandan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (gastandan). Compare Old High German <i>gistantan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġestaþolung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġestaþelode</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġestaþelaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġestaþolian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġestaþoliend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġestaþolung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to establish, secure from disturbances</li><li>to carry into effect</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>staþolfæstnian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fix, place firmly, set</li><li>to settle, lodge</li><li>to build, erect</li><li>to found, establish</li><li>to make firm, stable, or effectual</li><li>(<i>literal&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) to restore something fallen or lost</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>staþol</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a founder, establisher</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>staþolian</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firmness, stability</li><li>placing, ordering</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>staþolian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>constitution, frame, foundation</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>steall</i>. Akin to German <i>Gestell</i>, Middle High German <i>gestelle</i>, Old High German <i>gistelli</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring to a standstill; halt</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;stedegian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stallion</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>stōdhors</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gæsthūs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leap, spring</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>stiellan</i> (“leap”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to climb, ascend</li><li>to set out</li><li>to attain a place spiritually</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>stīgan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gistīgan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐍄𐌴𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (gasteigan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rest, cease, mute, quiet, allay, make still; bestill</li><li>to restrain, still, stop, calm</li><li>to stop motion, silence</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gastillijan</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>stillan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gistillian</i>, Old High German <i>gistillen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smell (sense with the nose&#59; transitive)</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>stincan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiestlīþnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiestlīþnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasure, wealth</li><li>production, procreation</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>strēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>begetting</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) genitive case</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>strīenan</i> (“to beget, generate”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get, gain, acquire</li><li>to beget, procreate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>strȳnan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġestrīenendlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noise, stun, crash</li><li>whirlwind</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;stun</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>healthy</li><li>sound</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;sund</i>. The latter descends from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sundaz</i>. Compare to Old Saxon <i>gisund</i> and Old High German <i>gisunt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesundfull</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sound, whole, healthy, prosperous</li></ol><p>From <i>ġesund</i> +&lrm; <i>-full</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ġesund</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sunder, separate, divide</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sundrian</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gisuntarōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sup, sip, suck up, swallow</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sūpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lull to sleep</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>swebban</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swelling</li><li>tumor</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whirlpool, gulf, abyss</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gulf, chasm</li><li>abyss, pit</li><li>whirlpool</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>swelġe</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swalgī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swalgiz</i> (“open gape, gullet, throat”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swel-</i> (“to wash, wash down, gulp, swallow”). Cognate with Middle Low German <i>swalch</i>, <i>swelch</i> (“whirlpool”), Middle Dutch <i>swelch</i>, <i>zwelge</i>, <i>zwelgh</i> (“chasm, gape, gullet, throat”), Old Norse <i>svelgr</i> (“whirlpool, eddy, current, stream”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeswel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die, perish</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sweltan</i> (“to die”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause distress, fatigue</li><li>to ill-treat, harass, punish, torture</li><li>(<i>mentally</i>) to distress, disturb</li><li>(<i>materially</i>) to oppress</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>swenċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sorrow</li><li>affliction</li><li>tribulation</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeswenċed</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeswenċednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male cousin</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>swēor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloud, mist</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to darken</li><li>to be darkened, saddened, angry</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sweorcan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gloom</li></ol><p>From <i>ġesweorc</i> (“cloud, mist”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>filings</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to file, rub in order to get something off</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sweorfan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sisters (used as a plural of <i>sweostor</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sweostor</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeswutelian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeswutelian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swear an oath, promise</li><li>to confirm by oath</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>swerian</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>giswerian</i>, Old High German <i>giswerian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stop, quit</li><li>to fail</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>swīcan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male cousin</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;swiġra</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>melody</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaswiną</i>, <i>&#42;swiną</i> (“sound, melody”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swin-</i> (“to sound”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hard work: toil, labor</li><li>travail, hardship</li></ol><p>A deverbal of <i>swincan</i>, or from <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>swinc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work hard: labor, toil</li><li>to be afflicted with</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>swincan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) day of labour, work or toil; time of hardship</li></ol><p><i>ġeswinc</i> +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cunning, crafty</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crafty, cunning</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>swipor</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make strong</li><li>to confirm, comfort</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>swīþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reveal, show, declare, manifest, explain</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeswutelian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeswutelian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compliment</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>swǣs</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeswǣsnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place in which to wallow; mudhole</li></ol><p>Derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;seud-</i> (“mud; soil”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sew-</i> (“sap; moisture; juice”). Cognate with Middle Low German <i>sudde</i> (“marsh; marshy meadow”), Middle High German <i>sutte</i> (“mudhole; puddle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesidian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gesihþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gesihþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesundrian</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>syndrian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late West Saxon</i>) alternative form of <i>ġesīene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeseġen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesehtlian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to happen, take place, befall</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lucky</li><li>happy</li></ol><p>From <i>ge-</i> +&lrm; <i>sælig</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>happy, fortunate</li></ol><p>From <i>ġesǣliġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>happily</li></ol><p><i>ġesǣliġ</i> (“happy”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>happiness</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġesǣliġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesǣliġnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesǣliġnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesǣliġnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesǣliġlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>happiness, felicity, prosperity</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>sǣlþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeat</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġīet</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġīeta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to signify, denote, mean</li><li>(<i>literal</i>) to sign, mark</li><li>to mark as a witness; to set one's seal</li><li>to represent symbolically, be the symbol of</li><li>to prefigure</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>tācnian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indicative, significant</li><li>typical</li><li>symbolic</li></ol><p>From <i>ġetācnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>figuratively</li><li>symbolically</li></ol><p>From <i>ġetācniendlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġetācniendlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġetācniendlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mark, sign, token</li><li>gesture</li><li>signal, warning</li><li>figurative representation</li><li>verbal signification</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>tācnung</i>, else <i>ġetācnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġetæl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tell, number, reckon, consider</li><li>to account</li><li>to assign</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>talian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġēatan</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>ġietan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) instruments, apparatus</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;tawa</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>attested only in compounds</i>) alternative form of <i>ġetawa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġetawa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġetæl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġetæl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tent, pavilion; awning</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gateld</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>teld</i>. Cognate with Old Dutch <i>gitelt</i>, Old High German <i>gizelt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dyed, colored</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>telg</i> (“dye”) +&lrm; <i>-ed</i>, else the past participle of an otherwise unattested verb <i>&#42;telgan</i>, *<i>ġetelgan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tell, number, count, enumerate, reckon, assign</li><li>to esteem, consider, estimate</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>tellan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gitellian</i>, Old High German <i>gizellen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to temper, moderate</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>temprian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>near to, close to, approximate</li><li>pressing upon, oppressing</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;tenġe</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>bitengi</i> (“touching, reaching to, close, allied”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw, pull</li><li>to educate</li><li>to do, effect, cause</li><li>to determine (a course of action), to decide</li><li>to destine</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>tēon</i> (“to pull”).</p><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>tēon</i> (“to make; to furnish”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be tired, faint, or weary</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fail, languish, cease, perish</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;material) to get exhausted</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>tēorian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġetēorian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġetēorian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>defective, imperfect</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġetēorian</i> +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>, else <i>ġetēoriende</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exhaustion</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġetēorod</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>failing, fainting, languishing, weariness</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġetēorian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>toothed</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>tēþ</i> (“teeth”) +&lrm; <i>-ed</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to betide, happen</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>tīdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie, bind</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>tīeġan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to vex, provoke, oppress</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>tierġan</i> (“to vex”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġetīeġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġetilian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strive after</li><li>to gain, get by labour or striving, obtain, procure, acquire</li><li>to attend to, treat, cure [with genitive]</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gatilōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gatilōną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>tilian</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gizilōn</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍄𐌹𐌻𐍉𐌽</i> (gatilōn).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch</li><li>to reach, attain</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gatilljan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gatiljaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>tillan</i>. Compare Old High German <i>gizilēn</i> (“to hit, plunge, overthrow, hurl down”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gain</li></ol><p>From <i>ġetilian</i> +&lrm; <i>-þ</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>tilþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>timbran</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġetimbran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>building (both senses)</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>timbring</i> or <i>ġetimbran</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġetimbring</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to happen, to befall <i>(often + dative to something)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>tīma</i> (“time, hour”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġetynġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press upon, throng</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;tingan</i>; see <i>tengan</i> (“to press”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġetynġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġetynġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġetēorian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>military expedition or excursion; campaign</li><li>battle</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;toht</i>, related to <i>tohte</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stone (someone)</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>torfian</i> (“to stone”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pomp, parade, vainglory</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>tot</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abhor, feel repugnant toward</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>tregian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġetrīewe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loyal, faithful</li><li>trustworthy, true</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gatriuwī</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gitriuwi</i>, Old High German <i>gitriuwi</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>trīewe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rub down; wear down or wear away by rubbing</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>trifulian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trust, hope, believe</li><li>to make a treaty</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gatrūōn</i>, variant of <i>&#42;gatrūēn</i>; by surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>trūwian</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gitruoian</i>, <i>gitrūōn</i>, Old Dutch <i>gitrūon</i>, Old High German <i>gitrūēn</i>, <i>gitrūwēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confidence, trust</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġetrūwian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trust</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;tryccan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>faithful</li><li>content</li></ol><p>From <i>ġetryccan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġetrīewe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seem doubtful, be doubtful</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>twēonian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġetwinn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a twin</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>twinn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twin</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astrology</i>) Gemini (Zodiac sign)</li></ol><p>From the plural of <i>ġetwisa</i> (“twin”), translating Latin <i>Geminī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġetwisa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ġetwisan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, part</li><li>to stop, put an end to</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;twǣfan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġetīdan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a yoke of oxen</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhetoric</li></ol><p>From <i>ġetynġe</i> +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>skilful with the tongue, eloquent</li><li>rhetorical</li><li>talkative</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>eloquently, courteously</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>tynġe</i>. Compare Old Dutch <i>gitungel</i> (“eloquent”), Old High German <i>gizungal</i> (“eloquent, talkative”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eloquence</li></ol><p>From <i>ġetynġe</i> (“eloquent”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġetēorian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>number, series, reckoning, computation</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) grammatical number</li><li>company, race, tribe</li><li>a book of reckoning, register, catalog</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quick; ready; active</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ga-</i> + <i>&#42;talą</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>tæl</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>gital</i>, Old High German <i>gizal</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;talaz</i>, <i>&#42;gatalaz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gital</i>, Old High German <i>gizal</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commodity; profit; advantage</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;tǣsu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;taisį̄</i> (“foredeal, advantage, profit”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to subject</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>underþēodan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to allow, grant or bestow (+ <i>dat</i> (person) + <i>gen</i> (thing))</li><li>to concede (+ <i>dat</i> (person) + <i>gen</i> (thing))</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>unnan</i>. Cognate with North Frisian <i>gunne</i>, Old Saxon <i>giunnan</i> (Low German <i>günnen</i>), Dutch <i>gunnen</i>, Old High German <i>giunnan</i> (German <i>gönnen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disprove, refute, prove false</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>unsōþian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to weaken, sicken</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be or become weak or sick</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>untrumian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eject, banish</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>ūtian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to proceed fully into or throughout, wade or go into altogether (with <i>ymb</i>, <i>on</i>, or <i>in</i>)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to go through, pervade, penetrate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wadan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeweald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġewealdan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġewealdleþer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concern, hesitation, doubt, fear</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gawand</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;wand</i>, related to Old English <i>wandian</i> (“to hesitate”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>giwand</i> (“end, turning point, doubt, objection”), Old High German <i>giwant</i> (“state, condition”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;intransitive) to shrink or lessen</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to impair; infringe, annul</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wanian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wash; wash fully or completely</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gawaskan</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wascan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rolling, turning; motion, surging</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> + Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;walką</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;walkaną</i> (“to turn, roll”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go, traverse</li><li>to roll; roll together; press together</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ga-</i> (perfective prefix) + <i>&#42;walkaną</i> (“to twist, turn, roll about, stomp, full”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wolg-</i>, <i>&#42;welg-</i> (“to bend, twist, run, roll”), equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wealcan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>walchan</i> (“to full, press”), Old Norse <i>valka</i> (“to wander about”), Old English <i>weallian</i> (“to roam about, ramble”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>control</li><li>power</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gawald</i>; equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>weald</i>, derived from <i>wealdan</i> (“to control”). Cognate with Old High German <i>giwalt</i> (whence modern German <i>Gewalt</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rule <i>(+ genitive, dative, or accusative)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gawaldan</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wealdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rein</li></ol><p>From <i>ġeweald</i> (“control”) +&lrm; <i>leþer</i> (“leather (strap)”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow, wax, increase, grow up, be made or produced</li><li>to thrive, prosper; bloom, flourish, progress</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>weaxan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>giwahsan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bear, carry, move, proceed, go</li><li>to be the same in weight of a certain amount, weigh</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wegan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>giwegan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (gawigan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>everywhere</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>welhwær</i> (“everywhere”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġewealdleþer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disfigure, mar, blemish</li><li>to impair, diminish</li><li>to impair the quality of, corrupt, degrade, profane</li><li>to pollute, defile</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wemman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defilement</li><li>pollution</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġewemmednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fornicator, adulterer</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġewemman</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corruption, violation, profanation</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġewemman</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hope, expect, believe, suppose</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wēnan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a spiral shell</li><li>a snailshell, cochlea</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change, turn</li><li>to translate, incline, bring about</li><li>to bring to a condition, change into</li><li>to return, cause to move, wend</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wendan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>giwendian</i>, Old High German <i>giwenten</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gawandjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>work (something done or made)</li><li>fort, fortress</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>weorc</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>giwerk</i>, Old High German <i>giwerk</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌺𐌹</i> (gawaurki).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an instance of tossing or throwing</li><li>a heap</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>weorpan</i> (“to throw”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>perfective</i>) to throw and so to reach (+ preposition <i>on</i>, onto or into)</li><li>(<i>perfective</i>) to cast off altogether; to cast out (+ adverb <i>ūt</i>, out)</li><li>to turn one's self away from, avert</li><li>to go away, pass by, depart</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>weorpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeweorþod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġeweorþode</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become</li><li>to be (used to form the passive voice)</li><li>to happen</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>weorþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set price, value</li><li>to honour, worthy</li><li>to worship</li><li>to adorn, endow</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>weorþian</i> (“to worship”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weep, lament</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wēpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give way, fail</li><li>to depart, retire</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wīcan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lodge, dwell, encamp</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wīcian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weather</li><li>air temperature</li><li>storm, tempest</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gawidrī</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>weder</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>power, control, dominion</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;wield</i>. Origin uncertain. Perhaps from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gawaldī</i> (“power, might”), cognate with Old High German <i>giwalti</i>. Alternatively, perhaps a merger of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wald</i> (“might, power, authority”) and <i>&#42;waldi</i> (compare Old Saxon <i>giwald</i>&nbsp;f or n); or derived from Old English <i>ġewieldan</i> (“to exercise power, rule over”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exercise power over</li><li>to subdue, conquer</li><li>to seize, take</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wieldan</i> or <i>ġewield</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>powerful, mighty, victorious, in control</li><li>subject; under one's power or control; in one's possession</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wielde</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recover (from sickness, etc.)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;wierpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fortune, fate, destiny</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to marry, take a wife</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wīfian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weight</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gawihtī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġewield</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġewieldan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wish, desire, seek</li><li>to expect</li><li>to strive for</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wilnian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>desire</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ġewilnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġewinn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which has a curved, winding, or spiral shape; spiral</li><li>a winding, circuitous ascent</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wind</i> (“a winding; wrapping”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twist, weave, bend, wind</li><li>to go, turn about, revolve</li><li>to roll (together)</li><li>to unroll</li></ol><p>From <i>ge-</i> +&lrm; <i>windan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>giwintan</i>, Gothic <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;gawindan) (<i>𐌳𐌿𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (dugawindan)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>windwian</i> (“to fan”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>war</li><li>battle</li><li>warfare</li><li>conflict, struggle</li><li>(<i>Anglian</i>) labour, toil</li><li>gain, profit</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gawinn</i>, derived from <i>&#42;gawinnan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gawinnaną</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>giwin</i>, Old High German <i>giwin</i> (modern German <i>Gewinn</i> (“gain”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conquer, obtain, gain</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gawinnan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gawinnaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>winnan</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>giwinnan</i>, German <i>gewinnen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full-grown</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġewiss</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġewiss</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġewȳsċendlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>exactly</li></ol><p>From <i>gewīs</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ġewīslīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġewīslīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sure <i>(+ genitive of something)</i></li><li>certain, particular</li><li>unfailing</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for sure, certainly</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>certainty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gawissaz</i> (“certain”), equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; the Proto-Germanic past participle of <i>&#42;witaną</i> (whence Old English <i>witan</i>).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wiss</i>, Old Saxon <i>giwiss</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;giwis</i>, Old High German <i>giwiss</i>, Old Norse <i>viss</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to know, instruct, inform</li><li>to command, govern, direct</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wissian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġewiss</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>information</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ge-</i> +&lrm; <i>wiss</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġewitt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>witness (someone who testifies or has witnessed something)</li><li>accomplice</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gawitō</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wita</i>, Old Saxon <i>giwito</i>, and Old High German <i>giwizzo</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wita</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to depart</li><li>to know, understand, be familiar with, have knowledge of</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wītan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>giwītan</i>.</p><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>witan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġewītaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>witness (all senses)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>witnes</i> or <i>ġewita</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wits, senses, (right) mind</li><li>a sense</li><li>knowledge, understanding, consciousness</li><li>the conscience</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gawiti</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>giwitt</i> and Old High German <i>giwizzi</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>witt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wise, knowing</li><li>sane</li><li>conscious</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wittiġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make proud, exalt, enrich</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wlenċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to anger</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrāþ</i> (“anger”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to avenge, wreak</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gawrekan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gawrekaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrecan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thicket, group of shrubs</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;wrid</i> (“plant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writing or a writing</li><li>a statement, passage, deed, inscription, orthography</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>writ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write, give or bestow by writing</li><li>to write along with, write together, make a grant</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gawrītan</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrītan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a writer or composer</li></ol><p>From <i>ġewrit</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>written agreement</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġewrit</i> +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turn, change, vicissitude</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġewrixlian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change; alter the character or nature of</li><li>to exchange or give in compensation or requital for; compensate; reward</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wihslōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wihslōną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġewrixl</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crown, coronate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wuldorbēagian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glorify; to fill with glory; to make glorious</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wuldorfullian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glorify</li><li>to glory in; to exult, rejoice</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wuldrian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>custom</li><li>ritual</li><li>habit, wont</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gawunô</i> (“habit, wont”), equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wuna</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wound</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gawundōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gawundōną</i>; equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wundian</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>giwundon</i>, Middle Dutch <i>gewonden</i>, Middle High German <i>gewunden</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌿𐌽𐌳𐍉𐌽</i> (gawundōn).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>normal, common, ordinary, usual</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gawunalīk</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>gewoonlijc</i> (modern <i>gewoonlijk</i>), Old High German <i>giwonalīh</i> (modern <i>gewöhnlich</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>usually, normally, ordinarily</li></ol><p>From <i>ġewunelīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand by (someone), support</li><li>to be used/accustomed to (sometimes impersonal, with dative of person)</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wunian</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>giwunōn</i>, Old High German <i>giwonēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġewierpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeweorþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġewieldan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do, carry out (together)</li><li>to make, build (together)</li><li>to make into</li><li>to cause, accomplish (together)</li><li>to work, form, dispose, succeed in doing, get by working; produce</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gawirkijan</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġewyrċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fate, destiny</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrd</i> (“fate, destiny”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speech, conversation</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>-wyrde</i> (“word”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>eloquently verbatim</li></ol><p>From ġewyrde (“speech, conversation, sentence”) (<i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>-wyrde</i> + <i>-līċe</i>), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurdiją</i> + <i>&#42;-līka</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worker, doer</li><li>accomplice</li><li>coworker, fellow worker</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“wright”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġewierpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a smelling-box, perfume box</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;ġewyrt</i> +&lrm; <i>box</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to season with herbs, spice</li><li>to perfume</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrtian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wish; desire</li><li>to adopt</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wȳsċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adoption</li></ol><p>From <i>ġewȳsċed</i>, past participle of <i>ġewȳsċan</i> (“to adopt”), + the suffix <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>desirable</li><li>adoptive</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) optative</li></ol><p>From <i>ġewȳsċan</i> (“to wish or to adopt”) +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġewȳsċing</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adoption</li></ol><p>From <i>ġewȳsċan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>covering, shroud</li><li>clothing, dress</li><li>equipment</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wǣde</i> (“clothing”); see <i>wǣd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dress, clothe, equip</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wǣdian</i>, else <i>ġewǣde</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġewealdan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) weaponry; weapons, arms</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gawāpnī</i>, derived from an earlier form of Old English <i>wǣpn</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>giwāfani</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arm</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wǣpnian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>armed</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aware <i>(+genitive of something)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gawar</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wær</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remind, warn, admonish</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġewær</i> +&lrm; <i>-lǣċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wash; a washing up; an overflow of water</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gawask</i>, <i>&#42;gawaskī</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;wæsċ</i>. Cognate with Middle High German <i>gewesche</i> (whence German <i>Gewäsche</i>, <i>Gewäsch</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be fruitful</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>wæstmbǣre</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to water, irrigate</li></ol><p>From <i>ge-</i> +&lrm; <i>wæterian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġewæterian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġewēpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeīeċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeīewan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jezebel (a Biblical character)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) respect, esteem</li></ol><p>From <i>eahtian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who favours, consents to, helps, acquiesces to, agrees to or with, or supports something (<i>+ genitive or dative of thing</i>).</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indicates consent to, agreeance with, acquiescence to, or support for something (<i>+ genitive or dative of thing</i>)</li></ol><p>Likely from <i>ġeþafian</i> (“to favour; to permit, allow”) +&lrm; <i>-a</i>. Alternatively, possibly from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ga-</i> + <i>&#42;þabaz (“fitting; appropriate”)</i> (see Usage Notes and Etymology 2), whence also <i>ġeþafian</i>.</p><p>See Etymology 1.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to favor</li><li>to permit, allow</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þafian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consent, assent, permission, allowance</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þafung</i> (“consent, permission”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thought, mind</li><li>intention, purpose</li><li>design, what is thought-out</li><li>act of thinking</li><li>imagination</li><li>opinion</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaþank</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaþankaz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þanc</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gidank</i> (German <i>Gedanke</i>), Old Saxon <i>githanko</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) counsel, consultation, deliberation, advice, thought, a determination, resolution, device, plan, purpose</li><li>(<i>countable</i>) a council, assembly</li></ol><p>Derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þankijaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þeaht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remember, bethink, consider</li><li>to conceive, perceive, reflect upon, bear in mind</li><li>to devise, invent, excogitate, design</li><li>to determine, resolve, mean</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þenċan</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaþankijan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>githenkian</i>, Old High German <i>gidenken</i> (German <i>gedenken</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeþīedan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>language</li></ol><p><i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þēode</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a joining, juncture, joint</li><li>a conjunction</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) conjugation</li><li>a translation</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þēodnes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeþēodnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consent, assent, agreement</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flourish, prosper, be successful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaþinhaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þēon</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>githīhan</i>, Old High German <i>gidīhan</i> (German <i>gedeihen</i>), Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌸𐌴𐌹𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (gaþeihan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a howl (as of a wolf)</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þēotan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make dense</li></ol><p>From <i>ge-</i> +&lrm; <i>þicce</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-fieldan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to receive, accept</li><li>to receive (food); to consume</li></ol><p>Corresponding to <i>ġe-</i> + <i>þicgan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to join, unite, connect, associate</li><li>to apply, adjust, translate</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þīedan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeþingþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agreement, treaty</li><li>result, issue; destiny</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to determine, fix, settle</li><li>to thrive, grow, become excellent</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þingan</i>, apparently modified from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þingōną</i> (class-2 weak verb).</p><p>Compare to þēon and þīhan.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meeting, council</li><li>result, outcome</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaþingī</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>githingi</i>, Old Dutch <i>githingi</i>, Old High German <i>gidingi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make terms with</li><li>to be reconciled</li><li>to come to an agreement, settle (a dispute)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaþingōn</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þingian</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>githingōn</i>, Old High German <i>gidingōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honor, dignity, rank</li><li>intercession</li><li>agreement, composition</li><li>court, legal assembly</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeþingian</i> +&lrm; <i>-þu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>companionship, fellowship</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þoft</i> +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>companionship, society</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þoft</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grown up</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thought, what is determined after thought, idea, opinion, decree</li><li>mind, purpose, intention</li><li>what is thought out, device, design, deliberation, counsel; rede</li><li>plan</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þōht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be subjected, to suffer what is evil, thole, endure, sustain</li><li>to undergo treatment</li><li>to bear some condition</li><li>to stop, wait, continue, be patient under, remain</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þolian</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>githolōn</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌸𐌿𐌻𐌰𐌽</i> (gaþulan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throng, crowd, tumult</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þrang</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gidrengi</i> (German <i>Gedränge</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a crowd, throng</li><li>a tumult</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press, thring, oppress</li><li>to exert pressure on</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þringan</i>. Cognate Old Saxon <i>githringan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suffer</li><li>to be martyred</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þrōwian</i> (“to suffer”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compress, repress, restrain</li><li>to oppress, depress, thrutch upon, press down</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þryċċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeþring</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>growing luxuriously, lush</li><li>(<i>of trees</i>) having leaves or foliage</li><li>(<i>of plants</i>) having leaves, leafy, bushy</li><li>(<i>of leaves</i>) growing thickly together, dense, bushy</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;þūf</i> (“lush, thriving”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þūb</i> (“lush”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þūbaz</i> (“flourishing, thriving”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ġeþūf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeþuxian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grown, advanced, thriven</li><li>pious</li><li>excellent, noble, perfect</li></ol><p><i>ġeþingan</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>, with the root vowel mutated by ablaut.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġeþungen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġeþungen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow dark</li><li>to get obscured by clouds</li></ol><p>From <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þuxian</i>, a variant of <i>þuhsian</i>. Related to Old Norse <i>þoka</i> (“fog, mist”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curd, coagulum</li></ol><p>From <i>ge-</i> +&lrm; a word related to <i>&#42;ðeran</i> (“to bestir”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)twerH-</i> (“to agitate, stir up”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to churn; to stir, beat, or mix together</li><li>(<i>literal</i>) to make thick (by churning)</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to forge</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þweran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>agreeable, in agreement, harmonious, concordant</li><li><i>(of creatures)</i> gentle, peaceful</li><li><i>(of objects)</i> at rest, still</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to agree, consent, or be in accord</li><li>to cause to agree; to make agreeable or mild</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeþwǣre</i> (“agreeable, concordant, harmonious”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ġeþwǣrian</i> (“to agree, consent; to make agreeable, cause to agree”)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġeþwǣre</i> (“agreeable, concordant, harmonious”) +&lrm; <i>-lǣċan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concord, agreement, gentleness</li></ol><p>From <i>ġeþwǣre</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeþicfieldan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeþīedan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patience</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaþuldi</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þyld</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gidult</i> (German <i>Geduld</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>patient</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaþuldīgaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gidultig</i> (German <i>geduldig</i>). Equivalent to <i>ġeþyld</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seem, appear</li><li>to seem fit</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>þynċan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġēa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġe-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġyċċan</i> (“to itch”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jukkjan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġyċċe</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jukkjā</i>, related to <i>&#42;jukkjan</i> (“to itch”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġyċċiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>icicle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jikil</i>, <i>&#42;jekul</i> (“icicle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ġiċel</i></li></ol><p>Derived from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jikil</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>icicle</li></ol><p>Presumably from earlier <i>&#42;ġiċelle</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jikilljā</i> (“icicle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>icy</li></ol><p>From <i>ġiċel</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hailstone, piece of ice</li></ol><p>From <i>ġiċel</i> (“icicle”) +&lrm; <i>stān</i> (“stone”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġecwēman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġecwēmlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġecwēme</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiedd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiedd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġieddung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gyden</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gydiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġēa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiedd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) story, tale, song, poem</li></ol><p>There are no exact cognates, though the shape of the word points to a Proto-Germanic <i>*gadją</i>. This would make the ⟨ie⟩ from fronting of *a, palatal diphthongization and i-umlaut, and the geminate ⟨d⟩ from West Germanic gemination which was eventually leveled into the nominative/accusative singular, a development which happened to almost all ja-stems with light root syllables (see <i>hyse</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing; to recite, speak</li></ol><p><i>ġiedd</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġieddung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a song; saying; discourse</li></ol><p><i>ġieddian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giver</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gebô</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġiefan</i> (“to give”) +&lrm; <i>-a</i> (agent suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>present, gift</li><li>(<i>theology</i>) grace</li><li>the runic character ᚷ (/ɡ/ or /j/)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gebu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gebō</i>. Cognates include Old Frisian <i>jeve</i>, Old Saxon <i>geva</i>, Old High German <i>geba</i>, Old Norse <i>gjǫf</i>, and Gothic <i>𐌲𐌹𐌱𐌰</i> (giba).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>payment, pay</li><li>tax</li><li>tribute</li><li>cost, loss</li><li>guild</li><li>pagan god, idol</li><li>worship, especially to an idol or pagan god</li><li>sacrifice (religious offering)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;geldą</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>jeld</i>, Old Saxon <i>geld</i>, Old High German <i>gelt</i>, Old Norse <i>gjald</i>, and Gothic <i>𐌲𐌹𐌻𐌳</i> (gild).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>payer</li><li>worshiper</li><li>guild member</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;geldō</i>, equivalent to <i>ġieldan</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay</li><li>to reward</li><li>to worship (+ dative)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;geldan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;geldaną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>jelda</i>, Old Saxon <i>geldan</i>, Old High German <i>geltan</i>, Old Norse <i>gjalda</i>, Gothic <i>&#42;𐌲𐌹𐌻𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;gildan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p><i>ġield</i> (“tax, payment”) +&lrm; <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to yell</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gellaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġielleþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) bunch of plant stems</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;galmi</i>, possibly ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰelh₃-</i> (“green, yellow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a boast</li><li>boasting</li><li>pride</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gelp</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gelpą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰel-</i> (“to shout, chant”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glory, boast, brag <i>(+ genitive about something)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gelpan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gelpaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) boastful word</li></ol><p><i>ġielp</i> +&lrm; <i>word</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to care for; to take care of [<i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> accusative]</li><li>to regard, observe, keep [<i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> accusative]</li><li>to heed; to take heed of [<i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> accusative]</li><li>to cure [<i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> accusative]</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaumijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>care</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaumō</i>. The umlauted vowel is unexpected; perhaps the noun was influenced by the related verb <i>ġīeman</i>, where the umlaut is expected.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>careless, negligent</li><li>uncared for, wandering, stray</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġīeme</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to neglect; to be careless</li><li>to despise</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġīemelēas</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>carelessly</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġīemelēas</i> (“careless”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carelessness</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġīemelēas</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġīemelīest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carelessness</li><li>negligence, neglect</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>ġīemelēas</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i> or <i>ġīeme</i> +&lrm; <i>-līest</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>care</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaumīniz</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġīeman</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a governor</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġīeman</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeond</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rod, staff</li><li>branch, twig</li><li>(<i>unit of measurement</i>) the rod, perch, or pole of around 15 feet used in computing acres</li><li>(<i>unit of measurement</i>) the yard, yardland, or virgate of around 30 acres used in dividing hides of land</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaʀdi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apparel, dress, clothing</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;garwīlō</i> (“equipment, outfitting”), from <i>&#42;garwijan</i> (“to ready, prepare”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeornan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiestrandæġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare, make ready</li><li>to adorn, clothe</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;garwijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garwijaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ġearu</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>gerva</i>, <i>gørva</i>, <i>gera</i>, <i>gøra</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>yes</li></ol><p>Apparently from earlier <i>&#42;jǣsī</i> (“so be it”), from <i>&#42;jǣ</i> (“yes, so”) + <i>&#42;sīe</i> (“be [it]”), equivalent to <i>ġēa</i> +&lrm; <i>sīe</i>. Compare <i>nese</i> (“no”). More at <i>ġēa</i>, <i>wesan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stranger</li><li>guest</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) enemy</li><li>yeast</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gasti</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gast</i>, Middle Dutch <i>gast</i> (Dutch <i>gast</i>), Old High German <i>gast</i> (German <i>Gast</i>), Old Norse <i>gestr</i> (Swedish <i>gäst</i>), Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (gasts, “stranger”). The Indo-European root is also the source of Latin <i>hostis</i> (“enemy”), Old Church Slavonic <i>ⰳⱁⱄⱅⱐ</i> (gostĭ), <i>гость</i> (gostĭ) (Russian <i>гость</i> (gostʹ)) and Polish <i>gość</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jestu</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>gest</i>, <i>gist</i> (modern Dutch <i>gist</i>), Old Norse <i>jǫstr</i> (Swedish <i>jäst</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guest-place, place for shelter</li><li>guest room</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġiest</i> +&lrm; <i>-ern</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a guesthouse or guestroom</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gastihof</i>, equivalent to <i>ġiest</i> +&lrm; <i>hof</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a guesthouse</li><li>a guest-chamber</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gastihūs</i>, equivalent to <i>ġiest</i> (“guest”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to stay as a guest, lodge</li></ol><p>From <i>ġiest</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hospitable</li></ol><p>From <i>ġiest</i> (“guest”) +&lrm; <i>līþe</i> (“gentle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kindness shown to guests, hospitality</li></ol><p>From <i>ġiestlīþe</i> (“hospitable”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i> (“-ness”), or <i>ġiest</i> (“guest”) +&lrm; <i>līþnes</i> (“kindness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a band or force of guests</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġiest</i> +&lrm; <i>mæġen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>yester-, of the preceding day</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gesteran</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gesteran</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰǵʰyésteros</i>. More at yesterday.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yesterday</li></ol><p>From <i>ġiestran</i> +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>last night</li></ol><p>From <i>ġiestran</i> +&lrm; <i>niht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yesterday evening</li></ol><p>From <i>ġiestran</i> +&lrm; <i>ǣfen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a guest-hall, hostel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gastisali</i>, equivalent to <i>ġiest</i> +&lrm; <i>sele</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>still</li><li>(in negative phrases) yet</li><li>even (used to emphasize comparatives)</li><li>keep, continuing an action (+verb)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;juta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>yet</li><li>still</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;juta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;getaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰed-</i> (“take, seize, grasp”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>jeta</i>, Old High German <i>gezan</i>, <i>gezzan</i>, Old Norse <i>geta</i> (whence English <i>get</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>if</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jabē</i>, <i>&#42;jabu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jabai</i> (“when, if”) with anomalous apocope. The expected form is attested once as <i>ġyfe</i> in the early 7th-century Law of Æthelberht, if not simply a scribal error.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiefa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiefan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fate</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>granted</li></ol><p>Cognate with Old Norse <i>gipta</i> (“good luck, fate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>liberal, generous</li><li>bountiful, gracious</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gebul</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gebulaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>ġiefan</i> (to give) +&lrm; <i>-ol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>greedy</li><li>eager</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaībrī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġīfre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) throne</li></ol><p>From <i>ġift</i> +&lrm; <i>stōl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marriage payment, dowry</li><li>(<i>in the plural and in compounds</i>) wedding, marriage</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gifti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;giftiz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>jeft</i>, Old Saxon <i>&#42;gift</i> (in <i>sundargift</i> (“privilege”, literally “special gift”)), Dutch <i>gift</i>, Old High German <i>gift</i> (German <i>Gift</i>), Old Norse <i>gipt</i> (> English <i>gift</i>), Gothic <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌹𐍆𐍄𐍃</i> (fragifts).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wedding</li></ol><p>See <i>ġift</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiefu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a giant (<i>mythical being of superhuman size</i>)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>γίγας</i> (gígas).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a giant</li></ol><p>From <i>gīgant</i> (“giant”) +&lrm; <i>mæċġ</i> (“man”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeġeġnian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeheald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeocsa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġield</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġielda</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġieldan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġielm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġielp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġielpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boasting, arrogance</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ġielpan</i> (“to boast”) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gylt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young sow</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gyltiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġimm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġīeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġemǣrsung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gem, jewel</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) sun</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gimmu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġimwyrhta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jewel; gem; precious stone; gemstone</li></ol><p>From <i>ġimm</i> +&lrm; <i>stān</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>gimsteinn</i> (Icelandic <i>gimsteinn</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jeweler</li></ol><p>From <i>ġimm</i> (“gem”) +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“worker”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to yawn, gape</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeond</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeond-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ġeong</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jungist</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;junhistaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gingifer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ginger</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gingiberō</i>, from Late Latin <i>gingiber</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ġeong</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disciple</li><li>vassal</li><li>follower</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jungiʀō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;junhizô</i>.</p><p>Derived from the above adjective. For the sense compare Old High German <i>jungiro</i>, whence modern German <i>Jünger</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to yawn</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ginōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ginōną</i>. Akin to Old Saxon <i>ginōn</i>, Old High German <i>ginōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wide, spacious, vast</li><li>ample</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ginn(ī)</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ginn(ij)az</i>, of uncertain origin. Cognate with Old Norse <i>ginn-</i> (“great-”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-directed, well-shown; wise</li></ol><p>From <i>ġinn</i> (“ample”) +&lrm; <i>wīsed</i>, the past participle of <i>wīsan</i> (“to show”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeō</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeoc</i> (“yoke”)</li><li>alternative form of <i>ġēoc</i> (“help”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeōdagas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiefan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeoguþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeoguþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeoloca</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeond</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġeong</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeornan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeornful</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeornfulnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ipswich (a port town and borough of Suffolk, England).</li></ol><p><i>Ġipes</i> +&lrm; <i>wīc</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġierd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gyrdan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġierela</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>grin</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeornan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġyrran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sigh, sob</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;giskōn</i> (“to yawn”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵeys-</i> (“gaping, cracked”) +&lrm; <i>-cian</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>geskōn</i> (“to yawn”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sobbing</li></ol><p><i>ġiscian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġēse</i></li></ol></html>:/jiˈseːɑ̯/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Northumbrian</i>) alternative form of <i>ġesēon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġīsl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġesīene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hostage</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gīsl</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gīslaz</i>, a loanword from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;geistlos</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gīsal</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;gīsal</i>, Old High German <i>gīsal</i>, Old Norse <i>gísl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Gīslahari</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Gīslaharjaz</i>, from <i>&#42;gīslaz</i> (“hostage”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;harjaz</i> (“army”). Equivalent to <i>ġīsl</i> +&lrm; <i>here</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give hostages</li></ol><p>From <i>ġīsl</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Compare Dutch <i>gijzelen</i> (“to take hostage”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiestlīþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li><i>(the second-person dual nominative)</i> you two</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġīet</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jit</i>, with the *i leveled in from <i>&#42;wit</i>. Further from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jut</i>. Cognate with North Frisian <i>jat</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġīeta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to covet</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gīdisōn</i>, derived from <i>&#42;gīd</i> (“greed”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greed</li><li>desire (without or without negative connotation)</li></ol><p><i>ġītsian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>geola</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) July.</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>giuli</i> (not related to <i>iūlius</i> (“July”)), alternative form of <i>geola</i> ("December/January") + <i>-ling</i> (<i>diminutive suffix</i>), the connection being that December is the month of the winter solstice, July of the summer (i.e. little) solstice. <i>geola</i> may also be etymologically connected with the solstice—see further there.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġeong</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġingra</i> (“younger”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>griffin</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>grȳps</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>γρύψ</i> (grúps).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask (for something), request [<i>with</i> <b>fram</b> (+ dative) <small>‘from someone’</small>]</li><li>to demand</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;giwēn</i>, cognate with Old High German <i>anagiwēn</i> (“to desire, long for”).</p><p>Alternatively, perhaps from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;giwō</i> (“boasting; audacity”), related to Old Norse <i>gjó</i> (“enjoyment; sensuality; lust”), Middle High German <i>giude</i> (“loud revelry; boasting”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be glad</li><li>to make glad</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>glæd</i> (“glad”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>glæd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wise, prudent</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;glauw</i>, cognate with Old High German <i>glau</i>, Old Saxon <i>glau</i>, Old Norse <i>glǫggr</i>, <i>gløggr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Glēawċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>glēaw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gloucester</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Glēvum</i> +&lrm; <i>ċeaster</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gloucestershire</li></ol><p>From <i>Glēawċeaster</i> +&lrm; <i>sċīr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Glēawċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wisely, prudently, well</li></ol><p>From <i>glēaw</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Glēawċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>glēaw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wise, wary, astute</li></ol><p>From <i>glēaw</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>prudently, wisely, clearly, well</li></ol><p>From <i>glēaw</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prudence, wisdom, skill, ability, sagacity, acuteness</li></ol><p>From <i>glēaw</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>glēaw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>glēaw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sagacity, wisdom</li><li>diligence</li><li>argument, proof, calculation</li></ol><p>From <i>glēaw</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burning or live coal, gleed, ember, fire, flame, spark</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;glōdiz</i> (“incandescence, glowing ember, burning ash”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰelh₃-</i> (“to shine”). Cognate with Scots <i>gleed</i> (“burning coal, ember”), Saterland Frisian <i>Gloud</i> (“blaze, fire”), Dutch <i>gloed</i> (“glow, blaze”), German <i>Glut</i> (“ember”), Scots <i>glude</i> (“glow from a fire”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>glenġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>glenġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>glenġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ornament, decoration</li><li>jewel</li><li>glory, honor</li></ol><p>Unknown. See <i>glenġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adorn with material ornaments; decorate</li><li>to be an ornament to; be becoming to</li><li>to arrange gracefully</li></ol><p>Unknown. Perhaps related to <i>clenġan</i> (“to adhere; stick to; cling to; hang; exhilarate”).</p><p>Alternatively, perhaps related to Middle Low German <i>lenge</i> (“rope, band, strap”), Old Norse <i>lengja</i> (“strap, thong, sling, belt”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joy, glee</li><li>pleasure, mirth</li><li>sport, play</li><li>music, musical accompaniment; entertainment</li><li>mockery, jesting</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;glīw</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;glīwą</i> (“joy, music, entertainment, jesting, sport”). Akin to Old Norse <i>glý</i> (“glee”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) harp</li></ol><p>Kenning of <i>glēo</i> (“glee, joy”) +&lrm; <i>bēam</i> (“beam, wood”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minstrelsy, music, gleecraft</li></ol><p>From <i>glēo</i> +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a minstrel, musician</li></ol><p>From <i>glēo</i> (“glee”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“man”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>splendour</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;glimu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;glimō</i> (“shine”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰley-</i> (“to shine”), from <i>&#42;ǵʰel-</i> (“to shine, shimmer”). Related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;glimjaną</i>, whence Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;glimmjan</i>, whence Middle Low German <i>glimmen</i> (“to glow”) and Middle High German <i>glimmen</i> (“to glow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) joy, mirth</li></ol><p>From <i>glēo</i> (“joy, glee”) +&lrm; <i>stæf</i> (“staff”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>glēo</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To play an instrument.</li><li>To sing.</li><li>To joke, jest, act a fool.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;glīwōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;glīwōną</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;glīwą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Glēawċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Glēawċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slippery</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kite (bird)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;glidā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;glidǭ</i>; related to <i>glīdan</i> (“to glide”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glide</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;glīdan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;glīdaną</i> (“to glide, slide”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>glēo</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>glēowian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine, glisten</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glisten</li><li>to glitter</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;glisnōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;glisnōną</i>, possibly from <i>&#42;glisnaz</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ōną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰlis-nó-s</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰleys-</i>, from <i>&#42;ǵʰley-</i> (“to shine”). Cognate with Norwegian <i>glisna</i>, Swedish <i>glesna</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine, gleam, sparkle, glisten</li><li>to be splendid</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;glitinōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;glitinōną</i>, from a root cognate with Old Norse <i>glitra</i> + Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-inōną</i>. Ultimately from <i>&#42;ǵʰel-</i> (“to shine, shimmer, glow”). Related to Old English <i>glisnian</i> (“to glisten”) and <i>glīdan</i> (“to glide”). More at glitter, glisten, glint, glass.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>glēo</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>glēowian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>glēostæf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a glove</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;galōfō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;galōfô</i>, equivalent to <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>lōf</i> (“palm of the hand”). Compare Old Norse <i>glófi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gloom; twilight, gloaming</li><li>darkness</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;glōm</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;glōmaz</i>. Cognate with Norwegian <i>glom</i> (“transparent cuticle or membrane”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twilight</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>glōm</i> (“twilight”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;glōan</i>. Related to Old Saxon <i>glōian</i>, Old High German <i>gluoen</i>, Old Norse <i>glóa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>glēowian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Glēawċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>glad</li><li>bright</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gladaz</i> (“smooth”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰladh-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰel-</i> (“to shine”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>glad</i> (“glad, happy”), Old High German <i>glat</i> (“smooth”) (German <i>glatt</i> (“smooth”)), Old Norse <i>glaðr</i> (“smooth; happy”) (Swedish <i>glad</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>gladly, pleasantly, kindly, cheerfully, with gladness, joyously</li></ol><p>From <i>glæd</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) gracious, kind</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a happy person</li><li>a happy man (adult male)</li></ol><p>From <i>glæd</i> +&lrm; <i>mann</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gleam, splendor, radiance</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;glaimi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;glaimiz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰley-</i> (“to shine”), from <i>&#42;ǵʰel-</i> (“to shine, shimmer”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amber</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;glāʀ</i>, <i>&#42;glēʀ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;glēzą</i>, related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;glasą</i> (“glass, amber”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>glēr</i> (“resin”), Middle Low German <i>glâr</i>, <i>glar</i> (“resin, gum”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>like glass, glassy, transparent, vitreous</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;glaʀīn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;glazīnaz</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;glasīnaz</i>; equivalent to <i>glæs</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>. Doublet of <i>glæsen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glass</li><li>a glass</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;glas</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;glasą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰel-</i> (“to shine, shimmer, glow”). Compare Old Saxon <i>glas</i>, Old Dutch <i>glas</i>, Old High German <i>glas</i>, Old Norse <i>gler</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) glass (<i>made of glass</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;glasīn</i>. Equivalent to <i>glæs</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>. Doublet of <i>glæren</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Glastonbury (a city in Somerset, England)</li></ol><p><i>&#42;Glæstinga</i> +&lrm; <i>burg</i> (“fort”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gnaw</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gnagan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gnætt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rub</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gnīdaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sad, sorrowful, dejected, complaining</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sorrow, sadness</li><li>affliction</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gnuʀn</i> (“sad”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>gnornian</i></li></ol><p>From <i>gnorn</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>gnornaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a house of grief</li><li>a prison; prison cell</li></ol><p>From <i>gnorn</i> +&lrm; <i>hof</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;intransitive</i>) To grieve, mourn or lament; to feel or express grief</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) To complain or murmur; to express discontent</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gnoʀnōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>gnorn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gnætt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small flying insect or midge; gnat</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gnatt</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gnattaz</i> (“gnat”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>good</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>good (something good or good things collectively)</li><li>goods, possessions</li><li>god</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>God</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gōd</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gōdaz</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;god</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gudą</i>. Originally neuter, then changed to masculine to reflect the change in religion to Christianity.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: God)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>God</li></ol><p>See <i>god</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>God help someone</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>God wāt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Likely from Old English <i>gōd</i> (“good”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>godchild</li><li>the Son of God (Jesus)</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>god</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>bearn</i> (“child”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pledge affirmed by an appeal to God</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>god</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>borg</i> (“pledge”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atonement paid to a church</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>god</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>bōt</i> (“help, assistance”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>divine, godlike, heavenly</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;godakund</i>, equivalent to <i>god</i> +&lrm; <i>-cund</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>godkund</i>, Old High German <i>gotkunt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>divine</li></ol><p><i>godcund</i> (“divine”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i> (“like”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>divine majesty</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>godcund</i> (“divine”) +&lrm; <i>miht</i> (“power, might”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>divinity, godliness</li><li>Godhead</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>godcund</i> (“divine”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i> (“-ness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>godcundnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goddaughter</li></ol><p>From <i>god</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>dohtor</i> (“daughter”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) heavenly joy</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>god</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>drēam</i> (“joy, music”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Gode þanc</i></li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Gode þanc)</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>thank God</li></ol><p>Originally a calque of Latin <i>Deō grātias</i> and/or a shortening of <i>Gode sīe þanc</i> (compare German <i>Gott sei Dank</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Son of God (Jesus)</li></ol><p><i>godes</i> (“God's”) + <i>sunu</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thank God</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Godaskalk</i>, from <i>&#42;god</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;skalk</i> (“servant”). Equivalent to <i>god</i> +&lrm; <i>sċealc</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Goteskalk</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>godwebb</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>god-fearing</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>god</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>fyrhtu</i> (“fright”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>godfather (male godparent)</li><li>God the Father</li></ol><p>From <i>god</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>fæder</i> (“father”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oracle</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>god</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>ġesprǣċe</i> (“speech”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idol</li></ol><p><i>god</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>ġield</i> (“idol”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Godiva</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Godagebu</i>, from <i>&#42;god</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;gebu</i> (“gift”). Equivalent to <i>god</i> +&lrm; <i>ġiefu</i>. Cognate with Old French <i>Gaudiveau</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>godġield</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) a heavenly gem</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>god</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>ġimm</i> (“gemstone”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>god</i> (“God”) +&lrm; <i>gūþ</i> (“battle, war”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>divine nature</li></ol><p>From <i>god</i> +&lrm; <i>-hād</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Godahari</i>, from <i>&#42;god</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hari</i> (“army”). Equivalent to <i>god</i> +&lrm; <i>here</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Guther</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>god</i> +&lrm; <i>hild</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be or become good; to prosper</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make good; to give an appearance of being good</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to endow, furnish with</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to set right, put in good order</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make abundant, accumulate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gōdōn</i>, equivalent to <i>gōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without good, miserable, wretched</li></ol><p>From <i>gōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>goodly, good</li><li>godlike, divine; godly</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gōdalīk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gōdalīkaz</i>, equivalent to <i>gōd</i> (“good”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p><p>From <i>god</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>gōdlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>godmother (female godparent)</li></ol><p>From <i>god</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>mōdor</i> (“mother”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Godamundu</i>, from <i>&#42;god</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;mundu</i> (“protection, protector”). Equivalent to <i>god</i> +&lrm; <i>mund</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Codemund</i>, Old French <i>Goudemant</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gumley (a village and civil parish in Harborough district, Leicestershire, England)</li></ol><p><i>Godmund</i> +&lrm; <i>lēah</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Goodmanham (a village and civil parish in the East Riding of Yorkshire, England).</li></ol><p><i>Godmund</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> +&lrm; <i>hām</i>, literally “Godmund's home”</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>divinity, divine power, godly power; numen.</li></ol><p>From <i>gōd</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>mæġen</i> (“power”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the quality or condition of being good; goodness</li><li>a good act or good deed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gōdnassī</i>, equivalent to <i>gōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>guotnessī</i> (“goodness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>gōdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gōdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Godric of Finchale; Anglo-Saxon saint.</li><li>Godric of Mappestone; Anglo-Saxon þegn.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Godarīk</i>, from <i>&#42;god</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;rīk</i> (“king, ruler”). Equivalent to <i>god</i> +&lrm; <i>rīc</i>. Cognate with Old French <i>Godry</i>, <i>Coutray</i> (via Frankish).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sin against a god</li><li>impiety</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>god</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>sċyld</i> (“sin”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impious</li></ol><p>From <i>god</i> +&lrm; <i>scyldig</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who has become spiritually related to another (a baptized child or its parents) by acting as sponsor at baptism; godparent; sponsor</li></ol><p>From <i>god</i> +&lrm; <i>sibb</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the state of being a <i>godsibb</i> (“godparent, religious sponsor”) to another</li></ol><p>From <i>godsibb</i> (“godparent, sponsor”) +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>godspell</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a copy of the gospels</li></ol><p>From <i>godspell</i> +&lrm; <i>bōc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evangelism</li></ol><p>From <i>godspell</i> +&lrm; <i>bodung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gospel</li></ol><p>Analysed as <i>God</i> +&lrm; <i>spell</i>, literally “God-message”, an alteration of earlier <i>gōdspell</i> (“good news”), from <i>gōd</i> (“good”) +&lrm; <i>spell</i> (“message, news”), a calque from Ecclesiastical Latin <i>bona annūntiātiō</i> or <i>bonus nūntius</i>, which was a then-current explanation of the meaning of Ecclesiastical Latin <i>ēvangelium</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>εὐαγγέλιον</i> (euangélion, “good news”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>godspelbōc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>godspelbodung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evangelist</li></ol><p><i>godspell</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to evangelize</li></ol><p>From <i>godspell</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gospel</li><li>belonging to the times of the gospel</li><li>belonging to the dispensations of the gospel</li></ol><p>From <i>gōdspel</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>evangelical</li></ol><p>From <i>gōdspel</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>godspeltraht</i></li></ol><p>From <i>godspell</i> +&lrm; <i>traht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an exposition of part of the gospels</li><li>homily</li></ol><p>From <i>godspell</i> +&lrm; <i>traht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oracle</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>god</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>sprǣċe</i> (“speech”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>godson</li></ol><p>From <i>god</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>sunu</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>godwebb</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a divine weave; excellently woven fabric or material</li><li>a precious garment</li><li>purple cloth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;godawabi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gudawabją</i>, <i>&#42;gudawabjaz</i>, equivalent to <i>god</i> +&lrm; <i>webb</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>godwebb</i>, Old Saxon <i>godowebbi</i>, <i>godoweb</i>, Old High German <i>gotawebbi</i>, <i>cotiweppi</i>, Old Norse <i>guðvefr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>purple</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;godawabbjīn</i>, equivalent to <i>godwebb</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gotaweppin</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of cloak</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>godwebb</i> (“precious garment”) +&lrm; <i>cynn</i> (“kind, type”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a costly robe</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a weaver of fine fabric</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>godwebb</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Godwin of Wessex; Anglo-Saxon Earl of Wessex and father of Harold Godwinson.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Godawini</i>, from <i>&#42;god</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wini</i> (“friend”). Equivalent to <i>god</i> +&lrm; <i>wine</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Goduin</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wickedness</li><li>impiety</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>godwrecnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quince (<i>Cydonia oblonga</i>)</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>gōd</i> (“good”) +&lrm; <i>æppel</i> (“fruit”), perhaps a folk-etymological alteration of <i>coddæppel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>godly majesty</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>god</i> +&lrm; <i>þrym</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sigh, groan; to lament</li></ol><p>From the same root as Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gawjaną</i> with a different ablaut grade (-ō-) in the root. Compare <i>&#42;ġīeġan</i>, <i>ġēġan</i> from Proto-West-Germanic <i>&#42;gauwjan</i>. The weak class 2 formation (compare <i>twēoġan</i>, <i>sċōġan</i>) would correspond to the following reconstructions in linguistic stages ancestral to Old English: northern Ingvaeonic <i>&#42;gō(w)ōjan</i>, Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gō(w)ōn</i>, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gō(w)ōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gold</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;golþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gulþą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰĺ̥tom</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Gold)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ornamented or adorned with gold</li></ol><p><i>gold</i> +&lrm; <i>fāh</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goldfinch (bird)</li></ol><p>From <i>gold</i> (“gold”) +&lrm; <i>finċ</i> (“finch”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ring finger</li></ol><p>From <i>gold</i> (“gold”) +&lrm; <i>finger</i> (“finger”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) giver of gold, lord</li></ol><p>From <i>gold</i> (“gold”) +&lrm; <i>ġiefa</i> (“giver”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasure</li></ol><p><i>gold</i> (“gold”) +&lrm; <i>hord</i> (“hoard”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>privy, latrine</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>goldhord</i> (“treasure”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hoard or store treasure</li></ol><p>From <i>goldhord</i> (“treasure”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orichalcum</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;golþamassing</i>. Equivalent to <i>gold</i> (“gold”) +&lrm; <i>mæstling</i>, <i>mæsling</i> (“brass”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a hall decorated with gold, or in which gold is disbursed</li></ol><p>From <i>gold</i> (“gold”) +&lrm; <i>sele</i> (“hall”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>goldsmiþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goldsmith</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gulþasmiþaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>goldsmith</i>, Old Saxon <i>goldsmith</i>, Old Dutch <i>goltsmith</i>, Old High German <i>goldsmid</i>, Old Norse <i>gullsmiðr</i>. Equivalent to <i>gold</i> +&lrm; <i>smiþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) good friend, generous lord</li></ol><p>From <i>gold</i> (“gold”) +&lrm; <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Goldwine)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p><i>gold</i> (“gold”) +&lrm; <i>wine</i> (“friend”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inlay with gold</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>gold</i> +&lrm; <i>wrecan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palate, the roof of the mouth</li><li>jaws</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gōmō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gōmô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tribute</li></ol><p>Unknown, but possibly borrowed from a Celtic language. See English <i>gombeen</i>, from Irish <i>gaimbín</i> (“monetary interest”), from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;kambos</i> (“twisted, crooked”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ḱambos</i>, <i>&#42;(s)kambos</i> (“crooked”). If so, cognate to Latin <i>cambiō</i> from <i>cambium</i> borrowed from Gaulish <i>cambion</i>, whence English <i>change</i> and <i>exchange</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gamol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gamolfeax</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gamen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gamol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gamolfeax</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gomorrah, a city destroyed in the Bible for its sinfulness</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Γόμορρᾰ</i> (Gómorrhă), from Biblical Hebrew <i>עֲמֹרָה</i> (ʿămōrā).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>captive, slave</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Compare Old English <i>ġēopan</i> (“to acquire, receive”), Old Norse <i>hergopa</i> (“female captive of war”), Old Norse <i>gopi</i> (“vain fellow”). Alternatively, compare Old English <i>ġeap</i>, <i>gæp</i> (“crooked, sly, crafty”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dirt, dung, feces</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gor</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gurą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰer-</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old High German <i>gor</i>, Middle (and modern) Dutch <i>goor</i>, Old Norse <i>gor</i>, and outside the Germanic languages with Welsh <i>gôr</i> (“pus”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to let one's eyes wander.</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pour forth.</li></ol><p>Unknown. Apparently from a root <i>&#42;gor-</i> (related to Old English <i>gorian</i> (“to look, gaze”)) +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gaze, look.</li></ol><p>Unknown. Apparently from a root <i>&#42;gor-</i> (related to Old English <i>gorettan</i> (“to stare about, pour forth”)) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gorst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gorse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gorst</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gurstaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goose</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gans</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰans-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goose</li></ol><p>From <i>gōs</i> (“goose”) +&lrm; <i>fugol</i> (“bird”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goshawk</li></ol><p>From <i>gōs</i> (“goose”) +&lrm; <i>hafoc</i> (“hawk”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gōshafoc</i></li></ol><p>From <i>gōs</i> (“goose”) +&lrm; <i>heafoc</i> (“hawk”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Goth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gotō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gutô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Gothic (of the Goths)</li></ol><p><i>Gota</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gotland</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grade, step, order, degree, rank</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>gradus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grove</li></ol><p>Uncertain, lacking cognates in other Germanic languages. Perhaps from <i>grafan</i> (“to dig”), similar to <i>drāf</i> (“drove”) and <i>drifan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grove, copse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;graib</i>, <i>&#42;graibō</i> (“branch, group of branches, thicket”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;graibaz</i>, <i>&#42;graibô</i> (“branch, fork”), perhaps related to <i>&#42;grīpaną</i> (“to grasp”) and Ancient Greek <i>χρίμπτω</i> (khrímptō, “scratch, graze, wound”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig, dig up</li><li>to grave; engrave, chisel, carve</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;graban</i> (“to dig, scrape”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sculptor</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>grafan</i> (“to carve”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trench, excavation</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>grafu</i> +&lrm; <i>-et</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>den</li><li>cave</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grabu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>angry, hostile</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gram(i)</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gramaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anger, rage, wrath, indignation, fury; trouble</li><li>demonic spirit, devil, fiend, demon; imp, puck</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gramô</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gramo</i>, Old Saxon <i>gremi</i>, Old High German <i>gramo</i>, Old Norse <i>gremi</i>. Akin also to Old English <i>gram</i> (“angry, cruel, fierce”), <i>grimm</i>, <i>grim</i> (“fierce, savage”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>angry, passionate</li></ol><p>From <i>gram</i> +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fierce-minded</li></ol><p>From <i>gram</i> (“angry, hostile”) +&lrm; <i>heorte</i> (“heart”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grammatical, of grammar</li></ol><p>Like Old High German <i>gramatich</i>, from Latin <i>grammaticus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>γραμματικός</i> (grammatikós, “skilled in writing”), from <i>γράμμα</i> (grámma, “line of writing”), from <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō, “write”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gerbʰ-</i> (“to scratch”). Compare English <i>grammatic</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grammarian</li></ol><p>From <i>grammatiċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gramatichare</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Definition unclear, perhaps "one who has a mustache".</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to groan</li><li>to murmur</li><li>to lament</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grainōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grainōną</i> (“to howl; weep”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grīnaną</i> (“to whine; howl; whimper”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Grantanbryċġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Grantchester (a village and civil parish in South Cambridgeshire district, Cambridgeshire, England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cambridge (city in England)</li></ol><p>The first element is likely Celtic in origin; <i>bryċġ</i> is the Old English term for bridge.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Grantanbryċġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Grantanbryċġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moustache</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;granō</i> (“awn, beard-hair, hair-tips, moustache”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰrān-</i> (“edge, end, tip”). Cognate with Old High German <i>grana</i> (“beardhair, moustache”), Old Norse <i>grǫn</i> (“beardhair, moustache, lip”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>groaning</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>grānian</i> (“to groan”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chaff</li></ol><p>Partially from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wiskaz</i> (“bundle of hay/straw”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grip, grasp, clutches</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;graipu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;graipō</i>, related to <i>&#42;grīpaną</i> (“to grasp, grab”).</p><p>Cognate with Middle Low German <i>grēpe</i>, Middle Dutch <i>grepe</i> (Dutch <i>greep</i>), Old High German <i>greifa</i>, Old Norse <i>greip</i> (Swedish <i>grep</i> and Danish <i>greb</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grope; to touch with the hands</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>grāp</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to graze</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grasōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grasōną</i>. Equivalent to <i>græs</i> (“grass”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Literally "to grass."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>great, massive</li><li>tall</li><li>thick; stout</li><li>coarse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;graut</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grautaz</i> (“big in size, coarse, coarse grained”), from <i>&#42;gʰer-</i> (“to rub, grind, remove”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>grōt</i> (“large, thick, coarse, stour”), Old High German <i>grōz</i> (“large, thick, coarse”), Old English <i>grot</i> (“particle”). More at groat.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) meadow saffron (<i>Colchicum autumnale</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>grēat</i> (“great”) +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i> (“plant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greatness, bigness, thickness, coarseness of a material</li></ol><p>From <i>grēat</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>grēatnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Greeks (as a people)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Greek</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a Greek</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the land of the Greeks, Greece</li></ol><p><i>Grēc</i> (“Greek”) +&lrm; <i>land</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>grǣdiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>grǣġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Pope Gregory I</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Grēgorius</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Γρηγόριος</i> (Grēgórios).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>grǣġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>griellan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to anger; annoy, irritate</li><li>to provoke</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grammjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gramjaną</i> (“to irritate, anger”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>&#42;gremia</i>, &#42;gremma; Saterland Frisian <i>grääme</i>; Old Saxon <i>&#42;gremian</i>, &#42;gremmian; Old High German <i>gremen</i>, gremmen; German <i>grämen</i>; Old Norse <i>gremja</i>; Icelandic <i>gremja</i>; Norwegian Bokmål <i>gremme</i>; gremmes; Norwegian Nynorsk <i>gremja</i>, gremje, gremma, gremme; Swedish <i>gräma</i>; Danish <i>græmme</i>; Gothic <i>𐌲𐍂𐌰𐌼𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gramjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Greenwich (a town in Greater London, England)</li></ol><p><i>grēne</i> (“green”) +&lrm; <i>wīc</i> (“village”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the monster Grendel in the poem <i>Beowulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>green</li><li>(of a vegetable) raw</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grōnī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grōniz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>grēne</i>, Old Saxon <i>grōni</i>, Old High German <i>gruoni</i>, Old Norse <i>grœnn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Grēnawīc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become green, flourish</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grōnijōną</i>. Equivalent to <i>grēne</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greenness</li></ol><p>From <i>grēne</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bare the teeth due to anger, pain, etc.; to grin</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grannjōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;granjōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>grēnnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frighten, scare</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;greusaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰréws-e-</i>. Related to Old Saxon <i>gruri</i> (“fright”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earth</li><li>sand</li><li>grit</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;greutą</i>, whence also Old Saxon <i>griot</i>, Old Norse <i>grjót</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weep, cry, shed tears</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;greutan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;greutaną</i>. See also <i>grǣtan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>furrow, burrow</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ditch, drain, privy</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>græs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to greet, address</li><li>to say goodbye, bid farewell</li><li>to touch, handle</li><li>to have to do with a person</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to treat a person medically</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) to have sex with a person</li></ol><li>to attack or lay hands on someone</li><li>alternative form of <i>grǣtan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grōtijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grōtijaną</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grētaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>grēteþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to annoy, provoke</li><li>to offend</li><li>to gnash one's teeth (at)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grallijan</i>, a byform of <i>&#42;gralljan</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>grellskapr</i> (“anger, wrath”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>greatest</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>greater</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greatness, size</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grautī</i> (“greatness, size”). Akin to Old English <i>grēat</i> (“great, large”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greyhound</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;grīeġ</i> (cognate with Old Norse <i>grøy</i> (“bitch”)) + <i>hund</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>griellan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>grimm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mask</li><li>helmet, visor</li><li>specter, ghost; apparition</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grīmō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grīmô</i> (“mask”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>grīma</i>, Old Saxon <i>grīmo</i>, Old High German <i>grīmo</i>, Old Norse <i>gríma</i>, Gothic <i>&#42;𐌲𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌼𐌰</i> (&#42;greima).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>grymetian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>grymettan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a visored helmet</li></ol><p>From <i>grīma</i> (“mask”) +&lrm; <i>helm</i> (“helmet”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grim, fierce, cruel, severe, bloody</li></ol><p><i>grimm</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fierce, savage, terrible, cruel, angry</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grimm</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grimmaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰrem-</i> (“to thunder”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>grim</i>, Old High German <i>grim</i> (German <i>grimm</i>, <i>grimmig</i>), Old Norse <i>grimmr</i> (Danish <i>grim</i>, Swedish <i>grym</i>); and with Greek <i>χρεμίζω</i> (chremízo), Old Church Slavonic <i>грьмѣти</i> (grĭměti) (Russian <i>греме́ть</i> (gremétʹ)), Latvian <i>gremt</i>. See grim for further information.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rage, roar, clamour</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>grim</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be fierce or cruel</li><li>to rage</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grimmisōn</i>, equivalent to <i>grimm</i> +&lrm; <i>-sian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fierceness, roughness</li></ol><p><i>grimsian</i> (“to hate, abhor”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Grīmōwald</i>, from <i>&#42;grīmō</i> (“mask, helmet”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wald</i> (“might, authority”). Equivalent to <i>grīma</i> +&lrm; <i>weald</i>. Cognate with Lombardic <i>Grimuald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snare</li><li>noose</li></ol><p>Origin obscure. Probably from earlier <i>*gren(e)</i>; partly from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;granī̆</i>&nbsp;n, a metathetic alteration of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;garnī</i> (“intestine”), derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garnō</i> (“intestine, guts”); and partly from a metathesis of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;garni</i>&nbsp;f, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garniz</i>, a byform of <i>&#42;garnō</i>. Animal intestines were often cured and dried to create durable cords which could be used in making snares: compare Ancient Greek <i>χορδή</i> (khordḗ, “gut", also "string made of gut”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy by crushing</li><li>to grind</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grindan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grindaną</i>. Compare Albanian <i>grij</i> (“to grind”) and <i>grind</i> (“I brawl, wrangle over”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bar; bolt</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) latticework; gratings; hurdle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grindil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grindiz</i> (“barrier, gate”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰrendʰ-</i> (“bar, beam”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>grindil</i> (“bar”), Old High German <i>grintil</i> (“bar, beam, crossbar, barrier”), Old Norse <i>grind</i> (“grid, lattice door”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who or that which grinds, especially corn or grain; grinder</li></ol><p>From <i>grindan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gripe, grip, grab</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grīpan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grīpaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grip, clutch, grasp</li><li>costliness, preciousness</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gripi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gripiz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>&#42;grif</i> (in <i>hantgrif</i>, etc.; &gt; modern German <i>Griff</i>), Old Norse <i>gripr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who seizes</li><li>a robber</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>grīpan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>captivity</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>gripen</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gripennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>gripul</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seize; grip</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gripjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>able to grasp much; capacious</li></ol><p>From <i>grīpan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ul</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shudder, to feel horror, to dread, to quake</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grīsan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grīsaną</i>, related to causative verb Old English <i>grēosan</i> and Old English <i>gryre</i>. Cognate with German <i>grausen</i>. More at grisly and gruesome.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grisly, horrible, dreadful</li></ol><p><i>grīsan</i> (“to shudder”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gnash or grind the teeth</li><li>to rage</li></ol><p>From <i>grist</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;bātian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gnash or grind the teeth</li></ol><p>From <i>grist</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;bitian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cartilage</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gristil</i>, of obscure origin, perhaps from a non-Indo-European substrate language. Or, perhaps equivalent to <i>grist</i> (“grinding”) +&lrm; <i>-el</i>, in which case see <i>grindan</i> (“to grind”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>griþ</i> (“truce, peace”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>truce, peace</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>grið</i>, originally ‘home’, in plural ‘truce, sanctuary’.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make peace</li><li>to protect</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>griþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unprotected, without protection</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>griþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gram</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gramheort</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ditch, drain</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grōpō</i> (“furrow, ditch, trench”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰreb-</i>, <i>&#42;gʰrebʰ-</i> (“to dig, furrow, scratch”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>particle</li><li>fragment</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grutą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Consisting of or resembling particles, particulate, earthy</li></ol><p>From <i>grot</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of plants</i>) to grow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grōan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grōaną</i> (“to grow, become green”), Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰreh₁-</i> (“to grow, grow green”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>grōwa</i> (“to grow”), Middle Dutch <i>groeyen</i>, <i>grōyen</i> (“to grow”) (Dutch <i>groeien</i>), Old High German <i>gruoen</i> (“to grow, thrive, flourish”), Old Norse <i>grōa</i> (“to grow, become green”), Old English <i>græs</i> (“grass”), Old English <i>grēne</i> (“green”). More at grass, green.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to long for, desire, wish</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from a metathesised base related to Old English <i>ġeornian</i>, <i>ġiernan</i> (“to desire”) +&lrm; <i>-cian</i>. Alternatively, perhaps related to Old English <i>grunian</i> (“to cry out, make a loud noise, grunt”). Compare also Old Swedish <i>grunka</i> (“to mumble, grumble”), Middle English <i>groneken</i> (“to groan, grunt”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ground</li><li>bottom, foundation</li><li>abyss, <i>(in the plural)</i> depths</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grundu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grunduz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>groundsel</li></ol><p>From <i>grunde</i> (“ground, bottom, abyss”) +&lrm; <i>swelge</i> (“to swallow, to eat up”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>grundeswelge</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>grundeswelge</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>grundeswelge</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bottomless</li><li>boundless</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grundulausaz</i>, equivalent to <i>grund</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bottomless pit</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>completely, totally, to the ground, to the root</li></ol><p>From <i>grund</i> (“ground”) +&lrm; <i>-linga</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>grundlinga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>groundstone, foundation cornerstone</li></ol><p>From grund + stān. Cognate with German <i>Grundstein</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>grundeswelge</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foundation</li></ol><p>From <i>grund</i> (“ground”) +&lrm; <i>weall</i> (“wall”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>grundweall</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grunt</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grunnattjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grunnatjaną</i> (“to grunt”). Related to Old High German <i>grunnezzen</i> (German <i>grunzen</i>), Old Norse <i>&#42;grynta</i> (Swedish <i>grymta</i>). Equivalent to <i>grunnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grunt</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grunnōną</i> (“to grunt”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰrun-</i> (“to shout”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>malt mash</li></ol><p>Probably from a Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grūtą</i>, <i>&#42;grutą</i>, probably related to <i>&#42;greutą</i> (“grit”). Compare Old Norse <i>grautr</i>; from which Icelandic <i>grautur</i> (“porridge”), Swedish <i>gröt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>griellan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>grymettan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gramitōn</i>, <i>&#42;gramittjōn</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gremmizzōn</i> (“to be angry, grumble”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>grymetian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rage</li></ol><p>From the conflation of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gramittjan</i> and <i>&#42;grumittjan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>grymettan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>grin</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abyss</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grundī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grundiją</i> (“abyss”), from <i>&#42;grunduz</i> (“ground, foundation”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fright, terror</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gruʀi</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gruri</i>, from the same root as <i>grēosan</i> and <i>grorn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bran</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gruti</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greed</li><li>a cry, call</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cry, call out</li><li>to crow</li></ol><p>From <i>grǣd</i> (“cry”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>grǣdiġlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>grǣdiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(very) hungry, ravenous</li><li>greedy, covetous</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grādag</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grēdagaz</i>, from <i>&#42;grēduz</i> (“hunger”), equivalent to <i>grǣd</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gradag</i>, Old High German <i>gratag</i>, Old Norse <i>gráðugr</i> (Danish <i>grådig</i>), Gothic <i>𐌲𐍂𐌴𐌳𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (grēdags, “hungry”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>eagerly</li><li>with great appetite</li></ol><p>From <i>grǣdiġ</i> (“hungry, greedy”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greed, greediness</li></ol><p>From <i>grǣdiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grave</li><li>trench</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grab</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grabą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰrābʰ-</i> (“to dig, scratch, scrape”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>gref</i>, Old Saxon <i>graf</i>, Old Dutch <i>graf</i>, Old High German <i>grab</i>, Old Norse <i>grǫf</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐍂𐌰𐌱𐌰</i> (graba).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>grāfa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>grafere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a house of the dead, a grave house.</li></ol><p>From <i>græf</i> (“grave”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grey</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grāg</i>, a byform of <i>&#42;grāu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grēwaz</i> (“grey”). Doublet of <i>grǣw</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grass</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grasą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰreH₁-</i> (“grow, become green”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>gres</i>, Old Saxon <i>gras</i>, Old High German <i>gras</i>, Old Norse <i>gras</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐍂𐌰𐍃</i> (gras).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grass-green</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grasagrōniz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>grasgrœnn</i>. Equivalent to <i>græs</i> +&lrm; <i>grēne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grasshopper, locust</li></ol><p>From <i>græs</i> (“grass”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hoppa</i> (“hopper”). Compare Old High German <i>hewispranga</i>, literally meaning "hay jumper."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grassland</li><li>greensward</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>græs</i> (“grass”) +&lrm; <i>molde</i> (“earth, soil”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grasshopper, locust</li></ol><p>From <i>græs</i> (“grass”) +&lrm; <i>stapa</i> (“stepper”). Compare Old High German <i>hewistafo</i> (literally “hay stepper”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weep, cry</li><li>to bemoan, lament, bewail</li><li>to deplore</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grātan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grētaną</i> (“to weep, moan, lament”).</p><p>Akin to Middle High German <i>grāȥen</i>, Old Norse <i>gráta</i> (“to groan, weep”), Gothic <i>𐌲𐍂𐌴𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (grētan). Compare also Old English <i>grēotan</i> (“to weep, lament”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>grǣġ</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grāwi</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>grāwi</i> (“grey”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġerēfa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>grēne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>grēne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>grētan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;grōtijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grōtijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġeoguþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) man, hero</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gumō</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gumo</i>, Old High German <i>gumo</i>, <i>gomo</i>, Old Norse <i>gumi</i> (Icelandic <i>gumi</i> and Norwegian <i>gume</i>), Gothic <i>𐌲𐌿𐌼𐌰</i> (guma), Latin <i>homō</i>, Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;źmṓ</i> (Lithuanian <i>žmogùs</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) mankind</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) race, nation</li></ol><p>From <i>guma</i> +&lrm; <i>cynn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manly excellence, valour</li></ol><p><i>guma</i> (“man”) +&lrm; <i>cyst</i> (“excellence”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>gumkust</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Gumōwald</i>, from <i>&#42;gumō</i> (“man”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wald</i> (“might, authority”). Equivalent to <i>guma</i> +&lrm; <i>weald</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Gomold</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>matter, pus</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gund</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gundaz</i> (“sore, boil”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰendʰ-</i> (“ulcer, sore, abscess, boil”). Cognate with Old High German <i>gunt</i> (“purulent matter”), dialectal Norwegian <i>gund</i> (“the scab of an ulcer”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>grundeswelge</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġeong</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġingra</i> (“younger”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buttock, rump</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) buttocks</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gupā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gupǭ</i> (“rump, hip, loin”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰewb-</i>, <i>&#42;gʰewbʰ-</i> (“to bend, stoop”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gut, entrail</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gutt</i>, a byform of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gutō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gutô</i> (“channel, trough, gutter”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰewd-</i> (“to pour”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gūþfugol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) battle, combat</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gunþi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gunþiz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰéntis</i> (“killing, blow”). Shifted into the ō-stems at some point before i-umlaut; the inherited form <i>*gȳþ</i> occurs as the final element of names.</p><p>Germanic cognates include Old Norse <i>gunnr</i> ~ <i>guðr</i>. Old Saxon <i>gūđ-</i> and Old High German <i>gund-</i> appear in compounds.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>gūþ</i> (“battle, combat”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) warrior</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>beorn</i> (“warrior”). Equivalent to Old Norse <i>Gunnbjǫrn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Gunþiberht</i>, from <i>&#42;gunþi</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”). Equivalent to <i>gūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>berht</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Cundbrect</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a sword</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>bil</i> (“sword”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a shield</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>bord</i> (“shield”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) corselet, battle-mail</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>byrne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warfare, military strategy, tactics</li></ol><p><i>gūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a warrior queen</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>cwēn</i> (“queen”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a warrior or warlike king</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>cyning</i> (“king”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) death in battle</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>dēaþ</i> (“death”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>war banner</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gunþifanô</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gundfano</i>, Old Norse <i>gunnfáni</i>. Equivalent to <i>gūþ</i> (“war”) +&lrm; <i>fana</i> (“flag”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Gūþfriþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a dragon</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>floga</i> (“flier”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) bold in war</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>frec</i> (“greedy, bold”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) war-maker, warrior</li></ol><p><i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>fremmend</i> (“committing, making”), present participle of <i>fremman</i> (“to do, accomplish, carry out, perform”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Gunþifriþu</i>, from <i>&#42;gunþi</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;friþu</i> (“peace”). Equivalent to <i>gūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>friþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Guntfrid</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a warlike chief</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>fruma</i> (“chief”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gūþfugol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bird of war</li><li>an eagle</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>fugol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>War-Geats, a term for the tribe of Geats used in <i>Beowulf</i></li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“war”) +&lrm; <i>Ġēatas</i> (“Geats”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) warlike dress or equipment</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>ġetawa</i> (“instruments, tools”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>battle, fight</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>ġemōt</i> (“meeting”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) equipment for battle</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>ġetawa</i> (“instruments, tools”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) battle</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>ġewinn</i> (“battle”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) battle-dress, armour</li></ol><p><i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>ġewǣde</i> (“dress”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>kenning</i>) eagle</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>hafoc</i> (“hawk”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Gunþihard</i>, from <i>&#42;gunþi</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hard</i> (“hard”). Equivalent to <i>gūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>heard</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Gundart</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a helmet</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>helm</i> (“helmet”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an army, band of warriors</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>here</i> (“army”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Guþhere)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Gunþihari</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Gunþiharjaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>here</i> (“army”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle, combat”) and <i>lāc</i> (“gift, offering”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) having a heart for battle; battle-minded, war-spirited</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>mōd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Gunþimundu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Gunþimunduz</i>, from <i>&#42;gunþiz</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;mundō</i> (“gift, offering”). Equivalent to <i>gūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>mund</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>Guðmundr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) warlike man</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>mæċġa</i> (“man”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gūþrēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) fierce in fighting</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>rēow</i> (“fierce, cruel”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a warrior</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>rinc</i> (“warrior”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) battle-famous</li></ol><p><i>gūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-rōf</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) warlike attack</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>rǣs</i> (“run, rush”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) war-clothing</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>sċeorp</i> (“clothing”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) war-clothing</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>sċrūd</i> (“clothing”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) war-hall, hall of warriors</li></ol><p><i>gūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>sele</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a sword</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>sweord</i> (“sword”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a warrior</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>wiga</i> (“warrior”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) comrade in battle</li></ol><p><i>gūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>wine</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a spear</li></ol><p>From <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>wudu</i> (“wood”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to itch</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jukkjan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an itch</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;jukkjā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jukjǭ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>irritating, itchy</li><li>putrid</li></ol><p>From <i>ġyċċe</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiċela</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiedd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jacksnipe, sandpiper</li><li>proverb</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġieddung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goddess</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gudinī</i>, equivalent to <i>god</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>gutin</i> (German <i>Göttin</i>), Old Norse <i>gyðja</i>, Middle Dutch <i>godinne</i> (Dutch <i>godin</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gyden</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>possessed by an evil spirit; mad</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gudīg</i>, equivalent to <i>god</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġif</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiefa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiefan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġift</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiefu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġield</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġieldan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>golden, (<i>relational</i>) gold</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gulþīn</i>, equivalent to <i>gold</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>gelden</i>, Old Saxon <i>guldin</i>, Old High German <i>guldīn</i>, Old Norse <i>gullinn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>golden-haired</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>gylden</i> (“golden”) +&lrm; <i>feax</i> (“hair”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gold mine, vein of gold</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>gylden</i> (“golden”) +&lrm; <i>weċġ</i> (“mass of metal”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġilm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġielp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġielpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġielpword</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guilt</li><li>offense, sin, wrongdoing</li></ol><p>Apparently from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gulti</i>. Further origin unknown. Compare <i>ġieldan</i>, which may have been connected, though the OED finds this "inadmissible phonologically."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sin</li><li>to be guilty</li></ol><p>Apparently from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gultijan</i>, by surface analysis, <i>gylt</i> (“guilt”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i> (infinitive suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who is guilty of a debt or offense; a debtor; offender</li></ol><p>From <i>gyltan</i> (“to be guilty; commit sin”) +&lrm; <i>-end</i> (agent suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>guilty; culpable</li><li>liable; bound</li><li>delinquent</li></ol><p>From <i>gylt</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġīeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġīeme</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġīemelēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġīemelēasian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġīemelēasnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġīemelīest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġīemend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mourn</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġimstān</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeond-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġinian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mud</li><li>fen, marsh</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gurwī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gurwijaz</i> (“manure, dung, mud”), related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gurą</i> (“manure”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>gere</i>, <i>jere</i>, <i>iere</i> (“liquid manure, dirty water”). Alternatively, perhaps derived from Old English <i>gyru</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġierd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gird</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gurdijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>belt</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gurdilaz</i> (“girdle, belt”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare</li><li>to clothe</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġierela</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cry out, roar</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeornan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>affliction, trouble</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ġeorran</i> (“to creak, chatter”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;garrijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garrijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roar, rumble, bellow</li></ol><p>From <i>ġyrran</i> +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiestran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiestrandæġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiestranniht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>gyr</i> (“mud; fen, marsh”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gurwiju</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gurwijō</i>, variant of <i>&#42;gurwijaz</i> (“manure, dung”). Alternatively, yet less likely, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;garwiju</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garwijō</i> (“yeast, filth, sediment, excrement”), whence Middle High German <i>gerwe</i> (“yeast, residue, excretion, faeces”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġierwan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jarrow</li><li>the Gyrwas</li></ol><p>Likely derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gurwijō</i> (“marsh, fen”). Equivalent to <i>ġyru</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i> ("marsh folk")</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gæsthūs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be a guest or lodged in another's house</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġiest</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiestran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġīet</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġīeta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pouring</li><li>shedding (of blood, sweat, tears)</li><li>inundation, flood</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;guti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gutiz</i> (“gush, outflow”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰewd-</i> (“to pour”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>gete</i>, Old High German <i>guz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġēa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġe-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>union, assembly, joining, fellowship</li><li>collection, congregation</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gad</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰedʰ-</i> (“to unite, assemble, keep”). Related to <i>gaderian</i>, <i>gaderung</i>, <i>geador</i>, <i>gadere</i>. More at <i>gather</i> and <i>together</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kinsman</li><li>comrade, companion</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaduling</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gadilingaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>together</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gadar</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰedʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gaderian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gaderung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gaderian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gafol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeġn</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gagin</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gagin</i>, whence also Old Saxon <i>gigin</i>, Old High German <i>gagan</i> (-> German <i>gegen</i>), Old Norse <i>gegn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hinder, delay, impede</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to suspend, keep in suspense; to dupe</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to hesitate, linger, stall</li><li>(<i>transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive</i>) to remit, relax; to neglect</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gailijan</i>, possibly from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gailō</i> (“lane, passageway”). Compare Old Norse <i>geil</i> (“narrow glen, passage”), Middle English <i>gale</i> (“way, course”). Compare also assumed Old English <i>&#42;gāl</i> ("obstacle, boundary, marker"&#59; &gt; Middle English <i>gol</i> (“boundary, limit”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġealpettung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>luxury; extravagance</li><li>a glutton</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gailisōn</i> (“to be wanton, live luxuriously, be voluptuous”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gailaz</i> (“luxurious, voluptious, extravagant”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰoylo-</i> (“to foam, be arrogant, be boisterous, be violent”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gamenian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To gape</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ghent (a city in East Flanders, Belgium).</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġēar</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġearcian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>græs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gærsum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>græsgrēne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>græshoppa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>græsstapa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>græsstapa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A treasure or valuable.</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) A payment or fee.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Norse <i>gersemi</i>, <i>gørsemi</i> (“treasure”), from <i>gerr</i>, <i>gǫrr</i> (“ready”) +&lrm; <i>-semi</i> (“-ness”), with the ending assimilated to native <i>-sum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deprived of, void of; barren, lifeless</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaisnī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gāst</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>ġiest</i> (“guest”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frighten; gast</li><li>to torment; afflict</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaistijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaistijaną</i>, related to <i>&#42;gaistaz</i> (“fear, terror”). Cognate with Gothic <i>𐌿𐍃𐌲𐌰𐌹𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (usgaisjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiesthūs</i></li></ol><p>From <i>gæst</i> (“guest”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gāstliċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġiestlīþnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) goat; goaten</li><li>made of a goat's pelt or fur: goatskin</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaitīn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaitīnaz</i>, equivalent to <i>gāt</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>geiȥīn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have, possess</li><li>(<i>auxiliary</i>) have (<i>used with a past participle to express the perfect tense</i>)</li><li>(<i>catenative</i>) have to (<i>+ to-infinitive</i>)</li><li>to keep</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;habbjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;habjaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kap-</i> (“take, seize”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hebba</i>, Old Saxon <i>hebbian</i>, Old High German <i>habēn</i>, Old Norse <i>hafa</i>, Gothic <i>𐌷𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (haban), Latin <i>capere</i>, Old Irish <i>cacht</i>, Albanian <i>kap</i> (“grip”), Russian <i>ха́пать</i> (xápatʹ), Lithuanian <i>kàpteleti</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>habbaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hæfern</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ābrahām</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hook, a door fastening</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hakō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hakô</i> (“hook”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloak</li></ol><p>An extension of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hakul</i>, <i>&#42;hakil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hakulaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) pike</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hakud</i>, <i>&#42;hakid</i> (“pike”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hacele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Hakon Sweynson; son of Swegn Godwinson, Earl of Hereford and brother of Harold Godwinson.</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>Hákon</i>. Cognate with Norwegian <i>Håkon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hacele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person, individual</li><li>a character</li><li>individuality</li><li>rank, status</li><li>a person of the Trinity</li><li>honor, dignity</li><li>office (<i>especially religious</i>)</li><li>state, condition; nature, manner</li><li>sex, gender</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) grammatical person</li><li>race; kindred, family; tribe, group</li><li>choir</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haidu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haiduz</i> (“state, condition, rank, person”). Akin to Old Norse <i>heiðr</i> (“dignity, honor”), Gothic <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌿𐍃</i> (haidus, “manner”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recompense, compensation, or atonement for injury done to persons in holy orders</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hād</i> +&lrm; <i>bōt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ordain</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clear (of sound or light)</li><li>bright</li><li>serene</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ordination</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hādian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Anglian</i>) alternative form of <i>hacele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Anglian</i>) alternative form of <i>hacele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heardhara</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hafela</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hæfst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hæfþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hafocere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) head</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;habulō</i>. Compare Old Frisian <i>heila</i> and <i>holla</i> (“head”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hæfern</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to applaud, clap</li><li>to beat; (<i>birds</i>) to flap</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps an alteration of <i>hafettan</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;habattjan</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hab</i>, <i>&#42;habu</i> (“lift, lifting”) + <i>&#42;-attjan</i>; or derived from the related noun <i>&#42;hafet</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;habati</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;habatją</i> (“a lifting, an elevating”), equivalent to <i>&#42;hafet</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p><p>Alternatively, an alteration of <i>āfētian</i> (“to stomp with the feet, applaud, praise”). Possibly a merger of both theories.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>hafetian</i></li></ol><p>Alteration of <i>hafetian</i>, modelled on verbs in <i>-ettan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hafetian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hawk</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;habuk</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the hawk species; hawk-kind</li></ol><p>From <i>hafoc</i> +&lrm; <i>cynn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hawker, falconer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;habukārī</i>, equivalent to <i>hafoc</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>hafoc</i> (“hawk”)</li></ol><p>From <i>hafoc</i> +&lrm; <i>fugol</i>, literally “hawk-bird”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hafetian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hafoc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hēafodland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hæfern</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hedge, thicket, haw</li><li>an enclosure, a fenced-in area, a yard</li><li>a homestead, house</li><li>used figuratively as a suffix to denote military formations containing spearmen (as in anhaga, cumbolhaga, færhaga, wighaga)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hagō</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hago</i>, Middle Dutch <i>haghe</i>, Old Norse <i>hagi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hagalode</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to hail</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haglōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haglōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>haguþorn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Hagen, a Burgundian hero of Germanic legend.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hagunō</i>. Equivalent to <i>haga</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>. Compare Old High German <i>Hagano</i>, Old West Norse <i>Hǫgni</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hæġl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hagalode</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hagalian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hagalode</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hailstone</li></ol><p>From <i>hagol</i> (“hail”) +&lrm; <i>stān</i> (“stone”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unmarried man belonging to a royal court; young warrior</li><li>bachelor</li><li>young man; liegeman</li><li>celibacy</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unmarried</li><li>independent</li><li>(<i>referring to young men</i>) military</li><li>virgin</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hagustaldaz</i> (“unmarried man”, literally “set at the hedge”), a compound of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hagą</i>, <i>&#42;hagaz</i>, <i>&#42;hagô</i> (“fence, enclosure, hedge”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kagʷh-</i> (“fence, box”) + a derivative of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stal-</i> (“to place, set”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stelə-</i> (“stem, trunk, base”). Equivalent to <i>haw</i> + <i>&#42;stold</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hagastald</i> (“servant, young man”), Old High German <i>hagastalt</i>, <i>hagastolt</i> (“mercenary, unwed person”), Proto-Norse <i>ᚺᚨᚷᚢᛊᛏᚨᛚᛞᚨᛉ</i> (hagustaldaʀ).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hagastaldą</i> (“celibacy, virginity”) (see above).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hexham (a town in Northumberland, England)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Hagustaldes</i> (“Hagustald's”) +&lrm; <i>ēa</i> (“island”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hexham (a town in Northumberland, England)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Hagustaldes</i> (“Hagustald's”) +&lrm; <i>hām</i> (“home, village, farm”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hexham, a town in Northumberland, England</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Hagustaldes</i> (“Hagustald's”) +&lrm; <i>ēa</i> (“river”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hawthorn</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haguþorn</i>, from <i>&#42;hagu</i> (“enclosure; pasture”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;þorn</i> (“thorn; briar”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>haguthorn</i>, Old High German <i>hagudorn</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;haguthorn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sound, healthy, intact</li><li>whole, undivided</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hail</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hailaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kéh₂ilos</i> (“healthy, whole, intact, well-omened”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hēl</i> (West Frisian <i>hiel</i>), Old Saxon <i>hēl</i> (Low German <i>hel</i>, <i>heel</i>, <i>heil</i>) Dutch <i>heel</i>, <i>geheel</i> (“healthy”), Old High German <i>heil</i> (German <i>heil</i> (“healthy”)), Old Norse <i>heill</i> (Danish and Swedish <i>hel</i> (“whole”)), Gothic <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (hails), Vandalic <i>eils</i>. The Proto-Indo-European root is also the source of Old Church Slavonic <i>целъ</i> (celŭ) (Russian <i>це́лый</i> (célyj, “whole, healthy”)), Ancient Greek <i>κοῖλυ</i> (koîlu, “goodness, beauty”), Welsh <i>coel</i> (“sign”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wholesome, salutary</li></ol><p>From <i>hāl</i> +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>healdan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haldan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hāliġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hail, salute, greet</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hailattjan</i>, <i>&#42;hailittjan</i>, <i>&#42;hailuttjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hailatjaną</i>, <i>&#42;hailitjaną</i>, <i>&#42;hailutjaną</i> (verbal suffix). From <i>hāl</i> +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>healf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holy man, saint (<i>male or of unspecified gender</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hailagō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hailagô</i> (“holy person”), weak masculine singular of <i>&#42;hailagaz</i> (“holy”). Cognate with Old High German <i>heilego</i> (“saint”). Formally equivalent to Old Norse <i>Helgi</i> (proper name, literally “Holy One”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Halga)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Halga, the brother of Hrothgar in the poem <i>Beowulf</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holy woman, (female) saint</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hailagā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hailagaberht</i>, equivalent to Old English <i>hāliġ</i> (“holy sacred”) + <i>berht</i> (“bright”). Cognate with Old High German <i>Heligbert</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make holy; hallow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hailagōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hailagōną</i>. Equivalent to <i>hāliġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> or <i>hāl</i> +&lrm; <i>-gian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hålogaland, i.e. a region of Norway</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>hāliġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hallowing, consecration</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hālgian</i> (“to hallow”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>healh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hāliġern</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>holy, sacred</li><li>healthy, sound</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hailag</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hailagaz</i>, equivalent to <i>hāl</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hēlich</i>, Old Saxon <i>hēlag</i>, Old High German <i>heilag</i>, Old Norse <i>heilagr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (hailags).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Holy Spirit</li></ol><p>Calque of Latin <i>Spīritus Sānctus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holiness, sanctity</li><li>holy thing, relic</li><li>holy place, sanctuary</li></ol><p>From <i>hāliġ</i> +&lrm; <i>dōm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holy day, the Sabbath</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hailagadagaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>hāliġ</i> (“holy”) +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i> (“day”). Cognate with West Frisian <i>hjeldei</i>, Icelandic <i>helgidagur</i> and Swedish <i>helgdag</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holy place, sanctuary</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hāliġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ern</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>holily</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hāliġ</i> (“holy”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the ninth month of the year according to the Anglo-Saxon calendar</li><li>September</li></ol><p>From <i>hāliġ</i> +&lrm; <i>mōnaþ</i>, lit. "holy month."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holiness, sanctity</li><li>something holy, such as a relic</li><li>sanctuary (holy place)</li><li>worship; practices, rites</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hailaganassī</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>heilagnessi</i>. Equivalent to <i>hāliġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hāliġnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hāliġnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holiness</li></ol><p>From <i>hāliġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a holy one; a hallow or saint</li></ol><p>From <i>hāliġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ware</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holy water</li></ol><p>From <i>hāliġ</i> +&lrm; <i>wæter</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salvation</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hāl</i> (“sound, intact”) +&lrm; <i>-or</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Old English</i>) halfpenny</li></ol><p><i>healf</i> +&lrm; <i>penning</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beseech, entreat, make a solemn appeal to</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hailisōną</i> (“to greet”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supplication, beseeching, entreaty, adjuration</li><li>exorcism</li><li>augury</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hālsian</i> (“to entreat”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>healswriþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>healing, healthful, salutary</li></ol><p>From <i>hāl</i> +&lrm; <i>-wende</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>hālwende</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>salutary, healthful</li></ol><p><i>hālwende</i> (“salutary, healthful”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hālwende</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>home</li><li>property, estate, farm</li><li>village; community</li><li>alternative form of <i>hamm</i> (“enclosure”)</li><li>alternative form of <i>hamm</i> (“inner knee”)</li><li>covering</li><li>garment, dress, gown; shirt</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>home, homeward</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haim</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haimaz</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hammaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>ham</i>, Middle Low German <i>hamme</i> (Low Low German <i>Hamm</i>).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hammō</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ham</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hamaz</i> (“covering”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>hamr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a covering</li><li>a skin or membrane</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hamō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hamô</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Hama)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>hām</i> (“home, realm”) +&lrm; <i>-a</i>, cognate with Old High German <i>Haimo</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a homecoming; return</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haimakumiz</i>, equivalent to <i>hām</i> +&lrm; <i>cyme</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>heimkváma</i>, <i>heimkoma</i> (“homecoming”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>broken, rugged</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hamalaz</i> (“mutilated”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mutilate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hamalōną</i>, from <i>&#42;hamalaz</i> (“mutilated”), whence Old English <i>hamel</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>homelia</i>, Old Norse <i>hamla</i>, Old High German <i>hamalōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to provide a home (for/to); house</li><li>to bring back (to a home, village, etc.)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haimattjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haimatjaną</i>, equivalent to <i>hām</i> +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>heimta</i> (“to fetch”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>journey home</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hām</i> +&lrm; <i>færeld</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>homeless</li><li>(<i>of a place</i>) without habitation</li></ol><p>From <i>hām</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ham, inner knee</li><li>enclosure, piece of enclosed land (especially land enclosed by a river)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hammō</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>hamme</i> (Dutch <i>ham</i>), Old High German <i>hamma</i> (dialectal German <i>Hamm</i>), Old Norse <i>hǫm</i>.</p><p>Either from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hammaz</i>, possibly related to <i>&#42;haimaz</i> (“village, estate”); or Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hamjaz</i>, deverbal of <i>&#42;hamjaną</i> (“pinch, hem in, enclose”), whence Old English <i>&#42;hemman</i> (Modern English <i>hem</i>).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>ham</i>, Middle Low German <i>hamme</i> (Low Low German <i>Hamm</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hammer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hamar</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hamaraz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱmoros</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂éḱmō</i> (“stone”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hamar</i> (West Frisian <i>hammer</i>), Old Saxon <i>hamar</i> (Low German <i>Hamer</i>), Dutch <i>hamer</i>, Old High German <i>hamar</i> (German <i>Hammer</i>), Old Norse <i>hamarr</i> (Danish <i>hammer</i>, Swedish <i>hammare</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sword</li></ol><p>From the genitive plural of hamor (“hammer”) + lāf (“remains, legacy”), in reference to the manner in which swords are made.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homestead; residence</li></ol><p>From <i>hām</i> +&lrm; <i>steall</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hampton (English toponym)</li></ol><p><i>hām</i> (“home”) +&lrm; <i>tūn</i> (“town”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hampshire</li></ol><p>From <i>Hāmtūn</i> +&lrm; <i>sċīr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>homeward; in the direction of home</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haimawarda</i>, equivalent to <i>hām</i> (“home”) +&lrm; <i>-weard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hāmweard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a projecting stone that forms part of a boundary</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rooster</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hanō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hanô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) the period of the early morning when roosters begin to crow; the wee hours of morning</li></ol><p><i>hana</i> (“rooster”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;crēd</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hancrēd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hand</li><li>side (of a conflict)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;handu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;handuz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hond</i>, Old Saxon <i>hand</i>, Old High German <i>hant</i>, Old Norse <i>hǫnd</i>, Gothic <i>𐌷𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌿𐍃</i> (handus).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hand in hand</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handbook, manual</li></ol><p>From <i>hand</i> (“hand”) +&lrm; <i>bōc</i> (“book”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palm (of the hand)</li></ol><p>From <i>hand</i> (“hand”) +&lrm; <i>bred</i> (“board, surface”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>handcosp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handcuff</li></ol><p>From <i>hand</i> (“hand”) +&lrm; <i>cosp</i> (“shackle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dexterity</li><li>handicraft, manual occupation: a skill or trade involving the hands</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;handukraftu</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>handkraft</i>. Equivalent to <i>hand</i> +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dexterous</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hand</i> +&lrm; <i>cræftiġ</i> or <i>handcræft</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handful</li></ol><p><i>hand</i> +&lrm; <i>-full</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surrender</li><li>laying on of hands</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;handugangaz</i>, derived from the phrase <i>&#42;ana handų ganganą</i> ~ <i>&#42;gāną</i>, whence Old English <i>on hand gān</i> and Old Norse <i>ganga á hǫnd</i>. Compare the pair <i>tō hlāfe gān</i> (“to go to eat bread,” literally “go to bread”) and <i>hlāfgang</i> (“going to eat bread,” literally “bread-going”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a battle</li></ol><p>From <i>hand</i> (“hand”) +&lrm; <i>ġemōt</i> (“meeting”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manual labor</li><li>something made with the hands: handiwork, handcraft</li></ol><p><i>hand</i> (“hand”) +&lrm; <i>ġeweorc</i> (“work”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something written by hand, manuscript; handwriting</li><li>autograph</li><li>deed, contract, agreement</li><li>holograph</li></ol><p>From <i>hand</i> (“hand”) +&lrm; <i>ġewrit</i> (“writing”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handgrip, grasp</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;handugripi</i>, equivalent to <i>hand</i> +&lrm; <i>gripe</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>hantgrif</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a moment</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hand</i> (“hand”) +&lrm; <i>hwīl</i> (“while”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;handulā</i>, from <i>&#42;handulōn</i> (“to handle”), equivalent to <i>handlian</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to handle (touch or feel with the hands)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;handulōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;handulōną</i>. Equivalent to <i>hand</i> +&lrm; <i>-lian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with the hands; by hand</li><li>(<i>in combat</i>) in close quarters; hand-to-hand</li></ol><p>From <i>hand</i> +&lrm; <i>-linga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>touching, handling</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;handulungu</i>, equivalent to <i>handlian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>handlinga</i></li></ol><p>From <i>hand</i> +&lrm; <i>-lunga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fingernail</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hand</i> (“hand”) +&lrm; <i>næġl</i> (“nail”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fighting, combat</li></ol><p>From <i>hand</i> (“hand”) +&lrm; <i>plega</i> (“play, exercise”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chaplain</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hand</i> (“hand”) +&lrm; <i>prēost</i> (“priest”), originally implying a "priest at someone's hand."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>liable to lose a hand</li></ol><p>From <i>hand</i> +&lrm; <i>scyldig</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a short sword; dagger</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;handusahs</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;handusahsą</i>, equivalent to <i>hand</i> +&lrm; <i>seax</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>handsax</i> (“short sword, dirk”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a giving of something (into another's hand)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hand</i> (“hand”) +&lrm; <i>sellan</i> (“to give”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>signature</li></ol><p><i>hand</i> +&lrm; <i>seten</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tame enough to be handled</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;handutam</i>, equivalent to <i>hand</i> +&lrm; <i>tam</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>hantzam</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manual labor</li><li>something made with the hands: handiwork, handcraft</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;handuwerką</i>. Equivalent to <i>hand</i> (“hand”) +&lrm; <i>weorc</i> (“work”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>handmade</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;handuwurhtaz</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐌷𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌿𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (handuwaurhts). Equivalent to <i>hand</i> + <i>(ġe)worht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hatchet</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;handakwisī</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>handøx</i>. Equivalent to <i>hand</i> +&lrm; <i>æx</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handwashing</li></ol><p><i>hand</i> (“hand”) +&lrm; <i>þwēal</i> (“washing”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an implement that hangs; an attachment</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hangalljā</i>, <i>&#42;hangilljā</i> (“that which hangs”), equivalent to <i>hangian</i> +&lrm; <i>-elle</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>hangilla</i>, <i>hengila</i> (“cluster of grapes”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang</li><li>to depend (with <i>on</i> + dative = on something)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hangēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wooded hillside or slope</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>hangian</i>. Compare Middle Low German <i>hanc</i> (“slope”), Old Dutch <i>&#42;hanga</i> (“slope”, in placenames), Middle Dutch <i>hangende</i> (“slope, steep slope”), Middle High German <i>hanc</i> (“slope”). Compare also Middle Low German <i>hangeren</i>, <i>hangern</i> (“hanging”, adjective).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hannibal, a Carthaginian general</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Hannibal</i>, from Punic <i>𐤇𐤍𐤁𐤏𐤋</i> (ḥnbʿl /⁠ḥannībaʿl⁠/, “May the Lord (Baal) grace me”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hāmtūnsċīr</i>, appearing in the Domesday Book</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grey</li><li>grey-haired, old and grey, venerable</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hair</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sirius</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rabbit, hare</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hasō ~ &#42;haʀ-</i>, from the voiced Verner alternant of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hasô</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hasa</i>, Old Saxon <i>haso</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;haso</i>, Old High German <i>haso</i>, Old Norse <i>heri</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harefoot (<i>Trifolium arvense</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>haran</i> (“hare's”) +&lrm; <i>hige</i>, from <i>hīgian</i> (“to hasten, race”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hārhune</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horehound (herb)</li></ol><p>From <i>hār</i> (“hoar”) +&lrm; <i>hune</i> (“horehound”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hoar, have one's hair become hoary</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hār</i> (“hoar”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>Haraldr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Harjawaldaz</i>, from <i>&#42;harjaz</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;waldaz</i> (“ruler, authority”), equivalent to <i>here</i> +&lrm; <i>weald</i>. Doublet of native Hereweald.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hoarse</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haisaz</i>, whence also Old High German <i>heis</i>, Old Norse <i>háss</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hoarseness</li></ol><p>From <i>hās</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coarse grass</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dusky; grey; ashen</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hasu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grey; greyish</li></ol><p>From <i>hasu</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hot</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a promise</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hait</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hēt</i> (West Frisian <i>hjit</i>), Old Saxon <i>hēt</i>, Dutch <i>heet</i>, Old High German <i>heiz</i> (German <i>heiß</i>), Old Norse <i>heitr</i> (Swedish <i>het</i>). Cognate to Albanian <i>ethe</i> (“shiver, fiever”), dialectal <i>hethe</i> and <i>ith</i> (“warmth, body heat”), dialectal <i>hith</i>.</p><p>From <i>hātan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hater</li></ol><p>From <i>hatian</i>, equivalent to <i>hatian</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call, name</li><li>to order, command, give orders, bid</li><li>to promise</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be called</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haitan</i> (“to command, name”).</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hēta</i>, Old Saxon <i>hētan</i>, Old High German <i>heizzan</i>, Old Norse <i>heita</i> (Swedish <i>heta</i>), Gothic <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (haitan). The Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek <i>κίειν</i> (kíein, “put in motion”), Latin <i>ciēre</i> (“rouse, make active”) and Albanian <i>cys</i> (“to spur, set in motion”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hātaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>furious, enraged</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fury, anger, wrath</li></ol><p>From <i>hāt</i> (“hot”) +&lrm; <i>-heort</i> (“hearted”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anger, fury, rage</li></ol><p>From <i>hāt</i> +&lrm; <i>heorte</i>. Compare Old High German <i>heizherzī</i> (“fury”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fury</li><li>fervor, zeal</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hātheort</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hātheortnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hātheortnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>hātheort</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make angry</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hāt</i> +&lrm; <i>hiertan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hate</li><li>to become hot</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hatēn</i>.</p><p>From <i>hāt</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>hāt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>hāt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hedgehog</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hot, torrid</li></ol><p>From <i>hāt</i> (“heat”) +&lrm; <i>-wende</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to view, to look around</li><li>to survey, to inspect</li><li>to observe</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hawēn</i> (“to observe”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>he</li><li>it (when the thing being referred to is masculine)</li><li>they (singular) <i>(denotes someone of unknown gender)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hiʀ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hiz</i> (“this, this one”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hēahberht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hēahberht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hēof</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hēahfore</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a head, cephalized</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hēafod</i> +&lrm; <i>-eht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to behead</li></ol><p>From <i>hēafod</i> (“head”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hafela</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hafoc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) head</li><li>top</li><li>captain: head, leader</li><li>source, origin</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haubud</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haubudą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;káput-</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hāved</i>, Old Saxon <i>hōƀid</i>, Dutch <i>hoofd</i>, Old High German <i>houbit</i>, Old Norse <i>hǫfuð</i>, and Gothic <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐌱𐌹𐌸</i> (haubiþ). The Germanic source is related to Latin <i>caput</i> and Sanskrit <i>कपाल</i> (kapāla), though the Sanskrit cognate doesn't directly come from *káput-, but rather its first element.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake one's head</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>main; head, chief, principle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haubida-</i> (“main”), derived from the noun <i>&#42;haubid</i> (“head”) (Old English <i>hēafod</i>). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hāved-</i>, Old Saxon <i>hōvid-</i>, Old Dutch <i>hōvit-</i>, and Old High German <i>houbit-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skull</li></ol><p><i>hēafod</i> (“head”) +&lrm; <i>bān</i> (“bone”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crown</li></ol><p><i>hēafod</i> +&lrm; <i>bēag</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hēafodbēag</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pillow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haubidabolstr</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>houbitbolstar</i>. Equivalent to <i>hēafod</i> +&lrm; <i>bolster</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capital city</li><li>main city, town, fortress, or castle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haubidaburg</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>houbitburg</i>. Equivalent to <i>hēafod-</i> +&lrm; <i>burg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hēafodburg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cathedral</li><li>main church</li></ol><p><i>hēafod-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċiriċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>clothing</i>) headcloth, headdress</li></ol><p>From <i>hēafod</i> +&lrm; <i>clāþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>headache</li></ol><p><i>hēafod</i> (“head”) +&lrm; <i>eċe</i> (“ache”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crown</li></ol><p><i>hēafod</i> (“head”) +&lrm; <i>gold</i> (“gold”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boundary, headland</li></ol><p>From <i>hēafod</i> (“head”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“stone”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>headless</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haubidalaus</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hāvedlās</i>, Old High German <i>houbitlōs</i>. Equivalent to <i>hēafod</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>position</i>) top</li><li>(<i>rank</i>) main; principal, chief</li><li>(<i>sins</i>) deadly, mortal</li></ol><p>From <i>hēafod</i> and <i>hēafod-</i> + <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an equal, fellow, fellow-servant, mate</li></ol><p>From <i>hēafod</i> +&lrm; <i>-ling</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>captain; leader, head</li><li>main person</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haubidamann</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hāvedmon</i>, Old Saxon <i>hōvidmann</i>, Old Dutch <i>hōvitman</i>, Old High German <i>houbitman</i>. Equivalent to <i>hēafod-</i> +&lrm; <i>mann</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>close or important relative</li></ol><p><i>hēafod-</i> +&lrm; <i>mǣġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chief virtue</li></ol><p><i>hēafod-</i> +&lrm; <i>mæġen</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skull</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haubudapannā</i>, equivalent to <i>hēafod</i> (“head”) +&lrm; <i>panne</i> (“pan”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a post on which a criminal's head was placed after a beheading, often used to mark boundaries of land</li></ol><p><i>hēafod</i> (“head”) +&lrm; <i>stocc</i> (“post”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>main place, capital</li><li>main chair</li></ol><p><i>hēafod-</i> +&lrm; <i>stōl</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deadly sin: cardinal sin, mortal sin</li><li>main sin</li></ol><p>From <i>hēafod-</i> +&lrm; <i>synn</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>houbitsunta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>main road</li></ol><p><i>hēafod-</i> +&lrm; <i>weġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wind from one of the four chief points of the compass</li><li>main wind</li></ol><p>From <i>hēafod-</i> +&lrm; <i>wind</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>höfuðvindr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>main song</li></ol><p>From <i>hēafod-</i> (“main”) +&lrm; <i>wōþ</i> (“song”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>headwound</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haubidawundu</i>, equivalent to <i>hēafod</i> +&lrm; <i>wund</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hāvedwunde</i> and Old Saxon <i>hōvidwunda</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head pain, headache</li></ol><p>From <i>hēafod</i> (“head”) +&lrm; <i>wærċ</i> (“pain”). Cognate with Danish <i>hovedvark</i> (“headache”), Swedish <i>huvudvärk</i> (“headache”), Icelandic <i>höfuðverkur</i> (“headache”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cephalic vein</li></ol><p><i>hēafod-</i> +&lrm; <i>ǣdre</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) alternative form of <i>hafela</i></li></ol><p>Apparently from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;habulō</i>. Compare Old Frisian <i>heila</i>, <i>holla</i> (“head”), Sanskrit <i>कपाल</i> (kapā́la, “skull; forehead”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hēahfore</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heofon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīehst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high, tall</li><li>exalted, important</li><li>proud</li><li>deep</li><li>right (<i>as opposed to left</i>)</li><li>(<i>in compounds</i>) main, principal, arch-</li><li>(<i>in compounds</i>) denotes intensification, completion or perfection</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hauh</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hauhaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hāh</i>, Old Saxon <i>hōh</i>, Old High German <i>hōh</i>, Old Dutch <i>hōh</i>, Old Norse <i>hár</i>, Gothic <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐌷𐍃</i> (hauhs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hauhaberht</i>, from <i>&#42;hauh</i> (“high”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”). Equivalent to <i>hēah</i> +&lrm; <i>berht</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Hōhberht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archbishop</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hēah</i> (“high”) +&lrm; <i>bisċop</i> (“bishop”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hēahberht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hauhaburg</i>, from <i>&#42;hauh</i> (“high”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;burg</i> (“fortification”). Equivalent to <i>hēah</i> +&lrm; <i>burg</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Hōhburg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a primary room</li></ol><p>From <i>hēah</i> (“high”) +&lrm; <i>clēafa</i> (“room”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a chief, high king (usually used of God)</li></ol><p>From <i>hēah</i> +&lrm; <i>cyning</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deer</li><li>stag</li></ol><p><i>hēah</i> (“high”) +&lrm; <i>dēor</i> (“animal”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deerhound, staghound</li></ol><p><i>hēahdēor</i> (“stag, deer”) +&lrm; <i>hund</i> (“dog”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hēahhelm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hēahengel</i></li></ol><p>From <i>hēah</i> +&lrm; <i>enċġel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an archangel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hauhaangil</i>, equivalent to <i>hēah</i> +&lrm; <i>enġel</i>. Cognate with Middle High German <i>hōchengel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>high tide</li><li>deep water</li></ol><p>From <i>hēah</i> +&lrm; <i>flōd</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>háflæðr</i> (“a high flood-tide”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heifer</li></ol><p>This first element of this word is customarily identified with <i>hēah</i> (“tall, high”), beginning with Franciscus Junius's 17th-century <i>Etymologicum Anglicanum</i>; the remainder is now usually held to be from either <i>faru</i> (“journey”) or <i>fearr</i> (“bull”), though the latter is less likely.</p><p>Discussion of Liberman's alternative etymology</p><p>Liberman instead suggests that this compound is from <i>&#42;hæg-</i> (“pen”, see <i>haga</i>) + <i>-fore</i> (“inhabitant”). However, this etymology fails to explain the phonological shape of this word (a long vowel is required by Middle English forms in /ɛː/, while <i>&#42;hæg</i> should become palatalised <i>&#42;hæġ</i>, which would stymie breaking of /æ/ and devoicing of ⟨h⟩ /x/. Additionally, this palatalisation would create an open syllable, resulting in /f/ being voiced to /v/), while his putative suffix <i>-fore</i> lacks a clear etymology and is not securely attested (<i>elver</i> is simply Middle English <i>el</i> (“eel”) +&lrm; <i>fare</i> (“group of journeyers”); the word originally referred to a group of young eels). Furthermore, Liberman's semantic objections to the traditional etymology do not hold water. Old English <i>hēah</i> can mean "tall", and <i>faran</i> already means "to live (in a specified way)"; the word would simply mean "one who lives (while being) tall", originally being a generic term for any adult cow.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hauhaburg</i>, from <i>&#42;hauh</i> (“high”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;friþu</i> (“peace”). Equivalent to <i>hēah</i> +&lrm; <i>friþ</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>Hoffridus</i> (via Frankish).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hēahfore</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a patriarch</li><li>(<i>theology</i>) God the Father</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hauhafader</i> (“patriarch”), equivalent to <i>hēah</i> +&lrm; <i>fæder</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very fixed, stable, constant</li></ol><p>From <i>hēah</i> (“high”) +&lrm; <i>fæst</i> (“fixed, stable, constant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>theology</i>) the Holy Ghost</li></ol><p>From <i>hēah</i> +&lrm; <i>gāst</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a title apparently higher in rank than a <i>ġerēfa</i> (“reeve”)</li></ol><p>From <i>hēah</i> (“high”) +&lrm; <i>ġerēfa</i> (“reeve”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palace</li></ol><p>From <i>hēah</i> +&lrm; <i>heall</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>heah</i> (“high, tall”) and <i>helm</i> (“protection, defense”). Variant of Heahelm.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high-hearted, haughty, proud</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hauhahertaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>hēah</i> (“high”) +&lrm; <i>heorte</i> (“heart”). Cognate with Gothic <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐌷𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍄𐍃</i> (hauhhairts, “proud, haughty”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>high ground</li></ol><p>From <i>hēah</i> (“high”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>headteacher</li><li>head monk</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hēah</i> +&lrm; <i>lārēow</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>hēah</i> (“high, tall”) and <i>mund</i> (“protector”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>height, tallness, highness</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>altitude</li></ol><li>the highest point, the top</li><li>a high place</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the sky, the heavens</li></ol><li>the summit, the pinnacle, the zenith</li><li>loftiness, sublimity, excellence</li><li>high prestige, eminence</li><li>ecstasy</li><li>self-exaltation, haughtiness, pride</li><li>depth, deepness,</li></ol><p>From <i>hēah</i> (“high”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>hēah</i> (“high”) +&lrm; <i>nōþ</i> (“daring”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hauharād</i>, from <i>&#42;hauh</i> (“high”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;rād</i> (“counsel, advice”). Equivalent to <i>hēah</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣd</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Hōhrāt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīehst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hauhastain</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Hauhastainaz</i>, from <i>&#42;hauhaz</i> (“high”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;stainaz</i> (“stone”). Equivalent to <i>hēah</i> +&lrm; <i>stān</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>Hōhstein</i>, Old Norse <i>Hásteinn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) high (or important) place, promontory</li></ol><p><i>hēah</i> +&lrm; <i>stede</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>festival, solemnity, high day</li></ol><p>From <i>hēah</i> (“high”) +&lrm; <i>tīd</i> (“time”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hauhaward</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Hauhawardaz</i>, from <i>&#42;hauhaz</i> (“high”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wardaz</i> (“guard”). Equivalent to <i>hēah</i> +&lrm; <i>weard</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Hōhowart</i>, Old Norse <i>Hávarðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>main road, road between towns</li></ol><p>From <i>hēah</i> +&lrm; <i>weġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hauhawolf</i>, from <i>&#42;hauh</i> (“high”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wulf</i> (“wolf”). Equivalent to <i>hēah</i> +&lrm; <i>wulf</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Hōhulf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exalted glory, high majesty</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hēah</i> +&lrm; <i>þrym</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīehþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rupture</li><li>hernia</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haulaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>healoc</i> (“nook”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hold, guardianship, protection, rule</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bent, inclined</li></ol><p>From or related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hulþaz</i> (“bent, inclined”). Distantly related to Etymology 2 below.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;halþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;halþaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep watch over (cattle etc.)</li><li>to hold fast, to grasp</li><li>to contain</li><li>to possess</li><li>to keep, hold, preserve something in a specific position or state</li><li>to keep to, maintain, observe a custom or habit</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to maintain one’s position against an enemy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haldan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haldaną</i> (“to watch, look after”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>halda</i> (West Frisian <i>hâlde</i>), Old Saxon <i>haldan</i> (Low German <i>holen</i>), Old Dutch <i>haldan</i> (Dutch <i>houden</i>), Old High German <i>haltan</i> (German <i>halten</i>), Old Norse <i>halda</i> (Swedish <i>hålla</i>, Danish <i>holde</i>), Gothic <i>𐌷𐌰𐌻𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (haldan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>protective</li></ol><p>From <i>heald</i> +&lrm; <i>-sum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>side</li><li>half</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>half</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;halbu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;halbō</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;halb</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;halbaz</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>half</i>, Old Saxon <i>half</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;half</i>, Old High German <i>halb</i>, Old Norse <i>halfr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌷𐌰𐌻𐌱𐍃</i> (halbs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a half-hour, half an hour</li></ol><p><i>healf</i> (“half”) +&lrm; <i>tīd</i> (“hour”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally</i>) half-</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) partially</li></ol><p>From <i>healf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-broken</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>healf-</i> +&lrm; <i>brocen</i> (past participle of <i>brecan</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>halfdead</li></ol><p><i>healf-</i> (“half-”) +&lrm; <i>cwic</i> (“alive”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>halfdead</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;halbadaudaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>healf-</i> +&lrm; <i>dēad</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Cognate with Old Norse <i>Halfdanr</i>, Old High German <i>Halbdani</i>. Equivalent to <i>healf</i> (“half”) + <i>Dene</i> (“Dane”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-naked</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;halbanakwadaz</i>. Composed of <i>healf-</i> +&lrm; <i>nacod</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>costing or worth the value of half a penny</li></ol><p>From <i>healf</i> (“half”) +&lrm; <i>peniġ</i> (“penny”) +&lrm; <i>weorþ</i> (“worth”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reddish</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>healf-</i> +&lrm; <i>rēad</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half guilty</li></ol><p>From <i>healf-</i> +&lrm; <i>scyldig</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-cooked</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>healf-</i> + <i>(ġe)soden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hemisphere</li></ol><p>From <i>healf-</i> (“half-”) +&lrm; <i>trendel</i> (“sphere”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half-way, a point equidistant from two extremes</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>healf-</i> +&lrm; <i>weġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A minor elevation in marshland.</li><li>A corner of an area, building or room; a nook.</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) A hidden or remote place.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;halh</i>, probably from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;halhaz</i>, either from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;klHk(ᵂ)-(n)-</i>, related to Serbo-Croatian <i>kuk</i> (“thigh, hip”), Bulgarian <i>кълка</i> (kǎlka, “hip”), Russian <i>колк</i> (kolk, “bony stump”), Latvian <i>kulksnis</i> (“hock”), Lithuanian <i>kulksnis</i> (“ankle”), Old Prussian <i>culczi</i> (“hip”), or from Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;(s)kel-</i> (“to bend”) (and if so related to Old Norse <i>hallr</i> (“slope, incline”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;halluz</i>), both of which are suggested roots cognate with Latin <i>calx</i> (“heel”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high, elevated, lofty</li><li>proud, very great, distinguished</li></ol><p>From <i>hēah</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>highly, on high, to a high position or degree, greatly, extremely</li><li>with a high quality, nobly</li><li>elaborately, with perfect workmanship</li></ol><p>From <i>hēah</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>hēalīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>hēalīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hall; residence</li><li>a large room forming part of a residence, in which the social and public affairs of the household are conducted</li><li>a stone; rock</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hallu</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hallu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;halluz</i>. Cognate with Proto-Norse <i>ᚺᚨᛚᚨᛉ</i> (halaʀ) whence Old Norse <i>hallr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) the stalk of a plant or grass</li><li>straw; hay; stubble</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;halm</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;halmą</i>, <i>&#42;halmaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A little corner or recess; concavity; nook.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;halhuk</i>, equivalent to <i>healh</i> +&lrm; <i>-oc</i>. Cognate with Middle Low German <i>hallik</i> (“cavity, hollow space between a kiln and oven”). Compare also Middle Low German <i>hallig</i>, <i>hallich</i>, <i>halg</i>, <i>halch</i> (“land beyond or unprotected by a dike, low-lying island”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>healræċed</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) palace</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>heall</i> (“hall”) +&lrm; <i>ræċed</i> (“palace”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neck</li><li>prow of a ship</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;halsaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hals</i>, Old Saxon <i>hals</i>, Old High German <i>hals</i>, Old Norse <i>hals</i>; and with Latin <i>collum</i> (“neck”), earlier <i>collus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collar or necklace, often made of gold or silver</li></ol><p><i>heals</i> +&lrm; <i>beag</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fine prescribed in substitution for capital and other punishments, preferential share of the wergeld</li></ol><p>From <i>heals</i> (“neck”) +&lrm; <i>fang</i> (“seizure”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) bedfellow, consort, lover, beloved</li></ol><p><i>heals</i> +&lrm; <i>ġebedda</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hālsian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) neck collar</li></ol><p>From <i>heals</i> (“neck”) +&lrm; <i>wriþa</i> (“collar, ring”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lame, limping</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;halt</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haltaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to limp, be lame</li><li>to halt</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haltōn</i>, equivalent to <i>healt</i> (“limping”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>low, mean, abject, humble</li><li>poor, miserable</li><li>humiliated; despicable</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hauniz</i>, whence also the Old High German noun <i>hōna</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Haimarīks</i>, from <i>&#42;haimaz</i> (“home”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;rīks</i> (“king”). Cognate with English <i>Henry</i>, French <i>Henri</i>, Welsh <i>Harri</i>, German <i>Heinrich</i>, Danish <i>Henrik</i>, and Old Norse <i>Heimríkr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Late Old English form of <i>Hēanric</i> (male given name), Influenced by and quickly replaced by Norman <i>Henri</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wolverhampton (a city in the West Midlands, England).</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hever (a village and civil parish in Sevenoaks district, Kent, England)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hēah</i> (“high”) +&lrm; <i>yfer</i> (“bank, ridge”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>group</li><li>heap</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haup</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hāp</i>, Old Saxon <i>hōp</i>, Old High German <i>houf</i>. Old Norse <i>hópr</i> differs from the expected form <i>*haupr</i> because it is a borrowing from Middle Low German.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by groups</li><li>in heaps</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haupamālum</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>houfmālum</i>. Equivalent to <i>hēap</i> +&lrm; <i>-mǣlum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heora</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hard, harsh, austere, severe, rigorous, stern, stubborn, firm, hardy, brave</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hard(ī)</i>. Compare Old Frisian <i>herd</i>, Old Saxon <i>hard</i>, Old Dutch <i>hart</i>, Old High German <i>hart</i>, Old Norse <i>harðr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌷𐌰𐍂𐌳𐌿𐍃</i> (hardus).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hardaberht</i>, from <i>&#42;hard</i> (“hard, brave”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”). Equivalent to <i>heard</i> +&lrm; <i>berht</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Hardebert</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>severely, greatly, very much, sorely</li></ol><p><i>heard</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) hard-edged</li></ol><p><i>heard</i> (“hard”) +&lrm; <i>eċġ</i> (“edge”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>heard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mullet (fish of the family Mullidae or Mugilidae)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>heard</i> (“hard”) and a second element either corresponding to <i>hara</i> (“hare”) or connected to Swedish <i>harr</i> (“grayling”); compare Dutch <i>harder</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chisel</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>heard</i> (“hard”) +&lrm; <i>hēawan</i> (“to hew”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hardhearted</li></ol><p>From <i>heard</i> (“hard”) +&lrm; <i>-heort</i> (“hearted”). Compare English <i>hardhearted</i> and German <i>hartherzig</i>, which are formed similarly.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be or become hard; to harden</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hardōn</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;hardēn</i>. Equivalent to <i>heard</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brave man, hero</li><li>warrior</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>heard</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Hearding)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>hearding</i> (“brave man, hero”). Suffixed with <i>-ing</i>. The first element may either be <i>heard</i> (“hard”) or <i>heord</i>, <i>hād</i> (“hair”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hazdaz</i> (“hair”). For the latter compare Old Norse <i>haddingjar</i>, Latin <i>Hasdingi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>heard</i> (“harsh, brave”) + <i>lāc</i> (“sport, gift, offering”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>severe, fierce, strict</li></ol><p>From <i>heard</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>boldly, bravely, hardily</li><li>hardly, not easily, inflexibly, severely, harshly, sternly, sorely</li></ol><p>From <i>heard</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hardness</li><li>callosity</li><li>obduracy</li><li>strictness</li><li>severity</li><li>austerity</li></ol><p>From <i>heard</i> (“hard”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>hearde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>hearde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heardhara</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Heardred; a legendary Geatish king living around the early sixth century CE.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hardurād</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Hardurēdaz</i>, from <i>&#42;harduz</i> (“hard, brave”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;rēdaz</i> (“advice, counsel”). Equivalent to <i>heard</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣd</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Hartrāt</i>, Old Norse <i>Harðráðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unhappy, unfortunate, unlucky</li></ol><p>From <i>heard</i> +&lrm; <i>sǣliġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>āheardung</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>heardian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hardawini</i>, from <i>&#42;hard</i> (“hard, brave”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wini</i> (“friend”). Equivalent to <i>heard</i> +&lrm; <i>wine</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Hartwin</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pile of stones</li><li>altar</li><li>sacred place; grove; temple</li><li>idol</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;harug</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;harugaz</i> (“stoneheap, pile of stone; altar”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hearg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harm</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;harmaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱormo-</i> (“suffering, pain”). Compare Old Norse <i>harmr</i>, Old Saxon <i>harm</i>, Modern German <i>Harm</i>, Avestan <i>𐬟𐬱𐬀𐬭𐬆𐬨𐬀</i> (fšar<sup><small>ə</small></sup>ma), Middle Persian (šarm), Modern Persian شرم (šarm).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ermine</li><li>shrewmouse</li><li>fieldmouse; dormouse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;harmō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;harmô</i> (“ermine; weasel”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱormen-</i>, <i>&#42;ḱormō</i> (“weasel”). Cognate with Old High German <i>harmo</i> (“ferret”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ermine</li></ol><p>From <i>hearmasċinn</i> (“ermine-skin”) +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hearmcweþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to revile; to speak abusively or badly of</li><li>to calumniate; to slander; to libel</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hearm</i> +&lrm; <i>cweþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to harm</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hearm</i> (“harm”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harp</li><li>lyre</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;harpǭ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harper</li></ol><p>From <i>hearpe</i> (“harp”) + <i>-ere</i> (“er”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the string of a harp</li></ol><p>From <i>hearpe</i> (“harp”) +&lrm; <i>strenġ</i> (“string”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) lord, master</li></ol><p>From Old Saxon <i>hērro</i>, from Old High German <i>hēriro</i>, <i>hērro</i>, the comparative form of <i>hēr</i> (“noble, venerable”) (German <i>hehr</i>), by analogy with Latin <i>senior</i> (“elder”). The Old High German word originally meant "grey, grey-haired", and descends from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hairaz</i> (“grey”), making it cognate with Old English <i>hār</i> (English <i>hoar</i>), Old Norse <i>hárr</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hēra</i>, Old Dutch <i>hērro</i>, <i>hēro</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heorre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hierstepanne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hasu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike, smite, slash, cut</li><li>to chop, hew, hash, fell wood</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hawwaną</i>, a reduplicating verb, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kewh₂-</i> (“to hew; to hit, strike; to forge”). Cognate with Old Dutch <i>houwan</i> (modern Dutch <i>houwen</i>), Old Frisian <i>hawa</i>, Old High German <i>houwan</i> (modern German <i>hauen</i>), Old Norse <i>hǫggva</i> (Icelandic <i>höggva</i>, Swedish <i>hugga</i>), Old Saxon <i>hauwan</i>, <i>hauuan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heaþulāc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a restraint</li><li>confinement</li></ol><p>Pokorny derives it from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kot-</i> (“dwelling space”), comparing words such as Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kutą</i> (“shed, hut, stall”), Old Norse <i>kytja</i> (“small cottage”), Ancient Greek <i>κοτύλη</i> (kotúlē, “cup, pint”); however, the relation and origin of all of these words is unclear. Also compare the cognates listed at Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kotьcь</i> (“cottage, paddock”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to restrain</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>heaþor</i> (“restraint”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) alternative form of <i>heaþurinc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heaþurōf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) alternative form of <i>heaþuwǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) battle, fighting</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;heaþu</i> (“war”) +&lrm; <i>-lāc</i> (“-ation”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) warrior, hero</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;heaþu</i> +&lrm; <i>rinc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brave</li><li>famous in war</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;heaþu</i> +&lrm; <i>-rōf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) war-dress, armour</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;heaþu</i> +&lrm; <i>wǣd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lift, raise</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;habbjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;habjaną</i> (“to lift”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heofon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hæfern</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ebrēisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) kid (young goat)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hōkīn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fence</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haggju</i>. Cognate with Danish <i>hæk</i>, Dutch <i>heg</i>, German <i>Hecke</i>, Icelandic <i>hekk</i>, Swedish <i>häck</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Anglian</i>) alternative form of <i>hacele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Anglian</i>) alternative form of <i>hacele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to pay attention, take notice (<i>+ genitive</i>)</li></ol><p>Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hōdijan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hodian</i> (Middle Low German <i>höden</i>), Dutch <i>hoeden</i>, Old High German <i>huoten</i> (German <i>hüten</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>storehouse</li><li>storeroom</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hēdd</i> +&lrm; <i>-ern</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hood, chasuble</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hadinaz</i>, <i>&#42;hidanaz</i> (compare <i>&#42;hōdaz</i> (“hood”)), cognate with Old Norse <i>héðinn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heofonrīċe</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hefaen</i> +&lrm; <i>rīċe</i>, possibly as a calque of Latin <i>regnum caelōrum</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hevanrīki</i>, Old High German <i>himilrīhhi</i> (German <i>Himmelreich</i>), Old Norse <i>himinnrīki</i> (Danish <i>himmerige</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hæfde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hefiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thread used for weaving</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heofon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heofon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heofonisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hæfern</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Northumbrian</i>) alternative form of <i>hēahfore</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hefigan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heavy</li><li>grievous, serious</li><li>important</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;habīgaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kap-</i> (“take, seize”). Germanic cognates include Old Saxon <i>hevig</i>, Dutch <i>hevig</i>, Old High German <i>hebig</i>, Old Norse <i>hǫfigr</i>. The Indo-European root is also the source of Latin <i>capere</i>, Old Irish <i>cacht</i>, Albanian <i>kap</i> (“grip”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xopiti</i> (Old Church Slavonic <i>хапѭште</i> (xapjǫšte), Russian <i>ха́пать</i> (xápatʹ)), Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;kap-</i> (Lithuanian <i>kàpteleti</i>, Latvian <i>kàmpt</i> (“bite”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>hefiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to get worse</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to become heavier</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make heavy</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to oppress</li></ol><p>From <i>hefiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>heavily, grievously, sluggishly, dully, with grief, displeasure, or anger</li><li>deeply, intensely, vehemently</li></ol><p>From <i>hefiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heaviness, slowness, weight, grief, affliction, dullness of the body or senses</li><li>burdensomeness, oppressiveness, oppression</li><li>trouble, difficult matter</li><li>disease</li></ol><p>From <i>hefiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hefiġnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>hefiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>hefiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>hefiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hefiġnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heofon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heofonrīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heofon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīeġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fence</li><li>hedge</li></ol><p>From earlier Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;(ga)hagi</i>, likely a remodelling from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hagiwi</i>, the plural of <i>&#42;hagu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haguz</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>ġehæg</i>, Old High German <i>gahagi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hedge, fence</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>heċġ</i> (“fence”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hagosteald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hēah</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Northumbrian</i>) alternative form of <i>hēahfore</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hēahfore</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hagosteald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eahtafeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīehþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīehst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hell</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heel (of the foot)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēalā</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hą̄hilō</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hēla</i>.</p><p>Variant of <i>ēalā</i>, equivalent to <i>&#42;hē</i> +&lrm; <i>lā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide, conceal</li><li>to calumniate, slander</li><li>to defame</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;helan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;helaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱel-</i> (“to hide, conceal”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hela</i>, Old Saxon <i>helan</i>, Dutch <i>helen</i>, Old High German <i>helan</i> (German <i>hehlen</i>); and with Latin <i>cēlō</i>, Ancient Greek <i>καλύπω</i> (kalúpō, “to hide”). Compare <i>helian</i>, <i>hulu</i>, <i>hol</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hōlijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hōlijaną</i>, From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱeh₁l-</i>, <i>&#42;keh₁l-</i> (“to beguile, deceive”).</p><p>See also Old High German <i>huolēn</i>, huolan; and Old Norse <i>hœla</i>. Compare <i>hōlian</i>, and <i>hōl</i> (etymology 2).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hyld</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tansy (plants of the genus <i>Tanacetum</i>)</li><li>(<i>possibly</i>) estragon (<i>Artemisia dracunculus</i>)</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hǣlend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hielfe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hellrūna</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide, conceal, cover</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hell</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hallju</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haljō</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱel-</i> (“to cover, hide, conceal”).</p><p>Compare German <i>hell</i> (“light”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;poetic) the chains of hell</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>hell</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>bend</i> (“bond, chain”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an infernal servant</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>hell</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>cniht</i> (“boy, servant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>"hellish art"</li></ol><p><i>hell</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i> (“art, skill”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>infernal, hellish</li><li>devilish</li></ol><p>From <i>hell</i> +&lrm; <i>-cund</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the torment of hell</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>hell</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>cwalu</i> (“killing”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) the woe of hell</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>hell</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>bealu</i> (“harm, evil”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) the terror of hell</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>hell</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>brōga</i> (“terror”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hellfire</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>hell</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>bryne</i> (“fire”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) the jaws of hell</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>hell</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>ċeafl</i> (“jaw”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) "the race of hell"</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>hell</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>cynn</i> (“kind, race”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) the key to hell(?)</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>hell</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>cǣġ</i> (“key”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the doors of hell</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>hell</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>duru</i> (“door”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) hellfire</li></ol><p>From <i>helle</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>fȳr</i> (“fire”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a spirit of Hell</li></ol><p>From <i>hell</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>gāst</i> (“spirit”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gate of hell</li></ol><p>From <i>hell</i> +&lrm; <i>geat</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>god of the Underworld</li><li>god of Hell</li></ol><p>From <i>hell</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>god</i> (“god”). Cognate with Old High German <i>hellagot</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the depths of hell</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>hell</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>grund</i> (“ground”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) the devil (especially after his fall from heaven)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hell</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>hincian</i> (“to limp”) +&lrm; <i>-a</i>, literally “hell-limper”</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) hellhound</li></ol><p>From <i>helle</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>hund</i> (“dog, hound”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hell</li></ol><p>From <i>hell</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a captive or prisoner of hell</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>hell</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>hæft</i> (“captive”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a captive or prisoner of hell</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>hell</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>hæftling</i> (“captive”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an enclosure in hell</li><li>hell as a prison</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>hell</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>loc</i> (“lock, restraint, enclosure”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Styx</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>hell</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>mere</i> (“lake, pool”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the host or forces of hell</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>hell</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>mæġen</i> (“force, army”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>witch, sorceress</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hell</i> +&lrm; <i>rūn</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fiend</li><li>hell</li><li>the grave</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>hell</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>sċeaþa</i> (“enemy, ravager”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the torments of hell; eternal punishment, hellfire</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;halljuwītī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haljōwītiją</i>. Equivalent to <i>hell</i> (“hell”) +&lrm; <i>wīte</i> (“punishment”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sorceress; one who has a spirit of divination</li></ol><p>Presumably from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haljōrūnō</i>. A possible cognate of Gothic <i>&#42;𐌷𐌰𐌻𐌾𐍉𐍂𐌿𐌽𐌰</i> (&#42;haljōruna) (mentioned in a Latin text in the form of <i>haliurunna</i> (“witch”)); see the Latin entry for details. More at <i>hellrūna</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one skilled in the mysteries of hell; a sorcerer; necromancer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haljōrūnô</i>, equivalent to <i>hell</i> (“underworld; hell”) +&lrm; <i>rūna</i> (“consulter; counsellor”). Cognate with Old High German <i>hellirūna</i> (“necromancy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>helmet</li><li>protection, defence</li><li>covering, crown</li><li>summit, top (of trees)</li><li>protector, lord</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;helm</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;helmaz</i> (“helmet”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱel-</i> (“to cover, to hide”). Compare Old Frisian <i>helm</i>, Old Saxon <i>helm</i>, Old High German <i>helm</i>, Old Norse <i>hjalmr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌷𐌹𐌻𐌼𐍃</i> (hilms).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>helm (of boat), tiller</li></ol><p>Cognate with Old Norse <i>hjálm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Helmaberht</i>, from <i>&#42;helm</i> (“helmet”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”). Equivalent to <i>helm</i> +&lrm; <i>beorht</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Helmberht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>helm</i> (“helmet”) +&lrm; <i>stān</i> (“stone”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Helmawald</i>, from <i>&#42;helm</i> (“helmet”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wald</i> (“strength”). Equivalent to <i>helm</i> +&lrm; <i>weald</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Helmold</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Helmaþrūþi</i>, from <i>&#42;helm</i> (“helmet”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;þrūþi</i> (“strength”). Equivalent to <i>helm</i> +&lrm; <i>þrȳþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Helmdrud</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heolor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>help</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;helpu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;helpō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to help [<i>with</i> dative; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> genitive]</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;helpan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;helpaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>helper</li></ol><p><i>helpan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Helparīks</i>, equivalent to <i>help</i> (“help, aid”) + <i>rīċ</i> (“king, ruler”). Cognate with Frankish <i>&#42;Helparīk</i> (English <i>Chilperic</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>help</i> (“help, aid”) + <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hellrūna</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sorceress, witch</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;halljurūnagī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haljōrūnagį̄</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heolor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hem, border</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shirt</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hamiþi</i> (“shirt”). Doublet of <i>cemes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hymlīc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a boot made of raw hide</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hemp</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hanapi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hanapiz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hanap</i>, Old High German <i>hanaf</i>, Old Norse <i>hampr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a steep cliff</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hanging</li><li>that on which one is hung or hanged (e.g. gibbet, gallows, cross, etc.)</li><li>prison, confinement, durance</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hangini</i> (stem <i>&#42;hangunnj-</i>). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>henginnia</i>, <i>hengenna</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stallion</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hangist</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hangistaz</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Hengest)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Hengist, one of the kings who supposedly led the Anglo-Saxon invasion of Britain</li></ol><p>Old English <i>henġest</i> (“stallion”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The site of the Battle of Hingston Down, probably the hill of Hingston Down in Cornwall</li></ol><p><i>henġest</i> (“stallion”) +&lrm; <i>dūn</i> (“hill”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Henġestdūn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hengest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fine paid for not keeping a criminal in custody so that he may be brought before the proper tribunal</li></ol><p>From <i>henġen</i> (“prison”) +&lrm; <i>wīte</i> (“punishment”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hen</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hannju</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hanjō</i>. Cognate with West Frisian <i>henn</i> (West Frisian <i>hin</i>), Dutch <i>hen</i>, Old High German <i>henna</i> (German <i>Henne</i>). Compare also Norwegian <i>hœna</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>henbane, <i>Hyoscyamus niger</i></li></ol><p>From <i>henne</i> (“hen's”) +&lrm; <i>belle</i> (“bell”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pursue; follow after</li><li>to seize; take; carry off</li><li>to capture (an animal)</li><li>to arrest (a person)</li><li>to strike with a weapon; get at with a blow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hantijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hantijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>behold</li></ol><p>Compare Old High German <i>senu</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīenþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>she</li><li>it (when the thing being referred to is grammatically feminine)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hiju</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hijō</i>&nbsp;f (“this, this one”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) on this day; today</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hiu dagu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hinō dagō</i> (“on this day”, instrumental), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hiz</i> (“this”) + <i>&#42;dagaz</i> (“day”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hiūdega</i>, <i>hiōda</i> (“today”), Old Saxon <i>hōdigo</i>, <i>hūdigu</i> (“today”), Old High German <i>hiu tagu</i>, <i>hiutu</i>, <i>hiuto</i> (“today”) &gt; German <i>heute</i>, Gothic <i>𐌷𐌹𐌼𐌼𐌰 𐌳𐌰𐌲𐌰</i> (himma daga). More at he, day.</p><p>Possibly a calque of the Old Saxon word (see the Usage Notes section).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grief</li><li>mourning</li><li>wailing; lamentation</li></ol><p>From <i>hēofan</i> (“to grieve, mourn”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lament, grieve, wail</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heofon</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;heufaną</i>, akin to Old Saxon <i>hiovan</i>, Old High German <i>hioban</i>, Old Norse <i>hjófa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heofon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heofon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heofonisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heofonrīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sky</li><li>heaven</li><li>ceiling</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hebn</i>. Probably related to both Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;himil</i> and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;himinaz</i>, but the origins of the relationship are mysterious.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heavenly, celestial</li></ol><p>From <i>heofon</i> (“heaven”) +&lrm; <i>-cund</i> (“coming from, -like”). Cognate with Gothic <i>himinakunds</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heavenly, celestial</li></ol><p>From <i>heofoncund</i> (“from heaven”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i> (“like”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the king of heaven, heavenly king.</li></ol><p>From <i>heofon</i> (“heaven”) +&lrm; <i>cyning</i> (“king”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>himilkuning</i>, Old High German <i>himilkuning</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Heavenly joy, joy of Heaven.</li></ol><p>From <i>heofon</i> (“heaven”) +&lrm; <i>drēam</i> (“joy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heavenly host, angels</li></ol><p>Kenning of <i>heofon</i> (“heaven”) +&lrm; <i>duguþ</i> (“host”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heofon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>An angel of Heaven</li></ol><p>From <i>heofon</i> (“heaven”) +&lrm; <i>enġel</i> (“angel”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heofonrīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bird of the sky</li></ol><p>From <i>heofon</i> (“sky”) +&lrm; <i>fugol</i> (“bird”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lightning</li></ol><p>From <i>heofon</i> (“sky”) +&lrm; <i>fȳr</i> (“fire”). Means "fire from the sky".</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a heavenly home, heaven.</li></ol><p>From <i>heofon</i> (“heaven”) +&lrm; <i>hām</i> (“home”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a house with a ceiling</li></ol><p>From <i>heofon</i> (“ceiling”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heavenly</li></ol><p>From <i>heofon</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>. Compare with Old Saxon <i>himilisk</i>, Old High German <i>himilisk</i>, Old Frisian <i>himelesk</i>, Old Norse <i>hifneskr</i>, <i>himneskr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heavenly light</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>heofon</i> (“heaven”) +&lrm; <i>lēoht</i> (“light”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heavenly light, radiance</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>heofon</i> (“heaven”) +&lrm; <i>lēoma</i> (“light”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>celestial, heavenly</li><li>from heaven</li><li>divine</li><li>chaste</li></ol><p>From <i>heofon</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A title for God; Heavenly Father</li></ol><p>From <i>heofonlīċ</i> (“heavenly”) + <i>fæder</i> (“father”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the power(s) of heaven</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>heofon</i> (“heaven”) +&lrm; <i>mæġen</i> (“power”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kingdom of heaven</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>heofon</i> +&lrm; <i>rīċe</i>, possibly as a calque of Latin <i>regnum caelōrum</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hevanrīki</i>, Old High German <i>himilrīhhi</i> (German <i>Himmelreich</i>), Old Norse <i>himinnrīki</i> (Danish <i>himmerige</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a star of heaven.</li></ol><p>From <i>heofon</i> (“heaven”) +&lrm; <i>steorra</i> (“star”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heavenly body</li></ol><p>From <i>heofon</i> (“heaven”) +&lrm; <i>tungol</i> (“star, planet”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>himiltungal</i>, Old High German <i>himilzungal</i>, Icelandic <i>himintungl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the inhabitants of heaven</li></ol><p>From <i>heofon</i> (“heaven”) +&lrm; <i>-ware</i> (“residents”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The ruler of Heaven.</li></ol><p>From <i>heofon</i> (“heaven”) +&lrm; <i>wealdend</i> (“ruler”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The guardian of Heaven, God.</li></ol><p>From <i>heofon</i> (“heaven”) +&lrm; <i>weard</i> (“guard”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hevanwarda</i> (“angel”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the heavenly host</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>heofon</i> (“heaven”) +&lrm; <i>werod</i> (“army”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of heavenly beauty</li></ol><p>From <i>heofon</i> (“heaven”) +&lrm; <i>wlitiġ</i> (“beautiful”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cloud in the sky, cloud of heaven</li></ol><p>From <i>heofon</i> +&lrm; <i>wolcen</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>himilwolcan</i>, Old High German <i>himilwolchen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heofonwolcen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the glory of heaven; heavenly glory</li></ol><p>From <i>heofon</i> +&lrm; <i>wuldor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the glory of heaven</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>heofon</i> (“heaven”) +&lrm; <i>þrymm</i> (“glory”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heofon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mourning, grieving, lamentation</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hēofan</i> (“to mourn”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a time of mourning</li></ol><p>From <i>hēofung</i> (“mourning”) +&lrm; <i>tīd</i> (“time”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hoarfrost, rime</li></ol><p>From a metathesis of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hehlā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hehlǭ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱel(h₂)-</i> (“to freeze; cold”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>héla</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>elene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heolor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>balance, scales</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;heluru</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;helurō</i> (“balance, scales”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kel-</i> (“to protrude; high”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darkness, veil, covering</li><li>place of concealment</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hulistrą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a dark enclosure, prison</li></ol><p>From <i>heolstor</i> (“darkness, place of concealment”) +&lrm; <i>loca</i> (“enclosure”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>him</i>: (to) him/it/them</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from here, hence</li></ol><p>From a variant of the Germanic root of <i>hē</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hinana</i>, Old High German <i>hinana</i> (German <i>hinnen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from now on, beyond this point</li></ol><p>From <i>heonan</i> (“from here”) +&lrm; <i>forþ</i> (“onwards”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heonan</i> (“hence”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hip (the fruit of a rose)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;heupǭ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱewb-</i> (“briar, thorn”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hearken, listen (to), obey</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;heorcian</i> +&lrm; <i>-nian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a herd</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;herdō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kerdʰ-</i> (“file, row, herd”). Compare Old Saxon <i>herda</i>, Old High German <i>herta</i>, Old Norse <i>hjǫrð</i>, Gothic <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌳𐌰</i> (hairda).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hards of flax</li><li>oakum</li><li>tow (bundle of fiber)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Old English</i>) alternative form of <i>eorþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pleasant, secure</li><li>gentle, mild, pure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;heuraz</i>, <i>&#42;hiurijaz</i>. Compare Old Armenian <i>սէր</i> (sēr, “love”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīred</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heoru</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Heorugār</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deer</li><li>stag</li><li>hart</li><li>cervid</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;herut</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;herutaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kerud-</i> (Ancient Greek <i>κόρυδος</i> (kórudos, “crested lark”)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱerh₂-</i> (Latin <i>cervus</i> (“horn”)). Germanic cognates include Old Saxon <i>hirot</i>, Old High German <i>hiruz</i>, and Old Norse <i>hjǫrtr</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Heorot)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Heorot, the mead hall of Hrothgar in <i>Beowulf</i></li></ol><p>From <i>heorot</i> (“deer, stag”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hertford</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>heorot</i> (“stag”) +&lrm; <i>ford</i> (“ford”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>heorra</i> (“hinge”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hinge, door hinge</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;herʀō</i> or <i>&#42;her(r)ō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;herzô</i> or <i>&#42;her(r)ô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>heorra</i> (“hinge”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;herʀā</i> or <i>&#42;her(r)ā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;herzǫ</i> or <i>&#42;her(r)ǫ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heorot</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heart (muscle)</li><li>heart (seat of emotion)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hertā</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>herte</i>, Old Saxon <i>herta</i>, Old Dutch <i>herta</i>, Old High German <i>herza</i>, Old Norse <i>hjarta</i>, Gothic <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍄𐍉</i> (hairtō). The Indo-European root is also the source of Greek <i>καρδία</i> (kardía), Latin <i>cor</i>, Welsh <i>craidd</i>, Russian <i>се́рдце</i> (sérdce), Lithuanian <i>širdis</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heartache</li></ol><p><i>heorte</i> +&lrm; <i>eċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Heorotford</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hertfordshire (a county of England)</li></ol><p><i>Heorotford</i> +&lrm; <i>sċīr</i> (“county”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entrails, vitals</li></ol><p><i>heorte</i> (“heart”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;ġesidu</i> (in the plural <i>ġesida</i>)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) mortal terror</li></ol><p><i>heorte</i> +&lrm; <i>gryre</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without courage or spirit, cowed</li></ol><p>From <i>heorte</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sword</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;heru</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;heruz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>hjǫrr</i> (“sword”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Heorugar, elder brother of Hrothgar</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Herugaizaz</i>, equivalent to <i>heoru</i> (“sword”) +&lrm; <i>gār</i> (“spear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heorot</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hearth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;herþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) hearth-companion; retainer (member of retinue)</li></ol><p>From <i>heorþ</i> (“hearth”) +&lrm; <i>ġenēat</i> (“companion”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīew</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hall, room</li></ol><p>Unclear. Possibly related to <i>hēah</i> (“high”), or perhaps related to Ancient Greek <i>κῠ́τος</i> (kŭ́tos, “hollow”), also of unclear origin.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>here</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hǣr</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hēr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hē₂r</i>, apparently from the stem <i>&#42;hi-</i> (“this”); the exact formation is unclear. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hēr</i>, Old High German <i>hiar</i>, Old Norse <i>hér</i>, Gothic <i>𐌷𐌴𐍂</i> (hēr).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>follower, servant, one who obeys another</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīeran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heorcnian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>army, military (especially of the enemy)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hari</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;harjaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ker-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) war-signal</li><li>military ensign, standard</li><li>(<i>literal&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) fleet signal, beacon, lighthouse</li></ol><p>From <i>here</i> +&lrm; <i>bēacen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>the son of Hreþel in the poem <i>Beowulf</i>.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Harjabalþ</i>, from <i>&#42;hari</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;balþ</i> (“bold, brave”). Equivalent to <i>here</i> +&lrm; <i>beald</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Heribald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shelter, refuge</li><li>military quarters</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;harjabergu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shelter</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;harjabergōn</i>. By surface analysis, <i>herebeorg</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Harjaburg</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Harjaburgz</i>, from <i>&#42;harjaz</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;burgz</i> (“fortification”). Equivalent to <i>here</i> +&lrm; <i>burg</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Heriburg</i>, Old Norse <i>Herborg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Herefriþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;rare) osprey</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>heoru</i> +&lrm; <i>fang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hereford (a city in Herefordshire, England)</li></ol><p><i>here</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>ford</i> (“ford”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Herefordshire</li></ol><p><i>Hereford</i> + <i>sċīr</i> (“shire”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Harjafriþu</i>, from <i>&#42;hari</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;friþu</i> (“peace”). Equivalent to <i>here</i> +&lrm; <i>friþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Herifrid</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Heorugār</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>war-gear</li><li>heriot</li></ol><p><i>here</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeatwa</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Harjagern</i>, from <i>&#42;hari</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;gern</i> (“eager, willing”). Equivalent to <i>here</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeorn</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Herigern</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) battle-mask, helmet</li></ol><p><i>here</i> +&lrm; <i>grīma</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Harjagunþi</i>, from <i>&#42;hari</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;gunþi</i> (“battle”). Equivalent to <i>here</i> +&lrm; <i>gūþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Herigund</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plunder, booty</li></ol><p>From <i>here</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>hȳþ</i> (“plunder”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Harjalubu</i>, from <i>&#42;hari</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;lubu</i> (“love”). Equivalent to <i>here</i> +&lrm; <i>lufu</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Heriluba</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soldier</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;harjamann-</i>. Equivalent to <i>here</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“man”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Harjamōd</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Harjamōdaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>here</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>mōd</i> (“spirt, courage”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Heremōd</li></ol><p>From <i>Heremōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Harjamundu</i>, from <i>&#42;hari</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;mundu</i> (“protection, protector”). Equivalent to <i>here</i> +&lrm; <i>mund</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Herimunt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hoarseness</li></ol><p>From <i>herian</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Harjarād</i>, from <i>&#42;hari</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;rād</i> (“advice, counsel”). Equivalent to <i>here</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣd</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Herirāt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;poetic) success in war</li></ol><p>From <i>here</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>spēd</i> (“success”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Harjaswinþ</i>, from <i>&#42;hari</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;swinþ</i> (“strong”). Equivalent to <i>here</i> +&lrm; <i>swīþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Heriswind</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>general</li><li>duke</li><li>leader of a people</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;harjatogō</i>. Equivalent to <i>here</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;toga</i> (“leader”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heretoga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Harjawald</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Harjawaldaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>here</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>weald</i> (“ruler, authority”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hariward</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Harjawardaz</i>, from <i>&#42;harjaz</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wardaz</i> (“guard”). Equivalent to <i>here</i> +&lrm; <i>weard</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Heriwart</i>, Old Norse <i>Hervarðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>highway</li></ol><p><i>here</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>weġ</i> (“way, road, path”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to despise</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;harwijōn</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;harwijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Herewine; Bishop of Lichfield.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Harjawini</i>, from <i>&#42;hari</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wini</i> (“friend”). Equivalent to <i>here</i> +&lrm; <i>wine</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Heriwin</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Harjawulf</i>, from <i>&#42;hari</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wulf</i> (“wolf”). Equivalent to <i>here</i> +&lrm; <i>wulf</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Hariulf</i>. Compare <i>Wulfhere</i>, which has the same elements reversed.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>here</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>wynn</i> (“joy”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hærfest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hearg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lord of Hosts (title for God)</li></ol><p>From <i>herġa</i> (“of armies”) + <i>fruman</i> (“king, ruler”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>herian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hergaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hergoþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hergian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heriġendlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>herienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pillage, plunder</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;harīgōn</i>, <i>&#42;harjagōn</i>, variants of <i>&#42;harjōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;harjōną</i> (“to overrun with an army”). Related to Old English <i>here</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harrying, plundering, making war</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hergian</i> (“to hunt”) +&lrm; <i>-oþ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plundering</li><li>harrying, harrowing</li><li>incursion, raid, invasion</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hergian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to praise; honor</li><li>alternative form of <i>herewian</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haʀjan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hearg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>herian</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>herġian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>praiseworthy, laudable</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>herian</i> +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>, else <i>heriende</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>praiseworthily, laudably</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>heriġendlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>, else <i>heriende</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>heriġendlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>herienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>praise</li></ol><p><i>herian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>King Herod</li></ol><p>Ancient Greek <i>Ἡρῴδης</i> (Hērōídēs)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hearra</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hierstan</i></li></ol><p>See <i>hierstan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīersum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hering</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hierwan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hereafter</li></ol><p><i>hēr</i> (“here”) +&lrm; <i>æfter</i> (“after”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) testicles</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scrotum</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>herþan</i> (“testicles”) +&lrm; <i>beliġ</i> (“belly”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>herþbeliġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hyspan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hǣst</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīehst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hatred</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hati</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>heti</i>, Old High German <i>haz</i>, Old Norse <i>hatr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌷𐌰𐍄𐌹𐍃</i> (hatis).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hateful, hostile, malicious</li></ol><p>From <i>hete</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(of a motion) violently, furiously</li><li>malignantly</li></ol><p>From <i>hete</i> (“hate”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hettend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deep enmity, hostility</li></ol><p><i>hete</i> +&lrm; <i>nīþ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full of hate, hateful</li></ol><p><i>hete</i> (“hatred, malice”) +&lrm; <i>-rōf</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hateful speech: verbal abuse, invective</li></ol><p><i>hete</i> +&lrm; <i>sprǣċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hateful, hostile, malignant</li></ol><p>From <i>hete</i> (“hatred”) +&lrm; <i>-ol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pursue as an enemy</li><li>to persecute</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hatjaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱeh₂d-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enemy</li></ol><p>From <i>hettan</i> (“to pursue”) +&lrm; <i>-end</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>persecution</li></ol><p>From <i>hettan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīwisċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīehst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hǣþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīe</i> (“they”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hēo</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(A type of bird)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Perhaps related to Old English <i>&#42;haccian</i> (“to hack”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodpecker</li></ol><p>Apparently equivalent to <i>hice</i> +&lrm; <i>-el</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blue titmouse</li></ol><p>From <i>hice</i> +&lrm; <i>māse</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hyċġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a portion of land; a hide</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to here, hither</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hidrê</i>. Related to Old Norse <i>heðra</i>, Gothic <i>𐌷𐌹𐌳𐍂𐌴</i> (hidrē).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coming hither</li><li>advent</li></ol><p>From <i>hider</i> (“hither”) +&lrm; <i>cyme</i> (“coming”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>they</li></ol><p>Reflecting an earlier regularised form of <i>&#42;hijai</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hiʀ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hīz</i> (“these, these ones”), masculine plural of <i>&#42;hiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hay</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hawi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>highest</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hauhistaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>highness, height</li><li>a high place</li><li>glory</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hauhiþu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hauhiþō</i> (“highness, height”), equivalent to <i>hēah</i> +&lrm; <i>-þu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to incline, tilt, bend</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;halþijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;halþijaną</i> (“to bend, incline, pour, empty”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kel-</i> (“to tilt, tip, incline”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to halve, bisect</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;halbijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;halbiją</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kelH-</i> (“to split, divide”), probably related to <i>&#42;halbaz</i> (“half”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Lameness; the state of being crippled.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haltī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haltį̄</i>; equivalent to <i>healt</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>.</p><p>Compare Old Frisian <i>strumphelte</i> (“lameness from mutilation”), Old Saxon <i>spurihelti</i> (“lameness (of horses)”), Old High German <i>sērhelzī</i> (“powerlessness”), Old Norse <i>helti</i> (“lameness”), and Old Swedish <i>hælti</i> (“lameness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humiliation, abasement, disgrace</li><li>injury, loss, affliction</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hauniþu</i>. Equivalent to <i>hēan</i> +&lrm; <i>-þu</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>hōnida</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heora</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with dative</i>) to obey</li><li>(<i>with dative</i>) to listen with compliance or assent</li><li>(<i>with tō, intō</i>) to belong to</li><li>to hear</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hauʀijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hauzijaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ḱh₂owsyéti</i>.</p><p>Germanic cognates include Old Frisian <i>hēra</i>, Old Saxon <i>hōrian</i>, Old High German <i>hōren</i>, Old Norse <i>heyra</i>, and Gothic <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hausjan). Indo-European cognates include Ancient Greek <i>ἀκούω</i> (akoúō) and Albanian <i>qyr</i> (“to look, observe”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heord</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make hard</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make strong or tight</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make bold, embolden, or encourage</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hardijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hardijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herdsman</li><li>guardian, keeper, protector</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hirdijaz</i>, derived from <i>&#42;herdō</i> (“herd”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hirdi</i>, Old High German <i>hirti</i> (German <i>Hirte</i>), Old Norse <i>hirðir</i> (Swedish <i>herde</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pastoral Care, a text by Pope Gregory I</li></ol><p><i>hierde</i> (“herdsman”) +&lrm; <i>bōc</i> (“book”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pastoral</li></ol><p><i>hierde</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pastoral care</li></ol></html>:/ˈhiy̯re/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hire</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>someone who obeys someone else: subject, subordinate</li><li>someone who listens to someone else: listener, student, parishioner</li></ol><p><i>hīeran</i> (“to hear, to obey”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Jeremiah (major prophet of the Hebrew Bible)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Hieremias</i>, cognate with English <i>Jeremy</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>higher</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hauhiʀō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ierre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roast</li><li>to fry</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;harstijan</i> (“to roast”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frying pan</li></ol><p>From <i>hierstan</i> (“to fry”) +&lrm; <i>panne</i> (“pan”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>obedient</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hauʀisam</i>, equivalent to <i>hīeran</i> +&lrm; <i>-sum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obey [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hauʀisamōn</i>. Equivalent to <i>hīersum</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obedience</li></ol><p>From <i>hīersum</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hearten, encourage, animate</li><li>to refresh, revive, comfort</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jerusalem</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>Hierusalem</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak evil of, blaspheme, malign, condemn</li><li>to abuse, harass, vex, oppress</li><li>to feel contempt for, despise</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;harwijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>form, appearance, likeness</li><li>form, character</li><li>shape</li><li>color</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hiwi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hiwją</i> (“shape, form, appearance, blee”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kew-</i> (“skin, complexion”).</p><p>Cognate with Bornholm Danish <i>hy</i> (“complexion”), Gothic <i>𐌷𐌹𐍅𐌹</i> (hiwi, “form, appearance”), Old Norse <i>hý</i> (“down of birds”), Swedish <i>hy</i>, Sanskrit <i>छवि</i> (chavi, “skin, hide, beauty, splendour”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pretender, decoy, fabricator</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hīew</i> (“shape, form”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to form, fashion, shape, transform</li><li>to pretend</li></ol><p><i>hīew</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shapely; having good form or colour</li><li>beautiful</li><li><i>(of language)</i> figurative</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hīew</i> +&lrm; <i>līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīeġ</i></li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hyġe</i> (“thought, mind”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hyġebeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hyġelāc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jay</li><li>magpie</li><li>jackdaw</li><li>woodpecker</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;higurō</i> (“a type of bird, jay”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with dative</i>) to strive, exert oneself</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hīgōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hīgōną</i> (“to breathe, pant”). Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>higen</i> (Dutch <i>hijgen</i>), Middle Low German <i>higen</i> (German <i>heichen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hysċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>hīehþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīehþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hylċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) battle, war</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hildi</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hild</i>, Old High German <i>hiltia</i>, Old Norse <i>hildr</i>. The Proto-Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek <i>κόλος</i> (kólos), Latin <i>clādēs</i>, Old Irish <i>coll</i> (Breton <i>claza</i>), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolti</i> (Russian <i>коло́ть</i> (kolótʹ)).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Hild)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From the common noun <i>hild</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hildiberht</i>. Equivalent to <i>hild</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>berht</i> (“bright”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hildebeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) war-shield</li></ol><p><i>hild</i> +&lrm; <i>bord</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hildiburg</i>, from <i>&#42;hildi</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;burg</i> (“fortification”). Equivalent to <i>hild</i> +&lrm; <i>burg</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Hiltiburg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hildeburg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) brave in battle</li></ol><p>From <i>hild</i> +&lrm; <i>dēor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hildifriþu</i>, from <i>&#42;hildi</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;friþu</i> (“peace”). Equivalent to <i>hild</i> +&lrm; <i>friþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Hiltifrid</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hildigunþi</i>, from <i>&#42;hildi</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;gunþi</i> (“battle”). Equivalent to <i>hild</i> +&lrm; <i>gūþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Hiltigund</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>weaponry</i>) sword</li></ol><p>From <i>hild</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>lēoma</i> (“sword”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) warrior</li></ol><p>From <i>hild</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>rinc</i> (“warrior”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) charge, onslaught</li></ol><p><i>hild</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣs</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hildiswinþ</i>, from <i>&#42;hildi</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;swinþ</i> (“strong”). Equivalent to <i>hild</i> +&lrm; <i>swiþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Hiltisuint</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) battle-weapon</li></ol><p><i>hild</i> +&lrm; <i>wǣpen</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hildiþrūþi</i>, from <i>&#42;hildi</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;þrūþi</i> (“strength”). Equivalent to <i>hild</i> +&lrm; <i>þrȳþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Hilditrut</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hilt, handle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;heltą</i>, <i>&#42;heltǭ</i>, <i>&#42;heltō</i>, <i>&#42;hiltijō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hilt, handle, feminine equivalent of <i>hilt</i></li></ol><p>See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</p><p>Ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;heltǭ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) hence, from here</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hin-</i>. Related to heonan (“hence”). Akin to Old High German <i>hina</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to limp, hobble</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hinkaną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>hinchan</i>, <i>hinkan</i>, German <i>hinken</i>; Old Dutch <i>&#42;hinkan</i>, Dutch <i>hinken</i>; Old Norse <i>hinka</i>, Icelandic <i>hinka</i>. Attested in the agent noun <i>hellehinca</i> (“hell-hobbler”), and in single use as <i>hincodon</i> (they limped), however there is debate over the manuscript text which, according to Lindelöf, may read <i>luncodon</i>, and should therefore read <i>&#42;luncian</i>, cognate with Swedish <i>lunka</i>, Norwegian <i>lunke</i> and Scots <i>lunk</i> in Shetland dialects. Related to Old English <i>huncettan</i> (“to cause to limp, halt”), from a related noun + <i>&#42;hunc</i> +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hind, doe</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hindi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hindō</i>, <i>&#42;hindiz</i>, whence also Old High German <i>hinta</i>, Old Norse <i>hind</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from behind</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hinder</i>, akin to German <i>hinter</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at the further end, hindwards</li></ol><p>From <i>hindan</i> +&lrm; <i>-weard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raspberry</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hindibaʀi</i>. Equivalent to <i>hind</i> (“hind”) +&lrm; <i>berġe</i> (“berry”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hindberġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hindberġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raspberry bush</li></ol><p>From <i>hind</i> (“female deer”) +&lrm; <i>brēr</i> (“briar”). <i>Hind</i> is the origin of <i>hindberiġe</i>, meaning raspberry.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hind</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fawn</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hindikalbaz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hindkalf</i>, Old High German <i>hintkalb</i>, and Old Norse <i>hindarkálfr</i>. Equivalent to <i>hind</i> (“hind, doe”) +&lrm; <i>ċealf</i> (“calf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>last</li></ol><p>From <i>hind</i> (“behind”) +&lrm; <i>-uma</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>back, behind, down</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hinder</i> (“behind, back”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>behind, after</li><li>wicked, cruel, deceitful</li><li>lower, nether-</li></ol><p>From <i>hinder</i> (“behind, after”, adjective and adverb).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) artifice, stratagem, snare</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hinder-</i> +&lrm; <i>hōc</i> (“hook”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mean, base, contemptible person</li></ol><p>From By surface analysis, <i>hinder-</i> +&lrm; <i>-ling</i> (diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hinder, obstruct, repress, impede</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hindrōną</i>, <i>&#42;hinderōną</i>, from <i>&#42;hinder</i> (“back”). Cognate with Old High German <i>hintarōn</i> (German <i>hindern</i>), Old Norse <i>hindra</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hinsīþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) journey hence, departure, death</li></ol><p>From <i>hin-</i> (“away”) +&lrm; <i>sīþ</i> (“journey, going”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>she</li><li>it (when the thing being referred to is feminine)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hijō</i>&nbsp;f (“this, this one”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heofon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heolor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heora</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hierde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hire</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heoru</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Heorugār</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīew</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heora</i>: their, of them</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīeran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hierde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hierdelīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>family, household</li><li>retinue</li><li>company, brotherhood</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hīwarād</i> (“family, marriage”). Cognate with Old High German <i>hīrāt</i> (“marriage”) (German <i>Heirat</i>), Old English <i>hīwen</i> (“household”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Īrland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>it</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hit</i> (“this, this one”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱe-</i>, <i>&#42;ḱey-</i> (“this, here”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hit</i> (“it”), Old High German <i>iz</i> (“it”), Gothic <i>𐌷𐌹𐍄𐌰</i> (hita, “it”). More at <i>hē</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hit upon</li><li>to meet with</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>hitta</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hittijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hēo</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hīwisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīew</i></li><li>fortune</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hīwō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>members of a household or family</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>domestic</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;hīw</i> (“marriage”) +&lrm; <i>-cund</i> (“born”). The former from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hīwą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>familiar <i>(+ dative with something)</i></li><li>of the family or household: familial, domestic</li></ol><p>From <i>hīw</i> (“form”) +&lrm; <i>cūþ</i> (“known”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>familiarly</li></ol><p><i>hīwcūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>familiarity</li></ol><p><i>hīwcūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>hīwcūþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>household</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hīw</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hīwą</i> (“husband, wife, spouse”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱeyw-</i>, <i>&#42;ḱeym-</i> (“relative, tribesman”), from <i>&#42;ḱey-</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hīwa</i> (“wife”), Old High German <i>hīwun</i> (“servant, spouse, members of the family”), Latin <i>cīvis</i> (“burgher, citizen”). More at city.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīewere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beautiful, fair</li></ol><p>From <i>hīw</i> (“form, appearance”) +&lrm; <i>-fæst</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīewian</i></li><li>to marry</li></ol><p>From <i>hīw</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p><p>From <i>&#42;hīw</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>, found in compound <i>hīwcund</i>. From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hīwą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱey-</i> (“to be lying down”). More at <i>hewe</i>, hind and hird.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>hīwisċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>household</li><li>a hide (of land)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hīwiskī</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hīskithe</i> (“household unit, household”), Old High German <i>hīwiski</i> (“household, family”), Old Norse <i>hýski</i> (“domestic unit, household”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>matronly; relating to a married woman.</li><li>alternative form of <i>hīewlīċ</i></li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;hīw</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. The first element also occurs in <i>hīwian</i> (“to marry”) and <i>hīwcund</i> (“domestic”). Cognate with Old High German <i>hīwlīh</i> (“conjugal”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>family, household</li><li>a body of people dwelling together for a common cause</li><li>a religious house, such as an abbey or a monastery</li><li>tribe, nation</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hīw</i> +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shaping, forming</li><li>shape, form, frame (of an object)</li><li>species, kind</li><li>transformation</li><li>illusory shape, deceptive appearance</li><li>pretense, trick</li><li>hypocrisy</li><li>false speech</li><li>irony</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hlāford</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scoffing, mockery, scorn</li></ol><p>Derived from an unattested verb <i>&#42;hlacerian</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hlakarōn</i> (“to make fun of”), an iterative form of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hlakōn</i>, <i>&#42;hlakkōn</i> (“to laugh”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlakkōną</i> (“to laugh”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hlakkia</i>, <i>hlakia</i> (“to laugh”), Old Norse <i>hlakka</i> (“to shout for joy, squeal, scream, screech”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to load</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hlaþan</i>, whence also Old Norse <i>hlaða</i>, Gothic <i>&#42;𐌷𐌻𐌰𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;hlaþan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bread</li><li>loaf</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hlaib</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlaibaz</i>, which see for cognates.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hlāford</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hlāford</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bethlehem</li></ol><p><i>hlāf</i> +&lrm; <i>hūs</i>, calque of Bethlehem.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lammas (the first day of August)</li></ol><p>From <i>hlāf</i> (“bread”) +&lrm; <i>mæsse</i> (“mass”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lord, master of servants or slaves</li><li>a man of superior rank; sovereign, ruler; nobleman</li><li>male head of a household</li><li>master, owner of an animal</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>hlāfweard</i>, from <i>hlāf</i> (“bread”) + <i>weard</i> (“ward”). See also <i>hlǣfdīġe</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Hlaford)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>hlāford</i>; the Lord (the Christian God)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jurisdiction; governance; rule</li></ol><p>From <i>hlāford</i> +&lrm; <i>-dōm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lordless, without a lord</li></ol><p>From <i>hlāford</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lordly, noble, heroic</li></ol><p>From <i>hlāford</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>domination, supremacy</li></ol><p>From <i>hlāford</i> (“lord”) +&lrm; <i>-sċiepe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hlāfordsċiepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hlāford</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hlāford</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal</i>) bread-eater</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) a domestic servant (as opposed to a lord)</li></ol><p>From <i>hlāf</i> (“bread”) +&lrm; <i>ǣta</i> (“eater”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prone to laughter; laughing</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlagulaz</i>, equivalent to <i>hliehhan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lanky or lean</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hlank</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlankaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urine</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hland</i> (“urine”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>hland</i> (“urine”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hlǣw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laughter</li><li>a laugh</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlahtraz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>lahtar</i>, Old Norse <i>hlátr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>productive of laughter; amusing, funny</li><li>given to laughter</li></ol><p>From <i>hleahtor</i> +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who causes laughter, mirth, or joy</li></ol><p>From <i>hleahtor</i> +&lrm; <i>smiþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump</li><li>to dance</li><li>to run</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hlaupan</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hlāpa</i>, Old Saxon <i>hlōpan</i>, Old High German <i>loufan</i>, Old Norse <i>hlaupa</i>, Gothic <i>𐌿𐍃𐌷𐌻𐌰𐌿𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (ushlaupan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dancer</li><li>runner, courier</li></ol><p>From <i>hlēapan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female dancer</li></ol><p>From <i>hlēapan</i> +&lrm; <i>-estre</i>. Feminine form of <i>hlēapere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lapwing</li></ol><p>From <i>hlēapan</i> (“to leap”) +&lrm; <i>wincian</i> (“to wink”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having cracks or rents; leaky</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lek</i>, <i>&#42;lak</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lekaz</i>, <i>&#42;lakaz</i> (“leaky”). Compare Icelandic <i>lekur</i>, Dutch <i>lek</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hliehhan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hleahtor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hlimme</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hlimman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a coat of mail, chain mail</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hlankijā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlankiz</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;hlankaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kleng-</i>, <i>&#42;klenk-</i> (“to bend; wind; twist”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shelter</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hlaiw</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hlaiwaberht</i>, from <i>&#42;hlaiw</i> (“shelter, protection”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”). Equivalent to <i>hlēo</i> +&lrm; <i>beorht</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Lēoberht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, alternative form of <i>Hleobeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) brooklime</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) protector-kinsman</li></ol><p>From <i>hlēo</i> (“shelter”) +&lrm; <i>mǣġ</i> (“kinsman”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hlēomāga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hlinian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheek</li><li>face</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hleuʀ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hleuzą</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hleor</i>, Old Norse <i>hlýr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheek-guard (on a helmet)</li></ol><p><i>hlēor</i> +&lrm; <i>berg</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cast lots</li><li>to receive by appointment; be appointed to an office</li><li>to obtain (by lot), get a share, be allotted</li><li>to share in something, participate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hleutan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hleutaną</i>; akin to Old Saxon <i>hliotan</i>, Old High German <i>hliozan</i>, <i>liozan</i>, and Old Norse <i>hljóta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hlēo</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hlēo</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shelter, protection</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hlēoþor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hlēoþrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hlēoþrung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hearing</li><li>sound, noise</li><li>voice</li><li>speech</li><li>song (in nature or from man)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hleuþr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an arrival that is heralded by voices and music</li></ol><p>From <i>hlēoþor</i> +&lrm; <i>cyme</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) a rhetorician</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hlēoþor</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a sound</li><li>to cry out, proclaim; to speak out, exclaim, utter</li><li>to sing</li><li>to sound, resound; to be heard</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hlēoþor</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sound, noise; musical sound</li><li>speaking</li><li>reproof</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hlēoþrian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lot</li><li>casting of lots</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cover, lid</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hlid</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlidą</i> (“lid”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>postern, swing gate</li></ol><p>From <i>hlid</i> (“lid, cover”) +&lrm; <i>ġeat</i>, <i>ġæt</i> (“gate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laugh <i>(+ genitive of thing laughed at)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hlahhjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlahjaną</i>. Cognates include Old Saxon <i>hlahhian</i>, Old High German <i>hlahhan</i>, Old Norse <i>hlæja</i>, Gothic <i>𐌷𐌻𐌰𐌷𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hlahjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jump</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlaupiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tower, stand tall</li></ol><p>Often connected to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlaibaz</i> (“loaf of bread”). However, compare Ancient Greek <i>λόφος</i> (lóphos, “crest of a hill, ridge”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hliehhan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sound, roar, clang, crash</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlumjaną</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>hlymja</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sound, roaring</li><li>torrent</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maple tree</li><li>din (<i>hlyn</i>)</li><li>torrent (<i>hlyn</i>)</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>hlynr</i> (“maple tree”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hluniz</i> (“maple”). Cognate to German <i>Lehne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hill, rising ground, slope</li><li>a bank separating strips of arable land on a slope; a bank forming a boundary</li><li>ridge</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hlink</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlinkaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kleng-</i> (“to bend, turn, wind, twist”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean, bend, lie down, recline, rest</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hlinēn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlināną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱley-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prison</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leaning, resting</li><li>couch</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hlinian</i> (“to lean, recline”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fame</li><li>reputation</li><li>praise, applause</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>famous</li><li>honorable</li></ol><p>From <i>hlīsa</i> +&lrm; <i>-ful</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>hlīsful</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hlēt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hliþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slope, incline</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hliþą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱley-</i> (“to lean, slope”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pigsty</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to listen, be silent in expectation of hearing, listen for the coming of a person, watch, await, be on the look out</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlusnōną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱlew-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>portion, share, lot</li><li>choice, decision</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hlut</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlutą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a bovine</i>) to moo, low, bellow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hlōan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lowing</li><li>noise</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hlōwan</i> (“to low”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hlōþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gang, band of criminals</li><li>the crime of taking part in a <i>hlōþ's</i> action</li><li>prey, spoil</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>loþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compensation or fine to be paid by a member of a <i>hlōþ</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hlōþ</i> +&lrm; <i>bōt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a robber or despoiler, specifically as a member of a gang</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hlōþian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Lewis or French Louis</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hlūdawīg</i>; equivalent to <i>hlūd</i> (“loud”) +&lrm; <i>wīġ</i> (“war”). This is the original English form of the name <i>Louis</i>; it was present in England before the Norman Conquest of 1066, thenceforth being displaced by the Anglo-Norman-derived <i>Lewis</i> / <i>Lowis</i> in Middle English, from the same Germanic source (via Frankish).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Hlothhere of Kent; Anglo-Saxon King of Kent.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hlūdhari</i>. Cognate with German <i>Lothar</i>, Italian <i>Lotario</i>, and French <i>Lothaire</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hlōþhere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take booty, rob, despoil</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hlōþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gang murder</li></ol><p><i>hlōþ</i> +&lrm; <i>sliht</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hlōþewīġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loud</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hlūd</i> (“loud, famous”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>hlūd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>loudly</li><li>out loud</li></ol><p>From <i>hlūd</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i></p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Hlude)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Teme, a river which rises in Powys, Wales, before entering England, flowing through Shropshire, Herefordshire, for a short distance, and Worcestershire</li></ol><p>From the adjective <i>hlūd</i> (“loud”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clamor</li></ol><p>From <i>hlūd</i> (“loud”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clear, pure, bright, sincere</li><li>(<i>of a liquid</i>) free from mixture or impurities</li><li>(<i>of air or weather</i>) clear, not cloudy</li><li>(<i>of mentality or intellect</i>) clear, understood, free from obscurity</li><li>(<i>of people</i>) splendid, glorious, illustrious; clear from evil, guilt or deceit</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hlūtr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>clearly, plainly, simply</li><li>sincerely</li></ol><p>From <i>hlūtor</i> +&lrm; <i>-liċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clearness, purity</li><li>sincerity, simplicity</li></ol><p>From <i>hlūtor</i> +&lrm; <i>-ness</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hlūtor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become clear</li><li>to make clear, purify</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sound, noise, tumult</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlūdijō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱlew-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the third month of the year according to the Anglo-Saxon calendar</li><li>March</li></ol><p>From <i>hlȳdan</i> (“to make noise”) +&lrm; <i>-a</i> (agentive suffix), literally “noise-maker”, referring to the strong winds of March.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sound, make a noise, clamour, vociferate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hlūdijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>hlȳd</i> (“sound, clamor”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of a tree, possibly a maple.</li><li>alternative form of <i>hlynn</i> (“sound, noise”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make noise</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hlunnjan</i>, from <i>&#42;hlunni</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-jan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hlynan</i> (“to make noise”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sound, noise</li><li>roaring stream</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hlunni</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to resound</li></ol><p>From <i>hlynn</i> (“sound, noise”) + <i>-sian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hlīep</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to listen</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlusnijaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱlew-</i>. Cognate with Middle High German <i>lüsenen</i> (“to listen”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>listening</li><li>the sense of hearing (<i>one of the five senses</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlustiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to listen, hear</li></ol><p>From a hypothetical Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlustijaną</i>, or derived from <i>hlyst</i> (“listening, hearing”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hlēt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hlytta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who casts lots</li><li>one with a share of an inheritance</li></ol><p>From <i>hlȳt</i> (“lot”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hlæder</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ladle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlaþaną</i> (“to load”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kleh₂-</i> (“to put, lay out”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ladder</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlaidrijō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water wheel</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lady, the mistress of a household</li><li>noblewoman; queen</li><li>a form of courteous address; lady</li><li>the Virgin Mary</li></ol><p>From <i>hlāf</i> (“bread”) +&lrm; <i>dǣġe</i> (“kneader”, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;daigijǭ</i>); compare <i>dāg</i> (“dough”) and <i>hlāford</i>.</p><p>The discrepancy in vocalism between <i>dǣġe</i> and the second element of <i>hlǣfdīġe</i> is presumably due to vowel reduction (though an older theory instead postulates a Proto-Germanic ablaut variant <i>&#42;dīgijǭ</i>), while that between <i>hlāf</i> and the first element is due to <i>i</i>-umlaut from the Proto-Germanic <i>-ij-</i> in the second element (compare <i>ǣnēge</i>, <i>ǣnlīpiġ</i>, <i>endlufon</i>, <i>enwintre</i>; the variant <i>hlāfdīġe</i> is due to readjustment to <i>hlāf</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hliehhan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hliehhan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to lean, to incline</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lean; meager</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlainiz</i> (“inclined, leaning”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱley-</i> (“to lean”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make lean (<i>thin, meager</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlainisōną</i>, equivalent to <i>hlǣne</i> +&lrm; <i>-sian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>load; freight, cargo</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlastuz</i>, cognate with Old Frisian <i>hlest</i> and Old High German <i>hlast</i>. Related to <i>hladan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cargo ship</li></ol><p><i>hlæst</i> (“load”) +&lrm; <i>sċip</i> (“ship”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>læt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mound, hill, barrow, cairn</li><li>cave</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hlaiw</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlaiwą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱloy-wo-m</i>, from <i>&#42;ḱley-</i> (“shelter, cover”). Cognate with Proto-Norse <i>ᚺᛚᚨᛁᚹᚨ</i> (hlaiwa).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>humble, lowly</li><li>abject, poor</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hnaigu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnaigwaz</i>. Compare Gothic <i>𐌷𐌽𐌰𐌹𐍅𐍃</i> (hnaiws).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nap</li><li>to become drowsy for a short while</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hnappōn</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>hnaffezzen</i> (“to nap”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hnappian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stingy, niggardly, miserly</li><li>near, close</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hnauw</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnawwaz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>hnøggr</i>. Sense development of “narrow” to “near” was possibly influenced by <i>nēah</i> (“near”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nēhwaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>back of the neck</li><li>back of the head</li><li>neck</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hnakkō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnakkô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soft; delicate, tender</li><li>(<i>of a person</i>) effeminate</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) soft to the touch, yielding easily to pressure</li><li>(<i>of energy</i>) soft, gentle</li><li>(<i>of condition</i>) free from hardship</li><li>lacking in energy</li><li>yielding to temptation, inclined to wantonness</li><li>unable to endure hardship</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>softly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnaskuz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>hnesċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>hnesċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hnīgan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnīganą</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hnīga</i>, Old Saxon <i>hnīgan</i>, Old High German <i>nīgan</i>, Old Norse <i>hníga</i>, Gothic <i>𐌷𐌽𐌴𐌹𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (hneiwan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend down, bow or lower one's head</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnigōną</i>, derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnīganą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clash, butt</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hnītan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnītaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>given to striking, thrusting, or pushing; (<i>of an animal</i>) having the head bent or lowered as to headbutt</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hnitul</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnitulaz</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnitōną</i>, from <i>&#42;hnītaną</i> (“to bump, knock”), equivalent to <i>hnītan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nit</li></ol><p>From West Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnits</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>niz</i> (German <i>Nisse</i>), Middle Dutch <i>neet</i> (Dutch <i>neet</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) penis</li></ol><p>Derived from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hnukēn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnukāną</i> (“to be bent, flex”), possibly through Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hnokuk</i> (diminutive noun), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;knewg-</i> (“to bend, pinch”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ken-</i> (“to pinch”). Compare Icelandic <i>hnokki</i> (“small hook on the end of a spindle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hnoll</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>top of the head, crown</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike or beat</li></ol><p>Perhaps related to Old Norse <i>hnoss</i> (“ornament”), both related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hneupaną</i> (“to tear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hnossiaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bald</li><li>cropped, pollarded (of trees, plants)</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nutshell</li></ol><p><i>hnutu</i> +&lrm; <i>sċiell</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nut</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hnut</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnuts</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;knew-</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>nuz</i>, Old Norse <i>hnot</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hnesċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush, destroy</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. Perhaps an alteration of <i>&#42;hnǣtan</i>, the original form of <i>nǣtan</i> (“to trample upon, crush, subdue”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hnaitijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnaitijaną</i> (“to revile”). Compare Old Norse <i>hneita</i> (“to insult, beat, overcome”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to neigh</li><li>(rare,&#32;poetic) to humble; to bring low or cause to bow (of an enemy)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hnaijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnajjaną</i> (“to neigh”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>tōhnēgian</i> (“to neigh at”), Old Norse <i>hneggja</i>, <i>gneggja</i> (“to neigh”).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hnaigijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnaigijaną</i> (“to humble”), the causative of <i>&#42;hnīganą</i> (Old English <i>hnīgan</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neighing</li></ol><p>From <i>hnǣġan</i> (“to neigh”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hnæpp</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnappaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hnæpp</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnappaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cup</li><li>bowl</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hnapp</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hnappian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>Call of attention.</li><li>Hey!</li></ol><p>Inflection of <i>hōn</i>.</p><p>Probably from Old Norse <i>hó&#33;</i> (interjection, also, a shepherd's call). Compare Dutch <i>ho</i>, German <i>ho</i>, Old French <i>ho&#33;</i> (“hold!, halt!”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a couch</li></ol><p>From <i>hōh</i> (“hough, heel, headland”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;banca</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marshmallow (plant)</li><li>angle, (<i>of land</i>): point</li><li>hook</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hōk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hōkaz</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Hoc)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hoc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hogg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sickle</li></ol><p>From <i>hōc</i> (“hook”) +&lrm; <i>īsern</i> (“iron”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mallow</li></ol><p>From <i>hoc</i> (“marshmallow”) +&lrm; <i>lēaf</i> (“leaf”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hood</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hōd</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hōdaz</i> (“hood”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>court, hall</li><li>house, building</li><li>a hoof</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hof</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hufą</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hōfaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chief, leader</li></ol><p>Cognate with Old Norse <i>hǫfðingi</i>, equivalent to <i>heafod</i> + <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ground-ivy</li></ol><p>Of obscure origin, lacking Germanic and even Indo-European cognates. However, suggestions include:</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hofor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>humpbacked, gibbous</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hump</li><li>swelling</li><li>bulge</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hofr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hufraz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hofor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hōh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deliberate thought; care; effort; thoughtfulness</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>careful; thoughtful; prudent</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hugô</i> ("thought"&#59; &lt; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hugōną</i> (“to think, consider”)), n-stem variant of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hugiz</i>, <i>&#42;huguz</i> (“mind, spirit, understanding”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hog, especially a castrated male (family Suidae)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly derived from Old Norse <i>hǫggva</i> (“to strike, chop, cut”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hawwaną</i> (“to hew, forge”).</p><p>Alternatively, perhaps from Celtic, compare Welsh <i>hwch</i> (“sow”), Cornish <i>hoch</i> (“pig”) (whence probably modern English <i>hoggan</i> (“pork pasty”)); however, the possibility of British Celtic origin [Watkins, etc.] is regarded by OED as "improbable.".</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think, think about, consider</li><li>to know, understand, be wise, be prudent</li><li>to care, take heed, be solicitous or anxious, mind, be cautious</li><li>to purpose, strive, intend, be intent on, resolve, busy oneself</li><li>to decide</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hugōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hugōną</i>. Cognate with Middle Low German <i>hogen</i>, <i>hȫgen</i> (“to think, remember, imagine”), Middle Dutch <i>hogen</i> (“to think about, remember, rejoice, desire, remind”), Old High German <i>hogēn</i> (“to consider, heed, be careful”), Middle High German <i>hogen</i>, <i>hugen</i>, <i>hügen</i> (“to think, ponder, desire, rejoice, be pleased, long for, want, remember”), Old Norse <i>huga</i> (“to think about, consider”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>care, anxiety, anxiousness</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hugu</i>. Cognate with Middle High German <i>huge</i>, <i>hoge</i> (“spirit, mind, courage”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The heel (rear of a foot)</li><li>promontory, cliff</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hą̄h</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hanhaz</i>.</p><p>Related to <i>hōn</i> (“to hang”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>careful</li><li>thoughtful, prudent</li><li>anxious, troubled</li><li>mournful, sad</li><li>persistent, persevering</li></ol><p>From <i>hoga</i> (“fear, worry, trial, effort”) +&lrm; <i>-ful</i> and/or <i>hogu</i> (“care, trouble”) +&lrm; <i>-ful</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hohful</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hogian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hogian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) the shank</li></ol><p>From <i>hōh</i> +&lrm; <i>sċanca</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foresight, caution, prudence, thoughtfulness, deliberation</li></ol><p>From <i>hoga</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i>. Compare Old Frisian <i>hochnisse</i> (“mind, memory”), Middle Low German <i>hochenisse</i> (“mind, memory”), Middle Dutch <i>hogenisse</i>, <i>hoochnisse</i> (“mind, memory”), Middle High German <i>hugnisse</i> (“mind, memory”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hōhsinu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hamstring</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hą̄hasinu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hanhasinwō</i>; equivalent to <i>hōh</i> (“heel”) +&lrm; <i>sinu</i> (“sinew”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hole</li><li>calumny; slander</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hol</i> (“hollow space, cavity”).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hōlą</i> (“vain speech, slander, calumny”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱeh₁l-</i>, <i>&#42;keh₁l-</i> (“to beguile, deceive”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hollow, cavity</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;holhuk</i>, <i>&#42;hulhic</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hulhukaz</i> (“something hollow or hollowed out, cavity”), equivalent to <i>holh</i> +&lrm; <i>-uc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dead body; carcass</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gracious, loyal, kind</li><li>friendly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;huldą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kol-</i>, <i>&#42;kwol-</i>. Cognates include Old Norse <i>hold</i> (“flesh”) (Icelandic <i>hold</i>, Swedish <i>hull</i>), and (from Indo-European) Old Irish <i>colainn</i>, Welsh <i>celain</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;holþ</i>, a variant on a root meaning ‘lean, incline’ (compare Old English <i>heald</i>, <i>hieldan</i>).</p><p>Cognates include Old Frisian <i>hold</i>, Old Saxon <i>hold</i>, Old High German <i>hold</i> (German <i>hold</i>), Old Norse <i>hollr</i> (Danish <i>huld</i>, Swedish <i>huld</i>), Gothic <i>𐌷𐌿𐌻𐌸𐍃</i> (hulþs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>kindly, graciously</li></ol><p>From <i>hold</i> (“gracious, kind”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>faithfulness, loyalty</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hold</i> +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loyalty, allegiance</li></ol><p>From <i>hold</i> +&lrm; <i>-scipe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>holen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) holly</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hulis</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>holh</i> (“hollow, hole”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A hollow, cavity, hole.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;holh</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hulhwą</i> (“hollow, cavity”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hulhwaz</i> (“hollow”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱel-</i> (“to cover, hide”). Cognate with Old High German <i>huliwa</i> (“swamp, pool, puddle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hollow something out, to make something hollow</li><li>to make a hole; dig</li><li>to oppress</li><li>to slander</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;holōn</i>, related to Old English <i>holh</i>, <i>hol</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>holōn</i> (“to dig out, excavate”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hōlōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hōlōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>holen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hōlunga</i></li></ol><p>See <i>hōlunga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) ocean, sea, waters</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;holm</i> (“island”), though the meaning was influenced by Old Norse <i>holmr</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>holm</i> (German <i>Holm</i>), Old Dutch <i>holm</i> (Dutch <i>holm</i>); also Latin <i>culmen</i> (“peak”); compare culminate.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>holc</i> (“hollow, cavity”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;holhuk</i>, <i>&#42;hulhic</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hulhukaz</i> (“something hollow or hollowed out, cavity”), equivalent to <i>holh</i> +&lrm; <i>-oc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wood, woodland, holt</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;holt</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hultą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in vain</li><li>without reason</li></ol><p>From <i>hōlian</i> +&lrm; <i>-unga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ham</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hama</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hamor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hamora lāf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang</li><li>to suspend</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hą̄han</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hand</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>handweorc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hōc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Old English</i>) a small enclosed valley</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hope</li></ol><p>Late shortened form of <i>tōhopa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hope <i>(+genitive for something)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hopōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an upper outergarment; mantle or cope</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;hopp</i> (“garment, dress”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;pāda</i> (“covering, cloak”), literally “dress-cloak, covering for a garment, outergarment”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dancer</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female dancer</li></ol><p>From <i>hoppian</i> +&lrm; <i>-estre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>frequentative of</i> <b>hoppian</b>: to hop around, skip around, etc.</li><li>(of a wound) to throb</li></ol><p><i>hoppian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hop</li><li>to skip, to bound</li><li>to bounce</li><li>to jump, to leap</li><li>to dance</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;huppōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;huppōną</i> (“to hop”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kewb-</i> (“to bend, bow”). Cognate with Dutch <i>huppen</i>, German <i>hüpfen</i>, Swedish <i>hoppa</i>, and Icelandic <i>hoppa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sheet or coverlet; counterpane</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;hopp</i> +&lrm; <i>sċīete</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the train of a dress</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;hopp</i> (“cover, dress, garment”) +&lrm; <i>steort</i> (“tail, train”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adulteress, whore</li></ol><p>From <i>hōre</i> (“whore”) +&lrm; <i>cwene</i> (“woman”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A hoard, especially of valuable items, hidden for preservation.</li><li>treasure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;huzdą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) treasure-chamber, breast</li></ol><p>From <i>hord</i> (“treasure”) +&lrm; <i>cofa</i> (“chamber”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasurer, steward</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hord</i> (“hoard”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>storehouse</li><li>storeroom</li><li>treasury</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hord</i> +&lrm; <i>-ern</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a vessel for holding treasure</li></ol><p>From <i>hord</i> (“hord”) +&lrm; <i>fæt</i> (“vessel”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hoard, lay up treasure, store</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hoʀdōn</i>, equivalent to <i>hord</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whore, prostitute</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hōrǭ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kéh₂ros</i> (“dear, loved”). Compare Old High German <i>huora</i> (German <i>Hure</i>), Dutch <i>hoer</i>, Old Norse <i>hóra</i> (Danish <i>hore</i>, Swedish <i>hora</i>); compare also Latin <i>carus</i> (“dear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>horh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phlegm</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;horhu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hurhwą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>horiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foul; dirty; defiled</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hurhwīgaz</i>, equivalent to <i>horu</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>horawīg</i>, <i>horīg</i>, <i>horg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horn</li><li>antler</li><li>(horn-shaped) gable</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;horn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hurną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱerh₂-</i> (“horn, head, top”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>horn</i>, Old Saxon <i>horn</i>, Old High German <i>horn</i>, Old Norse <i>horn</i>, Gothic <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌽</i> (haurn).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hornblower, trumpeter, bugler</li></ol><p>From <i>horn</i> (“horn”) +&lrm; <i>blāwere</i> (“blower”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of an animal or object</i>) equipped or fitted with horns; horn-bearing</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hurnabēriz</i>, equivalent to <i>horn</i> +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garfish</li></ol><p>From <i>horn</i> (“horn”) +&lrm; <i>fisċ</i> (“fish”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) gabled hall</li></ol><p>From <i>horn</i> +&lrm; <i>reced</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a ship with a rostrum (the projecting prow of a rowed warship)</li></ol><p><i>horn</i> (“horn”) +&lrm; <i>sċip</i> (“ship”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bastard, illegitimate child</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hurnung</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hurnungaz</i> (“bastard”), cognate with Old High German <i>hornung</i>, Old Norse <i>hornungr</i>, Old Frisian <i>horning</i>, Frankish <i>hōrni(n)g</i>. Found in the compound hornungsunu (“bastard son”), as well as various toponyms such as <i>Horninggesele</i> (Horningsea), <i>Horninggeshǣð</i> (Horningsheath), <i>Horningamǣre</i>, and <i>Horningdūn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bastard son</li></ol><p>From <i>hornung</i> (“bastard”) +&lrm; <i>sunu</i> (“son”). Compare Faroese <i>horkonusonur</i> (“bastard”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hross</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrussą</i> (“horse”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱers-</i> (“to run”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Along with his brother Henġest, one of the legendary leaders of the Anglo-Saxons during their invasion of Britain</li></ol><p>From the noun <i>hors</i> (“horse”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quick, ready, active, valiant</li><li>wise, sagacious, sharp, quick-witted</li><li>foul, dirty</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;horsk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hurskaz</i> (“fast, quick, lively”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱers-</i> (“to run”). Doublet of <i>hors</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horse-comb, currycomb</li></ol><p>From <i>hors</i> (“horse”) +&lrm; <i>camb</i> (“comb”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>readily, promptly</li><li>wisely, prudently</li></ol><p>From <i>horsċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horsecart, chariot</li></ol><p>From <i>hors</i> +&lrm; <i>cræt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild elecampane</li></ol><p>From <i>hors</i> +&lrm; <i>elene</i> (“elecampane”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>horselene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stable</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hors</i> +&lrm; <i>-ern</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>horselene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>groom (person who takes care of horses)</li></ol><p><i>hors</i> +&lrm; <i>hierde</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hippodrome</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hors</i> (“horse”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a walrus</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hors</i> (“horse”) +&lrm; <i>hwæl</i> (“whale”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>hrosshvalr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to horse (provide with horses)</li></ol><p>From <i>hors</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild mint, horsemint</li></ol><p>From <i>hors</i> (“horse”) +&lrm; <i>minte</i> (“mint”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chariot</li></ol><p>From <i>hors</i> +&lrm; <i>wæġn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>horswæġn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>horsern</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>groom</li><li>the title of an officer in the royal household</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>hors</i> (“horse”) +&lrm; <i>þeġn</i> (“servant, minister, officer”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whortleberry</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dirt; filth; foulness</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;horhu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hurhwą</i> (“manure; filth”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱer-</i> (“dark; grey; dirty”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>escort; company; troop</li><li>bramble</li><li>thorn</li><li>sprout, shoot, tendril</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hansu</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>hansa</i>.</p><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pants, trousers</li></ol><p>Plural of <i>hose</i> (“pant leg”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pant leg, stocking</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) pants, trousers; see <i>hosan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hosā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>hos</i> +&lrm; <i>here</i> (“army”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reproach, contempt, contumely, insult, blasphemy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hosp</i>, <i>&#42;husp</i> (“derision, mockery”), of obscure origin. Likely an alteration of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hosk</i>, <i>&#42;husk</i> (“derision, mockery”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;huskaz</i>, <i>&#42;huską</i> (“mockery”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kūd-</i> (“to shout, be naughty, mock”). More at Old English <i>husċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mock, ridicule</li></ol><p>From <i>hosp</i> +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>insulting, contumelious, opprobrious, blasphemous</li></ol><p>From <i>hosp</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pod, husk</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hosā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;husǭ</i>, <i>&#42;hausaz</i> (“covering, shell, leggings”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrǣw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrace</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrakō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kreg-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) throat, gullet</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) the jaws, maw</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) gorge; a narrow outlet at the upper end of a valley</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrakō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kreg-</i> (“to caw, crow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrace</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quicken, hasten</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrace</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrace</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) slaughter</li></ol><p><i>hrǣw</i> +&lrm; <i>fyl</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heron</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hraigrō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hraigrô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrāgra</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cramp, spasm</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hramjō</i>, related to hremman, Old Norse <i>hremma</i> and Old Dutch <i>&#42;remmen</i> from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hramjaną</i> (“to hinder, clutch”). Possibly cognate with Old Norse <i>hrammr</i> (“bear claw”) (from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hramjaz</i> (“clutching”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>onion</li><li>garlic, ramsons, wild garlic, <i>Allium ursinum</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hramusō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hramsa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small whale or large delphinid</li><li>reindeer</li></ol><p>Origin unknown, cf. Épinal Glossary <i>hran</i> (c. 700) and Futhorc ᚻᚱᚩᚾᚫᛋ (hrónæs) (gen. sg.) on the Franks Casket (early 8th c.). Perhaps related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;harzaz</i> (“a kind of fish”). Compare Norwegian <i>harr</i> (“grayling”), Swedish <i>harr</i> (“grayling”).</p><p>Calque of Old Norse <i>hreinn</i>, as if from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrain</i>, ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrainaz</i>. At the time, Old Norse and Old English were sufficiently close that the correspondence between <i>ā</i> and <i>ei</i> was transparent, as in words like <i>stān</i> (“stone”) and <i>steinn</i> (“id.”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) ocean</li></ol><p>Kenning of <i>hran</i> (“small whale or large delphinid”) +&lrm; <i>rād</i> (“riding”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) rattlewort</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hratalā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrat-</i> (“to rattle”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kerd-</i> (“to move, swing, jump”). Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>ratele</i>, <i>ratel</i>, whence Dutch <i>ratel</i>, <i>ratelaar</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush, hasten</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hratōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hratōną</i> (“to sway, reel, stagger, stumble”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kerd-</i> (“to move, swing, jump”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>hrata</i> (“to fall, tumble, stagger, rush”). Compare similar yet unrelated Old English <i>hraþian</i>, <i>hradian</i> (“to quicken, hasten”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrǣw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hraþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fast, quickly</li><li>soon</li><li>early</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hraþē</i> or <i>&#42;hraþō</i> ~ <i>&#42;hradō</i> (“quickly”), equivalent to <i>hræd</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hreþer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hradian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>hraþe</i>; faster, earlier</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>hraþe</i>; fastest, earliest</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consumption (dissipation, wasting away)</li><li>indigestion</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrawō</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrau</i> (“raw”). Compare Icelandic <i>hrái</i> (“rawness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stack, stook, rick (of stones, corn, hay etc.)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hraukaz</i>. Cognate with West Frisian <i>reak</i>, Middle Dutch <i>rooc</i> (Dutch <i>rook</i>), Old Norse <i>hraukr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scream, shout, yell, cry</li><li>screaming, shouting, yelling</li><li>outcry</li><li>infamy, offense</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hraumaz</i>, related to <i>&#42;hrōþiz</i> (“glory, renown”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>raw</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrǣw</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrau</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrawaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krewh₂-</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>rāo</i>, Old Norse <i>hrár</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hraiwaz</i>, <i>&#42;hraiwiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hrēaþemūs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bat (flying mammal)</li></ol><p>Origin obscure. Perhaps from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hraþamūs</i> (literally “fleet-mouse, swift-mouse”), equivalent to <i>hræd</i> +&lrm; <i>mūs</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>hradamūs</i> (“bat”), Old High German <i>rodamūs</i> (“bat”).</p><p>Alternatively, first element from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krew-</i> (“to shake, wave around”), related to Tocharian A <i>kru</i> (“reed”), Tocharian B <i>kärwats</i>, Lithuanian <i>krutéti</i> (“to move”); second element from <i>mūs</i> (“mouse”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to save, rescue</li><li>to deliver, free; take away</li><li>to recover</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hraddjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hradjaną</i> (“to save, rescue”). Akin to Old Frisian <i>hredda</i>, Old High German <i>&#42;retten</i> (German <i>retten</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrēþmōnaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hræfn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hræfn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrīeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) loud with emotion (joy or grief); exulting, joyous</li></ol><p>From <i>hrēam</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>, literally 'shouty' or 'screamy'.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hinder, obstruct</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrammjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hramjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hindrance, obstacle, obstruction, impediment</li></ol><p>From <i>hremman</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hræfn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a reed</li><li>a reedy place</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hreud</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hriād</i>, Old Saxon <i>hriod</i>, Old Dutch <i>ried</i>, <i>riet</i>, Old High German <i>hriot</i>, <i>riot</i>. Further etymology uncertain.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adorn, decorate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hreudan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hreudaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rough</li><li>rugged</li><li>leprous</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hreubaz</i> (“rough, scabby”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kreup</i> (“scab, crust”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leprosy</li><li>leper</li></ol><p>Related to <i>hrēof</i> (“rough, leprous”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>leprous</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hrēofla</i> (“leprosy”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rough, fierce, savage</li><li>tempestuous, disturbed, worried</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hreuhaz</i> (“bad, wild”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krewh₂-</i> (“raw meat, fresh blood”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>hrēoh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rēonian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Repton, a village in England</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hreopadūn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ruinous, ruined</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hreurīg</i> (“diligent, zealous, busy”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hreuzīgaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krow-</i> (“to fall, beat, break”). Equivalent to <i>hrēosan</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall</li><li>to collapse</li><li>to rush</li><li>to fall down</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hreusan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hreusaną</i> (“to fall down”). Cognate with Old High German <i>riosan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>regret, remorse</li><li>repentance</li><li>sorrow</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sad</li><li>repentant</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hreuwu</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hriwwijaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to regret, rue, repent, or grieve <i>(impersonal, with dative or accusative of subject)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hreuwan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hriuwan</i> and Old High German <i>riuwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>troubled, anxious, sorrowful</li></ol><p>From <i>hrēow</i> (“regret, remorse”) +&lrm; <i>cariġ</i> (“grieving”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>troubled, anxious, sorrowful</li></ol><p>From <i>hrēow</i> (“grief”) +&lrm; <i>ċeariġ</i> (“sorrowful”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sorrowful</li></ol><p>From <i>hrēow</i> (“sorrow”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grievous, pitiful, sad, wretched, cruel, lamentable, hapless, miserable</li></ol><p>From <i>hrēow</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>miserably, cruelly, grievously, in a way to excite pity</li></ol><p>From <i>hrēow</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>hrēowlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sorrow, penitence, repentance</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>hreppan</i> (“to touch, treat, concern”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrepēn</i> (“to be touching”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to touch; treat</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to attack</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrappjan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hreppa</i> (“to move”), Old Norse <i>hreppa</i> (“to grasp, obtain”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>touch, touching</li><li>the sense of touch (<i>one of the five senses</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>hrepian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rare; not thoroughly cooked; (<i>of eggs</i>) lightly boiled</li></ol><p>From <i>hrēran</i> (“to move quickly”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move</li><li>to shake</li><li>to stir</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrōʀijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrōzijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in reference to cooking, boiling, etc.</i>) quickly, briefly, lightly</li></ol><p>From <i>hrēr</i> (adjective), from <i>hrēran</i> (“to move quickly”). Equivalent to <i>hrēr</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) bat (<i>flying mammal</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>hrēran</i> (“to whisk, stir”) +&lrm; <i>mūs</i> (“mouse”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not fully cooked</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hrēþmōnaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glory, triumph, honour</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrōþiz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>hruodi</i> (used in personal names), Old Norse <i>hróðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a garment made of goatskin</li><li>a mantle</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fierce, cruel, savage, rough</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hrethel, a king of the Geats mentioned in <i>Beowulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) chest; bosom; torso</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) heart; mind; thought</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;herþrą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) breast, heart, mind</li></ol><p>From <i>hreþer</i> (“mind”) +&lrm; <i>loca</i> (“enclosure”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>hrēþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>triumphant, exultant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrōþīgaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>hrēþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>&#42;𐌷𐍂𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;hrōþs) (used in personal names), <i>𐌷𐍂𐍉𐌸𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (hrōþeigs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a member of the Geatish royal house in the poem <i>Beowulf</i>; a descendant of King Hreþel</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Hreþel</i> (a name) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“descendant of”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the third month of the year according to the Anglo-Saxon calendar</li><li>March</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;Hrēþe</i> +&lrm; <i>mōnaþ</i>. Alternatively, from <i>hrēþ</i> +&lrm; <i>mōnaþ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hreþer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hryċġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sieve</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrīdrā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrīdrą</i>, <i>&#42;hrīdrǭ</i> (“sieve”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrid-</i> (“to shake”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krey-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sieve (device used to winnow grain)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrīdrą</i>, <i>&#42;hrīdrǭ</i> (“sieve”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krey-</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to yell, shout, scream</li><li>to lament</li><li>to boast, brag</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hraumijaną</i>, derived from <i>&#42;hraumaz</i>, whence OE <i>hrēam</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>womb</li><li>belly</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrif</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frost</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrīmaz</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>rijm</i>, Old Norse <i>hrím</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrīeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>icy cold, frost-cold</li></ol><p>From <i>hrīm</i> (“frost”) +&lrm; <i>ċeald</i> (“cold”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hoarfrost</li></ol><p>From <i>hrīm</i> (“frost”) +&lrm; <i>forst</i> (“frost”). Compare Norwegian <i>rimfrost</i>, Swedish <i>rimfrost</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) icicle</li></ol><p>From <i>hrīm</i> (“frost”) +&lrm; <i>ġiċel</i> (“icicle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rimy, frosty</li></ol><p><i>hrīm</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>hrīmiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch</li><li>to reach</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrīnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hring</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrinġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to push, thrust</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrindan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrindaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kret-</i> (“to beat, strike, hit”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(countable,&#32;uncountable) touch</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hriniz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ring (circular band)</li><li>ring (circular mark); circle</li><li>circuit, cycle</li><li>a round object; orb, globe</li><li>a loop or ring used as a means of attachment, suspension, or compression; handle</li><li>a wreath, garland, or festoon</li><li>the border of a circular area or region</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) the limit of a jurisdiction</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hring</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hringaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hring</i>, Old Saxon <i>hring</i>, Old High German <i>ring</i>, Old Norse <i>hringr</i>, also Finnish <i>rengas</i>.</p><p>In some forms and senses, possibly influenced by related <i>hrinċġe</i>, <i>hrinġe</i> (“loop, handle, clasp”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrinġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive&#32;and&#32;intransitive</i>) to ring (make a ringing sound)</li><li>to surround</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hringijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hringijaną</i> (“to ring”), from <i>&#42;hringaz</i> (“ringing sound”), whence Old English <i>hring</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hringijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hringijaną</i> (“to surround”), from <i>&#42;hringaz</i> (“ring, circle”), whence Old English <i>hring</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrinġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) ‘ring-Danes’, the Danes</li></ol><p><i>hring</i> (“ring”) +&lrm; <i>Dene</i> (“Danes”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a ring employed as a means of attachment, suspension, or compression; a loop, catch; a buckle or clasp</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hringijā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hringijǭ</i> (“ring, loop”), diminutive of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hringaz</i> (“ring”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hringa</i> (“clasp, brooch”), Old High German <i>ringa</i> (“brooch, hook”), Old Norse <i>hringja</i> (“buckle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) curve-prowed ship, ringed vessel</li></ol><p><i>hringed</i> +&lrm; <i>stefna</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ring finger</li></ol><p>From <i>hring</i> (“ring”) +&lrm; <i>finger</i> (“finger”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrinġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a hall in which rings are distributed, used to refer to Hrothgar's palace in <i>Beowulf</i></li></ol><p>From <i>hring</i> (“ring”) +&lrm; <i>sele</i> (“hall”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circus</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hring</i> +&lrm; <i>setl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;poetic) gift of rings</li></ol><p>From <i>hring</i> (“ring”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;þegu</i> (“taking”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>touch</li></ol><p><i>hrīnan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twig; branch</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;collectively) brushwood; shrubbery</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrīs</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrīsą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrissan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to shake</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrissjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrisjaną</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hrisian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rustle</li></ol><p>Likely a rare variant of <i>&#42;hristlian</i>, a frequentative of <i>&#42;hristian</i> (“to shake, quiver”) (attested in Old English <i>hristung</i> (“quivering”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quivering, spasm</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>&#42;hristian</i> (“to quiver”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hrīþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrīþiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) snowstorm; storm, tempest</li><li>fever</li><li>a bull or cow</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) cattle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrīþu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrīþō</i> (“sudden attack; seizure; fit; storm”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>hríð</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hriþi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hriþiz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>rito</i>. Perhaps ultimately from the same source as Welsh <i>cryd</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrinþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrinþaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>hrind</i>, <i>rind</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>countable</i>) cow; bull; ox; head of cattle (any bovine animal)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrinþiʀ</i> (“cattle”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hrīther</i> (“cattle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herdsman</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have a fever, be feverish</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>snow-swept</li></ol><p>From <i>hrīþ</i> (“snowstorm”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i> (“-y”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rook (bird)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrōk</i> (“rook”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Hroc)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Proper noun form of <i>hrōc</i> (“crow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hrōþberht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roof</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) the sky or heavens</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrōf</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrōfą</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hrōf</i>, Old Saxon <i>rōf</i>, Dutch <i>roef</i> (“cabin, coffin-lid”), Old Norse <i>hróf</i> (“boat-shed”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hrofesċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>scribal abbreviation of <i>Hrofesċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rochester, a town in England</li></ol><p>Literally "Hrofi's fortified camp"</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roof tile</li></ol><p>From <i>hrōf</i> (“roof”) +&lrm; <i>stān</i> (“stone”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roof tile</li></ol><p>From <i>hrōf</i> (“roof”) +&lrm; <i>tiġele</i> (“tile”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a roofer</li></ol><p>From <i>hrōf</i> (“roof”) +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“worker, maker”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>horh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hramsa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hranfisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hranfisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hranrād</i></li></ol><p>Kenning of <i>hron</i> (“whale”) +&lrm; <i>rād</i> (“riding”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clamour, lamentation</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrōp</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shout, scream</li><li>to call, cry out</li><li>to make a noise, clamor</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrōpan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hrōpan</i>, Old High German <i>ruofan</i>, Old Norse <i>hrópa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ropwærċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stirring, active</li><li>nimble, agile</li><li>stout, vigorous, strong</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrōʀ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrōzaz</i> (“active, quick”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱerh₂-</i> (“to mix, stir, seethe”). Possibly cognate with Proto-Norse <i>ᚺᚱᛟᛉᚨᛉ</i> (hroʀaʀ, male given name)..</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wooden framework of a roof</li><li>a roost</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrōst</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrōstaz</i> (“wooden framework; grill”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thick fluid, scum</li><li>snot, mucus</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrutt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hrofesċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Hrōþmund</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Hrōþiberhtaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>hreþ</i> (“glory, triumph, honour”) +&lrm; <i>berht</i> (“bright”). Cognate with English <i>Robert</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Hrothgar; a semi-legendary Danish king living around the early sixth century AD.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Hrōþigaizaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>hreþ</i> (“glory, triumph, honour”) +&lrm; <i>gār</i> (“spear”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>Hróarr</i>, <i>Hróðgeirr</i> and English <i>Roger</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Hrōþimunduz</i>. Equivalent to <i>hrēþ</i> (“glory, triumph, honour”) +&lrm; <i>mund</i> (“protection”). Cognate with Old High German <i>Hruodmunt</i>, Old Norse <i>Hrómundr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hrōþwulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hrōþiwald</i>. Equivalent to <i>hrēþ</i> (“glory, triumph”) +&lrm; <i>wald</i> (“powerful, mighty”). Cognate with Latin <i>Chrodoaldus</i> via Frankish.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hrōþiwini</i>, from <i>&#42;hrōþi</i> (“glory, honor”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wini</i> (“friend”). Equivalent to <i>hrēþ</i> +&lrm; <i>wine</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>Chrodoin</i> (via Frankish).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hrōþiwulf</i>. Equivalent to <i>hreþ</i> (“glory, triumph, honour”) +&lrm; <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”). Cognate with English <i>Rudolph</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soot</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrūm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sooty</li></ol><p>From <i>hrūm</i> (“soot”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a ladder</i>) a rung</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrungu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrungō</i> (“pole, staff, stake, rod, spoke, rung”), possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)krengʰ-</i> (“to twist, bend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A sword given to Beowulf by Unferþ in the poem <i>Beowulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deciduous</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hruʀul</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hruzulaz</i> (“falling”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hruzōn</i> (“to fall”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krew-</i> (“to fall, plunge”), equivalent to <i>&#42;hror</i> +&lrm; <i>-ol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrurol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the surface of the earth; the ground</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrusā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrusǭ</i> (“ground; ice”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krus-</i> (“crust”). Cognate with Old High German <i>hroso</i>, <i>hrosa</i> (“ice, crust”), Latin <i>crusta</i> (“crust”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snore</li><li>to make a noise</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrūtan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrūtaną</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>rūta</i>; Old Saxon <i>hrūtan</i>; Old High German <i>rūzan</i>, rūzzan (Middle High German <i>rūzen</i>); Old Norse <i>hrjóta</i> (Faroese <i>rjóta</i>, róta; Icelandic <i>hrjóta</i>; Elfdalian <i>riuota</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrīþer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) back, spine</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrugi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrugjaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>rucki</i>, Old Norse <i>hryggr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) backbone; spine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrugjabainą</i>, equivalent to <i>hryċġ</i> +&lrm; <i>bān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a clearing, a patch of cleared land</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;hryd</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>, the first element deriving ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hreuþaną</i> (“to make room, clear, empty”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>hruðning</i> (“clearing, clearance”), Old Norse <i>hryðja</i> (“to empty out, unload, clear, make room for”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hryċġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hryċġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrīeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fall or descent</li><li>downfall, destruction, ruin</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hruzjaz</i> (“a fall, collision”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krow-</i> (“to fall, beat, break”). Related to Old English <i>hrēosan</i> (“to fall, plunge, rush, topple”). More at hurt.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a noise; make a creaking or whirring sound; rustle; hiss</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hurskijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hurskijaną</i>. Compare also Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rūskōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrīþiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrīþer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rotherfield (a village and civil parish in Wealden district, East Sussex, England)</li></ol><p>From <i>hrȳþera</i>, genitive plural of <i>hrȳþer</i> (“cattle”) +&lrm; <i>feld</i> (“field”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrīþerhyrde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrīþiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mastiff, large dog</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hruþþjō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hruþjô</i> (“male dog”), clearly connected to Old High German <i>rudio</i> and Dutch <i>reu</i>. Possibly from <i>&#42;hruttōn-</i> (“to roar”), from a Proto-Indo-European root shared by Ancient Greek <i>κόρυζα</i> (kóruza), Old English <i>hrot</i>. Or, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hreutaną</i>, <i>&#42;hrūtaną</i>, <i>&#42;hruttōną</i> (“to snore”), which would be related to Old Norse <i>hrjóta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clear the throat, hawk</li><li>to spit</li><li>to spit blood</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrākijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrēkijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fast, quick</li><li>early</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrad</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hradaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rushing; hurry, haste</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;hrædan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quick, hasty, sudden, speedy</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hrǣd</i> (“fast, quick”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quickly</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hræd</i> (“quick”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i> or <i>hrǣdlīċ</i> (“swift”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i> (adverbial suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>hrædlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrēþmōnaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speed, quickness</li><li>earliness</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>hræd</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrædnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hasty in speech</li></ol><p>From <i>hræd</i> (“fast, quick”) +&lrm; <i>-wyrde</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chariot</li></ol><p><i>hræd</i> (“fast”) +&lrm; <i>wæġn</i> (“wagon”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hræfn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raven</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrabn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrabnaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>raven</i>, Old Saxon <i>hraƀan</i>, Old Dutch <i>ravan</i>, Old High German <i>raban</i>, Old Norse <i>hrafn</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱorh₂-</i>, whence Latin <i>corvus</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Hræfn)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From the noun <i>hræfn</i> (“raven”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>raven-black: black like a raven</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrabnaswartaz</i>, equivalent to <i>hræfn</i> (“raven”) +&lrm; <i>sweart</i> (“black”). Cognate with Middle High German <i>rabenschwarz</i> and Old Norse <i>hrafnsvartr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hræġl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garment, piece of clothing</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hragil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hragilą</i> (“garment, clothing”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krek-</i> (“to beat, weave; web”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hreil</i>, Old High German <i>hregil</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hræfn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hræfn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wave, billow</li><li>flood, sea</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hraʀnu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hraznō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kres-</i> (“spring, wave”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hratele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) rattlewort</li></ol><p>From <i>hrætele</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i>. Compare German <i>Rasselwurz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corpse, carrion</li><li>(living) body</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hraiwą</i> (“body”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hrēo</i>, Old High German <i>hrēo</i>, Old Norse <i>hræ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hræd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hraþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hreaþemūs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hreþer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>hræþ</i> (“fast, quick”) and <i>hūn</i> (“bear cub”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrēran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrēremūs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrēþiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>how, in all senses, including:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to what degree</li><li>in what manner</li><li>in what state</li></ol><li>used in exclamations</li><li>used to introduce negative rhetorical questions</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>how, in all senses:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>in what manner</li><li>that, the fact that <i>(introducing direct statements)</i></li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwō</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hū</i>, Old Saxon <i>hū</i> (Dutch <i>hoe</i>), Old High German <i>wuo</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>what about, how about</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>how many, how much (used in questions)</li></ol><p>From <i>hū</i> (“how”) + <i>fela</i> (“much, many”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at what time of the year</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>how</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>what if</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>how many (used in questions)</li></ol><p>From <i>hū</i> (“how”) + <i>maniġ</i> (“much”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>how</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>how much (used in questions)</li></ol><p>From <i>hū</i> (“how”) + <i>miċel</i> (“much”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wherefore</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>husċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwæþre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwæþre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horned owl</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a covering for the head (e.g. bonnet, cap, hood, etc.)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hūbā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hūbǭ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hogian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>about, approximately</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hwiċċas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hwiċċewudu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwiċċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>hey</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Compare Latin <i>heu</i> (“ay! oh! alas!”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwīl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hwilpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a light ship, hulk</li><li>(<i>Late Old English</i>) a heavy ship of clumsy make</li><li>hut, hovel, cabin</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Relation to Medieval Latin <i>hulcus</i>, <i>holcas</i> (“a kind of ship”) is uncertain (the Old English may have borrowed from the Latin or vice versa). Compare also Old High German <i>holcho</i> (“a barge, freighter, cargo ship”), Old Norse <i>holkr</i> (“metal tube, ring", later also "barge, cargo ship”). It's possible that the word is originally the same as Old English <i>holc</i> (“a hollow, cavity”). Alternatively, the various senses may represent separate origins that coalesced into a single term.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to howl</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hūlōną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>hūilōn</i>, Old Norse <i>úla</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of what sort</li></ol><p><i>hū</i> (“how”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hull, husk</li></ol><p>Ultimately from the zero-grade of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱel-</i> (“to hide, conceal”). From the same root as <i>helan</i> (“to hide, conceal”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Humber (estuary in England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>how, in what manner</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a common element in given names</li></ol><p>Unclear. Possibly a shortening of <i>hund</i> (“dog”) or from Old Norse <i>hunn</i> (“bear cub”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Hun)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Huns</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>hūn</i> (“bear cub”) and <i>berht</i> (“bright”). Cognate with Italian <i>Umberto</i>. The modern English form is <i>Humbert</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dog</li><li>hundred</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hund</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hundaz</i>. Ultimately descended from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱwṓ</i>.</p><p>Germanic cognates include Old Frisian <i>hund</i>, Old Saxon <i>hund</i>, Old Dutch <i>hunt</i>, Old High German <i>hunt</i>, Old Norse <i>hundr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌷𐌿𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (hunds).</p><p>Indo-European cognates include Latin <i>canis</i>, Ancient Greek <i>κύων</i> (kúōn), Sanskrit <i>श्वन्</i> (śvan), Old Irish <i>cú</i>, Lithuanian <i>šuõ</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hund</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hundą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱm̥tóm</i>. Cognates include Old High German <i>hunt</i> and Gothic <i>𐌷𐌿𐌽𐌳</i> (hund), also Latin <i>centum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>prefix used at the beginning of numerals from 70 to 120</li><li>hundred</li><li>dog, canine-, canid-</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hund-</i>, apparently from a metathesis of numerals ending in Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-tēhundą</i>, placing the <i>hund-</i> at the fore. Akin to Old Saxon <i>ant-</i> (compare Dutch <i>tachtig</i> (“eighty”)).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hundą</i> (“hundred”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱm̥tóm</i> (“hundred”). Akin to Old English <i>hund</i> (“hundred”).</p><p>From <i>hund</i> (“dog, hound”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doghouse</li></ol><p>Literally “dogs’ house.” Compare <i>ċealfra hūs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ahtōutēhundą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eightieth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eightieth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eighty years old</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hundeahtatiġ</i> (“eighty”) +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hundeahtatiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dog; canine</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hundīn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hundīnaz</i>, equivalent to <i>hund</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>huntīn</i> (“canine”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>one hundred and ten</li></ol><p>From the prefix <i>hund-</i> +&lrm; <i>*endleftiġ</i>, a combination of an archaic form of <i>endleofan</i> (“eleven”) and <i>-tiġ</i> (“-ty”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>one hundred tenth</li></ol><p>From <i>hundendleftiġ</i> (“one hundred ten”) +&lrm; <i>-oþa</i> (“-th”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hundendleftiġ</i> (“one hundred ten”).</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dogfly</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hound's tongue (<i>Cynoglossum officinale</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>hundes</i> (“dog's”) +&lrm; <i>tunge</i> (“tongue”), a calque of Latin <i>cynoglossos</i>, which is also attested as Old English <i>cuneglæsse</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hundredfold</li></ol><p>From <i>hund</i> (“hundred”) +&lrm; <i>-feald</i> (“-fold”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>doglike; canine</li></ol><p>From <i>hund</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety</li></ol><p>From the prefix <i>hund-</i> +&lrm; <i>*nigontiġ</i>, a combination of <i>nigon</i> (“nine”) and <i>-tiġ</i> (“-ty”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninetieth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ninetieth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ninety years old</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hundnigontiġ</i> (“ninety”) +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>hundred</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hundaradą</i> (“hundred”), from <i>&#42;hundą</i> + <i>&#42;radą</i> (“count”), a neuter variant of <i>&#42;radō</i> (“row, line, series”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hundred</i>, Old Saxon <i>hunderod</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;hundert</i>, Old High German <i>hundert</i>, Old Norse <i>hundrað</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>centurion</li></ol><p>From <i>hundred</i> (“hundred”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sebuntēhundą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seventyfold</li></ol><p>From <i>hundseofontiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-feald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventieth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seventieth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seventy year(s) old</li></ol><p><i>hundseofontiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>hundred</li></ol><p>From the prefix <i>hund-</i> +&lrm; <i>*tēontiġ</i>, a combination of an archaic form of <i>tīen</i> (“ten”) and <i>-tiġ</i> (“-ty”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hundtēontiġoþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hundredfold</li></ol><p>From <i>hundtēontiġ</i> (“hundred”) +&lrm; <i>-feald</i> (“-fold”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>hundredth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hundredth</li></ol><p>From <i>hundtēontiġ</i> (“hundred”) +&lrm; <i>-oþa</i> (“-th”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one hundred years old</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hundtēontiġ</i> (“one hundred”) +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>120; one hundred and twenty</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one hundred years old</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hund</i> (“one hundred”) +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horehound (herb)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Hune)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hūnas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hūnfriþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Hūnfriþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hūnafriþu</i>, equivalent to <i>hūn</i> (“bear cub”) + <i>friþ</i> (“peace”). Cognate with English <i>Humphrey</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ungrie</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ungerland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ungrie</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>hūn</i> (“bear cub”) + <i>ġīsl</i> (“hostage”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunger</li><li>famine</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hungr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hungruz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hunger</i>, Old Saxon <i>hungar</i>, Old High German <i>hungar</i>, Old Norse <i>hungr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hungriġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hungry</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hungrug</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hungrugaz</i> (“hungry”); equivalent to <i>hungor</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>hūn</i> (“bear cub?”) +&lrm; <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honey</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hunag</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hunagą</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hunig</i>, Old Saxon <i>honeg</i>, and Old High German <i>honag</i>; also Old Norse <i>hunang</i>, from the alternative form <i>&#42;hunangą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>honied, honey-bearing</li></ol><p>From <i>huniġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honeycomb</li></ol><p>From <i>huniġ</i> +&lrm; <i>camb</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the taste or flavour of honey</li></ol><p>From <i>huniġ</i> +&lrm; <i>smæċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>privet</li><li>a plant honey can be sucked from</li></ol><p>The first element is <i>huniġ</i> (“honey”); the second element is from <i>sūcan</i> (“to suck”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>huniġsūce</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sweet as honey</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hunagaswōtuz</i>. Equivalent to <i>huniġ</i> (“honey”) +&lrm; <i>swēte</i> (“sweet”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>hūn</i> (“bear cub”) and <i>lāf</i> (“remains, legacy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>hūn</i> (“bear cub”) and <i>rǣd</i> (“counsel, advice”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>hūn</i> (“bear cub”) + <i>siġe</i> (“victory”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunter</li><li>spider</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;huntô</i>, from Pre-Germanic <i>*xundn-</i>. Equivalent to <i>huntian</i> (“to hunt”) +&lrm; <i>-a</i> (agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Huntingdon, England</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hunta</i> (“hunter”) +&lrm; <i>dūn</i> (“hill, mountain”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Huntingdonshire, England</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Huntandūn</i> (“Huntingdon”) +&lrm; <i>sċīr</i> (“shire”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>huntoþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hunt</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;huntōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>huntress</li></ol><p>From <i>huntan</i> +&lrm; <i>-iċġe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>huntress</li></ol><p>From <i>huntian</i> +&lrm; <i>-estre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>huntnoþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunting</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the act of hunting</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>huntian</i> (“to hunt”) +&lrm; <i>-noþ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>huntoþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunting, a hunt</li></ol><p>From <i>huntian</i> +&lrm; <i>-oþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunting</li></ol><p>From <i>huntian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hūnawald</i>, equivalent to Old English <i>hūn</i> (“bear cub”) + <i>wald</i> (“powerful, mighty”). Cognate with French <i>Hunaud</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>hūn</i> (“bear cub”) and <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwæþre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwamstān</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hyrnetu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>anyway, at any rate, in any case</li><li>certainly, at least, indeed</li><li>yet, however</li><li>especially</li></ol><p>Of obscure origin, perhaps related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwar</i> (“where”). Compare Swedish <i>huru</i> (“how”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>especially</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hūs</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hūs</i>, Old Saxon <i>hūs</i>, Old Dutch <i>hūs</i>, Old High German <i>hūs</i> and Old Norse <i>hús</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>master of the house</li></ol><p><i>hūs</i> +&lrm; <i>bonda</i>, a calque of Old Norse <i>húsbóndi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feminine form of <i>hūsbonda</i>; the mistress of a house</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burglary</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hūsabruki</i>; equivalent to <i>hūs</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>bryċe</i> (“breaking”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hūsbreke</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>burglarious, of or resembling a burglar</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>housefire</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hūsabruni</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hūsabruniz</i>. Equivalent to <i>hūs</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>bryne</i> (“fire”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hūsbonda</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insult; scorn; scoffing; mockery</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;husk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;huską</i>, <i>&#42;huskaz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hosk</i>, Old Dutch <i>hosk</i>, Old High German <i>hosc</i>, <i>hosk</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>member of a king's bodyguard</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>húskarl</i>, equivalent to <i>hūs</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>ċeorl</i> (“man”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ignominious; involving shame, scorn, or insult; unseemly</li></ol><p>From <i>husċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hūsl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a ceiling.</li></ol><p>From <i>hūs</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>heofon</i> (“ceiling”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give shelter, house</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hūsōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hūsōną</i>. Equivalent to <i>hūs</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>huizen</i>, German <i>hausen</i> and Norwegian Nynorsk <i>husa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small house</li></ol><p>From <i>hūs</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>-incel</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) the Eucharist</li><li>(religion,&#32;originally) offering, sacrifice</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hunsl</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hunslą</i> (“offering, sacrifice”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱwen-</i> (“holy”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>húsl</i> (“Eucharist”), Gothic <i>𐌷𐌿𐌽𐍃𐌻</i> (hunsl, “offering, sacrifice”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svętъ</i> (“holy, sacred”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paten</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>hūsl</i> (“Eucharist”) +&lrm; <i>disċ</i> (“plate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to housel, administer the Eucharist</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hunslōną</i> (“to sacrifice, offer”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱwen-</i> (“holy”)., equivalent to <i>hūsl</i> (“sacrifice, the Host”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>family, household</li><li>tribe, nation</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hūs</i> +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a household</li></ol><p>From <i>hūs</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the site of a building</li></ol><p>From <i>hūs</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>stede</i> (“place”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hūsl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hwæt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>husċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>husċlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hymlice</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hwæt</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hwæt</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hwæt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwæþre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plunder, booty</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hunþu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>who (interrogative)</li><li>anyone</li><li>someone</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwaʀ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwealf</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwealf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angle, corner</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwammaz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>hvammr</i> (“grassy hollow, vale”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwamstān</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cornerstone, corner stone, foundation <i>(figurative)</i></li></ol><p>From hwamm (“corner, angle”) + stan (“stone, rock”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>where from; whence</li><li>how (not the general meaning of "in which way", but meaning "from which source" or "from where")</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwananā</i>, with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-anē</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwǣr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>where</li></ol><p>From <i>hwǣr</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i> (from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ō</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwǣstrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>hwæt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>omen, augury</li><li>divination</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwatu</i> (“warning”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwatō</i> (“incitement”); compare Old Norse <i>hvǫt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roar</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwaþjaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kweth₂-</i> (“to bubble, foam”). Compare Gothic <i>to foam</i> (ƕaþjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vault, arch</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arched, vaulted, hollow, concave, bent</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwalbaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>walbe</i>, Old Norse <i>hvalf</i> (Icelandic <i>hólf</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwierfan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn on something (such as fate or a hinge), to revolve, to roll about</li><li>to wander, to move</li><li>to change</li><li>to wave</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwarbōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwarbōną</i> (“to wander around”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwæder</i></li></ol><p>Æ-raising alteration of <i>hwæder</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwǣġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>which</li><li>what</li><li>what kind of, what sort of</li><li>some, any</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>which one</li><li>anyone, anything; someone, something</li><li>who</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>what <i>(used in exclamations)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwilīkaz</i> ~ <i>&#42;hwalīkaz</i> (“what kind of”), a compound of <i>&#42;hwaz</i> and <i>&#42;-līkaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inflamed swelling; pustule</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>&#42;hwele</i> +&lrm; <i>-ca</i> (diminutive ending).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quality</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwalīkanassī</i>. Equivalent to <i>hwelċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>, literally "what-kind-ness". Perhaps originally a calque of Latin <i>quālitās</i>, which is formed in the same way.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a boil or sore</i>) to form pus; come to a head</li><li>(<i>of a wound or sore</i>) to blister; fester; ulcerate</li></ol><p>Unknown. Possibly derived from an unattested noun, Old English <i>&#42;hwele</i> (“boil, sore”), also of unknown origin. According to Pokorny, possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱewh₁-</i> (“to swell”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>puppy</li><li>cub, pup</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwelp</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give birth (<i>of some animals</i>)</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>hwelp</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Compare Middle English <i>chīlden</i> (already attested c. 1175 in the <i>Ormulum</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>a little, somewhat</li></ol><p>Likely a fossilized instrumental case form of hwōn</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwēol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwēol</i></li></ol><p>Apparently from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwehhwl</i>, alteration of <i>&#42;hwehwl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wheel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hweulō</i>, plural of <i>&#42;hwehwlą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷékʷléh₂</i>, plural of <i>&#42;kʷekʷlóm</i>, <i>&#42;kʷékʷlos</i>, derived from the root <i>&#42;kʷel-</i> (“going around”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hwēl</i>, Old Saxon <i>hwiol</i>, and Old Norse <i>hvél</i>. Extra-Germanic cognates include Ancient Greek <i>κύκλος</i> (kúklos).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something that turns</li><li>whorl</li><li>joint</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn, change</li><li>to depart, go, proceed</li><li>to travel, roam, go about, wander, move about</li><li>to return</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwerban</i> (“to turn”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hwerva</i>, Old Saxon <i>hwervan</i>, Old High German <i>werban</i>, Old Norse <i>hverfa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hweorfeþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Early Old English</i>) alternative form of <i>hwēol</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwehwlą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷékʷlos</i>, <i>&#42;kʷekʷlóm</i>, derived from the root <i>&#42;kʷel-</i> (“going around”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwēol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwiþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kettle, pot, basin, cooking vessel</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwǣr</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwer</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hweraz</i> (“kettle”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷer-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwierfan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwierfel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wandering</li><li>error</li></ol><p>From an unattested verb <i>&#42;hwerflian</i> + <i>-ung</i>. Related, ultimately, to modern English <i>whirling</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>somewhere</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwargin</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hwergin</i>, Old High German <i>hwergin</i>, <i>wergin</i>, Old Norse <i>hvargi</i>, <i>hvergi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cucumber</li></ol><p>Unknown. Possibly related to Latin <i>cucurbita</i> and Sanskrit <i>कर्कटी</i> (karkaṭī), whence Hindi <i>ककड़ी</i> (kakṛī), all in the sense "cucumber".</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwæt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hweteþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sharpening stone, whetstone</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwattjastain</i>. Equivalent to <i>hwettan</i> (“to sharpen”) +&lrm; <i>stān</i> (“stone”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sharpen</li><li>to incite, encourage, whet</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwattjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwatjaną</i> (“to incite, sharpen”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷeh₁d-</i> (“sharp”).</p><p>Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>wetten</i> (Dutch <i>wetten</i>), Old High German <i>wezzan</i> (German <i>wetzen</i>), Old Norse <i>hvetja</i> (Swedish <i>vässa</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwæþre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwæþre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwȳ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the people of Hwicce (a historical Anglo-Saxon kingdom)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chest; box</li></ol><p>From a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kewH-</i> (“to cover”), from which also derives English <i>hide</i> (noun and verb) and <i>sky</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Hwicce)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hwicce (an Anglo-Saxon kingdom)</li><li>alternative form of <i>Hwiċċas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wychwood</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>Hwicce</i> +&lrm; <i>wudu</i> (“wood, forest”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwiċċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwæder</i></li></ol><p>Alteration of <i>hwæder</i>, probably by analogy with <i>hider</i> and <i>þider</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn, revolve, walk, go, roam, or move about</li><li>(<i>transitive and intransitive</i>) to turn or change</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with genitive) to exchange or barter</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwarbijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circuit, circumference; perimeter, exterior</li><li>ring, crown</li><li>whirl, swirl</li><li>whirlpool, eddy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwirbil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwirbilaz</i>, equivalent to <i>hweorfan</i> +&lrm; <i>-el</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whirlpool, eddy</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from a derivative of <i>hwierfan</i> (“to turn, revolve”) +&lrm; <i>pōl</i>, or possibly an alteration of <i>&#42;hwierfelpōl</i>, from <i>hwierfel</i> (“whirl, whirlpool, eddy”) +&lrm; <i>pōl</i> (“pool”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwȳ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>while, period of time</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwīlu</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hwīle</i>, Old Saxon <i>hwīla</i>, Old High German <i>wīla</i>, Old Norse <i>hvíla</i>, Gothic <i>𐍈𐌴𐌹𐌻𐌰</i> (ƕeila).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late West Saxon</i>) alternative form of <i>hwelċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwelcnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwīlwende</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwīlum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwilpe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) a kind of bird</li></ol><p>Cognate with Scots <i>whaup</i> and Dutch <i>wulp</i>; possibly related to English <i>yarwhelp</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sometimes</li><li>once (at some point in the past)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwīlum</i>, dative plural of <i>hwīl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of duration</i>) temporary, not lasting long, not permanent</li><li>(<i>of a person</i>) concerned with the things of time</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) temporal, rather than spiritual</li></ol><p>From <i>hwīl</i> +&lrm; <i>-wende</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a whistling or whizzing sound</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwīnaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whine</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwinisōn</i> (“to whine”), equivalent to <i>hwīnan</i> +&lrm; <i>-sian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwierfan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwierfel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squeaking</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whisper</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwisprōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pipe or flute</li></ol><p>From <i>hwistlian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piper, fluter, flautist</li></ol><p>From <i>hwistle</i> (“hollow reed”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i> (“-er”, suffix forming masculine agent nouns).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whistle</li><li>to hiss</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) to lisp</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwistlōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwistlōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whistling, hissing</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hwistlan</i> (“to whistle”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>white</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwīt</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwītaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From the adjective <i>hwīt</i> (“white”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From the adjective <i>hwīt</i> (“white”). Feminine equivalent of <i>Hwīta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loaf of white bread</li></ol><p>From <i>hwīt</i> (“white”) +&lrm; <i>hlāf</i> (“bread, loaf”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blanket</li><li>cloak</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwītil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwītilaz</i> (“linen, sheet”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>hvítill</i> (“white bed cover”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Whithorn (a small town or royal burgh in Wigtownshire, Dumfries and Galloway council area, Scotland)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whiten</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwītnōną</i>. Equivalent to <i>hwīt</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whiteness</li></ol><p>From <i>hwīt</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwītnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>hwīt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breeze</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwæþre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwamm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a few, a little</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>few, little</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>slightly, a little, somewhat</li><li>why</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwanon</i> (“whence”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>little, slight, small</li></ol><p><i>hwōn</i> (“little”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for a little while</li><li>slightly, a little</li></ol><p><i>hwōnlīċ</i> (“little”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>hwōnlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>hwōnlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>when</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwannā</i>, extension of <i>&#42;hwan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>wanna</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to threaten</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwōpaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cough</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwōsaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cough</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwōstō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwōstô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>why</li></ol><p>From a presumed earlier *<i>hwīe</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwiu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwī</i> (“with what”), to which the more common instrumental ending *<i>-ō</i> had been added. Cognate with Old High German <i>hwiu</i> (instrumental case of <i>hwaz</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hwiċċas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late West Saxon</i>) alternative form of <i>hwelċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwelca</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwylċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwelcnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwierfan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwierfel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>revolution, turning, circuit, orbit</li><li>movement, motion, course</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>hweorfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwistlian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>where (to what place), whither</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwadrê</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐍈𐌰𐌳𐍂𐌴</i> (ƕadrē).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>somewhere (to some place)</li></ol><p><i>hwæder</i> +&lrm; <i>-hwugu</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whey</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwajją</i>, perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷey-</i> (“to pile up, build”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwǣġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whale</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwal</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwalaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kʷálos</i> (“large fish, sheatfish”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hwal</i>, Old High German <i>wal</i>, Old Norse <i>hvalr</i>, Latin <i>squalus</i> (“shark”). Perhaps also related to Finnish <i>kala</i>, from Proto-Uralic <i>&#42;kala</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whaler</li></ol><p>From <i>hwæl</i> (“whale”) +&lrm; <i>hunta</i> (“hunter”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whaling</li><li>an instance of whaling, a whale hunt</li></ol><p>From <i>hwæl</i> (“whale”) +&lrm; <i>huntoþ</i> (“hunting”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the ocean</li></ol><p>Kenning of <i>hwæl</i> (“whale”) +&lrm; <i>weġ</i> (“way”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwone</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwonne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>where</li><li>anywhere, somewhere</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwār</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwar</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwierfan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>somewhere</li></ol><p><i>hwǣr</i> +&lrm; <i>-hwugu</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sharp, keen</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwes</i>, genitive case of <i>&#42;hwaz</i> (“who, what”).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwassaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷh₁dstós</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>hvass</i> (“sharp, keen”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whisper, mutter, murmur</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>what</li><li>why</li><li>what kind [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>how many or how much [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>anything, something</li><li>who (in questions and implied questions about <i>who</i> someone is)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>indeed, how (exclamative), what (exclamative)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quick</li><li>active, vigorous</li><li>bold, brave</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwat</i>. Cognates include Old Frisian <i>hwet</i>, Old Saxon <i>hwat</i>, Old Dutch <i>wat</i>, Old High German <i>waz</i>, Old Norse <i>hvat</i>. The Indo-European root also led to Latin <i>quod</i>, Lithuanian <i>kàd</i> (“that”), and Irish <i>cad</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwat</i> (“quick, sharp, brave”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwataz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷh₁dós</i>. Cognate with Icelandic <i>hvatur</i> and Norwegian Bokmål <i>kvat</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>what if</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>hwæt</i> (“quick, active”) + <i>beorht</i> (“bright”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wheat</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwaitī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) wheat</li></ol><p><i>hwǣte</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwæthwugu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwæthwugu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwæthwugu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwæthwugu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwæthwugu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>something</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>somewhat</li></ol><p><i>hwæt</i> +&lrm; <i>-hwugu</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwæthwugu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwæthwugu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>hwæt</i> (“quick, active”) + <i>mōd</i> (“spirit, courage, pride, zeal”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>hwæt</i> (“what, anything”) + <i>rǣd</i> (“counsel, advice”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>who or what (of two)</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>whether</li><li>used to introduce a question</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwaþar</i>, see also Old High German <i>wedar</i>, Old Norse <i>hvaðarr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwæþre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>yet, however, nevertheless</li></ol><p>From <i>hwæþer</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwēne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwǣr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīe</i> (“they”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think</li><li>to think about, to think of, to consider</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;huggjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hugjaną</i> (“think, consider”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>huggian</i>, Old High German <i>huggen</i>, Old Norse <i>hyggja</i>, Gothic <i>𐌷𐌿𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hugjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hyċġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hyċġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skin, hide</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hūdi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hūdiz</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hūd</i>, Old Saxon <i>hūt</i>, Old High German <i>hūt</i>, Old Norse <i>húð</i>; and with Latin <i>cutis</i>, Ancient Greek <i>κύτος</i> (kútos).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hide or conceal</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to hide</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;huʀdijan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hēda</i>, Middle Dutch <i>huden</i>, Middle High German <i>hürten</i>. Also related to Welsh <i>cudd</i>, Latin <i>custōs</i>, and Ancient Greek <i>κεύθω</i> (keúthō). Equivalent to <i>hord</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hiding place, place of concealment</li><li>a cavern</li></ol><p>From <i>hȳdan</i> (“to hide”) +&lrm; <i>-els</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hȳdeþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of (animal) hide; leathern</li><li>alternative form of <i>hyġdiġ</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hūdig</i>, equivalent to <i>hȳd</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beehive</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hūfi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hyġebeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li><li>Hygd, the wife of Hyġelāc in the poem <i>Beowulf</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disposed, minded</li><li>careful, considerate</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) thought, mind, mood, desire, inclination</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hugi</i>, cognate with Old Saxon <i>hugi</i>, Old High German <i>hugu</i>, <i>hugi</i>, Old Norse <i>hugr</i>, Modern Norwegian <i>hug</i>, Modern Swedish <i>håg</i>, Gothic <i>𐌷𐌿𐌲𐍃</i> (hugs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Hugiberht</i>, equivalent to <i>hyġe</i> (“thought, mind, mood, desire”) + <i>beorht</i> (“bright”). Cognate with Old High German <i>&#42;Hugibert</i>/Old High German <i>&#42;Hugiperht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hyġebeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hyġebeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>hyġe</i> (“thought, mind, desire”) + <i>friþ</i> (“peace”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sad in mind, mournful, sorrowful</li></ol><p>From <i>hyġe</i> (“mind”) +&lrm; <i>ġeōmor</i> (“sad”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hyġeġeōmor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slow, sluggish</li></ol><p>Analyzable as <i>hyge</i> (“mind, thought”) + <i>gælsa</i> (“excess, wantonness”), but compare <i>gælan</i> (“to delay”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>hyġe</i> (“thought, mind, mood, desire”) + <i>lāc</i> (“offering”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>hyġe</i> (“thought, mind, desire”) + <i>mund</i> (“protector”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>hyġe</i> (“thought, mind, desire”) + <i>rǣd</i> (“counsel, advice”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) strong-minded, magnanimous</li></ol><p><i>hyġe</i> +&lrm; <i>rōf</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hyġeweald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>hyġe</i> (“thought”) +&lrm; <i>weald</i> (“power”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>hyġe</i> (“thought, mind, desire”) + <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>hyġe</i> (“thought, mind, desire”) + <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strength of heart or mind</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hyġe</i> +&lrm; <i>þrym</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hysċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hope</li><li>joy, exultation</li><li>expectation</li></ol><p>Unknown. Perhaps from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;huhtiz</i>, from <i>&#42;hugiz</i> (“thought; mind”). Compare Middle High German <i>huht</i> (“thought; mind; memory”), Old English <i>hyġd</i> (“meaning; thought; remembrance”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bend, turn, winding</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) a tortuous way</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hulhik</i>, variant of <i>&#42;holhuk</i> (“hollow, cavity”). Alternatively, perhaps equivalent to <i>hylu</i> +&lrm; <i>-uc</i>. Compare dialectal Norwegian <i>hylke</i> (“wooden barrel”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grace, favor, protection</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hulþǭ</i>, feminine form of <i>&#42;hulþô</i> (“trustee, friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hieldan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hyldu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fidelity</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>hyldu</i> +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kindness, affection, goodwill</li><li>favor, grace</li><li>loyalty, devotion</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hulþī</i>; equivalent to <i>hold</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>huldi</i>, Old High German <i>huldī</i> (German <i>Huld</i>), Dutch <i>hulde</i>, Old Norse <i>hylli</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hielfe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hill</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hulliz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hulljan</i>. See also Old High German <i>hullen</i>, Old Norse <i>hylja</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pennyroyal</li></ol><p>From <i>hyll</i> (“hill”) +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i> (“plant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hielt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>him</i>: (to) him/it/them</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hymlice</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hops, <i>Humulus lupulus</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;humilō</i> (“hops”), of uncertain ultimate origin, but assumed to be of Finnic/Uralic origin, as the Finno-Ugric tribes were the first to use hops for beer.</p><p>Related to Old Norse <i>humli</i> (“hops”), Medieval Latin <i>humlo</i>, <i>humulus</i> (“hops”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xъmèľь</i> (“hops”). Possibly influenced by Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;huppô</i> (“hops”), for which see also French <i>houblon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) hemlock</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) bryony</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) convolvulus</li></ol><p>Unknown. Perhaps from a diminutive or derivative of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hamirā</i> (“hellebore”), with which Liberman compares Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čemerъ</i> (“false hellebore”), also used to describe venom or poison caused by this plant, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kemer-</i>. The closest Germanic cognates are German Low German <i>Hemer</i>, <i>Hemern</i> and German <i>Hemere</i> (“hellebore”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>hymlic</i></li></ol><p>Unknown. Liberman compares Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čemerъ</i> (“false hellebore”), also used to describe venom or poison caused by this plant, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kemer-</i>. The closest Germanic cognate is German Low German <i>Hemer</i>, <i>Hemern</i> and German <i>Hemere</i> (“hellebore”). More at <i>hymlic</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hind</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) A group of one hundred men (comprised of ten tithings).</li></ol><p>From <i>hund</i> (“hundred”) +&lrm; <i>-en</i> (feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hine</i>: him</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be hungry (+ genitive = <i>for</i> something) (often impersonal, with dative or accusative of person)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hungrijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hungrijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hungrian</i>, Old High German <i>hungiren</i>, Gothic <i>𐌷𐌿𐌲𐌲𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (huggrjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hyngran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīenþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīenþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hip</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hupi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hire, wages</li><li>interest, usury</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hūʀiju</i> (“hire”), from the verb <i>&#42;hūʀijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hūzijaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kūs-</i> (“price, hire”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hēre</i> (“hire”), Old Saxon <i>hūria</i> (“lease, rent”), Middle Dutch <i>hūre</i> (“lease, rent”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>heora</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīeran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heorcnian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hierdan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hierde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a frame made of intertwined twigs or bars; hurdle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hurdil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hurdilaz</i>, diminutive of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hurdiz</i> ( &gt; Old English <i>&#42;hyrd</i> &gt; Middle English <i>hurde</i>), equivalent to <i>&#42;hyrd</i> +&lrm; <i>-el</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hierdelīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hyrdemann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herdsman, shepherd</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>monetary</i>) interest</li></ol><p>From <i>hȳr</i> (“hire”) +&lrm; <i>borg</i> (“security, pledge, loan”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to imitate, emulate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hurjan</i>, <i>&#42;harjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hurjaną</i>, <i>&#42;harjaną</i>. Related to Old High German <i>antharōn</i>, <i>anterōn</i>, <i>antrōn</i> (“to imitate”), Old Norse <i>herma</i> (“to report, tell, imitate”), Old High German <i>anthara</i>, <i>antera</i> (“imitator, actress”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hireling, one who works for hire</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hūʀijuling</i>. Equivalent to <i>hȳr</i> +&lrm; <i>-ling</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hȳrmann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who works for hire; hireling</li><li>mercenary</li></ol><p>From <i>hȳr</i> +&lrm; <i>mann</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corner</li><li>angle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hurnijā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hurnijǭ</i> (“horn, corner, angle”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱerh₂-</i> (“horn”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>herne</i> (“horn, corner, angle”), Old Norse <i>hyrna</i>. More at <i>horn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hornen, made of horn</li></ol><p><i>horn</i> (“horn”) +&lrm; <i>-en</i>, with umlaut of the vowel in <i>horn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hyrnetu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hornet</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hurnutu</i> (“hornet”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heorre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go on horseback</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;hyrs</i> (“horse”, suggested by derivatives and placenames) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hurst, hillock, eminence, height, wood, wooded eminence</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) ornament, decoration, jewel; accoutrements, trappings, armor</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hursti</i> (“thicket, eyrie, scrub, bush”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrusti</i> (“armour, armament”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decorate, ornament</li><li>alternative form of <i>hierstan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hrustijan</i>.</p><p>See <i>hierstan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ġehīersum</i> (“obedient”)</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>hīersum</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hauʀisam</i>, equivalent to <i>hīeran</i> +&lrm; <i>-sum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīersumnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hiertan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>his</i> (“his, its”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to mock, deride, taunt, reproach</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;huskijan</i>, equivalent to <i>husċ</i> (“insult, scorn, scoffing, mockery”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i>. See also <i>hux</i>, <i>hucs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) warrior</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) boy, young man</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;husjaz</i>. It is remarkable that the expected alteration between nom.-acc. sg. <i>*-Ce</i> and oblique <i>*-CC-</i> or <i>*-rġ</i>- in ja-stems with light root syllables has only survived in this word and <i>here</i> (“army”), when <i>hyse</i> only occurs as a prefix and in poetry. All other such words have leveled their paradigm in favor of the oblique cases: <i>bedd</i> (“bed”), <i>cynn</i> (“kind”), <i>hryċġ</i> (“back”), <i>nebb</i> (“face”), <i>nett</i> (“net”), <i>pytt</i> (“pit”), <i>ribb</i> (“rib”), <i>seċġ</i> (“man,” poetic), <i>webb</i> (“web”), <i>weċġ</i> (“wedge”), <i>wiċġ</i> (“horse,” poetic). Of unknown derivation, possibly to be grouped here, are <i>bridd</i> (“baby bird”) and the poetic word <i>ġiedd</i> (“song, poem, saying”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>hyse</i> (“warrior, young man”) +&lrm; <i>berht</i> (“bright, shiny”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male child</li></ol><p><i>hyse</i> +&lrm; <i>ċild</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>hyse</i> (“warrior, young man”) and <i>nōþ</i> (“daring”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the manner of young men</li></ol><p>From <i>hyse</i> (“man”) +&lrm; <i>wīse</i> (“way (manner)”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ysope</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mock, taunt</li><li>to scorn, revile; insult</li><li>to reproach</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;huspijan</i>, from <i>&#42;husp</i>, <i>&#42;hosp</i>, variants of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;husk</i>, <i>&#42;hosk</i> (“mockery”). Equivalent to <i>hosp</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hīwrǣden</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a harbour or landing-place, a hithe</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hunþjō-</i> (“landing place”), which could have originally meant "place for transfer, transshipment," related to <i>&#42;hunþu</i> (“plunder”) and the strong verb <i>&#42;hinþaną</i> (“to reach for”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hūþ</i>, Old High German <i>-hude</i> (in place-names).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pirate ship</li></ol><p><i>hȳþ</i> (“harbor”) +&lrm; <i>sċip</i> (“ship”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hæfern</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hæfern</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hatch (door)</li><li>a gate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hakkju</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>haca</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hæċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crosier</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hakkā</i> (“hoe, crook, hook”). Akin to <i>hōc</i>, <i>haca</i>, and to Old Norse <i>hœkja</i> (“crutch”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exactor</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A drink made of vinegar and water.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Northumbrian</i>) alternative form of <i>hacele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yeast</li><li>sea</li><li>water</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;habbjō</i> (“yeast, barm”). Akin to Old High German <i>hevo</i> (“yeast”).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;habą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Old English</i>) (sheltered) inlet; harbour, port</li><li>Possession; ownership [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>Property, possessions.</li></ol><p>As this word is only attested beginning in the 11th century and displays /a/ even in Middle English dialects where Old English /æ/ develops into /ɛ/, it is likely borrowed from Old Norse <i>hǫfn</i>, though inheritance from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;habanu</i> cannot be ruled out.</p><p>Both potential etymons are from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;habnō</i>, <i>&#42;habanō</i>, itself from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kap-</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;habīniz</i>; equivalent to <i>habban</i> (“to have”) +&lrm; <i>-en</i> (abstract deverbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hæfen</i> (“harbour”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>he-goat; buck</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hafraz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kapro-</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>hafr</i>, Latin <i>caper</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a snipe, bittern</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crab</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from <i>hæf</i> (“water, sea”) +&lrm; <i>ærn</i> (“house, home, dwelling”), in reference to its shell.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Northumbrian</i>) alternative form of <i>hæfst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hæfst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hefiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hæftnung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a handle, the haft of a weapon</li><li>a captive, a prisoner or slave</li><li>captivity, bondage, imprisonment</li><li>a bond or fetter</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haftī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haftiją</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kap-</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Middle Low German <i>hecht</i> (Dutch <i>hecht</i>, <i>heft</i>), Old High German <i>hefti</i> (German <i>Heft</i>), Old Norse <i>hepti</i>. The Indo-European root is also the source of Latin <i>capere</i>, Old Irish <i>cacht</i> (Welsh <i>caeth</i> (“slave”), Breton <i>keaz</i> (“poor”)), Albanian <i>kap</i> (“grip”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xopītī-</i> (Old Church Slavonic <i>хапѭште</i> (xapjǫšte), Russian <i>ха́пать</i> (xápatʹ)), Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;kap-</i> (Lithuanian <i>kàpteleti</i>, Latvian <i>kàmpt</i> (“bite”)).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haft</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haftaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kh₂ptós</i> (“grabbed”). See Etymology 1.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seize, make captive, to imprison</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haftijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>hepta</i>, Old High German <i>heften</i>, Old Saxon <i>heftian</i>, Old Frisian <i>hefta</i>, Gothic <i>𐌷𐌰𐍆𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (haftjan). Ultimately derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kap-</i>; compare Old English <i>hæft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>captive</li></ol><p>From <i>hæft</i> +&lrm; <i>-ling</i>. Compare German <i>Häftling</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>custody, imprisonment</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fastening, securing</li><li>captivity, confinement, imprisonment</li></ol><p>From <i>hæftnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fence, enclosure</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hæġl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hail</li><li>the runic character ᚻ (/h/)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hagl</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haglaz</i>, probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kagʰlos</i> (“pebble”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) hailstorm</li></ol><p>From <i>hæġl</i> (“hail”) +&lrm; <i>faru</i> (“march”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hagosteald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hedge sparrow</li></ol><p>From <i>hæġ</i> (“fence”) +&lrm; <i>sugga</i> (“a kind of bird”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hæġtesse</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>witch, hag, Fury</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hagatussjā</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>haghetisse</i>, Old High German <i>hagazussa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>haguþorn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>omen</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hāl</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hail</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hailaz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>heill</i> (Danish <i>held</i> (“luck”), Norwegian <i>hell</i>), Old High German <i>heil</i> (German <i>Heil</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hēla</i> (“heel”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hą̄hilō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heal</li><li>to cure</li><li>to save</li><li>to allay or remove anxiety</li><li>to hail</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hailijan</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>hēla</i>, Old Saxon <i>hēlian</i> (Low German <i>helen</i>), Dutch <i>helen</i>, Old High German <i>heilen</i> (German <i>heilen</i>), Old Norse <i>heila</i> (Danish <i>hele</i>, Swedish <i>hela</i>), Gothic <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hailjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hero</li><li>man</li><li>warrior</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haliþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haliþaz</i>. Compare cognates: Old Norse <i>halr</i> (“hero, person”), <i>hǫldr</i> (“free-born, prominent yeoman”), also German <i>Held</i> (“hero”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>healer</li><li>savior</li><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Hǣlend</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hailijand</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hēliand</i>, Old High German <i>heilant</i>. Equivalent to <i>hǣlan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Hælend)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jesus</li></ol><p>From <i>hǣlend</i> (“savior”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hēliand</i>, Old High German <i>heilant</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>healthy, salutary</li></ol><p>From <i>hǣlend</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>heilantlih</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greeting, salutation</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>hæleþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hæle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) "sons of heroes"</li></ol><p>From genitive plural of <i>hæleþ</i> + plural of <i>bearn</i>. Has a parallel in Old Saxon <i>helithō barn</i>, possibly stemming from a Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haliþō barnu</i>, with a possible analog in Old Norse <i>hǫlda synir</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hælftre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>halter</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;halftrijā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hælftre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inconsistent, fickle, unreliable</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>healing</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hailingō</i>, equivalent to <i>hǣlan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>heilunga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hǣlu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>divination, augury</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sound physical or spiritual health</li><li>salvation, a cure, healing</li><li>welfare, well-being, prosperity</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hailī</i>, equivalent to <i>hāl</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>health</li><li>healing, cure</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hailiþu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hailiþō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hailaz</i> (“whole, hale”). Equivalent to <i>hāl</i> +&lrm; <i>-þ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>heilida</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have sex with, to sleep with; to copulate</li><li>to marry</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haimijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sex (intercourse)</li><li>cohabitation, marriage</li></ol><p>A substantivised past participle of <i>hǣman</i>; compare <i>ǣled</i> for a similar formation.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a married man</li></ol><p>From <i>hǣmed</i> +&lrm; <i>ċeorl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sexual intercourse</li></ol><p>From <i>hǣmed</i> (“cohabitation; sexual intercourse”) +&lrm; <i>-lāc</i> (suffix denoting activity or process).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marriage, matrimony</li></ol><p>From <i>hǣmed</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a married woman</li></ol><p>From <i>hǣmed</i> +&lrm; <i>wīf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sexual intercourse; marriage.</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>hǣmed</i> (“sexual intercourse”) +&lrm; <i>þing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stone, lapidate</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>hān</i> (“boundary stone”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hainō</i> (“whetstone”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>henep</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>henn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slip, move accidentally</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hair</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hār</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hār</i>, Dutch <i>haar</i>, Old High German <i>hār</i> (German <i>Haar</i>), Old Norse <i>hár</i> (Swedish <i>hår</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sackcloth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of hair</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hārīn</i>, equivalent to <i>hǣr</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hedgehog</li></ol><p>First element perhaps <i>hǣren</i> ("of hair"). Compare <i>hattefagol</i>. This might, however, be somehow a corruption of <i>herinaciis</i> (variant of <i>erinaceus</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harvest</li><li>autumn, fall</li></ol><p>Apparently borrowed into West Saxon from Mercian; the expected West Saxon form would be <i>*hierfest</i>. From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;harbist</i>. A North Germanic variant <i>&#42;harbustaz</i> was the source of Old Norse <i>haust</i> (Swedish <i>höst</i>, Icelandic <i>haust</i>).</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>herfst</i> (West Frisian <i>hjerst</i>), Old Saxon <i>hervist</i> (Low German <i>harvst</i>), Dutch <i>herfst</i>, Old High German <i>herbist</i> (German <i>Herbst</i>). The Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek <i>καρπός</i> (karpós, “fruit”), Latin <i>carpō</i> (“pick, pluck”), Middle Irish <i>corrán</i> (“sickle”), Lithuanian <i>kirpti</i> (“cut”), Old Church Slavonic <i>чрьпати</i> (črĭpati) (Russian <i>серп</i> (serp, “sickle”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>autumnal; of harvest</li></ol><p>From <i>hærfest</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the ninth month of the year according to the Anglo-Saxon calendar</li><li>September</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;harbistamēnōþs</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>haustmánuðr</i> and Old High German <i>herbistmanoþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autumn</li><li>harvest, harvest time</li></ol><p>From <i>hærfest</i> +&lrm; <i>tīd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hærfest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herring</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hāring</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brain</li><li>wave, billow</li><li>flood, sea</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hirʀnī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hirzniją</i>, <i>&#42;hirsniz</i> (“brain”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱerh₂-s-on-</i>, from <i>&#42;ḱerh₂-</i> (reduced <i>&#42;ḱr̥h₂-</i>).</p><p>From earlier <i>hræn</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hraʀnu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hraznō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krosnéh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;kres-</i> (“spring, wave”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boat</li></ol><p><i>hærn</i> (“wave”) +&lrm; <i>flota</i> (“boat”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>herþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>command, order</li><li>obligatory office or service</li></ol><p>Related to <i>hatan</i> (“to command, order”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hæsl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nut</i>) hazelnut</li></ol><p>From <i>hæsel</i> (“hazel”) +&lrm; <i>hnutu</i> (“nut”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) asarabacca</li></ol><p>From <i>hæsel</i> (“hazel”) +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i> (“plant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hazel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hasl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>violence, fury</li><li>strife</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>violent</li><li>vehement</li></ol><p>Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haifsti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haifstiz</i> (“violence”). Akin to Old Saxon <i>hāste</i> (“haste”), Old Norse <i>heifst</i> (“hatred”), Gothic <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍆𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (haifsts, “quarrel”), Lithuanian <i>kaipstù</i>, <i>kaiptī</i> (“to be dizzy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hastings (a town in East Sussex, England), site of the Battle of Hastings</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;Hæsta</i> (“a personal name”) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“follower”) +&lrm; <i>-as</i> (nominative/accusative plural), literally “followers of Hæsta”, a term transferred to their settlement.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hætt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heat</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haitijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hǣtu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hæteru</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Old English</i>) alternative form of <i>hæteru</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hæteru</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clothing</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hatiʀu</i>, originally the fossilised plural of a z-stem noun; related to Middle High German <i>hāȥ</i>, <i>hæȥ</i>, <i>hæȥe</i> (“garment, clothing”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hāt</i> (“garment, clothing”) +&lrm; <i>-ru</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hǣtu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hat</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hattu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hattuz</i>, whence also Old Norse <i>hattr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scalp (remove the hair and skin from a person's head)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heat</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haitį̄</i>. Equivalent to <i>hāt</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hæfen</i> (“harbour”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blue, azure, purple</li><li>discolored</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hēwijaz</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heathen, pagan</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Heathenry, paganism</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wasteland, wilderness, heath</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haiþi</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>hede</i> (Dutch <i>hei</i>), Old High German <i>heida</i> (German <i>Heide</i>), Old Norse <i>heiðr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌸𐌹</i> (haiþi).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blueberry</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haiþibaʀi</i>. Cognate with German <i>Heidelbeere</i>. Equivalent to <i>hǣþ</i> (“heath”) +&lrm; <i>berġe</i> (“berry”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>fruit</i>) bilberry</li></ol><p>From <i>hǣþ</i> (“heath”) +&lrm; <i>beriġe</i> (“berry”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Hæþcyn, the son of Hreþel in the poem <i>Beowulf</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pagan, heathen</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a heathen</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>heden</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;haiþin</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haiþīnaz</i>, probably a specifically Christian use of the base of <i>&#42;haiþī</i> (“uncultivated land”).</p><p>Cognate to Old Frisian <i>hêthin</i>, <i>hêthen</i>, Old High German <i>heidan</i>, Old Norse <i>heiðinn</i>, Gothic <i>&#42;𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌸𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;haiþns, “gentile”) (attested as feminine <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌸𐌽𐍉</i> (haiþnō)). The Germanic word is primarily an adjective.</p><p>Perhaps influenced by Ancient Greek <i>ἔθνος</i> (éthnos, “nation, heathen”), via the loans from Greek in Coptic <i>ϩⲉⲑⲛⲟⲥ</i> (hethnos) and/or Old Armenian <i>հեթանոս</i> (hetʻanos). The stem-vowel was influenced by <i>haiþi</i> (“heath”). See also Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haiduz</i>, Old Norse <i>heiðr</i> (honour, bright, moor), Icelandic <i>heiður</i> (honour, bright weather).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hadinaz</i>, <i>&#42;hidanaz</i> (“skirt”) (compare <i>&#42;hōdaz</i> (“hood”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heathendom, paganism, the belief and practices of heathen people</li></ol><p>From <i>hǣþen</i> +&lrm; <i>-dōm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idolatry</li><li>an idol</li></ol><p><i>hǣþen</i> +&lrm; <i>ġield</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hǣþenġield</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hǣþenġield</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paganism, pagan beliefs</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) heathens</li></ol><p>From <i>hǣþen</i> (“heathen”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paganism, pagan beliefs</li></ol><p>From <i>hǣþen</i> (“heathen”) +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hēdan</i></li></ol><p>Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hōdijan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>hōdian</i> (>Middle Low German <i>hö̂den</i>), Old Dutch <i>huoden</i>, Old High German <i>huoten</i> (>German <i>hüten</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hēlan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>same, selfsame</li><li>alternative form of <i>ġe-</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;iʀ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;iz</i>.</p><p>Alternatively, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;íh₁</i> (emphatic particle).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġēa</i></li></ol></html>:/ˈja.kinθ/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hyacinth (gemstone)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>hyacinthus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jacob (father of Joseph in the Hebrew Bible)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰακώβ</i> (Iakṓb), from Biblical Hebrew <i>יַעֲקֹב</i> (yaʿaqóv, “he will/shall heel”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ēanbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Japheth, son of Noah</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>Iaphet</i>, <i>Iafeth</i>, from Koine Greek <i>Ἰάφεθ</i> (Iápheth), from Hebrew <i>יֶפֶת</i> (yépheth)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Jair</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Iaīrus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰάϊρος</i> (Iáïros), derived from Biblical Hebrew <i>יָאִיר</i> (Ya'ir).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ēanbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>January</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Iānuārius</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Northumbrian</i>) alternative form of <i>Iāirus</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>I</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ik</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ik</i>, unstressed form of <i>&#42;ek</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;eǵh₂óm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īeċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Icel of Mercia</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Icel</li></ol><p>From <i>Icel</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īeċend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Ida of Bernicia</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>empty, void; bereft</li><li>worthless, useless, vain</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idleness, vanity, futility, frivolity</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;īdalaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>īdel</i>, Old Saxon <i>īdal</i>, Dutch <i>ijdel</i>, Old High German <i>ītal</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vainglory</li></ol><p>From <i>īdel</i> (“empty, worthless”) +&lrm; <i>ġielp</i> (“boast”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>empty-handed</li></ol><p><i>īdel</i> +&lrm; <i>-hende</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>without cause</li><li>in vain</li></ol><p><i>īdel</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idleness, vanity, frivolity</li><li>uselessness, futility</li><li>emptiness</li></ol><p>From <i>īdel</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>īdel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) woman, lady; queen</li><li>virgin, young lady</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;idisi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;idisiz</i> (“woman”), potentially from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂idʰ-és-</i> (“fire, flame, burning”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>idis</i> and Old High German <i>itis</i>. According to Jacob Grimm it is also cognate with Old Norse <i>dís</i> ("goddess"), but this is heavily debated.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Ida</li></ol><p>From <i>Ida</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on the same day</li></ol><p>From <i>ī-</i> +&lrm; <i>dæġes</i>.</p></html>:/je/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġe-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to increase or enlarge</li><li>to add</li><li>to endow</li><li>to impregnate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aukijan</i> (“to increase”), equivalent to <i>ēaca</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>. Related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aukaną</i> (“to increase”) and Latin <i>augeō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a promoter, producer; father, progenitor</li><li>one who adds to, increases, or augments</li></ol><p>From <i>īeċan</i> (“to eke, increase”) +&lrm; <i>-end</i> (agent suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>island</li><li>dry land in a marsh</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;auwju</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;awjō</i>, originally a substantive adjective of <i>&#42;ahwō</i> (“river”) ( &gt; Old English <i>ēa</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ekʷeh₂</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old High German <i>ouwa</i> (German <i>Aue</i> (“meadow”)), Middle Dutch <i>ouwe</i>, Old Norse <i>ey</i> (Swedish <i>ö</i>). More distantly related to Latin <i>aqua</i> (“water”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>island</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;auwjuland</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;awjōlandą</i>, equivalent to <i>īeġ</i> (“island”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>alond</i>, <i>āland</i>, <i>eiland</i>, Old Norse <i>eyland</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a waterstream, current</li><li>river</li><li>sea</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;auwjustraum</i>, equivalent to <i>īeġ</i> (“island”) +&lrm; <i>strēam</i> (“current; stream”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ieldu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) "sons of men"; mankind</li></ol><p>From genitive plural of <i>ielde</i> + plural of <i>bearn</i>. Has parallels in Old Saxon <i>eldibarn</i> and Old Norse <i>alda bǫrn</i>, possibly stemming from a Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aldijǫ̂ barnō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put off, delay, prolong; hesitate, tarry; postpone, procrastinate</li><li>to delay the notice of</li><li>to dissimulate, connive at</li><li>to decay (with eld)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aldijaną</i>, from <i>&#42;aldaz</i> (“old”). Cognate with Old High German <i>alten</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) men</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aldī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aldīz</i> (“human beings, people, men”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂el-</i> (“to nourish, grow”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>eldī</i> (“human being, person, man”), Old Norse <i>aldir</i> (“men, mankind”). Related to Old English <i>eald</i> (“old”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a delay, a putting off, a deferring</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;alþin-</i>. Akin to <i>ieldan</i> (“to delay”), <i>eald</i> (“old”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>eald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>West Saxon</i>) older: comparative degree of <i>eald</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parent</li><li>ancestor</li><li>an elder more generally</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;alþizô</i>, whence also Old High German <i>altiro</i>, Old Norse <i>ellri</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>age</li><li>period</li><li>old age</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aldī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;alþį̄</i>. Equivalent to <i>eald</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elf</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;albiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disease caused by an elf</li><li>nightmare</li></ol><p><i>ielf</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>ādl</i> (“disease”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elfkind, the elven race</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;albikunją</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>alfakyn</i>. Equivalent to <i>ielf</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>cynn</i> (“race”). Compare <i>mancynn</i> (“humanity”), which is formed similarly.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female elf</li></ol><p>Feminine of <i>ielf</i>, equivalent to <i>ielf</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>. Cognate with or a parallel formation to Middle High German <i>elbinne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swan</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;albut</i> (“swan”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swan</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;albut</i> (“swan”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Insane, raving, frantic, as ascribed or related to an elf.</li></ol><p>From <i>ielf</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>, literally “elf-y”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>elvish, elven</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;albisk</i>. Cognate with Middle High German <i>elbisch</i>. Equivalent to <i>ielf</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gorgeous: as beautiful as an elf</li></ol><p><i>ielf</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>sċīene</i> (“beautiful”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elf magic (magic used by elves)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ielf</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;siden</i> (“magic”) (only attested in this compound).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A hiccup or heartburn ascribed to an elf.</li></ol><p><i>ielf</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>sogoþa</i> (“hiccup or heartburn”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ælfþone</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heir</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arbijô</i>, whence also Old High German <i>arbeo</i>, Old Norse <i>arfi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inherit</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arbijaną</i>, derived from <i>&#42;arbiją</i> (“inheritance,” whence Old English <i>ierfe</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inheritance</li><li>cattle, livestock</li><li>property</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;arbī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arbiją</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃erbʰ-</i> (“to change ownership”). Cognate with Old High German <i>erbi</i>, Old Norse <i>erfi</i>. Related to Old English <i>earm</i> (“poor”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>will, testament, document concerning inheritance</li></ol><p>From <i>ierfe</i> (“inheritance”) +&lrm; <i>ġewrit</i> (“writing”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inheritor, heir</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arbijanumô</i>, <i>&#42;arbijanumjô</i>, equivalent to <i>ierfe</i> (“inheritance”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;numa</i> (“taker”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>erfnoma</i>, Old High German <i>erbinomo</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐍂𐌱𐌹𐌽𐌿𐌼𐌾𐌰</i> (arbinumja). Compare also Old Norse <i>arftaki</i> and <i>arftakari</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inheritor, heir</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;arbijaward</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>erƀiward</i>. Equivalent to <i>ierfe</i> (“inheritance”) +&lrm; <i>weard</i> (“guardian”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cowardice</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;argiþō</i>, equivalent to <i>earg</i> +&lrm; <i>-þ</i>. Cognate to Old High German <i>argida</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ierġþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jericho</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Ierīchō</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰερῑχώ</i> (Ierīkhṓ), from Biblical Hebrew <i>יְרִיחוֹ</i> (Yərīḥō).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to afflict, vex</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>iermþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poverty</li><li>misery</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;armiþu</i>. Equivalent to <i>earm</i> (“poor”) +&lrm; <i>-þu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rinnan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anger, wrath, ire, rage</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>angry, wrathful, fierce</li><li>wandering, erring, perverse, depraved</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;irziją</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;irzijaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>irri</i> (German <i>irre</i> (“crazy, insane, mad”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rage, be angry with</li><li>to anger, make angry, enrage; provoke</li></ol><p>Akin to <i>ierre</i> (“angry, erring, astray”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>earthen, (<i>relational</i>) dirt</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;irþīnaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>eorþe</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>yrþland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farmer, husbandman, ploughman</li><li>a cuckoo or lapwing</li></ol><p><i>yrþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ling</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Jesse (father of King David)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Iessae</i>, cognate with English <i>Jesse</i>.</p></html>:/ˈje.troː/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jethro (father-in-law of Moses in the Hebrew Bible)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Iethrō</i>, from Biblical Hebrew <i>יִתְרוֹ</i> (yitró).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show</li><li>to appear</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;augijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;augijaną</i> (“to show”), a derivative of <i>&#42;augô</i> (“eye”), whence Old English <i>ēage</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ēaþe</i>: more easily</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy, to lay waste</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;auþijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>easy</li><li>empty, barren</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;auþī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;auþuz</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;auþī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;auþijaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>easily</li></ol><p><i>īeþe</i> (“up”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ēaþe</i>: most easily</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ease, easiness</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>īeþe</i> (“easy”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īfiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ivy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ibahs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>covered with ivy</li></ol><p>From or related to <i>īfiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-iht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īeġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small island; islet</li></ol><p><i>ī(e)ġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-oþ</i> (diminutive).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>iġeoþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>iġeoþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hedgehog; porcupine</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;igil</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īeġland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īeġland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>iġeoþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Northumbrian</i>) I</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>scribal abbreviation of <i>twi-</i></li></ol><p>From the Roman numeral II (2).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>iġil</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣlċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>the same</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ilīkaz</i> (“the same”, literally “it/he/she -like”), a compound of <i>&#42;iz</i> and <i>&#42;-līkaz</i> from the noun <i>&#42;līką</i> (“body”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ieldu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ieldan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the sole of the foot</li><li>callosity, corn</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ili</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ili</i> (“sole”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ielfete</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swan</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Illyria (a vaguely-defined geographic region in Southeast Europe in the western part of the Balkan Peninsula, approximately coincident with modern Albania)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Illyria, Illyricum (a much larger former province of the Roman Empire, covering the whole of Dalmatia and Pannonia, existing from 27 BC to c. 70 AD and reconstituted in 293 AD by Diocletian)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swarm of bees</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;imbī</i>, perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;a(m)pi-</i> (“stinging insect, bee”). Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>imme</i> (Dutch <i>imme</i>) and Old High German <i>imbi</i> (German <i>Imme</i>). The proposed Indo-European root would also be the source of Ancient Greek <i>ἐμπίς</i> (empís), Latin <i>apis</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>impe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an imp, graft, shoot of a plant.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to graft</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;impōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>inn</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;in</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;inn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>in, into</li><li>internal, inside</li><li>(<i>intensifying</i>) very</li></ol><p>From <i>in</i> (“in”). More at in.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;in-</i> (“strong”, adjective), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;indʰro-</i> (“swelling; strong”), from <i>&#42;oyd-</i> (“to swell”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to introduce</li></ol><p>From <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>belǣdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inspire, breathe upon; inbreathe</li><li>to inflate; blow up</li></ol><p>From <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>blāwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break into</li></ol><p>From <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>brecan</i>. Compare Old High German <i>inbrehhan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring in, present</li></ol><p>From <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>bringan</i>. Compare Old High German <i>inbringan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hall; vestibule</li></ol><p>From <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>burh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doubt, uncertainty, question, suspicion, fear</li><li>cause for complaint, offense, ill-will, grievance, grudge; quarrel</li><li>opportunity, occasion</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;inkô</i> (“ache, regret”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yenǵ-</i> (“illness”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>jinc</i> (“angered”), Old Norse <i>ekki</i> (“pain, grief”), Norwegian <i>ekkje</i> (“lack, pity”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>your (<i>referring to two people</i>); yours</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call upon, invoke</li></ol><p>From <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċīeġan</i> (“to call”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a calling; invocation</li></ol><p>From in- + <i>ċīeġan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a servant in the home; domestic servant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;inknehtaz</i>, equivalent to <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>cniht</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>inkneht</i> (“valet”), Old High German <i>inkneht</i> (“valet”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come in, enter</li></ol><p>From <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>cuman</i>. Compare Old High German <i>inkweman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physical&#32;or&#32;spiritual</i>) internal, inward, intimate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>in</i> +&lrm; <i>-cund</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>India</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the people of India</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Indea</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Indian</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) an Indian</li></ol><p>From <i>Indēas</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i> or adapted from Latin <i>indicus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>noble, courtly</li></ol><p>From <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>dryhten</i> (“lordly”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>indryhtu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) nobleness, honour, glory</li></ol><p>From <i>in-</i> (“in-”) +&lrm; <i>dryht</i> (“army”) +&lrm; <i>-u</i> (“-ness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Ine of Wessex; 7th/8th century King of Wessex.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dwell (+ preposition <i>in</i> or <i>on</i>, in)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to inhabit</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>eardian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>eardiend</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the right to judge one's own thief when taken within the jurisdiction, and the privilege consequent upon that jurisdiction</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>fangen</i> +&lrm; <i>þēof</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go in, enter</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;infaran</i>, equivalent to <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>faran</i>. Compare Old High German <i>infaran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an invasion, incursion, inroad, march into a country</li></ol><p>From <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>faru</i>. Related to <i>infare</i> (“entrance, ingress”), <i>infaran</i> (“to go into, enter”), and <i>inswogan</i> (“invade”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an attack made upon a person by one inhabiting the same dwelling; infighting</li></ol><p>From <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>feoht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enter, go in, enter in</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;infōrijan</i>, equivalent to <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>fēran</i> (“to go”). Cognate with Old High German <i>infuoren</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>infeoht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>infeoht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entrance, entry or entryway</li><li>right or permission of going in an entryway; ingress</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>færeld</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meadow, water meadow, ing</li></ol><p>Apparently borrowed from Old Norse <i>eng</i> or possibly inherited directly from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;angijō</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Ing)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from legend</li><li>the runic character ᛝ (/ŋɡ, ŋ/)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Inguz</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go in, enter</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ingān</i>. Equivalent to <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>gān</i>. Compare Old High German <i>ingān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entrance (place of entry or act of entering)</li><li>permission to enter: access, admission</li><li>a beginning, first step</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;inngangaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>ingong</i>, Old Saxon <i>ingang</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;ingang</i>, Old High German <i>ingang</i>, Old Norse <i>inngangr</i>. Equivalent to <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>gang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go in, enter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;innganganą</i>. Equivalent to <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>gangan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to indicate, point out; inform</li><li>to show, declare, proclaim, reveal</li><li>to publish, announce; appoint</li><li>to bewray, expose, betray</li></ol><p>From <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġefeallan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>civil war</li></ol><p>From <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġefeoht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consciousness, thought, thought, mind</li><li>sense, conscience, inwit, understanding</li><li>intent, intention, purpose, meaning, effect</li></ol><p>From <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġehyġd</i> (“mind”). Akin to <i>hyċġan</i> (“to think about, consider”), <i>hogian</i> (“to be concerned about, care for”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ingeld (legendary warrior of Germanic myth)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Ingeldaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour into</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġēotan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>civil war</li></ol><p><i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġewinn</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thought, thinking, cogitation</li><li>intent, mind, heart, conscience</li></ol><p>From <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeþanc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>a brother of Ine of Wessex</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>son of Ingild</li></ol><p>From <i>Ingild</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ingweald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>Ing</i> +&lrm; <i>weald</i> (“power”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Ingwô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>utterly loyal; loyal from the heart</li></ol><p>From <i>in-</i> (“very, thoroughly”) +&lrm; <i>hold</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to restore to the protection of the law, inlaw</li></ol><p>From <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>lagian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a native</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>in</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>native, indigenous</li></ol><p><i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>native; indigenous</li></ol><p>From <i>inland</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>. Cognate with German <i>inländisch</i>, Icelandic <i>innlenskur</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>inlīhtan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inner, inward, internal</li></ol><p>From <i>in</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>inlīk</i>, Old High German <i>inlīh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>inwardly, internally, thoroughly, heartily</li></ol><p>From <i>in</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>inlīhho</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to illuminate, make bright, cause to shine</li><li>to restore sight, give clear sight; to clear the mental fog, to enlighten</li><li>to give light to</li><li>to shine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;inliuhtijaną</i>, equivalent to <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>līhtan</i>, Related to Old High German <i>inliuhten</i> (“to enlighten, illuminate”), Gothic <i>𐌹𐌽𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌷𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (inliuhtjan, “to enlighten, illuminate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>inlīhtan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead in, bring in</li><li>to introduce</li><li>to conduct, lead up, bring forward</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;in</i> (“in, into”) + Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laidijaną</i> (“to lead, cause to go”), causative of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;līþaną</i> (“to go”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leit-</i>, <i>&#42;leith-</i> (“to leave, depart, die”). Equivalent to <i>in-</i> (“in, into”) +&lrm; <i>lǣdan</i> (“to lead”). Cognate with Old High German <i>inleiten</i> (“to introduce”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in (with allative direction)</li><li>inside (with allative direction)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inn</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;inn</i>.</p><p>Probably from <i>inne</i> (“in, inside”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>within</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>within</li></ol><p>A variant of <i>inne</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>domestically, at home</li></ol><p><i>innan</i> +&lrm; <i>bordes</i> (genitive of <i>bord</i>)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inward, internal</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) deep, thorough</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) earnest, sincere, genuine</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>innan</i> +&lrm; <i>-cund</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inward, internal, interior</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>innan</i> +&lrm; <i>-weard</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>innoþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>inside, in; indoors</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;innai</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inward, internal</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>inne</i> +&lrm; <i>-cund</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intestine</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>innermost</li></ol><p>From <i>inne</i> (“inside”) +&lrm; <i>-mest</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) inner, interior</li></ol><p>Comparative of the preposition <i>in</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>innri</i>, Old Dutch <i>innero</i>, Old High German <i>innaro</i>, Old Frisian <i>inra</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inward, internal, interior</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intestine, innard</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>inne</i> +&lrm; <i>-weard</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>innoþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get inside</li><li>to put in</li><li>to bring in, put up, lodge</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>inne</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>inne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>innoþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the inner part of the body that holds the intestines and bowels</li><li>the stomach, womb, or belly</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) the seat of feelings</li><li>the seat of hunger</li><li>a gut or entrail in and of itself</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>innian</i> +&lrm; <i>-oþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a weakness of the intestines</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>innoþ</i> (“inner part of the body”) +&lrm; <i>tȳdernes</i> (“frailty, weakness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>innera</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a putting or getting in</li><li>contents (what was put or gotten in)</li><li>lodging</li></ol><p>From <i>innian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>innera</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send in</li></ol><p>From <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>sendan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appoint, institute</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>settan</i>. From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;insattjan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narrative, account</li><li>argument</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;insahtiz</i> (“account, narrative, story”), equivalent to <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>seht</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐌹𐌽𐍃𐌰𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (insahts, “narrative, presentation, discussion”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immediately</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Northumbrian</i>) alternative form of <i>intinga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cause, reason</li><li>matter, affair, business</li><li>occasion, pretense</li></ol><p>From <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;tinga</i>, related to <i>ġetingan</i> (“to press, squeeze”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>into</li></ol><p><i>in</i> +&lrm; <i>tō</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>internal, inward, internal</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>within</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>in</i> +&lrm; <i>-weard</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inward, internal</li></ol><p><i>inweard</i> (“inward”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>inwardly</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indoor work</li><li>something made indoors</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>in-</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>weorc</i> (“work”), cf. <i>inne</i> (“indoors”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deceit, guile</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;inwidją</i> (“malice, wickedness”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>inwid</i> (“fraud”), Old High German <i>inwit</i>, Old Norse <i>ívið</i> (“deceit, evil”) (attested in compound <i>íviðgjarn</i>). Further root-related to Old Norse <i>íviðja</i> (“malicious female being(?)”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resident scribe, private secretary</li></ol><p>From <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrītere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inscription</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;inwrittan</i> (“to inscribe”) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>habitation, dwelling, residence</li></ol><p>From <i>in-</i> +&lrm; <i>wunung</i> (“residence, home, dwelling”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an internal or intestinal worm</li></ol><p><i>in</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>wyrm</i> (“worm”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gut, bowel</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Īne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onǣlan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Job (biblical character)</li><li>(<i>Roman mythology</i>) Jove, Jupiter</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Iōb</i>, Ancient Greek <i>Ἰώβ</i> (Iṓb), from Biblical Hebrew <i>אִיּוֹב</i> (ʾiyyōḇ).</p><p>From Latin <i>Iuppiter, Iovis</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Iofes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeoc</i> (“yoke”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Iofes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jupiter (Roman god)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Iovis</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>John the Evangelist</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>Iōhannēs</i>, variant of Latin <i>Iōannēs</i>, from Koine Greek <i>Ἰωάννης</i> (Iōánnēs), from Biblical Hebrew <i>יוֹחָנָן</i> (Yôḥānān).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>John the Baptist</li></ol><p>Calque of Latin <i>Iōannēs Baptista</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jaffa</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Jordan river in Asia</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Iordanēs</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰορδάνης</i> (Iordánēs), from Biblical Hebrew <i>יַרְדֵּן</i> (yardén, “Jordan (river)”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ierre</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ierre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) alternative form of <i>eorþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Joshua</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Iosue</i>, cognate with English <i>Joshua</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ēotas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēower</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ireland</li></ol><p>Literally "land of the Irish."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Īrland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Irish</li><li>Ireland</li></ol><p>Derived from Old Irish <i>Ériu</i> (“Ireland”), perhaps through Old Norse <i>Írar</i> (“the Irish”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īsern</i></li></ol><p>Syncopic form of <i>isern</i>. Syncope of <i>-s-</i> in words for iron is an areal feature shared with insular Celtic languages.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īsernsmiþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ierfe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Milky Way</li></ol><p>Named for mythical Germanic hero <i>Irung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ireland (an island and country in northwestern Europe)</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>Īras</i> (“the Irish”) and <i>land</i> (“land, country”). The first element could also be an unattested singular form <i>*Īr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rinnan</i></li></ol><p>Metathesised form of <i>rinnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>iersian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ierþling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ice</li><li>the runic character ᛁ (/i(ː)/)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;īs</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;īsą</i>. See there for more.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ist</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;isti</i> (a form of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wesaną</i> (“to be”)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ésti</i> (“is”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Isaac (Biblical character)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Isaāc</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰσαάκ</i> (Isaák), from Biblical Hebrew <i>יצחק</i> (Yiṣḥāq, literally “he laughs”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īsċeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) ice-cold</li></ol><p>From <i>īs</i> +&lrm; <i>ċeald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kingfisher</li></ol><p>From <i>īs</i> (“ice”) +&lrm; <i>earn</i> (“eagle”). Compare Dutch <i>ijsvogel</i>, German <i>Eisvogel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īsern</i> (“iron, steel”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ordeal by iron</li></ol><p>From <i>īsen</i> +&lrm; <i>ordāl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īsernsmiþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blacksmith</li></ol><p>From <i>īsen</i> (“iron”) +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“wright”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the metal iron</li><li>an instrument or tool made from iron, especially an iron sword</li><li>an iron shackle or fetter</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>consisting or made of iron</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;īsarn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;īsarną</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognates include Old Frisian <i>īsern</i> (West Frisian <i>izer</i>), Old Saxon <i>īsarn</i> (Low German <i>Iesen</i>), Old Dutch <i>&#42;īsarn</i> (Dutch <i>ijzer</i>), Old High German <i>īsarn</i> (German <i>Eisen</i>), Old Norse <i>járn</i> (Swedish <i>järn</i>), Gothic <i>𐌴𐌹𐍃𐌰𐍂𐌽</i> (eisarn), Old Irish <i>īärn</i> (Irish <i>iarann</i>, Welsh <i>haearn</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>iron gray, the color of iron</li></ol><p>From <i>īsern</i> (“iron”) +&lrm; <i>grǣġ</i> (“gray”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iron helmet</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>īsern</i> (“iron”) +&lrm; <i>helm</i> (“helmet”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an ironstone quarry</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>īsern</i> (“iron”) +&lrm; <i>ore</i> (“mine”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blacksmith</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;īsarnasmiþaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>&#42;īsernsmith</i>, Old Saxon <i>īsarnsmith</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;īsarnsmith</i>, Old High German <i>īsarnsmid</i>, Old Norse <i>járnsmiðr</i>. Equivalent to <i>īsern</i> (“iron”) +&lrm; <i>smiþ</i> (“smith”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>icy</li></ol><p>From <i>īs</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>icy-feathered</li></ol><p>From <i>īsiġ</i> (“icy”) +&lrm; <i>feþere</i> (“feathered”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>icy</li></ol><p>From <i>īs</i> +&lrm; <i>-iht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at the same time</li><li>at once; directly; immediately</li></ol><p>From <i>ī-</i> +&lrm; <i>sīþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-es</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Spain (a country in Southern Europe, including most of the Iberian peninsula)</li><li>the Iberian Peninsula</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Hispānia</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Spanish</li></ol><p>From <i>Ispania</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) of or relating to the Israelites (Biblical group)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Israēl</i> (“Israel”) +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Israel</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Israhel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īsern</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Italy (a country in Southern Europe)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Italia</i>. Doublet of <i>Eotol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeō</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeocian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeōdagas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Judas</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Iūdās</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰούδας</i> (Ioúdas), from Hebrew <i>יְהוּדָה</i> (y'hudá).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jew</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>iudaeus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Judea</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Iudaea</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Judea</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Iūdaea</i> + <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Jews</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>iudaeī</i> (“the Jews”), also the plural of <i>iudaeus</i> (“Jew”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Jewish</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a Jew</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>iūdaeicus</i>, or from <i>Iūdēas</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeoguþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeoguþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>December</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ġēol</i> (“yule”) +&lrm; <i>mōnaþ</i> (“month”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeong</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġeongling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ġingra</i> (“younger”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Iutas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jutes</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ġeōwine</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yew (tree)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;īwaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁eyHw-</i>, a suffixed form of the base <i>&#42;h₁eyH-</i> (“reddish, colourful”).</p><p>Cognate with Old High German <i>īwa</i> (German <i>Eibe</i>), Middle Low German <i>īwe</i>, Dutch <i>ijf</i>, Old Norse <i>ýr</i> (“bow, yew”). The Indo-European root is perhaps also the source of Old Irish <i>ibar</i> (Irish <i>iúr</i>), Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;eiwas</i> (Lithuanian <i>ievà</i>), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьva</i> (Russian <i>и́ва</i> (íva, “willow”)), Latin <i>ūva</i> (“bunch of grapes”)).</p><p>The West Germanic languages have a variant <i>&#42;īhwaz</i> with a guttural consonant, represented by Old English <i>ēoh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yew berry</li></ol><p><i>īw</i> (“yew”) +&lrm; <i>berġe</i> (“berry”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īeþelīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>calend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>cāsere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cemes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>cenep</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cenrēd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Centingas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cēnwealh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cyning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cyning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>citelung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Cherbourg (a town in Manche department, Normandy, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Northern French <i>&#42;Kieresburg</i>, <i>Chieresburg</i>, with <k> representing the pronunciation /k/ north of the Joret Line, in turn from Old Norse <i>kjarr</i> + <i>borg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Crīst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cyning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cyne-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cyne-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, alternative form of <i>Cyneburg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, alternative form of <i>Cyneburg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cynecynn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Cynegils</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cyneheard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cynecynn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cynelīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cynemǣresford</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cyneswīþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cyneswīþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cyning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cyning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>cynn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Cynrīc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kirtlington (a village in Oxfordshire, England).</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cæppe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scribal abbreviation of <i>calend</i></li></ol></html>:/lɑː/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>lo!, oh!, ah!</li><li><i>Enclitic particle used to emphasise interrogation, exclamation, entreaty, affirmation, negation</i></li></ol><p>Unknown. Probably onomatopoeic. Compare Polish <i>ła</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>play, sport</li><li>battle, strife</li><li>gift, offering, sacrifice, booty; message</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laik</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laikaz</i>, compare <i>&#42;laikaną</i>. Cognates include Old Norse <i>leikr</i> (whence Danish <i>leg</i> (“game”), Swedish <i>leka</i> (“to play”)), Gothic <i>𐌻𐌰𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (laiks, “dance”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move about or up and down, like rolling water or flames; to soar, to wave, to sway</li><li>to fight, contend; to fence (<i>with swords</i>)</li><li>to play; to play an instrument</li><li>to sing</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laikan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laikaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leyg-</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>leika</i>, Gothic <i>𐌻𐌰𐌹𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (laikan, “to spring”), Middle High German <i>leichen</i> (German <i>laichen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doctor (female)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lācnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-estre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to treat (in the medical sense)</li><li>to cure</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lākinōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lēkinōną</i>. Originally equivalent to <i>lǣċe</i> +&lrm; <i>-nian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>healing, cure, remedy, medicine</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>lācnian</i> (“to treat, cure”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pool, pond</li><li>expanse of water, lake</li><li>stream, watercourse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laku</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lakō</i> (“drainage, pool”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leǵ-</i> (“to leak”). Related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lakjaną</i> (“to water, wet, irrigate, drain”), causative of <i>&#42;lekaną</i> (“to leak, drain”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>laca</i> (in placenames, “lake, stream, brook”), Old Norse <i>lækr</i> (“slow flowing stream”), Old English <i>leċċan</i> (“to make wet, moisten”), Old Norse <i>leka</i> (“to drip, leak”). Maybe related to Old High German <i>lacha</i> (“pool, water collected in a ditch, swamp”), Middle Dutch <i>lāke</i> (“pond, lake, stream, brook”), Middle Low German <i>lāke</i> (“water pooled in a riverbed”), which could also be borrowed from <i>lacus</i> (“lake, basin, tank”). More at leak.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrying, bringing, leading</li><li>way, course</li><li>passage, watercourse</li><li>provision, sustenance</li><li>excuse <i>(+ genitive for something)</i></li><li>apology <i>(+ genitive for something)</i></li><li>defense or exoneration <i>(+ genitive against an accusation)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laidu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laidō</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>leita</i> (German <i>Leite</i>), Old Norse <i>leið</i>. Akin to <i>līþan</i> (“to go, travel, fare”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laidu</i>, formally identical with Etymology 1 above. Akin to Old Frisian <i>lēde</i>, <i>lāde</i> (“leading, reasoning”) found in Old Frisian <i>dēdlâde</i> (“oath of purification”), Old High German <i>leita</i> (“justification, excuse”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A byname.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to excuse or make an excuse <i>(+ genitive for something)</i></li><li>to apologize <i>(+ genitive for something)</i> <i>(generally reflexive)</i></li><li>to exonerate <i>(+ genitive of an accusation)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>lād</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a leader; guide</li></ol><p>From <i>lād</i> +&lrm; <i>mann</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leader</li><li>guide</li><li>general</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>lādþēow</i>, equivalent to <i>lād</i> (“leading”) +&lrm; <i>þēow</i> (“servant”). The original form appears on occasion. Compare Old Norse <i>leiðtogi</i> (“guide”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leadership</li><li>guidance</li></ol><p><i>lādtēow</i> +&lrm; <i>-dōm</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>excuse <i>(+ genitive for something)</i></li><li>apology <i>(+ genitive for something)</i></li><li>exculpation or defense <i>(+ genitive against an accusation)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>lādian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lādtēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remains, remainder, left</li><li>legacy</li><li>heirloom</li><li>widow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laibu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laibō</i>. More at lǣfan.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lāwerce</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wet, make wet, bathe (something) in a fluid or liquid</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pour</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to ladle out</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;labōn</i> (“to refresh, strengthen”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leopard</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make into law, ordain</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lagōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lagōną</i> (“to set in place, fix”), equivalent to <i>lagu</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>lagia</i> (“to fix”), Old Norse <i>laga</i> (“to prepare”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lagu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea, water, lake</li><li>the runic character ᛚ (/l/)</li><li>law</li><li>rule</li><li>right, legal privilege</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lagu</i> (“water, sea”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laguz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lókus</i> (“pond, pool”). Cognate with Latin <i>lacus</i> (“hollow, pond”), Old Irish <i>loch</i> (“lake, pond”), Ancient Greek <i>λάκκος</i> (lákkos, “waterhole, pond, pit”).</p><p>Borrowed (ca. 1000 <small>C.E.</small>) from Old Norse <i>lǫg</i> (“the things that are laid down, the laws”), originally a neuter plural but reanalysed as a feminine singular when it was borrowed into Old English. From the singular Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lagą</i> (“something laid”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;legʰ-</i>, the root of English <i>lie</i>, <i>lay</i>.</p><p>The Old Icelandic word means "something laid down or fixed", both in the literal sense of "layer, stratum" and in the figurative "agreed share", "fixed price", "partnership", etc. The plural had the collective sense of "[body of] law". The native Old English word replaced by the Old Norse loan was <i>ǣ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>skilled at seamanship, nautical</li></ol><p>From <i>lagu</i> +&lrm; <i>cræftiġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) water-way, sea</li></ol><p>From <i>lagu</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>lād</i> (“way”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) sea, river, water</li></ol><p><i>lagu</i> (“sea, water”) +&lrm; <i>strēam</i> (“stream”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ocean</li></ol><p><i>lagu</i> (“water”) +&lrm; <i>strǣt</i> (“road”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lawful</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>lagu</i> (“law”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>lawfully</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who is familiar with the law and is charged with declaring the law</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>lǫgmaðr</i>, equivalent to <i>lagu</i> (“law”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person, man”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>leahtor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clay, loam</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laimą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lame</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a lame person</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lam</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lamaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lamb</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lambaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>potter</li></ol><p>From <i>lām</i> (“clay”) +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“wright”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lǣn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land (dry portion of the Earth's surface)</li><li>a country</li><li>region within a country: district, province</li><li>the country, countryside</li><li>owned or tilled land, an estate</li><li>ground</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;land</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;landą</i>. See there for more.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nostalgia, homesickness</li></ol><p>From <i>land</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>ādl</i> (“disease”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>landowner</li><li>a native</li></ol><p>From <i>land</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>āgend</i> (“owner”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a husbandman, farmer</li><li>inhabitant, native</li></ol><p>From <i>land</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>bīġenġa</i> (“inhabitant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>land</i> (“land”) + <i>beorht</i> (“bright”). Cognate with English <i>Lambert</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Landbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a charter in which land is granted</li></ol><p><i>land</i> +&lrm; <i>bōc</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inhabitant, native</li><li>farmer</li><li>settlement, colony</li></ol><p>From <i>land</i> + <i>būende</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land-tax, tax on land</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>land</i> +&lrm; <i>feoh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>people of a country</li></ol><p><i>land</i> +&lrm; <i>folc</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>land</i> (“land”) + <i>friþ</i> (“peace”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) ruler of the land, king</li></ol><p>From <i>land</i> +&lrm; <i>fruma</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fortified place on land</li><li>a pass</li></ol><p>From <i>land</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>fæsten</i> (“fortified place”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boundary, limit</li></ol><p>From <i>land</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>ġemirċe</i> (“limit, boundary”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>border, boundary</li></ol><p>From <i>land</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>ġemǣre</i> (“border”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a land-based military force or army (as opposed to a naval force)</li></ol><p><i>land</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>here</i> (“army”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>landlord, lord of a manor</li></ol><p><i>land</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>hlāford</i> (“lord”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) muse associated with the countryside</li></ol><p>From <i>land</i> +&lrm; <i>ielf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>landless, not having land; without land</li></ol><p>From <i>land</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inhabitant or native of a country</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the people of a country</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>land</i> +&lrm; <i>lēod</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boundary marker to denote the extent of one's land; border-marker</li><li>landmark</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;landamarku</i>. Equivalent to <i>land</i> +&lrm; <i>mearc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a land-couch; grave</li></ol><p>From <i>land</i> +&lrm; <i>rest</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>landlord</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>land</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>rīċe</i> (“kingdom, authority, dominsion”) +&lrm; <i>-a</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a territory, region, estate</li></ol><p>From <i>land</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>rīċe</i> (“kingdom, territory”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the law of the land</li><li>the rights of rural districts</li><li>the rights and privileges belonging to:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a native of a country; or,</li><li>an owner of land</li></ol><li>that which is owed from land or estates to the sovereign authority</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>land</i> +&lrm; <i>riht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>landsċipe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a share, portion, or division of land</li></ol><p>From <i>land</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>sċearu</i> (“division”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a tract of land; region</li><li>landscape</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;landaskapi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;landaskapiz</i>. Equivalent to <i>land</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the custom of a country</li></ol><p>From <i>land</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>sidu</i> (“custom”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small plot or portion of land or ground</li></ol><p>From <i>land</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>splott</i> (“plot of land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) land, country</li></ol><p>From <i>land</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>stede</i> (“place”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colonist</li></ol><p>From <i>land</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>-sǣta</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) the people of a country</li></ol><p>From <i>land</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>-ware</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>landielf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lane, narrow path</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lanu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lanō</i> (“lane, passageway”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long</li><li>tall</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lang</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;langaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dlongʰos</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>long</i>, Old Saxon <i>lang</i>, Old High German <i>lang</i>, Old Norse <i>langr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌻𐌰𐌲𐌲𐍃</i> (laggs), and outside of Germanic, with Latin <i>longus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Good Friday</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Langeland, a Danish island located between the Great Belt and Bay of Kiel</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lombards</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Lombardic</li></ol><p><i>Langbeardas</i> (“the Lombards”) +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i> (“-ish”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lombardy</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>long, for a long time</li></ol><p><i>lang</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to get longer</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) (with accusative subject) to long or grieve</li><li>to summon, call</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;langōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;langōną</i> (“to grow long, seem long, yearn”). Equivalent to <i>lang</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at length</li><li>for a long time</li></ol><p>From <i>lang</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>langlīf</i> (“long-lived”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long-lived: living a long time</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;langalībijaz</i>, equivalent to <i>lang</i> +&lrm; <i>-līfe</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>langlībi</i> and Old Norse <i>langlífr</i> (Icelandic <i>langlífur</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>patient</li><li>long-suffering</li></ol><p><i>lang</i> (“long”) +&lrm; <i>-mōd</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>length</li></ol><p><i>lang</i> (“long”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>longing</li><li>discontent</li></ol><p><i>langian</i> (“to long”) +&lrm; <i>-oþ</i> (noun suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>longship (a type of naval vessel made by the Vikings)</li></ol><p>Calque of Old Norse <i>langskip</i>. By surface analysis, <i>lang</i> (“long”) +&lrm; <i>sċip</i> (“ship”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of time</i>) long, long-lasting</li><li>(<i>of actions</i>) wearisome, prolix, tedious, tardy, dilatory</li><li>(<i>of space relations</i>) lengthy, extended; outstretched</li><li>(<i>of someone</i>) long-suffering, patient</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;langasam</i>. Equivalent to <i>lang</i> +&lrm; <i>-sum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>langsum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tedious, too long</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>long, during or for a long time</li><li>patiently, with long-suffering</li></ol><p>From <i>langsum</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>length</li><li>patience, long-suffering</li></ol><p>From <i>langsum</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>langsumnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long-necked</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>lang</i> (“long”) +&lrm; <i>swēora</i> (“neck”) +&lrm; <i>-ed</i> (participial suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>granted for a long time</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>longing, desire</li><li>weariness, grief</li><li>lengthening, prolongation, delay</li></ol><p>From <i>langian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lane, street</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lanu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lanō</i> (“lane, passageway”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>lane</i>, <i>lone</i>, <i>lona</i>, Old Saxon <i>lana</i>, Old Norse <i>lǫn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lick, lap</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lappōną</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>lambō</i>, Ancient Greek <i>λάπτω</i> (láptō), Albanian <i>lap</i>, and Lithuanian <i>lapènti</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teaching, learning, education</li><li>lesson</li><li>teaching</li><li>doctrine</li><li>advice, counsel</li><li>school</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laiʀu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laizō</i>, from <i>&#42;laizijaną</i> (“to teach”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>lēra</i>, Dutch <i>leer</i>, Old High German <i>lēra</i> (German <i>Lehre</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a book for instruction or for learning</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) epistle</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lār</i> +&lrm; <i>bōc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lārbȳsn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>example, specimen</li></ol><p><i>lār</i> +&lrm; <i>bȳsn</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knowledge acquired by study; erudition</li><li>a discipline</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lār</i> +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) teaching, lesson, lore-speech</li></ol><p>From <i>lār</i> (“teaching, lesson”) +&lrm; <i>cwide</i> (“saying”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teacher</li></ol><p>From <i>lār</i> (“teaching; doctrine”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;ēow</i>, a word or suffix of uncertain origin and meaning, yet apparently found also in Old Saxon <i>lērio</i>, <i>lēreo</i> (“teacher, prophet”).</p><p>Some authorities believe the second element to be a corruption of Old English <i>þēow</i> (“servant; minister”), which shows up in later forms of the word, e.g. Old English <i>lārþēaw</i> (for <i>&#42;lārþēow</i>), Middle English <i>lorthew</i>, etc., yet this might be assimilation in retrospect caused by confusion or folk-etymology.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the office of a teacher; mastership</li><li>teaching, instruction, guidance</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lārēow</i> +&lrm; <i>-dōm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>after the manner of a teacher or master</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lārēow</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lārēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>school</li></ol><p>From <i>lār</i> (“learning”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of learning or instruction</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>&#42;lārlēas</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>, else <i>lār</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēast</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>under instruction, in training</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) learning</li><li>learned</li><li>doctrinal, scholarly</li><li>instructive, persuasive</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lār</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lārlēast</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lārsmiþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wise man</li><li>a counselor</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lār</i> +&lrm; <i>smiþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) discourse, sermon, homily, treatise</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lār</i> +&lrm; <i>spell</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ready to learn, docile</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lār</i> +&lrm; <i>-sum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lārēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lārēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a learned man</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lār</i> +&lrm; <i>wita</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lārēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>footstep, track</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laist</i>, along with the feminine variant <i>lǣst</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>leest</i> (Dutch <i>leest</i>), Old High German <i>leist</i> (German <i>Leist</i>), Old Norse <i>leistr</i> (“foot, sock”) (Swedish <i>läst</i>, Danish <i>læst</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>report, reputation</li></ol><p>From <i>lāst</i> (“track, path”) +&lrm; <i>word</i> (“word”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>slow(ly)</li><li>late</li></ol><p>Adverbial form of <i>læt</i>, composed with the suffix <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>late</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be slow</li><li>to linger, loiter</li><li>to delay</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>læt</i> (“late”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>late</i>; later</li><li>slowly</li></ol><p>Related to <i>late</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>læt</i>; latest</li><li>last</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>recently. lately</li><li>latest</li></ol><p><i>læt</i> +&lrm; <i>-ost</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leading rein</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lādtēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delay, hindrance</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laurel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laur</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bay tree, laurel, <i>Laurus nobilis</i></li></ol><p>From <i>laur</i> +&lrm; <i>bēam</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laurel berry</li></ol><p>From <i>laur</i> +&lrm; <i>beriġe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>laurberiġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bay tree, laurel, <i>Laurus nobilis</i></li></ol><p>From <i>laur</i> +&lrm; <i>trēow</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>laurbēam</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>laur</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lark</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laiwarikǭ</i> (“lark”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>laurtrēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lāwerce</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lāþlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loathsome, hateful, unpleasant, evil</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a person or thing that is evil, hated or unpleasant</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laiþ</i> (“loath, hateful”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abhor, hate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laiþattjan</i>; equivalent to <i>lāþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) hated assailant, hostile attacker, enemy</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to invite</li><li>to call</li><li>to loathe</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laþōną</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>lathia</i>, Old Saxon <i>lathōn</i>, Old High German <i>ladōn</i>, Old Norse <i>laða</i>, Gothic <i>𐌻𐌰𐌸𐍉𐌽</i> (laþōn). Equivalent to <i>&#42;laþu</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laiþōną</i>. Equivalent to <i>lāþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hateful, loathsome, disgusting, horrible, detestable, abominable</li></ol><p>From <i>lāþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laiþalīkaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>leidlīh</i>, Old Saxon <i>lēthlīk</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>lāþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>lāþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>invitation</li><li>calling</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laþungu</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>ladunga</i>. Equivalent to <i>laþian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>generally</i>) An allium; a plant of the genus <i>Allium</i>.</li><li>(<i>specifically</i>) A leek (<i>Allium ampeloprasum</i>) or garlic (<i>Allium sativum</i>).</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lauk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laukaz</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>lōk</i> (Low German <i>Look</i>), Dutch <i>look</i>, Old High German <i>louh</i> (German <i>Lauch</i>), Old Norse <i>laukr</i> (Danish <i>løg</i>, Swedish <i>lök</i>, Icelandic <i>laukur</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) lettuce (<i>Lactuca sativa</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herb garden, kitchen garden</li></ol><p>From <i>lēac</i> (“allium”) +&lrm; <i>tūn</i> (“enclosure, garden”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lead</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līeden</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leaf</li><li>page</li><li>permission</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laub</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laubą</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>lōf</i>, Old High German <i>loub</i>, Old Norse <i>lauf</i>, Gothic <i>𐌻𐌰𐌿𐍆𐍃</i> (laufs).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laubu</i>. Cognate with German <i>Laube</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>belief</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laubu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laubō</i> (compare Dutch <i>geloof</i> and German <i>Glaube</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>believing, faithful</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>permission</li></ol><p>From <i>lēaf</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) password</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caterpillar</li></ol><p>From <i>lēaf</i> (“leaf”) +&lrm; <i>wyrm</i> (“worm”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lye (a mixture of ashes and water), detergent</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laugu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laugō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clearing</li><li>meadow</li><li>alternative form of <i>lēag</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lauh</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lauhaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lówkos</i> (“clearing”), derived from <i>&#42;lewk-</i> (“bright”).</p><p>The use of the word meaning "meadow" is a later development of the word, possibly aided by confusion between <i>lēas</i> (plural of the original meaning of <i>lēah</i>) with <i>lǣs</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laugu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>leahtor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vice, sin, fault, crime</li><li>malady, disease, bodily defect</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lahtraz</i> (“vice”), a variant form of <i>&#42;lahtrą</i>, from the root of <i>&#42;lahaną</i> (“to blame, fault, reproach”). Compare Middle Dutch <i>lachter</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to charge with a crime</li><li>to revile</li><li>to reproach</li><li>to corrupt</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lēactric</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lēactūn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reward</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to blame, fault, reproach</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laun</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>lān</i>, Old Saxon <i>lōn</i>, Old High German <i>lōn</i>, Old Norse <i>laun</i>, and Gothic <i>𐌻𐌰𐌿𐌽</i> (laun). The Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek <i>λεία</i> (leía) (from *λαϝία), Latin <i>lucrum</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>ловъ</i> (lovŭ) (Russian <i>лов</i> (lov)), Old Irish <i>lóg</i>, Lithuanian <i>lãvinti</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lahan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>lahan</i>, Old High German <i>lahan</i>, Old Norse <i>lá</i>, Gothic <i>𐌻𐌰𐌹𐌰𐌽</i> (laian).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reward</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;launōną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>lānia</i>, Old Saxon <i>lōnon</i>, Old High German <i>lōnōn</i> and Old Norse <i>launa</i>. Equivalent to <i>lēan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basket</li><li>container, vessel</li><li>(<i>measurement</i>) basketful</li><li>a weel for catching fish; weely</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laup</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laupaz</i> (“container, basket”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewbʰ-</i>, <i>&#42;lewb-</i> (“to peel, break off, damage”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lew-</i>, <i>&#42;lewH-</i> (“to cut, divide, separate, release”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>lēpen</i> (“vessel, grain measure”), Middle Low German <i>lôp</i> and <i>lö̂pen</i> (“measuring vessel, small bushel, grain measure”), Old Norse <i>laupr</i> (“basket”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>false</li><li>devoid of, free from</li><li>bereft of; without</li><li>vain, worthless</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>falsehood, lie</li><li>mistake</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laus</i> (“loose, free, vacant”). Akin to Old Norse <i>lauss</i>, German <i>los</i> (“loose, free”). More at <i>-less</i>, <i>loose</i>.</p><p>see <i>lēah</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a false or phony person; hypocrite</li><li>one who acts or feigns; a faker; buffoon; jester</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lausārī</i>, equivalent to <i>lēas</i> (“falsehood”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>lōsāri</i> (“liar; cheater”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie (<i>tell lies</i>); falsify</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lausēn</i>. Equivalent to <i>lēas</i> (“false”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>false, vain</li></ol><p>From <i>lēas</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dissemble, feign</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>lēaslīċ</i> (“false”) +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pretense, dissimulation</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>lēaslīċettan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>falseness</li><li>lying</li><li>levity, frivolity</li><li>fickleness</li></ol><p>From <i>lēas</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>. Compare Old High German <i>lōsheit</i> (“falseness, deceitfulness, levity, frivolity, recklessness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>lēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lying, false, mendacious</li></ol><p>From <i>lēas</i> (“false”) +&lrm; <i>-sagol</i> (“saying”), the latter from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sagulaz</i>, which shows up only in compounds.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spy</li></ol><p>From <i>lēas</i> (“false”) +&lrm; <i>sċēawere</i> (“watcher, spy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lēasspell</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>made-up story</li><li>fiction</li><li>false news</li></ol><p><i>lēas</i> (“false”) +&lrm; <i>spell</i> (“story”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>release on giving an equivalent; compensation</li><li>lying, vain or frivolous speech, fiction; a false witness or testimony</li><li>falsehood, hypocrisy</li><li>deception, deceitfulness, trickery</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lausungu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lausungō</i>, equivalent to <i>lēasian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>lōsunga</i> (“redemption, payment, ransom, fraud, deception”), Old Norse <i>lausung</i> (“lying, falsehood”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forefinger</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a salmon</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lahsaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lakso-</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>lahs</i> (German <i>Lachs</i>), Old Norse <i>lax</i> (Swedish <i>lax</i>). The Indo-European root is also the source of Lithuanian <i>lašiša</i>, Latvian <i>lasis</i>, Russian <i>лосо́сь</i> (losósʹ).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of nitre used for soap, detergent</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lauþr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lauþrą</i> (“that which is used for washing, lye soap”), equivalent to <i>lēag</i> (“lye”) +&lrm; <i>-þor</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>lōthar</i> (“detergent”), Old Norse <i>lauðr</i> (“sea foam; nitre used as a detergent”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>look, regard</li><li>sight</li><li>(<i>Anglian</i>) alternative form of <i>lēac</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sweet</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>leolc</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lōki</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lōkiz</i>, <i>&#42;lōkijaz</i> (“a look, view”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leǵ-</i> (“to look, see”). Cognate with Old English <i>lōcian</i> (“to look, see”), Old Saxon <i>lōcōn</i> (“to see”), Dutch <i>leuk</i>, <i>geleuk</i> (used in reference to dikes, “a look, survey"; <i>in general</i>, "good-looking”). More at look.</p><p>Unknown. Compare Middle High German <i>lecker</i> (“tasty”), Old English <i>liccian</i> (“to lick”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lēk</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to water, irrigate; wet, moisten</li><li>to slake</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lakkjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lakjaną</i> (“to water, wet, irrigate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lǣċedōm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>some part of a weapon, possibly the crossbar in the hilt</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laggju</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lagjō</i> (“layer, ledge”). Cognate with Middle High German <i>legge</i>, <i>lecke</i> (“position, layer, tier”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to put or place</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bury (a person)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lay (an egg)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laggjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lagjaną</i>, a causative form of <i>&#42;ligjaną</i> (Old English <i>licgan</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;legʰ-</i>.</p><p>Compare Old Frisian <i>ledza</i>, <i>lega</i>, <i>leia</i>, Old Saxon <i>leggian</i>, Old Dutch <i>leggen</i>, Old High German <i>leggen</i>, Old Norse <i>leggja</i>, Gothic <i>𐌻𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (lagjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Lǣden</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>weak, frail; sick</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lēb</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>lēf</i>, Old Saxon <i>lēf</i>. Further origin obscure. Compare Dutch <i>laf</i> and <i>loof</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become weak, feeble, languid, disabled</li><li>alternative form of <i>līefan</i> (“to allow, to permit; to believe”)</li></ol><p>Related to <i>lēf</i>, and <i>left</i>, <i>lyft</i>, attested in <i>lyftādl</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;luft-</i>, from <i>&#42;lubjaną</i> (“to castrate, lop off”) (compare dialectal English <i>lib</i>, West Frisian <i>lobje</i>, Dutch <i>lubben</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)lewp-</i>, <i>&#42;(s)lup-</i> (“hanging limply”). More at <i>left</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>læfel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lēafnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līeġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chester (a city, the county town of Cheshire, in northwest England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cheshire (a county in northwest England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līeġcwalu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lieġdraca</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the state or action of lying, lying down, or lying ill</li><li>resting-place; couch, bed</li><li>deathbed, grave</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;legr</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>leger</i>, Old Saxon <i>legar</i>, Dutch <i>leger</i> (“bed, camp, army”), Old High German <i>legar</i> (German <i>Lager</i> (“camp”)), Old Norse <i>legr</i> (Danish <i>lejr</i>, Swedish <i>läger</i> (“bed”)), Gothic <i>𐌻𐌹𐌲𐍂𐍃</i> (ligrs). The Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek <i>λέχος</i> (lékhos), Latin <i>lectus</i> (“bed”), Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;legyom</i> (Old Irish <i>lige</i>, Irish <i>luí</i>), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ležati</i> (Russian <i>лежа́ть</i> (ležátʹ)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lēogere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cemetery, graveyard</li></ol><p>From <i>leġer</i> (“grave”) +&lrm; <i>stōw</i> (“place”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sexual intercourse</li></ol><p>From <i>leġer</i> +&lrm; <i>tēam</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līeġetu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līeġetu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Roman legion</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>legō</i> (“to gather, collect”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līeġetu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līeġloccod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līeġspīwol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>light</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>leahtor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lēactūn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A kind of dock, presumably bitter dock (<i>Rumex obtusifolius</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown; compare Middle Low German <i>lodwort</i>, Middle High German <i>lotwurz</i> (“pimpernel”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lame</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>lemman</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lamjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lamprey</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>lamprēda</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lenġu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spring (<i>season</i>)</li><li>Lent</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;langatīn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>typhus</li><li>tertian fever</li></ol><p>From <i>lencten</i> +&lrm; <i>ādl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spring heat, heat in spring</li></ol><p>From <i>lencten</i> +&lrm; <i>hǣte</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vernal, springlike</li><li>Lenten</li></ol><p>From <i>lencten</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spring</li><li>springtime</li><li>Lent</li></ol><p>From <i>lencten</i> +&lrm; <i>tīd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spring, springtime</li></ol><p>From <i>lencten</i> +&lrm; <i>tīma</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arrive, come to land</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;landijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;landijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>land</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loin</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>landī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;landį̄</i> (“loins”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>longer (<i>comparative degree of</i> <b>lange</b>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;langi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;langiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make or become long; extend, lengthen</li><li>to protract, delay</li><li>to pertain, belong</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;langijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;langijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>lang</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>lengja</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>lang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lenġu</i></li></ol></html>:/len.d͡ʒest/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>lang</i>: longest</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>lange</i>: longest</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;langist</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;langistaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lenġu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>lang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lenġest</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lenġest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>length; distance, duration</li><li>height, stature</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;langį̄</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>lendze</i>, <i>lentze</i>, Old High German <i>lengi</i>, Gothic <i>𐌻𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌴𐌹</i> (laggei). Equivalent to <i>lang</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lengþu</i> (“length”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>length</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;langiþō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lentil</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>lēns</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lion</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lēwō</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Leo)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Leo</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man, chief, leader</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a prince</li><li>a fine for slaying a man, wergild</li><li>a people, people group, nation</li><li>(<i>in compounds</i>) one's own people; home</li><li>alternative form of <i>lēode</i></li></ol><p>Cognate with Old Norse <i>ljóði</i> (“prince, leader”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;liudi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;liudiz</i>. Closely related to <i>lēode</i> and <i>lēodan</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognates include Old High German <i>liut</i>, Old Norse <i>lýðr</i>, and West Frisian <i>-lju</i>; and, outside the Germanic languages, Lithuanian <i>liáudis</i> (“common people”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ľudъ</i> (Russian <i>люд</i> (ljud)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a man, person, individual of a nation or country</li></ol><p>Akin to <i>lēod</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;leudan</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognates include Old Saxon <i>liodan</i>, Old High German <i>liotan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (liudan). The Indo-European root is also the source of Persian: رستن (rostan), Albanian <i>lind</i> (“to be born, to spring”) &lt; <i>lej</i> (“to generate, produce, give birth to”), Ancient Greek <i>ἐλεύθερος</i> (eleútheros, “free, freeman”), Latin <i>liber</i> (“free”), Old Irish <i>luss</i> (“plant”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Liudiberht</i>, equivalent to <i>lēod</i> (“people, nation”) + <i>berht</i> (“bright”). Cognate with German <i>Luitbert</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Suffragan bishop.</li><li>The bishop of a shire or diocese, synonymous to <i>sċīrbisċop</i>.</li><li>A bishop subordinate to an archbishop.</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>lēod</i> + <i>bisċop</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) king, ruler</li></ol><p><i>lēod</i> +&lrm; <i>cyning</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>men, people, people of a country</li></ol><p>Originally the plural of <i>lēod</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;liud(i)</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;liudiz</i> (“people”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a language, especially a national or popular language</li><li>one's native language</li></ol><p>From <i>lēod</i> (“people”) +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>lēod</i> (“man, leader, prince”) + <i>friþ</i> (“peace”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) patriarch, prince, chieftain, king</li></ol><p>From <i>lēod</i> (“people”) +&lrm; <i>fruma</i> (“ruler”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the protector of the people</li><li>prince, head man</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēod</i> +&lrm; <i>ġebyrġa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rank among a people</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēod</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeþincþu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the fall of a people</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēod</i> +&lrm; <i>hryre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the territory of a people</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēod</i> +&lrm; <i>mearc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the might of a people, that is a people's fighting men</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēod</i> +&lrm; <i>mæġen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>public law, common law, law of the land</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēod</i> +&lrm; <i>riht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a population or people</li><li>the country of a people</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēod</i> +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a people</li><li>a nation</li><li>the country of a people</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēod</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċiepe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lēodsċiepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lēodsċiepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a race</li><li>a people</li><li>a family</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēod</i> +&lrm; <i>stefn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Lēodweald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēod</i> (“prince, people”) +&lrm; <i>weald</i> (“power, might”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Lēodweald</li></ol><p>From <i>Lēodweald</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the guard or government of a people or country</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēod</i> +&lrm; <i>weard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a host of a people, such as a people's army</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēod</i> +&lrm; <i>werod</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>lēod</i> (“man, leader, prince”) + <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wise man in a people</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēod</i> +&lrm; <i>wita</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēod</i> +&lrm; <i>wulf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joy from being among one's own people</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēod</i> +&lrm; <i>wynn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dear, beloved</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friend, loved one</li><li>as a form of address, sir, can be applied to one or more people, regardless of gender.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leubaz</i> (“dear, beloved”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>liāf</i>, Old Saxon <i>liof</i>, Old High German <i>liob</i>, Old Norse <i>ljúfr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐍆𐍃</i> (liufs). Non-Germanic cognates include Polish <i>luby</i> (“dear, beloved”). Related to <i>lufu</i> (“love”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Mercian</i>) alternative form of <i>liofa</i></li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Leofa)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“dear, beloved”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Mercian</i>) alternative form of <i>liofas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>leofaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Mercian</i>) alternative form of <i>liofaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“beloved”) +&lrm; <i>ċild</i> (“child”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“beloved”) +&lrm; <i>cwēn</i> (“woman, queen”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>A derivative of the given name <i>Leofa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>lēof</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“dear, beloved”) + <i>gār</i> (“spear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“beloved”) +&lrm; <i>ġiefu</i> (“gift”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“beloved”) +&lrm; <i>gūþ</i> (“battle, war”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“beloved”) +&lrm; <i>hēah</i> (“high”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“beloved”) +&lrm; <i>helm</i> (“helmet”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēof</i> (“beloved”) +&lrm; <i>hild</i> (“grace”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be dear, be pleasant</li><li>to delight, please</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;leubēn</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;leubōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worthy of love: lovely, lovable</li><li>dear</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leubalīkaz</i>, equivalent to <i>lēof</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>graciously, kindly, lovingly</li></ol><p>From <i>lēoflīċ</i> (“lovely, dear”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i> or <i>lēof</i> (“dear”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“beloved”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;flǣd</i> (“beauty”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“dear, beloved”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“dear, beloved”) + <i>mǣre</i> (“famous”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“dear, beloved”) and <i>nōþ</i> (“daring”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>lēof</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>lēof</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li><i>inflection of</i> <b>lēof</b>:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li><i>strong genitive and dative feminine singular</i></li><li><i>nominative feminine singular comparative degree</i></li><li><i>nominative and accusative neuter singular comparative degree</i></li></ol></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li><i>used to specify a choice or preference</i>: rather</li></ol><p>The adverbial use dates back at least to Proto-West Germanic, when the word would have been <i>*leubōzā</i>. This usage persists into Modern German to this day: Proto-West Germanic <i>*miz isti leubōzā</i> (“I would rather”) → Old English <i>mē is lēofre</i>, German <i>mir ist lieber</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Leofric, Anglo-Saxon Earl of Mercia</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“dear, beloved”) and <i>rīc</i> (“king, ruler”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“dear, beloved”) and <i>rūn</i> (“whisper, rune, mystery”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“beloved”) +&lrm; <i>rǣd</i> (“counsel”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“dear, beloved”) and <i>siġe</i> (“victory”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“dear, beloved”) and <i>stān</i> (“stone”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“dear, beloved”) + <i>sunu</i> (“son”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“beloved”) +&lrm; <i>swiþ</i> (“powerful, strong”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lēoftǣle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lovable, dear, desirable, estimable, grateful, pleasant, gracious</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;tǣle</i> (“worthy of”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>lēoftǣle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēof</i> (“beloved”) +&lrm; <i>waru</i> (“care, guard”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“beloved”) +&lrm; <i>weald</i> (“power”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“dear, beloved”) and <i>weard</i> (“guard”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amiable, gracious, kind, loving</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> +&lrm; <i>-wende</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“dear, beloved”) and <i>wīġ</i> (“war, battle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Leofwine Godwinson</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“dear, beloved”) and <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“beloved”) +&lrm; <i>wynn</i> (“joy”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>lēof</i> (“beloved”) +&lrm; <i>þeġn</i> (“servant”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie (tell an untruth)</li><li>to be wrong, err</li><li>to deceive</li><li>to belie</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;leugan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>liāga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a liar</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;leugārī</i> (“liar”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>light, bright, clear, luminous</li><li>shining, resplendent, cheerful, beautiful</li><li>renowned</li><li>light (of little weight)</li><li>inconsiderable</li><li>not slow; quick, ready, nimble</li><li>fickle</li><li>easy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;leuht</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leuhtaz</i> (“light, brightness”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;leuht</i> (“light, bright”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lį̄ht</i> (“light, not heavy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lucifer</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>illumination</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēoht</i> +&lrm; <i>brǣdnes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>luminous; bright, light-bearing</li><li>splendid</li></ol><p>From <i>lēoht</i> +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>lēoht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lamp, lantern</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;leuhtafat</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>liohtfat</i>, Old Dutch <i>liehtfat</i>, Old High German <i>liohtfaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>lēoht</i> (“light”) +&lrm; <i>fæt</i> (“container”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līehting</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without light, lightless</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;leuhtalaus</i>, equivalent to <i>lēoht</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>light (of little weight)</li><li>bright, shining</li></ol><p>From <i>lēoht</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>lightly, slightly, gently, easily, quickly, without trouble, effort, or force</li><li>without harshness, with little inconvenience</li></ol><p>From <i>lēoht</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>, equivalent to <i>lēohtlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>līhtlīhho</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sower of light; one who casts light</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēoht</i> +&lrm; <i>sāwend</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lim</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radiance, light, ray of light</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;leuhmō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leuhmô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewk-mon-</i>, which is derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewk-</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>liomo</i>, Old Norse <i>ljómi</i> (Icelandic <i>ljómi</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ray (give rays to); provide or furnish with rays</li></ol><p>From <i>lēoma</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>ljóma</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Leominster (a market town and civil parish in northern Herefordshire, England)</li></ol><p>From a Celtic placename + <i>mynster</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lend, loan</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;līhwaną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>lihan</i> (German <i>leihen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Leonidas I, king of Sparta</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Λεωνίδᾱς</i> (Leōnídās).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass away, to depart</li><li>to pass over or pass through</li></ol><p>Apparently a mixture of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leusaną</i> (“to go”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leuzēną</i> (“to leave, depart”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>student</li><li>disciple</li><li>scholar</li></ol><p><i>leornian</i> + <i>-ere</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to learn</li><li>to study</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;liʀnōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lizaną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>lirnia</i>, Old Saxon <i>līnon</i>, and Old High German <i>lernēn</i>. Related to <i>lǣran</i> and <i>lār</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>leornung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>student, disciple</li></ol><p>From <i>leornung</i> (“study, learning”) +&lrm; <i>ċild</i> (“child, youth”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>leornungcniht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>learning</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;liʀnungu</i>, equivalent to <i>leornian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scholar, pupil, disciple</li></ol><p>From <i>leornung</i> (“learning”) +&lrm; <i>ċild</i> (“child”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a youth engaged in study: student, apprentice, disciple</li></ol><p><i>leornung</i> +&lrm; <i>cniht</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>school</li></ol><p>From <i>leornung</i> (“learning”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A learner, pupil, scholar, student, disciple.</li></ol><p>From <i>leornung</i> (“learning”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a school for [book-]learning</li></ol><p>From <i>leornung</i> (“learning”) +&lrm; <i>scōl</i> (“school”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) groin</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) flank</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;leuskō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leg (shank or thigh)</li><li>ham</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lahw</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lahwaz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>lær</i> (“thigh”), Middle High German <i>geliune</i> (“frame, build, physique”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>lēoþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poem</li><li>song</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;leuþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leuþą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leh₁w-</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>liod</i> (German <i>Lied</i>), Old Saxon <i>lioð</i> (Dutch <i>lied</i>), Old Norse <i>ljóð</i> (“verse, song”), Latin <i>laus</i> (“praise”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poetry</li><li>poem</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>lēoþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i>, literally “art of poetry”. Compare Icelandic <i>ljóðlist</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>skilled in poetry</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēoþcræft</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poem</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēoþ</i> +&lrm; <i>cwide</i> (“saying”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poet</li></ol><p>From <i>lēoþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poem</li><li>song</li></ol><p>From <i>lēoþ</i> +&lrm; <i>ġiddung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing or sound</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēoþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poetic</li></ol><p>From <i>lēoþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>advice sent through poetry</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēoþ</i> +&lrm; <i>rūn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a poem, song</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēoþ</i> +&lrm; <i>sang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a poetical manner</li><li>verse</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēoþ</i> +&lrm; <i>wīse</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a word in a poem</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēoþ</i> +&lrm; <i>word</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a poet</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēoþ</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gather, collect</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lesan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lesaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lysu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hinder, obstruct</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lattjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;latjaną</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lataz</i> (“slow, late”), equivalent to <i>læt</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>letta</i>, Old Saxon <i>lettian</i>, Old High German <i>lezzen</i>, Old Norse <i>letja</i>, Gothic <i>𐌻𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (latjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lǣwend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lewes (a town in East Sussex, England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>leþer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hide, skin, leather</li><li>(<i>in compounds</i>) leathern strip or strap</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;leþr</i> (“leather”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>liþren</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leather helmet</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>leþer</i> (“leather”) +&lrm; <i>helm</i> (“helmet”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a leather covering for the legs</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>leþer</i> (“leather”) +&lrm; <i>hose</i> (“pant leg”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>liþren</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;libbjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;libjaną</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>leva</i>, Old Saxon <i>libbian</i>, Old High German <i>lebēn</i>, Old Norse <i>lifa</i>, Gothic <i>𐌻𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (liban).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Libya</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Libya</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Λιβύη</i> (Libúē)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dead body, corpse</li><li><i>(rare outside of poetry)</i> body (living or dead)</li><li>form</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līċettan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līċettere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lick</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;likkōn</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>&#42;likkia</i>, Old Saxon <i>likkon</i>, Old High German <i>lecchōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lichfield (a city in Staffordshire, England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feign, pretend, profess falsely, fake</li><li>to simulate, imitate</li><li>to flatter</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;līkattjan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>līhhezzen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypocrite, pretender</li></ol><p>From <i>līċettan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypocrisy, feigning, pretence</li><li>flattery</li></ol><p>From <i>līċettan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to lie (be in a horizontal position)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to lie down</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be located somewhere (often translated with "to be")</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to lead (e.g. of a road or a river)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;liggjan</i>, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ligjaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;legʰ-</i>.</p><p>Compare Old Frisian <i>lidza</i>, Old Saxon <i>liggian</i>, Old High German <i>liggen</i>, Old Norse <i>liggja</i>, Gothic <i>𐌻𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (ligan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>liċġaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>body</li><li>trunk (of the body)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;līkahamō</i>, equivalent to <i>līċ</i> +&lrm; <i>hama</i>. More details at English <i>likam</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>physical</li><li>bodily, corporal</li></ol><p>From <i>līchama</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>physically</li><li>bodily, empirically</li></ol><p>From <i>līchamlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a device used for torture</li></ol><p>From <i>līċ</i> (“body, corpse”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hanga</i> (“hanger”), literally “body-hanger”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līchama</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to like (impersonal, with dative subject)</li><li>to please, appeal to (+ dative)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;līkēn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;līkāną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dirge, elegy, song for the dead</li></ol><p>From <i>līc</i> (“corpse”) +&lrm; <i>lēoþ</i> (“song, poem”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a person having to do with a corpse</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>līċ</i> (“corpse”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>likeness, similarity</li><li>figure, stature, image</li></ol><p>A shortening of <i>ġelīcnes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tomb</li><li>cemetery</li><li>vehicle or bier used to carry a corpse</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>līċ</i> +&lrm; <i>rest</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līcrest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līcrest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>graveyard, cemetery</li></ol><p>From <i>līċ</i> (“body”) +&lrm; <i>tūn</i> (“enclosure, yard”). Compare Old High German <i>līhhof</i> (“cemetery”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>funeral</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) funeral rites</li></ol><p><i>līċ</i> (“dead body”) +&lrm; <i>þeġnung</i> (“service”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pore</li></ol><p>From <i>līċ</i> (“body”) +&lrm; <i>þēote</i> (“pipe”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ship, vessel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lidą</i> (“followers, flock”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) sailor</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>lid</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>-a</i> (agentive noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailor</li></ol><p>From <i>lid</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>liþren</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lead</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lēad</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to give leave; grant; allow; consent</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to believe; trust; confide in</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be dear or pleasant; delight</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laubijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laubijaną</i>, related to Old English <i>lēaf</i> (“permission, leave”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;liubijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;liubijaną</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leubaz</i>, related to Old English <i>lēof</i> (“lief, dear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flame</li><li>fire; lightning</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laugi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laugiz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>loug</i>, Old Norse <i>leygr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flame-bearing, fiery</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>līeġ</i> +&lrm; <i>berende</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burning of fire or flame</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>līeġ</i> +&lrm; <i>bryne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flame-bearing, flaming, fiery</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>līeġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torment</li><li>death by fire</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>līeġ</i> +&lrm; <i>cwalu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) firedrake, fire-spewing dragon</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>līeġ</i> +&lrm; <i>draca</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fear of fire, pyrophobia</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>līeġ</i> +&lrm; <i>eġesa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>līeġetu</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laugiti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laugitją</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laugitjaną</i> (“to lighten up, flash”). Equivalent to <i>līeġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-et</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>līeġetu</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laugiti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laugitją</i>. Equivalent to <i>līeġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-et</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lightning strike</li><li>lightning bolt, flash of lightning</li></ol><p>From <i>līeġet</i> (“lightning”) +&lrm; <i>sliht</i> (“strike”). Compare German <i>Blitzschlag</i> and Modern English <i>lightning strike</i>, which are formed similarly. Note also the phrase <i>þunres</i> <i>sleġe</i> (“clap of thunder”), which contains another word for "strike."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lightning</li><li>a flash of lightning</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laugittju</i>, plural of <i>&#42;laugiti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laugitją</i>, deverbal of <i>&#42;laugitjaną</i> (“to enflame, light up”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flame spewing</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>līeġ</i> +&lrm; <i>fāmblāwende</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flaming fire</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>līeġ</i> +&lrm; <i>fȳr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flame spewing</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>līeġ</i> +&lrm; <i>fǣmende</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having flaming locks</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>līeġ</i> +&lrm; <i>locc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having flaming locks</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>līeġ</i> +&lrm; <i>loccod</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deny</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laugnijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laugnijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lightning, a flash of lightning</li><li>a bright light</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>līeġ</i> +&lrm; <i>ræsċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lightning</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>līeġ</i> +&lrm; <i>ræsċettung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flame-spewing</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>līeġ</i> +&lrm; <i>spīwol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) wave of flame</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>līeġ</i> +&lrm; <i>ȳþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>violent thrust of flame</li></ol><p>From <i>līeġ</i> +&lrm; <i>þracu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine; illuminate, glow, light</li><li>to make light or easy, lighten, alleviate, relieve of burden</li><li>to descend, light, alight</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;liuhtijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;liuhtijaną</i>, from the Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewk-</i> (“to shine, bright, to see”). Cognate with Old High German <i>liuhten</i> (German <i>leuchten</i>).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lį̄htijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;linhtijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>lichta</i>, Old High German <i>līhten</i>, Old Norse <i>létta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light, shining, illumination</li><li>lightening, alleviation</li></ol><p>From <i>līehtan</i> (“to light”) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to emit rays, beam, shine</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;liuhmijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leuhmô</i> (“light, shine”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewk-</i> (“light; to shine”), equivalent to <i>lēoma</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>ljóma</i> (“to radiate, shine”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set free, redeem, release</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lausijan</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>lōsian</i>, Dutch <i>lozen</i>, Old High German <i>lōsen</i> (German <i>lösen</i>), Old Norse <i>leysa</i> (Swedish <i>lösa</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līxan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soap, lather</li><li>to anoint, daub, smear</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lauþrijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lauþrijaną</i> (“to soap, wash”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>leyðra</i> (“to wash, lather”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>life</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lībą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līefan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that by which one lives, support, sustenance</li></ol><p>Related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lībą</i>. Akin to Gothic <i>𐌻𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (libains).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liver (<i>interior organ</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;libru</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>livere</i> (West Frisian <i>lever</i>), Old Saxon <i>levara</i>, Dutch <i>lever</i>, Old High German <i>lebara</i> (German <i>Leber</i>), Old Norse <i>lifr</i> (Swedish <i>lever</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(countable,&#32;uncountable) liver disease</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>lifer</i> +&lrm; <i>ādl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lobe of the liver</li></ol><p>From <i>lifer</i> (“liver”) +&lrm; <i>læppa</i> (“lobe”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Old English</i>) liverwort</li></ol><p>From <i>lifer</i> (“liver”) +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i> (“plant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) lord of life, God</li></ol><p>From <i>līf</i> +&lrm; <i>frēa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>libban</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lifġenne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lifġu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>separation from life; death</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>līf</i> +&lrm; <i>ġedāl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>libban</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lifeless, inanimate</li></ol><p>From <i>līf</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) life; living, causing life, vital</li><li>having life, animate, lively</li></ol><p>From <i>līf</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>vitally, in such a way as to infuse life</li></ol><p>From <i>līf</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clotted, coagulated</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>lifer</i> (“liver”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a way which leads to life; one's path in life; way of life</li></ol><p>From <i>līf</i> +&lrm; <i>weġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fire; flame</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laugi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laugiz</i> (“fire, flame, lightning”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leuk-</i> (“light; white; to shine”). Cognate with Old High German <i>loug</i>, Old Norse <i>lǫygr</i>, <i>log</i>, <i>loga</i> (“flame, low”). More at low.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>busy with fire</li></ol><p>From <i>līġ</i> (“fire”) +&lrm; <i>bisiġ</i> (“busy”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līġbisiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līeġbǣre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līeġcwalu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) firedrake, fire-spewing dragon</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>līġ</i> (“fire”) +&lrm; <i>draca</i> (“dragon”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flaming, fiery</li></ol><p>From <i>līġ</i> (“flame, fire, lightning”) +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ligoraċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>līeġetu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lightning</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>līġet</i> +&lrm; <i>ræsċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līeġetsliht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līeġetu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līeġet</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līeġloccod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līeġloccod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līeġnan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the city of Leicester</li></ol><p>From an old tribal name <i>Ligore</i> (attested as <i>Ligera</i> <i>ceastre</i>) + <i>ċeaster</i> (“fortified settlement”). The former is from the name of the river <i>Ligor</i>. The river name is thought to be of Brythonic/Celtic origin and possibly from the same source as the river <i>Loire</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līeġræsċettung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līeġspīwol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Līġwulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>līġ</i> (“fire; flame”) + <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līeġet</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līeġȳþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Anglian</i>) alternative form of <i>lēoht</i> (“light in weight, not heavy”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līehtan</i>: to light, to illuminate</li><li>alternative form of <i>līehtan</i>: to make light; to descend, alight</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līehting</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līehting</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lightness, brightness</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>lēoht</i> (“light, bright”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i> or <i>līehtan</i> (“to shine, glow, light”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>līf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lily</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>līlia</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lilie</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>limb, bodily member; branch (of tree etc)</li><li>glue; mortar, paste, lime</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;limu</i>, probably related to <i>&#42;liþu-</i> (whence <i>liþ</i>). Cognate with Old Norse <i>limr</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;līmaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂leyH-</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>līm</i> (Dutch <i>lijm</i>), Old High German <i>līm</i> (German <i>Leim</i>), Old Norse <i>lím</i> (Swedish <i>lim</i>). The Proto-Indo-European root is also the source of Latin <i>līmus</i> (“mud”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the disposition or arrangement of the limbs; form, shape</li></ol><p>From <i>lim</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;ġeleċġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>limb by limb</li></ol><p>From <i>lim</i> +&lrm; <i>-mǣlum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to happen, occur</li><li>to happen to (someone); befall</li><li>to affect, concern</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;limpan</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old High German <i>limphan</i>, <i>limpfan</i> (Middle High German <i>limpfen</i>), Danish <i>lempe</i>, Norwegian <i>lempe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>limpeþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>linen, flax</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;līnā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;līnǭ</i> (“line, rope, flaxen cord, thread”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;līną</i> (“flax, linen”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;līno-</i> (“flax”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Lindcylene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Old English</i>) Lincolnshire (a county of England)</li></ol><p>From <i>Lindcolen</i> +&lrm; <i>sċīr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lime, linden</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) shield (made from linden wood)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lindu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lindō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Lindcylene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the city of Lincoln, England</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Lindum</i> <i>Colonia</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of lime-wood</li></ol><p>From <i>lind</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lindsey, the north part of Lincolnshire in England</li></ol><p>From <i>lind</i> (“lind”) +&lrm; <i>īeġ</i> (“island”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Lindisfarena</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Lindisfarena</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Lindesēġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) shield-bearer, warrior</li></ol><p><i>lind</i> (“shield”) +&lrm; <i>hæbbende</i> (“having”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lindisfarne (an island off the coast of England)</li></ol><p>Genitive plural of an unattested nominative/accusative *Lindisfaran.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Lindisfarena</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Lindesēġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the people of Lindsey</li></ol><p>From <i>Lindis</i> +&lrm; <i>-ware</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) battle, fighting</li></ol><p>From <i>lind</i> (“shield”) +&lrm; <i>plega</i> (“play, exercise”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>line</li><li>rope, cable</li><li>row, series</li><li>direction, rule</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;līnǭ</i> (“line, rope, flaxen cord, thread”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;līną</i> (“flax, linen”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;līno-</i> (“flax”). Akin to Old High German <i>līna</i> (“line”) (German <i>Leine</i> (“rope”)), Middle Dutch <i>līne</i> (“rope, cord”) (Dutch <i>lijn</i> (“rope”)), Old Norse <i>līna</i> (“cord, rope”) (Danish <i>line</i> (“rope, cord”)), Old English <i>līn</i> (“flax, linen, cloth”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) linen</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;līnīnaz</i>, equivalent to <i>līne</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>linnet (a type of bird)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>linnet (a type of bird)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līnetwiġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land used for growing flax</li></ol><p>From <i>līn</i> (“linen, flax”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stop, cease from, desist, quit, forlet</li><li>to lose; (<i>ones life</i>) yield up, surrender</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;linnan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;linnaną</i> (“to turn aside, swerve from”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leyh₂-</i> (“to deflect, turn away, vanish, slip”). Cognate with Old High German <i>bilinnan</i> (“to yield, stop, forlet, give away”), Old Norse <i>linna</i> (“to stop, rest”), Gothic <i>𐌰𐍆𐌻𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (aflinnan, “to go away”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līnen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>linseed, flaxseed</li></ol><p>From <i>līn</i> (“flax”) +&lrm; <i>sǣd</i> (“seed”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Northumbrian</i>) alternative form of <i>lifo</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>leornian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) lip</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lippjō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lippa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fleshy part of the body without fat or bone; brawn; muscle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lihwizô</i>, <i>&#42;ligwizô</i> (“thigh; groin”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lekʷs-</i>, <i>&#42;lewks-</i> (“groin”). More at lire.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brawny; fleshy; muscley</li></ol><p>From <i>līra</i> +&lrm; <i>-eht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mildness, mercy, kindness, grace</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>līþs</i>, from <i>líþe</i> (“gentle, mild”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>art; cunning, guile, craft</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;listi</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>list</i>, Dutch <i>list</i>, Old High German <i>list</i> (German <i>List</i>), Old Norse <i>list</i> (Swedish <i>list</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having skilled or artful hands, deft</li></ol><p>From <i>list</i> (“art, skill”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hendiġ</i>, the latter from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;handīg</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;handug</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;handugaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lȳtling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine, flash, glitter, gleam</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;liuhsijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;liuhsijaną</i> (“to light, to shine”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>lýsa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>splendor, brightness</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>līxan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līþelīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>līþs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>limb, member</li><li>a strong drink</li><li>a fleet</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;liþu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;liþuz</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;līþu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>designating the sixth & seventh months of the Anglo-Saxon calendar (equivalent to June & July), as well as the leap month inserted between them during a leap year.</li></ol><p>Apparently related to <i>līþe</i> (“mild”) probably cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>ljeto</i>, Czech <i>léto</i>, Polish <i>lato</i>, Russian <i>ле́то</i> (léto, “summer, year”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go; pass; travel (especially by water)</li><li>to suffer loss</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;līþan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>līthan</i>, Old High German <i>līdan</i>, Old Norse <i>líða</i> (Swedish <i>lida</i>), Gothic <i>&#42;𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;leiþan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gentle, mild; pleasant</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;linþ(ī)</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;linþaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līþiġian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>softly, gently, kindly, graciously, mildly</li></ol><p>From <i>līþe</i> (“soft, gentle”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i> (“in manner or form”, adverbial suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>traveller</li><li>sailor</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>liþere</i> (“sling, slingshot”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;liþrijā</i> (“strip of leather, leather pouch”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;leþr</i> (“lether”). Equivalent to <i>leþer</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to soften</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to charm, flatter</li></ol><p>From <i>līþ(e)ra</i> (“gentler”, comparative of <i>līþe</i>) +&lrm; <i>-cian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a leather slinging pouch or sling; slingshot</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;liþrijā</i> (“strip of leather, leather strap”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;leþr</i> (“lether”). Equivalent to <i>leþer</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>liþren</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līþiġian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pliant, supple, flexible, soft, yielding</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;liþugaz</i>, equivalent to <i>liþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>leeg</i> (“empty, hollow”), German <i>ledig</i> (“unmarried”) and Icelandic <i>liðugur</i> (“flexible, supple”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assuage, calm</li><li>to make soft or yielding</li></ol><p>From <i>līþe</i> +&lrm; <i>-gian</i>, by surface analysis, <i>līþiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>softness, gentleness, mildness</li></ol><p><i>līþe</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līþnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>līþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>līþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) made of leather</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;liþrīn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;liþrīnaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>leþer</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>liss</i></li></ol><p>From <i>líþe</i> (“gentle, mild”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synovial fluid</li></ol><p><i>liþ</i> (“joint”) +&lrm; <i>sēaw</i> (“juice”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>loppe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lock</li><li>that by which anything is closed (e.g. bolt, bar, etc.)</li><li>an enclosed space, enclosure, fold</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lōca</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lok</i> (“shutter, lock”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>locked enclosure</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>look, see here</li><li>-ever</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lukô</i>, related to <i>&#42;lūkaną</i> (“to shut”).</p><p>Possibly an imperative form of <i>lōcian</i>. See also <i>lo</i> (from Old English <i>lā</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a woman with long hair; a freewoman</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>locc</i> (“lock (of hair)”) +&lrm; <i>-bora</i> (“bearer”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i> (feminine)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lock (of hair)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lukk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lukkaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to allure or entice; to win over by gentle means</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lukkōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look, see; gaze</li><li>to observe, regard, take heed</li><li>to look to; to belong, pertain</li><li>to determine something by looking <i>(with interrogative pronoun)</i></li><li>to make sure that something happens, is done, etc.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lōkōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in compounds</i>) looking</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poor person, pauper; beggar</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ludraz</i> (“useless; worthless; bad”). Compare Old Norse <i>loddari</i> (“juggler; tramp”), Old High German <i>lotar</i> (“vain; void; insignificant”), Middle High German <i>lotter</i>. See also Czech <i>lotr</i> and Polish <i>łotr</i>, Russian <i>ло́дырь</i> (lódyrʹ).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nonsense</li><li>triviality</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ludrōną</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ludraz</i> (“vain”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>praise</li><li>song of praise, hymn</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) the palm of the hand</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lob</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>lof</i>, Dutch <i>lof</i>, Old High German <i>lob</i> (German <i>Lob</i>), Old Norse <i>lof</i> (Swedish <i>lov</i>). Related to <i>lēof</i>, <i>lufu</i>, <i>lofian</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lōfō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lōfô</i>. Cognate with Icelandic <i>lófi</i>, Gothic <i>𐌻𐍉𐍆𐌰</i> (lōfa).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>praiseworthy deed</li></ol><p>From <i>lof</i> (“praise, song of praise”) +&lrm; <i>dǣd</i> (“deed, action; event, exploit”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eager for fame (usually with negative connotations, but used positively in <i>Beowulf</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lobagern</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lubagernaz</i> (“willing, eager”); equivalent to <i>lof</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeorn</i>. Compare Old High German <i>lobogernī</i>, Old Norse <i>lofgjarn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>lofġeorn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to praise; exalt</li><li>to appraise; value; put a price on</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lobōn</i>. Equivalent to <i>lof</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hymn, song of praise</li></ol><p>From <i>lof</i> (“praise, hymn”) +&lrm; <i>sang</i> (“song”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>praiseworthy</li></ol><p>From <i>lof</i> +&lrm; <i>-sum</i>. Piecewise doublet of <i>lufsum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lyft</i> (“air”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>praising</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>lofian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place, stead</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lōgą</i> (“site, situation, camp”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;legʰ-</i> (“to be situated, lie”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>lōch</i> (“place, locality”), Old High German <i>luog</i> (“cave, den, cubicle”), Old Norse <i>lóg</i> (“place”). The Indo-European root is also the source of Greek <i>λέκτρον</i> (léktron), Latin <i>lectus</i> (“bed”), Albanian <i>log</i> (“place for men, gathering”), Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;legeti</i> (Old Irish <i>lige</i>, Irish <i>luí</i>), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ležati</i> (Russian <i>лежа́ть</i> (ležátʹ)).</p><p>Inflected forms.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liar</li><li>deceiver</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lugô</i> (“liar”). Cognate with Old High German <i>logo</i> (“liar”) and Old Norse <i>logi</i> (“liar”). More at <i>warlock</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lodge; place; put in order; arrange; frame</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lōgōn</i>, equivalent to <i>lōg</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>lōgia</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lōg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Leeds, a city in West Yorkshire, England</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anglian form of <i>lamb</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>frequently, often</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>land</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>landstede</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>longode</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>lange</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>langian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>langsum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>langung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spider (<i>perhaps also</i> flea)</li><li>silkworm</li></ol><p>Sense of "spider, silkworm" from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lubbō</i>, <i>&#42;lubbǭ</i> (“that which hangs or dangles”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lep-</i> (“to peel, skin”).</p><p>Cognate with Saterland Frisian <i>lobbe</i> (“hanging lump of flesh”), Middle Low German <i>lobbe</i> and Middle Dutch <i>lobbe</i> (“dangling part”), Dutch <i>lob</i> (“hanging lip, ruffle or sleeve”). More at lobe.</p><p>Sense of "flea" from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;luppǭ</i> (“flea, sandflea”, originally “jumper”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;luppijaną</i> (“to jump, dart”). Cognate with Danish <i>loppe</i> (“flea”), Swedish <i>loppa</i> (“flea”), Middle High German <i>lüpfen</i>, <i>lupfen</i> (“to release and raise aloft, move quickly”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lobster</li></ol><p>Possibly a corruption of Latin <i>locusta</i> (“lobster, locust”) + the Old English feminine agent suffix <i>-estre</i>; or from <i>lobbe</i>, <i>loppe</i> (“spider”) + the feminine agent suffix <i>-estre</i>, equivalent to <i>lop</i> +&lrm; <i>-ster</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>loppestre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>loppestre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loss, destruction</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loss</li><li>destruction</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lusą</i> (“loss”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewHs-</i> (“to cut loose; sever; lose”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>los</i> (“looseness; breaking up”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>losaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be lost; stray</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to die; perish; be destroyed</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of inanimate things) to come to nothing; decay</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to escape</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;losōn</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Hlōþhere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lynx</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;luhs</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>lohs</i>, Old High German <i>luhs</i>, Old Swedish <i>lō</i>. See los.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lot (nephew of Abraham in the Bible)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Hebrew <i>לוֹט</i> (lot).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an upper garment; mantle; cloak</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;luþō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;luþô</i> (“coarse woolen cloth”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)lūt-</i>, <i>&#42;lūd-</i> (“weak, languid”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>lotha</i> (“thick robe”), Old Saxon <i>lotho</i> (“thick wool robe”), Old High German <i>lodo</i>, <i>ludo</i> (“thick woolen cloth”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Hlōþhere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lock</li><li>to close</li><li>to pull up, pluck</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lūkan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lūkaną</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;leukan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leukaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Luke the Evangelist</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Lūcās</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lufu</i> (“love”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lovage</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lubistikō</i>, <i>&#42;lubistik</i>, from Latin <i>levisticum</i> (“lovage”), with folk-etymological alteration after <i>lufu</i> (“love”) and <i>stiċe</i> (“stitch”): literally “love-stitch”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>female</i>) lover</li><li>a sweetheart</li></ol><p>From <i>lufu</i> +&lrm; <i>-estre</i> or <i>lufian</i> +&lrm; <i>-estre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lufian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to love</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lubōn</i>, derived from the noun <i>&#42;lubu</i> (“love”). Equivalent to <i>lufu</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>luvia</i>, Old High German <i>lubōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lover</li></ol><p>From <i>lufian</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lufiend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worthy of love; lovable</li><li>amiable; pleasant; likable; lovely; dear</li></ol><p>Probably an alteration of older <i>lēoflīċ</i>. Equivalent to <i>lufu</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>amiably, kindly</li></ol><p>From <i>luflīċ</i> (“lovely, amiable”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i> or <i>lufu</i> (“love”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love</li></ol><p>From <i>lufu</i> (“love”) +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loving, amiable, pleasant, lovable</li></ol><p>From <i>lufu</i> +&lrm; <i>-sum</i>. Piecewise doublet of <i>lofsum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>token of love</li></ol><p>From <i>lufu</i> +&lrm; <i>tācen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lubu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lubō</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>luve</i>, Old High German <i>luba</i>, Gothic <i>&#42;𐌻𐌿𐌱𐍉</i> (&#42;lubō).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Lufu)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From the noun <i>lufu</i> (“love”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love, the act of loving</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>lufian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beloved</li><li>pleasant</li></ol><p>From <i>luf</i> (“love”) +&lrm; <i>-wende</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pool</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>London</li></ol><p>Together with Middle Welsh <i>Llundein</i> cannot be directly from Latin <i>Londinium</i>, which would yield *Lynden via i-mutation. But both may continue Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;Lundeinju</i> (Late British Latin) if the medial <i>-i-</i> of the original word was short.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>London, England</li></ol><p><i>Lunden</i> (“London”) +&lrm; <i>burg</i> (“city”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Lundenburg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the city of London</li></ol><p>From <i>Lunden</i> +&lrm; <i>ċeaster</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>London Bridge</li></ol><p>Literally, “London's bridge”, or more loosely translated as “the bridge of London”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) London; of or from London</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a Londoner</li></ol><p><i>Lunden</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>London; London Town</li></ol><p>From <i>Lunden</i> (“London”) +&lrm; <i>tūn</i> (“town”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the people of London; Londoners</li></ol><p>From <i>Lunden</i> (“London”) +&lrm; <i>-waran</i> (“people of”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Lundenwaran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Lundenwaran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the "London-road"</li></ol><p>From <i>Lunden</i> (“London”) +&lrm; <i>weġ</i> (“people of”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>London</li></ol><p><i>Lunden</i> (“London”) +&lrm; <i>wīc</i> (“settlement”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kidney</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lundulagō</i>, equivalent to <i>&#42;lund</i> (“loin, fat”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;laga</i> (“lier, that which is fixed”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>lundlaga</i> (“kidney”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lung</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lunganjō</i>, an extension of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lungô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lengʷʰ-</i> (“light, agile, nimble”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>lungen</i>, Old Saxon <i>lungannia</i>, Old High German <i>lunganna</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lung disease</li></ol><p><i>lungen</i> +&lrm; <i>ādl</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quick to act, prompt</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lungr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lungraz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ln̥gʷʰrós</i> (“lightweight”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quickly, soon, at once, straightaway</li></ol><p>See <i>lungor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>louse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lūs</i>. Compare Old High German <i>lūs</i>, Old Norse <i>lús</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>desire, pleasure, appetite, lust</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lustu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lustuz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>desirous, desirable, pleasant, agreeable</li></ol><p>From <i>lust</i> +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>desirable</li></ol><p>From <i>lustbǣre</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>desirous</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>lust</i> +&lrm; <i>-ful&#59;</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with joy or pleasure, joyfully, gladly, with lust</li></ol><p>From <i>lustful</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pleasure, delight</li><li>desire</li></ol><p>From <i>lustfull</i> (“desirous”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lustfulnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>gladly, willingly</li></ol><p>From <i>lust</i> (“desire, pleasure”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botanical</i>) lady's smock (<i>Cardamine pratensis</i>)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Apparently from <i>&#42;lust</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;moce</i>. The first element may be <i>lust</i> (“pleasure”) or may refer to a different unknown term. The second element is probably related to <i>&#42;moc</i> (“dung, muck”) (found in <i>hlōsmoc</i> (“pigsty dung”)), distantly related to Old Norse <i>myki</i> (“manure, dung”), whence Middle English <i>muk</i>, <i>mok</i> (“dung, filth”), English <i>muck</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pleasant, delectable</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;lustsum</i> (“pleasant”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>lust</i> (“desire, pleasure, lust”) +&lrm; <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lout, bow, bend forward, stoop</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lūtan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lurk, skulk, lie hidden</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lutōn</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lybb</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drug, medicine</li><li>poison</li><li>charm</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lubi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lubją</i> (“herb, poison, love potion”). Akin to Old High German <i>luppi</i>, Old Norse <i>lyf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>libban</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magic, witchcraft</li></ol><p>From <i>lyb</i> (“drug, medicine”) +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charm, amulet</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lubisnu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lubisnō</i>, from <i>&#42;lubją</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-snō</i>. Equivalent to <i>lybb</i> (“drug, medicine, poison”) +&lrm; <i>-sn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lybesn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lyft</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lyge</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lēoden</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>līf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līefan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lybesn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flatter, pay court to</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lubittjan</i>, <i>&#42;lobattjan</i>, equivalent to <i>lof</i> +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>. Alternatively, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lubbjattjan</i> (“to drug, charm”), equivalent to <i>lybb</i> +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flattery, adulation</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>lyffettan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lēafnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) The atmosphere or the sky.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lufti</i>, possibly rebuilt from the plural or remodelled as a ti-stem from <i>&#42;luftu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;luftuz</i>. Doublet of <i>loft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) air; aerial</li></ol><p>From <i>lyft</i> (“air”) +&lrm; <i>-en</i> (“of or relating to”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the air, atmosphere</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>lyft</i> (“air”) +&lrm; <i>helm</i> (“helmet”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lying</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lie</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lugi</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>luggi</i> (“lying”), Old High German <i>lucki</i> (“lying”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lugi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lugiz</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Lyge)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the River Lea in England</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lying, false</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lie, falsehood</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lugin</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;luginaz</i> (“lying”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewgʰ-</i> (“to tell lies, swear, bemoan”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;luginu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;luginō</i>, <i>&#42;lugį̄</i> (“lie, falsehood”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewgʰ-</i> (“to tell lies, swear, bemoan”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>lugina</i> (“falsehood”), Danish <i>løgn</i> (“lie”), Norwegian <i>lygn</i> (“lie”). More at lie.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līehtan</i>: to light, to illuminate</li><li>alternative form of <i>līehtan</i>: to make light; to descend, alight</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līehting</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lim</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fat, grease</li><li>fatness</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lundiju</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lund(ij)ō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an axletree</li><li>board, plank</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lunis</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lunisaz</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lunaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lnH-</i>, zero-grade of <i>&#42;lénHos</i> (“axletree, linchpin”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loss, damage, destruction</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;luʀi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;luziz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līesan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lyswen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to like or want (impersonal, with accusative of person) (+ genitive of object)</li><li>to desire</li><li>to please</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lustijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lustijaną</i>, derived from the noun <i>&#42;lustuz</i> (“pleasure, lust”) (Old English <i>lust</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an evil deed, a depravity</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>evil; base, corrupt</li><li>false</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lusiu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lusiwaz</i>. Alternatively, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lasiu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lasiwaz</i> (“weak, unreliable, false”). Akin to Old English <i>lyswen</i> (“corrupt, purulent”), Old English <i>lēas</i> (“false, lax”), Old Norse <i>lasinn</i> (“limpy, weak”), <i>las</i> in <i>lasmeyr</i> (“wretched, frail”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full of pus; attery; purulent</li></ol><p>From <i>lysu</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>little, few</li><li>(<i>in compounds</i>) inferior, subordinate</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little, few, a few (<i>substantive use of the adverb</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>small, little</li><li>not much, few</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lūtil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lūtilaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pinky, little finger</li></ol><p>From <i>lȳtel</i> (“little”) +&lrm; <i>finger</i> (“finger”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>littleness</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>lȳtel</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lȳtelnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>little, very little</li></ol><p>From <i>lȳt</i> +&lrm; <i>hwōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crafty, cunning; prudent</li></ol><p>Related to <i>lutian</i> (“to deceive”) and <i>lot</i> (“deceit”), both related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leutaz</i> (“deceptive”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young child, little kid; little one</li></ol><p><i>lȳtel</i> +&lrm; <i>-ling</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lȳtel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>evil, wicked, sordid, depraved</li><li>base, mean, poor</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lūþrijaz</i> (“bad; dissolute; neglected; useless”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)lew-</i> (“limp, slack”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lǣce</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move quickly, spring, leap</li><li>whip, flicker (as a flame)</li></ol><p>Etymology treeProto-Germanic <i>&#42;laikaną</i>Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laikijaną</i>Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laikijan</i>Old English <i>læcan</i></p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laikijaną</i>, causative of <i>&#42;laikaną</i>. Related to Old English <i>lācan</i> and <i>lāc</i> (“play, sport”), which see.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a boggy stream</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grab (sometimes violently: snatch, catch, apprehend)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lakkjan</i> (“to seize”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lakjaną</i> (“to grasp, seize”), evidently only attested in Old English, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lh₂g-ie-</i>, which could be an isogloss shared with Ancient Greek <i>λάζομαι</i> (lázomai, “I seize, grasp”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doctor (physician)</li><li>(in compounds) medical</li><li>leech (blood-sucking invertebrate)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lākī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lēkijaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>lētza</i>, Old Saxon <i>lāki</i>, Old High German <i>lāhhi</i>, Old Norse <i>&#42;lækir</i>, Gothic <i>𐌻𐌴𐌺𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (lēkeis).</p><p>Probably assimilated to <i>Etymology 1</i> above, by popular etymology, though its use probably predated the term for a doctor.</p><p>Of uncertain ultimate origin, but possibly from a Germanic base meaning “to pluck, tear” (compare Old High German <i>liochan</i> (“to tear”)), evolving into “to suck”, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewǵ-</i> (“to break, tear”), see also Ancient Greek <i>λευγαλέος</i> (leugaléos), <i>λυγρός</i> (lugrós), Sanskrit <i>रुजति</i> (rujati, “to break open, shatter, injure, cause pain”), Latvian <i>lauzt</i> (“to break, fracture”).</p><p>Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>lake</i>, <i>leke</i> (modern Dutch <i>laak</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>book on medicine or of recipes</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lǣċe</i> +&lrm; <i>bōc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medicine chest</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>lǣċe</i> (“doctor”) +&lrm; <i>ċist</i> (“chest”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medicine (profession/field of study)</li><li>medicine (substance which promotes healing)</li></ol><p>From <i>lǣċe</i> (“doctor”) +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>skilled in medicine</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>lǣċecræft</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>physicians</li></ol><p>From <i>lǣċe</i> (“doctor”) +&lrm; <i>cynn</i> (“kind”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lacedaemon, Sparta</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Lacedaemon</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Λακεδαίμων</i> (Lakedaímōn).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the practice of medicine</li><li>medicine, a remedy, cure</li><li>healing</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lākijadōm</i>, equivalent to <i>lǣċe</i> +&lrm; <i>-dōm</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>lāhtuom</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ring finger</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) little finger</li></ol><p>From <i>lǣċe</i> (“leech, physician”) +&lrm; <i>finger</i> (“finger”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hospital</li></ol><p>From <i>lǣċe</i> (“doctor”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scalpel</li></ol><p><i>lǣċe</i> (“doctor”) +&lrm; <i>seax</i> (“knife”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medicinal herb</li></ol><p><i>lǣċe</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tend to</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>lǣċe</i> (“doctor”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead</li><li>to bring, take</li><li>to carry, convey</li><li>to guide, conduct</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laidijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laidijaną</i>, causative of <i>&#42;līþaną</i> (“to go, depart, travel”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leyt-</i> (“to leave, depart, die”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>lēda</i>, Old Saxon <i>lēdian</i>, Old High German <i>leiten</i>, Old Norse <i>leiða</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Lǣden</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Lǣden</i></li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Læden)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Latin</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Latin</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Latīnus</i>. More at Latin.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Latin</li></ol><p>From <i>Lǣden</i> +&lrm; <i>ġereord</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Romans, Latins</li></ol><p><i>Læden</i> (“Latin”) +&lrm; <i>-ware</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who leads; a leader; guide</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laidijārī</i>, equivalent to <i>lǣdan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>lēdere</i>, Old High German <i>leitāri</i> (“leader”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laibijan</i> (“to leave”). Compare Old Frisian <i>lēva</i>, Old Saxon <i>lēvian</i>, Old High German <i>leiben</i>, Old Norse <i>leifa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cup, vessel, bowl</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;labal</i>, <i>&#42;labil</i>, from Latin <i>lābellum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rush (plant)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>laver</i> (“water plant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Leicestershire (a county of England).</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>Ligoraċeaster</i> +&lrm; <i>sċīr</i> (“shire”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>līeġet</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loan; grant; gift</li><li>(<i>in relation to land</i>) lease; fee; fief</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laihną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lend; grant; lease</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lǣn</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>. Akin to Old Norse <i>lána</i> and Old High German <i>lēhanōn</i>, <i>lēhanen</i>. Compare Danish <i>låne</i>, Dutch <i>lenen</i> and German <i>lehnen</i> (“to borrow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>land</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transitory day</li></ol><p><i>lǣne</i> +&lrm; <i>dagas</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>transitory, temporary, fleeting</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lenġu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land that is loaned or temporarily granted, without any ownership passing to the new occupier</li></ol><p>From <i>lǣn</i> (“loan”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skirt of a garment</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) lobe</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lappō</i>, <i>&#42;lappā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lappaz</i> (“cloth; rag”); further etymology uncertain, perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leb-</i> (“to hang down loosely (?)”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>[<i>with</i> accusative] to teach, instruct, train</li><li>[<i>with</i> accusative] to advise, exhort, urge</li><li>[<i>with</i> accusative] to admonish, warn, dissuade</li><li>[<i>with</i> accusative] to enjoin; to command, prescribe</li><li>[<i>with</i> accusative] <i>(malicious)</i> to instigate, incite, provoke</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laiʀijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laizijaną</i>, from <i>&#42;laizō</i> (“teaching”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>lēra</i>, Old Saxon <i>lērian</i>, Dutch <i>lēren</i>, Old High German <i>lēren</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teacher (female)</li></ol><p><i>lār</i> +&lrm; <i>-estre</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instruction, teaching</li></ol><p>From <i>lǣran</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a disciple</li></ol><p>From <i>lǣring</i> (“teaching, instruction”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a girl who is receiving instruction, a female pupil</li></ol><p>From <i>lǣring</i> (“teaching, instruction”) +&lrm; <i>mǣden</i> (“girl”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pasture</li><li>a letting; an allowing or permitting of something to move, pass, or occur</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>less</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) minus</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lāsu</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laisi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laisiz</i> (“smaller, lesser, fewer, lower”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leys-</i> (“to shrink, grow thin, become small, be gentle”); see also Old Frisian <i>lēs</i> (“less”), Old Saxon <i>lēs</i> (“less”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lāsi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lēsiz</i>, <i>&#42;lēssiz</i> (“a letting”), related to Old English <i>lǣtan</i> (“to let, release”).</p></html>:/ˈlæːs.est/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lǣst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lǣswian</i></li></ol></html>:/ˈlæːs.sɑ/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>lȳtel</i>: smaller, less</li></ol></html>:/læːst/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>lȳtel</i>: smallest, least</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>least</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>lǣsest</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>lēst</i>, <i>lērest</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pinky, little finger</li></ol><p>From <i>lǣst</i> (“least”) +&lrm; <i>finger</i> (“finger”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to follow, pursue (a leader, course of action etc.)</li><li>to perform, carry out</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;laistijan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>lēstian</i>, Old High German <i>leisten</i> (German <i>leisten</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>last (a shoemaker's tool)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laistiz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leys-</i> (“track, furrow”), similar to the verb <i>leornian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoemaker</li></ol><p>From <i>lǣste</i> (“last (tool)”) +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“wright”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pasture, graze, feed</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>lǣs</i> (“pasture”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slow</li><li>late</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a class of man ranking between a slave and a ceorl</li><li>a serf</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lat</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>laz</i> and more distantly Old Norse <i>latr</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lētaz</i> (“servant, slave”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lē-</i>. Akin to Middle Dutch <i>laet</i> (Dutch <i>laat</i>), Old High German <i>laz</i> (“half-freedman, serf”), Old Frisian <i>lethar</i> (“freedman”), Gothic <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌻𐌴𐍄𐍃</i> (fralēts). More at allegiance, liege.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let, allow</li><li>(<i>auxiliary</i>) to have someone do something, have something done [<i>with</i> infinitive]</li><li>to leave someone/something in a certain situation</li><li>to suppose</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lātan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>læte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Lǣden</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>slowly</li></ol><p><i>læt</i> (“slow, late”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slowness</li><li>lateness</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>læt</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>læt</i></li></ol></html>:/ˈlætˌsum/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slow; late</li></ol><p>From <i>læt</i> +&lrm; <i>-sum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>injury, harm</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>traitor, betrayer</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>lǣwan</i> (“to betray”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to betray</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lāwijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lēwijaną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>firlāwen</i>, <i>gilāwen</i> (“to betray”), Gothic <i>𐌻𐌴𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (lēwjan, “to betray”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Lewes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>layman</li></ol><p>From the adjective <i>lǣwede</i> (“lay, non-clerical”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lay; non-clerical</li></ol><p>According to the OED, of uncertain origin, but likely from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;laigo-</i>, from Late Latin <i>laicus</i> (“of the people”) +&lrm; <i>-ede</i>, although the phonetic development is a difficulty.</p><p>Otherwise, formally resembling a derivative of the past participle of <i>lǣwan</i> (“to reveal, betray”) in the sense of "exposed as being unlearned" or "easily betrayed, clueless", from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lāwijan</i> (“to betray”), fitting a scenario where the word was influenced in meaning by similar sounding Latin <i>laicus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who betrays, traitor</li></ol><p>From <i>lǣwan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>lāwerce</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land</li><li>lathe (administrative unit of land)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lēþą</i> (“possession”)</p></html>:/m/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>the numeral 1000 (<i>þūsend</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>more</li><li>further</li><li>instead, rather</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>more (<i>often + genitive</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maiz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>mā</i>, Old Saxon <i>mēr</i>, Old Dutch <i>mēr</i>, Old High German <i>mēr</i>, Old Norse <i>meir</i>, Gothic <i>𐌼𐌰𐌹𐍃</i> (mais).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make or create:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to perform or carry out (a miracle)</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) to form (a word by inflection)</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) to prepare (especially food or a feast)</li></ol><li>to cause or bring about</li><li>to act or behave</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) to confer or grant</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) to treat; to behave towards</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) to fare or perform</li></ol><p>Usually held to be inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;makōn</i>, although borrowing from Old Saxon <i>makōn</i> has been suggested. Compare also Old Dutch <i>macon</i>, Old Frisian <i>makia</i>, and Old High German <i>mahhōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Macclesfield (a market town and civil parish of Cheshire East, Cheshire, England)</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;Maccles</i> (the genitive form of an unattested name <i>&#42;Maccel</i>) +&lrm; <i>feld</i> (“field”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>making, the act of making</li><li>doing</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>macian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>māþm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>capable</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stomach</li><li>maw</li><li>son</li><li>relative</li></ol><p>From the verb <i>magan</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;magō</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>maga</i> (West Frisian <i>mage</i>), Old Saxon <i>mago</i> (Low German <i>mage</i>), Middle Dutch <i>maghe</i> (Dutch <i>maag</i>), Old High German <i>mago</i> (German <i>Magen</i>), Old Norse <i>magi</i> (Swedish <i>mage</i>,</p><p>Norwegian <i>mage</i>, stomach). The Indo-European root is also the source of Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;makno-</i> (Welsh <i>megin</i> (“bellows”)), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mošьnā</i> (Old Church Slavonic <i>мошьна</i> (mošĭna), Russian <i>мошна́</i> (mošná, “pocket, bag”)), Baltic <i>*maka-</i> (Lithuanian <i>mãkas</i> (“purse”)).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;māg</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>mēch</i> (“relative, kinsman”), Old Saxon <i>māg</i> (“a relation”), Old High German <i>māg</i> (“relative, kinsman”), Old Norse <i>mágr</i> (“father-in-law”), Gothic <i>𐌼𐌴𐌲𐍃</i> (mēgs, “son-in-law”). More at may.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(auxiliary,&#32;with an infinitive) can, to be able</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) can (do something), to be able (to do something)</li><li>avail, prevail</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) To get along; to be in any state, or pass through any experience (often with a qualifier denoting the nature of one's state)</li><li>may, might</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;magan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maganą</i>. See there for cognates.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meċġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prevail</li></ol><p>Akin to Old English <i>magan</i> (“to be able to”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mǣġester</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>magu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>magudryht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boy, son</li><li>servant, retainer</li><li>man, hero, warrior</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;magu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maguz</i> (“boy”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mogʰus</i> (“fellow, bachelor, unmarried”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>magu</i> (“boy”), Old Norse <i>mǫgr</i> (“son, boy”), Gothic <i>𐌼𐌰𐌲𐌿𐍃</i> (magus, “child, boy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a troop of young men</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>magu</i> (“man, warrior”) +&lrm; <i>dryht</i> (“troop, army”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) servant, retainer</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) man, warrior</li></ol><p><i>magu</i> +&lrm; <i>þeġn</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>miht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mole (spot on the skin)</li><li>mark, spot</li><li>a suit, legal case, prosecution, defense</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic&#32;<i>&#42;mailą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Scots Gaelic, equivalent to English Malcolm.</li><li>Malcolm I, king of Scotland</li></ol><p>From Scottish Gaelic <i>Maol</i> <i>Chaluim</i> or <i>Calum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Malcolm.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bewitching</li><li>fascination</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;malskrungu</i>, equivalent to <i>&#42;malscrian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>malskrung</i> (“witchcraft”), Old High German <i>malscrunc</i> (“fascination, empty praise”). More at English <i>masker</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mealt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Manchester</li></ol><p>Unclear. The first element may be from a word related to Breton <i>mamm</i> (“mother; female animal; womb”), in reference to a breast-shaped hill.</p><p>The second part is known to be from <i>ceaster</i> (“city”),</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lethargy, deep or heavy sleep</li></ol><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mansfield (a town in Nottinghamshire, England).</li></ol><p>From a Celtic word meaning "breast-like hill" + Old English <i>feld</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lethargy, heavy or deep sleep</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deliberate, think through; design, plan out</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mamera</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think out, design</li><li>to reflect upon a matter; to brood</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;maimrōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maimrōną</i> (“to worry, be mindful”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)mer-</i> (“to resort to thinking, remember, take care”). Akin to Old English <i>māmor</i> (“lethargy, sleep”), <i>ġemimor</i> (“mindful”), <i>mimorian</i> (“to remember”). See <i>mimor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>one, you (<i>indefinite pronoun&#59; construed as a third-person singular</i>)</li><li>they, people (people in general)</li><li>someone, somebody (some unspecified person)</li><li>they (some unspecified group of people)</li><li><i>often used where modern English would use the passive voice</i></li><li>it</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mann</i></li><li>crime, sin, wickedness</li></ol><p>From <i>mann</i>.</p><p>See <i>mann</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mainą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perjury, false oath</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mainaiþaz</i>, equivalent to <i>mān</i> +&lrm; <i>āþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lethal fire (if <i>manbryne</i>) or criminal fire (if <i>mānbryne</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>mann</i> (“person”) +&lrm; <i>bryne</i> (“burning”) or <i>mān</i> (“crime”) +&lrm; <i>bryne</i> (“burning”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>producing people</li></ol><p><i>mann</i> +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plague, pestilence</li></ol><p><i>mann</i> +&lrm; <i>cwealm</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mancynn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humanity, mankind</li><li>a people; nation, race</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;manakunją</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>mankunni</i>, Old High German <i>mancunni</i>, and Old Norse <i>mannkyn</i>. Equivalent to <i>mann</i> (“human”) +&lrm; <i>cynn</i> (“race”). Compare <i>ielfcynn</i> (“elfkind”) and <i>entcynn</i> (“giant-kind”), which are formed similarly.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basket</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mandu</i> (“basket”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mandrake</li></ol><p>From Medieval Latin <i>mandragorās</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evil deed, crime, sin</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>mān</i> (“evil”) +&lrm; <i>dǣd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>doing-evil; wicked; reprehensible</li></ol><p>From <i>mān</i> (“shameful act; crime; wickedness”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;dǣde</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dēdiz</i> (“doing”). Compare Old Saxon <i>mēndādig</i>, Old High German <i>meintātig</i>. Equivalent to <i>mandǣd</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>maniġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meniġu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) wicked destroyer</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wicked, evil, dire</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>mān</i> (“crime, sin”) +&lrm; <i>-ful</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trader, merchant, dealer, monger</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>mangian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trade, traffic, do as a monger</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mangōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trade, traffic, business, commerce, dealing</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>mangian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a house of commerce or business</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>mangung</i> +&lrm; <i>hūs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring to mind what should be done or what should not be forgotten; to urge, to admonish</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;manōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;manōną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;men-</i> (“think”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>monia</i>, Old Saxon <i>manōn</i> (Dutch <i>manen</i>), Old High German <i>manōn</i> (German <i>mahnen</i>). The Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek <i>μένος</i> (ménos), Latin <i>mens</i>, Welsh <i>mynnu</i>, Russian <i>мнить</i> (mnitʹ), Lithuanian <i>minti</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>many</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>many</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;manag</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;managaz</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>manich</i> (West Frisian <i>mannich</i>), Old Saxon <i>manag</i> (Low German <i>männich</i>), Middle Dutch <i>mēnich</i> (Dutch <i>menig</i>), Old High German <i>manag</i> (German <i>manch</i>), Gothic <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (manags). The Proto-Indo-European root is also the source of Russian <i>мно́гий</i> (mnógij), Old Irish <i>menic</i> (Scottish Gaelic <i>minig</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>many, multiple, multi-</li></ol><p>From <i>maniġ</i> (“many, much”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>manifold, of many parts or kinds, complex, various, numerous</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) plural</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;managafalþaz</i>, equivalent to <i>maniġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-feald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>maniġfealdian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>maniġfeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to multiply, increase, make manifold</li></ol><p><i>maniġfeald</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>manifold, having many parts, of many kinds, various</li></ol><p>From <i>maniġfeald</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>manifoldly, in many ways</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) in the plural</li></ol><p>From <i>maniġfeald</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>complexity</li><li>abundance</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>maniġfeald</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>mancynn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without people, uninhabited, unpopulated; deserted</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mannlaus</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mannlausaz</i>, equivalent to <i>mann</i> (“person”) +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i> (“-less”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>mannlauss</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>courageously, nobly (in a person-befitting way, contrasting with reasonless, timid animals such as rats)</li></ol><p>Derived from an unattested adjective <i>&#42;manlīċ</i>. Equivalent to <i>&#42;manlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i> or <i>mann</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person</li><li>man (mankind)</li><li>(<i>rare&#32;or&#32;non-literary</i>) man (adult male)</li><li>the runic character ᛗ (/m/)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mann</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mann-</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>mon</i>, Old Saxon <i>mann</i>, Old Dutch <i>man</i>, Old High German <i>man</i>, Old Norse <i>maðr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌽𐌰</i> (manna).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Mann)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man</li><li>manna (food miraculously produced for the Israelites in the desert in the book of Exodus)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mann</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mann-</i>.</p><p>From Late Latin <i>manna</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>μάννα</i> (mánna), from Hebrew <i>מן</i> (mān, “'manna”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) "sons of men"; mankind</li></ol><p>From genitive plural of <i>mann</i> + plural of <i>bearn</i>. Has a parallel in Old Saxon <i>mannō barn</i>, possibly stemming from a Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mannō barnu</i>, with a possible analog in Old Norse <i>manna synir</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fine paid to the lord of a slain person</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>mann</i> +&lrm; <i>bōt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>manbǣre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) lord of people, liege lord</li></ol><p>From <i>mann</i> (“people, folk”) +&lrm; <i>dryhten</i> (“lord”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(euphemistic,&#32;plural only) genitals</li></ol><p>From <i>mannes</i> (“person's”) + <i>ġetawa</i> (“instruments, apparatus”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Son of Man (title of Jesus)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to man, garrison</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mannōną</i>. Equivalent to <i>mann</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>manlēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>manlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>manslaga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the worth or value of a man (apparently equal to 100 shillings)</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>mann</i> (“person”) +&lrm; <i>weorþ</i> (“worth, value”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homage</li></ol><p>From <i>man</i> (“person”) +&lrm; <i>rǣden</i> (“condition, state, terms, stipulation”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humanity, courtesy, kindness, chivalry</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mannskapi</i>, equivalent to <i>mann</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>guilty of a crime</li></ol><p>From <i>man</i> +&lrm; <i>scyldig</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>someone who kills a person: murderer, killer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;manaslagō</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>manslago</i> and Old High German <i>manslago</i>. The expected Old Frisian cognate, <i>monslaga</i>, exists but has come to mean "murder." Equivalent to <i>mann</i> (“person”) +&lrm; <i>slaga</i> (“killer”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homicide</li></ol><p>From <i>man</i> (“person”) +&lrm; <i>sleġe</i> (“slaying, murder”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(countable,&#32;uncountable) killing of a person: murder, killing</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;manaslahti</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>manslaht</i>. Equivalent to <i>mann</i> (“person”) +&lrm; <i>sliht</i> (“killing”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perjurer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mainaswarô</i>. Cognate with German <i>meinswero</i> and Old Norse <i>meinsvari</i>. Equivaent to <i>mān</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;swara</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to commit perjury, swear a false oath</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mainaswarjaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>mān</i> +&lrm; <i>swerian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mane</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;manu</i>. Near cognates include Old Norse <i>mǫn</i> and Old High German <i>mana</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mannweorþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kidnapper</li><li>a fine for kidnapping</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>mann</i> (“person”) +&lrm; <i>þēof</i> (“thief”). Compare Icelandic <i>mannræningi</i>, which is formed similarly.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mapultrēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mapulder</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maple</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mapulaz</i>, possibly a corruption or based on <i>&#42;aplaz</i> <i>&#42;trewą</i> (Old English <i>apuldor</i>, <i>æppeltrēow</i>).</p><p>Compare Old High German <i>mazaltra</i>, <i>mazzaltra</i> (German <i>Maßholder</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mapulder</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a maple tree</li></ol><p><i>&#42;mapul</i> +&lrm; <i>trēow</i></p></html>:/ˈmɑːrɑ/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>miċel</i>: more</li><li>greater</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;maiʀō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mark (as currency etc.)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;marką</i> (“mark, stamp”), possibly via Old Norse <i>mǫrk</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mark the Apostle</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Marcus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mare (evil spirit thought to torment people in their sleep)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;marā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mary</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Maria</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Μαρία</i> (María), <i>Μαριάμ</i> (Mariám), from Aramaic <i>מרים</i> (Maryām), corresponding to the Hebrew <i>מרים</i> (Miryām).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>market</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>mercātus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marble</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>marmstān</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>marmstān</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marble</li><li>a piece of marble</li></ol><p>From <i>marma</i> (“marble”) +&lrm; <i>stān</i> (“stone”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>martyr</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>March</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Mārtius</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>martyr</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ecclesiastical Latin <i>martyr</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>μάρτυρ</i> (mártur), later form of <i>μάρτυς</i> (mártus, “witness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a race or family of martyrs</li></ol><p>From <i>martyr</i> (“martyr”) +&lrm; <i>cynn</i> (“kind, race, family”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>martyrdom</li></ol><p>From <i>martyr</i> +&lrm; <i>-dōm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>martyrdom</li></ol><p>From <i>martyr</i> +&lrm; <i>-hād</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to martyr</li></ol><p>From <i>martyr</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an account of martyrs</li></ol><p>From <i>martyr</i> (“martyr”) +&lrm; <i>racu</i> (“account”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suffering as a martyr</li></ol><p>From <i>martyrian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>net</li><li>mash</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;maskā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maskwǭ</i> (“mask”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;maisk</i>, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maiską</i> (“mixture, mash”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>titmouse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;maisā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>merchant</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>miċel</i>; most</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Matthew the apostle</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Matthaeus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mattuc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mattock or pickaxe</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mattjuk</i> (“mattock, ploughshare”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;met-</i> (“to cut, reap”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Methuselah, the longest-lived biblical character</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Hebrew <i>מְתוּשֶׁלַח</i> (M'tušelaḥ)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Methuselah</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Hebrew <i>מְתוּשֶׁלַח</i> (M'tušelaḥ)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;māan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>masċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brewer's wort used in mixing, usually in a tub or vat; mashwort</li></ol><p>From <i>māx</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Mantes-la-Jolie (a town in Yvelines department, Île-de-France, France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old French <i>&#42;Meantes</i>, <i>&#42;Medantes</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>Medanta</i>, from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;medu</i> + <i>&#42;-anta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>maþelode</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worm; maggot</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;maþō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maþô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mat-</i>, <i>&#42;mot-</i>, used for insects and vermin.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>maðo</i> (Dutch <i>made</i> (“maggot, worm”)), Old High German <i>mado</i> (German <i>Made</i> (“maggot”)), Gothic <i>𐌼𐌰𐌸𐌰</i> (maþa); Old Norse had the diminutive form <i>maðkr</i> (dialectal English <i>mawk</i>; compare also maddock). The Indo-European root is also the source of Old Armenian <i>մաթիլ</i> (matʻil, “small louse”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to speak, declare, speak out, declaim</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;maþlōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maþlijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>precious object: treasure, jewel, valuable</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maiþmaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;moyth₂mos</i>, from <i>&#42;meyth₂-</i> (“to exchange”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>mēðom</i>, Old Norse <i>meiðmar</i>&nbsp;pl (“valuables”), Gothic <i>𐌼𐌰𐌹𐌸𐌼𐍃</i> (maiþms). The Proto-Indo-European root is also the source of Latin <i>mūtāre</i> (“to exchange”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) giver of treasures; generous man/ruler</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;maiþmagebō</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>mēðomgeƀo</i>. Equivalent to <i>māþm</i> (“treasure”) +&lrm; <i>ġiefa</i> (“giver”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasurer</li></ol><p><i>māþm</i> +&lrm; <i>hierde</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasury</li></ol><p><i>māþm</i> (“treasure”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>maþelode</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>māþm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>māþm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>māþmġiefa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mighty, emphatic</li><li>earnest, strenuous, firm</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;magul</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;magulaz</i>, equivalent to <i>magan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>miht</i></li></ol><p>From earlier <i>maht</i> due to breaking before [-ht], from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mahti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mahtiz</i>, <i>&#42;mahtuz</i> (“might, power”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mógʰtis</i>, <i>&#42;megʰ-</i> (“to allow, be able, help”). Cognates include Old Frisian <i>macht</i>, <i>mecht</i> (West Frisian <i>macht</i>), Middle Dutch <i>macht</i> (Dutch <i>macht</i>), Old High German <i>maht</i> (German <i>Macht</i>), Old Norse <i>máttr</i> (Icelandic <i>máttur</i>), Gothic <i>𐌼𐌰𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (mahts).</p><p>Inflection of <i>maġan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mihtlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mihtiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>meahtiġ</i>: mightier</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sand, chalk</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;malmaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sandy, chalky</li></ol><p>From <i>mealm</i> +&lrm; <i>-iht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>malmstone (a type of limestone)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>malt</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;malt</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maltą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>malthouse</li></ol><p>From <i>mealt</i> (“malt”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) mallow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;malwā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mearh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mark, sign, character</li><li>boundary, limit</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;marku</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;markō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Mierċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mearcung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mark</li><li>to notice</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;markōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;markōną</i>, from <i>&#42;markō</i> (“a mark”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>merkia</i> (“to notice”), Old Saxon <i>markon</i> (“to design”), Old High German <i>marchon</i> (“to limit”) (German <i>merken</i> (“to put a mark on”)), Old Norse <i>marka</i> (“put a mark on, observe”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>borderland</li></ol><p>From <i>mearc</i> (“boundary, limit”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a marking</li><li>a marking out, description</li><li>an arrangement, disposition</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;markungu</i>, equivalent to <i>mearcian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meord</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) marrow</li><li>alternative form of <i>mearh</i> (“horse”)</li><li>alternative form of <i>mearh</i> (“sausage”)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;maʀg</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mazgą</i>, <i>&#42;mazgaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mosgʰós</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mearh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horse, steed</li><li>sausage</li><li>alternative form of <i>mearg</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;marh</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>mar</i> (“horse”), <i>merie</i> (“mare”) (West Frisian <i>merje</i> (“mare”)), Old Saxon <i>merge</i>, <i>meriha</i> (“mare”), Dutch <i>merrie</i> (“mare”), Old High German <i>marah</i> (“horse”), <i>meriha</i> (“mare”) (German <i>Mähre</i> (“mare”)), Old Norse <i>marr</i> (“horse”) (Icelandic <i>mar</i>), <i>merr</i> (“mare”) (Swedish <i>märr</i> (“mare”)), Gothic <i>&#42;𐌼𐌰𐍂𐌷𐍃</i> (&#42;marhs). Beyond Germanic, cognate to Irish <i>marc</i> and Welsh <i>march</i> (“horse”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;marhu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mearþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tender, delicate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;maru</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;marwaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marten</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;marþuz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meatte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mat</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mattā</i> (“mat, mattress”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Anglian</i>) (<i>poetic</i>) alternative form of <i>mǣċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mǣċefisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mæċġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mix, stir; mingle</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps a denasalised variant of <i>menġan</i> (“to mix”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reward</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;miʀdu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>middle, mediocre, average (<i>often carrying a negative connotation</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mēdren</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mæþel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>medume</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>medeme</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>medumian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>medeme</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mēdren</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meduwyrt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Medway (a river in East Sussex and Kent, England)</li></ol><p>The first element <i>mede</i> may be related to <i>medu</i> (“mead”) or from Celtic. The second element is an ancient river name also found in modern English Wey.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Milan (a major city in modern Lombardy, Italy)</li></ol><p>Latin <i>Mediolānum</i> + <i>burg</i> (“city”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moderate, small, not great</li><li>trifling, venial, not important</li><li>lowly, mean, poor</li></ol><p><i>med-</i> +&lrm; <i>miċel</i> (“great, large”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Mǣdmōnaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>medmiċel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>medu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>medubenċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>medudrynċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meduwyrt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meduærn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>maternal; (<i>of lineage</i>) on the mother's side</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mōdrīn</i>, equivalent to <i>mōdor</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>móðerni</i> (“on the mother's side”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>infirm, weak, not especially healthy</li></ol><p><i>med-</i> +&lrm; <i>trum</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mead</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;medu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;meduz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;médʰu</i> (“sweet drink”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mead-bench, a bench in a hall</li></ol><p>From <i>medu</i> (“mead”) +&lrm; <i>benċ</i> (“bench”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>medudrynċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) mead-drink</li></ol><p>From <i>medu</i> (“mead”) +&lrm; <i>drynċ</i> (“drink”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) mead hall</li></ol><p>From <i>medu</i> (“mead”) +&lrm; <i>heall</i> (“hall”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not extreme: average, moderate, medium</li><li>decent</li></ol><p>Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;medumô</i>. A derivative of <i>med-</i> (akin to <i>mid</i> (“mid”)) + <i>-uma</i> (“superlative ending”). Akin to Old High German <i>metem</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>medume</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mete out, measure</li><li>to allot, assign, set</li><li>to moderate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;medumōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;medumōną</i>. Equivalent to <i>medume</i> (“average”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;hapax legomenon) mead-seat, mead-bench</li></ol><p>From <i>medu</i> +&lrm; <i>setl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meadowsweet</li></ol><p>From <i>medu</i> (“mead”) +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i> (“plant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;poetic) a banqueting house, a hall in which mead is drunk</li></ol><p>From <i>medu</i> (“mead”) +&lrm; <i>ærn</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not wise, foolish, stupid</li></ol><p>From <i>med-</i> ((often with negative connotation) middle-) +&lrm; <i>wīs</i>. Compare Modern English <i>midwit</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>mǣġ</i> (“(a) relative”) + <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>miht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mihtiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mǣl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>giving milk</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;melkaz</i>. As with <i>melcan</i>, the monophthong is inherited; the alternative form <i>meolc</i> has its diphthong leveled in from the noun.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to milk</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;melkan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;melkaną</i>. Related to Old Frisian <i>melka</i>, Old Saxon <i>&#42;melkan</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;melkan</i>, Old High German <i>melkan</i>. Note that this is the original form, as *e and *i before *l did not break unless *h followed: thus the alternative form <i>meolcan</i> has its diphthong by analogy with the noun <i>meolc</i>, which has its diphthong from back umlaut.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>informer</li></ol><p><i>meldian</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inform; notify; report</li><li>to declare; announce</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;melþōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honeydew, nectar</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;milidauw</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>melu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>milisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to melt</li><li>to digest</li><li>to be consumed (destroyed completely)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;meltan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;meltaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)mel-</i> (“to beat, crush, grind”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>melting</li><li>digestion</li></ol><p><i>meltan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>melting</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flour</li><li>meal, the edible part of a grain or pulse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;melu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;melwą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;melh₂-</i> (“grind”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meolc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meċġan</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>menġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>necklace, collar</li><li>ornament, jewel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;manī</i>, <i>&#42;manni</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;manją</i> (“necklace”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mony-</i> (“withers, crest, mane”). Cognate with Old High German <i>menni</i> (“necklace”), Old Norse <i>men</i> (“necklace, jewelry”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meniġu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mix, to mingle or blend</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mangijan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>mendza</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;mengen</i> (Dutch <i>mengen</i>), Old Saxon <i>mengian</i>, Old High German <i>mengen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meniġu</i></li></ol></html>:/ˈmen.ju/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meniġu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>maniġ-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>meniġu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meniġu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>maniġfeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meniġu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crowd</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;managī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;managį̄</i>. Equivalent to <i>maniġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meniġu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meniġu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>human nature</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>human</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>people</li><li>a people; nation, race</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mannisk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;manniskaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>mennisk</i>, Old Saxon <i>mennisk</i>, Old High German <i>mennisc</i>, Old Norse <i>mennskr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌽𐌹𐍃𐌺𐍃</i> (mannisks). Equivalent to <i>mann</i> (“a human”) +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i> (“-ish”).</p><p>Nominalization of the adjective.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>human being</li></ol><p>Literally "human person." Cognate with Old Norse <i>mennskr maðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>humane</li><li>human</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;manniskalīk</i>. Equivalent to <i>mennisċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humanity (humaneness, opposite of inhumanity)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>mennisċlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humanity (meaning "the state of being human", not "mankind"; the word for "mankind" was <i>mancynn</i>)</li><li>Incarnation (Christian doctrine that Jesus Christ was fully divine and fully human)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;manniskanassī</i>. Equivalent to <i>mennisċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mennisċnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humanity</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mantle (kind of cloak)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>medu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>medu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>meduheall</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meox</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milk</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;meluk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;meluks</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂melǵ-</i>. See there for cognates.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>melcan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>container for holding milk</li></ol><p>From <i>meolc</i> (“milk”) +&lrm; <i>fæt</i> (“container”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having the color of milk</li></ol><p>From <i>meolc</i> (“milk”) +&lrm; <i>hwīt</i> (“white”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to milk</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>meolc</i> (“milk”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>melu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meolc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>melu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meolc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reward, payment, wage</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;miʀdu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mizdō</i> (“meed”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;misdʰéh₂</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mey-</i> (“to exchange”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meord</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moss</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;meus</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;meusą</i>, whence also Old High German <i>mios</i>. Related to Old English <i>mos</i> (English <i>moss</i>) through Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;musą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>metan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>metod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mattuc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mattuc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>metod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a maid; damsel; virgin; woman</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mawilā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung, excrement</li><li>muck</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mihs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a dung-barrow or basket for carrying dung</li></ol><p>From <i>meox</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;bearwe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>mixen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place in England, known in modern English as Merton.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mearc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mereċe</i></li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Merce)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Mierċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mierċels</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Mierċisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mercia</li></ol><p>From the genitive of <i>Merce</i> (“Mercians”) + <i>land</i> (“land”). Literally "land of the Mercians."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mearcung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lake</li><li>pool</li><li><i>(poetic or in compounds)</i> sea</li><li>a mare (female horse)</li><li>a female camel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mari</i> (“sea, lake”).</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>mere</i> (West Frisian <i>mar</i>), Old Saxon <i>meri</i> (Low German <i>Meer</i>), Dutch <i>meer</i>, Old High German <i>meri</i> (German <i>Meer</i>), Old Norse <i>marr</i> (Swedish <i>mar</i>). The Indo-European root is also the source of Latin <i>mare</i>, Old Irish <i>muir</i> (Breton <i>mor</i>), Old Church Slavonic <i>море</i> (more) (Russian <i>мо́ре</i> (móre)), Lithuanian <i>mãre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>march, smallage (plant)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;marik</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>death at sea, death by drowning</li></ol><p>From <i>mere</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>dēaþ</i> (“death”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea beast; aquatic animal</li></ol><p>From <i>mere</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>dēor</i> (“animal, beast”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seafarer</li><li>sailor</li></ol><p>From <i>mere</i> +&lrm; <i>fara</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surging of the waves; sea billow</li><li>seashore; surf</li></ol><p>From <i>mere</i> +&lrm; <i>faroþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea-fish</li></ol><p>From <i>mere</i> + <i>fisċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>merefisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) flood of water, deluge</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) body of water, flood, ocean</li></ol><p>From <i>mere</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>flōd</i> (“flood”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pearl</li></ol><p>Probably from Late Latin <i>margarita</i>, recast by folk etymology as though <i>mere</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>grot</i> (“particle”). Compare also Old Saxon <i>merigrita</i>, <i>merigriota</i> (“pearl”), Old High German <i>merigrioz</i> (“pearl”), Gothic <i>𐌼𐌰𐍂𐌹𐌺𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍄𐌿𐍃</i> (marikreitus, “pearl”), and contrast Old Norse <i>hafnýra</i> (“pearl”, literally “sea-kidney”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meregrot</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) ship</li></ol><p>Kenning of <i>mere</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>henġest</i> (“steed”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Noah's ark (literally 'sea-house')</li></ol><p>From <i>mere</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) sailor</li></ol><p><i>mere</i> +&lrm; <i>līþend</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>siren</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meremennen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>siren</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) lamprey</li></ol><p>From <i>mere</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>nǣdre</i> (“snake”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>merenǣdre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) lamprey</li></ol><p>From <i>mere</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>nǣdre</i> (“snake”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dolphin or porpoise</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mariswīn</i>. Equivalent to <i>mere</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>swīn</i> (“pig”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>meriswīn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>weary by sea</li></ol><p>From <i>mere</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>wēriġ</i> (“weary”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a witch associated with water</li><li>mermaid</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mariwīb</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mariwībą</i> (“mermaid, siren”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>mere</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) the Meroving, the Merovingian king</li></ol><p>Related to Medieval Latin <i>Merovingus</i>, ultimately from Frankish <i>&#42;Mārīwīg</i> + <i>&#42;-ing</i>. This word appears in the <i>Beowulf</i> manuscript as three separate words <i>mere wio ingas</i>, which is taken by scholars to indicate that the scribe who copied the poem did not recognize the word.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mearh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>myrġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meriġen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>myrgþ</i> (“mirth”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to purify; refine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;marjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>morgen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>myrġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morning</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;morgin, &#42;murgin</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mersċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mersċland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a marsh, bog, or swamp</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;marisk</i> (“lake-like, marshy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>marshy</li></ol><p>From <i>mersċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marshland</li></ol><p>From <i>mersċ</i> (“marsh”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marshmallow</li></ol><p>From <i>mersċ</i> (“marsh”) +&lrm; <i>mealwe</i> (“mallow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mǣrsian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mǣrsung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mearu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mearþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) fertiliser, manure</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps derived from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mihsu</i>, the nominative/accusative plural of <i>&#42;mihs</i> (“dung, excrement; urine”), reanalysed as a singular noun. Compare Old Frisian <i>mēse</i> (“urine”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat, feed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mōsijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mȳse</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mǣst</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mǣst</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mæst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ġemet</i> (“measure”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to measure</li><li>to compare</li><li>to compare (<i>to something</i> = <i>wiþ</i> + accusative or <i>tō</i> + dative)</li><li>to meet, encounter</li><li>to find</li><li>to draw or paint</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;metan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;metaną</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mōtijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mōtijaną</i>.</p><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;maitijan</i> (“to sculpt”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maitijaną</i>, a byform of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maitaną</i> (“to cut”). Compare Old High German <i>meizan</i> (“to hew, cut, chisel”), Old Norse <i>meita</i> (“to cut”), Gothic <i>𐌼𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (maitan, “to cut”). Doublet of <i>mǣtan</i> (“to appear in a dream”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mati</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to moderate, temper</li><li>to regulate, govern</li><li>to consider</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;metagōn</i> (“to moderate”). Equivalent to <i>metan</i> (“to measure”) +&lrm; <i>-eġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>metigia</i> (“to moderate, mitigate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moderation, temperance</li><li>regulation, a regulation</li><li>governance</li></ol><p><i>metegian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without food, lacking food; foodless</li></ol><p>From <i>mete</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>painter</li></ol><p>From <i>mētan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hexameter verse</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>meter</i> (“meter”) +&lrm; <i>fers</i> (“vers”), both elements borrowed from Latin.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poet</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>meter</i> (“(poetic) meter”) +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“wright”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of measure</li><li>a spoonful</li></ol><p>From <i>mete</i> +&lrm; <i>sacca</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knife, especially one used to cut food</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;matisahs</i> (“food knife”). Equivalent to <i>mete</i> +&lrm; <i>seax</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>craving or desire for food; appetite</li></ol><p>From <i>mete</i> +&lrm; <i>sōcn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an edible mushroom</li></ol><p>From <i>mete</i> +&lrm; <i>swamm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mealtime</li></ol><p>From <i>mete</i> +&lrm; <i>tīd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wanting or needing food</li></ol><p>From <i>mete</i> +&lrm; <i>þearfende</i> (present participle of <i>þurfan</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a food-officer; one whose duty is to look after food; sewer</li></ol><p>From <i>mete</i> +&lrm; <i>þeġn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Meaning unclear, perhaps "strong from taking food"</li></ol><p><i>mete</i> (“food”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;þiht</i> (“tight”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>metegian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>metegung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>metsian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>picture: drawing, painting</li><li>meeting, assembly</li></ol><p>From <i>mētan</i> (“to draw, paint”) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p><p>From <i>mētan</i> (“to meet, encounter”) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;usually in compounds) fate, destiny; death</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) maker, creator; God</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;metōduz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;med-</i> (“to measure”). The word may originally have indicated "fate, destiny". Compare Old Saxon <i>metod</i> (“creator, God”), Old Norse <i>mjǫtuðr</i> (“God; fate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fate, doom</li><li>death</li></ol><p>From <i>metod</i> +&lrm; <i>sċeaft</i> (“creation, origin”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feed, provide with food</li><li>to furnish or supply with provisions</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;matisōn</i>, equivalent to <i>mete</i> +&lrm; <i>-sian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food, provision</li></ol><p><i>metsian</i> (“to feed”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mete</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mattuc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>medtrum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>metod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>meþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>mæþel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>weary, exhausted, tired</li><li>troubled, sad</li><li>troublesome, causing weariness</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mōþī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mōþijaz</i>, <i>&#42;mōþuz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>móðr</i> (“tired; moody”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mæþel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to weary, fatigue, or impair</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mōþīgōn</i>, equivalent to <i>&#42;mēþe</i> (“weariness”) +&lrm; <i>-gian</i> and/or <i>mēþiġ</i> (“weary”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to grow weary</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mōþijēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wearied, fatigued, exhausted</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mōþīg</i>, equivalent to <i>&#42;mēþe</i> (“weariness”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tiredness, weariness</li></ol><p>From <i>mēþe</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mēþnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>miċel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>miċelian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>big, large; great, physical context</li><li>great, abstract context</li><li>much, a lot</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mikil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mikilaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méǵh₂s</i> (“big, great, stout”). The Germanic is cognate with Ancient Greek <i>μέγας</i> (mégas, “great”). Compare Swedish <i>mycket</i> (much).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>large, great, magni-; big</li></ol><p>From <i>miċel</i> (“great, large, much”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>much</li></ol><p>From <i>miċel</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>way too, far too</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become great</li><li>to make great</li><li>to extol, magnify</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mikilōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>great, grand, magnificent, splendid, illustrious</li></ol><p>From <i>miċel</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>greatly, grandly, splendidly</li><li>exceedingly</li></ol><p>From <i>miċel</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>magnanimous</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mikilamōd</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>mihhilmuot</i>. Equivalent to <i>miċel-</i> +&lrm; <i>mōd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>size</li><li>quantity</li><li>greatness, magnificence</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mikilanassī</i>. Equivalent to <i>miċel</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>miċelnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>size</li></ol><p>From <i>miċel</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gluttonous, eating a lot</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>miċel-</i> (“great”) +&lrm; <i>ǣta</i> (“eater”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i> (adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mygge</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urine</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>miċġerne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fat located around the kidneys or bowels; suet</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;midġerne</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;midigarnī</i> (“bellyfat”). By surface analysis, <i>mid-</i> +&lrm; <i>grin</i> (“snare”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>migoþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Latin</li><li>Saint Michael</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Michaēl</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Μῐχᾱήλ</i> (Mĭkhāḗl), from Biblical Hebrew <i>מִיכָאֵל</i> (mîḵāʾēl, literally “who is like God?”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>miċelian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>with</li><li>by</li><li>as</li><li>on</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;midi</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>mid</i>, Old High German <i>mit</i>, Old Norse <i>með</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thereby</li><li>altogether</li><li>totally</li><li>utterly</li></ol><p>From <i>mid</i> (“with”) +&lrm; <i>ealle</i> (“all”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>by that</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>when, since, seeing that, given that</li></ol><p>From <i>mid þȳ</i> +&lrm; <i>þe</i>, literally "by that which".</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>with, in conjunction with; together (with)</li><li>the middle part</li><li>between</li></ol><p>From <i>miþ</i>, <i>mid</i> (“with, together”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>middle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;midi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>between, in the middle</li></ol><p>From <i>onmiddan</i>, from <i>on</i> + oblique (dative) case of <i>midde</i> (“middle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>middanġeard</i></li></ol><p>Late alteration of <i>middanġeard</i>, confusing the second element <i>ġeard</i> with <i>eard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the world</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;midjagardaz</i>, equivalent to <i>midd</i> (“mid, middle”) +&lrm; <i>ġeard</i> (“yard; enclosed land; realm”), although the second element's confusion or conflation with <i>eard</i> (“earth”) has occurred since Old English. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;médʰyos</i> (“middle”) and <i>&#42;gʰórdʰos</i> (“enclosure”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>middilgard</i>, Old High German <i>mittilgart</i> and <i>mittangart</i>, Old Norse <i>Miðgarðr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌼𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌿𐌽𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (midjungards).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the middle (used only in the phrase "on middan")</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;midjǭ</i> (“middle, center”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>middle, in the middle</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>middle, center</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;midlą</i>, <i>&#42;midilą</i>, <i>&#42;medalą</i> (“middle”), a diminutive of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;midjō</i> (“middle, midst”) (compare <i>&#42;midjaz</i> (“mid, middle”, adjective)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;médʰyos</i> (“between, in the middle, middle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>middle</li></ol><p>From <i>middel</i> (“middle”) +&lrm; <i>dǣl</i> (“part, portion”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Middle Angles of Leicestershire</li></ol><p><i>middel</i> +&lrm; <i>Engle</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>middle finger</li></ol><p>From <i>middel</i> (“middle”) +&lrm; <i>finger</i> (“finger”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>midniht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Middle Saxons</li><li>Middlesex</li></ol><p><i>middel</i> +&lrm; <i>Seaxan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Middleton, the name of several locations in England</li></ol><p>From <i>middel</i> (“middle”) +&lrm; <i>tūn</i> (“town”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>midniht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>halfway (to) [<i>with</i> genitive]</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Christmas Day</li><li>a day of Christmas</li></ol><p>Possibly a shortening of <i>middes wintres mæssedæġ</i>, which appears earlier. The closely analogous term <i>middes wintres mæsseniht</i> (“night before Christmas”) even appears in the Early West Saxon of the <i>Parker Chronicle</i> (late 9th century).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Christmas Day</li><li>a day of Christmas</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the night before Christmas</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>midward, the middle of</li></ol><p><i>midde</i> (“middle”) +&lrm; <i>-weard</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Middelseaxan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noon, midday</li><li>the middle of the day</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;midjaz dagaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>mid-</i> +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sext</li></ol><p>From <i>middæġ</i> (“midday”) +&lrm; <i>sang</i> (“song, liturgical service”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to count among, include</li></ol><p><i>mid-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġetellan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>midriff, entrails, diaphragm</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;midihrif</i>, equivalent to <i>mid-</i> +&lrm; <i>hrif</i> (“womb, belly”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>miċġerne</i></li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;midġirne</i>, equivalent to <i>mid-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;ġierne, &#42;ġyrne, &#42;ġirne</i>, the second element possibly from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;garni</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garniz</i>, a byform of <i>&#42;garnō</i> (“intestine, gut”). Compare related <i>miċġerne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mīþl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>middel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>midnight</li><li>the middle of the night</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;midjanahts</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>midhrif</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who speaks on behalf of another</li></ol><p>From <i>mid-</i> +&lrm; <i>spreca</i> (“speaker”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>midsumor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>midsummer</li><li>the middle of the summer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;midisumar</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;midjasumaraz</i>, equivalent to <i>mid-</i> +&lrm; <i>sumor</i>. Cognate with German <i>Mittsommer</i> and Swedish <i>midsommar</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the middle of winter; midwinter, Christmas</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;midjaz wintruz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>presence</li><li>society</li><li>participation, cooperation</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;midiwisti</i>, equivalent to <i>mid-</i> +&lrm; <i>wist</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>mitiwist</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a coworker, cooperator</li></ol><p>From <i>mid-</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“wright, worker”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consent</li></ol><p>From <i>mid-</i> +&lrm; <i>þeahtian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sympathize, sympathize with</li></ol><p>From <i>mid-</i> +&lrm; <i>þolian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>miht</i></li></ol><p>From i-umlaut of <i>meaht</i>, from earlier <i>maht</i> due to breaking before [-ht], from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mahti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mahtiz</i>, <i>&#42;mahtuz</i> (“might, power”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mógʰtis</i>, <i>&#42;megʰ-</i> (“to allow, be able, help”). Cognates include Old Frisian <i>macht</i>, <i>mecht</i> (West Frisian <i>macht</i>), Middle Dutch <i>macht</i> (Dutch <i>macht</i>), Old High German <i>maht</i> (German <i>Macht</i>), Old Norse <i>máttr</i> (Icelandic <i>máttur</i>), Gothic <i>𐌼𐌰𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (mahts).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>West Saxon</i>) to melt</li><li>(<i>West Saxon</i>) to digest</li><li>(<i>West Saxon</i>) to consume (destroy completely)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;maltijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maltijaną</i>, causative of <i>&#42;meltaną</i> (whence Old English <i>meltan</i>). Cognate with Icelandic <i>melta</i> (“to digest”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Mercians</li><li>Mercia</li></ol><p>Literally, “border people”, from <i>mearc</i> (“border”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>target</li><li>signet, seal</li><li>trophy</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>mearcian</i> +&lrm; <i>-els</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Mercian</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a Mercian</li></ol><p><i>Mierċe</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mare, female horse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;marhijā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;marhijō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hinder</li><li>to squander, waste</li><li>to err</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;marzijaną</i> (“to disturb, hinder”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mers-</i> (“to annoy, disturb, neglect, forget, ignore”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to urinate, piss, pee</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mīgan</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>&#42;mīga</i>, Old Saxon <i>&#42;mīgan</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;mīgan</i>, Old Norse <i>míga</i>, also Latin <i>meiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urine</li></ol><p>From <i>mīgan</i> +&lrm; <i>-oþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>migoþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Michahel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>power, ability</li><li>strength, power</li><li>virtue</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mighty, powerful</li><li>possible</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>mieht</i>, i-umlaut of <i>meaht</i>, from earlier <i>maht</i> due to breaking before [-ht], from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mahti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mahtiz</i>, <i>&#42;mahtuz</i> (“might, power”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mógʰtis</i>, <i>&#42;megʰ-</i> (“to allow, be able, help”). Cognates include Old Frisian <i>macht</i>, <i>mecht</i> (West Frisian <i>macht</i>), Middle Dutch <i>macht</i> (Dutch <i>macht</i>), Old High German <i>maht</i> (German <i>Macht</i>), Old Norse <i>máttr</i> (Icelandic <i>máttur</i>), Gothic <i>𐌼𐌰𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (mahts).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mihtlēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mighty, powerful</li><li>possible</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mahtilīk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mahtilīkaz</i>. Related to Old High German <i>mahtlīh</i>, Old Norse <i>máttuligr</i>. Equivalent to <i>miht</i> (“power, ability”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mihtlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>powerful, mighty, able</li><li>possible</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mahtīgaz</i>, equivalent to <i>miht</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>mechtich</i>, Old Saxon <i>mahtīg</i>, Old High German <i>mahtīc</i>, Old Norse <i>máttugr</i> ~ <i>máttigr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌼𐌰𐌷𐍄𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (mahteigs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>mihtiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mightless, weak, powerless, impotent; without might or power</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mahtilausaz</i>, equivalent to <i>miht</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mihtelīċ</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mahtilīkaz</i>. Related to Old High German <i>mahtlīh</i>, Old Norse <i>máttuligr</i>. Equivalent to <i>miht</i> (“power, ability”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>mightily, powerfully, in power; with might</li><li>possible; possibly</li></ol><p>From <i>miht</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>mihtlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mile</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>mīlia</i>, plural of the numeral <i>mīlle</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meolc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mild, gentle, kind, merciful, generous; meek</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mildijaz</i> (“gentle, mild”).</p><p>Akin to Old Frisian <i>milde</i>, Middle Dutch <i>milde</i> (Dutch <i>mild</i>), Old High German <i>milti</i> (German <i>mild</i>), Old Norse <i>mildr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌼𐌹𐌻𐌳𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (mildiþa, “kindness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honeydew, nectar</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;milidauw</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>graciously, kindly, mercifully</li></ol><p>From <i>milde</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>miltlīhho</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>milde</i> (“mild, gentle”) and <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>kindhearted, gentle</li><li>merciful</li></ol><p><i>milde</i> +&lrm; <i>-heort</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>mildheort</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>mildheort</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>compassionate, merciful</li></ol><p>From <i>mildheort</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>kindly, compassionately; mercifully</li></ol><p>From <i>mildheort</i> + <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mercy</li><li>compassion, pity</li></ol><p>From <i>mildheort</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mildheortnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>mildheort</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>milts</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>milde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>milde</i> (“mild, gentle”) +&lrm; <i>þrȳþ</i> (“strength”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal</i>) honeyed</li><li>sweet</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mili</i> (“honey”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mieltan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spleen</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;meltǭ</i> (“spleen”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>myltestre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Northumbrian</i>) alternative form of <i>mildheortnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mercy, grace, kindness, favour, mildness</li><li>joy</li></ol><p>From <i>miltsian</i>. Alternatively, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mildissju</i>, equivalent to <i>milde</i> +&lrm; <i>-s</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have mercy on, take pity on; show compassion or grace</li><li>to soften, mollify; mitigate</li><li>to make merciful</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>mildsian</i>, equivalent to <i>milde</i> +&lrm; <i>-sian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mercy, pity, compassion</li><li>an instance of showing mercy; a pardon, indulgence</li></ol><p>From <i>milts</i> or <i>miltsian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) a sword forged by Weland, a smith of legend</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remember, keep in the mind or memory</li><li>to commemorate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mī̆mrōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mīmrōną</i> (“to be mindful, remember”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)mer-</i> (“to remember, be mindful”). Akin to Saterland Frisian <i>mīmerje</i> (“to ponder, reflect on”), Middle Dutch <i>mîmeren</i> (“to ponder, reflect on”), Old English <i>ġemimor</i> (“mindful”). More at remember, mammer.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>my</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>small</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mīn</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>mīn</i>, Old Saxon <i>mīn</i> (Dutch <i>mijn</i>), Old High German <i>mīn</i> (German <i>mein</i>), Old Norse <i>mínn</i> (Swedish <i>min</i>), Gothic <i>𐌼𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (meins).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;minniz</i> (“small”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;min-</i> (“small”). Akin to Old High German <i>minniro</i> (“smaller”) (German <i>minder</i>), Old Norse <i>minni</i> (“smaller”) (Icelandic <i>minni</i>, <i>minnr</i>), Gothic <i>𐌼𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌹𐌶𐌰</i> (minniza, “younger”), <i>𐌼𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (mins, “young”), Latin <i>minor</i> (“smaller”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smallness; abjectness</li><li>pusillanimity, timidity, cowardice</li></ol><p><i>min</i> (“small”) +&lrm; <i>-dōm</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>petty</li></ol><p>From <i>min</i> (small) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to diminish, lessen, become small</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;minnisōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;minnisōną</i> (“to make smaller”), from <i>&#42;minniz</i> (“small, less”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(e)mey-</i> (“small, little”). Equivalent to <i>min</i> (“small”) +&lrm; <i>-sian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mint</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mintā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meolc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>mirce</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>murky, dark in color</li><li>dark, evil</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darkness</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mirkwī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;merkuz</i> (“dark”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mergʷ-</i> (“to flicker; to darken; to be dark”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>token, sign</li><li>signet, seal</li><li>mark, marked location</li><li>trophy</li></ol><p><i>mearc</i> (“mark”) +&lrm; <i>-els</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>myrgþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>myrġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>myrgþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>bad, badly, wrong, wrongly</li><li>lack or failure</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;missa-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;missa-</i>. Akin to Old English <i>missan</i> (“to miss”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do wrong to; to bid amiss, abuse, mistreat, ill-use, offend [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p>From <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>bēodan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miscarriage, abortion</li></ol><p><i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>byrd</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to injure, afflict</li></ol><p>Origin uncertain and obscure, but likely related to Old Norse <i>miski</i> (“misdeed, wrong, offense, harm”). Perhaps dissimilated from earlier Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;missikōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;missikōną</i> (“to do harm or damage to, offend”), equivalent to <i>miss</i> +&lrm; <i>-cian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) a wrong declaration, a mistake or variation in pleading before a court, or a fine exacted for this mistake</li></ol><p>Either from an unattested <i>&#42;miscennan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i> or <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>cennung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mix</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;miskōn</i>, <i>&#42;miskijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;miskijaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;meyḱ-</i> (“to knead; mix”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>miscweþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak amiss or wrongly; to misspeak</li><li>to curse</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>cweþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pervert</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċyrran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to offend, transgress, do wrongly; misdo</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;missadōną</i> (“to do wrongly”), equivalent to <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>dōn</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>misdūa</i>, Old High German <i>missatuon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a misdeed, transgression, offence</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;missadēdiz</i>, equivalent to <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>dǣd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>missenlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arrange or order incorrectly</li><li>to misconduct</li></ol><p>From <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>fadian</i> (“to arrange”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go astray, err, transgress</li><li>to fare badly, have ill success</li></ol><p>From <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>faran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mistake, fault, misdeed</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fail to take</li><li>to mistake</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>fōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to neglect, slight</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>healdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disobey, hear amiss, give no attention (to someone speaking)</li></ol><p>From <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>hīeran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pervert</li><li>to turn inwards, invert</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>hweorfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mishīeran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bad teaching or doctrine</li><li>an evil suggestion</li></ol><p>From <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>lār</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead a bad life</li></ol><p>From <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>libban</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>missenlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>diversely, differently, in various ways</li></ol><p><i>mislīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to displease</li></ol><p>From <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>līcian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diversity, variety</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>mislīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mislibban</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mislead, lead astray; deceive; bewile</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;missalaidijaną</i> (“to mislead”), equivalent to <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to teach wrongly</li><li>to persuade someone to do something wrong</li></ol><p><i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣran</i> (“to learn”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to counsel amiss, give bad advice; rede</li><li>to misread (riddles), misinterpret, give a wrong answer to</li></ol><p>From <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loss</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;miss</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;miss-</i> (“loss, want”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;meyth₂-</i> (“to change, replace”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>missir</i>, <i>missa</i> (“loss”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to miss (fail to hit) <i>(+ genitive)</i></li><li>to not notice something <i>(+ dative)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;missijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;missijaną</i> (“to go wrong”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mey-</i> (“to change”). Akin to Old Frisian <i>missa</i>, Old High German <i>missen</i>, Old Norse <i>missa</i>. Akin to Old English <i>miss</i> (“loss, absence”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clothe improperly</li></ol><p>From <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċrȳdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dissimilar, different, varied, manifold, diverse</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>missendlīċ</i>, equivalent to <i>missan</i> +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>differently, variously, diversely</li></ol><p>From <i>missen</i> (“different”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i> (“-ly”). Compare <i>mislīċe</i> (“differently”), from <i>mis-</i> (“diversely, in different ways”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) half-year</li></ol><p>Cognate with Old Norse <i>misseri</i> (“a season (of six months)”). Possibly related to <i>mæl</i> from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mēlą</i> (“measure, time, occasion”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;meh₁-</i> (“to measure”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to succeed badly</li></ol><p>From <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>spōwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to murmur, grumble</li></ol><p>From <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>sprecan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fog</li><li>mist</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mihstaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mistletoe</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mistil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mistilaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birdlime</li></ol><p>From <i>mistel</i> (“mistletoe”) +&lrm; <i>lām</i> (“clay”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) mistletoe</li></ol><p>From <i>mistel</i> (“mistletoe”) +&lrm; <i>tān</i> (“toe”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>missenlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misty gloom</li></ol><p>From <i>mist</i> (“mist”) +&lrm; <i>glōm</i> (“gloom”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a veil or covering of mist</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>mist</i> (“mist, fog”) +&lrm; <i>helm</i> (“helmet”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow dim</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to turn out badly</li></ol><p>From <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>tīdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>misty</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mistig</i> (“misty”). Equivalent to <i>mist</i> (“mist”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dissuade</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>tyhtan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to teach wrongly; teach amiss; misteach</li></ol><p>From <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>tǣċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pervert, apply to a wrong use, abuse</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to turn in a wrong direction, be perverted</li></ol><p>From <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>wendan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write incorrectly, make a mistake in writing; miswrite</li></ol><p>From <i>mis-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrītan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mid</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mite, small insect</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mītā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mītǭ</i> (“biting insect”, literally “cutter”), from <i>&#42;maitaną</i> (“to cut”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a dry and liquid measure of volume</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meox</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mixen; a dungheap; a dunghill</li></ol><p>From <i>meohx</i>, <i>meox</i> (“dung, filth”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mīgan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mīganą</i> (“to urinate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mid</i> (“with”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;midi</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>mid</i>, Old High German <i>mit</i>, Old Norse <i>með</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mid</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide, conceal, dissemble</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mīþan</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>mīthan</i> (Middle Low German <i>mîden</i>), Dutch <i>mijden</i>, Old High German <i>mīdan</i> (German <i>meiden</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>equestrianism</i>) a bit; curb (of a bridle)</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) a thong connecting an oar to the pin</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;minþil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;minþilą</i> (“teeth, denture, mouth”), of uncertain origin. Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;men-t-</i>, from <i>&#42;men-</i> (“to stick out, project”) or from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;menth₂-</i> (“to stir, grind, chew”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mind</li><li>heart, spirit</li><li>state of mind, mood</li><li><i>(in poetry and compounds)</i> courage, pride, zeal, or anger</li><li>affection</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mōdaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mē-</i>, <i>&#42;mō-</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>muot</i> (German <i>Mut</i>), Old Saxon <i>mōd</i>, Old Dutch <i>muot</i> (Dutch <i>(ge)moed</i>), Old Norse <i>móðr</i> (“anger, grief”) (Swedish <i>mod</i>), Gothic <i>𐌼𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (mōþs, “anger, emotion”). The Proto-Indo-European root was also the source of Ancient Greek <i>μῶθαι</i> (môthai) and Latin <i>mōs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anxious at heart, grieving</li></ol><p>From <i>mōd</i> (“mind”) +&lrm; <i>cariġ</i> (“grieving”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mōdcariġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mental power; skill; intelligence; shrewdness; cleverness</li></ol><p>From <i>mōd</i> +&lrm; <i>cræft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>possessing mental power; intelligent; skilled; judicious; having good judgement; skillful</li></ol><p>From <i>mōdcræft</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i> or <i>mōd</i> +&lrm; <i>cræftiġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mōdor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mōdorlēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mōdorlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imagination</li></ol><p>From <i>mōd</i> (“mind”) +&lrm; <i>hīwung</i> (“shaping, illusion”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>mōdiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) thought, intention</li></ol><p>From <i>mod</i> +&lrm; <i>gehygd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thought</li></ol><p>From <i>mōd</i> (“mind”) +&lrm; <i>ġeþanc</i> (“thought”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>proud</li><li>reckless</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) brave, courageous</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mōdagaz</i> (“courageous”), equivalent to <i>mōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>moedich</i> (Dutch <i>moedig</i>), Old High German <i>-muoti</i> (in compounds) (German <i>mutig</i>), Old Norse <i>móðugr</i>, Swedish <i>modig</i>, Danish <i>modig</i>, Gothic <i>𐌼𐍉𐌳𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (mōdags).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brave-looking</li></ol><p>From <i>mōdiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pride</li></ol><p><i>mōdiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mōdiġnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mother</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mōder</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mōdēr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méh₂tēr</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>mōder</i>, Old Saxon <i>mōdar</i>, Old Dutch <i>muoder</i>, Old High German <i>muoter</i>, Old Norse <i>móðir</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>motherless</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mōdērlausaz</i>, equivalent to <i>mōdor</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>motherly, maternal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mōdērlīkaz</i> (“motherly, maternal”), equivalent to <i>mōdor</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love for one's mother</li></ol><p><i>mōdor</i> +&lrm; <i>lufu</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mother's Night, a midwinter feast described by Bede</li></ol><p><i>mōdra</i> (“mothers'”) +&lrm; <i>niht</i> (“night”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mother's sister, maternal aunt</li><li>female cousin</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mōdrijā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mōdrijǭ</i> (“maternal aunt”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>mōdrie</i> (“maternal aunt”), Old High German <i>muotera</i> (“maternal aunt”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) heart, mind, character</li></ol><p>From <i>mōd</i> +&lrm; <i>sefa</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>mōdsevo</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the mind's foundation</li><li>intellectual or moral principle</li><li>character</li></ol><p>From <i>mōd</i> +&lrm; <i>staþol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firm-mindedness; established character or principles</li></ol><p>From <i>mōdstaþol</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mōdor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>proud in mind</li></ol><p>From <i>mōd</i> (“mind”) +&lrm; <i>wlanc</i> (“proud”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mōdwlanc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>mōd</i> (“mind, heart, spirit”) and <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) courage, bravery</li></ol><p><i>mōd</i> +&lrm; <i>þracu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>moþþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Moses (Biblical character)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Mōsēs</i>, <i>Mōȳsēs</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Μωυσῆς</i> (Mōusês), from Biblical Hebrew <i>מֹשֶׁה</i> (mōšê).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the top of the head; the mold</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;moldō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ml̥Hdʰṓ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earth, soil</li><li>the earth, world</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;muldō</i>, from <i>&#42;mel-</i> (“to grind”). Cognate with Old High German <i>molta</i> (dialectal German <i>Molt</i>), Old Norse <i>mold</i> (Swedish <i>mull</i>), Gothic <i>𐌼𐌿𐌻𐌳𐌰</i> (mulda).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earthworm</li></ol><p>From <i>molde</i> (“earth, ground”) +&lrm; <i>wyrm</i> (“worm”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to molder, become corrupt, decay</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>man</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moon</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mānō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mēnô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mḗh₁n̥s</i>, probably a suffixed form of an ultimate root <i>&#42;meh₁-</i> (“to measure”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lunar eclipse</li></ol><p>From <i>mōnan</i> (genitive of <i>mōna</i>) + <i>āsprungennes</i> ("eclipse").</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Monday</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mānini dag</i>; equivalent to <i>mōna</i> +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i>.</p><p>Corresponding to Old Frisian <i>mōnendei</i>, <i>mōnadei</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;mānendag</i> (Dutch <i>maandag</i>), Old High German <i>mānetac</i> (German <i>Montag</i>), Old Norse <i>mánadagr</i> (Icelandic <i>mánudagur</i>, Swedish <i>måndag</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sunday night</li></ol><p>From <i>mōnan</i> (“the moon's”) + <i>niht</i> (“night”), possibly formed by analogy with <i>mōnandæġ</i> (“Monday”, literally “the moon's day”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sunday evening</li></ol><p>From <i>mōnan</i> (“the moon's”) + <i>ǣfen</i> (“evening”), corresponding to <i>mōnandæġ</i> (“Monday”, literally “the moon's day”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>mōnaþ</i></li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>monaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>month</li><li>(<i>in compounds</i>) moon, lunar</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mānōþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mēnōþs</i>.</p><p>See <i>manian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>menstruation</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>mōnaþ</i> +&lrm; <i>ādl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>menstruum, menses</li></ol><p>From <i>mōnaþ</i> +&lrm; <i>blōd</i>. Compare Old High German <i>mānōdblōti</i> (“monthly bleeding, menstruation”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>monthly</li><li>lunar; pertaining to the moon; moonly</li></ol><p>From <i>mōnaþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>mānuthlīk</i>, Old High German <i>mānōdlīh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insane, lunatic, moonstruck</li><li>epileptic</li><li>menstruating</li></ol><p>From <i>mōnaþ</i> (“moon, month”) +&lrm; <i>sēoc</i> (“sick”), derived from the idea that the moon could cause intermittent insanity (compare <i>lūnāticus</i>, with the same origin).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lunacy</li></ol><p>From <i>mōnaþ</i> (“moon”) +&lrm; <i>sēocnes</i> (“sickness”) or <i>mōnaþsēoc</i> (“insane”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>mancynn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>manndryhten</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>manegum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mōnlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>manian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Isle of Man or Anglesey</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>maniġ</i></li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>maniġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>maniġfealdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>maniġfeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>maniġfealdlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>manlēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lunar</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mānalīk</i>, equivalent to <i>mōna</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>mānilīh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>manna</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mōnaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mōnaþlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mōnaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mōnaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mōnaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marsh, wilderness, moor</li><li>mountain</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mōr</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>mōr</i> (Dutch <i>moer</i>), Middle Low German <i>mōr</i> (German <i>Moor</i>), Old High German <i>muor</i>, Old Norse <i>mǫr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>mōraþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sweet boiled wine spiced with herbs</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>morātum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mulberry tree, blackberry bush</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mulberry</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mōrubaʀi</i>. Equivalent to <i>mōr</i> +&lrm; <i>berġe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mōrberġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrot</li><li>parsnip</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;morhā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;murhǭ</i> (“carrot”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>moraha</i> (“carrot”), Old High German <i>moraha</i> (German <i>Möhre</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morning</li><li>tomorrow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;morgin, &#42;murgin</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>morgen</i>, Old Saxon <i>morgan</i>, Old Dutch <i>morgan</i>, Old High German <i>morgan</i>, Old Norse <i>morgunn</i>. Compare also (from the alternative form <i>*murginaz</i>) Old Norse <i>myrginn</i> and Gothic <i>𐌼𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌲𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (maurgins).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chilled with the cold of early morning</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>morgen</i> (“morning”) +&lrm; <i>ċeald</i> (“cold”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a drink or potion that is to be taken in the morning</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>morgen</i> (“morning”) +&lrm; <i>drenċ</i> (“drink”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morning, daylight</li><li>the morrow</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>morgen</i> (“morning”) +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i> (“day”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morning gift</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;murgnagebō</i>, equivalent to <i>morgen</i> +&lrm; <i>ġiefu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>morgenġiefu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a long morning</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>morgen</i> (“morning”) +&lrm; <i>lang</i> (“long”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morning light, morning (in general)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>morgen</i> (“morning”) +&lrm; <i>lēoht</i> (“light”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) morning</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;murganalīkaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>morganlīh</i> and Old Norse <i>morgunligr</i>. Equivalent to <i>morgen</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a morning meal, morning food, breakfast</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>morgen</i> (“morning”) +&lrm; <i>mete</i> (“food”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morning rain, rain in the morning</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>morgen</i> (“morning”) +&lrm; <i>reġn</i> (“rain”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sick in the morning</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>morgen</i> (“morning”) +&lrm; <i>sēoc</i> (“sick”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a story or narrative told in the morning</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>morgen</i> (“morning”) +&lrm; <i>spell</i> (“story”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the periodical assembly of a guild held in the morning, or on the morrow after the guild-feast</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>morgen</i> (“morning”) +&lrm; <i>sprǣċ</i> (“speech”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the morning star</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;murgnasternǭ</i>, equivalent to <i>morgen</i> (“morning”) +&lrm; <i>steorra</i> (“star”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sound made in the morning</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>morgen</i> (“morning”) +&lrm; <i>swēġ</i> (“sound”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morning-tide, morning</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>morgen</i> (“morning”) +&lrm; <i>tīd</i> (“time, tide”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bright with the brightness of morning</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>morgen</i> (“morning”) +&lrm; <i>torht</i> (“bright, radiant”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>worhana</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mōraþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrot</li><li>parsnip</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;morhu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;murhō</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>morha</i>, <i>moraha</i> (“carrot, parsnip”), Old High German <i>moraha</i> (“carrot, parsnip”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Maurawulf</i>, from <i>&#42;Maur</i> (“Moor, dark skinned person”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wulf</i> (“wolf”). Cognate with Old High German <i>Maorolf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>murder</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>murder</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) death, crime</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;morþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;murþą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mr̥tós</i> (“dead”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>morð</i>, Dutch <i>moord</i>, Old High German <i>mord</i> (German <i>Mord</i>), Old Norse <i>morð</i>. The Proto-Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek <i>βροτός</i> (brotós, “mortal”) (earlier <i>&#42;μροτός</i> (&#42;mrotós)), Latin <i>mortis</i> (genitive of <i>mors</i> (“death”)), Old Church Slavonic <i>мрѣти</i> (mrěti) (Russian <i>мере́ть</i> (merétʹ)), Lithuanian <i>mirtis</i> (“death”). Compare Old English <i>morþor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>morþbealu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) murder</li></ol><p><i>morþ</i> +&lrm; <i>bealu</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>murder</li><li>great sin or crime, enormity</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;morþr</i>. Compare <i>morþ</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>(Gothic <i>𐌼𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌸𐍂</i> (maurþr), Old Norse <i>myrða</i>), Latin <i>mors</i>, Old Irish <i>marb</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>murder</li></ol><p>From <i>morþor</i> +&lrm; <i>bealu</i> (“harm”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) Hell</li></ol><p>From <i>morþor</i> (“murder”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>guilty of murder</li></ol><p>From <i>morþor</i> +&lrm; <i>scyldig</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assassin</li></ol><p><i>morþ</i> (“murder”) +&lrm; <i>slaga</i> (“killer”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assassination</li></ol><p><i>morþ</i> (“murder”) +&lrm; <i>sliht</i> (“killing”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>murderer, killer</li></ol><p>From <i>morþ</i> (“murder, death”) +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“worker, maker”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moss</li><li>food, nourishment, victuals</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mos</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;musą</i>, whence also Old High German <i>mos</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mōs</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mōsą</i> (“food”).</p><p>Akin to Old Saxon <i>mōs</i> (“food”), Old High German <i>muos</i> (German <i>Mus</i>, <i>Gemüse</i> (“food, vegetables”), Old English <i>mete</i> (“food”). More at <i>meat</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toll</li><li>tax</li><li>meeting</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mōtu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mōtō</i> (“tax, toll”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>can (to be allowed)</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) must</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mōtan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mōtaną</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>mōtan</i>, Old High German <i>muozan</i> (German <i>müssen</i>), Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌼𐍉𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (gamōtan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>courthouse</li><li>official meeting place, such as a Roman pretorium</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>mōt</i> +&lrm; <i>-ern</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place where a court or assembly is held</li></ol><p>From <i>mōt</i> (“meeting”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to address one's self, speak (to a person), converse, address an assembly</li><li>to discuss, dispute, moot a question</li></ol><p>From <i>mōt</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place of assembly, a forum</li></ol><p>From <i>mōt</i> (“moot, meeting”) +&lrm; <i>-stōw</i> (“place”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conversation</li><li>discourse</li></ol><p>From <i>mōtian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>motern</i> (“courthouse”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Moses</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Mō̆ȳsēs</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Μωϋσῆς</i> (Mōüsês), from earlier <i>Μωυσῆς</i> (Mōusês), from Biblical Hebrew <i>מֹשֶׁה</i> (mōšê)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moth-eaten</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>moþþe</i> (“moth”) +&lrm; <i>freten</i> (“eaten”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;moþþā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mugwort</li></ol><p>Likely from <i>mycg</i> (“midge”) +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i> (“wort, a plant”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stack (of hay, grain etc.)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mūgô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;muk-</i> (“heap”), similar to Ancient Greek <i>μύκων</i> (múkōn, “pile”).</p><p>Akin to Old Norse <i>múgi</i> (“swathe, crowd”), <i>múgr</i> (“crowd, mob”) (Norwegian <i>muge</i> (“pile, heap”), Faroese <i>múgva</i>/<i>múgvi</i> (“crowd”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>mucgwyrt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>mucgwyrt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mūga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mule</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mūl</i> (“mule”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Mul)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name held by Mul of Kent</li></ol><p>Apparently from the common noun <i>mūl</i> (“mule”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remember, be mindful of, be careful of</li><li>to think, consider</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;munan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;munaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;memóne</i>, from <i>&#42;men-</i> (“to think”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) hand, palm as a measure of length</li><li>trust, security, protection</li><li>protector, guardian</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mundō</i> (“hand, guard, security”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>mund</i>, Old Saxon <i>mund</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protector, guardian, advocate</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>mund</i> (“protection”) +&lrm; <i>bora</i> (“bearer”). Cognate with Dutch <i>momboor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protection, patronage, aid</li><li>the fine paid for a violation of <i>mund</i></li></ol><p>From <i>mund</i> +&lrm; <i>byrd</i>. Compare Old High German <i>mundiburd</i> and Old Saxon <i>mundburd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) grasp, hand-grip</li></ol><p><i>mund</i> (“hand”) +&lrm; <i>gripe</i> (“grip”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to protect, shelter, guard</li><li>to act as one's guardian</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mundōn</i>, equivalent to <i>mund</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>protection, guardianship</li></ol><p>From <i>mund</i> + <i>-wist</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>munuc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hill, mound</li><li>a mount or mountain</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>mōns, montem</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mountainous or hilly land</li></ol><p>From <i>munt</i> (“mountain”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oreades</li></ol><p><i>munt</i> +&lrm; <i>ælfen</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monk</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;munik</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>child training to be a monk</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>munuc</i> (“monk”) +&lrm; <i>ċild</i> (“child”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monkhood, the state of being a member of a monastery</li></ol><p>From <i>munuc</i> (“monk”) +&lrm; <i>-hād</i> (“-hood”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wall</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mūrā</i>, borrowed from Latin <i>mūrus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>myrġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to care for, worry about</li><li>to be sorrowful, to mourn</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;murnaną</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>mornan</i>, Old High German <i>mornēn</i>, Old Norse <i>morna</i>, Gothic <i>𐌼𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (maurnan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouse</li><li>muscle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mūs</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mūs</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;muh₂s</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>muscle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mussel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>mūscula</i>, from Latin <i>mūsculus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mousetrap</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mūsfallā</i>. Equivalent to <i>mūs</i> (“mouse”) +&lrm; <i>fealle</i> (“trap”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mouse-colored</li></ol><p>From <i>mūs</i> (“mouse”) +&lrm; <i>fealu</i> (“yellow, dusky, gray”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouse hawk</li></ol><p>From <i>mūs</i> (“mouse”) +&lrm; <i>hafoc</i> (“hawk”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mūshafoc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>muscle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>must (newly or partially fermented wine)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>mustum</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mūs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mūga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>muscle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>mūþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouth</li><li>opening, door, gate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;munþ</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognates include Old Frisian <i>mūth</i>, <i>mund</i>, <i>mond</i> (North Frisian <i>müth</i>, West Frisian <i>mûn</i>), Old Saxon <i>mūth</i>, Old Dutch <i>mund</i> (Dutch <i>mond</i>), Old High German <i>mund</i> (German <i>Mund</i>), Old Norse <i>muðr</i>, <i>munnr</i> (Danish <i>mund</i>, Icelandic <i>munnur</i>, Norwegian <i>munn</i>, Swedish <i>mun</i>), Gothic <i>𐌼𐌿𐌽𐌸𐍃</i> (munþs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the mouth of a river</li><li>an opening, a door</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without a mouth; lacking a mouth; mouthless</li></ol><p>From <i>mūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>miċel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>miċel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>miċel-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>miċelnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>miċelnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small fly; gnat; mosquito; midge</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;muggju</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mugjō</i> (“midge, mosquito, gnat”). Akin to Old Saxon <i>muggia</i>, Old High German <i>mucka</i>, <i>mugga</i>, Old Norse <i>mý</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>myċġ</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;muggjā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mugjǭ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mosquito net</li></ol><p>From <i>myċġ</i> (“mosquito”) +&lrm; <i>nett</i> (“net”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>units of measure</i>) a bushel</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>modius</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>middanġeard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>myċġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dust</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;muli</i>, a deverbal formation from <i>&#42;mulljan</i>, ultimately derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;melh₂-</i> (“to grind, mill”) and related to Old Norse <i>melr</i> (“sandbank”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mildijaz</i> (“soft”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mill (a building, or machinery)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mulīnu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>millwheel</li></ol><p>From <i>mylen</i> +&lrm; <i>hwēol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>millstone</li></ol><p>From <i>mylen</i> (“mill”) +&lrm; <i>stān</i> (“stone”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miller</li></ol><p>From <i>mylen</i> (“mill”) +&lrm; <i>weard</i> (“guard”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>mylen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>milisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mieltan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a whore, prostitute</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>meretrix</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brothel</li></ol><p>From <i>myltestre</i> (“prostitute”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mimorian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remind <i>(+ accusative = someone)</i> <i>(+ genitive = of something)</i></li><li>to remember <i>(+ genitive)</i></li><li>to commemorate <i>(+ accusative)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mundīgōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a reminding</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>myndgian</i> (“to remind”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>senseless, foolish</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;mynd</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mind; memory, remembrance</li><li>feeling, affection, love, favor</li><li>purpose, desire, wish</li><li>memorial</li><li>minnow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;muni</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;muniz</i> (“thought, feeling, emotion, desire”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>munr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌼𐌿𐌽𐍃</i> (muns).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;muniwu</i> (“minnow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nun</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;munikinnō</i> (“female monk”), from <i>&#42;munikaz</i> (“monk”), from Late Latin <i>monachus</i> (“monk”), from Ancient Greek <i>μοναχός</i> (monakhós, “hermit”, noun), from <i>μοναχός</i> (monakhós, “single, solitary”, adjective), from <i>μόνος</i> (mónos, “alone”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;men-</i>, <i>&#42;menw-</i>, <i>&#42;manw-</i> (“small, little, isolated”). Cognate with German <i>Mönchin</i> (“female monk”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female monk; a nun</li></ol><p>From <i>munec</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i> or <i>muneċen</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>; feminine variant of <i>munuc</i> (“monk”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remind (<i>+gen of thing</i>)</li><li>to remind pressingly, admonish</li><li>to urge, exhort</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;munigōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>admonition, exhortation</li></ol><p><i>mynegian</i> (“to urge, exhort”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>desirable, pleasing</li></ol><p>From <i>myne</i> (“love”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i> (“-ly”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>munligr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coin</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;munit</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minter</li><li>money changer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;munitārī</i>. Equivalent to <i>mynet</i> (“coin”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i> (“-er”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mint</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;munitōn</i>. Equivalent to <i>mynet</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minting, striking of a coin</li></ol><p>From <i>mynet</i> (“coin”) +&lrm; <i>sleġe</i> (“strike, hit”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mint (place where money is produced)</li></ol><p><i>mynet</i> (“coin”) +&lrm; <i>smiþþe</i> (“smithy”)</p></html>:/ˈmyn.ɣi.ɑn/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mynegian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monastery, nunnery, mother church, cathedral</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>monastērium</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>μοναστήριον</i> (monastḗrion).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minster-book</li></ol><p><i>mynster</i> +&lrm; <i>bōc</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloister, monastery, convent</li></ol><p>From <i>mynster</i> (“monastery”) +&lrm; <i>clūse</i> (“cell”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purification of a minster, when someone has been killed there</li></ol><p><i>mynster</i> +&lrm; <i>clǣnsung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head of a monastery</li></ol><p>Calque of Latin <i>monastēriī</i> <i>pāter</i> (“father of a monastery”), equivalent to <i>mynster</i> (“monastery”) +&lrm; <i>fæder</i> (“father”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an enemy or hater of monasteries</li></ol><p>From <i>mynster</i> (“monastery”) +&lrm; <i>hata</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land owned by a monastery</li></ol><p>From <i>mynster</i> (“monastery”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monastic life; life within a monastery</li></ol><p>From <i>mynster</i> (“monastery”) +&lrm; <i>līf</i> (“life”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monk; resident of a monastery</li></ol><p>From <i>mynster</i> (“monastery”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person, man”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a monk living in a monastery</li></ol><p>From <i>mynster</i> (“monastery”) +&lrm; <i>munuc</i> (“monk”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a priest who performs services at a monastery</li></ol><p>From <i>mynster</i> (“monastery”) +&lrm; <i>prēost</i> (“priest”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the provost of a monastery</li></ol><p>From <i>mynster</i> (“monastery”) +&lrm; <i>prōfost</i> (“provost”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the management of a monastery</li></ol><p>From <i>mynster</i> (“monastery”) +&lrm; <i>sċīr</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a town</li><li>a place in which there is a minister</li></ol><p>From <i>mynster</i> (“monastery”) +&lrm; <i>-stōw</i> (“place”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a custom of or followed in a monastery</li></ol><p>From <i>mynster</i> (“monastery”) +&lrm; <i>-wīse</i> (“custom of”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mean, intend</li><li>to think, suppose</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;muntijan</i>, a derivative of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;munaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;men-</i> (“to think”). Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>munten</i>. More at mint.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mirce</i></li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Myrce)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Mierċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mirċels</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>myrġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pleasant, attractive, enjoyable</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;murgī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;murgijaz</i>, <i>&#42;murguz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mréǵʰus</i> (“short, brief”).</p><p>For the sense-development, compare <i>pastime</i>, German <i>Kurzweil</i>, <i>kurzweilig</i>. Cognate with the first element of Middle Dutch <i>mergelijc</i> (“pleasant”), Old High German <i>murg</i> (“short”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>morgen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mirth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;murgiþu</i> (“briefness, brevity”); equivalent to <i>myriġe</i> (“merry”) +&lrm; <i>-þ</i> (“-th, -ness”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>myrġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>pleasantly, melodiously</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>myrġe</i> (“pleasent, enjoyable”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>myrrh</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>myrrha</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>μύρρα</i> (múrrha).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homicide, murder</li><li>a murderer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;murþrijō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;murþrijô</i> (“death, killing, murder”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mr̥tro-</i> (“killing”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mer-</i>, <i>&#42;mor-</i>, <i>&#42;mr-</i> (“to die”). Akin to Gothic <i>𐌼𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌸𐍂</i> (maurþr, “murder”), Old High German <i>mord</i> (“murder”), Old Norse <i>morð</i> (“murder”), Old English <i>morþ</i> (“death, homicide, murder”), Old English <i>morþor</i> (“secret slaying”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>prose</i>) to murder (according to the <i>Oxford English Dictionary</i> only attested in the compounded forms <i>amyrþrian</i>, <i>formyrþrian</i>, and <i>ofmyrþrian</i>, and the derived form <i>myrðrung</i>).</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>myrþra</i> (“murder, homicide”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Compare the noun <i>morþor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>misċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>table</li><li>things on a table</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meox</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>mixen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consort</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) sword</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mēkijaz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>māki</i>, Old Norse <i>mækir</i>, and Gothic <i>𐌼𐌴𐌺𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (mēkeis).</p><p>Borrowed into Slavic: see e.g. Russian <i>меч</i> (meč), Serbo-Croatian <i>mȁč</i>, Czech, Slovak and Slovene <i>meč</i>, Polish <i>miecz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Macedonians</li><li>Macedonia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>macedonī</i> (“Macedonians”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Macedonian, of Macedonia</li></ol><p>From <i>Mæcedonie</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i> or Latin <i>macedonicus</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mullet (fish)</li></ol><p><i>mǣċe</i> (“sword”) +&lrm; <i>fisċ</i> (“fish”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) "sword's edge"</li></ol><p>From <i>mǣċe</i> + <i>eċġ</i>. Has a parallel in Old Saxon <i>mākies eggia</i>, possibly stemming from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mākijas aggju</i>, with an analog in Old Norse <i>mækis egg</i>. Compare <i>sweordes eċġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a man</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;maggi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;magjaz</i>, diminutive of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maguz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a man</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;maggjō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;magjô</i>, from <i>&#42;maguz</i>. Compare Old English <i>mæċġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meadow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mādu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mēdwō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mædere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mæġden</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mæġdenċild</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>madder (<i>Rubia tinctorum</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;madarǭ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;modʰro-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meadow-land</li><li>grassland that is mown</li></ol><p>From <i>mǣd</i> (“meadow”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the seventh month of the year according to the Anglo-Saxon calendar</li><li>July</li></ol><p>From <i>mǣd</i> +&lrm; <i>mōnaþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mædere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relative, kinsman</li><li>female relative</li><li>alternative form of <i>mæġen</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;māg</i> (“kin”).</p><p>Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>maech</i> (Dutch <i>maag</i>), Old High German <i>māg</i>, Gothic <i>𐌼𐌴𐌲𐍃</i> (mēgs, “son-in-law”), Swedish <i>måg</i> (“make”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recompense paid to the family of a slain person</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>mǣġ</i> +&lrm; <i>bōt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>related</li><li>cognate</li></ol><p>From <i>mǣġ</i> (“kinsman”) +&lrm; <i>cūþ</i> (“known”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>girl</li><li>virgin, young unmarried woman</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;magadīn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>girl, female child</li></ol><p><i>mæġden</i> (“girl”) +&lrm; <i>ċild</i> (“child”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>girlhood</li><li>virginity</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>mæġden</i> +&lrm; <i>-hād</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>girlish, girly</li><li>virginal</li></ol><p>From <i>mæġden</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a virgin, a maiden</li></ol><p>From <i>mæġden</i> (“virgin”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Milan (a city in Lombardy, Italy)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strength, power</li><li>force, army</li><li>virtue, efficacy, efficiency</li><li>(<i>in compounds</i>) very</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maganą</i>, <i>&#42;maginą</i>, whence also Old Saxon <i>megin</i>, Old High German <i>magan</i>, <i>megin</i>, Old Norse <i>magn</i>, <i>megin</i>, <i>megn</i>. See also <i>magan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) strength, power</li></ol><p>From <i>mæġen</i> +&lrm; <i>cræft</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>meginkraft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a chief, a powerful king</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>mæġen</i> +&lrm; <i>cyning</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a powerful help or aid</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>mæġen</i> +&lrm; <i>fultum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>mæġen</i> (“strength, power”) + <i>rīċ</i> (“king, ruler”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) mighty spear</li></ol><p><i>mæġen</i> +&lrm; <i>wudu</i> (“wood”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>skinny; meager, lean</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;magr</i> (“lean, meager”). Compare Old High German <i>magar</i>, Old Norse <i>magr</i>. Also cognate, through Indo-European, with Latin <i>macer</i>, whence modern English <i>meager</i>, through Old French.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>master</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>magister</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mæġþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>girl, virgin</li><li>woman, wife</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;magaþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;magaþs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vulva</li></ol><p>From <i>mæġeþ</i> +&lrm; <i>blæd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>virginity</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>mæġeþ</i> (“virgin”) +&lrm; <i>-hād</i> (“-hood”). Compare Gothic <i>𐌼𐌰𐌲𐌰𐌸𐌴𐌹</i> (magaþei, “virginity”), with the same first element.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>mǣġ</i> +&lrm; <i>helm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incest</li></ol><p><i>mǣġ</i> +&lrm; <i>hǣmed</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mæġen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mǣġester</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>mæġþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>mæġþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mæġeþ</i></li><li>family, kindred</li><li>tribe, nation, province</li><li>importunate desire; ambition; greed</li><li>power, greatness</li></ol><p>Alteration of earlier <i>mæġeþ</i>, with irregular syncope. See there for more.</p><p>From <i>mǣġ</i> (“a relative”) +&lrm; <i>-þ</i> (suffix forming abstract nouns). Cognate with Old Norse <i>mægð</i> (“affinity by marriage”). Compare the formation of modern English <i>kindred</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maigiþō</i> (“shamelessness, wantonness, wickedness”), from <i>&#42;maigaz</i> (“wicked, shameless”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mæġeþblæd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mayweed</li></ol><p>Probably related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;madarǭ</i> (“madder”). Compare Middle Low German <i>mēgedeblōme</i>, Middle High German <i>magetbluome</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kinship, relationship</li><li>alternative form of <i>mæġeþhād</i></li></ol><p>From <i>mǣġþ</i> (“kindred”) +&lrm; <i>-hād</i> (“-hood”).</p><p>Alteration of earlier <i>mæġeþhād</i>. See there for more.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>miht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mihtiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>mǣġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a measure</li><li>mark, sign (especially a cross)</li><li>time, occasion, season</li><li>the time for eating, meal-time, a meal</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;māl</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mēlą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;meh₁-</i> (“to measure”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>mēl</i> (West Frisian <i>miel</i>), Old Saxon <i>-māl</i>, Dutch <i>maal</i>, Old High German <i>māl</i> (German <i>Mal</i> (“time”), <i>Mahl</i> (“meal”)), Old Norse <i>mál</i> (Danish and Swedish <i>mål</i>), Gothic <i>𐌼𐌴𐌻</i> (mēl, “time, hour”). The Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek <i>μέτρον</i> (métron), Latin <i>mensus</i>, Russian <i>ме́ра</i> (méra), Lithuanian <i>mẽtas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak</li><li>to mark, spot, blemish</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>mæþlan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maþlijaną</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mēlijaną</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐌼𐌴𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (mēljan, “to write”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maldon, a town in Essex, England</li></ol><p>From <i>mǣl</i> (“sign, cross, monument”) + <i>dūn</i> (“hill”), meaning something like "monument hill."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Malvern (a town in Worcestershire, England).</li></ol><p>From a Celtic phrase meaning "bare hill"</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mean</li><li>to say, to relate</li><li>to complain <i>(+ accusative about something)</i></li><li>to mourn</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mainijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mainijaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;meyn-</i> (“intention, opinion”). See English <i>mean</i> (“intend; lament”) for more.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>common</li><li>mean; wicked; false; evil</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mainiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>menġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>maniġ-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meniġu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>maniġfeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>maniġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>maniġ-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meniġu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>maniġfeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>maniġfealdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>meniġeo</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>meniġu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to marry</li><li>to have as a companion</li><li>to communicate with another</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make known, declare, proclaim</li><li>to praise, celebrate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mārijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mērijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>&#42;mǣr</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p><p>See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mearh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mearcung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boundary, border</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>great, excellent, splendid, illustrious, sublime</li><li>distinguished, celebrated</li><li>widely known, famous, renowned</li><li>pure, genuine</li><li>(<i>of money</i>) sterling</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mairī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mairiją</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>mêre</i>, Old Norse <i>landamæri</i> (“border-marks between lands”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mārī</i> (“famous”).</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>māri</i>, Old High German <i>māri</i>, Old Norse <i>mærr</i>. The Proto-Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek <i>-μωρος</i> (-mōros) (in <i>ἐγχεσίμωρος</i> (enkhesímōros, “mighty with the spear”)), Old Irish <i>már</i> (Scottish Gaelic <i>mòr</i>, compare English <i>claymore</i>), Welsh <i>mawr</i> (“big”).</p><p>Germanic <i>mār-</i>, <i>mǣr-</i> is also used in Germanic given names, such as Swedish <i>Ingemar</i>, English <i>Aylmer</i>, Italian <i>Ademaro</i>, German <i>Dietmar</i>, French <i>Omer</i> etc. Its Indo-European ancestor is conserved in Old Church Slavonic <i>мѣръ</i> (měrŭ), as in the Russian given name <i>Влади́мир</i> (Vladímir), and in Gaulish given names as <i>Segomārus</i> and <i>Viridomārus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>mǣre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mearh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>great, magnificent, glorious, splendid, illustrious</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>mǣrlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greatness; distinction; celebrity</li></ol><p>From <i>mǣre</i> (“famous”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>mǣre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>greater</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make great, extend</li><li>to make known, spread knowledge of, declare, proclaim, announce</li><li>to celebrate</li><li>to perform a rite, ceremony, sacrament, etc.</li><li>to exalt, praise, glorify</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mārisōn</i>. By surface analysis, <i>mǣre</i> (“famous, renowned, illustrious”) +&lrm; <i>-sian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>merestone, boundary marker, landmark</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>mǣre</i> +&lrm; <i>stān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>making known, report, rumor</li><li>fame, renown, celebrity</li><li>celebration of a festival, rite, etc.</li><li>making great, magnifying, glorification</li><li>greatness, magnificence, excellence, honor, favor</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>mǣrsian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a time of celebration or glorification</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>mǣrsung</i> +&lrm; <i>tīma</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>mǣrþu</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>mearþ</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;māriþu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mēriþō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mēraz</i>; equivalent to <i>mǣre</i> +&lrm; <i>-þ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glory, fame, honor</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;māriþu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mēriþō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mēraz</i>; equivalent to <i>mǣre</i> +&lrm; <i>-þu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Meuse (a river in France, Belgium and the Netherlands).</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>māsċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mesh</li><li>spot, blemish</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mask(þ)rā</i>, an extended form of <i>&#42;maskā</i> (“mesh”). Akin to Old English <i>masc</i> (“net”). More at mesh, mask.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>mæseren</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) maple, maple wood</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;mæser</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mæsling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of brass</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;mæssing</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;massing</i>, altered by <i>-ling</i> and an intrusive <i>-t-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mæsse</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mass (mostly in Roman Catholic churches)</li></ol><p>From *Vulgar Latin <i>messa</i>, from Late Latin <i>missa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>missal, mass-book</li></ol><p>From <i>mæsse</i> (“mass”) +&lrm; <i>bōc</i> (“book”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a festival or the day thereof</li></ol><p><i>mæsse</i> +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the night before a festival</li></ol><p><i>mæsse</i> +&lrm; <i>niht</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Roman Catholicism</i>) priest</li></ol><p>From <i>mæsse</i> +&lrm; <i>preost</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>celebration of mass (Catholic liturgy)</li><li>a church service held on a saint's day</li></ol><p>From <i>mæsse</i> (“mass”) +&lrm; <i>sang</i> (“song”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>day or evening before a festival</li></ol><p><i>mæsse</i> +&lrm; <i>ǣfen</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say mass</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>mæsse</i> (“mass”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>miċel</i><i>, </i><i>fela</i><i>, and</i> <i>maniġ</i>: biggest, most</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>miċele</i> and <i>swīþe</i>: most</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mast (of ship)</li><li>mast (forest nuts, often used to feed pigs)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;maist</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maistaz</i> and <i>&#42;maist</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mast</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>mast</i> (Dutch <i>mast</i>), Old High German <i>mast</i> (German <i>Mast</i>).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mastaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mad(z)do-</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>mast</i>, Old High German <i>mast</i> (German <i>Mast</i>); and related to Latin <i>madeō</i> (“to be wet”) and Old English <i>mete</i> (“food”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fatten (animals etc.)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>mæst</i> (“mast (forest nuts)”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>mesten</i> (Dutch <i>mesten</i>), Old High German <i>masten</i> (German <i>mästen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land on which mast (i.e. tree nuts) is produced</li><li>land used to feed pigs</li></ol><p>From <i>mæst</i> (“tree nuts”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mæsling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to dream <i>(+accusative of dream) (+dative or accusative of person)</i></li></ol><p>From an earlier sense meaning "to paint, sculpt, design," from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;maitijan</i> (“to cut”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maitaną</i>. Doublet of <i>mētan</i> (“to paint, sculpt, design”). The semantic shift of "to paint, sculpt, design" > "to dream" is likely analogical, i.e. a dream being likened to a series of images being painted for the dreamer (hence its impersonal use).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moderate, mean (between two extremes)</li><li>small, poor; bad</li><li>(<i>in the comparative</i>) inferior</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) belonging to the middle or lower class, common</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>poorly, badly</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mātī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Marne (a river in France).</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dream</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>mǣtan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gull, seagull</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maiwiz</i>, a byform of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maiwaz</i>. Cognate with West Frisian <i>meau</i>, <i>miuw</i>, Old Saxon <i>mēu</i> (Middle Low German <i>meve</i>, from which German <i>Möwe</i>), Dutch <i>meeuw</i>, Old Norse <i>már</i>, <i>mór</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>mæþel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>measure, degree, proportion</li><li>measure or extent of power; ability, capacity, efficacy</li><li>degree, rank, status, condition</li><li>due measure, right</li><li>due measure in regard to others; honour, respect</li><li>mowing, hay harvest</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;māþi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mēþiz</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mēþą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂meh₁-</i>. Equivalent to <i>māwan</i> +&lrm; <i>-þ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assembly, deliberative or judicial meeting</li><li>speech, address, conversation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maþlą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house of meeting or consultation</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>mæþel</i> +&lrm; <i>-ern</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mower</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>mǣþ</i> (“mowing”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>mǣþ</i> + <i>here</i> (“army”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to speak</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maþlijaną</i>, compare <i>maþelian</i>. Elder form of <i>mǣlan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mētan</i> (“to meet”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not</li><li>no</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) never</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>not</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nai</i> (“never”), equivalent to a contraction of <i>ne</i> (“not”) and <i>ā</i> (“ever”). Compare Old Norse <i>nei</i>, Old Frisian <i>nā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>not that <i>(+ subjunctive)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Denotes absence or lack</li></ol><p>From <i>nā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) alternative spelling of <i>nōmon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to not have, be without</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>ne</i> + <i>habban</i>. The same process occurred with <i>ne</i> + <i>willan</i> (<i>nillan</i>, to not want), <i>ne</i> + <i>wesan</i> (<i>nesan</i>, to not be), and <i>ne</i> + <i>witan</i> (<i>nytan</i>, to not know).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nafola</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) boat, ship</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nakwô</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>nako</i> (Dutch <i>aak</i>), Old High German <i>nahho</i> (German <i>Nachen</i>), Old Norse <i>nǫkkvi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strip, strip the clothes off or from; undress</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nakwōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nakwōną</i> (“to strip, bare, make naked”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;negʷ-</i>, <i>&#42;nogʷ-</i> (“to strip; naked”). See nacod.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>naked</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nakwad</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nakwadaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;negʷ-</i> (“bare, naked”); see there for the cognates.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nakedness</li></ol><p>From <i>nacod</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nafola</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nafugār</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) navel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nabulō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nabulô</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;nabalô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nave (of wheel), hub</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nabō</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>nave</i> (Dutch <i>naaf</i>), Old High German <i>naba</i> (German <i>Nabe</i>), West Old Norse <i>nof</i> (Icelandic <i>nöf</i>, Swedish <i>nav</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>auger</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nabugaiʀ</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>navugēr</i>, Old High German <i>nabagēr</i>, Old Norse <i>nafarr</i>. Equivalent to <i>nafu</i> (“nave”) +&lrm; <i>gār</i> (“spear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to not possess</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>ne</i> +&lrm; <i>āgan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nāwiht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from nowhere</li></ol><p><i>nā</i> +&lrm; <i>hwanon</i> (“whence”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nāhwǣr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>nowhither, to nowhere</li></ol><p>From <i>nā</i> (“no”) +&lrm; <i>hwider</i> (“whither”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>nowhere (in no place)</li><li>never, in no case</li><li>in no respect, not at all</li></ol><p><i>nā</i> (“no”) +&lrm; <i>hwǣr</i> (“where”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>neither</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>neither</li></ol><p><i>nā</i> +&lrm; <i>hwæþer</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nealles</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nealles</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nealles</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nealles</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nealles</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nealles</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) noun</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;namō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;namô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁nómn̥</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>book in which names are written; register</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>nama</i> +&lrm; <i>bōc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nōme</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nōmen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>etymology</li></ol><p>From <i>namena</i> (“genitive plural of nama 'name, noun'”) +&lrm; <i>ordfruma</i> (“origin”), literally 'origin of nouns'</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to name, mention the name of</li><li>to give a name</li><li>to nominate, appoint by name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;namōn</i> (“to name”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nōmon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>no; not a, not one, not any</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>no one, nobody; none</li></ol><p>Etymology treeProto-Germanic <i>&#42;nainaz</i>Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nain</i>Old English <i>nan</i></p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nain</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nainaz</i>, equivalent to <i>ne</i> (“not”) +&lrm; <i>ān</i> (“one”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>no one, nobody, no man</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>nothing</li></ol><p>From <i>nān</i> (“none, no”) + <i>þing</i> (“thing”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) Denotes absence or lack</li></ol><p>Etymology treeOld English <i>nān</i>Old English <i>nan-</i></p><p>From <i>nān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not at all</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nāwiht</i> (“nothing, no one”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spikenard</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>nardus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nosu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>various plants with a spicy flavor in the family Brassicaceae, especially the genus <i>Nasturtium</i>, watercress</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>nasturtium</i>, from Latin <i>nāsus</i> (“nose”) + <i>tormentum</i> (“torment”) or <i>torquēre</i> (“to twist”) for its pungence.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) nose</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nasu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nasō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not at all, by no means</li><li>not in any way,</li><li>in no wise</li><li>on no account</li><li>in the least</li><li>nothing</li><li>as an intensifier of the negative and left untranslated</li></ol><p>Likely a contraction of <i>nā</i> (“never”) +&lrm; <i>tō</i> (“to”) +&lrm; <i>þæs</i> (“of that”) +&lrm; <i>hwōn</i> (“a small amount”), <i>þæs</i> being the genitive masculine/neuter singular form of sē (“the, that”) and hwōn being generally undeclined. The uncontracted phrase nā tō þæs hwōn is also attested.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Nathan, son of David</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Nāthan</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Νάθαν</i> (Náthan), originally from Biblical Hebrew <i>נָתָן</i> (Natán).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nāwiht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nafola</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nāhwǣr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>nothing</li><li>none</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not (used to negate adjectives, especially in phrases like "not long ago" and "not far from")</li><li>used with <i>ne</i> for emphasis; not at all (often left untranslated)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;naiwwiht</i>. Originally equivalent to <i>ne</i> +&lrm; <i>āwiht</i> or <i>nā</i> +&lrm; <i>wiht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nāwiht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>neither</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>nāhwæþer</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Nazarenisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Nazareth; Nazarene</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>Nazarēnus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Ναζαρηνός</i> (Nazarēnós, “Nazarene”) +&lrm; <i>-isc</i> (“-ish”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nazareth (an ancient city in modern northern Israel)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Nāzareth</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>nāhwæþer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nān þing</i> (“nothing”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>nāhwæþer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li><i>(in negative phrases)</i> or, and not (optionally translated as "nor")</li><li><i>ne... ne...</i> is used to mean "[not...] or..." (optionally translated as "neither... nor...")</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ne</i> (“not”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>not</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ne</i> (“not”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;né</i> (“no, not”). Akin to Old English <i>ne</i> (“not”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīed</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīedhǣs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>close, near</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>close, near</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>close, near</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a neighbour</li></ol><p><i>nēah</i> +&lrm; <i>būend</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intimate, close-knit</li></ol><p>From <i>nēah</i> +&lrm; <i>-feald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neighbour</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nāhwagabūrō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nēhwagabūrô</i>. Equivalent to <i>nēah</i> +&lrm; <i>ġebūr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female neighbour</li></ol><p>From <i>nēahġebūr</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a neighboring country</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>nēah</i> (“near”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a neighbour</li></ol><p>From <i>nēah</i> (“near”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīehst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīehsta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neighborhood, vicinity</li></ol><p>From <i>nēah</i> +&lrm; <i>-stōw</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>niht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not at all, by no means</li></ol><p>From <i>ne</i> +&lrm; <i>ealles</i>. Some variants show possible formation as <i>nā</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to approach</li><li>to be near, be near to</li><li>to be like, resemble</li></ol><p>From <i>nēah</i> (“near”) +&lrm; <i>-lǣċan</i>. Cognate with Middle Low German <i>naleken</i>, Old High German <i>nahhlihhan</i>, Old Norse <i>nálgask</i> (Icelandic <i>nálgast</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>almost</li><li>nearly</li><li>about</li><li>from near</li><li>close at hand</li><li>near</li></ol><p>Related to nēah</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>nēah</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nearunes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>narrow, confined</li><li>constricted, difficult, causing anxiety</li><li>distressing, oppressive</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nearunes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>nearu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>narrow</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narrowness</li><li>confinement; prison, hiding place</li><li>danger, distress</li><li>affliction, difficulty</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;naru</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;narwaz</i>, probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ner-</i> (“to turn, bend, twist, constrict”). Compare Old Saxon <i>naro</i>, West Frisian <i>near</i>, Dutch <i>naar</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a strait, a narrow space, a place of small extent</li><li>(<i>of the body</i>) oppression, distress</li><li>(<i>of the mind</i>) distress, anxiety, tribulation, trouble, grief</li><li>(<i>of room or means</i>) scantiness</li></ol><p>From <i>nearu</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make narrow, straighten, compress</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to become narrow, contracted</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>nearu</i> (“narrow”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>nearu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>animal, beast</li><li>cow, ox, cattle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;naut</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nautą</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>nāt</i>, Old Saxon <i>nōt</i>, Dutch <i>noot</i>, Old High German <i>nōz</i> (dialectal German <i>Nos</i>), Old Norse <i>naut</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neighbourhood (area around one's home)</li><li>the vicinity of anything: proximity, neighbourhood, surrounding area</li><li>companionship, fellowship</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nāhawisti</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>nāhwist</i> (“neighbourhood”), Icelandic <i>návist</i> (“presence”). Equivalent to Old English <i>nēah</i> (“close, near”) +&lrm; <i>wist</i> (“being, essence”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nebb</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>face</li><li>nose</li><li>bill or beak</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nabi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nabją</i>. Akin to Old Norse <i>nef</i>, Middle Low German <i>nebbe</i> and Middle Dutch <i>nebbe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nihtegale</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīed</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīedan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīedbād</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīedes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nǣdl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nephew</li><li>grandson</li><li>stepson</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nefō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nefô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;népōts</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>neva</i>, Old Saxon <i>nevo</i> (German Low German <i>Neev</i>), Old High German <i>nevo</i> (German <i>Neffe</i>), Old Norse <i>nefi</i>; also Avestan <i>𐬥𐬀𐬞𐬁𐬝</i> (napāt̰), Latin <i>nepōs</i>, Old Irish <i>nia</i>, Welsh <i>nai</i>, Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;netьjь</i> (Slovene <i>nečak</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>granddaughter</li><li>the daughter of one's nephew</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nefā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>niece</li><li>granddaughter</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nefanā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nefanǭ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nefô</i> (“nephew”). Compare Middle High German <i>nefin</i>, <i>nevin</i>, German <i>Neffin</i> (“niece”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>unless</li><li>except</li></ol><p>Probably cognate with Old Saxon <i>neba</i>, <i>nebo</i>, <i>nebu</i> (“except, unless”), Old Norse <i>nema</i> (“except, unless”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nepte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a carrion bird</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;nē</i> (“corpse”) +&lrm; <i>fugol</i> (“bird”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nēah</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīehst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>niht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nihtegale</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>neorxnawang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīehst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to name, call</li><li>to enumerate</li><li>to address, speak to</li><li>to nominate</li><li>to invoke, mention, mention by name</li><li>to relate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;namnijan</i> (“to name”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>nefne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nemnan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>naming, calling, addressing</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) nominative</li></ol><p>From <i>nemnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nemniendlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name, appellation</li></ol><p>From <i>nemnan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nymþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Nene (a river in Northamptonshire, England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a corpse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nawi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nawiz</i>, <i>&#42;nawaz</i> (“corpse”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;neh₂w-</i> (“the deceased, corpse”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>desire, longing; zeal, eagerness, diligence, earnestness, earnest endeavor</li><li>pleasure, delight</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;niudi</i> (“desire, eagerness”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;newdʰ-</i> (“seeking, need”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;new-</i> (“to incline, push, move, nod, wave”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>earnest, zealous</li></ol><p>From <i>nēod</i> +&lrm; <i>-ful</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be necessary</li></ol><p><i>nēod</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an urgent summons; an earnest, hospitable invitation; a wish</li></ol><p>From <i>nēod</i> (“desire, eagerness, earnest”) +&lrm; <i>laþu</i> (“inviation, summons”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>diligently, eagerly, earnestly, zealously, sedulously, carefully, greatly</li></ol><p>From <i>nēod</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>niudlīko</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>neowol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>neowolnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>nēah</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>neorxnawang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Paradise.</li></ol><p>From <i>neorxna</i> (an element of unknown provenance) + <i>wang</i> (“field”). Many attempts have been made to explain the etymology of <i>neorxna</i>, but little agreement has been reached; the available evidence probably does not permit a definite answer.</p><p>Selected explanations</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>neorxnawang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>neorxnawang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seek, go to</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;neusōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;neusōną</i>, related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;niuhsijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>niusōn</i>. Compare also Old English <i>nēosan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>visit, visitation</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>nēosian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to use, make use of, utilise</li><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to enjoy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;neutan</i>, akin to Old High German <i>niozan</i>, Old Norse <i>njóta</i>, Gothic <i>𐌽𐌹𐌿𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (niutan, “to reach, to catch”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīewan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīewe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>neowol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>neowollīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>neowolnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīewian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīewnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prone (lying face down)</li><li>head-first</li><li>deep, low</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nīwalaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>profound</li></ol><p>From <i>neowol</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abyss</li></ol><p>From <i>neowol</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>neoþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>neath</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>beneath</li><li>below</li><li>from below</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;niþanē</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>the bottom (of something)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>neoþan</i> +&lrm; <i>-weard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scanty, lacking</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a very low tide; a neaptide</li></ol><p>From <i>nēp</i> +&lrm; <i>flōd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>catnip</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nepetā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>nēah</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>neorxnawang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nerienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to save; rescue; preserve</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;naʀjan</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>nera</i>, Old Saxon <i>nerian</i>, Old High German <i>nerian</i> (German <i>nähren</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nerienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>neorxnawang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>not to be</li><li>to survive, escape from, be saved</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>ne</i> +&lrm; <i>wesan</i>. Compare <i>nillan</i> and <i>nytan</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nesaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>no</li></ol><p>From <i>ne</i> (“not”) +&lrm; <i>sīe</i> (“let it be”). Compare <i>ġīese</i> (“yes”, literally “so be it”), whence English <i>yes</i>. More at <i>ne</i>, <i>wesan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nest</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīehst</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nest</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nestą</i>. Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>гнѣздо</i> (gnězdo, “nest”), Old Irish <i>net</i> (“nest”), Latin <i>nīdus</i> (“nest”), Sanskrit <i>नीड</i> (nīḍa, “nest”), Albanian <i>neth</i> (“sprout, bud”), Old Armenian <i>նիստ</i> (nist, “sitting; seat; property”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nihstiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a nest</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>nest</i> (“nest”) +&lrm; <i>-lian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nett</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nytan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nettle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;natilā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīeten</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>netele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>net</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nati</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>net</i>, Old Saxon <i>netti</i> and <i>fisknet</i>, Old High German <i>nezzi</i>, Old Norse <i>net</i>, Gothic <i>𐌽𐌰𐍄𐌹</i> (nati). Perhaps related to Albanian <i>neth</i> (“sprout, bud”) and Russian <i>нить</i> (nitʹ, “thread”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīehsta</i> (“neighbor”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dare, risk, venture</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nanþijaną</i> (“to dare, to have courage”). Related to nōþ (“daring, presumption”) in names such as Bēagnōþ and Byrhtnōþ.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>new-, newly</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>*niwi-</i>, before a regular sound change in which non-word-initial *w was lost before fully unstressed *i. The same sound change occurred to <i>ǣ</i> (“law”) ← <i>*ǣi</i> ← <i>*āwi</i>, <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) ← <i>*sǣi</i> ← <i>*sāwi</i>, and <i>glī</i> (“joy”) ← <i>*gliwi</i>. It must have also occurred to <i>nīewe</i> (“new”) in the nominative singular, producing <i>*nī</i> ← <i>*niwi</i>, but its *w was restored by analogy with its inflected forms, which had a following *j instead of *i.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fresh-baked</li></ol><p><i>nī-</i> +&lrm; <i>bacen</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>not I, not me</li></ol><p>From <i>ne-</i> +&lrm; <i>ic&#47;iċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>niċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say 'no'; to refuse</li></ol><p>From <i>nic</i> (“not I”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīcenned</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>newborn</li></ol><p><i>nī-</i> +&lrm; <i>cenned</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nicor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water demon, water monster</li><li>hippopotamus</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nikraz</i>, perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;neygʷ-</i> (“to wash”). Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>necker</i> (Dutch <i>nikker</i> (“demon”)), Old High German <i>nicchus</i> (German <i>Nix</i> (“water sprite”)), Old Norse <i>nykr</i> (Faroese <i>nykur</i>, Swedish <i>näck</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;hapax legomenon) the abode of a <i>nicor</i> (“water monster”)</li></ol><p>From <i>nicor</i> (“water monster”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nihtegale</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīedan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>force, compulsion</li><li>need as an abstract concept, distress</li><li>a need or necessity for something</li><li>a situation of distress or lack of something</li><li>emergency</li><li>the runic character ᚾ (/n/)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;naudi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to force</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>nīed</i> (“need, compulsion”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an exaction, due, or toll</li></ol><p>From <i>nīed</i> +&lrm; <i>bād</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>necessary, needful, needed</li></ol><p><i>nīed</i> (“need”) +&lrm; <i>behēfe</i> (“necessary”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prison</li></ol><p><i>nīed</i> (“force”) +&lrm; <i>clēofa</i> (“chamber”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>out of necessity</li><li>not willingly</li></ol><p><i>nīed</i> (“need”) +&lrm; <i>-es</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tribute, exaction, a forced payment</li></ol><p><i>nīed</i> (“force”) +&lrm; <i>ġield</i> (“payment”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who is forced to pay</li></ol><p>From By surface analysis, <i>nīedgield</i> (“force payment”) +&lrm; <i>-a</i> or <i>nīed</i> (“force”) +&lrm; <i>ġielda</i> (“payer”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rape</li></ol><p><i>nīed</i> (“force”) +&lrm; <i>hǣmed</i> (“sex”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>command through compulsion</li></ol><p>From <i>nīed</i> +&lrm; <i>hǣs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>necessary</li></ol><p><i>nīed</i> (“need, necessity”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) great suffering, deep distress</li></ol><p>From <i>nīed</i> +&lrm; <i>wracu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>West Saxon</i>) superlative degree of <i>nēah</i>: nearest, next</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nāhwist(ō)</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nēhwist</i>, <i>&#42;nēhwistaz</i> (“nearest, closest”). Compare Old High German <i>nāhisto</i>, Old Norse <i>næstr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neighbour</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>nīehst</i> (“nearest, next”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nēhwist</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>niht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to constrain, repress</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;narwijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confinement, prison</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>nierwan</i> (“to constrain”) +&lrm; <i>-þ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>animal</li><li>cattle</li></ol><p>From <i>nēat</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of animal</li></ol><p><i>nīeten</i> (“animal”) +&lrm; <i>cynn</i> (“kind”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>lately, recently</li></ol><p>From <i>nīewe</i> (“new”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>new</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;niwi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;niwjaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;néwyos</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;néwos</i> (“new”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>nī</i>, Old Saxon <i>niuwi</i>, Old High German <i>niuwi</i>, Old Norse <i>nýr</i>, and Gothic <i>𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (niujis). Extra-Germanic cognates include Lithuanian <i>naũjas</i>, Old Irish <i>nuae</i>, and Sanskrit <i>नव्य</i> (návya).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to renew: renovate, restore</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;niuwjōn</i>, equivalent to <i>nīewe</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>niuwōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>recently, lately</li></ol><p>From <i>nīewe</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>newness, novelty</li></ol><p>From <i>nīewe</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beginning, rudiment</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>nīewian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>neowol</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nibilaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>niece</li><li>granddaughter</li><li>stepdaughter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;niftiz</i> (“niece, granddaughter”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;néptih₂</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nigon</i> (“nine”).</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nigon</i> (“nine”).</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nigoþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>nine</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nigun</i>, an unexplained variant of <i>&#42;neun</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;newun</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁néwn̥</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-nine</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-ninth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-nine</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-ninth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-nine</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-ninth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-nine</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-ninth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventy-nine</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventy-ninth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-nine</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-ninth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-nine</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-ninth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>nine hundred</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ninefold</li></ol><p>From <i>nigon</i> +&lrm; <i>-feald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nine hundred years old</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>nigon hund</i> (“nine hundred”) +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nigontīene</i> (“nineteen”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>nineteenth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nineteenth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;newundô tehundô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>nineteen</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;newuntehun</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>niguntīne</i>, Old High German <i>niunzehan</i>, Old Norse <i>nítján</i>, Gothic <i>&#42;𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌽𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿𐌽</i> (&#42;niuntaihun).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hundnigontiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nine year(s) old</li><li>lasting nine years</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;newunwintruz</i>, though in the North Sea Germanic daughter languages, the word for "nine" somehow changed to <i>*nigun</i>. Equivalent to <i>nigon</i> +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ninth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nigunþō</i>, alternative form of <i>&#42;nigundō</i>, <i>&#42;neundō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;newundô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>niċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>neowol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>neowol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīehst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīehsta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not partaking, abstaining, fasting</li><li>sober</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;nihwistiġ</i>, from <i>&#42;nih-</i> (perhaps from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nehw</i>) + <i>&#42;wistiġ</i> (“supping, dining”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>night</li><li>day (when computing spans of time)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nahti</i>, inflected form of <i>&#42;naht</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nahts</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nókʷts</i>; the alternative forms <i>næht</i>, <i>neaht</i> directly continue Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;naht</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>nacht</i>, Old Saxon <i>naht</i>, Old High German <i>naht</i>, Old Norse <i>nátt</i>, Gothic <i>𐌽𐌰𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (nahts); also with Ancient Greek <i>νύξ</i> (núx), Latin <i>nox</i>, Russian <i>ночь</i> (nočʹ).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) alternative form of <i>nihtbealu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) nocturnal evil, evil of the night</li></ol><p><i>niht</i> +&lrm; <i>bealu</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nihtegale</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nightingale</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nahtigalā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nocturnal</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>niht</i> (“night”) +&lrm; <i>-erne</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at night</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nahtas</i> (“at night”), derived from <i>&#42;naht</i> (“night”) but with the ending taken from <i>&#42;dagas</i> (“during the day”), an adverbial use of the genitive of <i>&#42;dag</i> (Old English <i>dæġ</i>). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>nachtes</i>, Old Saxon <i>nahtes</i>, Old High German <i>nahtes</i> (German <i>nachts</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an evil being active at night</li></ol><p><i>niht</i> (“night”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;genġa</i> (“walker”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hyena</li></ol><p>From, and identical to, the feminine form of <i>nihtgenġa</i> (“night walker”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a number or count of days</li></ol><p>From <i>niht</i> (“night”) +&lrm; <i>ġerīm</i> (“count, number”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darkness of night</li></ol><p>From <i>niht</i> (“night”) +&lrm; <i>glōm</i> (“gloom”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) "night-helm"; cover of night</li></ol><p>From <i>niht</i> (“night”) +&lrm; <i>helm</i> (“helmet”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raven</li></ol><p>From <i>niht</i> (“night”) +&lrm; <i>hrōc</i> (“rook”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nightbird</li></ol><p>From <i>niht</i> +&lrm; <i>hræfn</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>nahthraƀan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nightlong; lasting the length of a night</li></ol><p>From <i>niht</i> (“night”) +&lrm; <i>lang</i> (“long”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nightly, of the night, nocturnal</li></ol><p>From <i>niht</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>nahtlīh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fasting for a night</li></ol><p><i>niht</i> (“night”) +&lrm; <i>nihstiġ</i> (“fasting”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bed</li></ol><p>From <i>niht</i> (“night”) +&lrm; <i>rest</i> (“bed, resting place”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compline, the last of the canonical hours</li></ol><p>From <i>niht</i> (“night”) +&lrm; <i>sang</i> (“liturgical service”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nightshade (plant belonging to the genus <i>Solanum</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nahtskadō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) night-shade</li></ol><p>From <i>niht</i> (“night”) +&lrm; <i>sċūa</i> (“shadow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abundant</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>niht</i> +&lrm; <i>-sum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>nihtsum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be enough or sufficient</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>nihtsum</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abundance</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>nihtsum</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nihtwacu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>night watch</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nahtwaku</i>. Equivalent to <i>niht</i> (“night”) +&lrm; <i>wacu</i> (“watch”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nightwear</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>niht</i> +&lrm; <i>waru</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>night-guardian</li></ol><p>From <i>niht</i> (“night”) +&lrm; <i>weard</i> (“guard”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>night watch</li><li>vigil</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>niht</i> (“night”) +&lrm; <i>wæċċe</i> (“watch”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nyllan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Nile (a river in Africa).</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Nīlus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) [<i>with</i> accusative] to take</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;neman</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nemaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nem-</i>.</p><p>Germanic cognates include Old Frisian <i>nima</i>, Old Saxon <i>niman</i>, Old Dutch <i>neman</i>, Old High German <i>neman</i>, Old Norse <i>nema</i>, Gothic <i>𐌽𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (niman). The Indo-European root was also the source of Old Irish <i>nem</i> (“present”), Latvian <i>ņemt</i> (“to take”), Albanian <i>nëm</i> (“to take, curse”), Ancient Greek <i>νέμω</i> (némō).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>nimaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ninevah</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Nīnevē</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Νινευή</i> (Nineuḗ), ultimately from Akkadian <i>𒌷𒉌𒉡𒀀 𒀏</i> (/⁠Ninua⁠/) or Old Babylonian <i>𒌷𒉌𒉡𒀀</i> (/⁠Ninuwā⁠/)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ninevite</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nēod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nigon</i> (“nine”).</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nēosian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nihstiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nēosung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīewe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīewnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>neoþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nigoþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>neoþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Meaning unclear, glosses Latin <i>rudente</i>, a form of <i>rū̆dēns</i> (“rope, roaring”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow dark, darken</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Has been connected with Lithuanian <i>niáukti</i> (“to become overcast, gloomy”), via a hypothetical English pre-form <i>&#42;(kʷ)niaukʷ-</i>; however, this is rather ad hoc.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>nīpeþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nierwan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nierwþ</i></li></ol></html>:/ˈnis/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>ne</i> +&lrm; <i>is</i>, literally “is not”</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nistlan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nihstiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nestlian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nytan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīeten</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neophyte</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīewan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>newcome, newly come</li></ol><p><i>nīewe</i> (“new”) +&lrm; <i>cumen</i> (“come”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīewe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>neowol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>neowellīċ</i></li></ol><p>From <i>niwel</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>neowolnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>neowolnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīewian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīewlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīewnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīewnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>neowol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīewung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nihstiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>nīþing</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>envy, jealousy</li><li>hostility, hatred, evil, violence</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nīþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nīþą</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>nīth</i>, Dutch <i>nijd</i>, Old High German <i>nīd</i> (German <i>Neid</i>), Old Norse <i>níð</i> (regional Swedish <i>nid</i>), Gothic <i>𐌽𐌴𐌹𐌸</i> (neiþ).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to envy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nīþijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nīþijaną</i> (“to envy, be jealous of, begrudge, revile”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;neyH-</i> (“to be angry”). By surface analysis, <i>nīþ</i> (“envy”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i> (infinitive suffix). Cognate with Old High German <i>nīden</i> (“to envy”), Old Norse <i>níða</i> (“to revile”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) hostile dragon</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>nīþ</i> (“hostile”) +&lrm; <i>draca</i> (“dragon”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>down, downwards</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;niþer</i> from the comparative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ni-</i> (“down, below”). Cognate with Old High German <i>nidar</i>, Old Norse <i>niðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>below, beneath; situated in a lower position, lower</li><li>down, downward</li><li>inferior</li></ol><p>From <i>niþer</i> (“below, down”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lower, lowest, under</li><li>inferior</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;niþeraz</i>, from the comparative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ni-</i> (“below, down”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring low, humiliate</li><li>to condemn</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>niþer</i> (“below”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix). Cognate with Old High German <i>nidaren</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>low</li></ol><p>From <i>niþer</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lowness, a low place, the bottom</li></ol><p>From <i>niþera</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>descent</li></ol><p>From By surface analysis, <i>niþer-</i> +&lrm; <i>stīgan</i> (“to go (up)”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>downward</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>turned downward</li></ol><p>From By surface analysis, <i>niþer-</i> +&lrm; <i>-weard</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīþfull</i></li></ol><p>From <i>nīþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ful</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full of jealousy or envy; jealous</li></ol><p>From <i>nīþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-full</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>enviously, maliciously</li></ol><p><i>nīþfull</i> (“envious”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an attack, malice with intent to wound or kill</li></ol><p>From <i>nīþ</i> (“strife, enmity, attack, war: evil, hatred, spite”) +&lrm; <i>ġetēon</i> (“to draw, lead, incite, excite”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) hostile, battle-fierce</li></ol><p><i>nīþ</i> +&lrm; <i>grimm</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>envious</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nīþīg</i>, equivalent to <i>nīþan</i> (“to envy”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>nīdīg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>villain, vile person</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nīþą</i> (“envy, hate, malice”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>níðingr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>down</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;niþrai</i>. Cognate with Icelandic <i>niðri</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>niþerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>niþerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a hall in which one is exposed to an enemy's hatred</li></ol><p>From <i>nīþ</i> (“hatred”) +&lrm; <i>sele</i> (“hall”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) battle, conflict</li></ol><p>From <i>nīþ</i> (“strife, enmity, attack, war: evil, hatred, spite”) +&lrm; <i>weorc</i> (“work”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;rare) men</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;niþjaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nā</i></li></ol><p><i>ne</i> +&lrm; <i>ā</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Noah (Biblical character)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Nōē</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Νῶε</i> (Nôe), from Hebrew <i>נֹחַ</i> (Nōaḥ).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nāwiht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nam</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nama</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Nones, the ninth hour after sunrise</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Nones, the religious service appointed to this hour</li><li>a form of address from younger to older monks</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>nōna</i> (“ninth; ninth hour”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a meal occurring after Nones (the ninth hour of the day)</li></ol><p>From <i>nōn</i> (“Nones”) +&lrm; <i>ġereord</i> (“meal”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>afternoon meal, lunch</li></ol><p>From <i>nōn</i> (“ninth hour”) +&lrm; <i>mete</i> (“food”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nones</li></ol><p>From <i>nōn</i> (“Nones”) +&lrm; <i>sang</i> (“song, liturgical service”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nones; the ninth hour after sunrise</li></ol><p>From <i>nōn</i> (“ninth hour”) +&lrm; <i>tīd</i> (“time”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nones; the ninth hour after sunrise</li></ol><p>From <i>nōn</i> (“ninth hour”) +&lrm; <i>tīma</i> (“time”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Normandy</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Norren</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Norwegian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Norse <i>norrǿnn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nurþrōnijaz</i>; thus a doublet of <i>norþerne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Norþweġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Norþmandisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Norþmann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>norþmest</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>norþmest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>north</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>north, northward</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;norþr</i> (compare Old High German <i>nord</i>, Old Norse <i>norðr</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from the north</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;norþanā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nurþanē</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Northumbrians</li><li>Northumbria (an Anglo-Saxon kingdom in the Northeast of England; in full, Kingdom of Northumbria)</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>norþan</i> (“from the north”) + <i>Humbre</i> (“the Humber”), thus meaning “people from north of the Humber”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) ‘north-Dane’, the northern Danes</li></ol><p><i>norþ</i> (“north”) +&lrm; <i>Dene</i> (“Danes”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the north, a northern part</li></ol><p>From <i>norþ</i> (“north”) +&lrm; <i>dǣl</i> (“part”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>northeast</li></ol><p>From <i>norþ</i> +&lrm; <i>ēast</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the northeastern end or extremity</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>norþ</i> (“north”) +&lrm; <i>ēast</i> (“east”) +&lrm; <i>ende</i> (“end”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>northern</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nurþrōnijaz</i>. Compare Old High German <i>nordrōni</i>, Old Norse <i>norrœnn</i> (borrowed into Old English as <i>Norren</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>norþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>northward, north</li></ol><p><i>norþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-weard</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the people of Norfolk</li></ol><p>From <i>norþ</i> (“north”) +&lrm; <i>folc</i> (“people”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Northampton</li></ol><p>From <i>norþ</i> (“north”) +&lrm; <i>Hāmtūn</i> (its earlier name, changed to distinguish from other towns), from <i>hām</i> (“home”) +&lrm; <i>tūn</i> (“town”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the north side, north (of a place)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>norþ</i> (“north”) +&lrm; <i>healf</i> (“side, half”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Norþanhymbre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Northumbrian</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a Northumbrian</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>Norþanhymbre</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>, perhaps from an earlier <i>*Norþanhymbrisċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a northern land or country</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>norþ</i> (“north”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Norþmann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Norman</li></ol><p>From <i>Norþmann</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Norwegian</li><li><i>(more generally)</i> a Scandinavian, Norseman</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>norþ</i> +&lrm; <i>mann</i>. Perhaps either derived from or the source of Old Norse <i>norðmaðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>northmost</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>northmost</li></ol><p>From <i>norþ</i> (“north”) +&lrm; <i>-mest</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the northern Mercians</li></ol><p><i>norþ</i> (“north”) +&lrm; <i>Mierċe</i> (“Mercians”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>norþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>norþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>due north</li></ol><p><i>norþ</i> (“north”) +&lrm; <i>rihte</i> (“straight”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the northern Scots</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>norþ</i> (“north”) +&lrm; <i>Sċottas</i> (“Scots”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Welsh</li></ol><p><i>norþ</i> +&lrm; <i>Wēalas</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Norway (a country in Scandinavia in Northern Europe)</li></ol><p>Originally a calque of Old Norse <i>Norvegr</i> ~ <i>Norðvegr</i>. Equivalent to <i>norþ</i> (“north”) +&lrm; <i>weġ</i> (“way”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a narrow band or ribbon worn around the head, usually as an ornament; headband</li><li>any narrow strip, as of wood or metal</li><li>a strip of any material used for binding or securing</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nustilǭ</i>, <i>&#42;nustō</i> (“knotting; band; clasp”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ned-</i> (“to twist; know; plait”). Cognate with Middle Low German <i>nusche</i> (“clasp; barrette”), Old High German <i>nusta</i> (“band; strap; sling”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nose</li><li>a ness</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nosu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nostril</li></ol><p>From <i>nosu</i> (“nose”) +&lrm; <i>þȳrel</i> (“hole”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>nosterle</i> (“nostril”).</p><p>Compare also Middle Low German <i>noster</i>, <i>nüster</i> (whence German <i>Nüster</i>), which is usually regarded as a different construct, however.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sign; mark; a mark made on an object</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>nota</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make use of, employ, use</li><li>to enjoy</li><li>to discharge an office</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;notōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nutōną</i> (“to make use of”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nutō</i> (“use, utilisation, enjoyment”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;newd-</i> (“to acquire, make use of”). Akin to Old English <i>notu</i> (“use”), Old English <i>nēotan</i> (“to use”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>notiaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>notiende</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>use</li><li>enjoyment</li><li>profit, advantage, utility</li><li>an office; occupation, employment, function, work</li><li>the conducting of business, business affairs; the discharge of duty or office</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;notu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nutō</i> (“use, enjoyment”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;newd-</i> (“to acquire, make use of”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>note</i> (“income from agriculture and animal husbandry”), Middle Dutch <i>not</i> (“income from the land”), Old High German <i>nuz</i> (“use”, noun). Related to Old English <i>nytt</i> (“use”), Old English <i>nēotan</i> (“to use, enjoy, possess”), Old English <i>nēat</i> (“cattle, property”). More at neat.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>useful</li></ol><p><i>notu</i> (“use”) +&lrm; <i>-wierþe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who takes notes; stenographer, notary (glosses Latin <i>notārius</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>not</i> +&lrm; <i>wrītere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>November</li></ol><p>Unadapted borrowing from Latin <i>Nōvember</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shipmaster; sailor; skipper; mariner</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;nō</i>, <i>&#42;nōw</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>-end</i> (agent suffix), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nō</i> (“ship”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nāwiht</i></li></ol><p><i>ne</i> +&lrm; <i>ōwiht</i></p></html>:/noːθ/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daring, presumption</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nanþu</i>, from <i>&#42;nanþ</i> (“bold, daring”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-u</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>nand</i> (“bravery”), West Frisian <i>noed</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>nōþ</i> (“daring”) and <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>nōþ</i> (“daring”) + <i>heard</i> (“brave, hard”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>nōþ</i> (“daring”) and <i>helm</i> (“helmet, protection”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>nōþ</i> (“daring”) and <i>here</i> (“army”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>nōþ</i> (“daring”) and <i>mund</i> (“protector”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>nōþ</i> (“daring”) and <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”). Compare <i>Wulfnoþ</i> which has the same elements reversed.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>now</li><li>just (in the sense "recently")</li><li>since</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>now that</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nū</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>right now</li><li>just now</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>capacious: able to hold or take a lot</li></ol><p>Derived from the verb <i>niman</i> (“to take”) via the suffix <i>-ol</i> (“apt to, able to”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>nū</i> (“now”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nūnō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nun; vestal</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nunnā</i> (“nun”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nunnery</li></ol><p>From <i>nunne</i> (“nun”) +&lrm; <i>hīred</i> (“household, company”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nunnery</li></ol><p>From <i>nunne</i> (“nun”) +&lrm; <i>mynster</i> (“monastery”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a nun's habit</li></ol><p>From <i>nunne</i> (“nun”) +&lrm; <i>sċrūd</i> (“clothing”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Nicene</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīed</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīedan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīedbād</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīedes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīedġield</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīedwracu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nigon</i> (“nine”).</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>niht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nihtsum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>negation of <i>willan</i>: not to want, not to intend, etc.</li><li>to refuse</li></ol><p>From a contraction of the particle <i>ne</i> (“not”) + <i>willan</i> (“to want”). The same development happened with <i>nytan</i> (“to not know”), <i>nesan</i> (“to not be”), <i>nabban</i> (“to not have”), and <i>nāgan</i> (“to not own”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>nilla</i>, <i>nella</i> (“to not want”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>unless</li><li>except</li></ol><p>Possibly a fossilized dative/instrumental form of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;numþi</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trunk of an elephant</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;niebel</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nabīl</i>, equivalent to <i>nebb</i> +&lrm; <i>-el</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nierwan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nis</i> (“is not”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to not know, not be aware of</li><li>to not feel a certain emotion</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>ne</i> (“not”) + <i>witan</i> (“to know”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīeten</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ignorant</li></ol><p>Derived from, and identical to, the past participle of <i>nytan</i> (“to not know”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīetencynn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ignorance <i>(+genitive of something)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>nyten</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>useful</li></ol><p>From <i>nytt</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Compare Old High German <i>nuzzilīh</i> (“useful”), German <i>nützlich</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>usefully</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>nytt</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i> or <i>nytlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>usefulness, utility</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>nytlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>usefulness</li><li>use</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>nytt</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>useful</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>use</li><li>benefit</li><li>office, duty</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nuti</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nutjō</i> (“profit, yield, use”). Cognate with Dutch <i>nut</i>, Old High German <i>nuz</i>, Swedish <i>nytta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to make use of, enjoy</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>nytt</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>useful</li><li>advantageous</li><li>beneficial</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>nyttian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>nytt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>nytt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>profit</li><li>advantage</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>nyttian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nytwierþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>useful; advantageous; profitable</li></ol><p>From <i>nytt</i> +&lrm; <i>-wierþe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>usefulness, utility</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>nytwierþe</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>nytwierþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>nytwierþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nytwierþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nytwierþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>nytwyrþnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nytwierþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nytwierþnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>neowol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nihstiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>niþer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>niþerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>niþernes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nacod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill</li></ol><p>Of unknown origin. Perhaps an alteration of <i>nǣtan</i> (“to trample upon, crush, subdue”). Compare also Old Norse <i>hneita</i> (“to beat, overcome”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nakedness</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nakwadi</i>, <i>&#42;nakwadī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nakwadį̄</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nacodnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nacodnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nihtegale</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nǣdre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mongoose; ichneumon.</li></ol><p><i>nǣdre</i> (“snake”) +&lrm; <i>bita</i> (“biter”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>needle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nāþlu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nēþlō</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>nādle</i>, Old Saxon <i>nādla</i>, Old Dutch <i>nālda</i>, Old High German <i>nādala</i>, Old Norse <i>nál</i>, Gothic <i>𐌽𐌴𐌸𐌻𐌰</i> (nēþla).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snake</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nadrā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nadrǭ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>natra</i>, Old Norse <i>naðra</i>, Gothic <i>𐌽𐌰𐌳𐍂𐍃</i> (nadrs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nǣderbita</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) adderwort</li></ol><p>From <i>nǣdre</i> (“snake”) +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i> (“plant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nefne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>never</li></ol><p><i>ne</i> +&lrm; <i>ǣfre</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>never again</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>never again</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poverty, indigence</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>address, accost, speak to</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nāhwijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>næġl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the nail of a finger or toe</li><li>a nail or peg as a fastening</li><li>an instrument used to play the strings of a harp, a plectrum; (<i>in compounds</i>) handle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;nagl</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;naglaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃nogʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nail</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;naglijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;naglijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>næġl</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>næġlan</i> (“to nail”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;naglōn</i>, alteration of earlier <i>&#42;naglijan</i>, or derived from the noun.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nīehst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>niht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>numol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>no one, none, not any</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not any, none, no</li></ol><p>From <i>ne</i> +&lrm; <i>ǣniġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turnip</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>nāpus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>νᾶπυ</i> (nâpu).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rapeseed, turnip seed</li></ol><p>From <i>nǣp</i> (“turnip”) +&lrm; <i>sǣd</i> (“seed”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not, not at all</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>not</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a ness, promontory</li></ol><p>From <i>nals</i>, <i>nālæs</i>, <i>nāles</i>, <i>nālles</i> (“not at all, by no means, absolutely not”), from a blend of <i>nā</i> (“never”) +&lrm; <i>ealles</i> (“completely”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>nās</i> (“not, not at all”).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nasją</i> (“promontory; ness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fawn-skin</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;naskiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hnesċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Northumbrian</i>) no</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lipped vessel</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nosþȳrel</i> (“nostril”)</li></ol><p>From <i>næs</i> (“nose”) +&lrm; <i>þȳrel</i> (“hole”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trample upon, crush, subdue</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from earlier <i>&#42;hnǣtan</i> (whence also Old English <i>hnǣċan</i> and <i>nǣċan</i>), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hnaitijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ā</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>of</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ōfer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ac</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ocea</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ocing</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Ocea</li></ol><p>From <i>Ocea</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) armpit</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Otto</li><li>Otto II, Holy Roman Emperor</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Odyssey</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>from</li><li>out of</li><li>off</li><li>by (indicating the creator of a work)</li><li>of (Denoting material made of)</li></ol><p>Unstressed form of <i>æf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>off, away, from, out of, away from</li><li>down</li><li>excessively, negatively</li><li>for, for the purpose of</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aba-</i> (“away, away from”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂epó</i> (“off, away”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>af-</i>, Old Norse <i>af-</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-), Old High German <i>ab</i>; and with Latin <i>ab-</i>, Ancient Greek <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>slēan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very cold</li></ol><p>From the past participle of <i>&#42;ofcalan</i>, equivalent to <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġecalen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obtain by calling out</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>clipian</i> (“call out”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>down</li></ol><p>From <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>dūne</i> (“hill”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dūnǭ</i> (“sand dune”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ofn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>over, above</li><li>across</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>over, above</li><li>over, Indicating relative status, authority, or power</li><li>across</li><li>beside (+accusative)</li><li>beyond</li><li>after</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>border, edge; specifically, shore, riverbank</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;obar</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uber</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;upér</i>, a comparative form of <i>*upo</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ōferaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>overnight</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>used to form nouns, verbs, adjectives, adverbs and prepositions having intensive force, or with a sense of "going over, above"</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uber</i>. See <i>ofer</i> (“over”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to outlive, outlast, survive; overlive</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>bīdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to infringe, violate (an agreement)</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>brecan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eyebrow</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>brū</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bridge over</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>bryċġian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to avoid, shun</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>būgan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>oferċierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a passing over</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċierr</i> (“turn, occaision”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass from one place to another; to transfer</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċierran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to climb over, ascend, climb upon</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>climban</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cry out, exclaim</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>clipian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overcome or overtake</li><li>to win or be victorious</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>cuman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>oferċierr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>oferċierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overdo; do too much, do in excess</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>dōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overcome, defeat, repel, refute</li><li>to cover by drifting</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>drīfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overdrink, inebriate</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>drincan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who drinks excessively; drunkard, alcoholic</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>drincan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surplus</li><li>rest, remainder</li></ol><p><i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>ēaca</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gluttonous</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> (“over”) +&lrm; <i>etol</i> (“gluttonous”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go off, pass or cross something, go or pass through, penetrate, traverse</li><li>to come upon, come across, meet with</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>faran</i>. Compare Old High German <i>ubarfaran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall upon, attack; beset; onfall</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;obarfallan</i>, <i>&#42;ubirfallan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ubirfallaną</i>; equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>feallan</i>. Cognate with Old Dutch <i>overfallan</i>, <i>ovirfallon</i> (“to attack; assault”), German <i>überfallen</i> (“to assault, attack, raid”), Swedish <i>överfalla</i> (“to attack”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass through, pass over</li><li>to cross, traverse</li></ol><p><i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>fēran</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overflow, overflood, cover with water</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>flōd</i> (“flood”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i> (infinitive suffix) (with <i>i</i>-umlaut).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overcome, get the better of</li></ol><p><i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>flītan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to overflow; cover with water</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>flōwan</i>. Cognate with Middle Low German <i>ȫvervloien</i> (“to overflow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frozen over</li></ol><p><i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>froren</i> (“frozen”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pursue, persecute, attack</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fill to overflowing, cram, feed in excess; feed too much</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>fyllan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passage, transit</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Passover</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>færeld</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>obese</li></ol><p><i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>fǣtt</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overspread, overrun, conquer, be over anything, come upon, attack; onfall</li><li>to go or pass over a limit, transgress</li><li>to go across, traverse, pass off or away, come to an end</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>gān</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>ubargān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a going across</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>gang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go over, cross, pass</li><li>to conquer, come upon</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uberganganą</i>. Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>gangan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sow seed over other sown seed</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġesāwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transgress</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;gǣġan</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;gaigijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to command, govern</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>habban</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>generally, in general, in every case</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>hēafod</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) towering</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer</i> +&lrm; <i>hēah</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold over, delay, neglect</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>healdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass by, neglect, omit</li><li>to uplift, exalt</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>hebban</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>ubarhevan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂𐌷𐌰𐍆𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (ufarhafjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overshadow</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>helmian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ravage</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>hergian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with dative</i>) to not listen to, disregard, disobey</li><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to overhear</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uberhauzijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>hīeran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump over <i>(+accusative)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>hlēapan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unhearing</li><li>inattentive to sound</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>hlēoþor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to outsound, exceed soundwise</li><li>to exceed</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>hlēoþrian</i>, else <i>oferhlēoþor</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to despise, scorn, be contemptuous of</li><li>to hold in contempt</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>hyċġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pride</li></ol><p><i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;hyġd</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>proud, arrogant, haughty</li></ol><p><i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>hyġdiġ</i> (“disposed, mindfull”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>oferhīeran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) great-horned</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer</i> +&lrm; <i>ġehyrned</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay over, above, or upon something else, cover the surface of</li><li>to overburden, oppress; weigh down</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;obarlaggjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uberlagjaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>leċġan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>overledza</i>, <i>ūrledza</i>, Old High German <i>ubarleggen</i>, Gothic <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂𐌻𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (ufarlagjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) loved</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer</i> +&lrm; <i>lēof</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to outlive, survive</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>libban</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>ubarlebēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oppress</li><li>to cover; overspread; bethatch; draw over</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣdan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>ubarleiten</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave over; not consume</li><li>to be left over</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ofermettu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pride</li></ol><p>From pre-Old English <i>*obærmōdiþu</i>, equivalent to <i>ofermōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-þu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>proud</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) pride</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> + <i>-mōd</i>. Compare German <i>Übermut</i> (“high spirits”), Dutch <i>overmoed</i> (“overconfidence”), Danish <i>overmod</i> (“overconfidence”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>proudly</li></ol><p><i>ofermōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take by force or violence</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>niman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>afternoon; the ninth hour after sunrise</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer</i> (“over”) +&lrm; <i>nōn</i> (“the ninth hour after sunrise”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extravagant</li></ol><p><i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>ranc</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to convince</li><li>to convict</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>reċċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ride across (on horseback)</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>rīdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to read over, read through</li><li>to read over to another or oneself</li><li>to consider</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sow seed over other sown seed; to oversow</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>sāwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sprinkle over with something</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>sceādan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overshadow</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċeadwian</i>, potentially as a calque of Latin <i>obumbrāre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overlook, superintend</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċēawian</i>. Compare Dutch <i>overschouwen</i> (“to oversee”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overshadow</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transmit; send something over</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>sendan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to observe, survey, see; oversee</li><li>to overlook, neglect, despise</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēon</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>ovarsehan</i>, Old High German <i>ubarsehan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set over, put in a position of authority</li><li>to be overcome with something</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ubarsattjan</i>, equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>settan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit upon, occupy; sit over, take possession of</li><li>to abstain from; forbear</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>sittan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an outer garment</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;slop</i> (“garment”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say too much, use too many words</li><li>to say or speak too much, more than is just</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>sprecan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>ubarsprehhan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>overtalkative, too loquacious</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>sprecol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>talkativeness, loquaciousness</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ofersprecol</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>. Literally <i>*overspeakleness</i> if it survived in Modern English.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>excess speech, loquaciousness</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>sprǣċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>talkative, loquacious</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>sprǣċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overspread, cover, bethatch</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>sprǣdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand or be above, stand over</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>standan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overstep, cross; step over, exceed</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>steppan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>climb over: surmount, rise above</li><li>to go past, cross</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) <i>(by extension)</i> to be more or better: surpass, transcend, exceed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uberstīganą</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>obarstīgan</i>, Old Norse <i>yfirstíga</i>, Gothic <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂𐍃𐍄𐌴𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (ufarsteigan). Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>stīgan</i>, literally "to climb over or across."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) superlative</li></ol><p>From <i>oferstīgan</i> +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overcome</li><li>to defeat</li><li>to conquer</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>swīþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oppression</li><li>distress</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>swīþness</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>oferswīþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>transmarine</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>sǣlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>excessive happiness</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>sǣlþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw one thing over another</li><li>to cover by drawing one thing over another; to eclipse; to obscure</li><li>to bring to an end; to end, conclude</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>tēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the condition of being covered; obstruction; obfuscation</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expound, comment upon</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>trahtnian</i> (“to expound”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cross over (something) by wading</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>wadan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn over, overturn</li><li>to throw upon, sprinkle</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fall down</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>weorpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conquer, vanquish, subdue</li><li>to defeat</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;obarwinnan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>ubarwinnan</i>. Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>winnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to winter, pass the winter</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overwhelm</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrecan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover over</li><li>to veil, hide, or conceal</li><li>to overspread</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>covering</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>oferwrēon</i> +&lrm; <i>-els</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover by working, overlay, work a covering over something</li></ol><p>From <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>oferȳþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an excessive wave such as from a stormy sea</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>ȳþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think over</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ofer-</i> +&lrm; <i>þenċan</i> (“to think”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ofett</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fruit</li><li>bean, legume; vegetable</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;obaet</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Offa of Mercia</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall upon, kill by falling</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>feallan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fostermother</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Offa</li></ol><p>From <i>Offa</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to offer</li><li>to sacrifice</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>offerō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offering, sacrifice</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>offrian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to require, demand, seek satisfaction for, exact with violence, extort; wrest</li><li>to begin, start from</li></ol><p>From <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>gān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to require</li><li>to extort</li><li>to acquire, obtain</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>gangan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>derivative</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ofgangende</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ofgangan</i> +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give up, leave, abandon</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;abagebaną</i>, equivalent to <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġiefan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>afgevan</i>, Old High German <i>abageban</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ofġiefan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to keep from, hold back</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>habban</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to withhold, keep back, retain</li></ol><p>From <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>healdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lost, taken away, out of one's hand</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;abahandijaz</i>, equivalent to <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>hand</i> (“hand”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i> (adjective-forming suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>oflǣte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay down</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;abalagjaną</i>, equivalent to <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>lecgan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>oflǣte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oblation, offering</li><li>sacramental wafer</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>oblāta</i> (“offered”), past participle of <i>offerō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call to mind, recollect</li></ol><p>From <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>munan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oven, stove</li><li>furnace</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ofn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uhnaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ofer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efestlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ofraþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>offrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deny a charge</li></ol><p>From <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>sacan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divide off, separate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċeādan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to observe, see</li></ol><p>From <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beset</li><li>to press hard</li><li>to oppress</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>settan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;abslahan</i>, equivalent to <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>slēan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>abaslahan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>killing, destruction</li></ol><p>From <i>ofsleġen</i> (“slain”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offspring</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stop in place, remain, persist, continue; keep on</li><li>to condition, restore</li></ol><p>From <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>standan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to descend</li><li>to depart</li><li>to ascend</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>stīgan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pierce, stab, wound or kill with a thrust</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>stingan</i> (“to sting, stab”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>often, oft</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ufta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>oft</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take away, withdraw</li><li>to withhold, deprive</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;abateuhan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;abateuhaną</i>; cognates include Gothic <i>𐌰𐍆𐍄𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (aftiuhan). By surface analysis, <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>tēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>oft</i>: ofter</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>oft</i>: oftest</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>oft</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ufta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knock down and kill with a projectile</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>weorpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be exceedingly heated</li></ol><p>From <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>þefian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>too moist; overly damp</li></ol><p>From <i>of-</i> (“excessively”) +&lrm; <i>þinen</i> (past participle of <i>þīnan</i> (“to become moist or damp”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oppress, repress</li><li>to occupy by force</li></ol><p>From <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>þryċċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to displease, offend (impersonal, with dative)</li></ol><p>From <i>of-</i> +&lrm; <i>þynċan</i> (“to seem”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horror or terror</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ōgan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ōganą</i> (“to be afraid”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ocea</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ocing</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ōxta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chase or pursuit</li><li>persecution</li><li>fear, terror</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ą̄htu</i> (“persecution, fate, destiny, fear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Uhtaharjaz</i>. The first element is the ancestor to Old Norse <i>ótti</i> (“fear”), the second is equivalent to <i>here</i> (“warrior”), a common element in given names. Cognate with Old Norse <i>Óttarr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>olfend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soothe, caress, treat gently</li><li>to fawn over</li><li>to gratify</li><li>to act submissively towards</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>camel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;olbandu</i>. Doublet of <i>elpend</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>she-camel</li></ol><p>From <i>olfend</i> (“camel”) +&lrm; <i>mere</i> (“mare”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mount Olympus</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Olympus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Ὄλυμπος</i> (Ólumpos).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ā̆mber</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ā̆mber</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>on, in, at, among [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> dative <i>or</i> instrumental]</li><li>on, during [<i>with</i> accusative]</li><li>onto, into (to express allative motion or a change of state) [<i>with</i> accusative]</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with verbs of taking or depriving</i>) from</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ana</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ana</i>. See Old English <i>in</i> for more.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>forever</li></ol><p>Literally "in eternity."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>at the end</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on foot</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in danger</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in memory of, in memoriam</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;in gamundi</i>. The preposition <i>in</i> was replaced by <i>on</i> (earlier <i>*an</i>) in the West Saxon dialect during the separate prehistory of Old English. Cognate with Old High German <i>in gimunt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>on Godes naman</i></li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: on Godes naman)</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in God's name <i>(used as an intensifier)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surrender or give oneself up (+ dative = <i>to</i> someone)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ana handų gāną</i>, literally “to go into [someone's] hand” (= "hands"). Cognate with Old Norse <i>ganga á hǫnd</i>, cf. Old English <i>gangan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for nothing, in vain</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to occur (of a thought or idea): come to mind, cross one's mind</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>tō setle gān</i> (“to set (of the sun)”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on the/one's way</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;in</i> <i>&#42;wegą</i> (“on the/one's way”). Judging by Old High German <i>in weg</i> (“away, on the/one's way”), the sense "away" dates back at least to Proto-West Germanic. Note that the word <i>in</i> was replaced with <i>on</i> in the West Saxon dialect of Old English.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>at work</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall in battle</li></ol><p>Literally "to fall into slaughter." Paralleled by Old Norse <i>falla í val</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>these days</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>forming verbs and adjectives from participles and nouns, with various senses of on, upon, to, there, thither</li><li>(<i>inchoative</i>) in, into; prefix used to intensify an action or to denote origin or initiation</li><li>denoting reverse action</li><li>off, away</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;an-</i>, <i>&#42;ana-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;an</i>, <i>&#42;ana</i> (“on”).</p><p>From the unstressed form of <i>ond-, and-</i> (“against, back, in return”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a burning, kindling</li><li>that which is burned</li></ol><p>From <i>on</i> (“on”) +&lrm; <i>āl</i> (“fire”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to diminish, impair</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>beran</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wait, to await</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>bīdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unbind, set free, untie, free from a bond</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andabindaną</i>, equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>bindan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to taste or partake <i>(+ genitive of something)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>bītan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inspire</li><li>to animate, incite</li><li>to cause remorse</li><li>to stimulate</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>bryrdan</i>, apparently with the literal meaning of “prick (someone) into.”</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inspiration</li><li>animation, incitement</li><li>regret</li></ol><p>Composed of either <i>onbryrdan</i> or its past participle <i>onbryrded</i> + <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onbryrdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ābufan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend</li><li>to bow submissively</li><li>to submit, yield</li><li>to bend aside, deviate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>būgan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>on, about, round about, on the outside</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;andabiūtan</i>, equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>būtan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>abūta</i>, Middle Low German <i>enbûten</i>, Middle High German <i>enbūzen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ambehtan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>backwards, back</li></ol><p><i>on</i> + <i>bæc</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set fire to, to burn</li><li>to heat</li><li>to incite, kindle desire to do something</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>bærnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ancor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onċierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) to turn; to change position or direction</li><li>to turn into another form; to change</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go</li><li>to turn aside, avert</li><li>to turn back, revert, reverse</li><li>to revoke, retract</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċierran</i>. Compare Old High German <i>anakerēn</i> (normalized spelling), Old Frisian <i>ankēra</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onċierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recognize</li><li>to acknowledge</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;andaknāan</i>, equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>cnāwan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>intknāen</i>, <i>inknāen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>oncnāwnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knowledge, conception</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>oncnāwan</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knowledge</li></ol><p><i>oncnāwan</i> (“to know”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike down, cast down</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>cnyssan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to respond, to answer</li><li>to echo a sound back</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>cweþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>oncwæþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onċierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unacquainted with; unfamiliar with</li><li>lacking; suffering due to lack</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;andkunþīg</i>, equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>cȳþiġ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>antkundīg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>and</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>and-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>anda</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>andettan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>andettan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onfōn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>andlang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to undo</li><li>to open</li><li>to destroy</li><li>to release</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andadōną</i>, equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>dōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to get drunk</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>druncnian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>terrible, dreadful</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be scared <i>(+accusative or genitive of something)</i> <i>(often with a dative reflexive pronoun)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;andarādan</i>. Equivalent to <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>andswerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to extinguish</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> (“on, in, into”) +&lrm; <i>dwǣsċan</i> (“to extinguish, put out”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow old, become old</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>ealdian</i> (“to grow old”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dwell (+ preposition <i>on</i>, in)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to inhabit</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>eardian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>eardiend</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fear <i>(often with dative reflexive pronoun)</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;ēgan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ōgijaną</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Onela; a semi-legendary Swedish king living around the early sixth century CE.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Anulô</i>, literally “little ancestor”. Cognate with Proto-Norse <i>ᚨᚾᚢᛚᚨ</i> (anula) (Old Norse <i>Áli</i>) and Old High German <i>Anilo</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onerienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plough up</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>erian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onerian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onerienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move quickly; hurry; hasten</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anahatjaną</i>, equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>hettan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>anazzan</i> (“to solicit, excite, inflame, instigate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ōnetteþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unfealdan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find</li><li>to find out, discover</li><li>to feel, experience</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;andifindan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>andfindan</i> and Old High German <i>intfindan</i>. Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>findan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to receive, accept</li><li>to take</li><li>to perceive, catch on to</li><li>to undertake, undergo</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>ondfōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anda-</i> + <i>&#42;fanhaną</i> (“to accept”). Cognate with obsolete West Frisian <i>ûntfean</i> (“to receive”), Old High German <i>intfahan</i> (German <i>empfangen</i>). Equivalent to <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>fōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>before, in front of</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>finding</li><li>finding out, discovery</li><li>experience</li></ol><p><i>onfunden</i> + <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entrance, ingress</li><li>(<i>spiritual</i>) incursion</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>færeld</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>anga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>opposite</li><li>back</li><li>with</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>against (+dative)</li><li>against as in to oppose with hostility (+accusative)</li></ol><p>from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;in gagin</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;in gagin</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>angegin</i>, Middle Dutch <i>entegen</i> (Dutch <i>entegen</i>), Old High German <i>ingegin</i> and <i>ingagan</i> (German <i>entgegen</i>). By surface analysis, <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġean</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>towards; against, opposite to</li><li>back</li><li>again</li><li>until, for later, until such time as</li></ol><p>From <i>onġēan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a return</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>onġēan-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċyrring</i>, else <i>onġēanċyrran</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn or be against</li><li>to lie in the way</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>onġēan-</i> +&lrm; <i>hweorfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>what lies in the way</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>onġēanhworfen</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inborn, innate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;anagaboran</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>anagiboran</i>, Dutch <i>aangeboren</i> and Old High German <i>anagiboran</i>. Equivalent to <i>on-</i> (“on”) +&lrm; <i>ġeboren</i> (“born”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attack, assault</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġefeoht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onġēan</i></li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onġēan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Angelċiriċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Angelþēod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>among</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>on</i> +&lrm; <i>ġemang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onġemang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ongenþēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ongentheow, a legendary Swedish king mentioned in <i>Beowulf</i></li></ol><p>Second element <i>þēow</i> (“servant, slave”). Cognate with Old High German <i>Angandio</i>, Old Norse <i>Angantýr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ongenþēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to infuse</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġēotan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to understand, recognize, comprehend</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to seize</li><li>to perceive</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġietan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>anġinn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin, start</li><li>to attempt, try</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andaginnaną</i>, equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;ginnan</i>, found only with prefixes, from Proto-West Germanic simplex verb <i>&#42;ginnan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ginnaną</i> (“to make an opening, begin”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>andginnan</i>, Dutch <i>ontginnen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>andġit</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>onġietan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Engle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Angel-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Angelcyning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Angelcynn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Angelþēod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attack, assault</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;anagripi</i>, equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>gripe</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>anagrif</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horror, dread</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>grīsan</i> (“to shudder, feel horror”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>horrible, dreadful</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>grislīċ</i> or <i>ongrisa</i> + <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āhabban</i> (“to restrain; support”)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>habban</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be within a person's power or means; to be in accordance with a person's will or convenience [+ accusative or dative]</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;anhagōn</i>, equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;hagian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to promise</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>hātan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise up, heave up, erect, lift up, exalt</li><li>to leaven</li><li>to begin</li><li>to take away, remove</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>hebban</i> (“raise”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strengthen, comfort, or encourage</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>hierdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive&#32;and&#32;intransitive</i>) to bow down, bend down, press down, bow</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>hnīgan</i> (“to bow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, stir violently</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to disturb, agitate</li><li>to stir up, excite, arouse</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>hrēran</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to change, reverse</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to change, turn, revert</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>hweorfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consider</li><li>to reflect on</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>hyċġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onhierdan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imitation</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>onhyrian</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onhyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onhyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onhyriend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to imitate, emulate</li><li>to resemble</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>hyrian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imitator, emulator</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>onhyrian</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imitation, emulation</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>onhyrian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spring open, give way, yield</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>irnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>onlook; regard; respect</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>lēċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onlīesan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ānlīcnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) to unloose bonds</li><li>to release, deliver, liberate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;andalausijan</i>, equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>līesan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>inlōsen</i>, <i>intlōsen</i> (“to loosen, release”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>inlīhtan</i></li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>līhtan</i>, Related to Old High German <i>inliuhten</i> (“to enlighten, illuminate”), Gothic <i>𐌹𐌽𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌷𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (inliuhtjan, “to enlighten, illuminate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>along</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unlock</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;andalūkan</i>. Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>lūcan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onlīesan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead on or to; bring to</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;analaidijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;analaidijaną</i>, equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inscription</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>mearcung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>anmedla</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>amid, in the middle of</li></ol><p>From <i>on</i> +&lrm; <i>middan</i>, the dative of <i>midde</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to esteem, consider worthy, think highly of</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>munan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>anmedla</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unpen, open</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>penn</i> (“pen”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an onrush, assault, attack</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣs</i> (“rush”). See <i>onrǣsan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strive against, attack</li><li>to resist, refuse to comply</li><li>to deny</li><li>to make an excuse</li><li>to sacrifice</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ansakaną</i>, equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>sacan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incantation</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>sang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take shoes off of someone or something, (<i>reflexive</i>) to take one's shoes off</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andaskōhōną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>antscuohōn</i>. Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċōġan</i> or <i>on-</i> + <i>sċōh</i> + <i>-ian</i>, literally 'to unshoe'.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to loathe, abhor, detest</li><li>to reject, shun</li><li>to be afraid of</li><li>to irritate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċunian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send forth, emit, yield up</li></ol><p><i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>sendan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>onsendeþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to regard, look upon</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>visible</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>-sīene</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oppress, assail</li><li>to dread</li><li>to sit on</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>sittan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unfasten, unclasp</li><li>to open the mind, to speak, disclose the thoughts</li></ol><p><i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>spannan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onsteall</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrangement, disposition, institution, provision</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>steall</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to establish, initiate, give rise to, set foot upon, install, institute, create</li><li>to provide an example of</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>stellan</i> (“to position, place, set”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onsteall</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entrance, ingress</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>stæpe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ansund</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, take apart</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>sundrian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>separately, apart</li><li>especially</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>sundor</i> (“apart”) +&lrm; <i>-um</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ansīen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kindle, to set on fire, to set fire to</li><li>to kindle emotion or passion, to excite, inflame</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> (“on, at”) +&lrm; <i>tendan</i> (“to kindle”). Similar words can also be found in other, later Germanic languages; compare German <i>anzünden</i>, Norwegian <i>antenne</i>, Swedish <i>antända</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fire, burning</li><li>burning sensation, inflammation</li><li>passion, strong desire</li></ol><p>From <i>ontendan</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to withdraw, pull away, extract</li><li>to pull apart, untie</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>tēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>andtimber</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open, to loose, to free</li><li>to disclose, reveal, display</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>tȳnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>upon, on</li><li>besides</li><li>over, above</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>uppan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to awake, wake up</li><li>to arise, come forth, be born</li></ol><p><i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>wacan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wade into, penetrate</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to enter, invade, take possession of</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>wadan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ānweald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ānweald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>away</li></ol><p>Originally the same as <i>on weġ</i> (“on the/one's way”). See there for more information.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go away</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;in weg gān</i>; note that <i>in</i> was regularly replaced with <i>on</i> during the separate prehistory of Old English, specifically in the West Saxon dialect. Cognate with Dutch <i>weggaan</i> and German <i>weggehen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw away</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;in weg werpan</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>wegwerpen</i> and German <i>wegwerfen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>away</li></ol><p>From <i>onweġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onweġāċyrrednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turning away from; apostasy</li></ol><p>From <i>onweġ-</i> +&lrm; <i>āċyrrednes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>departure</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>onweġ-</i> +&lrm; <i>færeld</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change</li><li>to overturn, turn aside</li><li>to reverse, pervert</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>wendan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>onwendeþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw or turn aside</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>weorpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to yield, retreat</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>wīcan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to avenge</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrecan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to uncover, reveal, disclose, show, expose</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to reveal oneself,</li></ol><p>Contraction of earlier form <i>&#42;ondwrīhan</i>, from <i>and-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrēon</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andawrīhaną</i>, from <i>&#42;wrīhaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inscription</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrīting</i> (“writing”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wake up</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>wæcnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>onwæcneþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to set fire to; kindle; ignite; light (a fire, lamp, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to burn; burn up</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make hot; (<i>by extension</i>) to inflame; excite intense feeling; kindle passions</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> +&lrm; <i>ǣlan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause fear, to cause dread</li></ol><p>From <i>on-</i> (“fear”) +&lrm; <i>þracian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>open</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;opan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;upanaz</i>.</p><p>Originally a past participle of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ūpaną</i> (“to lift up, open”). Related to Old English <i>upp</i> (“up”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>open</i>, Old Saxon <i>opan</i>, Old High German <i>offan</i>, and Old Norse <i>opinn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medlar (fruit)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>open</i> (“open”) +&lrm; <i>ears</i> (“arse”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;opanōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;upanōną</i>, from <i>&#42;upanaz</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>openen</i> (Dutch <i>openen</i>), Old High German <i>offanōn</i> (German <i>öffnen</i>), Old Norse <i>opna</i> (Swedish <i>öppna</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>public, open</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;opanalīk</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>offanlīh</i> (German <i>öffentlich</i>). Equivalent to <i>open</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>openly, publicly</li><li>clearly</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;opanalīkō</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>opanlīko</i>, Old High German <i>offanlīhho</i>. Equivalent to <i>openlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manifestation</li><li>revelation</li></ol><p>From <i>openian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>. Compare Old High German <i>offanunga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>openears</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>origin, beginning</li><li>front, vanguard</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ōʀō</i>, <i>&#42;ōʀ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ōzô</i>, <i>&#42;ōsaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃éh₁os</i> (“mouth”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>original</li><li>former</li><li>ex-, out of</li><li>without, lacking</li><li>causing deprivation, privative</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;oʀ-, &#42;uʀ-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uz-</i> (“out”), from unstressed Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;uss-</i>, from <i>&#42;uds-</i> (“up, out”). A doublet (or alternative form) of Old English <i>ā-</i> (“a-”). Cognate with Old High German <i>ur-</i>, <i>ir-</i>, <i>ar-</i>, <i>er-</i> (German <i>ur-</i>, and hence the English borrowing English <i>ur-</i>); West Frisian <i>oar-</i>; Dutch <i>oor-</i>/<i>oer-</i>; Gothic <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-). More at <i>out</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ore, unwrought metal</li><li>brass</li><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in placenames</i>) bank, shore; border, edge</li><li>slope, hill</li></ol><p>Uncertain; compare <i>ār</i>, <i>ǣr</i> (“ore, brass, copper”). Perhaps related to Low German <i>Ur</i>, <i>Uurt</i>, <i>Uhr</i>, <i>Urt</i> (“compact, reddish, iron-bearing soil”), Early Modern Dutch <i>oor</i>, <i>oore</i> (“mine; lead ore; vein bearing lead and silver”), Old Norse <i>úr</i> (“metalic slag”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ōʀō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ōzô</i>, <i>&#42;ōsô</i> (“mouth of a river, inlet”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃éh₁os</i> (“mouth”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>ósi</i> (“river mouth”). Possibly influenced by Latin <i>ōra</i> (“shore”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>oroþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lacking blood; bloodless</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀblōdī</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>urbluoti</i>. Equivalent to <i>or-</i> +&lrm; <i>blēde</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cup, tankard</li><li>hell</li><li>a demon</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ork</i>.</p><p>From Latin <i>Orcus</i> (“the underworld; the god Pluto”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Orkney Islands</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ortġeard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ortġeard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ortġeard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ortġeard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ortġeard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;poetic) Definition unclear, but apparently refers to some form of (sea&#63;) monster or monstrous creature</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>orc</i> (“hell, demon?”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;nē</i> (“corpse”) +&lrm; <i>-as</i> (masculine nominative//accusative plural)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ortġeard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>point (especially of a weapon)</li><li>point of origin, beginning</li><li>front; vanguard, chief</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;oʀd</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzdaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wes-</i> (“stab”).</p><p>Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>ort</i> (Dutch <i>oord</i>), Old High German <i>ort</i> (German <i>Ort</i>), Old Norse <i>oddr</i> (Icelandic <i>oddur</i>, Swedish <i>udd</i>, Danish <i>od</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>judgement</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀdailī</i>; equivalent to <i>or-</i> (“out”) +&lrm; <i>dǣl</i>, <i>dāl</i> (“part, portion”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ord</i> (“point (of a weapon)”) and <i>berht</i> (“bright”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ordbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ordberht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ortġeard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ordāl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>origin</li><li>originator, creator</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) chief, leader</li></ol><p>From <i>ord</i> +&lrm; <i>fruma</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Uzdagaizaz</i>, equivalent to <i>ord</i> (“point”) +&lrm; <i>gār</i> (“spear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ord</i> (“point, beginning”) +&lrm; <i>hēah</i> (“high”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ord</i> (“point, beginning”) +&lrm; <i>helm</i> (“helmet”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Oʀdalaib</i>, from <i>&#42;oʀd</i> (“sharp point”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;laib</i> (“remainder, legacy”). Equivalent to <i>ord</i> +&lrm; <i>lāf</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ortleib</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ord</i> (“point, beginning”) +&lrm; <i>nōþ</i> (“courage”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ord</i> (“point of a weapon”) and <i>rīc</i> (“king, ruler”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Uzdawulfaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>ord</i> (“point (of a spear, sword)”) +&lrm; <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A mine, place in which ore is dug</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very old</li></ol><p><i>or-</i> +&lrm; <i>eald</i>; compare Dutch <i>oeroud</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>orgol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ōretmæċġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) hero</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) warrior</li></ol><p><i>ōret</i> (“struggle, fight”) +&lrm; <i>mæċġ</i> (“man”). Compare <i>ōretta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) warrior, champion</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀhaitō</i>; equivalent to <i>ōret</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i> and thus cognate with Old High German <i>urheizo</i>. Originally an agent noun to <i>āhātan</i> (“to call forth”) (compare Gothic <i>𐌿𐍃𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (ushaitan, “to provoke, challenge”)). For the variation between <i>-t-</i> and <i>-tt-</i>, compare <i>ǣmette</i>, variant of <i>ǣmete</i> (“ant”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>oroþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cattle</li><li>livestock</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;orb</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;urbą</i>. Cognate with Old Swedish <i>orf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Orpheus (figure from Greek mythology)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ὀρφεύς</i> (Orpheús).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>orgol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>orgol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pride</li><li>arrogance</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀgōllju</i> (“pride, arrogance”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>out of sight</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uzaugī</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>urougi</i>. Equivalent to <i>or-</i> +&lrm; <i>-īeġe</i>, literally meaning “out of eyes.” The word could have originally been modeled after a Proto-Germanic phrase <i>*uz augammaz</i>, cf. Modern German <i>aus den Augen</i> (“out of sight,” lit. “out of the eyes”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a divider, separator</li><li>an interval time or space</li><li>a division, separation</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>war, strife; hostility</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzlugi-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uz-</i> (“out”) + <i>&#42;lugi-</i>, a derivative of <i>&#42;leuganą</i> (“to lie, tell a lie”). Akin to Old Frisian <i>orloch</i>, Old Saxon <i>urlogi</i>, Old High German <i>urluogi</i>, <i>urliugi</i> (“war”), Dutch <i>oorlog</i> (“war”), Old English <i>lēogan</i> (“to lie”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a time of war</li><li>battle-time</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a time of strife or trouble</li></ol><p>From <i>orleġe</i> (“strife”) +&lrm; <i>stund</i> (“period of time”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fate, destiny</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzlagą</i> (“fate”), equivalent to <i>or-</i> (“original, fundamental”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;læġ</i> (“that which is laid, law”). Compare Old Norse <i>ørlǫg</i>, Icelandic <i>örlög</i>, Old Frisian <i>orloch</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>desperate, in despair</li></ol><p><i>or-</i> +&lrm; <i>-mōd</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>despair</li><li>desperation</li></ol><p><i>ormōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>huge, excessive, enormous, extravagant, limitless, boundless</li><li>intense</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immensely, excessively</li></ol><p>From <i>or-</i> (“without”) +&lrm; <i>mǣte</i> (“average, moderate, mean”). Akin to Old English <i>metan</i> (“to measure”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breath, exhalation</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀanþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzanþą</i>, a compound of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uz</i> (“out”) + <i>&#42;anþą</i> (“breath”). The latter word is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂enh₁-</i>. Germanic cognates include Old Frisian <i>omma</i>, Old Norse <i>ǫnd</i>, <i>andi</i> (Swedish <i>ande</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grown up, at full strength, bold, active</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Probably from <i>or-</i> (“without”) +&lrm; <i>pād</i> (“coat, shirt”), literally “shirtless”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>boldly, in full force</li></ol><p><i>orped</i> (“bold”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>battle</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soulless</li><li>lifeless</li></ol><p>Equivalent of <i>or-</i> +&lrm; <i>sāwle</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) free from care or anxiety; worry-free</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) secure; prosperous</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀsorg</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzsurgaz</i>, equivalent to <i>or-</i> +&lrm; <i>sorg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without security, anxious</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;orsorg</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>secure [+genitive]</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;orsorg</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>without anxiety</li><li>carelessly, rashly</li><li>securely, safely</li></ol><p>From <i>orsorg</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freedom from care or anxiety, tranquility</li><li>prosperity</li></ol><p>From <i>orsorg</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>orsorgnes</i></li></ol><p>From <i>orsorg</i> +&lrm; <i>-ness</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>orsorg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>orsorg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orchard, garden</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;ort</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeard</i>. The first element is related to Old High German <i>orzōn</i> (“to care for, maintain, cultivate”). Compare also similarly formed Gothic <i>𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍄𐌹𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (aurtigards).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without confidence; distrustful</li><li>hopeless; despairing</li></ol><p>From <i>or-</i> +&lrm; <i>trēowe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to despair of, be without hope of</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>or-</i> +&lrm; <i>trīewan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>oroþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) without guard or protection, in an unprotected state</li></ol><p><i>or-</i> (“lacking”) +&lrm; <i>weard</i> (“guard”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i> (adverbial suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hopeless, despondent, despairing</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>orþanc</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>orþanc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>orþonc</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>orþonc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>oroþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>original thought, creativity</li><li>cunning, skill</li><li>something made therewith: contrivance, invention</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>creative; cunning, skillful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzþankaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>urdank</i>. Equivalent to <i>or-</i> +&lrm; <i>þanc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>creative, inventive</li><li>cunning, skillful</li></ol><p><i>orþanc</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to breathe, pant</li></ol><p>From <i>orþ</i>, <i>oreþ</i> (“breath, breathing”), equivalent to <i>orþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>orþanc</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>orþanc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breath</li></ol><p>From <i>orþian</i> (“to breathe, pant”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a god</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the runic character ᚩ (/o(ː)/)</li></ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) a mouth</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>alternative sense for the runic character ᚩ (/o(ː)/)</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ansu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ansuz</i> (“god, deity”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ems-</i> (“engender, beget”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>áss</i>.</p><p>Potentially from a Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ōs</i>, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ōsaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃éh₁os</i> (“mouth”), or from Latin <i>ōs</i> (“the mouth”). Only found in the Old English rune poem, which is analog to Old Norse runology, where the rune ᚬ (/o(ː)/) later on had the cognate name <i>óss</i>, Middle Norwegian <i>ōs</i>, Old Swedish <i>ōs</i> (“inlet”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ōsbeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ōs</i> (“god”) and <i>beald</i> (“brave, bold”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Osberht of Northumbria</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Ansuberht</i>, from <i>&#42;ansu</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”). Equivalent to <i>ōs</i> +&lrm; <i>beorht</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ansbert</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ōs</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>beorn</i> (“warrior”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>Ásbjǫrn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ōs</i> (“god”) and <i>berht</i> (“bright, clear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, variant of Ōsbeorht</li><li>Osberht of Northumbria</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Ansuberht</i>, from <i>&#42;ansu</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”). Equivalent to <i>ōs</i> +&lrm; <i>beorht</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ansbert</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li><li>Osburh of Wessex</li></ol><p>From <i>ōs</i> (“god”) and <i>burg</i> (“city, castle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ōsburg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ōs</i> (“god”) and <i>friþ</i> (“peace”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ōs</i> (“god”) and <i>gār</i> (“spear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ōs</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>ġifu</i> (“gift”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ōs</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Ansuhari</i>. Equivalent to <i>ōs</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>here</i> (“army”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ōs</i> (“god”) and <i>lāc</i> (“gift, offering”). Cognate with Norwegian <i>Aslak</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Ansulaibaz</i>, equivalent to <i>ōs</i> (“god, deity”) +&lrm; <i>lāf</i> (“remnant”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a (European) blackbird, an ouzel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;amslā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ossett (a market town in West Yorkshire, England).</li></ol><p>Equivalent to a personal name <i>*Ōsla</i> or <i>ōsle</i> (“blackbird”) +&lrm; <i>set</i> (“seat”). Attested in the 1086 Domesday Book.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ōsle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ōs</i> (“god”) and <i>mōd</i> (“mind, heart, spirit”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ōs</i> (“god, deity”) +&lrm; <i>mund</i> (“protector, protection”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ōsrǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>one of several Anglo-Saxon kings by this name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ōs</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>rīc</i> (“kingdom, empire, dominion”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ōs</i> (“god”) and <i>rǣd</i> (“counsel, advice”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knot in a tree</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ōst</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oyster</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>ostrea</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Baltic Sea and Kattegat</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ēast</i> (“east”) +&lrm; <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ōsweald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ōs</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>waru</i> (“care, protection”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ōs</i> (“god, deity”) +&lrm; <i>weald</i> (“power”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>Ásvaldr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ōs</i> (“god”) and <i>weard</i> (“guard”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Ōsweo</li></ol><p>From <i>Ōsweo</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ōs</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>wīġ</i> (“battle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Ansuwiniz</i>. Equivalent to <i>ōs</i> (“(pagan) god”) +&lrm; <i>wine</i> (“friend”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>Ásviðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ōsweo</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Ansuwulfaz</i>, equivalent to <i>ōs</i> (“god”) + <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>Ásólfr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ōs</i> (“god”) + <i>wynn</i> (“joy, delight”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Ansuþrūþi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Ansuþrūþiz</i>, from <i>&#42;ansuz</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;þrūþiz</i> (“strength”). Equivalent to <i>ōs</i> +&lrm; <i>þrȳþ</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>Ásþrúðr</i> (whence Icelandic <i>Ásþrúður</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>otor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>oþþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>otter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;utraz</i> (“otter”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;udrós</i> (“water animal, otter”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>otor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Otford (a village and civil parish in Sevenoaks district, Kent, England), site of the Battle of Otford</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>otor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ofer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āweb</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>aught, anything</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>āwiht</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ōwæstm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>branch</li><li>shoot, sprout</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ōþen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ox</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ohsō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oxtail</li></ol><p>From <i>oxan</i> (“ox's”) +&lrm; <i>tæġl</i> (“tail”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oxhey, England</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>oxan</i> (“ox's”) +&lrm; <i>ġehæġ</i> (“enclosed piece of land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oxherd</li></ol><p><i>oxa</i> +&lrm; <i>hierde</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>oxanhierde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oxlip (<i>Primula elatior</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>oxan</i> (“ox's”) +&lrm; <i>slyppe</i> (“paste, sticky substance”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Oxnaford</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oxfordshire</li></ol><p>From <i>Oxnaford</i> +&lrm; <i>sċīr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) armpit</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oxford</li></ol><p>From <i>oxna</i> (“oxen's”, genitive plural of <i>oxa</i>) +&lrm; <i>ford</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) armpit</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ōþ</i></li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ōþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ōþer</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ōþer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ōþer twēġa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ōþen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on the other side</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>oþþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>oþþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>oþþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ōþþringan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ōðþringeþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) until, as far as, up to, down to</li><li>(<i>with dative</i>) until</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often with þæt</i>) until</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to be scared to death <i>(+accusative or genitive of something)</i> <i>(often with a dative reflexive pronoun)</i></li></ol><p>Could be an ancient phrase, since similar phrases exist in modern Germanic languages.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ōþþæt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>separation or departure, from, away</li><li>to</li><li>toward, in the direction of, at</li><li>perfective&#59; completely&#59; intensifying an action</li></ol><p>From unstressed Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;unþa-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry off, carry away</li></ol><p>From <i>ōþ-</i> (“away-”) +&lrm; <i>beran</i> (“carry”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to die</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>oþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>cwelan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn away</li><li>to be perverted</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>oþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċyrran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ōþīewan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Odin, the Norse chief deity.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Norse <i>Óðinn</i>. Doublet of <i>Wōden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ōþīewan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>second</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>other</li><li>second</li><li>one of two</li><li>next</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;anþar</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anþeraz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>andar</i>, Old Norse <i>annarr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) the second person</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ōþer</i> (“second”) + <i>hād</i> (“grammatical person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>one and a half</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anþeraz halbaz</i>, literally "second half". Cognate with Old Frisian <i>ōther half</i> (West Frisian <i>oardeheal</i>), Old Saxon <i>ōthar half</i> (Low German <i>annerthalf</i>), Old Dutch <i>&#42;andar half</i> (Dutch <i>anderhalf</i>), Old High German <i>andar halb</i> (German <i>anderthalb</i>), Old Norse <i>halfr annarr</i> (Danish <i>halvanden</i> and Swedish <i>halvannan</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surname, cognomen</li></ol><p>From <i>ōþer</i> (“second”) +&lrm; <i>nama</i> (“name”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of two, one or the other; <i>introduces the phrase "either... or..."</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anþera twajjǫ̂</i> (literally “one of two”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>annat tveggja</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>otherwise</li><li>differently</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anþeralīkaz</i> (“otherwise”), equivalent to <i>ōþer</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>ōþarlīk</i>, Old High German <i>andarlīh</i>, Old Norse <i>annarligr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐌽𐌸𐌰𐍂𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍉</i> (anþarleikō, “otherwise”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ōþīewan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flee, escape</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ōþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>flēon</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snatch away; to seize and take away</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>oþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>grīpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēþian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show oneself, appear</li></ol><p><i>ōþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>īewan</i> (“to show, appear”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to escape, turn aside, hurry off, shoot away</li></ol><p><i>oþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċēotan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stop in one's course; come to a standstill</li><li>to remain or remain standing</li></ol><p>From <i>oþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>standan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take away</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>oþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>tēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn away, divert</li></ol><p>From <i>ōþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>wendan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ōþīewan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>oþþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>or</li><li>(oþþe... oþþe) either... or</li></ol><p>Probably an alteration of earlier <i>ofþe</i> (“or”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;efþau</i> (“or”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>jeftha</i>, <i>joftha</i> (“or”), Old Saxon <i>eftha</i>, Old High German <i>odo</i>; compare different ablaut-grades in Old English <i>eþþa</i>, Old Norse <i>eða</i>. Ultimately thought to be a Germanic suffixed dual form of the base of <i>if</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disjoin, detach, dismember</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>oþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>þēodan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dismember, disjoin</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ōþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>þīedan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obtain by unfair means</li></ol><p><i>oþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>þingian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>or; either</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to force away</li></ol><p>From <i>ōþ-</i> (“from”) +&lrm; <i>þringan</i> (“press, constrict, push on”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>until</li></ol><p>From <i>ōþ</i> +&lrm; <i>þæt</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>untat</i>, Old High German <i>untaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an outer garment, coat, cloak</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;paidu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;paidō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stake</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>pālus</i> (“stake”), possibly through a Proto-West Germanic intermediate <i>&#42;pāl</i>. Compare Old High German <i>pfāl</i> (German <i>Pfahl</i>), Old Dutch <i>pāl</i> (Dutch <i>paal</i>). Doublet of <i>pǣl</i>, from the variant Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pāli</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palace</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>palatium</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>palent</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>palatial</li></ol><p>From <i>palent</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>palent</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Palestine</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Palaestīna</i> (“Roman province of Palestine”), from Ancient Greek <i>Παλαιστίνη</i> (Palaistínē, “Philistia and the surrounding region”), from Hebrew <i>פְּלֶשֶׁת</i> (p'léshet, “Philistia, land of the Philistines”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ballista (siege engine)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>ballista</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>βαλλίστρα</i> (ballístra).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>palent</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palm tree</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>palma</i> (“palm-tree, palm-branch, palm of the hand”). Doublet of <i>folm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>palm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Palm Sunday</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>palmtrēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palmtree</li></ol><p>From <i>palm</i> +&lrm; <i>trēow</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>date (fruit)</li></ol><p>From <i>palm</i> (“palm”) +&lrm; <i>æppel</i> (“apple, fruit”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pan, frying pan</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pannā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Blackwater, a river in Essex, England</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pope</li></ol><p>From Ecclesiastical Latin <i>pāpa</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>πάπας</i> (pápas, “bishop, patriarch”), variant of <i>πάππας</i> (páppas, “father”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Catholicism</i>) the papacy; the office of the pope</li></ol><p>From <i>pāpa</i> (“pope”) +&lrm; <i>-dōm</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>papolstān</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pebble</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paradise</li></ol><p>From Old French <i>paradis</i>, from Latin <i>paradīsus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>παράδεισος</i> (parádeisos), ultimately from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;paridayjah</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leopard</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>pardus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>πάρδος</i> (párdos)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>part</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>partem</i>, accusative of <i>pars</i> (“piece, portion, share, side, party, faction, role, character, lot, fate, task, lesson, part, member”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Parthians</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the passion of Christ or the passage of the Bible describing it</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>passiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passion of Christ</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>passiō</i> (“suffering”), noun of action from perfect passive participle <i>passus</i> (“suffered”), from deponent verb <i>pati</i> (“suffer”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Lord's Prayer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Pater noster</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Paul</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Paulus</i>, from <i>paulus</i> (“small”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peacock</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pāwō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pēo</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pāwa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>refers to the Peak District of England</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Peak District of England</li></ol><p><i>Pēac-</i> (“Peak (District)”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the residents of Peak District of England</li></ol><p><i>Pēac-</i> (“Peak (District)”) +&lrm; <i>-sǣta</i> (“inhabitants”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Peada of Mercia</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fence used to enclose a space, or the area enclosed by such a fence</li><li>an enclosure; an area of enclosed land</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;parrukaz</i> (“fencing; enclosed land”). Akin to Old High German <i>pfarrih</i> (“fencing; enclosure”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pearroc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Picts</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pelican</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>pellicanus</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Penda of Mercia</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>penning</i> (“penny”)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Pending)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Penda</li></ol><p>From <i>Penda</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Carthaginians</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Poenī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>penning</i> (“penny”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>penning</i> (“penny”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pen, enclosure, fold</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pennō</i>, <i>&#42;pannijō</i> (“pin, bolt, nail, tack”), of uncertain origin, but perhaps related to <i>pinn</i> (“peg, pin”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>penning</i> (“penny”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>penny</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;panningaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>money-monger</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>penning</i> +&lrm; <i>mangere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pentecost</li></ol><p>From Ecclesiastical Latin <i>pentēcostē</i>, ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>πεντηκοστή</i> (pentēkostḗ, “fiftieth”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Land's End (a headland in western Cornwall), the westernmost place in England.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an insect</li><li>a biting fly</li></ol><p>Unknown. Perhaps a dissimilated form of <i>bēo</i> (“bee”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Pictland</li></ol><p>Literally "Land of the Picts."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Peohta land</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Picts</li><li>Pictland</li></ol><p>Probably from Celtic/Pictish; see <i>Pictus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peony</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>paeonia</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>peorþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the runic character ᛈ (/p/)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;perþō</i>, meaning uncertain, but suspected to derive from <i>&#42;peru</i> + <i>&#42;-iþō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>fruit</i>) pear</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;peru</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>perwince</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Persians</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Persian Empire</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Persēa</i> (“of the Persians”) +&lrm; <i>rīċe</i> (“empire”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Persepolis, capital of the Persian Empire</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Persia</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Persian</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peach</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;persik</i>, from Late Latin <i>persicum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peach tree</li></ol><p>From <i>persoc</i> +&lrm; <i>trēow</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>persoc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pear</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;peru</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) the periwinkle</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>petersiliġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parsley</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;petarsilljā</i> (“parsley, celery”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>petersiliġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>prættiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Peter</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Petrus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Πέτρος</i> (Pétros), from <i>πέτρος</i> (pétros, “stone, rock”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pharaoh (biblical character)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Pharao</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Φαραώ</i> (Pharaṓ), from Biblical Hebrew <i>פַּרְעֹה</i> (par‘ōh), from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">pr&#58;aA</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (<i>pr-ꜥꜣ</i>, “palace”, “pharaoh”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Fariseisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Philip II of Macedon</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Philippus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Φίλιππος</i> (Phílippos)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>philosopher</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>φῐλόσοφος</i> (phĭlósophos).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pitch</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pik</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>fruit and nuts</i>) glans, mast, acorns</li><li>(<i>feed</i>) fodder for hogs</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>&#42;picga</i> (“pig”) +&lrm; <i>brēad</i> (“bread, food”). Compare <i>bēobrēad</i> (“honey"; literally, "bees' bread”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pitch, cover with pitch</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pikōn</i>; equivalent to <i>piċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pēo</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pyffan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Peohtas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pictish</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a Pict</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pictish (language)</li></ol><p>From <i>Peohtas</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p><i>&#42;Piht</i> (“Pict”) +&lrm; <i>wine</i> (“friend”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pīc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pointed stick</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pīl</i>, from Latin <i>pīlum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pyle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>traveler, wanderer</li></ol><p>From Old French <i>pilegrin, pilegrine, pilgrim</i>, variants of <i>pelerin</i>, and its etymon Late Latin <i>pelegrīnus</i>, a dissimilated form of <i>peregrīnus</i>. See also <i>Pilegrī̆m</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Pilegrim)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>name or epithet in a list of witnesses</li></ol><p>See <i>pilegrim</i> (“traveller, wanderer”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pound in a mortar</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>appears only in compounds</i>) pine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>pīnus</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>tree</i>) pine tree</li></ol><p>From <i>pīn</i> (“pine”) +&lrm; <i>bēam</i> (“tree”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peak</li><li>point</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pinkō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pinkô</i>, a nasalised variant of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pīkô</i>, <i>&#42;pīkaz</i> (“peak, summit, point”). Cognate with dialectal Norwegian <i>pink</i> (“summit, peak”), Dutch <i>pink</i> (“little finger, pinkie”), German Low German <i>Pink</i> (“penis”).</p><p>Alternatively, from a suffixed variant of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pinnaz</i> (“pin”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tormentor, torturer</li></ol><p>From <i>pīnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pine nut</li><li>fir-cone</li></ol><p>From <i>pīn</i> (“pine”) +&lrm; <i>hnutu</i> (“nut”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to torment, torture</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pīnōn</i>, ultimately from Latin <i>pēna</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ποινή</i> (poinḗ).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pin; peg</li><li>an instrument of writing; pen</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pinn</i>, <i>&#42;pint</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pinniz</i>, <i>&#42;pinnaz</i>, <i>&#42;pintiz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bendn-</i>, <i>&#42;bend-</i> (“to protrude, jut out; peak”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>pinn</i>, <i>pin</i> (“pin; peg”), Old High German <i>pfin</i>, <i>phin</i> (“peg, pin, nail”), Middle Low German <i>pinne</i> (“small, long, pointed object”) (whence also late Old Norse <i>pinni</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pinne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bottle, flask</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torment, pain</li></ol><p>From <i>pīnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ness</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weigh, judge, estimate, consider, examine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>pēnsō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>penis</li></ol><p>A diminutive form of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pint(i)</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pint-</i> (“protrusion”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bendʰn-</i> (“peg, tip, protruding point, edge”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>pint</i>, Middle Low German <i>pint</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pine tree</li></ol><p>Alteration of <i>pīnbēam</i> (“pine tree”) with <i>trēow</i> (“tree”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pine tree</li></ol><p>From <i>pīntrēow</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pīntrēowen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torment, torture, pain</li></ol><p>From <i>pīnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instrument of torture</li></ol><p>From <i>pīnung</i> +&lrm; <i>tōl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pise</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pīpdrēam</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the sound of a pipe (<i>musical instrument</i>); pipe music</li></ol><p>From <i>pīpe</i> +&lrm; <i>drēam</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pipe (musical instrument)</li><li>pipe (for channeling liquid)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pīpā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pipor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piper</li></ol><p>From <i>pīpe</i> (“pipes (instrument)”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to breathe, blow</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly related to Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pīpan</i> or to Old English <i>pyffan</i>.</p><p>Alternatively, perhaps from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pīpfijan</i>. Compare also Middle Low German <i>pîven</i>, <i>pîwen</i> (“to blow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pipe, play the pipes</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>pīpe</i> (“pipe”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow pimply</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>papula</i> (“pimple”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>piplian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(black) pepper, in the following senses:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a plant (<i>Piper nigrum</i>)</li><li>the dried berries of that plant, used as a spice</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pipar</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peppercorn</li></ol><p>From <i>pipor</i> (“pepper”) +&lrm; <i>corn</i> (“corn”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pepper</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the pear tree</li></ol><p>Apparently from a Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;pirea</i>, though such a form is unknown in the Romance languages.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heavy, weighty</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>pēnsus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pea</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>pisa</i>, from Latin <i>pisum</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>πίσον</i> (píson), <i>πῐ́σος</i> (pĭ́sos), <i>πισός</i> (pisós).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weigh</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>pīs</i> (“heavy”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>heavily</li></ol><p>From <i>pīs</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(religion,&#32;Christianity) letter, epistle</li><li>(religion,&#32;Christianity,&#32;biblical) an epistle of the New Testament</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>epistola</i>, <i>epistula</i>. Compare Middle Dutch <i>pistele</i>, <i>pistel</i>, Middle Low German <i>pistele</i>, Middle High German <i>pistel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a book containing the Epistles</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>pistol</i> (“epistle”) +&lrm; <i>bōc</i> (“book”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a garment worn while reading the epistle</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>pistol</i> (“epistle”) +&lrm; <i>rocc</i> (“upper garment”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a subdeacon, one who reads the epistle in a church</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>pistol</i> (“epistle”) +&lrm; <i>rǣdere</i> (“reader”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lesson delivered in a church service</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>pistol</i> (“epistle”) +&lrm; <i>rǣding</i> (“reading”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pēo</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pith</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>plegian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>planet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>planēta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plant, shoot</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;plantu</i>, from Latin <i>planta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plant</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;plantōn</i> (“to plant”), from Late Latin <i>plantāre</i> (“to plant”). Cognate with Old High German <i>pflanzōn</i> (“to plant”), Old Norse <i>planta</i> (“to plant”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gardening tool, dibble</li></ol><p>From <i>plante</i> +&lrm; <i>sticca</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>planting</li><li>plant</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>plantian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plaster</li></ol><p>From late Latin <i>plastrum</i>, shortened from Classical Latin <i>emplastrum</i> (“a plaster, bandage”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>playing</li><li>a game</li><li>a toy</li><li>a play</li><li>exercise</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;plegō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>pleġa</i> (“play”) and <i>berht</i> (“bright”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Pleġmund</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>player</li><li>athlete</li><li>wrestler</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>plegian</i> (“to play”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>pleġa</i> (“play, game”) + <i>hard</i> (“brave”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>pleġa</i> (“play, exercise”) + <i>helm</i> (“defense, protection”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>pleġa</i> (“play, game”) + <i>here</i> (“army”)..</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>playhouse, theater</li></ol><p>From <i>plega</i> +&lrm; <i>hūs</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>spilehūs</i> (“theatre”), Old High German <i>spilehūs</i>, <i>spilahūs</i>, <i>spilohūs</i> (“theatre”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play</li><li>to play a game</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Plegmund; 9th and 10th Century Anglo-Saxon Archbishop of Canterbury.</li></ol><p>From <i>pleġa</i> (“play”) and <i>mund</i> (“protector, guardian”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>given to playfulness; playful; sportive</li></ol><p>From <i>plegian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>pleġa</i> (“play”) and <i>rǣd</i> (“counsel, advice”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place for play</li><li>gymnasium</li><li>place for wrestling</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>plega</i> (“play”) +&lrm; <i>-stōw</i> (“place”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>pleġa</i> (“play, game”) + <i>wealh</i> (“Celt, foreigner”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>pleġa</i> (“exercise, game”) + <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>danger, peril; risk</li><li>injury, hurt, harm</li><li>responsibility</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;plegą</i> (“responsibility, care”), of unknown origin. Akin to Old Frisian <i>plē</i>, <i>plī</i> (“danger”), Old English <i>pliht</i> (“danger, risk”). More at plight.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to endanger, risk</li></ol><p>Presumably from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;plehan</i>, but ultimate origin unknown. The OED comments "perhaps ult. borrowed from a non-Germanic (substrate) language, although this would be unusual for a strong verb."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>danger, risk</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pligiþu</i> (“danger”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>plicht</i> (“danger”), Old High German <i>pfligida</i> (“danger”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to endanger, compromise</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>pliht</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>. Compare Old Frisian <i>plichta</i>, Middle Low German <i>plichten</i>, Middle High German <i>phlihten</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pluccian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the measure of land that can be ploughed in one day, ploughland</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;plōgaz</i>, <i>&#42;plōguz</i> (“plough”). Compare Old Frisian <i>ploch</i>, Old High German <i>pfluog</i>, Old Norse <i>plógr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>plōg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plot (of land)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pluck, pull away, tear</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;plukkōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;plukkōną</i> (“to pluck”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>plūme</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plum</li><li>plum tree</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;plūmā</i>, from Latin <i>prūnum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plum juice</li></ol><p>From <i>plum</i> (“plum”) +&lrm; <i>sēaw</i> (“sap, juice”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plum tree</li></ol><p>From <i>plūme</i> (“plum”) +&lrm; <i>trēow</i> (“tree”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scrape, scratch</li><li>to pluck</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;plukkijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>plūme</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Plympton (A former town which is now a suburb of Plymouth, Devon, England)</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;plȳmen</i> (from plūme (“plum”)) +&lrm; <i>tūn</i> (“town”). First attested around 900.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place, open space</li><li>street</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>platea</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>πλατεῖα</i> (plateîa). This must have been borrowed into early Old English after Anglo-Frisian brightening, but before i-mutation as <i>*platiǣ</i>, cf. <i>Læden</i>, <i>mæġester</i>. The Aldredian stem <i>plæċ-</i>, and the Rushworth Gospel gloss <i>plæ(t)s-</i>, illustrate a change in later Old English whereby the sequence <i>-tj-</i> began to be conflated with <i>-ċċ-</i>, cf. <i>ortġeard</i>, <i>fetian</i>; thus serving as evidence that both palatalization, and later affrication, did likely also occur in Old Northumbrian.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>plega</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>plega</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>plegian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pool</li><li>puddle</li></ol><p>Apparently from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;plask</i> (“pool”), of unknown further origin. Possibly imitative. Apparently related to Middle Dutch <i>plasch</i>, <i>plas</i> (“pool”), whence also Middle French <i>plache</i> (“pool”), Middle French <i>plascq</i> (“damp meadow”), Anglo-Norman <i>plasseis</i> (“marshes”, plural).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>plæċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>plæċum</i>, found once in the Rushworth Gospel gloss</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a slap; smack; a sounding blow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;platt</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;plat-</i> (“to slap, strike, blow”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;blod-</i> (“to strike, beat”).</p><p>Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>plat</i> (“blow, smack, slap”), Middle Low German <i>plat</i> (“a smack”), Middle High German <i>blaz</i>, <i>plaz</i> (“a smack, blow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to buffet; smack; to give a sounding blow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;plattjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;platjaną</i> (“to strike, beat”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;blod-</i> (“to strike, beat”). Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>platten</i>, <i>pletten</i> (“to strike, bruise, crush, rub”), Middle High German <i>blatzen</i>, <i>platzen</i> (“to strike, break, throw down”). More at <i>plat</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pocc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pock, pustule, ulcer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pukkaz</i>, <i>&#42;pukkǭ</i> (“pock; swelling”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bew-</i>, <i>&#42;bʰew-</i> (“to grow; swell”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Northumbrian</i>) alternative form of <i>pohha</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pohha</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poke; pouch; bag</li><li><i>(anatomy)</i> sinus</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pokkō</i>, <i>&#42;pokō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pukkô</i>, <i>&#42;pukô</i> (“bag; pouch”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bew-</i>, <i>&#42;bʰew-</i> (“to swell; blow; inflate”). Cognate with Middle High German <i>pfoch</i> (“pouch; purse”), Old Norse <i>poki</i> (“pouch; bag”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pool</li></ol><p>West Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pōlaz</i>, of uncertain origin. Cognate with Old High German <i>pfuol</i> (German <i>Pfuhl</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parched corn</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>polenta</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pennyroyal, <i>Mentha pulegium</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>pūlegium</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>popiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>popiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>popiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>popiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poppy</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>papāver</i>, probably with alteration of the ending after <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>porr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leek</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;porr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a port, a haven (a harbor or harbor-town)</li><li>a town, particularly one with special trading privileges</li><li>portal (a door or gate&#59; an entrance)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>portus</i> (“harbour, port, haven, warehouse”).</p><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>porta</i> (“gate, entrance, passage, door”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Port)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name attributed to one of the Saxon invaders of Britain, apparently in an inference from <i>Portesmūþa</i>.</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>Portesmūþa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>portcwēn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harlot, prostitute</li></ol><p>From <i>port</i> (“port, town”) +&lrm; <i>cwēn</i> (“woman”). Compare Old Norse <i>portkona</i>, Finnish <i>portto</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Portsmouth (a city in southern England)</li></ol><p>From <i>port</i> (“port”) +&lrm; <i>mūð</i> (“mouth”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Portsmouth (a city in southern England)</li></ol><p>From <i>port</i> (“port”) +&lrm; <i>mūþ</i> (“mouth”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a portreeve: an office equivalent to a mayor (<i>historical</i>) in several major English towns or (<i>dialect</i>) in various minor boroughs of Wales and Southwest England</li></ol><p>From <i>port</i> (“a walled market town”) +&lrm; <i>ġerēfa</i> (“reeve”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porch, portico, covered entrance</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>porticus</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Portland (in Dorset, England)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>port</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Porlock (a coastal village and civil parish in Somerset, England)</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>port</i> (“port”) +&lrm; <i>loc</i> (“enclosure”). Attested in the Domesday Book of 1086.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen</li></ol><p>From <i>port</i> (“port”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pusa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an unknown location in England, site of the Battle of Posentesbyrg.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>post</li><li>pedestal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>postis</i> (“post, pedestal”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apostle</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>apostol</i> or Late Latin <i>apostolus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pott</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thrust, push</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;potōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;putōną</i> (“to push, thrust, stick, poke”); compare Middle Dutch <i>pōten</i> (“stick into ground”) (Dutch <i>poten</i> (“to plant”)), Norwegian <i>pota</i> (“poke”), Icelandic <i>pota</i> (“to stick, put, poke”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pot</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;puttaz</i> (“pot”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;budnós</i> (“a type of vessel”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>prōfost</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preacher</li></ol><p>From <i>predician</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to preach</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>praedīcō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preaching</li></ol><p>From <i>predician</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pin, broach, fastening</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;preunaz</i> (“knitting needle, pin”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>priest</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>presbyter</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>πρεσβύτερος</i> (presbúteros), from <i>πρέσβυς</i> (présbus, “elder, older”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>priesthood</li></ol><p>From <i>prēost</i> (“priest”) +&lrm; <i>-hād</i> (“-hood”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the time it takes to shut then open one's eyes; the blink or twinkling of an eye; (<i>by extension</i>) moment; instant</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;prēowt, &#42;prēowot</i> (“blinking”) +&lrm; <i>hwīl</i> (“time; while”). More at modern <i>pry</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>prættiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prick, (small) hole, perforation</li><li>point, dot</li><li>jot, whit; (tiny) particle</li><li>point (fraction of time)</li><li>prickle, thorn, spike</li><li>nail, pin, peg</li><li>point, stage (in a progression)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;prik-</i>, related to the verb <i>&#42;prikjaną</i> (“to prick”). Cognate with Middle Low German <i>prik</i>, Old West Norse <i>prik</i> (Icelandic <i>prik</i> (“dot; small stick”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>prician</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prick</li></ol><p>From <i>prica</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Prime, the first hour or tide (3-hour period) after dawn</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Prime, the divine office appointed for the hour in the liturgy</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>prīma</i> (“first; first hour”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Prime, the religious service of the first hour of daylight</li></ol><p>From <i>prīm</i> + <i>sang</i>, equivalent of <i>prime</i> + <i>song</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the twinkling of an eye, wink</li><li>a moment, instant</li></ol><p>Most likely related to the root of <i>prician</i> (“to prick”) (modern <i>prick</i>), with the notion of "prick" as a tiny point or instant in time.</p><p>Probably related to modern <i>pry</i> (“to look, inquire closely”). The word survived as Middle English <i>prinken</i> and modern English <i>prink (sense 1)</i> (“to look”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Late Old English,&#32;rare) prison, jail</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old French <i>prisun</i>, <i>prison</i>, from Latin <i>prehensiō</i>, <i>prehensiōnem</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to esteem; regard as</li><li>to test, try, prove</li><li>to show evidence of, evince</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;prōbōn</i>, from Late Latin <i>probō</i> (“test, try, examine, approve, show to be good or fit, prove”, verb), from <i>probus</i> (“good, worthy, excellent”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pro-bʰuH-s</i> (“being in front, prominent”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pro-</i>, <i>&#42;per-</i> (“toward”) + Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰuH-</i> (“to be”). Related to Old Frisian <i>prōvia</i>, Old Norse <i>prófa</i>. More at for, be.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>officer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>prōpositus</i>, variant of Latin <i>praepositus</i> (“[one] placed in command”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>proud</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old French <i>prod</i>, possibly from Late Latin <i>prōde</i>, from Latin <i>prosum</i>. Akin to Old Norse <i>prúðr</i> (“stately, fine”). More at English <i>proud</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>prūd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>proudly, magnificently, haughtily</li></ol><p><i><i>prūd</i>, <i>prūt</i></i> (“proud”); <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pride</li></ol><p>From the adjective <i>prūd</i> (“proud”) by analogy with e.g. <i>hǣlu</i> (“health”) : <i>hāl</i> (“healthy”). Compare Old English <i>prȳt</i> (“pride”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Privett (a village in Froxfield and Privett parish, East Hampshire district, Hampshire, England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>prȳde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>prȳde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trick, prank, practical joke</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;prattuz</i> (“boastful talk; deceit, cunning, trick”). Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>perte</i> (“trick, stratagem”), Old West Norse <i>prettr</i> (Norwegian <i>prette</i> (“trick”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wily, crafty, astute</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;prattug</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;prattugaz</i>, equivalent to <i>prætt</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>prættiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>psealm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psalm</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>psalmus</i>; the initial <i>p-</i> was probably silent except for in careful, learned speech.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goblin</li><li>a mischievous spirit</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pūkō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pūkô</i> (“a goblin, spook”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)pāug(')-</i> (“brilliance, spectre”) or from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bew-</i> (“to swell, blow”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Norse <i>púki</i> (dialectal Swedish <i>puke</i> (“devil”)). Compare also Middle Low German <i>spôk</i>, <i>spûk</i> (“apparition, ghost”), Middle Dutch <i>spooc</i> (“apparition, ghost”) (Dutch <i>spook</i>), Middle High German <i>gespük</i> (“a haunting”) (German <i>Spuk</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a goblin, demon, a mischievous spirit</li></ol><p>Diminutive of <i>pūca</i> (“devil, demon”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pūkô</i> (“goblin, imp”), equivalent to <i>pūca</i> +&lrm; <i>-el</i>. Cognate with Danish <i>pokker</i> (“devil, deuce”). More at puck.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ditch, furrow</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wen</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;pud</i> +&lrm; <i>-uc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bulgaria</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>Pulgare</i> (“the Bulgarians”) + <i>land</i> (“land”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Bulgarians</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Bulgarisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pool, creek</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pullaz</i> (“pool”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull</li><li>to pluck</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Garnett compares Latin <i>vello</i> (“to pluck out, to pull”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welh₃-</i>. However, compare <i>pluccian</i> (“to pluck, pull out”).</p><p>Cognate with Middle Low German <i>pūlen</i> (“to shell, husk, pluck”), Middle Dutch <i>pōlen</i> (“to shell, husk”), Icelandic <i>púla</i> (“to work hard”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pound (weight or currency)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pund</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pundą</i>, from Latin <i>pondus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pinfold</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;pund</i> +&lrm; <i>fald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pound, crush (up)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pūn-</i> (“rubble, debris”). Of obscure ultimate origin, but perhaps related to Ancient Greek <i>βυζόν</i> (buzón, “compact, close together”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Carthaginians</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>purple</li></ol><p>From <i>purpure</i> (“purple (colour)”, noun).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a purple garment or material</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>purpura</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>purple</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;purpurīn</i>. Equivalent to <i>purple</i>, <i>purpure</i> (“purple”) +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purse, leather bag</li><li>wallet, scrip</li></ol><p>Conflation of Old English <i>burse</i>&nbsp;f (“bag, pouch”) and Old English <i>pusa</i>&nbsp;m (“wallet, bag, scrip”). More at <i>burse</i>, <i>pusa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purse, bag, scrip</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pusō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pusô</i> (“bag, wallet, scrip”). Akin to Old High German <i>pfosa</i> (“purse”), Old Norse <i>posi</i> (“bag, purse”), <i>púss</i> (“pocket, pouch”). More at pussy.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pick out the best bits, cull, select</li></ol><p>Apparently from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pus(a)lōn</i> (“to work on, belabour, toil over, exert; fiddle or tinker with; pick (at)”), whence also Middle Dutch <i>pueselen</i> (“to trifle, feel, touch, pick at, nibble”). Perhaps from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pus-</i> (“to wheeze, breathe hard, pant, be exhausted”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bew-</i> (“to blow, inflate, swell”). If so, related to Old English <i>ġeposu</i> (“headcold”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Pybba of Mercia</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Pybba</li></ol><p>From <i>Pybba</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to breathe out, puff, blow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;puffjan</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;puf(f)-</i>, of imitative origin.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Peohtas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Pihtwine</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pyleċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pillow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pulwī</i>, from Latin <i>pulvīnus</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pilch (A gown or case of skin, or one trimmed or lined with fur)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>pellicia</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tidal pool, inlet, sheltered bay</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pullijaz</i> (“small pool, ditch, creek”), diminutive of <i>&#42;pullaz</i> (“pool, stream”),</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pyle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pinca</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prick</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pise</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>piplian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pellitory (plant of the genus <i>Parietaria</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>pyrethrum</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>piriġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pise</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to poke, push, thrust</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;putōn</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pit</li><li>well, pool</li><li>grave</li><li>pockmark; pustule</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;puti</i> (“a well”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a deceiver</li></ol><p>From <i>pǣċan</i> (“to deceive”) +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deceive</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;paikijan</i> (“to seduce, deceive”) (compare Middle Low German <i>pêke</i> (“mistress, whore”, pejorative swear word) and <i>pichte</i>, <i>picht</i> (“argument, dispute, manslaughter”), connected to Old Prussian <i>paik-</i> 'deceive' and Lithuanian <i>pìktas</i> 'evil', which correspond to Old English <i>fāh</i> (<i>foe</i>) and Middle High-German <i>vêch</i>, with Grimm's Law applied. The forms showing unshifted consonants are therefore probably loan words from another IE language, maybe an unattested one (see Nordwestblock).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>bepǣċung</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>pǣċan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gill, small liquid measure</li><li>wine pot; pail</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pagil</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>javelin</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;pāli</i>, a later variant of <i>&#42;pālu</i> (whence also Old English <i>pāl</i>), from Latin <i>pālus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>penning</i> (“penny”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>penning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>prættiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>prættiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>path</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;paþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;paþaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to traverse</li><li>to tread a path</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>pæþ</i> (“path”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roe deer, roebuck</li></ol><p>A contraction of earlier <i>rāha</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;raihō</i>, <i>&#42;raih</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raihô</i>, <i>&#42;raihą</i>. The 5th-century runic form <i>ᚱᚨᛇᚺᚨᚾ</i> (raïhan) is possibly an ancestor of this word, but may be North Germanic instead.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Early OE</i>) alternative form of <i>rā</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rage</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>rabiō, rabere</i> (“to be mad”), itself perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;rebʰ-</i> (“be violent”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rake</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rakō</i>, <i>&#42;rekô</i> (“rake”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) a cord forming part of a ship's rigging</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chain, fetter</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rakantā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rakantǭ</i> (“fetter, bond”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;reǵ-</i> (“to bind”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chain</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>racente</i> +&lrm; <i>tēag</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chain</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>racentēag</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rule, to direct</li><li>to take a course of action</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rakōn</i> (“to choose a direction; run”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃reǵ-</i> (“straight; right; direct; line”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>explanation</li><li>argument, reasoning</li><li>logic, reason</li><li>narrative</li><li>rhetoric</li><li>comedy</li><li>rake</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;raku</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rakō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃reǵ-</i> (“to straighten, direct, make right”). Akin to Old High German <i>rahha</i> (“affair, cause, reason, account”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;raku</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rakō</i> (“rake”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rak-</i> (“to gather, heap up”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃reǵ-</i> (“to straighten, direct, make right”). Akin to Old High German <i>rehho</i> (“rake”) (German <i>Rechen</i> (“rake”)), Old Norse <i>reka</i> (“shovel, spade”), Gothic <i>𐍂𐌹𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (rikan, “to collect, heap up”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>journey, ride, the act of riding</li><li>raid, expedition</li><li>the runic character ᚱ (/r/)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;raidu</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>reið</i>.</p><p>See <i>ridan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rodor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ragged, shaggy, bristly</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lichen</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ragō</i> (“lichen, beard”) and related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rūhaz</i> (whence <i>rough</i>) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rawwō</i> (whence <i>rag</i>), therefore from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hrewk-</i> (“to pluck”). However compare Russian <i>ракита</i> (rakita, “broom, willow tree”), which could be from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂erkʷ-</i> (“bend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rā</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ramm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hramsa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ram (animal)</li><li>battering ram</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rammaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unlawful seizure of property; robbery</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>proud, haughty, arrogant, insolent, froward, overbearing</li><li>showy; mature, full-grown</li><li>noble, bold, valiant, courageous</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rank</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outer part of something: edge, brink, rim, margin</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) shield or the boss of a shield</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rand</i>, <i>&#42;randu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;randaz</i>, <i>&#42;randō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;rem-</i> (“to come to rest, prop or support oneself”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>rǫnd</i> (“edge, rim; (poetic) shield”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boss (of a shield)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;randabaug</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>randbōg</i> and Old High German <i>rantboug</i>. Equivalent to <i>rand</i> (“shield”) +&lrm; <i>bēag</i> (“ring”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rope</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;raip</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raipaz</i>, <i>&#42;raipą</i> (“rope, cord, band, ringlet”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;roypnós</i> (“strap, band, rope”). Compare Old Frisian <i>rāp</i> (West Frisian <i>reap</i>), Old Dutch <i>reip</i>, <i>rēp</i> (Dutch <i>reep</i>), Old High German <i>reif</i> (German <i>Reif</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bittern</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;rār(i)a</i> (“roarer”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;dumbla</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>rōridumbil</i> (“bittern”), Old High German <i>rōratumbil</i> (“bittern”).</p><p>Compare also Old Saxon <i>horodumbil</i> (“bittern”), Old High German <i>horotumbil</i> (“bittern”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bittern</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roar, bellow, boom</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rairōn</i> (“to roar, cry”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to explore</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;raisōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rǣw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hræþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>red</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;raud</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raudaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rowdʰós</i> &lt; <i>&#42;h₁rewdʰ-</i>.</p><p>Germanic cognates: Old Frisian <i>rād</i> (West Frisian <i>read</i>), Old Saxon <i>rōd</i> (Low German <i>root</i>, <i>rod</i>), Dutch <i>rood</i>, Old High German <i>rōt</i> (German <i>rot</i>), Old Norse <i>rauðr</i> (Danish <i>rød</i>, Swedish <i>röd</i>, Icelandic <i>rauður</i>), Gothic <i>𐍂𐌰𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (rauþs).</p><p>Indo-European cognates: Ancient Greek <i>ἐρυθρός</i> (eruthrós), Latin <i>ruber</i>, Old Irish <i>rúad</i>, Lithuanian <i>raũdas</i>, Russian <i>рудо́й</i> (rudój).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Biblical Red Sea</li></ol><p>Calque of Latin <i>Mare Rubrum</i>, equivalent to <i>rēad</i> (“red”) +&lrm; <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) robin, ruddock</li></ol><p>Apparently equivalent to <i>rēad</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>, literally “[the] red one”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Readingas, an Anglo-Saxon tribe that inhabited Reading</li><li>Reading (town)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>redness</li></ol><p>From <i>rēad</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rēadnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rēadnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plunder; spoil, booty</li><li>raiment, garment, robe, vestment</li><li>armor</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;raub</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raubaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reaver; robber; spoiler</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raubārijaz</i>, equivalent to <i>rēaf</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>roubari</i>, Old Norse <i>raufari</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rob</li><li>to plunder</li><li>to reave</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;raubōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raubōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>robbery</li></ol><p><i>rēaf</i> (“to rob”) +&lrm; <i>-lāc</i> (“-lock”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rapacity, ferociousness</li></ol><p>From <i>rēafol</i> (“rapacious”) +&lrm; <i>-ness</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rēafolness</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plundering</li></ol><p>From <i>rēafian</i> (“to plunder”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cream</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;raum</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raumaz</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Middle Low German <i>rōm</i>, Middle Dutch <i>room</i>, Old High German <i>roum</i> (German <i>Rahm</i>), Old Norse <i>rjúmi</i> (Icelandic <i>rjómi</i>, Norwegian <i>rømme</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smoke</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rauki</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raukiz</i>, whence also Old Frisian <i>rēk</i>, Old Saxon <i>rōk</i>, Old Dutch <i>rouc</i>, Old High German <i>rouh</i>, Old Norse <i>reykr</i>. Possibly a loan from the Old Norse instead.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to care</li><li>to go quickly, go wildly, hasten, run</li><li>to come, go</li></ol><p><i>The <b>spelling of this entry has been normalized</b>&nbsp;from </i>reċċan<i>&nbsp;according to the principles established by Wiktionary's editor community or recent spelling standards of the language.</i></p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rōkijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rōkijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>rōkian</i>, Old High German <i>ruohhen</i>, Old Norse <i>rǿkja</i> (“to care, to pay heed to”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rekan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rekaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to explain</li><li>to argue</li><li>to narrate, tell</li><li>to stretch out, extend</li><li>to reach out to someone</li><li>to rule, direct</li><li>alternative form of <i>rēċan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rakkjan</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rōkijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruler, governor</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>reċċan</i> (“to explain, make an argument”) +&lrm; <i>-end</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) alternative form of <i>ræċed</i> (“hall; house, building”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rakud</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>rakud</i> (used of the Temple of Jerusalem).</p><p>From <i>reċċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rochdale (a town in Greater Manchester, England)</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>reċed</i> (“hall, house”) +&lrm; <i>hām</i> (“home”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reckless, careless</li></ol><p>From <i>rēċan</i> (“to care”) +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>carelessly, negligently</li></ol><p>From <i>rēċelēas</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carelessness, negligence</li></ol><p>From <i>rēċelēas</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incense</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ready</li><li>swift, quick</li><li>prompt</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rekanaz</i> (“correct, proper, in order, ready”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;reǵ-</i> (“straight, right”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quickly</li><li>straightway, at once</li></ol><p>From <i>reċen</i> (“quick”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i> (“-ly”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arrange, dispose, reckon</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rekanōn</i> (“to count, explain”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rēcels</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) alternative form of <i>ræċed</i> (“hall; house, building”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ræċede</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ræd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rǣdan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, alternative form of <i>Rǣdbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rǣden</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rǣdere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rǣdiċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rǣden</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prefect</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reprove; rebuke</li><li>to seize; appropriate</li><li>to blame</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rafsijan</i> (“to blame, flog, chastise, punish”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>regol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name.</li></ol><p><i>reġn-</i> (“very”) +&lrm; <i>beald</i> (“bold”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>reġn-</i> (“wondrous, mighty”) + <i>hēah</i> (“high, exalted”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rain</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;regn</i>&nbsp;m, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;regną</i>&nbsp;n.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>very</li><li>wondrous, wondrously; mighty</li><li>arch-; downright</li><li>solemn</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ragin-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raginą</i>. Akin to Old Saxon <i>regin-</i>, Old High German <i>ragin-</i>, <i>regin-</i> (compound found in personal names), Old Norse <i>ragn-</i>, <i>rǫgn-</i>, <i>regin</i> (“the Powers (ie. the gods)”), Old English <i>reġnian</i>, <i>rēnian</i> (“to prepare, arrange, order”), Gothic <i>𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (raginōn, “to rule”), <i>𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌹𐌽𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (ragineis, “ruler”), <i>𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌹𐌽</i> (ragin, “ordinance, counsel”). More at <i>riht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rainbow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;regnabogō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;regnabugô</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>reinboga</i>, Old Saxon <i>&#42;reganbogo</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;reganbogo</i>, Old High German <i>reginbogo</i>, and Old Norse <i>regnbogi</i>. Equivalent to <i>reġn</i> (“rain”) +&lrm; <i>boga</i> (“bow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raindrop</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;regnadropō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;regnadrupô</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>regantropfo</i>. Equivalent to <i>reġn</i> +&lrm; <i>dropa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare, arrange, order</li><li>(mainly impersonal) to rain</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;raginōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raginōną</i> (“to counsel, advise”). See also <i>reġn-</i>, related to <i>riht</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;regnōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;regnōną</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>regna</i>, Swedish <i>regna</i>, Old High German <i>reganōn</i>, German <i>regnen</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;reganon</i>, Dutch <i>regenen</i>, Old Saxon <i>&#42;reganōn</i>, German Low German <i>regen</i>, Old Frisian <i>reinia</i>, West Frisian <i>reine</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rainy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;regnag</i>, <i>&#42;rignīg</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;regnagaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>reġn</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rain shower</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;regnaskūr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;regnaskūrō</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>regnskúr</i>. Equivalent to <i>reġn</i> +&lrm; <i>sċūr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earthworm</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;regnawurmi</i>. Equivalent to <i>reġn</i> (“rain”) +&lrm; <i>wyrm</i> (“worm”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rainwater</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;regnawatar</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;regnawatōr</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>reganwazzar</i> and Icelandic <i>regnvatn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a rule</li><li>a ruler</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;regulu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a ruler</li></ol><p>From <i>regol</i> (“rule”) +&lrm; <i>sticca</i> (“stick”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>regol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, held by Marcus Atilius Regulus</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>riht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rihtnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to repair, restore</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps representing a variant or derivative of unrecorded <i>&#42;remman</i> (compare Old English <i>fremian</i> ~ <i>fremman</i>), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rammijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rammijaną</i> (“to make strong”), cognate with Old Norse <i>remma</i> (“to make strong, bind up”); or related to Old Frisian <i>remia</i>, <i>ramia</i> (“to build up, erect”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hramu</i> (“frame, framework”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to Rome; Roman</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rūmisk</i>, equivalent to <i>Rōm</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>rūmisk</i>, <i>rōmisk</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hremming</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>reġn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>reġnboga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rend, tear</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rendlesham</li></ol><p>The first element is the genitive form of an uncertain noun, perhaps an unattested name *Rendel or an unattested word *rendel meaning 'little shore.' The second element is <i>hām</i> (“home”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spider</li><li>spiderweb</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>arānea</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>reġnian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>reġniġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>reġnscūr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>reġnwæter</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fierce, savage, furious</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smoke</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;reukan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;reukaną</i>, whence also Old Frisian <i>riāka</i>, Old High German <i>riohhan</i>, Old Norse <i>rjúka</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>red</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>red</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;reud</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;reudaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rewdʰós</i>, from <i>&#42;h₁rewdʰ-</i> (“red”). Cognate with Ancient Greek <i>ἐρυθρός</i> (eruthrós), Latin <i>ruber</i>, Old Irish <i>rúad</i>, Lithuanian <i>raũdas</i>, Russian <i>рудо́й</i> (rudój).</p><p>See above.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to redden, make red</li><li>stain with blood, bloody</li><li>to wound, kill</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;reudaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rewdʰ-</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>rjóða</i> (“to redden”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pheasant</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear, rend, rip</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;reufan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;reufaną</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>rjúfa</i>; also distantly cognate to Latin <i>rumpō</i> (“break”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>regol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ray (<i>fish</i>)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps related to Old English <i>ruhha</i> (“ray”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>right, law, justice, moral correctness</li><li>a right, a justifiable entitlement to something or to some action</li><li>one’s right, one’s proper due</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rima</i> (“rim, border, edge”)</li><li>membrane</li><li>ligament</li></ol><p>See <i>rima</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;reumō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mourning, lament</li></ol><p>Akin to rēonian.<br></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mutter, whisper</li><li>to conspire</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;reunōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rīpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voice</li><li>speech, language</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. Other Germanic cognates suggest descent from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;raʀdu</i> (“voice, language, speech, sound”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;razdō</i>. However, the required sound changes to develop the present spelling indicate a reflex of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;razdī</i>. Attested neuter forms also suggest Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;razdia</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Gothic <i>𐍂𐌰𐌶𐌳𐌰</i> (razda) and Old Norse <i>rǫdd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>"voice-bearer", person</li></ol><p>From <i>reord</i> + <i>berend</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak, say, talk</li><li>to read</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a noise</li><li>to make a noise in grief, to lament, wail</li><li>to weep, shed tears</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;reutaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;HrewdH-</i>. Compare Icelandic <i>rjóta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrēowlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>refsan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rǣswan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rǣswung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ræst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rest <i>(intransitive or transitive)</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>rīestan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rastijan</i>, derived from the noun <i>&#42;rastu</i> (Old English <i>ræst</i>). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>resta</i>, Old Saxon <i>restian</i>, Old High German <i>resten</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;raustijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raustijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>reysta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ræstedæġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a day of rest; Sabbath</li></ol><p>From <i>rest</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i> +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sleeping-chamber</li></ol><p>From <i>rest</i> (“rest”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ræstlēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rǣswung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(the act of) rowing</li><li>a ship</li></ol><p><i>rōwan</i> (“to row”) +&lrm; <i>-et</i>, with i-mutation</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rēþiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fierce, cruel, savage</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rōþī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rōþijaz</i> (“wild”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;rey-</i> (“to bellow, roar”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fierce; savage; cruel</li></ol><p>From <i>rēþe</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fierceness, rage, cruelty</li></ol><p>From <i>rēþe</i> (“savage, fierce”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rower, oarsman</li></ol><p>Apparently from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rōþrijō</i> (literally “rudder-er”), though there are no cognates. Ultimately derived from an earlier form of <i>rōþor</i> (“oar, rudder”). Compare German <i>Ruderer</i>, which is formed similarly with a different agent suffix.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rib</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ribb</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ribją</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hound's tongue (<i>Cynoglossum officinale</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rīċe</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rīkī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rīkiją</i>, from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;rīgyom</i> (“kingdom”), derived from <i>&#42;rīxs</i> (“king”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>rīke</i>, Old Saxon <i>rīki</i>, Old Dutch <i>rīki</i>, Old High German <i>rīhhi</i>, Old Norse <i>ríki</i>, Gothic <i>𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌺𐌹</i> (reiki). The Indo-European root is also the source of Latin <i>rēx</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Ricberht of East Anglia; an Anglo-Saxon King of East Anglia.</li></ol><p>From <i>rīċ</i> (“rich, powerful”) + <i>berht</i> (“bright”). Compare Old Dutch <i>Rīcberht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kingdom, empire</li><li>reign</li><li>authority, dominion</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rich</li><li>powerful</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rīkī</i> (“kingdom, realm”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rīkī</i> (“mighty, rich”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kingly rule, power, dominion</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rīkijadōmaz</i> (“power, wealth”), equivalent to <i>rīċe</i> +&lrm; <i>-dōm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rēcels</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female ruler, a female endued with power</li><li>a goddess</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rīkinjō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>powerfully</li><li>splendidly</li></ol><p><i>rīc</i> (“powerful, rich”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>riċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rule; govern, reign</li><li>to dominate, domineer; tyrannize</li><li>to prevail</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rīkisōn</i>. Equivalent to <i>rīċe</i> (“kingdom, reign”) +&lrm; <i>-sian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a ruler</li></ol><p>From <i>rīcsian</i> (“to rule”) +&lrm; <i>-end</i> (“agent suffix”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>rīc</i> (“kingdom, authority”) +&lrm; <i>þrȳþ</i> (“might”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rider</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ride</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rīdan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rīdaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rider</li><li>horseman, cavalryman, knight</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;riddjō</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old High German <i>ritto</i> and Old Norse <i>-riði</i>. Related to <i>rīdan</i> (“to ride”) and <i>rād</i> (“riding, a ride”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horseman</li></ol><p>From <i>rīdan</i> (“to ride”) +&lrm; <i>-end</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rider, horseman</li><li>knight</li></ol><p>From <i>rīdan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>riddari</i> (“knight”) and German <i>Reiter</i> (“knight”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>riht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exult, rejoice</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;raustijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raustijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>reysta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a riveling, a kind of shoe</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a veil; curtain; cloak</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;riftą</i>, <i>&#42;riftiją</i>, perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rebʰ-</i> (“to cover; arch over; vault”). Cognate with Old High German <i>peinrefta</i> (“legwear; leggings”), Old Norse <i>ript</i>, <i>ripti</i> (“a kind of cloth; linen jerkin”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an instrument for reaping; a sickle or scythe</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;riftr</i>, further origin uncertain. Perhaps from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;riftrą</i> (“sickle, scythe”), equivalent to <i>rīpan</i> (“to reap”) +&lrm; <i>-þor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who reaps, a reaper</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Probably dissimilated from <i>rifter</i> (“sickle”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rain (often impersonal)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rignijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>rigna</i>, Gothic <i>𐍂𐌹𐌲𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (rignjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>right (as opposed to wrong)</li><li>lawful</li><li>real or genuine</li><li>straight</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) right (<i>as opposed to left</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>right (as opposed to wrong)</li><li>a right</li><li>law</li><li>truth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;reht</i> (“right, straight”).</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>riucht</i>, Old Saxon <i>reht</i>, Old High German <i>reht</i>, Old Norse <i>réttr</i>, Gothic <i>𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (raihts). Non-Germanic cognates include Latin <i>rēctus</i>, Ancient Greek <i>ὀρεκτός</i> (orektós), Old Irish <i>recht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to right, restore</li><li>to rectify, correct, amend</li><li>to keep right, direct, rule, straighten</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rihtijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rihtijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>rihtian</i> (“to straighten”), Old Norse <i>rétta</i> (“to straighten, stretch, raise, adjust”) and German <i>richten</i> (“to direct, judge, follow, depend on”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>straight</li><li>right, in the following senses:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>directly</li><li>exactly</li><li>correctly, justly</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>riht</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>square (instrument for creating right angles)</li></ol><p>From <i>riht</i> +&lrm; <i>bred</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruler, director</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>riht, rihtan</i>; <i>-end</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruler, director</li></ol><p>From <i>riht</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>. Cognate to Old High German <i>rihtāri</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rihtung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>right, just, proper</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>riht</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>rightly, justly</li><li>directly (of direction)</li></ol><p>From <i>riht</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>rihtlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>rihtlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rightness, straightness, perpendicularity</li><li>rightness, justice, equity</li><li>ratio</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rehtanassī</i>, equivalent to <i>riht</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>riht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>direction, guidance</li><li>correction</li><li>rule</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rihtungu</i>. By surface analysis, <i>rihtan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>. Compare German <i>Richtung</i> (“direction, route”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>just, fair</li><li>righteous</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rehtawīs</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>rehtwīs</i>. Equivalent to <i>riht</i> +&lrm; <i>-wīs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>justice, fairness</li><li>righteousness</li></ol><p>From <i>rihtwīs</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>rihtwīs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>rihtwīs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who writes correctly</li></ol><p>From <i>riht</i> +&lrm; <i>wrītere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>legitimate marriage</li><li>legitimate wife</li></ol><p>From <i>riht</i> (“right, legitimate”) +&lrm; <i>ǣ</i> (“marriage”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>number</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rīm</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rīmą</i> (“number, count, series”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂rey-</i> (“to reason, count”). Akin to Old Frisian <i>rīm</i>, Old Saxon <i>-rīm</i>, Old High German <i>rīm</i>, Icelandic <i>rím</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>edge; rim; border</li><li>bank (<i>of a river, stream, etc.</i>)</li><li>coast</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rimō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rimô</i>, possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;rem-</i>, <i>&#42;remə-</i> (“to rest, support, be based”). Cognate with Middle Low German <i>remme</i>, Old West Norse <i>rimi</i> (Norwegian <i>rime</i>), Old Saxon <i>rimi</i> (“edge; border; trim”), Icelandic <i>rimi</i> (“a strip of land”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to count, number, reckon</li><li>to enumerate, recount, describe in succession, tell</li><li>to calculate, compute, count up</li><li>to account, esteem as</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rīmijaną</i>, <i>&#42;rīmaną</i> (“to count”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂rey-</i> (“to customise, regulate, order, count, add”). Cognate with Old High German <i>rīman</i> (“to count”), Latin <i>reor</i> (“count, reckon, think”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calendar</li></ol><p><i>rīm</i> (“number”) +&lrm; <i>bōc</i> (“book”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mathematics</li></ol><p>From <i>rīm</i> (“number”) +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i> (“study of, -ology”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mathematician</li><li>someone skilled at math</li></ol><p>Regularly derived from <i>rīmcræft</i> (“mathematics”) using the suffixes <i>-iġ</i> and <i>-a</i>. Also equivalent to <i>rīm</i> (“number”) +&lrm; <i>cræftiga</i> (“skilled worker, professional”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who computes, counts, or calculates; a reckoner</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rīmą</i> (“number”) + Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ārijaz</i> (nominal agent suffix), equivalent to Old English <i>rīm</i> (“enumeration”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i> (“-er”). Related to Old English <i>rīman</i> (“to count, number, reckon, enumerate, calculate, compute”). More at rime.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Rhine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Rīnaz</i>, from Celtic. Ultimately derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃reyH-</i> (“to flow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>riġnan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) man, warrior</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rinkiz</i> (“upright man”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rinkaz</i> (“straight, upright”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>rink</i>, Old Norse <i>rekkr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bark (of a tree)</li><li>crust, rind</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rindu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rindō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rind</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rindā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrinġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run</li><li>(of time) to pass, elapse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rinnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reap</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rīpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ripe</li><li>mature</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rīpī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rīpiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reaper</li></ol><p>From <i>rīpan</i> (“to reap”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ripen</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rīpēn</i>, equivalent to <i>rīpe</i> (“ripe”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sickle; an iron tool used to reap grain</li></ol><p>From <i>rīp</i> (“reap”) +&lrm; <i>īsern</i> (“iron”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rise</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rīsan</i>, see also Old Frisian <i>rīsa</i>, Old Saxon <i>rīsan</i>, Old High German <i>rīsan</i>, Old Norse <i>rísa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rysċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rīcsian</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rīkisōn</i> (“to rule”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃reǵ-</i> (“chief, king”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a stream or rivulet</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rīþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rīþiz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃riHtís</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>ryd</i>, Middle Low German <i>rid</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an upper garment, such as a tunic, smock, or shirt</li><li>that which is chewed; cud</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rukkaz</i> (“garment; apron”).</p><p>Derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rukjaną</i> (“to ruminate”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;rewg-</i> (“to vomit; belch; burp”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rock</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rukkōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rukkōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to belch, burp</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rukatjaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rewg-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rugby (a town in Warwickshire, England)</li></ol><p>Attested in the 1086 Domesday Book. Probably from earlier *Hrōcaburg, composed of <i>hrōc</i> (“rook”) or an unattested name <i>&#42;Hrōca</i> + <i>burg</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cross (method of execution)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) The cross on which Christ was crucified</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>metonymical</i>) Christlike suffering or tribulation</li></ol></ol><li>a measure of land length, equal to a perch</li><li>a measure of land area, equal to a quarter of an acre</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rōdō</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>rōd</i>, Old Saxon <i>rōda</i>, Dutch <i>roede</i> (“rod”), Old High German <i>ruota</i> (German <i>Rute</i>), Old Norse <i>róða</i> (“rod, cross”) (Danish <i>rode</i> (“gauge, rod”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rōde tācn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sign of the cross</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rodor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crucify</li></ol><p>From <i>rōd</i> +&lrm; <i>fæstnian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sky, heaven, heavens</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;radur</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raduraz</i> (“wheel, sun, heaven, sky”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) the "king of heaven;" Jesus Christ</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>rodor</i> (“sky, heavens”) +&lrm; <i>cyning</i> (“king”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) firmament; celestial; heavenly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raduralīkaz</i>, equivalent to <i>rodor</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heavenly throne</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>rodor</i> +&lrm; <i>stōl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in charters</i>) clearing, glade</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rodu</i>, variant of <i>&#42;rod</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rudą</i>, from <i>&#42;reudaną</i> (“to clear”); compare <i>rēodan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a worshipper of the cross; a Christian</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>rōd</i> +&lrm; <i>weorþiend</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rōdweorþiend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Rēmisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vigorous, strong; valiant, brave, keen</li><li>noble, renowned</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rōf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hrofesċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hrofesċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flourish</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rome (a major city in Italy)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Rome, Ancient Rome (an ancient empire based out of the city of Rome, covering vast territories in Europe, Asia and Africa; in full, Roman Empire)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Rūmu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Roman Empire (an ancient empire based out of the city of Rome, covering vast territories in Europe, Asia and Africa)</li></ol><p>From the genitive form of <i>Rōmāne</i> + <i>rīċe</i>. Literally "empire of the Romans."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Romans, the nation of Rome</li><li>(people of) the Holy Roman Empire</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>rōmānī</i> (“Romans”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Roman</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a Roman</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>rōmānus</i> (“Roman”) + the native suffix <i>-isċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the city of Rome</li></ol><p>Literally "the city of Rome."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Rōme burg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Rōme burg</i>: Rome (a major city in Italy; the ancient capital of the Roman Empire)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Peter's pence</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>Rōm</i> +&lrm; <i>feoh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Peter's pence or alms-fee</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Rōm</i> (“Rome”) +&lrm; <i>ġesċot</i> (“contribution, local tax”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strive after [+genitive]</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rāmōn</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;rāmēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blackened, sooty</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rāmag</i> (“dirty, dark”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rēmaz</i>, <i>&#42;rēmiz</i> (“dirt, soot”). Cognate with Old High German <i>rāmag</i> (“dirty, dark, black”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rōmian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alms-fee</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>Rōm</i> (“Rome”) +&lrm; <i>penning</i> (“penny”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Romans</li></ol><p>From <i>Rōm</i> (“Rome”) +&lrm; <i>-ware</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Romans</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Rōm</i> (“Rome”) +&lrm; <i>Wēalas</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ropp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ropp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intestine</li><li>colon</li></ol><p>Unknown. Apparently related to Middle Dutch <i>roppe</i>, <i>rop</i> (“fish stomach, fish intestines”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abdominal pain, colic</li></ol><p>From <i>ropp</i> +&lrm; <i>wærċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rose</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>rosa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rose; rosy</li></ol><p>From <i>rōse</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>glad, cheerful</li><li>noble, great</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rutland (a small landlocked county in the East Midlands of England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rot, get corrupt, ulcerate, putrify</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rotēn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rutāną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hrewd-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>rōt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>rōt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corruption, decay, putrefaction</li><li>sore, ulcer</li></ol><p>From <i>rotian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hrofesċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quiet, rest, calm</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rōu</i> (“calm, rest”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to row (with oars etc.)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rōan</i> (“to row”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rower, oarsman</li></ol><p><i>rōwan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Rouen (the capital city of Normandy, France, on the River Seine)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old French <i>&#42;Rodem</i>, earlier form of <i>Roem</i>, from Latin <i>Rotomagus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mastiff</li></ol><p><i>ryþþa</i> +&lrm; <i>hund</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oar</li><li>rudder, scull</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rōþr</i> (“rudder”, literally “instrument for rowing”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>rōther</i>, Old High German <i>ruodar</i>. Equivalent to <i>rōwan</i> (“to row”) +&lrm; <i>-þor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hrōþwulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) ruddock; robin (<i>bird</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>rudu</i> +&lrm; <i>-uc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rue (plants in the genus <i>Ruta</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rūtā</i> (“rue”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be ruddy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rudāną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ruddy</li></ol><p>From <i>rudu</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Red, redness, <i>ruddy</i> complexion.</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rewdʰ-</i> (“red”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rūh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the rye-harvest; the name of a month</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rough, hairy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rūhwaz</i>. Cognate with Middle Low German <i>rūch</i>, Middle Dutch <i>ruuch</i>, <i>ru</i>, <i>rou</i> (Dutch <i>ruig</i>, <i>ruw</i>, <i>rouw</i>), Old High German <i>rūh</i> (German <i>rau</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ray (<i>fish</i>)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rūhwaz</i> (“rough”). Compare Middle Low German <i>roche</i>, <i>ruche</i>, Middle Dutch <i>roche</i>, <i>rogghe</i>, Danish <i>rokke</i>, Swedish <i>rocka</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spacious, roomy</li><li>long, extended (<i>of time</i>)</li><li>liberal, extensive, ample, abundant, bountiful, expansive, generous</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>room, space</li><li>a space of time, an interval</li><li>opportunity</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rūm</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>rūm</i>, Dutch <i>ruim</i>, Old High German <i>rūm</i>, Old Norse <i>rúmr</i>, Gothic <i>𐍂𐌿𐌼𐍃</i> (rums).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rūm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ample</li><li>large</li></ol><p>From <i>rūm</i> (“room, space”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whisper; counsel; consultation</li><li>rune; letter</li><li>mystery; secret</li><li>advice</li><li>writing</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rūnu</i>. Cognate with the Old Saxon <i>rūna</i>, Old High German <i>rūna</i> (German <i>Raun</i>), Old Norse <i>rún</i>, and Gothic <i>𐍂𐌿𐌽𐌰</i> (runa).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whisperer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rūnārī</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>rūnāri</i>. Equivalent to <i>rūn</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whisper</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rūnōn</i>, equivalent to <i>rūn</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rune, letter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rūnōstabaz</i>, equivalent to <i>rūn</i> (“rune”) +&lrm; <i>stæf</i> (“letter”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rust</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rust</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rustaz</i> (“rust”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;rudʰso-</i> (“red”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rewdʰ-</i> (“red”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rusty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rustagaz</i> (“rusty”), equivalent to <i>rust</i> (“rust”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rēcels</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>reċen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>reċene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rye</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rugi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rugiz</i>. Cognate with Russian <i>рожь</i> (rožʹ) and Old Prussian <i>rugis</i>.</p><p>See also Old Frisian <i>rogga</i> (West Frisian <i>rogge</i>), Old Saxon <i>roggo</i> (Low German <i>Rogg</i>), Dutch <i>rogge</i>, Old High German <i>rocko</i> (German <i>Roggen</i>, <i>Rocken</i>), Old Norse <i>rugr</i> (Danish <i>rug</i>, Swedish <i>råg</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>riht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make roomy, extend, spread, enlarge, amplify</li><li>to clear the way, clear by removing obstructions</li><li>to make room, give place (to another)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rūmijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rūmijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>room, empty space</li><li>extent</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>rȳman</i> +&lrm; <i>-et</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rȳmetlīest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of room or space, roomlessness</li></ol><p>From <i>rȳmet</i> +&lrm; <i>-līest</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>course, path along which motion takes place</li><li>running, a run</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;runiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>runner; messenger; courier</li><li>runnel; stream</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;runilaz</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;runiz</i> (“course; track”). Compare Swedish <i>rännil</i> (“runnel; runner”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rennet (an enzyme used in cheesemaking)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rysċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) a rush</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ruskijā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fat</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rusilą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hryþþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reach, attain</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;raikijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raikijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>reka</i>, Middle Dutch <i>reiken</i>, Old High German <i>reichen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a dog that hunts by scent</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) building, structure</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) house, abode, dwelling place</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) hall; palace</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) prison</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rakid</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;rakud</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pertaining to a palace, palatine, palatial</li></ol><p>From <i>ræċed</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>advice</li><li>counsel</li><li>that which is advisable.</li><li>decision</li><li>plan</li><li>judgement, reason</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rād</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rēdaz</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>rēd</i>, Old Saxon <i>rād</i>, Old High German <i>rāt</i>, Old Norse <i>ráð</i> (Proto-Norse <i>&#42;ᚱᚨᛞᚨᛉ</i> (&#42;radaʀ /⁠*rādaz⁠/)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to read</li><li>to advise</li><li>to guess</li><li>to interpret, explain</li><li>to decide</li></ol><p>From a merger of two verbs, which came to be pronounced the same in the present tense and the infinitive due to regular sound change:</p><p>The past tense form <i>reordon</i> preserves the zero-grade reduplicated plural stem <i>&#42;re-rd-un</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Rādaberht</i>, from <i>&#42;rād</i> (“advice, counsel”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”). Equivalent to <i>rǣd</i> +&lrm; <i>beorht</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Rātberht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counselor, advisor</li></ol><p><i>rǣd</i> (“counsel”) +&lrm; <i>-bora</i> (“bearer”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ready, prompt, prepared; ready to ride, mounted (on a horse)</li><li>skilled</li><li>simple, plain</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lesson, reading</li><li>design, device</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raidijaz</i>, <i>&#42;raidaz</i> (“arranged, ready”).</p><p>From <i>rǣdan</i> (“to read, interpret”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mounted fighting force; cavalry</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guess: conjecture, interpretation</li><li>counsel, consideration</li><li>debate, speech in council</li><li>enigma, riddle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rādislī</i>. Equivalent to <i>rǣdan</i> +&lrm; <i>-els</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>rǣdels</i></li></ol><p>From <i>rǣdan</i> +&lrm; <i>-else</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>condition, state; terms, stipulation</li><li>government, rule; direction, guidance</li><li>an estimate, account, or reckoning; calculation; figure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raidijaną</i> (“to arrange”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who reads; a reader</li><li>a scholar</li><li>a lector</li><li>a reader or interpreter of riddles; a diviner</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rēdārijaz</i>, equivalent to <i>rǣd</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i> and/or <i>rǣdan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>rātari</i> (“adviser”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>resolute, prudent, wise</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>someone who gives advice: advisor, counselor</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rēdagebô</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>rēdjeva</i>, Old Saxon <i>rādgeƀo</i>, Old High German <i>rātgebo</i>, Old Norse <i>ráðgjafi</i>. Equivalent to <i>rǣd</i> +&lrm; <i>ġiefa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rǣdġiefa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rǣdġiefa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radish</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rādik</i>, borrowed from Latin <i>rādix</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reading</li><li>passage in a book, lesson</li><li>rule, government</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rādingu</i>, equivalent to <i>rǣdan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>rātunga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a book containing lessons; lectionary</li></ol><p>From <i>rǣding</i> +&lrm; <i>bōc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hrædnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>rǣd</i> (“advice, counsel”) + <i>nōþ</i> (“daring”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Rǣdweald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Rædwald of East Anglia; an Anglo-Saxon King of East Anglia.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Rādawald</i>, from <i>&#42;rād</i> (“advice, counsel”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wald</i> (“power, authority”). Equivalent to <i>rǣd</i> +&lrm; <i>weald</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Rātolt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Rǣdweald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Rādawulf</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Rēdawulfaz</i>, equivalent to Old English <i>rǣd</i> (“advice, counsel”) + <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to involve; wrap</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;raibijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raibijaną</i> (“to make happy, please, delight”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;reyp-</i> (“to tear; border, edge”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>reifa</i> (“to help, delight, promote, give gifts, decorate”), Icelandic <i>reifa</i> (“to wrap up, swaddle; unloose, disclose; rip up”). Possibly also related to Old English <i>rift</i> (“curtain, veil”), Old English <i>ārāfian</i> (“to uncoil, wind off”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rafter (element of a roof)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roe deer</li><li>wild she-goat</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>rā, raha</i> (“roe”); <i>-e</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>reġn-</i> (“very”) and <i>beald</i> (“brave, bold”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>reġn-</i> (“wondrous, mighty”) + <i>here</i> (“army”). Cognate with German <i>Rainer</i> and Old Norse <i>Ragnarr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Reynold.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Raginawaldaz</i>, equivalent to <i>reġn-</i> +&lrm; <i>weald</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind with rope, make captive</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raipijaną</i>, <i>&#42;raipōną</i> (“to tie, bind”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raipaz</i> (“string, strap, band, rope”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁reyp-</i> (“to peel off, rip, tear; strip, strap”). Compare Old High German <i>bireifen</i> (“to wrap up, bind”), Old Norse <i>reipa</i> (“to fasten with rope”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rǣpling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one bound; captive, prisoner, criminal</li></ol><p>From <i>rǣpan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ling</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise, set upright, begin, create, rouse, promote</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;raiʀijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raizijaną</i>, causative of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rīsaną</i> (“to rise”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>run</li><li>rush</li><li>counsel, deliberation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rēsō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁reh₁s-</i> (“to flow, rush”). Related to Old English <i>rinnan</i> (“to run”). Doublet of English <i>race</i> via Old Norse <i>rás</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rēswō</i> (“counsel, reckoning”). Related to Old English <i>rǣswan</i> (“to consider, conjecture”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush, move violently or impetuously</li><li>to proceed against something with violence, to assault, to attack</li><li>to rush into anything</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rāsijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rēsijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>rǣs</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>ræsa</i> (Icelandic <i>ræsa</i>) and possibly Old Dutch <i>&#42;rāson</i> (Dutch <i>razen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a heavy shower</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crackle as a fire</li><li>to sparkle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;raskuttjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raskutjaną</i>, equivalent to <i>&#42;ræsċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sparkling, gleaming</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ræsċettan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rǣdels</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rǣswan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plank, beam</li><li>panel, rafter, ceiling</li></ol><p>Unknown. Perhaps from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;raʀn</i> (“dwelling, house”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;razną</i> (“house”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rest</li><li>resting place; bed, couch</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rastō</i>. The alternative form <i>rest</i> has its -e- by analogy with the verb <i>restan</i>, which inherited the vowel through i-umlaut.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sabbath</li></ol><p>Originally the phrase <i>ræste</i> <i>dæġ</i>, literally "day of rest."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>restless</li></ol><p><i>ræst</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) counsellor</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) leader, chief, king</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rēswô</i>&nbsp;m (“counsellor”), compare <i>&#42;rēswō</i>&nbsp;f (“counsel, deliberation”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hreh₁dʰ-</i> (“to arrange; to advise”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>ræsir</i> (“leader, chief, king, prince”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think, consider</li><li>to suppose, suspect, conjecture</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rāswijan</i> (“to think, reckon, calculate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rǣswan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reasoning</li><li>conjecture, supposition</li></ol><p>From <i>rǣswian</i> (“to think, suspect”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rætt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rat</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ratt</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rattaz</i> (whence also Old Frisian <i>&#42;rat</i>). The various cognates reflect different preforms. The most common would be the feminine ō-stem <i>&#42;rattā</i>: Old Frisian <i>&#42;ratte</i>, Old Saxon <i>ratta</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;ratta</i>, Old High German <i>ratza</i>. Old High German <i>rato</i> and <i>ratta</i> can only reflect borrowings post-dating the High German consonant shift, thus the non-geminate /t/ in <i>rato</i> is of unknown significance.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>row, line</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;raiwu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raiwō</i>, <i>&#42;raigwō</i>, <i>&#42;rīgǭ</i> (“row, streak, line”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;reyk-</i> (“to carve, scratch, etch”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rēcels</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Sabbath</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>sabbatum</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>σάββατον</i> (sábbaton, “Sabbath”), from Biblical Hebrew <i>שַׁבָּת</i> (šabbāṯ, “Sabbath”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sawbridgeworth (a town and civil parish of East Hertfordshire district, Hertfordshire, England)</li></ol><p>Attested in the Domesday Book. Equivalent to <i>Sǣbeorht</i> +&lrm; <i>weorþ</i>, literally “Sǣbeorht's enclosure”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight</li><li>to argue, disagree</li><li>to accuse, blame</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sakan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sakaną</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>sakan</i>, Old High German <i>sahhan</i>, Old Norse <i>saka</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐌰𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (sakan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sack, bag</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sakk</i>, <i>&#42;sakku</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sakkuz</i>, from Latin <i>saccus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sāclēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>priest</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>sacerdōs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strive, brawl</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free from charge or accusation, innocent; guiltless</li><li>free from contention, unmolested, secure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sakōlausaz</i>, equivalent to <i>sacu</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>saklauss</i>, of identical meaning.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dispute, strife, contention</li><li>crime, sin</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sakō</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>sake</i>, <i>seke</i>, <i>sek</i> (West Frisian <i>saak</i>), Old Saxon <i>saka</i> (Low German <i>sake</i>), Dutch <i>zaak</i>, Old High German <i>sahha</i> (German <i>Sache</i>), Old Norse <i>sǫk</i> (Danish <i>sag</i>, Icelandic <i>sök</i>, Swedish <i>sak</i>), Gothic <i>𐍃𐌰𐌺𐌾𐍉</i> (sakjō, “dispute”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cord, halter, snare</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;saidō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saidô</i> (“band, cord”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) the Sadducees</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to satisfy, satiate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sadōn</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saddle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sadul</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sadulaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saddle bow</li></ol><p>From <i>sadol</i> (“saddle”) +&lrm; <i>boga</i> (“bow”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to saddle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sadulōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sadol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sadolboga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>savin (the plant <i>Juniperus sabina</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>sabina</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saliva; slobber, drool</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;saifr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saifraz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seyp-</i> (“to flow, run, drip”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saw (tool)</li><li>saying; statement</li><li>story, tale; narrative</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sagu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sagô</i> (“saw, scythe”), <i>&#42;sagō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sek-</i>, <i>&#42;sēik-</i> (“to cut”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>sage</i> (West Frisian <i>seage</i>), Old Saxon <i>saga</i>, Middle Dutch <i>sage</i>, <i>saghe</i> (Dutch <i>zaag</i>), Old High German (“saga”) (German <i>Säge</i>), Old Norse <i>sǫg</i> (Icelandic <i>sög</i>, Danish <i>sav</i>, Swedish <i>såg</i>).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sagā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sagō</i>, <i>&#42;sagǭ</i> (“saying, story”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sekʷe-</i>, <i>&#42;skʷē-</i> (“to tell, talk”). More at saw.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say, tell</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sagōn</i>, equivalent to <i>sagu</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>club, cudgel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;saigul</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>statement, discourse, report</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sagǭ</i>, whence also Old Frisian <i>sege</i>, Old High German <i>saga</i>, Old Norse <i>saga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sehtlian</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sahtilōną</i>, equivalent to <i>saht</i> +&lrm; <i>-lian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rope, cord, rein</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sail</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sailą</i> (“rope”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>sēl</i> (Dutch <i>zeel</i>), Old High German <i>seil</i> (German <i>Seil</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sale</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;salō</i> (“delivery”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;selh₁-</i> (“to grab”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sealf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sealf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sealh</i> (“willow”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sealh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Salmonby (a small village in Tetford parish, East Lindsey district, Lincolnshire, England)</li></ol><p>From Old Norse, from <i>Salmundr</i> (male given name) +&lrm; <i>býr</i> (“farm; village”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silence</li></ol><p>From a derivative of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sail</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sailaz</i> (“silent”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seyl-</i> (“to be silent”) + <i>-nes</i> (“-ness”). Related to Gothic <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰𐍃𐌹𐌻𐌰𐌽</i> (anasilan, “to cease, become silent”), Latin <i>sileō</i> (“be silent”), Old English <i>seoloþ</i> (“sea, lake”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Solomon, son of David</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Salomōn</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Σολομών</i> (Solomṓn), from Biblical Hebrew <i>שְׁלֹמֹה</i> (šĕlōmōʰ), from <i>שָׁלוֹם</i> (šālōm, “peace”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a residence; dwelling</li><li>a hall; palace</li><li>an upper room; raised platform; soler</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;salaʀ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;salaz</i>, <i>&#42;salą</i>, <i>&#42;saliz</i> (“house, room”). In the sense of "upper room, raised platform", influenced by Latin <i>solarium</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dusky</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;salu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;salwaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sal(w)-</i>. Cognate with Irish <i>salach</i>, Latin <i>saliva</i>, English <i>sallow</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark brown</li></ol><p>From <i>salu</i> (“dark”) +&lrm; <i>brūn</i> (“brown”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark-feathered</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>salu</i> (“dusky”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i> +&lrm; <i>feþere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark-cloaked, having dark plumage</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>salu</i> (“dusky”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i> +&lrm; <i>pād</i> (“cloak”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>whether, or</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally</i>) half-</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) partially; incompletely, imperfectly</li><li>union, combination, agreement; together, con-</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sām-</i>, a variant of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sāmi-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sēmi-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sēmi-</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>sām-</i>, Old High German <i>sāmi-</i>. Compare English <i>semi-</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sam</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;samaz</i> (“together”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;somHós</i> (“same”), Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sem-</i> (“one, together”). Compare Old English <i>samen</i> (“together”), Old English <i>same</i> (“manner, similitude”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>same</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Samaritan</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>born early; premature</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>sām-</i> + <i>(ġe)boren</i>, literally “half-born.”</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-burnt</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>sām-</i> + <i>(ġe)bærned</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sāmcwic</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-dead</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sāmikwiku</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sēmikwikwaz</i> (“half-dead”, literally “half-alive”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sēmigʷih₃wós</i>, equivalent to <i>sām-</i> (“half-”) +&lrm; <i>cwic</i> (“living, alive”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>sāmkwik</i>, Old High German <i>sāmikwek</i>, <i>sāmiquek</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the same way</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;samō</i> (“in the same way; similarly, likewise”), influenced by the related adjective <i>&#42;sam</i> (“same”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;samaz</i> (“same”), <i>&#42;samô</i> (adverb), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;somHós</i> (“same”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>together</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;saman</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saman</i>, <i>&#42;samanai</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>adolescent</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>sam-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeong</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-green</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) immature</li></ol><p>From <i>sām-</i> +&lrm; <i>grēne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unwell; sick, infirm</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sāmihail</i>, equivalent to <i>sām-</i> +&lrm; <i>hāl</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>sāmiheil</i> (“spoiled, weakened”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-closed</li></ol><p>From <i>sām-</i> (“half-”) +&lrm; <i>(ġe)locen</i> (“closed”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-educated, half-taught; imperfectly taught</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>sām-</i> +&lrm; <i>(ġe)lǣred</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to collect, bring together, gather</li><li>to congregate, come together, assemble</li><li>to join, draw together, unite</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;samnōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>samnunga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an assembly or council</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;samnungu</i>. Equivalent to <i>samnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>all at once, all of a sudden, suddenly</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>simultaneously, at the same time, together; entirely, also, as well, too, in addition to, plus</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>together with, at (of time)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;samaþa</i> (“together”). Akin to Old High German <i>samit</i> (“together”), German <i>samt</i> (“together with”), <i>sammt</i> (“together with”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>together, with; co-</li><li>at the same time, simultaneously</li><li>entirely, completely</li><li>common, in common, shared</li><li>against</li></ol><p>From <i>samod</i> (“together”) from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;samaþa</i> (“together”). Akin to Old High German <i>samit</i> (“together”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common country or dwelling-place</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>samod-</i> +&lrm; <i>eard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a consonant; consonant sound</li></ol><p><i>samod</i> (“with”) +&lrm; <i>swēġende</i> (“sounding”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>being together</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>samod-</i> +&lrm; <i>wist</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>living together</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>samod-</i> +&lrm; <i>wunung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>working together, cooperating</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>samod-</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrċende</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wedlock</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sam-</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-cooked</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>sām-</i> + <i>(ġe)soden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Samuel</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Samuel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-grown</li><li>adolescent</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>sām-</i> + <i>(ġe)weaxen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dumb, unwise</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>sām-</i> +&lrm; <i>wīs</i>, literally “half-smart.”</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a living together, cohabitation, matrimony</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;samawisti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;samawistiz</i>, equivalent to <i>sam-</i> +&lrm; <i>wist</i>. Compare Old High German <i>samwist</i>, <i>samawist</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-finished</li></ol><p>past participle of <i>sāmwyrċan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do something incompletely</li></ol><p>From <i>sām-</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saint</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>sanctus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Michaelmas</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>action of sending, embassy, mission, deputation; message</li><li>sending, service, course of food, dish of food, repast, mess, victuals</li><li>sand, gravel</li><li>sand by the sea, sands, seashore, sandy shore, beach</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sandō</i>. See also the verb <i>sendan</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;samd</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;samdaz</i>. Compare Old Frisian <i>sand</i>, Old Saxon <i>sand</i>, Old High German <i>sant</i>, Old Norse <i>sandr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sand, gravel</li></ol><p><i>sand</i> +&lrm; <i>ċeosol</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grain of sand</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sandakorn</i>, <i>&#42;samdakorn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;samdakurną</i>. Equivalent to <i>sand</i> (“sand”) +&lrm; <i>corn</i> (“grain”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quicksand</li></ol><p>From <i>sand</i> (“sand”) +&lrm; <i>ġeweorp</i> (“a tossing”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sandbank</li></ol><p>From <i>sand</i> (“sand”) +&lrm; <i>hryċġ</i> (“spine”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sandy</li></ol><p>From <i>sand</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) shore (of the sea)</li></ol><p>From <i>sand</i> (“sand”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sandpit</li></ol><p>From <i>sand</i> (“sand”) +&lrm; <i>pytt</i> (“pit”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quicksand</li></ol><p>From <i>sand</i> (“sand”) +&lrm; <i>rid</i> (“a swaying”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sandwich (a town and civil parish of Dover district, Kent, England)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>sand</i> (“sand”) +&lrm; <i>wīċ</i> (“settlement”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>song</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) liturgical service</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sangu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sangwaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>sanc</i>, Old Norse <i>sǫngr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>songbook, music-book</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) church service book of canticles and hymns</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sang</i> +&lrm; <i>bōc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the art of singing; music (vocal or instrumental)</li><li>the art of composing poetry</li></ol><p>From <i>sang</i> +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>singer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sangwārijaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>sang</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>singer (female)</li></ol><p>From <i>sang</i> +&lrm; <i>-estre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sangestre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amber, resin</li><li>pomade</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;saipu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saipō</i> (“resin”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seyb-</i>, <i>&#42;seyp-</i> (“to pour, sile, flow, drip”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soap</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;saipā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saipǭ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>painful</li><li>sore</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pain</li><li>soreness</li><li>sorrow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sair</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sairaz</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sair</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sairą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Saracens</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Saracēnus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Σᾰρᾰκηνός</i> (Sărăkēnós).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sorely, grievously, bitterly</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lament, complain</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sairattjan</i>, equivalent to <i>sār</i> +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>sērezzen</i> (“to be in pain, grieve, suffer, be in labour or childbirth”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be sore</li><li>to be hurt; feel pain for; feel sorry or apologetic for</li><li>to be sad; be pained; grieve</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sairōn</i>, equivalent to <i>sār</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sad, sorrowful, distressed, grieving, mournful</li><li>causing distress, causing sorrow; painful, grievous, dismal</li><li>grieved or vexed or regretful [<i>with</i> <b>for</b> (+ dative) <small>‘for or (because) of something’</small>]</li><li>remorseful, penitent, apologetic</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a wretched or sorrowful person</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sairagaz</i> (“sad”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>sāriġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sad, mournful, melancholy</li></ol><p><i>sāriġ</i> (“sad, sorrowful”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sadness, sorrowfulness</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>sāriġ</i> (“sad, sorrowful”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lamentable, sad, mournful, grievous</li></ol><p><i>sār</i> (“sore, painful”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>causing pain, sorrow, or grief</li><li>sorely, bitterly</li></ol><p>From <i>sārlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i> or <i>sār</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of the body</i>) pain</li><li>(<i>of the mind</i>) pain, affliction, grief; woe; sorrow</li></ol><p>From <i>sār</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sārnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sārnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Saracene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Saracene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sārspell</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>painful story</li><li>painful news</li></ol><p><i>sār</i> (“pain”) +&lrm; <i>spell</i> (“story”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Satan, the Devil</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Satan</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Σατάν</i> (Satán), from Hebrew <i>שָׂטָן</i> (satán, “adversary, accuser”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sāwol</i></li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Saul)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saul (biblical king of Israel)</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Late Latin <i>Saūl</i>, from Koine Greek <i>Σαούλ</i> (Saoúl), from Biblical Hebrew <i>שָׁאוּל</i> (šāʾûl).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sāwol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sow (seed, etc.) (literally or figuratively)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sāan</i> (“to sow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sāwol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sāwolberend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sāwolcund</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sāwolġedāl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sāwolhūs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sāwollēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sāwolsċeatt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sāwolþearf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sower</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sāwan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sower</li></ol><p>From <i>sāwan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sāwol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give up the ghost; to die</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sāwol</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the giving up the ghost; dying</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sāwlian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soul</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;saiwalu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saiwalō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soul-bearer; a being endowed with a soul</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sāwol</i> +&lrm; <i>berend</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spiritual</li></ol><p>From <i>sawol</i> (“soul”) +&lrm; <i>-cund</i> (“natured”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the parting between body and soul; death</li></ol><p><i>sāwol</i> +&lrm; <i>ġedāl</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) body</li></ol><p>Kenning of <i>sāwol</i> (“soul”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soulless</li><li>lifeless</li></ol><p><i>sāwol</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) a church-imposed burial fee</li></ol><p>From <i>sāwol</i> +&lrm; <i>sċeatt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a need of the soul</li></ol><p>From <i>sāwol</i> +&lrm; <i>þearf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sāwol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sāwollēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Seaxan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċāffōt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to pass or depart</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to quiver or vibrate</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to flourish or wave (an object)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cause something to quiver, flutter, or shake</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skakan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>skakan</i>, Old Norse <i>skaka</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shackle</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċēadan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skaiþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċeadu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċāfa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shave</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skaban</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>scavan</i>, Old High German <i>skaban</i>, Old Norse <i>skafa</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (skaban).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>splay-footed</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;sċāf</i> +&lrm; <i>fōt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċeaga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Anglian form of <i>sċeal</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċealfor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċealfor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>modest</li></ol><p><i>sċamu</i> +&lrm; <i>-fæst</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>modesty</li></ol><p><i>sċamfæst</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be ashamed or embarrassed <i>(+genitive of cause)</i> <i>(often impersonal with dative or accusative subject)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skamēn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skamāną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċamian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shameless, bold</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skamalausaz</i>, equivalent to <i>sċamu</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>modest, bashful</li><li>shameful, base, disgraceful</li></ol><p>From <i>sċamu</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>scamalīh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stool</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skamil</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shame, embarrassment</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skamō</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>skome</i>, Old Saxon <i>skama</i>, Old Dutch <i>skama</i>, Old High German <i>scama</i>, Old Norse <i>skǫmm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(in compounds) alternative form of <i>sċamol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leg</li></ol><p>Fom Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skankô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shame, disgrace</li><li>a disgraceful person: disgrace, embarrassment</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skandu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skandō</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>skonde</i>, Old Saxon <i>&#42;skanda</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;skanda</i>, Old High German <i>skanda</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰</i> (skanda).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skand</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skandaz</i>, <i>&#42;skamdaz</i> (“shameful person”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kem-</i> (“to cover”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house of ill-repute</li><li>brothel</li></ol><p>From <i>sċand</i> (“shame; scandal; disgrace”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>people</i>) disgraceful, infamous, vile, base; lowly</li><li>(<i>things</i>) vile in nature, disgraceful, foul, shameful, obscene</li><li>causing shame</li></ol><p>From <i>sċand</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>skantlīh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>sċandlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċēap</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċearp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċeaþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sceaþian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scab</li><li>scabies</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skabb</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skabbaz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>skabb</i> ( &gt; English <i>scab</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċacan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shackle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skakul</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skakulaz</i> (“shackle”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rough hair, wool</li><li>headhair</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skaggō</i>, equivalent to <i>sċeaga</i> (“copse, thicket”) +&lrm; <i>-ga</i> (diminutive suffix). Compare Old Norse <i>skegg</i> (“beard”), Old Norse <i>skeggi</i> (“bearded one, bearded man”), Old Norse <i>skaga</i> (“to jut out, project”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċeacel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shade</li></ol><p>Possibly a back-formation from <i>sċeadu</i>, interpreted as a neuter <i>a</i>-stem plural.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a parting of the hair</li><li>the top of the head</li></ol><p>Origin obscure. Possibly from an earlier <i>*scēadel</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skaidilu</i> (“part in the hair, crown, summit”) ; if so, then cognate with Old Dutch <i>skēthila</i>, Old High German <i>sceitila</i>, <i>skeitila</i>. Otherwise, equivalent to <i>sċēadan</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>. In any case, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skey-</i> (“to cut, part, separate, divide”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to separate, divide, make a line of separation between</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to separate, divide, part</li><li>to distinguish, decide</li><li>to shed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skaiþan</i>. Compare Old Frisian <i>skētha</i>, Old Saxon <i>skēthan</i>, Old High German <i>skeidan</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (skaidan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shad (kind of fish)</li></ol><p>When comparing Old Norse <i>skaddr</i> and dialectal Norwegian <i>skadd</i> (“a small whitefish”), unless the languages all borrowed from each other, the <i>[d]</i> that occurs in all three is best explained as a borrowing from some European substrate language.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cast a shadow</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to sneak</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shady</li></ol><p>From <i>sċeadu</i> (“shadow”) +&lrm; <i>-iht</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shadow</li><li>shade</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skadu</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old High German <i>scato</i> (German <i>Schatten</i>), Old Saxon <i>skado</i>, Old Dutch <i>scado</i> (Dutch <i>schaduw</i>), Gothic <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌳𐌿𐍃</i> (skadus), Icelandic <i>skodda</i>. The Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek <i>σκότος</i> (skótos), Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;skāton</i> (Irish <i>scáth</i>, Breton <i>squeut</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) one who walks in darkness</li></ol><p><i>sċeadu</i> (“shadow”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;genga</i> (“goer”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) the cover of darkness</li></ol><p>From <i>sċeadu</i> (“shadow”) +&lrm; <i>helm</i> (“helmet”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to overshadow, cover with shadow</li></ol><p>From <i>sċeadu</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>skadowan</i>, <i>skadoian</i>, Old Dutch <i>scedewon</i>, Old High German <i>skatawen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheaf, bundle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skaub</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skaubaz</i>. Cognate with German <i>Schaub</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shave (tool), plane, paring knife</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skabô</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>schave</i> (Dutch <i>schaaf</i>), Old High German <i>scaba</i> (German <i>Schabe</i>).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Sceafa)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Sceafa, a mythological Lombardic king sometimes included in genealogies of English monarchs</li></ol><p>Most likely from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skaubaz</i> (“sheaf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Sheaf by sheaf, sheafwise</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sċēaf</i> +&lrm; <i>-mǣlum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shaft (of an arrow or spear), pole</li><li>creation, origin</li><li>a creation, creature, something created</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skaft</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skaftaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skeh₂p-</i> (“rod, shaft, staff, club”), potentially from a root <i>&#42;(s)ke(H)p-</i> (“to strike, beat”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>skapt</i>.</p><p>From <i>sċieppan</i>; compare <i>ġesċeaft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shaftesbury (a town in Dorset, England).</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>sċeaftes</i> (genitive singular of <i>sċeaft</i> (“shaft”)) +&lrm; <i>burg</i> (“fortified city”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>copse, thicket</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skagō</i>, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skōgô</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>skagi</i> (“outjutting headland, promontory”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>servant</li><li>man, soldier, sailor</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skalk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skalkaz</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌻𐌺𐍃</i> (skalks, “slave, servant”) and Old High German <i>skalk</i> (“serf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shallow</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skalidaz</i>, past participle of <i>&#42;skaljaną</i> (“to go dry, dry up, become shallow”), from <i>&#42;skalaz</i> (“parched, shallow”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kelh₁-</i> (“to dry out”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loon (bird of order Gaviiformes), a diving bird</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċealfor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċealfor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) testicles</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skal-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shell, husk</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skalu</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċamfæstnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċamfæst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċamfæstnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċamlēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċamlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċamol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċamu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċanca</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċand</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċandlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheep</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skāp</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skēpą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flock of sheep</li></ol><p>From <i>sċēap</i> (“sheep”) +&lrm; <i>heord</i> (“herd”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shepherd</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skāpahirdī</i>. Cognate with German <i>Schafhirte</i>. Equivalent to <i>sċēap</i> (“sheep”) +&lrm; <i>hierde</i> (“herdsman”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċēaphierde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Isle of Sheppey</li></ol><p>From <i>sċēap</i> (“sheep”) +&lrm; <i>īeġ</i> (“island”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Sċēapīeġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scissors, shears</li><li>plowshare</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;sċǣr</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skāri</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skēriz</i>. Related to Old High German <i>skāra</i> (“shears, tonsure”), West Frisian <i>skjirre</i>, Dutch <i>schaar</i>, German <i>Schere</i>, Icelandic <i>skæri</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skar</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skarą</i>, <i>&#42;skaraz</i> (“ploughshare”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to cut”), whence also Old High German <i>skaro</i> and <i>skara</i> (“ploughshare”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>notched, chipped, broken</li><li>gashed, cut, lacerated</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>notch, gap</li><li>shard, sherd</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skardą</i>, participle form of <i>&#42;skar-</i> (“shear”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>skard</i>, Old High German <i>scart</i>, Old Norse <i>skarðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung</li><li>filth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skarn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skarną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung beetle</li></ol><p><i>sċearn</i> (“dung”) +&lrm; <i>budda</i> (“beetle”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung beetle</li></ol><p>From <i>sċearn</i> (“dung”) +&lrm; <i>wibba</i> (“beetle”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung beetle (beetles of the family Scarabaeidae)</li></ol><p>From <i>sċearn</i> (“dung”) +&lrm; <i>wifel</i> (“beetle”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sharp</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skarp</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skarpaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kerb-</i>, from <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to cut”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>skarp</i>, <i>skerp</i>, Old Saxon <i>skarp</i>, Old Dutch <i>skarp</i>, Old High German <i>scarf</i>, Old Norse <i>skarpr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>sċearp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut the skin, make an incision</li><li>to scarify</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skarpōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sharply, keenly, smartly, effectually, quickly</li><li>(<i>of the mind</i>) sharply, keenly</li><li>painfully</li></ol><p>From <i>sċearp</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of one's sight and mind</i>) sharpness</li><li>(<i>of a surface</i>) roughness</li><li>acidity, pungency; sharpness</li></ol><p>From <i>sċearp</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċearpnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>sċearp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sharp-sighted</li></ol><p><i>sċearp</i> +&lrm; <i>-sīene</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċierseax</i></li></ol><p>Probably an alteration of <i>sċierseax</i> (literally “shave knife”), replacing the first element with <i>sċēara</i> (“scissors”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċierseax</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shaving or a shaving; a shave</li><li>shearing or a shearing</li><li>(<i>ecclesiastical</i>) tonsure</li><li>a part; portion; share; division</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) the groin; pubic region; genitals; stomach; bowel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skaru</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skarō</i> (“division; share; troop”), whence also Old High German <i>scara</i>, Dutch <i>schaar</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corner, angle, projection</li><li>nook, area, region</li><li>lap, bosom</li><li>bay</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skautaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>skat</i>, Middle Dutch <i>scoot</i> (Dutch <i>schoot</i>), Old High German <i>scōz</i> (German <i>Schoß</i>), Old Norse <i>skaut</i> (Danish <i>skød</i>), Gothic <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌿𐍄𐌰</i> (skauta).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasure, money, wealth</li><li>a coin or unit of money</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skatt</i> (“cattle, treasure”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jesting song, song of a jester</li></ol><p>From <i>sċēawiend</i> (“buffoon”) +&lrm; <i>wīse</i> (“manner”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an observer, one who examines a matter</li><li>a watchman, guard; watchtower</li><li>spy</li><li>mirror</li><li>an actor, jester, buffoon</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skauwārī</i> (“watcher, onlooker”), equivalent to <i>sċēawian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to watch or look at</li><li>to examine, scrutinize</li><li>to show (+accusative)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skauwōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skawwōną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>skawia</i>, Old Saxon <i>skawon</i>, Old Dutch <i>skouwon</i> (Dutch <i>schouwen</i>), Old High German <i>scouwōn</i> (German <i>schauen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a spectator, observer</li><li>an actor, buffoon</li></ol><p>From <i>sċēawian</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a looking at, a viewing of</li><li>inspection, examination</li><li>contemplation, consideration</li><li>a showing, exhibiting, manifestation of</li><li>a toll on the exposure of goods</li></ol><p>From <i>sċēawian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>schouwing</i> (“viewing; inspection; contemplation; showing”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to injure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skaþōną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>scadōn</i>, Old Norse <i>skaða</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheath</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skaiþiju</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skaiþiz</i>, possibly from a root <i>&#42;skaiþ-</i> (“divide, separate”), based on the notion of a split stick with a sword blade inserted; compare <i>&#42;skaiþaną</i> (“separate”).</p><p>Compare Old Frisian <i>skethe</i>, Old Saxon <i>scethia</i>, Old High German <i>sceida</i>, <i>skaida</i>, Old Norse <i>skeið</i>, <i>skeiðir</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who injures; enemy, ravager, robber</li><li>(heroic) warrior</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) injury</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skaþō</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>skaðo</i> (Middle Low German <i>scade</i>), Dutch <i>schade</i>, Old High German <i>skado</i> (German <i>Schaden</i>), Old Norse <i>skaði</i> (Swedish <i>skada</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) alternative form of <i>sceþþan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skaþjaną</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>skeðja</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (skaþjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hurt, harm, injure</li><li>to spoil, rob</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skaþōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skaþōną</i> (“to injure”). Cognate with Old High German <i>scadōn</i>, Old Norse <i>skaða</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>injurious, harmful, damaging</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skaþag</i>, equivalent to <i>sċeaþa</i> (“injury, harm”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċeacel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scania</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scania</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Skadinawjō</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>Skáney</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Sċēafa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) ‘Descendant of Sceafa’.</li></ol><p>From <i>Scēf</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċeġþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small, light ship</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Norse <i>skeið</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pirate</li></ol><p>From <i>sċeġþ</i> (“small, light ship”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċeal</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċield</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċildan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake, quiver, totter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skelbaną</i>, possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kelH-</i> (“to split, cleave”), and therefore related to Old English <i>sċylfe</i> (<i>shelf</i>) Cognate with Old Norse <i>skjálfa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċiell</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ring, resound, sound, clatter</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skellan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skellaną</i> (“to sound, clatter, ring”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>skjalla</i>, Dutch <i>schellen</i>, German <i>schellen</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skel-</i>, from <i>&#42;kelh₁-</i> (“to call, to shout, to make noise, to sound”) with an extra s-mobile.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cup</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour out, give (someone) to drink</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skankijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skankijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>skenka</i>, Old Saxon <i>skenkian</i>, Dutch <i>schenken</i>, Old High German <i>scenken</i> (German <i>schenken</i>), Old Norse <i>skenkja</i> (Swedish <i>skänka</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put to shame; abuse; insult; harm</li><li>to confound; confuse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skandijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċīene</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skaunī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skauniz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>sċēne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċīenra</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloud</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skiwō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skiwô</i>, <i>&#42;skiwją</i> (“cloud, cloud cover, haze”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kewH-</i> (“to cover”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>scio</i>, <i>skio</i>, <i>skeo</i> (“light cloud cover”), Old Norse <i>ský</i> (“cloud”), Old Irish <i>ceó</i> (“sky”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċucca</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċufan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċōġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shy</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċōh</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skeuh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sculan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċildan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>squinting, oblique, slanted, wry</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skelhaz</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>scheel</i> and Icelandic <i>skjálgur</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cross-eyed</li></ol><p><i>sċeolh</i> (“crooked”) +&lrm; <i>-īeġe</i> (“-eyed”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċeolhīeġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċolu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>modest, chaste</li></ol><p>From <i>sċeomu</i> +&lrm; <i>-ful</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall to a person's lot (happen by fate)</li><li>to go quickly, hasten, fly</li><li>alternative form of <i>sċōġan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skehan</i> (“to move quickly, run”). Akin to Old Frisian <i>schīa</i> (“to go quickly”), Old High German <i>giskehan</i> (“to happen”). More at chic.</p><p>Unknown. Compare Gothic <i>𐍃𐌺𐌴𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (skēwjan, “to go, proceed, go forth”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skēwijaną</i> (“to walk, go”), which is probably related to Etymology 1 above.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċanca</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċand</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċīene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>sċēone</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċop</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>scoppa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċieran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>scurf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gnaw, bite</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skerpaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kerb-</i>, from <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to cut”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clothing, apparel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skerp</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scrape, irritate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skerpan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skerpaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kerb-</i>, from <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to cut”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċort</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċortlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċortnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shot, act of shooting</li><li>missile, shot</li><li>darting, rapid movement</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skutą</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>skot</i>, Old Saxon <i>sīlscot</i>, Old High German <i>scoz</i> (German <i>Schoß</i>), Old Norse <i>skot</i>. Compare English <i>scot</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a shote, grayling</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to shoot, throw a missile</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to push, move quickly, pay (money)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to move quickly, flow, rush, shoot (of pain)</li></ol><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>skiata</i> (West Frisian <i>sjitte</i>), Old Saxon <i>skiotan</i> (Low German <i>scheten</i>), Dutch <i>schieten</i>, Old High German <i>skiozan</i> (German <i>schießen</i>), Old Norse <i>skjóta</i> (Danish <i>skyde</i>, Swedish <i>skjuta</i>). The Proto-Indo-European root is also the source of Old Church Slavonic <i>искыдати</i> (iskydati), Russian <i>кида́ть</i> (kidátʹ), Lithuanian <i>skudrùs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shooter, archer; warrior</li></ol><p>From <i>sċēotan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Sċottas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċēap</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shepperton (a town in Spelthorne borough, Surrey, England)</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>sċēaphierde</i> (“shepherd”) +&lrm; <i>tūn</i> (“town”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċēaphierde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċieppan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a unit of measure, bushel</li><li>tub, container, basket</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>skeppa</i>, <i>skjappa</i> (“a bushel, measure”), from Old Saxon <i>&#42;skeppio</i>, whence Middle Low German <i>scheppe</i> (“a unit of weight”). Ultimately from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skappjō</i> (compare Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skap</i> (“shape”)), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skapjô</i> (“vessel, tub, bushel”), from <i>&#42;skapą</i> (“a creation, shape”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Scieppend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċieran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċēar</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>actress</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps an alteration of earlier <i>&#42;sċēawiċġe</i> (compare Old English <i>sċēawere</i> (“actor, clown”)), equivalent to <i>sċēawian</i> (“to show”) +&lrm; <i>-iċġe</i>. Alternatively, perhaps an alteration of an earlier <i>&#42;sċerniċġe</i>, where the first element is derived from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skern</i> (“fun, play, sport, joking, mockery, jest”) (compare Old Saxon <i>skirno</i>, <i>skernio</i>, <i>skirno</i> (“actor”), Old High German <i>skerno</i>, <i>skernio</i>, <i>skirnio</i> (“joker, actor, clown”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċiering</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċearn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċēar</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċierpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċierseax</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċīete</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċucca</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to injure, to harm, to scathe</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skaþjaną</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>skeðja</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (skaþjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shin</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a thin piece of wood; shingle; billet; shide</li><li>a course</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skīd</i> (“log; tile”).</p><p>Unknown. Compare Old Norse <i>skeið</i> (“a race; course”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skaidą</i>, <i>&#42;skaiþą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skeyt-</i> (“to cut, divide, separate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shield</li><li>protection</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skelduz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċildan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shield wall</li><li>city of refuge</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skelduburgz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>skiltburg</i>, Old Norse <i>skjaldborg</i>. Equivalent to <i>sċield</i> (“shield”) +&lrm; <i>burg</i> (“fortress”).</p><p>From a compound of the same words, but with <i>burg</i> having the sense “city” instead of “fortress.”</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shield wall</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sċield</i> (“shield”) +&lrm; <i>weall</i> (“wall”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shield maker, shieldwright</li></ol><p><i>sċīeld</i> (“shield”) +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“maker”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cliff, crag</li><li>peak, pinnacle, tower</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skelf</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skelfaz</i>, <i>&#42;skelfō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ledge</li><li>shelf</li><li>a flat surface (e.g. floor, deck, etc.)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skilfijā</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skelfu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skelfō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shellfish</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skaljafiskaz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>skelfiskr</i> → Icelandic <i>skelfiskur</i>. Equivalent to <i>sċiell</i> (“shell”) +&lrm; <i>fisċ</i> (“fish”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shell</li><li>scale (keratin pieces covering the skin of certain animals)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skallju</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skaljō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be frightened or startled; recoil in fear</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skiuhijan</i>, derived from <i>&#42;skeuh</i> (Old English <i>sċēoh</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beautiful, fair, bright</li><li>brilliant, light, shining</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skaunī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skauniz</i> (“beautiful”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ḱown-</i> (“quick, dashing, beautiful”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>skēne</i> (“beautiful”), Old Saxon <i>skōni</i> (“shiny, beautiful”), Old High German <i>scōni</i> (“handsome, brilliant, pure, lovely, good, pleasant”), Gothic <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌿𐌽𐍃</i> (skauns, “beautiful”), Middle Dutch <i>scone</i> (“beautiful, bright”), Dutch <i>schoon</i> (“beautiful”), German <i>schön</i> (“beautiful”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>sċīene</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skaunistaz</i>, superlative of <i>&#42;skauniz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>sċīene</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skaunizô</i>, comparative of <i>&#42;skauniz</i>, equivalent to <i>sċīene</i> +&lrm; <i>-ra</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to create</li><li>to shape; mould, form</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skappjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skapjaną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>skeppa</i>, Old High German <i>scaffan</i>, Old Norse <i>skapa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Sċieppend</i></li></ol><p>Composed of <i>sċieppan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Scieppend)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Creator: a god who created the universe</li></ol><p>From and identical to <i>sċieppend</i> (“a creator”), composed of <i>sċieppan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take shape</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>sċieppan</i> (“to form; shape”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shave</li><li>to shear</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skeran</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skeraną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>skera</i>, Old Saxon <i>skeran</i>, Dutch <i>scheren</i>, Old High German <i>skeran</i>, Old Norse <i>skera</i>. The Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek <i>κείρω</i> (keírō, “to cut off”), Latin <i>carō</i> (“flesh”), Lithuanian <i>ski̇̀rti</i> (“separate”), Old Irish <i>scaraim</i> (“separate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shaving</li><li>shearing</li></ol><p><i>sċieran</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sharpen</li><li>to whet</li><li>to clothe</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skarpijan</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skirpijan</i>, equivalent to <i>sċeorp</i> (“dress, apparel”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>razor</li></ol><p><i>sċieran</i> (“to shave”) +&lrm; <i>seax</i> (“knife”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>sċeort</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>sċeort</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheet (piece of linen cloth)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skautijā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skautijǭ</i>, diminutive of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skautaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divide, separate into shares</li><li>to appoint, ordain, arrange</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skiftijaną</i>, <i>&#42;skiptijaną</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;skipatjaną</i> (“to organise, put in order”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skeyb-</i> (“to separate, divide, part”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skēy-</i> (“to cut, divide, separate, part”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċīr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċield</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to protect (<i>from something</i> = <i>wiþ</i> + accusative)</li><li>to protect (<i>from something</i> = <i>wiþ</i> + dative)</li><li>to shield</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skildijaną</i>, derived from <i>&#42;skelduz</i>, whence Old English <i>sċield</i> (“shield”). <i>Sċildan</i> is the inherited form of the verb; the alternative form <i>sċieldan</i> has its <i>ie</i> leveled in from the noun.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protector</li></ol><p><i>sċildan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Sċylding</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) phalanx</li><li>(<i>military</i>) testudo</li></ol><p>From <i>sċild</i> (“shield”) +&lrm; <i>truma</i> (“troop, group of soldiers”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Sċylding</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċieldweall</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, part, remove</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skilōną</i>, <i>&#42;skiljaną</i> (“to divide, limit”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kelH-</i> (“to split, cut”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>sċilling</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>sċiell</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>money</i>) a shilling</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skillingaz</i></p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Scilling)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shine, brightness</li><li>ray or beam (of light)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skīmô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċimrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine, gleam</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skimarōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a deceptive appearance or occurrence</li><li>spirit, phantom</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skīnan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skīnaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ḱeh₁y-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shinbone</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skinōbainą</i>, equivalent to <i>sċinu</i> +&lrm; <i>bān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magic</li><li>a trick produced via magic</li></ol><p>From <i>sċin</i> (“spirit, deceptive appearance”) +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shingle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skindulā</i>, borrowed from Late Latin <i>scindula</i>, from Latin <i>scandula</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sked-</i> (“to split, scatter”), from <i>&#42;sek-</i> (“to cut”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magic, sorcery</li><li>necromancy</li><li>delusion, frenzy, rage</li><li>a phantom or apparition</li></ol><p>From <i>sċīn</i> (“deceptive appearance or occurrence, spirit”) +&lrm; <i>lāc</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċīn</i></li><li>a skin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Norse <i>skinn</i> (“animal hide”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skinþą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sken-</i> (“to split off”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shin</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skinu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skinō</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>skina</i>, Middle Dutch <i>schene</i> (Dutch <i>scheen</i>), Old High German <i>scina</i> (German <i>Schiene</i> (“rail, rack, skid, frame”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ship</li><li>alternative form of <i>sċēap</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skip</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>skip</i>, Old Saxon <i>skip</i>, Old High German <i>skif</i>, Old Norse <i>skip</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐌺𐌹𐍀</i> (skip).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Unclear. Perhaps "an oak usable for shipbuilding."</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>āc</i> (“oak”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>affliction or labour when traveling on a ship</li></ol><p>From <i>scip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>broc</i> (“affliction, disease”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>causing shipwrecks</li></ol><p>From <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;brucol</i> (“breakable”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ship wreck; that which washes ashore from shipwreck; wreckage</li></ol><p>From <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>bryċe</i> (“breaking, fragment”). Compare Faroese <i>skipbrot</i>, Dutch <i>schipbreuk</i>, and German <i>Schiffbruch</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a "ship-trumpet;" a trumpet associated with ships</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>bȳme</i> (“trumpet”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>naval power</li></ol><p><i>sċip</i> +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) causing a shipwreck</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>drincende</i> (“drinking”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rank, office, dignity, position</li><li>stipend; payment</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>sċipian</i> (“to take shape, put in order, equip”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skapjōną</i> (“to form, put in order, arrange”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailor</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skipārī</i> (“sailor”). Equivalent to <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i> (“-er”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailor</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>faran</i> (“to travel”) +&lrm; <i>-end</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailor</li></ol><p>From <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>fērend</i> (“sailor”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fleet or other naval force</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>fierd</i> (“army”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailor</li><li>pirate</li></ol><p>From <i>sċip</i> +&lrm; <i>flota</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preparation of ships</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>forþung</i> (“furtherance”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a naval force</li></ol><p>From <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>fultum</i> (“military force”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċipfierd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a naval force</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>fyrding</i> (“army, host”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voyage</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sċip</i> +&lrm; <i>færeld</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a nef or other vessel shaped like a ship</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>fæt</i> (“vessel”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shipwreck</li></ol><p>From <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>ġebroc</i> (“breaking”). Compare Dutch <i>schipbreuk</i>, German <i>Schiffbruch</i>, and Icelandic <i>skipbrot</i>, which have the same literal meaning but with a different word for "breaking."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>naval battle or war</li></ol><p>From <i>sċip</i> +&lrm; <i>ġefeoht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a going by ship</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>ġefēre</i> (“going, way”, variant of <i>ġefær</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a going by ship</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>ġefær</i> (“going, way”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>furniture of a ship</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>ġetawu</i> (“instruments, tools”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a tax to supply money to maintain a fleet</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>ġield</i> (“payment”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċiphamor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hammer used to keep time for and signal rowers</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>hamor</i> (“hammer”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>navy</li><li>naval fleet</li></ol><p><i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>here</i> (“army”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>naval, relating to a fleet</li></ol><p><i>sċiphere</i> (“navy”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shipmaster</li></ol><p><i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>hlāford</i> (“lord”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċiphlǣder</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a ship's ladder, used to go ashore from a ship</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>hlǣder</i> (“ladder”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the body of warriors carried on a ship</li></ol><p>From <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>hlæst</i> (“load”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Scipio Africanus, a Roman general</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Scīpiō</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċieppian</i></li><li>to put into order, to arrange, equip</li><li>to make shipshape, to man and ready a ship</li><li>to take ship; board a ship; embark</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>sċippan</i>, <i>sċieppan</i> (“to form; shape”).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skipōną</i> (“to arrange, equip”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skipą</i> (“ship”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>skipia</i> (“to arrange, order, ship, embark”), Old Norse <i>skipa</i> (“to arrange, set up”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small ship</li></ol><p>From <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>-incel</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailing, navigating</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>lād</i> (“course, way”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>naval</li></ol><p>From <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a naval force</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>liþ</i> (“fleet”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who goes aboard a ship</li></ol><p><i>sċip</i> +&lrm; <i>līþend</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailor</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skipamann-</i>. Equivalent to <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċieppan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċieppend</i></li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Scippend)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Scieppend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cable</li></ol><p>From <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>rāp</i> (“rope”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sailor</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>rēþra</i> (“rower”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rower</li><li>sailor</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>rōwend</i> (“rudder”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rudder, oar (of a ship)</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>rōþor</i> (“rudder”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a course or channel for ships</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>ryne</i> (“course”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bench or seat for rowers</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>setl</i> (“bench”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place for a ship</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>steall</i> (“place”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steersman, pilot (of a ship)</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>stēora</i> (“steersman”). Compare Icelandic <i>skipstjóri</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the North Star (Polaris)</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>steorra</i> (“star”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċipstēora</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċipteora</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċipteora</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċipteora</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pitch</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>teoru</i> (“tar”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>money paid for passage on a ship</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>toll</i> (“toll, duty”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a servant whose work related to ships</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>wealh</i> (“Celt, foreigner, slave”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who has charge of a ship</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>weard</i> (“guard”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a ship's crew</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>werod</i> (“troop”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having the fashion or form of a ship</li></ol><p><i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>-wīse</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is cast up from a shipwreck (attested only in Latin texts)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>wræc</i> (“that which is driven; exile”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shipbuilder, shipwright</li></ol><p>From <i>sċip</i> (“ship”) +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“worker, builder”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>office (status of an official)</li><li>district (under an official or governor)</li><li>administrative region, shire (consisting of a number of hundreds or wapentakes, ruled jointly by an alderman and a sheriff)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bright, shining</li><li>clear, pure</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skīru</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skīraz</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skīriz</i> (“pure, bright”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>skire</i>, Old Saxon <i>skīr</i>, Dutch <i>schier</i> (“white, grey”), German <i>schier</i> (“pure”)), Old Norse <i>skírr</i> (Swedish <i>skir</i>), Gothic <i>𐍃𐌺𐌴𐌹𐍂𐍃</i> (skeirs) Related to <i>scinan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sherborne (place in Dorset, England)</li></ol><p>From <i>sċīr</i> (“bright, clear”) +&lrm; <i>burna</i> (“stream”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bright purple</li></ol><p>From <i>sċīr</i> (“bright”) +&lrm; <i>basu</i> (“purple”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bishop responsible for a shire or a diocese</li></ol><p>From <i>sċīr</i> (“shire”) +&lrm; <i>bisċop</i> (“bishop”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Sċīraburna</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Sċīraburna</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheriff, chief officer of a shire</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a ruler of a shire</li><li>an inhabitant of a shire</li><li>a sheriff</li></ol><p>From <i>sċīr</i> (“shire”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċierpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċierseax</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>sċīr</i> (“shire, district”) and <i>weald</i> (“power, authority”).</p><p>From <i>sċīr</i> (“bright, shining”) and <i>weald</i> (“power, authority”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defecate, shit</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skītan</i> (“to shit, defecate, excrete”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>skītan</i>, Old Frisian <i>skīta</i>, Old High German <i>skīzan</i>, Old Norse <i>skíta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skitil</i>, diminutive of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skiti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skitiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loose stool, diarrhoea</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skittjā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Scyttisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Scythia (a geographic region encompassing the Pontic-Caspian steppe in Eastern Europe and Central Asia, inhabited by nomadic Scythians from at least the 11th century <small>BCE</small> to the 2nd century <small>CE</small>)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Scythia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Σκυθία</i> (Skuthía).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Sciþþia</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċōġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċofl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċucca</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shovel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skuflō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to put shoes on someone or something, (<i>reflexive</i>) to put shoes on</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to give someone shoes</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skōhōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skōhōną</i>, derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skōhaz</i> (“shoe”). Cognate with Old High German <i>scuohōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċōġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoe</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skōh</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>skōch</i>, Old Saxon <i>skōh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoemaker, cobbler</li></ol><p>From <i>sċōh</i> (“shoe”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoemaker, cobbler</li></ol><p>From <i>sċōh</i> (“shoe”) +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“worker, maker”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>school</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skōlu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċeolde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a scholar; learner</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>scholāris</i>, analysed to be equivalent with <i>scōl</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>student</li><li>scholar</li></ol><p>From <i>scōl</i> (“school”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a troop or band of people</li><li>host, division of an army</li><li>multitude</li><li>(<i>of fish</i>) shoal, school</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skolu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skulō</i> (“crowd”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kelH-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċamlēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċamlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċandlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>Sċedenīġ</i>: Scania (a former province, historical region, and peninsula in Northern Europe in modern Sweden, occupying the southern tip of the Scandinavian Peninsula; belonging to Denmark in Old English times)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Norse <i>Skáney</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poet</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skop</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skupą</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>skopf</i> (“poet”), Old Norse <i>skop</i> (“mocking”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poetic diction</li></ol><p>From <i>sċop</i> (“poet”) +&lrm; <i>ġereord</i> (“speech”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poetic</li></ol><p>From <i>sċop</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a shop; a booth or shed used for work or trade</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skupp-</i>, <i>&#42;skup-</i> (“barn, shed”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skub-</i>, <i>&#42;skup-</i> (“to bend, bow, curve, vault”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shoreham (a village and civil parish in Sevenoaks district, Kent, England)</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;sċora</i> (“shore”) +&lrm; <i>hām</i> (“home”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to refuse, reject</li><li>to project, to jut out</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skur-</i>, related to <i>&#42;skeraną</i> (“to cut”). Cognate with Old High German <i>scurgan</i> (“to thrust, impel, propel”), Old Saxon <i>scurgan</i> (“to avert, turn away, expel”).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skar-</i>, <i>&#42;skaraz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to cut”). Cognate with Old High German <i>scorrēn</i> (“to project”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċeorp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) scorpion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>scorpiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>short</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skurt</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skurtaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>scurz</i>, Old Norse <i>skortr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to become short, shorten</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fall short, fail</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make short, shorten</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skurtōn</i> (“to make short”) and Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skurtēn</i> (“to be short”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of time</i>) short, not lasting</li></ol><p>From <i>sċort</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of time</i>) shortly, before long, soon</li><li>(<i>of speech, narrative, etc.</i>) shortly, briefly, compendiously</li></ol><p>From <i>sċort</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shortness</li><li>a brief account, an epitome</li></ol><p>From <i>sċort</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>notch, mark, tally</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>skor</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skurō</i> (“incision; tear; rift”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shot, act of shooting</li><li>missile, shot</li><li>darting, rapid movement</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skutą</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>skot</i>, Old Saxon <i>sīlscot</i>, Old High German <i>scoz</i> (German <i>Schoß</i>), Old Norse <i>skot</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exempt from imposts; scot-free</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>skot</i> (“payment”) + Old English <i>frēo</i> (“free”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to shoot someone with a weapon</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to shoot, hurl</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shoot</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to move rapidly, dart</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skotōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skutōną</i>, equivalent to <i>sċot</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>land of the Scots in Hibernia, i.e. Ireland (a large island of Northern Europe)</li><li>land of the Scots in Britain, i.e. Scotland (a medieval kingdom in Northern Europe)</li></ol><p><i>Sċott</i> (“Scot”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A Scot</li><li>An Irishman</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Scōtī</i>, or from the same source.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scotland</li><li>Ireland</li></ol><p>Literally "land of the Irish."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Scots (the Scottish people of northern Britain)</li><li>Scotland</li><li>the Irish (the Gaelic-speaking people of Ireland and Scotland)</li><li>Ireland</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Scōtī</i>, or from the same source.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Scyttisċ</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Scyttisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>christening</li><li>a missile; something that is shot or fired</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>sċōtian</i> (“to shoot”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scrape</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skrapōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a piece cut off; a paring; shred</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skraudō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shred, cut up, cut off, prune</li></ol><p>A weak verb formed on the same base as the strong Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skraudan</i>, from an extension of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to cut”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċræf</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skrab</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skrabą</i> (“cliff; cave”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to cut”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shrew</li></ol><p>Of Germanic origin, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skrawwaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to shorten, cut”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċrȳdan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trip up, ensnare, place a stumbling block in front of</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skrankijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cry out, proclaim</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skrīan</i> (“to cry out, proclaim”). Compare Old High German <i>scrīan</i> (“to shout, proclaim”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scrape</li><li>to scratch</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skrepan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shrike (bird of prey)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skrīkijaną</i> (“to cry out”), in reference to the shrike's call</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decree, appoint</li><li>to pass judgement, ordain, prescribe</li><li>to prescribe penance or absolution (<i>of a priest</i>)</li></ol><p>From West Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skrībaną</i>, a late borrowing from Latin <i>scrībō</i> (“write”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>skrīva</i> (West Frisian <i>skriuwe</i>), Old Saxon <i>skrīvan</i>, <i>skrīƀan</i> (Low German <i>schrieven</i>), Old Dutch <i>scrīvan</i> (Dutch <i>schrijven</i>), Old High German <i>scrīban</i> (German <i>schreiben</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>what is prescribed as punishment; penalty</li><li>penance, shrift</li><li>one who passes sentence; judge</li><li>a confessor (in Christianity, one who hears confession)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>scrīptum</i>. Cognate with German <i>Schrift</i> (“font, writing, script”), Dutch <i>schrift</i> (“script, notebook, scripture”), Swedish <i>skrift</i> (“text, written work, book, report, script”) and Icelandic <i>skrift</i> (“writing”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confession</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċrēon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shrink, draw up, contract</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skrimman</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skrimbaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reliquary</li><li>the Ark of the Covenant</li><li>a cage used to hold criminals</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Medieval Latin <i>scrīnium</i> (“reliquary”). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to turn, bend”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shrink</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skrinkwaną</i> (“shrink”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>effeminate or castrated man</li><li>hermaphrodite</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>sċieran</i> (“to shear; to shave”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skeran</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skeraną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, glide, wander</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skrīþan</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>skrīthan</i>, Middle Dutch <i>schriden</i> (Dutch <i>schrijden</i>), Old High German <i>skrītan</i> (German <i>schreiten</i>), Old Norse <i>skríða</i> (Swedish <i>skrida</i>). The Indo-European root also gave Latin <i>crīsāre</i> and Middle Irish <i>crith</i> (Breton <i>skrija</i> (“tremble with fear”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shrewsbury</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;sċrob</i> (“shrub, scrub”) +&lrm; <i>burh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shropshire</li></ol><p>From <i>Sċrobbesburh</i> +&lrm; <i>sċīr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garment</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skrūdą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċrūtnian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to examine carefully; scrutinise; investigate; consider</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skrudōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skrudōną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>scrodōn</i>, <i>scrutōn</i> (“to examine; scrutinise”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an examination, investigation, inquiry, search</li></ol><p>Likely from Old High German <i>scrodon</i>, <i>scruton</i> (“inquiry, investigation”) (+ <i>-ung</i>), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skrūdą</i>, said to have come from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skrew-</i>. Related to Old English <i>sċrūtnian</i> (“to examine, scrutinize, consider, investigate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>underbrush</li></ol><p>Fom Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skrubbiju</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skrubbijō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kerb-</i> (“to be sharp”), an extended form of <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to cut”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clothe, to provide with clothes</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skrūdijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cave, cavern</li><li>miserable dwelling: den, hell</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skrabą</i> (“cave, cavern, pit”), possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to cut off”), and thereby cognate with Latin <i>scrobis</i> (“ditch, trench”), and related to Old English <i>scrapian</i> (“to scrape”), screpan (“to scratch”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adulteress, harlot, prostitute</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skrattuz</i> (“goblin”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shadow</li><li>shade; darkness</li><li>protection</li><li>a shady person (<i>possibly</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skūwō</i> (“shadow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an evil spirit; demon; devil</li></ol><p>Unknown. Perhaps from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skuh-</i>, from <i>&#42;skiuhijaną</i> ("to frighten"&#59; see <i>&#42;skeuhaz</i> (“frightened”)) +&lrm; <i>-ga</i> or +&lrm; <i>-ca</i>. Cognate with German <i>Scheuche</i> (“scarecrow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push, shove</li><li>to launch (a ship)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skeuban</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skeubaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skewbʰ-</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>skūva</i> (West Frisian <i>skowe</i>), Middle Low German <i>schūven</i> (Low German <i>schuven</i>), Middle Dutch <i>scūven</i> (Dutch <i>schuiven</i>), Old High German <i>skioban</i>, <i>sciopan</i> (German <i>schieben</i>), Old Norse <i>skúfa</i> (Faroese <i>skúgva</i>, Danish <i>skubbe</i>, Swedish <i>skuffa</i>), Gothic <i>𐌰𐍆𐍃𐌺𐌹𐌿𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (afskiuban).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>auxiliary</i>) should</li><li>(<i>auxiliary</i>) must</li><li>(<i>auxiliary</i>) to have to</li><li>to be supposed to do something (used like "supposedly" or "they say...")</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to owe</li><li>(<i>auxiliary</i>) will (indicating the future)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skulan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skulaną</i> (“to owe”). Compare this sense of Swedish <i>skola</i> for sense&nbsp;4.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>skela</i>, Old Saxon <i>skulan</i>, Old High German <i>sculan</i>, Old Norse <i>skulu</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐌺𐌿𐌻𐌰𐌽</i> (skulan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċuldra</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoulder</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skuldru</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċuldra</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>Skúli</i>, originally a byname connected to Old Norse <i>skýla</i> meaning "to protect".</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide or flee from; to avoid</li><li>to be scared of</li><li>to hate; abhor; detest</li></ol><p>Uncertain; despite the lack of secure Germanic cognates (though compare <i>scunner</i>, which may derive from an Old Norse <i>&#42;skuna</i>), possibly derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kewH-</i> (“to cover, hide”), as either a nasal-present <i>&#42;skunéHti</i> identical to Sanskrit <i>स्कुनाति</i> (skunāti, “to cover”) or a later inner-Germanic formation, through either Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skunjaną</i>, Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skunnjan</i> (if the variant <i>sċynian</i> shows that this verb was formerly a weak <i>j</i>-present) or <i>&#42;skunaną</i>/<i>&#42;skunōną</i>, <i>&#42;skunēn</i>/<i>&#42;skunōn</i> (if the variant <i>sċynian</i> exhibits a development /ʃu/ &gt; /ʃy/ as potentially seen in Northumbrian Old English <i>sćylun</i> (“we must”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an abomination</li></ol><p><i>sċunian</i> (“to hate, abhor”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shower</li></ol><p>From the merger of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skūrō</i> and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skūraz</i> (“rainshower”). Cognate with Old High German <i>scūr</i>, Old Norse <i>skúr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kew-(e)ro-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) rainbow</li></ol><p><i>sċūr</i> +&lrm; <i>boga</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scurf</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a dish (glosses Latin <i>catinus</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċūa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prompt, urge</li><li>to persuade, suggest</li><li>to sky, be afraid, flee</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skiuhijan</i> (“to put to flight, startle, frighten”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skeuhaz</i> (“shy”).</p><p>Cognate with Old English <i>sċyhhan</i>, Old High German <i>&#42;scūhen</i> attested in <i>scūhenti</i>. Compare Middle Low German <i>schü̂wen</i>, <i>schûwen</i>, <i>schûen</i> (“to fear, shun, avoid”), Middle High German <i>schiuhen</i>, <i>schiuwen</i>, <i>schūen</i>, <i>schūhen</i> (“to fear, scare, frighten”).</p><p>Possibly related to Old English <i>sċyndan</i> from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skundijaną</i>. It may be backformed in the present tense from the alternative form scȳhtan (<i>scȳhte</i> in the preterite), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skūhtijaną</i>, itself perhaps from the from an unattested Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skūhtiz</i> (see tyht and tyhtan for a similar pattern).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċiftan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċȳan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shy away from, shun</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skuhhjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skuhjaną</i>, variant of <i>&#42;skuhwijaną</i> (“to frighten”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skuhuz</i>, variant of <i>&#42;skeuhaz</i> (“shy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>sċyan</i> (“to prompt, urge”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċiell</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċield</i></li><li>guilt, sin</li><li>debt, obligation, liability, due</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>sċield</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skuldi</i> (“guilt”).</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>schuld</i>, Old Saxon <i>skuld</i> (“crime, due”), Old High German <i>sculd</i> (German <i>Schuld</i>), Old Norse <i>skuld</i>. Related to shall, should.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Scyld)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scyld, the legendary ancestor of the Danish royal lineage known as the Scyldings.</li></ol><p><i>sċield</i> (“shield”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>Skjǫldr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accuse</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>sċyld</i> (“guilt”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċyldigian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bailiff</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>guilty <i>(+ genitive or instrumental of a crime or offense)</i></li><li>sinful</li><li>in debt</li><li>liable <i>(+ genitive or instrumental for a crime or offense) (+ genitive or instrumental to forfeit something) (+ genitive or instrumental to receive a punishment)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skuldīg</i>, equivalent to <i>sċyld</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>skeldich</i>, Old Saxon <i>skuldig</i>, Old High German <i>sculdīg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to commit a fault; sin</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skuldigēn</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>skuldigēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guiltyness, guilt</li></ol><p>From <i>sċyldiġ</i> (“guilty”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) ‘descendant of Scyld’; a Dane</li></ol><p>From <i>Sċyld</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>Skjǫldungr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>guiltless</li></ol><p>From <i>scyld</i> +&lrm; <i>-leas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bailiff</li></ol><p>From <i>scyld</i> (“guilt”) +&lrm; <i>lǣta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċildtruma</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>sċyld</i> (“guilt, debt”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skuldī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skuldį̄</i>, equivalent to <i>sċyld</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Sċylding</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) warrior</li></ol><p>From <i>sċyld</i> +&lrm; <i>wiga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fine for a crime of violence</li></ol><p><i>sċyld</i> +&lrm; <i>wīte</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) punishing guilt (used to describe hell)</li></ol><p><i>sċyld</i> (“guilt”) +&lrm; <i>wrecende</i> (present participle of <i>wrecan</i> (“to punish”))</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċielf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċielfe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, remove</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċiell</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċilling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċielf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċulon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċimrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċincræft</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to hurry; hasten; rush</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cause to hurry; hasten; rush</li><li>to urge; incite; exhort</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skundijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skundijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċīene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċunian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċunian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċinn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>sċȳne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċip</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cattleshed, stall for cattle</li></ol><p>From the same root as German <i>Schuppen</i> (“shed”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċiplād</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċieppan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skapjaną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>skeppa</i>, Old High German <i>scaffan</i>, Old Norse <i>skapa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċieppend</i></li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Scyppend)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Scieppend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċīr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċieran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċierpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċierseax</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a short garment; skirt; kirtle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skurtijā</i> (“a short garment; apron”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shooting</li><li>shot</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skuti</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forefinger</li></ol><p>From <i>sċyte</i> (“shooting”) +&lrm; <i>finger</i> (“finger”). Compare Old Frisian <i>skotfinger</i> (“forefinger”), Middle Low German <i>schotvinger</i> (“forefinger”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oblique, inclined</li><li>bent in order to shoot downward</li></ol><p>From <i>sċyte</i> (“shooting”) +&lrm; <i>-heald</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċyttel</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>sċitel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) forefinger</li></ol><p>From <i>sċytel</i> (“dart, arrow”) +&lrm; <i>finger</i>. Compare Old Frisian <i>skotfinger</i> (“forefinger”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shooter, archer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skuttjō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skutjô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shut</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skuttjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skutjaną</i>, from <i>&#42;skuttą</i>, <i>&#42;skuttjō</i> (“bar, bolt, shed”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kewd-</i> (“to drive, fall upon, rush”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bolt, dart</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Scottish (of the Scottish people of northern Britain)</li><li>Irish (of the Gaelic-speaking people of Ireland and Scotland)</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a Scot or Irish person</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scots Gaelic</li><li>Irish</li></ol><p><i>Scott</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Scyttisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Sċeaftesburg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Anglian form of <i>sċeal</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċēap</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sċeatt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>the</li></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>that</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>that</li><li>the one / that one</li><li><i>(relative)</i> that, who, what, which</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Anglian</i>) alternative form of <i>sēo</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;siz</i>, replacing earlier <i>*sā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a giving</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>seald</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ointment, salve</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;salbu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to anoint, apply a salve</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;salbōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;salbōną</i>, equivalent to <i>sealf</i> (“salve”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>willow, sallow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;salh</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;salhô</i> (“willow”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sl̥H-ik-</i> (“willow”). Cognate with Old High German <i>salaha</i> (“willow”), Old Norse <i>selja</i> (“willow”). More at sallow.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>psealm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>couch</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psalmist</li></ol><p>From <i>sealm</i> (“psalm”) +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“wright”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common salt (sodium chloride)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>salt, salty, salted</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;salt</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saltą</i> (“salt”), <i>&#42;saltaz</i> (“salty”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seh₂l-</i>.</p><p>Akin to Old Frisian <i>salt</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;salt</i>, Old Saxon <i>salt</i>, Old High German <i>salz</i>, Old Norse <i>salt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to salt</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;saltan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saltaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Uncertain&#59; apparently 'a brook that runs from salt works'.</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sealt</i> (“salt”) +&lrm; <i>brōc</i> (“brook”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>salt; salted; salty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saltanaz</i> (“salted”), past participle of <i>&#42;saltaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salt worker</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>sealt</i> (“salt”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saltworks, saltern</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sealt</i> +&lrm; <i>-ern</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a vessel or cellar for salt</li></ol><p>From <i>sealt</i> (“salt”) +&lrm; <i>fæt</i> (“vessel”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the name of a bird</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a salthouse</li></ol><p>From <i>sealt</i> (“salt”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dance</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;saltōn</i>, from Latin <i>saltō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saltness</li></ol><p>From <i>sealt</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a salt-pit or salty spring</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sealt</i> (“salt”) +&lrm; <i>sēaþ</i> (“pit, spring”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rock salt</li><li>a stone or pillar formed from salt</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;saltastain</i>, equivalent to <i>sealt</i> (“salt”) +&lrm; <i>stān</i> (“stone”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salt-wave, sea-wave</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sealt</i> +&lrm; <i>ȳþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a saltern, a building where salt is stored</li></ol><p>From <i>sealt</i> (“salt”) +&lrm; <i>ærn</i> (“buildiing”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>salu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seam</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;saum</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saumaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tailor</li></ol><p>From <i>sēam</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seamstress, seamster</li></ol><p>From <i>sēam</i> +&lrm; <i>-estre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sīere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>sēaraþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wither</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sauʀēn</i>. Equivalent to <i>sēar</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the sixth month of the year according to the Anglo-Saxon calendar</li><li>June</li></ol><p>From <i>sēar</i> +&lrm; <i>mōnaþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>searu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Salisbury (a city in Wiltshire, England)</li></ol><p><i>searu</i> (“armor”) +&lrm; <i>burg</i> (“fort”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Searoburg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>machine</li><li>device, contrivance</li><li>deceit, stratagem</li><li>armor, war-gear, trappings</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;saru</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sarwą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;serw-</i> (“to guard”) or <i>&#42;ser-</i> (“to bind together”). Cognate with Gothic <i>𐍃𐌰𐍂𐍅𐌰</i> (sarwa, “weaponry, armour”), Old High German <i>saro</i> (“war equipment”), Old Norse <i>sörvi</i> (“sword”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) armour-haver, armed warrior</li></ol><p><i>searu</i> (“armour”) +&lrm; <i>hæbbende</i> (“haver, wearer”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>setl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>setl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>juice, sap</li><li>moisture</li><li>bodily fluid</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sauw</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>sou</i> (“juice”), Old Norse <i>sǫggr</i> (“moist”) (Icelandic <i>söggur</i>), dialectal Swedish <i>sögg</i>, <i>sygg</i> (“damp, humid”). Probably related to English <i>sog</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knife</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sahs</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sahsą</i>. Compare Old English <i>sagu</i>, <i>seċġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Saxon</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sahs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Saxons</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Sahsan</i>. See also seax.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>seax</i> (“knife”) +&lrm; <i>burg</i> (“city”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Seaxburg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Seaxan</i> (“the Saxons”)</li></ol><p>The older form is <i>Seaxan</i>, which is also quite common. The i-stem plural <i>Seaxe</i> formed sometime after i-umlaut had gone to completion, by analogy with other names of peoples such as <i>Engle</i> (“the Angles”), <i>Mierċe</i> (“the Mercians”), and <i>Norþanhymbre</i> (“the Northumbrians”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saxony (a historical region and former duchy in north-central Germany)</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) England</li></ol><p><i>Seaxa</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A descendant of Seaxnēat</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;Seaxnēat</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Seaxwulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Seaxwulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From By surface analysis, <i>seax</i> (“dagger”) +&lrm; <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pit; hole</li><li>well</li><li>lake; reservoir</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sauþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sauþaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>sāth</i>, Old Norse <i>seyðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sēoc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look for, seek</li><li>to visit</li><li>to attack (especially by an organized group or a military force)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sōkijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sōkijaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seh₂g-</i> (“track down, seek out”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>sēka</i> (West Frisian <i>sykje</i>), Old Saxon <i>sōkian</i> (Low German <i>sooken</i>), Dutch <i>zoeken</i>, Old High German <i>suohhen</i> (German <i>suchen</i>), Old Norse <i>sǿkja</i> (Swedish <i>söka</i>), Gothic <i>𐍃𐍉𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (sōkjan); and with Latin <i>sagiō</i> (“sense by smell”), Albanian <i>shikoj</i> (“to see, observe, look for”), Old Irish <i>saigid</i> (“seek”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sēċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>sēċeþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) man, warrior, hero</li><li>abbreviation of <i>gārseċġ</i></li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) sword</li><li>sedge</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sagi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sagjaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sekʷ-</i> (“follower, companion”).</p><p>Germanic cognates include Old Frisian <i>siā</i>, Old Saxon <i>segg</i>, Old Norse <i>seggr</i> (Norwegian <i>segg</i>). The Indo-European root is also the source of Latin <i>socius</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sagjō</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sagi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sagjaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sak-</i> (“marsh plant”) (probably related to Etymology 2). Cognate with Middle Low German <i>segge</i>, Dutch <i>zegge</i>, Russian <i>осо́ка</i> (osóka).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say, tell</li><li>to recite, pronounce</li><li>to mention, talk about</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;saggjan</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>sedza</i>, Old Saxon <i>seggian</i>, Old Dutch <i>seggen</i>, Old High German <i>sagēn</i>, Old Norse <i>segja</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sekʷ-</i> (“to say”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seċġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seċġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seċġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chives</li></ol><p>From <i>seċġ</i> (“sedge”) +&lrm; <i>lēac</i> (“plant in the genus Allium”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quail</li><li>corncrake</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seċġum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sǣdian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) spirit, heart, mind</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sebō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sebô</i> (“mind, sense”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sep-</i> (“to try, research”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>sevo</i>, Old Norse <i>sefi</i>, Latin <i>sapiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sȳfre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>softe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soft, luxurious</li><li>gentle, not harsh</li><li>easy, pleasant</li><li>quiet, undisturbed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;samft(ī)</i> (“at the same height, level, flat, smooth, not rough”) (compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sōmiz</i> (“agreeable, fitting”)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sóm-tu-</i>, possibly from <i>&#42;sem-</i> (“one, whole”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>softness</li></ol><p><i>sēfte</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seġl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seġlġyrd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>seġn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seċġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sail</li><li>alternative form of <i>siġel</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;segl</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;seglą</i>. Related to Old Frisian <i>seil</i>, Old Saxon <i>segal</i>, Old Dutch <i>segil</i>, Old High German <i>segal</i>, Old Norse <i>segl</i>.</p><p>See <i>siġel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>siġlan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seġlġyrd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailyard</li></ol><p>From the fusion of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;seglagaʀdī</i> and <i>&#42;seglagaʀd</i>; equivalent to <i>seġl</i> +&lrm; <i>ġierd</i> and <i>seġl</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeard</i> respectively. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>segalgard</i>, <i>segelgerd</i>, Old Dutch <i>segilgerda</i>, Old High German <i>segalgerta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seġlġyrd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sail</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;seglōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailing (travel by ship or boat)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;siglingu</i>, equivalent to <i>seġlan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>segling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sign; mark; token</li><li>(<i>military</i>) a banner; standard; ensign</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>signum</i> (“sign”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>standard-bearer</li></ol><p>From <i>seġn</i> (“sign”) +&lrm; <i>-bora</i> (“bearer”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mark the sign of the cross; bless, consecrate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;segnōną</i>, from Latin <i>signō</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>zegenen</i> and German <i>segnen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blessing, consecration</li></ol><p>From <i>seġnian</i> (“to bless, consecrate”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zoar, an ancient city in the Jordan valley</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Biblical Hebrew <i>צֹעַר</i> (tso`ar, “insignificance, smallness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>settlement; arrangement; agreement; truce</li><li>friendship; peace</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sahtiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle (<i>a dispute</i>), bring to an agreement</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sahtilōną</i>, equivalent to <i>seht</i> +&lrm; <i>-lian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sæl</i> (“room, great hall, castle”)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>good, noble</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sal</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;salą</i>, from Indo-European. Cognate with Old High German <i>sal</i>, German <i>Saal</i> (“hall, large room”), Old Saxon <i>sal</i>, Dutch <i>zaal</i>. Compare <i>sele</i>, from a Germanic variant stem.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sāliz</i>. See <i>sǣliġ</i> (“blessed, fortunate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seld-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>setl</i> (“seat, throne”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>rare, infrequent; unusual; special</li></ol><p>From <i>seld</i>. More at <i>seldan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not often: seldom, rarely</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;seldanā</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>seltan</i>, Old Norse <i>sjaldan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>weird, strange</li></ol><p><i>seld-</i> (“seldom”) +&lrm; <i>cūþ</i> (“known”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rare, infrequent</li><li>few</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;seldanaz</i> (“rare, strange”). Akin to Old Frisian <i>selden</i>, Old High German <i>seltan</i>, Old Norse <i>sjaldan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sellīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>seldan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>seldan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seldsīene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seldom seen, uncommon, unfamiliar</li></ol><p><i>seld-</i> +&lrm; <i>-sīene</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seldan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great hall, house, dwelling, prison</li><li>tabernacle, gesele</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sali</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saliz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sel-</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>seli</i>, Old High German <i>sali</i>, Old Norse <i>salr</i> (Swedish <i>sal</i>), Lombardic <i>sala</i>; and with Old Church Slavonic <i>село</i> (selo), Russian <i>село́</i> (seló). There was also a Germanic variant <i>*saloz-</i>, Old English <i>sæl</i> (“great hall, (large) house, castle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>sele</i> (“hall”) +&lrm; <i>burg</i> (“city”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) hall-joy</li></ol><p>From <i>sele</i> (“hall, house”) +&lrm; <i>drēam</i> (“joy, mirth”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hall-sad</li></ol><p>From <i>sele</i> (“great hall, house”) +&lrm; <i>drēoriġ</i> (“sad”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tradition</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) hall-counsellor</li></ol><p><i>sele</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣdende</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) retainer, hall-man</li></ol><p>From <i>sele</i> (“hall”) +&lrm; <i>seċġ</i> (“man”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>sēl</i></li><li>(<i>suppletive</i>) superlative degree of <i>gōd</i>: best</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sēlistaz</i>, superlative of <i>&#42;sēliz</i> (“good, happy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>sele</i> (“hall”) + <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sherbourne (a village in England)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>sealh</i> (“willow”) +&lrm; <i>wudu</i> (“wood”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>sele</i> (“hall”) +&lrm; <i>þrȳþ</i> (“might”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>self; oneself, personally</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;selbaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>self</li></ol><p>From pronoun <i>self</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>someone who commits suicide</li></ol><p>From <i>self-</i> +&lrm; <i>bana</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>someone who commits suicide</li></ol><p><i>self-</i> +&lrm; <i>cwalu</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suicide</li></ol><p>From <i>self-</i> +&lrm; <i>cwalu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of one's own accord, voluntary, spontaneous</li></ol><p>From <i>self</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-love, self-complacency, self-satisfaction, arrogance</li></ol><p><i>self</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sylfmyrþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>someone who destroys himself; a suicide</li></ol><p>From <i>self-</i> +&lrm; <i>myrþere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>voluntary</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;selbawiljaz</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐍃𐌹𐌻𐌱𐌰𐍅𐌹𐌻𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (silbawiljis). Equivalent to <i>self-</i> +&lrm; <i>-wille</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cannibal</li></ol><p><i>self</i> (“self”) +&lrm; <i>ǣta</i> (“eater”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the name of a plant, possibly wild oats</li></ol><p>From <i>self</i> (“same”) +&lrm; <i>ǣte</i> (“oat”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>sēl</i></li><li>comparative degree of <i>gōd</i>: better</li></ol><p>An assimilated form of <i>sēlra</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give</li><li>to give up for money; betray</li><li>to sell</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saljaną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>sella</i>, Old Saxon <i>sellian</i>, Old High German <i>sellen</i>, Old Norse <i>selja</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐌰𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (saljan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a giver</li><li>a betrayer</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sellan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>being given, givable</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sellend</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strange, fantastic, marvellous, extraordinary</li><li>excellent, wonderful, marvellous</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;seldalīkaz</i>, equivalent to <i>seld</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐍃𐌹𐌻𐌳𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (sildaleiks).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sealma</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sēlest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>sēl</i></li><li>comparative degree of <i>gōd</i>: better</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sēlizô</i>, comparative of <i>&#42;sēliz</i> (“good, happy”). See <i>sēlla</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sieltan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with accusative of person</i>) to bring to agreement, satisfy</li><li>(<i>with accusative of thing</i>) to settle a dispute</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to arbitrate, bring about agreement</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sōmijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sōmijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>sœma</i> (whence English <i>seem</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seomian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>samnunga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the senate of Rome</li><li>senator, the senators of Rome as a body</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>senātus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) [<i>with</i> accusative] to sink, to make sink, to cause to sink</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sankwijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>senġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sandijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sandijaną</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>senda</i>, Old Saxon <i>sendian</i>, Dutch <i>zenden</i>, Old High German <i>sendan</i> (German <i>senden</i>), Old Norse <i>senda</i> (Swedish <i>sända</i>), Gothic <i>𐍃𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (sandjan). The Indo-European root is also the source of Welsh <i>hynt</i> (“path”), Lithuanian <i>siusti</i> (“send”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>sendaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mustard</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;senap</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to singe, scorch, burn</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sangijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seġnian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>synn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>senep</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seonoþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pupil (of the eye)</li><li>apple of the eye</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sehwā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sehwǭ</i> (“pupil”). Akin to Old High German <i>seha</i> (“pupil”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sick</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;seuk</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>sioh</i>, Old Norse <i>sjúkr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sickness, disease</li></ol><p>From <i>sēoc</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sēocnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sēocnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sēocnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a moneybag; purse; pouch</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;seud</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;seudaz</i> (“purse; bag; pouch”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>sjóðr</i> (“purse”), Middle High German <i>siuwet</i>, <i>siut</i> (“seam; stitches; suture”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sidu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sefa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seofon</i> (“seven”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>sēofodon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Old English</i>) a week</li></ol><p>From <i>seofon</i> (“seven”) +&lrm; <i>niht</i> (“night”). Appears in the Peterborough Chronicle.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sigh, lament, complain of, bemoan</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;seufōn</i>. Related to Dutch <i>zucht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seofon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seven</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sebun</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>siƀun</i>, Old High German <i>sibun</i>, Old Norse <i>sjau</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐌹𐌱𐌿𐌽</i> (sibun). Extra-Germanic cognates include Latin <i>septem</i>, Ancient Greek <i>ἑπτά</i> (heptá), Old Church Slavonic <i>сєдмь</i> (sedmĭ), Sanskrit <i>सप्त</i> (saptá), Old Irish <i>secht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-seven</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-seventh</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-seven</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-seventh</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-seven</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-seventh</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-seven</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-seventh</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventy-seven</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventy-seventh</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seofon and hundseofontiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-seven</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-seventh</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-seven</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-seventh</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-seven</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-seventh</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seven hundred</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sevenfold</li></ol><p>From <i>seofon</i> +&lrm; <i>-feald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seven hundred year(s) old</li></ol><p><i>seofon hund</i> +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seven day(s) old</li><li>seven days long; seven-day</li></ol><p>Formed as <i>seofon</i> +&lrm; <i>-nihte</i>. Compare the English <i>sevennight</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Pleiades</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sebunstirnī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sebunstirniją</i>, a neuter collective meaning literally 'seven stars'. Cognate with Old High German <i>sibunstirni</i>, Old Norse <i>sjaustirni</i>. Compare <i>sinnihte</i>, which is formed similarly.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seofontīene</i> (“seventeen”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventeenth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seventeenth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sebundô tehundô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventeen</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sebuntehun</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>sibunzehan</i>, Old Norse <i>sjaután</i>, Gothic <i>&#42;𐍃𐌹𐌱𐌿𐌽𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿𐌽</i> (&#42;sibuntaihun).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seventeen year(s) old</li></ol><p><i>seofontīene</i> +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hundseofontiġ</i> (“seventy”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seven year(s) old</li><li>lasting seven years</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sebunwintruz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>sjauvetr</i>. Equivalent to <i>seofon</i> +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seofoþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventh</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seventh</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sebunþō</i>, alternative form of <i>&#42;sebundō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sebundô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seofon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strainer</li></ol><p>See the verb <i>seon</i> (“to filter, strain”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pipe through which a small stream is directed; drain</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sihtrā</i> (“drain; sewer”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silk</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;seluk</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) silk</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>seolc</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silkworm</li></ol><p>From <i>seolc</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Selsey (a town in Chichester district, West Sussex, England).</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sēoles</i> (genitive of <i>seolh</i> (“seal”)) +&lrm; <i>īeġ</i> (“island”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Sēolesīeġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Anglian</i>) self</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;selbaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>self-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>selfcwalu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seolfor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silver</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;silubr</i>, whence also Old High German <i>silbar</i>, Old Norse <i>silfr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silversmith</li></ol><p>from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;silubrasmiþaz</i> (“silversmith”). By surface analysis, <i>seolfor</i> +&lrm; <i>smiþ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) silver</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;silubrīnaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>seolfor</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seal (animal)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;selhaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;kenning) the ocean (literally 'seal-bath')</li></ol><p>From <i>seolh</i> (“seal”) +&lrm; <i>bæþ</i> (“bath”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(plural only,&#32;poetic) waves where the seal swims</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>seolh</i> +&lrm; <i>ȳþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seolc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seolfor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;silōþu</i>, from a verb derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;silāną</i> (“to be still, be silent”) +&lrm; <i>-oþ</i>; or perhaps from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;silōþuz</i>. If so, then related to Old Norse <i>sil</i> (“slowly or calmly flowing water between two falls in a stream”), Gothic <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰𐍃𐌹𐌻𐌰𐌽</i> (anasilan, “to cease, stop, fall silent”), Old English <i>sālnes</i> (“silence”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seolc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seolcen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie heavily, lurk, stand close</li><li>to rise, undulate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;semōną</i> (“to linger”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sēy-</i> (“to linger”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see, look</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to strain, filter</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to run as a sore, ooze, trickle, drop, drip</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sīen</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sehwan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sehwaną</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>sīa</i> (West Frisian <i>sjen</i>), Old Saxon <i>sehan</i> (Low German <i>sehn</i>), Old Dutch <i>sian</i> (Dutch <i>zien</i>), Old High German <i>sehan</i> (German <i>sehen</i>), Old Norse <i>sjá</i> (Swedish <i>se</i>, Danish <i>se</i>, Icelandic <i>sjá</i>), Gothic <i>𐍃𐌰𐌹𐍈𐌰𐌽</i> (saiƕan).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sīhwan</i> (“to strain, drip”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;siuni</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sindon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sinewealt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sinu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seonoþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>seonoþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synod, council, meeting</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>synodus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>σῠ́νοδος</i> (sŭ́nodos).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synodal, synodic</li></ol><p>From <i>seonoþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sinu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to violate (sexually)</li><li>to commit adultery (glosses Latin <i>moechor</i>)</li></ol><p>Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;serþaną</i> (“to hit, violate”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>afflicted; troubled; vexed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;seuhslag</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;seuhslagaz</i>, equivalent to <i>sūsl</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>sýsliga</i> (“busily”), Old Norse <i>sýsl</i> (“eager; zealous”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>setl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>siwian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>siwian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>six</i> (“six”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cook</li><li>to boil</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;seuþaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to perceive, to teach</li></ol><p>Unclear, possibly from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sabjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>September</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>September</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sierċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sierċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Disputed, but traditionally interpreted as Saracens.</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly from Latin <i>Serica</i> (“an empire in East Asia”) or the <i>Siracī</i> tribe mentioned by Tacitus. Compare Old Norse <i>Serkland</i> (“Arabia, the Middle East”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) service</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old French <i>servise</i>, from Latin <i>servitium</i>, from Latin <i>servus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Most likely refers to the Seres (northern Chinese people), but may refer to the Saracens, in which case it is identical in meaning to <i>Sercingas</i>. "Syrians" has also been suggested as a potential meaning.</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly from Latin <i>Sērēs</i>, which is derived from Ancient Greek <i>Σῆρες</i> (Sêres), and possibly ultimately from Old Chinese <i>絲</i> (*sə, “silk”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sierwan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seat</li><li>bench</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sess</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sessaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sedstós</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sixta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vessel, jar, pitcher</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seat</li></ol><p>Compare the verb <i>settan</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>sæti</i>, Old High German <i>gesazi</i> (German <i>Gesäß</i>), Middle Dutch <i>gesaete</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sētiją</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>setl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sitting, residence</li><li>seat, bench, throne</li><li>setting (of sun etc.)</li></ol><p>From a merger of two nouns of similar meaning, which came to be pronounced the same in West Saxon because of a sound change whereby <i>-þl</i> and <i>-þm</i> became <i>-tl</i> and <i>-tm</i> in some words (cf. <i>botl</i>, <i>botm</i>):</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>only in compounds</i>) a person sitting beside</li><li>(<i>only in compounds</i>) an assessor or fellow judge</li></ol><p>From <i>setl</i> (“a sitting”) +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to settle, seat, put at rest</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to settle, rest</li><li>(<i>of the sun</i>) to set</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>setl</i> (“seat”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>setting</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;seþlagang</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>sedalgang</i>. Equivalent to <i>setl</i> +&lrm; <i>gang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>setting <i>(of heavenly bodies)</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>setl</i> +&lrm; <i>gangende</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taking of a seat; a sitting down</li><li>a session</li><li>setting (of the sun)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>setlan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ordinance, regulation</li><li>institution, constitution</li><li>arrangement</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>settan</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set, establish, place, make</li><li>to appoint</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sattjan</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>settian</i> (Dutch <i>zetten</i>), Old High German <i>sezzen</i> (German <i>setzen</i>), Old Norse <i>setja</i> (Swedish <i>sätta</i>), Gothic <i>𐍃𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (satjan). Compare Old English <i>sittan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seax</i> (“shortsword, dagger, knife”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>seax</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣd</i>. West Saxon "sex" is a record varient of seax and Anglican "rēd" is a record varient of rǣd.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Sexrēd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sēþend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>setl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>sēþung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prove</li><li>to attest, affirm</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sanþijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sanþijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>setl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a person who affirms or asserts</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sēþan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attestation</li><li>affirmation</li><li>proof</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sēþan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sīe</i></li></ol></html>:/seːɑ̯n/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sēan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relationship</li><li>peace</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sibjō</i>, cognate with German <i>Sippe</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swé</i>, also found in the ethnonym <i>Suebi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peace and quiet</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) band of kinsmen</li></ol><p>From <i>sibb</i> +&lrm; <i>gedryht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reconcile</li></ol><p>From <i>sibb</i> (“peace”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sibleġer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incest</li><li>(<i>countable</i>) an incestuous relationship or an incidence of incest</li></ol><p><i>sibb</i> +&lrm; <i>leġer</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relative, kinsman</li></ol><p>From <i>sibb</i> +&lrm; <i>-ling</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>familial love</li></ol><p><i>sibb</i> +&lrm; <i>lufu</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kinship, consanguinity</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sibb</i> +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>peacable; peaceful, friendly</li></ol><p>From <i>sibb</i> +&lrm; <i>-sum</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>sibbisam</i>, <i>sippisam</i> (“peaceful”) (<i>sippi</i> +&lrm; <i>-sam</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peaceableness; tranquility.</li></ol><p>From <i>sibsum</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a watercourse, stream</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(reflexive pronoun,&#32;hapax legomenon) Reflexive pronoun of the third person singular or plural&#58; herself, himself, itself, oneself, themselves</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sīk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sīką</i>.</p><p><i>The <b>spelling of this entry has been normalized</b>&nbsp;according to the principles established by Wiktionary's editor community or recent spelling standards of the language.</i></p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sik</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to sigh</li><li>to yearn for</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sīkan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sicol</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sikilu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ooze; (<i>of a liquid</i>) to pass through a small opening; seep</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sikrōną</i>, <i>&#42;sīkrōną</i>, frequentative verb derived from or related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sīką</i> (“trickling water”). Cognate with Saterland Frisian <i>sikkerje</i> (“to trickle”), German Low German <i>sickern</i> (“to trickle”), German <i>sickern</i> (“to trickle”). More at sike, sie.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sigh, groan</li></ol><p>From <i>sīcan</i> (“to sigh”) +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sicily (an island and former kingdom in Europe)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Sicilia</i> from Ancient Greek <i>Σικελία</i> (Sikelía), from <i>Σίκελος</i> (Síkelos, “Sicel”), the name of the <i>Sicel</i> people.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Sicilians</li><li>Sicily (an island and former kingdom in Europe)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Siculī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Sicilian</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a Sicilian</li></ol><p><i>Sicilie</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sickle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sikilu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>secure, free from guilt or punishment, safe, free from danger or harm</li><li>sure, certain, free from doubt</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sikur</i> (“secure, sure”), from Latin <i>sēcūrus</i> (“carefree”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with full certainty</li></ol><p>From <i>sicor</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>certainty</li></ol><p>From <i>sicor</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sicol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wide, spacious, vast, great, large, broad</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sīd</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sīdaz</i> (“drooping, long, ample”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>síðr</i> (Swedish <i>sid</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>widely</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>side</li><li>silk</li></ol><p>From the adjective <i>sīd</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sīdǭ</i>, whence also Old High German <i>sīta</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sīdā</i> (whence also Old High German <i>sīda</i> (“silk”)), from Late Latin <i>sēta</i> (“silk”), Classical Latin <i>saeta</i> (“bristle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of good behaviour or manners, honest, modest, virtuous, sober</li><li>(<i>of dress</i>) sober, modest, decorous</li></ol><p>From <i>sidu</i> +&lrm; <i>-ful</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sideful</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>virtuously</li></ol><p><i>sideful</i> (“virtuous, sober”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honesty, modesty, sobriety</li></ol><p><i>sideful</i> + <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zedoary (<i>Curcuma zedoaria</i>, an herb native to India)</li></ol><p>From Medieval Latin <i>zedoāria</i>, from Arabic جَدْوَار (jadwār), from Classical Persian ژدوار (žadwār). Compare German <i>Zitwer</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long-haired</li></ol><p><i>sīd</i> (“large, great, broad”) +&lrm; <i>feaxede</i> (“long-haired”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) broad-beamed, broad-bosomed</li></ol><p><i>sīd</i> +&lrm; <i>fæþmed</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a broad or spacious land</li></ol><p>From <i>sīd</i> (“broad, wide, spacious”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“stone”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sidu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>custom, habit; practice</li><li>use, manner</li><li>ritual, religious practice, rite</li><li>morality, good conduct; purity; modesty</li><li>(<i>in compounds</i>) ware, tool, instrument, gear</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sidu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;siduz</i> (“custom”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swedʰ-</i> (“custom, habit”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>side</i> (“custom”), Old Saxon <i>sidu</i> (“custom”), Old High German <i>situ</i> (“custom”), Old Norse <i>siðr</i> (“custom, habit; conduct”). Related to Old English <i>ġesidian</i> (“to arrange, order, set right”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silkworm</li></ol><p>From <i>sīd</i> (“silk”) +&lrm; <i>wyrm</i> (“worm”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sylf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to salt</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to season</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;saltijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saltijaną</i>, equivalent to <i>sealt</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>simle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>senses</i>) power of sight, vision</li><li>the instrument of sight; eye; pupil</li><li>appearance, countenance</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;siuni</i> (“appearance, sight, face”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>siōne</i>, <i>siūne</i> (“face, countenance”), Old Saxon <i>siun</i> (“perception, vision, sight,”), Old Norse <i>sýn</i> (“face, appearance, countenance”), Gothic <i>𐍃𐌹𐌿𐌽𐍃</i> (siuns, “face, form, countenance”).</p><p>From the 0-grade of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁es-</i> (“to be”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sindon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shirt, chemise</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sarki</i>; related to Old Norse <i>serkr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dry, withered, barren</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sauʀ(ī)</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sauzaz</i> (“dry, parched”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂sews-</i>, <i>&#42;sh₂ews-</i> (“to be dry”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plot, devise (generally in a negative sense)</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to arm</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sarwijan</i>, derived from the noun <i>&#42;saru</i> (Old English <i>searu</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>six</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sixfeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sixta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sixtēoþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sixtīene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sixtiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sixtiġfeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sieve</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sibi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sȳfre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sift</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;siftijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seofon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sīe</i></li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>reflexive pronoun</i>) alternative form of <i>sic</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sik</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sink, fall</li><li>to march, go, proceed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sīgan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sīganą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Short form of <i>Siġegār</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>siġþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>victory</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sigi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;segaz</i> (“victory”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sigorbēacn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) tree of victory, Christian cross</li></ol><p>From <i>siġe</i> +&lrm; <i>bēam</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) Jesus Christ; literally 'victory-child'</li></ol><p>From <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) +&lrm; <i>bearn</i> (“child”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>St. Sigeberht of East Anglia, king of East Anglia</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) +&lrm; <i>beorht</i> (“bright”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) +&lrm; <i>brand</i> (“firebrand, sword”). Cognate with Dutch <i>Siebrand</i>, Frankish <i>Sigobrand</i>, Faroese <i>Sigbrandur</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Siġebeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Siġebeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) +&lrm; <i>burg</i> (“city”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a victorious warrior (applied to King David)</li></ol><p>From <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) +&lrm; <i>cempa</i> (“warrior”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a victorious judge (applied to Jesus at the Last Judgement)</li></ol><p>From <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) +&lrm; <i>dēma</i> (“judge”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a victorious people</li></ol><p>From <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) +&lrm; <i>folc</i> (“people, folk”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) and <i>friþ</i> (“peace”). Cognate with German <i>Siegfried</i> and Old Norse <i>Sigfriðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>victorious</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a victor</li></ol><p><i>siġe</i> (“victory”) +&lrm; <i>-fæst</i> (“firm”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) and <i>gār</i> (“spear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Sigigautaz</i>, equivalent to <i>sige</i> (“victory, triumph”) + <i>gēat</i> (“Geat”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>Siggautr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a victorious battle</li><li>victory</li></ol><p>From <i>siġe</i> +&lrm; <i>ġefeoht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Sigihardu</i>, from <i>&#42;sigi</i> (“victory”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hardu</i> (“hard, brave”). Equivalent to <i>siġe</i> +&lrm; <i>heard</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Sigihart</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) and <i>helm</i> (“protection, defence”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) triumphant</li></ol><p><i>siġe</i> +&lrm; <i>hrēþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the sun</li><li>the runic character ᛋ (/s, z/)</li><li>clasp, button, brooch</li><li>jewel, gem</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly an alteration (due to <i>siġel</i> "jewel", see Etymology 2) of a term from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sawili</i>, derived from the oblique stem of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sōl</i> (“sun”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sigil</i>, from Latin <i>sigillum</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Sigel)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>siġel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) + <i>lāc</i> (“battle, gift”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>defeated, victoryless</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a song of victory</li></ol><p>From <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) +&lrm; <i>lēoþ</i> (“song”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Ethiopian</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) Ethiopians or Ethiopia</li></ol><p>From <i>siġel</i> (“sun”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hearwa</i> (“worshiper”), a derivative related to <i>herian</i> (“to praise”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ethiopian</li></ol><p><i>Siġelhearwa</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ethiopia</li></ol><p>Literally "land of the sun worshippers."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a heliotrope flower</li></ol><p>From <i>siġel</i> (“sun”) +&lrm; <i>hweorfa</i> (“spindle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>siġelhweorfa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>victorious</li></ol><p>From <i>siġe</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Siġelhearwena land</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Ethiopians</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) +&lrm; <i>mund</i> (“protector, protection”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>Sigmundr</i>, German <i>Siegmund</i> and <i>Sigismund</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Sigimēraz</i>, equivalent to <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) +&lrm; <i>mǣre</i> (“famous, renowned, illustrious”). Cognate with Proto-Norse <i>ᛋᛁᚷᛁᛗᛡᚱᛡᛉ</i> (sigimᴀrᴀʀ /⁠Sigimāraʀ⁠/) (whence Old Norse <i>Sigmarr</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the River Seine</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) and <i>nōþ</i> (“daring”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sigorian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) and <i>rīc</i> (“king, ruler”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) and <i>rǣd</i> (“counsel, advice”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) triumphal attire</li></ol><p>From <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) +&lrm; <i>sċeorp</i> (“clothing”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;poetic) the Danes</li></ol><p><i>siġe</i> (“victory”) +&lrm; <i>Sċyldingas</i> (“Danes”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sign of victory</li></ol><p>From <i>siġe</i> +&lrm; <i>tācen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a plain where a victory is won</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) +&lrm; <i>wang</i> (“plain”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) and <i>weald</i> (“power, authority”). Cognate with Icelandic <i>Sigvaldur</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Sigiwardu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Sigiwarduz</i>; equivalent to <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) +&lrm; <i>weard</i> (“guard”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) and <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) and <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a victorious nation</li></ol><p>From <i>siġe</i> (“victory”) +&lrm; <i>þēod</i> (“nation”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Siġefriþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>siġel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sail</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;siglijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;siglijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>sīla</i>, Old Norse <i>sigla</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rye</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>secale</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>victory, triumph, success</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sigʀu</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;sigiʀu</i>, plural of <i>&#42;seg</i> (“victory”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;segaz</i> (“victory”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;segʰ-</i> (“to hold”). Akin to Old Norse <i>sigr</i> (“victory”), Old English <i>siġe</i> (“victory”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a symbol of victory (applied to the cross of Jesus)</li></ol><p>From <i>sigor</i> +&lrm; <i>bēacn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to vanquish, triumph, triumph over</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sigʀōn</i>, <i>&#42;sigiʀōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sigizōną</i>, equivalent to <i>sigor</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>sigirōn</i>, Old Norse <i>sigra</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>victor</li></ol><p>From <i>sigorian</i> (“to vanquish”) +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sign of victory</li><li>convincing sign</li></ol><p>From <i>sigor</i> +&lrm; <i>tācen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the glory of the victor</li></ol><p>From <i>sigor</i> +&lrm; <i>wuldor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sigorian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Siġelhearwa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an implement for mowing; scythe, sickle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sigiþī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sigiþiz</i> (“sickle”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sek-</i> (“to cut, cut off, sever”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sixta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seohtre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sight (ability to see)</li><li>something seen, a sight</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sihti</i>, equivalent to <i>sēon</i> +&lrm; <i>-þ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seolcen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seolfren</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Siġelhearwa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Siġelhearwa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seolfor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sieltan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cord, rope</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sīmô</i> (“rope, cord”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seh₁i-</i> (“to tie, bind”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>simle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>simle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>simle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>always, constantly</li></ol><p>Represents either the adverb <i>simle</i> or the adjective <i>simel</i>, which is otherwise recorded only in the phrase <i>on simel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>always-traveling; nomadic</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>simel-</i> (“always”) +&lrm; <i>farende</i> (“traveling”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>constant companion</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>simel-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġefēra</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>always, constantly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;simlê</i> (“always, ever”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;chiefly&#32;dialectal,&#32;reflexive possessive pronoun) his; her; its; their</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sīn</i> (“his, her, its, their”, genitive reflexive).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>sīn</i> (“his, its”), Old Saxon <i>sīn</i> (“his”) (Middle Low German <i>sin</i>), Dutch <i>zijn</i>, Old High German <i>sīn</i> (“his”) (German <i>sein</i>), Old Norse <i>sínn</i> (“one's own”), Old English <i>sē</i> (“that, that one, he”). More at the.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>ever-, continual, perpetual</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) huge, immense; very</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sin-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sin-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sem-</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>sin-</i>, Old Saxon <i>sin-</i>, Old High German <i>sin-</i>, Old Norse <i>sí-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sinai (a mountain in Sinai Peninsula, Egypt)</li></ol><p>From Vulgate Latin <i>Sinai</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Σινάι</i> (Sinái), from Hebrew <i>סִינַי</i> (Sináy).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) treasure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sinkwą</i>, cognate with Old Norse <i>søkk</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to sink</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sinkwan</i>, akin to Old Frisian <i>sinka</i> (West Frisian <i>sinke</i>), Old Saxon <i>sinkan</i>, Old High German <i>sinkan</i> (German <i>sinken</i>), Old Norse <i>søkkva</i> (Danish <i>synke</i>, Swedish <i>sjunka</i>, Icelandic <i>sökkva</i>, Faroese <i>søkka</i>), Gothic <i>𐍃𐌹𐌲𐌵𐌰𐌽</i> (sigqan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very cold, frigid</li></ol><p><i>sin-</i> (“very”) +&lrm; <i>ċeald</i> (“cold”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) adorned with treasure</li></ol><p><i>sinc</i> +&lrm; <i>fāg</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a valuable or costly vessel, such as one made of silver or gold</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a receptacle for treasure</li></ol><p>From <i>sinc</i> (“treasure”) +&lrm; <i>fæt</i> (“vessel”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a lord; the giver of treasures to his thanes</li></ol><p>From <i>sinc</i> (“treasure”) +&lrm; <i>ġiefa</i> (“giver”), from <i>ġiefan</i> (“to give”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jewel, precious gem</li></ol><p>From <i>sinc</i> (“treasure”) +&lrm; <i>ġimm</i> (“gem”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>sincġiefa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jewel</li></ol><p>From <i>sinc</i> (“treasure”) +&lrm; <i>stān</i> (“stone”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) acceptance of a treasure, lord's gift</li></ol><p>From <i>sinc</i> (“treasure”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;þegu</i> (“taking, accepting”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cinder, ashes</li><li>dross, scoria, slag</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sindr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sindraz</i> (“dross, cinder, slag”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sendʰro-</i> (“coagulating fluid, scale, cinder”). Akin to Old High German <i>sintar</i> (“cinder”), Middle Low German <i>sinder</i> (“cinder”), Old Norse <i>sindr</i> (“cinder”), Old Church Slavonic <i>сядра</i> (sjadra, “lime cinder, gypsum”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rust</li></ol><p>From <i>sinder</i> (“impurity”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sindon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sinewealt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>round (circular, spherical, or cylindrical)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sinwaltaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>sin-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;wealt</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waltaz</i> (“changing; unstable”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wel-</i> (“to turn; wind; twist”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roundness (circularity or sphericity)</li></ol><p>From <i>sinewealt</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sinuwind</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sinewealt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>constant, continuous; perpetual</li></ol><p>From <i>sin-</i> +&lrm; <i>-gal</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ever, continually</li></ol><p><i>singal</i> (“perpetual”) +&lrm; <i>-es</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>constantly, continuously</li></ol><p>From <i>singal</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing, chant</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;singwan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;singwaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sengʷʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>singeþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>houseleek</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>evergreen</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;singrōnijā</i>, equivalent to <i>sin-</i> +&lrm; <i>grēne</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>singruona</i> (“evergreen, houseleek”).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;singrōnī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;singrōniz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>singruoni</i> (“evergreen”), Old Norse <i>sígrǿnn</i> (“evergreen”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marriage, the relationship of marriage</li></ol><p>From <i>sin-</i> +&lrm; <i>hīwsċipe</i>. By surface analysis, <i>sin-</i> +&lrm; <i>hīw</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn or change perpetually</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sin-</i> +&lrm; <i>hweorfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ever-turning</li></ol><p>From <i>sin-</i> +&lrm; <i>hweorfende</i> (“turning”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>synn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heed, care for, mind</li><li>to meditate over, care about, think upon, consider</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sinnan</i> (“to journey, spend time, sojourn”). Akin to Old High German <i>sinnan</i>, Old Norse <i>sinn</i> (“time, journey”), Old English <i>sīþian</i> (“to travel”), Old English <i>sendan</i> (“to send”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sinnihte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sinnihte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) endless night, eternal night</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sinnahtī</i>. Equivalent to <i>sin-</i> (“ever-”) +&lrm; <i>niht</i> (“night”) + <i>-e</i> (collective noun suffix). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>sinnahti</i>. Compare <i>seofonstierre</i>, which is formed similarly.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sinewealt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seonoþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(countable,&#32;uncountable) marriage</li></ol><p>From <i>sin-</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sinew, nerve, tendon</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sinu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sinwō</i>, <i>&#42;senawō</i> (“sinew”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sinewealt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) an artery</li></ol><p>From <i>sinu</i> +&lrm; <i>wind</i> (“that which winds, winding”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sinewealt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sēo</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sicol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sidu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seofon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seofon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>seolhȳþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seolfor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seolc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seolc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seolcwyrm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seolfor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seomian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sēon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sindon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sinewealt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seonoþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sindon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sinu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to absorb moisture</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sipōną</i> (“to drip, trickle”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seyb-</i> (“to pour out, trickle, leak out”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sierċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shirland (a village in Shirland and Higham parish, North East Derbyshire district, Derbyshire, England)</li></ol><p>Recorded in the Domesday Book. Probably from <i>sċīr</i> (“shire”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plot, machination</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dormouse</li></ol><p>The <i>mūs</i> element is from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mūs</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mūs</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;múHs</i>; the <i>sise</i> element is uncertain.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>setl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit</li><li>to stay, remain</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sittjan</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>sitta</i> (West Frisian <i>sitte</i>), Old High German <i>sizzen</i> (German <i>sitzen</i>), Old Saxon <i>sittian</i>, Dutch <i>zitten</i>, Old Norse <i>sitja</i> (Icelandic <i>sitja</i>, Faroese <i>sita</i>, Swedish <i>sitta</i>), Gothic <i>𐍃𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (sitan). The Proto-Indo-European root is also the source of Sanskrit <i>सीदति</i> (sīdati), Old Armenian <i>նիստ</i> (nist, “sitting”), Old Irish <i>saidid</i> (Irish <i>suigh</i>), Ancient Greek <i>ἕζομαι</i> (hézomai), Latin <i>sedeō</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>сѣдѣти</i> (sěděti) (Russian <i>сиде́ть</i> (sidétʹ)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>sittaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Sīweard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Short form of <i>Siġeweard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sew</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;siwjaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sīw-</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>sīa</i>, Old High German <i>siuwen</i>, Old Norse <i>sýja</i> (Danish <i>sy</i>, Swedish <i>sy</i>), Gothic <i>𐍃𐌹𐌿𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (siujan); and with Latin <i>suō</i>, Ancient Greek <i>ὑμήν</i> (humḗn).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Siġewine</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>six</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sehs</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sehs</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swéḱs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-six</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-sixth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-six</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-sixth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-six</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-sixth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-six</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-sixth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventy-six</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-six</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-sixth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-six</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-sixth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-six</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-sixth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>six hundred</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>six thousand</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sixfold</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sehsfald</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sehsfalþaz</i>, equivalent to <i>six</i> +&lrm; <i>-feald</i>. Cognate with Middle Low German <i>sesvōlt</i>, <i>sesvōldich</i>, Middle High German <i>sehsfalt</i>, <i>sehsvalt</i>, Old Norse <i>sexfaldr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>criminal law</i>) Worth six hundred (shillings in wergeld).</li></ol><p>From <i>six</i> +&lrm; <i>-hynde</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sixth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sehstô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sixtīene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixteenth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sixteenth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sehstô tehundô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixteen</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sehstehun</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>sextīne</i>, Old Saxon <i>sehstein</i>, Old High German <i>sehszehan</i>, and Old Norse <i>sextán</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sixteenfold</li></ol><p><i>sixtīene</i> +&lrm; <i>-feald</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sehs tigiwiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sixtyfold</li></ol><p><i>sixtiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-feald</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixtieth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sixtieth</li></ol><p><i>sixtiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-oþa</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sixtīene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>six year(s) old</li><li>six-year, lasting six years</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sehswintruz</i>. Equivalent to <i>six</i> +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i>. Attested once, in Genesis 16:16 of the Old English Hexateuch: <i>Hundeahtatiġwintre and <u>sixwintre</u> wæs Ābrahām þā þā Agar him ācende Ismahēl</i> ("Abraham was 86 years old [literally "80 years old and <u>six years old</u>"] when Hagar bore him Ishmael").</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sīe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>sīþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>siþþan</i></li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>siþþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time (instance or occurrence)</li><li>time (as in <i>the first time</i>, <i>many times</i>)</li><li>times (indicating the multiplication of two numbers or a ratio of comparison)</li><li>journey</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>late</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>late</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>after</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>after</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sinþ</i>, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sinþaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sent-</i> (“to head for, go”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>sīth</i>, Old Saxon <i>sīð</i>, Old High German <i>sind</i>, Old Norse <i>sinn</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐌹𐌽𐌸𐍃</i> (sinþs).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sīþuz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>sīd</i> and Old Norse <i>síðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>itinerary</li></ol><p>From <i>sīþ</i> (“travel”) +&lrm; <i>bōc</i> (“book”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>born late; postmature</li></ol><p><i>sīþ</i> (“late”) +&lrm; <i>boren</i> (“born”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>siġþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expedition, journey</li><li>road, path, course</li></ol><p><i>sīþ</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;fæt</i> (“journey”). Second element is obsolete in attested Old English (cf. Icelandic <i>fet</i>) and only occurs in compounds.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to travel, journey</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sinþōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sinþōną</i> (“to go, travel”), equivalent to <i>sīþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>last, final</li></ol><p>Superlative of <i>sīþ</i> (“late”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>sīþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>afterwards, subsequently</li><li>since</li><li>ago, since</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>after, since</li></ol><p>From an earlier (unrecorded) form *<i>sīþ þon</i> (“subsequent to that”), with contraction and corresponding shortening of long vowel.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sceolde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>slāh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slacken, make slack</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slakōn</i> (“to weaken, slacken”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>killer, murderer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;slagō</i>. Agent noun of <i>slēan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) sloe</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;slaihā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slaihǭ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blackthorn (<i>Prunus spinosa</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>slāh</i> (“sloe”) +&lrm; <i>þorn</i> (“thorn”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>slǣp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>slǣpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cause to sleep</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slēpōną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>slāphōn</i>, <i>slāpfōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clary sage (<i>Salvia sclarea</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lazy</li><li>slow, inert</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;slaiw</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slaiwaz</i> (“blunt, dull, faint, weak, slack”), of uncertain origin.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>slowly, sluggishly</li></ol><p>From <i>slāw</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slowworm, blindworm</li></ol><p>Perhaps equivalent to <i>slāw</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>slæc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>slæcnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>slæcnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>sliht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hit; strike, punch</li><li>to kill</li><li>to slaughter (an animal)</li><li>(<i>of a snake, insect, or arachnid</i>) to bite or sting</li><li>to pitch (a tent)</li><li>to mint (coins, money)</li><li>to move quickly and suddenly</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;slahan</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>slā</i>, Old Saxon <i>slahan</i> (Low German <i>slaan</i>), Dutch <i>slaan</i>, Old High German <i>slahan</i> (German <i>schlagen</i>), Old Norse <i>slá</i> (Danish <i>slå</i>, Swedish <i>slå</i>), Gothic <i>𐍃𐌻𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (slahan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>slǣp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>slīefesċōh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>slæc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>slæċċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sledgehammer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slagjō</i>, <i>&#42;slagjǭ</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>sleggja</i> (“sledgehammer”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>slæcnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>slīef</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>slīeflēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>sleċġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hit: strike, blow, punch</li><li>killing, murder</li><li>slaughter (of an animal)</li><li>a defeat</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;slagi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slagiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an instrument for striking a harp; plectrum</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;slagil</i>, equivalent to <i>slæġe</i> +&lrm; <i>-el</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>slegil</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sliht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>slǣp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>slǣpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Northumbrian</i>) alternative form of <i>slǣpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hammer</li></ol><p>From the same source as <i>slean</i> (“to hit”), both related to or derived from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;slahan</i> (“to strike”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sliċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slide</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;slīdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>slidorian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slippery</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sleydʰ-</i> (“slippery”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slide</li><li>to slither</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;slidrōn</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>slidorian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleeve</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;slaubiju</i>, from <i>&#42;slaubijōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slaubijōną</i> (“to slip into”), perhaps allied to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sleupaną</i> (“to slip, glide, sneak”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>slīef</i> (“sleeve”)</li></ol><p>See <i>slīef</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slipper</li></ol><p>From <i>slīefe</i> +&lrm; <i>sċōh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sleeveless</li></ol><p><i>slīef</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sliht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to slip</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slaupijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slipper</li></ol><p>From <i>slīepan</i> (“to slip”) +&lrm; <i>sċōh</i> (“shoe”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sliht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>slīef</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut, slip, or slice off</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;slīban</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hit or strike</li><li>killing, murder</li><li>(<i>of a coin</i>) striking</li><li>(<i>of lightning</i>) stroke; flash</li><li>deadly stroke of disease</li><li>that which is to be killed; animals for slaughter</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>level; smooth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;slahti</i>, <i>&#42;slahtu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slahtuz</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sliht</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slihtaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slime</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slīmą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sley-</i> (“smooth; slick; sticky; slimy”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crawl, slink</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;slinkan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slinkaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a reptile</li></ol><p>From <i>slincan</i> (“to slink”) +&lrm; <i>-end</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wind, twist, coil, worm</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;slingwan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slingwaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sleaford (a town in Lincolnshire, England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>slipiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slippy, slimy</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>slipan</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slippery, not easy to hold, moving easily</li><li>slipping easily, easily moved</li><li>uncertain, unsteady, without fixed principles</li><li>foul</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;slipr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slipraz</i> (“smooth, slippery”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear, rend</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;slītan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>formed, moulded, shaped, artificial</li><li>graven (of idols or images), sculpted (also used substantively)</li><li>feigned, false</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) fierce, cruel, terrible, severe</li></ol><p>Possibly cognate with Old Norse <i>slíðr</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (sleiþs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) cruel, hard, evil</li></ol><p>From <i>slīþe</i> (“cruel”) +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a graven image</li></ol><p><i>slīþe</i> (“graven”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slough</li><li>mud</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slōhaz</i>, of unknown ultimate origin.</p><p>Related to Middle High German <i>sluoche</i> (“ditch”), Middle Low German <i>slōch</i> (“muddy place”). Compare also dialectal Swedish <i>slaga, slage</i> (“swamp”).</p><p>The related term Old English <i>&#42;slōhtre</i> (“slough” or “(river) ravine”), the source of the place name of <i>the Slaughters</i>, <i>Upper Slaughter</i> and <i>Lower Slaughter</i> (Middle English <i>Sloghtre, Slouhtre</i>), has been suggested to furthermore related to the German placename <i>Schlüchtern</i> (attested in 999 and 1025 as <i>Sluohterin</i>), related to <i>Schlucht</i> (“ravine, gorge”). Kroonen further compares <i>slōh</i> to German <i>Schluche</i> (name of a waterfall near Erfurt), Icelandic <i>slagi</i> (“dampness”) and a variety of other terms.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bit, morsel, mouthful</li></ol><p>Unknown. If <i>sloca</i> be the original form, then perhaps related to or derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slukiz</i> (“throat, gulp”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slūkaną</i> (“to swallow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slumber</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slip, slide</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sleupan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>slīef</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>slīefe</i> (“sleeve”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>slīeflēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sliht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in compounds</i>) alternative form of <i>slyppe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>slīepan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paste, sticky substance</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inactive, slothful, lazy, not willing to make an effort</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;slak</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slakaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)leg-</i>, <i>&#42;(s)leh₁g-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make slack or slow</li><li>to delay</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;slakkjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slakjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laziness, sloth</li><li>inertness</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>slæc</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleep</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;slāp</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>slaep</i>, <i>slāp</i> (Low German <i>Slaap</i>), Middle Dutch <i>slaep</i> (Dutch <i>slaap</i>), Old High German <i>slāf</i> (German <i>Schlaf</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sleep</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;slāpan</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>slēpa</i> (West Frisian <i>sliepe</i>), Old Saxon <i>slāpan</i> (Low German <i>slapen</i>), Old Dutch <i>slāpan</i> (Dutch <i>slapen</i>), Old High German <i>slāfan</i> (German <i>schlafen</i>), Gothic <i>𐍃𐌻𐌴𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (slēpan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dormitory</li></ol><p>From <i>slǣp</i> +&lrm; <i>ærn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sleepless</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;slāpalaus</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>slāflōs</i>. Equivalent to <i>slǣp</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>slǣplīest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleeplessness, insomnia</li></ol><p><i>slǣplēas</i> +&lrm; <i>-þ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) weary from a lack of sleep, sleep-weary</li></ol><p>From <i>slǣp</i> (“sleep”) +&lrm; <i>wēriġ</i> (“weary”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sloth; laziness</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slaiwiþō</i> (“slowness, lateness”), equivalent to <i>slāw</i> +&lrm; <i>-þ</i>. Cognate with Scots <i>sleuth</i> (“sloth, slowness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pat; stroke, caress</li><li>to coax, flatter; allure, seduce</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smaikōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>smæl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>creeping in, penetrating</li><li>subtle, crafty</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smaug</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smaugaz</i> (“slippery, slick”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)mewk-</i> (“to slip, slide”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>consider, think about</li><li>meditate</li><li>examine, scrutinize, question</li></ol><p>From pre-Old English <i>&#42;smauhōjan</i>, an extended form of <i>&#42;smauhōn</i>. The verb was contracted after loss of intervocalic <i>-h-</i> (compare <i>twēoġan</i>, <i>sċōġan</i>). Equivalent to <i>smēag</i> (“penetrating, acute, subtle, effective, clever”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Ultimately from the root of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smeuganą</i> (“to creep, slip through”). Akin to Old English <i>smūgan</i> (“to creep, progress gradually or deliberately”), Old Norse <i>smjúga</i> (“to creep”) (> Danish <i>smyge</i>), Old English <i>smyġel</i> (“a burrow, place to creep into”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>smēaġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thinking, thoughts, meditation</li><li>inquiry, investigation</li></ol><p>From <i>smēaġan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smēag</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>searchingly, carefully</li><li>deeply, acutely</li><li>closely</li><li>clearly, accurately (of thinking)</li></ol><p><i>smēag</i> (“crafty”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smēaġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smēagung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smile</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smarukōn</i>, <i>&#42;smarikōn</i>, derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smarōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>painful, smarting</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smartaz</i> (“hurting”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)merd-</i> (“to burn”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>smert</i> (“hurting, painful”). More at smart.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smart</i>, first and third person singular preterite of <i>&#42;smertaną</i> (“to smart”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smēagung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smēagung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meditation</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smīc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smæċċan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smakkijaną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>smekken</i>, Old Frisian <i>smekka</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smæċċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smēaġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smelt (a fish)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smeltaz</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amber</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smeltan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>. Compare Old High German <i>smelzi</i>, <i>gismelzi</i> (“fused mass, electrum, alloy”), Old Norse <i>smeltr</i> (“enamelled”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to emit smoke, fume, smoulder</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to smoke, fumigate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smeukan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smeukaną</i> (“to smoke”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smēocan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a smarting, an itching</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;smeortan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fat, grease, suet, tallow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smeru</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smerwą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;smérus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fatty, greasy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smerwig</i>, equivalent to <i>smeoru</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>. Cognate with Middle Low German <i>smērich</i>, Middle High German <i>smerwic</i>, <i>smerewic</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smearwort (<i>Aristolochia rotunda</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>smeoru</i> (“grease, ointment, fat”) +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i> (“plant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entrail</li></ol><p>From <i>smeoru</i> (“fat, grease”) +&lrm; <i>þearm</i> (“intestine”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smearcian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smireness</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smeoruwiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deride, laugh to scorn</li><li>alternative form of <i>smierwan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smerēn</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smirwijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smeortung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smierwan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smēagung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smooth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smanþī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to smooth</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to become smooth</li></ol><p>From <i>smēþe</i> (“smooth”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smoothness</li><li>a smooth surface</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>smēþe</i> (“smooth”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smoke, vapor</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beauteous, beautiful, elegant, fair, fine, tasteful</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smikr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smikraz</i> (“fine, elegant”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>smicer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smicer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smear, anoint</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smirwijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ointment, oil</li></ol><p>From <i>smierwan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ness</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smierwan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smierwan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to daub, smear, pollute, infect</li><li>to smite</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smītan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a smear, blot, mark, or spot</li><li>that which mars, pollutes, or defiles; pollution, defilement</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smittō</i>, <i>&#42;smittā</i> (“fleck, spot, smudge, stain”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smittōn</i> (“to smear”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smittōną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;smeyd-</i> (“to smear, streak, whisk, rub”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pollution, defilement</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smittā</i>, <i>&#42;smittjā</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smittōn</i>, <i>&#42;smittjōn</i> (“to smear”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smittōną</i>, <i>&#42;smitjōną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;smidnó-</i>, <i>&#42;smidi-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;smeyd-</i> (“to smear, streak, whisk, rub”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smear</li><li>to pollute, defile</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smittōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>smiþian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smith</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smiþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forge: to make skillfully, especially of metal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smiþōną</i> (“to smith”), equivalent to <i>smiþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a smith's workshop: smithy, forge</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smiþþjā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smiþjǭ</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>smithe</i>, Old Saxon <i>smitha</i>, Old High German <i>smidda</i> (whence Modern German <i>Schmiede</i>), Old Norse <i>smiðja</i> (whence Modern English <i>smithy</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smoke</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smukô</i> (“smoke, nebulous air”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;smug-</i> (“to smoke; a smoke”). Cognate with German <i>Schmauch</i> (“dense smoke”), Old Irish <i>múch</i> (“smoke”), Ancient Greek <i>σμύχω</i> (smúkhō, “burn with smoldering flame”) and Old Armenian <i>մուխ</i> (mux, “smoke”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smock</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smukkaz</i> (“something slipped into”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smoke (emit smoke)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smokōn</i> (“to smoke”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>serene, quiet, peaceful</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to choke or suffocate someone</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smurōną</i> (“to suffocate, strangle”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smēþe</i> (“smooth”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to creep, crawl, move gradually</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smeugan</i>, with the diphthong analogically replaced by ū in the present stem.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smīc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burrow</li></ol><p>From <i>smugan</i> (“to creep”) +&lrm; <i>-el</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>calm (of someone's mood)</li><li>calm (of the weather, the sea, etc.)</li></ol><p>From a variant or alteration of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smultaz</i>. The expected form <i>smolt</i> occurs, but is not the regular form in West Saxon.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>smelting</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smelting</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smireness</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smierwan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smitta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smack, taste, flavour</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smakku</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>smæc</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smakku</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smakkuz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;smeg-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to taste</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;smakkijan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>smekken</i>, Old Frisian <i>smekka</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>narrow, slim; not broad</li><li>slender; not thick</li><li>fine; not coarse</li><li>small; not large</li><li>quiet; not loud</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smalaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>smel</i>, Old Saxon <i>smal</i>, Old Dutch <i>smal</i>, Old High German <i>smal</i>, Old Norse <i>smalr</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐌼𐌰𐌻𐍃</i> (smals).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>smæl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>smæl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snake</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;snakō</i> (“snake”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a swift sailing vessel; a small warship</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;snakk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snakkaz</i>, <i>&#42;snakkǭ</i> (“sailing vessel”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snak-</i> (“to breathe, blow, sigh”). Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)neh₂-</i> (“to swim, float”). Cognate with Old High German <i>snagga</i> (“a small warship”), Old Norse <i>snekkja</i> (“warship”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>snǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spit, skewer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;snaisu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snaisō</i> (“twig, stick, spit”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sneyt-</i> (“to cut”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;snaiw</i> (“snow”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snaiwaz</i> (“snow”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;snóygʷʰos</i>, from <i>&#42;sneygʷʰ-</i> (“to snow”) + <i>&#42;-os</i> (suffix forming action nouns from verbs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cold as snow</li></ol><p>From <i>snāw</i> (“snow”) +&lrm; <i>ċeald</i> (“cold”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snowstorm</li></ol><p><i>snāw</i> +&lrm; <i>ġebland</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>snow-white</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snaiwahwītaz</i>, equivalent to <i>snāw</i> (“snow”) +&lrm; <i>hwīt</i> (“white”). Cognate with Dutch <i>sneeuwwit</i>, German <i>schneeweiß</i>, and Old Norse <i>snjóhvitr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>snowy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snaiwagaz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>snēgig</i>, Old High German <i>snēwag</i>, Old Norse <i>snjóigr</i>. Equivalent to <i>snāw</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snare, noose</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;snarhā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snarhǭ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a snail or slug, a gastropod</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snagilaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sneġel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sneġel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lively, quick, rapid, strong</li><li>keen, ready</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;snell</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snellaz</i> (“quick, swift”). Cognate with <i>schnell</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>snell</i> (“lively, quick”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>swift, moving rapidly</li><li>quick in action, ready, bold</li></ol><p><i>snell</i> (“lively, quick”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>rapidly, quickly</li></ol><p><i>snell</i> (“lively, quick”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boldness, bravery</li></ol><p>From <i>snell</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċiepe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quickly</li><li>immediately, at once</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crawl</li><li>to creep</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;snīkan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snīkaną</i> (“to creep; crawl”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sneyg-</i> (“to crawl”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cut</li><li>a saw</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snidą</i> (“cut”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snīwaną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>snīwan</i> (Modern German <i>schneien</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>snīþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut, make an incision in</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;snīþan</i> (“to cut”).</p><p>Compare Old Frisian <i>snītha</i>, Old Saxon <i>snīthan</i>, Old High German <i>snīdan</i>, Old Norse <i>sníða</i>. More at snithe.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nasal mucus, snot</li></ol><p>Related to the verb <i>&#42;snyflan</i> (“to run at the nose”), ultimately related to Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;snūt</i> (“snout”). Compare modern <i>snivel</i>, <i>snuffle</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daughter-in-law</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;snuʀu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snuzō</i> (“daughter-in-law”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;snusós</i> (“daughter-in-law”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>snore</i>, Middle Dutch <i>snare</i>, Old High German <i>snur</i>, Old Norse <i>snor</i>. The Indo-European root is also the source of Sanskrit <i>स्नुषा</i> (snuṣā́), Ancient Greek <i>νυός</i> (nuós), Latin <i>nurus</i>, Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;snъxa</i> (Russian <i>сноха́</i> (snoxá)), Albanian <i>nuse</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Compare <i>snotor</i> (“clever, wise”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Snotingahām</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>snotor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>snotor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>snotor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>snotor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nottingham</li></ol><p>From <i>Snotinga</i> (“Snot's people's”) +&lrm; <i>hām</i> (“village”), equivalent to <i>Snot</i> (“a Saxon chieftain who conquered the settlement”) + <i>-ing</i> (“belonging to, people of”) + <i>hām</i> (“settlement, farmstead, village, home”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nottinghamshire</li></ol><p><i>Snotingaham</i> + <i>scir</i> (“office, district”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Snotingahām</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wise, clever, prudent, intelligent, discerning, sagacious</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;snotr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snutraz</i>, perhaps from the same Indo-European root as Ancient Greek <i>νόος</i> (nóos, “mind”). Cognate with Old High German <i>snottar</i>, Old Norse <i>snotr</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐌽𐌿𐍄𐍂𐍃</i> (snutrs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wise, prudent</li></ol><p><i>snotor</i> (“wise”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wisely, prudently</li></ol><p><i>snotorlīċ</i> (“wise”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>snotorlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>snotorlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prudence; wisedom; sagacity</li></ol><p>From <i>snotor</i> (“wise”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prudent in speech</li></ol><p><i>snotor</i> (“wse”) +&lrm; <i>-wyrde</i> (“worded”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>snotor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at once, directly</li></ol><p><i>snūd</i> (“coming at once”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow one's nose</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;snūtijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snūtijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wisdom</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>snottor</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morsel, bit</li><li>a piece of land with defined limits</li><li>the handle of scythe</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;snaidi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snaidiz</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;snaidō</i> (“cut”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;snaid</i> (“an area of cut vegetation, boundary, border”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;snaidijan</i> (“to cut, chop, hew, slice”). Cognate with Middle Low German <i>snêde</i>, <i>sneide</i> (“border, boundary line, forest mark”).</p><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from <i>snǣdan</i> (“to cut, slice”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slice</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;snaidijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snaidijaną</i>, equivalent to <i>snǣd</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sneġel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sneġel</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;snagil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snagilaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sneġel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sock</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sokk</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sōcn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a seeking; search; exploration</li><li>a desiring; attempt to acquire (something); probing</li><li>a visit; visiting</li><li>a place of attendance; a resort</li><li>protection; refuge; sanctuary; asylum</li><li>a seeking with hostile intent; an attack</li><li>(<i>Anglo-Saxon&#32;law</i>) Frequently used in connection with <i><i>sacu</i></i>&#58; the exercise of judicial power; jurisdiction; right of inquisition; right of seeking, taking, or levying fines; revenue</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sōkni</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sōkniz</i> (“examination; contest; struggle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sodom (ancient Biblical city in Canaan, destroyed due to its sinfulness)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Σόδομα</i> (Sódoma), from the Biblical Hebrew <i>סְדֹם</i> (s'dóm).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Sodoma</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Sodom; sodomitic</li></ol><p>From <i>Sodoma</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sodomisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweostor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>softly</li><li>without opposition</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sēfte</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;samftō</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;samftī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ease, comfort</li><li>effeminacy</li></ol><p>From <i>sōfte</i> (“soft”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>softe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>softe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hiccup, or possibly heartburn</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sun</li><li>the Sun</li><li>mud, wet sand, mire</li><li>wallowing-place, slough, miry-place</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark, dirty, soiled</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sōl</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sōl</i> (“sun”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sol</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sulą</i> (“mud, spot”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sūl-</i> (“thick liquid”). Cognate with Old High German <i>sol</i>, <i>gisol</i> (“pool of excrement”), Middle Dutch <i>sol</i> (“puddle, dirt, filth”). More at soil.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Possibly from Old English <i>sōl</i> (“sun”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sole</li><li>shoe, sandal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>solea</i>, from <i>solum</i> (“bottom, base”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swol-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>salor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to soil; become defiled; make or become foul</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;solōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sulōną</i> (“to soil”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sūl-</i> (“thick liquid, muck”). Cognate with Old High German <i>solōn</i> (“to make dirty”). More at soil.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) sundial</li></ol><p>From <i>sōl</i> +&lrm; <i>merca</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the second month of the year according to the Anglo-Saxon calendar</li><li>February</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>sol</i> (“mud”) +&lrm; <i>mōnaþ</i> (“month”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>salor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sōlsece</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the heliotrope flower</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>solsequium</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mire</li><li>a miry place</li></ol><p>Uncertain. No corresponding cognates (i.e. feminine o-stem nouns) in the other Germanic languages. Likely a back-formation from <i>solu</i> (“swamp, bog”, literally “miry places, muds”), the nominative/accusative plural of <i>sol</i> (“soil, mud, miry place”), taken collectively.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agreement, concord</li><li>reconciliation, a setting aside of differences</li><li>a meeting for agreement, arrangement of dispute</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sōmu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sōmō</i>, related to <i>&#42;sōmiz</i> (“seemly”). Related to Old Norse <i>sǿmr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sām-</i> (“half-”)</li></ol><p>See <i>sām-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>samod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>samnian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>samnung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>samod</i></li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>samod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a musical sound; vocal, instrumental</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>sonus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immediately, straightaway</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sānō</i>. Related to Old Norse <i>senn</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐌿𐌽𐍃</i> (suns).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>music</li></ol><p>From <i>sōn</i> +&lrm; <i>cræft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sand</i> (“sending”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Sandwīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worry, anxiety</li><li>sorrow, grief</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sorgu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;surgō</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>sorge</i>, Old Saxon <i>sorga</i>, Old Dutch <i>sorga</i>, Old High German <i>sorga</i>, Old Norse <i>sorg</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌲𐌰</i> (saurga).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sorhful</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to worry</li><li>to grieve, sorrow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sorgōn</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;sorgēn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;surgāną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sorhlēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sorg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full of care or anxiety, careful, anxious</li><li>sorrowful, mournful, grievous, sad, causing grief or sorrow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;surgafullaz</i> (“full of care; anxious”), equivalent to <i>sorh</i> +&lrm; <i>-ful</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>sorgfol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sorhful</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free from care or anxiety, secure</li><li>free from sorrow, sorrowless</li></ol><p>From <i>sorh</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sorry, grieved</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sairagaz</i> (“sad”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soot</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sott</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sōtą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sed-</i> (“to sit”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foolish, stupid</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fool</li></ol><p>Either borrowed from Old French <i>sot</i>, or directly from Medieval Latin <i>sottus</i>, of unknown origin.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sāwol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>sōþ</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>sōþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>sōþġiedd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>sōþlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>sōþword</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>true, real</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>truth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sanþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sanþaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a truth-bearer, one who has knowledge of something</li></ol><p><i>sōþ</i> (“truth”) +&lrm; <i>-bora</i> (“bearer”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a true statement</li></ol><p>From <i>sōþ</i> (“true”) +&lrm; <i>cwide</i> (“saying”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) the "king of truth;" God</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sōþ</i> (“truth”) +&lrm; <i>cyning</i> (“king”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>truly, genuinely, really</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sōþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>truly, really, forsooth</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sōþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-es</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>sōþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>true, soothfast</li><li>honest</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sanþafast</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>sōthfast</i>. Equivalent to <i>sōþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-fæst</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>sōþfæst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>truth</li><li>honesty</li><li>sincerity</li><li>justice</li></ol><p><i>sōþfæst</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sōþfæstnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sōþfæstnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>sōþfæst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sōþġiedd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) true story</li></ol><p>From <i>sōþ</i> +&lrm; <i>ġiedd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prove</li><li>to attest, affirm</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sanþōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sanþōną</i>; equivalent to <i>sōþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>true, sincere</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sanþalīk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sanþalīkaz</i>, equivalent to <i>sōþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>sōthlīk</i>, Old Norse <i>sannligr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>truly, really, certainly</li><li>(<i>indefinite</i>) indeed, surely, truly</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) amen</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>now, then, for</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sanþalīkā</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;sanþalīkō</i>, equivalent to <i>sōþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>, or <i>sōþlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>truthful</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sanþasagul</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sanþasagulaz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>sannsǫgull</i> (Icelandic <i>sannsögull</i>). Equivalent to <i>sōþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-sagol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a true statement, truism</li><li>truth</li></ol><p>From <i>sōþ</i> (“truth”) +&lrm; <i>sagu</i> (“saying”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say truly or correctly</li><li>to declare</li></ol><p>From <i>sōþ</i> +&lrm; <i>seċġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sōþspell</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>true story</li><li>nonfiction</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) history</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sanþaspell</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>sōthspell</i>. Equivalent to <i>sōþ</i> (“true”) +&lrm; <i>spell</i> (“story”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a true statement</li></ol><p>From <i>sōþ</i> (“true”) +&lrm; <i>sprǣċ</i> (“speech”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a true statement</li><li>a statement of the facts of a case</li></ol><p>From <i>sōþ</i> (“true”) +&lrm; <i>sæġen</i> (“saying”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>true sign</li><li>prodigy</li></ol><p>From <i>sōþ</i> +&lrm; <i>tācen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a true word, proverb</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sanþaword</i>, equivalent to <i>sōþ</i> (noun) +&lrm; <i>word</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spoke (of a wheel)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spaikā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spaikō</i>. Cognate with Gutnish <i>spaik</i> (“a mark carved in a tree”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spātl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spade</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spadō</i>, a byform of <i>&#42;spadô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spearhafoc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>substitute</li><li>representative</li></ol><p>Unknown; possibly related to <i>spilian</i> (“to play”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entice, attract, allure, lure</li><li>to persuade, induce, urge</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spanan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spananą</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>spanan</i>, Old High German <i>spanan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) teat</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spanô</i>, <i>&#42;spenô</i>. See English <i>spean</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>span (of a hand; used as a measurement, typically considered about nine inches)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spannō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)pend-</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>spanne</i> (Dutch <i>spanne</i>), Old High German <i>spanna</i> (German <i>Spanne</i>), Old Norse <i>spǫnn</i> (Icelandic <i>spönn</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to span, clasp, fasten, attach, join, link</li><li>to join in matrimony; join in wedlock</li><li>to harness or yoke (oxen)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spannaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spane</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spanô</i>, <i>&#42;spenô</i>. See English <i>spean</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spare</li><li>to save (keep or store for future use)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sparōną</i>, whence also Old High German <i>sparōn</i>, Old Norse <i>spara</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spit, spittle</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spit out</li></ol><p>From <i>spātl</i> (“spittle”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spātl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) a spark</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sparkō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sprakô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spark, sparkle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sparkōn</i>, equivalent to <i>spearca</i> (“spark”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sparrowhawk</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sparwahabuk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sparwahabukaz</i>, equivalent to <i>spearwa</i> (“sparrow”) +&lrm; <i>hafoc</i> (“hawk”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>sparrhaukr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calf (of the leg)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>spearwa</i> (“sparrow”) +&lrm; <i>lira</i> (“the fleshy parts of the body”). Compare Old English mūs (“mouse, muscle”) and Latin mūsculus (“small mouse, muscle”) for similar animal-anatomy comparisons.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sparrow</li><li>the calf of the leg</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sparwô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spiċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sprecan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speck, spot, blot</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>success, prosperity</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spōdi</i> (“prosperity, success”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have success; to succeed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spōdijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>successful</li><li>wealthy</li><li>rich <i>(+ dative in something)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spōdigaz</i> (“successful, hurried”). Equivalent to <i>spēd</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spell</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a splint; splinter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spelkǭ</i>, <i>&#42;spilkiz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)pelg-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)pel-</i> (“to split”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>spelka</i>, <i>spjalk</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind with splints</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spalkijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spalkijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>spelkja</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spelċan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spalkijaną</i>, <i>&#42;spilkijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>spelkja</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>splinter</li><li>a thin piece of wood used as a torch; torch</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;speldą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>spellian</i></li><li>to substitute for, take the place of</li><li>to stand in proxy or in the stead of another; represent</li><li>to act as a representative</li></ol><p>From <i>spala</i> (“substitute”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a representative, one who takes the place of another</li></ol><p>From <i>spelian</i> (“to represent”) +&lrm; <i>-end</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>story</li><li>news</li><li>prose or a work of prose</li><li>argument</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spell</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spellą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to talk, converse</li><li>to announce, proclaim; to relate, tell; to utter, verbalize</li><li>to conspire</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spellōną</i>. Equivalent to <i>spell</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spend</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spendōn</i>, a borrowing from Latin <i>expendō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spending</li></ol><p>From <i>spendan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Spanish</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spiweþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spear, lance, pike, javelin</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;speru</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;speru</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sperH-</i>. Cognate with West Frisian <i>spear</i>, Dutch <i>speer</i>, Old High German <i>sper</i> (German <i>Speer</i>), Old Norse <i>spjǫr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male line of descent</li></ol><p><i>spere</i> +&lrm; <i>healf</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of plant</li></ol><p>From <i>spere</i> (“spear”) +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i> (“plant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike, spar</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bacon</li><li>lard</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spik</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spiką</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>spek</i>, German <i>Speck</i>, and Icelandic <i>spik</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>larder (room used for food storage)</li></ol><p>From <i>spiċ</i> (“lard”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spike</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spīking</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spīku</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spīkō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>titmouse</li></ol><p>From <i>māse</i> (“titmouse”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sparkle</li><li>to fall in drops, sprinkle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sparkijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spelc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spelċan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spilkijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spilkijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destruction, ruin</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spelþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spelþą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spillan</i> (“destroy”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play; to make sport of, to revel</li><li>to act lasciviously</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spilōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) [<i>with</i> accusative] to destroy</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) [<i>with</i> accusative] to spill</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spilþijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fat</li><li>grease</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spindaz</i> (“fat, grease”), of unknown origin.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sprindel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spindle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spinnilu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female line of descent</li></ol><p><i>spinel</i> +&lrm; <i>healf</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spinel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spinel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spin</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spinnan</i>, see also Old Frisian <i>spinna</i>, Old High German <i>spinnan</i>, Old Norse <i>spinna</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐍀𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (spinnan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spire (stalk of a plant)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spirian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spirienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spirian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spirienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sperran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spit</li><li>to dig with spade</li></ol><p>Imitative.</p><p>From the noun <i>spitu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spit (of wood, metal etc. for roasting meat)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spituz</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>spit</i> (Dutch <i>spit</i>), Old High German <i>spiz</i> (German <i>Spieß</i>), Swedish <i>spett</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to vomit, spew</li><li>to spit</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spīwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vomiting, the act of vomiting</li><li>vomit</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>spiwan</i> (“to spew”) +&lrm; <i>-þ</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spiweþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spider</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spinþrijō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)penh₁-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a plot or parcel of land</li><li>a spot</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;splott</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spluttaz</i> (“segment”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;splt-no-</i> (“an off-split, segment”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)pel-</i> (“to split”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wooden chip or shaving</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spānu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spēnuz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)peH-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>track, trace</li><li>footprint</li><li>vestige, any mark left by some event</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spurą</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>&#42;spor</i>, Old Saxon <i>spor</i>, Old Dutch <i>spor</i>, Old High German <i>spor</i>, Old Norse <i>spor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spura</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spurnan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to succeed <i>(+ genitive at something)</i> <i>(impersonal, with dative of person)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spōan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alder buckthorn (<i>Frangula alnus</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sprawl</li><li>to move convulsively</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speaker, talker</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sprekō</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>&#42;spreka</i> (attested in e.g. <i>forspreka</i>) and Old High German <i>sprehho</i>. Equivalent to <i>sprecan</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak, talk</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sprekan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sprecel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) a spot</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sprecol</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sprekl</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sprekal</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;sprekul</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>talkative</li></ol><p>From <i>sprecan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>talkativeness, loquacity</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sprecol</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;accusative) to scatter something</li><li>(transitive,&#32;accusative) to sprinkle something (onto or over something else) (+ on, intō, ofer with dative or accusative)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to burst apart, splinter</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to apply an enema or a suppository</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sprangijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sprangijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sprenġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sprecan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pole</li></ol><p>Originally "sprout, shoot," from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sprut-</i>, <i>&#42;spreutaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>spricþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>spricþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tenterhook</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a spring (source of water)</li><li>an ulcer, sore, pustule</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spring</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;springaz</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leap, bound</li><li>to burst forth (as a fluid), to spurt</li><li>to grow (as a plant)</li><li>to rise (as the sun)</li><li>to spread, be diffused</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;springan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sprout, shoot, twig</li></ol><p>See the verb <i>sprūtan</i> (“to sprout”)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Sprot)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sprat (fish in the genus <i>Sprattus</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sprout</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spreutan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spring</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to spring, sprout, germinate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spruttjan</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)per-</i> (“to strew, sow, sprinkle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speech</li><li>language</li><li>conversation, conference, discussion</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sprāki</i>, variant of <i>&#42;sprāku</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>sprēke</i>; the ō-stem variant led to Old Saxon <i>sprāka</i> and Old High German <i>sprāhha</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a manner or mode of speaking</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sprǣċ</i> (“speech”) +&lrm; <i>cynn</i> (“kind”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a talk</li><li>discourse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sprākijā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>talkative, loquacious</li></ol><p>From <i>sprǣċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ful</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>auditorium of a monastic school</li><li>location in a monastery for receiving guests</li></ol><p>From <i>sprǣċ</i> (“speech”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>speechless</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sprākilausaz</i>, equivalent to <i>sprǣċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>sprāhhalōs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread, expand; outspread, stretch forth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spraidijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spraidijaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)per-</i> (“to strew, sow, sprinkle”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>spreidan</i>, Old High German <i>spreiten</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sprenġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spur</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spurō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spurô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sperH-</i> (“to kick”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kick</li><li>to spurn, reject</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spurnan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spurnaną</i> (“to kick”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stadium</li><li>course, racetrack, racecourse</li><li>a measure of distance, furlong, league; mile</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;spurd</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spurdz</i> (“stadium, racetrack”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sperdʰ-</i> (“contest”). Cognate with Old High German <i>spurt</i> (“racetrack, racing course”), Gothic <i>𐍃𐍀𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (spaurds, “racetrack”), Sanskrit <i>स्पर्धा</i> (spardhā, “competition”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spyrienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to track</li><li>to make a track</li><li>to investigate, examine</li><li>to enquire after or into, seek to know about</li><li>to look for, seek to attain</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spurjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spyrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spyrienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enquiry, investigation</li></ol><p>From <i>spyrian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spyriġung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sperran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sprǣċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sprǣclēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scanty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sparaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sper-</i>. See also Old High German <i>spar</i>, Old Norse <i>sparr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frugal</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sparian</i> +&lrm; <i>-hende</i>, literally “spare-handed”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gypsum, chalk</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spit</li><li>to squirt</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>spēd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stake</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stakō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stakô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stag</li></ol><p>Origin uncertain, yet apparently cognate with Old Norse <i>steggi</i> (“male animal, drake”).</p><p>Possibly from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stakugō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stakugô</i>, <i>&#42;stakigô</i> (“stag, male animal, porcupine”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steg-</i> (“pole, beam, stake, stick”), equivalent to <i>&#42;stac-</i> (compare Old English <i>staca</i>) +&lrm; <i>-ga</i> (diminutive suffix).</p><p>Alternatively, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stagugō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stagugô</i>, <i>&#42;stagigô</i> (“stag, male animal, porcupine”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ste(n)gʰ-</i> (“to prick, stick, pierce, sting”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a staking, piercing with a stake</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>&#42;stacian</i> (“to stake”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i> (compare also <i>staca</i> (“stake”))</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stæf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stacga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>steall</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stealcung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal</li><li>to go, proceed stealthily</li></ol><p>See <i>stelan</i> (“to steal”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>steall</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stealing</li><li>stolen goods</li><li>a fine for stealing</li><li>something done through stealth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stalu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stalō</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>stala</i> (“theft”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stammer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stamrōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stamrōną</i> (“to stammer”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stone</li><li>the manuscript only runic character ᛥ (/st/)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stain</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stainaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steyh₂no-</i>, <i>&#42;stih₂-no-</i> (a suffixed form of <i>&#42;steyh₂-</i> (“to be solid, to crowd together”)).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>stēn</i>, Old Saxon <i>stēn</i> (German Low German <i>Steen</i>), Old Dutch <i>sten</i>, <i>stein</i> (Dutch <i>steen</i>), Old High German <i>stein</i> (German <i>Stein</i>), Old Norse <i>steinn</i> (Icelandic <i>steinn</i>, Faroese <i>steinur</i>, Norwegian Nynorsk <i>stein</i>, Norwegian Bokmål <i>stein</i>, <i>sten</i>, Danish <i>sten</i>, Swedish <i>sten</i>), Gothic <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (stains). The Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek <i>στῖον</i> (stîon, “pebble”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stěnà</i> (Bulgarian <i>стена</i> (stena), Russian <i>стена́</i> (stená), Czech <i>stěna</i> (“wall”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>landform</i>) rocky hill</li></ol><p>From <i>stān</i> (“stone”) +&lrm; <i>beorg</i> (“mountain, hill”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stānbeorg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a natural stone arch, as the entrance of a cave</li></ol><p>From <i>stān</i> (“stone”) +&lrm; <i>boga</i> (“arch”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mountain goat</li></ol><p>From <i>stān</i> (“stone”) +&lrm; <i>bucca</i> (“he-goat”). Cognate with Dutch <i>steenbok</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stony cliff, crag</li></ol><p>From <i>stān</i> (“stone”) +&lrm; <i>clif</i> (“cliff”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a rock</li></ol><p>From <i>stān</i> (“stone”) +&lrm; <i>clūd</i> (“rock”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a rocky peak</li></ol><p>From <i>stān</i> (“stone”) +&lrm; <i>cnoll</i> (“summit”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stonecrop (a plant)</li></ol><p>From <i>stān</i> (“stone”) +&lrm; <i>cropp</i> (“sprout, top of a plant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of stone</li></ol><p>From <i>stān</i> (“stone”) +&lrm; <i>cynn</i> (“kind”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) delay</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;standaz</i>, related to the verb <i>&#42;stāną</i> (“to stand”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand</li><li>to be positioned, located</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;standan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;standaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steh₂-</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>stonda</i>, Old Saxon <i>standan</i>, Old High German <i>stantan</i>, Old Norse <i>standa</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (standan). The Germanic source is cognate with Latin <i>stare</i> and Ancient Greek <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who stands</li></ol><p>From <i>standan</i> (“to stand”) +&lrm; <i>-end</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>standeþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worked with stones; paved</li></ol><p><i>stān</i> (“stone”) +&lrm; <i>fāh</i> (“decorated, adorned”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a stone vessel</li></ol><p>From <i>stān</i> (“stone”) +&lrm; <i>fæt</i> (“vessel”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pelican</li></ol><p>From <i>stān</i> (“stone”) +&lrm; <i>ġiellan</i> (“to yell”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a rocky slope</li></ol><p>From <i>stān</i> (“stone”) +&lrm; <i>hliþ</i> (“slope”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stony, rocky</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stainagaz</i> (“stony”), equivalent to <i>stān</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stony, rocky</li></ol><p>From <i>stān</i> +&lrm; <i>-iht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mortar</li></ol><p>From <i>stān</i> (“stone”) +&lrm; <i>līm</i> (“lime”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parsley</li></ol><p>From <i>stān</i> (“stone”) +&lrm; <i>mereċe</i> (“wild cabbage”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a high rock; peak</li><li>an obelisk</li></ol><p>From <i>stān</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;rocc</i>. Compare Middle Low German <i>stênrotse</i>, <i>stênrutse</i> (“rock, rockface, cave, grotto”), Middle High German <i>steinrotsche</i>, <i>steinrosche</i> (“rock face, cliff, rock fissure, mountainside”), modern German <i>Steinrutsche</i> (“rock chute”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Stānwulf</i>, <i>recorded in the Thorney Liber Vitae</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stonewall, stone wall, rampart</li></ol><p>From <i>stān</i> +&lrm; <i>weall</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a way made of stone</li></ol><p>From <i>stān</i> (“stone”) +&lrm; <i>weġ</i> (“way”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stonework, stone building</li></ol><p>From <i>stān</i> +&lrm; <i>weorc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worship of stones</li></ol><p>From <i>stān</i> (“stone”) +&lrm; <i>weorþung</i> (“worship, reverence”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>stān</i> (“stone”) and <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mason, stonewright</li></ol><p>From <i>stān</i> (“stone”) +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“wright”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stepper</li><li>grasshopper, locust</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stapō</i> (“stepper”). The sense "grasshopper or locust" is possibly a clipping of <i>græsstapa</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>&#42;stafo</i> (attested in <i>hewistafo</i> “grasshopper, locust”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stapol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basis</li><li>post; trunk of a tree, support, stay</li><li>pillar, column</li><li>threshold; flight of stairs</li><li>market, court</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stapul</i>.</p><p>Akin to Old Frisian <i>stapul</i> (“tooth-stem”), Middle Low German <i>stapel</i> (“execution block”) (German <i>Stapel</i> (“beam, stake”)), Old High German <i>staffal</i>, Old Norse <i>stöpull</i>, Old English <i>stæf</i> (“staff”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stapol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stare</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;starēn</i>, see also Old High German <i>starēn</i>, Old Norse <i>stara</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>staþolfæstnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>staþolfæstnung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>staþolfæstnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>staþol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>staþolian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foundation, establishment</li><li>fixed position</li><li>heavenly firmament</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;staþulaz</i>, whence also Old High German <i>stadal</i>, Old Norse <i>stöðull</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>steadfast, stable, firm</li><li>stationary</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) unwavering, unyielding, constant</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>staþol</i> +&lrm; <i>-fæst</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>steadfast, firm</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>staþolfæst</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>steadfastly, firmly</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>staþolfæst</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>, else <i>staþolfæstlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>staþolfæstlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steadfastness, stability</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>staþolfæst</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ġestaþolfæstnian</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>staþolfæst</i> +&lrm; <i>-nian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foundation</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>staþolfæstnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>staþolfæstnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to establish; to found</li><li>to make steadfast</li></ol><p>From <i>staþol</i> (“base; foundation”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>founder</li></ol><p>From <i>staþolian</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firmament, foundation</li></ol><p>From <i>staþol</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>founding, foundation, settling</li></ol><p>From <i>staþol</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>estate, landed possession</li></ol><p>From <i>staþol</i> +&lrm; <i>ǣht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>staþol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>staþole</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lofty</li><li>steep, precipitous</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stalkaz</i>, <i>&#42;stilkaz</i> (“high, lofty, precipitous, steep”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)tAlg- &#47; -e-</i> (“to jut out, protrude”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the act of going stealthily</li><li>cautious or careful walking; stalking</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stalukungu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stalukōną</i> (“to stalk, move stealthily”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)telg</i>, <i>&#42;(s)tolg-</i>. Equivalent to <i>&#42;stealcian</i> (“to stalk”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>. Compare Old Frisian <i>stalkense</i> (“stealthiness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a position, especially one that is standing; position of affairs, state, condition, standing, situation; stead</li><li>place, stead</li><li>a place for cattle, stall, stable</li><li>fishing ground, place to catch fish</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stallaz</i>, <i>&#42;staþl-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steh₂-</i> (“to stand”) and Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stel-</i>. Akin to Old Frisian <i>stal</i>, Old High German <i>stall</i> (German <i>Stall</i> (“stable”), <i>Stelle</i> (“place”)), Old Norse <i>stallr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steam (water vapor)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;staumaz</i>, compare also Dutch <i>stoom</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>steep</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;staupaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>severe, stern, harsh</li><li>stiff, rigid, not bending, unyielding</li><li>hard, rough</li><li>strong, violent, impetuous, vehement</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stark</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old High German <i>stark</i> (German <i>stark</i>), Old Norse <i>sterkr</i> (Swedish <i>stark</i>, Danish <i>stærk</i>, Norwegian Bokmål <i>sterk</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stubborn, obstinate</li></ol><p>From <i>stearc</i> +&lrm; <i>-mōd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>some kind of bird</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;staraz</i>, compare the modern <i>starling</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>steorra</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kick or struggle</li><li>to caper</li><li>to react with fear</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;startlōn</i>, <i>&#42;startilōn</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>stirtla</i> (“to straighten up, raise up, hobble, stagger”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>staþolfæstnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stybb</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a steed, stallion</li><li>a male camel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stōdijō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place</li><li>position</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stadi</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>stat</i>, Old Norse <i>staðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>firmly fixed; rooted to one spot; secure</li><li>(of a person,&#32;figurative) steadfast</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stadifast</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stadifastaz</i>; equivalent to <i>stede</i> +&lrm; <i>-fæst</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steadfastness, consistency</li></ol><p>From <i>stedefæst</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voice</li><li>alternative form of <i>stemn</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stebnu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stebnō</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>stifne</i>, Old Saxon <i>stemna</i>, Old High German <i>stimna</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐍄𐌹𐌱𐌽𐌰</i> (stibna).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stamniz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) prow, stern</li></ol><p>Weak variant of Old English <i>stemn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>&#42;Steinmarr</i>, likely from the compounds <i>steinn</i> (“stone”) + <i>mærr</i> (“famous, glorious”). Cognate with Icelandic <i>Steinmar</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stalk (of a plant)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stīele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to place, set</li><li>alternative form of <i>stiellan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stalljan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stīeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stem</li><li>alternative form of <i>stefn</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stamni</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stamniz</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stebnō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stamp or stomp</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stampijan</i>, possibly from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stampijaną</i> (“to compress, squeeze, stamp”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to groan loudly; roar</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stenan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smell</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stankwiz</i>, whence also Old High German <i>stank</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stenecian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pant, emit breath with effort</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stanikōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stanikōną</i>, <i>&#42;stanukōną</i> (“to make a sound repeatedly, pant”), equivalent to <i>stenan</i> +&lrm; <i>-cian</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>stanka</i> (whence Swedish <i>stånka</i> (“to pant”), Scots <i>stank</i>, <i>stunk</i> (“to breathe heavy, pant”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pole</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stangi</i>, variant of <i>&#42;stangu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stangō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Stānwulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>deprived of a relative</li><li>step-</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;steupa-</i> (“bereft, pushed out”).</p><p>Akin to Old Frisian <i>stiap-</i>, Old High German <i>stiof-</i> (German <i>stief-</i>), Old Norse <i>stjup-</i> (Swedish <i>styv-</i>), Middle Low German <i>stef-</i>, Dutch <i>stief-</i>, Old English <i>āstīepan</i> (“to bereave, orphan”), <i>bestīepan</i> (“to deprive of children”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orphan</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;steupabarną</i>; equivalent to <i>stēop-</i> +&lrm; <i>bearn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stepchild</li><li>orphan</li></ol><p>From <i>stēop-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċild</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stepdaughter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;steupaduhtēr</i>. Equivalent to <i>stēop-</i> +&lrm; <i>dohtor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stepfather</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;steupafadēr</i>. By surface analysis, <i>stēop-</i> +&lrm; <i>fæder</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stepmother</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;steupamōdēr</i>. By surface analysis, <i>stēop-</i> +&lrm; <i>mōdor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stepson</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;steupasunuz</i>. Equivalent to <i>stēop-</i> +&lrm; <i>sunu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young bull</li><li>rudder</li><li>steering, direction, guidance</li><li>rule, regulation; correction, discipline, reproof; rebuke, check, restraint</li><li>punishment, penalty</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;steur</i>, see also Old High German <i>stior</i>, Old Norse <i>stjórr</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stiuriją</i>, from the verb <i>&#42;stiurijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stīeran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) The right side of a ship when facing forward; starboard.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;steurubord</i>, equivalent to <i>stēor</i> (“steering, guidance, direction”) +&lrm; <i>bord</i> (“board, side”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>direction, discipline</li><li>a rule, regulation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;steuri-</i> (“to guide, direct”). Akin to Old English <i>stīeran</i> (“to guide, steer”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>director; ruler, leader, governor</li><li>someone who brings correction, reprover, guide</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;steuri-</i> (“to guide, direct”). Akin to Old English <i>stīeran</i> (“to guide, steer”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steersman, captain (of a ship)</li></ol><p>From <i>stēor</i> (“steering”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person, man”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pestilence, mortality</li><li>dead animals and their flesh</li><li>dead things and their places of death</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sterbō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sterbô</i>; compare <i>steorfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sterban</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sterbaną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>sterva</i>, Old Saxon/Old Dutch <i>stervan</i>, Old High German <i>sterban</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without restrain; unruly, ungovernable, fierce</li><li>without regulation, rule or instruction, profligate, foolish, ignorant</li></ol><p>From <i>stēor</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steersman, captain (of a ship)</li></ol><p>From <i>stēor</i> (“steering”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person, man”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forehead</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stirniju</i> (“forehead”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>star</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sterrō</i>, variant of <i>&#42;sternō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sternǭ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂stḗr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astronomer, astrologer</li></ol><p>From <i>steorra</i> (“star”) +&lrm; <i>sċēawere</i> (“watcher”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) tail</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stert</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stertaz</i> (“tail”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astrology</li></ol><p>From <i>steorra</i> (“star”) +&lrm; <i>wiġle</i> (“divination”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astrologer</li></ol><p>From <i>steorra</i> +&lrm; <i>wiġlere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astrology</li></ol><p>From <i>steorra</i> +&lrm; <i>wiġlung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stīepan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stæpe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stīepel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stæppan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stǣr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>steorra</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stēopfæder</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stick</li><li>peg</li><li>rod or beam</li><li>the pointer of a dial</li><li>a piece of wood used as a spoon, wooden spoon</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stikkō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stikkô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)teyg-</i> (“to pierce, prick, be sharp”), whence also Old High German <i>stecko</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>styċċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>styċċemǣlum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stitch, in the following senses:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a prick, puncture, or stab</li><li>a pricking or stabbing pain or sensation</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stiki</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stikiz</i>, whence also Old High German <i>stih</i> (“prick, sting, stitch”), Gothic <i>𐍃𐍄𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (stiks, “point”), Old Frisian <i>stek</i>, <i>steke</i> (“point”) and Old Saxon <i>stiki</i> (“prick, stab”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sticol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pierce, to stab, to prick, to stick</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to remain embedded, to be fastened, to stick</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to run, to lie (<i>in a particular direction</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stikōną</i>, closely related to <i>&#42;stikaną</i> (“to stick”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lofty</li><li>rugged, difficult, steep</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stahlī</i> (“steel weapon”), derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stahlą</i> (“steel”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of steel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stahlīn</i>, equivalent to <i>stīele</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leap</li><li>to stand</li></ol><p>Compare the noun <i>stiell</i>, <i>styll</i> (“leap”).</p><p>Equivalent to <i>steall</i> (“position, place”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i>. For the phonetic development of <i>&#42;-allij-</i>, compare Early West Saxon <i>fiellan, fielde</i>, Anglian <i>fellan, felde</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give off, emit (a scent, odour)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;staumijaną</i>, from <i>&#42;staum-</i> (“steam”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise, build high, erect</li><li>to exalt, elevate</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>stēap</i> (“steep”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tower</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;staupil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;staupilaz</i>, from <i>&#42;staupaz</i> (“high, steep”) + <i>&#42;-ilaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>stēap</i> +&lrm; <i>-el</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>stǫpull</i> (“steeple, tower”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steer, guide, rule, direct</li><li>to correct, restrain, withhold</li><li>to reprove, chide, rebuke</li><li>to punish</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stiurijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stiurijaną</i>, whence also Old High German <i>stiuren</i>, Old Norse <i>stýra</i>. Related to Albanian <i>shtie</i> (“urge, drive, pour”) and <i>shtyj</i> (“to push”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Stiff, firm, hard</li><li>Strong, fixed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stīf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>path (especially steep or narrow)</li><li>hall, building, sty</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stīgu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stīgō</i>, from <i>&#42;stīganą</i> (“climb”) ( &gt; Old English <i>stīgan</i>).</p><p>Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>stige</i>, Old High German <i>stiga</i>. A masculine Germanic variant <i>*stīgaz</i> is indicated by Old High German <i>stic</i> (German <i>Steig</i>), Old Norse <i>stígr</i> (Swedish <i>stig</i>).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stiją</i>, possibly related to Etymology 1 above, or otherwise possibly ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steyh₂-</i> (“to stiffen”), similar to <i>&#42;stainaz</i> (“stone”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>stí</i> (Danish <i>sti</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go</li><li>to go up; ascend; climb</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stīgan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stīganą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steygʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Stigand; last Anglo-Saxon Archbishop of Canterbury.</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>Stígandr</i> meaning "wanderer".</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stile</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stigillju</i>, <i>&#42;stigilu</i>, whence also Old High German <i>stigilla</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailor</li><li>stye (inflammation of the eyelid)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>stīgan</i> (“to ascend”) +&lrm; <i>-end</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stirrup</li></ol><p>From <i>stiġe</i> (“ascent or descent”) +&lrm; <i>rāp</i> (“rope”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steward</li></ol><p>From <i>stiġ</i> (“hall”) +&lrm; <i>weard</i> (“guard”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dispose, arrange, regulate</li><li>to instigate, incite</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stihtijan</i>, <i>&#42;stihtōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stihtiz</i> (“a step, track, overpass”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steygʰ-</i> (“to go, climb”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) become calm, abate, subside</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) calm, pacify, assuage <i>(+ dative or accusative)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stillijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>still</li><li>quiet</li><li>calm</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stillī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stilnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stilnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stillness</li><li>quiet</li><li>calm, calmness, peace</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stillinassī</i>. Equivalent to <i>stille</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stilnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stenan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smell (give off a scent; intransitive)</li><li>to stink (smell bad; intransitive)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stinkwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sting</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sting, stinging (of an animal)</li><li>stab, thrust made with a pointed instrument; the wound made by a stab or sting</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stingaz</i>; akin to <i>stingan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stab</li><li>to sting</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stingan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stinganą</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>stinga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stician</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stirc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>steorra</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stīepel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stiġrāp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stirk</li><li>calf</li><li>bullock</li><li>heifer</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stirian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stirienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>styrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>styria</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stirian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stirienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>styring</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>stīþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>stīþnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stiff</li><li>hard, firm</li><li>(of people) stern, harsh, strict</li><li>(of things that cause discomfort or require effort, such as weather, conflict, illness, punishment) harsh, severe</li><li>(of taste) harsh, bitter</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stinþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stinþaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>resolute</li><li>stern</li><li>stubborn, obstinate</li></ol><p>From <i>stīþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-mōd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stiffness</li><li>hardness, firmness</li><li>(of people) sternness, harshness, strictness</li><li>(of things that cause discomfort or require effort, such as weather, conflict, illness, punishment) harshness, severity</li><li>(of taste) harshness, bitterness</li></ol><p><i>stīþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>stīþ</i> (“stern, harsh”) + <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house, dwelling; place</li><li>alternative spelling of <i>stocc</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stukką</i>, related to <i>&#42;stukkaz</i> (“post, beam”), whence also Old High German <i>stoc</i>, Old Norse <i>stokkr</i>, Old English <i>stocc</i> (“stock, stump”).</p><p>see <i>stocc</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>log</li><li>stump</li><li>stake, post</li><li>a post to which a person may be tied, stocks</li><li>trumpet, wooden trumpet</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stokk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stukkaz</i> (“tree-trunk”). Akin to Old High German <i>stoc</i> (German <i>Stock</i>), Old Norse <i>stokkr</i> (“tree-trunk”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enclosure for breeding horses</li><li>stud (horse used for breeding)</li><li>a group of studs</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stōdą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steh₂-</i> (“stand, set”).</p><p>Cognate with Old High German <i>stuot</i> (“herd of horses”) (German <i>Stute</i> (“mare”)), Old Norse <i>stóð</i> (Swedish <i>sto</i> (“mare”)). The Indo-European root is also the source of Albanian <i>shtazë</i> (“animal, beast”) and Old Church Slavonic <i>стадо</i> (stado, “herd”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a studhorse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stōdahross</i> (“breeding horse”), equivalent to <i>stōd</i> +&lrm; <i>hors</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>stuotros</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>stofu</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stubō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a plant</i>) stem</li><li>(<i>of a tree</i>) stump</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) offspring; progeny</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stōmnaz</i>, a derivative of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stōmô</i> (“base, foundation”). Compare Old Norse <i>stomn</i>, <i>stofn</i> (“stem; stump; foundation; stock”), Gothic <i>𐍃𐍄𐍉𐌼𐌰</i> (stōma).</p><p>Alternatively, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stubnaz</i>, a derivative of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stubbaz</i> (“stump”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a room with a heated bath</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stubu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chair, seat</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stōl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>standan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>stondeþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bucket</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stoppō</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>stoppo</i>. Equivalent to <i>&#42;stoppian</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>, literally “stopper”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stop</li><li>to close (a hole or aperture)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stoppōn</i> (“to stop, close, push, prick”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>stuppōn</i> (“to stop up”), Old High German <i>stoffōn</i>, <i>stoppōn</i> (“to stop up”), Old High German <i>stophōn</i> (“to pierce”). More at stump.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frankincense</li><li>storax</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>great</li><li>strong, violent</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>storax</i></p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stōraz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stork</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sturkaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>storm</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sturm</i>, whence also Old Saxon <i>storm</i>, Old High German <i>sturm</i>, Old Norse <i>stormr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resin</li></ol><p>From <i>stōr</i> (“frankincense”) +&lrm; <i>sæp</i> (“sap”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) an inferior type of horse</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stott</i>, <i>&#42;stutt</i> (“castrated animal, gelding”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stuttijan</i> (“to mutilate, cut off, castrate”); or alternatively from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;steutaz</i>, <i>&#42;stūtaz</i> (“something clipped or docked, stump”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)tewd-</i> (“to bump, push, hit”). Compare Old Norse <i>stútr</i> (“a young ox, bull”) (whence Danish <i>stud</i> (“bullock, steer”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place</li><li>a place on the body</li><li>a place that is built; house, collection of houses, habitation, dwelling</li><li>a place, position, or spot in a series</li><li>a room, stead</li><li>a place or passage in a book</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stōu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stōō</i> (“a place, location, position”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steh₂-</i> (“to stand, place, put”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold back, restrain</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stōjaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stoh₂éyeti</i>. Equivalent to <i>stōw</i> (“place”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix) and cognate with Gothic <i>𐍃𐍄𐍉𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (stōjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>local</li></ol><p>From <i>stōw</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>locally</li></ol><p>From <i>stōwlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stroke</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;straikōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beach</li><li>shore</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;strandō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;strang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong-handed</li></ol><p>Only attested in a single passage by Ælfric, translating the Latin phrase <i>fortis manum</i>. It is impossible to know whether Ælfric coined the word on the spot or it was already in use. Given the commonness of the suffix <i>-hende</i>, it could have been a very natural construction, being coined many times independently when the occasion arose in idiomatic speech. Equivalent to <i>strang</i> +&lrm; <i>-hende</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>strongly</li><li>violently</li><li>powerfully, forcefully</li><li>boldly, bravely</li><li>firmly, sternly</li></ol><p><i>strang</i> (“strong”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>stranglīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>stranglīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>resolute</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strength</li><li>force, violence</li></ol><p><i>strang</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>strangnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>strēawberġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>strēawberġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stream</li><li>current</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;straum</i>.</p><p>Germanic cognates include Old Frisian <i>strām</i>, Old Saxon <i>strōm</i>, Old High German <i>stroum</i>, Old Norse <i>straumr</i>. Extra-Germanic cognates include Ancient Greek <i>ῥεῦμα</i> (rheûma), Polish <i>strumień</i>, Albanian <i>rrymë</i> (“flow, current”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>current, flow</li></ol><p>From <i>strēam</i> (“stream”) +&lrm; <i>faru</i> (“journey”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>streambed</li></ol><p>From <i>strēam</i> (“stream”) +&lrm; <i>rād</i> (“road”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shore</li></ol><p>From <i>strēam</i> (“stream”) +&lrm; <i>weall</i> (“wall”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>straw</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;strau</i> (“that which is strewn”).</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>strē</i>, Old Saxon <i>strō</i> (Dutch <i>stro</i>), Old High German <i>strō</i> (German <i>Stroh</i>), Old Norse <i>strá</i> (Swedish <i>strå</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strawberry</li></ol><p>From <i>strēaw</i> (“straw”) +&lrm; <i>berġe</i> (“berry”). See English <i>strawberry</i> for possible explanations.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>strēaberġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strew</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;strauwjan</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>stroien</i>, (Dutch <i>strooien</i>), Old High German <i>strewen</i>, Old Norse <i>strá</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch, hold out, extend</li><li>to spread out</li><li>to prostrate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;strakkjan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>streġdan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to strew</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;strauwjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;strawjaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;strow-éye-ti</i>. Doublet of <i>strēawian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strew, spread, scatter, sprinkle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;streþaną</i> (“to swirl, seethe”), related to Middle High German <i>streden</i>, Old High German <i>stredan</i> (“to swirl”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;srét-e-</i>, related to Old Cornish <i>stret</i> (“latex”). See also modern English <i>strut</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>strǣl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>strȳnan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>string, rope, cord</li><li>strength, power, vigour, fortitude</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;strangiz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;strengʰ-</i>. Compare Old High German <i>strang</i> and Old Norse <i>strengr</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;strangiju</i>, perhaps a regularisation of <i>&#42;strangī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;strangį̄</i> (“strength”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>severe, hard, strict</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;strangī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;strangijaz</i> (“tense, tense”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a gallant leader</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>strenġu</i> (“strength”) +&lrm; <i>þengel</i> (“prince”), as if Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;strangil</i>. Compare <i>fenġel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>strenġu</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>strang</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>strang</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;strangist</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;strangistaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>strenġu</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>strang</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>.</p></html>:/ˈstrenɡrɑ/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>strang</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;strangiʀō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strength, power, vigour, fortitude</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;strangī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;strangį̄</i>. Equivalent to <i>strang</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>strengþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>strengþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strength</li><li>moral strength; virtue</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;strangiþu</i>, equivalent to <i>strang</i> +&lrm; <i>-þu</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>strengida</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gain, acquisition, treasure</li><li>begetting, generating</li><li>power</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;streuną</i> (“heap, treasure, profit, gain”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;strew-</i> (“to spread, strew”) (cognate with Old Saxon <i>gistriuni</i>, Old High German <i>gistriuni</i> (“gain, property, wealth, business”), Latin <i>strues</i> (“heap”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To gain, acquire, beget. See <i>strȳnan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>strēon</i> (“treasure, power”) + <i>berht</i> (“bright”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Whitby (a town in North Yorkshire, England)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>strēones</i> (genitive of <i>strēon</i> (“treasure, power”)) +&lrm; <i>healh</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>strēaw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>strēawberġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>strēawberġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bed, resting place</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>strēawian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Anglian</i>) alternative form of <i>strǣt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>strēawian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Meaning unclear&#59; potentially <i>plague</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass lightly over, stroke, smooth, rub</li><li>to go, move, proceed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;strīkan</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Dutch <i>strijken</i>, German <i>streichen</i> and <i>streiken</i>, Danish <i>stryge</i>, Icelandic <i>strýkja</i>, <i>strýkva</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strickle</li><li>breast, teat</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stride, straddle, mount</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;strīdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>strȳnan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>generation; race; kin; stock; tribe</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;striundiz</i>, from <i>&#42;streuną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>strȳnan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>strēon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>struggle, fight, contest</li><li>dispute, contention, quarrel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Saxon <i>strīd</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;strīd</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;strīdaz</i>. Occurs only in the Genesis text. Cognate with Old Norse <i>stríð</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marshy land (overgrown with brushwood)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;strōd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>strang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>strengra</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strap</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>struppus</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>strop</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pillage, bereave; rob, plunder, loot; defraud</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ostrich</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>strūthiō</i> (“ostrich”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand out stiffly or projectingly</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gain, acquire</li><li>to beget</li></ol><p>I-mutated form of <i>strēonan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;streuną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;strew-</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>strīend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to waste</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>strūta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strict, severe, firm, rigorous, stern, hard</li><li>rigid, unyielding, obstinate, persistent</li><li>violent, using force, uncompromising, vehement, strenuous</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strictness, rigor</li><li>violence, force</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;strak(k)</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;strakaz</i>, <i>&#42;strakkaz</i> (“straight”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;streg-</i>, <i>&#42;treg-</i> (“still, rigid”). Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>strac</i> and Middle Low German <i>strac</i> (“tight, taut”), Middle High German <i>strac</i> (“straight”). More at stretch.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;straką</i>, <i>&#42;strakką</i> (“straightness”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;streg-</i>, <i>&#42;treg-</i> (“still, rigid”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Britons of Strathclyde; the Cumbrians</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrow</li><li>beam, ray (e.g. of light)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;strālu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a plant, possibly club moss</li></ol><p>From <i>strǣl</i> (“arrow”) +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i> (“plant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>street, road</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;strātu</i>, from Latin <i>strāta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stybb</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prop</li><li>pillar</li><li>post</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stuþ</i>, see also Old Norse <i>stoð</i>. Compare German <i>Stütze</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hot-air or vapour bath</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;stufu</i> +&lrm; <i>bæþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>furtive, secretive, clandestine, acting with stealth, stealthy</li><li>stolen</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stulr</i>. Related to Old Saxon <i>stulina</i> (“theft”), Old High German <i>stulingūn</i> (“secretly, in a stealthy way, stealthily”), Old Norse <i>stuldr</i> (“theft”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time, while</li><li>a period of time, an hour</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at once, forthwith, immediately</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stundu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stundō</i> (“point in time, hour”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stut-</i> (“prop”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stā-</i>, <i>&#42;sth-</i> (“to stand”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>stunda</i> (Dutch <i>stonde</i>), Old High German <i>stunta</i> (German <i>Stunde</i>), Old Norse <i>stund</i> (Swedish <i>stund</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>little by little, at intervals, gradually, at times</li><li>at other times, alternately</li><li>now and then</li></ol><p>From <i>stund</i> +&lrm; <i>-mǣlum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to make a loud sound; crash</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to strike with a loud sound; dash</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stunōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stunōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stupid, foolish</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) idiot, fool</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stunt</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stuntaz</i> (“short, stunted; stupid”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foolish</li></ol><p>From <i>stunt</i> (“foolish, stupid”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>foolishly, stupidly</li></ol><p>From <i>stuntlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>stuntlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stoop, bend over</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stūpōną</i>, <i>&#42;stūpijaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)tewb-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gnat, midge, biting fly</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stybb</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stump of a tree; stub</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stubb</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stubbaz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>stubbr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stepney, England</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;Stybban</i> (genitive form of an unattested personal name) or <i>&#42;stybban</i> (“stump's”) + <i>hȳþ</i> (“landing-place”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piece</li><li>a piece of money, a mite; a coin</li><li>a short span of time, a little while</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stukkī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stukkiją</i> (“piece”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>stukki</i>, Old High German <i>stucki</i>, Old Norse <i>stykki</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>piece by piece</li></ol><p><i>styċċe</i> +&lrm; <i>-mǣlum</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>styċċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stede</i> (“position, place”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stedefæstnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to root up</li><li>to clear away</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;stȳfan</i> (“to blunten, cut off, shorten”) +&lrm; <i>-ician</i>. Cognate with Middle Low German <i>stûven</i> (“to blunt, trim, cut down”), Old Norse <i>stýfa</i> (“to cut off, chop off, curtail”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stīele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stīelen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stīeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sting</i></li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stungi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stungiz</i>.</p><p>Less likely, a spelling variant of <i>sting</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sting</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stingaz</i>. Either way, from the root of <i>stingan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blunt, to dull</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stuntijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stīepel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stīeran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>styria</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>styrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>styrienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) sturgeon</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sturjō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sturjô</i> (“sturgeon”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>sturio</i> (“sturgeon”), Old High German <i>sturjo</i> (“sturgeon”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive&#32;and&#32;intransitive</i>) to move</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sturjan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stirc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moving</li><li>animate</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>styria</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>styrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>styrienne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>movement, motion</li><li>(<i>of violent movement</i>) disturbance, agitation, commotion</li><li>(<i>of the mind</i>) perturbation, emotion</li><li>(<i>of a body</i>) shaking</li></ol><p>From <i>styrian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to storm, be stormy, rage</li><li>to storm, cry aloud, shout</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sturmijaną</i> (“to storm, be stormy”). Cognate with Old High German <i>sturmen</i>, Old Norse <i>styrma</i>. More at storm.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>steorra</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>styring</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>staff</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) letter, written character</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stabaz</i>, whence also Old High German <i>stab</i>, Old Norse <i>stafr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) grammar</li><li>knowledge of letters, lettering</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) learning, study</li></ol><p>From <i>stæf</i> (“letter”) +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i> (“knowledge, skill”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grammarian</li></ol><p>Regularly derived from <i>stæfcræft</i> (“grammar”) using the suffixes <i>-iġ</i> and <i>-a</i>. Also equivalent to <i>stæf</i> (“letter”) +&lrm; <i>cræftiga</i> (“skilled worker, professional”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Staffordshire</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a combination of letters</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) a syllable</li><li>(<i>when formed of two letters</i>) diphthong</li><li>formation of letter in writing</li><li>letters</li></ol><p>From <i>stæf</i> (“letter”) + <i>ġefēġ</i> (“joining, combination”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grammatical error</li></ol><p>From <i>stæf</i> (“letter; grammarical”) +&lrm; <i>leahtor</i> (“fault, error”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stæfleahter</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>literal</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) of or pertaining to letters</li><li>literate, literary</li></ol><p>From <i>stæf</i> (“letter”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stefn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alphabet</li></ol><p>From <i>stæf</i> (“letter”) +&lrm; <i>rōf</i> (“set, array”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alphabet</li></ol><p>From <i>stæf</i> (“letter”) +&lrm; <i>rǣw</i> (“row”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sword cane</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stabaswerd</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>stafswerd</i>, Old High German <i>stabswert</i>. Equivalent to <i>stæf</i> +&lrm; <i>sweord</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>literate</li><li>lettered</li></ol><p><i>stæf</i> (“letter”) +&lrm; <i>wīs</i> (“wise”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writer, author, writer of letters</li><li>grammarian</li></ol><p><i>stæf</i> (“letter, grammar”) +&lrm; <i>wrītere</i> (“writer, scribe”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a stay (rope); a rope supporting a mast</li><li>a pool; pond</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stag</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stagą</i> (“stay, rope”).</p><p>From Latin <i>stagnum</i> (“pond”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>steep</li><li>abrupt</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;staigil</i> (“steep”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stair, step; staircase</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>steep</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;staigri</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;staigriz</i> (“stair, step, scaffolding”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;staigr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;staigraz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>steall</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to establish, found, institute</li><li>to continue, carry on</li><li>to admit, confess; base, declare</li><li>to charge, accuse, impute wrong-doing to, lay blame</li></ol><p>Perhaps an alteration of earlier <i>&#42;stæþlan</i> or derived from <i>stāl</i> (“charge, lawsuit, strife, resistance”). Compare Old English <i>staþol</i> (“base, foundation”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decoy reindeer (used to catch wild reindeer)</li></ol><p>First element unclear, but the second part of the compound is <i>hrān</i> (“reindeer”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>steall</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a person</i>) able to stand in good stead; serviceable</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly a contraction of <i>staþol</i> +&lrm; <i>-wierþe</i>, or from <i>stǣl</i> +&lrm; <i>-wierþe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stǣlwierþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a stean (<i>stone or earthen vessel</i>), jar, pot</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stainijā</i> (“stone pot, crucible”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set or adorn with stones</li><li>to stone, throw stones (at)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stainijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of stone</li><li>stony</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stainīnaz</i>, equivalent to <i>stān</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stony ground</li></ol><p>Formed on analogy with other fossilised z-stem plurals (e.g. Old English <i>hæteru</i> (“clothing”), Old English <i>sċerero</i>/<i>sċeruro</i> (“scissors”), Old English <i>sunor</i> (“swine herd”), etc.); perhaps hearkening back to a Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stainiʀu</i>. Equivalent to <i>stān</i> +&lrm; <i>-ru</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stǣner</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stǣner</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stenġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stāniġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stony</li></ol><p>From <i>stǣniġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>, literally “stony-like”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stāniht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stǣniġlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>step</li><li>degree</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stapiz</i>, whence also Old High German <i>stapfo</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>step by step; gradually, by degrees</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stapimālum</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>stapfmālum</i>. Equivalent to <i>stæpe</i> +&lrm; <i>-mǣlum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to step</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stappjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stapjaną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>steppa</i>, Old Saxon <i>steppian</i>, Old High German <i>stepfen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>starling</li><li>a stare</li><li>history</li><li>story; narrative</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;star</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;staraz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)tern-</i> (“starling”) and/or Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;storo-</i> (“starling”).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;star-</i> (“to be rigid”), from <i>&#42;ster-</i> (“to be stiff, to be strong”).</p><p>Uncertain. Perhaps an alteration of earlier <i>&#42;stœ̄r</i> (compare Old High German <i>storia</i> (“history”)), ultimately from Latin <i>historia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἱστορία</i> (historía). Compare also Old English <i>stēor</i> (“guidance, direction”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>completely blind; stone-blind</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;starablindaz</i>, literally “stiff blind.” Composed of <i>&#42;stær</i> +&lrm; <i>blind</i>, if the first element was still a word and not a cranberry morpheme.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>historian</li></ol><p>From <i>stǣr</i> (“history”) +&lrm; <i>leornere</i> (“student, scholar”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>starling</li></ol><p>From <i>stær</i> (“starling”) +&lrm; <i>-ling</i> (diminutive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stearn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writer of history, historian</li></ol><p>From <i>stǣr</i> +&lrm; <i>wrītere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>staithe, riverbank, river shore</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;staþaz</i>. Cognate with Crimean Gothic <i>statz</i>, Old High German <i>stad</i>, <i>stado</i> (German <i>Gestade</i>), Old Norse <i>stóð</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stæþswealwe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sand martin (a bird)</li></ol><p>From <i>stæþ</i> (“shore”) +&lrm; <i>swealwe</i> (“swallow”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>stēda</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sow (female pig)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sū</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sūz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;suH-</i>. Germanic cognates include Old Saxon <i>sū</i>, Old High German <i>sū</i> (whence German <i>Sau</i>), Old Norse <i>sýr</i> (whence Swedish <i>so</i>). Other Indo-European cognates: Ancient Greek <i>ὗς</i> (hûs), Latin <i>sus</i>, Russian <i>свинья́</i> (svinʹjá).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweþel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Swafield (a village and civil parish of North Norfolk district, Norfolk, England).</li></ol><p>Attested in the 1086 Domesday Book. From earlier <i>&#42;Swæþfeld</i>, equivalent to <i>swæþ</i> (“track”) + <i>feld</i> (“field”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Swǣfhām</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subdeacon</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>subdiāconus</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suck</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sūkaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;suk-</i>. Akin to <i>sūgan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outcome, result</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of bird, potentially the whitethroat; some sources connect this with the hedge sparrow</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Sūþġeweorc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweġer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweġelhorn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything eaten with bread, e.g. meat, vegetables, butter, cheese, etc</li><li>any food as flavor for bread</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;suflą</i> (“entremets, viands”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seu-</i>, <i>&#42;sew-</i> (“juice; moisture; rain”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sufel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seofon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suck</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sūgan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sūganą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>suċġa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swīgian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sow (female pig)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sugu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) sickness</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;suhti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;suhtiz</i>. Equivalent to <i>sēoc</i> +&lrm; <i>-þ</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>suht</i>, Old High German <i>suht</i>, Old Norse <i>sótt</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐌰𐌿𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (sauhts).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plough</li><li>furrow, gully</li><li>a measure of land</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sulh</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sulhs</i> (“plough”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;selk-</i> (“to drag, to furrow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plough (implement)</li><li>plough, ploughland (measure of land)</li><li>furrow; gully</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sulh</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sulhs</i> (“plough”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;selk-</i> (“to drag, to furrow”). Cognate with Latin <i>sulcus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>some</li><li><i>used to indicate a specific example of a thing:</i> a, a certain</li><li>some of <i>(with another pronoun or article)</i></li><li>one of <i>(+ genitive)</i></li><li>one of a certain number of things <i>(+ genitive of number)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sum</i>, see also Old High German <i>sum</i>, Old Norse <i>sumr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>sumor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>summerhouse</li></ol><p>From <i>sumer</i> +&lrm; <i>hūs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>summer heat</li></ol><p>From <i>sumer</i> +&lrm; <i>hǣte</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sumorlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw near to summer</li></ol><p>From <i>sumer</i> +&lrm; <i>-lǣċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>summer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sumar</i>, see also Old Saxon <i>sumar</i>, Old High German <i>sumar</i>, Old Norse <i>sumar</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sumerhūs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) (of a day) as long as in the summer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sumaralang</i>. Cognate Old Saxon <i>sumarlang</i>, Middle High German <i>sumerlang</i>. Equivalent to <i>sumor</i> +&lrm; <i>lang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>summer; summerly; summerlike</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sumaralīkaz</i>, equivalent to <i>sumor</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>sumarlīh</i>, Old Norse <i>sumarligr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the people of Somerset</li><li>Somerset</li></ol><p>An elliptical form of <i>*Sumortūnsǣte</i>, meaning "dwellers at Somerton". Equivalent to <i>sumor</i> (“summer”) +&lrm; <i>-sǣte</i> (“dwellers”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Somerset; of or from Somerset</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) someone from Somerset</li></ol><p><i>Sumorsǣte</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>sumor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sunburn</li></ol><p><i>sunne</i> (“sun”) +&lrm; <i>bryne</i> (“burn”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swimming</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) sea, water</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sund</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sundą</i>, from pre-Germanic <i>*swm-to-</i> ( &gt; English <i>swim</i>). Cognate with Old Norse <i>sund</i> (Norwegian <i>sund</i> (“a swimming, strait”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) saxifrage</li></ol><p>From <i>sund</i> (“the act of swimming”) +&lrm; <i>corn</i> (“grain, seed”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a plant, possibly rosemary</li></ol><p>From <i>sund</i> +&lrm; <i>dēaw</i> (“dew”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sundor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a rod to measure the depth of water, a sounding rod</li></ol><p>From <i>sund</i> (“swimming, body of water, sound”) +&lrm; <i>ġierd</i> (“yardstick, rod”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sundġierd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sundġierd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>water</i>) a covering of water, the sea as a covering</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sund</i> (“water”) +&lrm; <i>helm</i> (“helmet”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sounding line</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apart, alone, by one's self</li><li>separate or separately</li><li>differently</li><li>privately</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sundr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sundraz</i>, whence also Old High German <i>suntar</i>, Old Norse <i>sundr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>separate</li><li>different, special</li><li>private</li></ol><p>From <i>sundor</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>sundr-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>born different or apart from others</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sundraburanaz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>sundrborinn</i> (“of different origin”). Equivalent to <i>sundor-</i> +&lrm; <i>boren</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>special ability or skill</li></ol><p><i>sundor-</i> +&lrm; <i>cræft</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>private property, private estate</li></ol><p>From <i>sundor-</i> +&lrm; <i>feoh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>someone going or walking apart from others; a loner</li><li>a rogue or solitary animal</li></ol><p>Comprised of <i>sundor-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;genġa</i>, literally "one who walks apart." Compare <i>āngenġa</i>, of similar form and meaning.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>special gift</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sundragebu</i>. Equivalent to <i>sundor-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġiefu</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>suntargeba</i>. Also corresponds to Old Saxon <i>sundargift</i>, with a different word for "gift."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pharisee</li><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) scribe</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sundor</i> (“apart, separate”) +&lrm; <i>hālga</i> (“holy man”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>synderlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>private life</li></ol><p><i>sundor-</i> +&lrm; <i>līf</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>special right, privilege</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sundrareht</i>. Cognate with German <i>Sonderrecht</i>. Equivalent to <i>sundor-</i> +&lrm; <i>riht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>private conversation</li></ol><p><i>sundor-</i> +&lrm; <i>sprǣċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) special friend</li></ol><p><i>sundor-</i> +&lrm; <i>wine</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>special miracle</li></ol><p><i>sundor-</i> +&lrm; <i>wundor</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>playing in the water</li></ol><p><i>sund</i> (“swimming, water”) +&lrm; <i>plega</i> (“play”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sounding line</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sunder</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sundrōną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>sundrōn</i>, Old Norse <i>sundra</i> (Swedish <i>söndra</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) ship</li></ol><p>From <i>sund</i> + <i>wudu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>solar, of the sun</li></ol><p>From <i>sunne</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>sunnalīh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sun</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sunnō</i>. Masculine form of <i>sunne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solar eclipse</li></ol><p>From <i>sunnan</i> (genitive of <i>sunne</i>) + <i>āsprungennes</i> ("eclipse").</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sunshine</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sunday</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sunnōn dag</i>, a calque of Latin <i>dies Solis</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>sunnandei</i>, Old Saxon <i>sunnundag</i>, Old High German <i>sunnuntag</i>, Old Norse <i>sunnudagr</i>. Equivalent to <i>sunnan</i> (“the sun's”) + <i>dæġ</i> (“day”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sunnandæġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sunday morning</li></ol><p>From <i>sunnan</i> (“the sun's”) + <i>morgen</i> (“morning”), analogous to <i>sunnandæġ</i> (“Sunday”, literally “the sun's day”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saturday night</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sunnan</i> (“the sunn's”) + <i>niht</i> (“night”), analogous to <i>sunnandæġ</i> (“Sunday”, literally “the sun's day”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saturday evening</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sunnōn āband</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>sunnūnāband</i> (whence German <i>Sonnabend</i> (“Saturday”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sunnebēam</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sun</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sunnā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sunnǭ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sh̥₂uén-</i>, oblique stem of <i>&#42;sóh₂wl̥</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sunbeam</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sunne</i> +&lrm; <i>bēam</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the heliotrope flower</li></ol><p>Calque of Latin <i>solisequia</i> (literally “sun-follower”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li><li>Saint Sunniva</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sunne</i> (“sun”) + <i>ġifu</i> (“gift”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sunlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solstice</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sunne</i> (“sun”) +&lrm; <i>stede</i> (“stead, place”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sunne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herd of swine; sounder</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;suniʀu</i> (“pigs”), plural of <i>&#42;sun</i> (“pig, boar”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sunaz</i> (“boar”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sewH-</i> (“to give birth”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sunshade</li></ol><p>From <i>sun</i> (“sun”) +&lrm; <i>sċeadu</i> (“shade”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grandson (lit., "son's son", specifically the son of one's son)</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>sunu</i>. For similar formations, compare Swedish <i>sonson</i>, <i>dotterdotter</i>, <i>barnbarn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>son, male child</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sunu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sunuz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;suHnús</i>.</p><p>Germanic cognates include Old Frisian <i>sunu</i>, Old Saxon <i>sunu</i>, Dutch <i>zoon</i>, Old High German <i>sunu</i> (German <i>Sohn</i>), Old Norse <i>sonr</i> (Swedish <i>son</i>), Gothic <i>𐍃𐌿𐌽𐌿𐍃</i> (sunus). The Indo-European root is also the source of Sanskrit <i>सूनु</i> (sūnu), Lithuanian <i>sūnus</i>, Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;synъ</i> (Old Church Slavonic <i>сꙑнъ</i> (synŭ), Russian <i>сын</i> (syn)); and more distantly of Ancient Greek <i>υἱύς</i> (huiús), Old Irish <i>suth</i> (“birth, fruit”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sunwlitiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beautiful with the sunshine</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweostor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swētnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swōl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweord</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swōtnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sup, take fluid into the mouth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sūpan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sūpaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sewb-</i> (“to pour out”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>súpa</i> (“to sip, drink”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sour</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sūr</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>sūr</i>, Old High German <i>sūr</i>, Old Norse <i>súrr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) sorrel</li></ol><p>From Germanic, related to <i>sūr</i> (“sour”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sour</li></ol><p>From <i>sūr</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sourness</li></ol><p>From <i>sūr</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sūsl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torment; anguish; agony</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;seuhslu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;seuhslō</i> (“pain; torment; sickness”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>sýsla</i> (“busyness; business; work”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweotol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoemaker</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>sūtor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweotol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweþel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swīgian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>south</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sunþ</i>, <i>&#42;sunþr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sunþrą</i>, whence also Old High German <i>sunt</i>, Old Norse <i>suðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from the south</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sunþanā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sunþanē</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>to or from the southeast</li></ol><p>From <i>sūþan</i> (“south”) +&lrm; <i>ēastan</i> (“from the east”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>southeastern</li></ol><p>From <i>sūþan</i> (“south”) +&lrm; <i>ēasterne</i> (“eastern”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Southumbrians, Mercians</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>sūþan</i> (“from the south”) + <i>Humbre</i> (“the Humber”), thus meaning “people from south of the Humber”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from the southwest</li></ol><p>From <i>sūþan</i> (“from the south”) +&lrm; <i>westan</i> (“from the west”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) 'south-Dane', the southern Danes</li></ol><p><i>sūþ</i> (“south”) +&lrm; <i>Dene</i> (“Danes”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the south, a southern part or region</li></ol><p>From <i>sūþ</i> (“south”) +&lrm; <i>dǣl</i> (“part”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the southern end of something</li></ol><p><i>sūþ</i> (“south”) +&lrm; <i>ende</i> (“end”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>sūþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>southern</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sunþrōnijaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>southernwood</li></ol><p>From <i>sūþerne</i> (“southern”) +&lrm; <i>wudu</i> (“wood, tree”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the people of Suffolk</li><li>people living to the south</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>sūþ</i> (“south”) +&lrm; <i>folc</i> (“people”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Southwark, England</li></ol><p>From <i>sūþ</i> (“south”) +&lrm; <i>ġeweorc</i> (“earthwork, fortress”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Southampton</li></ol><p><i>sūþ</i> (“south”) +&lrm; <i>Hāmtūn</i> (“Hampton”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Sūþanhymbre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a south land or country</li><li>the south bank of a river</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sūþ</i> (“south”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>southerner</li></ol><p>From <i>sūþ</i> (“south”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>sūþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the southern Mercians</li></ol><p><i>sūþ</i> (“south”) +&lrm; <i>Mierċe</i> (“Mercians”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>sūþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Sūþriġea</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Surrey, a region of England</li></ol><p>From <i>sūþ, sūþerne</i> (“south, southern”); <i>-ġe</i> (district, region)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>due south</li></ol><p><i>sūþ</i> (“south”) +&lrm; <i>rihte</i> (“straight”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the South Saxons</li><li>Sussex</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>Seaxan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Sūþseaxan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of Sussex or its people: South Saxon</li></ol><p>Either <i>Sūþseaxan</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i> or <i>sūþ</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;Seaxisċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>southwest</li></ol><p>From <i>sūþ</i> (“south”) +&lrm; <i>west</i> (“west”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>southwestern</li></ol><p>From <i>sūþ</i> (“south”) +&lrm; <i>westerne</i> (“western”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>that, of that</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in that way, like that</li><li>to the extent stated; to a great extent, so, very</li><li>doubled (with an interrogative pronoun) to mean 'whatever', 'whoever', etc</li><li>doubled as a correlative: the..., the...</li><li>doubled as a comparative: as... as...</li><li>used once as a comparative</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>like, as, the way <i>(often doubled as "swā swā")</i></li><li><i>(temporal)</i> when, while, as</li><li>so, with the result that</li><li>on condition that</li><li><i>(swā (hwæþer swā)... swā)</i> whether... or</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swā</i>. See there for more.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>whatsoever</li></ol><p>From <i>swa</i> (“so”) +&lrm; <i>hwilc</i> (“what”) +&lrm; <i>swa</i> (“so”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wheresoever</li></ol><p>From <i>swa</i> (“so”) +&lrm; <i>hwær</i> (“where”) +&lrm; <i>swa</i> (“so”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>anything</li></ol><p>From <i>swa</i> (“so”) +&lrm; <i>hwæt</i> (“what”) +&lrm; <i>swa</i> (“so”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the same way, manner, similarly</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>just as</li><li>as</li><li>even as</li><li>like</li><li>according to</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>swā</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swā</i>. Compare Old High German <i>sōsō</i> and <i>sōse</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>even so, nevertheless, however</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become dark</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mushroom, fungus</li><li>sponge</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swamm</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swammaz</i> (“tree-fungus, mushroom, sponge”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swombh-</i> (“sponge, tree-fungus, swamp”).</p><p>Cognate with Old High German <i>swamb</i> (“mushroom”) (German <i>Schwamm</i>), Old Norse <i>svǫppr</i> (“sponge, fungus”), Gothic <i>𐍃𐍅𐌰𐌼𐌼𐍃</i> (swamms, “sponge”). More at <i>swamp</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swan</li><li>man; warrior</li><li>herdsman; herder</li><li>servant</li><li>boy; lad</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swanaz</i>, probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swenh₂-</i> (“to sound, resound”). Compare Old Saxon <i>swan</i> (Low German <i>Swaan</i>), Dutch <i>zwaan</i>, Old High German <i>swan</i> (German <i>Schwan</i>), Old Norse <i>svanr</i> (Swedish <i>svan</i>).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swainaz</i>. Doublet of <i>sweġen</i>, a borrowing from Old Norse.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an officer responsible for managing forests, especially pasturing of pigs within them, as well as the use of wood</li></ol><p>From <i>swān</i> +&lrm; <i>ġerēfa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slow, sluggish, slothful, indolent, heavy (of the body or mind)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swangor</i>. Cognate with German <i>schwanger</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kenning for the sea</li></ol><p>Kenning of <i>swan</i> (“swan”) +&lrm; <i>rād</i> (“road”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Evening Star</li></ol><p>From <i>swān</i> (“servant”) +&lrm; <i>steorra</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweep</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swaipan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swaipaną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>sweifan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swǣr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>faintheartedness, timidness</li><li>pettiness, meanness, narrow-mindedness</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;swārcmōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swearcung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an oath, vow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swarō</i> (“oath, vow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweat</li><li>used of other moisture that comes from the body, especially blood</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swait-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swoyd-</i>, <i>&#42;sweyd-</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>swêt</i>, Old High German <i>sweiz</i>, Old Norse <i>sveiti</i> (“sweat, blood”), Latin <i>sudor</i>, Sanskrit <i>स्वेद</i> (sveda).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stained with blood</li></ol><p>From <i>swāt</i> +&lrm; <i>fāh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sweaty</li><li>sweatlike</li><li>(<i>especially hunting</i>) bloody</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swaitagaz</i>, equivalent to <i>swāt</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pore, sweat pore</li></ol><p>From <i>swāt</i> (“sweat”) +&lrm; <i>þȳrel</i> (“hole”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Swēoþēod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to retreat, withdraw, retire</li><li>to subside, become still, calm down</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swaþrōn</i> (“to lose consciousness, swoon”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) that which swathes or wraps?</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swā</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swā swā</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swallow (bird)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swalwā</i>, related to Old High German <i>swalwa</i>, Old Norse <i>svala</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make or become dark</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swarkōn</i>, derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swerkaną</i> (“to darken”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darkening; darkness</li></ol><p>From <i>swearcian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skin, rind</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swarduz</i>; akin to Old Norse <i>svǫrðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swarm</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swarmaz</i>, whence also Old High German <i>swarm</i>, Old Norse <i>svarmr</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swer-</i> (“to buzz, hum”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>black</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swart</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swartaz</i> (“black”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>swart</i>, Old Saxon <i>swart</i>, Old Dutch <i>swart</i>, Old High German <i>swarz</i>, Old Norse <i>svartr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>sweart</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blacken</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swartōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swartōną</i>. Equivalent to <i>sweart</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweartiaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blackness</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sweart</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweartnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>sweart</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send to sleep, lull</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swabbjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swabjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swæcc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sleep</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to be dead</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swefan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swefaną</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>sofa</i> (Danish <i>sove</i>, Norwegian <i>sove</i>, Swedish <i>sova</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swefl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swefn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweofot</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sulfur, brimstone</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sweblaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sulfurous, brimstony</li></ol><p><i>swefl</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sulphurous vapor</li></ol><p>From <i>swefl</i> (“sulfur”) +&lrm; <i>þrosm</i> (“vapor”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dream</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swefn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swefnaz</i> (“sleep, dream”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swépnos</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>sweƀan</i>, Old Norse <i>svefn</i>. The Indo-European root also led to Ancient Greek <i>ὕπνος</i> (húpnos), Latin <i>somnus</i>, Old Irish <i>suan</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>сънъ</i> (sŭnŭ), Lithuanian <i>sãpnas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to dream <i>(+dative or accusative of person)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>swefn</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dreamer</li></ol><p>From <i>swefnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interpreter of dreams</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>swefn</i> (“dream”) +&lrm; <i>reċċan</i> (“to explain”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i> (-er)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swefl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sound, noise</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) accent, stress</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swōgi</i> (“sound, noise”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>sœgr</i> (“tumult, noise”). More at sough, swoon.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a noise</li><li>to rush, roar, crash</li><li>to produce tones</li><li>to sing or play music</li><li>to signify by sound</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swōgijan</i>, form Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swōgijaną</i>; related to <i>swōgan</i> (“to resound, rush, roar”). Compare Gothic <i>𐍃𐍅𐌴𐌲𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (swēgnjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The art of music</li></ol><p><i>sweg</i> +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Sun</li><li>the heavens, the sky</li><li>Heaven, the Kingdom of God</li></ol><p>Possibly related to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sóh₂wl̥</i> (“sun”), perhaps via a metathesis of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sawili</i>, the oblique stem of <i>&#42;sōl</i> (“sun”). Compare <i>siġel</i> (“sun”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) the "king of heaven;" God</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sweġel</i> (“Heaven”) +&lrm; <i>cyning</i> (“king”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) heavenly joy</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sweġel</i> (“heaven”) +&lrm; <i>drēam</i> (“joy, music”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of musical instrument</li></ol><p>From <i>sweġel</i> (“music”) +&lrm; <i>horn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the glory of heaven; heavenly glory</li></ol><p>From <i>sweġel</i> +&lrm; <i>wuldor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young man</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Norse <i>sveinn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swainaz</i> (“relative, young man, servant”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swé</i> (“oneself; separate; apart”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Swegen)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Swein Forkbeard, king of Denmark</li></ol><p>The name of the Danish king is an Anglicization of Old Norse <i>Sveinn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mother-in-law</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swegru</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swegrō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sweḱrúh₂</i> (“mother-in-law”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweġel</i> (“sun”)</li><li>music</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sweglu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sweglō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Sweyn Godwinson; Earl of Hereford and brother of Harold Godwinson.</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>Sveinn</i>. Cognate with Danish <i>Svend</i> and Swedish <i>Sven</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweġer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Sweġen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to burn, perish from heat</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to burn (feel a hot sensation)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swelan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swelaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swel-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>such; like that, that kind of</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swalīk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swalīkaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>selik</i>, Old Saxon <i>sulik</i>, Old Dutch <i>sulik</i>, Old High German <i>sulīh</i>, Old Norse <i>slíkr</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐍅𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (swaleiks). Equivalent to <i>swā</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>also, likewise</li><li>about (approximately)</li><li>like, as</li><li><i>expletive</i>: like, as it were</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li><i>(with subjunctive)</i> like</li><li><i>(with subjunctive)</i> as if</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>swelċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swelh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swallow</li><li>to eat up, devour</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swelgan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swelganą</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>swelhan</i>, Old Norse <i>svelga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whirlpool</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whirlpool</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chasm</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swelgaz</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swell</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swellan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swellaną</i> (“to swell”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sweltan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sweltaną</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>sweltan</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;sweltan</i>, Old High German <i>swelzan</i>, Old Norse <i>svelta</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐍅𐌹𐌻𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (swiltan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to labor; to cause trouble (to somebody)</li><li>to harass, vex, afflict, distress</li><li>to chasten</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swankijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swankijaną</i> (“to cause to swing or sway, sling”), causative of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swinkaną</i> (“to swing, bend”). Cognate with Old High German <i>swenken</i> (“to beat, whip, paddle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strike, blow (with a weapon)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swangwi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) To strike at; to charge at.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swangijan</i> (“to cause to swing”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swicol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleep; slumber</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;swefian</i> (“to lull”) +&lrm; <i>-ot</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swīgian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sweden (a country in Scandinavia in Northern Europe)</li></ol><p><i>Swēo</i> (“Swede”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”). Cognate with Swedish <i>Svealand</i> (“the central region of Sweden”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Swede</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swihô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swipian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>father-in-law</li><li>cousin</li><li>pillar; column</li><li>that which is shaped like a pillar</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swehuraz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swéḱuros</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>swehur</i> (German <i>Schwäher</i>), Middle Dutch <i>sweer</i>, Old Old Swedish <i>svœr</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐍅𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍂𐌰</i> (swaihra). The Indo-European root is also the source of Sanskrit <i>श्वशुर</i> (śváśura), Ancient Greek <i>ἑκυρός</i> (hekurós), Latin <i>socer</i>, Old East Slavic <i>свекръ</i> (svekrŭ) (Russian <i>свёкор</i> (svjókor)). Compare <i>sweġer</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sweruz</i> (“pole, pillar”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swer-</i> (“stick, pole”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neck</li><li>(of water) the part where the distance between two shores is the least</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swerhô</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>svíra</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>object worn around the neck, often made of gold or silver: collar, necklace</li></ol><p><i>swēora</i> +&lrm; <i>bēag</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swēorbēag</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to darken, be obscured</li><li>to become troubled, gloomy, sad</li><li>to become heavy with grief, troublesome, saddening</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swerkaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sword</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swerdą</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>swerd</i>, Old Saxon <i>swerd</i>, Old Dutch <i>swert</i>, Old High German <i>swert</i>, Old Norse <i>sverð</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) "sword's edge"</li></ol><p>From <i>sweord</i> + <i>eċġ</i>. Has a parallel in Old Saxon <i>swerdes eggia</i>, possibly stemming from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swerdas aggju</i>. Compare <i>mǣċes eċġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Anglian tribe mentioned in the Tribal Hidage situated in what is now Huntingdonshire</li></ol><p>From <i>sweord</i> (“sword”) +&lrm; <i>ōra</i> (“shore, edge”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swordplay, fighting</li></ol><p>From <i>sweord</i> (“sword”) +&lrm; <i>plega</i> (“play, exercise”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scour, polish, file, rub</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swerbaną</i>, whence also Old High German <i>swerban</i>, Old Norse <i>sverfa</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (swairban).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sweden (a country in Scandinavia in Northern Europe)</li></ol><p><i>Swēo</i> (“Swede”) +&lrm; <i>rīċe</i> (“kingdom”). Cognate with Swedish <i>Sverige</i>, <i>Svea rike</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweostor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>West Saxon</i>) sister</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swester</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swestēr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swésōr</i>. Compare Old Frisian <i>swester</i>, Old Saxon <i>swestar</i>, Old High German <i>swestar</i>, Old Norse <i>systir</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a child of one's sister (niece or nephew)</li></ol><p>From <i>sweostor</i> +&lrm; <i>bearn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sororal nephew</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swestersunu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swestērsunuz</i>, equivalent to <i>sweostor</i> (“sister”) +&lrm; <i>sunu</i> (“son”). Compare Icelandic <i>systursonur</i> and Swedish <i>systerson</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>band, troop, squadron</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sweut</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swajutą</i> (“troop, host, band”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scrotum</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweotol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clear, intelligible; evident, distinct</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;switul</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;switulaz</i> (“clear; evident”), possibly from an early merging of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sw-</i> (“self”) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swō</i> + <i>witan</i> (“to know”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;witaną</i>, with the suffix +&lrm; <i>-ol</i> (“prone to doing”) from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ulaz</i> (adjective suffix), with the general meaning "self-knowable", thus "evident", however possible derivatives such as <i>&#42;witulaz</i>, <i>&#42;witol</i> (evident) and <i>&#42;switaną</i>, <i>&#42;switan</i> (to know oneself) do not exist outside of this adjective.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>clearly, openly, plainly</li></ol><p>From <i>sweotol</i> (“clear”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i> (“-ly”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>clear, plain, obvious</li></ol><p>From <i>sweotol</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>clearly</li></ol><p>From <i>sweotollīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>, from <i>sweotol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>sweotol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweotol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweotole</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swīgian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sweden, the Swedish people</li></ol><p>From <i>Swēo</i> (“Swede”) +&lrm; <i>þēod</i> (“nation”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>Svíþjóð</i> (“Sweden”) (Icelandic <i>Svíþjóð</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweþel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swear (an oath or a promise)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swarjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swarjaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swer-</i> (“to protect, to guard”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>titlark</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweostor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweostor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweeten</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swōtijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swōtijaną</i>, derived from <i>&#42;swōtuz</i> (Old English <i>swēte</i>) + <i>&#42;-janą</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>suozen</i>, Old Norse <i>&#42;sœta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sweet (of smell or taste)</li><li>pure, untainted (of a taste)</li><li>harmonious</li><li>agreeable, pleasant, sweet (of personality)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swōtī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swōtuz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sweh₂dus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>swēte</i> (“sweet”) +&lrm; <i>rūn</i> (“secret, whisper, mystery”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>swēte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sweetly</li><li>cutely</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>swēte</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweetness</li><li>fragrance</li><li>cuteness</li><li>pleasantness, agreeableness</li></ol><p>From <i>swēte</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swētnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a swathe; wrap; band; bandage</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swaþil</i>, equivalent to <i>swæþ, swaþu</i>; <i>-el</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>swedil</i>, <i>swedila</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweþel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) poultice</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deception, illusion</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swiką</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sweyg-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deceiver</li><li>traitor</li></ol><p>From <i>swic</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wander, to move about</li><li>to deceive, be treacherous</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swīkwan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swīkwaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swem(bʰ)-</i> (“to bend, turn, swing”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>swīka</i> (“stay far from”), Old Saxon <i>swīkan</i> (“betray, languish”), Middle Dutch <i>swiken</i> (Dutch <i>bezwijken</i> (“give way, sink”)), Old High German <i>swīhhan</i> (dialectal German <i>schweichen</i> (“wander round, deceive”)), Old Norse <i>svíkva</i> (“betray”) (Swedish <i>svika</i>, Danish <i>svige</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deception, treachery, fraud</li></ol><p>From <i>swic</i> (“deception”) +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i> (“craft”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deceit, fraud</li><li>treachery, failure in loyalty, treason</li></ol><p>From <i>swic</i> +&lrm; <i>-dōm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wander</li><li>to depart, turn</li><li>to deceive</li><li>to offend</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swikōn</i>, equivalent to <i>swic</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deceitful, false, treacherous</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swikulaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>swicol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swēora</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swirhijô</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>svíri</i> (Icelandic <i>svíri</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move in a course, revolve</li><li>to sweep</li><li>to intervene</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swībaną</i> (“to move”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>swīva</i>, <i>swīfa</i> (“to waver”), Old Norse <i>svífa</i> (“to drift, ramble, rove”), Gothic <i>𐍃𐍅𐌴𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (sweiban).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>swift, quick</li></ol><p>From the verb <i>swīfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slipper, shoe</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>subtelāris</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>swiftly</li></ol><p>From <i>swift</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swiftness, fleetness, celerity</li></ol><p>From <i>swift</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swiftnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>swift</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silence</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>silent</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swīgā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swīġe</i> (“silence”)</li></ol><p>Alternative spelling of <i>swīġe</i>. Because /ij/ tended to merge phonetically with /ī/, the digram "ig" was sometimes used as a back spelling for long "ī" in Old English.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be silent <i>(+ genitive = about something)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swīgēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late West Saxon</i>) alternative form of <i>swelċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swelċe</i></li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swelċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swillan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Meaning unclear, possibly "pit".</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gargle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swilljan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swiljaną</i> (“to gulp, swallow”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swel-</i> (“to drink, gulp, swallow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweltan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swimming in the head, dizziness, giddiness, vertigo</li><li>a state of unconsciousness, a swoon</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swīmō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swīmô</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swīmāną</i> (“to move, sway”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swimman</i>, whence also Old Frisian <i>swimma</i>, Old Saxon <i>swimman</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;swimman</i>, Old High German <i>swimman</i>, Old Norse <i>svimma</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>swimmaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pig</li><li>pork</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swīn</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>swīn</i>, Old Saxon <i>swīn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hard work: toil, labor</li><li>hardship, travail</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>swincan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work hard: toil, labour</li><li>to strive</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swinkaną</i> (“to swing, bend”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sweng-</i>, <i>&#42;swenk-</i> (“to bend, swing, swivel”). A parallel form to <i>swingan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to waste away, languish, grow languid, be consumed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swindan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pig; of or relating to pigs</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swīnīn</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>swīnīn</i>. Equivalent to <i>swīn</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swinge, flog, beat, scourge</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to chastise, afflict, plague</li></ol><li>to give a blow with the hand</li><li>to whip a top, cream, etc.</li><li>to strike, dash</li><li>(of wings) to beat, flap</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swingan</i> (“to swing”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a stripe, stroke, or lash</li><li>a scourging, whipping, or flogging</li><li>a scourge, rod, or whip</li><li>the part of a flail which beats the corn out of the ear.</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) chastisement, affliction</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swingillju</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swingiljō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swineherd</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swīnahirdī</i>. Equivalent to <i>swīn</i> (“pig”) +&lrm; <i>hierde</i> (“herder”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swīnhierde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>melody</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swin-</i> (“to sound; sound, melody”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swenh₂-</i> (“to sound”). Cognate with Latin <i>sonō</i> (“sound, resound”), Latin <i>sonus</i> (“sound, noise”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pork food</li></ol><p>From <i>swīn</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sound melodiously; make melody; sing</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swinisōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swinisōną</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swin-</i> (“to sound”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>melody</li><li>harmony</li></ol><p>From <i>swinsian</i> (“to sing, make a melody”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>music</li></ol><p>From <i>swinsung</i> (“melody”) +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>melody</li></ol><p>From <i>swinn</i> (“melody”) +&lrm; <i>swēġ</i> (“sound”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweþel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scourge, strike, beat, lash</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swippjan</i>, <i>&#42;swipōn</i>, <i>&#42;swipēn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swipōną</i>, <i>&#42;swipjaną</i>, <i>&#42;swipāną</i> (“to move”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sweyb-</i> (“to bend, turn, swerve, sway, swing, sweep”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cunning, astute</li><li>shifty</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swipr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swipraz</i> (“quick, skilful, smart”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sweyb-</i> (“to bend, turn, swerve, sway, swing, sweep”). Compare Old Norse <i>svipull</i>, <i>svipall</i> (“changeable, shifty”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whip, scourge</li><li>affliction, chastisement</li></ol><p>Cognate with Old Norse <i>svipa</i> (“to move swiftly; whip”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swēora</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swarm (of bees)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swarmijaną</i> (“to swarm”). Equivalent to <i>swearm</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwistlian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweotol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweotol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>swīþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>swīþor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>swiþost</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swīþra</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong, mighty</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swinþ</i> (“strong”). Also see English <i>swith</i> for its usage and cognates.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strengthen, support</li><li>to exercise strength, prevail</li></ol><p>From <i>swīþ</i> (“strong, powerful, mighty”); equivalent to <i>swīþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>very</li><li>much</li><li>(<i>in&#32;comparative</i>) more</li><li>(<i>in&#32;superlative</i>) mostly</li><li>(<i>in&#32;superlative</i>) especially</li><li>(<i>in&#32;superlative</i>) exceedingly, related to the associated verb</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>swīþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>. From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swinþaz</i>, <i>&#42;swenþaz</i> (“strong”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swent-</i> (“active, healthy”). More at swith.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) strong-hearted, brave, daring</li></ol><p><i>swīþ</i> +&lrm; <i>ferhþ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>swīþ</i> (“strong, mighty”) + <i>ġīsl</i> (“hostage”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>swīþ</i> (“strong, mighty”) and <i>helm</i> (“protection, defence”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, notably held by Saint Swithun of Winchester</li></ol><p>The first element is <i>swīþ</i> (“strong”), the second cognate with German Hüne (“giant”). The ethnonym <i>Hun</i> was adopted by Germanic languages with this meaning.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immense, great, violent</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swinþalīkaz</i>, cognate with Old Norse <i>svinnligr</i> (“sensible”) from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swinþaz</i> (“strong”), equivalent to <i>swīþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. More at <i>swith</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strength</li><li>violence</li></ol><p>From <i>swīþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ness</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>swīþe</i>; rather, more</li><li>more so, further</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>swīþe</i>; especially, mostly, most</li><li>above all</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>right (direction)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>swīþ</i>: stronger</li></ol><p>From, and identical to, the comparative of <i>swīþ</i> (“strong”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Swīþwulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>swīþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>swīþ</i> (“strong, mighty”) + <i>wealh</i> (“Celt, foreigner”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>swīþ</i> (“strong, mighty”) + <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become drowsy</li><li>to fall asleep</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swoþrōn</i>, related to Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swaþrōn</i> (“to lose consciousness, faint”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swēte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to resound, sound, rush, roar</li><li>to move with violence, enter with force, invade</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swōgan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>senseless, overcome, rushed, swooned, giddy, faint</li></ol><p>Past participle of <i>swōgan</i> (“to make a sound; move with noise; rush; roar”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swōgan</i>. More at sough.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heat</li><li>burning</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swul</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swulą</i> (“heat, burning”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take into the throat, gargle</li><li>to gulp down, swallow</li></ol><p>From unattested <i>&#42;swolg</i>, a term represented by Old Saxon <i>swolga</i> (“whirlpool, abyss”) +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i> (“-ize”). Compare also Old Norse <i>sylgr</i> (“drink, beverage”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heat, burning</li></ol><p>Related to <i>swōl</i> (“heat”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweord</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pant, sigh, breathe heavily</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweostor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>swēte</i> (“sweet”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sweetly</li><li>cutely</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swōtō</i> ~ <i>&#42;swōtē</i>. Equivalent to <i>swēte</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>. Compare Old High German <i>sozo</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweetness</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swōtunassī</i>, equivalent to <i>swōt</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swōtnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swīgian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swēora</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweord</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweostor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweostor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweostor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweotollīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweotollīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>explanation</li><li>manifestation</li><li>declaration, setting forth, exposition</li><li>evidence, testimony, declaration, testament, title-deed, certificate, prescript</li></ol><p>From swutelian.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweotol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweotollīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweotol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swīgian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swiftlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late West Saxon</i>) alternative form of <i>swelċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swelċe</i></li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swelċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>death, destruction</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swultiz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>sultr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweltan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>death</li></ol><p>From <i>swylt</i> (“death”) +&lrm; <i>dēaþ</i> (“death”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deathday</li></ol><p>From <i>swylt</i> (“death”) +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i> (“day”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swīn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swīnhierde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swēora</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweord</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweostor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweostor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweostor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweotol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swīþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swīþor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>swīþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swīþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swīþor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>swȳþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swā</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swæcc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taste</li><li>smell</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swakku</i>, <i>&#42;swakkī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swakkuz</i>, <i>&#42;swikkiz</i>, <i>&#42;swakiz</i> (“smell, odour”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sweg-</i>, <i>&#42;swekn-</i> (“to smell”). Seems to have adopted a-stem inflections by EWS.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Suebi</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Suēbī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swēbaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Swǣfas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Swaffham (a market town and civil parish with a town council in Breckland district, Norfolk, England)</li></ol><p>Recorded in the Domesday Book as <i>Suāfhām</i>. From <i>Swǣfe</i> (“Suebians”) +&lrm; <i>hām</i> (“home, homestead”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swefl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweġel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to burn</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heavy</li><li>oppressive, grievous</li><li>sad</li><li>(<i>said of physical or mental activities</i>) lazy, indolent, slow</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a burden, labour, trouble, grief</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swār</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swēraz</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>swere</i> (West Frisian <i>swier</i>), Old Saxon <i>swār</i>, Middle Dutch <i>swaer</i> (Dutch <i>zwaar</i>), Old High German <i>swāri</i> (German <i>schwer</i>), Old Norse <i>svárr</i> (Swedish <i>svår</i>). The noun appears to derive from the adjective.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>swǣr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>swǣr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dear, beloved</li><li>own</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swēsaz</i> (“one's own, familiar, trusted”), <i>&#42;swēsa-</i> (“own, relation”), from Proto-Indo-European. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>swēs</i> (“related”), Old Saxon <i>swās</i> (“dear”), Old Norse <i>sváss</i> (“dear, beloved”), Gothic <i>𐍃𐍅𐌴𐍃</i> (swēs, “own”). The Indo-European root is also the source of Latin <i>sibi</i>, Russian <i>себя́</i> (sebjá).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flatter, talk up; cajole, coax</li></ol><p>From <i>swǣs</i> (“one's own, dear”) +&lrm; <i>-lǣċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweostor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweat</li></ol><p>Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swaitijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swaitijaną</i> (“to sweat”), derived from <i>&#42;swait-</i> (“sweat”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>track, trace</li><li>footprint</li><li>bandage; swathe</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swaþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swaþō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swem(bʰ)-</i> (“to bend, turn, swing”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>svæði</i> (“open field”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swaþ-</i>. Compare Middle Low German <i>swēde</i> (“dressing for a wound, wrap, compress, bandage”), Middle High German <i>swede</i> (“wound dressing, bandage”), Middle High German <i>swade</i> (“strip, band, film”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sweþel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>whichever (of two)</li></ol><p>From <i>swa</i> + <i>hwæþer</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swēġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swēte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sīe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sideful</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seofonfeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sober, moderate</li><li>neat, clean</li></ol><p>From <i>sȳfre</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>cleanly, purely</li><li>soberly, prudently, circumspectly</li></ol><p>From <i>sȳfre</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sobriety</li><li>moderation, temperance</li><li>abstinence, purity</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>syfre</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sȳfernes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moderate in eating, abstemious</li></ol><p>From <i>sȳfre</i> (“moderate”) +&lrm; <i>ǣt</i> (“eating”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dish eaten with bread</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>syliġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seofon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sober, pure, temperate, circumspect</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sūbrī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>sȳfre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>siftan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sīe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>siġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sigor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pillar, column; support</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sūli</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sūliz</i> (“pillar”). Akin to Gothic <i>𐍃𐌰𐌿𐌻𐍃</i> (sauls, “pillar”), German <i>Säule</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late West Saxon</i>) self, oneself</li></ol><p>Late West Saxon spelling of <i>self</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>self-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>selfcwala</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>selfcwalu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) suicide? The word occurs only in two textually related glosses on the Greek-derived Latin word <i>biothanatus</i> in Aldhelm of Malmesbury's <i>Prosa de virginitate</i>. Clayton suggests 'the act of suicide', while other Old English glosses on the same sentence suggest the meaning 'self-killer'. Bosworth and Toller suggest 'self-destructive'.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seolfor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seolfren</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sully, soil, pollute, defile</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sulwijōn</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;sulwijan</i> (“to make or become dirty; to sully”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sūl-</i> (“thick liquid, muck”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a beam that serves as a foundation or support, basis, sill</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) a base, support, foundation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sulī</i> (“bar, sill”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sel-</i>, <i>&#42;swel-</i> (“beam, board, frame, threshold”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sellan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sellend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sellīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sellicre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sieltan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a miry place</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sulwijan</i>, ultimately derived from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sulą</i> (“mire, filth”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feast</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sumblą</i> (“banquet”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>sumbl</i>, Old Saxon <i>symbal</i>, <i>sumbal</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>banquet hall</li></ol><p>From <i>symbel</i> (“feast”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>simle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Simon.</li><li>Simon, original name of the apostle Peter</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Biblical Hebrew <i>שִׁמְעוֹן</i> (šimʿōn, “hearkening”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sīen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recompense for sin; penance</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>synn</i> +&lrm; <i>bōt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sundor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>special</li><li>specific, particular</li></ol><p>From <i>synder</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>suntarlīh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>particularly, in particular</li></ol><p>From <i>synder</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sindon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sunder; separate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sundrijaną</i> (“to separate, divide”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>separate, alone</li><li>special, set apart</li><li>extraordinary, remarkable</li><li>of that which concerns a single person; private, own</li><li>(<i>distributive</i>) one each</li></ol><p>From <i>sundor</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sinewealt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sinewealt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sinful</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a sinner</li></ol><p>From <i>synn</i> +&lrm; <i>-ful</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>synful</i></li></ol></html>:/ˈsyn.ɣi.ɑn/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sin</li></ol><p>From <i>synniġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>sendigia</i>. Compare also Old Saxon <i>sundiōn</i>, Old High German <i>suntōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without sin, sinless</li></ol><p>From <i>synn</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misdeed, fault, crime, wrong</li><li>sin</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sunnju</i> (“actuality, distress, concern”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sunjō</i> (“truth, reality”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁es-</i> (“to be”); with semantic influenced from similar sounding Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sundiju, &#42;sundī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sundī</i> (“crime, sin”).</p><p>Cognates include Old High German <i>sunna</i> (“justification”), Old Norse <i>syn</i> (“denial”), and Gothic <i>𐍃𐌿𐌽𐌾𐌰</i> (sunja, “truth”). Replaced the reflex of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sundi</i>, which provides the word for "sin" in most other West Germanic languages.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>synful</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>synful</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sinful woman; (female) sinner</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sunnīggjā</i>, equivalent to <i>synn</i> +&lrm; <i>-iċġe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sinful; wicked</li></ol><p>From <i>synn</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>sundig</i>, Old High German <i>suntig</i>, Old Norse <i>syndigr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sinnihte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seonoþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sierċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sierċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>tree</i>) service tree (<i>Sorbus torminalis</i>)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>sorbus</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>service tree (<i>Sorbus torminalis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>syrfe</i> (“service tree”) +&lrm; <i>trēow</i> (“tree”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Syria</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Syria</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sierċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sour whey</li><li>buttermilk</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sūrą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Syrian</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Syria</i> (“Syria”) +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>; compare Dutch <i>Syrisch</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Syrophoenician</li></ol><p>From <i>Syria</i> (“Syria”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;Fēnisċ</i> (“Phoenician”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sierwan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>six</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sixhynde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sixta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sixtiġfeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sixtiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sixtiġfeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>siþþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>sūþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>siþþan</i></li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>siþþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;saiwi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saiwiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ship</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> + <i>bāt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) and <i>beorht</i> (“bright”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Sǣbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Sǣbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sæċċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>battle, strife, fighting</li><li>sacking, sackcloth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sakkju</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sakjō</i> (“accusation, lawsuit”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sakku</i>, <i>&#42;sakk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sakkuz</i>, from Latin <i>saccus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sacking, sackcloth</li></ol><p><i>sæcc</i> (“sack, sacking”) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>seċġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cockle</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>cocc</i> (“cock”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a "sea-king"; a king who holds power on the sea</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sǣ</i> +&lrm; <i>cyning</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a withdrawal of the sea</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sǣ</i> +&lrm; <i>ċyrr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seed</li><li>progeny</li><li>semen</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full, sated</li><li>weary</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sād</i>. See there for more.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sad</i>. See there for more.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) ‘sea-Danes’, the Danes</li></ol><p><i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>Dene</i> (“Danes”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea beast; aquatic animal ("seadeer")</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>dēor</i> (“animal, beast”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sādōn</i>, equivalent to <i>sǣd</i> (“seed”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A basket or wooden vessel used to hold seed while a sower sows seed; seed basket.</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣd</i> +&lrm; <i>lēap</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sowing</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sǣdian</i> (“to seed”) +&lrm; <i>-noþ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea dragon</li><li>sea serpent</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>draca</i> (“dragon”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the season for sowing, seedtime</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sādatīmō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sēdatīmô</i> (“seedtime”), equivalent to <i>sǣd</i> +&lrm; <i>tīma</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>sáðtími</i> (“seedtime”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Severn</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;Sabrinā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the mouth of the Severn</li></ol><p>From <i>Sæfern</i> +&lrm; <i>mūþa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) journey by sea, voyage, seafarer</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>fōr</i> (“going”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seafowl</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>fugol</i> (“bird”)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Sæfugol)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>fugol</i> (“bird”); else from the common noun <i>sǣfugol</i> (“seafowl”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea passage</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sǣ</i> +&lrm; <i>færeld</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make sink, fell, drop</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saigijaną</i>, the causative of <i>sīgan</i> (“to sink, fall, drop”), (compare <i>senċan</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) and <i>gār</i> (“spear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sea-Geats, a term for the tribe of Geats used in <i>Beowulf</i></li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>Ġēatas</i> (“Geats”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saying</li><li>statement, claim</li><li>tradition, something generally said</li><li>a narration</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sagjaną</i> (“to say”); compare Old Norse <i>sǫgn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailor</li><li>vessel, ship</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;genġa</i> (“goer”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Soham (a town and civil parish in East Cambridgeshire district, Cambridgeshire, England)</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;sǣġe</i> +&lrm; <i>hām</i> (“home(stead)”), literally “homestead by a swampy pool”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>ġifu</i> (“gift”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>siġel</i></li></ol><p>See <i>siġel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the bottom of the sea, seabed</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> +&lrm; <i>grund</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sehtlian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>room, great hall, (large) house, castle</li><li>happiness, prosperity</li><li>(proper) occasion, time; season, opportunity, condition, position</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sal</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;salą</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>sal</i>, German <i>Saal</i> (“hall, large room”), French <i>salle</i> (“room”), Old Saxon <i>sal</i>, Dutch <i>zaal</i>. Compare <i>sele</i>, from a Germanic variant stem.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sāli</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sēliz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) sea-gift</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> (“sea, lake”) +&lrm; <i>-lāc</i> (“gift”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind (with rope), restrain</li><li>to moor (a boat etc.)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sailijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sailijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a maritime district</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the sea; marine, maritime</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;saiwilīk</i>, equivalent to <i>sǣ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>sēolīh</i> (“marine, maritime”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lucky, fortunate, prosperous, happy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sālīg</i>, equivalent to <i>sæl</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>happily</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣliġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) seafarer, sailor</li></ol><p><i>sǣ</i> +&lrm; <i>līþend</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>salimuria</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sealthaga</i> (“a type of bird”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>happiness; joy; felicity</li><li>good fortune; prosperity</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sēliþō</i>, equivalent to <i>sǣl</i> +&lrm; <i>-þ</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>sālitha</i>, <i>sāltha</i>, Old Dutch <i>sālda</i>, Old High German <i>sālida</i>, Old Norse <i>sæld</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailor</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> +&lrm; <i>mann</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>zeeman</i>, German <i>Seemann</i>, Swedish <i>sjöman</i> and Icelandic <i>sjómaður</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worst, most inferior, weakest</li></ol><p>Cognate with Old Swedish <i>sämaster</i> (modern Swedish <i>sämst</i>), further etymology uncertain.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) sea-weary</li></ol><p><i>sǣ</i> +&lrm; <i>mēþe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worse, inferior, weaker</li></ol><p>Cognate with Old Swedish <i>sämbre</i> (modern Swedish <i>sämre</i>) and Norwegian Nynorsk <i>semmer</i>, further etymology uncertain.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) and <i>mund</i> (“protector”). Cognate with Icelandic <i>Sæmundr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>marine; of or relating to the sea</li></ol><p><i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>-en</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>senġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sendan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slow, sluggish</li><li>bad</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a ness or promontory extending into the sea; a cape</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>næs</i> (“ness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sap (of plants, etc.); juice</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sap</i>. Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>sap</i> (Dutch <i>sap</i>), Old High German <i>saf</i> (German <i>Saft</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full of sap, succulent, sappy, juicy</li></ol><p>From <i>sæp</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) and <i>rīc</i> (“king, ruler”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the sea-coast, seashore</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> +&lrm; <i>rima</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a seaman, one who travels by sea (used to describe the Scandinavians)</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>rinc</i> (“warrior”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seashell</li></ol><p><i>sǣ</i> +&lrm; <i>sċiell</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sǣsċiell</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) sea snail</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> +&lrm; <i>snæġel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sǣsnæġl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a star that guides sailors at night; also used as a title of the Virgin Mary</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>steorra</i> (“star”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seashore</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>strand</i> (“shore”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ambush</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sātu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sētō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sed-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sæternesdæg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sæternesdæg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sæternesdæg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saturday; the seventh day of the week</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Sāturnas dag</i>; equivalent to <i>Sætern</i> (“Saturn”) +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i> (“day”). Ultimately a calque of Latin <i>diēs Sāturnī</i> (“day of Saturn”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Friday night</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>setl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p><i>sǣ</i> +&lrm; <i>ward</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>waru</i> (“care, protection”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cliff by the sea</li><li>a wall formed by the sea (used to describe the waters of the Red Sea when parted by Moses during the Exodus)</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>weall</i> (“wall”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coast guard</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> +&lrm; <i>weard</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Sæweard)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) and <i>weard</i> (“guard”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a billow</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>wielm</i> (“upwelling (of water)”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea creature</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>wiht</i> (“creature”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) and <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boat; ship</li></ol><p><i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>wudu</i> (“wood”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) and <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sǣwielm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>wynn</i> (“joy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seawater</li></ol><p>From <i>sǣ</i> +&lrm; <i>wæter</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>sǣȳþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea-wave</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>sǣ</i> +&lrm; <i>ȳþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pirate</li></ol><p><i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>þēof</i> (“thief”), so “seathief”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>þrȳþ</i> (“might”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sēċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) toe</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;taihā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;taihwǭ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tabule</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tabele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tabele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tabule</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>table</li><li>tablet for writing</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>tabula</i> (“tablet, board, plank, chart”), ultimately from Proto-Italic <i>&#42;taðla</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take</li><li>to touch, make contact with</li></ol><p>A late borrowing (c. 1100), from Old Norse <i>taka</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tēkaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tācn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sign</li><li>token, credential</li><li>sign of the Zodiac</li><li>a distinguishing mark</li><li>a sign to attract attention, signal</li><li>a sign of anything future, a prognostic</li><li>an action that conveys a meaning</li><li>an indication, a mark which shows condition or state</li><li>a sign which shews the truth or reality of anything, proof, demonstration, evidence</li><li>a supernatural sign, miracle</li><li>a signal event, remarkable circumstance</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;taikn</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>tēken</i>, Old Saxon <i>tēkan</i>, Old High German <i>zeihhan</i>, Old Norse <i>teikn</i>, Gothic <i>𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌺𐌽𐍃</i> (taikns).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mark, make a mark upon something</li><li>to be a token of something</li><li>to indicate, point out</li><li>to signify, to symbolize</li><li>to indicate the future, to portend</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tācen</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an indication, sign, signification</li><li>an evidence, proof</li><li>a forewarning, prognostication</li><li>a direction, ordering</li></ol><p>From <i>tācnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tācn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>husband's brother, brother-in-law</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;taikuraz</i> (“brother-in-law”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dayh₂wḗr</i> (“husband's brother”). Akin to Old Frisian <i>tāker</i> (“husband's brother”), Old High German <i>zeihhor</i>, <i>zeihhur</i>, <i>zeihhir</i> (“husband's brother”), Middle High German <i>zeicher</i> (“brother-in-law”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tācor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tādiġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toad</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin and not clearly related to any Germanic languages outside of Old English. Liberman argues that it's best understood as an expressive or sound-symbolic development within Old English. He rejects connections to <i>tosca</i> (“toad”) and Old High German <i>zuscen</i> (“to burn”) as weak conjectures.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evil-speaking, calumny, disparagement, slander</li><li>(<i>religious</i>) blasphemy</li><li>reproach</li><li>scorn, derision, mocking</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tālu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tēlō</i> (“deception, deceit, persecution”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;del-</i> (“to take aim, calculate, damage, count”). Cognate with Latin <i>dolus</i> (“deception, strategem, trap”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>talent (<i>money, weight</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>talenta</i>, plural of <i>talentum</i> (a Greek weight and sum of money), from Ancient Greek <i>τᾰ́λᾰντον</i> (tắlănton).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suppose, consider, reckon, account</li><li>to maintain, claim</li><li>to impute, ascribe</li><li>to reckon, count, enumerate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;talōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;talōną</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>talōn</i> (Low German <i>talen</i>, <i>tahlen</i> (“to pay”)), Dutch <i>talen</i> (“to ask”), Old High German <i>zalōn</i> (German <i>zahlen</i> (“to pay”)), Old Norse <i>tala</i> (Swedish <i>tala</i> (“to speak”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blasphemous</li><li>reproachable, reprehensible</li></ol><p><i>tāl</i> (“blasphemy, reproach, scorn”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>account, reckoning</li><li>tale; narration</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;talu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;talō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tame</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tam</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twig, branch</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tain</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tongs</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tangu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tangō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;denḱ-</i> (“to bite”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diviner; one who divines by casting lots, specifically twigs</li></ol><p><i>tān</i> (“twig, branch”) +&lrm; <i>hlyta</i> (“lot-caster”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tānhlyta</i></li></ol><p><i>See <i>tānhlyta</i>.</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who tans hides; tanner</li></ol><p>From <i>tannian</i> (“to tan”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i> (agent suffix) and/or a borrowing from Latin <i>tannārius</i> (“tanner”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tan (<i>hides</i>)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>tannāre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Taunton (a town and civil parish of Somerset, England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taper</li></ol><p>No cognates exist in other Germanic languages: perhaps borrowed from Latin <i>papyrus</i> (via an unattested form <i>*papur</i>), which in later use also meant ‘candle-wick’ (compare Spanish <i>pabilo</i> (“candlewick”)). In early Middle English glossaries, Latin <i>papyrus</i> was glossed as <i>taper</i>. See also German <i>Kerze</i> (“candle”), ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>χάρτης</i> (khártēs, “papyrus”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small axe</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>taparøx</i> (“small axe”), from Old East Slavic <i>топоръ</i> (toporŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;toporъ</i>, probably from an Iranian language.</p><p>Judging by dictionaries, the axe is only mentioned as tool to define shore width by throwing it from a ship that is floating next to shore during high tide (Danish king Cnut gave to Christ Church at Canterbury the Sandwich port and landings along River Stour from Sandwich to the river mouth).</p><p>Compared with francisca (throwing axe) and also:</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a light shield, a targe</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;targǭ</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>targa</i>, Old High German <i>zarga</i> (German <i>Zarge</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>targa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>teosol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>glad, cheerful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;taitaz</i> (“serene, tender”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>teitr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tait</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;taitaz</i> (“radiant, glad, cheerful, tender”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>tāt</i> (“glad, cheerful”) + <i>berht</i> (“bright”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>tāt</i> (“glad, cheerful”) + <i>friþ</i> (“peace”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>tāt</i> (“glad, cheerful”) + <i>helm</i> (“defense, protection”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>tāt</i> (“glad, cheerful”) + <i>here</i> (“army”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>tāt</i> (“glad, cheerful”) + <i>hyse</i> (“warrior”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Tatnoth; an Anglo-Saxon Bishop of Rochester.</li></ol><p>From <i>tāt</i> (“glad, cheerful”) and <i>nōþ</i> (“daring”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>tāt</i> (“glad, cheerful”) and <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare, make ready</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tauwjan</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>touwen</i> (Dutch <i>touwen</i>), Old High German <i>zawjan</i>, Gothic <i>𐍄𐌰𐌿𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (taujan), Old Norse <i>tæja</i>, <i>tœja</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toad</li></ol><p>Unknown. Compare Old English <i>tādie</i> (“toad”), Old English <i>tosca</i>, <i>toxa</i> (“frog”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>to</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ta</i> (“to”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tō-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Northumbrian</i>) alternative form of <i>tīen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tīenfeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>material used for making salve; balm, ointment</li><li>material used for colouring; pigment, paint</li><li>red ochre for marking sheep; tiver</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;taubr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;taubrą</i> (“magic”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cord, band; thong; fetter</li><li>enclosure (enclosed land)</li><li>case, chest</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;taugu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;taugō</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dewk-</i> (“to draw, pull”). Akin to Old Norse <i>taug</i> (“rope”), and also Old English <i>tēon</i> (“to pull, train”).</p><p>Unknown; possibly identical to Etymology 1.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tēar</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tela</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(literal,&#32;figurative) unsteady, unstable</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;talt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake, stagger, be unsteady, be uncertain, vacillate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;taltrōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;taltrōną</i>, a frequentative of <i>&#42;taltōną</i> (“to sway, dangle, hesitate”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;del-</i>, <i>&#42;dul-</i> (“to shake, hesitate”). Equivalent to <i>tealt</i> +&lrm; <i>-rian</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Dutch <i>touteren</i> (“to tremble”), Norwegian dialectal <i>totra</i> (“to quiver, shake”), North Frisian <i>talt</i>, <i>tolt</i> (“unstable, shaky”). Related to tilt.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>childbirth</li><li>family, offspring</li><li>a team of draught animals</li><li>an Anglo-Saxon legal procedure in a stolen goods suit</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;taum</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;taumaz</i> (“pull, draw”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tear (drop of liquid from the tear duct)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tahr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wallow, roll over</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tarbulōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;terbaną</i> (“to turn, wind”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;derbʰ-</i> (“to turn, twist, writhe”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tart</li><li>sharp</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tart</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tartaz</i> (“rough, sharp, tearing”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;teraną</i> (“to tear”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;der-</i> (“to flay, split, cleave”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>teart</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of taste</i>) sharpness, severity, asperity</li></ol><p>From <i>teart</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>teartnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>teart</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tȳdre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tīefring</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tīeġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>well, rightly</li><li>completely, thoroughly, certainly</li><li>happily, prosperously, pleasantly</li><li>very</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>well! good!</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>til</i> (“good”) plus an adverbial suffix <i>-a</i> of uncertain origin (see Campbell 1959, §666).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tent</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;teld</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;teldą</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>zelt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread (e.g. a tent, an awning, a net, a snare, etc.)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;teldōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;teldōną</i> (“to tent, cover”), equivalent to <i>teld</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tentmaker</li></ol><p>From <i>teld</i> (“tent”) +&lrm; <i>wyrta</i> (“wright”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dye</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;talg</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;talgaz</i> (“tallow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a branch, bough</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;telgō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;telgô</i>, <i>&#42;telguz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;delgʰ-</i> (“to split, carve, hew”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put forth shoots, flourish</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>telgor</i> (“shoot, twig, stem”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>telga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plant, shoot, twig</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tilgiʀu</i>, nom/acc plural of <i>&#42;telg</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;telgaz</i> (“branch, twig”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twig, branch, shoot</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;telgō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;telgô</i>, <i>&#42;telguz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;delgʰ-</i> (“to split, carve, hew”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tellan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to count, calculate</li><li>to tell, narrate, recount</li><li>to regard, consider</li><li>to attribute</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;talljan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;taljaną</i>, whence also Old Frisian <i>tella</i>, Old Saxon <i>tellian</i>, Old Dutch <i>tellen</i>, Old High German <i>zellen</i>, Old Norse <i>telja</i>. Related to Old English <i>talu</i> (“tale, account”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tela</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tīeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the River Thames</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Tamesis</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Temes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sift</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tamisōn</i>, from <i>&#42;tamisu</i> (“sift”). Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>temsen</i>, Middle Low German <i>temesen</i>, <i>temsen</i> (“to sift”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tame</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tammjan</i>, <i>&#42;tamjōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tamjaną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>tema</i>, <i>temia</i>, Old High German <i>zemen</i>, <i>zemmen</i>, Old Norse <i>temja</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temple</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;templ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tempel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to moderate, regulate</li><li>to mingle</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>temperāre</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>temesian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Mercian</i>) ten</li></ol><p>See <i>tīen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kindle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tandijaną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>zunden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Isle of Thanet in Kent</li></ol><p>From Brythonic.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Isle of Thanet in Kent</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Tenet</i> (“Isle of Thanet”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Teign (a river in England).</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;tīn</i> (“river”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;teh₂-</i> (“to flow”). Attested in a 739 charter.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press, harry</li><li>to hasten</li><li>to proceed with haste or violently</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tangijan</i>, inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tangijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tǣnel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tēoþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tēoþian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decide, determine</li><li>to intend</li><li>to judge</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;(ge)-teohhian</i>, related to <i>&#42;tehwō</i> (“array, order”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tela</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tilian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tilung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag, draw, pull</li><li>to bring, lead, put</li><li>to teach, educate, bring up</li><li>to draw near, go, proceed</li><li>to make, frame</li><li>to adorn, furnish</li><li>to accuse</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>damage, hurt, vexation</li><li>insult, abuse, reproach</li></ol><p>Contracted from earlier <i>tēohan</i>; from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;teuhan</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>tīan</i>, Old Saxon <i>tiohan</i> (Middle Dutch <i>tīen</i>), Old High German <i>ziohan</i> (German <i>ziehen</i>), Old Norse <i>toginn</i>, Gothic <i>𐍄𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (tiuhan); and, from Indo-European, with Latin <i>dūcō</i>, Middle Welsh <i>duyn</i>. Compare <i>tēam</i>, <i>tēag</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tehwōn</i>. Cognate with Middle High German <i>zechen</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tīhan</i> (“to blame”). Cognate with Old High German <i>zīhan</i>, Old Norse <i>tjá</i>.</p><p>From a merger of Etymology 3 with the reflex of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;teun</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>damage, harm, hurt, destruction</li><li>mischief, trouble, annoyance, vexation</li><li>reproach, insult, shame, calumny, abuse</li><li>strife, discord</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;teuną</i> (“destruction, ruin”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who draws or pulls</li><li>an accuser</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tēon</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calumny</li><li>reproach</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tīenfeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grievous, troublesome, woeful</li><li>insolent, abusive, contemptuous</li><li>causing injury or annoyance</li></ol><p>From <i>tēon</i> (“damage, insult”) +&lrm; <i>-ful</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tēonful</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to vex, irritate</li><li>to reproach, revile, rebuke</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>tēona</i> (“harm, annoyance”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to tire, faint, fail, cease</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to tire, to make faint</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;teuʀōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>penis</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ters</i> (“nail; penis”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tar, resin</li><li>earwax</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;terwą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;derwo-</i>. Compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;trewą</i> ( &gt; English <i>tree</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dóru</i> (“tree”). Cognate with Middle Low German <i>ter</i> (Dutch <i>teer</i>, German <i>Teer</i>), Old Norse <i>tjara</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>die</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tesu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tesusprǣċ</i></li></ol><p>From <i>teosu</i> +&lrm; <i>sprǣċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>teswian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>tēoþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>tēoþung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>tēoþung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>tenth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tenth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tehunþō</i>, alternative form of <i>&#42;tehundō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tehundô</i>.</p><p>The failure of Anglian smoothing to occur in <i>teogoþa</i> is possibly due to the influence of the alternative form <i>tēoþa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take a tenth (of something)</li><li>to pay a tithe</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>tēoþa</i> (“tenth”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a tithe</li><li>a tenth part of something</li><li>a tithing (association of ten men)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>tēoþa</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i> or <i>tēoþian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear (rend)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;teran</i>, whence also Old Saxon <i>teran</i> and Old High German <i>zeran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tierġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a term, fixed date, end</li></ol><p>From or cognate with Old Norse <i>termin</i> (“term, terminus”), from Latin <i>termen</i>, <i>terminus</i> (“boundary, end”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>termen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Test (a river in Hampshire, England).</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>teoru</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tesu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hurt, injury, damage</li><li>wrong, wrongdoing, fraud</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;taswi</i>. Further etymology unknown. Perhaps related to Old English <i>tǣsan</i> (“to tear up, pull apart, tease”).</p><p>Alternatively, perhaps from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tehsu</i>, <i>&#42;tehswō</i>&nbsp;n (“the right, right side”), related to Old High German <i>zeso</i>&nbsp;n (“the right, right side”), though the sense evolution is obscure.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>teosol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hurtful, deceitful speech</li></ol><p>From <i>tesu</i> +&lrm; <i>sprǣċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hurt, injure</li><li>to vex, annoy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;taswijōn</i>, equivalent to <i>tesu</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Further etymology unknown. Perhaps related to Old English <i>tǣsan</i> (“to tear up, pull apart, tease”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) tetter</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tetru</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tetruz</i>, <i>&#42;tetruhaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dedru-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;der-</i> (“to flay, split, crack”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þefanþorn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Þeodorus</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Þȳle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Þȳle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þō</i> (“clay”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þō</i> (“clay”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Thomas</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Thōmās</i>&nbsp;m, from Ancient Greek <i>Θωμᾶς</i>&nbsp;m (Thōmâs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þōþor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þōþor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þwǣle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Þȳle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tifer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a young goat; kid</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tikkīn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tick (<i>parasitic blood-sucking arachnid</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tīkō</i> (“tick”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a dictator (absolute ruler of the Roman Republic)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>dictātor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time in general</li><li>time as a defined period or span, particularly:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a tide, a fourth of the day or night</li><li>an hour, a twelfth of the day or night</li><li>a season, a fourth of the year</li><li>(<i>especially&#32;in the plural</i>) an age, an era</li></ol><li>the hour, the moment determined by a sundial or other device marking the division between the tides or hours</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) the religious service held at a canonical hour, four of which were equivalent to the daylight tides</li><li>the season, the favorable or proper period for an action, <i>especially</i> with regard to farming or (<i>Christianity</i>) the holy seasons of the liturgical year</li><li>the time, the hour, the favorable, proper, or allotted moment for an action or event, the occasion when something can or ought to be done</li><li>a commemoration; an anniversary; a festival, <i>especially</i> a saint's day</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) tense, the time indicated by the form of a verb</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to betide, befall, happen</li></ol><p><i>tīd</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tīd</i> (“time”) +&lrm; <i>beald</i> (“bold”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tīd</i> (“time”) +&lrm; <i>beorht</i> (“bright”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>tīd</i> (“time”) +&lrm; <i>burg</i> (“city”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tȳdre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) fear of time, fear of death</li></ol><p>From <i>tīd</i> (“time”) +&lrm; <i>eġe</i> (“fear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tȳdernes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>tīd</i> (“time”) + <i>friþ</i> (“peace”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>tīd</i> (“time”) + <i>helm</i> (“defence, protection”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>tīd</i> (“time”) +&lrm; <i>hild</i> (“battle, war”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>tīd</i> (“time”) + <i>hūn</i> (“bear cub”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for a time, temporarily</li><li>conveniently, at a suitable time</li><li>in time, in good time, betimes, early, soon, quickly</li></ol><p>From <i>tīd</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>zītlīhho</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tȳdre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a seasonable rain</li></ol><p>From <i>tīd</i> (“time”) +&lrm; <i>reġn</i> (“rain”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a liturgical service held at one of the canonical hours</li></ol><p>From <i>tīd</i> (“time”) +&lrm; <i>sang</i> (“liturgical service”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astrologer</li></ol><p>From <i>tīd</i> (“time”) +&lrm; <i>sċēawere</i> (“spy, guard”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tīd</i> (“time”) +&lrm; <i>siġe</i> (“victory”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tiding</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>tīdan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>tīd</i> (“time”) + <i>wald</i> (“powerful, mighty”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>tīd</i> (“time”) + <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chronicler, annalist</li></ol><p>From <i>tīd</i> +&lrm; <i>wrītere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>tīd</i> (“time”) + <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astrologer</li></ol><p>From <i>tīd</i> (“time”) +&lrm; <i>ymbwlātend</i> (“spectator, observer”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tȳdre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>painting</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;tīefran</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>; possibly conflated with <i>&#42;tēafrung</i>, from <i>&#42;tēafrian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to tie</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;taugijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;taugijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tigele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tihting</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tilung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tīma</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give birth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;taumijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;taumijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>tēam</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ten</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tehun</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tehun</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;déḱm̥</i>.</p><p>The vocalism (for expected <i>&#42;tēon</i>, as in <i>tēoþa</i>) has presumably been levelled in from inflected forms that were subject to <i>i</i>-umlaut.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tenfold</li></ol><p>From <i>tīen</i> +&lrm; <i>-feald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ten-stringed</li></ol><p><i>tīen</i> +&lrm; <i>-strenġe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ten year(s) old</li><li>lasting ten years</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tehunwintruz</i>. Equivalent to <i>tīen</i> +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear or water (<i>of eyes</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tahhrijan</i>. Equivalent to <i>tēar</i> (“tear”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to worry, exasperate</li><li>to provoke, pain, irritate</li><li>to excite</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;targijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;targijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female dog; a bitch</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tibā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sacrifice, offering</li><li>victim</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tibr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tibrą</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Tifer)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tiber (a river in Italy)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Tiberis</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tēag</i></li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Tig)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Tīw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tīeġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tile</li><li>brick</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tigulā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tile or brick</li></ol><p><i>tiġele</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roof tile, imbrex</li></ol><p>From <i>tiġele</i> (“tile”) +&lrm; <i>stān</i> (“stone”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>potter, brickmaker</li></ol><p>From <i>tiġele</i> (“tile”) +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“wright”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tiger</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tīgr</i> (“tiger”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tigele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tiġele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tigris (a river in West Asia)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Tigris</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Τίγρις</i> (Tígris), an alternative form of <i>Τίγρης</i> (Tígrēs), from Old Persian <i>𐎫𐎡𐎥𐎼𐎠</i> (Tigrā), from Akkadian <i>𒀀𒇉𒈦𒄘𒃼</i> (<sup>ÍD</sup>Idiqlat), from Sumerian <i>𒀀𒇉𒈦𒄘𒃼</i> (<sup>ÍD</sup>Idigna, <sup>ÍD</sup>Idigina, literally “fast as an arrow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) of or relating to a tiger; tigerish</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>tiger</i> (“tiger”) +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tigele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tēag</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tyht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tyhtan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tyhtend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tyhtendlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tyhtere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tyhtend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tyhting</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accusation</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tyhtnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Tīw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>good (morally good; competent; useful, etc.)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>use, service, convenience</li><li>goodness, kindness</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>to, until, unto</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tilaz</i>, whence also Old Frisian <i>til</i>, Old High German <i>zil</i> (German <i>Ziel</i>), Old Norse <i>tilr</i>, Gothic <i>𐍄𐌹𐌻</i> (til).</p><p>Possibly borrowed from Old Norse <i>til</i>, though the OED has it as "Germanic" and related to Old Norse <i>til</i> and to Old Frisian <i>til</i>. If not borrowed, then inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;til</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;til</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>til</i> (“good”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>til</i> (“good”) + <i>berht</i> (“bright”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>til</i> (“good”) + <i>friþ</i> (“peace”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obtain</li><li>to strive</li><li>(with genitive) to provide for</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tilōną</i>, whence also Old High German <i>zilōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>tiliġen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>striving, labor, effort</li><li>gain from labor, acquisition, fruit obtained via tilling</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>tilian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>til</i> (“good”) + <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something gained or acquired through labor</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic. By surface analysis, <i>tilian</i> (“to labor”) +&lrm; <i>-þ</i>. Cognates include Middle Dutch <i>teelt</i>, Old Frisian <i>tilath</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a time, hour (period of time)</li><li>an age (of the world), era</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tīmō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tīmô</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>&#42;zīmo</i>, Old Norse <i>tími</i>. Related to Old English <i>tīd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>timber</li><li>building (both senses)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;timr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;timrą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dem-</i> (“build, house”) (see Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dṓm</i>).</p><p>Cognates include Old Saxon <i>timbar</i>, Old High German <i>zimbar</i>, Old Norse <i>timbr</i>, Gothic <i>𐍄𐌹𐌼𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (timrjan, “to build”), and Latin <i>domus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>timberland</li></ol><p>From <i>timber</i> (“wood”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;timrijaną</i>, derived from <i>&#42;timrą</i> (“timber”) (Old English <i>timber</i>) + <i>&#42;-janą</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>timbra</i>, Old Saxon <i>timbrian</i>, Old High German <i>zimbaren</i>, Gothic <i>𐍄𐌹𐌼𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (timrjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>builder</li></ol><p><i>timbran</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>timbran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>timbrend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>building (both senses)</li></ol><p><i>timbran</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>timbring</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in good time, soon</li></ol><p>From <i>tīma</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small drum, tabor, timbrel, tabret</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>tympanum</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>τύμπανον</i> (túmpanon), from <i>τύπτω</i> (túptō, “to strike, to hit”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tin</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tiną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tyne (a river in the counties of Northumberland and Tyne and Wear, in north east England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tynemouth (a town in the Metropolitan Borough of North Tyneside, Tyne and Wear, England), formerly in Northumbria</li></ol><p>From <i>Tīna</i> (“River Tyne”) +&lrm; <i>mūþ</i> (“mouth”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tickle</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from earlier <i>&#42;ticclian</i>, derived from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tikkōn</i> (“to tick, tap, touch, push”), related to Old High German <i>zehhōn</i> (“to stimulate, incite”). Alternatively, perhaps from <i>&#42;tincian</i> +&lrm; <i>-lian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a point or prong on a weapon or implement; a tine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tinduz</i>, <i>&#42;tindaz</i>, of unknown ultimate origin. Cognate with Old High German <i>zint</i>, Old Norse <i>tindr</i>, and related to Old High German <i>zinna</i> (German <i>Zinne</i> (“pinnacle”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Tīna</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tin</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tinīnaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>tin</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tynġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tynġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beam, rafter</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tinnu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tinnō</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;tindnō</i>, <i>&#42;tindnijō</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>zinna</i> (“pinnacle, merlon”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch, extend</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>tintrega</i></li></ol><p>From <i>tinnan</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;treg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torment, pain, punishment</li></ol><p>From <i>tinnan</i> +&lrm; <i>trega</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to torment, torture, afflict</li></ol><p>From <i>tintrega</i> (“torment, punishment”), equivalent to <i>tinnan</i> (“to stretch, extend”) +&lrm; <i>tregian</i> (“to vex, afflict, grieve”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>tormenting, torturous</li></ol><p>From <i>tintrega</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tela</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tela</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tilung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tēon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fame; glory; honour</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tīraz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dey-</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Norse <i>tírr</i> (“glory, honour”) and Old Saxon <i>tīr</i> (“glory, renown”). Compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tiari-</i> (“neat, splendid”), whence Old High German <i>ziari</i> (“neat, beautiful, splendid”), Old High German <i>zierī</i> (German <i>Zier</i> (“splendour, beauty”)), German <i>zieren</i> (“to decorate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) God</li></ol><p>From <i>tīr</i> (“fame”) +&lrm; <i>fruma</i> (“lord”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tierġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tierġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tierġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inglorious</li></ol><p>From <i>tīr</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tar, resin, gum</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tierġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>titt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tit, teat, breast</li><li>nipple</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;titt-</i> (“teat; nipple; breast”), possibly ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tata-</i> (“father; parent; nipple”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>title (name of a work)</li><li>superscription</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>titulus</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tiw, the Anglo-Saxon name for Tyr</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the runic character ᛏ (/t/)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Tīw</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Tīwaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deywós</i> (“god”).</p><p>Proto-Germanic cognate with Old Norse <i>Týr</i>, Old Frisian <i>Tii</i>, Old High German <i>Ziu</i>, Gothic <i>&#42;𐍄𐌴𐌹𐍅𐍃</i> (&#42;teiws).</p><p>Proto-Indo-European cognate with Latin <i>deus</i>, Luwian <i>𒋾𒉿𒊍</i> (tiwaz), Lithuanian <i>diẽvas</i>, Sanskrit <i>देव</i> (devá), and</p><p>Classical Persian دیوْ (dēw).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tuesday</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Tīwas dag</i> (“Tuesday”, literally “Tiw's Day”), a Germanic interpretation of Latin <i>dies Martis</i>, itself a translation of Ancient Greek <i>Ἄρεως ἡμέρα</i> (Áreōs hēméra) (interpretatio romana).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Monday night</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>to, into</li><li>towards</li><li>at</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) used to mark the infinitive (supine) of the verb</li><li>as (in the role of)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>besides</li><li>in addition, also, too; moreover</li><li>to an excessive degree; too</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tō</i>, <i>&#42;ta</i> (“to”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;de</i>, <i>&#42;do</i> (“to”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>tō</i> (“to”), Old High German <i>zuo</i> (“to”), Old Irish <i>do</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go to bed</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>for sale</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōdæġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōdæġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōdæġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>emulously</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>gratis</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>why</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as reward</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>tomorrow</li></ol><p>Could go back to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tō murganō</i>, considering Gothic <i>𐌳𐌿 𐌼𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌲𐌹𐌽𐌰</i> (du maurgina).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>tonight</li><li>last night</li></ol><p><i>tō</i> (“to; at”) +&lrm; <i>niht</i> (“night”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at noon</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(of the sun, and perhaps other heavenly bodies) to set</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tō seþlē gān</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>te sedle gān</i>, Old High German <i>&#42;zi sedale gān</i> (Middle High German <i>ze sedele gān</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at dawn</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the evening</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>verbal prefix with a sense of "in pieces, apart, asunder", or with intensive force</li><li>used to form substantives from other nouns</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tō-</i>, <i>&#42;twiʀ-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twiz-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dwís</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>ti-</i>, <i>te-</i>, Old Saxon <i>te-</i>, Old High German <i>zi-</i>, <i>zir-</i>, <i>zar-</i>, <i>zur-</i> (German <i>zer-</i>), Gothic <i>𐌳𐌹𐍃-</i> (dis-), and with Latin <i>dis-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to carry off (in different directions)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to separate</li></ol><p>From <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>beran</i> (“to bear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burst asunder</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) break in two or in pieces, be rent asunder</li><li>to break out</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tebrestaną</i> (“to burst or break apart”), equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>berstan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>tebrestan</i>, Old High German <i>zibrestan</i> (German <i>zerbersten</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break asunder or into pieces, break apart, break in two, overthrow, ruin, crush, destroy</li><li>to infringe, violate</li><li>to interrupt; inbreak</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tebrekaną</i>, <i>&#42;twizbrekaną</i> (“to break apart”), equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>brecan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>tōbreka</i>,</p><p>Old Dutch <i>tebrekan</i>, Old Saxon <i>tebrekan</i>, Old High German <i>zibrehhan</i> (German <i>zerbrechen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull to pieces, pull apart</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to separate</li><li>to distract</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>breġdan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōċyrran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cleave (separate)</li></ol><p>From <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>clēofan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tell apart; distinguish, differentiate</li></ol><p><i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>cnāwan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush to pieces, smash, shatter</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>cnyssan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>tōcweþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forbid</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>cweþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush</li><li>to shatter, break into pieces</li><li>to bruise</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>cwīesan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōcwīesan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōcwīesan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrival, approach, coming</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>cyme</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn in different directions; to part</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċyrran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>division, separation</li><li>difference, disagreement</li><li>a part, portion, section</li><li>a dividing mark; a comma</li></ol><p>From <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>dāl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall to pieces</li><li>to fall away</li><li>to decay</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>drēosan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive apart, scatter, dispel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tedrībaną</i>, <i>&#42;twizdrībaną</i> (“to scatter”), equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>drīfan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>tōdrīva</i>, Old High German <i>zitrīban</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to extinguish</li></ol><p>From <i>tō-</i> (“apart, asunder”) +&lrm; <i>dwǣsċan</i> (“to extinguish, put out”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>today</li></ol><p>From <i>tō</i> +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōdæġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divide, separate, distribute, disperse</li></ol><p>From <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>dǣlan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in addition, besides</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in addition to, besides [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>ēaca</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōīeċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōīeċnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go in different directions</li><li>to separate, part</li><li>to disperse, scatter</li><li>to go away, depart; to become extinct</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>faran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall to pieces; fall apart; fall asunder or into bits, fall away, fall down, collapse</li></ol><p>From <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>feallan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>tefallan</i>, Old High German <i>zefallan</i>, <i>zifallan</i> (German <i>zerfallen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the town of Towcester</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>&#42;Tōfe</i> (“the River Tove”) +&lrm; <i>ċeaster</i> (“fort”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go in different directions, go off separately</li><li>to disperse</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>fēran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow in different directions, melt away</li><li>to be dispersed or destroyed</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to wander, be distracted</li><li>to spread</li><li>to get separated, take different directions</li><li>to pass away; to be dissipated, scattered</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to separate in confusion, become disconnected</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>flōwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in front of</li><li>previous to</li><li>superior to, greater than</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>foran</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leader (only found in compounds)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;togō</i> (“leader”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōgædere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of living things</i>) to go in different directions, part, separate; go asunder</li><li>(<i>of material things</i>) to be sundered, part</li><li>to disperse, go away, go in many directions</li></ol><p>From <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>gān</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>zigān</i> (German <i>zergehen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>access</li><li>approach</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tōgang</i>. Equivalent to <i>tō</i> +&lrm; <i>gang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go in different directions, part ways</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>gangan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>towards</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>towards</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġēan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>towards</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>against, towards</li></ol><p>An adverbial genitive form of <i>togægn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adoption</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġecoren</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōgædere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) adjectival, adjective</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) an adjective</li></ol><p>From <i>tō</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeīeċendlīċ</i> (“adjective”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) [<i>with</i> accusative] to diffuse or to spread <i>(a liquid)</i>, to pour away</li></ol><p>From <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;ġelan</i> (“to pour”), the latter element of unknown origin.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to diffuse, spread</li><li>to pour away, exhaust</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġēotan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expound, interpret</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġesceādan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;togōn</i>, see also Old High German <i>zogōn</i>, Old Norse <i>toga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōġecorennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diffusion, spreading, effusion</li></ol><p>From <i>tōgoten</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōgotennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>together</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tōgadura</i>. By surface analysis, <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>gædere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>togædere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tough</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tą̄h(ī)</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tanhuz</i>, whence also Old High German <i>zāh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hew or cut to pieces; hew asunder</li></ol><p>From <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>hēawan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>tehawa</i>, Middle Low German <i>tohouwen</i> (German <i>zerhauen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōhyht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disband, disperse</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>hladan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hope</li><li>expectation</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tōhopōn</i> (“to hope, expect”), equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>hopa</i>. Related to Old Dutch <i>tōhopa</i>, <i>tuohopa</i> (“wishful expectation, hope”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake apart</li><li>to destroy</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>hrēran</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fight, battle, conflict; campaign, military expedition</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Compare Old English <i>toga</i> (“leader”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go in different directions</li><li>to separate; to part</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>hweorfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hope</li><li>confidence, trust</li><li>glad expectation</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to add to</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>īeċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>increase, augmentation</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tōīeċan</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tool, implement, instrument</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tōlą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dewh₂-</i> (“to tie to; secure”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to lie or run in different directions</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lie between and so to part; to divide, separate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>liċġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figurative&#32;or&#32;literal</i>) to release from a bond or bonds</li><li>to break the connection between things; to separate</li><li>to dissolve, dissipate</li><li>to relax, destroy the force of, weaken</li><li>to desolate, destroy</li><li>to undo a bond</li><li>to discharge an obligation; to pay</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>līesan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tax, toll, fare</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;toll</i>, <i>&#42;tolnu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tullaz</i>, <i>&#42;tullō</i> (“that which is counted or told, reckoning”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;del-</i> (“calculation, fraud”).</p><p>Alternate etymology derives Old English <i>toll</i> from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tollą</i>, from Vulgar Latin <i>toloneum</i>, from Late Latin <i>teloneum</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>τελώνιον</i> (telṓnion, “toll-house”), from <i>τέλος</i> (télos, “tax”).</p><p>Germanic cognates include Old Saxon <i>tol</i> (Dutch <i>tol</i>), Old High German <i>zol</i> (German <i>Zoll</i>), Old Norse <i>tollr</i> (Swedish <i>tull</i>). See also parallel forms represented by Old English <i>toln</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tax collector</li></ol><p>From <i>toll</i> (“tax”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toll</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tolnu</i>, of uncertain origin.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal</i>) to pull or wrench asunder; to dislocate</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to root out, destroy</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>lūcan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōlīesan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let go in different directions</li><li>to make go in different ways</li><li>to disperse</li><li>to release, relax</li></ol><p>From <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣtan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>empty</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) free from</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tōmaz</i> (“empty”). Akin to Old Norse <i>tómr</i> (“empty”), whence Icelandic <i>tómur</i> (“empty”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to distinguish</li><li>to describe</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>mearcian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a description</li></ol><p>From <i>tōmearcod</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surname, cognomen</li></ol><p>From <i>tō-</i> (“to”) +&lrm; <i>nama</i> (“name”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to name separately</li><li>to distinguish by name into parts</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>nemnan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>topp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>top, summit (of something)</li><li>lock or tuft of hair</li><li>(<i>possibly</i>) a top (toy)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;topp</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tuppaz</i> (“braid, pigtail, end”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>torr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turd</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;turdą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung beetle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tordawibil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;turdawibilaz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>torðvifill</i>. Equivalent to <i>tord</i> (“turd”) +&lrm; <i>wifel</i> (“beetle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw, shoot missiles at, launch</li><li>to stone</li><li>to be tossed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;turbōną</i>, <i>&#42;turbijaną</i> (“to turn, twist”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;derbʰ-</i> (“to tie together, weave”). Related to Old English <i>tearflian</i> (“to turn, roll, wallow”), Alemannic German <i>zirbeln</i> (“to swirl, whirl, roll”), Icelandic <i>tyrfa</i> (“to cover with turf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;poetic</i>) bright; shiny; radiant</li><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;poetic</i>) resplendent; splendid; glorious; beautiful; noble</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clearness; brightness; shine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;turhtaz</i> (“light; bright; clear”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;derḱ-</i> (“to glance; look; see”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>torht</i>, Old High German <i>zorht</i>, <i>zoraht</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;turhtą</i> (“lightness; brightness”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;derḱ-</i> (“to glance; look; see”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>torht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>torht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>torht</i> (“shiny, radiant”) + <i>friþ</i> (“peace”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>torht</i> (“bright, shiny”) and <i>helm</i> (“helmet”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>torht</i> (“shiny, radiant”) + <i>mund</i> (“protector”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>torht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>torht</i> (“bright, clear”) + <i>rǣd</i> (“counsel, advice”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>torht</i> (“bright, shiny, glorious”) + <i>siġe</i> (“victory”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>torht</i> (“bright, clear”) + <i>weald</i> (“power, authority”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>torht</i> (“shiny, radiant”) + <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>torht</i> (“shiny, radiant”) + <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bitter; painful; severe</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anger, anguish, distress, wrath</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;torn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;turnaz</i> (“bitter”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;torn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;turnaz</i> (“bitterness, anger”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>torn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tower</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>turris</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>together</li></ol><p>From <i>to-</i> +&lrm; <i>samne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assemble, collect</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>samnian</i> or else to <i>tōsamne</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal</i>) to sow broadcast; to disperse, scatter</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to disseminate (ideas); to distribute (favors); to sow dissension</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>sāwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toad</li><li>frog</li></ol><p>According to Kroonen, perhaps from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tuską</i>. The further etymology of the term is uncertain.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;literal) to divide into two; to separate one thing from another (+ on tū)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;figurative)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to divide into parties; to cause division</li><li>to separate (such as contending parties); to judge or decide between</li><li>to make a distinction; to distinguish between; to treat or look upon differently</li><li>to separate one thing from another</li><li>to separate with the mind; to discern, discriminate, or distinguish</li><li>to separate things from another; to order, dispose, appoint</li><li>to separate the parts of a confused whole; to expound, interpret, or render intelligible</li></ol><li>to send in different directions; to scatter or disperse</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be separated; to differ</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>sceādan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow in different directions, disperse</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;teskrīþaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċrīþan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>teskrīdan</i> (“to dissolve”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send in different directions, disperse, scatter</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>sendan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disposition</li></ol><p>From <i>tōseted</i> (“disposed”, past participle of <i>tōsettan</i>) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set things apart from one another</li><li>to dispose</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tōsattjan</i>, equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>settan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>tōsetta</i> (“to add, appoint, install”), Old High German <i>zuosezzen</i> (“to add”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōsēþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prove</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to demolish, destroy</li><li>to break to pieces</li><li>to slice in two</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>slēan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to split, cleave</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>slifan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear apart, rip up, lacerate</li></ol><p>From <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>slītan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slip away or apart</li><li>to be relaxed, dissolved</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>slūpan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to examine in detail</li><li>to inquire into several parts</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>smēaġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>Tostig</i>, from Old Norse <i>Tósti</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Tostig Godwinson; 11th Century Earl of Northumbria and brother of Harold Godwinson.</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>Tósti</i>, derived from Old Norse <i>Þórstæinn</i> "Thor + stone".</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) to separate, distinguish</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>sundrian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōsundrian</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>syndrian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pomp, splendour, parade, vainglory</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tot</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tut(t)-</i> (“to stick out, protrude”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reputation</li></ol><p><i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>talu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear or pull to pieces</li><li>to pull away</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>tēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick out</li><li>to peep out, look</li></ol><p>Unknown. Compare Old English <i>&#42;tōt</i> (“point, projection”, in placenames), Old English <i>&#42;tot</i> ("projection, point"&#59; found in <i>totrida</i> (“swing, swinging device”)). Compare also Old English <i>tot</i>, <i>ġetot</i> (“pomp, splendour, vainglory”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to toss about, fling in different directions</li></ol><p><i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>torfian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>totridda</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is ridden from a projected point (i.e. a "high-rider" or swing)</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;tot</i> (“point, projection”) +&lrm; <i>ridda</i> (“rider”). Compare Old High German <i>ritiscupha</i> (“see-saw”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divide, stand between</li><li>to part, dissociate, break connections between</li><li>to disperse, scatter</li><li>to divide in the mind; to distinguish, discern</li></ol><p>From <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>twǣman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>division, disunion</li></ol><p>From <i>tōtwǣmed</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>toward (<i>with genitive or dative</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>future</li><li>coming; impending, approaching</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tōward</i>. Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>-weard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) future tense</li></ol><p><i>tōweard</i> (“towards, future”) + <i>tīd</i> (“time”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the future</li></ol><p>From <i>tōweard</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw in different directions, scatter, disperse</li><li>(<i>literal&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) to overturn, destroy, demolish</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>weorpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>destructible</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tōweorpan</i> +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>, else <i>tōweorpende</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōweorpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a house or building used for spinning</li></ol><p><i>&#42;tow</i> (“spinning”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house, building”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show, make aware</li></ol><p>From <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>witan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōweorpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>desolation, destruction</li><li>overthrow, subversion</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tōworpen</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōworpennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive in different directions</li><li>to scatter</li><li>to disperse</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrecan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to detail in writing, describe</li></ol><p>From <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrītan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōweorpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōweorpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōweorpendlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tosċa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōīeċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōætīeċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to increase</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>ætīeċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>increase, augmentation</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tōætīeċan</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tōþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tooth</li><li>tusk (of a walrus)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tanþ</i> (by loss of the <i>n</i> and the resulting compensatory lengthening of the vowel).</p><p>Cognates with Old Frisian <i>tōth</i>, Old Saxon <i>tand</i>, Dutch <i>tand</i>, Old High German <i>zan</i>, <i>zand</i> (German <i>Zahn</i>), Old Norse <i>tǫnn</i> (Swedish <i>tand</i>), Gothic <i>𐍄𐌿𐌽𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (tunþus); and with Sanskrit <i>दन्त</i> (danta) (Hindi <i>दांत</i> (dānt)), Ancient Greek <i>ὀδών</i> (odṓn), Latin <i>dens</i> (French <i>dent</i>), Old Irish <i>dét</i>, Lithuanian <i>danti̇̀s</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toothache</li></ol><p>From <i>tōþ</i> (“tooth”) +&lrm; <i>eċe</i> (“ache”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toothpick</li></ol><p>From <i>tōþ</i> (“tooth”) +&lrm; <i>gār</i> (“spear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set things apart from one another</li><li>to dispose</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tō-</i> +&lrm; <i>þindan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>toothless</li></ol><p>From <i>tōþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>zanlōs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toothpick</li></ol><p>From <i>tōþ</i> (“tooth”) +&lrm; <i>sticca</i> (“stick”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swollenness</li><li>pride, arrogance</li></ol><p>From <i>tōþunden</i> (“swollen”, past participle of <i>tōþindan</i>) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a worm in one's tooth</li></ol><p>From <i>tōþ</i> (“tooth”) +&lrm; <i>wyrm</i> (“worm”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>traht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>text, passage, exposition, treatise, commentary</li></ol><p>From <i>trahtnian</i>, <i>trahtian</i> (“to treat, comment on, expound, consider”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;derk-</i> (“to see, behold, observe, notice”), cognate with Old High German <i>trahta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a commentary</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expositor, interpreter, comentator</li></ol><p>From <i>trahtian</i> (“to treat, comment on”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to treat, comment on, expound, explain, consider, discuss</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;trahtōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>trahtere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>trahtian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>explanation</li><li>exposition, comment</li></ol><p>From trahtnian.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exposition, comment</li></ol><p>From trahtian.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>page (of a book)</li></ol><p>Borrowing from Latin <i>trāmes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll, move hastily</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tread</li><li>to step on, trample</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tredan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;trudaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to walk, tread</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;traddjōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tradjōną</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;trudaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>step</li><li>sole of the foot</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tripod, trivet</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;tréyes</td></tr></table></p><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>tripēs, tripedis</i> (“tripod”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>&#42;trīeġ</i> (“tray”)</li><li>pain, grief, hurt</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tregą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pain, grief, vexation, hurt, ill</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tregō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tregô</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>trego</i>, Old Norse <i>tregi</i>, Gothic <i>𐍄𐍂𐌹𐌲𐍉</i> (trigō).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trouble, annoy, vex</li><li>to abhor</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move by turning or turning around, roll</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;trandijan</i>, of unclear origin. Perhaps derived from ghost verb Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;trindan</i>; or related to Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;trannijan</i> (“to split, divide”) and <i>&#42;trinnan</i> (“to separate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>trendan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ring; circle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;trandil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;trandilaz</i>, equivalent to <i>trendan</i> +&lrm; <i>-el</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll, trundle</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Trēonta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>trēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the River Trent</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tree</li><li>wood</li><li>(collective) trees, a forest</li><li>a piece of wood: beam, log, stake, staff, cudgel</li><li>gallows; cross</li><li>loyalty, faithfulness</li><li>grace, favour, help</li><li>an assurance of faith; word, promise, engagement, covenant, league</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;treu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;trewą</i>. Germanic cognates include Old Frisian <i>trē</i>, Old Saxon <i>trio</i>, Old Norse <i>tré</i>, Gothic <i>𐍄𐍂𐌹𐌿</i> (triu).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;treuwu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;trewwō</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Middle Dutch <i>trouwe</i> (Dutch <i>trouw</i>), Old Saxon <i>treuwa</i>, Old High German <i>triuwa</i> (German <i>Treue</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>trīewan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of tree or wood</li></ol><p>From <i>trēow</i> (“tree”) +&lrm; <i>cynn</i> (“kind”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>trīewe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>trīewen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>trēowe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a thicket of trees</li></ol><p>From <i>trēow</i> (“tree”) +&lrm; <i>ġewrid</i> (“thicket”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>faithfully, truly</li></ol><p>From <i>trēowe</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>triulīhho</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>faithful, true love</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>trēow</i> (“true”) +&lrm; <i>lufu</i> (“love”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fidelity, faithfulness, trueness, loyalty</li></ol><p>From <i>trēow</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to commit oneself, pledge oneself</li><li>to prove oneself to be true</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worship of trees</li></ol><p>From <i>trēow</i> (“tree”) +&lrm; <i>weorþung</i> (“worship, reverence”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carpenter</li><li>woodworker</li></ol><p>From <i>trēow</i> (“tree”) +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“worker”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caterpillar</li></ol><p><i>trēow</i> (“tree”) +&lrm; <i>wyrm</i> (“worm”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fruit from a tree</li></ol><p>From <i>trēow</i> (“tree”) +&lrm; <i>wæstm</i> (“fruit”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>trēowwyrhta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>truth, good faith, honour</li><li>fidelity</li><li>covenant, assurance of good faith</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;triwwiþō</i>. Equivalent to <i>trēowe</i> +&lrm; <i>-þ</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>tryggð</i> (“loyalty, fidelity”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>træppan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>træppe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>trēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wooden board with a low rim; tray</li></ol><p>From <i>trauj-</i>, from <i>trauwʲ-</i>, the oblique stem of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;trawi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;trawją</i> (“wooden vessel”). Cognate with Old Swedish <i>trø</i> (“wooden grain measure”), Low German <i>Treechel</i> (“dough trough”), possibly Old Norse <i>treyja</i> (“carrier”). Related to Old English <i>trog</i> (“trough”).</p><p>Thought to be from the same root as <i>trēow</i> (“tree”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;trewą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dóru</i> (“tree”), <i>&#42;drew-</i> (“hard, firm, strong, solid”). Potential cognates from this root include Sanskrit <i>द्रोण</i> (droṇa, “trough”) and Ancient Greek <i>δροίτη</i> (droítē, “tub, vat”).</p><p>For the phonetic development, compare <i>hīeġ</i> and <i>īeġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trust</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>trīewan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loyal: faithful, trustworthy, true</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;triuwī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;triwwiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wooden</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;triwīnaz</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐍄𐍂𐌹𐍅𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (triweins). Equivalent to <i>trēow</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>trēowþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tribute</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tribut</i>, from Latin <i>tribūtum</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>tribuz</i> (“tribute”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>trifet</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pound, grind</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>trībulāre</i> (“to press, squeeze”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grinding, pounding, threshing</li></ol><p>From <i>trifulian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>&#42;trīeġ</i> (“tray”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something round: a lump or ball</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;trind</i> (“round”) +&lrm; <i>-a</i>; first element from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;trind</i> (“round”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something round: a lump or ball</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;trind</i> (“round”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i>; first element from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;trind</i> (“round”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>trēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>trēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>trīewan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>trīewen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trough</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;trog</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;trugaz</i>. Related to Dutch <i>trog</i>, German <i>Trog</i>, Icelandic <i>trog</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a ship of an unclear type</li></ol><p><i>trog</i> (“trough”) +&lrm; <i>sċip</i> (“ship”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>trog</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Troy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Troia</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Trojans</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Trojan</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Trōiāna</i> (“the Trojans”) +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Trōisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Trojan</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Trōia</i> (“Troy”) +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fail, run out, fall short</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to disappoint; deceive</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;trokōn</i> (“to miss, lack”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trout</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>tructa</i>, possibly from Ancient Greek <i>τρώκτης</i> (trṓktēs).</p></html>:/ˈtrum/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong, firm; stable, steadfast</li><li>(<i>of living things</i>) physically strong or healthy; sound</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Proto-Brythonic <i>&#42;trum</i>, or perhaps from theorized Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;trum</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;trumaz</i>, meaning "root". See Breeze 1993.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>trum</i> (“strong, firm”) +&lrm; <i>beorht</i> (“bright”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Trumbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Trumbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong, firm, stable</li></ol><p><i>trum</i> (“strong, firm”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>firmly, strongly, steadfastly</li></ol><p>From <i>trumlīċ</i> (“strong, firm”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i> or <i>trum</i> (“strong, firm”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>trumlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>trumlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>trum</i> (“strong, steadfast”) + <i>wald</i> (“powerful, mighty”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>trum</i> (“strong, firm”) and <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circumference; perimeter, vicinity</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;trundul</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>trundulnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brushwood</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;drewH-</i>, <i>&#42;deru-</i> (“firm, hard, solid, faithful, true”), see also Old Prussian <i>druwit</i> (“to believe”), Old Irish <i>derb</i> (“certain”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>faith</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;trūwu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;trūwō</i> (“faith, trust”). Cognate with Old High German <i>trūwa</i>, Old Norse <i>trú</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trust, have faith in</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;trūēn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;trūjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a trumpeter; actor; buffoon</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;trūþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;trūþaz</i> (“performer, actor, buffoon”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>trúðr</i> (“juggler”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strengthen, make firm</li><li>to settle, arrange</li><li>to confirm, establish, corroborate</li><li>to give strength to mind or heart, admonish, comfort, encourage</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;trummjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;trumjaną</i> (“to make fast, strengthen”). Possibly akin to the noun <i>þrymm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>trendel</i> (in compounds)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>treow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>trīewen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>trēowwyrhta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>trēowþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tent</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pluck</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>trendel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tread</li><li>to trap</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;trappjan</i>.</p><p>From <i>træppe</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a trap</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;trappjā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;trappjan</i> (“to step”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disturb, mistreat, ill-treat; offend; afflict, harass, vex</li><li>to punish, torment</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. Possibly from the merger of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tukkōn</i> (“to knock, strike”) and Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;teukōn</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;teugōn</i> (“to pull, tug, tease or draw out, bring forth”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tudor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offspring, progeny</li><li>child</li><li>family, race</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tūdr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twēġen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>twelf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>twi-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>strongly; firmly</li><li>(<i>as an intensifier</i>) very</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tulguz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dl̥h₁gʰós</i> (“long”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>tulgo</i> (“very”, adverb).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tumbler, dancer, player, gleeman</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tumbian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tumble</li><li>to leap; dance (about)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tūmōną</i> (“to turn, rotate”). Cognate with Old High German <i>tūmōn</i> (“to turn round, rotate”), Old Norse <i>tumba</i> (“to fall, tumble”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an enclosed piece of ground, an enclosure or garden</li><li>the enclosed ground belonging to an individual dwelling</li><li>the group of houses on an area of enclosed land, a homestead</li><li>a village or town</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tūn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tūną</i> (“enclosure”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tunic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>tunica</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a tongue</li><li>a language</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tungā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>talkative, verbose</li></ol><p>From <i>tunge</i> (“tongue, language”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) star; sidereal</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tungol</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astrologer or astronomer</li></ol><p>From <i>tungol</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heavenly body; planet, star</li><li>constellation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tunglą</i> (“heavenly body, star, orb”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>starry</li></ol><p><i>tungol</i> (“star”) +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i> (“bearing”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astronomy</li><li>astrology</li></ol><p><i>tungol</i> (“star”) +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astronomer</li><li>astrologer</li></ol><p>Regularly derived from <i>tungolcræft</i> (“astronomy”) using the suffixes <i>-iġ</i> and <i>-a</i>. Also equivalent to <i>tungol</i> (“star”) +&lrm; <i>cræftiga</i> (“skilled worker, professional”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astronomy</li><li>astrology</li></ol><p>From <i>tungol</i> (“star”) +&lrm; <i>ġesċēad</i> (“reason”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) star</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>tungol</i> (“star”) +&lrm; <i>ġimm</i> (“gem”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tungolwītga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astrologer</li><li>the Three Wise Men</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>tungol</i> (“star”) +&lrm; <i>wītga</i> (“prophet”). Displaced by English <i>astrologer</i>. Compare Old Polish <i>gwiazdomówca</i> or Ancient Greek <i>ἀστρολόγος</i> (astrológos), which are formed using similar lexemes.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astronomy</li></ol><p>From <i>tungol</i> (“heavenly body”) +&lrm; <i>ǣ</i> (“law”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the land of an estate or farm</li></ol><p>From <i>tūn</i> (“enclosure”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tūnmann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>townman, villager</li></ol><p>From <i>tūn</i> (“town”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barrel, cask</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tunnǭ</i>, <i>&#42;tunnō</i> (“tun, barrel, cask”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the bottom of a cask</li></ol><p>From <i>tunne</i> (“cask”) +&lrm; <i>botm</i> (“bottom”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the people of a town</li></ol><p>From <i>tūn</i> (“town”) +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>tūn</i> (“town”) + <i>wald</i> (“power, authority”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tower</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>turris</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a village in Lincolnshire; modern Torksey</li></ol><p>The first element appears to be the genitive of a name *Turec; the second element is <i>īeġ</i> (“island”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turf</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;turb</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;turbz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to turn on a lathe</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to turn, revolve</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;turnēn</i> (“to turn, lathe”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tyrning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>turtle</i> (“turtledove”)</li><li>a male turtledove</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turtle dove</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>turtur</i> (“turtledove”), of imitative origin.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>canine tooth</li><li>tusk</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tunsk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tunþskaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tūxl</i></li></ol><p>From <i>tūsc (&gt; tūsċ-)</i> +&lrm; <i>-el</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Tīw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tweowa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tweowa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tūsc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tuxford (a village and civil parish with a town council in Bassetlaw district, Nottinghamshire, England)</li></ol><p>First recorded in the Domesday Book</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>canine tooth, tusk</li><li>molar</li></ol><p>From <i>tūx</i> +&lrm; <i>-el</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twelf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-two</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-second</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-two</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-second</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-two</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-second</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-two</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-second</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-two</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-second</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-two</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;twai andi twaintig</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-second</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-two</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-second</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>two hundred</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adulation</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;twaddian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twēġen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>two</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;twai-</i>: cognate with Old Saxon and Old Dutch <i>twēne</i>, Old High German <i>zwēne</i>, whence archaic German <i>zween</i>, compare German <i>zwei</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twelve</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twalif</i>, a compound of <i>&#42;twa-</i> (compare two) and <i>&#42;-lif</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>twelvefold</li></ol><p>From <i>twelf</i> +&lrm; <i>-feald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twelfhynde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>criminal law</i>) Worth twelve hundred (shillings in wergeld).</li></ol><p>From <i>twelf</i> +&lrm; <i>-hynde</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twelfth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>twelfth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twaliftô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>twelve years old / twelve-year-old</li><li>twelve-year</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twalifwintruz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>tolfvetr</i> and Gothic <i>𐍄𐍅𐌰𐌻𐌹𐌱𐍅𐌹𐌽𐍄𐍂𐌿𐍃</i> (twalibwintrus). Equivalent to <i>twelf</i> +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squeeze, pinch</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;twangijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twangijaną</i> (“to jam, pinch”), causative of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twinganą</i> (“to press, clamp”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twēntiġfeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twaintigaz</i>, <i>&#42;twai tigiwiz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian and Old Saxon <i>twentig</i>, Old High German <i>zweinzug</i>, Old Norse <i>tuttugu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twentieth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>twentyfold</li></ol><p>From <i>twēntiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-feald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twentieth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>twentieth</li></ol><p>From <i>twentiġ</i> (“twenty”) +&lrm; <i>-oþa</i> (“-eth”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>twenty year(s) old</li><li>lasting twenty years</li></ol><p><i>twēntiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twentieth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doubt</li><li>ambiguity</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;twihō</i> (“doubt”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to doubt [<i>with</i> genitive] (often impersonal, with accusative subject)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;twihōn</i>. Equivalent to <i>twēo</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twēonung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>doubtfully</li><li>ambiguously</li></ol><p><i>twēo</i> (“doubt”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twēoġan</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doubt</li></ol><p>Shortened from <i>twēoġan</i>.</p><p>Derived from <i>twēo</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to doubt</li><li>to cause one to doubt</li></ol><p>From <i>twēon</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>doubtful</li></ol><p>From <i>twēonian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doubt, uncertainty, hesitation</li></ol><p>From <i>twēon</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twēonung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>twice, two times</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;twiwō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>two, double</li><li>twice, bi-</li><li>implying doubt or uncertainty</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;twi-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twi-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dwi-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tweowa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twitch</li><li>to pluck</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;twekkōn</i> (“to fasten; clamp; pinch”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place where two roads meet</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Anglian</i>) twig, shoot; sapling</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Apparently from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;twiggu</i>, perhaps a diminutive or related to Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;twīg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to halve, bisect</li></ol><p>From <i>twi-</i> +&lrm; <i>dǣlan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>double-edged</li></ol><p>From <i>twi-</i> (“double”) +&lrm; <i>eċġ</i> (“edge”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i>, from the noun <i>eċġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>double-edged</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>twi-</i> (“two”) +&lrm; <i>eċġed</i> (“edged”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i> (adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twifealdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>double</li><li>doubtful, irresolute</li><li>placed together</li><li>(<i>of dealing</i>) double, unstraightforward, deceitful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twifaldaz</i>, equivalent to <i>twi-</i> +&lrm; <i>-feald</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>twifald</i>, Old Norse <i>tvífaldr</i>, Old Saxon <i>twifald</i>, Old High German <i>zwifalt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diphthong</li></ol><p>Calque of Latin <i>diphthongus</i> (literally “twofold-sound”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doubleness, doubling; twofoldness</li><li>irresolution</li><li>duplicity, deceitfulness</li></ol><p>From <i>twifeald</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>accessible by two ways, having two ways</li></ol><p>From <i>twi-</i> +&lrm; <i>fēre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dissociate</li></ol><p>From <i>twifēre</i> (“two-wayed”) +&lrm; <i>-lǣċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bipedal, two-footed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twifōtijaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>twi-</i> +&lrm; <i>-fēte</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having two prongs, forked</li></ol><p>From <i>twī-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;fyrclede</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twig</li><li>branch</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;twīg</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twīgą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tweowa</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>twiġ</i></li></ol><p>From <i>twiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tweowa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tweowa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>between</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>a balance with two scales</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>twi-</i> (“two”) +&lrm; <i>heolor</i> (“balance”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think differently</li><li>to disagree</li><li>to dissent</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>twi-</i> +&lrm; <i>hyċġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>criminal law</i>) Worth two hundred (shillings in wergeld).</li></ol><p>From <i>twi-</i> +&lrm; <i>-hynde</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twilīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>double, woven from double thread</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twinn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twinkle</li></ol><p>Frequentative of <i>twincan</i> (“to blink, wink”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twēonian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>occurring as a pair; double; dual</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twihnaz</i>, Northwest Germanic variation of <i>&#42;twīhnaz</i> (“twofold; double”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twēonung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twirǣdness</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of two minds, undecided</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>twi-</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣd</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discord; dissension; disagreement</li></ol><p><i>twi-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;rǣdnes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twirǣdness</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>condemned to a double penalty</li></ol><p>From <i>twi-</i> +&lrm; <i>scyldig</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forked</li><li>double</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the fork of a river, road, etc</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twisilô</i>, <i>&#42;twisilą</i>, <i>&#42;twisilō</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>zwisila</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>two-stringed</li></ol><p><i>twi-</i> +&lrm; <i>-strenġe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tweowa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>two years old / two-year-old</li><li>two-year</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twiwintruz</i>. Equivalent to <i>twi-</i> +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tweowa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twi-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twieċġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twifeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twifealdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twihynde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twēonian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twēonol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twēonung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twirǣdness</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adulation</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;twæddan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>two thirds</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;twaidī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twelf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divide, separate, part</li><li>to cause dissent among</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>division, separation, dissociation</li><li>distinction</li></ol><p>From <i>twǣman</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twēġen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tiċċen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tihting</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tyhtan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tīd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tȳdernes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tȳdernes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tȳdre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frailty, weakness (physical, mental, moral, etc.)</li><li>infirmity, ill health</li><li>spiritual infirmity, sinfulness</li></ol><p>From <i>tȳdre</i> (“fragile, weak, infirm”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to propagate, bring forth, produce</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tūdor</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>easily broken; fragile; brittle</li><li>weak; frail</li><li>in failing health; infirm</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tūdrijaz</i>, perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;der-</i> (“to flay; cleave; rive”). Compare Old Frisian <i>teddre</i>, Middle Dutch <i>teder</i>, Middle Low German <i>teder</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tȳdran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tīeġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tug, pull</li><li>dragging</li><li>leading, conducting</li><li>a draught of drink</li><li>a drawing of an inference, deduction</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tugi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tigele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is drawn</li><li>training; instruction; motion; progress</li><li>way; manner of conducting one's self; usage; practice</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tuhti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tuhtiz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>zuht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw, stretch, pull</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to invite, incite, instigate, provoke; to talk over, discuss, persuade, solicit, urge; to attract, lead astray, seduce</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>taking accusative object followed by preposition <i>on</i> or <i>tō</i> and its object, <i>towards something</i></li><li>preceding a dependent clause, the condition being urged</li></ol><li>to suggest, bring to mind; to teach, train</li><li>to accuse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tuhtijan</i> (“to tow, pull, discipline”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tuhtiz</i> (“pulling, discipline”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dewk-</i> (“to lead, draw”), equivalent to <i>tyht</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>zuhten</i> (“to raise, bring up”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incitement, inducement, incentive</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tyht</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inciter, instigator</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tyhtan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>able to urge or encourage</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tyhtende</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>, else <i>tyhtan</i> +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inciter, instigator</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tyht</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>persuasion, exhortation, instigation</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tyhtan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tihtle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instigation</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>tyht</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tīma</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tīeman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>timberland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tīen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enclose, fence in</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tūnijan</i>. By surface analysis, <i>tūn</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>. Compare Old Frisian <i>tēna</i>, Old High German <i>zūnen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small barrel; cask</li><li>bladder</li></ol><p>From <i>tunne</i> +&lrm; <i>-ċen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tinder</li></ol><p>From a <i>-j-</i>suffixed derivative of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tundr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tundrą</i>. Cognate to Old High German <i>zuntra</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tīenfeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>skilful with the tongue, eloquent; rhetorical</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tungī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tungijaz</i> (“relating to the tongue”), from <i>&#42;tungǭ</i> (“tongue”); equivalent to <i>tunge</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antidote, medicine</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>&#42;triaca</i>, <i>&#42;tiriaca</i>, late form of <i>theriaca</i>, ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>θηριακή</i> (thēriakḗ, “antidote”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tierġan</i> (“to worry, vex”)</li><li>alternative form of <i>tēorian</i> (“to tire, faint”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to turn, revolve</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to turn, cause to turn</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;turnijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tyrning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an instance of turning around</li><li>roundness</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>tyrnan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buzzard</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tuskijō</i> (literally “frog-catcher, frog-eater”), related to Old English <i>tosċa</i> (“frog, toad”) (compare Old English <i>frysċa</i> (“bittern”) from <i>frosċ</i> (“frog”)); or alternatively derived from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tūsōn</i> (“to ruffle”), related to Old English <i>tysse</i> (“coarse cloth”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be conspicuous, stand out</li><li>to sparkle, shine</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tūtijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tut(t)-</i> (“to protrude”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Cognate with Latin <i>Totila</i>, from Gothic <i>&#42;Tutila</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Tytla</li></ol><p>From <i>Tytla</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show, point out</li><li>to show (someone) the way; to direct, guide</li><li>to teach</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;taikijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;taikijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the pointing out of a course to be followed; direction; teaching</li><li>doctrine</li></ol><p>From <i>tǣċan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tǣċing</i></li></ol><p>From <i>tǣċan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tættec</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tæfl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gameboard</li><li>a board game</li><li>a die used to play a game</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tablā, &#42;tabulā</i>, from Latin <i>tabula</i> (“tablet, board, plank, chart”), ultimately from Proto-Italic <i>&#42;taðla</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>tafl</i> (“board game”), Old Norse <i>tǫfl</i> (“a die”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gamble, play a game</li></ol><p>From <i>tæfl</i> (“game board, a game”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>tefla</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gambler</li></ol><p>From <i>tæflan</i> (“to gamble”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>die, game piece</li></ol><p>From <i>tæfl</i> (“die”) +&lrm; <i>stān</i> (“stone”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tēag</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tæġl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tail</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tagl</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;taglą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deḱ-</i>. Cognate with Middle Low German <i>tagel</i> (“twisted whip”), Old High German <i>zagal</i> (“animal’s tail”) (dialectal German <i>Zagel</i>), Old Norse <i>tagl</i> (“horse’s or cow’s tail”), Gothic <i>𐍄𐌰𐌲𐌻</i> (tagl, “hair of the head”); and, beyond Germanic, with Old Irish <i>dūal</i> (“lock of hair”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tēar</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evil-speaking, calumny, disparagement, slander</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Either a variant of <i>tāl</i>; a deverbal of <i>tǣlan</i>; or possibly from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tāli</i>, related to Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tālu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blame, accuse</li><li>to rebuke, reprove</li><li>to criticise, censure, reprimand</li><li>to scorn, despise</li><li>to insult, mock</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tālijan</i>, <i>&#42;tālēn</i> (“to deceive, strategise, persecute”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tēlō</i> (“persecution, deceit”), equivalent to <i>tāl</i> (“blame”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>zālēn</i> (“to persecute”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>telg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>telg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blame</li></ol><p><i>tǣlan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>date</li></ol><p>From <i>tæl</i> +&lrm; <i>mearc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slander, reproach</li></ol><p>From <i>tǣl</i> (“calumny”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reprehensible, blameworthy</li></ol><p>From <i>tǣl</i> +&lrm; <i>-wierþe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tǣlwierþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Temes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wicker basket</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tainīl</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tainīlaz</i>, a diminutive of <i>&#42;tainijǭ</i> (“wickerwork; basket”), from <i>&#42;tainaz</i> (“rod; twig”). Related to Old High German <i>zeina</i> (“basket”), Old Norse <i>teina</i> (“wicker basket”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Tenet</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tap, spigot</li><li>band, ribbon, tape</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tappō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tappô</i>, whence also Old High German <i>zapho</i>, Old Norse <i>tappi</i>.</p><p>Normally prehistoric *æ was retracted to *a when followed by a geminate and back vowel. Reason for consistent failure of retraction in this word remains unknown. If the word is infrequently attested a commoner variant *tappa may well have existed, cf. hnappian alongside less common hnæppian.</p><p>Unknown. Related to Old Frisian <i>tapia</i> (“to pluck”), Middle Low German <i>teppen</i> (“to pluck, pick”). Possibly from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tappaz</i>; see <i>&#42;tuppaz</i> (“tuft”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>band; ribbon; tape</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tavern keeper, bartender</li><li>wine seller</li></ol><p>From <i>tæppa</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female tavern-keeper, tapster, barmaid</li></ol><p>From <i>tæppa</i> +&lrm; <i>-estre</i>; compare <i>tæppere</i> (“barkeep, tavern-keeper”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tap</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tappōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tappōną</i>, equivalent to <i>tæppa</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tease (fibres)</li><li>to tear apart</li><li>to rip apart or wound (with a weapon)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;taisijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tǣsl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teasel (genus <i>Dipsacus</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;taisilu</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tesu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make cheerful, gladden</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;taitijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;taitijaną</i> (“to please”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dey-</i> (“to gleam, shimmer, shine”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tættec</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a rag, tatter</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;tætte</i> +&lrm; <i>-ec</i> (diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rag, tatter</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;tætte</i> +&lrm; <i>-ca</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tætteca</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tættec</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tættec</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weall</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Early Old English</i>) alternative form of <i>weard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>udder</li><li>breast</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ūdarą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wierman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>fers</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>owl</li><li>vulture</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>above</li><li>from above</li><li>down</li><li>upon</li><li>at the top</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;obanā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ubanē</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;upp</i> (“up”). Cognate with Old High German <i>obana</i> (German <i>oben</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>top of</li></ol><p><i>ufan</i> +&lrm; <i>-weard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>yfemest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>yferra</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to elevate, make higher</li><li>to make later, to delay</li></ol><p>Compare Old High German <i>obarōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>higher</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>yferra</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>upper (part of)</li><li>the top (of something)</li><li>latter part of (a period of time)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ufan</i> +&lrm; <i>-weard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>higher (<i>locally</i>)</li><li>later, after (<i>temporal</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uberō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;upér</i>, <i>&#42;upéri</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ufeweard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ūhta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the last part of the night, before dawn</li><li>the time or service of nocturns</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unhtwǭ</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>ūhta</i>, Old High German <i>ūhta</i>, regional German <i>Uchte</i> (“midnight mass”), Old Norse <i>ótta</i>, Norwegian Bokmål <i>otte</i>, and as the enhanced form Dutch <i>ochtend</i> (“morning”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pre-dawn anxiety</li></ol><p>From <i>ūhta</i> +&lrm; <i>caru</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ūhtcaru</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ūhtena</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>matins</li></ol><p>From <i>uhta</i> (“nocturns”) +&lrm; <i>sang</i> (“liturgical service”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vienna, in the former province of Dauphiné</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wille</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vulcan, the Roman god of fire and blacksmithing</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Vulcānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>owl</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uwwalǭ</i>, originally a diminutive of <i>&#42;uwwô</i> (“owl”) (Old High German <i>hūwo</i>, Old Saxon <i>hūo</i>), probably a word imitative of the animal's call, or a variant of <i>&#42;ūfaz</i>, <i>&#42;ūfǭ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;up-</i>.</p><p>See also Old English <i>ūf</i> or <i>hūf</i>, Swedish <i>uv</i> (“horned owl”), Bavarian <i>Auf</i>; also Middle Low German <i>ūle</i>, Dutch <i>uil</i>, Old Norse <i>ugla</i>. A Germanic variant <i>&#42;uwwilǭ</i> was the source of Old High German <i>ūwila</i> (German <i>Eule</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vulture</li></ol><p>Perhaps from a British dialect form of Latin <i>vultur</i> (“vulture”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) child</li></ol><p>Origin obscure. Possibly from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;unbur</i> (“unborn child, womb-child”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unburaz</i> (“unborn”, adjective), equivalent to <i>un-</i> (“un-, not”) +&lrm; a derivative of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;beraną</i> (“to bear”), related to Old English <i>byre</i> (“son, child, descendant”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>negation or absence of: un-, non- (<i>added to nouns and adjectives</i>)</li><li>bad (added to nouns to denote a pejorative sense; compare <i>mis-</i>, <i>mal-</i>)</li><li>forms verbs from verbs, with an opposite or reversive sense</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;un-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;un-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥-</i>, a prefix use of the particle <i>&#42;né</i> (“not”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>un-</i>, Old Saxon <i>un-</i>, Old Dutch <i>un-</i>, Old High German <i>un-</i>, Old Norse <i>ó-</i>, Gothic <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-). The Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek <i>ἀ-</i> (a-), Latin <i>in-</i>, and Old Irish <i>in-</i>.</p><p>Originally an alternative form of <i>on-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;and-</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>und-</i>, Old Saxon <i>ant-</i>, Old High German <i>ant-</i> (German <i>ent-</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unbearable</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>āberendlīċ</i>. Compare German <i>unerträglich</i>, of similar form and meaning.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unnumbered, countless, numberless</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ārīman</i> +&lrm; <i>-ed</i> (“past participle suffix”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>innumerable, countless, boundless, infinite</li></ol><p><i>unārīmed</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inseparable</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>āsundrodlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unwritten</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>āwriten</i> (“written”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not bold, not confident, irresolute</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>beald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>innocence</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>bealu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>innocent, harmless</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>bealuful</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without a beginning</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> (“un-”) +&lrm; <i>begunnen</i> (“begun”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>barren, sterile, not bearing</li><li>unbearable</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unberandz</i>, equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>berende</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unbieldu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weakness, irresolution, inconsistency</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>bieldu</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unbind</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andabindaną</i>, equivalent to <i>un-</i> + <i>bindan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sad, grieved</li><li>unkind</li><li>not peaceful</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>blīþe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unborn</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unburanaz</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐌿𐌽𐌱𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰𐌽𐍃</i> (unbaurans).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unbroken, unbreakable, inviolate</li><li>useless, profitless, unprofitable</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>bryċe</i> (“breakable”).</p><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>brȳċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unbieldu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unwarlike</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>camp</i> +&lrm; <i>-rōf</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>our (<i>referring to two people</i>); ours</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free (without cost)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> (“not”) +&lrm; <i>ċīepe</i> (“for sale”) or <i>un-</i> (“un-”) + <i>ċēap</i> (“cost”) + <i>-e</i> (“having”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unclǣnnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unclean, dirty</li><li>impure</li><li>(of food) unfit to eat</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>clǣne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unclean, impure</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>clǣnlīċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncleanness, impurity, foulness, squalor</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>unclǣne</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i> or <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>clǣnnes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unclǣnnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unclǣnnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unknot, untie</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>cnyttan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inhospitable</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>cumlīþe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inhospitality</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>cumlīþnes</i> or <i>uncumlīþe</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unknown</li><li>unfamiliar, strange</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>cūþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unkunþaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ignorant, unacquainted</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>cȳþiġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ignorance</li><li>unknown land</li><li>strange land</li><li>a country not one's own</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immortal</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>dēadlīċ</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>ódauðligr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>immortality</li></ol><p>From <i>undēadlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i> or <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>dēadlīcnes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>undēadlīcnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>undēadlīcnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immortal</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>dēaþlīċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immortally</li></ol><p>From <i>undēaþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) undeclinable</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>declīnigendlīċ</i> (“declinable”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Undolum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shallow (literally and figuratively)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;undeup</i>. Equivalent to <i>un-</i> (“un-”) +&lrm; <i>dēop</i> (“deep”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>narrow-minded</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>under, beneath</li><li>among</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;undar</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;under</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>undar</i>, Old High German <i>untar</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>between, among</li><li>beneath</li><li>subordinate to</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;undar</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;under</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nter-</i> (“between, among”), akin to Old English <i>under</i> (“under, beneath”), Old High German <i>untar</i> (“between, among”), Latin <i>inter</i> (“between, among”). More at inter-.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;undar</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;under</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ndhero-</i> (“lower”), akin to Old English <i>under</i> (“between, among, in the presence of”), Old High German <i>untar</i> (“under”), Latin <i>infra</i> (“below, beneath”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to support</li><li>to endure</li></ol><p>From <i>under-</i> (“under”) +&lrm; <i>beran</i> (“to bear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread under</li></ol><p>From <i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>breġdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to submit</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>būgan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suburb</li></ol><p>From <i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>burg</i>. Perhaps originally a calque of Latin <i>suburbium</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>backwards, back</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to subvert</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċierran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a vassal king, an underking, one who rules under the authority of another</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>under</i> +&lrm; <i>cyning</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>underċierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig under, undermine, dig out</li></ol><p>From <i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>delfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>undertaking, acceptance (glosses Latin <i>susceptio</i>)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>fōn</i> (“to snatch, grap (past participle <i>fangen</i>)”) +&lrm; <i>-el</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow under</li></ol><p>From <i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>flōwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to receive, get</li><li>to submit to</li><li>to take in, accept, or admit</li><li>to take upon one's self; to undertake with effort</li><li>to take up a burden; to undergo, bear</li><li>to take surreptitiously; to steal</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;undarfą̄han</i>. Equivalent to <i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>fōn</i>. Cognate to Old Dutch <i>underfān</i> and Old High German <i>untarfāhan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ruin, undermine</li><li>to undergo</li><li>to go under</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;undergān</i>. Equivalent to <i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>gan</i>. Akin to Old Saxon <i>undargan</i>, <i>undargangan</i>, Old High German <i>untargān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to undergo, bear, endure; thole</li><li>to undermine</li></ol><p>From <i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>gangan</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>undargangan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin</li><li>to undertake</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;undarginnan</i>, derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ginnaną</i>; equivalent to <i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;ginnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>innoxious, innocent, harmless</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>derian</i> +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run beneath</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>irnan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to underlay, support</li></ol><p>From <i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>leċġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>underling, subordinate</li></ol><p><i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>-ling</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>terce, the third hour of the day (around 9 am)</li><li>(<i>in some later use</i>) midday, the sixth hour of the day (12 pm)</li><li>(<i>in compounds</i>) morning generally</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;undurniz</i> (“interval”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁n̥ter</i>, <i>&#42;h₁enter</i> (“between”). Influenced in sense by Latin <i>tertia</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>undorn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>underneath</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;underniþer</i>; corresponding to <i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>neoþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breakfast, morning meal</li></ol><p>From <i>undern</i> (“third hour of the day”) +&lrm; <i>ġereord</i> (“meal, food”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to receive, take upon oneself, undertake (to take into the mind what is said or taught)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;underneman</i>. Equivalent to <i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>niman</i>. Akin to Old Saxon <i>undarniman</i> and Old High German <i>untarneman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food eaten in the morning, breakfast</li></ol><p>From <i>undern</i> (“third hour of the day”) +&lrm; <i>mete</i> (“food”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morning-time</li></ol><p><i>undern</i> +&lrm; <i>mǣl</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>terce</li></ol><p>From <i>undern</i> (“third hour of the day”) +&lrm; <i>sang</i> (“liturgical service”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>terce; the third hour after sunrise</li><li>a church service held at the third hour</li></ol><p>From <i>undern</i> (“third hour”) +&lrm; <i>tīd</i> (“time”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>terce; the third hour after sunrise</li></ol><p>From <i>undern</i> (“third hour”) +&lrm; <i>tīma</i> (“time”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move to a place beneath, to intercept</li><li>to underprop, support</li></ol><p><i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċēotan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to investigate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;undersōkijan</i> (literally “to search under or between”). Cognate with Old High German <i>&#42;untarsuohhen</i> → German <i>untersuchen</i>. Equivalent to <i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to search or examine beneath</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>smēaġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to understand</li><li>to accept as correct</li><li>to observe, notice, consider</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;understandan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;understandaną</i> (“to stand between, stand in the midst, understand”), from <i>&#42;under</i> (“between, among”), <i>&#42;standaną</i> (“to stand”). Akin to Old Frisian <i>understanda</i>, <i>understonda</i> (Saterland Frisian <i>unnerstounde</i>), Old High German <i>untarstantan</i>, Old Norse <i>undirstanda</i> (Middle Danish <i>understande</i>). Equivalent to <i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>standan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>existence</li><li>essence</li></ol><p>From <i>under-</i> (“under; among”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;standennes</i> (“existence”). Possibly a calque of Latin <i>substantia</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>understanding</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intelligence</li><li>perception, understanding</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;undarstandingu</i>, equivalent to <i>understandan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>understandan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to support, sustain</li></ol><p>From <i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>wreþian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write under, sign; underwrite, subscribe</li></ol><p>From <i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrītan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look into, consider</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;underþankijan</i>, equivalent to <i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>þenċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dative&#32;or&#32;preposition</i>) to subjugate, make subject (beneath or under) (+ under)</li><li>to subject to something; to make endure; to make liable</li><li>to subjoin, add</li><li>to support</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>þēodan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) relating to the subjunctive mood</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>underþēodende</i> (“subjugating, subjoining”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Compare Latin <i>subiunctivus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to subjugate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>under-</i> +&lrm; <i>þīedan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cheap</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;undiurijaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>untiuri</i>, Old Norse <i>ódýrr</i>. Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>dīere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>undēop</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oundle (a market town and civil parish with a town council in North Northamptonshire district, Northamptonshire, England, previously in East Northamptonshire district)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ondōn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sober (not drunk)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;undrunkanaz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>ódrukkinn</i>. Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>druncen</i>, literally "un-drunk."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crime, misdeed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;undēdiz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>undād</i>, Middle Dutch <i>ondaet</i>, Old High German <i>untāt</i> (German <i>Untat</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>barely, hardly</li><li>not easily, with difficulty</li><li>reluctantly, unwillingly</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unīeþe</i></li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ēaþe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unequal, unlike, dissimilar, diverse, irregular</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>efen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unefen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unefen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>disorderly, in a disorderly manner, without order</li><li>irregularly</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>endebyrd</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>, <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>endebyrdlīċe</i>, or unattested <i>&#42;unendebyrdlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unfold, unroll, open (a book)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to unfold by narrating</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>fealdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>nearby, close</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> (“not”) +&lrm; <i>feorr</i> (“far”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Unferth, a character in the poem <i>Beowulf</i></li></ol><p>Unclear; perhaps <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>friþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inevitable, unavoidable</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>inevitably</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>unforbugendlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unburnt, unburned, not burned up or burned down</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>forburnen</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unforeseen, unconsidered</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>fore-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċēawian</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furaskawwōną</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>voorschouwen</i>, German <i>vorschauen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unafraid, fearless</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>forht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>unfriþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absence of peace, hostility</li><li>the state of being out of the king's peace</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;unfriþu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unfriþuz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>friþ</i>. Compare Dutch <i>onvrede</i> (“displeasure, quarrel”), German <i>Unfriede</i> (“strife”) and Old Norse <i>úfriðr</i> (“war, hostilities”). Compare to modern English <i>fray</i> ("conflict, quarrel, heated argument,") which derives by aphesis from <i>affray</i>—an Old French loan—ultimately equivalent to a compound of French <i>é-</i> ("out, away from, de-") + Frankish <i>&#42;friþu</i> (“peace, sanctuary”), thus an equivalent formation to <i>unfriþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hostile fleet</li></ol><p>From <i>unfriþ</i> (“hostility”) +&lrm; <i>flota</i> (“fleet”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hostile army</li></ol><p>From <i>unfriþ</i> (“hostility”) +&lrm; <i>here</i> (“army”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hostile country</li></ol><p>From <i>unfriþ</i> (“hostility”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“country”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a person from a hostile country</li></ol><p>From <i>unfriþ</i> (“hostility”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a ship engaging in hostilities</li><li>an enemy ship</li></ol><p><i>unfriþ</i> (“hostility”) +&lrm; <i>sċip</i> (“ship”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not old</li><li>unwise, ignorant</li><li>rude</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>frōd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ignorance</li><li>rudeness</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>unfrōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unfrōdnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>imperfect</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>fulfremed</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imperfection</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>fulfremednes</i> or <i>unfulfremed</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unfulfremednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unfulfremednes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ugly</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unfagraz</i>. Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>fæġer</i>. More at <i>fæġer</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not firmly</li><li>uncertainly</li><li>vaguely</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>fæstlīċe</i> (“firmly”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not long ago, recently</li><li>soon, before long</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġēara</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beardless</li><li>young</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>beard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unġebeard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unġebeard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unchosen, unselected</li><li>evil</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġecoren</i> (past participle of <i>ċēosan</i>)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unnatural, supernatural, contrary to nature</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġecyndelīċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Unnaturally, abnormally; in a way contrary to expectations or nature.</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġecyndelīċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indefinite</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) infinitive</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeendian</i> +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>infinite</li><li>endless</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> (“un-”) + <i>ġeendod</i> (“ended”) + <i>-līċ</i> (“-ly”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indiscretion</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġefæd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unġehīersum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unġehīersumnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disobedient</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ungahauʀisam</i>. Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġehīersum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disobedience</li></ol><p>From <i>unġehīersum</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unġehīersum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unġehīersumnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unġeþwǣrnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unġeþwǣrnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unġehīersum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unġehīersumnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unbelief</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġelēafa</i> (“belief”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unbelieving, infidel</li><li>not Christian</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>different</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ungalīkaz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġelīċ</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>ongelijk</i>, German <i>ungleich</i>, Swedish <i>olik</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>difference</li><li>inequality</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġelīcnes</i> or <i>unġelīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unseasonably, unhappily</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> (“un-”) +&lrm; <i>ġelimplīċe</i> (“suitably”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unlearned, ignorant, unskilled</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġelǣred</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immoderate, excessive, too great</li><li>inordinate</li><li>immense</li><li>diverse</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġemetlīċ</i> (“moderate”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>beyond measure</li><li>too much</li><li>immensely</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immeasurable, immense, excessive, enormous</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġemǣte</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>ungimāzi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unnameable, unknowable</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġenemnendlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not having an intelligible language</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġereord</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a countless number</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>countless, innumerable, incalculable</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġerīm</i> (“number”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hungary (a former kingdom in Central Europe, generally much larger than the modern state of Hungary)</li></ol><p>The first element is derived from Latin <i>Ungaria</i> (“Hungary”) or <i>Ungarī</i> (“Hungarians”), or else is an unattested Old English word meaning “a Hungarian,” from the same source. Other country names contain similar first elements, as in <i>Swēoland</i> (“Sweden”, literally “Swede land”) and <i>Francland</i> (“France”, literally “Frank land”); see also the prefix <i>Angel-</i>.</p><p>The second element is <i>land</i> (“land, country”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indiscreet</li><li>unreasonable, irrational</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indiscretion</li><li>unreason</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġesċeād</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unreasonable, irrational, unwise, foolish</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġesċēadwīs</i> or <i>unġesċēad</i> +&lrm; <i>-wīs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foolishness</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġesċēadwīsnes</i> (“wisdom”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>invisible</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġesewenlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unrelated</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a non-relative</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġesibb</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>invisible</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġesīene</i> (“visible”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unwearied, unceasing</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeswenċed</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unlucky, unfortunate, unprosperous</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġesǣliġ</i> (“lucky, fortunate”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calamity</li><li>misery, unhappiness</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġesǣliġnes</i> or <i>unġesǣliġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>unġesǣliġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>unġesǣliġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bad faith</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ungatriuwiþu</i>, equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġetrēowþ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unspotted, immaculate, uncorrupted, uninjured</li><li>undefiled, unstained, inviolate, immaculate</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purity</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not full-grown</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġewintred</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of people</i>) ignorant <i>(+ genitive of something)</i></li><li>(<i>of things</i>) unknown</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncertainty, ignorance, unconsciousness</li><li>unknown</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġewiss</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unwriten</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unusual, abnormal</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġewunelīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unnaturally</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġewunelīċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unarmed</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġewǣpnod</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not agreeable, in disagreement, disharmonious, discordant</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeþwǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discord, dissension, division, quarrel</li><li>trouble, disquiet</li></ol><p>From either <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeþwǣrnes</i> or <i>unġeþwǣre</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>. Variant "h" forms such as <i>unġehwǣrnes</i> may have arisen due to conflation with <i>hwǣr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unġeþwǣrnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impatient</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeþyldiġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carelessness</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>gīeman</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inhospitable</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> (“ġiestlīþe”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not ample, contracted</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġinn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unwise, without understanding, ignorant, unskilled</li><li>dull, unusable</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>glēaw</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>without understanding, unwisely, imprudently</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>glēawlīċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>want of understanding, unskillfulness, foolishness</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>glēawnes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of understanding; foolishness</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>glēawsċipe</i> or else <i>unglēaw</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dull, not shiny</li><li>cheerless, not glad</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;unglad</i>, equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>glæd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not good, bad</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ungōd</i>. Equivalent to <i>un-</i> (“un-”) +&lrm; <i>gōd</i> (“good”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not green</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ungrōnī</i>, equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>grēne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Hungarians</li><li>Hungary (a historical kingdom in Central Europe, occupying a larger area than modern Hungary)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Ungarī</i> (“Hungarians”) or <i>Ungaria</i> (“Hungary”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>irreprehensible</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>grīpendlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bottomless, deep</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;grynde</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not guilty; innocent</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>gyltiġ</i> (“guilty”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ungird, take or strip off a girdle, take off what is girded</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>gyrdan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>antgurten</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in bad health, sick, weak, ill, infirm, unhealthy, unsound</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unhailaz</i> (“not whole, unhealthy”), equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>hāl</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>unheil</i>, Old Norse <i>úheill</i>, Gothic <i>𐌿𐌽𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (unhails).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unholy, profane</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unhailiagaz</i>, equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>hāliġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disobedience</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>hīersumnes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infamy</li><li>bad reputation</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>hlīsa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disreputable</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>hlīsful</i> (“famous”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>unhlīsful</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfriendly, hostile</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unhulþaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>difficult, hard</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>īeþe</i> (“easy”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>unīeþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>unīeþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is not land (e.g. sea or marsh)</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evil teaching</li><li>incitement to evil</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>lār</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>true, not false</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>lēas</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>truly</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>lēas</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onlīesan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incorporeal</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>līchamlīċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not to be denied or rejected; incontrovertible</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;līeġne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unlīeġne</i> (“undeniable”)</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;līġne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onlūcan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absence of desire; disgust, disinclination, listlessness</li><li>want of pleasure; joylessness, weariness</li><li>evil pleasure, lust</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unlustuz</i>. Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>lust</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐌿𐌽𐌻𐌿𐍃𐍄𐌿𐍃</i> (unlustus, “apathy, listlessness”) and German <i>Unlust</i> (“lack of desire, aversion”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who makes poisons (especially for witchcraft)</li><li>sorcerer</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>lybb</i> (“poison”) +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“wright”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bad air, malaria</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>lyft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onlīesan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not little; great, large</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>lȳtel</i> (“little”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poor; miserable; unhappy; unfortunate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unlēdaz</i>, equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;lǣd</i>, <i>lǣþ</i> (“a division of land, lathe”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lēdą</i>, <i>&#42;lēþą</i> (“allotment of land, owndom, possessions”). Cognate with Old High German <i>unlāt</i> (“poor, miserable”), Gothic <i>𐌿𐌽𐌻𐌴𐌳𐍃</i> (unlēds, “poor”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a non-human</li><li>brute, thug</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;unmann</i> (“non-human, monster”), equivalent to <i>un-</i> (“non-; bad”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”). Cognate with Middle Low German <i>unman</i> (“monster, fiend”), Middle High German <i>unman</i> (“evildoer”), and Old Norse <i>úmannan</i> (“wretch, laughing-stock”). Compare also Old High German <i>unmennisko</i> (“a non-human”) → German <i>Unmensch</i> (“brute”), which is formed similarly.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unmiht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unmiht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unmihtiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inhumane, inhuman</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>mennisċlīċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unmiht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of power, weakness</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unmahtiz</i>. Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>miht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impossible</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;unmahtulīk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unmahtulīkaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>unmahtlīh</i>, Old Norse <i>úmáttuligr</i>. Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>mihtelīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not mighty; unmighty, weak, impotent, of little power or means</li><li>impossible</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unmahtīgaz</i>; cognate with Old High German <i>unmahtīg</i>, Gothic <i>𐌿𐌽𐌼𐌰𐌷𐍄𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (unmahteigs). By surface analysis, <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>mihtiġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unmihtelīċ</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unmahtilīkaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>unmahtlīh</i>, Old Norse <i>ómáttuligr</i>. Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>mihtlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unforgivable, unpardonable</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>carelessly, without compunction or anxiety</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>murnan</i> (“to worry about”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unmiltsiendlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unmyndlinga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unexpectedly</li><li>unknowingly, unintentionally</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;mynd</i> (“mind”) +&lrm; <i>-linga</i> (adverbial suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unmihtiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unġemǣte</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;unmātī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unmētiz</i>, equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>mǣte</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>unmāzi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grant or bestow (+ <i>dat</i> (person) + <i>gen</i> (thing))</li><li>to wish</li><li>to intercede</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;unnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unnytt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>useless</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>nytlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>uselessly</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>nytlīċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uselessness</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>nytlīċnes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uselessness</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>unnytt</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>useless</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>nytt</i>. Cognate with German <i>unnütz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>unnytt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>useless</li><li>disadvantageous</li><li>unprofitable</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>nytwierþe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unnytwierþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>invincible</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>oferswīþendlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unbeatable, invincible</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>oferwinnendlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unslain</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ofsleġen</i> (“slain”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not right, wrong</li><li>illegal</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wrong, evil, injustice, depravity</li><li>a defect</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unrehtaz</i>, equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>riht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unjustly, wrongly</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adultery</li></ol><p><i>unriht</i> +&lrm; <i>hǣmed</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adulterer</li></ol><p><i>unriht</i> (“wrong”) +&lrm; <i>hǣman</i> (“to copulate”) +&lrm; <i>-end</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adulterer</li></ol><p>From <i>unrihthǣmed</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unrighteous, unjust, wicked, wrongful</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>unriht</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i> or <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>rihtlīċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a woman of bad character</li></ol><p>From <i>unriht</i> +&lrm; <i>wīf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unjust, unfair</li><li>unrighteous</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>rihtwīs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>injustice, unfairness</li><li>unrighteousness</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>rihtwīs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evildoer</li></ol><p>From <i>unriht</i> (“wrong”) +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“worker”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a countless number or immeasurable amount (+ genitive = of something)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;unrīm</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>unrīm</i>. Equivalent to <i>un-</i> (“non-”) +&lrm; <i>rīm</i> (“number”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unripe</li><li>immature</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unrīpijaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>rīpe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sorrowful, sad</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not sore</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>sār</i> (“sore”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsown; not sown</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>sāwen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shameless, impudent</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċamfæst</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unsċamfæst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not sharp</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;unskarp</i>, equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċearp</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>innocent, harmless</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċeþþende</i> (“scathing, harmful”, present participle of <i>sċeþþan</i>)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without shoes</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unskōhōdaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġesċōd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to undress</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċrȳdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>innocence</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċyld</i>.</p><p>See also Danish and Norwegian Bokmål <i>uskyld</i> (“innocence”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>innocent</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċyldiġ</i> or <i>unsċyld</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p><p>See also Danish and Norwegian Bokmål <i>uskyldig</i> (“innocent”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ungiven</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>seald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discord, variance</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>seht</i> (“agreement”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hundseofontiġ</i> (“seventy”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unseemliness</li><li>impurity</li><li>vice</li><li>bad custom</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>sidu</i>; compare German <i>Unsitte</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sleepless</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;slǣpiġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rough, not smooth, uneven</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>smēþe</i> (“smooth”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>unsmēþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unwise, foolish</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>snotor</i> (“wise”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>imprudently, unwisely</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>snotorlīċe</i> (“wisely”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unsnotor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not softly, not gently</li><li>with trouble, difficultly</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>softe</i> (“softly”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>untrue, false</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>untruth, falsehood</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>sōþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>untrue</li><li>dishonest</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>sōþfæst</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>sōþfæst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>untruth</li><li>dishonesty</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>sōþfæstnes</i> or <i>unsōþfæst</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>unsōþfæst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disprove, prove false</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>sōþian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lying, speaking falsely</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; (“truthful”) +&lrm; <i>sōþsagol</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unstaþolfæstnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unstaþolfæstnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unstaþolfæstnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unstaþolfæstnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsettled, not stationary</li><li>wishing for change</li><li>unsteadfast, unstable, not enduring, easily moved</li><li>wavering, unstable in mind</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>staþol</i> +&lrm; <i>-fæst</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instability</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>staþolfæst</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unstaþolfæstnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unstaþolfæstnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disturbance, disquietude, clamor, restlessness, noise</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>stilnes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>motionless: stationary, immobile</li><li>inanimate</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>styriende</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>illiterate</li><li>unlettered</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>stæfwīs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not sour</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>sūr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bad dream</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> (“bad”) +&lrm; <i>swefn</i> (“dream”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Not sweet; unsweet.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;unswōtī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unswōtuz</i> (“unsweet”), equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>swēte</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not strongly, weakly</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>swīþe</i> (“very, much”) or <i>unswīþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>unswīþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impure, unclean</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>sȳfre</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impurity, uncleanness (physical or moral)</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>sȳfernes</i> (“purity, temperance, sobriety”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unsȳfernes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impure, unclean, foul</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>sȳfre</i> (“pure”). Compare Dutch <i>onzuiver</i> (“impure, mixed”) and German <i>unsauber</i> (“unclean, untidy, inaccurate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsatisfied, insatiable</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>sæd</i> (“satisfied”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>evil, wicked</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of people</i>) unhappy, unblessed, miserable as being evil</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) unhappy, bringing misery</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>sǣliġ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>unsālig</i>. Compare Swedish <i>osalig</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sapless, not sappy</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>sæpiġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to untie, unbind, loose</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>tīeġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>untīeġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wrong or improper time</li><li>a mishap</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>tīma</i> (“time”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>untimely</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>tīme</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indivisible, inseparable</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>tōdǣled</i> (“divided”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bad faith</li><li>faithlessness</li><li>perfidy, fraud</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>trēow</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in bad faith, perfidiously</li></ol><p>From <i>untrēow</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i> or <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>trēowlīċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perfidy, bad faith</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;untriuwiþu</i>, equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>trēowþ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>untrue, not faithful</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;untriuwī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;untriwwiz</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>weak, sick, infirm</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>trum</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weaken</li></ol><p><i>untrum</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weakness, sickness</li></ol><p><i>untrum</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>undoubtedly</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>untwifeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not double, honest, sincere, pure, simple; onefold</li><li>without devision, united, not double</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>twifeald</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>unzwifalt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>untīeġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ontȳnan</i></li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>tȳnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not poor, not paltry, noble, splendid</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>wāclīċ</i> (“poor, paltry”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>steady, stable</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unwaltaz</i>, equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;wealt</i>; see modern <i>walt</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>úvaltr</i> (“steady”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>unwealt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) defenceless, unprotected, unprepared ?</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> (“un-”) +&lrm; <i>wearn</i> (“refusal, resistance”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>young, not grown</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>weaxen</i> (“grown”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bad weather, storm</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;unwedr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unwedrą</i>, equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>weder</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>óveðr</i> (Swedish <i>oväder</i>) and Old High German <i>unwetar</i> (German <i>Unwetter</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>concrete objects</i>) spotless, without blemish, without defect, uninjured</li><li>(<i>abstract objects</i>) uninjured, inviolate</li><li>(<i>morally</i>) undefiled, pure, immaculate, perfect</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncorruptedness, immortality</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>wemming</i>, else <i>unwemman</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purity</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>unwemme</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hopeless</li><li>unexpected</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, from <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>wēnan</i> (“to hope”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) unsuitable for work; hard to work in (in reference to midnight)</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>weorc</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>unweorþlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of no value, worthless, contemptible</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>weorþ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of little worth, value, or importance, humble</li><li>that has little honor, not famous or splendid, poor</li><li>ignoble; mean, infamous, disgraceful; shameful</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>weorþlīċ</i> or <i>unweorþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-liċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>unweorþlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dishonor, disgrace, indignation</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>unweorþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unweorþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>displeasure</li><li>that which causes displeasure</li><li>absence of goodwill; dislike</li><li>unwillingness, reluctance, unwill</li><li>that which is unwanted or undesired</li><li>(<i>often + genitive unwilling person</i>) <i>used in the dative plural to express lack of willingness or consent, where Modern English would use an adverb or prepositional phrase:</i> unwillingly, unconsensually, without one's consent, against one's will</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>willa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unwind, unwrap what is wrapped up</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andawindaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>windan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>antwintan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unwitod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unwise, foolish, stupid, dumb</li><li>ignorant</li><li>mad, insane</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>wīs</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>unwīs</i>, Old High German <i>unwīs</i>, Old Norse <i>úvíss</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foolishness, stupidity, ignorance</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>wīsdōm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unwisely, foolishly</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>wīslīċe</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>unwīslīhho</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idiot</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;unwitō</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>unwizzo</i>. Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>wita</i>, literally "un-knower."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unaware, unwitting</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unwitandz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>unwitande</i>, Old Saxon <i>unwitandi</i>, Old High German <i>unwizzenti</i>, Old Norse <i>úvitandi</i>. Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>witende</i>, the present participle of <i>witan</i> (“to know”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uncertain</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>witod</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without understanding or wit</li><li>dumb, stupid, unaware, ignorant</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>wittiġ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>unwizzīg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incomparable</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> (“not”) +&lrm; <i>wiþmetendlīċ</i> (“comparable”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>onwrēon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unwritten</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> (“un-”) +&lrm; <i>writen</i> (“written”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bad or incorrect writer</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrītere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unweorþlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unwitod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unpleasant</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>wynsum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unpleasantness</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>wynsumnes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unweorþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unwemme</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unwary</li><li>unaware, not on one's guard, unprepared</li><li>inconsiderate</li><li>uncautious</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>wær</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heedlessness, imprudence</li></ol><p><i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>wærsċipe</i> (“caution”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>barren, sterile, unproductive</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>wæstmbǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nonproductivity</li><li>barrenness, infertility</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>wæstmbǣrnes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unwæstmbærnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unwatery, without water, dry</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>wæteriġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not noble; ignoble, mean, infamous</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>æþele</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ignobility</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>æþelnes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>unþēaw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ill-will, anger, displeasure, an unpleasing act, an offense or annoyance</li><li>not thanks, displeasure expressed in words</li><li>(<i>+ genitive unwilling person</i>) <i>used in the genitive singular to express lack of willingness or consent, where Modern English would use an adverb or prepositional phrase:</i> unwillingly, unconsensually, without one's consent, against one's will</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unþankaz</i>, equivalent to <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>þanc</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>undances</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bad habit, vice</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> +&lrm; <i>þēaw</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unbidden, uninvited</li></ol><p>From <i>un-</i> (“un-”) +&lrm; <i>þinged</i> (“invited”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>up</li><li>upwards</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;upp</i>, akin to Old High German <i>ūf</i>, Old Norse <i>upp</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>up, upward</li><li>heavenly, from above; upper</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>up</i> (“up, upward”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an uplifting, elevation</li></ol><p>From <i>ūp-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;āhafu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;+ accusative) to elevate, raise, raise up, lift, lift up</li></ol><p><i>up-</i> +&lrm; <i>āhebban</i> (“lift, raise”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ascent</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reproach, upbraid</li></ol><p>From <i>up-</i> +&lrm; <i>breġdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come up, arise</li></ol><p>From <i>up-</i> +&lrm; <i>cuman</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>supernal; celestial</li></ol><p>From <i>up</i> +&lrm; <i>-cund</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rising, up-coming</li></ol><p>From <i>up-</i> (“up”) +&lrm; <i>cyme</i> (“coming”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwelling on high</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>up-</i> +&lrm; <i>eard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a heavenly angel</li></ol><p><i>upp</i> (“up”) +&lrm; <i>engel</i> (“angel”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>upper floor or storey</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>up-</i> +&lrm; <i>flēring</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an upper floor or storey</li><li>an upper room</li></ol><p>From <i>up-</i> +&lrm; <i>flōr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>upflōr</i></li></ol><p>From <i>up-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;flōre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>journey up, ascension</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>up-</i> +&lrm; <i>færeld</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go up, raise</li></ol><p>From <i>up-</i> +&lrm; <i>gān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a rise, ascent</li><li>an approach: a way up</li><li>a landing: a going from sea to land</li><li>an incursion: a going inland</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uppgang</i>. Equivalent to <i>up-</i> +&lrm; <i>gang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>upgang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;+ accusative) to elevate, raise, raise up, lift, lift up</li></ol><p><i>up-</i> +&lrm; <i>hebban</i> (“lift, raise”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rural, rustic</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) of or relating to the upland or countryside</li></ol><p>From <i>up</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>, with umlaut due to the presence of the <i>-isċ</i> suffix.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>on high</li><li>sublime</li><li>celestial</li></ol><p>From <i>upp</i> (“up”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>uppan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>up</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;upp</i>, <i>&#42;ūp</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;upp</i>, <i>&#42;eup</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>up</i>, Old Saxon <i>up</i>, Old Dutch <i>up</i>, Old High German <i>ūf</i>, Old Norse <i>upp</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>on, onto, upon [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> dative]</li></ol><p>From <i>upp</i> +&lrm; <i>on</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>upastigennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>up</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uppē</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uppai</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rural districts, the country (as opposed to towns or cities)</li></ol><p>From <i>uppe</i> (“up”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>upflōr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>uplīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>uppan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>upright, erect</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uppreht</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;upprehtaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>upp</i> +&lrm; <i>riht</i>, literally 'straight up'.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>upright, erect</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>upriht</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firmament, upper heavens, ether, sky</li></ol><p>From <i>up-</i> (“upper”) +&lrm; <i>rodor</i> (“firmament, heavens”). Compare <i>upheofon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a springing up</li><li>rising of a heavenly body</li><li>birth</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>up-</i> +&lrm; <i>springan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ascension</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) the Ascension of Jesus</li></ol><p>From <i>up-</i> +&lrm; <i>stīgan</i> (“to go up”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>turned upwards</li><li>moving upwards</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>upward</li></ol><p><i>up</i> (“up”) +&lrm; <i>-weard</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>upwards</li></ol><p><i>upweard</i> +&lrm; <i>-es</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aurochs</li><li>the runic character ᚢ (/uː/ or /u/)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ūraz</i>. This root survives in the modern English <i>aurochs</i> (though that word is a loan from German), hence its meaning.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>our</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;unsar</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unseraz</i>. For development compare Old Norse <i>órr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wet-feathered</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;ūriġ</i> (“wet”) +&lrm; <i>feþere</i> (“feathered”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ūriġfeþera</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wūsc</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wunskaz</i> (“wish, desire”). Whence also Old High German <i>wunsc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li><i>(Northumbrian or poetic)</i> alternative form of <i>ūre</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unseraz</i> (“of us, our”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥s-ero-</i> (“our”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>ūse(r)</i> (“our”), Old Saxon <i>ūser</i> (“our”), Old High German <i>unsēr</i>, <i>unsār</i> (“our”), Old Norse <i>órr</i>, <i>várr</i> (“our”), Gothic <i>𐌿𐌽𐍃𐌰𐍂</i> (unsar, “our”). Related to Old English <i>ūs</i> (“us”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li><i>(Mercian)</i> alternative form of <i>ūre</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unseraz</i> (“of us, our”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥s-ero-</i> (“our”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>ūse(r)</i> (“our”), Old Saxon <i>ūser</i> (“our”), Old High German <i>unsēr</i>, <i>unsār</i> (“our”), Old Norse <i>órr</i>, <i>várr</i> (“our”), Gothic <i>𐌿𐌽𐍃𐌰𐍂</i> (unsar, “our”). Related to Old English <i>ūs</i> (“us”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ure</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>out</li><li>outside, outdoors</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ūt</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ūt</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;úd</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Dutch <i>ūt</i> (Dutch <i>uit</i>), Old Saxon <i>ūt</i> (Low German <i>ut</i>), Old High German <i>ūz</i> (German <i>aus</i>), Old Norse <i>út</i> (Swedish <i>ut</i>), Gothic <i>𐌿𐍄</i> (ut).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>out</li><li>out of, from</li><li>without; outside of</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ūt-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ūt</i> (“out”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;úd</i> (“away; out; outwards; upwards”). Akin to Old Frisian <i>ūt-</i>, Old Norse <i>út-</i>, Gothic <i>𐌿𐍄-</i> (ūt-, “out”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ūtan-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from without, outside</li><li>on the outside</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ūtanā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ūtanē</i> (“outside”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ūt</i>. Related to <i>ūt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>on the outside of</li><li>strange, foreign</li><li>from without, external</li></ol><p>From <i>ūtan</i> (“outside”). More at <i>ūt-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>abroad</li></ol><p><i>ūtan-</i> +&lrm; <i>bord</i> +&lrm; <i>-es</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>abroad</li></ol><p>From <i>ūtan</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i>; compare Old Norse <i>útanlands</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>outside, exterior</li></ol><p><i>ūtan-</i> +&lrm; <i>-weard</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ūtcwealm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>utter destruction</li></ol><p><i>ūt-</i> (“out”) +&lrm; <i>cwealm</i> (“death, plague”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remove; draw out</li></ol><p>From <i>ūt-</i> +&lrm; <i>dragan</i>. Compare Old High German <i>ūztragan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>outside, outdoors</li><li>at a distance, out</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ūtē</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ūtai</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ūtera</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>outer, exterior</li></ol><p>Formed from <i>ūt</i> (“out”). Cognate with Old High German <i>ūzaro</i>, Old Frisian <i>ūtera</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>open sea</li></ol><p>From <i>ūtera</i> (“outer”) +&lrm; <i>mere</i> (“sea”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ūtera</i> (“outer, exterior”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;hapax legomenon) eager to leave, ready to depart</li></ol><p>Etymology treeOld English <i>ūt</i>Old English <i>fūs</i>Old English <i>utfus</i></p><p>From <i>ūt</i> (“out”) +&lrm; <i>fūs</i> (“ready”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a going out</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Exodus</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ūt-</i> +&lrm; <i>færeld</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go out</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ūtgān</i>, equivalent to <i>ūt-</i> +&lrm; <i>gān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exit (a way out or a departure)</li><li>excrement</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ūtgangaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>ūtgong</i>, Old Saxon <i>ūtgang</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;ūtgang</i>, Old High German <i>ūzgang</i>, Old Norse <i>útgangr</i>. Equivalent to <i>ūt-</i> +&lrm; <i>gang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foreign war</li></ol><p>From <i>ūt-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġefeoht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put out, expel</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ūt</i> (“out”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an outlaw; fugitive</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>útlagi</i>, equivalent to <i>ūt-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;laga</i>. Compare Old English <i>lagu</i> (“law”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to outlaw</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ūt</i> (“out”) +&lrm; <i>lagian</i> (“to make law”) or <i>ūtlaga</i> (“outlaw”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outlawry</li></ol><p><i>ūt-</i> (“out”) +&lrm; <i>lagu</i> (“law”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>outlawed</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) an outlaw</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a foreign country</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ūtlandą</i> (“outland”), equivalent to <i>ūt</i> (“out”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a non-native</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ūtland</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>. Alternatively, as <i>ūt-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;lenda</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>outlandish; foreign; strange</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ūtlandiskaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>ūtland</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>. Cognate with German <i>ausländisch</i>, Swedish <i>utländsk</i>, Icelandic <i>útlenskur</i> (“foreign; alien”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead out, bring out</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ūtlaidijan</i>. Equivalent to <i>ūt-</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pasture away from a house</li></ol><p>Equivalent of <i>ūt-</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ūtera</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>let's</li></ol><p>Usually said to originate from a form of <i>witan</i> (“to know”). Ringe explains it as an allegro form of PWGmc 1pl. <i>&#42;(ga)wītum</i> (“we're going, we'll go”) which escaped the North Sea Germanic displacement of 1pl. endings because speakers reinterpreted it as a separate word.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an outfall, outlet</li><li>and exit; an escape hatch</li></ol><p>Derivative of <i>ūtsċēotan</i> (“to aid the escape of”), from <i>ūt-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċēotan</i> (“to shoot”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diarrhea</li><li>dysentery</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>ūt-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;siht</i> (literally “straining out”), the latter a derivative of <i>sēon</i> (“to strain”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>outward, to the outside</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>forth, outside, to the outside</li></ol><p>From <i>ūt</i> +&lrm; <i>-weard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>outwards, to the outside</li></ol><p><i>ūtweard</i> +&lrm; <i>-es</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wælcyrġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>webb</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weosule</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weosule</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wifel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Continental spelling of <i>Willibeald</i>, 8th C. missionary bishop of Eichstätt</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>winterfylleþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wituma</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wiþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>woldon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>worhana</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wrīd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>witodlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weolocbasu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wæs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ūþwitlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>from, away</li><li>intensive prefix</li></ol><p>From stressed Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;unþa-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immense, very great, huge</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>philosopher</li><li>scholar, expert; a person distinguished for wisdom or learning in general, or in some special field</li></ol><p>From <i>ūþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>wita</i> (“wise person”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>philosophy</li></ol><p>See <i>ūþwita</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to philosophize or study philosophy</li></ol><p>From <i>ūþwita</i> (“philosopher”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>philosophical</li></ol><p>From <i>ūþwita</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ūþwitegung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Veronica, a Catholic saint said to have wiped the face of Jesus during his journey to Calvary.</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Veronīca</i>, variant of <i>Berenīcē</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Φερενῑ́κη</i> (Pherenī́kē).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>woe! alas! (expression of grief or anguish)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>woe, ill</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wai</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wai</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alas, oh woe</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pliant</li><li>weak</li><li>mean, poor, of little value</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;waikw</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waikwaz</i>, whence also Old High German <i>weih</i>, Old Norse <i>veikr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to awake, arise, originate, to be born</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wakan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wakaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weǵ-</i> (“to be strong, be lively, be vigilant”). Cognate with Old High German <i>wahhen</i>, Old Norse <i>vaka</i>, Danish <i>våge</i>, Dutch <i>waken</i>, German <i>wachen</i>. Akin to Latin <i>vigil</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wacor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be/stay awake</li><li>to keep watch, be on guard</li><li>to become weakened or tired; to weaken</li><li>to calm; to lose one's bravery</li><li>to lose one's riches; to end up in poverty</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wakēn</i>.</p><p>From <i>wāc</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poor, of little dignity or worth, paltry</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waikalīkaz</i> (“weakly; weak”), equivalent to <i>wāc</i> (“weak”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>weakly, feebly</li><li>poorly, cheaply</li></ol><p><i>wāclīċ</i> (“feeble, poor, paltry”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>wāclīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weakness</li></ol><p>From <i>wāc</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>watchful, vigilant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wakulaz</i> (“watchful”). Cognate with Old High German <i>wahhal</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>watchful, vigilant; alert</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wakr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wakraz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>&#42;wekker</i>, Old Saxon <i>&#42;wakkar</i>, Old Dutch <i>&#42;wakkar</i>, Old High German <i>wahhar</i>, and Old Norse <i>vakr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>wāc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wascan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wake, watch</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wakō</i>, related to <i>&#42;wakjaną</i>, whence also Old High German <i>wahha</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vigilance</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woad</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;waiʀd</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waizdaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go, to advance, to travel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wadaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weh₂dʰ-</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wada</i>, Middle Dutch <i>waden</i> (Dutch <i>waden</i>), Old High German <i>watan</i> (German <i>waten</i>), Old Norse <i>vaða</i> (Icelandic <i>vaða</i>, Swedish <i>vada</i>). The Indo-European root is also the source of Latin <i>vādō</i> (“to go”), <i>vadum</i> (“a ford”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wave</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be agitated, astonished, amazed</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to gaze at, wonder at, admire; hesitate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wabbjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wabōną</i>, <i>&#42;wabjaną</i> (“to wander, sway”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;webʰ-</i> (“to move to and fro, wander”). More at wave.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wabōną</i>. Related to Old English <i>wifer</i> (“missile, arrow, dart”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wall (of a building or a house)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;waig</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waigaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wagian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a veil, as found covering the entrance of the Holy of Holies</li></ol><p>From <i>wāg</i> (“wall”) +&lrm; <i>hræġl</i> (“garment”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to move back and forth, wag, wave, shake</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shake, totter, sway</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be loose</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wagōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wagōną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>wagōn</i>, Old Norse <i>vaga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curtain</li><li>veil</li></ol><p>From <i>wāg</i> (“wall”) +&lrm; <i>rift</i> (“curtain”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a doorway or an entrance to a room</li></ol><p>From <i>wāg</i> (“wall”) +&lrm; <i>þȳrel</i> (“hole”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wæl</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>weall</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wela</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Wēalas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wælcyrġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weald</i> (“forest”)</li><li>alternative form of <i>weald</i> (“power, authority”)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weald</i> (“powerful”)</li></ol><p>See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wealdend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, variant of Wealdhere, equivalent to English Walter</li></ol><p>Syncopic form of <i>Waldhere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, variant of Wealdhere, equivalent to English Walter</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Walþēof</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wealh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wealhhnutu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weall</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wælrēaf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mark caused by a blow or hit</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;walu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waluz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Waltheof of Bamburgh; 10th century Anglo-Saxon high-reeve/ealdorman of Bamburgh.</li><li>Waltheof, Earl of Northumbria; last Anglo-Saxon earl of Northumbria.</li></ol><p>From Old Norse <i>Valþjófr</i>, equivalent to Old English <i>weald</i> (“powerful, mighty”) or <i>wæl</i> (“slaughter, carnage”) + <i>þēof</i> (“thief”), Cognate with Norwegian Nynorsk <i>Valtjov</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weallþrǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>belly, stomach</li><li>womb</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wambō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stomach ache</li></ol><p>From <i>wamb</i> (“stomach”) +&lrm; <i>ādl</i> (“sickness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>womm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>lacking, without; un-</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wana-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wanaz</i> (“lacking”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁weh₂-</i> (“to be lacking, be empty”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a want, lack, absence</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>want of necessaries, lack, want, defect</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wanō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wanô</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wanaz</i> (“lacking, deficient, absent”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>tree</i>) spindle tree</li></ol><p>From <i>wanan</i> +&lrm; <i>bēam</i> (“beam, tree”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unstable, unsteady, uncertain, fickle, fluctuating, tottering, vacillating, weak</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wankul</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mole (animal)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wand</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wanduz</i> (“mole”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wendʰ-</i> (“to turn, twist, wind, braid”).</p><p>From <i>windan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Wendle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Wendle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mole (mammal of the Talpidae family)</li></ol><p>From <i>wand</i> (“mole”) +&lrm; <i>weorpe</i> (“thrower”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wandeweorpe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hesitate (with <i>æt</i> = about something)</li><li>to spare (+ dative)</li><li>to care, have regard (with <i>for</i> for something)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wandōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wandōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wander</li><li>to travel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wandarōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turning aside from a task</li><li>a feeling of respect</li></ol><p><i>wandian</i> (“to care, have regard for”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a flat surface: plain, field, ground</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) the side of the face, cheek</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wang</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wangaz</i>. Doublet of <i>wange</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wang</i>, Old High German <i>-wang</i> (in <i>holzwang</i>), Old Norse <i>vangr</i> (Swedish <i>vång</i>), Gothic <i>𐍅𐌰𐌲𐌲𐍃</i> (waggs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a whisker</li></ol><p>From <i>wang</i> +&lrm; <i>beard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheek, jaw</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wangā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wangô</i> (“cheek”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wenǵ-</i> (“neck, cheek”). More at English <i>wang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pillow, cushion, bolster</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wangārī</i>, equivalent to <i>wange</i> (“cheek”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>wangāri</i> (“pillow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place in an open plain</li><li>a town on a plain</li></ol><p>From <i>wang</i> +&lrm; <i>stede</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) molar (<i>tooth</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>wange</i> +&lrm; <i>tōþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grassland</li></ol><p>From <i>wang</i> (“plain”) +&lrm; <i>turf</i> (“turf, grassy land”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a poor man</li></ol><p>From <i>wan</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;hafa</i> (“holder, owner”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>needy, destitute</li></ol><p>From <i>wan</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;hafol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foolish, imprudent, thoughtless, careless, reckless</li></ol><p>From <i>wan-</i> (“un-”) +&lrm; <i>hȳdiġ</i> (“minded”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;intransitive) to shrink or lessen</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to impair; infringe, annul</li><li>to complain, bewail, lament, bemoan</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wanōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wanōną</i>, <i>&#42;wanāną</i> (“to lessen”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wainōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wainōną</i> (“to cry, weep”). Cognate with Old High German <i>weinōn</i> (“to weep”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wānian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark</li><li>blue-black</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wann</i> (“dark”), of uncertain origin, possibly related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wanōną</i> (“to lessen”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wann</i>, <i>wonn</i> (“dark”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) misery, misfortune</li></ol><p>From <i>wan-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċeaft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to diminish</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wansōn</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;wanisōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wanisōną</i> (“to lessen”). Equivalent to <i>wana</i> +&lrm; <i>-sian</i>. Alternatively, perhaps borrowed from cognate Old Norse <i>vansa</i> (“to suffer loss”) or derived from Old Norse <i>vansi</i> (“lack, want”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unhappy</li></ol><p>From <i>wan-</i> +&lrm; <i>sǣliġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Wantsum Channel</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lessening, diminution</li><li>abatement</li><li>a weakening, impairing, injury, wound</li><li>wailing, lamentation</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wanian</i> (“to wane”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p><p>Equivalent to <i>wānian</i> (“to wail”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seaweed</li><li>sand</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wair</i>, related to <i>&#42;wīraz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>waraþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>waroþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wearg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to beware</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make ware, make aware</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to warn</li><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to guard oneself (against), be on one's guard</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to take heed, be careful, take precaution</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to guard, keep, hold</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to ward off, repel</li><li>to remain</li><li>to continue</li><li>to make an agreement with</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;warōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;warōną</i>, whence also Old High German <i>warōn</i>, Old Norse <i>vara</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;waʀōn</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;warjan</i> (“to ward off, defend, protect”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wearnian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shore</li><li>seaweed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waruþaz</i>&nbsp;m or <i>&#42;waruþą</i>&nbsp;n.</p><p>Cognate with Old High German <i>werid</i> (German <i>Werd</i>, <i>Werder</i>, <i>Wört</i>), Dutch <i>waard</i>.</p><p>By surface analysis, <i>wār</i> (“seaweed”) +&lrm; <i>-oþ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a shore wave; breaker</li><li>surf</li></ol><p>From <i>waroþ</i> +&lrm; <i>faroþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wearp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an article of merchandise, ware</li><li>care, watch, observation</li><li>guard, protection, defence</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;waru</i>, <i>&#42;waʀu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;warō</i>, <i>&#42;wazō</i> (“ware”), of uncertain origin.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;waru</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;warō</i> (“heed, attention, care”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wor-</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wara</i>, Old High German <i>wara</i>. Compare also Gothic <i>𐍅𐌰𐍂𐌴𐌹</i> (warei).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;waru</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;warō</i> (“protection”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wer-</i> (“to defend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>waroþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wash</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;waskan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waskaną</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wed-</i> (“wet, water”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soft mud; mire</li><li>marsh</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;waisā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waisǭ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weys-</i> (“to flow”). Akin to Old Saxon <i>wāso</i> (“mud, wet ground, mire”), Old Norse <i>veisa</i> (“stagnant pond, stagnant water”), Old English <i>wōs</i> (“moisture; juice, sap”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the throat or gullet</li></ol><p>Apparently representing a Proto-West Germanic present participle. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wāsande</i>, Old Saxon <i>wāsendi</i>, Old High German <i>weisant</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cuttlefish</li></ol><p>From <i>wāse</i> (“mud”) +&lrm; <i>sċyte</i> (“shooting”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wattle (construction of branches and twigs woven together to form a wall, barrier, fence, or roof)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>watel</i> (“wattle”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wēa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow, be moved by the wind</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wāan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wēaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wḗh₁ti</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂weh₁-</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wāian</i> and Gothic <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐌰𐌽</i> (waian), and more distantly with Old Church Slavonic <i>вѣꙗти</i> (vějati), Ancient Greek <i>ἄημι</i> (áēmi) and Sanskrit <i>वाति</i> (vāti).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wascan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wandering</li><li>hunting, chase</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;waiþu</i> (“hunt, pasture, food”). Cognate with German <i>Weide</i> (“pasture”) and Icelandic <i>veiði</i> (“hunting”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wave, billow</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moving water, wave, flood</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea</li></ol><p>From the genitive plural of waþem (“wave”) and ġebind (“binding, bond”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misfortune, evil, harm, trouble</li><li>woe, grief, misery</li><li>sin, wickedness</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waiwô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wealh</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>weall</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Wēalas</i></li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Wealas)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Welsh</li><li>Wales (a medieval principality and region of the Kingdom of England)</li></ol><p>From plural of <i>wealh</i> (“Celt, Welsh person”). Compare the second element in Cornwall, and also Gaul.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move around</li><li>to revolve</li><li>to roll, roll around, be rolling</li><li>to toss</li><li>to fluctuate</li><li>to revolve in one's mind; scheme; reflect</li><li>to discuss</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;walkan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;walkaną</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Akin to Old High German <i>walchan</i>, Old Norse <i>valka</i>, Old English <i>ġewealcan</i> (“to go, go about, walk”), <i>wealcian</i> (“to roll up, curl, twist”), <i>weallian</i> (“to roam about, ramble”), German <i>wallen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fuller, waulker, walker</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to roll up, curl, twist</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wrinkle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;walkōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;walkōną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wel-</i> (“to bend, turn, roll, twist”). Akin to Old High German <i>walchan</i> (“to beat, to full”) (German <i>walken</i> (“to full”)), Middle Dutch <i>walken</i> (“to press, knead, full”), Danish <i>valke</i> (“to full”), Icelandic <i>vælka</i> (“to stamp, roll”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mobile</li><li>having a tendency to roll or move</li><li>loose, not firmly fixed</li><li>rolling (in the waves)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;walkulaz</i> (“moveable”), equivalent to <i>wealcan</i> (“to move around, roll”) +&lrm; <i>-ol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forest</li><li>power</li><li>authority</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>powerful, mighty</li><li>reigning, ruling, having authority</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;walþu</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>See also Old High German <i>wald</i> (German <i>Wald</i>) and Old Norse <i>vǫllr</i> (Faroese <i>vøllur</i>, Norwegian <i>voll</i>, Icelandic <i>völlur</i>). See also the related Anglian-derived term <i>wald</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wald</i>, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waldą</i>, whence also Old High German <i>walt</i>, Old Norse <i>vald</i> (Danish <i>vold</i>).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wald</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waldaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruler, God</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Wealda)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>wealda</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to control <i>(+ genitive, dative, or instrumental)</i></li><li>to wield (e.g. a sword) <i>(+ genitive, dative, or instrumental)</i></li><li>to rule <i>(+ genitive, dative, or accusative)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;waldan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>waldan</i>, Old High German <i>waltan</i> (German <i>walten</i>), Old Norse <i>valda</i> (Swedish <i>vålla</i>), Gothic <i>𐍅𐌰𐌻𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (waldan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>controller, master</li><li>ruler, governor, sovereign</li><li>possessor, lord</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waldandz</i>; equivalent to the present participle of wealdan (“to rule”). Cognate with Gothic <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌻𐌳𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (&#42;waldands), Old Saxon <i>waldand</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>powerful</li></ol><p>From, and identical to, the present participle of <i>wealdan</i> (“to control”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Waldfriþu</i>, from <i>&#42;wald</i> (“might, authority”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;friþu</i> (“peace”). Equivalent to <i>weald</i> +&lrm; <i>friþ</i> and cognate with Old High German <i>Waltfrid</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>weald</i> (“power, authority”) and <i>helm</i> (“protection, defence”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Waldahari</i>. Equivalent to <i>weald</i> (“power, authority”) +&lrm; <i>here</i> (“army”). Cognate with Old High German <i>Waltheri</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rein</li></ol><p>From <i>weald</i> (“control”) +&lrm; <i>leþer</i> (“leather (strap)”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>weald</i> (“power, authority”) and <i>rǣd</i> (“counsel, advice”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Meaning unclear, potentially "nauseous"</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Celt</li><li>Welsh person</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) Roman</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) foreigner</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) slave</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;walh</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;walhaz</i>, from a Celtic name also represented by Latin Volcae.</p><p>Having originally apparently referred to a neighboring Celtic tribe, it was broadened to refer to any inhabitant of the Western Roman Empire and then, in Britain, narrowed to refer to native Brythons, and later to <i>Welsh</i> people in particular. Owing to the presence of native Brythonic slaves in some areas, it also came to be used to refer to slaves (compare semantic formation of <i>Slav</i>), though only alongside &ndash; never supplanting &ndash; its ethnic meaning. The narrowing of meaning away from the continental Germanic meaning of <i>Roman</i> towards referring to Insular Celtic peoples was finalized by the late seventh century; rare occurrences of this term referring to Romans, such as the term <i>Rumwalas</i> found in <i>Widsith</i>, are explained as archaisms inherited from an older tradition.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Wealh)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>wealh</i></li></ol><p>See <i>wealh</i> (“servant, slave”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vermilion</li></ol><p>From <i>wealh</i> (“foreign”) +&lrm; <i>basu</i> (“purple, red”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a "Welsh expedition," apparently referring to troops defending the Welsh Marches.</li></ol><p><i>wealh</i> (“foreigner, Welsh”) +&lrm; <i>færeld</i> (“going, journey”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gyrfalcon</li><li>a foreign hawk</li></ol><p>From <i>wealh</i> (“foreigner”) +&lrm; <i>hafoc</i> (“hawk”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wealhhafoc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Walhahari</i>, from <i>&#42;walh</i> (“foreigner, Celt, Roman”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hari</i> (“army”). Equivalent to <i>wealh</i> +&lrm; <i>here</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Walahari</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>walnut</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;walhahnuts</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>walnoot</i>, German <i>Walnuss</i>, Swedish <i>valnöt</i>. Equivalent to <i>wealh</i> (“foreigner”) +&lrm; <i>hnutu</i> (“nut”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foreign country</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Normandy</li></ol><p>From <i>wealh</i> (“foreign”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“country”). Compare Old High German <i>Walho lant</i> (“Gallia”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrot</li><li>parsnip</li></ol><p>From <i>wealh</i> (“foreign”) +&lrm; <i>more</i> (“carrot, parsnip”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>translator, interpreter</li></ol><p>From <i>wealh</i> (“Roman/Celt/Welsh person”) plus an unknown element <i>&#42;stod</i>. See Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;walhaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) wallwort, dwarf elder</li></ol><p>From <i>wealh</i> (“foreigner”) +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i> (“plant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Wealhþēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Welsh people</li></ol><p>From <i>wealh</i> (“Welshman, foreigner”) +&lrm; <i>þēod</i> (“nation”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Danish queen in the epic poem Beowulf</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;wala</i> (“chosen”) +&lrm; <i>þēow</i> (“servant”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wall</li><li>fervour; uproar</li><li>boiled or mulled wine</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wall</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wallu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wallō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to boil; well, bubble; teem, abound with</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wallan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wallaną</i>, whence also Gothic <i>𐍅𐌿𐌻𐌰𐌽</i> (wulan, “well up, boil over”), Old High German <i>wallan</i> (German <i>wallen</i>), Old Norse <i>vella</i> (“to wall over, boil”) (Danish <i>vælde</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wander, roam</li><li>to go on pilgrimage</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a stone for building</li></ol><p>From <i>weall</i> +&lrm; <i>stān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) wallstead, place with buildings</li></ol><p>From <i>weall</i> (“wall”) +&lrm; <i>steall</i> (“place, stead”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wall-builder</li><li>mason, builder</li></ol><p>From <i>weall</i> (“wall”) +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“worker”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plumbline</li></ol><p>From <i>weall</i> +&lrm; <i>þrǣd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>weallsteall</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll</li><li>to wallow</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fade, wither</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;walwōn</i>, variant of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;walwijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;walwijaną</i>.</p><p>Unknown. Perhaps an alteration of Old English <i>fealwian</i>, <i>fealuwian</i>, <i>fealewian</i> (“to turn yellow, ripen, wither”) (compare modern English <i>fallow</i>). According to Kluge, related to Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wolkn</i> (“cloud”), source of German <i>Wolke</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guard</li><li>guard, the action of looking out for danger</li><li>alternative form of <i>wād</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ward</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wardaz</i>, related to a stem-verb <i>&#42;warōną</i> (“be on guard”) (whence <i>warian</i>).</p><p>Cognate with Old High German <i>warta</i> (“watch”) (German <i>Warte</i> (“watchtower”)), Old Saxon <i>warda</i> (Middle Dutch <i>warde</i>), Old Norse <i>varða</i> (“cairn”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wardu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wardō</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;waiʀd</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waizdaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who holds a country, an inhabitant</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>weardian</i> (“to guard”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guard, protect</li><li>to occupy, keep, hold, possess</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wardēn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wardijaną</i>, <i>&#42;wardāną</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wardon</i>, Old High German <i>wartēn</i> (German <i>warten</i> (“look after”)), Old Norse <i>varða</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guard, watchman</li></ol><p>From <i>weard</i> (“guard”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watchtower</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>weard</i> +&lrm; <i>steall</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>criminal, reprobate, felon</li><li>monster, evil spirit</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;warg</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>warag</i>, Old High German <i>warc</i>, Old Norse <i>vargr</i> (“outlaw, wolf”) (Swedish <i>varg</i> (“wolf”)). See also English <i>warg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weargcweþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to curse</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wearg</i> +&lrm; <i>cweþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gallows, gibbet, cross</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wargatrewą</i> (“gallows; (literally) outlaw-tree”). Equivalent to <i>wearg</i> +&lrm; <i>trēow</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>waragtreo</i> and Old Norse <i>vargtré</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wearg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>warm</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;warm</i>.</p><p>See also Old Frisian <i>warm</i> (West Frisian <i>waarm</i>), Old Saxon <i>warm</i> (Low German <i>warm</i>), Dutch <i>warm</i>, Old High German <i>warm</i> (German <i>warm</i>), Old Norse <i>varmr</i> (Danish <i>varm</i>, Swedish <i>varm</i>); also possibly Ancient Greek <i>θερμός</i> (thermós), Latin <i>formus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to get warm</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;warmōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warmness, warmth</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;warmnassī</i> (“warmness”), equivalent to <i>wearm</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wearmnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reluctance, resistance</li><li>refusal</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;warnu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;warnō</i> (“warding off; refusal; warning”), from the Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;wer-</i> (“to cover, guard, protect, heed, notice”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be aware, take care</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to warn, caution</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;warnōn</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>warnōn</i> (German <i>warnen</i>). Compare <i>wiernan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>waroþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warp</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;warpą</i>, whence also Old High German <i>warf</i> and Old Norse <i>varp</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a piece of hard skin; callus, callosity</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;warʀ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;warzaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>callous</li></ol><p>From <i>wearr</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>calloused, (of trees) knotted</li></ol><p>From <i>wearr</i> +&lrm; <i>-iht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of skin</i>) hardness, roughness</li><li>knottiness</li></ol><p>From <i>wearriht</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wearrihtnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wart</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wartā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wartǭ</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>warte</i>, Old Saxon <i>warta</i> (Middle Low German <i>wratte</i>), Old High German <i>warza</i> (German <i>Warze</i>), Old Norse <i>varta</i> (Icelandic <i>varta</i>, Swedish <i>vårta</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by chance, by accident, accidentally</li><li>fortuitously, fatefully</li></ol><p>Possibly an a-stem genitive adverb from Old English <i>wēa</i>, specifically concerning misfortune, or from a related word. If so, equivalent to <i>wēa</i> +&lrm; <i>-es</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>chance</li></ol><p>From <i>wēas</i> (“by chance, accidentally”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>what happens by chance; an accident</li></ol><p>From <i>wēas</i> (“by chance”) +&lrm; <i>ġelimp</i> (“event, accident”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wēaspell</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tale of woe</li><li>tragic news</li></ol><p><i>wēa</i> +&lrm; <i>spell</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Westseaxan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>token of woe</li><li>woeful signal</li></ol><p>From <i>wēa</i> +&lrm; <i>tācen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wax</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wahs</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wahsą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wahsan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wahsijaną</i>, whence also Old Frisian <i>waxa</i>, Old Saxon <i>wahsan</i>, Old High German <i>wahsan</i>, Old Norse <i>vaxa</i>, Gothic <i>𐍅𐌰𐌷𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wahsjan), all ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂weg-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wax candle</li></ol><p>From <i>weax</i> +&lrm; <i>candel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weaxcandel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wax-salve, cerate</li></ol><p>From <i>weax</i> +&lrm; <i>sealf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) sorrowful need</li></ol><p>From <i>wēa</i> (“evil, woe”) +&lrm; <i>þearf</i> (“need”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>web</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wabją</i> (whence also Old High German <i>weppi</i>, Old Norse <i>vefr</i>), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;webʰ-</i> (“weave”). The Indo-European root is also the source of <i>wasp</i>, due to the insect's woven nests.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weaver</li></ol><p>From <i>webbian</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(female) weaver</li></ol><p>From <i>webba</i> (“weaver”) +&lrm; <i>-estre</i> (feminine agent suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weave</li><li>to contrive</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fuller</li></ol><p>From <i>webb</i> (“web”) +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“worker”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wake, rouse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wakkjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wakjaną</i>. Doublet of <i>wæċċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wedge</li><li>a mass of metal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wagi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wagjaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>weggi</i>, Old Norse <i>veggr</i>, Lithuanian <i>vagis</i>, and perhaps Latin <i>vāgīna</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, agitate, drive hither and thither</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wagjaną</i>. Cognate with the noun <i>weċġ</i>, Lithuanian <i>vagis</i> (“wedge”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be or go insane, or to rage</li><li><i>(especially with dogs and other animals)</i> to have rabies</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wōdijaną</i>, derived from <i>&#42;wōdaz</i> (“insane or enraged,” whence Old English <i>wōd</i>) + <i>&#42;-ijaną</i> (causative suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pledge, contract</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wadi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wadją</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wedʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pledge, to promise</li><li>to betroth</li><li>to wed, to marry</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;waddjōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wadjōną</i>, whence also Old Frisian <i>weddia</i>, Frankish <i>&#42;wadjōn</i>, Old High German <i>wettōn</i>, Old Norse <i>veðja</i>. Equivalent to <i>wedd</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>betrothal, espousal</li></ol><p>From <i>weddian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>insanity, madness</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wōdīnaz</i> (“insanity”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weather</li><li>wind, storm, breeze, air</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wedr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wedrą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Geats</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Weather-Geats, a term for the tribe of Geats used in <i>Beowulf</i></li></ol><p>From <i>weder</i> (“weather”) +&lrm; <i>Ġēatas</i> (“Geats”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the land occupied by the Geats; Geatland</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>Wederas</i> (“Geats”) +&lrm; <i>mearc</i> (“boundary, limit”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sign of good weather</li></ol><p>From <i>weder</i> +&lrm; <i>tācen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wǣdla</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pledge</li><li>plighted troth</li><li>wedlock</li></ol><p>From <i>wedd</i> (“pledge, agreement”) +&lrm; <i>-lāc</i> (suffix denoting activity or process).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weave</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;weban</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Germanic cognates include Old Frisian <i>weva</i>, Old Saxon <i>weƀan</i>, Old Dutch <i>wevan</i>, Old High German <i>weban</i>, Old Norse <i>vefa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weft (horizontal threads that are interlaced through the warp in a woven fabric)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>way</li><li>road</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;weg</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wegaz</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Old Frisian <i>wei</i>, Old Saxon <i>weg</i>, Old Dutch <i>weg</i>, Old High German <i>weg</i>, Old Norse <i>vegr</i>, Gothic <i>𐍅𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (wigs). Compare West Frisian <i>wei</i>, Dutch <i>weg</i>, Icelandic <i>vegur</i>, Faroese <i>vegur</i>, Danish <i>vej</i>, Norwegian <i>vei</i>, and Swedish <i>väg</i>, which are all still the main word for both "way" and "road" in their respective languages.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wā lā wā</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry, bear</li><li>to wear</li><li>to carry on, inflict</li><li>to weigh, consider</li><li>to feel (emotions)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wegan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;weganą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weǵʰ-</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wega</i>, Old Saxon <i>wegan</i>, Dutch <i>wegen</i>, Old High German <i>wegan</i> (German <i>bewegen</i>), Old Norse <i>vega</i> (Swedish <i>väga</i>), Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (gawigan). The Proto-Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek <i>ϝοχος</i> (wokhos), <i>ὄχος</i> (ókhos, “vehicle”), Latin <i>vehō</i> (“carry”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weġbrǣde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plantain (<i>Plantago major</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wegabraidā</i>; equivalent to <i>weġ</i> (“way”) +&lrm; <i>brǣde</i> (“breadth”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wayfaring</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a wayfarer</li></ol><p>From <i>weġ</i> (“way”) +&lrm; <i>farende</i> (“travelling”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without a road, impassable</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) out of the way, erroneous, unreasonable</li></ol><p>From <i>weġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plunder taken on a highway</li><li>robbery done on a road</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>weġ</i> (“way, road”) +&lrm; <i>rēaf</i> (“plunder”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>well</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wela</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welh₁-</i>. Akin to Old Frisian <i>wela</i>, Old Saxon <i>wela</i>, Old High German <i>wola</i>, Old Norse <i>vel</i>, Gothic <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌰</i> (waila).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>well, good; abundantly; very, exceedingly; every (<i>added to nouns, verbs, adjectives and pronouns</i>)</li></ol><p>See <i>wel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wealth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;welō</i>, <i>&#42;walō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;welô</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wayland, a smith of Germanic legend</li></ol><p>Compare Old Norse <i>Vǫlundr</i> and the Latin phrase <i>Wielandia fabrica</i> (“Waylandian craftsmanship”) (<i>Waltharius</i>), which corresponds to the Anglo-Saxon phrase <i>Wēlandes weorc</i> in the poem <i>Waldere</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-born, noble</li></ol><p><i>wel-</i> +&lrm; <i>boren</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>good deed</li><li>act of kindness</li></ol><p>From <i>wel-</i> (“well”) +&lrm; <i>dǣd</i> (“deed”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weliġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) lip</li></ol><p>From a metathetic alteration of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;werulō</i>, a diminutive of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;weruz</i> (“lip”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wer-</i> (“high, raised area, tuber, lump, blister”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>everywhere</li><li>generally, commonly</li></ol><p><i>wel-</i> +&lrm; <i>hwær</i> (“where”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weliġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wielwan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) willow</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wealthy, rich</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wilig</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>weliġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wīelisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiell</i> (“spring, fountain, well”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wielm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weler</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiellspring</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiellspring</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiellspring</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wieltan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weoloc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Wēland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weler</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wielwan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>benevolent</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;welawilljandī</i>. Equivalent to <i>wel</i> (“well”) + <i>willende</i> (“wishing”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>benevolently</li></ol><p>From <i>welwillende</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>benevolence</li></ol><p>From <i>welwillende</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sound, be heard; announce</li><li>to persuade, convince, lead astray</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wōhmijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wōhmijaną</i> (“to sound, make a noise”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wekʷ-</i> (“to speak, sound”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>womm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spot, mar, spoil, disfigure</li><li>to defile, pollute, profane</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wammaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fornicator, adulterer</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wemman</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pollution, defilement</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wemman</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expectation</li><li>likelihood</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) hope</li><li>probability</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wēniz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wenh₁-</i> (“love”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wen</i>, Old Saxon <i>wan</i>, Old High German <i>wān</i> (German <i>Wahn</i> (“delusion”)), Old Norse <i>ván</i>, Gothic <i>𐍅𐌴𐌽𐍃</i> (wēns).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opinion</li><li>imagination</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>wēnan</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i> or <i>wēn</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>, with West Germanic parallels in given names (Latin <i>Audoënus</i>, Latin <i>Gondoënus</i>)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think (be of a certain opinion)</li><li>to expect <i>(+ genitive)</i></li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to hope</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wēnijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wēna</i>, Old Saxon <i>wānian</i>, Old High German <i>wānen</i> (German <i>wähnen</i>), Old Norse <i>væna</i>, Gothic <i>𐍅𐌴𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wēnjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>child</li><li>servant or slave</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wankil</i> (“one who totters, staggerer", hence "toddler”), related to Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wankijan</i> (“to sway, waver”). Cognate with Middle High German <i>wenkel</i> (“small movement”), Old Dutch <i>wankilhēt</i> (“unsteadiness, despondency”). Related also to Old English <i>wancol</i> (“unstable, wavering, fickle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;intransitive) to turn</li><li>(transitive,&#32;intransitive) to change</li><li>to translate</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go or depart</li><li>to happen</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wandijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wandijaną</i>, originally ‘to make something twist or wind’, a causative form of <i>&#42;windaną</i> (“to twist”) (whence <i>windan</i>).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wenda</i>, Old Saxon <i>wendian</i>, Old Dutch <i>wenden</i>, Old High German <i>wenten</i>, Old Norse <i>venda</i>, Gothic <i>𐍅𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wandjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Vendil (the northern part of Jutland, now called Vendsyssel)</li><li>(<i>uncertain, yet possibly</i>) a Vandal; (<i>in the plural</i>) the Vandals</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Wandil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wandilaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Wendlas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Mediterranean Sea</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Wandilasaiwi</i>, from <i>&#42;Wandil</i> (“Vandal”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;saiwi</i> (“sea”). Equivalent to <i>Wendlas</i> +&lrm; <i>sǣ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Wentilsēo</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>change</li><li>translation</li></ol><p><i>wendan</i> (“to change”) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“-ing”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wange</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to accustom</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wannjan</i> (“to accustom, be used to”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wennia</i>, Old Saxon <i>wennian</i> (Dutch <i>wennen</i>), Old High German <i>wennen</i> (German <i>gewöhnen</i>), Old Norse <i>venja</i> (Danish <i>vænne</i>, Swedish <i>vänja</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>possibly, perchance, maybe</li></ol><p>From <i>wēnan</i> (“to believe, expect, hope”) +&lrm; <i>-inga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Wendlas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wen (cyst on the skin)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wanjaz</i>, diminutive of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wan-</i> (“swelling, growth, lump, tumor”), of uncertain origin. According to Watkins, it is related to <i>&#42;wundō</i> (“wound”).</p><p>Cognate with Middle Low German <i>wene</i> (“wen”), Middle Dutch <i>wanne</i>, <i>wan</i> (“goiter”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wenian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Windrush (a river in Gloucestershire and Oxfordshire, England, a tributary of the Thames)</li><li>Windrush (a village and civil parish in Cotswold district, Gloucestershire)</li></ol><p>Recorded in the Domesday Book. From Old Welsh <i>gwynn</i> (“white”) + <i>reisko</i> (“fen”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Wintanċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wēninga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wick (of a candle)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;weukā</i> (“flax bundle, wick”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a weed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;weud</i>; cognate with Old Saxon <i>wiod</i>. Related to Old High German <i>wiota</i> (“fern”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>widuwe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hoe, weed hook</li></ol><p>From <i>wēod</i> (“weed”) +&lrm; <i>hōc</i> (“hook”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weed</li></ol><p><i>wēod</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the eighth month of the year according to the Anglo-Saxon calendar</li><li>August</li></ol><p>From <i>wēod</i> +&lrm; <i>mōnaþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>widuwe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>altar</li></ol><p>From pre-Old English <i>*wīohbēod</i>, equivalent to <i>wēoh</i> (“idol, image”) +&lrm; <i>bēod</i> (“offering stage, dish”). The replacement of /b/ with /f/ is motivated by the fact that medial /b/ historically developed to [v], and the word was no longer felt to be a compound.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Weogornaċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Worcester (a town in Worcestershire, England)</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;Weogor(e)na</i>, genitive form of a pre-English tribal name <i>&#42;Weogoran</i> + <i>ċeaster</i>. Thus, “fort of the Weogoran”.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Worcestershire</li></ol><p>From <i>Weogornaċeaster</i> +&lrm; <i>sċīr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idol, image</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wīh</i> (“sacred place or thing”). Compounds with <i>wīġ</i>- or <i>weoh</i> (short vowel) use the noun from <i>&#42;wīhą</i> (“idol”), while those with <i>&#42;wēoh</i>- (long vowel) derive from the adjective <i>&#42;wīhaz</i> (“holy”). More at witch, Weihnachten and victim.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>someone who makes idols; literally "idol smith"</li></ol><p><i>wēoh</i> +&lrm; <i>smiþ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wēohsteall</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Wīhastainaz</i>, equivalent to <i>wēoh</i> (“sanctuary, holy place”) +&lrm; <i>stān</i> (“stone”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>Vésteinn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>where the altar stands in a church, the apse</li><li>defensive position, bulwark</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wēoh</i> +&lrm; <i>steall</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>véstall</i> (“pagan altar”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) idolatry</li></ol><p><i>wēoh</i> +&lrm; <i>weorþung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wela</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weoloc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wolcn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weolocrēad</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weler</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weliġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wellington (various locations in England).</li></ol><p>Probably from an unattested name <i>&#42;Wēola</i> + <i>-ing</i> + <i>tūn</i> (“town”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whelk</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;weluk</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>purple</li></ol><p>From <i>weoloc</i> (“welk”) +&lrm; <i>basu</i> (“scarlet”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the reddish color derived from whelk snails; scarlet or purple</li></ol><p>From <i>weoloc</i> (“whelk”) +&lrm; <i>rēad</i> (“red”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weler</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weoloc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weler</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>Wineda land</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>work, in various senses, including:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>labor</li><li>a creation, such as a building or a work of art</li><li>act, deed</li></ol><li>pain, travail, grief</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;werk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;werką</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wérǵom</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>werk</i>, Old Saxon <i>werk</i>, Old High German <i>werk</i>, Old Norse <i>verk</i>. Non-Germanic cognates include Ancient Greek <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mechanics</li></ol><p><i>weorc</i> +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>workday (day on which work is done; in Anglo-Saxon England, typically any day but Sunday)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;werkadagaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>werctag</i> and Old Norse <i>verkdagr</i>. Equivalent to <i>weorc</i> +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>workshop</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;werkahūsą</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>werchūs</i> and Old Norse <i>verkhús</i>. Equivalent to <i>weorc</i> +&lrm; <i>hūs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>workman, labourer</li></ol><p>From <i>weorc</i> +&lrm; <i>mann</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>work, labor</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>weorc</i> +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a stone used for building</li></ol><p>From <i>weorc</i> (“work”) +&lrm; <i>stān</i> (“stone”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>painful, grievous, noxious</li></ol><p>From <i>weorc</i> (“labour, travail, pain, grief”) +&lrm; <i>-sum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a working slave</li><li>bondsman/bondswoman</li></ol><p>From <i>weorc</i> (“work”) +&lrm; <i>þēow</i> (“slave”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wyrd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beast</li><li>cattle</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorold</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wyrm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wyrmcynn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiernan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>werod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>world</li><li>existence, state of existence, worldly affairs (often of mundane or worldly things, as opposed to spiritual)</li><li>temporal things or possessions</li><li>men and things upon earth</li><li>an age (period of time)</li><li>a person's lifetime</li><li>the course of human affairs</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;weraldi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;weraldiz</i>, a compound of <i>&#42;weraz</i> (“man”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;aldiz</i> (“age”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concern for worldly things</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>weorold</i> (“world”) +&lrm; <i>ċearu</i> (“care, concern”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>woruldlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>secularly (as opposed to spiritually)</li><li>temporally (as opposed to eternally)</li></ol><p><i>weoroldlīċ</i> (“worldly”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) world-kingdom, earthly kingdom</li></ol><p>From <i>weorold</i> (“world”) +&lrm; <i>rīċe</i> (“kingdom”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the way of the world</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>weorold</i> +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worldly wealth</li><li>worldly success</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>weorold</i> +&lrm; <i>spēd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw</li><li>to cast (<i>into a form or shape</i>)</li><li>to cast or drive out, eject</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;werpan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;werpaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;werb-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorold</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>weorðaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorþsċiepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worth, deserving</li><li>honoured, esteemed</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worth, price, value</li><li>honour, dignity</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;werþaz</i> (“towards, opposite”), used substantively as a noun. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>werth</i>, Old Saxon <i>werth</i>, Old High German <i>werd</i>, German <i>wert</i> (adjective), <i>Wert</i> (noun), Dutch <i>waard</i> (adjective), Old Norse <i>verðr</i> (adjective), <i>verð</i> (noun) (Swedish <i>värd</i>), Gothic <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐍃</i> (wairþs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become</li><li>(<i>auxiliary</i>) to be (used to form the passive voice)</li><li>to happen, come to pass</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;werþan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;werþaną</i> (“to become”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wért-e-ti</i> (“to be turning”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>weorþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>weorþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>weorþeþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to honour</li><li>to worship</li><li>to adorn, endow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;werþōną</i>. Equivalent to <i>weorþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worthless, of no worth or value</li></ol><p>From <i>weorþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of worth or value, valuable</li><li>worthy, noble, distinguished, excellent, splendid</li><li>worthy, meet, fit, becoming</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;werþalīkaz</i> (“worthy”), equivalent to <i>weorþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>werdlīh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>weorþliċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worldly honour; glory</li></ol><p>From <i>weorþ</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;mynd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorþmynd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worthiness, honorable character</li><li>dignity, nobility, honor</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>weorþ</i> (“worth, deserving”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>weorþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>weorþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dignity; worth, honor</li><li>worship</li><li>glory, magnificence</li><li>an honor, ornament, decoration</li></ol><p>From <i>weorþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċiepe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorþsċiepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorþsċiepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an evaluation, an appreciation</li><li>honouring, reverence, worship</li></ol><p>From <i>weorþian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>. Cognate with German <i>Wertung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>weorþe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wesan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wesend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wisnian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weasel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wisulā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wisulǭ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wita</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>witan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>witod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>witod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wituma</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>witod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wituma</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clean, wipe</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wiskijaną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>wisken</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Wēohstān</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wituma</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;weþmō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weep</li><li>to complain, bewail</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wōpijan</i>, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wōpijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wēpa</i>, Old Saxon <i>wōpian</i>, Old High German <i>wuofan</i>, Old Norse <i>ǿpa</i>,</p><p>Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vъpiti</i> (“to weep”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man</li><li>male</li><li>husband</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) hero, warrior</li><li>(<i>in compounds</i>) civil</li><li>weir</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wer</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;weraz</i>.</p><p>Germanic cognates include Old Frisian, Old Saxon, Old High German <i>wer</i>, Old Norse <i>verr</i>, Gothic <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂</i> (wair). The Indo-European root is also the source of Latin <i>vir</i>, Sanskrit <i>वीर</i> (vīrà), Old Irish <i>fer</i>, Lithuanian <i>výras</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wari</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;warją</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wer</i>, <i>werr</i>, Old High German <i>wer</i>, Old Norse <i>ver</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Wærburg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mankind (men as a group, as opposed to womankind)</li></ol><p><i>wer</i> +&lrm; <i>cynn</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wyrd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wierdan</i> (“to injure”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>werod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>werġield</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>werewolf</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;werawulf</i>. By surface analysis, <i>wer</i> (“man”) +&lrm; <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>waroþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wearg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>werian</i> (“to defend”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>werġield</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wergeld: a kind of blood money paid by a criminal to a victim, or to the victim's family if they were dead, set according to social rank</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;werageld</i>. Equivalent to <i>wer</i> (“man”) +&lrm; <i>ġield</i> (“payment”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>werġield</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wierġnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>werġield</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the male gender</li></ol><p><i>wer</i> +&lrm; <i>hād</i> or <i>-hād</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wareham (a market town in Wareham Town parish, Dorset, England)</li></ol><p>From <i>wer</i> (“weir”) +&lrm; <i>hām</i> (“home”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wyrhta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to use, put on, wear (clothes, ornaments, etc.)</li><li>to hinder, restrain; to dam up</li><li>to defend, protect</li><li>to remain, continue, dwell</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;waʀjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wazjaną</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>werian</i>, Old High German <i>werien</i>, Old Norse <i>verja</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;warjan</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>werian</i>, Middle Dutch <i>weren</i> (Dutch <i>weren</i>), Old High German <i>werien</i> (German <i>wehren</i>), Old Norse <i>verja</i> (Swedish <i>värja</i>).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;weʀēn</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;weʀēn</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wes-</i> (“to remain, dwell”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>weary, tired, exhausted, fatigued</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wōrīgaz</i>, <i>&#42;wōrijaz</i>, possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂weH-</i> (“spend night, sleep”). Compare Old Norse <i>ǿrr</i>, Old Saxon <i>wōrig</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weariness, tiredness</li></ol><p>From <i>wēriġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without a husband</li></ol><p>Equivalent of <i>wer</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>male</li><li>manly, masculine</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) husband; marital</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) part of the masculine grammatical gender</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;weralīkaz</i>. Related to Gothic <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍉</i> (wairaleikō). Equivalent to <i>wer</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the manner of a man; in a manly way</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>euphamism</i>) penis</li></ol><p>From <i>werlīċe</i> (“manly, male”) + <i>līm</i> (“limb”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wierman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a man's measure; stature of a man</li></ol><p>From <i>wer</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;met</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wierming</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) wormwood, absinthium</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;warjamōdā</i> (“wormwood”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>troop, company, army</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;werud</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lord of Hosts (title for God)</li></ol><p>From <i>weroda</i> (“of armies”) + <i>drihten</i> (“lord”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>waroþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Old English</i>) war (large-scale military conflict)</li></ol><p>From Anglo-Norman and Old Northern French <i>guerre</i>&nbsp;/ <i>werre</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>werra</i>, borrowed from Frankish <i>&#42;werru</i> (“confusion; quarrel”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wers-</i> (“to mix up, confuse, beat, thresh”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wyrt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>werod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a people, nation</li><li>humanity</li></ol><p>From <i>wer</i> (“man”) +&lrm; <i>þēod</i> (“people”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorþnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wæs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be, exist</li><li>to consume or feast</li><li>to soak; to macerate; to dye</li><li>to ooze</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wesan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wesaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wes-</i>.</p><p>The simple present forms originate from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁es-</i> (“to be”), which had no infinitive or past tense in Proto-Germanic, but had already formed a single paradigm with <i>&#42;wesaną</i> supplying the infinitive and past tense.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wesan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wesaną</i> (“to consume, feast”), derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wes-</i> (“to graze”). The only (possible) attestation is in the form <i>weaxan</i> in line 3115 of <i>Beowulf</i>, argued to be a misspelling of <i>weosan</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wōsijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wōsijaną</i>, from <i>&#42;wōsą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moist</li><li>macerated</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wōsijaz</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wōsaz</i> (“moist”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wes-</i> (“moisture; wet”). Akin to Old English <i>wēsan</i> (“to soak”), Old English <i>wōs</i> (“moisture, juice, sap”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aurochs, bison, buffalo, wild ox</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wisundz</i> (“wild ox, aurochs”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weosule</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wæstling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Westseaxan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Westseaxna</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Westseaxna</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>west</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;westr</i>, see also Old High German <i>west</i>, Old Norse <i>vestr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Westseaxe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ravage, spoil</li><li>to lay waste, devastate; desolate</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from the west</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wōstijan</i> (“to lay waste”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;westanā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;westanē</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from the west, in the west</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from or to the northwest</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>westan</i> (“from the west”) +&lrm; <i>norþan</i> (“from the north”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Apparently refers to the region or bishopdom west of Sherbourne</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>west</i> (“west”) +&lrm; <i>wudu</i> (“wood”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) 'West-Danes', the Danes</li></ol><p><i>west</i> (“west”) +&lrm; <i>Dene</i> (“Danes”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the west, a western part or region</li></ol><p>From <i>west</i> (“west”) +&lrm; <i>dǣl</i> (“part”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>desolate, waste, barren, deserted; uninhabited, empty</li><li>void</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wōstī</i> (“waste, desolate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wæstmlēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wasteland, desert, wilderness</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>desolate, waste</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wōstini</i>. Related to Old English <i>wēste</i> (“void, desolate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the western end or extremity of something</li></ol><p><i>west</i> (“west”) +&lrm; <i>ende</i> (“end”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>desolation</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wōstīnassī</i>, equivalent to <i>wēste</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>. Cognate with Middle High German <i>wuostnüsse</i>.</p><p>Some forms may actually represent <i>wēst</i> (past participle of <i>wēstan</i>) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wasteland, deserted place</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>wēsten</i> (“wasteland”) +&lrm; <i>staþol</i> (“foundation”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>western</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;westrōnijaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the west side, west (of a place)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>west</i> (“west”) +&lrm; <i>healf</i> (“side, half”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>waste, wasty, desert</li><li>desolate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wōstīg</i>, equivalent to <i>wēste</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lying in a westerly direction</li></ol><p>From <i>west</i> (“west”) +&lrm; <i>lang</i> (“lang”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>westmost</li></ol><p>From <i>west</i> (“west”) +&lrm; <i>-mest</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Westmynster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the people of Westmorland</li></ol><p>From <i>west</i> (“west”) +&lrm; <i>mōr</i> (“moor”) +&lrm; <i>-ingas</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Westminster (a county, a Greater London in England).</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>west</i> (“west”) +&lrm; <i>mynster</i> (“monastery”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>due west</li></ol><p><i>west</i> (“west”) +&lrm; <i>rihte</i> (“straight, directly”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>westrihte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a West Saxon (someone from Wessex)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the West Saxons, Wessex</li></ol><p>From <i>west</i> (“west”) +&lrm; <i>Seaxa</i> (“Saxon”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the West Saxons</li><li>Wessex</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Westseaxan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wessex</li></ol><p>From <i>Westseaxna</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Westseaxan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Westseaxna</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the southwestern end or extremity (of a landmass)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>west</i> (“west”) +&lrm; <i>sūþ</i> (“south”) +&lrm; <i>ende</i> (“end”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Welsh of Cornwall</li><li>Cornwall</li></ol><p><i>west</i> (“west”) +&lrm; <i>Wēalas</i> (“Wales”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>westward, in the west</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>westward</li></ol><p><i>west</i> +&lrm; <i>-weard</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>west wind</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;westawind</i>, equivalent to <i>west</i> +&lrm; <i>wind</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wǣtan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wituma</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weax</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weaxan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weaxcandel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>weþer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mild, pleasant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wōþijaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wether</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;weþru</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;weþruz</i> (“a yearling lamb; wether”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wet-</i> (“year”). Cognate with Latin <i>vitulus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wǣdla</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorold</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worm, beetle</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wifer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>village</li><li>temporary abode: camp, encampment, lodging</li><li>bight, small bay</li><li>creek, inlet</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wīk</i>, a merger of Latin <i>vīcus</i> and Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wīhs</i>; both from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyḱ-</i> (“village”). Compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīhsą</i> (“settlement, village”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wīku</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīkō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyk-</i> (“to bend, curve”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to yield, give way</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wīkwan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīkwaną</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wīkan</i> and Old Norse <i>víkja</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(male) witch, wizard, sorcerer, magician, druid, necromancer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wikkō</i> (“necromancer, sorcerer”).</p><p>Further etymology uncertain; apparently from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyk-</i> (“separate, divide”), conjectured to be because of early Germanic divinatory practices to do with casting lots (cleromancy).</p><p>The exact etymology is problematic. R. Lühr (<i>Expressivität und Lautgesetz im Germanischen</i>, Heidelberg (1988), p. 354) connects <i>wigol</i> "prophetic, mantic", <i>wīglian</i> "to practice divination" (Middle Low German <i>wichelen</i> (“bewitch”) and suggests Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wigōn</i>, via Kluge's law becoming <i>&#42;wikkōn</i>. The basic form would then be the feminine, <i>wicce</i> /ˈwɪt͡ʃe/, from <i>&#42;wikkæ</i>, from <i>&#42;wikkōn</i> with palatalization due to the preceding <i>i</i> and the following <i>*æ</i>, from <i>*ōn</i>. The palatal <i>-cc-</i> /t͡ʃ/ in <i>wicca</i> would then be analogous to the feminine.</p><p>An alternative possibility is to derive the palatal /t͡ʃ/ directly from the verb <i>wiccian</i>, from <i>&#42;wikkija</i> (OED, s.v. <i>witch</i>). Lühr conversely favours derivation of this verb from the noun.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(female) witch</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wiċċa</i> (“witch”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i> (suffix denoting females), though <i>wiċċa</i> could have been the derived term instead.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>witchcraft</li></ol><p>From <i>wiċċe</i> +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>witchcraft, sorcery, magic</li></ol><p>From <i>wiċċe</i> +&lrm; <i>-dōm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to practice witchcraft</li></ol><p>From <i>wiċċe</i> (“witch”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix), or from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wikk(ij)ōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>witching, witchcraft</li></ol><p>From <i>wiċċian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>day of the week</li><li>weekday</li></ol><p><i>wiċe</i> (“week”) +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i> (“day”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>week</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wikā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wikǭ</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wike</i>, Old Saxon <i>wika</i>, Old High German <i>wëhha</i>, Old Norse <i>vika</i>, Gothic <i>𐍅𐌹𐌺𐍉</i> (wikō).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwelling-place</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wīc</i> +&lrm; <i>eard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) horse, steed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wigi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wigją</i>, from <i>&#42;weganą</i> (“to carry”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wigg</i>, Old Norse <i>vigg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insect</li><li>earwig</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;weganą</i> (“to move, wiggle”) +&lrm; <i>-ga</i> (diminutive suffix). Related to Old English <i>weġan</i> (“to move”), Middle English <i>wiglen</i> (“to wiggle”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Wicga)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiċġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the reeve of a <i>wīc</i> or settlement</li></ol><p>From <i>wīc</i> +&lrm; <i>ġerēfa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lodge, take up one's quarters</li><li>to stop in the course of an expedition or march</li><li>to occupy a position for a time</li><li>(<i>of a boat or ship</i>) to make land</li></ol><p>From <i>wīc</i> (“dwelling-place, lodge, quarters”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p><p>From <i>wīc</i> (“bight, inlet”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pirate</li><li>Viking</li></ol><p>It is unlikely that this word was borrowed or calqued from Old Norse <i>víkingr</i> (“pirate”) (itself from Old Norse <i>vík</i> (“inlet”) + <i>-ingr</i> (“one belonging to”, “one who frequents”)), since the Old English word predates the Old Norse word by approximately two centuries (the Oxford English Dictionary even suggests the possibility that the word entered North Germanic from the Anglo-Frisian area). In any case, equivalent to <i>wīc</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>. Compare Old Frisian <i>wītsing</i> (“pirate, viking”) and Old Saxon <i>wīking</i> (“pirate”), which are formed identically.</p><p>The North Sea Germanic form, existing in Old English since at least the eighth century, could also have been derived from or influenced by <i>wīċ</i> (“camp”), on account of the temporary encampments which were often a prominent feature of the Vikings’ raids.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wide, far</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīdaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wīd</i>, Old Saxon <i>wīdo</i> and Old Dutch <i>wīdo</i>, Old High German <i>wīt</i>, Old Norse <i>víðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>widely-known</li></ol><p><i>wīd</i> (wide) +&lrm; <i>cūþ</i> (known).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>widely, afar, far and wide</li></ol><p><i>wīd</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>far and wide</li></ol><p>From <i>wīde</i> +&lrm; <i>and</i> +&lrm; <i>sīde</i>. Compare Middle Low German <i>wîde unde sîde</i>, Middle Dutch <i>wîde ende sîde</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>widuwe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spacious, broad, wide, vast</li></ol><p>From <i>wīd</i> (“wide”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wīdherian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to celebrate, spread praise widely or publicly</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wīd</i> +&lrm; <i>herian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to widen</li></ol><p>From <i>wīd</i> (“wid”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wide land, the face of the Earth</li></ol><p>From <i>wīd</i> (“wide”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>far-famed, famous, celebrated</li><li>notorious</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wīd</i> +&lrm; <i>mǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread knowledge or fame of something or somebody</li><li>to proclaim, publish, celebrate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wīd</i> +&lrm; <i>mǣrsian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proclamation, publication</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wīdmǣrsian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>width</li></ol><p>From <i>wīd</i> (“wide”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>wīde</i></li><li>widely, afar, far and wide</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wīdsīþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a long journey</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a far-traveller</li></ol><p>Bahuvrīhi compound of <i>wīd</i> (wide) +&lrm; <i>sīþ</i> (journey).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Widsiþ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p><i>wīd</i> (“wide”) +&lrm; <i>sīþ</i> (“journey”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the open sea: the sea envisioned as expansive, referring especially to a part far away from land</li></ol><p><i>wīd</i> (“wide”) +&lrm; <i>sǣ</i> (“sea”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>witlēast</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wudu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>widower</li></ol><p>From <i>widuwe</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>widow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;widuwā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;widuwǭ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁widʰéwh₂</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>widwe</i>, Old Saxon <i>widowa</i>, Old Dutch <i>widowa</i>, Old High German <i>wituwa</i>, Gothic <i>𐍅𐌹𐌳𐌿𐍅𐍉</i> (widuwō).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Wiht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quail (<i>bird</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wahtilu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to control</li><li>to possess power or authority over</li><li>to subdue, conquer; to tame</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;waldijan</i> (“to control, make manageable”), a derivative of <i>&#42;waldaną</i> (“to rule”), whence Old English <i>wealdan</i> (“to govern, rule”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Celtic, <i>narrow sense</i>: Welsh</li><li>servile (South of England only)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;walhisk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;walhiskaz</i> (“foreign, non-Germanic”), corresponding to <i>wealh</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>waalsch</i> (“Walloon”), German <i>welsch</i>, Swedish <i>välsk</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spring; fountain</li><li>well</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wallijā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wallijǭ</i> (“fountain, spring, wave, well”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to boil</li><li>(transitive,&#32;figurative) to torment, agitate with violent feelings</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wallijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fountain, spring</li><li>well</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wallijā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wallijǭ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>springwater</li></ol><p>From <i>wielle</i> +&lrm; <i>wæter</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>wiellspring</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literal,&#32;figurative) a wellspring, fountain, source</li></ol><p>From <i>wiell</i> +&lrm; <i>spring</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which wells (up) or seethes; surge; fount; spring</li><li>a seething; boiling</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;walmiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to roll</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waltijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to roll</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to join, compound, compose</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;walwijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;walwijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hurt, injure</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wardijaną</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐍂𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;wardjan), Old Frisian <i>werda</i>, Old Dutch <i>werden</i>, Old Saxon <i>&#42;wardian</i>, Old High German <i>werten</i>. Compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;werþaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to curse</li><li>to do evil</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wargijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wargijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>she-wolf</li><li>evil-doer, sinner (<i>female</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wargini</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;warginī</i>. Equivalent to <i>wearg</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curse</li></ol><p><i>wierġan</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curse</li><li>condemnation</li><li>punishment</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wargiþu</i>. Equivalent to <i>wierġan</i> (“to curse”), <i>wearg</i> (“criminal”) +&lrm; <i>-þu</i> (abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to warm, make warm</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;warmijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;warmijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warming</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;warmīngu</i>, equivalent to <i>wierman</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hinderance, obstacle, difficulty</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to refuse, deny</li><li>to reject, decline</li><li>to withhold</li><li>to forbid, prevent</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;warnijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;warnijaną</i>, from <i>&#42;warnō</i> (“obstacle”); related to <i>werian</i> (“to protect”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>werna</i>, Old Norse <i>verna</i> (Danish <i>værne</i>). Compare <i>wearnian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>yfel</i>: worst</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>yfel</i>: worst</li></ol><p>The adjective is from the Verner's alternative of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wirsistaz</i>, the adverb from the alternative of <i>&#42;wirsist</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>worse; comparative degree of <i>yfele</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wirsiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>yfel</i>: worse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wirsiʀō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wirsizô</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wirra</i>, Old High German <i>wirsiro</i>, Old Norse <i>verri</i> (Swedish <i>värre</i>), Gothic <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍃𐌹𐌶𐌰</i> (wairsiza).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deserving <i>(+gen. of something)</i></li><li>entitled <i>(+gen. to something)</i>, qualified <i>(+gen. for something)</i></li></ol><p>From earlier <i>*weorþī</i>, derived from <i>weorþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wita</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>witodlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woman</li><li>female</li><li>wife</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wīb</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wībą</i>, of uncertain origin.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wīf</i>, Old Saxon <i>wīf</i>, Old Dutch <i>wīf</i>, Old High German <i>wīb</i>, Old Norse <i>víf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>womankind</li><li>the female gender</li></ol><p><i>wīf</i> +&lrm; <i>cynn</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beetle, weevil</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wibil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wibilaz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wivil</i>, Old High German <i>wibil</i>, Old Norse <i>vifill</i>, <i>vífill</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>missile, arrow, javelin, dart</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wibr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wibraz</i> (“missile”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyp-</i>, <i>&#42;weyb-</i> (“to oscillate; swing”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>vifr</i> (“sword”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hair (of a woman)</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>wif</i> (“woman”) +&lrm; <i>feax</i> (“hair of the head”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female friend</li></ol><p>From <i>wīf</i> (“woman”) +&lrm; <i>frēond</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sexual intercourse with a woman</li></ol><p>From <i>wīf</i> +&lrm; <i>ġemāna</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the female gender</li></ol><p><i>wīf</i> +&lrm; <i>hād</i> or <i>-hād</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the female side or line of a family</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wīf</i> +&lrm; <i>hand</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a household of women; nunnery, convent</li></ol><p>From <i>wīf</i> +&lrm; <i>hīred</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to marry (a woman)</li></ol><p>From <i>wīf</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intercourse with a woman</li></ol><p>From <i>wīf</i> (“woman”) +&lrm; <i>-lāc</i> (“play, sport”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without a wife; wifeless, unmarried</li></ol><p>From <i>wīf</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>female, feminine</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) part of the feminine grammatical gender</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wībalīkaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>wīf</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the manner of a woman</li></ol><p>From <i>wīflīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i> or <i>wīf</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wīfmann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woman</li><li>female</li><li>handmaid, female servant</li></ol><p>From <i>wīf</i> +&lrm; <i>mann</i>, literally “female person”. Compare <i>wǣpnedmann</i> (“man, a male”, literally “male person”), which <i>wīfmann</i> was often used alongside; cf. Icelandic <i>karlmaður</i> and <i>kvenmaður</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wīfmann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wīfmann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clothing for a woman; women's dress, female attire</li></ol><p>From <i>wīf</i> +&lrm; <i>sċrūd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an implement used in weaving</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(poetic or in compounds)</i> war, battle</li><li>idol</li><li>(<i>in compounds</i>) holy, consecrated</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wīg</i>.</p><p>Variant of <i>wēoh</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Wig)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) warrior, fighter</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) hero, man</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wigō</i>, equivalent to <i>wīgan</i> (“to fight”) +&lrm; <i>-a</i> (agentive noun suffix).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Wiga)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>wīġ</i> (“war, battle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight, make war, do battle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wīgan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wiganą</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>wīgan</i>, Old Norse <i>vega</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wīġ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>beald</i> (“bold”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wēofod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wīġ</i> (“war, battle”) and <i>beorn</i> (“man, warrior”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a belligerent</li></ol><p><i>wīġ</i> (“war”) +&lrm; <i>-bora</i> (“bearer”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) shield</li></ol><p>From <i>wīġ</i> (“war”) +&lrm; <i>bord</i> (“board, shield”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>warlike, martial, eager for war or battle</li></ol><p>From <i>wīġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wīcing</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiġlung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a warrior, soldier</li><li>a fighting man</li></ol><p>From <i>wīgan</i> (“to fight”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wīġ</i> (“war, battle”) + <i>friþ</i> (“peace, sanctuary”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wīġ</i> (“war, battle”) + <i>fūs</i> (“ready, eager”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiċġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wīġ</i> (“war, battle”) + <i>heard</i> (“brave”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wīġ</i> (“war, battle”) and <i>helm</i> (“helmet, protection”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tower</li><li>fortification</li></ol><p>From <i>wig</i> +&lrm; <i>hus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>wīgan</i> (“to fight”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Wig</li></ol><p>From <i>Wig</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Wiġlāf of Mercia</li><li>Wiglaf, a character in <i>Beowulf</i></li></ol><p>From <i>wiġ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>lāf</i> (“remainder, legacy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>divination</li></ol><p>From <i>wiġlian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diviner, augur, sorcerer, soothsayer</li></ol><p>From <i>wigle</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divine, foretell</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wigulōn</i>, <i>&#42;wihulōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wigulōną</i>, <i>&#42;wihulōną</i> (“to conjure, divine”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyk-</i> (“to separate, choose, cull, weed out, consecrate”). Related to Dutch <i>wichelen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>divination, sorcery, augury</li></ol><p>From <i>wiglian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man of war, fighting man, soldier</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wīgamann</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīgamann-</i>. Equivalent to <i>wīġ</i> +&lrm; <i>mann</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wīgman</i>, Old High German <i>wīgman</i> and Old Norse <i>vígmaðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wīġ</i> (“joy”) +&lrm; <i>mund</i> (“protection”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fighting, warfare</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>wīgan, wīgian</i>; <i>-noþ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wīġ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>rǣd</i> (“counsel”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warrior, hero</li><li>alternative form of <i>wēohsmiþ</i></li></ol><p>Kenning of <i>wīġ</i> (“war”) +&lrm; <i>smiþ</i> (“smith”).</p><p><i>wēoh</i> +&lrm; <i>smiþ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wīġ</i> (“war, battle”) + <i>stān</i> (“stone”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wēohsteall</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wēohweorþung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>war chariot</li></ol><p>From <i>wīġ</i> (“war”) +&lrm; <i>wæġn</i> (“wagon, carriage”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p><i>wīġ</i> (“battle”) +&lrm; <i>þeġn</i> (“servant”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Wīgþeġn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wēoh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Wēohstān</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thing</li><li>being, creature</li><li>weight</li></ol><p>From a fusion of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wihtiz</i> (feminine) and <i>&#42;wihtą</i> (neuter), both meaning "thing". These words became <i>&#42;wihti</i> and <i>&#42;wiht</i> in West Germanic and then merged in ante-historic Old English by regular sound change.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wiht</i> (Low German <i>Wicht</i>), Old Dutch <i>wiht</i> (Dutch <i>wicht</i>), Old High German <i>wiht</i> (German <i>Wicht</i>), Old Norse <i>vætr</i> (Swedish <i>vätte</i>), and Gothic <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (waihts) and <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍄</i> (waiht).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wihti</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Middle Low German <i>wicht</i>, Dutch <i>wicht</i>, <i>gewicht</i>, Old Norse <i>vætt</i> (compare also Danish <i>vægt</i>, Norwegian <i>vekt</i>); Old High German <i>giwihti</i> (German <i>Gewicht</i>).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Wiht)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Isle of Wight</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Vectis</i> (c.&nbsp;150), probably of Brythonic origin.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wiht</i> (“wight, spirit”) +&lrm; <i>burg</i> (“settlement”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wiht</i> (“thing, creature”) and <i>gār</i> (“spear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wiht</i> (“thing, creature”) and <i>lāc</i> (“gift, offering”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wight</li></ol><p><i>Wiht</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Wihtlæg, a legendary king of the Angles</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Wihtlæg</li></ol><p>From <i>Wihtlæg</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wiht</i> (“thing, creature”) and <i>mund</i> (“protector”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a resident or inhabitant of the Isle of Wight</li></ol><p>From <i>Wiht</i> +&lrm; <i>-sǣta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>Wihtsǣtan</i> (“people of Wight”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the people of the Isle of Wight</li></ol><p>From a variant of <i>Wihtware</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Wihtware</i> (“people of Wiht”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used in the plural</i>) the inhabitants or residents of a the Isle of Wight</li></ol><p>From <i>Wiht</i> +&lrm; <i>-ware</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wīf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wīcian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wile, trick or device</li><li>pleasure, will</li><li>something desirable</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīlą</i> (“craft, deceit”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wey-</i> (“to turn, bend”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>vél</i>.</p><p>Related to <i>willan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Willjōberht</i>, from <i>&#42;willjō</i> (“will, desire”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;berht</i> (“bright”). Equivalent to <i>willa</i> +&lrm; <i>beorht</i>. Cognate with English <i>Wilbert</i>, German <i>Wilbrecht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Willjōburg</i>, from <i>&#42;willjō</i> (“will, desire”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;burg</i> (“fortification”). Equivalent to <i>willa</i> +&lrm; <i>burg</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Wilpurc</i>, and further with Old Norse <i>Vilbjǫrg</i>, the second element instead from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bergō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>welcome guest</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wiljakumô</i>. Equivalent to <i>willa</i> +&lrm; <i>cuma</i>. Compare <i>wilspell</i> (“welcome news”), which is formed similarly.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>Unclear; perhaps well done, hooray, or in later years welcome. Glosses Latin euge.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wiljakumô</i>. Compare <i>wilcuma</i> (“welcome guest”), formed from the same root. Cognate with Old High German <i>willicumo</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild animal</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wilde</i> (“wild”) +&lrm; <i>dēor</i> (“animal”). Cognate with German <i>Wildtier</i>, Icelandic <i>villidýr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or related to a wild animal</li></ol><p>From <i>wilddēor</i> (“wild animal”) +&lrm; <i>-en</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>like a wild animal; brutish, brutal, bestial, animalistic</li></ol><p>From <i>wilddēor</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>brutishly</li><li>in the manner of a wild animal</li></ol><p><i>wilddēor</i> (“wild animal”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>, or <i>wilddēorlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wild, savage</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wilþijaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wilde</i>, Old Saxon <i>wildi</i>, Old High German <i>wildi</i>, Old Norse <i>villr</i>, Gothic <i>𐍅𐌹𐌻𐌸𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (wilþeis).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>onager</li></ol><p>From <i>wilde</i> (“wild”) +&lrm; <i>assa</i> (“male ass”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wildfire</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wilþī fuir</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>&#42;wilde fiur</i> (West Frisian <i>wyldfjoer</i>), Old High German <i>&#42;wildi fiur</i> (Middle High German <i>wilde fiur</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wilddēor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wilddēor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wild beast</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wilþiʀu</i>, the plural of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;welþ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;welþaz</i> (oblique <i>*wilþiz-</i>), from the same root as Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wilþijaz</i> (“wild”). Cognate with Old High German <i>wild</i> (dative plural <i>wildiran</i>), whence modern German <i>Wild</i> (“game”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Wilfriþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wil</i> (“will, desire”) and <i>friþ</i> (“peace”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) dear companion</li></ol><p><i>willa</i> (“welcome”) +&lrm; <i>ġesīþ</i> (“companion, comrade”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wil</i> (“will, desire”) + <i>ġīsl</i> (“hostage”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Willjōgunþi</i>, from <i>&#42;willjō</i> (“will, desire”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;gunþi</i> (“battle”). Equivalent to <i>willa</i> +&lrm; <i>gūþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Wilgund</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wielwan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basket</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wilgijā</i>, equivalent to <i>weliġ</i> (“willow”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wīelisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiell</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>will</li><li>desire</li><li>(<i>in compounds</i>) <i>denotes something welcome or desired</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;willjō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wiljô</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>willio</i>, Old Dutch <i>willo</i>, Old High German <i>willo</i>, Old Norse <i>vili</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to want, desire</li><li>to intend</li><li>to be willing</li><li>used to express habitual behavior</li><li>Used to express the future tense</li><li>to want or intend to go (to or from somewhere)</li><li>used in the subjunctive past to express hypotheticals, where Modern English would use "would"</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;willjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wiljaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Maybe from <i>willa</i> (“will, desire”) and <i>hād</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to will</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;willjan</i>.Equivalent to <i>willa</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, variant of Willibeald.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Willjōbalþ</i>, from <i>&#42;willjō</i> (“will, desire”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;balþ</i> (“bold”). Equivalent to <i>willa</i> +&lrm; <i>beald</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Willibald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wille</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wielm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>wil</i> (“will, desire”) + <i>mund</i> (“protector”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wil</i> (“will, desire”) and <i>mǣre</i> (“famous, great”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handmaid</li></ol><p>A contraction of <i>&#42;wīelen</i>; equivalent to <i>wealh</i> (“Welsh person, slave”) +&lrm; <i>-en</i> (feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to desire</li><li>to ask for</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wiljinōną</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>vilna</i>. Equivalent to <i>willa</i> +&lrm; <i>-nian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>desire</li></ol><p><i>wilnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weoloc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wil</i> (“will, desire”) and <i>rǣd</i> (“counsel, advice”). Cognate with Gothic <i>𐍅𐌹𐌻𐌾𐌰𐍂𐌹𐌸</i> (wiljariþ).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>welcome news</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;willjaspell</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wilspell</i>. Equivalent to <i>willa</i> +&lrm; <i>spell</i>. Compare <i>wilcuma</i>, which is formed similarly.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiellspring</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>wil</i> (“will”) +&lrm; <i>swiþ</i> (“powerful, strong”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the people of Wiltshire</li></ol><p>From <i>*wil</i>, an element of uncertain origin preserved in English <i>Wilton</i> + <i>-sǣte</i> (“residents”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wieltan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wilton</li></ol><p>From <i>willa</i> +&lrm; <i>tūn</i>, literally “welcome town.” Compare <i>wilcuma</i> and <i>wilspell</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Wilton; of or from Wilton</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a Wiltonian</li></ol><p><i>Wiltūn</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wiltshire</li></ol><p><i>Wiltūn</i> (“Wilton”) +&lrm; <i>sċīr</i> (“county”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally</i>) well worship (the worship of a well or fountain that is characterized by tying cloth to trees near them)</li></ol><p>From <i>wel</i> (“well”) +&lrm; <i>weorþung</i> (“worship”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wielwan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wil</i> (“will, desire”) + <i>þeġn</i> (“servant, warrior”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>wil</i> (“will”) +&lrm; <i>þrȳþ</i> (“might”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wīfmann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wīfmann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wīfmann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a veil, an article of women's dress; a covering for the neck, a cloak, a hood</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wimpilaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wine</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wīn</i> from Latin <i>vīnum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wineskin, winebottle</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>wīn</i> (“wine”) +&lrm; <i>belg</i> (“bag”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grape</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wīnabaʀi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīnabazją</i>, <i>&#42;wīnabasją</i>. Equivalent to <i>wīn</i> (“wine”) +&lrm; <i>berġe</i> (“berry”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wīnberġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wīnberġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>town where wine is drunk or where there is feasting</li><li>a place where a prince feasts his followers</li><li>a chief town</li><li>a walled vineyard</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wīn</i> (“wine”) +&lrm; <i>burg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winch, reel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;winkijǭ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weng-</i> (“to bow, bend, arch, curve”). Related to the verb <i>wincian</i> (“to wink”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nook; corner</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;winkil</i> (“angle, corner”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weng-</i> (“to bend, bow, arch, curve”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>winkel</i> (“corner”), Old High German <i>winkil</i> (“corner”), German <i>Winkel</i> (“corner”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wink</li></ol><p>From <i>wincian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ettan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to close the eyes; blink</li><li>to wink; make a sign; signal; nod</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;winkōn</i> (“to close the eyes, wink”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wind</li><li>flatulence</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wind</i>.</p><p>Germanic cognates include Old Frisian <i>wind</i>, Old Saxon <i>wind</i>, Dutch <i>wind</i>, Old High German <i>wint</i> (German <i>Wind</i>), Old Norse <i>vindr</i> (Swedish <i>vind</i>), Gothic <i>𐍅𐌹𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (winds). The Indo-European root is also the source of Latin <i>ventus</i> (French <i>vent</i>), Welsh <i>gwynt</i>, Tocharian A <i>want</i>, Tocharian B <i>yente</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twist, wind, circle, curl, spiral, eddy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;windaną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>winda</i> (West Frisian <i>wine</i>), Old Saxon <i>windan</i> (Low German <i>winden</i>), Dutch <i>winden</i>, Old High German <i>wintan</i> (German <i>winden</i>), Old Norse <i>vinda</i> (Danish <i>vinde</i>, Swedish <i>vinda</i>), Gothic <i>&#42;𐍅𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;windan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>windy</li></ol><p>From <i>wind</i> +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>There are no direct attestations of this word in Old English, but exists in several derived compounds.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;windā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;windǭ</i>,</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basket</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;windil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;windilaz</i>, equivalent to <i>windan</i> +&lrm; <i>-el</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>windiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>windy</li><li>windy, flatulent; gassy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;windigaz</i> (“windy”), equivalent to <i>wind</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>windel</i> (“basket”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Windsor (a town in the Royal Borough of Windsor and Maidenhead, Berkshire, England)</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;windel</i> +&lrm; <i>ōra</i> (“shore, bank, hill”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wine drunk, drunk on wine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīnadrunkanaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>wīntruncan</i>, Old Norse <i>víndrukkinn</i>. Equivalent to <i>wīn</i> (“wine”) +&lrm; <i>druncen</i> (“drunk”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fan</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;windaskuflu</i>. Equivalent to <i>wind</i> (“wind”) +&lrm; <i>sċofl</i> (“shovel”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fan</li><li>to winnow</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;windwōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) friend</li></ol><p>From earlier Old English <i>wini</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wini</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;winiz</i>, whence also Old Dutch <i>wini</i>, Old Saxon <i>wini</i>, Old High German <i>wini</i>, Old Norse <i>vinr</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wenh₁-</i> (“to seek, desire, love, win”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Wine)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vineyard</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wine</i> (“friend”) + <i>berht</i> (“bright”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Slavia</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Slavs</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) Slavia</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;winidaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) friendly lord, lord and friend</li></ol><p>From <i>wine</i> (“friend”) +&lrm; <i>dryhten</i> (“lord”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wine</i> (“friend”) + <i>friþ</i> (“peace”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wine</i> (“friend”) + <i>lāc</i> (“offering, gift”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>friendless</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>wine</i> (“friend”) +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>wine</i> (“friend”) + <i>mund</i> (“protector”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kinsman</li></ol><p>From <i>wine</i> +&lrm; <i>mǣġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>winemāga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>winemāgum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friendship</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;winiskapi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;winiskapiz</i>. Equivalent to <i>wine</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>vinskapr</i> (“friendship”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Wineshlǣw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Winslow, an English toponym</li></ol><p><i>Wines</i>, genitive of the personal name <i>Wine</i> + <i>hlǣw</i> (“mound”). Attested in 795.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>left (direction)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;winistr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;winistraz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wine</i> (“friend”) + <i>þrȳþ</i> (“strength, power”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Wynnfriþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flown with wine, wanton with wine, intoxicated</li></ol><p>From <i>wīn</i> (“wine”) +&lrm; <i>gāl</i> (“wanton, lustful; wicked”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vineyard</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wīnagard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feast</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wīn</i> +&lrm; <i>hāte</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winehouse</li></ol><p>From <i>wīn</i> (“wine”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wine</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land used for growing grapes</li></ol><p>From <i>wīn</i> (“wine”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to struggle, suffer, contend</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;winnan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;winnaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wimpel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wīnsalu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>winestra</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>left (opposite right)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;winistraz</i>. Akin to Old Norse <i>vinstri</i> (Danish <i>venstre</i>, Norwegian <i>venstre</i>, and Swedish <i>vänster</i>), Old High German <i>winistar</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) wine-hall</li></ol><p>From <i>wīn</i> (“wine”) +&lrm; <i>sæl</i> (“hall”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Winchester (city in England)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Venta Belgārum</i> (literally “Venta of the Belgae”), possibly from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;wentā</i> (“town, place”), Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁wen-</i> (“place (?)”) + <i>ċeastre</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winter</li><li>year</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>*wintr</i> &lt; <i>*wintru</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wintruz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>winter</i>, Old Saxon <i>wintar</i>, Old Dutch <i>winter</i>, Old High German <i>wintar</i>, Old Norse <i>vetr</i>, Gothic <i>𐍅𐌹𐌽𐍄𐍂𐌿𐍃</i> (wintrus).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Winter)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From the noun <i>winter</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bitter with winter</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>biter</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a stream that is full in winter</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>burna</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>winter-cold</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wintrukald</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wintarkald</i>. Equivalent to <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>ċeald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sad from old age or winter's gloom, sad as winter</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>ċeariġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>down or hill for winter-pasture</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>dūn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winter-day</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Christmas feast</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>feorm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the tenth month of the year according to the Anglo-Saxon calendar</li><li>October</li></ol><p>From <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>fylleþ</i> (“a filling; a full moon”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destiny, fate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>ġegang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reckoning by years</li><li>quantity of years</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>ġerīm</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>number of years</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>ġetæl</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wintry storm</li><li>storm of snow or hail</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeweorp</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winter garment; wintry weed</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>ġewǣde</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winterhouse</li></ol><p>From <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>hūs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) winter</li><li>wintry</li></ol><p><i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw near to winter</li></ol><p>From <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>-lǣċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>number of years</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>rīm</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winter shower</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>sċūr</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winter house</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>selde</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>somewhere to stop in for winter</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) winter-quarters</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>set</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>somewhere to stop in for winter</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) winter-quarters</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>setl</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food (aside from bread) for winter</li></ol><p>From <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>sufel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wintertime</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>tīd</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>winterwylle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a spring that can be used in winter</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>wylle</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>winter</i> (“winter”), likely via the dative singular/genitive plural form <i>wintra</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wintriġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīnatrewą</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>víntré</i>, Gothic <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐌽𐌰𐍄𐍂𐌹𐌿</i> (weinatriu). Equivalent to <i>wīn</i> (“wine”) +&lrm; <i>trēow</i> (“tree”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the first day of winter, given in one Anglo-Saxon source as November 7th</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wintry</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>winter</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winepress</li></ol><p>From <i>wīn</i> +&lrm; <i>trog</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wine cask</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>wīn</i> (“wine”) +&lrm; <i>tunne</i> (“barrel, cask”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wudu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weoloc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorold</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wita</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>witodlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wipe</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wīpōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wire</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) myrtle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīraz</i>. Cognate with Middle Low German <i>wīre</i>, and related to late Old Norse <i>víravirki</i> (“filigree work”), Old High German <i>wiara</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wyrċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wearmouth, England</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwierfel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wierġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wierġnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Wirral, a peninsula in England.</li></ol><p><i>wīr</i> (“myrtle”) +&lrm; <i>healh</i> (“corner”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wierġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wierġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wierġnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wierman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiern</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiernan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiers</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>myrtle tree</li></ol><p>From <i>wīr</i> (“myrtle”) +&lrm; <i>trēow</i> (“tree”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wise</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīsaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weydstos</i> (“knowledgeable”), an extension of <i>&#42;weyd-</i> (“to see, to know”). Akin to Old High German <i>wīs</i> and Old Norse <i>víss</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) leader, chief</li></ol><p>Formally <i>wīsan</i> (“to show”) +&lrm; <i>-a</i> (agent suffix), cognate with Old High German <i>wīso</i>, Old Norse <i>vísi</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wīsijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīsijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wīsian</i>, Old High German <i>wīsen</i>, Old Norse <i>vísa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wisdom</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīsadōmaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wīsdom</i>, Old Saxon <i>wīsdom</i>, Old High German <i>wīstuom</i>, Old Norse <i>vísdómr</i>. Equivalent to <i>wīs</i> +&lrm; <i>dōm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>way (manner)</li><li>a sprout, stalk</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wīsā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīsō</i>, <i>&#42;wīsaz</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>wijze</i>, German <i>Weise</i>, Swedish <i>vis</i>, Italian <i>guisa</i>, Spanish <i>guisa</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waisō</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weys-</i> (“to increase”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>vísir</i> (“sprout, bud”), Lithuanian <i>veisti</i> (“propagate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>wīs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show the way, guide, point out</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wīsōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīsōną</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wīsōn</i>, Old Dutch <i>wīson</i>, Old High German <i>wīsōn</i>, and Old Norse <i>vísa</i> (Swedish <i>visa</i>); also compre Old Saxon <i>wīsian</i>, Old Dutch <i>wīsen</i>, and Old High German <i>wīsen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vistula</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the land around the river Vistula in what is now Poland</li></ol><p>From <i>Wīsle</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Wīsleland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the mouth of the Vistula</li></ol><p>From <i>Wīsle</i> +&lrm; <i>mūþa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wise, prudent, sagacious</li></ol><p><i>wīs</i> (“wise”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wisely, with wisdom, sagaciously, prudently</li><li>wisely, skillfully, cunningly</li></ol><p>From <i>wīs</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i> or <i>wīslīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wīslīko</i>, Old High German <i>wīslīhho</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teaching, wisdom</li></ol><p>From <i>wīs</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dry up, wither</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wisnōjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiste</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show (the way), guide, direct</li><li>to declare, make known</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wissōn</i>, cognate with Old Frisian <i>wissia</i> (“to assure”), Middle Low German <i>wissen</i> (“to assure, insure”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guidance, direction, teaching</li><li>rule, authority</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>wissian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>being, existence</li><li>well-being</li><li>provisions, food</li><li>feast, meal</li><li>delicacy</li><li>abundance, plenty</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wisti</i> (“essence”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wisti</i> (“food”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the forests of the Vistula, described in the poem "Widsith" as the home of the Goths.</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwistlian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>we two</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wit</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wet</i>, from a suffixed form of <i>&#42;wéy</i> (see <i>wē</i>). Cognate with North Frisian <i>wat</i>, Old Norse <i>vit</i>, Gothic <i>𐍅𐌹𐍄</i> (wit), and Lithuanian <i>vèdu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wise person; <i>(especially in compounds)</i> knower</li><li>advisor</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;witō</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wita</i>, Old Saxon <i>&#42;wito</i> (attested in <i>giwito</i> “witness”), and Old High German <i>wizzo</i>. Equivalent to <i>witan</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to know, be aware of</li><li>to feel a certain emotion</li><li>to blame, accuse, reproach</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;witan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;witaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wóyde</i>, originally a perfect form of <i>&#42;weyd-</i> (“see”).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wītaną</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>víta</i>, Dutch <i>wijten</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>punishment</li><li>penalty, fine</li><li>torment</li><li>plague</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wītī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wītiją</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wīte</i>, Old Saxon <i>wīti</i>, Old High German <i>wīzi</i>, Old Norse <i>víti</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>witod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>witodlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wītga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prophetess</li></ol><p>From <i>wītega</i> +&lrm; <i>-estre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wītgian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wītgung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sackcloth</li><li>a garment worn as punishment</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>wīte</i> (“punishment”) +&lrm; <i>hræġl</i> (“garment”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prison</li><li>an amphitheater in which Christians were martyred</li><li>Hell</li></ol><p>From <i>wīte</i> (“punishment, torture”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parliament, assembly of the wise</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>knowing, wise</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;witraz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>vitr</i> (“wise”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Hwītern</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hell</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>wīte</i> (“punishment”) +&lrm; <i>sċræf</i> (“cave, den”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seer, prophet</li><li>wiseman, sage</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) foreshadowing, presage, omen</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>*wītægā</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wītagō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prophesy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wītagōn</i>. Equivalent to <i>wītga</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(countable,&#32;uncountable) prophecy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wītagungu</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>wīzagunga</i>. Equivalent to <i>wītgian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wittiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wītega</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(countable,&#32;uncountable) prophecy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wītagadōm</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>wīzagtuom</i>. By the literary period equivalent to <i>wītiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-dōm</i>, but probably formed originally from the etymons of <i>wītga</i> +&lrm; <i>-dōm</i>; <i>wītiġdōm</i> is the expected outcome either way. Compare <i>lǣċedōm</i> (“medicine”), which is formed similarly.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>senseless; witless</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;witjalausaz</i>, equivalent to <i>witt</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>vitlauss</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>senselessness, witlessness, folly</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>witlēas</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>witness</li></ol><p>From <i>witt</i> (knowledge, wisdom) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i> (-ness), akin to Old Norse <i>vitni</i> (whence the Danish <i>vidne</i> and Norwegian Bokmål <i>vitne</i>) and Gothic <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍄𐍅𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (weitwōþs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to punish</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wītinōn</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wītnia</i>, Old Saxon <i>wītnon</i>, and Old High German <i>wīzinōn</i>. Equivalent to <i>wīte</i> +&lrm; <i>-nian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>appointed, ordained, assured, certain, sure</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>certainly, assuredly</li><li>indeed, surely</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;witōd</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;witōdaz</i>, past participle of <i>&#42;witōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>certain</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>witod</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>certainly</li><li>(<i>indefinite</i>) indeed, surely, truly</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>witod</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>, else <i>witodlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mind, understanding, sense, sanity</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;witi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;witją</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyd-</i> (“see, know”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wit</i>, Old Saxon <i>wit</i>, Old High German <i>wizzi</i> (whence German <i>Witz</i>), Old Norse <i>vit</i> (whence Swedish <i>vett</i>). Compare <i>witan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having knowledge, wisdom, sense; having wit; witty</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;witīg</i>, <i>&#42;witag</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;witagaz</i>, <i>&#42;wītagaz</i>, equivalent to <i>witt</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>witig</i>, Old High German <i>wizzīg</i> and German <i>witzig</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>witod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>witodlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dowry</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;weþmō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a statement which bears witness to something; testament; covenant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;witawurdą</i>, equivalent to <i>wit</i> +&lrm; <i>word</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>vitorð</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a people or tribe living in England</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wrixlan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wiþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wiþcweþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wiþercweþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wiþerrǣde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wiþersaca</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiþerwinna</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wiþfōn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wiþgrīpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wiþheardian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wīþig</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wiþmetendlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiþmetennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiþmetennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiþstandan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiþstandan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiþseċġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wiþweorpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>against, opposite (typically + accusative with sense of motion or active opposition, typically + dative otherwise)</li><li>along, towards (typically + genitive)</li><li>with</li><li>for, in exchange for,</li><li>compared with (+ accusative or dative)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with it; together; therewith</li></ol><p>Probably a shortening of a Germanic preposition represented by Old English <i>wiþer</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wiþrą</i> (“against”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>with</i>, Old Saxon <i>with</i>, Old Norse <i>við</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>against, opposed to, counter to</li><li>away, off</li><li>again, back</li><li>completely, fully, entirely (<i>perfective prefix</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wiþi-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wiþi-</i>, related to Old English <i>wiþ</i> (“against”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>with-</i>, Old Saxon <i>with-</i>, Old Norse <i>við-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow away</li><li>to strain at</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>blāwan</i>. Compare Middle High German <i>widerblæjen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to withhold, hold back, restrain, check</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>breġdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choose against; deselect, reject; refuse</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþ-</i> (“against, away”) +&lrm; <i>ċēosan</i> (“to choose”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reprobate, wicked</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disapprove, condemn</li><li>to reject</li></ol><p><i>wiþ-</i> (“against, away”) +&lrm; <i>costian</i> (“to try, prove, examine”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contradiction</li><li>objection, opposition</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþcweden</i> (“contradicted”) + <i>-nes</i> (“-ness”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contradict</li><li>to oppose, resist</li><li>to refuse, reject</li><li>to reply</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wiþikweþan</i>, equivalent to <i>wiþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>cweþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive back, repel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wiþidrīban</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;wiþradrīban</i> (“to drive back”), equivalent to <i>wiþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>drīfan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>withdrīva</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>against (+accusative)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wiþr</i> from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wiþrą</i>. Cognates include Old Saxon <i>withar</i>, Old Frisian <i>wither</i>, Old Dutch <i>wither</i>, and Old High German <i>widar</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>against, contrary, opposed to; anti-, counter-</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>wiþer</i> (“against”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>going against, resistance</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>wiþer-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċierr</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an antichrist</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþer-</i> (“anti-”) +&lrm; <i>crist</i> (“Christ”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contradiction</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>wiþercweþan</i> (“to contradict”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiþcweþan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wiþrakweþan</i>, equivalent to <i>wiþer-</i> +&lrm; <i>cweþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who contradicts</li><li>a rebel</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþer-</i> (“against”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;cwida</i> (“speaker”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to murmur</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resistance, opposition, contesting</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþer-</i> +&lrm; <i>cwide</i>. Compare Old High German <i>widarqueta</i> (“contradiction, opposition”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a going against; resistance</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wiþer-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċyrr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opponent</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiþhabban</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wiþer-</i> +&lrm; <i>habban</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean against</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþer-</i> +&lrm; <i>hlinian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to resist, oppose</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþer</i> (“toward, against”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wiþr</i>, +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recompense</li><li>retribution</li></ol><p><i>wiþer-</i> +&lrm; <i>lēan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opponent</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþer</i> (“against”) +&lrm; <i>-ling</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a (legal) case against someone</li></ol><p><i>wiþer-</i> (“against”) +&lrm; <i>mal</i> (“legal case”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recompense, compensation; reparation</li><li>retribution, payback</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wiþrareht</i>, equivalent to <i>wiþer-</i> +&lrm; <i>riht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>adverse, contrary</li><li>hostile</li><li>disagreeable, offensive</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþer-</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣde</i>. Compare Old High German <i>widarredīg</i> (“contradictory”) and German <i>Widerrede</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adversary, enemy, betrayer, apostate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wiþrasakō</i>, equivalent to <i>wiþer-</i> +&lrm; <i>saca</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who contradicts; a contradicter</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;wiþersprecan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>. <br>Alternatively from <i>wiþer-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;sprecend</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>denial</li><li>contradiction</li><li>apostasy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wiþrasak</i>. Equivalent to <i>wiþer-</i> +&lrm; <i>sæc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>contrary, opposite</li><li>arrogant, perverse, depraved, opposed to what is right</li><li>adverse, hostile</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wiþrawardaz</i> (“contrary, adverse, in opposition”); equivalent to <i>wiþer</i> +&lrm; <i>-weard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enemy, opponent, rival</li></ol><p>Akin to Old High German <i>widarwinno</i>; equivalent to <i>wiþer-</i> +&lrm; <i>winnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiþwinde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come off, escape; flee; go or fare off</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>faran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay hold of, seize upon, grasp at, clutch</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wiþifą̄han</i>, equivalent to <i>wiþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>fōn</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>withfāhan</i>. Compare also Old High German <i>widarfāhan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go against; withstand</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>gān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go against</li><li>to go off</li><li>to withdraw, fail</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþ-</i> (“against”) +&lrm; <i>gangan</i>. Compare Faroese <i>viðganga</i> (“to confess”), Old Norse <i>viðganga</i> (“to access, admit”), and Scots <i>withgang</i> (“to give license, indulge”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seize on or upon</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wiþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>grīpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold out against, withstand</li><li>to restrain</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wiþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>habban</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) to make obdurate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wiþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>heardian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be adverse in thought or purpose</li><li>to be disposed to resist</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>wiþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>hogian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have contrary or adverse intent or purpose (to somebody or something)</li><li>to set one's self against, be set against (somebody or something)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wiþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>hyċġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>willow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīþijaz</i> (“willow”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wéh₁itis</i> (“that which twines or bends, branch, switch”). Related to German <i>Weide</i>, Dutch <i>wijde</i>, Swedish <i>vide</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wīþig</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>within</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>within</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþ</i> +&lrm; <i>innan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead away, take away, carry off</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compare <i>(+dative to something</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþ-</i> (“against”) +&lrm; <i>metan</i> (“to measure”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparable</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) comparative</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) (<i>substantive</i>) the comparative degree</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþmetan</i> (“to compare”) +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiþmetennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiþmetendlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comparison</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþmeten</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiþmetennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>below</li></ol><p><i>wiþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>neoþan</i> (“below”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiþwinde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deny</li><li>to reject</li><li>to withhold, refuse to give something</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>sacan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>negative, expressing negation</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþsacan</i> +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) to renounce, to give up</li><li>to gainsay, to oppose in speech (and by extension writing)</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþ-</i> (“with-, against”) +&lrm; <i>seċġan</i> (“say”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>witharseggian</i> (“to object”), Low German <i>wedderseggen</i> (“to renounce”), German <i>widersagen</i> (“to renounce”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set against, withstand, oppose</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>settan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak against, revile, withsay</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wiþisprekan</i>, equivalent to <i>wiþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>sprecan</i>. Compare Old Frisian <i>withspreka</i>, Old High German <i>widarsprehhan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand against, stand in the way of, resist, be a match for <i>(+ dative)</i></li><li>to obstruct, prevent, hinder <i>(+ dative)</i></li><li>to keep away, be absent</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>standan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leap back</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>stiellan</i> (“leap”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dash against</li></ol><p>From <i>wiþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>stunian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dative&#32;or&#32;accusative</i>) to withdraw, draw back</li><li>to draw away</li><li>to draw to</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wiþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>tēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>above</li></ol><p><i>wiþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>ufan</i> (“above”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>outside of, without</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>outside</li></ol><p><i>wiþ</i> +&lrm; <i>ūtan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reject</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wiþ-</i> +&lrm; <i>weorpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>withwind <i>Convolvulus arvensis</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tie, band</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wiþjǭ</i>, <i>&#42;wiþiz</i> (“rope, cord”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyt-</i> (“something twisted, rod, switch, whip”), from <i>&#42;weh₁y-</i> (“to wind, twist, bend, turn”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>withthe</i>, Middle Dutch <i>wisse</i> (Dutch <i>wis</i>), Old High German <i>wit</i>, <i>withi</i>, Old Norse <i>við</i>, <i>viðja</i> (Swedish <i>vidja</i>). Compare also Ancient Greek <i>ἰτέα</i> (itéa, “willow”), Latin <i>vītis</i> (“vine”), Old Irish <i>féith</i> (“fibre”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contradict, talk against</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wlæc</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wlaku</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wlakwaz</i> (“lukewarm”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weleg-</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>wealg</i> (“insipid, nauseous”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>proud, boastful</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pride</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wlank</i> (“grown-up”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow proud or great; to aggrandize one's self</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wlanc</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gaze, look upon, behold</li><li>synonym of <i>wlǣtian</i> (“to loathe”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wlaitōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wlaitōną</i> (“to see, look”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wel-</i> (“to see”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>leita</i> (“to look for, seek”). Related to Old English <i>wlītan</i> (“to gaze, observe”), <i>wlite</i> (“appearance, form, aspect”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wlātōn</i>, <i>&#42;wlātijōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loathing</li><li>sight, spectacle</li></ol><p><i>wlātian</i> (“to gaze; to loathe”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make proud</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wlencu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pride, high spirit</li><li>(<i>negative</i>) prideful behavior, such as arrogance, haughtiness, insolence, etc</li><li>(<i>positive</i>) proud condition, such as greatness, pomp, splendor, etc</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wlankī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wlankį̄</i>, equivalent to <i>wlanc</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wlisp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lisping</li><li>stammering</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wlispaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look</li><li>to behold</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wlītan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>looks, appearance, aspect</li><li>good looks, beauty, splendour</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wlitiz</i>, <i>&#42;wlituz</i> (“appearance, look, aspect”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wel-</i> (“to see”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wlite</i>, Old Saxon <i>wliti</i>, Old Norse <i>litr</i>, Gothic <i>𐍅𐌻𐌹𐍄𐍃</i> (wlits). Related to <i>wlītan</i> (“to see, look”), <i>wlātian</i> (“to gaze, observe”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) beautiful</li></ol><p><i>wlite</i> +&lrm; <i>beorht</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>wlitiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>wlitiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ugly, uncomely, hideous</li></ol><p>From <i>wlite</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beautiful</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wlitīg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wlitiġaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to became or make beautiful</li></ol><p>Related to <i>wlitiġ</i> (“fairsome, beautiful”) and <i>wlite</i> (“beauty”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>wlitiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fringe, hem</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wlanc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wlitiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lukewarm; tepid</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wlaku</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wlakwaz</i> (“lukewarm”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weleg-</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>wealg</i> (“insipid, nauseous”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be nauseous, feel sick <i>(often impersonal, with dative or accusative of person)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wlātōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nausea</li><li>deformity, disfigurement</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wlātiþō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sickness, nausea</li><li>defilement, disfigurement</li></ol><p>From <i>wlǣtian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offspring, progeny</li><li>(<i>collectively</i>) living creatures</li><li>(<i>law</i>) usury, interest</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wōkr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wōkraz</i> (“progeny, earnings, profit”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂weg-</i> (“to add, increase”). More at English <i>oker</i>, <i>ocker</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wōcon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crazy, insane, mad</li><li><i>(esp. with dogs and other animals)</i> rabid</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) mad with anger, enraged</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>madness</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wōd</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wōdaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>wuot</i>, Old Norse <i>óðr</i>, Gothic <i>𐍅𐍉𐌳𐍃</i> (wōds).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wōd</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wōdaz</i>. Derived from the adjective.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crazy person, madman</li><li>danger</li></ol><p>Derived from the adjective <i>wōd</i> (“crazy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Woden</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Wōdanaz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>Wōden</i>, Old High German <i>Wodan</i>, Old Norse <i>Óðinn</i>. Doublet of <i>Ōþen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Woden</li></ol><p><i>Woden</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ravenous, raging</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>wōd</i> (“insane”) +&lrm; <i>frec</i> (“greedy”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>madness, insanity</li></ol><p>From <i>wōd</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wednesday</li></ol><p>From <i>Wōdnes</i> (“gen. of <i>Wōden</i>”) +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i> (“day”), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Wōdanas dag</i>, a calque of Latin <i>diēs Mercuriī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wōdnesdæġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tuesday night</li></ol><p>From <i>Wōdnes</i> (“gen. of <i>Wōden</i>”) +&lrm; <i>niht</i> (“night”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Wōdening</i> (“son of Odin”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>madness, insanity</li></ol><p>From <i>wōd</i> (“madness”) +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wēstenes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wēsten</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wēstiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wēstiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wōh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wooer, suitor</li></ol><p>From <i>wōgian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to woo, court, marry</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wrong</li><li>crooked, bent</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wrong (something wrong or wrong things collectively)</li><li>error or an error; a mistake</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wą̄h</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wanhaz</i>. Akin to Gothic <i>𐌿𐌽𐍅𐌰𐌷𐍃</i> (unwāhs, “blameless”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bent, crooked</li></ol><p>From <i>wōh</i> +&lrm; <i>bogen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fine to be paid for trading against market regulations</li></ol><p>From <i>wōh</i> +&lrm; <i>ċēapung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unjust judgement</li></ol><p>From <i>wōh</i> +&lrm; <i>dōm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wicked deed</li><li>crime</li></ol><p>From <i>wōh</i> +&lrm; <i>dǣd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crook-footed</li><li>splayfooted</li><li>clubfooted</li></ol><p>From <i>wōh</i> +&lrm; <i>fōtede</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wrong-doing, malfeasant</li></ol><p>From <i>wōh</i> +&lrm; <i>fremmende</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wōhfull</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wicked, evil</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wōh</i> +&lrm; <i>-full</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wickedness, iniquity</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wōhful</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eager in wickedness; loving iniquity</li></ol><p>From <i>wōh</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeorn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ill-gotten gain</li></ol><p>From <i>wōh</i> +&lrm; <i>ġestrēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crook-handed</li><li>having a maimed hand</li></ol><p>From <i>wōh</i> +&lrm; <i>hɑndede</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go astray mentally</li><li>to rave</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wōh</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adultery</li><li>fornication</li></ol><p>From <i>wōh</i> +&lrm; <i>hǣmed</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>adulterous</li><li>fornicating</li></ol><p>From <i>wōh</i> +&lrm; <i>hǣmende</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adulterer</li><li>fornicator</li></ol><p>From <i>wōh</i> +&lrm; <i>hǣmere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wrong</li><li>perverse</li><li>evil</li></ol><p>From <i>wōh</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wrongly, unjustly</li><li>perversely</li><li>wickedly, evilly</li></ol><p>From <i>wōh</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crookedness</li><li>a crooked place</li><li>wrongdoing, injustice</li><li>perversity, depravity</li><li>wickedness, iniquity</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wōh</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>evil, wicked</li></ol><p>From <i>wōh</i> +&lrm; <i>-sum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a tribe mentioned in Widsith, derived or descended from the Vangiones</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Wanhingōs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plague, pestilence</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wōl</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wōl</i>, Old High German <i>wuol</i>, Old Norse <i>ól</i> (“troll-woman”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pestilential, pestiferous</li><li>pernicious</li></ol><p>From <i>wōl</i> +&lrm; <i>berende</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pestilential</li></ol><p>From <i>wōlberende</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deadly violence</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wōl</i> +&lrm; <i>bryne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pestiferousness</li><li>destructiveness</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wōlbǣre</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wolcn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wolcn</i></li><li>a convolution, ball, lump</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wulkną</i> (“cloud”).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wulk-</i>, from <i>&#42;walkaną</i> (“to twist, roll”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloud</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the sky, the heavens</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wolkn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wulkną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weolocrēad</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>woldon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a day of pestilence or death</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wōl</i> +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a conflict with a high death toll</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wōl</i> +&lrm; <i>ġewinn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pest, pestilence, plague</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wōl</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sound, noise</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wōhm</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wōhmaz</i> (“noise, sound, shout, voice”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wekʷ-</i> (“to speak, sound”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>ómr</i> (“sound”), Old Norse <i>ómun</i> (“voice”), Old High German <i>giwahan</i> (“to mention”), Latin <i>vōx</i> (“voice”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sound, noise</li><li>alarm, terror</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wōhmō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wōhmô</i> (“noise, sound, shout, voice”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wekʷ-</i> (“to speak, sound”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>ómr</i> (“sound”), Old Norse <i>ómun</i> (“voice”), Old High German <i>giwahan</i> (“to mention”), Old Norse <i>œmta</i> (“to utter, mutter”), Latin <i>vōx</i> (“voice”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wamb</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) spot, mark, blot</li><li>(<i>literal&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) filth, impurity, uncleanness</li><li>evil, sin; a shameful word or deed</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foul</li><li>evil, wicked</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wamm</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wammaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wemh₁-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wann</i></li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wand</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wandian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wangtōþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wansǣliġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wanung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weeping, lamentation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wōpaz</i> (“clamour, weeping”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>word</li><li>speech, utterance, statement</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) verb</li><li>news, information, rumour</li><li>command, request</li><li>Logos, the Word of God, the second person of the Trinity, Jesus</li><li>thornbush</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;word</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurdą</i>.</p><p>Unknown. Perhaps ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wr̥dʰos</i> (“sweetbriar”). Compare Latin <i>rubus</i> (“bramble”), Persian گل (gol, “flower”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dictionary</li></ol><p>From <i>word</i> +&lrm; <i>bōc</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) adverb</li></ol><p><i>wordes</i> (“verb's”) +&lrm; <i>ġefēra</i> (“companion”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>true to one's word, keeping one's word</li></ol><p>From <i>word</i> +&lrm; <i>-fæst</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a verbal agreement; an expressed agreement; a formal contract</li></ol><p>From <i>word</i> (“word, statement”) +&lrm; <i>ġecwide</i> (“agreement”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) treasure of words, word-hoard</li></ol><p>From <i>word</i> +&lrm; <i>hord</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wordy, verbose</li></ol><p>From <i>word</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conversation; speech</li></ol><p><i>word</i> (“word”) +&lrm; <i>laþu</i> (“invitation”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a similitude, literary comparison, collection</li><li>a bringing together; collation</li></ol><p>From <i>word</i> +&lrm; <i>mittung</i> (“meeting, collaboration”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sermon</li></ol><p>From <i>word</i> +&lrm; <i>predicung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an ordinance, law, especially one expressed in words</li></ol><p>From <i>word</i> (“word”) +&lrm; <i>riht</i> (“right”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a word-sower; one who disseminates ideas</li></ol><p>From <i>word</i> +&lrm; <i>sāwere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eloquent</li><li>learned</li><li>expert in speech</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>word</i> (“word”) +&lrm; <i>snotor</i> (“wise”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>hwierfel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pheasant</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;worʀahanō</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;worʀō</i> ~ <i>&#42;ūrzini</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurzô</i> ~ <i>&#42;ūrziniz</i> (“grouse”), + Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hanō</i> (“cock, rooster”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hanô</i> (“cock, rooster”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>worhana</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) to wander about, ramble, be a vagabond</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wōriaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a vagabond</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wōrian</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wandering</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wōriende</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorold</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>woruldlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great many, multitude</li><li>crowd, swarm, band, flock</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Northumbrian form of <i>weorold</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worldly honour</li></ol><p><i>worold</i> +&lrm; <i>ār</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>woruldlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weoroldlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weoroldrǣden</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorold</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worldly, earthly, natural, physical, temporal, mundane</li><li>secular, civil, worldly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;weraldilīkaz</i>, equivalent to <i>woruld</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>weraltlīh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>weoroldrīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goods or blessings of the world</li></ol><p>From <i>weorold</i> (“world”) +&lrm; <i>sǣlþa</i> (“prosperities”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>worþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enclosure</li><li>hall</li><li>enclosed homestead</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurþiz</i> (“elevation, mound”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an enclosed homestead</li><li>a place surrounded by buildings</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>juice, sap; moisture</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wōs</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wōsą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weys-</i> (“to flow”). Akin to Middle Low German <i>wose</i> (“foam; froth”), Old English <i>wāse</i> (“marsh; mire”).</p><p>Inflected forms.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moist, juicy</li></ol><p>From <i>wōs</i> (“liquid, juice”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wēste</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wōþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sound; noise; cry</li><li>speech; articulation; eloquence</li><li>song; poetry</li><li>voice</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wōþō</i> (compare <i>wōd</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wéh₂t-eh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;weh₂t-</i> (“possessed, excited”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>revenge</li><li>wrath</li><li>persecution</li><li>misery, affliction</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrakō</i>, likely related to <i>&#42;wrēkō</i> (“persecution, revenge, vengeance”). Cognate with German <i>Rache</i> (“revenge”), Gothic <i>𐍅𐍂𐌰𐌺𐌰</i> (wraka, “persecution”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) a ship's hold</li><li>wrong, injustice</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rough, uneven</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wrang</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrangaz</i> (“crooked, bent, curved”). Compare Alemannic German <i>Rang</i> (“convolution, bend”), Old Norse <i>vröng</i> (“a ship's rib”) (whence Icelandic <i>röng</i>, Old Norwegian <i>vrǫng</i>).</p><p>Attested in later OE. Pons-Sanz and R. Dance argue that it's a native word inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wrang</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrangaz</i>, and only the meaning was acquired from Old Norse <i>rangr</i> by Middle English.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wrang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wrongous, rough, uneven</li></ol><p>From <i>wrang</i> (“wrong”) +&lrm; <i>-wīs</i> (“-wise”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wrǣstlian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wrāþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very angry, irate</li><li>hostile, savage, violent</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wraiþaz</i> (corresponding to past tense of <i>wrīþan</i>). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wređ</i> (Dutch <i>wreed</i>), Old High German <i>reid</i>, Old Norse <i>reiðr</i> (Danish <i>vred</i>, Swedish <i>vred</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>support, help</li><li>benefit</li><li>a support</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wraþu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>miserable, wretched</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;intransitive) to punish (+ dative = a person) (+ accusative = a wrong)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;intransitive) to avenge or take revenge (+ accusative or with <i>on</i> + dative = "on someone")</li><li>to unleash or take out (one's anger) (with <i>on</i> + dative = on someone)</li><li>to drive, press</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrekaną</i> (“to pursue, to drive out”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exile, outcast</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wrakkjō</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wrekkio</i>, Old High German <i>recko</i> (German <i>Recke</i> (“warrior”)).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise, lift up</li><li>to undertake</li><li>to rouse</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrakjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an avenger</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wrecan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vengeance</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exile</li></ol><p>From <i>wrec</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accuse, impeach, blame</li><li>to incite, stir up, excite</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrōgijaną</i> (“to tell, speak, shout”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;were-</i>, <i>&#42;wrē-</i> (“to tell, speak”). Akin to Old Frisian <i>wrēia</i> (“to accuse”), Old Saxon <i>wrōgian</i> (“to accuse”), Dutch <i>wroegen</i> (“to accuse”), Old High German <i>ruogen</i> (“to accuse”) (German <i>rügen</i> (“to censure, reprimand”)), Old Norse <i>rœgja</i> (“to accuse”) (Swedish <i>röja</i> (“to betray”)), Gothic <i>𐍅𐍂𐍉𐌷𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wrōhjan, “to accuse”), Old English <i>wrōht</i> (“blame, accusation”). More at bewray, betray.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>accusative</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) accusative case</li></ol><p>Present participle of <i>wrēġan</i> (“to accuse”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wile, stratagem; trick, deceit</li><li>melody, song; modulation</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrankiz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wreng-</i> (“to turn”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twist</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wrankijan</i>, whence also Old High German <i>renchen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wren</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrandijô</i> (“wren”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cover (up), put a cover over</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to cover, act as covering for</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wrīan</i>, <i>&#42;wrīhan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to support</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;wreþþan</i> (attested in <i>underwreþþan</i>), from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wraþþjan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plant</li><li>shoot</li><li>bush</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wrīd</i>, <i>&#42;wrīdi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrīdaną</i>, <i>&#42;wrīþaną</i> (“to grow, flourish, thrive”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be productive, burgeon, flourish</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wrīþan</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wreyt-</i> (“to twist, turn”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wrīdan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move forward</li><li>to incline, tend towards</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrigōną</i> (“to wriggle”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wreyḱ-</i> (“to turn, wrap, tie”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wer-</i> (“to turn, bend”). Perhaps related to Low German <i>wriggen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wring</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wringan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wringaną</i>, cognate with Old High German <i>ringan</i>, Middle Dutch <i>wringen</i> (Dutch <i>wringen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wrixlan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wrist</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wristuz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writ</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;writą</i>, whence also Old High German <i>riz</i>, Old Norse <i>rit</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wrītan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrītaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writer</li><li>used in numerous more specific senses, including:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>scribe</li><li>secretary</li><li>draughtsman</li><li>painter</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>wrītan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scratch; make marks, scribble</li><li>to draw, sketch</li><li>to tweet, chirp</li><li>to chatter, gossip</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;writōn</i> (“to scratch”). Cognate with Old High German <i>rizzōn</i> (“to scratch, inscribe”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;writōn</i> (“to chatter”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>writolōn</i> (“to gossip”), Old High German <i>rizzolōn</i> (“to chat, whisper, lisp”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writing</li></ol><p>From <i>wrītan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exchange</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wihslaz</i>, although the gender does not match. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to change</li><li>to exchange, barter</li><li>to reciprocate</li><li>to lend</li></ol><p>Alternation of <i>wixlan</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wihslijan</i>, <i>&#42;wihslōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wihslijaną</i>, <i>&#42;wihslōną</i> (“to change”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyḱ-</i> (“to change”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wixlia</i>, Old Saxon <i>wehsalōn</i>, Old High German <i>wehsalōn</i> (German <i>wechseln</i>). More at wrixle.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wrixlan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>band</li><li>collar</li><li>ring</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wrīþan</i> (“to twist, wrap”) +&lrm; <i>-a</i> (agentive suffix). <i>Wrīþan</i> is from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrīþaną</i> (“to twist; to weave”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wreyt-</i> (“to twist”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twist</li><li>to bewrap</li><li>to bind</li><li>alternative form of <i>wrīdan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wrīþan</i>, whence also Old High German <i>rīdan</i>, Old Norse <i>ríða</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blame, accusation, slander; reproach</li><li>fault, crime; sin, injustice</li><li>enmity, strife, contention; dispute</li><li>injury, hurt, calamity, misery</li><li>a tattle-tale, tale-bearer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrōhiz</i>, <i>&#42;wrōgiþō</i> (“accusation”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;were-</i>, <i>&#42;wrē-</i> (“to tell, speak”). Akin to Old Saxon <i>wrōht</i> (“strife”), Gothic <i>𐍅𐍂𐍉𐌷𐍃</i> (wrōhs, “complaint, accusation”), Old Norse <i>rōg</i> (“quarrel, defamation”), Old English <i>wrēġan</i> (“to accuse, impeach; incite”). More at <i>bewray</i>, <i>betray</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrōga-</i> (“tale-bearer, accuser”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;were-</i>, <i>&#42;wrē-</i> (“to tell, speak”). Akin to Old English <i>wrēġan</i> (“to accuse”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sower of strife</li></ol><p>From <i>wrōht</i> +&lrm; <i>sāwere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snout</li><li>(<i>of an elephant</i>) trunk; proboscis</li></ol><p>Apparently a clipping of earlier <i>&#42;wrōtl</i>, <i>&#42;wrōtul</i>, <i>&#42;wrōtel</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wrōtil</i>, equivalent to <i>wrōtan</i> +&lrm; <i>-el</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rummage or root up with one's snout</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wrōtan</i> (literally “to snout”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exile (state of being exiled)</li><li>misery, torment</li><li>persecution, punishment</li><li>revenge, vengeance</li></ol><p>From a variant base of <i>wrecan</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wrāku</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrēkō</i>. Cognate with Old Dutch <i>wrāca</i> and Old High German <i>rahha</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wreċċa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>path of exile</li></ol><p>From <i>wræc</i> (“exile, punishment, misery”) +&lrm; <i>lāst</i> (“track”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foreign travel</li><li>exile</li><li>misery, wretchedness</li></ol><p>From <i>wræc</i> (“exile”) +&lrm; <i>sīþ</i> (“journey”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>band</li><li>bandage</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>horny; lustful, aroused</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wrainī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrainiz</i> (“rutting, in heat”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wer-</i> (“to twist, wind”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change, twist, alter</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrest, twist</li><li>to pluck a harp</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wraistijaną</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>elegantly, delicately</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrestle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wraistijaną</i> (“to wrest, twist, turn”) + <i>&#42;lōną</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wrestling</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>wrǣstlian</i> (“to wrestle”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wrætte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wondrous: curious (interesting)</li><li>wondrous: excellent</li></ol><p>From <i>wrǣtt</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ornament, jewel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wraitijō</i>, perhaps a derivative of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wraituz</i> (“fissure; crack; furrow; channel; groove”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>red dye; scarlet colouring</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) madder; crosswort</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wrattjā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wratuz</i>, of unknown origin.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wrǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to anger, be angry</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wraiþijaną</i> (“to anger”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wrǣþþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wrath; ire</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wraiþiþu</i> (“wrath, fury”). Equivalent to <i>wrāþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-þ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wrēġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wrēġendlīċ</i></li></ol><p>Present participle of <i>wrœ̄ġan</i> (“to accuse”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wuducoc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bearer of wood</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wudian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut wood</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wudu</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>woody, full of woods or trees</li></ol><p>Equivalent of <i>wudu</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>woody, full of trees</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wudu</i> +&lrm; <i>-iht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wudu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wood</li><li>forest, woods</li><li>tree</li></ol><p>Assimilated from earlier <i>widu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;widuz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;widʰu-</i> (“tree, beam”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>widu</i>, Old High German <i>witu</i>, Old Norse <i>viðr</i>. The Indo-European root is also the source of Old Irish <i>fid</i>, Welsh <i>gwŷdd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wudubinde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodbine (type of vine)</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>wudu</i> (“wood”) +&lrm; <i>binde</i> (“band”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bearer of wood</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wudu</i> +&lrm; <i>-bora</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild goat</li></ol><p>From <i>wudu</i> (“wood”) +&lrm; <i>bucca</i> (“he-goat”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>woody</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wudu</i> +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) wild chervil (<i>Anthriscus sylvestris</i>)</li></ol><p><i>wudu</i> (“wood, forest”) + <i>cerfille</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a woodcock</li></ol><p>From <i>wudu</i> +&lrm; <i>cocc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wuducoc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wood pigeon</li></ol><p>From <i>wudu</i> +&lrm; <i>culfre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wuduielfen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wood-tax, tax on forests</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wudu</i> +&lrm; <i>feoh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bird of the woods</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wudu</i> +&lrm; <i>fugol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place that is made safe by the woods</li><li>a secure place that is made out of wood (such as Noah's ark)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wudu</i> +&lrm; <i>fæsten</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodcock</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wudu</i> +&lrm; <i>hana</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodhen</li><li>quail</li></ol><p>From <i>wudu</i> +&lrm; <i>henn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild honey</li></ol><p>From <i>wudu</i> (“wood, forest”) +&lrm; <i>huniġ</i> (“honey”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female wood-elf, woodnymph, dryad</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wudu</i> +&lrm; <i>ielfen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wooded land; woodland; forest</li></ol><p>From <i>wudu</i> +&lrm; <i>land</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) wild lettuce (<i>Lactuca sativa</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>wudu</i> (“wood, forest”) +&lrm; <i>lēactric</i> (“lettuce”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of a wood</li><li>wild</li></ol><p>Equivalent of <i>wudu</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pasture in the woods</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wudu</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) woodmarch (<i>Sanicula europaea</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>wudu</i> (“wood, forest”) +&lrm; <i>merċe</i> (“smallage”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) woodruff (<i>Galium odoratum</i>)</li></ol><p><i>wudu</i> (“wood”) + <i>rofe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodcutting</li><li>the right of cutting timber in a wood</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wudu</i> +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a snipe</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild man of the woods, woodwose; faun, satyr</li></ol><p>From <i>wudu</i> (“wood”) + a second element that is probably from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;waiso</i> (“orphan”); compare Dutch <i>wees</i>, German <i>Waise</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>widuwe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forester</li></ol><p>From <i>wudu</i> (“forest”) +&lrm; <i>weard</i> (“ward”). Compare <i>wyrtweard</i> (“gardener”, literally “plant ward”), which is formed similarly.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) woad-waxen (<i>Genista tinctoria</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>wudu</i> (“wood”) +&lrm; <i>weax</i> (“wax”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wuduielfen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crab-apple</li></ol><p>From <i>wudu</i> (“wood”) +&lrm; <i>æppel</i> (“apple”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wood-thistle</li></ol><p>From <i>wudu</i> +&lrm; <i>þistel</i>. Calque of Latin <i>cardus silvāticus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wuldor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glory</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wuldr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wuldrą</i> (“shine, radiance, glory, splendor”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wel-</i> (“to see”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crown</li></ol><p><i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>bēag</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crown, coronate</li></ol><p>From <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>bēagian</i>, or else <i>wuldorbēag</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wuldorbēag</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the king of glory; God</li></ol><p>From <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>cyning</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joy in glory (of heaven); celestial joy</li></ol><p>From <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>drēam</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>glorious</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>-full</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glorify; to fill with glory; to make glorious</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>fullian</i>, else to <i>wuldorfull</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>gloriously</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wuldorfull</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>wuldorfull</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the father of glory; the heavenly father</li></ol><p>From <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>fæder</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>glorious</li></ol><p>From <i>wuldor</i> (“wonder”) +&lrm; <i>-fæst</i> (“firm, stable”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a spirit of glory; a glorious ghost; an angel</li></ol><p>From <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>gāst</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fugitive from glory; an evil spirit</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeflogena</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a glorious work; a work of glory</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>ġeweorc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a glorious gift</li><li>a gift from heaven (such as God's grace)</li></ol><p>From <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>ġifu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a glorious gem</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) the Sun</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>ġimm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glorious garb</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>hama</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a glorious band</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>hēap</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a crown</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>helm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) a glorious or heavenly reward (such as eternal life)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>lēan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>glorious</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) orthodox</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>gloriously</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>, else to <i>wuldorlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) a man that reaches the glory of heaven; an heir of heaven (such as a saint)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>māga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a glorious use; an advantage of glory</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>nyttung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a song of glory; a glorious song</li></ol><p>From <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>sang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glorious abundance</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>spēd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rich in glory, glorious</li></ol><p>From <i>wuldorspēd</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;poetic) twig of glory, thunderbolt</li></ol><p>From <i>wuldor</i> (“glory”) +&lrm; <i>tān</i> (“toe, twig”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wuldorwerod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the host of heaven; glorious company</li></ol><p>From <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>werod</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a glorious word</li></ol><p>From <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>word</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glorious majesty</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>þrymm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glorify</li><li>to glory, exult, rejoice</li><li>to get glorified; to receive glory</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>glorious</li></ol><p>From <i>wuldor</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glorifying</li><li>glorying, exulting, rejoicing</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wuldrian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wuldor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wolf</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wulf</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wulfaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wĺ̥kʷos</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) + <i>beorht</i> (“bright”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Wulfhelm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Wulfagaizaz</i>, equivalent to <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) +&lrm; <i>gār</i> (“spear”). compare <i>Garwulf</i> which has the same elements reversed. Cognate with Old Norse <i>Ulfgeirr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) and <i>Ġēat</i> (“Geat”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) +&lrm; <i>ġifu</i> (“gift”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) +&lrm; <i>gūþ</i> (“battle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) +&lrm; <i>hēah</i> (“high”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Wulfahardu</i>, from <i>&#42;wulf</i> (“wolf”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hardu</i> (“hard, brave”). Equivalent to <i>wolf</i> +&lrm; <i>heard</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Wolfhart</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name &lsqb;8-10th C. <small>CE</small>&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Wulfahelm</i>, from <i>&#42;wulf</i> (“wolf”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;helm</i> (“helmet”). Equivalent to <i>wulf</i> +&lrm; <i>helm</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Uuolfhelm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vicious, savage</li></ol><p>From <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) +&lrm; <i>-heort</i> (“hearted”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) and <i>here</i> (“army”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) +&lrm; <i>hild</i> (“battle, war”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) +&lrm; <i>hūn</i> (“bear cub?”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) and <i>lāf</i> (“legacy, remainder”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) and <i>mǣre</i> (“famous, renowned”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) and <i>nōþ</i> (“daring”). Compare <i>Noþwulf</i> which has the same elements reversed.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Wulfrǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Wulfric Spot, an Anglo-Saxon Nobleman.</li></ol><p>From <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) and <i>rīċ</i> (“king, ruler”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) +&lrm; <i>rūn</i> (“secret, whisper”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) and <i>rǣd</i> (“counsel, advice”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) and <i>siġe</i> (“victory”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p><i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) +&lrm; <i>stān</i> (“stone”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) +&lrm; <i>swiþ</i> (“strong, powerful”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) +&lrm; <i>weald</i> (“power”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) and <i>weard</i> (“guard”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) and <i>wīġ</i> (“war, battle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) and <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) and <i>wynn</i> (“joy, delight”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”) +&lrm; <i>wynn</i> (“joy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Wulfaþrūþi</i>, from <i>&#42;wulf</i> (“wolf”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;þrūþi</i> (“strength”). Equivalent to <i>wulf</i> +&lrm; <i>þrȳþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Vuolftrud</i> (<i>Swabian</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wool</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wullu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wullō</i>, from <i>*wulnā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wĺ̥h₁neh₂</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Short form of <i>Wulflāf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>habit, custom</li><li>practice, rite (usually with <i>ġe-</i>); ritual</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wunô</i> (“habit, wont”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wenh₁-</i> (“to be satisfied, wish, desire”). Akin to Old English <i>wunian</i> (“to be accustomed, dwell”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wunode</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wounded, injured, sore</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wound, an injury</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wund</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wundaz</i>, from the verb <i>&#42;wundōną</i> (“to wound”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wundu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wundō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wound</li><li>a sore</li></ol><p>From <i>wund</i> +&lrm; <i>-el</i> (diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wundorlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wundorlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wound or injure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wundōną</i> (“to wound”). Cognates with Old Saxon <i>wundon</i>, Dutch <i>verwonden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miracle, wonder, marvel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wundrą</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wunder</i>, Old Saxon <i>wundar</i>, Old Dutch <i>wundar</i>, Old High German <i>wuntar</i>, Old Norse <i>undr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wonderful, glorious</li></ol><p>From <i>wundor</i> +&lrm; <i>-ful</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miraculous gift</li></ol><p><i>wundor</i> +&lrm; <i>ġiefu</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the upper chamber of a house</li></ol><p>From <i>wundor</i> (“wonder”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wonderful</li><li>miraculous</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wundralīk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wundralīkaz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wundarlīk</i>, Old High German <i>wuntarlīh</i>. Equivalent to <i>wundor</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wonderfully</li><li>miraculously</li></ol><p>From <i>wundor</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>wundorlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wundortācn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miraculous sign; miracle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wundrataikn</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wundartēkan</i> and Old High German <i>wuntarzeihhan</i>. Equivalent to <i>wundor</i> (“miracle”) +&lrm; <i>tācn</i> (“sign”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be amazed or astonished, to marvel</li><li>to be surprised</li><li>to wonder</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wundrōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wundrōną</i>. Equivalent to <i>wundor</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the mark left by a wound, scar</li></ol><p>From <i>wund</i> (“wound”) +&lrm; <i>swaþu</i> (“mark, trace, vestige”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wundswaþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wundorlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wunodon</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live somewhere</li><li>to stay somewhere</li><li>to live or be in a certain condition</li><li>to be located somewhere</li><li>to consist</li><li>to remain, last, continue, endure</li><li>to exist</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wunēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wuniaþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>residence, home, dwelling; place to stay or live</li><li>staying or living in a certain place</li><li>existence, life</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wunungu</i>, equivalent to <i>wunian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wonunga</i>, Old High German <i>wonunga</i> (German <i>Wohnung</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wyrm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a shellfish from which purple dye is obtained</li><li>purple dye</li><li>woad</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurmô</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>worma</i>, Old High German <i>wurmo</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wyrmcynn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) wild marjoram (<i>Origanum majorana</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorold</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorþlēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>worþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorþian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorþliċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorþmynd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorþnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorþsċiepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorþsċiepe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wish</li><li>choice</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wunsk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wunskaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>wunsc</i>, Old Norse <i>ósk</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adopted child</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wunskabarn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wunskabarną</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>óskabarn</i>. Equivalent to <i>wūsc</i> (“wish”) +&lrm; <i>bearn</i> (“child”). Compare also Middle High German <i>wunschkint</i> (“adopted child”) and Old Norse <i>óskasunr</i> (“adopted son”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wusċ</i> (“wish”) + <i>frēa</i> (“lord”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>witod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>witodlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>uton</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>witod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>witodlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>witodlīċe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiċġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>widuwe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>widuwe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiell</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>she-wolf</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wulbi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>like a wolf; wolfish; wolven</li><li>fierce</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(female) wolf</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wulfīn</i> (“of a wolf; wolflike”). Equivalent to <i>wulf</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wulfini</i> (“female wolf”). Equivalent to <i>wulf</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i> (feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wielwan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiliġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weliġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiliġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wīelisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiell</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiellan</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>willan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wille</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of wool; woolen</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) woolen objects</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wullīnaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>wullīn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiellspring</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiellspring</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wielm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wīln</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weoloc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiellspring</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiellspring</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wieltan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wielwan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wynn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the art of weaving</li></ol><p><i>wynde</i> +&lrm; <i>-cræft</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wynn</i> (“joy, delight”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;flǣd</i> (“beauty”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>joyful, happy</li></ol><p>From <i>wynn</i> +&lrm; <i>full</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wynful</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>joyful, joyous</li></ol><p>From <i>wynn</i> (joy, delight) +&lrm; <i>-fæst</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wynn</i> (“joy”) and <i>helm</i> (“helmet”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wynn</i> (“joy, delight”) and <i>here</i> (“army”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>joyless, dreary</li></ol><p>From <i>wynn</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>wynlēas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>delightful, pleasing, agreeable, charming</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wunnjulīk</i> (“joyous”), equivalent to <i>wynn</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>wunnilīh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joy, delight</li><li>the runic character ᚹ (/w/)</li><li>the letter wynn: <b>Ƿ</b>, <b>ƿ</b> (/w/)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wunnju</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wynn</i> (“joy, delight”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wynn</i> (“joy”) +&lrm; <i>beald</i> (“bold”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Old English <i>wynn</i> (“joy”) + <i>friþ</i> (“peace”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wynn</i> (“joy”) +&lrm; <i>stān</i> (“stone”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wynn</i> (“joy, delight”) and <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wynn</i> (“joy”) and <i>siġe</i> (“victory”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>winestra</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pleasant</li><li>joyful</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wunnjusam</i> (“joyful”); equivalent to <i>wynn</i> +&lrm; <i>-sum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>wynsum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>wynsum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>pleasantly, agreeably, graciously</li></ol><p><i>wynsum</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pleasantness, agreeableness, delight</li><li>joyousness, exultation</li><li>devotion</li></ol><p>From <i>wynsum</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wynsumnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make</li><li>to work</li><li>to make a gesture</li></ol><p>A merger of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wurkijan</i> (from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurkijaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wr̥ǵyéti</i>) and Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wirkijan</i> (from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wirkijaną</i>). More at modern <i>work</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wyrċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worker; labourer</li><li>maker</li><li>doer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurkijandz</i>, equivalent to <i>wyrċan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wyrhta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>working, doing</li></ol><p>From <i>wyrċan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wyrċing</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fate, destiny</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the Fates</li><li>event, occurrence</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wurdi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurdiz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wr̥ti-</i>, a verbal abstract from the root <i>&#42;wert-</i> (“to turn”).</p><p>Related to Latin <i>vertere</i>, the Old English verb <i>weorþan</i> (“to grow into, become”), Dutch <i>worden</i>, German <i>werden</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wurd</i>, Old High German <i>wurt</i>, Old Norse <i>urðr</i> (“fate”) (Old Norse <i>Urðr</i> (“one of 3 norns”)).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Wyrd)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) Wyrd, the goddess of fate</li></ol><p>The word <i>wyrd</i> used as a name. From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurdiz</i> (“fate”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>Urðr</i> (“one of the three Norns”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wierdan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decree of fate</li></ol><p>From <i>wyrd</i> +&lrm; <i>stæf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strangle</li><li>(<i>senses 4 and 6</i>) to worry</li><li>alternative form of <i>wierġan</i> (“to curse”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurgijaną</i>, from <i>&#42;wargaz</i> (“outlaw”) (Old English <i>wearg</i>), or from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;werǵʰ-</i> (“bind, squeeze”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wergia</i>, Dutch <i>wurgen</i>, Old High German <i>wurgen</i> (German <i>würgen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wierġen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiergþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(a) work, deed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurhtiz</i> (“act, deed”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;werǵ-</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worker</li><li>maker</li><li>doer</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wurhtijō</i>; By surface analysis, <i>wyrht</i> (“deed”) +&lrm; <i>-a</i> (agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>inflection of</i> <b>wyrhta</b>:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li><i>accusative, genitive, and dative singular</i></li><li><i>nominative and accusative plural</i></li></ol></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female worker</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>wyrht</i> (“deed”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any crawling animal, especially:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>worm</li><li>maggot, grub</li><li>insect</li><li>reptile, especially a snake</li><li>dragon</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurmiz</i> (“worm, serpent, snake”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wr̥mis</i> (“worm”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wirm</i>, Old Saxon <i>wurm</i>, Old High German <i>wurm</i>, Old Norse <i>ormr</i>, and Gothic <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌼𐍃</i> (waurms). The Indo-European root is also the source of Latin <i>vermis</i> (“worm”), Lithuanian <i>varmas</i> (“midge”), Old East Slavic <i>вермие</i> (vermije, “locusts, worms”), and Ancient Greek <i>ῥόμος</i> (rhómos, “earthworm”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wierman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wyrmcynn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reptiles, snakes</li><li>a kind of reptile or snake</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wyrm</i> +&lrm; <i>cynn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snake charmer</li><li>sorcerer</li></ol><p>From <i>wyrm</i> (“worm, dragon”) +&lrm; <i>galere</i> (“enchanter”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a treasure guarded by a dragon</li></ol><p>From <i>wyrm</i> (“worm, dragon”) +&lrm; <i>hord</i> (“hoard”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>snakelike, ophidian</li></ol><p>From <i>wyrm</i> (“snake”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i> (“-like”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>purple; scarlet</li></ol><p>From <i>wyrm</i> +&lrm; <i>rēad</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>virus</li><li>corrupt matter</li><li>pus</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiern</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiernan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorold</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throw, toss</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurpiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wierrest</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wierrest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiers</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wiersa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>vile</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>wiers</i> (“worse”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wrist</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wyrrest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plant</li><li>vegetable</li><li>herb, spice</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) root of a plant</li><li>wort (as in brewing)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wurti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurt-</i>, the oblique stem of <i>&#42;wrōts</i> (whence <i>root</i>).</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wurt</i>, Old High German <i>wurz</i>, Old Norse <i>urt</i>, Gothic <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍄𐍃</i> (waurts).</p><p>From a merger of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wurtiju</i> (“spice”) and Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wurti</i> (“herb”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wurtia</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perfume from a plant</li><li>odor, aroma</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>wyrt</i> (“plant, herb”) +&lrm; <i>brǣþ</i> (“odor, smell”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>type or species of plant</li></ol><p>From <i>wyrt</i> (“plant”) +&lrm; <i>cynn</i> (“kind”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A herbal drink, tea, potion</li><li>antidote</li></ol><p>From <i>wyrt</i> +&lrm; <i>drenċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spice</li></ol><p><i>wyrt</i> +&lrm; <i>ġemang</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vortigern, a semi-legendary warlord or king of the Britons</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>Vortigernus</i>, from Proto-Brythonic <i>&#42;Gworθɨɣern</i>, from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;Uɸortigernos</i>, from <i>&#42;uɸor-</i> (“over”) + <i>&#42;tigernos</i> (“lord, ruler”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a woman who uses herbs for charms</li></ol><p>From <i>wyrt</i> (“herb”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;gælstre</i> (“witch”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to season, spice, perfume</li></ol><p>From <i>wyrt</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>countable</i>) vegetable</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) vegetables, greens</li></ol><p>From <i>wyrt</i> +&lrm; <i>mete</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wyrttruma</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>root</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garden</li></ol><p><i>wyrt</i> (“plant”) +&lrm; <i>tūn</i> (“enclosure”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the root of a plant</li><li>a root, a source, an origin</li><li>the foot of a hill</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>wyrt</i> (“plant, herb”), ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurt-</i> (see wort), and &#42;wala (“root”), compare Old Norse <i>vǫlr</i> (“round stick”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To plant, set</li><li>To root up; uproot; destroy</li></ol><p>Likely converted from the noun <i>wyrtwala</i>, "root, stock;" possibly from <i>wyrt</i> (“plant, herb”) (ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurt-</i>; see <i>wort</i>) + &#42;wala "root" (Compare vǫlr "round stick").</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wyrtwala</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gardener</li></ol><p>From <i>wyrt</i> (“plant”) +&lrm; <i>weard</i> (“ward”). Compare <i>wuduweard</i> (“forester”), which is formed similarly.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>worþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorþ</i></li></ol><p>From <i>weorþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorþliċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) honor</li></ol><p>From <i>wyrþ</i> +&lrm; <i>ġemynd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorþnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weorþnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wūsc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wish <i>(+genitive for something)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wunskijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wunskijaną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>wunsken</i>, Old Norse <i>œskja</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wītega</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wā</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weaken, afflict, oppress</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;waikwijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waikwijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be or stay awake</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wakkjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wakjaną</i>. Doublet of <i>weċċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watch</li></ol><p>From the verb <i>wæċċan</i>, doublet of <i>wacian</i>. See the modern English <i>wake</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;intransitive) to wake up; to arise, come into being</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waknaną</i> (“to awaken”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>vakna</i> ( &gt; Danish <i>vågne</i>), German <i>wachen</i>, Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌰𐌺𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (gawaknan). Compare <i>wacan</i>, <i>wacian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garment, item of clothing</li><li>shallow water</li><li>ford</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wēdiz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wād</i>, Old High German <i>wāt</i>, Old Norse <i>váð</i> (Icelandic <i>voð</i>, Swedish <i>våd</i>).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wadą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wadʰom</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>wat</i>, Old Norse <i>vað</i> (Icelandic <i>vað</i>, Swedish <i>vad</i>); Latin <i>vadum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blue, purple</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>wād</i> (“woad”) +&lrm; <i>-en</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poor, in need</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a poor or needy man, beggar</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wāþlō</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wāþl</i> (“poor, needy”). Cognate with Old High German <i>wādal</i> (“poor, needy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be poor, needy, indigent</li><li>to beg</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>wǣdla</i> (“poor, in need”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap up, clothe</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>show, spectacle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wabara-</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>wabar-</i>. Related to Old English <i>wafian</i> (“to be agitated, astonished, amazed”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theatre, amphitheater</li></ol><p>From <i>wæfer-</i> (“show”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a spectacle, a wondrous sight</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wabarasiuni</i>, equivalent to <i>wæfer-</i> +&lrm; <i>sīen</i> (“vision, countenance”). Cognate with Old High German <i>wabarsiuni</i> (“spectacle, show, play”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place for spectacles, theatre, amphitheatre</li></ol><p>From <i>wæfer-</i> +&lrm; <i>stōw</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to talk or speak foolishly; to waffle</li></ol><p>Origin obscure, but likely from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wablōn</i>, a dissimilated variant of <i>&#42;bablōn</i> (“to babble”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bablōną</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;babalōną</i>. See babble. For change of <i>b</i> to <i>w</i>, compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;baswǭ</i> and <i>&#42;waswǭ</i> (“aunt”); Middle High German <i>behaben</i> and <i>wehaben</i> (“to behave”), a phenomenon of known occurrence in Proto-West Germanic descendants.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wavering</li><li>restless</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wæps</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wæfþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sight, show, spectacle</li></ol><p>From <i>wafian</i> +&lrm; <i>-þ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) wave</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) water (as an open place / means of transportation instead of a substance)</li><li>a wall, usually of a building</li><li>alternative form of <i>wāg</i></li><li>a weight</li><li>a balance or scales</li><li>alternative form of <i>weġ</i> (“a way; a road”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wāg</i> (“wave”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>vágr</i> (“sea”), Swedish <i>våg</i> (“wave”), German <i>Woge</i> (“wave”).</p><p>Possibly from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wajjuz</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wāgu</i> (“scales; weight”).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wegaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deceive, delude</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;waigijan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cup, flagon</li><li>alternative form of <i>wǣġ</i></li><li>a balance, scales</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;waigī</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>veig</i> (“strong drink; draught”).</p><p>From the same root as <i>wǣġ</i>&nbsp;f (“weight”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wæġn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) sea, ocean</li></ol><p><i>wǣġ</i> +&lrm; <i>holm</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wagon, carriage</li><li>the Plough constellation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wagnaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wein</i>, Old Saxon <i>wagan</i>, Old High German <i>wagan</i>, and Old Norse <i>vagn</i>. The Indo-European root is also the source of <i>way</i> and <i>weigh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charioteer, wagoneer</li></ol><p>From <i>wæġn</i> (“wagon”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wainwright, cartwright</li></ol><p><i>wæġn</i> (“wagon”) +&lrm; <i>wyrhta</i> (“worker, maker”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slaughter, carnage</li><li>the slain</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) a slain person, corpse</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;walaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welh₃-</i> (“to hit, to strike”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wal</i>, Old High German <i>wal</i>, and Old Norse <i>valr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) deadly, mortal</li></ol><p>From <i>wæl</i> (“carnage”) +&lrm; <i>blēat</i> (“wretched”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a raven</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>wæl</i> (“slaughter, the slain”) +&lrm; <i>ċēosan</i> (“to choose”), literally 'chooser of the slain.' Compare Old English <i>wælcyrġe</i> and Old Norse <i>valkyrja</i>, which are constructed similarly.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wælcyrġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a malevolent female demon</li><li>a sorceress, a female witch</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;walakuzjǭ</i>, either by descent or through Old Norse <i>valkyrja</i>. Equivalent to <i>wæl</i> +&lrm; <i>cyre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wælcyrġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wælcyrġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) fill of slaughter or carnage</li></ol><p><i>wæl</i> +&lrm; <i>fyllo</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) greedy of killing</li></ol><p>From <i>wæl</i> (“slaughter”) +&lrm; <i>ġīfre</i> (“greedy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cruel</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>wælhrēow</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>cruelly</li></ol><p>From <i>wælhrēow</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cruelty</li></ol><p>From <i>wælhrēow</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wīelisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wælcyrġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wille</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wielm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) deadly hatred, violence</li></ol><p><i>wæl</i> +&lrm; <i>nīþ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spoils of war, spoils taken from the slain</li></ol><p>From <i>wæl</i> (“slaughter, slain”) +&lrm; <i>rēaf</i> (“plunder, spoils”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>valrauf</i> (“spoils”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) fierce in slaughter</li></ol><p>From <i>wæl</i> +&lrm; <i>hrēoh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a deadly shaft (of a weapon)</li></ol><p><i>wæl</i> (“slaughter”) +&lrm; <i>sceaft</i> (“shaft”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>wælsliht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) slaughter in battle, slaughter, carnage</li></ol><p>From <i>wæl</i> (“slaughter”) +&lrm; <i>sliht</i> (“killing”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>battlefield</li></ol><p><i>wæl</i> +&lrm; <i>stōw</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Unclear&#59; but perhaps refers to a sinew or other part of the thigh.</li></ol><p>Possibly cognate with Old High German <i>walza</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wieltan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wægn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wange</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Wendle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wenn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>Wintanċeaster</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wǣpn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a vote made by touching one's weapon; a council where such votes are taken</li><li>wapentake; a district governed by such a council, generally under Scandinavian control</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Norse <i>vápnatak</i>, from <i>vápn</i> (“weapon”) + <i>tak</i> (“taking”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wǣpnhūs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) hermaphrodite</li></ol><p>From <i>wǣpen</i> (“penis”, literally “weapon”) + <i>wīf</i> (“woman”) + <i>-estre</i> (“-ster”), literally "phallused woman"</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weapon</li><li>penis</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wāpn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wēpną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>male</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a male or a man</li></ol><p>From <i>wǣpn</i> (“weapon; penis”) +&lrm; <i>-ed</i> (adjective-forming suffix indicating possession of the suffixed word). Compare modern <i>weaponed</i> in the sense of "having a weapon".</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male child, a boy</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>wǣpned</i> (“male”) +&lrm; <i>ċild</i> (“child”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mankind (men as a group, as opposed to womankind)</li><li>the male gender</li></ol><p><i>wǣpned</i> +&lrm; <i>cynn</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the male side or line of a family</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wǣpned</i> +&lrm; <i>hand</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male half or side (of a family)</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>wǣpned</i> (“male”) +&lrm; <i>healf</i> (“half”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male</li><li>a man</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>wǣpned</i> (“male,” derived from the noun <i>wǣpn</i> “weapon; penis”) + <i>mann</i> (“person”). Compare <i>wīfmann</i> and Icelandic <i>karlmaður</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armory, arsenal (a stock of weapons or place where weapons are kept)</li></ol><p>From <i>wǣpn</i> (“weapon”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arm</li></ol><p>From <i>wǣpn</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>weaponless, unarmed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wēpnalausaz</i>, equivalent to <i>wǣpn</i> (“weapon”) +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wasp</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wapsu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cautious, on guard, watching out for something <i>(+genitive)</i></li><li>true, correct</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>truth, faith</li><li>fidelity, friendship</li><li>agreement, promise</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;war</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>war</i>, Old High German <i>war</i>, Old Norse <i>varr</i>, Gothic <i>𐍅𐌰𐍂𐍃</i> (wars).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wār</i> (“true”).</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Germanic cognates include Old High German <i>wār</i>, <i>wāra</i> (German <i>wahr</i>), Old Saxon <i>wār</i> (Low German <i>war</i>), Old Dutch <i>wār</i> (Dutch <i>waar</i>), Old Norse <i>várar</i>&nbsp;pl. The Indo-European root is also the source of Latin <i>vērus</i>, Old Irish <i>fír</i> (Irish <i>fíor</i>), Welsh <i>cywir</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>вѣра</i> (věra), Russian <i>ве́ра</i> (véra).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wāru</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wērō</i> (“truth”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wār</i> (“truth”), Old High German <i>wāra</i> (“truth”), Latin <i>vērus</i> (“true”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wær</i> (“cautious”) and <i>beald</i> (“bold, brave”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wær</i> (“cautious”) and <i>burg</i> (“city, town”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Wærburg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pain; ache</li><li>suffering; anguish; grief</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;warki</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;warkiz</i> (“ache, pain”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;werǵ-</i>, <i>&#42;wreǵ-</i> (“to work, act”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>verkr</i> (“pain”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be in pain, hurt</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;warkijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;warkijaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>wærċ</i> (“pain”) +&lrm; <i>-an</i> (infinitive suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Wærfriþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wær</i> (“cautious”) and <i>friþ</i> (“peace”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Varangian</li></ol><p>From <i>wǣr</i> (“fidelity, loyalty”) +&lrm; Old Norse <i>gangi</i> (“companion”). Cognate with Norwegian Nynorsk <i>væring</i> and Faroese <i>væringi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wierġþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wierġþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Werhām</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wearrihtnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wearrihtnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Warwick</li></ol><p>From <i>wer</i> (“weir”) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“of the”, used with masculine nouns) +&lrm; <i>wīċ</i> (“dwelling place, village”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Warwickshire</li></ol><p>From <i>Wǣringwīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>sċīr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wend, turn</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps derived from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wratōn</i> (“to wander”). Alternatively, perhaps from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;was-</i> (“to wind, twist, wrap”), related to Old Norse <i>vasast</i> (“to involve oneself, get involved”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>warily, cautiously</li></ol><p><i>wær</i> (“cautious”) +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>traitor, oath-breaker, liar</li></ol><p>From <i>wǣr</i> (“promise, agreement”) +&lrm; <i>loga</i> (“deceiver”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wierman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wierming</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Wermund, legendary king of the Angles</li></ol><p>From <i>wær</i> (“cautious”) and <i>mund</i> (“protector”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Wærmund</li></ol><p>From <i>Wærmund</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>wær</i> (“cautious, on guard”) + <i>nōþ</i> (“daring”).</p><p>From <i>wǣr</i> (“true, correct”) + <i>nōþ</i> (“daring”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cautious in speech, careful in what one says</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wær</i> +&lrm; <i>sagol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caution, prudence, circumspection</li></ol><p><i>wær</i> (“cautious”) +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i> (“-ship”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wærsċipe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cautious of speech, prudent in speech, careful of one's words</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wær</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrde</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wash, washing</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wasku</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waskō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wascan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wash-house</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wæsċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ern</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>washhouse, laundry</li></ol><p>From <i>wasċan</i> (“to wash”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wæps</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>west</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wēsten</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a coverlet</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;wæst</i>, <i>&#42;wæste</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wasti</i>, <i>&#42;wastiju</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wastiz</i>, <i>&#42;wastijō</i> +&lrm; <i>-ling</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wæstling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fruit</li><li>growth; increase</li><li>product; result</li><li>benefit</li><li>stature, height</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wastm</i>, from the root of <i>weaxan</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>wastum</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fruitful, fertile, productive</li></ol><p>From <i>wæstm</i> +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fertility; fruitfulness, productivity</li></ol><p>From <i>wæstmbǣre</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wæstmbǣru</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fertility</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wæstmbǣre</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring forth fruit</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wæstm</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfruitful, fruitless</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wæstm</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fruitful</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wæstm</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>usury, interest</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wæstm</i> +&lrm; <i>sċeatt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wet</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a drink, a liquid</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wāt</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wēt</i>, Old Norse <i>vátr</i>; ultimately related to English <i>water</i>. Also akin to Old Armenian <i>գետ</i> (get, “river”). Compare Luwian (wida-, “wet”) (Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wed-o-</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moisture, liquid, wet</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wet, moisten</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to become wet, emit moisture</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wātijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wētijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;watar</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;watōr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wédōr</i>, collective of <i>&#42;wódr̥</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>edema, dropsy</li></ol><p>From <i>wæter</i> (“water”) +&lrm; <i>ādl</i> (“disease”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water bearer</li></ol><p>From <i>wæter</i> (“water”) +&lrm; <i>beran</i> (“to bear”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dropsy</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water spider</li></ol><p>From <i>wæter</i> (“water”) +&lrm; <i>bucca</i> (“he-goat”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a towel</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>wæter</i> (“water”) +&lrm; <i>clāþ</i> (“cloth”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a waterflood; a flooding of water, inundation, deluge</li></ol><p>From <i>wæter</i> +&lrm; <i>flōd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waterfall</li></ol><p><i>wæter</i> +&lrm; <i>ġefeall</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a watercourse, an aqueduct</li></ol><p><i>wæter</i> (“water”) +&lrm; <i>ġelǣt</i> (“leat”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a "water-crab," an aquatic crab?</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>wæter</i> (“water”) +&lrm; <i>hæfern</i> (“crab”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to water, supply water to, give drink to</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;watarōn</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;watar</i>, equivalent to <i>wæter</i> + <i>-ian</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wetria</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>watery</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;watarīg</i>. Equivalent to <i>wæter</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without water, dry; waterless</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;watōrlausaz</i>, equivalent to <i>wæter</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>wazzarlōs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aquatic; of water</li></ol><p>From <i>wæter</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>wazzarlīh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a watersnake</li></ol><p>From <i>wæter</i> +&lrm; <i>nǣdre</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>wazzarnatra</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a plumb line, plummet</li><li>a level, specifically, a water level (<i>instrument</i>)</li><li>a carpenter's square</li></ol><p>From <i>wæter</i> +&lrm; <i>pund</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>body of water</li></ol><p><i>wæter</i> (“water”) +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i> (“-ship”). Compare Old Saxon <i>watriscapum</i> (“watercourse, watersource”) and Middle Dutch <i>waterschap</i> (“watercourse, waterway”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wætersċipe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>edematous, dropsical</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;watarseuk</i>. Equivalent to <i>wæter</i> (“water”) +&lrm; <i>sēoc</i> (“sick”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>edema</li></ol><p>From <i>wæter</i> +&lrm; <i>sēocnes</i> or <i>wætersēoc</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>, literally 'water sickness'.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wætersēocnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bucket</li></ol><p>From <i>wæter</i> (“water”) +&lrm; <i>stoppa</i> (“bucket”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a stream of water; waterstream</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;watarstraum</i>, equivalent to <i>wæter</i> +&lrm; <i>strēam</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watering, providing with water</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wæterian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ārȳþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wave of water</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>wæter</i> +&lrm; <i>ȳþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water pipe</li></ol><p>From <i>wæter</i> (“water”) +&lrm; <i>þrūh</i> (“pipe”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>St. Albans (cathedral city in Hertfordshire, England)</li></ol><p>The second element is from <i>ceaster</i> (“city”),</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wæterian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wæteriġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weax</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weaxcandel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hunt, pursue, chase</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;waiþijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waiþijaną</i> (“to hunt, pursue, graze, feed”). Cognate with Dutch <i>weiden</i> (“to graze”) and Icelandic <i>veiða</i> (“to hunt, fish”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weþer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>weġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wēn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wēnan</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wēnijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>wēna</i>, Old Saxon <i>wānian</i>, Old High German <i>wānen</i> (German <i>wähnen</i>), Old Norse <i>væna</i>, Gothic <i>𐍅𐌴𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wēnjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wœ̄ndun</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wœ̄nu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wēpan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wēriġ</i></li></ol><p>See <i>wēriġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wēstan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wēste</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wēsten</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wēstiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Xerxes, emperor of Persia</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ξέρξης</i> (Xérxēs), from Old Persian <i>𐎧𐏁𐎹𐎠𐎼𐏁𐎠</i> (x-š-y-a-r-š-a /⁠Xšayāršā⁠/, “ruler among kings”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Xerxes, the King of Persia from 485 <small>BCE</small> to 465 <small>BCE</small></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Xerxes</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Ξέρξης</i> (Xérxēs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toad; poisonous frog</li><li>alternative form of <i>hice</i> (a type of bird)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ūkijā</i>, diminutive of <i>&#42;ūkā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bad</li><li>bad in a moral sense, evil</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something bad or bad things collectively; harm, misfortune</li><li>evil</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ubil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ubilaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>yfelcweþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to curse</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>yfel</i> +&lrm; <i>cweþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>badly</li><li>evilly, wickedly</li></ol><p><i>yfel</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrong, mistreat, injure</li><li>to get bad or worsen</li><li>to become ill or sick (impersonal, with dative or accusative of person)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>yfel</i> (“bad, misfortune, evil”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evil, wickedness, badness</li><li>misfortune</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>yfel</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>yfelnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blaspheme; to reproach, insult, calumniate</li></ol><p>From <i>yfel</i> +&lrm; <i>sacian</i>. Alternatively, a folk etymology of &#42;eofolsacian; compare yfelsian. Further evidence of this interpretation exists in the hapax eofulsæc.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calumny, vituperation</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>yfelsacian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>yfelsung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(West Saxon)</i> alternative form of <i>eofolsian</i></li></ol><p>Folk etymology of eofolsian, see there for more. By surface analysis <i>yfel</i> +&lrm; <i>-sian</i>, but this derivation does not adequately explain the meaning. Compare yfelsacian, which may have undergone a similar reanalysis.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(West Saxon)</i> alternative form of <i>eofolsung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>malicious, malevolent</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ubilawilljandī</i>. Cognate with German <i>übelwollend</i>. Equivalent to <i>yfel</i> (“bad”) + <i>willende</i> (“wishing”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>malevolence, malice</li></ol><p><i>yfelwillende</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uppermost, highest</li><li>in the highest degree or position</li></ol><p>Per Köbler, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ubamaz</i> + superlative ending <i>-est</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>upper, higher</li><li>later, after, following</li></ol><p>Compare Old Frisian <i>uvera</i>, Old High German <i>ubarōr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>yfele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ilca</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ieldu</i></li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aldiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ielda bearn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ieldan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ielde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ieldu</i></li></ol></html>:/ˈyldrɑ/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ieldra</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ielf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ielfete</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ielfetu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>elp</i></li></ol><p>Probably a clipping of <i>ylpend</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>elpendbān</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>elpend</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>around (surrounding or near)(+accusative)</li><li>about (concerning)(+accusative)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;umbi</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>umbi</i> (Dutch <i>om</i>), Old High German <i>umbi</i> (German <i>um</i>), Old Norse <i>um</i> (Swedish <i>om</i>).</p><p>The Indo-European root is also the source of Latin <i>ambi-</i>, Ancient Greek <i>ἀμφί</i> (amphí), Old Irish <i>imb</i>, Sanskrit <i>अभि</i> (abhí, “towards, over, upon”), Old Persian <i>𐎠𐎲𐎡𐎹</i> (abiy, “towards, against, upon”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>around</li><li>about, regarding</li><li>near, by</li><li>restraining, lessening</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;umbi</i> (“around”). Akin to Old English <i>ymb</i>, <i>ymbe</i> (“around, about”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind about or around</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;umbibindan</i>. Equivalent to <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>bindan</i>. More at <i>um-</i>, <i>umbe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to restrain, curb</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ymb-</i> (“around”) +&lrm; <i>bǣtan</i> (to restrain)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to circumcise</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċeorfan</i>. See <i>um-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circumcision</li></ol><p>From <i>ymbċeorfan</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ymbċeorfnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to revolve around</li><li>to turn one's self around</li><li>to turn away, avert</li><li>to turn over, move, stir</li><li>to change</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;umbikaʀʀjan</i>, <i>&#42;umbikaiʀwijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;umbikazjaną</i>, <i>&#42;umbikaizwijaną</i>, equivalent to <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċierran</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>umbekêra</i>, Old High German <i>umbikēren</i> (whence German <i>umkehren</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circumcision</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>cyrf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a going from one point to another</li><li>a turning about or over; moving, stirring</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>ċyrr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ymbċierran</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>around, about</li><li>near, along</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;umbi</i> (“around, near”), akin to Old High German <i>umbi</i> (“around”), Latin <i>ambi</i> (“both, around”), Ancient Greek <i>ἀμφί</i> (amphí, “on both sides, around”), Old Irish <i>imb</i>, Old English <i>bī</i> (“near, by”). More at by.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ymb-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dwell around</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>eardian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dwelling around</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>eardiende</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ymbgang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ymbhwyrft</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ymbþenċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surround</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;umbifaran</i>. Equivalent to <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>faran</i>. See <i>um-</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>umbifaran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grasp, clasp</li><li>to encompass, envelope, surround; beset</li><li>to conceive, comprehend; understand</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>fōn</i>. See <i>um-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a circuit, going round; faring round</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>fær</i>. More at <i>um-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a going around, circuit</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>færeld</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go around, about, or through</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;umbigān</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;umbigāną</i>, equivalent to <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>gān</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>umbigān</i>, Old High German <i>umbigān</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circumnavigation, orbit</li><li>circuit (path around a space)</li><li>circumference</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;umbigang</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;umbigangaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>gang</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go around, about, or through</li><li>to surround, encompass</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;umbiganganą</i>. Equivalent to <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>gangan</i>. See <i>um-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ymbgang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gird about or around, surround, encompass; beset</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>gyrdan</i>. See <i>um-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surround, encompass</li><li>to contain</li><li>to detain</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>habban</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to encompass; hold around</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>healdan</i>. See <i>um-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>care, solicitude, anxiety</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> (“around, about”) +&lrm; <i>hoga</i> (“care, consideration”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be solicitous</li><li>to exercise the mind</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>hogian</i> (to think about)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang around with clothing, ornament, drape, clothe, deck; behand</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>hōn</i>. See <i>um-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surround, encompass</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>hrinġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn around, to revolve</li><li>to turn something around</li><li>to go about, over, or through something; to revolve around something</li><li>to encompass something</li><li>to go about one's business</li><li>to attend to something, to cultivate something</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>hweorfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>globe</li><li>turn, regular course</li><li>a circular course, circuit, orbit</li><li>surrounding space, extent</li><li>a bend or turn</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>hwyrft</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think about, consider</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>hyċġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anxious, careful, solicitous</li><li>causing anxiety</li></ol><p>From <i>ymbhyċġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>care, anxiety, solicitude</li></ol><p><i>ymbhygdig</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie round, surround, encompass; beset; beleaguer</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>liċġan</i>. See <i>um-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to circumnavigate</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>līþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look around</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>lōcian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circle</li><li>circuit</li><li>circumference</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ember (making a circuit of the year or the seasons)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ember day</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ymbren</i> +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the course of a moving body</li><li>course of time, revolution, period</li></ol><p><i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>ryne</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look around, behold</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine around, surround with brightness</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċīnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go round, revolve</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċrīþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surround</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>sellan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to behold, look, look around</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set or put round, surround; beset</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>settan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>umbisezzen</i>. See <i>um-</i>, <i>umb</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit or be around, meet</li><li>to beset</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>sittan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>umbisizzen</i>. See <i>um-</i>, <i>umb</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) neighbouring people, those living nearby</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> (“around-, about-”) +&lrm; <i>sittende</i> (“sitting”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circumcision</li></ol><p>From <i>ymbsniden</i>, past participle of <i>ymbsnīþan</i> (“to circumcise”), + the suffix <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ymbsnidennes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to circumcise; cut around</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>snīþan</i>. See <i>um-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand around</li><li>to surround</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>standan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bystander</li></ol><p>From <i>ymbstandan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i> or <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>standend</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circumstance</li></ol><p>From <i>ymbstanden</i> (“surrounded”) + <i>-nes</i> (“-ness”). Perhaps originally a calque of Latin <i>circumstantia</i>. Compare West Frisian <i>omstannichhed</i>, Dutch <i>omstandigheid</i>, German <i>Umstand</i>, and Swedish <i>omständighet</i>, which are formed similarly.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>about, around, without (+ dative or accusative)</li></ol><p><i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>būtan</i> (“without, outside of”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn around</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> (“around”) +&lrm; <i>wendan</i> (“to turn”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surround</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>weorpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>observer, spectator</li></ol><p>From <i>ymbwlātian</i> (“to contemplate, observe”) +&lrm; <i>-end</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contemplate</li><li>to look about, observe</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>wlātian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap around, clothe</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ymb-</i> (“around”) +&lrm; <i>wǣfan</i> (to wrap)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consider; bethink; think about or around something</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>þenċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throng or crowd around; thring around, surround</li></ol><p>From <i>ymb-</i> +&lrm; <i>þringan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>umbidringan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hymn</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>hymnus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ὕμνος</i> (húmnos).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hymnal; hymnbook; book of hymns</li></ol><p>From <i>ymen</i> (“hymn”) +&lrm; <i>bōc</i> (“book”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ymbgang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>onion</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ūniju</i>, <i>&#42;unnjā</i> (“onion”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inch</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;unciju</i> (“ounce; unit of money”), borrowed from Latin <i>uncia</i> (“Roman inch, various similar units”). Doublet of <i>yntse</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>yntse</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ynnelēac</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>onion</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;unnjalauk</i>. Equivalent to <i>ȳn</i> (“onion”) +&lrm; <i>lēac</i> (“leek”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ounce</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;unciju</i> (“ounce; unit of money”), borrowed from Latin <i>uncia</i>. Doublet of <i>ynċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring up, bring forth</li><li>to disclose, reveal, declare, manifest</li><li>to come forth, proceed, be disclosed</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uppijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uppijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>up</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>yppa</i> (“to lift, raise”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>known</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>platform, dais, upland</li></ol><p>Probably an umlaut form of <i>up</i>. Compare Gothic <i>𐌹𐌿𐍀</i> (iup)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manifestation</li><li>what mounts up</li></ol><p>From <i>yppan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the runic character ᚣ (/y(ː)/)</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from Old Norse <i>ýr</i> (“yew, a bow, the runic letter ᛦ”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Īras</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>yrþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ierfe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ierfenuma</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ierġþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Īrland</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ierman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eormen-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>earming</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>iermþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>iermþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>iermþu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>irnan</i></li></ol><p>Metathesised form of <i>rinnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēored</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ierre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>iersian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plowing</li><li>tilling</li><li>a crop</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ierþen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arable land</li></ol><p>From <i>yrþ</i> (“plowing, tilling”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ierþling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ierþling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ierþling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>is</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ysle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a spark, cinder, ash, ember</li></ol><p>Cognate with Middle High German <i>usele, üsele</i> (“ashes”) and Icelandic <i>usli</i> (“conflagration, field of burning embers”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sending forth sparks, glowing</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ysle</i> (“spark, ember”, via an inferred verb <i>&#42;yslen</i>) +&lrm; <i>-ende</i> (“-ing”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hyssop</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>hȳsōpum</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ὕσσωπος</i> (hússōpos), ultimately from Semitic.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ysope</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>storm, tempest, whirlwind, hurricane</li><li>storm surge, storm tide, rough seas, rough water</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;unsti</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to storm, be stormy, rage</li></ol><p>From <i>ȳst</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stormy</li><li>of or relating to a storm</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ȳst</i> (“storm”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put out a person from a place, expel, banish</li><li>to put out a thing from one's possession, alienate, give away</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ūtijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ūtijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>ūt</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>. Cognate with Icelandic <i>ýta</i> (“to push”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ȳtera</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expeller</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ūtera</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ūtera</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) otter; of or relating to otters</li><li>made of an otter's fur or pelt: otterskin</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;utrīnaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>otor</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īewan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ȳþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ȳþian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ȳþlid</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ȳþmearh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ȳþmere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ȳþung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wave (of water)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unþī</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;unt-</i>. Germanic cognates include Old Saxon <i>ūthia</i>, Old High German <i>undea</i>, Old Norse <i>unnr</i>, <i>uðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>īeþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a ship's side</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ȳþ</i> (“wave”) +&lrm; <i>bord</i> (“shield”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēaþdǣde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ȳþiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a course on the waves</li><li>the sea</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ȳþ</i> (“wave”) +&lrm; <i>faru</i> (“passage”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēaþfynde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tossing of the waves at sea</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ȳþ</i> +&lrm; <i>ġebland</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strife of waves, billow</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ȳþ</i> +&lrm; <i>ġewinn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ȳþian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ȳþung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ȳþung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) ship</li></ol><p>Kenning of <i>ȳþ</i> (“wave”) +&lrm; <i>henġest</i> (“steed”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vessel</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ȳþ</i> (“wave”) +&lrm; <i>hof</i> (“hall”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;literal&#32;or&#32;figurative) to overflow, be filled</li><li>(<i>literal&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) to move in waves, toss, roll; to wave, fluctuate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ȳþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fluctuating</li><li>stormy</li><li>(of the sea) rough</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ȳþ</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) voyage, crossing</li></ol><p>From <i>ȳþ</i> (“wave”) +&lrm; <i>lād</i> (“course”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>what is left by the waves; detritus on the shore</li></ol><p>From <i>ȳþ</i> +&lrm; <i>lāf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ship</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ȳþ</i> +&lrm; <i>lid</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) ship</li></ol><p>Kenning of <i>ȳþ</i> (“wave”) +&lrm; <i>lida</i> (“sailor”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ship</li></ol><p>Kenning of <i>ȳþ</i> (“wave”) +&lrm; <i>mearh</i> (“steed”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the billowy main, tossing sea</li></ol><p>Kenning of <i>ȳþ</i> (“wave”) +&lrm; <i>mere</i> (“sea”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overflowing, inundation</li><li>wavelike movement, fluctuation</li><li>agitation</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ȳþian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wander about on the waves</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ȳþ</i> +&lrm; <i>wōrian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wave-wandering</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ȳþ</i> +&lrm; <i>wōriende</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ȳþwōriende</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ealdwine</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Æþelrīc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ēadmund</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ēadwine</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Analyzable as <i>agan</i> (“to possess, own”) + <i>beald</i> (“bold, brave”). Latinized as <i>Einboldus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Analyzable as <i>agan</i> (“to possess, own”) + <i>wulf</i>, similar to <i>Ægenbeald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Egyptisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aylesbury (a town in Buckinghamshire, England)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Egypt (a country and ancient kingdom in North Africa and West Asia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ægypta</i> (“of the Egyptians”) + <i>land</i> (“land”). Literally "land of the Egyptians."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Egyptians</li><li>Egypt (a country in North Africa and West Asia)</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>Aegyptus</i> (“Egypt”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Egyptian</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) an Egyptian</li></ol><p>From <i>Ægypte</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ealdrǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ælfflǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li><li>St. Ælfflæd of Whitby</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Albiflādi</i>, from <i>&#42;albi</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;flādi</i> (“beauty”). Equivalent to <i>ælf</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;flǣd</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>Albofledis</i> via Frankish.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Albigaiʀ</i>, from <i>&#42;albi</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;gaiʀ</i> (“spear”). Equivalent to <i>ælf</i> +&lrm; <i>gār</i>. Cognate with Old Dutch <i>Alfgēr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Albigaut</i>, from <i>&#42;albi</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;Gaut</i> (“a Geat”). Equivalent to <i>ælf</i> +&lrm; <i>Ġēat</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Albgōz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ælfġiefu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li><li>one of several notable individuals named Ælfgifu</li></ol><p><i>ælf</i> +&lrm; <i>ġiefu</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ælfġiefu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Albigunþi</i>, from <i>&#42;albi</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;gunþi</i> (“battle”). Equivalent to <i>ælf</i> +&lrm; <i>gūþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Alpigund</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name &lsqb;933 <small>CE</small>&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Albihauh</i>, from <i>&#42;albi</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hauh</i> (“high”). Equivalent to <i>ælf</i> +&lrm; <i>hēah</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Alphoh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Albihelm</i>, from <i>&#42;albi</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;helm</i> (“helmet”). Equivalent to <i>ælf</i> +&lrm; <i>helm</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Alfhelm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Albiharjaz</i>, equivalent to <i>ælf</i> (“elf”) and <i>here</i> (“army”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Albihildi</i>, from <i>&#42;albi</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hildi</i> (“battle”). Equivalent to <i>ælf</i> +&lrm; <i>hild</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Alphilt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, variant of Ælfflǣd</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Albimār</i>, from <i>&#42;albi</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;mār</i> (“famous”). Equivalent to <i>ælf</i> +&lrm; <i>mǣre</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Albmār</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Albinanþ</i>, from <i>&#42;albi</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;nanþ</i> (“bold, daring”). Equivalent to <i>ælf</i> +&lrm; <i>nōþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Albnand</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ælfrǣd</i></li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ælf</i> +&lrm; <i>rēd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Ælfric of Eynsham (<i>c.</i>&nbsp;955–<i>c.</i>&nbsp;1010), Benedictine abbot, student of Æthelwold of Winchester, and prolific writer in Old English of hagiography, homily, Biblical commentary, and other genres</li><li>Ælfric of Abingdon (died 1005), Archbishop of Canterbury 995–1005</li><li>Ælfric Bata (<abbr title=floruit><i>fl.</i></abbr> 1005), disciple of Ælfric of Eynsham and monk</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Albirīk</i>, from <i>&#42;albi</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;rīk</i> (“king, ruler”). Equivalent to <i>ælf</i> +&lrm; <i>rīċ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Elbrīh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Albirūnu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Albirūnō</i>, from <i>&#42;albiz</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;rūnō</i> (“secret, mystery”). Equivalent to <i>ælf</i> +&lrm; <i>rūn</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Albrun</i>, Old Norse <i>Alfrún</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Albirād</i>, from <i>&#42;albi</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;rād</i> (“advice, counsel”). Equivalent to <i>ælf</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣd</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Albrāt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ælf</i> (“elf”) and <i>siġe</i> (“victory”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Ælfstan (bishop of London); 10th Century Bishop of London.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Albistain</i>, from <i>&#42;albi</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;stain</i> (“stone”). Equivalent to <i>ælf</i> +&lrm; <i>stān</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Albstein</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Albiswinþ</i>, from <i>&#42;albi</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;swinþ</i> (“strong”). Equivalent to <i>ælf</i> +&lrm; <i>swiþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Alpsuind</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Albiwaru</i>, from <i>&#42;albi</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;waru</i> (“care, watch”). Equivalent to <i>ælf</i> +&lrm; <i>waru</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Albuera</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Ælfweard of Wessex; brother of King Æþelstan of England and disputed King of Wessex.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Albiward</i>, from <i>&#42;albi</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;ward</i> (“guard, guardian”). Equivalent to <i>ælf</i> +&lrm; <i>weard</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Albwart</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Albiwīg</i>, from <i>&#42;albi</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wīg</i> (“battle”). Equivalent to <i>ælf</i> +&lrm; <i>wīġ</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>Albouichus</i> (via Frankish).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Albiwini</i>, from <i>&#42;albi</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wini</i> (“friend”). Equivalent to <i>ælf</i> +&lrm; <i>wine</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Albouin</i>, Italian <i>Alboino</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ælf</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>wynn</i> (“joy”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ælf</i> (“elf”) +&lrm; <i>þrȳþ</i> (“strength”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ælle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Æþelwacer</i> (<i>Domesday Book</i> spelling)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Engla land</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Engle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Englisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Eardwulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Æschere (The advisor of the Danish king Hrothgar)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Askahari</i>, from <i>&#42;ask</i> (“spear”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hari</i> (“army”). Equivalent to <i>æsċ</i> +&lrm; <i>here</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ascheri</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Askamundu</i>, from <i>&#42;ask</i> (“spear”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;mundu</i> (“protector”). Equivalent to <i>æsċ</i> +&lrm; <i>mund</i>. Compare placename <i>Ascmundesheim</i> (via Frankish).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Æscwine of Wessex; an Anglo-Saxon King of Wessex.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Askawini</i>, from <i>&#42;ask</i> (“spear”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wini</i> (“friend”). Equivalent to <i>æsċ</i> +&lrm; <i>wine</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Ascuin</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Askawulf</i>, from <i>&#42;ask</i> (“spear”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wulf</i> (“wolf”). Equivalent to <i>æsċ</i> +&lrm; <i>wulf</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Asculf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Æþelþrȳþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attila the Hun</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Attilō</i> (compare German <i>Etzel</i>). Further origin uncertain; see English <i>Attila</i> for more.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Etna</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Æþelflǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Æþelhere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, variant of Æþelred or Æþelrǣd, equivalent to English Ethelred, Aethelred, or Æthelred</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Æþelrīc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Æþelstān</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Æþelwine</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Æþelþrȳþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Æthelbald, King of Wessex</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalabalþ</i>, from <i>&#42;aþal</i> (“noble”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;balþ</i> (“bold, brave”). Equivalent to <i>æþele</i> +&lrm; <i>beald</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Adalbald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Æþelbald</li></ol><p>From <i>Æþelbald</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Æthelberht of Kent</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalaberht</i>. Equivalent to <i>æþele</i> +&lrm; <i>beorht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Æþelbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Æþelbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Æþelbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalaburg</i>. Equivalent to <i>æþele</i> (“noble”) +&lrm; <i>burg</i> (“fortification”). Cognate with Old High German <i>Adalburg</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Æþelburg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Æþelbeorht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Æþelfriþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalaflādi</i>, from <i>&#42;aþal</i> (“nobility”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;flādi</i> (“cleanliness, beauty”). Equivalent to <i>æþele</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;flǣd</i>. Cognate with Middle High German <i>Adalvlat</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalafriþu</i>, from <i>&#42;aþal</i> (“noble”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;friþu</i> (“peace”). Equivalent to <i>æþele</i> +&lrm; <i>friþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Adalfrid</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalagaiʀ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalagaizaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>æþele</i> (“noble”) +&lrm; <i>gār</i> (“spear”). Cognate with Old High German <i>Adalger</i>, Latin <i>Adalgarius</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>æþele</i> (“noble”) and <i>ġifu</i> (“present, gift”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalahard</i>, from <i>&#42;aþal</i> (“noble”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hard</i> (“might, authority”). Equivalent to <i>æþele</i> +&lrm; <i>heard</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Adalhart</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalahelm</i>, from <i>&#42;aþal</i> (“nobility”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;helm</i> (“helmet”). Equivalent to <i>æþele</i> +&lrm; <i>helm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalaheri</i>, from <i>&#42;aþal</i> (“noble”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hari</i> (“army”). Equivalent to <i>æþele</i> +&lrm; <i>here</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Adelher</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalahildi</i>, from <i>&#42;aþal</i> (“noble”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;hildi</i> (“battle”). Equivalent to <i>æþele</i> +&lrm; <i>hild</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Adalhilt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Athelney (a village in Somerset, England)</li></ol><p>From <i>æþelinga</i> (“genitive plural of <i>æþeling</i> (“prince, noble”)”) +&lrm; <i>īeġ</i> (“island”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>æþele</i> (“noble”) and <i>mōd</i> (“mind, heart, spirit”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>æþele</i> (“noble”) and <i>mund</i> (“protector”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalamār</i>. Equivalent to <i>æþele</i> +&lrm; <i>mǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>æþele</i> (“noble”) and <i>nōþ</i> (“daring”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, alternative form of <i>Æþelrǣd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalarīk</i>, from <i>&#42;aþal</i> (“noble”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;rīk</i> (“king”). Equivalent to <i>æþele</i> +&lrm; <i>rīc</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Alrich</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Æþelrīc</li></ol><p>From <i>Æþelrīc</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“son”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Ethelred, Aethelred, or Æthelred</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalarād</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalarēdaz</i>, from <i>&#42;aþalaz</i> (“noble”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;rēdaz</i> (“counsel, advice”). Equivalent to <i>æþele</i> +&lrm; <i>rǣd</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Adalrāt</i>, Old Norse <i>Aðalráðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>æþele</i> (“noble”) and <i>siġe</i> (“victory”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Æthelstan; an Anglo-Saxon King of England.</li><li>Æthelstan Ætheling; eldest son of Æthelred the Unready.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalastain</i>, from <i>&#42;aþal</i> (“noble”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;stain</i> (“stone”). Equivalent to <i>æþele</i> +&lrm; <i>stān</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Adelstein</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalaswiþ</i>, from <i>&#42;aþal</i> (“noble”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;swinþ</i> (“strong”). Equivalent to <i>æþele</i> +&lrm; <i>swiþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Adelswind</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>æþele</i> (“noble”) and <i>wacor</i> (“watchful, vigilanty”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalawald</i>, from <i>&#42;aþal</i> (“noble”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wald</i> (“might, authority”). Equivalent to <i>æþele</i> +&lrm; <i>weald</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Adalwalt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalaward</i>, from <i>&#42;aþal</i> (“noble”) (modified by Old English <i>æþele</i>) + <i>&#42;ward</i> (“guard, guardian”). Equivalent to <i>æþele</i> +&lrm; <i>weard</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Adalwart</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalawīg</i>, from <i>&#42;aþal</i> (“noble”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;wīg</i> (“battle”). Equivalent to <i>æþele</i> +&lrm; <i>wīġ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Adalwīh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalawini</i>, equivalent to Old English <i>æþele</i> (“noble”) and <i>wine</i> (“friend”), cognate with German <i>Adalwin</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalawulf</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalawulfaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>æþele</i> (“noble”) +&lrm; <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>æþele</i> (“noble”) and <i>wynn</i> (“joy, delight”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Aþalaþrūþi</i>, from <i>&#42;aþal</i> (“noble”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;þrūþi</i> (“strength”). Equivalent to <i>æþele</i> +&lrm; <i>þrȳþ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Adeltrud</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Æþelwulf</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Þēodrīc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p><i>þeġn</i> (“servant”) +&lrm; <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Þeudubalþ</i>, from <i>&#42;þeudu</i> (“people, tribe”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;balþ</i> (“bold, brave”). Equivalent to <i>þēod</i> +&lrm; <i>beald</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>Diotbald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Þeudugaiʀ</i>, equivalent to <i>þēod</i> (“people, nation”) + <i>gār</i> (“spear”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Þeudumār</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Þeudōmēraz</i>. Equivalent to <i>þēod</i> (“people, nation”) +&lrm; <i>mǣre</i> (“famous, renowned”). Cognate with English <i>Theodemar</i>, German <i>Dietmar</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Theodore</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Theodōrus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Θεόδωρος</i> (Theódōros)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Þeudōrīks</i>, equivalent to <i>þēod</i> (“people, nation”) + <i>rīc</i> (“king, ruler”). Cognate with English <i>Derek</i>, English <i>Theoderic</i>, French <i>Thierry</i> and German <i>Dietrich</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>þēod</i> (“nation, language”) and <i>rǣd</i> (“counsel, advice”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>þēod</i> (“people”) +&lrm; <i>wine</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Þeuduwulf</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Þeudōwulfaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>þēod</i> (“nation, people”) +&lrm; <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”). Cognate with Old High German <i>Diotolf</i>, Old Norse <i>Þjóðólfr</i>, French <i>Theodolph</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>þing</i> (“thing, matter, concern”) + <i>friþ</i> (“peace”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thronds, Throwends; a Norwegian people inhabiting the area of modern Trøndelag.</li></ol><p>Compare Proto-Norse <i>&#42;þrōwendīʀ</i>&nbsp;m&nbsp;pl, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þrōwōną</i> (“to grow, thrive, prosper”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ndz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>þrǿndir</i>, <i>þrǿndr</i>, Icelandic <i>Þrændir</i> and Norwegian <i>trønder</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>þrȳþ</i> (“power”) +&lrm; <i>wulf</i> (“wolf”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Þunor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thuringians</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>law</li><li>marriage</li><li>rite</li><li>alternative form of <i>ēa</i>: river, running water</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aiwi</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian and Old High German <i>ēwa</i> ~ <i>ē</i>, Old Saxon <i>ēo</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>without, lacking (<i>usually forms nouns or adjectives</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ā-</i> (“away, missing, lacking, without”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>ā-</i>, Old Dutch <i>ā-</i>, Old High German <i>ā-</i> (“from, away, missing, without, wrong, un-”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēac</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēalā</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>notorious</li><li>proved (to be), exposed, manifest, apparent, blatant</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;auwjabārī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lawbook</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣ</i> +&lrm; <i>bōc</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fir, firtree</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>abiēs</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make angry, to offend</li></ol><p><i>ǣbylġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æċed</i> (“vinegar”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eċe</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēċe</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aki</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;akiz</i> (“ache, pain”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ajukī</i> (“eternal”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vinegar</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aket</i>, from Latin <i>acētum</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>ecid</i>, West Frisian <i>jittik</i>, Old High German <i>ezzih</i> (German <i>Essig</i>), Old Norse <i>edik</i> (Danish <i>eddike</i>, Swedish <i>ättika</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) oak</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aikīnaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>ētsen</i>, Old High German <i>eihhīn</i>, Old Norse <i>eikinn</i>. Equivalent to <i>āc</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>field (specifically one used to grow crops or hold farm animals)</li><li>acre (measure of land)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;akr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;akraz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éǵros</i> (“field”). Cognate with Ancient Greek <i>ᾰ̓γρός</i> (ăgrós), Latin <i>ager</i>, Sanskrit <i>अज्र</i> (ájra).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a farmer, ploughman</li></ol><p><i>æcer</i> +&lrm; <i>ċeorl</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æcermann</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farmer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;akramann-</i>, equivalent to <i>æcer</i> (“field”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“man”). Cognate with German <i>Ackermann</i> (“plowman”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acorn</li><li>tree mast (fruit of forest trees)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;akraną</i>, perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ógeh₂</i> (“berry”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>akern</i>, Old Saxon <i>akeran</i>, Old High German <i>ackeran</i>, Old Norse <i>akarn</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐌺𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (akran).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fieldwork, agricultural labour</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;akrawerką</i>. Equivalent to <i>æcer</i> (“field”) +&lrm; <i>weorc</i> (“work”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ǣcern</i> (“acorn”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scalpel, lancet</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>ǣdre</i> +&lrm; <i>seax</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) vein, artery; sinew</li><li>channel for liquids, a river</li><li>kidney</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>directly, instantly, at once</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) fully, entirely</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ēþrǭ</i>. Cognates include Old Frisian <i>ēdere</i>, Old High German <i>ādra</i>, Old Norse <i>æðr</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ēdraz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;āt-</i> (“quick”). Cognate with Old High German <i>atar</i>, Old Saxon <i>adro</i>, Latvian <i>ātrs</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Ædre)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣdre</i> (“stream”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣderseax</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>of [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>away, off, from, away from</li><li>absence, detriment, reduction; dis-, de-, un-</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aba-</i> (“from”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a descent, decline, or downward slope</li></ol><p>From <i>æf-</i> +&lrm; <i>dȳne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a descent</li></ol><p>From <i>æf-</i> +&lrm; <i>dæl</i> (“valley”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evening</li><li>eve: the day/evening/night before something</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) vespers</li></ol><p>Form reflects Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;āban</i>, a byform or derivative of <i>&#42;ābanþ</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>ēvend</i>, Old Saxon <i>āƀand</i>, Old Dutch <i>āvont</i>, Old High German <i>ābant</i>, Old Norse <i>aptann</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evening song, evensong</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣfen</i> +&lrm; <i>drēam</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evening prayer, evening service</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣfen</i> +&lrm; <i>ġebed</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supper, evening meal</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣfen</i> (“evening”) +&lrm; <i>ġereord</i> (“meal, food”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the evening gloom: twilight</li></ol><p><i>ǣfen</i> (“evening”) +&lrm; <i>glōm</i> (“gloom, gloaming”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A sacrifice offered in the evening</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣfen</i> (“evening”) +&lrm; <i>lāc</i> (“gift, offering”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evening light</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣfen</i> (“evening”) +&lrm; <i>lēoht</i> (“light”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) for evening to approach (glosses Latin <i>advesperāscere</i>)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ǣfen</i> (“evening”) +&lrm; <i>-lǣċan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supper</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣfen</i> (“evening”) +&lrm; <i>mete</i> (“food, act of dining”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vespers, vespers</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣfen</i> (“eveneing”) +&lrm; <i>sang</i> (“liturgical service”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣfensċop</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evening scop, bard</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣfen</i> (“evening”) +&lrm; <i>sċop</i> (“poet”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the evening star, i.e. Venus as seen in the early evening</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ēbanþsternǭ</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>āƀandsterro</i>, Old High German <i>ābantsterno</i>, Old Norse <i>aptanstjarna</i>. Equivalent to <i>ǣfen</i> (“evening”) +&lrm; <i>steorra</i> (“star”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evening, eventide</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣfen</i> +&lrm; <i>tīd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evening time, eventide</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣfen</i> +&lrm; <i>tīma</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the evening star, Hesperus, i.e. Venus as seen in the evening</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ǣfen</i> (“evening”) +&lrm; <i>tungol</i> (“star”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an evening office</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣfen</i> (“evening”) +&lrm; <i>þēowdōm</i> (“service”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jealousy, envy</li><li>zeal</li><li>rivalry, emulation</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣfæst</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;abunsti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;abundiz</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>avunst</i>, Old High German <i>abunst</i>, Old Norse <i>ǫfund</i>.</p><p>From <i>ǣ</i> +&lrm; <i>-fæst</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to envy, be envious</li><li>to grow envious</li></ol><p>From <i>æfest</i> (“envy”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>jealous, envious</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;abunstīg</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>abunstig</i> and Old High German <i>abunstig</i>. Equivalent to <i>æfest</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piety, devotion</li></ol><p><i>ǣ</i> (“law”) +&lrm; <i>fæstnes</i> (“firmness, rigor”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣfestnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abyss</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;abagrundī</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>afgrundi</i> and Old High German <i>abgrunti</i>; also, inexactly, with Gothic <i>𐌰𐍆𐌲𐍂𐌿𐌽𐌳𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (afgrundiþa). Equivalent to <i>æf-</i> +&lrm; <i>grynde</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>idolatrous</li><li>superstitious</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>superstition</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;abgaliþō</i>, equivalent to <i>æf-</i> +&lrm; <i>gælþ</i> from galan.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry out, perform, execute</li><li>to cause, prepare, make ready</li><li>to endure, suffer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;abnijaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;op-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become evening</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣfen</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evening</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣfnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ever</li></ol><p>Not found in other Germanic languages. Possibly representing <i>ā</i> (“always, ever”) +&lrm; <i>in fēore</i> (“in life”), the final element being the dative form of Old English <i>feorh</i> (“life, existence”). Compare similar Old English construction <i>ā tō fēore</i> (“ever in life”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eft</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>behind</li><li>from behind</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>æfter</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>after</li><li>behind</li><li>according to</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>after</li><li>behind</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;after</i>, whence also Old High German <i>aftar</i>, Old Norse <i>eptir</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂epoteros</i> (“further behind, further away”), comparative form of <i>&#42;h₂epó</i> (“off, behind”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>after</li></ol><p>From <i>æfter</i> +&lrm; <i>þām</i> +&lrm; <i>þe</i>, meaning "after the (time) that".</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>after, behind</li><li>afterward</li><li>for, for the sake or purpose of</li><li>later, in the later part (of the year)</li><li>against</li><li>out, out after</li></ol><p>From <i>æfter</i> (“behind, after”). More at æfter.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>æfter</i> (adverb)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æfterra</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>æftercweþan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak after; to repeat; to answer</li><li>to revoke, renounce, abjure</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>æfter-</i> +&lrm; <i>cweþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to follow after, pursue</li></ol><p>From <i>æfter-</i> +&lrm; <i>folgian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to follow</li><li>to come after</li></ol><p>From <i>æfter-</i> +&lrm; <i>fylġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>follower</li><li>descendant</li><li>successor</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>æfter</i> (“after, behind”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;genga</i> (“goer”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a successor</li></ol><p>From <i>æfter-</i> +&lrm; <i>gengel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hinder, rear, posterior</li><li>next, following</li><li>second</li></ol><p>From <i>æfter</i> +&lrm; <i>-ra</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the first month of the year according to the Anglo-Saxon calendar</li><li>January</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the seventh month of the year according to the Anglo-Saxon calendar</li><li>July</li></ol><p>Literally "the latter <i>līþa</i>".</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inquire after, examine</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>æfter-</i> +&lrm; <i>spyrian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>afterward</li></ol><p><i>æfter</i> +&lrm; <i>-weard</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>old age</li><li>an 'after-age,' a time after or since</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>æfter</i> (“after”) +&lrm; <i>ieldu</i> (“age”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æfter</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>absent</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;abaward</i>. Equivalent to <i>æf-</i> +&lrm; <i>weard</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absence</li></ol><p><i>æfweard</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a decrease of wealth</li></ol><p><i>æf-</i> +&lrm; <i>wela</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æfwyrdla</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æfwyrdla</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>damage, injury</li><li>loss</li><li>(<i>law</i>) amercement</li></ol><p>From <i>æf-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġewyrdlian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>firm in observing the law; religious; pious</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣ</i> +&lrm; <i>-fæst</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>religion</li><li>scrupulousness, lawfulness ("Afastness").</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣfæst</i> and +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offense, insult</li><li>grudge</li><li>displeasure</li><li>envy</li><li>zeal</li></ol><p><i>æf-</i> +&lrm; <i>þanc</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>egg</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aij</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ajją</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ōwyóm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>ever; every, each, all, denotes universality (<i>forms pronouns and adverbs</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ā</i> (“ever”) +&lrm; <i>ġe-</i> (collective and perfective prefix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āgen</i></li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Ægen)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Shortened form of names including <i>Ǣġenbeald</i> and <i>Ǣġenwulf</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the skin or membrane of an egg</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣġer</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;felma</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yolk (of an egg)</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣġ</i> (“egg”) +&lrm; <i>ġeolu</i> (“yellow”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>everyone, everything</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣġ-</i> +&lrm; <i>hwā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>each, every</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>each (one)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aiwgahwalīk</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>iāhwelik</i>, Old Saxon <i>iogihwilik</i>, Old High German <i>iogiwelīh</i>. Equivalent to <i>ǣġ-</i> +&lrm; <i>hwelċ</i> or <i>ā</i> +&lrm; <i>ġe-</i> +&lrm; <i>hwelċ</i> or <i>ā</i> +&lrm; <i>ġehwelċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣġhwelċ</i></li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣġhwelċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣġhwelċ</i></li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣġhwelċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>everywhere</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>whatever</li><li>anything</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣġ-</i> +&lrm; <i>hwæt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>each (of two)</li><li>both</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aiw</i> (“always”) ( &gt; Old English <i>ā</i>) + <i>&#42;gahwaþera</i> (“each”) (> Old English <i>ġehwæþer</i>). Ultimately pertaining to <i>&#42;aiw</i> + <i>&#42;hwaþar</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>eogihwethar</i>, Old High German <i>eogihwedar</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āglǣċa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eggshell</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣġ</i> (“egg”) +&lrm; <i>sċiell</i> (“shell”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣġsċiell</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;poetic) a "sea-guard" or guardianship</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ǣġ</i> (“sea”) +&lrm; <i>weard</i> (“guard”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dandelion</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣġ</i> (“egg”) +&lrm; <i>wyrt</i> (“plant”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣġþer</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>each (of two)</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>æghwæþer</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Northumbrian form of <i>ēar</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>possession</li><li>property, livestock</li><li>power, possession</li><li>valuation, estimation, reckoning</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aihtiz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>ēht</i>; related to <i>āgan</i> (“to own”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Anglian</i>) eight</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>awl, hook, fork</li><li>alternative form of <i>æl</i> (“awl”)</li><li>eel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;al</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;alaz</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ālu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ēlō</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;āl</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ēlaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>all; complete, completely</li><li>quite, very</li><li>alternative form of <i>el-</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ala-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ala-</i> (“all, whole”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂el-</i> (“other, beyond”), related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;allaz</i> (“all”), which comes from <i>&#42;h₂elnós</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;alja-</i> ~ <i>&#42;ali-</i>, combining form of <i>&#42;aljaz</i> (“other”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēalā</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>legislative</li></ol><p>From <i>æ</i> +&lrm; <i>lagol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kindle, light, burn</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ailijan</i> (“to kindle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a doctor of the law</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) a Pharisee</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ǣ</i> +&lrm; <i>lārēow</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>every, each</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>ǣġhwelċ</i>. See there for more.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ælce dæge</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ǣlċe</i> (“each”) +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i> (“day”). <i>ǣlċe</i> is in the instrumental case.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>every day</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣlċe</i> (“each”) +&lrm; <i>dæġe</i> (“day”). <i>ǣlċe</i> and <i>dæge</i> are in the instrumental case.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>elsewhere</li><li>besides</li><li>otherwise</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ieldu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ielda bearn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ielde</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ieldra</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ieldu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fire; conflagration</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ailid</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ailidaz</i> (“fire”), past participle of <i>&#42;ailijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>ēld</i> (“fire”), Old Norse <i>eldr</i> (“fire”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long, protracted, lengthy, troublesome</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burbot</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ielf</i> (“elf”)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Ælf)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Elbe</li></ol><p>From <i>ælf</i> (“elf”).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;albī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;albī</i> (“river”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ielfādl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ielfcynn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ielfen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ielfisc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>elfremd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>elfremd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ielfsċīene</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ielfsiden</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ielfsogoþa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nightshade (probably the scrambling shrub <i>Solanum dulcamara</i>), as ascribed to (and/or having apotropaic powers against) elves.</li></ol><p>From <i>ælf</i> (“elf”) + <i>&#42;-þone</i>, so named because it was considered a remedy for <i>ælfādl</i> (“elf disease”). The second element may be a cognate to <i>þennan</i> (“to stretch”), referring to the weakly climbing nature of the plant's stalk.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of pure gold</li></ol><p><i>æl-</i> (“all”) +&lrm; <i>gylden</i> (“golden”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>all-white</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;alahwītaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>æl-</i> (“all”) +&lrm; <i>hwīt</i> (“white”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>alahwīt</i> and Old Norse <i>alhvítr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burning</li><li>ardor</li><li>weariness</li></ol><p><i>ǣlan</i> (“to burn”) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>endlyfta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ælmihtiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an act of charity</li></ol><p>From <i>ælmesse</i> (“alms”) +&lrm; <i>dǣd</i> (“deed”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a begger, almsman</li></ol><p>From <i>ælmesse</i> (“alms”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alms</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;alemōsinā</i>, a borrowing from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;alemosyna</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>omnipotence</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;alamahti</i>. Equivalent to <i>æl-</i> +&lrm; <i>miht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ælmihtiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>all-powerful</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;alamahtīg</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;alamahtīgaz</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Ælmihtig)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Almighty; God</li></ol><p>Nominalization of <i>ælmihtiġ</i> (“almighty”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eel net: a net for catching eels</li></ol><p><i>ǣl</i> (“eel”) +&lrm; <i>nett</i> (“net”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brand new</li></ol><p><i>æl-</i> (“all”) +&lrm; <i>nīewe</i> (“new”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>elreord</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lawgiver; legislator</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣ</i> (“law”) +&lrm; <i>lǣdend</i> (“leader; bearer; bringer”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>elþēodiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>elþēodiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣmielnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣmielnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>empty (of people)</li></ol><p><i>ǣ-</i> +&lrm; <i>-menne</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣmyrġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣmetta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ant</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;āmaitijā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>free time, leisure, respite</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣmetta</i> (“leisure”) +&lrm; <i>hwīl</i> (“time”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anthill</li></ol><p><i>ǣmete</i> (“ant”) +&lrm; <i>hyll</i> (“hill”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leisure, free time</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uz-</i> + <i>&#42;mōtiþō</i> (“idleness, leisure, opportunity”), equivalent to <i>ǣ-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;metta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣmete</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æmtiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsavoury, without taste</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣ-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;mielle</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mallī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mallijaz</i> (“void, vain”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loathsomeness, weariness, disdain</li><li>falseness, unfaithfulness</li><li>false dealing, treason</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣmielle</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣmetta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æmtiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣmtian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be at leisure, have time for</li><li>to be vacant</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣmta</i> (“leisure”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>empty, vacant</li><li>free, idle</li><li>unmarried</li><li>barren</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣmtian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣmielnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of mindfulness or attention; forgetfulness; neglect</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣ-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;mynde</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ember</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aimuzjǭ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣmette</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣmette</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>endlyfta</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>once (one time)</li><li>once (at some former time)</li></ol><p>Originally the instrumental masculine/neuter singular of <i>ān</i> (“one”) before it was replaced by regularized <i>āne</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ened</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncommon</i>) once</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣne</i> (“once”) +&lrm; <i>-es</i>; compare <i>ānes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ange</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>anyhow</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>enġel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>enġellīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ened</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>any</li><li>anyone</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ainag</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ainagaz</i>, from <i>&#42;ainaz</i> (“one”). Akin to Old Frisian <i>enich</i>, Old Saxon <i>ēnig</i>, Old Norse <i>einigr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āninga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>only, sole</li><li>unique, peerless</li><li>incomparable, excellent, beautiful</li></ol><p>From <i>ān</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i> (showing i-mutation; compare <i>ānlīċ</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ǣnlīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ānlīepiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ānlǣċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apple</li><li>ball, sphere</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) eyeball</li><li>any fruit</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æppelbearu</i></li></ol><p>From <i>æppel</i> (“fruit”) +&lrm; <i>bearo</i> (“grove”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orchard</li></ol><p>From <i>æppel</i> (“fruit”) +&lrm; <i>bearu</i> (“grove”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bearing apples; apple-bearing, fruit-bearing</li></ol><p>From <i>æppel</i> +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pomegranate</li></ol><p>From <i>æppel</i> (“apple”) +&lrm; <i>cyrnel</i> (“kernel”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>apuldor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>apuldor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reddish-yellow in color</li></ol><p>From <i>æppel</i> (“apple”) +&lrm; <i>fealu</i> (“yellow, dusky”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an "apple house" or place for fruit</li></ol><p>From <i>æppel</i> (“apple, fruit”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the violet</li></ol><p><i>æppel</i> (“apple”) +&lrm; <i>lēaf</i> (“leaf”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apple tree</li></ol><p>From <i>æppel</i> (“apple”) +&lrm; <i>trēow</i> (“tree”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cider</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;appluwīn</i>. Equivalent to <i>æppel</i> (“apple”) +&lrm; <i>wīn</i> (“wine”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crab apple</li></ol><p>From <i>æppel</i> (“apple”) +&lrm; <i>þorn</i> (“thorn”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>appled, made into balls</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>æppel</i> (“fruit, apple”) +&lrm; <i>-ed</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æspe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>early, previous, former</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>before, <i>(in negative sentences)</i> until</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>before, <i>(in negative sentences)</i> until</li><li>rather than (in preference to)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>previously</li><li>already</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;airiz</i>, originally a comparative form (=‘earlier’).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sooner or later</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>before</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>before, early, former, past, pre-</li><li>ancient, coming before, preceding</li><li>premature, coming too early or too soon</li><li>very, exceeding; prime</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;airi-</i> (“before”), cognate with Old English <i>ǣr</i> (“ere, before”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>earc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>arċe-</i> (“arch-”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>arċebisċop</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>arċebisċop</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>arċebisċop</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an early death</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ǣr-</i> +&lrm; <i>dēaþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eardung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the early part of the day; dawn, early morning</li><li><i>(in the plural, idiomatic)</i> the old days, days gone by, days of yore</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣr-</i> +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) brass, brazen</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ār</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>message</li><li>errand</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ārundī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bound on an errand</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣrende</i> +&lrm; <i>-fæst</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a spiritual or spirit messenger; an angel</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣrende</i> +&lrm; <i>gāst</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>letter (written message)</li></ol><p><i>ǣrende</i> (“message”) +&lrm; <i>ġewrit</i> (“writing”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go on an errand, carry news, carry a message, send word to</li><li>to go on a mission, negotiate for</li><li>to intercede, plead (a cause)</li><li>to look for, obtain</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ārundijōn</i>, variant of <i>&#42;ārundijan</i>, equivalent to <i>ǣrende</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>messenger</li><li>ambassador</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣrende</i> (“message”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;raca</i> (“someone who narrates or expounds,” agent noun of <i>reċċan</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small boat, skiff</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ǣrende</i> (“message”) +&lrm; <i>sċip</i> (“ship”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>messenger, ambassador</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ǣrende</i> +&lrm; <i>wreca</i>. See <i>ǣrendraca</i> also.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send a message</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ǣrende</i> +&lrm; <i>wreċċan</i>.</p></html>:/ˈæːrest/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ǣr</i> (“early”); first</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at first</li></ol><p>Superlative form of <i>ǣr</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;airist(ō)</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>first of all</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ierfe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forefather</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣr</i> (“before”) +&lrm; <i>fæder</i> (“father”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beforenamed</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ǣr-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġenemned</i>, from <i>ġenemnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ancient hostility</li></ol><p><i>ǣr</i> +&lrm; <i>ġewinn</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) good of old, good from olden days; good before other men <i>(precise meaning uncertain)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ǣr-</i> +&lrm; <i>gōd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(countable,&#32;uncountable) rising or getting up</li><li>(countable,&#32;uncountable) resurrection</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzristiz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>urrist</i> and Gothic <i>𐌿𐍂𐍂𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (urrists). By surface analysis, <i>ǣ-</i> (“up, out”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;rist</i> (“rising”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>early</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ǣr</i> (“before”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>early</li></ol><p><i>ǣr</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the early morning, daybreak</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ǣr-</i> (“early”) +&lrm; <i>morgen</i> (“morning”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house, habitation</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;raʀn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;razną</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>rann</i>.</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. Loss of initial <i>*r-</i> not mentioned.</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to run</li><li>to cause to flow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rannijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>early</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>formerly, before</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>before</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣrest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ǣr</i> (“early”); earlier, former</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the twelfth month of the year according to the Anglo-Saxon calendar</li><li>December</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣrra</i> (“earlier”) +&lrm; <i>ġēola</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the sixth month of the year according to the Anglo-Saxon calendar</li><li>June</li></ol><p>Literally "the former <i>līþa</i>".</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ears</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣrest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ierþling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food, meat</li><li>carrion, dead carcass</li><li>bait</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ēsą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ēdsto-</i>. Cognate with West Frisian <i>ies</i> (“bait, carrion”), Dutch <i>aas</i> (“bait, carrion”) and German <i>Aas</i> (“bait, carrion”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ash tree</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) spear</li><li>a kind of small, light Viking ship used for raids</li><li>the runic character ᚫ (/æ(ː)/)</li><li>The name of the Latin script letter <i>Æ</i>/<i>æ</i>..</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ask</i>. See there for more.</p><p>Cognate with Latin <i>ornus</i> (“a mountain ash tree, rowan tree; a lance made of the wood of an ash”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Æsc)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From the noun <i>æsċ</i> (“ash tree”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ash (combustion residue)</li><li>examination, interrogation, inquisition; inquiry, question</li><li>(<i>law</i>) A search for something stolen</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;askā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;askǭ</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>&#42;eske</i>, Old Saxon <i>aska</i>, Old High German <i>asca</i>, Old Norse <i>aska</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐌶𐌲𐍉</i> (azgō).</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aiskijā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ashen (made of ashwood)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wooden vessel, especially one made of ashwood</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;askīn</i>, equivalent to <i>æsċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>eskīn</i>, Middle High German <i>eschīn</i>, <i>eschen</i> (modern German <i>eschen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ash gray, the color of ash</li></ol><p>From <i>æsċ</i> (“ash”) +&lrm; <i>grǣġ</i> (“gray”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) spear</li></ol><p>From <i>æsċ</i> +&lrm; <i>holt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ship-man, sailor, pirate</li></ol><p>From <i>æsċ</i> (“a type of small ship”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glory, spear-glory, fame from battle</li></ol><p>From <i>æsċ</i> (“ash, spear, lance, ship”) +&lrm; <i>tīr</i> (“fame, glory, honor”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lawgiver</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ǣ</i> +&lrm; <i>sellend</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æspe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an aspen</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aspu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aspō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) aspen</li></ol><p>From <i>æspe</i> or <i>æsp</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a spring (of water), fountain</li></ol><p>From <i>ēa</i> (“water, river”) +&lrm; <i>spring</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēst</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣrest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tablet; a waxed tablet</li><li>table for notes</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>hastula</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to offend, scandalize; to depart from the law or from good practice</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣswica</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eating</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li><i>(+dative)</i> at a certain place</li><li><i>(+dative)</i> at a certain time</li><li><i>(+accusative, rarely)</i> to, up to, as far as</li><li><i>(+dative)</i> from, of</li><li>with, for, at, to, against</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;āt</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ētą</i>. Related to <i>etan</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;at</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at first, from the start</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at home</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æt nīehstan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at last, finally, in the end</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>at, near; toward</li><li>away, off</li><li>to</li><li>intensely, excessively</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;at-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;at-</i> (“at, toward”). More at Old English <i>æt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>attested only in compounds</i>) eater</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ētijô</i>. Equivalent to</p><p><i>etan</i> (“to eat”) +&lrm; <i>-a</i> (agent suffix).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be present</li></ol><p>From <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>bēon</i>. From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;beuną</i> (“to be”), related to <i>būan</i> (“to dwell”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰewH-</i>, <i>&#42;bʰuH-</i>. The past tense forms are from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wesaną</i> (from which also <i>wesan</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wes-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring, carry, bear away</li></ol><p>From <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>beran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break out</li><li>to escape, get away</li></ol><p>From <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>berstan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take away, withdraw</li><li>to set free, release</li><li>to rescue</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>breġdan</i> (“to drag, bend, move back and forth”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) ablative, indicating what is taken or received from others, or the position from which one proceeds</li></ol><p><i>ætbreġdan</i> (“to take away, carry off, deprive of”) +&lrm; <i>-endlīċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick to, adhere</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>clifian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>āte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>too aged, too old</li></ol><p>From <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>ealdod</i> (“aged”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ætgār</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ætīewan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adhere, cleave to, hang on, stick to</li><li>to insist on</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>fēolan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flee away, eschew; escape by flight</li></ol><p>From <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>flēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take hold of</li><li>to lay claim</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>fōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>before [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>close by [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>foran</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>javelin</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;atgaiʀ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;atgaizaz</i>, equivalent to <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>gār</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grasping at, seizing</li></ol><p>From <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;grǣpe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>together</li></ol><p>From <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>gædere</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ætgædere</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ætgār</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to retain</li><li>to detain</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>habban</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to withhold</li></ol><p>From <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>healdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take, move, touch; rine</li></ol><p>From <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>hrīnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn</li><li>to return</li><li>to accede</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>hweorfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to add to, increase, augment</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>īeċan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to appear</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to show, reveal</li></ol><p>From <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>īewan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appearance (act of appearing)</li></ol><p><i>ætīewed</i> + <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appearance (act of appearing), showing</li></ol><p><i>ætīewan</i> + <i>-nes</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epiphany</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ætīewan</i> (“to appear, show”) +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣtynġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead out, drive away</li></ol><p>From <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>lǣdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ātor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poisonous</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aittrīn</i>, equivalent to <i>ātor</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to poison</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to envenom</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to make or become poisonous</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aitrōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aitrōną</i> (“to fester, putrify”), equivalent to <i>ātor</i> (“poison”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>eitarōn</i>, <i>eittarōn</i>, Old Norse <i>eitra</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poisonous, venomous</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aitrīgaz</i>, equivalent to <i>ātor</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a sum; together</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;at</i> + <i>&#42;samanai</i>, equivalent to <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>samen</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>atsamna</i>, <i>atsamne</i>, Old High German <i>az samane</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slip, fall, slip away, slide away</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>slīdan</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ætsamne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to spring out; rush forth; spurt out</li></ol><p>From <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>springan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ætsteall</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand, stop, remain, cease, rest, stand still, stand up, withstand</li></ol><p>From <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>standan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bystander</li></ol><p>From <i>ætstandan</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i> or <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>standend</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ætsteall</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>station, camp</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>steall</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ætsteall</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣtren</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ātor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣtren</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣtrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣtriġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry off, to carry away</li></ol><p>From <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>wegan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be present</li></ol><p>From <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>wesan</i>. From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wesaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wes-</i>. The simple present forms originate from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁es-</i> (“to be”), which had no infinitive or past tense in Proto-Germanic, but had already formed a single paradigm with <i>&#42;wesaną</i> supplying the infinitive and past tense.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with dative</i>) to escape</li></ol><p>From <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>windan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reproach, blame, upbraid</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>æt-</i> +&lrm; <i>wītan</i> (“to blame, reproach”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ætīeċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tongueless</li><li>speechless</li><li>without eloquence</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ātungī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ētungijaz</i> (“tongueless, speechless”), equivalent to <i>ǣ-</i> +&lrm; <i>tynġe</i>. Compare Old High German <i>āzunglīh</i> (“speechless”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ætīewan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ætīewnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>earlier form of</i> <i>æ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to despise, scorn</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aiwijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adulterer</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adultery</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aiwibruki</i>. Equivalent to <i>ǣ</i> (“marriage”) +&lrm; <i>bryċe</i> (“breach”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) A guardian of the divine law; a priest</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣwe</i> (“law”) +&lrm; <i>weard</i> (“guard”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣfæst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ǣwignes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a welling up of water</li><li>a spring, fountain</li><li>source or head of a river</li></ol><p>From <i>ēa</i> (“river”) +&lrm; <i>wielm</i> (“upwelling (of water)”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eternity</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;ǣwig</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>. Compare Old Frisian <i>ēwichēd</i>, Old High German <i>ēwīgheit</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disgraced, shameless, foul</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shame, dishonor, disgrace, offense</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aiwiskaz</i>, related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aiwijaną</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aiwiskō</i>, <i>&#42;aiwiskiją</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disgrace, obscenity, filthiness, shameless conduct</li></ol><p>From <i>æwisċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counselor, one skilled in the law</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣ</i> +&lrm; <i>wita</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>legal, lawful</li></ol><p><i>ǣw-</i> (“law”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>legally, lawfully</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣwlīċ</i> +&lrm; <i>-e</i> (adverbial ending).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to marry, wed</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣ</i> (“law, marriage”) +&lrm; <i>-nian</i>. The <i>w</i> comes from the earlier form <i>ǣw</i> (from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aiwaz</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wedlock</li></ol><p>From <i>ǣwn(ian)</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throwing away or rejecting</li><li>something thrown out or rejected; a castaway, reject</li><li>(countable,&#32;uncountable) abortion</li><li>an aborted fetus</li></ol><p><i>ǣ-</i> +&lrm; <i>wyrp</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>axe</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>*æces</i> ← <i>*ækysi</i> ← <i>*akusi</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;akusi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;akwisī</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>axa</i>, Old Saxon <i>akus</i>, Old High German <i>accus</i>, Old Norse <i>øx</i>, and Gothic <i>𐌰𐌵𐌹𐌶𐌹</i> (aqizi).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>æþelboren</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>æþelcund</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>æþelcyning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>æþele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>æþeling</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ǣþm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homeland (ancestral territory) alternative form of <i>ēþel</i></li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æþele</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;ōþil</i> (“homeland”) from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ōþalą</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>oðil</i>, Old Frisian <i>ethel</i>, Old High German <i>uodil</i>, Old Norse <i>óðal</i>.</p><p>Apocopic form of <i>æþele</i> (adjective).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Prefix denoting nobility, highness in hierarchy</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>æþele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>born a noble or aristocrat; highborn</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) innate, natural</li></ol><p><i>æþele</i> (“noble”) +&lrm; <i>boren</i> (“born”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of noble birth, noble</li></ol><p>From <i>æþele</i> (“noble”) +&lrm; <i>-cund</i> (“born”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noble king (Christ)</li></ol><p>From <i>æþele</i> +&lrm; <i>cyning</i> (“king”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>athalkuning</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>noble</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aþulī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aþulijaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>adal</i> (German <i>edel</i>), Old Norse <i>aðall</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>æþele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ennoble, improve</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prince</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;aþuling</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aþulingaz</i> (“prince, nobleman”). Equivalent to <i>æþele</i> +&lrm; <i>-ing</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) "sons of men"; mankind</li></ol><p>From genitive plural of <i>æþeling</i> + plural of <i>bearn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nobility</li></ol><p>From <i>æþele</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>æþelu</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>æþele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a "noble smell"</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>æþele</i> (“noble”) +&lrm; <i>stenċ</i> (“smell”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nobility, aristocracy</li><li>family, origin, descent</li><li>genius, talent; preeminence</li><li>nature</li><li>produce, yield; growth, increase</li></ol><p>Akin to Old High German <i>adal</i> (“nobility”), Old English <i>æþele</i> (“noble”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>æþele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breathing</li><li>breath</li><li>vapour</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;āþm</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ēþmaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁eh₁tmén</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old High German <i>ātum</i> (German <i>Atem</i>), Old Saxon <i>āthom</i>, Old Frisian <i>ēthma</i>, <i>ādema</i>, Sanskrit <i>आत्मन्</i> (ātmán, “soul; self; essence”), Dutch <i>adem</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise vapor, boil, be heated</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ǣþm</i> (“breath, vapor”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tedious</li><li>troublesome</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weariness</li><li>disgust</li><li>tediousness</li></ol><p>Related to <i>þrēotan</i> and <i>æþrȳttan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weary</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þā</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þā</i></li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þā</i></li></ol></html>:/θɑːx/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þāh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þām</i></li></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þām</i></li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þām</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þāra</i></li></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þāra</i></li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þāra</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þēah</i></li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þēah</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þec</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þēod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þēoden</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þefanþorn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þerh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þæder</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þīn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þing</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ðynne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þēod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þoden</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þonne</i></li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þonne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þorh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þost</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þrotbolla</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þū</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þūma</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þunwange</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þurh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þus</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þȳ</i></li></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þȳ</i></li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þȳ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þæder</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þyrs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þæder</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þām</i></li></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þām</i></li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þām</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þone</i></li></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þone</i></li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þone</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þǣr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þǣre</i></li></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þǣre</i></li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þǣre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þǣron</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þǣrrihte</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þǣrtōēacan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þǣs</i></li></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þǣs</i></li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þǣs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þæt</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the letter <b>Ð</b>, <b>ð</b> (/θ/ or /ð/)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>then</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>when, since, because, where</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þai</i>, which shares the same root as Old English <i>þæt</i>.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>while</li><li>as long as</li></ol><p>Literally "for the time that...", using the accusative of duration.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch softly, stroke</li><li>to pat</li><li>to strike gently, tap; to clap</li><li>to beat</li><li>to put one thing into another, add to</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þakkōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þakwōną</i> (“to touch, touch softly”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;teh₂g-</i> (“to touch”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>thakolōn</i> (“to stroke, caress”), Icelandic <i>þjökka</i>, <i>þjaka</i> (“to thwack, thump, beat”), Norwegian <i>tjåka</i> (“to strike, beat”), Latin <i>tangō</i> (“to touch”). More at thwack, tangent.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consent or agree to, to let happen or be done, to take up</li><li>to submit to, to suffer, to bear</li><li>to bear with, to tolerate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þabōną</i>, from <i>&#42;þabaz</i> (“fitting; appropriate”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consent, permission</li></ol><p>From <i>þafian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þæġe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moist</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þain</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þainaz</i> (“moist”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;teh₂-</i> (“to melt, flow”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thence; from there</li><li>thereupon; after that</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þananā</i>; cognate with Old Saxon <i>thanan</i>, Old High German <i>danan</i> (German <i>dannen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thanks, gratitude</li><li>satisfaction, pleasure</li><li>thought</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þankaz</i>, whence also Old Frisian <i>thonk</i>, Old Saxon <i>thank</i>, Old Dutch <i>thank</i>, Old High German <i>danc</i>, and Gothic <i>𐌸𐌰𐌲𐌺𐍃</i> (þagks). Old Norse <i>þǫkk</i>, though a feminine ō-stem, is clearly related; probably it was originally a collective of PGmc <i>*þankaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thank <i>(+ dative of the person, whom the subject thanks) (+ genitive of the thing, for which the subject thanks)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þankōn</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>thankon</i>, Old High German <i>dankōn</i>, Old Norse <i>þakka</i> (Icelandic <i>þakka</i>), and Dutch <i>danken</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gratitude, thankfulness <i>(+ dative = to someone)</i> <i>(+ genitive = for something)</i></li><li>thanksgiving</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þancungu</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>dankunga</i>. Equivalent to <i>þancian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thankworthy, deserving of thanks</li><li>acceptable</li></ol><p>From <i>þanc</i> +&lrm; <i>weorþ</i> and <i>þanc</i> +&lrm; <i>-wierþe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þancweorþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þancweorþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þancweorþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þancweorþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þone</i></li></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þone</i></li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þone</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>whenever, as soon as ever, as often as</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be or become moist</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þainēn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þaināną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;teh₂-</i> (“to melt, flow, run”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þonne</i></li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þonne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þanan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þǣr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þār</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þǣron</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þǣræfter</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thaw</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þauwjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þawjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>that, who, which</li></ol><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>(<i>demonstrative</i>) the, he: late variant of <i>se</i></li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>or (correlated to hwæþer)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þa</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tó</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þiz</i>.</p><p>From earlier <i>sē</i>, through influence of the <i>þ-</i> forms.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þēod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for all that, however, even so, yet</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>though, notwithstanding that</li><li>though, even if</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þauh</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þauh</i>, corresponding to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þau</i> (“in that case”) + <i>&#42;-uh</i> (“and”) (cognate with Latin <i>-que</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>however, but, nevertheless</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>þēah</i> +&lrm; <i>hwæþre</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counsel, consultation</li></ol><p>Derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þankijaną</i>, equivalent to <i>þenċan</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take counsel, consult</li></ol><p>From <i>þeaht</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counsel, consultation</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>þeahtian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>need (+ genitive = "for" something)</li><li>hardship, privation</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þarbu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be in need</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þarbēn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þarbāną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>necessary</li></ol><p>From <i>þearf</i> (“need”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>accurately</li><li>usefully, profitably</li></ol><p><i>þearf</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(of a person) severe</li><li>(of a thing) utter, excessive</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>very hard, keenly, harshly, severely</li><li>greatly</li></ol><p>From <i>þearl</i> (“severe, harsh”) +&lrm; <i>-e</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a gut; an intestine</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þarm</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þarmaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>habit, custom</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) customs, virtue, conduct, character</li></ol><p>Unknown origin, only in West Germanic, as it were from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þawwaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>thāw</i>, Old Saxon <i>thau</i> (“custom”). A possible Old High German reflex is <i>&#42;dou</i> (“discipline, coercion, tuition”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ill-mannered; ill-conditioned</li></ol><p>From <i>þēaw</i> +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þakkjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þakjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þeċċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þæċele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þæċele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The European buckthorn (<i>Rhamnus cathartica</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þebōnþorn</i>; compare <i>þefian</i> (“to heat”) and <i>þorn</i> (“thornbush”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mulled wine</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from <i>þefian</i> +&lrm; <i>-el</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pant (from heat); gasp</li><li>to be agitated (be hot in one's emotions)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þebōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þebōną</i> (“to be warm”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tep-</i> (“warm; hot”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>þefa</i> (“to sniff; smell; catch wind of”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þeġn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þeġnboren</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>servant</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) man, warrior, hero</li><li>officer</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þegn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>born a servant</li></ol><p><i>þeġn</i> (“servant”) +&lrm; <i>boren</i> (“born”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þēnest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to serve <i>(+ dative)</i></li><li>to serve food</li><li>to serve in the office of</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þegnōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>service</li></ol><p><i>þeġnian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>workshop</li><li>place in which employment is carried out</li></ol><p><i>þeġnung</i> (“service”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house, building”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þēah</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plank</li><li>thin plate or piece of metal or wood</li><li>floor, flooring</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þelą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þeġn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þankijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þankijaną</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>thenka</i>, Old Saxon <i>thenkian</i>, Old Dutch <i>thenken</i>, Old High German <i>denken</i>, Old Norse <i>þekkja</i>, Gothic <i>𐌸𐌰𐌲𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (þagkjan). All ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;teng-</i> (“to think”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þenċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þenċeþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) then</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>meanwhile, while</li><li>as long as, until</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þandê</i>, from <i>&#42;þana</i> (“at that, at that time, then”), <i>&#42;þan</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐌸𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌴</i> (þandē, “while, as long as, because”) and Old High German <i>thanta</i>, <i>danta</i> (“because”). More at <i>then</i> and <i>than</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>service</li><li>entertainment</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þewanōstaz</i>. Akin Old Norse <i>þjónusta</i> (“service”), and to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þignaz</i> (“servant, thane”), Old High German <i>dionōst</i> (“service”) (German <i>Dienst</i> (“service”)), Dutch <i>dienst</i> (“service”), Old English <i>þeġnian</i> (“to serve”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a prince</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þangil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þangilaz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>þengill</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þeġnian</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>þennan</i></li></ol><p>From <i>þēn</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>, from a variant of <i>þeġn</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þanjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þeġnian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þeġnian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch; stretch out; spread out; extend</li><li>(<i>of a bow</i>) to bend; draw</li><li>to prostrate; overthrow</li><li>to exert oneself; make an effort; strain</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þanjaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ten-</i> (“to stretch”).</p><p>Germanic cognates include Old Saxon <i>þennian</i>, Old High German <i>dennen</i> (German <i>dehnen</i>), Old Norse <i>þenja</i> (Norwegian <i>tenja</i>).</p><p>Indo-European cognates are Sanskrit <i>तनोति</i> (tanóti), Greek <i>τείνω</i> (teíno), Latin <i>tendō</i> (all three meaning “to stretch; extend”), Latin <i>teneō</i> (“to hold; grasp”), Welsh <i>tant</i> (“string of a musical instrument”), Breton <i>ardant</i> (“[nautical] toggle”), Russian <i>тенёто</i> (tenjóto, “net trap for rabbits etc.”), and Lithuanian <i>ti̇̀nti</i> (“to swell; bloat”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þeġnung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sēo</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þēow</i>, servant</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þeu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þewaz</i>, probably from earlier <i>&#42;þegwaz</i>, <i>&#42;þehwaz</i> (“runner, gofer”), likely from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tekʷ-</i> (“to run”). Cognate with Old High German <i>dio</i>, Proto-Norse <i>ᚦᛖᚹᚨᛉ</i> (þewaʀ) (whence Old Norse <i>-þér</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nation, people</li><li>language</li><li>(poetic,&#32;in compounds) arch-, main, great</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þeudu</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīedan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) (great) king, monarch, ruler</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þeudōkuningaz</i>, equivalent to <i>þēod</i> (“people”) +&lrm; <i>cyning</i> (“king”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>thiodkuning</i> and Old Norse <i>þjóðkonungr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) prince, lord, ruler, chief</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þeudan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þeudanaz</i>, from the same source as <i>þēod</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>thiodan</i>, Old Norse <i>þjóðann</i>, Gothic <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌽𐍃</i> (þiudans).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>leaderless, without leadership</li></ol><p>From <i>þēoden</i> (“prince, lord, leader”) +&lrm; <i>-lēas</i> (“-less”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) throne</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) people's treasure, national treasure hoard</li></ol><p><i>þēod</i> +&lrm; <i>ġestrēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>national</li><li>Gentile</li><li>native, of one's native tribe/nation</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a language</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þiudisk</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þiudiskaz</i> (“of or relating to a people”). Equivalent to <i>þēod</i> +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þēodcyning</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabited region or country</li></ol><p>From <i>þēod</i> (“people”) +&lrm; <i>land</i> (“land”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a great liar; an arch-imposter</li></ol><p>From <i>þēod</i> +&lrm; <i>loga</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tie, connection; suture; seam</li><li>assembly, conjugation, union</li></ol><p>From <i>þēod</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>. Compare related Middle Low German <i>dü̂denisse</i>, <i>dü̂tnisse</i>, Middle High German <i>diutnisse</i> (modern German <i>Deutnis</i>), Gothic <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌹𐌽𐌰𐍃𐍃𐌿𐍃</i> (þiudinassus).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þēodnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>people</li><li>association</li><li>teaching, learning, understanding</li><li>custom, mode of conduct</li></ol><p>From <i>þēod</i> (“people”) +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þēodsċipe</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warrior of the tribe</li><li>great warrior</li></ol><p><i>þēod</i> (“nation, people, tribe, race”) +&lrm; <i>wiga</i> (“warrior, fighter”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to avenge thoroughly; to take great vengeance</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þēod</i> +&lrm; <i>wrecan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) national disaster, great calamity</li></ol><p><i>þēod</i> +&lrm; <i>þrēa</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thief</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) theft</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þeubaz</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>thiāf</i>, Old Saxon <i>thiof</i>, Old High German <i>diob</i>, Old Norse <i>þjófr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐍆𐍃</i> (þiufs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a thieves' den or cave</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>þēof</i> (“thief”) +&lrm; <i>denn</i> (“den”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a payment or fine made in compensation for theft</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>þēof</i> (“thief”) +&lrm; <i>ġield</i> (“payment”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal, thieve, carry off</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>þēof</i> (“thief”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i> (infinitive suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a thief, bandit, brigand</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>þēof</i> (“thief”) +&lrm; <i>mann</i> (“person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a robber</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>þēof</i> (“thief”) +&lrm; <i>sċeaþa</i> (“ravager, robber”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pirate ship</li></ol><p><i>þēof</i> (“thief”) +&lrm; <i>sċip</i> (“ship”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a gang of thieves</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>þēof</i> (“thief”) +&lrm; <i>sċolu</i> (“troop, host”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>theft guilty, guilty of theft</li></ol><p>From <i>þeof</i> +&lrm; <i>scyldig</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thief-slaying</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>þēof</i> (“thief”) +&lrm; <i>sleġe</i> (“slaying”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thief-slaying</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>þēof</i> (“thief”) +&lrm; <i>sliht</i> (“slaying”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stolen by thieves</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>þēof</i> (“thief”) +&lrm; <i>stolen</i> (“stolen”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīefþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīefþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thigh</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þeuh</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flourish, prosper, be successful</li><li>alternative form of <i>þȳwan</i></li></ol><p>From an earlier (unattested) form <i>&#42;þīhan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þinhaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tenk-</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>thīhan</i>, Old Dutch <i>thīon</i> (Dutch <i>gedijen</i>), Old High German <i>dīhan</i> (German <i>gedeihen</i>), Gothic <i>𐌸𐌴𐌹𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (þeihan).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þeuhaną</i>, <i>&#42;þūhaną</i> (“to press”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tūk-</i> (“to beat”). Cognate with Old High German <i>dūhen</i> (“to press, press down”), Middle Dutch <i>duwen</i>, <i>douwen</i> (“to push, press, force”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þēnest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unleavened</li><li>(<i>of milk</i>) not sour; fresh; skim</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used attributively</i>) unleavened bread</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þerbaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīestre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīestre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīestre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to darken, to grow dark</li><li>to dim the sight</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þeustrōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīestru</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīestru</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to howl (like a wolf)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þeutan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þeutaną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>dioȥan</i>, Middle High German <i>dieȥen</i>, and Old Norse <i>þjóta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pipe, tube</li><li>channel or vessel through which water rushes</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þeutǭ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slave</li><li>servant</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>servile</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þeu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þewaz</i>, probably from earlier <i>&#42;þegwaz</i>, <i>&#42;þehwaz</i> (“runner, gofer”), likely from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tekʷ-</i> (“to run”). Cognate with Old High German <i>dio</i>, Old Norse <i>-þér</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>þēow</i> (“servant”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press, impress, force</li><li>to urge, drive</li><li>to press or thrust with a weapon, pierce, stab</li><li>to threaten</li><li>to rebuke, check</li><li>to subjugate, overpower, oppress; to crush</li><li>(<i>with dative</i>) To serve.</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þeuhan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þeuhaną</i>, <i>&#42;þūhaną</i> (“to press”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tūk-</i> (“to beat”). Cognate with Old High German <i>dūhen</i> (“to press, press down”), Middle Dutch <i>duwen</i>, <i>douwen</i> (“to push, press, force”).</p><p>From <i>þēowa</i> (“servant, slave”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slaveborn</li></ol><p>From <i>þēow</i> (“slave”) +&lrm; <i>boren</i> (“born”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>service, servitude</li><li>slavery, bondage</li></ol><p>From <i>þēow</i> +&lrm; <i>-dōm</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīewen</i></li></ol><p>From <i>þēow</i> (“servant”) +&lrm; <i>-en</i> (feminine suffix)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slavery</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þēow</i> +&lrm; <i>-et</i> or <i>þēowian</i> +&lrm; <i>-et</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to serve (as a servant, slave, or devotee)</li><li>to enslave, reduce to servants</li><li>synonym of <i>þēowan</i> (“to press”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þewāną</i> (“to enslave”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>þjá</i> (“to enslave”), Gothic <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌸𐌹𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (anaþiwan, “to subjugate, make subservient”).</p><p>See <i>þēowan</i> (“to press”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þēowet</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þēowet</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>servile work, work done by a slave</li></ol><p>From <i>þēow</i> (“slave”) +&lrm; <i>weorc</i> (“work”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a spear</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þehs-</i> (“to hew; hackle”). Cognate with Old High German <i>dehsa</i> (“axe”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þǣr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Northumbrian form of <i>þurh</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þerhw</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐌸𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌷</i> (þairh).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thresh</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þreskaną</i>, whence also Old High German <i>dreskan</i> (German <i>dreschen</i>), Dutch <i>dorsen</i>, Old Norse <i>þreskja</i> (Danish <i>tærske</i>), Gothic <i>𐌸𐍂𐌹𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (þriskan). Liquid metathesis had occurred in this verb. However, it was its unmetathesized form that survived into modern English.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þresċold</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þresċold</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þresċold</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>this</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>this</li></ol><p>From West Germanic, the base being <i>&#42;þes-</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þat</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þat</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>thīs</i>, Old High German <i>dese</i>, Old Norse <i>þessi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīestrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þēowan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þȳ</i></li></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þȳ</i></li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þȳ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þēowan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thick</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þikkwī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þekuz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tégus</i> (“thick”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thicket, growth of dense bushes</li><li>any place where there is dense growth or foliage</li></ol><p>Late Old English, from <i>þicce</i> (“thick, dense”) +&lrm; <i>-et</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þiccet</i></li></ol><p>See <i>þiccet</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make thick</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to become thick</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of persons) to throng</li></ol><p>From a merger of Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þikkwōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þekwōną</i> (“to make thick”); and Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þikkwēn</i> (“to become thick”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þicnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>corpulent, stout, fat, obese</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þikkul</i>, equivalent to <i>þiccian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ol</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þiccol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to receive, take, accept</li><li>to take (food), consume</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þigjaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tegʰ-</i>, <i>&#42;tēgʰ-</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>þiggja</i> (Swedish <i>tigga</i>), Old Saxon <i>thiggian</i>, Old High German <i>dikken</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þiċġan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>consistency</i>) thickness, viscosity</li><li>(<i>transparency</i>) thickness, darkness, cloudiness, obscurity</li><li>(<i>dimension</i>) thickness, depth</li><li>a thicket</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þikkwīnassī</i>, equivalent to <i>þicce</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>diknissi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þicnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīedan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þæder</i></li></ol><p>Alteration of <i>þæder</i>, probably by analogy with <i>hider</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;relational) of or pertaining to that (aforementioned) people</li></ol><p><i>þider</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;lēodisċ</i>, else <i>þider</i> (“there”) +&lrm; <i>lēod</i> (“people”) +&lrm; <i>-isċ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thitherward; in that direction; towards that</li></ol><p><i>þider</i> (“thither”) +&lrm; <i>-weard</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þiderweard</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to join, attach</li><li>to join, associate with, or attach one's self to somebody or somebody's group</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þiudijaną</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þeudō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tewtéh₂</i>. Akin to Old Saxon <i>thioda</i>, Old High German <i>diota</i>, Old Norse <i>þjóð</i>, Gothic <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰</i> (þiuda). The Proto-Indo-European root is also the source of Old Irish <i>túath</i>, Lithuanian <i>tauta</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thither</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þidr-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theft</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þiubiþu</i>. Equivalent to <i>þēof</i> +&lrm; <i>-þ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark</li><li>gloomy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þiustrī</i> (“dark”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be dark</li><li>to become dark</li><li>to make dark</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þiustrijēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>þīestre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darkness</li><li>gloom</li></ol><p><i>þīestre</i> +&lrm; <i>-u</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slave (female)</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þēow</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīefþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female servant, female attendant, handmaid</li></ol><p>From <i>þeġen</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plank; wooden flooring; floorboard</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þiljǭ</i>, whence also Old High German <i>dilla</i>, Old Norse <i>þilja</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>your (singular), thy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þīn</i>, whence also Old High German <i>dīn</i>, Old Norse <i>þinn</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to become moist or damp</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þīnan</i>, presumably from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þīnaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;teh₂-</i> (“to melt, flow, run”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þynċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þinċeþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þing</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal</i>) to swell up</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to swell up with indignation, pride, etc</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þindan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þindaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ten-</i> (“to stretch”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þiġnen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thing</li><li>matter, concern</li><li>event, meeting, court</li><li>case</li><li>reason</li><li>means</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þing</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þingą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to invite, address</li></ol><p>Apparently modified from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þingōną</i> (class-2 weak verb).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þing</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to intercede, supplicate</li><li>to settle, make terms</li><li>to determine, address, discourse</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þingōn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þingōną</i>, from <i>&#42;þingą</i> (“matter, issue”). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>thingia</i> (West Frisian <i>tingje</i>), Old Saxon <i>thingōn</i>, Middle Dutch <i>dingen</i> (Dutch <i>dingen</i>), Old High German <i>dingōn</i> (German <i>dingen</i>), Old Norse <i>þinga</i> (Swedish <i>tinga</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intercession, advocacy, pleading, intervention, mediation</li></ol><p>Compound noun from Old English <i>þing</i> (“thing”) +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i> (“direction, guidance”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place where a meeting (<i>þing</i>) is held</li></ol><p>From <i>þing</i> +&lrm; <i>stede</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intercession, intervention</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þynne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sēo</i></li></ol><p>From earlier <i>sīo</i>, through influence of the <i>þ-</i> forms, compare <i>þē</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þēod</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīedan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þēoden</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þēodisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīestre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīestru</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīestre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þyrel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīxl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þyslīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thistle</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þistil</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>distil</i>, Old Norse <i>þistill</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīestre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīestru</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þēowan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīewen</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þēowan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a beam</li><li>the pole of a wagon</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þį̄hslu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þinhslō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīxl</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clay</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>þōhæ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þanhǭ</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>þá</i>, Gothic <i>𐌸𐌰𐌷𐍉</i> (þāhō).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run to and fro, run up and down, run about</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;þocian</i> +&lrm; <i>-erian</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>þoka</i> (“to move, give way, yield, budge”), Middle English <i>thoke</i> (“lacking firmness, flabby”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tornado, whirlwind</li><li>whirlpool</li></ol><p>Unknown. Compare Old English <i>þoddettan</i> (“to push, strike, batter”), Old English <i>þyddan</i> (“to strike, thrust, push”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a rower's bench</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þoft</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellowship</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þoft</i> +&lrm; <i>-rǣden</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellowship</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þoft</i> +&lrm; <i>-sċipe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thought</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þą̄ht</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þanhtaz</i>, whence also Old High German <i>gidāht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thole, rowlock</li></ol><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>tholl</i>, Middle Dutch <i>dolle</i> (Dutch <i>dol</i>), Old Norse <i>þollr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of a patient disposition, patient, longsuffering</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patience</li></ol><p>From <i>þolian</i> +&lrm; <i>-mōd</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patience, endurance</li></ol><p>From <i>þolemōd</i> (“patient”) +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suffer, endure</li><li>to experience a punishment</li><li>to lose something that one has</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þolēn</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þulāną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;telh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þol</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) frying pan</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þolemōd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þonne</i></li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þonne</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þanō</i> (instrumental form of <i>&#42;sa</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tónoh₁</i> (instrumental form of <i>&#42;só</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þanan</i> (“thence”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þanc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þancian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þancweorþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>then, at that time</li><li>then, soon afterward</li><li>then, in that case</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>than</li><li>(<i>relative</i>) when</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þannē</i>, extension of <i>&#42;þan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þan</i> (“then”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;só</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þunor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>scribal abbreviation of <i>þonne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þurhġefeoht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Mercian form of <i>þurh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þurhġefeoht</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thorn</li><li>thorny bush</li><li>the runic character ᚦ (/θ, ð/)</li><li>the letter <b>Þ</b>, <b>þ</b> (/θ/ or /ð/)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þornu</i> (“thorn, sloe”).</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Germanic cognates include Old Saxon <i>thorn</i> (Low German <i>Dorn</i>, <i>Doorn</i>), Dutch <i>doorn</i>, Old High German <i>thorn</i> (German <i>Dorn</i>), Old Norse <i>þorn</i> (Swedish <i>törne</i>), Gothic <i>𐌸𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌽𐌿𐍃</i> (þaurnus). The Indo-European root is also the source of Old Church Slavonic <i>трънъ</i> (trŭnŭ) (Russian <i>тёрн</i> (tjorn, “sloe, blackthorn”), Slovak <i>tŕň</i>), Sanskrit <i>तृण</i> (tṛṇa, “grass”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thorny, full of thorns</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þornag</i>. Equivalent to <i>þorn</i> +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>village</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þorp</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>excrement; dung</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þustaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>howling, wailing, crying</li></ol><p><i>þoterian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þōþor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ball, sphere</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þanþr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þanþrą</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þanjaną</i> (“to extend, spread out”), equivalent to <i>þennan</i> +&lrm; <i>-þor</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þōþor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>power, force</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to urge</li><li>to rebuke, correct</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þrabōną</i> (“to trot, trample, press, urge”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;trep-</i> (“scamper, trip, trample, tremble, step”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time, season, while</li></ol><p>Unknown; not represented in any cognate languages. The form implies a Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þrēg-</i>, whence also Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þrag-</i> (compare Old English <i>þræġan</i> (“to run, proceed in a course”), Gothic <i>𐌸𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (þragjan, “to run”)). If so, then from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tregʰ-</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;dʰregʰ-</i> (“to run”). Compare also Old Norse <i>þræll</i> (“serf, slave”, literally “gofer, runner”). See English <i>thrall</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>occupied for a time, time-busy</li></ol><p>From <i>þrāg</i> (“time”) +&lrm; <i>bysiġ</i> (“busy”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrāg</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twist, turn, writhe</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þrāan</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>thrāia</i>, Old Saxon <i>thrāian</i>, Middle Dutch <i>draeien</i> (Dutch <i>draaien</i>), Old High German <i>draen</i>, <i>drāen</i> (German <i>drehen</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>threat</li><li>rebuke</li><li>affliction</li><li>punishment</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þrau</i>, oblique stem <i>*þrawā-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þrawō</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>drō</i>, Old Norse <i>þrá</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to threaten</li><li>to rebuke</li></ol><p>The forms imply contraction from pre-Old English <i>&#42;þrauhōjan</i>, although it is difficult to find cognates in Germanic that confirm the reconstructed *h. See also <i>&#42;þrawōną</i> and <i>&#42;þrawjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrēaung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þreax</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) dire distress, severe deprivation</li></ol><p><i>þrēa</i> +&lrm; <i>nīed</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) alternative form of <i>þrēanīed</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>band, troop</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reprove, correct, reprehend</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þraupōną</i> (“to punish”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þrawō</i> (“torment, punishment”), from <i>&#42;þrawjaną</i> (“to torment, injure, exhaust”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;trōw-</i> (“to beat, wound, kill, torment”). Akin to Old English <i>þrēagan</i> (“to rebuke, punish, chastise”), <i>þrēa</i> (“correction, punishment”), <i>þrōwian</i> (“to suffer”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rebuke, reproof</li></ol><p>From <i>þrēapian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crowd, swarm, throng, troop, army</li><li>violence, force, compulsion, oppression, punishment, retaliation, mistreatment</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þrautaz</i>, <i>&#42;þrautą</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to threaten</li><li>to force, urge (with threats)</li><li>to afflict, harass</li></ol><p>From <i>þrēat</i> +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>&#42;thrōton</i>, Middle Low German <i>drōten</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compulsion, force, violence, oppression</li><li>rebuke, reproof</li></ol><p>From <i>þrēatian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>threat</li><li>rebuke</li><li>punishment</li></ol><p>From pre-Old English <i>*þrēahung</i>. Equivalent to <i>þrēaġan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrēaung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rottenness; decay</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þraki</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þrekkaz</i> (“dirt”) (variant of <i>&#42;þrakjaz</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)terǵ-</i> (“manure, dung; to sully, decay”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrī</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-three</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-third</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-three</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-third</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-three</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-third</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-three</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-third</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventy-three</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-three</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-third</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-three</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þriju andi twaintig</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-third</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-three</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-third</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>three hundred</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrifeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>three hundred year(s) old</li></ol><p><i>þrēo hund</i> +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrim-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weary; to make or become weary</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þreutan</i> (“to harass, to weary”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>thriotan</i>, Dutch <i>verdrieten</i>, German <i>verdrießen</i>, Old Norse <i>þrjóta</i> (Icelandic <i>þrjóta</i>, Swedish <i>tryta</i>). Related to o-grade iterative verb Old English <i>þrēatian</i> (“to threaten, to push”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrēotīene</i> (“thirteen”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirteen</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þritehun</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>thretīne</i>, Old Saxon <i>thriutein</i>, Old High German <i>drīzehan</i>, Old Norse <i>þrettán</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirteenth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thirteenth</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>three times, thrice</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þriwō</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>threshold</li></ol><p>Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þreskwaþluz</i>, akin to Old High German <i>driscūfli</i>, Old Norse <i>þreskǫldr</i>, <i>þreskjǫldr</i>, Norwegian <i>terskel</i>, <i>treskel</i>, Swedish Swedish <i>tröskel</i> and Danish <i>tærskel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þersċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þresċold</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>three</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þrīz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tréyes</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old High German <i>drī</i>, French <i>trois</i>, Ancient Greek <i>τρεῖς</i> (treîs), Russian <i>три</i> (tri).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>three, tri- (combining form of <i>þrī</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þri-</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þri-</i>, combining form of <i>&#42;þrīz</i> (“three”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þreowa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>third</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>third</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þridjô</i>, related to the cardinal numeral <i>þrī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>a third</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great-great-grandfather</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) the third person</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þridda</i> (“third”) + <i>hād</i> (“grammatical person”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þridda</i>: third</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrī</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrifeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrifeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrēotan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>threefold, triple</li></ol><p>From <i>þri-</i> +&lrm; <i>-feald</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>thrēfald</i>, Old High German <i>drīfalt</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>three-footed</li></ol><p><i>þri-</i> +&lrm; <i>-fēte</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þreowa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þreowa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>threefold</li></ol><p><i>þrī-</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leap month inserted between ǣrra līþa/sēarmōnaþ and æfterra līþa/Mǣdmōnaþ during a leap year</li></ol><p>From <i>þri-</i> +&lrm; <i>līþa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) three</li><li>a third</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þrim</i>, dative form of <i>&#42;þrīz</i> (“three”). Akin to Old English <i>þrīe</i> (“three”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the fifth month of the year according to the Anglo-Saxon calendar</li><li>May</li></ol><p>From a combining form of <i>þri-</i> + <i>&#42;milċe</i>, apparently because animals could be milked three times a day at this time of year.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the fifth month of the year according to the Anglo-Saxon calendar</li><li>May</li></ol><p>From <i>þri</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;milċe</i> +&lrm; <i>mōnaþ</i>, lit. "three-milch-month."</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trinity</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) the Trinity</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þrinassī</i>, unless Old High German <i>drinessi</i> is a parallel formation or a calque from the Old English of Anglo-Saxon missionaries. In any case, equivalent to <i>þri-</i> +&lrm; <i>-nes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press, constrict, push on or through</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þringwan</i>.</p><p>Cognates</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>thringa</i>, Old Saxon <i>thringan</i> (Low German <i>dringen</i>), Dutch <i>dringen</i>, Old High German <i>dringan</i> (German <i>dringen</i>), Old Norse <i>þryngva</i>, Gothic <i>𐌸𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (þreihan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrīnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrīnes</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrī</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrifeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þreowa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an instrument of punishment</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>three-sided; triangular</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bold, brave</li><li>presumptuous, audacious, shameless</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>boldly, confidently</li><li>presumptuously, audaciously, shamelessly</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þrīstī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>three-stringed</li></ol><p><i>þri-</i> +&lrm; <i>-strenġe</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty</li></ol><p><i>þrī</i> +&lrm; <i>-tiġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thirtyfold</li></ol><p>From <i>þrītiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-feald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirtieth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thirtieth</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þrītiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-oþa</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thirty year(s) old</li></ol><p><i>þrītiġ</i> +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrītiġfeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrītiġfeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þreowa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þreowa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>three years old / three-year-old</li><li>three-year</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þriwintruz</i>. Equivalent to <i>þri-</i> +&lrm; <i>-wintre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rancid</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þrą̄h</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þranhaz</i>. It is the term's sole West Germanic descendant. Cognate with Icelandic <i>þrár</i>, Faroese <i>tráur</i> and Norwegian <i>trå</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oppressive; grievous</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oppression; affliction; hardship</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þruhtaz</i> (“relentless, oppressive”).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þruhtuz</i> (“strength, perseverance”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>þróttr</i> (“strength, might, valour, fortitude”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assembly</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>þorp</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrostle</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vapor, smoke</li><li>darkness</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smoky</li></ol><p><i>þrosm</i> (“smoke”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>song thrush</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þrostlā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gullet, windpipe</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þrote</i> +&lrm; <i>bolla</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>throtbola</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throat</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þrutǭ</i> (“swell”), perhaps ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ster-</i> (“to be strong, ready, stiff”). Cognate with Old High German <i>drozza</i> (German <i>Drossel</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrote</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þrōwode</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scorpion</li><li>martyr</li></ol><p>From <i>þrowian</i> +&lrm; <i>-end</i>. Literally, "the <i>throe</i> +&lrm; <i>-er</i>".</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>martyr</li></ol><p>From <i>þrowian</i> (“to be martyred”) +&lrm; <i>-ere</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suffer, endure</li><li>to be martyred</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þrōēn</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>druoen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>capable of suffering</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) in the passive voice</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>þrowiende</i> (“suffering”) +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrōwung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suffering</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) passion</li></ol><p><i>þrōwian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pipe or water-channel</li><li>a coffin or tomb</li></ol><p>Cognate with Old Norse <i>þró</i> (“hollowed receptacle, chest, trough”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swell with pride or anger</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þrūtan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þrūtaną</i> (“to swell”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrūtian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a swelling of the mind with anger</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>þrūtian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrī</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to press, crush, trample on, afflict, oppress</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to press, push, force away</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þrukkijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þrukkijaną</i> (“to press, crowd”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrifeald</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þyrel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þrymm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrim-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) the "king of glory;" God</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>þrymm</i> (“glory”) +&lrm; <i>cyning</i> (“king”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>host, army</li><li>force, power, glory</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þrumi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þrumjaz</i>, akin to Old Norse <i>þrymr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throne</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þrymm</i> (“power, glory”) +&lrm; <i>setl</i> (“seat”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrīpel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>þryċċan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>bird</i>) thrush</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þruskijā</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choke</li><li>to stifle</li><li>to suffocate</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þrusmijan</i>, <i>&#42;þrus(i)mōn</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>thresma</i>, Old Saxon <i>thrusmōn</i>. Equivalent to <i>þrosm</i> +&lrm; <i>-an</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þrysman</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þreowa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) strength, power, force</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þrūþi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þrūþiz</i> (“strength”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>þrúðr</i> (“strength”), whence the name <i>Þrúðr</i> (“daughter of Thor and Sif”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) mighty king (used of God)</li></ol><p>From <i>þrȳþ</i> +&lrm; <i>cyning</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) mighty</li></ol><p>From <i>þryþ</i> +&lrm; <i>swīþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þrȳþswīþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thread</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þrēduz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;treh₁-tu-</i>, from <i>&#42;terh₁-</i> (“rub, twist”). Germanic cognates include Old Frisian <i>thrēd</i>, Old Saxon <i>thrād</i>, Old High German <i>drāt</i>, Old Norse <i>þráðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tregʰ-</i>, apparently a variant of <i>&#42;dʰregʰ-</i> (“to run”). Compare Gothic <i>𐌸𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (þragjan). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slave</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Norse <i>þræll</i>, partly displacing the native word <i>þēow</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twist, write, roll about</li><li>to torture, torment</li><li>to press, complain</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>you, thou (nominative singular)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þū</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;túh₂</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>thū</i>, Old Saxon <i>thū</i>, Old Dutch <i>thū</i>, Old High German <i>dū</i>, Old Norse <i>þú</i>, Gothic <i>𐌸𐌿</i> (þū). Non-Germanic cognates include Latin <i>tū</i> and Ancient Greek <i>σύ</i> (sú).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tuft of foliage or branches</li><li>crest of a helmet</li><li>a type of standard or banner, bearing tufts or feathers</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þūb</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þūbaz</i>. Related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þūbǭ</i> (“hillock”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bearing foliage, leafy</li></ol><p>From <i>þūf</i> +&lrm; <i>-bǣre</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become leafy or bushy</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þūbōn</i> (“to grow lush”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place full of bushes; a thicket</li></ol><p>Possibly from earlier <i>&#42;þūfþ</i>, <i>&#42;þūfuþ</i>, derived from <i>þūfian</i> (“to become bushy”) +&lrm; <i>-uþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þuxian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thumb</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þūmō</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þūmô</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Frisian <i>thūma</i> (West Frisian <i>tomme</i>), Old Saxon <i>thūmo</i> (Low German <i>Dumen</i> or <i>Duum</i>), Dutch <i>duim</i>, Old High German <i>dūmo</i> (German <i>Daumen</i>), Danish <i>tomme</i>, Swedish <i>tumme</i>; and also with Latin <i>tumēre</i> (“to swell”), through the Indo-European root.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þunor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þunor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poisonous plant</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) aconite (genus <i>Aconitum</i>)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) hellebore (genus <i>Helleborus</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand out, stick up, be prominent</li><li>to make a noise</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thunder</li><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Þunor</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þunr</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Sanskrit <i>स्तनति</i> (stanati, “resound, reverberate, roar, thunder”), Ancient Greek <i>στένω</i> (sténō, “moan, groan, sigh”), Latin <i>tonō</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>стенати</i> (stenati) (Russian <i>стена́ть</i> (stenátʹ)), Lithuanian <i>steneti</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Þunor)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Anglo-Saxon god of thunder, equivalent to Old Norse Þórr and Old High German Donar</li><li>Jupiter (Roman god)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Þunr</i>. Identical to the word for thunder.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) gilt-head bream (<i>Sparus aurata</i>)</li></ol><p>The first element is from <i>þunor</i> (“thunder”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) bugle (<i>Ajuga reptans</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>þunor</i> (“thunder”) +&lrm; <i>clāfre</i> (“clover”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thursday</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Þunras dag</i> (“Thursday”). Equivalent to <i>Þunres</i> (“Thunor's, Thor's”) +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i> (“day”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wednesday night</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>Þunres</i> (“Thunor's, Thor's”) +&lrm; <i>niht</i> (“night”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thunder</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þunrōn</i>, equivalent to <i>þunor</i> (“thunder”) +&lrm; <i>-ian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þunor</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>þunwange</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temple (region of the skull)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þuniwang(ij)ā</i>, equivalent to <i>þynne</i> +&lrm; <i>wange</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>dunwengi</i>, Old Norse <i>þunnvangi</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þunwange</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þursdæġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to need [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>to be obliged (to do something)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þurban</i> (“to need”).</p><p>Germanic cognates include Old Frisian <i>&#42;thurva</i>, Old Saxon <i>thurƀan</i>, Old Dutch <i>thurvan</i>, Old High German <i>durfan</i>, Old Norse <i>þurfa</i>, Gothic <i>𐌸𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (þaurban).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þurh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>through</li><li>by means of: by, through, via</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þurhw</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;terh₂-</i> (“to pass through”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>through, over (with verbs of motion; compare Latin <i>trans-</i>)</li><li>thoroughly, completely; continually (with nouns, adjectives, adverbs; compare Latin <i>per-</i>)</li></ol><p>Old English <i>þurh</i>, <i>þuruh</i> (“through”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn through or thoroughly</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>birnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inspire</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>blāwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bore through, perforate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>borian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break through</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>brecan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>duruhbrehhan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transport; to move across or through</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>breġdan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring, bear, or lead through</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>brenġan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>duruhbringan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enjoy thoroughly</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>brūcan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cleanse thoroughly</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>clǣnsian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to creep through</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>crēopan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig or delve through</li><li>to bore through or pierce</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>delfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry through, perform, pass time</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>drēogan</i>; a modern descendant would be <i>&#42;throughdree</i> (see <i>dree</i>).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive through, pierce, transfix</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>drīfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dive through</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>dūfan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat through, eat out</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>etan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go over, traverse, go through; fare through, pass beyond, transcend, penetrate</li></ol><p>From <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>faran</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>duruhfaran</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transfix</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>fæstnian</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go over or go through, pierce (by weapon), penetrate, permeate, pervade</li></ol><p>From <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>gān</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>duruhgān</i>, Gothic <i>𐌸𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌷𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (þairhgaggan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go over or go through, perambulate, pierce</li></ol><p>From <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>gangan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>duruhgangan</i>, Gothic <i>𐌸𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌷𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (þairhgaggan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>war</li></ol><p>From <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>ġefeoht</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an act of looking through or looking over</li></ol><p><i>þurh-</i> (“through”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;lōcung</i> (“looking”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shoot through, transfix, pierce</li></ol><p>From <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċēotan</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>thruchskiata</i>, Old High German <i>duruhsciozzan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass through, glide through</li><li>to go through a subject; examine, consider</li></ol><p>From <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>sċrīþan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very guilty</li></ol><p>From <i>þurh</i> +&lrm; <i>scyldig</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seek out, seek through, make a search for, examine</li></ol><p>From <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēċan</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>duruhsouhhen</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see through, see into, penetrate with light</li></ol><p>From <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>sēon</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>duruhsehan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>see-through, transparent</li></ol><p><i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>-sīene</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to search through</li><li>to inquire into</li><li>to investigate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>smēaġan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to creep or crawl slowly through something</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to go painstakingly over or through details</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>smūgan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stab through, pierce, thrust through</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>stingan</i> (“to sting, stab”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to grant a request/proposal</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to carry something out</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to carry something through or on, accomplish, perform, perpetrate</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to achieve something, bring something about</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to afford something</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to undergo something</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to draw, drag something</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>tēon</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pass through</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to penetrate</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>wadan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look through, penetrate with sight</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>wlītan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thrust through</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>wrecan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to continue</li></ol><p><i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>wunian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pierce</li></ol><p><i>þurh-</i> +&lrm; <i>þȳrlian</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small ship</li><li>the bottom of a ship</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þurruk</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thursday</li></ol><p>Possibly from a contraction of <i>þunresdæġ</i> (“Thursday”, literally “Thunor's day”), but more likely from Old Norse <i>þōrsdagr</i> or Old Danish <i>þūrsdag</i> (“Thursday”) (compare modern Danish <i>torsdag</i>); all from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Þunras dag</i> (“day of the thunder god”), a calque of Latin <i>dies Iovis</i>, equivalent to <i>Þunres</i> (“genitive of the god's name <i>Þunor</i>”) +&lrm; <i>dæġ</i> (“day”). More at thunder, day.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thirst</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þurstu</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þurstuz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ters-</i> (“dry”).</p><p>Cognates include Ancient Greek <i>τέρσομαι</i> (térsomai), Albanian <i>djersë</i> (“sweat”), Sanskrit <i>तृष्यति</i> (tṛṣyati) and Latin <i>terra</i>.</p><p>The alternative form <i>þyrst</i> has the vocalism of <i>þyrstan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thirsty</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þurstug</i>, equivalent to <i>þurst</i> (“thirst”) +&lrm; <i>-iġ</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þurh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in this way, like this; thus</li><li>this (meaning "to this extent")</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þus</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thousand</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þūsundi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þūsundī</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leader of a thousand men, chiliarch</li></ol><p>From <i>þūsend</i> (“thousand”) +&lrm; <i>ealdormann</i> (“alderman”). Compare with Gothic <i>𐌸𐌿𐍃𐌿𐌽𐌳𐌹𐍆𐌰𐌸𐍃</i> (þusundifaþs).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thousandfold; a thousand</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þūsundīfalþaz</i>, equivalent to <i>þūsend</i> +&lrm; <i>-feald</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þyslīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make misty or dark</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þuksōn</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;þukisōn</i> (“to make cloudy, darken”), derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þukōną</i> (“to fog, be misty”), of uncertain origin. Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tewh₂-</i> (“to swell”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>þoka</i> (“fog, mist”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thong (of leather etc.)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þwanguz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>þvengr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>washing</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þwahl</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þwahlą</i>, derived from <i>&#42;þwahaną</i> (“to wash”), whence Old English <i>þwēan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wash</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þwahan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þwahaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þwǣnan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þwang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þweorh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cross, transverse</li><li>adverse, opposed</li><li>crooked, bent</li><li>angry</li><li>perverse, depraved</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þwerh</i> (“against, contrary”). Akin to Old English <i>þwēorian</i> (“to oppose, thwart”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>thwerh</i>, Old Norse <i>þverr</i>, Dutch <i>dwars</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þweorh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twirl</li><li>to stir</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þweraną</i> (“to stir”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þweorh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dwindle, diminish</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þweorh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cooking</i>) a stick for whipping milk, a whisk</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þwiril</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þwirilaz</i>, equivalent to <i>þweran</i> (“to stir, twirl”) +&lrm; <i>-el</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>thwiril</i>, Old Norse <i>þyrill</i> (“whisk”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þwirel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut, cut off</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þwītaną</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þwang</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þweorh</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a strip of material (e.g. bandage, ribbon, fillet, headband, etc.)</li><li>towel</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þwahilā</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to moisten, soften</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gentle</li><li>agreeable</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cooking</i>) a tool for beating or stirring (such as milk or dough)</li></ol><p>Form reflects Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þwairijaz</i> or <i>&#42;þwērijaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>therefore, for this reason, on that account; because, since; then</li><li><i>(used with comparative)</i> the</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>for the reason that, by reason of; because, since; then</li></ol><p>From an earlier *<i>þiu</i>, apparently formed from the demonstrative base *<i>þi-</i> with the nominal instrumental ending <i>-ō</i>. Cognate with Old Frisian <i>þiu</i>, Old Saxon <i>þiu</i>, Old High German <i>diu</i>, Old Norse <i>því</i>, Gothic <i>𐌸𐌴</i> (þē).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li><i>so that... not</i>: before, or else</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þȳ lǣs</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīedan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike, thrust, push</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þæder</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a leafy plant</li><li>a bush</li><li>a thicket</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þūbil</i>, equivalent to <i>þūf</i> +&lrm; <i>-el</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þefanþorn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female servant</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīefþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þēowan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þyslīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patience, long-suffering; endurance</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þuldi</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þuldiz</i>, <i>&#42;þulþiz</i> (“endurance, patience”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orator</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þuli</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Old English (Capitalized: Þyle)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thule (a legendary island said to be the northernmost place in the inhabited world)</li></ol><p>From Classical Latin <i>Thūlē</i>, <i>Thȳlē</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Θούλη</i> (Thoúlē), <i>Θῡ́λη</i> (Thū́lē)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þyslīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þyslīċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thumbstall</li></ol><p>From <i>þūma</i> (“thumb”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þēowan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seem, (impersonal, with dative subject) to think</li><li>to appear</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þunkijaną</i>. Cognate with Old Saxon <i>thunkian</i>, Old High German <i>dunken</i>, Old Norse <i>þykkja</i>, Gothic <i>𐌸𐌿𐌲𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (þugkjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honor, dignity, rank</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þing</i> +&lrm; <i>-þ</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þyncþ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thin</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þunnī</i>, whence also Old High German <i>dunni</i>, Old Norse <i>þunnr</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þynnu meolc</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>þynne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skim milk</li></ol><p>From <i>þynnu</i> (“thin”) +&lrm; <i>meolc</i> (“milk”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hole (perforation); an opening or aperture</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pierced, perforated</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þurhil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þurhilą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ter-</i> (“through, over”), equivalent to <i>þurh</i> (“through”) +&lrm; <i>-el</i>. More at through.</p><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þurhil</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þurhilaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ter-</i> (“through, over”), equivalent to <i>þurh</i> (“through”) +&lrm; <i>-el</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>durihhil</i>, <i>durchil</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a turner's shop</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>þȳrel</i> (“hole, perforation”) +&lrm; <i>hūs</i> (“house”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þȳrlian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þȳrlung</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þyrel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pierce</li><li>to make hollow, vain</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ter-</i> (“through, over”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piercing, perforation</li></ol><p><i>þȳrlian</i> +&lrm; <i>-ung</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a thistle, a small prickly plant.</li></ol><p>From <i>þyrne</i> +&lrm; <i>-ċen</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bush, thornbush</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of or consisting of thorns; thornen</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þurnīnaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>þorn</i> +&lrm; <i>-en</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crown of thorns</li></ol><p>Translation of Latin <i>spinea corona</i>, itself translating Hellenistic Ancient Greek <i>ἀκάνθινος στέφανος</i> (akánthinos stéphanos).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dry, become dry</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dry, make dry</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þurʀijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þurzijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not wet; dry</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þurʀī</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þursuz</i> (“dry”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ters-</i> (“dry”). Cognate with Old Saxon <i>thurri</i> (“dry”), Old High German <i>durri</i> (“dry”), Old Norse <i>þurr</i> (“dry”), Latin <i>terra</i> (“dry land”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monster, demon, giant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þuris</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þurisaz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>duris</i> (“demon”), Old Saxon <i>thuris</i> (“the rune þ”), Old Norse <i>þurs</i> (“giant”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þresċold</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þurst</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be thirsty (+ genitive = <i>for</i> something) (often impersonal, with dative or accusative of person)</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þurstijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þurstijaną</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Saxon <i>thurstian</i>, Old High German <i>dursten</i>, Old Norse <i>þyrsta</i>, Gothic <i>𐌸𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (þaursjan).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þurstiġ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>like this, this kind of</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) something like this</li><li>such</li></ol><p>Originally <i>þus</i> +&lrm; <i>-līċ</i>. Compare <i>swelċ</i> and <i>hwelċ</i>, which were formed similarly.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þisses</i></li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þisses</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Prince of Darkness; Satan</li></ol><p>Calque of Latin <i>prīnceps tenebrārum</i>, equivalent to <i>þȳstre</i> (“dark”) +&lrm; <i>ealdor</i> (“prince”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīestre</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīestrian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīestru</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þīestru</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þēowan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þēowan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þā</i></li></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þā</i></li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þā</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) roof</li><li>thatch</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þak</i>, whence also Old Frisian <i>thak</i>, Old Saxon <i>thak</i>, Old High German <i>dah</i>, Old Norse <i>þak</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þæċele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þæċele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þæċele</i></li></ol></html>:/ˈθæt͡ʃele/
<h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torch, light</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þakilā</i>, <i>&#42;þakilljā</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þakiljǭ</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þakjaną</i>, <i>&#42;þak-</i> (“to burn”). Related to Old High German <i>dahhezzen</i>, <i>dachezzen</i> (“to flame, flare up, blaze”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þæċele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þæċele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roof tile</li></ol><p>From <i>þæc</i> (“roof”) +&lrm; <i>tiġele</i> (“tile”). Akin to German <i>Dachziegel</i>, Swedish <i>taktegel</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>there (to that place)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þadrê</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>they, these; those, them</li></ol><p>Likely a late borrowing from Old Norse <i>þeir</i> (“they, those”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þai</i> (“they, those”), making it a doublet of native <i>þā</i> (“those”). Compare English <i>they</i>, a later (c. 1200s) borrowing from the same Old Norse source.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þeġn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make moist; moisten</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þainijan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þainijaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;teh₂-</i> (“to melt, flow, run”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þone</i></li></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þone</i></li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þone</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þanne</i></li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þanne</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>there</li><li>where</li><li>if</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þār</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þar</i>, whence also Old High German <i>dār</i>, Old Norse <i>þar</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>therein</li></ol><p><i>þǣr</i> (“there”) +&lrm; <i>in</i> (“in”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>þǣrin</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>theremid, therewith, with that, straightway, at the same time</li></ol><p>From <i>þǣr</i> (“there”) +&lrm; <i>mid</i> (“with”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thereof</li></ol><p><i>þǣr</i> (“there”) +&lrm; <i>of</i> (“of”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thereon</li><li>therein</li><li>thereof</li><li>thereinto</li></ol><p><i>þǣr</i> (“there”) +&lrm; <i>on</i> (“on, in”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immediately, right away, at once</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>þǣr</i> (“there”) +&lrm; <i>rihte</i> (“directly, exactly”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thereto</li></ol><p><i>þǣr</i> (“there”) +&lrm; <i>tō</i> (“to”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>besides, moreover</li><li>in addition thereto or to that</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þǣr</i> +&lrm; <i>tōēacan</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>opposite</li><li>on the contrary, thereagainst</li><li>reciprocally, in return</li><li>in reply against or refutation</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þǣr</i> +&lrm; <i>tōġēanes</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thereout</li><li>outside</li></ol><p><i>þǣr</i> +&lrm; <i>ūt</i></p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>therewith, in return, in exchange, against</li></ol><p>From <i>þǣr</i> (“there”) +&lrm; <i>wiþ</i> (“against, opposite, toward”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thereafter</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>þǣr</i> (“there”) +&lrm; <i>æfter</i> (“after”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>afterwards</li><li>regarding, in respect of, on that account, therefore, so</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>as, because</li><li>after</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>as, to the extent that</li><li>because</li><li>after</li><li>what</li><li>since</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leaven; yeast</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þaisimō</i> (“yeast, leaven”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;teh₂-</i> (“to melt, flow, knead, mold, dwindle”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>exophoric</i>) they, that, those (regardless of gender and number; compare Old Norse <i>þat</i>, Dutch <i>dat</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>that</li><li>so (so that)</li><li>without <i>(followed by a negative phrase)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>only</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>i.e., that is</li></ol><p>From Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;þat isti</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>daz ist</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li><i>(relative)</i> what, that which</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>that</li><li>so, so that</li></ol><p>Assimilation of <i>þæt</i> <i>þe</i>.</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>scribal abbreviation of <i>oþþe</i> (“or”)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>vel</i> (“or”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>efest</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ele</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>oxan</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>eþel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēþel</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ēþian</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scribal abbreviation of <i>bisċeop</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Xmas, Christmas</li></ol><p>From <i>Cristes mæsse</i>, the abbreviation of <i>Crist</i> by the symbol <i>X</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Χ</i> (Kh, “(letter chi)”), from <i>Χριστός</i> (Khristós, “Christ”).</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>for</i> (“for”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>fisċ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>flōd</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>þæt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>þis</i> (“this (neuter)”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>þēr</i> (“there”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>æfter</i> (“after”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>on</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>Ōswine</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>and</i> (“and”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>eall</i> (“all”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>æþele</i> (“noble”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>hring</i> (“ring”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>ġe-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>Iūdēas</i> (“Jews”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>ġe-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>wudu</i> (“wood”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>wylf</i> (“she-wolf”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>wæs</i> (“was”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>hwæþre</i> (“yet, however”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>hīe</i> (“they, them”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>Hildeþrȳþ</i> (“a female given name”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>Hildegȳþ</i> (“a female given name”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>hēr</i> (“here”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>iċ</i> (“I”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>iċ</i> (“I”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>in</i> (“in”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>is</i> (“is”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>siġe</i> (“victory”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>sigorbēacn</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>twēġen</i> (“two”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>til</i> (“to, until”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>bita</i> (“biter”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>Bēagnōþ</i> (“a male given name”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>Eþelwine</i> (“a male given name”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>Eþelbeorht</i> (“a male given name”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>and</i> (“and”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>mǣġ</i> (“kinsman”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>mec</i> (“me”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>mēd</i> (“reward”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>dōm</i> (“judgement”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>dēad</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>Ēadrǣd</i> (“a male given name”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, variant of Cynnburg &lsqb;c.670 <small>CE</small>&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>cynn</i> (“family”) +&lrm; <i>burg</i> (“fortress, burgh”)</p></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, variant of Cyneswiþ &lsqb;c.670 <small>CE</small>&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Runic spelling of <i>Crist</i> (“Christ”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>scribal abbreviation of <i>and</i> (“and”)</li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>scribal abbreviation of <i>þæt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>þæt is</i></li></ol></html><h3>Old English</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>þurh</i></li></ol></html>